consiStSearch([["Alaska Supreme Court","Alaska · courts","Final appellate jurisdiction in all actions. Civil appeals of right from the superior court go straight here (there is no intermediate civil appeals court), as do superior-court decisions on administrative appeals. Reviews the Court of Appeals, and superior-court decisions on civil appeals from district court, only by discretionary petition for hearing. Also adopts the court rules and regulates the bar.","#st-ak"],["Alaska Court of Appeals","Alaska · courts","Four judges. Hears criminal, post-conviction, juvenile delinquency (AS 47.12), extradition, habeas corpus, probation and parole, and bail appeals, plus excessive-sentence appeals. It does not hear ordinary civil appeals.","#st-ak"],["Superior Court (four judicial districts)","Alaska · courts","One statewide trial court of general jurisdiction, organized in the First (Southeast), Second, Third, and Fourth Judicial Districts. Original jurisdiction in all civil and criminal matters, including probate and guardianship, and appellate jurisdiction over district court and agency appeals. A case within the district court’s concurrent jurisdiction may not be filed in superior court except as supreme court rules all","#st-ak"],["District Court and magistrate judges","Alaska · courts","A district court sits in each judicial district. Civil jurisdiction covers money claims up to $100,000 per defendant (exclusive of costs, interest, and fees), personal property, penalties, and evictions up to $100,000, plus misdemeanors. Claims up to $10,000 are heard as small claims on request. Magistrate judges handle many rural and limited matters.","#st-ak"],["No anti-SLAPP statute found in the Alaska Statutes","Alaska · anti-SLAPP","Alaska has no special motion to strike, discovery stay, or speech-specific fee shifting in the statute chapters where such laws normally sit. AS 09.65 (Actions, Immunities, Defenses, and Duties) lists dozens of immunities, from emergency aid to defensive force, but none for speech or petitioning. AS 09.60 (Costs and Attorney Fees) has no SLAPP provision either, and no Title 9 chapter is devoted to the subject. Defend","#st-ak-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: Civil Rule 81(a)(2) motion, local counsel, Bar fee","Alaska · out of state","A member in good standing of a federal court bar or the highest court of any state or territory may appear in a particular case on motion. Serve the motion on the Alaska Bar Association’s executive director and on all parties. File it with (A) the name, address, and phone number of the associated Alaska Bar member, (B) that member’s signed written consent, (C) a good-standing and character certificate executed no ear","#st-ak-oos"],["The fee is a full year’s active dues, per case, per year","Alaska · out of state","The Bar bylaws set the Civil Rule 81 fee at the annual active-member fee. It is owed per case per year until you notify the Bar that the case is closed or that you have withdrawn. The bylaws leave the dollar amount to the Board of Governors, so check the Bar’s current dues schedule. Alaska Bar Bylaws art. III, §§ 1(a), 4 (as amended through Oct. 15, 2026)","#st-ak-oos"],["Local counsel signs everything and outranks you","Alaska · out of state","You may take part in all respects, but every document that needs counsel’s signature must also bear local counsel’s signature. Local counsel is primarily responsible to the court for all stages of the case, and local counsel’s authority is superior to yours. Alaska R. Civ. P. 81(a)(2), (a)(3)","#st-ak-oos"],["Appellate courts: motion, or carry over the trial-court admission","Alaska · out of state","Out-of-state lawyers may be admitted in the Supreme Court or Court of Appeals on motion under Civil Rule 81(a)(2)–(3), and the clerk may decide the motion. If you were already admitted in the trial court in the same case, no motion is needed. File the trial court’s order, the good-standing certificate given to the trial court, and local counsel’s contact information with the appellate clerk. Alaska R. App. P. 103(b),","#st-ak-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances are allowed in civil cases","Alaska · out of state","In a non-criminal case, a lawyer may appear for limited purposes, such as a deposition, hearing, discovery, or a motion. The entry of appearance must expressly say it is limited, define the limit by date, time period, or subject matter, and be served on all parties. When the limited task ends, withdraw without court approval by filing a notice certifying completion. The notice must give the client’s current service a","#st-ak-oos"],["TrueFiling e-filing is mandatory for lawyers","Alaska · out of state","TrueFiling is used in all trial court locations. All lawyers registered with the Alaska Bar must use it, and after a location goes live paper is accepted only during an outage. A filing made before 11:59 p.m. Alaska Time is filed that day, except protective-order and eviction petitions, which are filed the next business day if submitted after hours. Files must be under 10 MB, and a motion, memorandum, and exhibits go","#st-ak-oos"],["Temporary practice without pro hac vice (Rule 5.5)","Alaska · out of state","Alaska follows the Model Rule 5.5 safe harbors. A lawyer admitted elsewhere may work temporarily in association with an actively participating Alaska lawyer, on matters related to a pending or expected proceeding where the lawyer is or expects to be authorized, on ADR related to the home practice, or on other work arising from the home practice. In-house and federally authorized work is also permitted. An office or s","#st-ak-oos"],["Governing code: Alaska Rules of Professional Conduct","Alaska · ethics","Adopted by Supreme Court order, mostly rescinded and repromulgated by SCO 1680 (effective April 15, 2009), and amended since, including SCO 1905 (2017) and SCO 1984 (2022). The structure follows the ABA Model Rules, with Alaska comments, but definitions appear at Rule 9.1 instead of 1.0, and Rules 7.4 (fields of practice) and 7.5 (firm names and letterheads) are still in force. Alaska R. Prof. Conduct (court PDF, cur","#st-ak-ethics"],["Written disclosure if malpractice coverage is under $100,000/$300,000","Alaska · ethics","You must tell each client in writing if you lack malpractice insurance of at least $100,000 per claim and $300,000 annual aggregate, and again if coverage drops below those amounts or ends. Keep a record of the disclosures for six years after the representation ends. Salaried government lawyers and in-house counsel are exempt. The Model Rules have no such duty. Alaska R. Prof. Conduct 1.4(c)","#st-ak-ethics"],["Written fee agreement over $1,000, with a fee-shifting warning","Alaska · ethics","If the fee will exceed $1,000, the basis or rate must be in a written fee agreement that describes the scope and includes the Rule 1.4(c) insurance disclosure. In litigation, the agreement must warn that the client may be liable for the opposing party’s costs and fees if the client loses (because of Civil Rule 82). Contingent-fee agreements must also state the costs and fees the client may owe the lawyer or the other","#st-ak-ethics"],["Confidentiality uses “confidences and secrets,” with resolve-doubts-against-disclosure","Alaska · ethics","Rule 1.6 protects privileged “confidences” and “secrets.” A secret is other information that the client asked to keep confidential or whose disclosure would foreseeably embarrass or harm the client. Any uncertainty must be resolved against disclosure. The exceptions add preventing wrongful execution or incarceration of another and include the crime-fraud financial-harm exceptions. The Model Rule instead protects all ","#st-ak-ethics"],["No screening cure for lateral hires","Alaska · ethics","Alaska’s imputation rule has only the personal-interest exception. It lacks the Model Rule 1.10(a)(2) provision that lets a firm screen a newly hired lawyer’s former-client conflict, so a lateral’s conflict is imputed to the whole firm unless the affected client waives under Rule 1.7. Alaska R. Prof. Conduct 1.10(a), (c)","#st-ak-ethics"],["Misconduct rule: harassment clause, no ‘prejudicial to justice’ catch-all","Alaska · ethics","Rule 8.4(f) bans knowing harassment or invidious discrimination toward court staff, other firms’ lawyers and staff, parties, witnesses, and seated jurors. Within the lawyer’s own firm, it applies only after a final agency or judicial finding of employment misconduct or discrimination. Alaska’s list has no Model Rule 8.4(d) ban on conduct “prejudicial to the administration of justice.” Its (d) is the improper-influenc","#st-ak-ethics"],["Fee splitting between firms","Alaska · ethics","Lawyers in different firms may divide a fee only in proportion to each firm’s contribution, or if each firm assumes joint responsibility by written agreement with the client. The client must agree to each firm’s participation, including its share. Domestic-relations contingency fees and criminal-defense contingency fees are banned. Alaska R. Prof. Conduct 1.5(d), (e)","#st-ak-ethics"],["Losing costs you fees: Civil Rule 82","Alaska · land mine","Alaska shifts partial fees to the prevailing party in nearly every civil case. On a money judgment after a contested trial, the award is 20% of the first $25,000 and 10% of the rest. Without a money judgment, it is 30% (tried) or 20% (not tried) of reasonable actual fees. Budget for it, tell the client in the fee agreement (RPC 1.5(b)), and file your own motion within 10 days after the clerk’s distribution date on th","#st-ak-unique"],["Ignoring a Rule 68 offer can cost up to 75% of actual fees","Alaska · land mine","If the final judgment is at least 5% worse for you than an offer you rejected (10% with multiple defendants), you pay all costs plus 75%, 50%, or 30% of the offeror’s reasonable actual fees from the offer date. The percentage depends on whether the offer was served within 60 days after the initial-disclosure date in the pretrial order, more than 90 days before trial, or later. This applies to plaintiffs and defendant","#st-ak-unique"],["Five days to bump the judge","Alaska · land mine","The peremptory change of judge must be filed within 5 days after notice of assignment (or of your appearance, if you enter late), and before trial. Taking part in an evidentiary merits proceeding or a pretrial conference before the judge waives it. Alaska R. Civ. P. 42(c)(3), (4)","#st-ak-unique"],["Answer in 20 days, not 21","Alaska · land mine","The answer is due 20 days after service (40 for the State, its officers and agencies, and some others). After a Rule 12 motion is denied, the answer is due 10 days after notice of the ruling. Alaska R. Civ. P. 12(a)","#st-ak-unique"],["Clocks run from the clerk’s certificate of distribution","Alaska · land mine","Time to appeal and to seek reconsideration or review runs from the “date of notice.” For written orders, that is the date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution, not the date the judge signed. But a motion to alter or amend under Rule 59(f) must be served within 10 days after entry, and a new-trial motion within 10 days after the distribution date. Reconsideration under Rule 77(k) is due within 10 days, is limite","#st-ak-unique"],["A notice of appeal needs a package, and fee proceedings don’t toll","Alaska · land mine","File in the Supreme Court within 30 days from the distribution date on the judgment. Include a docketing statement, the judgment, a statement of points on appeal, the fee or a waiver motion, a $750 cost bond (or supersedeas or deposit), a transcript designation, and proof of service. The clerk will refuse a notice that lacks these items. The appeal clock is not stopped while costs, prejudgment interest, or Rule 82 fe","#st-ak-unique"],["Ten days to seek interlocutory review","Alaska · land mine","A petition for review of a non-appealable order is due 10 days after the date of notice of the order, with a 15-page limit. A timely reconsideration motion in the trial court restarts the period. Alaska R. App. P. 402, 403(a)(1)","#st-ak-unique"],["Pro hac vice fee recurs every year","Alaska · land mine","The Civil Rule 81 fee is the full annual active-member dues, charged per case per year until you tell the Bar the case is closed or you have withdrawn. Local counsel must co-sign every filing. Alaska Bar Bylaws art. III, § 4; Alaska R. Civ. P. 81(a)(2)","#st-ak-unique"],["Disclose thin malpractice coverage in writing","Alaska · land mine","If you appear in Alaska without malpractice coverage of at least $100,000 per claim and $300,000 aggregate, you must tell the client in writing. The disclosure belongs in any written fee agreement over $1,000. Alaska R. Prof. Conduct 1.4(c), 1.5(b)","#st-ak-unique"],["Nonresident plaintiffs can be made to post security","Alaska · land mine","When the plaintiff lives out of state or is a foreign corporation, a defendant who demands it within 30 days after discovering the nonresidence stays the case until an undertaking of at least $200 is filed. The undertaking secures the costs and attorney fees that may be awarded against the plaintiff. AS 09.60.060","#st-ak-unique"],["Wrong court for smaller cases","Alaska · land mine","Claims within the district court’s concurrent civil jurisdiction (up to $100,000 per defendant) may not be filed in superior court except as supreme court rules provide. Protective-order petitions are an exception. Check the rules before filing a smaller case in superior court. AS 22.10.020(a); AS 22.15.030(a)(1)","#st-ak-unique"],["E-filing mechanics","Alaska · land mine","TrueFiling is mandatory for lawyers. Combine the motion, memorandum, and exhibits into one PDF, with the proposed order as a separate document in the same bundle. Files must be under 10 MB, and the cutoff is 11:59 p.m. Alaska Time. Admin. Bull. No. 92 ¶¶ 1(a), 7(a), 13(a)–(b), 15","#st-ak-unique"],["One peremptory change of judge per side: 5 days, no reasons","Alaska · judges","In superior and district court, each side gets one change of judge (and one of a master) as of right. Parties aligned on the same side count as one side, though the presiding judge may allow an extra change for a party adverse to a co-party. File a “Notice of Change of Judge” naming the judge, with no grounds and no affidavit, before trial starts and within 5 days after notice that the case is assigned to a specific ","#st-ak-unique"],["Waiver by participation","Alaska · judges","You waive the peremptory change against a permanently assigned judge by knowingly taking part before that judge in a merits proceeding that considers evidence or affidavits, a pretrial conference, or the start of trial, or by agreeing to that judge. After a timely notice, the presiding judge reassigns within the district. If the new judge later becomes unavailable, the parties’ rights are restored as they stood befor","#st-ak-unique"],["Statutory peremptory disqualification affidavit (AS 22.20.022)","Alaska · judges","The statute separately lets a party or attorney file a good-faith affidavit that a fair and impartial trial cannot be had. The presiding judge then reassigns without proof. The affidavit is due within five days after the case is at issue on a question of fact or after assignment to a judge, whichever is later, unless good cause is shown. A party may file only one, and no more than two may be filed in an action. How t","#st-ak-unique"],["Disqualification for cause","Alaska · judges","A judicial officer may not act where the officer is a party, a material witness, related within the third degree to a party or counsel, or has a direct financial interest (personally or through a spouse or child). Disqualification also applies if the officer represented or counseled a party, or opposed one, within two years, or was represented by a party’s attorney within two years, or if the officer’s former firm ad","#st-ak-unique"],["Constitutional claimants are shielded from fee shifting","Alaska · speech","In a civil case or appeal to establish, protect, or enforce a federal or Alaska constitutional right, a claimant who prevails on the right gets full reasonable fees and costs for the constitutional claims. This applies only if the claimant lacked sufficient economic incentive to sue anyway. A losing claimant may not be ordered to pay the other side’s fees on the constitutional claims if the claim was not frivolous an","#st-ak-unique"],["No speech-specific procedural shield","Alaska · speech","No anti-SLAPP motion or other speech-specific early-dismissal procedure appears in the collected statutes or rules (see the anti-SLAPP entry). Alaska constitutional speech provisions were not collected and are not summarized here. AS 09.60, 09.65 (checked)","#st-ak-unique"],["Rule 82: the loser pays part of the winner’s fees","Alaska · unique rules","In civil cases the prevailing party is awarded partial fees on a schedule. For a money judgment with a contested trial, it is 20% of the first $25,000 and 10% of the rest. Contested without trial, it is 18%, 8%, 6%, and 2% across bands at $25,000, $100,000, and $500,000; non-contested, 10%, 3%, 2%, and 1%. With no money judgment, the award is 30% of reasonable actual fees if tried and 20% if not. The court may vary t","#st-ak-unique"],["Rule 68 offers shift actual fees, both directions","Alaska · unique rules","Either side may serve an offer more than 10 days before trial; it stays open 10 days. If the judgment is at least 5% less favorable to the offeree (10% with multiple defendants), the offeree pays costs plus 75%, 50%, or 30% of the offeror’s reasonable actual fees from the offer date, depending on how early the offer was served. The offeror gets the larger of this or the Rule 82 award, not both. Cases filed before Aug","#st-ak-unique"],["Tighter discovery than the federal rules","Alaska · unique rules","Without a stipulation, each side (plaintiffs, defendants, third-party defendants) may take only three depositions of witnesses other than parties, independent trial experts, treating physicians, and necessary document custodians; more requires leave. Depositions are limited to 6 hours for parties, independent experts, and treating physicians, and 3 hours for others. Each party may serve 30 interrogatories on another ","#st-ak-unique"],["Divorce cases: automatic financial disclosure in 45 days","Alaska · unique rules","In divorce and legal separation actions, each party must disclose detailed financial information without a request within 45 days after the answer is filed. This includes signed releases, two years of account history, three years of tax returns, and recent pay stubs. Alaska R. Civ. P. 26.1","#st-ak-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope and construction of rules","Alaska · civil rules","These rules govern all civil proceedings (legal, equitable, or otherwise) in the superior court and, as far as they apply, in the district court. They are to be construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","Alaska · civil rules","There is only one form of action, called a “civil action.”","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencement of action and venue","Alaska · civil rules","A civil action starts by filing a complaint, which the clerk will accept only with a completed case description form and (unless filed by fax or email where allowed) a postage-paid envelope addressed to the plaintiff for mailing the summonses. Real property actions (ejectment, quiet title, partition, lien enforcement, etc.) must be filed in the judicial district where the property lies; other actions may be filed whe","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 4. Process (summons and service)","Alaska · civil rules","The clerk issues a summons on filing of the complaint, and process is served by a peace officer, a process server specially appointed by the Commissioner of Public Safety, or, where allowed, by registered or certified mail with restricted delivery (complete when the return receipt is signed); the rule lists how to serve individuals, minors, incompetent persons, corporations, partnerships, the State (by mail to the At","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 5. Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","Alaska · civil rules","Papers after the complaint (orders, pleadings, discovery papers, written motions, notices, offers of judgment) must be served on every party, on the attorney if a party is represented, by hand delivery, first-class mail (complete on mailing), fax or email under Rule 5.1, or commercial delivery (complete on delivery). Papers must be filed before or within a reasonable time after service, but discovery materials, Rule ","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 5.1. Filing and service by fax and email","Alaska · civil rules","Documents may be filed by fax or email only as permitted by a presiding judge’s administrative order or with prior written consent of the assigned judge, and the filer must keep the original; filings received before 4:30 p.m. on a court business day are filed that day, and later ones on the next business day. Courts must accept faxed certified copies of out-of-state, tribal, or territorial domestic violence protectiv","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 5.2. Foreign orders and judgments","Alaska · civil rules","When state law requires notice of registration of an out-of-state support order, income withholding order, or child custody determination, the court sends it by first-class mail unless the registering party asks for certified mail or personal service, and the time to request a hearing runs from mailing or service. The court mails notice of filing of a foreign judgment by first-class mail, and the creditor may mail an","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 5.3. Electronic distribution by the court","Alaska · civil rules","The court system may email notices, orders, and judgments to attorneys, court-appointed professionals, and agencies, and self-represented parties may opt in (filing by email counts as opting in). Alaska bar members must keep a current email address with the Alaska Bar Association, agencies and appointed professionals register addresses with the court, and email changes must be reported immediately. Emailed distributi","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 5.4. Registration of tribal court orders","Alaska · civil rules","Any tribe, tribal organization, or person may ask the superior court to register a federally recognized tribe’s court order in a divorce, dissolution, custody, paternity, or name change case by filing a request, a copy of the order, a sworn statement that it has not been vacated, stayed, or modified, and contact information; the court mails these and a notice to the tribal court and the parties. A party who objects m","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 6. Time","Alaska · civil rules","When counting time, exclude the triggering day and include the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday, or legal holiday; for periods under 7 days, intermediate weekends and holidays are excluded. The court may extend a deadline for cause if asked before it expires, or after it expires for excusable neglect, but may not extend deadlines under Rules 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), and (f), and 60(b) except as those rules a","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings allowed and form of motions","Alaska · civil rules","The permitted pleadings are a complaint, answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a cross-claim, third-party complaint, and third-party answer; the court may order a reply to an answer. A request for a court order must be made by motion, in writing unless made at a hearing or trial, stating the grounds with particularity and the relief sought, and motion procedure is governed by Rule 77. Demurrers, pleas, and excep","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","Alaska · civil rules","A claim for relief needs a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment, which may seek alternative or different types of relief. Defenses must be stated plainly and each allegation admitted or denied (lack of knowledge counts as a denial), affirmative defenses listed in the rule (such as statute of limitations, release, res judicata, contributory negligence, assumption of risk, a","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","Alaska · civil rules","A party need not plead capacity to sue or be sued except to show jurisdiction, and anyone challenging capacity or legal existence must do so by specific negative averment. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity, while intent and other states of mind may be alleged generally; conditions precedent may be alleged generally but must be denied specifically. Official documents, judgments, and statutes may be ","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","Alaska · civil rules","Every pleading needs a caption with the court, judicial district, city, case title, case number, and the Rule 7(a) designation, and the complaint must name all parties with as much of each full legal name as is known. Claims and defenses go in numbered paragraphs, separate claims and defenses in separate counts where that helps clarity, and attached written instruments are part of the pleading. A complaint, countercl","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing of papers and representations to the court","Alaska · civil rules","Every pleading, motion, and other paper must be signed by an attorney of record or by the unrepresented party and must list the signer’s address, email, and phone; an unsigned paper is struck unless promptly corrected, and verification is not required unless a rule or statute says so. By presenting a paper, the signer certifies after reasonable inquiry that it is not for an improper purpose, its legal contentions are","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections; motions on the pleadings","Alaska · civil rules","A defendant must answer within 20 days after service of the summons and complaint (the same 20 days applies to answering a cross-claim or replying to a counterclaim), while the State, its officers and agencies, defendants served through a state agent, and individuals served in a foreign country get 40 days; Alaska’s 20-day period is shorter than the federal 21 days. If a Rule 12 motion is denied or deferred, or a mor","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaims and cross-claims","Alaska · civil rules","A party must plead as a counterclaim any claim against an opposing party arising from the same transaction or occurrence, unless it is already pending in another action or the court lacks needed third parties, and may plead unrelated claims as permissive counterclaims, which may exceed or differ from the opposing claim. Counterclaims maturing after pleading, or omitted through oversight or excusable neglect, may be a","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","Alaska · civil rules","A defending party may serve a third-party complaint on a nonparty who may be liable for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim, without leave if filed within 10 days after serving the original answer and otherwise only by motion. The third-party defendant responds under Rules 12 and 13 and may assert defenses and related claims against the plaintiff, who may in turn assert related claims against the third-party defenda","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Alaska · civil rules","A party may amend once as a matter of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 20 days after service if no response is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar; otherwise amendment requires the opponent’s written consent or leave of court, freely given when justice requires, and the response to an amended pleading is due within the remaining original time or 10 days after service, whichever","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 16. Pretrial conferences, scheduling, and management","Alaska · civil rules","The court may hold pretrial conferences to speed disposition, manage the case, improve trial preparation, and encourage settlement, including through mediation or other alternative dispute resolution. Except in exempt cases, the judge must issue a scheduling order within 90 days after the defendants appear (or as a local uniform pretrial order provides) setting deadlines to join parties, identify and join potentially","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 16.1. Special procedures for reducing litigation delay (rescinded)","Alaska · civil rules","This rule has been rescinded and is no longer in effect.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 16.2. Informal trials in domestic relations cases","Alaska · civil rules","In divorce, property division, custody, and child support cases (including motions to modify), the parties and court may voluntarily agree to an informal trial, where the court may admit any relevant evidence even if inadmissible under formal evidence rules and normally only the parties testify. Each party speaks to the court under oath, only the judge questions the parties, documents and expert reports may be admitt","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 17. Parties plaintiff and defendant; capacity","Alaska · civil rules","Every action must be brought in the name of the real party in interest, though executors, guardians, trustees, and others authorized by statute may sue in their own names, and no case is dismissed on this ground until there has been a reasonable time after objection for ratification, joinder, or substitution. An individual’s capacity to sue is set by the law of the person’s domicile, a corporation’s by the law where ","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims and remedies","Alaska · civil rules","A party may join as many claims, legal or equitable, as it has against an opposing party. Claims that once had to await the outcome of another claim may be joined in one action, so a plaintiff may seek money and to set aside a fraudulent conveyance without first getting a money judgment.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 19. Required joinder of parties","Alaska · civil rules","A person who can be served and whose joinder will not destroy subject matter jurisdiction must be joined if complete relief is impossible without the person or if the person’s absence would impair the person’s interest or expose existing parties to inconsistent obligations; a joined party who validly objects to venue is dismissed. If joinder is not feasible, the court decides in equity and good conscience whether to ","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","Alaska · civil rules","Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, in one action if the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact. Not every party needs an interest in all the relief, and judgment may be given for or against parties according to their rights and liabilities. The court may order separate trials or other protective orders to prevent delay, expense, or prej","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","Alaska · civil rules","Misjoinder of parties is not a ground for dismissal. The court may add or drop parties at any stage on motion or on its own, on just terms, and may sever any claim against a party.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","Alaska · civil rules","A plaintiff exposed to double or multiple liability may join the competing claimants as defendants and require them to interplead, even if their claims do not share a common origin or the plaintiff denies liability. A defendant facing similar exposure may seek interpleader by cross-claim or counterclaim, and this rule supplements Rule 20 joinder.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","Alaska · civil rules","One or more members may sue or be sued for a class only if the class is too numerous for joinder, there are common questions, the representatives’ claims are typical, and they will adequately protect the class, plus one of three conditions: risk of inconsistent or impairing separate judgments, conduct generally applicable to the class warranting injunctive or declaratory relief, or predominance of common questions an","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 23.1. Derivative actions by shareholders","Alaska · civil rules","A holder of shares, voting trust certificates, or a beneficial interest may sue on a corporation’s behalf through a verified complaint alleging ownership at the time of the wrong, though the court may allow a non-qualifying shareholder to proceed after a hearing on a strong prima facie case and other listed factors. The plaintiff must first make a formal demand on the board unless excused because a majority of direct","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 23.2. Actions relating to unincorporated associations","Alaska · civil rules","An action by or against members of an unincorporated association as a class, through named representative members, may proceed only if the representatives will fairly and adequately protect the association and its members. The court may issue management orders like those in Rule 23(d), and dismissal or compromise follows Rule 23(e).","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","Alaska · civil rules","On timely application, a person must be allowed to intervene if the person claims an interest in the subject of the action that may be impaired and is not adequately represented, and may be allowed to intervene when the person’s claim or defense shares a common question of law or fact, or when a government officer or agency administers a statute or order a party relies on. The applicant must serve a motion stating th","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","Alaska · civil rules","If a party dies and the claim survives, the successors, representatives, or any party may move to substitute, serving parties under Rule 5 and nonparties under Rule 4, and the action is dismissed as to the deceased party unless the motion is made within 90 days after the death is suggested on the record. The court may continue the case by or against the representative of a party who becomes incompetent, or substitute","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 26. General discovery provisions and duty of disclosure","Alaska · civil rules","Except in Rule 16(g)-exempt cases, adoptions, and prisoner suits against the State under AS 09.19, each party must, without a request, disclose the factual basis of its claims or defenses, witnesses with relevant information, recorded statements, relevant documents and photos, insurance agreements, damages computations, and (an Alaska addition) potentially responsible persons under AS 09.17.080 against whom it will s","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 26.1. Discovery and disclosure in divorce and legal separation actions","Alaska · civil rules","In divorce and legal separation cases, each party must, without a request and within 45 days after the answer is filed, disclose real property interests with appraisals and assessments from the last 2 years; signed releases valid for 6 months for employer earnings and benefits, retirement plans, and financial accounts; all accounts used in the past 2 years with 3 months of statements; debts; personal property worth o","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions before action or pending appeal","Alaska · civil rules","A person who expects to be a party to an Alaska action but cannot yet bring it may file a verified petition in superior court to perpetuate testimony, describing the expected case, the facts to be proved, expected adverse parties, and witnesses, and must serve notice at least 20 days before the hearing under Rule 4(d) (or 4(h) mail service), with court-appointed counsel for unserved adverse parties. If perpetuation m","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Alaska · civil rules","In Alaska, depositions are taken before an officer authorized to administer oaths or a court-appointed person; outside Alaska, before a person authorized locally or by U.S. law, a court-commissioned person, or under a letter rogatory, and foreign letter-rogatory evidence is not excluded merely for being non-verbatim or unsworn. A deposition may not be taken before a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or of a ","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations about discovery practice","Alaska · civil rules","Unless the court directs otherwise, parties may agree in writing to take depositions before any person, at any time or place, on any notice and in any manner, and to modify other discovery procedures and limits. However, stipulations extending response times under Rules 33, 34, and 36 need court approval if they would interfere with deadlines set for motions, hearings, or trial.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions upon oral examination","Alaska · civil rules","Any person may be deposed without leave, but leave is required for a prisoner, for a deponent already deposed, for a deposition before the Rule 26(d) time (unless the deponent is certified to be leaving Alaska), or if without stipulation it would exceed 3 depositions per side (plaintiffs, defendants, or third-party defendants) of witnesses other than parties, independent trial experts, treating physicians, and necess","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 30.1. Audio and audio-visual depositions","Alaska · civil rules","Any oral deposition may be recorded by audio or audio-visual means without a stenographer, and the recording is an official record usable like a stenographic deposition, though the court may order a transcript for good cause and any party may make its own simultaneous record at its own expense. The notice and subpoena must say the deposition will be so recorded and whether a court reporter will be absent. The recordi","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions upon written questions","Alaska · civil rules","Any person may be deposed on written questions, subject to the same leave requirements and per-side limit of 3 nonparty, non-expert depositions as Rule 30, with the notice naming the deponent and officer. Cross questions may be served within 30 days after the notice and questions, redirect questions within 10 days after cross questions, and recross questions within 10 days after redirect, unless the court changes the","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 32. Use of depositions in court proceedings","Alaska · civil rules","At trial or a hearing, admissible parts of a deposition may be used against a party who attended or had reasonable notice: any deposition to impeach or as the evidence rules allow, a party’s or its officer’s or designee’s deposition by an adverse party for any purpose, and any witness’s deposition if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or out of state, unable to attend, not subpoenable, in exceptional circu","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories to parties","Alaska · civil rules","Without leave or written stipulation, a party may serve no more than 30 interrogatories, including discrete subparts, on another party (versus 25 under the federal rule), counting up to 10 served early under Rule 26(d)(1), and must leave space for the answers. Answers must be made separately and fully under oath and served within 30 days, any objection must be specific or it is waived absent good cause, and contentio","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 34. Production of documents, ESI, and things; entry on land","Alaska · civil rules","A party may request another party to produce for inspection, copying, testing, or sampling documents, electronically stored information, and tangible things in its possession, custody, or control, or to permit entry onto land, describing items with reasonable particularity and possibly specifying the ESI form; requests may not be served before the Rule 26(d) time without leave or stipulation. The responding party mus","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examinations","Alaska · civil rules","When a party’s physical or mental condition (including blood group), or that of a person in its custody or legal control, is in controversy, the court may order an examination by a suitably licensed or certified examiner, on motion for good cause and notice, specifying the time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner. On request, the party who caused the exam must provide the examiner’s detailed written repor","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","Alaska · civil rules","A party may serve written requests to admit facts, opinions of fact, application of law to fact, or genuineness of documents (with copies attached), but not before the Rule 26(d) time without leave or stipulation; each matter is admitted unless a signed written answer or objection is served within 30 days. Denials must fairly meet the substance, lack of knowledge is allowed only after reasonable inquiry, and a party ","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to disclose or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","Alaska · civil rules","A party may move to compel Rule 26(a) or Rule 26.1(b) disclosures or discovery responses after certifying a good-faith effort to confer, evasive or incomplete answers count as failures, and the losing side (or its attorney) must generally pay the winner’s reasonable expenses and attorney’s fees unless substantially justified. For disobeying a discovery order, the court may deem facts established, bar evidence, strike","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury trial","Alaska · civil rules","Preserves the right to a jury trial under article I, section 16 of the Alaska Constitution or a state statute. A party demands a jury by serving a separate signed written demand any time after the case starts and no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading on the issue; if the demand covers only some issues, any other party may demand a jury on other issues within 10 days after service (or less if the co","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","Alaska · civil rules","Issues for which a jury was properly demanded are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate in writing or in open court to a bench trial, or the court finds no jury right exists. Issues not demanded are tried by the judge, though the court may still order a jury on motion. In cases with no jury right, the court may use an advisory jury or, with both parties’ consent, a jury whose verdict is binding.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 40. Assignment of cases, calendars, and continuances","Alaska · civil rules","The clerk keeps a master calendar, and a case is set for a pretrial or trial-setting conference or trial once it is at issue and a party files a memorandum to set the case for trial listing required information; a party who disagrees must file its own memorandum within 10 days after service. Orders are sought from the assigned judge, with exceptions for emergencies, uncontested matters, and similar situations. A tria","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","Alaska · civil rules","A plaintiff may dismiss without a court order by notice before the other side serves an answer or summary judgment motion, or by stipulation of all parties who have appeared; this is without prejudice unless stated, but a second notice dismissal of the same claim (after a prior dismissal in any state or federal court) operates as a judgment on the merits. Otherwise dismissal requires a court order, and a defendant ma","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation, separate trials, and change of judge","Alaska · civil rules","The court may consolidate cases sharing common questions (the motion is filed in the court where consolidation is sought, listing every case, with notice filed in all affected courts) and may order separate trials of claims or issues while preserving the jury right. Under 42(c), each side in superior or district court gets one peremptory change of judge and one of a master as a matter of right; parties on the same si","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 43. Evidence","Alaska · civil rules","Rescinded by Supreme Court Order 366, effective August 1, 1979.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 43.1. Exhibits","Alaska · civil rules","Parties must mark all intended exhibits before trial and file an original and one copy of an exhibit list with the in-court clerk at the start of trial, using procedures set by the administrative director’s bulletin (Administrative Bulletin No. 9, reproduced after the rule). Offered exhibits go into the clerk’s custody, and before the case is submitted counsel and self-represented parties must check the exhibits agai","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 44. Proof of official record","Alaska · civil rules","Rescinded by Supreme Court Order 366, effective August 1, 1979.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 44.1. Determination of foreign law","Alaska · civil rules","Rescinded by Supreme Court Order 366, effective August 1, 1979.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","Alaska · civil rules","The clerk issues subpoenas under the court’s seal, including blank signed witness subpoenas for parties to fill in, and a subpoena may require production of documents, electronically stored information, or things; the court may quash or modify an unreasonable and oppressive one on a motion made at or before the compliance time, or require advance payment of production costs. Any nonparty at least 18 years old or a pe","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 45.1. Interstate depositions and discovery","Alaska · civil rules","Adopted from the Uniform Interstate Depositions and Discovery Act, this rule lets a party in an out-of-state case submit a foreign subpoena to any Alaska clerk of court, who promptly issues an Alaska subpoena incorporating its terms and listing contact information for all counsel and unrepresented parties; doing so is not an appearance in Alaska courts. The subpoena is served under Civil Rule 45(c), Alaska’s discover","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 46. Conduct of trials","Alaska · civil rules","Sets the order of trial: the plaintiff states the claim, the defendant states the defense, the plaintiff then the defendant present evidence, followed by rebuttal only unless the court allows more. Unless the court orders otherwise, only one attorney per side may examine a given witness, a lawyer who testifies on the merits for a client may not argue to the jury without permission, and formal exceptions are unnecessa","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 47. Jurors","Alaska · civil rules","The court or the attorneys may conduct jury selection, and the court may impose reasonable time limits. The court may seat one or two alternates, either designated alternates or extra jurors with excess jurors removed at random before deliberations, and each party gets one extra peremptory challenge when alternates are used. The rule lists 14 grounds for challenge for cause (including bias, relationship within the fo","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 48. Order of trial and management of juries","Alaska · civil rules","After argument, the court charges the jury in writing, reads the instructions, and the jury takes the written charge to the jury room. The court may order a jury view of property, must admonish jurors who separate not to discuss the case, may discharge a juror who cannot continue (proceeding with the rest by consent, swearing a new juror and restarting, or forming a new jury), and keeps deliberating jurors in an offi","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 49. Verdicts, special verdicts, and interrogatories","Alaska · civil rules","The foreperson delivers a written, signed verdict in court; the court may allow a sealed after-hours verdict, and any party may have the jury polled, with further deliberation if agreement is lacking. The court may require a special verdict with written findings on each fact issue (an omitted issue is waived unless a party demands its submission before the jury retires) or a general verdict with written interrogatori","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 50. Directed verdict and judgment notwithstanding the verdict","Alaska · civil rules","A motion for directed verdict must state specific grounds, does not waive the right to present evidence if denied, and does not waive the jury even if all parties move. A party who moved for a directed verdict at the close of all evidence may move for judgment notwithstanding the verdict no later than 10 days after the date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment (or within 10 days after the jury i","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 51. Instructions to jury","Alaska · civil rules","Any party may file written instruction requests at the close of evidence or earlier as directed, and the court must tell counsel the final instructions before closing arguments and may instruct before or after arguments and at any stage of trial. No party may claim error in instructions unless it objected before the jury retired, stating the matter and grounds, and the court must allow objections outside the jury’s h","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings by the court","Alaska · civil rules","In bench trials and with advisory juries, and when granting or refusing interlocutory injunctions, the court must state findings of fact and conclusions of law, which are set aside on review only if clearly erroneous; findings are not needed for decisions on Rule 12 or 56 motions or other motions except under Rule 41(b). A motion to amend or add findings must be made no later than 10 days after the date in the clerk’","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters","Alaska · civil rules","The presiding judge, with the chief justice’s approval, may appoint standing masters, and courts may appoint special masters (including referees, auditors, examiners, and magistrate judges), with compensation set by the court. Unless the order says otherwise, the master sets the first meeting within 20 days after the order of reference, may take evidence and swear witnesses, and files a report with notice mailed to a","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgments and costs","Alaska · civil rules","A judgment includes a decree, may not recite pleadings or prior proceedings, and money judgments must follow Rule 58.2, with proposed judgments governed by Rule 78. In multi-claim or multi-party cases, the court may enter final judgment on fewer than all claims or parties only on an express finding of no just reason for delay and an express direction for entry. A default judgment cannot differ in kind from or exceed ","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 55. Default","Alaska · civil rules","A party seeking default must serve the application on all parties, including the party in default, and the clerk may enter default no sooner than 7 days after service of the application. The clerk may enter a default judgment for a sum certain against a defendant who failed to appear, on an affidavit of the amount due and of the defendant’s non-minor, competent, and military status, but not in a multi-defendant case ","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","Alaska · civil rules","A claimant may move any time after 20 days from the start of the action (or after the opponent moves), and a defending party may move at any time, following Rule 77 with a memorandum showing no genuine issue of material fact. The opponent may file affidavits and a concise “statement of genuine issues,” may not rest on its pleadings, and may get a continuance for discovery if it shows by affidavit why it cannot yet pr","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments and confession of judgment","Alaska · civil rules","Declaratory judgment actions follow these rules, including jury demands under Rules 38 and 39; another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the court may expedite the hearing. A defendant may confess judgment in a pending case for no more than, and nothing different from, what the complaint demands, by a signed written confession with the plaintiff’s written assent, acknowledged before an oath-giving ","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 58. Entry of judgment","Alaska · civil rules","Subject to Rule 54(b), the court or clerk enters judgment promptly on a general verdict or a decision for a sum certain, costs, or denial of all relief, and the court promptly enters judgment on other decisions and special verdicts. Every judgment must be a separate document distinct from findings, opinions, or memoranda, and entry is not delayed, nor the appeal time extended, for taxing costs or awarding fees. Money","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 58.1. Effective dates and start of time for appeal and reconsideration","Alaska · civil rules","Orders and judgments take effect when entered: an oral order when the judge puts it on the official electronic record (unless the judge specifies otherwise), a written order or judgment when signed unless it states otherwise, and all judgments must be in writing. Time to appeal or seek reconsideration or review runs from the date of notice, which for written orders and judgments is the date in the clerk’s certificate","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 58.2. Judgments for the payment of money","Alaska · civil rules","A money judgment must identify each creditor and debtor (with full legal names and dates of birth if known) and include, as applicable, the principal, the portion bearing prejudgment interest with rate and start date, blanks for the court to fill in prejudgment interest, attorney’s fees, costs, and the total, punitive damages, the post-judgment interest rate, and any State of Alaska interest in punitive damages, foll","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 59. New trials and amendment of judgments","Alaska · civil rules","The court may grant a new trial on all or some issues in jury or bench cases if required in the interest of justice. A new-trial motion must be served no later than 10 days after the date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment, opposing affidavits are due within 10 days after service (extendable up to 20 more days by court or stipulation), and newly discovered evidence must be supported by specifi","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from judgment or order","Alaska · civil rules","Clerical mistakes, including in electronic case records, may be corrected at any time, but during an appeal only before the record is filed in the Supreme Court or later with its leave. The court may relieve a party from a final judgment or order for mistake or excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, fraud or misconduct, a void judgment, a satisfied or no-longer-equitable judgment, or any other justifying reaso","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","Alaska · civil rules","No error in admitting or excluding evidence, or in any ruling or act by the court or parties, justifies a new trial or disturbing a judgment unless refusing to act would be inconsistent with substantial justice. The court must disregard errors that do not affect the parties’ substantial rights.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","Alaska · civil rules","Except for default, consent, or confession judgments, no execution or enforcement may occur until 10 days after the date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment, but injunction and receivership judgments are not automatically stayed. The court may stay enforcement pending motions under Rules 50, 52(b), 59, or 60, and may suspend, modify, or grant an injunction pending appeal on terms securing the o","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 63. Disability of a judge","Alaska · civil rules","If a judge dies, becomes ill, or is otherwise disabled before trial, another judge assigned by the presiding judge or the chief justice may take over. During trial, a substitute judge may continue as if present from the start, provided a stenographic or electronic record has been made so the new judge can become familiar with the proceedings. After a verdict or findings, a substitute judge may complete the case but m","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 64. Seizure of person or property","Alaska · civil rules","At the start of and during a case, remedies for seizing a person or property to secure satisfaction of the eventual judgment, such as arrest, attachment, garnishment, replevin, and sequestration, are available as provided by the law in effect when the remedy is sought. A statutory note says AS 34.40.113(f) bars attachment or similar relief in certain cases involving discretionary interests in irrevocable trusts.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions","Alaska · civil rules","No preliminary injunction issues without notice, and the court may consolidate the preliminary injunction hearing with trial on the merits. A temporary restraining order without notice requires specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint showing immediate irreparable harm and an attorney certification about notice efforts; it expires within a time the court sets, not exceeding 10 days, unless extended for good ","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 65.1. Protective orders: confidential petitioner contact information","Alaska · civil rules","A self-represented petitioner seeking a domestic violence protective order (AS 18.66.100 or 18.66.110) or a stalking or sexual assault protective order (AS 18.65.850 or 18.65.855) may put a mailing address and phone number on a separate form and leave them off other filings. Access is limited to the court, authorized court personnel, and the petitioner, except that it may be shared with the Child Support Services Div","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers","Alaska · civil rules","A case in which a receiver has been appointed may be dismissed only by court order. Administration of estates by receivers follows the statutes, and otherwise receivership cases are governed by law and these rules.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit in court","Alaska · civil rules","With notice to all parties and court permission, a party may deposit money or other deliverable property that is the subject of the case or due under a judgment, managed under Administrative Rule 5. The court releases it to the party entitled to it, and no interest accrues against the depositing party on the deposited amount after deposit.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 68 (older cases). Offer of judgment (cases filed before August 7, 1997)","Alaska · civil rules","Any time more than 10 days before trial, a claimant or defending party may serve an offer to allow judgment on stated terms with costs then accrued; it cannot be revoked for 10 days, is accepted by written notice within 10 days (after which the clerk enters judgment), and is otherwise deemed withdrawn and inadmissible except on costs. If the final judgment is not more favorable to the offeree than the offer, prejudgm","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of judgment","Alaska · civil rules","Any time more than 10 days before trial, either side may serve an offer to allow judgment on stated terms with costs then accrued; it cannot be revoked for 10 days, is accepted by written notice within 10 days, after which the clerk enters judgment, and is otherwise deemed withdrawn and inadmissible except on costs, though new offers may follow. If the final judgment is at least 5 percent less favorable to the offere","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution and examination of judgment debtor","Alaska · civil rules","Judgments are enforced by writ of execution unless the court directs otherwise, and creditors may take discovery in aid of judgment; the court may order the debtor to appear and answer under oath about property, order property applied to the judgment, and bar its transfer, with contempt for disobedience. If five years pass with no writ outstanding, a writ issues only on a motion and affidavit showing just and suffici","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 70. Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","Alaska · civil rules","If a party fails to perform a court-ordered act such as conveying land or delivering documents within the time set, the court may have another appointed person do it at that party’s expense with the same effect, and the clerk will issue a writ of attachment or sequestration on request; contempt is also available. For property in Alaska, the court may instead enter a judgment transferring title, which acts as a convey","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 71. Process for and against nonparties","Alaska · civil rules","A nonparty in whose favor an order is made may enforce it with the same process as a party. A nonparty against whom an order may lawfully be enforced is subject to the same enforcement process as a party.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 72. Eminent domain","Alaska · civil rules","Condemnation cases follow the Civil Rules except as this rule provides: the complaint (with a case description form, and a declaration of taking if used) must state the authority, necessity, use, property, interests taken, estimated just compensation (a judicial admission), and known claimants, with the decisional document and a map attached. A defendant who contests authority and necessity or the declaration of taki","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 72.1. Expert advisory panels in health care malpractice cases","Alaska · civil rules","In a malpractice action subject to AS 09.55.536, either party may ask the court to appoint an expert advisory panel; once the case is at issue the court nominates three members, parties may move to disqualify within 10 days after notice (opposition within five days, no reply), and nominees must disclose conflicts within 10 days. Within 30 days after the initial nominations, the plaintiff and each provider defendant s","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 73. The clerk","Alaska · civil rules","The clerk’s office is open during business hours except judicial holidays and Saturdays. The clerk may enter certain orders without court direction, such as consent substitutions of attorneys, consent satisfactions, Rule 55(a) defaults, and orders for issuing process, and must promptly notify the judge, who may change them for cause. The clerk may also enter Rule 55(b) default judgments on the required affidavits and","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 74. Clerk’s books and records","Alaska · civil rules","The clerk keeps a case file of all papers in date order, an alphabetical index of every civil case and party, a record of every final judgment or order, a posted calendar of hearings and trials distinguishing jury from nonjury cases, and other records the administrative director requires. Court records may not leave the clerk’s custody except as allowed, such as by court officers on a signed receipt. After final judg","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 75. Record of proceedings; transcript as evidence","Alaska · civil rules","The superior court must keep a stenographic or electronic record of all open-court proceedings, unless the parties with the judge’s approval agree otherwise, and of other proceedings required by rule or order. When earlier recorded testimony is admissible at a later trial, it may be proved by a transcript certified by the reporter or a court officer.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 76. Form of papers","Alaska · civil rules","Filed papers must be on 8-1/2 x 11 inch opaque white paper of at least sixteen pound weight, typed in black at least 12 point Courier (or another Appellate Rule 513.5(c) typeface), with footers and certificates no smaller than 10 point, double- or one-and-one-half spaced (with single-spaced headings, footnotes, and indented long quotations), printed on one side, two-hole punched at the top, and stapled at the upper l","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 77. Motions","Alaska · civil rules","Motions must be served with supporting evidence, a memorandum of points and authorities, and a proposed order (self-represented parties need not file a proposed order); an opposition is due 10 days after service, or 15 days for motions to dismiss, for summary judgment, or for judgment on the pleadings (or, if served before an answer is due, by the later of that period and the answer date), while rules with their own ","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 78. Findings, conclusions, judgments and orders","Alaska · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, counsel for the successful party prepares, files and serves proposed findings, conclusions, judgments and orders, and in child custody matters must do so within 10 days after the judge announces on the record who will prepare them. Other parties may file detailed written objections within 5 days after service, and the court may then hold a conference or sign the document as prepared","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 79. Costs: taxation and review","Alaska · civil rules","The prevailing party recovers reasonable, necessarily incurred costs, but only the items listed in the rule (such as filing and service fees, deposition and transcript costs, witness and interpreter fees, computerized legal research, copying at no more than $.15 per page for in-house attorney copies, exhibit preparation, and limited travel for one attorney when no local attorney attends). An itemized, verified cost b","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 80. Bonds and undertakings","Alaska · civil rules","The clerk may approve bonds executed by approved surety companies; individual sureties must be Alaska residents worth the bond amount net of exempt property and debts (or, with more than two sureties, together worth twice the amount), and attorneys and court officers cannot be sureties. On 3 days’ notice an adverse party may have a surety examined under oath about its qualifications, and an attorney must certify a bo","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 81. Attorneys","Alaska · civil rules","Only Alaska Bar members may practice in Alaska courts, but a lawyer in good standing elsewhere may appear in a particular case on motion served on the Bar’s executive director, with a fee paid to the Bar, the name of and written consent from an associated Alaska Bar member, and a good-standing certificate dated within 60 days of the motion; local counsel must also sign all papers and has primary responsibility and su","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 82. Attorney’s fees","Alaska · civil rules","The prevailing party in a civil case is awarded partial attorney’s fees: for a money judgment (plus any prejudgment interest) the court applies a schedule of 20% of the first $25,000, 10% of the next $75,000, 10% of the next $400,000 and 10% over $500,000 if contested with trial; 18%, 8%, 6% and 2% if contested without trial; and 10%, 3%, 2% and 1% if non-contested. If the prevailing party recovers no money judgment,","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 83. Fees of witnesses, physicians, interpreters and translators","Alaska · civil rules","Fees and mileage for witnesses, physicians, interpreters and translators are governed by the rules for the administration of the courts (the Administrative Rules), not by this rule.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 84. Change of name","Alaska · civil rules","A name change starts with a verified petition giving the desired name and reasons, and the court sets a hearing at least 40 days after its order, with notice posted for four consecutive weeks beforehand on the Alaska Court System’s legal notice website unless the court excuses it for the petitioner’s safety. If there is no reasonable objection, the judgment authorizes the new name effective no sooner than 30 days aft","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 85. Forcible entry and detainer","Alaska · civil rules","Eviction actions follow ordinary civil procedure with special rules: the summons must be served at least 2 days before the eviction hearing, the hearing is set within 15 days after the complaint is filed unless ordered otherwise, and continuances longer than 2 days require the defendant to post an undertaking for accruing rent. Appearing at the eviction hearing is an appearance in the whole case, and mail service to ","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 86. Habeas corpus","Alaska · civil rules","Habeas corpus actions follow the civil rules where applicable, starting with a verified complaint stating who is restrained, by whom, where, why the restraint is illegal, and that its legality has not already been decided on a prior writ. The court issues a writ, an order to show cause or a warrant unless relief plainly is not warranted, the custodian must answer under oath explaining its authority, failing to produc","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 87. Civil arrest","Alaska · civil rules","A motion for an order of arrest in a civil action must be supported by an affidavit stating the facts justifying arrest. The order must state the bail amount, and the arresting officer must give the defendant copies of the order and supporting affidavits at the time of arrest.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 88. Prejudgment delivery of personal property","Alaska · civil rules","A plaintiff suing to recover personal property may move, with a detailed affidavit (description, ownership, value, defendant’s possession, no intent to defraud creditors, and no known bankruptcy), for its seizure before judgment. Except in ex parte cases, the defendant gets notice and a hearing held 3 to 7 business days after service, the plaintiff must show by a preponderance the probable validity of its claim and n","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 89. Attachment","Alaska · civil rules","After filing suit, a plaintiff (or any claimant) may seek prejudgment attachment of the defendant’s property under AS 09.40.010–.110, but only on a contract claim for money supported by an affidavit (existing debt, inadequate security, no intent to defraud creditors, no known bankruptcy) and only after posting a written undertaking. Except in ex parte cases, a hearing is held 3 to 7 business days after service, the p","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 90. Contempts","Alaska · civil rules","A judge may punish contempt summarily if the judge certifies seeing or hearing the conduct in the court’s presence, with a signed order reciting the facts. For other contempts, on an ex parte motion with affidavits the court issues an order to show cause or a bench warrant, and the proceeding may be brought by the state or the aggrieved party, in the same case or separately. The court may allow a bond instead of arre","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 90.1. Dissolution, divorce and legal separation actions","Alaska · civil rules","A dissolution of marriage starts with a petition, verified by both spouses if filed jointly, that must be filed within 60 days after the first signature, and no divorce or dissolution may be heard on the merits within 30 days after filing absent good cause; corroborating residency witnesses are not required unless ordered. In disputed property division cases, each party must file and serve a table of all assets and l","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 90.2. Settlements and judgments for minors","Alaska · civil rules","A parent or guardian may settle a minor’s claim, but the release is not effective until the court approves it on a petition or motion describing the minor, the claim, insurance, injuries and why the settlement is fair; the court approves attorney’s fees and costs and may approve without a hearing if net proceeds do not exceed $25,000. After paying reasonable expenses, costs and fees, the remaining proceeds must be ha","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 90.3. Child support awards","Alaska · civil rules","When one parent has primary physical custody, support equals the non-custodial parent’s adjusted annual income (total income minus taxes, mandatory deductions, voluntary retirement contributions capped with mandatory ones at 7.5% of income, prior support obligations, work-related child care, the parent’s own health premiums up to 10% of income, and life insurance premiums up to $1,200) times 20% for one child, 27% fo","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 90.4. Proceedings to establish parentage","Alaska · civil rules","When genetic testing is ordered under AS 25.20.050, the results must be served on all parties at least 20 days before any hearing or trial where they may be offered, with an affidavit from a qualified person on the affiant’s qualifications and the validity of the testing. Objections must be filed and served at least 10 days before the hearing or trial, and without a timely objection the results are admissible as evid","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 90.5. Expedited relief from license actions for unpaid child support","Alaska · civil rules","A person seeking expedited court relief from a Child Support Services Division decision against an occupational or driver’s license under AS 25.27.244 or 25.27.246 must file a petition on the court form, stating the statutory grounds and filed (original plus one copy) within 30 days after receiving CSSD’s decision, ideally with an explanation of no more than 5 pages, documents, a witness list and copies of the decisi","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 90.6. Child custody investigators","Alaska · civil rules","In custody, divorce and domestic violence cases the court may appoint a qualified custody investigator as an Evidence Rule 706 expert, who must disclose potential conflicts within 10 days of appointment and file a written report by the court’s deadline. The report is confidential unless the court orders otherwise and is deemed admitted into evidence on filing, but a party may require the investigator to testify by li","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 90.7. Guardians ad litem in child custody proceedings","Alaska · civil rules","In custody, support or visitation cases the court may appoint a guardian ad litem only when separate representation of the child’s best interests is necessary, such as when needed evidence would not otherwise be presented or the case is unusually complex, and the appointment order must include findings and specify duties, deadlines, duration and compensation. The guardian ad litem advocates the child’s best interests","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 90.8. Expedited redistricting plan challenges","Alaska · civil rules","An application under article VI, section 11 of the Alaska Constitution to compel the Redistricting Board to correct an error in its plan must be filed within 30 days after adoption of the final plan and proclamation and served on the Board, the Attorney General’s office and the Lieutenant Governor’s office. These cases have priority over all other matters, the court must decide no later than 120 days before the statu","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 91. Applicability of civil rules in general","Alaska · civil rules","The writs of scire facias, quo warranto and mandamus are abolished, and relief formerly available through them is sought by ordinary action or motion under these rules. The civil rules also apply to court proceedings to enforce state agency subpoenas unless the court orders otherwise.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 92. Construction of rules","Alaska · civil rules","The rules are meant to provide for the efficient operation of Alaska’s courts. Where no procedure is specified, the court may proceed in any lawful manner consistent with the rules, the constitution and the common law.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 93. Legal effect of rules","Alaska · civil rules","Because the rules are adopted under the supreme court’s constitutional rulemaking power, they supersede inconsistent procedural provisions of any statute that was not enacted specifically to change a rule.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 94. Relaxation of rules","Alaska · civil rules","The court may relax or dispense with any of these rules in a case where it is clear that strict adherence would work an injustice.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 95. Penalties","Alaska · civil rules","For any rule violation, after reasonable notice and an opportunity to be heard, the court may withhold or assess costs or attorney’s fees against offending attorneys or parties. Separately, after notice, an opportunity to show cause and a hearing if requested, the court may fine an attorney for failing to comply with court rules up to $50,000 in cases filed on or after August 7, 1997, or up to $1,000 in cases filed b","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 97. Title","Alaska · civil rules","These rules may be cited as the Rules of Civil Procedure.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 98. Effective date","Alaska · civil rules","The rules take effect on a date set by supreme court order and govern all civil actions begun afterward and, where just and practicable, actions then pending.","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 99. Telephonic participation","Alaska · civil rules","For good cause and absent substantial prejudice, the court may let parties, counsel, witnesses or the judge participate by telephone in any hearing or deposition, and must allow video or Internet testimony at the request of a deployed parent in a custody or visitation matter. The court designates before the hearing who arranges and pays for the call under Administrative Rule 48, the judge opens by reciting the case d","#st-ak-rules"],["Rule 100. Mediation and other alternative dispute resolution","Alaska · civil rules","Any time after a complaint is filed, a party may move for mediation (or the court may order it in non-family cases), but in domestic violence situations family-law mediation is limited by statute and mediation may never be ordered in AS 18.66.100–.180 protective order cases; costs are split equally unless apportioned otherwise, and each party may peremptorily challenge a court-appointed mediator once under Rule 42(c)","#st-ak-rules"],["Appeals in Alaska: civil cases go straight to the Supreme Court, and the clock starts on the clerk’s distribution date","Alaska · appeals","Alaska’s Court of Appeals handles only criminal and related matters. A civil appeal from superior court goes directly to the Alaska Supreme Court as of right. A party gets only one appeal of right, so a district court civil case is appealed to the superior court, and further Supreme Court review is discretionary. Superior-court decisions on administrative appeals may be appealed to the Supreme Court as of right.\nMost","#st-ak-appeals"],["Supreme Court: final superior-court judgments in civil cases","Alaska · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","An appeal lies to the Supreme Court from a final superior-court judgment, as AS 22.05.010 allows, and from final decisions of the Workers’ Compensation Appeals Commission. Criminal, post-conviction, delinquency, extradition, habeas, probation, and bail matters go to the Court of Appeals instead. Alaska R. App. P. 202(a), (b); AS 22.07.020","#st-ak-appeals"],["District court and agency appeals go first to superior court","Alaska · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","Appeal a district court judgment to the superior court within 30 days from the distribution date. Appeal an agency decision within 30 days after it is mailed or distributed to you. If you timely ask the agency to reconsider, the 30 days run from the reconsideration decision or the deemed denial, whichever is earlier. The agency clock does not start until the decision clearly says it is final and that you have 30 days","#st-ak-appeals"],["Partial final judgments","Alaska · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","If you appeal a Civil Rule 54(b) partial final judgment, attach the 54(b) order to the notice of appeal. The brief’s jurisdictional statement must say whether the judgment is final as to all claims and parties. Alaska R. App. P. 204(b)(2), 212(c)(1)(D)","#st-ak-appeals"],["30 days from the clerk’s certificate of distribution","Alaska · appeals · Starting the appeal","File the notice of appeal with the Clerk of the Appellate Courts within 30 days from the date shown in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment. Shorter times apply under Rules 216–219, such as 10 days for extradition and criminal peremptory-challenge appeals. A notice filed after the decision is announced but before distribution is treated as filed on the distribution date. Alaska R. App. P. 204(a)(1)","#st-ak-appeals"],["The required package","Alaska · appeals · Starting the appeal","The notice must identify the appellant, the judgment, and the court. With it, file: (1) a docketing statement listing the parties; (2) a copy of the judgment (and any 54(b) order); (3) a statement of points on appeal; (4) the filing fee, a fee-waiver motion, or a motion to appeal at public expense; (5) the cost bond or deposit, a superior-court order approving a supersedeas bond (or the motion for one), or a motion t","#st-ak-appeals"],["Statement of points limits the appeal","Alaska · appeals · Starting the appeal","The court considers only points in the statement and points it can decide without reviewing untranscribed parts of the electronic record. The statement may be supplemented on motion for cause. Alaska R. App. P. 204(e)","#st-ak-appeals"],["$750 cost bond","Alaska · appeals · Starting the appeal","In a civil case, file a $750 cost bond with the notice (no approval needed at that amount) unless a supersedeas bond is filed, the court sets a different amount, or you deposit cash. Criminal cases, indigent parties with appointed counsel, and certain employee benefit appeals are exempt. Alaska R. App. P. 204(c)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Cross-appeals and later appeals","Alaska · appeals · Starting the appeal","Once a timely notice is filed, any other party may appeal within 14 days of that filing or within 30 days of the distribution date, whichever expires last. All parties below are parties to the appeal. Non-appellants are appellees and may file a notice of non-participation, but they are still bound by the result. Alaska R. App. P. 204(a)(2), (g)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Motions that restart the appeal clock","Alaska · appeals · Tolling and post-judgment motions","A timely motion under Civil Rule 50(b), 52(b), or 59, or for reconsideration under Civil Rule 77(k), terminates the running of appeal time. The full 30 days start again from the date of notice of the order deciding it. For reconsideration, the period restarts on that date or on the Rule 77(k)(4) deemed-denial date, whichever is earlier. Alaska R. App. P. 204(a)(3)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Short trial-court deadlines feed the tolling","Alaska · appeals · Tolling and post-judgment motions","A new-trial motion must be served within 10 days after the distribution date. A motion to alter or amend must be served within 10 days after entry of judgment. Reconsideration is due within 10 days after notice, is limited to 5 pages, and is deemed denied if not decided within 30 days. Alaska R. Civ. P. 59(b), (f), 77(k)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Costs and fee proceedings do not toll","Alaska · appeals · Tolling and post-judgment motions","Time to appeal keeps running while costs are taxed, prejudgment interest is calculated, or Rule 82 fees are decided. Add those rulings by moving to amend your statement of points and transcript designation. If no appeal is pending, a post-judgment costs or fees order is separately appealable on those issues. The trial court keeps jurisdiction over costs and fees while the appeal is pending. Alaska R. App. P. 204(a)(5","#st-ak-appeals"],["Supersedeas bond in the superior court first","Alaska · appeals · Stays and settlement","To stay a money judgment, present a supersedeas bond to the superior court. It must cover the whole unsatisfied judgment plus appeal costs and interest unless the court, after notice and hearing for good cause, sets a different amount or other security. Enforcement is automatically held off for only 10 days after the distribution date. The Supreme Court normally considers a stay only after the trial court has denied ","#st-ak-appeals"],["Mandatory settlement discussion in civil appeals","Alaska · appeals · Stays and settlement","Counsel for all parties in a civil appeal to the Supreme Court must discuss settlement by the date in the clerk’s opening notice, and the appellant’s lawyer reports to the clerk whether it happened and what settled. Self-represented appeals and certain other appeals are exempt. Any party may move for a settlement conference after the notice of appeal. Alaska R. App. P. 221, 222(a)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Schedule: 30 / 30 / 20","Alaska · appeals · Briefing","After the record and transcript are complete, the clerk sends notice of the due date. The appellant’s brief is due 30 days after that notice and the appellee’s 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief. Within 20 days after the appellee’s brief, the appellant files a reply or a notice that none will be filed. Separate briefs by multiple appellants or appellees get 10 extra days when excerpts are being prepared. ","#st-ak-appeals"],["Page limits, not word counts","Alaska · appeals · Briefing","Principal briefs are limited to 50 numbered pages, counted from the jurisdictional statement and excluding appendices, and replies to 20 pages. A combined appellee/cross-appellant brief is 50 pages, and a cross-appellee’s combined reply and response is 50, of which the reply part is at most 20. A motion to exceed the limits must attach the proposed over-length brief. Alaska R. App. P. 212(c)(3), (c)(4), (c)(6)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Format: 13-point serif or 12.5-point sans serif, one-inch side margins","Alaska · appeals · Briefing","Use 8½ x 11 paper, double spaced, with text (including footnotes) of at least 13-point Times New Roman or similar, or 12.5-point Arial or similar. Brief side margins are one inch. File the original and one copy for a compliance check. Within ten days after the brief is returned, file bound copies (ten for the Supreme Court, seven for the Court of Appeals) and serve two on each party. Covers are ivory or tan for the a","#st-ak-appeals"],["Contents that trip up newcomers","Alaska · appeals · Briefing","For each issue, the statement of the case must show whether, when, and how it was raised and ruled on below. The argument must state the standard of review and, for unpreserved issues, address plain error. Property-division appeals need an asset-and-liability table. Avoid calling parties “appellant” and “appellee.” Alaska R. App. P. 212(c)(1)(G)–(J), (c)(7)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Extensions by notice","Alaska · appeals · Briefing","By filing a notice on or before the due date, an appellant may take up to 30 days for the opening brief and 15 for the reply, and an appellee up to 30 days. Expedited matters are excluded. Beyond that, a motion must show diligence and substantial need (up to 30 or 15 more days), or extraordinary and compelling circumstances. Alaska R. App. P. 503.5(b), (c)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Oral argument must be requested","Alaska · appeals · Briefing","In appeals as of right, oral argument is held only if requested no later than 10 days after the reply brief’s due date (or the appellee’s brief’s, if no reply is allowed). Alaska R. App. P. 505(a)(2)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Rehearing: 10 days, 5 pages","Alaska · appeals · Decision, rehearing, costs, and fees","A petition for rehearing is due within 10 days after the date of notice of the opinion and may not exceed five pages. It must specify the overlooked statute, decision, fact, or question. No response is received unless the court requests one. Alaska R. App. P. 506(a), (b)","#st-ak-appeals"],["No mandate; jurisdiction returns automatically","Alaska · appeals · Decision, rehearing, costs, and fees","The opinion or dispositive order is the judgment, and no mandate issues. In the Supreme Court, jurisdiction returns to the trial court the day after rehearing is decided, the day after the rehearing deadline passes, or the day after a petition for hearing is denied. Alaska R. App. P. 507(a)–(c)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Costs and attorney’s fees on appeal","Alaska · appeals · Decision, rehearing, costs, and fees","The prevailing party files a verified bill of costs within 10 days after the date of notice of the opinion, and objections are due 7 days later. Fees on appeal are awarded only if a statute, caselaw, or contract provides for them, the appeal is frivolous or in bad faith, or justice requires. In superior-court appeals under Part Six, the prevailing party gets 20% of actual fees. A fee motion with an affidavit is due w","#st-ak-appeals"],["Precedent quirks","Alaska · appeals · Decision, rehearing, costs, and fees","Issues decided by an equally divided court, or 2–1 when only three justices sit, have no precedential effect. Memorandum opinions and judgments may not be cited, according to the court’s appellate webpage. Alaska R. App. P. 106; courts.alaska.gov/appellate","#st-ak-appeals"],["Petition for hearing (review of an intermediate appellate decision)","Alaska · appeals · Discretionary review","To seek Supreme Court review of a Court of Appeals decision, or review of a superior-court appellate decision in a district court case, file within 30 days after the date of notice (or after notice of the rehearing ruling). The limit is 15 pages, with reasons tied to the Rule 304 criteria. Cross-petitions and responses are due 30 days after service. Any deadline may be extended up to 15 days by notice. Alaska R. App.","#st-ak-appeals"],["Petition for review of non-final orders: 10 days","Alaska · appeals · Discretionary review","Review of an order that is not appealable is discretionary. It is granted only for listed reasons: injustice from postponement, an important contested question whose resolution would advance the case, a serious departure from usual procedure, or an issue that would otherwise evade review. File within 10 days after the date of notice (15-page limit). Cross-petitions and responses are due in 10 days. A timely reconside","#st-ak-appeals"],["Original applications and certified questions","Alaska · appeals · Discretionary review","Original applications for relief and certified questions of state law from other courts are handled under Part Four. Alaska R. App. P. 404, 407","#st-ak-appeals"],["Time computation and extensions","Alaska · appeals · Filing mechanics and out-of-state counsel","Exclude the trigger day and roll past weekends and holidays. Add 3 calendar days for mail service, but no days for electronic service. Only the appellate courts, not the trial court, may extend appellate deadlines. Alaska R. App. P. 502(a)–(c)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Motions practice","Alaska · appeals · Filing mechanics and out-of-state counsel","Oppositions to motions are due 7 days after service. Reconsideration of an order is due within 10 days after its date of notice. Alaska R. App. P. 503(d), (h)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Pro hac vice in the appellate courts","Alaska · appeals · Filing mechanics and out-of-state counsel","Appear on motion under Civil Rule 81(a)(2)–(3), which the clerk may decide. If you were admitted below in the same case, file the trial court order, the good-standing certificate, and local counsel’s details instead. Alaska R. App. P. 103(b), (c)","#st-ak-appeals"],["E-filing in the appellate courts: confirm with the clerk","Alaska · appeals · Filing mechanics and out-of-state counsel","Administrative Bulletin 92 governs TrueFiling for trial-court case types. The collected texts do not confirm whether, or how, appellate filings go through TrueFiling, and the rules still call for paper originals and bound copies. Confirm with the Clerk of the Appellate Courts before filing. Admin. Bull. No. 92; Alaska R. App. P. 212(a)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (civil, to Supreme Court)","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from date shown in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment.\nFile with the Clerk of the Appellate Courts with the full Rule 204(b) package and the $750 cost bond. Alaska R. App. P. 204(a)(1)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Cross-appeal / subsequent appeal","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of another party’s timely notice of appeal (or 30 days from distribution, whichever is later).\nAlso the route to appeal a later costs or fees order. Alaska R. App. P. 204(a)(2)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Appeal to superior court from district court","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment. Alaska R. App. P. 602(a)(1)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Appeal to superior court from an agency","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from mailing or distribution of the final decision (or of the reconsideration ruling or deemed denial).\nThe clock starts only if the decision states it is final and that there are 30 days to appeal. Alaska R. App. P. 602(a)(2)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Motion for new trial","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment.\nServed, not just filed. Tolls appeal time under App. R. 204(a)(3). Alaska R. Civ. P. 59(b)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Motion to alter or amend judgment","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of judgment.\nMeasured from entry, not from the distribution date. Alaska R. Civ. P. 59(f)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Trial-court reconsideration","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from date of notice of the ruling.\n5-page limit; deemed denied after 30 days; tolls appeal time. Alaska R. Civ. P. 77(k)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Rule 82 attorney’s fee motion","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment.\nWaived if late unless the court allows more time. Does not toll appeal time. Alaska R. Civ. P. 82(c)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Cost bill in trial court","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment.\nObjections due 7 days after service. Alaska R. Civ. P. 79","#st-ak-appeals"],["Appellant’s opening brief","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from clerk’s notice of the brief due date.\n50 numbered pages. Alaska R. App. P. 212(a)(1)(A)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of appellant’s brief.\n50 numbered pages. Alaska R. App. P. 212(a)(1)(A)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Reply brief (or notice of no reply)","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from service of appellee’s brief.\n20 pages. Alaska R. App. P. 212(a)(1)(A)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Request for oral argument","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from due date of the reply brief (or of the appellee’s brief if no reply is allowed).\nNo argument without a timely request. Alaska R. App. P. 505(a)(2)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from date of notice of the opinion or decision.\n5-page limit. Alaska R. App. P. 506(b)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Petition for hearing","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from date of notice of the intermediate appellate decision (or of the rehearing ruling).\n15 pages; response 30 days after service; extend up to 15 days by notice. Alaska R. App. P. 303(a)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Petition for review (non-final order)","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from date of notice of the order.\n15 pages; response and cross-petition 10 days after service. Alaska R. App. P. 403(a)(1)(A)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Appellate cost bill / fee motion","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from date of notice of the opinion or Rule 214 order.\nObjections to the cost bill are due within 7 days. Alaska R. App. P. 508(f)(1)–(2)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Opposition to appellate motion","Alaska · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from service of the motion. Alaska R. App. P. 503(d)","#st-ak-appeals"],["Supreme Court of Alabama","Alabama · courts","Nine members (a chief justice and eight associate justices sign its orders). Hears civil appeals not within the Court of Civil Appeals’ exclusive jurisdiction, reviews both intermediate courts by discretionary certiorari, decides permissive interlocutory appeals (Ala. R. App. P. 5) and mandamus petitions in its cases, answers certified questions from federal courts, and makes all court rules. May transfer (“pour over","#st-al"],["Court of Civil Appeals","Alabama · courts","Exclusive appellate jurisdiction of civil cases with $50,000 or less involved (exclusive of interest and costs), domestic-relations and workers’ compensation appeals, and appeals from administrative agencies other than the Public Service Commission (Ala. Code § 12-3-10).","#st-al"],["Court of Criminal Appeals","Alabama · courts","Hears criminal appeals. In criminal cases an application for rehearing there is a prerequisite to Supreme Court certiorari review (Ala. R. App. P. 40(d)(1)).","#st-al"],["Circuit Courts","Alabama · courts","General-jurisdiction trial courts organized by judicial circuit; jury trials in civil cases (struck juries of 12 under Ala. R. Civ. P. 47). Governed by the Alabama Rules of Civil Procedure; local rules are abolished (Ala. R. Civ. P. 83).","#st-al"],["District Courts","Alabama · courts","County-level courts of limited civil jurisdiction with no jury trials. Exclusive small-claims jurisdiction up to $6,000 (Ala. Code § 12-12-31). The civil rules apply as modified by each rule’s “(dc)” paragraph (shorter deadlines, discovery only by leave or agreement). Final judgments are appealed to circuit court for trial de novo (Ala. Code § 12-12-71).","#st-al"],["Probate and Municipal Courts","Alabama · courts","Probate courts handle estates and other statutory matters, with the civil rules applying where appropriate (Ala. R. Civ. P. 1(a)); appeals from probate final judgments lie under Ala. Code § 12-22-2. Municipal courts handle ordinance violations. Their own rules were not collected.","#st-al"],["No anti-SLAPP statute found in the Code of Alabama","Alabama · anti-SLAPP","A search of the official Code of Alabama on the Legislature’s ALISON site (Nov. 10, 2026) for “SLAPP,” “strategic lawsuit,” “public expression,” “public participation,” and “special motion” found no special motion to dismiss or strike, discovery stay, or fee-shifting procedure for claims based on speech or petitioning. A full-text search of bills in all sessions on ALISON (“SLAPP,” “strategic lawsuits,” “public expre","#st-al-slapp"],["Related protection that does exist: libel retraction demand","Alabama · anti-SLAPP","Punitive damages for libel require proof that the defendant published with knowledge of falsity or reckless disregard, and that the plaintiff made a written demand for a public retraction at least 5 days before suit and the defendant failed to publish a full and fair retraction within 5 days. This limits damages; it is not an early-dismissal procedure. Ala. Code § 6-5-186","#st-al-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: who is eligible","Alabama · out of state","A lawyer in good standing in another U.S. jurisdiction, of good moral character and familiar with Alabama’s ethics and practice, may appear pro hac vice in a particular case before any Alabama court or state administrative agency. Not eligible: lawyers who live in Alabama, are regularly employed in Alabama, or regularly engage in substantial business or professional activities in Alabama. Admission is discretionary, ","#st-al-oos"],["Verified application, $300 fee, and the Bar’s statement first","Alabama · out of state","File a verified application (on the State Bar’s form) with the court or agency, with proof of service by mail on the Alabama State Bar in Montgomery or by e-mail to PHV@alabar.org, plus a nonrefundable $300 fee to the Bar. If no defendant has appeared yet, serve each defendant too. The Bar then files a statement listing prior applications by you and your firm; no application may be granted before that statement is fi","#st-al-oos"],["Local counsel must appear in person and is jointly liable","Alabama · out of state","You must associate an Alabama State Bar member in good standing whose name appears on every filing. Local counsel must personally appear and participate in all pretrial conferences, hearings, trials, and other open-court proceedings unless the court excuses it, and takes on joint and several responsibility to the client, opponents, and the court. Before the application is granted, local counsel must appear as attorne","#st-al-oos"],["Repeat appearances: 5 cases in 12 months triggers scrutiny","Alabama · out of state","Repeated appearances by a lawyer or firm, absent special circumstances, are cause for denial. If you have appeared pro hac vice in 5 cases in the preceding 12 months, the court must examine you for good cause based on the client’s interests, such as a complex specialty, a long-standing client relationship, no local expertise, questions of your home jurisdiction’s law, or extensive discovery there. Rule VII.E","#st-al-oos"],["Annual certification by March 1 ($100 per case)","Alabama · out of state","Lawyers admitted on or after Oct. 1, 2020 must submit an annual certification for each case, received by the State Bar by March 1, with a nonrefundable $100 fee, showing continued compliance or that you are no longer counsel of record. Rule VII.G","#st-al-oos"],["Appeals and transfers","Alabama · out of state","Admission carries over when the case is transferred or appealed, but in the appellate court you must file proof of the earlier admission and certify that it has not been rescinded. If the appeal is your first appearance, move for admission in the appellate court with a certificate of good standing; local counsel is required (it may be waived for good cause) but need not actively participate, and you must designate le","#st-al-oos"],["Filings before admission are stricken; violation is unauthorized practice","Alabama · out of state","Clerks may not accept filings from a lawyer who has not complied, and noncompliant filings are stricken on motion or sua sponte. Permission ends automatically on suspension or disbarment anywhere, and you must report discipline elsewhere to the Alabama court. Violating Rule VII is deemed the unauthorized practice of law. Exceptions: pro bono counsel in capital or indigent postconviction cases (no fee, no local counse","#st-al-oos"],["Temporary practice without admission (Rule 5.5)","Alabama · out of state","Subject to Rule VII for court appearances, a lawyer admitted and in good standing elsewhere may provide temporary transactional, counseling, or other nonlitigation services reasonably related to the home-state practice, and services related to arbitration, mediation, or other ADR held in Alabama or elsewhere. No office or permanent presence in Alabama and no holding out as admitted here. Note: Rule 5.5(b)(3) cross-re","#st-al-oos"],["Authorized house counsel","Alabama · out of state","A lawyer licensed elsewhere who is exclusively employed by a business organization located in or relocating to Alabama may register as authorized house counsel without the bar exam, agreeing to the Alabama Rules of Professional Conduct and Rules of Disciplinary Procedure. Rules Governing Admission, Rule VIII.A–B","#st-al-oos"],["Admission without examination (reciprocity)","Alabama · out of state","Requires admission elsewhere, active practice in 5 of the 6 years before applying, reciprocity from your principal-practice or domicile jurisdiction, good standing everywhere, no pending discipline, no failed Alabama bar exam within 10 years, an MPRE score of 75 or more, and Alabama residence or a certified intention to do at least 75% of your practice in Alabama from an Alabama office. Fee $875 plus NCBE character-i","#st-al-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances and ghostwriting","Alabama · out of state","A lawyer may provide limited-scope representation in a court proceeding by filing and serving a notice of limited-scope representation at or before the start of the proceeding, and withdraws without leave by filing a notice of completion. A lawyer who only drafts papers for a self-represented person need not sign them but must include the certification that the document was prepared with the assistance of a licensed ","#st-al-oos"],["E-filing access","Alabama · out of state","Appellate courts: attorneys may e-file after registering; licensed Alabama lawyers and pro hac vice lawyers authorized in the appellate proceeding may register (pro se parties may not e-file). Trial courts: papers filed electronically “in accordance with an order or rules of the Supreme Court” count as filed, and service on registered users through the system is complete on sending; the trial-court e-filing (AlaFile)","#st-al-oos"],["Governing code: Alabama Rules of Professional Conduct","Alabama · ethics","Adopted by the Supreme Court of Alabama; enforced through the Alabama Rules of Disciplinary Procedure. Recent changes: Rule 1.15 (trust accounts) amended effective Feb. 15, 2025; Rules 7.1, 7.2, 7.3, and 8.5 (advertising, solicitation, jurisdiction) amended effective Jan. 1, 2026. Several rules keep older ABA Model Rule language, as noted below. Ala. R. Prof. Cond.; orders of Dec. 23, 2024 and May 13, 2025","#st-al-ethics"],["Confidentiality: much narrower exceptions (Rule 1.6)","Alabama · ethics","Disclosure is allowed only to prevent the client from committing a criminal act likely to result in imminent death or substantial bodily harm, and for the lawyer’s own claims and defenses. Unlike ABA Model Rule 1.6(b), there are no exceptions for client crimes or frauds causing financial injury, securing ethics advice, complying with other law or a court order, or detecting conflicts when changing firms. Ala. R. Prof","#st-al-ethics"],["Fees: “clearly excessive” standard; contingency rules (Rule 1.5)","Alabama · ethics","The standard is a “clearly excessive” fee (the ABA uses “unreasonable”), and the factors include whether there is a written fee agreement signed by the client. Contingency agreements must be in writing, state the percentages on settlement, trial, or appeal and how expenses are handled, and end with a written closing statement. No contingent fees in divorce, alimony, or support matters or for criminal defendants. Ala.","#st-al-ethics"],["Financial assistance to clients allowed in emergencies (Rule 1.8(e))","Alabama · ethics","Besides advancing costs, a lawyer may advance or guarantee emergency financial assistance to a litigation client if repayment is not contingent on the outcome and no assistance was promised before the lawyer was hired. In percentage-fee cases, the lawyer may pay costs from the lawyer’s own account and recover them from the proceeds. The ABA rule has no general emergency-assistance exception. Ala. R. Prof. Cond. 1.8(e","#st-al-ethics"],["No screening cure for lateral hires (Rule 1.10)","Alabama · ethics","Alabama’s imputation rule disqualifies a firm when an incoming lawyer (or the lawyer’s former firm) represented an adverse client in the same or a substantially related matter and the lawyer has material confidential information. The rule text contains no screening provision like ABA Model Rule 1.10(a)(2); the affected client can waive under Rule 1.7. Imputation also covers Rule 2.2 (intermediary), a rule the ABA del","#st-al-ethics"],["Broad misconduct catch-all; no anti-harassment rule (Rule 8.4)","Alabama · ethics","Rule 8.4(g) makes it misconduct to “engage in any other conduct that adversely reflects on [the lawyer’s] fitness to practice law.” Alabama has not adopted ABA Model Rule 8.4(g) on harassment and discrimination. Ala. R. Prof. Cond. 8.4","#st-al-ethics"],["Solicitation waiting periods (Rule 7.3)","Alabama · ethics","Even where live solicitation is not otherwise barred, a lawyer may not solicit about a personal-injury or wrongful-death claim within 21 days after the accident or disaster; about a pending civil or administrative proceeding until service on the prospective client has been obtained and filed; or about a pending criminal case until 7 days after service of the warrant or information. The ABA rule has no such waiting pe","#st-al-ethics"],["Disciplinary jurisdiction over visiting lawyers (Rule 8.5)","Alabama · ethics","Lawyers not admitted in Alabama are subject to Alabama discipline if they provide or offer legal services here, and pro hac vice lawyers also submit to the courts and the State Bar’s disciplinary bodies and authorize the Bar to report discipline to every licensing jurisdiction. Amended Rule 8.5 (eff. Jan. 1, 2026) contains no choice-of-law paragraph like ABA Model Rule 8.5(b); its comment leaves conflicts to “princip","#st-al-ethics"],["The 90-day deemed denial restarts your appeal clock without warning","Alabama · land mine","A Rule 50, 52, 55, or 59 motion not ruled on within 90 days is denied by operation of law, and the 42-day appeal period runs from that date, even if the judge later holds a hearing or rules. Only express consent of all parties on the record, or an appellate-court extension, stops it. Ala. R. Civ. P. 59.1; Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(3)","#st-al-unique"],["42 days to appeal, 14 for injunctions; no appellate extension","Alabama · land mine","The notice of appeal is due 42 days after entry (not 30), but only 14 days for interlocutory injunction, receivership, and similar orders. A late notice must be dismissed, and appellate courts cannot extend the time; the trial court may add up to 30 days only for failure to learn of the entry. Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(1), 2(a)(1), 2(b), 26(b); Ala. R. Civ. P. 77(d)","#st-al-unique"],["Two different weekend rules for computing time","Alabama · land mine","Under the civil rules, intermediate weekends and holidays are excluded for periods under 11 days; under the appellate rules, only for periods under 7 days. Three days are added after service by mail or through the e-filing system in the trial court. Ala. R. Civ. P. 6(a)(2), 6(d); Ala. R. App. P. 26(a)","#st-al-unique"],["Plaintiff’s claims against a third-party defendant are compulsory","Alabama · land mine","If a defendant impleads a third party, the plaintiff’s failure to assert claims against that third-party defendant arising from the same transaction has the same effect as failing to plead a compulsory counterclaim. Ala. R. Civ. P. 14(a)(3)","#st-al-unique"],["Two suits for the same cause: the first one bars the second","Alabama · land mine","A plaintiff may not prosecute two actions in Alabama courts at the same time for the same cause against the same party; the pendency of the earlier action is a defense to the later one, and simultaneous actions force an election. Ala. Code § 6-5-440","#st-al-unique"],["Local counsel must physically attend every hearing","Alabama · land mine","Pro hac vice local counsel is not a name on the pleadings: local counsel must personally appear at all pretrial conferences, hearings, and trials unless excused, and shares joint and several responsibility. Filings made before you comply with Rule VII are stricken, and the Bar’s statement on your prior applications must be filed before the court can grant admission. Rules Governing Admission, Rule VII.C, VII.D, VII.K","#st-al-unique"],["Jury demand within 30 days of the last pleading","Alabama · land mine","Serve a written jury demand no later than 30 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue (10 days in an appeal to circuit court), and file it. Failure to demand, or failing to appear at trial, waives the jury. Ala. R. Civ. P. 38(b), (d)","#st-al-unique"],["Supersedeas bond is 125% to 150% of the judgment","Alabama · land mine","To stay a money judgment pending appeal, the bond must be 150% of a judgment of $10,000 or less, or 125% of a larger one, approved by the trial-court clerk. The automatic stay lasts only 30 days after entry. Ala. R. App. P. 8(a)(1); Ala. R. Civ. P. 62(a)","#st-al-unique"],["Want the Supreme Court to see your facts? Put them in the rehearing application","Alabama · land mine","If the Court of Civil Appeals misstated the facts or affirmed without opinion, your additional or corrected facts must be presented in the application for rehearing in that court and copied verbatim into the certiorari petition. Certiorari is due 14 days after the decision (or the rehearing ruling) and cannot be extended. Ala. R. App. P. 39(c), 39(d)(5), 40(e)","#st-al-unique"],["Interlocutory permissive appeal only in Supreme Court cases","Alabama · land mine","A Rule 5 permissive appeal is available only in civil cases within the Supreme Court’s original appellate jurisdiction, needs a trial-judge certification (presumptively within 28 days) stating the controlling question, and the petition is due 14 days after certification. Cases within the Court of Civil Appeals’ jurisdiction (e.g., $50,000 or less, domestic relations) cannot use it. Ala. R. App. P. 5(a)","#st-al-unique"],["Wrongful death: 2 years and county limits","Alabama · land mine","Alabama wrongful-death actions belong to the personal representative, must be filed within 2 years after death, and only in a county where the decedent could have sued under the venue statutes (§§ 6-3-2, 6-3-7). Ala. Code § 6-5-410(a), (d), (e)","#st-al-unique"],["Confidentiality exceptions are narrower than you are used to","Alabama · land mine","Alabama Rule 1.6 permits disclosure only to prevent a client crime likely to cause imminent death or substantial bodily harm and for the lawyer’s own claims and defenses; there is no financial-fraud, ethics-advice, or court-order exception in the rule text. Ala. R. Prof. Cond. 1.6(b)","#st-al-unique"],["No peremptory strike of a judge; recusal for cause","Alabama · judges","Alabama has no peremptory challenge or automatic substitution of a judge in the collected rules or statutes. Recusal is for cause under Canon 3C of the Canons of Judicial Ethics: a judge should disqualify where impartiality might reasonably be questioned, including personal bias, prior work as a lawyer in the matter, a financial interest of the judge, spouse, or minor child, or a relative within the fourth degree who","#st-al-unique"],["Campaign-contribution recusal with a 30-day appeal","Alabama · judges","In civil cases a judge must recuse if a party’s substantial campaign contribution or electioneering communication in the judge’s last election would make a reasonable person perceive impaired impartiality, or creates a serious, objective probability of bias. Recusal is presumed when a party’s direct contribution exceeded 10% (appellate), 15% (circuit), or 25% (district) of the judge’s total contributions for the cycl","#st-al-unique"],["Contempt involving a personal attack on the judge","Alabama · judges","If alleged contempt involves gross disrespect toward or a personal attack on the judge, or the judge’s own conduct is entangled with it, the matter must be referred to another judge unless it is a direct contempt requiring prompt summary punishment. Ala. R. Civ. P. 70A(f)","#st-al-unique"],["Alabama Constitution free-speech provisions: not established here","Alabama · speech","The text of the Alabama Constitution’s free-speech and petition provisions, and whether Alabama courts read them more broadly than the First Amendment, was not collected. Do not argue an independent state standard without checking the constitution and case law. Ala. Const. (not collected)","#st-al-unique"],["Libel: retraction demand before suit for punitive damages","Alabama · speech","No punitive damages for libel unless the plaintiff proves knowing or reckless falsity and made a written retraction demand at least 5 days before suing that went unanswered by a full and fair retraction within 5 days. Ala. Code § 6-5-186","#st-al-unique"],["Fictitious-party pleading","Alabama · unique rules","Alabama lets a plaintiff sue an unknown defendant by a fictitious name and later substitute the true name, with relation back under principles of fictitious-party practice. Fictitious defendants still unnamed 75 days after a venue-transfer motion are ignored for venue. Ala. R. Civ. P. 9(h), 15(c)(4), 82(d)(4)","#st-al-unique"],["No local rules","Alabama · unique rules","All local court rules were abolished effective April 14, 1992, and none are permitted. Ala. R. Civ. P. 83","#st-al-unique"],["Struck juries","Alabama · unique rules","Civil juries are selected by alternately striking from a list of at least 24 qualified jurors until 12 remain, the party demanding the jury striking first; with multiple parties the plaintiff side receives half of all strikes. Ala. R. Civ. P. 47(b), (c)","#st-al-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope of rules","Alabama · civil rules","Govern civil actions in the circuit courts, in the district courts as modified by each rule’s “(dc)” subdivision, in small-claims courts through Small Claims Rule N, in probate courts where appropriate, and in other courts whose appeals go directly to the Supreme Court or Court of Civil Appeals. Construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","Alabama · civil rules","There is one form of action, the “civil action.” In district court it is subject to the statutory limits on equitable jurisdiction (§ 12-12-30).","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencement of action","Alabama · civil rules","An action is commenced by filing a complaint. Except in domestic-relations cases, a circuit-court complaint (and a notice of appeal to circuit court from district or probate court) must be filed with a signed informational cover sheet (Form 93); a missing sheet does not void the filing, but the court may stay or, after notice, dismiss until one is filed (amended eff. Sept. 3, 2025).","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 4. Process: general provisions","Alabama · civil rules","The clerk issues the summons on filing. Service must be made within 120 days of filing or the court, after at least 14 days’ notice, may dismiss without prejudice (time must be extended for good cause). Service may be made by sheriff or constable, by any nonparty at least 19 years old not related within the third degree, by certified mail (requested of the clerk or initiated by the attorney with an “Affidavit of Cert","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 4.1. Service of other process","Alabama · civil rules","Process other than a summons or subpoena is served by a sheriff, constable, or specially appointed person, anywhere in Alabama and outside it when authorized by law.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 4.2. Basis for and methods of out-of-state service","Alabama · civil rules","Out-of-state service is proper whenever the defendant has contacts with Alabama such that suit here is consistent with the Alabama and U.S. Constitutions (Alabama’s long-arm reaches the due-process limit). Also reaches personal representatives and guardians sued for a decedent’s or ward’s acts.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 4.3. Service by publication","Alabama · civil rules","Publication is available for certain historically equitable claims involving property or marital status where identity or residence is unknown or a resident has been absent more than 30 days, and for any claim against a defendant who avoids service. A filed affidavit must state specific facts of avoidance (mere failure of service is not enough). Notice runs once a week for 4 successive weeks; the answer is due 30 day","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 4.4. Service in a foreign country","Alabama · civil rules","Allows service abroad by internationally agreed means (e.g., the Hague Service Convention), by foreign-law methods, by letter rogatory, by personal delivery or clerk-dispatched signed-receipt mail unless foreign law forbids it, or by other court-directed means not barred by treaty. Tracks Federal Rule 4(f).","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 5. Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","Alabama · civil rules","Papers after the complaint go to every party, through counsel when represented. Service is by hand, leaving at office or residence, mail (complete on mailing), filing with the e-filing system to a registered user (complete on sending), or, as of Oct. 1, 2026, e-mail to an unrepresented party at the e-mail address that party listed. Every filed paper must state the filer’s address, e-mail address, and telephone number","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 5.1. Privacy protection for court filings","Alabama · civil rules","Filings may show only the last four digits of Social Security, taxpayer-ID, and financial-account numbers, with listed exemptions. The filer, not the clerk, is responsible for redaction. Filing one’s own information unredacted and not under seal waives protection.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 6. Computing and extending time; time for motion papers","Alabama · civil rules","Exclude the trigger day; if the period is less than 11 days, also exclude intermediate Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays (the federal rule dropped this in 2009). The last day rolls forward if it is a weekend, holiday, or (for filings) a day the clerk’s office is inaccessible. Legal holidays include those set by Ala. Code § 1-3-8 and Juneteenth; Rule 6 was amended effective Apr. 9, 2026. Extensions require good c","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings allowed; form of motions","Alabama · civil rules","Pleadings are the complaint, answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a cross-claim, third-party complaint, and third-party answer; a reply to an answer only if ordered. Motions must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state grounds with particularity, and be signed under Rule 11. Demurrers and pleas are abolished.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","Alabama · civil rules","Notice pleading: a short and plain statement of the claim and a demand for relief. Affirmative defenses must be pleaded, and the Alabama list includes contributory negligence, assumption of risk, and injury by fellow servant. Averments not denied are admitted, except as to the amount of damage.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","Alabama · civil rules","Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity; capacity is challenged by specific negative averment. Rule 9(h) allows fictitious-party pleading: a plaintiff ignorant of a defendant’s name may sue that party by a fictitious name and substitute the true name later (the federal rules have no equivalent). Relation back of the substitution is addressed in Rule 15(c)(4).","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","Alabama · civil rules","Caption with court, title, file number, and Rule 7(a) designation; numbered paragraphs; separate counts for separate transactions where helpful. Exhibits attached to a pleading are part of it.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 11. Signature, required information, certification, sanctions","Alabama · civil rules","Every paper must be signed by an attorney of record (or the unrepresented party) and, as of Oct. 1, 2026, state the signer’s address, e-mail address, and telephone number. The signature certifies only that the signer read the paper, that there is good ground to support it, and that it is not interposed for delay. A ghostwriting lawyer helping a self-represented person need not sign but must add the certification that","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections; motion for judgment on the pleadings","Alabama · civil rules","The answer is due 30 days after service (not 21 as in federal court); a reply to a counterclaim is due 30 days after service of the answer. A Rule 12 motion moves the deadline to 10 days after notice of the ruling (or after service of a more definite statement). The seven Rule 12(b) defenses and the waiver rules track federal practice; a motion to strike is due within 30 days if no response is permitted. District cou","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and cross-claim","Alabama · civil rules","Compulsory counterclaims must be pleaded, with three exceptions: the claim was already pending elsewhere, the opposing suit was by attachment without personal jurisdiction, or the opposing claim is for damage covered by a liability policy under which the insurer controls the defense; the rule warns res judicata may still apply. In a de novo appeal to circuit court, a compulsory counterclaim must be added within 30 da","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","Alabama · civil rules","A defendant may serve a third-party complaint without leave within 10 days after serving its original answer (not 14 as in federal court); later requires leave by motion. As amended effective Oct. 16, 2026, the rule follows the restyled federal structure, including the third-party defendant’s defenses and claims. A plaintiff who fails to assert a claim against a third-party defendant arising from the same transaction","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Alabama · civil rules","A party may amend without leave at any time more than 42 days before the first trial setting (unless the court orders otherwise), subject to the court’s disallowance or a motion to strike; after that, leave requires good cause. A response to an amended pleading is due within the time left to respond to the original or 10 days after service, whichever is longer (7 days in district court). Relation back covers changed ","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 16. Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","Alabama · civil rules","The court may hold pretrial conferences and enter a scheduling order, which may be modified only for good cause. Without an order, any party may require a conference at least 21 days before trial. A final pretrial order may be modified only to prevent manifest injustice. Sanctions include Rule 37(b)(2)(B)–(D) orders and mandatory expenses unless substantially justified. Initial disclosures and a Rule 26(f) meeting ar","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 17. Parties; capacity","Alabama · civil rules","Actions are prosecuted by the real party in interest, with time allowed to ratify or substitute. A guardian ad litem must be appointed for a minor defendant, and a minor 14 or older may nominate a guardian ad litem within 30 days of service (14 in district court). The rule bars appointment of a guardian ad litem related to or recommended by the plaintiff or plaintiff’s counsel.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims and remedies","Alabama · civil rules","Any number of legal and equitable claims may be joined. A creditor may join a money claim with a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance. Rule 18(c): no rule permits a jury to try an insurance-coverage question together with the related damage claim against the insured.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 19. Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","Alabama · civil rules","Tracks the pre-2007 federal rule: join required persons if feasible; if not, decide in equity and good conscience whether to proceed using four factors. A joined party who objects to venue and whose joinder makes venue improper must be dismissed.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","Alabama · civil rules","Plaintiffs or defendants may be joined if claims arise from the same transaction or series and share a common question; the court may order separate trials.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder","Alabama · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped and claims severed at any stage.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","Alabama · civil rules","As amended effective Oct. 16, 2026: a plaintiff or defendant exposed to double or multiple liability may interplead claimants even if their claims lack a common origin or the stakeholder denies liability. The stakeholder may deposit the money or property with the court and be discharged. A party that requested attorney fees in a pleading may be awarded a reasonable fee from the fund (no federal-rule counterpart).","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","Alabama · civil rules","Rule 23(a)–(b) track the federal prerequisites and categories. Certification is decided at an early practicable time “consistent with Alabama statutory law” (the statute is not named in the rule and was not collected). Notice for (b)(3) classes may be by mail, electronic, or other means. Settlement, dismissal, or compromise of a certified or proposed settlement class needs court approval and notice. Not available in ","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 23.1. Derivative actions","Alabama · civil rules","A verified complaint must allege contemporaneous ownership and particularized demand efforts or why none was made; court approval is needed to dismiss or compromise.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 23.2. Unincorporated associations","Alabama · civil rules","Actions by or against members of an unincorporated association as a class require representatives who fairly and adequately protect the association’s interests.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","Alabama · civil rules","Intervention as of right and permissive intervention on timely motion, with a pleading setting out the claim or defense. Tracks the pre-restyling federal rule.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","Alabama · civil rules","After death is suggested on the record by service, a motion to substitute must be made within 6 months or the action is dismissed as to the decedent absent excusable neglect (federal: 90 days). Also covers incompetency, transfer of interest, and automatic substitution of public officers.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 26. General provisions governing discovery","Alabama · civil rules","Scope: any nonprivileged matter relevant to the subject matter of the action (broader than the federal claim-or-defense standard), proportional to the needs of the case, and the “reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence” language is retained. No initial disclosures and no automatic expert reports; expert information comes through interrogatories. A privilege log is due within 21 days afte","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 27. Discovery before action or pending appeal","Alabama · civil rules","A verified petition in the circuit court of an expected adverse party’s county may seek depositions or Rule 34/35 discovery before suit, with notice served at least 30 days before the hearing. Similar relief is available pending appeal. Not available in district court.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Alabama · civil rules","Officers authorized by U.S., Alabama, or local law, or court appointees; abroad, by notice, commission, letters rogatory, or treaty. Rule 28(c) lets parties in other states’ cases obtain Alabama subpoenas from a circuit judge where the witness resides on a commission or proof of notice. No relatives, employees, or financially interested persons may serve as officer.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations regarding discovery","Alabama · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, parties may stipulate in writing to deposition logistics and to modify other discovery procedures.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions upon oral examination","Alabama · civil rules","Leave is needed for a plaintiff’s deposition within 30 days after service unless the defendant has sought discovery or special notice is given. No 7-hour or 10-deposition limits appear in the rule. A party deponent may be asked to bring documents; objections are due within 14 days (45 days if served with the complaint). Remote depositions and remote oaths by stipulation or order; objections must be concise and non-ar","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions upon written questions","Alabama · civil rules","Cross questions within 15 days of the notice, redirect within 10 days, recross within 5 days. Any party may demand to attend and cross-examine orally within the cross-question period.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 32. Use of depositions in court proceedings","Alabama · civil rules","Depositions may be used for any purpose if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or out of state, unable to attend, or unavailable by subpoena, and (unlike the federal rule) whenever the witness is a licensed physician or dentist. Waiver rules for errors and irregularities track the federal rule; objections to the form of written questions are due within 5 days after the last questions.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories to parties","Alabama · civil rules","Limit of 40 interrogatories per party, counting each subpart separately, with “party” including all parties represented by the same lawyer or firm (federal: 25). Answers are due in 30 days, but a defendant has 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. A motion to compel must quote the interrogatory and objection in full.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 34. Production of documents and things","Alabama · civil rules","Response due in 30 days (defendant: 45 days after service of process). Requests may specify the ESI form; documents must be produced as kept or organized by request category. Nonparties are reached under Rule 45.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examinations","Alabama · civil rules","On motion for good cause, when a condition is in controversy, the court may order an examination by a suitably licensed or certified examiner. Report exchange and privilege waiver track the older federal rule. Not available in district court.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","Alabama · civil rules","Matters are admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days (defendants need not respond earlier than 45 days after service of process). Admissions are conclusive unless withdrawn on motion. No numerical limit appears in the rule.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to make discovery; sanctions","Alabama · civil rules","A motion to compel must include a good-faith conferral statement. Expenses are mandatory to the winner unless substantially justified. Sanctions for violating orders range from deemed facts to dismissal or default. Rule 37(g) (eff. Dec. 21, 2018) adopts the federal Rule 37(e) framework for lost ESI: curative measures on prejudice; adverse inference, dismissal, or default only on intent to deprive.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury trial of right","Alabama · civil rules","A jury demand must be served within 30 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue (federal: 14). In an appeal or certiorari to circuit court, the appellant must demand within 10 days after filing and others within 10 days after service. Failure to demand, or to appear at trial, waives the jury. A demand is deemed a demand for a struck jury. Not applicable in district court (no juries there).","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","Alabama · civil rules","Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or no jury right exists; the court may order a jury despite a missing demand and may use an advisory jury.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 40. Assignment of cases for trial","Alabama · civil rules","Trials must be set at least 60 days in advance (30 days for de novo appeals; 14 days in district court), with listed exceptions. The clerk must notify out-of-county attorneys within 3 days after a case is placed on the trial docket.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","Alabama · civil rules","Voluntary dismissal by notice before an answer or summary-judgment motion, or by stipulation of all appearing parties; a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits. Involuntary dismissals are with prejudice unless the order says otherwise (except jurisdiction, venue, and Rule 19). Costs of a prior dismissed action may be ordered.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials","Alabama · civil rules","Consolidation for common questions; separate trials for convenience, prejudice, or economy, preserving the jury right under Ala. Const. art. I, § 11.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 43. Evidence","Alabama · civil rules","Testimony is taken orally in open court; remote testimony is allowed for good cause in compelling circumstances. Uncontested divorce testimony is taken before the clerk unless the judge orders otherwise. Motions on facts outside the record may be heard on affidavits. Admissibility is governed by statute and the Alabama Rules of Evidence.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 44. Proof of documents","Alabama · civil rules","Authentication of domestic and foreign official records, plus Alabama-specific provisions on altered documents, recorded instruments (prima facie evidence), judgments, learned treatises and maps, business records, private documents with subscribing witnesses, and handwriting comparison.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 44.1. Determination of foreign law","Alabama · civil rules","A party raising another state’s or country’s law must give written notice; the court may consider any relevant source, and the determination is a ruling on a question of law.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","Alabama · civil rules","Trial and deposition subpoenas issue from the court where the action is pending. A document-only subpoena requires 15 days’ notice to all parties with the proposed subpoena attached (not before 45 days after service on a defendant unless that defendant has sought discovery); any objection within 10 days blocks issuance until the court rules. The subpoena must allow at least 15 days to comply. Service by sheriff, nonp","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 46. Exceptions unnecessary","Alabama · civil rules","Formal exceptions are abolished; a party preserves error by making known the action desired or the objection and its grounds when the ruling is made.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 47. Jurors","Alabama · civil rules","Voir dire by court or counsel. Civil juries are “struck”: parties alternately strike from a list of at least 24 qualified jurors until 12 remain, the demanding party striking first. Up to 6 alternates, chosen from the last names struck; alternates may be retained during deliberations, with instructions to start anew if one is substituted. With multiple parties, the plaintiff side gets one-half of the total strikes.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 48. Juries of less than twelve; majority verdict","Alabama · civil rules","Parties may stipulate to fewer than 12 jurors or to a majority verdict; otherwise the default is 12 jurors.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 49. General verdicts, special verdicts, and interrogatories","Alabama · civil rules","General verdicts are the norm; special verdicts or interrogatories may be used, but only if counsel receive the questions a reasonable time before closing arguments.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 50. Judgment as a matter of law","Alabama · civil rules","JML motions may be made before submission to the jury. A renewed motion is due within 30 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28), with an alternative new-trial motion. A party against whom JML is entered has 30 days to move for a new trial.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 51. Instructions to jury; objection","Alabama · civil rules","Written requested charges are marked “given” or “refused.” The court instructs after closing arguments, and given charges are read without identifying the requesting party. To preserve error, a party must object before the jury retires, stating the matter and grounds. The judge may not comment on the evidence.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings by the court; judgment on partial findings","Alabama · civil rules","Findings of fact are made on written request or when required by statute (not automatically, as in federal court). A motion to amend findings is due within 30 days (14 days in district court; 7 calendar days in unlawful-detainer and eviction cases). Sufficiency of the evidence may be raised on appeal without a post-trial motion when findings were made.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters","Alabama · civil rules","Reference is the exception. In nonjury cases, findings stand unless clearly erroneous; objections within 10 days after notice of the report’s filing. Masters may not serve when related to a party, stockholder, or attorney within the fourth degree.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgments; costs","Alabama · civil rules","Rule 54(b) certification requires an express determination of no just reason for delay and an express direction for entry of judgment; otherwise a partial ruling is not final. A judgment may also be final as to served defendants under Rule 4(f). Costs go to the prevailing party; review of the clerk’s taxation must be sought within 5 days after notice. Attorney-fee motion procedure under Federal Rule 54(d)(2) has no c","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 55. Default","Alabama · civil rules","The clerk enters default; the clerk may enter judgment for a sum certain against a nonappearing adult defendant. If the defaulting party has appeared, a hearing is required with at least 3 days’ written notice (not required on the trial date). The court may set aside a default before judgment, and a default judgment on its own motion or on a party’s motion filed within 30 days after entry (14 in district court; 7 in ","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","Alabama · civil rules","A claimant may move 30 days after commencement; a defending party at any time. The motion must include a narrative summary of undisputed material facts with record citations, and the opponent must file a statement in opposition supported the same way. The motion and all supporting material must be served at least 10 days before the hearing, and opposition at least 2 days before (district court: 14 and 7 days). The ru","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments","Alabama · civil rules","Procedure under Ala. Code §§ 6-6-220 to 6-6-232 follows these rules; a jury may be demanded; another adequate remedy does not bar relief. Not available in district court.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 58. Rendition and entry of orders and judgments","Alabama · civil rules","A judge renders an order by separate document, opinion, notation (“granted,” “denied”) on a motion, notation in the record, or electronic transmission to the e-filing system. An order is “entered” when input into the State Judicial Information System, or when the judge electronically transmits it, and that date starts appeal and post-judgment clocks. No separate-document requirement. Interest runs from rendition.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 59. New trials; amendment of judgments","Alabama · civil rules","A new-trial motion and a motion to alter, amend, or vacate must be filed within 30 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28); the court may order a new trial on its own within 30 days. Opposing affidavits are due 15 days after service, extendable up to 20 more days. Remittitur acceptance does not prejudice cross-appeal. Post-trial motions stay pending until ruled on, subject to Rule 59.1. District court: 14 days (7 ","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 59.1. Disposition of post-judgment motions (deemed denial)","Alabama · civil rules","A post-judgment motion under Rules 50, 52, 55, or 59 is automatically denied by operation of law if not ruled on within 90 days, unless all parties expressly consent on the record or the appellate court extends the time. The 42-day appeal clock then runs from the deemed-denial date, whether or not the court later acts. District court: 14 days, extendable once by up to 14 days. No federal counterpart.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from judgment or order","Alabama · civil rules","Clerical errors may be corrected at any time. Rule 60(b) grounds track the federal rule; motions on grounds (1)–(3) must be filed within 4 months (federal: 1 year). An independent action may be brought within 3 years (or longer under §§ 6-2-3 and 6-2-8). A Rule 60(b) motion does not affect finality or toll the time to appeal; leave of the appellate court is needed only while an appeal is actually pending.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","Alabama · civil rules","Errors not affecting substantial rights are disregarded.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","Alabama · civil rules","Automatic stay of execution for 30 days after entry (14 in district court; 7 in eviction cases). Injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed. A stay on appeal is obtained by a supersedeas bond approved by the court; no bond is required of the State. The court may stay enforcement pending post-trial motions. In district court, execution is automatically stayed pending post-trial motions, and a costs bond or s","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 63. Inability of a judge to proceed","Alabama · civil rules","A successor judge may continue a trial or hearing after certifying familiarity with the record; in nonjury trials, must recall disputed material witnesses on request.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 64. Seizure of person or property","Alabama · civil rules","Pre-judgment seizure (attachment, detinue) requires a sworn affidavit and judicial examination. If the court finds a real risk to the property, the writ may issue without a hearing, but the defendant may demand a hearing within 5 days of seizure, and the writ then expires on the 15th day unless continued after a hearing.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 64A. Notice of right to claim exemption from garnishment","Alabama · civil rules","When garnishment issues on a judgment where the defendant did not appear, notice of exemption rights in a prescribed form must be served at least 5 days before judgment against the garnishee. The form notes up to $7,500 in personal property may be exempt under state law.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 64B. Contest of claim of exemption (garnishment)","Alabama · civil rules","The defendant files a sworn claim of exemption. If the plaintiff does not contest within 15 calendar days after the claim is filed, the garnishment is dismissed or modified. A contest hearing must be set within 7 calendar days after the contest is filed.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions","Alabama · civil rules","No preliminary injunction without notice. A TRO without notice requires an affidavit or verified complaint and a lawyer’s written certificate of notice efforts, and expires within 10 days unless extended (the 10-day limit does not apply in domestic-relations cases). The adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice. Security is mandatory, except for the State and, at the court’s discretion, in domestic cases. ","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 65.1. Security; proceedings against sureties","Alabama · civil rules","A surety submits to the court’s jurisdiction and appoints the clerk as its agent; liability is enforced by motion.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers","Alabama · civil rules","An action with a receiver cannot be dismissed without court order; administration follows Ala. Code §§ 6-6-620 to 6-6-628.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit in court","Alabama · civil rules","With leave and notice, a party may deposit money or property in court; money goes into an interest-bearing insured account and is paid out only on court order.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of judgment","Alabama · civil rules","A defending party may serve an offer more than 15 days before trial (federal: 14); the offeree has 10 days to accept. If the final judgment is not more favorable, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer. Only defending parties may make offers. District court: 14 and 7 days.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution","Alabama · civil rules","The clerk issues the writ on application, with a notice of exemption rights (Form 92) served by the sheriff at levy. The debtor may file a notarized claim of exemption before sale; the creditor must contest within the time in § 6-10-26. Post-judgment discovery is allowed under these rules.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 70. Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","Alabama · civil rules","If a party fails to perform a directed act, the court may appoint someone to do it, issue attachment or sequestration, hold the party in contempt, or vest title directly by judgment.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 70A. Contempt in civil cases","Alabama · civil rules","Defines direct, constructive, criminal, and civil contempt. Direct contempt may be punished summarily with a written order; sentence within 7 days if not imposed immediately. Constructive contempt starts by petition (or counterclaim) with notice and is governed by the civil rules; indigent alleged criminal contemnors get appointed counsel on request. Contempt judgments are reviewed by appeal. New Rule 70A(g)(3) (eff.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 71. Process for or against nonparties","Alabama · civil rules","A nonparty may enforce an order in its favor, and be bound by enforcement of an order against it, as if a party.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 71A. Condemnation of property","Alabama · civil rules","Omitted; Alabama eminent-domain procedure is governed by statute (Title 18).","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 71B. Appeals from arbitration awards","Alabama · civil rules","A party must file a notice of appeal of an arbitration award in circuit court within 30 days after service of notice of the award, or review is waived. The clerk or court enters the award as a judgment; a party opposing it must then file a Rule 59 motion to vacate on grounds in Ala. Code § 6-6-14 or other law, and the ruling on that motion is appealed under Rule 4, Ala. R. App. P. Subdivision (f) amended eff. Oct. 16","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 71C. Enforcement of arbitration awards","Alabama · civil rules","If no Rule 71B appeal is filed within 30 days after service of notice of the award, a party may move in circuit court for entry of judgment on the award; the clerk or court enters it promptly, and execution follows. Amended eff. Oct. 16, 2026.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 72-76. Appeals","Alabama · civil rules","Omitted; appellate procedure is governed by the Alabama Rules of Appellate Procedure.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 77. Circuit courts and clerks","Alabama · civil rules","Courts are always open for filing. The clerk serves notice of entry of orders by mail or electronically. Lack of clerk notice does not extend appeal time, except the circuit court may extend the time to appeal (or cross-appeal) by up to 30 days for excusable neglect based on failure to learn of the entry (or of another party’s notice of appeal).","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 78. Motion day","Alabama · civil rules","Courts may decide motions on written submissions and may deny a motion to dismiss without a hearing unless a hearing is requested. Unless the court orders otherwise, an order granting a motion to dismiss carries an automatic right to amend within 10 days after service of the order (no federal counterpart).","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 79. Books and records kept by the clerk","Alabama · civil rules","Civil docket, minute book of final judgments, indices, and calendars; records may be kept electronically; files may not be removed without permission.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 80. Stenographic transcript as evidence","Alabama · civil rules","Prior testimony admissible at a later trial may be proved by a certified transcript.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 81. Applicability in general","Alabama · civil rules","Lists statutory proceedings (e.g., mandamus and other remedial writs, election contests, workers’ compensation, will contests, quo warranto) where these rules apply to the extent the statute does not provide the practice. Not applicable to arbitrators or agencies. Translates older terms (demurrer, bill in equity, decree pro confesso) to their rule equivalents.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 82. Jurisdiction and venue","Alabama · civil rules","Rules do not change jurisdiction or venue statutes but resolve conflicts for individual defendants: residents are sued in the county of residence, or for personal non-contract actions in the county where the act or omission occurred; real-estate actions where the land lies. When claims or parties are joined, venue proper for one claim is proper for all. On timely motion the court must transfer (not dismiss) an action","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 83. Local court rules","Alabama · civil rules","All local rules were abolished effective April 14, 1992; no local rules are permitted.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 84. Forms","Alabama · civil rules","The Appendix of Forms is sufficient under the rules; district-court forms are encouraged.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 85. Title","Alabama · civil rules","Cited as the Alabama Rules of Civil Procedure (Ala. R. Civ. P.).","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 86. Effective date","Alabama · civil rules","Original effective date six months after adoption (1973); district-court modifications effective January 16, 1977.","#st-al-rules"],["Rule 87. Limited-scope representation","Alabama · civil rules","Under Rule 1.2(c), Ala. R. Prof. Cond., a lawyer may appear for a limited scope stated in a notice filed and served at or before the start of the proceeding. The lawyer’s role ends without leave of court on filing a notice of completion certifying service on the client. Service on the lawyer is required only for matters within the stated scope.","#st-al-rules"],["Appeals in Alabama: 42 days, two intermediate courts split by subject and amount, and a 90-day deemed-denial trap","Alabama · appeals","Civil appeals from circuit and probate courts go by notice of appeal, filed in the trial court within 42 days of entry of judgment. The Supreme Court of Alabama takes general civil appeals over $50,000; the Court of Civil Appeals has exclusive jurisdiction of civil cases of $50,000 or less (exclusive of interest and costs), domestic-relations and workers’ compensation appeals, and most agency appeals (Ala. Code § 12-","#st-al-appeals"],["Supreme Court vs. Court of Civil Appeals","Alabama · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","The Court of Civil Appeals has exclusive appellate jurisdiction of civil cases where the amount involved does not exceed $50,000 (the recovery below if any, otherwise the amount claimed), all agency appeals except from the Public Service Commission, workers’ compensation, and domestic-relations cases (annulment, divorce, adoption, custody). Other civil appeals go to the Supreme Court. Naming the wrong appellate court","#st-al-appeals"],["Pour-over transfers","Alabama · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","The Supreme Court may transfer to the Court of Civil Appeals any civil appeal within its own jurisdiction, except cases presenting a substantial federal or state constitutional question, a novel legal question with significant statewide impact, utility-rate cases, bond-validation proceedings, and bar discipline. Ala. Code § 12-2-7(6)","#st-al-appeals"],["Final judgments and Rule 54(b)","Alabama · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","An appeal of right lies from any final judgment of circuit or probate court. A ruling on fewer than all claims or parties is not final unless the trial court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay and expressly directs entry of judgment, or the judgment is final as to served defendants under Civil Rule 4(f). On appeal from a judgment, a party may obtain review of any judgment, order, or ruling of the ","#st-al-appeals"],["Interlocutory appeals of right (14 days)","Alabama · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","Only 14 days to appeal interlocutory orders granting, continuing, modifying, refusing, or dissolving an injunction (or refusing to dissolve or modify one); interlocutory orders appointing or refusing to appoint a receiver; interlocutory orders determining the right to public office; judgments validating public obligations; and final juvenile-court orders. Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(1)(A)–(E)","#st-al-appeals"],["Arbitration orders are appealable as of right","Alabama · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","An order granting or denying a motion to compel arbitration is appealable as of right within 42 days of entry. An order granting or denying a Civil Rule 59 motion to vacate a judgment entered on an arbitration award (Rule 71B) is also appealable within 42 days. Ala. R. App. P. 4(d), 4(e); Ala. R. Civ. P. 71B","#st-al-appeals"],["Contempt: pending claim denied by operation of law","Alabama · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","A pending contempt claim is denied by operation of law when an order disposes of all other claims, so that order is a final, appealable judgment unless it expressly reserves the contempt claim (effective Mar. 1, 2026). Contempt adjudications are reviewed by appeal. Ala. R. Civ. P. 70A(g)","#st-al-appeals"],["Denial of campaign-contribution recusal motion","Alabama · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","In civil actions, an order denying a motion to recuse under the campaign-contribution statute is appealable like a final order, but only within 30 days of the denial. If the statutory contribution threshold is met, the trial-court action is stayed in all respects while the appeal is pending. Ala. Code § 12-24-3(d)","#st-al-appeals"],["42 days, jurisdictional","Alabama · appeals · Notice of appeal: time, tolling, and form","File the notice of appeal with the trial-court clerk (it may be e-filed through the trial court’s system) within 42 days (6 weeks) of entry of the judgment. Entry is the date the order is input into the State Judicial Information System, or the date a judge electronically transmits it. An untimely appeal must be dismissed, and appellate courts cannot extend the time. Ala. R. App. P. 3(a)(1), 4(a)(1), 2(a)(1), 2(b), 2","#st-al-appeals"],["Tolling motions and the 90-day deemed denial","Alabama · appeals · Notice of appeal: time, tolling, and form","A post-judgment motion under Civil Rule 50, 52, 55, or 59 suspends the appeal time, and the full 42 days run from the order disposing of it. If the trial court does not rule within 90 days, the motion is denied by operation of law and the 42 days run from that date, unless all parties expressly consented on the record to extend the time or the appellate court extended it. A Rule 60(b) motion does not toll. Ala. R. Ap","#st-al-appeals"],["Premature notices","Alabama · appeals · Notice of appeal: time, tolling, and form","A notice filed after announcement but before entry is treated as filed on the day of entry. A notice filed while a Rule 50, 52, 55, or 59 motion is pending is held in abeyance and becomes effective when the last such motion is decided. Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(4), (5)","#st-al-appeals"],["Extension only in the trial court, only for lack of notice","Alabama · appeals · Notice of appeal: time, tolling, and form","The circuit court may extend the appeal time by up to 30 days beyond the original deadline only on a showing of excusable neglect based on a party’s failure to learn of the entry of judgment (likewise for cross-appeals when a party did not learn of another’s notice of appeal). Lack of clerk notice otherwise does not affect the deadline. Ala. R. Civ. P. 77(d); Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(1)","#st-al-appeals"],["Cross-appeals","Alabama · appeals · Notice of appeal: time, tolling, and form","Any other party may file a notice of appeal within 14 days (2 weeks) after the first notice is filed, or within the original time, whichever expires last. Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(2)","#st-al-appeals"],["Contents, service, docketing statement","Alabama · appeals · Notice of appeal: time, tolling, and form","The notice must name every appellant and appellee (no “et al.” or “etc.”), designate the judgment or order, and name the court; the designation does not limit review. It must be accompanied by the docketing statement (Form 24, 25, or 26). Appellants serve adverse parties; the clerk serves the appellate clerk, court reporter, and counsel. A missing docketing statement does not void the notice but can lead to a stay, d","#st-al-appeals"],["Docket fee and security for costs","Alabama · appeals · Notice of appeal: time, tolling, and form","Civil docket fee is $200, paid when the notice is filed; if the notice is e-filed, the fee goes directly to the appellate clerk and must be received within 7 days. Security for costs must be filed with the notice in the trial court unless exempt or covered by a supersedeas bond. Ala. R. App. P. 35A(a)(1), 12(a), 7","#st-al-appeals"],["Automatic 30-day stay, then bond","Alabama · appeals · Stays and supersedeas","No execution issues for 30 days after entry (except injunctions and receiverships). To stay a money judgment on appeal, post a supersedeas bond of 150% of the judgment if it is $10,000 or less, or 125% if more, approved by the trial-court clerk; approval (unless contested) stays the judgment. The State needs no bond. Relief is sought first in the trial court; the appellate court may act if that is impracticable or wa","#st-al-appeals"],["Designations and transcript order within 7 days","Alabama · appeals · The record on appeal","Within 7 days (1 week) after filing the notice, the appellant must file and serve a designation of the clerk’s record and pay the court reporter’s estimated cost and file the transcript purchase order (Form 1A), with a statement of issues if less than all is designated. If the appellant challenges the sufficiency of the evidence for a finding, all relevant evidence must be in the transcript. The appellee may counter-","#st-al-appeals"],["Completion deadlines","Alabama · appeals · The record on appeal","Clerk’s record and certificate of completion: 28 days after the notice of appeal. Reporter’s transcript: 56 days. The clerk assembles the full record within 7 days after the transcript is filed (28 days after the notice if no transcript). The trial court may give up to four 7-day extensions for the transcript, never past 84 days from the notice; further 7-day extensions come only from the appellate court on a timely ","#st-al-appeals"],["No transcript: statement of the evidence","Alabama · appeals · The record on appeal","If no report was made or a transcript is unavailable, serve a statement of the evidence within 28 days after the notice; the appellee has 14 days to object or propose amendments; the trial court settles it. Ala. R. App. P. 10(d)","#st-al-appeals"],["Supplementing or correcting the record","Alabama · appeals · The record on appeal","Motions to supplement or correct go first to the trial court; a dissatisfied party may seek appellate relief within 7 days after the order (or after the 14-day period for the trial court to rule). Ala. R. App. P. 10(f)","#st-al-appeals"],["Schedule","Alabama · appeals · Briefs: schedule, contents, and limits","Appellant: 28 days after the date on the certificate of completion of the record. Appellee: 21 days after the appellant’s brief. Reply: 14 days after the appellee’s brief. With a cross-appeal: appellee/cross-appellant 21 days; appellant/cross-appellee 21 days; cross-reply 14 days. One extension of up to 7 days may be granted. An appellant who misses the deadline faces a motion to dismiss; an appellee who files no bri","#st-al-appeals"],["Word limits and font","Alabama · appeals · Briefs: schedule, contents, and limits","Principal brief 14,000 words; reply 7,000 (death cases 16,000/8,000). Counted from the statement of the case onward, including headings, footnotes, and quotations. Motions to exceed must show extraordinary circumstances and be filed at least 7 days before the brief is due; overlength filings without leave may draw a penalty reducing the limit by up to 1,000 words. Century Schoolbook 14, double-spaced, fully justified","#st-al-appeals"],["Required contents","Alabama · appeals · Briefs: schedule, contents, and limits","Statement on oral argument, table of contents, statement of jurisdiction (with filing dates showing timeliness), table of authorities, statement of the case identifying the adverse rulings and record pages, issues, facts with record cites (“C.” for clerk’s record, “R.” for transcript), standard of review, summary of argument (never more than 5 pages), argument with pinpoint citations (Bluebook, ALWD, or Alabama Supre","#st-al-appeals"],["Supplemental authority","Alabama · appeals · Briefs: schedule, contents, and limits","Notice of supplemental authority by letter, 400 words maximum, stating the reasons and the brief page it supports; responses similarly limited. Ala. R. App. P. 28B","#st-al-appeals"],["Motions","Alabama · appeals · Motions, rehearing, and certiorari","Responses within 7 days; replies within 7 days after the response. Procedural motions (including extensions) may be decided without waiting for a response. Limits: motion 2,000 words, supporting or opposing memorandum 3,000, reply 1,000. Emergency motions need the capitalized caption, a ruling-by date, and a statement that the movant contacted the clerk’s office at least a day ahead and consulted opposing counsel. Al","#st-al-appeals"],["Rehearing: 14 days, brief required","Alabama · appeals · Motions, rehearing, and certiorari","A party that did not prevail may apply for rehearing within 14 days of the decision, filing a supporting brief with the application (3,000 words each). Only a party that filed a brief on original submission may apply as of right. A second application is considered only if the first produced a reversal or substantial modification. Opposition brief, if any, is due within 14 days. Ala. R. App. P. 40(a)–(c), (f), (g)","#st-al-appeals"],["Certiorari to the Supreme Court from the Court of Civil Appeals","Alabama · appeals · Motions, rehearing, and certiorari","Discretionary, and granted only on listed grounds: a decision first holding a statute or ordinance valid or invalid or first construing a controlling constitutional provision; one affecting a class of constitutional, state, or county officers; a question of first impression for the Supreme Court; a conflict with prior decisions of the U.S. Supreme Court, the Supreme Court of Alabama, or either Alabama court of appeal","#st-al-appeals"],["Facts on certiorari come from the rehearing application","Alabama · appeals · Motions, rehearing, and certiorari","If you disagree with the Court of Civil Appeals’ statement of facts (or it issued a no-opinion affirmance), put your proposed additional, corrected, or own statement of facts in the application for rehearing; the certiorari petition must copy that statement verbatim with record references, with a verification when you propose your own statement. Ala. R. App. P. 39(d)(5), 40(e)","#st-al-appeals"],["Certificate of judgment","Alabama · appeals · Motions, rehearing, and certiorari","Issues 18 days after entry of judgment (or 18 days after denial of rehearing in the courts of appeals). In the Supreme Court, denial of rehearing issues the certificate immediately unless the first application produced a substantial modification. Ala. R. App. P. 41(a)","#st-al-appeals"],["No-opinion affirmances","Alabama · appeals · Motions, rehearing, and certiorari","The appellate courts may affirm without opinion in specified circumstances. Ala. R. App. P. 53, 54","#st-al-appeals"],["Permissive appeal (Rule 5)","Alabama · appeals · Interlocutory review by permission and by mandamus","Available only in civil cases within the Supreme Court’s original appellate jurisdiction (not Court of Civil Appeals cases). The trial judge must certify a controlling question of law with substantial ground for difference of opinion, that an immediate appeal would materially advance termination, and that it would avoid protracted and expensive litigation, stating the question. Certification is presumptively timely w","#st-al-appeals"],["Mandamus and prohibition","Alabama · appeals · Interlocutory review by permission and by mandamus","A petition for a writ directed to a trial judge goes to the appellate court with jurisdiction, with a full appendix of the order, related filings, and transcripts (6,000 words). It must be filed within a reasonable time, presumptively the same as the time for appeal (42 days); a later petition must show good cause. Review in the Supreme Court of a court of appeals’ decision on an original writ petition is by a new pe","#st-al-appeals"],["Mailbox and carrier rules","Alabama · appeals · Filing, service, time computation, and e-filing","A paper filing is timely only if received by the deadline, except it is deemed filed on the mailing date if sent by USPS certified, registered, or express mail, or on the date a commercial carrier receives it for delivery within 3 calendar days (with the carrier’s dated documentation). Ala. R. App. P. 25(a)(3)","#st-al-appeals"],["Computing time","Alabama · appeals · Filing, service, time computation, and e-filing","Exclude the trigger day; roll a last day falling on a weekend, holiday, or inaccessible-clerk day to the next business day; exclude intermediate weekends and holidays only for periods under 7 days. Holidays include those set by the governor, chief justice, legislature, President, or Congress. Ala. R. App. P. 26(a)","#st-al-appeals"],["Appellate e-filing (ACIS)","Alabama · appeals · Filing, service, time computation, and e-filing","Attorneys may e-file in the appellate courts after registering; pro se parties are excluded from e-filing (except records). Licensed Alabama lawyers and pro hac vice lawyers admitted in the appeal may register. A document is filed when successfully uploaded, measured in Central time; PDFs at 200 DPI or more, with bookmarks for each section. In the Supreme Court, e-filed briefs and petitions still require 2 additional","#st-al-appeals"],["Signatures and certifications","Alabama · appeals · Filing, service, time computation, and e-filing","Every brief or motion must be signed by counsel of record with address and telephone; signing certifies proper purpose, legal warrant, and that factual statements are supported in the record. Ala. R. App. P. 25A","#st-al-appeals"],["Appeal to circuit court for trial de novo","Alabama · appeals · District-court judgments","Final district-court judgments are appealed to circuit court for a new trial, not to an appellate court (with exceptions in § 12-12-72 and juvenile cases). An appellant who wants a jury must demand it in the notice of appeal; the appellee must demand within 14 days of service of the notice. The time to take the de novo appeal is set by statute and was not collected. Compulsory counterclaims must be added within 30 da","#st-al-appeals"],["Notice of appeal from final civil judgment","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","42 days from entry of judgment (input into SJIS or e-transmission by judge).\nJurisdictional; no appellate extension (Rules 2(b), 26(b)). Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(1); Ala. R. Civ. P. 58(c)","#st-al-appeals"],["Notice of appeal after post-judgment motion","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","42 days from entry of order disposing of last Rule 50/52/55/59 motion, or deemed-denial date.\nRule 60(b) motions do not toll. Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(3); Ala. R. Civ. P. 59.1","#st-al-appeals"],["Post-judgment motion deemed denied","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","90 days from filing of the Rule 50/52/55/59 motion.\nUnless all parties consent on the record or the appellate court extends. District court: 14 days. Ala. R. Civ. P. 59.1","#st-al-appeals"],["Motion for new trial / to alter, amend, or vacate / renewed JML / amend findings","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended (Rule 6(b)). District court 14 days; eviction and unlawful detainer 7 calendar days. Ala. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)","#st-al-appeals"],["Motion to set aside default judgment","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of default judgment.\nTolls under App. Rule 4(a)(3); 59.1 applies. Ala. R. Civ. P. 55(c)","#st-al-appeals"],["Appeal of interlocutory injunction, receivership, public-office, bond-validation, or juvenile order","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of the order. Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(1)(A)–(E)","#st-al-appeals"],["Cross-appeal","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of first notice of appeal (or original 42 days, whichever later). Ala. R. App. P. 4(a)(2)","#st-al-appeals"],["Appeal of order granting or denying motion to compel arbitration","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","42 days from entry of the order.\nAppeal as of right. Ala. R. App. P. 4(d)","#st-al-appeals"],["Trial-court extension of appeal time for failure to learn of entry","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from expiration of original appeal time.\nExcusable neglect based on lack of notice only. Ala. R. Civ. P. 77(d)","#st-al-appeals"],["Appeal of denial of campaign-contribution recusal motion","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from order denying recusal.\nCivil actions; trial stayed if statutory threshold met. Ala. Code § 12-24-3(d)","#st-al-appeals"],["Designation of clerk’s record; transcript purchase order and payment","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from filing of notice of appeal.\nAppellee counter-designation 7 days after receipt. Ala. R. App. P. 10(b)(1), (2)","#st-al-appeals"],["Docket fee received by appellate clerk (e-filed notice)","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from e-filing of notice of appeal.\n$200 civil. Ala. R. App. P. 12(a), 35A","#st-al-appeals"],["Clerk’s record completed","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from filing of notice of appeal. Ala. R. App. P. 11(a)(1)","#st-al-appeals"],["Reporter’s transcript filed","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","56 days from filing of notice of appeal.\nTrial-court extensions cannot go beyond 84 days from the notice. Ala. R. App. P. 11(a)(2), 11(c)","#st-al-appeals"],["Statement of the evidence served (no transcript)","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from filing of notice of appeal.\nObjections 14 days after service. Ala. R. App. P. 10(d)","#st-al-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from date on certificate of completion of the record.\nOne extension up to 7 days (Rule 31(d)). Ala. R. App. P. 31(a)","#st-al-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from filing of appellant’s brief. Ala. R. App. P. 31(a)","#st-al-appeals"],["Reply brief","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of appellee’s brief. Ala. R. App. P. 31(a)","#st-al-appeals"],["Appellant/cross-appellee brief in cross-appeal","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from filing of appellee/cross-appellant’s brief.\nCross-reply 14 days after. Ala. R. App. P. 31(a)","#st-al-appeals"],["Motion to exceed word limit","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from before the brief’s due date.\nFiled at least 7 days before due date. Ala. R. App. P. 28(j)(3)","#st-al-appeals"],["Response to motion","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from service of motion.\nReply 7 days after response. Ala. R. App. P. 27(a)","#st-al-appeals"],["Application for rehearing (with brief)","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from release of decision.\nBrief on original submission required. Ala. R. App. P. 40(c)","#st-al-appeals"],["Petition for certiorari to Supreme Court from Court of Civil Appeals","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from court of Civil Appeals decision, or its ruling on rehearing.\nCannot be extended in civil cases (Rule 2(b)). Ala. R. App. P. 39(c)","#st-al-appeals"],["Certificate of judgment issues","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","18 days from entry of appellate judgment (or denial of rehearing in courts of appeals). Ala. R. App. P. 41(a)","#st-al-appeals"],["Trial judge’s Rule 5 certification (presumptive)","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from entry of interlocutory order.\nLater certification requires good-cause statement. Ala. R. App. P. 5(a)(1)","#st-al-appeals"],["Petition for permission to appeal (Rule 5)","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of trial judge’s certification.\nSupreme Court cases only; answer 14 days. Ala. R. App. P. 5(a)(2)","#st-al-appeals"],["Mandamus petition (presumptively reasonable time)","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","42 days from entry of challenged order.\nLater petitions must show good cause. Ala. R. App. P. 21(a)(3)","#st-al-appeals"],["Supreme Court review of court of appeals ruling on original writ petition","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from grant or denial by court of appeals. Ala. R. App. P. 21(e)(2)","#st-al-appeals"],["Automatic stay of execution","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of judgment.\nNot for injunction or receivership judgments; district court 14 days. Ala. R. Civ. P. 62(a)","#st-al-appeals"],["Jury demand by appellee in de novo appeal to circuit court","Alabama · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of notice of appeal.\nAppellant must demand in the notice of appeal. Ala. Code § 12-12-71","#st-al-appeals"],["Arkansas Supreme Court","Arkansas · courts","Hears appeals in the categories Supreme Court Rule 1-2(a) assigns to it (state constitutional questions, elections, writs against officials or circuit courts, attorney and judicial discipline, second appeals, and appeals required by law), takes cases reassigned from the Court of Appeals, and reviews Court of Appeals decisions only on discretionary petition for review. It also writes the court rules, controls bar admi","#st-ar"],["Arkansas Court of Appeals","Arkansas · courts","The default appellate court: every civil appeal is filed there unless Rule 1-2(a) places it in the Supreme Court. Wrongly filed cases are transferred, not dismissed. There is no appeal of right from its decisions.","#st-ar"],["Circuit courts","Arkansas · courts","The trial courts of general jurisdiction since Constitutional Amendment 80 merged law and equity. Each county has criminal, civil, juvenile, probate, and domestic relations divisions, but the divisions are for case management only and do not limit any circuit judge’s jurisdiction. Specialty dockets (drug, veterans, mental health courts) operate within divisions.","#st-ar"],["District courts (including small claims divisions)","Arkansas · courts","Under Administrative Order 18, district courts have exclusive jurisdiction of contract claims up to $100 and concurrent jurisdiction with circuit court of contract, personal-property recovery, and property-damage claims up to $5,000 (check the current statute, Ark. Code Ann. § 16-17-704, for any later change). The small claims division has the same limits, and lawyers may not take part. Procedure is governed by the s","#st-ar"],["Citizen Participation in Government Act","Arkansas · anti-SLAPP","Arkansas’s anti-SLAPP law is the Citizen Participation in Government Act, enacted by Act 1843 of 2005 as a new Subchapter 5 of Title 16, Chapter 63. It protects “privileged communications” and acts in furtherance of free speech or petitioning under the U.S. or Arkansas Constitution “in connection with an issue of public interest or concern.” Protected acts include statements made before or to a legislative, executive","#st-ar-slapp"],["Immunity from civil liability","Arkansas · anti-SLAPP","A person making a privileged communication or a protected speech or petition act is immune from civil liability unless the statement was made knowing it was false or with reckless disregard of whether it was false. Ark. Code Ann. § 16-63-504 (Act 1843 of 2005)","#st-ar-slapp"],["The mechanism is a verification requirement, not a special motion","Arkansas · anti-SLAPP","For any claim arising from a possible privileged communication or an act that could reasonably be seen as protected speech or petitioning, both the claimant and the claimant’s attorney must file, at the same time as the pleading, a written verification under oath. It must certify that they read the claim, that it is well grounded in fact and law after reasonable inquiry, that the act sued on is not a privileged commu","#st-ar-slapp"],["Strike for no verification; 10-day cure","Arkansas · anti-SLAPP","An unverified claim must be stricken unless it is verified within 10 days after the omission is called to the attention of the claimant or the claimant’s attorney. If the claim is verified in violation of § 16-63-505, the court, on motion or on its own, must sanction the signers, the represented party, or both. Sanctions may include dismissal and reasonable expenses, including a reasonable attorney’s fee. Ark. Code A","#st-ar-slapp"],["Discovery stay and 30-day hearing","Arkansas · anti-SLAPP","Filing a motion to dismiss or strike under § 16-63-506 stays all discovery and pending hearings and motions. The hearing must be held within 30 days after service unless emergency matters require a later date. On motion and for good cause, the court may allow specified discovery or other proceedings to go forward. The Act sets no filing deadline for the motion. Ark. Code Ann. § 16-63-507 (Act 1843 of 2005)","#st-ar-slapp"],["Fees and damages","Arkansas · anti-SLAPP","Fees come through the § 16-63-506(b)(1) sanction (reasonable expenses including attorney’s fees) for a falsely verified claim. Other compensatory damages require proof that the claim was brought or continued to harass, intimidate, punish, or maliciously inhibit protected speech or petitioning. The Act preserves any other recovery available by common law, statute, or rule. Ark. Code Ann. §§ 16-63-506(b), -508 (Act 184","#st-ar-slapp"],["Appeal: no listed interlocutory appeal","Arkansas · anti-SLAPP","The Act provides no immediate appeal, and Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 2(a) does not list orders under the Act among the appealable interlocutory orders. Its immunity exception, 2(a)(10), covers only sovereign immunity and the immunity of government officials. Whether Arkansas appellate courts have allowed an interlocutory appeal of an anti-SLAPP ruling by case law was not checked. Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 2(a); Ark. Code Ann. §","#st-ar-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: reciprocity, $200 per case, and an Arkansas sponsor","Arkansas · out of state","Rule XIV applies only to a lawyer licensed in a state, D.C., or territory that would let an Arkansas lawyer appear in its courts. Pay $200 per case to the Clerk of the Arkansas Supreme Court first, then file in the trial court a sworn motion with proof of payment. The motion must include contact details, the associated Arkansas lawyer’s name and bar number, every Arkansas case in the past 2 years, all licensing juris","#st-ar-oos"],["Mandatory cap: three Arkansas cases in 12 months","Arkansas · out of state","The court must deny the motion if the lawyer has participated, served as counsel, or appeared pro hac vice in 3 Arkansas cases during the 12 months before the motion. The court may also examine the lawyer on Arkansas ethics and deny the motion for unauthorized practice or other good cause. Filing the motion submits the lawyer to the jurisdiction of the Supreme Court Committee on Professional Conduct, and misconduct c","#st-ar-oos"],["Temporary practice without admission","Arkansas · out of state","Arkansas has adopted the multijurisdictional-practice rule: a lawyer in good standing elsewhere may provide temporary services in association with an actively participating Arkansas lawyer, services related to a pending or expected proceeding where the lawyer is or expects to be authorized, ADR-related services, and services arising out of the home-state practice. In-house and federally authorized practice is also al","#st-ar-oos"],["Permanent admission routes","Arkansas · out of state","Admission on motion requires an ABA-accredited J.D., active practice for 3 of the last 5 years, and reciprocity from the jurisdiction of the applicant’s principal practice for the prior 2 years. UBE score transfer requires a scaled score of at least 270 earned within the 36 months before applying. Ark. Bar Adm. R. XVI(1), XVIII(1)","#st-ar-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances are allowed by notice","Arkansas · out of state","A lawyer may appear for a limited scope by filing and serving a notice of limited scope representation, and the lawyer’s role ends without leave on filing a notice of completion. Ghost-writing is allowed if the document carries the required notation naming the assisting Arkansas lawyer. Opposing counsel may contact the client directly unless written notice of the limited representation has been given, and then only o","#st-ar-oos"],["E-filing access and attorney-issued subpoenas","Arkansas · out of state","E-filing uses the Administrative Office of the Courts’ system statewide. Each circuit or district court decides whether to adopt it, and once adopted it is exclusive, except for pro se filers and filers with special needs. Registered users consent to electronic service, and the e-filing fee is $20 to start a circuit or district court case or for appellate civil filings. Only an attorney “admitted to practice in this ","#st-ar-oos"],["Governing code and currency","Arkansas · ethics","The Arkansas Rules of Professional Conduct follow the ABA Model Rules’ numbering, with Arkansas variations. The compilation collected (dated June 12, 2025) may not include amendments adopted December 11, 2025 (2025 Ark. 221), which the court’s amendments list shows took effect that day. Check that order. Discipline runs through the Supreme Court Committee on Professional Conduct and the Office of Professional Conduct","#st-ar-ethics"],["Broader confidentiality exception for crimes","Arkansas · ethics","Rule 1.6(b)(1) lets a lawyer reveal information to prevent “the commission of a criminal act” of any kind. The ABA rule is limited to reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm. The fraud exceptions in (b)(2)–(3) follow the ABA pattern. Ark. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.6(b)(1)","#st-ar-ethics"],["Conflicts: related lawyers and no lateral screening","Arkansas · ethics","Rule 1.8(l) bars a lawyer from opposing a party represented by the lawyer’s parent, child, sibling, or spouse without the client’s informed consent confirmed in writing. Rule 1.8(e) allows advancing costs and paying an indigent client’s costs but has no humanitarian-gift exception. Rule 1.10(a) has no provision letting a firm screen a lateral hire to avoid imputing the hire’s former-client conflict. Ark. R. Prof’l Co","#st-ar-ethics"],["Trust accounts and client files","Arkansas · ethics","Trust records must be kept 5 years. No more than $500 of the lawyer’s own money may sit in trust to cover bank charges. Every trust account must report overdrafts to the Executive Director of the Office of Professional Conduct. Arkansas-only Rule 1.19 defines the client file, which the client gets on written request and which may not be held for unpaid copying costs. Files must be kept 5 years after the matter ends, ","#st-ar-ethics"],["Advertising and solicitation are stricter","Arkansas · ethics","Ads must name at least one Arkansas-licensed lawyer and the office location. Ads may not use clients or former clients, and dramatizations are banned; actors must be identified. Targeted solicitation letters go by regular mail only, must say “ADVERTISEMENT” in red ink on the envelope and on each page, must carry the Committee on Professional Conduct complaint notice, and in death cases may not be sent until 30 days a","#st-ar-ethics"],["Fees and misconduct","Arkansas · ethics","Contingent-fee agreements must be in writing and spell out the percentages at settlement, trial, and appeal, plus expenses and how they are deducted. Contingent fees are barred in divorce and support matters (except collecting amounts due under a final decree) and in criminal defense. Rule 8.4 has no counterpart to ABA Model Rule 8.4(g) on harassment and discrimination. Ark. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.5(c)–(d), 8.4","#st-ar-ethics"],["Generative AI in the courts","Arkansas · ethics","Administrative Order 25 (adopted December 11, 2025) warns that entering confidential or sealed client or court data into public generative-AI tools may violate Administrative Order 19, statutes, and the ethics rules. It bars court staff and clerks from exposing internal court data to public large language models. It is not a disclosure rule for filings. Admin. Order No. 25","#st-ar-ethics"],["120 days to serve, and extensions must be sought in time","Arkansas · land mine","Unserved defendants are dismissed at 120 days. An extension motion must be filed within the 120 days (or the current extension), and the order must be entered within 30 days after the motion or by the end of the period, whichever is later. Default judgments based on certified mail need a return receipt signed by the addressee or agent; unclaimed mail is not refusal. Ark. R. Civ. P. 4(g)(1)(A)(ii), 4(i)","#st-ar-unique"],["Plead facts and attach the contract","Arkansas · land mine","A complaint must state facts showing jurisdiction, venue, and entitlement to relief. Any written instrument a claim or defense is based on must be attached unless good cause is shown. An unspecified damages demand caps recovery below the federal diversity amount unless the demand shows more is sought. Ark. R. Civ. P. 8(a), 10(d)","#st-ar-unique"],["10-day motion responses, and hearings are waived","Arkansas · land mine","Responses to motions are due 10 days after service and replies 5 days after the response. Each must include a brief supporting statement with citations or it can be struck. Unless counsel requests a hearing, the court can rule on the papers once the reply time runs. Ark. R. Civ. P. 6(c), 7(b)(2), 78(c)","#st-ar-unique"],["Jury demand by 20 days before trial","Arkansas · land mine","There is no automatic jury. File a written demand no later than 20 days before the trial date or the jury is waived. Ark. R. Civ. P. 38(a), (c)","#st-ar-unique"],["Summary judgment cutoffs","Arkansas · land mine","Summary-judgment motions must be filed at least 45 days before a scheduled trial absent leave. Responses are due 21 days after service, replies 14 days after the response, and no supplemental materials may follow the reply. Ark. R. Civ. P. 56(a)–(c)","#st-ar-unique"],["Document subpoenas: 3 business days’ notice to the other side first","Arkansas · land mine","A subpoena for documents only must be served on all other parties at least 3 business days before it is served on the recipient, and everything produced must be shared promptly. Ark. R. Civ. P. 45(b)(1)","#st-ar-unique"],["Voluntary nonsuit: easy once, fatal twice","Arkansas · land mine","A plaintiff may dismiss without prejudice as of right up to final submission, but only by court order. A second voluntary dismissal of the same claim is an adjudication on the merits unless all parties stipulate otherwise. Twelve months with no action of record invites dismissal for want of prosecution. Ark. R. Civ. P. 41(a)–(b)","#st-ar-unique"],["Post-trial motions die on day 30, and the appeal clock starts","Arkansas · land mine","New-trial, JNOV, and amend-findings motions are due within 10 days after entry. If the judge has not ruled within 30 days after filing, the motion is deemed denied and the 30-day appeal period runs from that day, even if a hearing is scheduled later. Ark. R. Civ. P. 50(b)(2), 52(b)(1), 59(b); Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(1)","#st-ar-unique"],["Partial judgments need a Rule 54(b) certificate with findings","Arkansas · land mine","Without an executed Rule 54(b) certificate stating specific factual findings, an order leaving any claim or party unresolved is not appealable. Request the certificate within 14 days of entry. Ark. R. Civ. P. 54(b); Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 2(a)(11)","#st-ar-unique"],["Notice of appeal must abandon unresolved claims","Arkansas · land mine","A notice of appeal from a final order must state that the appellant abandons any pending but unresolved claim. That statement works as a dismissal with prejudice. Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 3(e)(vi)","#st-ar-unique"],["Directed-verdict motion at the close of all evidence","Arkansas · land mine","In a jury case, a party without the burden of proof waives any sufficiency-of-the-evidence argument on appeal unless it moved for a directed verdict at the close of all the evidence, not just the opponent’s case. Ark. R. Civ. P. 50(e)","#st-ar-unique"],["Pro hac vice: three cases a year and reciprocity","Arkansas · land mine","Arkansas courts must deny pro hac vice to a lawyer who appeared in 3 Arkansas cases in the prior 12 months. Lawyers from jurisdictions that do not admit Arkansas lawyers pro hac vice are not eligible. Each motion needs $200 paid in advance and a signed sponsor affidavit. Ark. Bar Adm. R. XIV(a)–(c), (f)","#st-ar-unique"],["No peremptory change of judge; disqualification for cause only","Arkansas · judges","The collected rules give no right to a peremptory substitution of judge. A judge must disqualify where impartiality might reasonably be questioned, including bias, family relationships to parties or lawyers, economic interest, prior involvement, or a public statement committing to a result. Paragraph (A)(4), the ABA ground for campaign contributions from a party or lawyer, is “[Reserved].” The comment says contributi","#st-ar-unique"],["Replacement judges and appellate recusal","Arkansas · judges","When a judge is disqualified, the Chief Justice (or a designee) assigns a sitting or retired judge under Administrative Order 16, which replaced the former special-judge election process (Administrative Order 1 is repealed). In the appellate courts, a party moves for a justice’s or judge’s disqualification under Rule 2-1, stating the specific facts, a reasonable time before submission. Admin. Order No. 16 §§ I–II; Ar","#st-ar-unique"],["Ark. Const. art. 2, § 6: whether it is broader than the First Amendment was not checked","Arkansas · speech","The Arkansas Constitution’s free speech and press guarantee was not among the collected texts, and no case law was reviewed. Do not assume protection beyond the First Amendment without research. Ark. Const. art. 2, § 6 (not collected)","#st-ar-unique"],["No pauper status for libel, slander, or malicious prosecution plaintiffs","Arkansas · speech","Rule 72 bars bringing an action for slander, libel, or malicious prosecution in forma pauperis, so a speech-tort plaintiff must pay filing fees. Ark. R. Civ. P. 72(d)","#st-ar-unique"],["Cameras need consent of the parties","Arkansas · speech","A judge may allow broadcasting or photographing, but a timely objection by any party or attorney prevents it, and a witness may refuse. Juvenile, probate, domestic relations, and drug court matters may never be broadcast. Admin. Order No. 6(b)–(c)","#st-ar-unique"],["Nine-of-twelve civil verdicts","Arkansas · unique rules","A civil verdict needs only 9 of 12 jurors, and every juror who agrees must sign. Ark. R. Civ. P. 48","#st-ar-unique"],["Nonparty fault must be pleaded","Arkansas · unique rules","To have a jury or judge allocate fault to a nonparty in an injury, death, or property case, the defendant must identify the nonparty and plead facts in its answer (or an amendment) and then make a prima facie case. The plaintiff gets 30 days to add the nonparty. Ark. R. Civ. P. 9(h), 49(c), 52(a)(2)","#st-ar-unique"],["Expert-consultation certification and no ex parte physician contact","Arkansas · unique rules","Signing a pleading certifies that, where a claim or defense requires expert proof, the party has consulted a qualified expert who finds a reasonable basis for it. Informal contact with an opposing party’s physician or psychotherapist is prohibited without that party’s express consent. Ark. R. Civ. P. 11(b)(5), 35(c)(2)","#st-ar-unique"],["Legislator-lawyer continuances","Arkansas · unique rules","Cases with a legislator party or attorney are stayed from 15 days before a General Assembly session until at least 30 days after adjournment. Proceedings set on days when an attorney-member’s legislative committee meets are reset if the attorney asks at least 3 days ahead. Ark. R. Civ. P. 40(c)","#st-ar-unique"],["Punitive damages trials are bifurcated on request","Arkansas · unique rules","On any party’s motion, if the evidence warrants, the jury decides compensatory liability and damages first and punitive damages in a second phase. Evidence of the defendant’s finances is kept out of phase one. Ark. R. Civ. P. 42(b)(2)","#st-ar-unique"],["Supersedeas bonds capped at $25 million","Arkansas · unique rules","No appeal bond in a civil case may exceed $25,000,000 regardless of the judgment amount, unless the appellant is shown to be dissipating assets to avoid payment. Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 8(c)","#st-ar-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope of rules","Arkansas · civil rules","Govern all civil suits and actions in the circuit courts, except as stated in Rule 81, and are to be construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","Arkansas · civil rules","There is one form of action, the “civil action.” Law and equity are merged in the circuit courts under Constitutional Amendment 80.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencement; “clerk” defined; separate actions and fees; medical-injury notice","Arkansas · civil rules","An action is commenced by filing a complaint with the clerk, who notes the date and precise time. A refiled case gets a new number and a new filing fee; no other claim (such as divorce or paternity) may be joined to a Domestic Abuse Act petition, and an adoption cannot be sought inside a guardianship case. Subdivision (d) requires 60 days’ written notice to each medical-care provider before a medical-injury suit, but","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 4. Summons and service of process","Arkansas · civil rules","The clerk issues the summons on filing; process is the summons plus complaint. Personal service rules cover individuals, minors, incarcerated persons, corporations, LLCs, partnerships, governments, and others; alternatives are certified mail restricted to the addressee (a default needs a return receipt signed by the addressee or agent, and unclaimed mail is not refusal), first-class mail with notice and acknowledgmen","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 5. Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","Arkansas · civil rules","Papers after the complaint are served on every party (on the attorney if represented) by delivery, regular mail, approved commercial delivery, or e-mail to an attorney; mail service is complete on mailing. Discovery is not filed unless the court orders. Confidential information under Administrative Order 19 must be left out or redacted, with an unredacted copy filed under seal. Clerks with fax machines must accept fa","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 6. Time","Arkansas · civil rules","Exclude the trigger day; if the last day is a weekend, legal holiday, or a day the clerk’s office is closed, run to the next open day; for periods under 14 days, skip intermediate weekends and holidays. Motions and notice of hearing must be served 20 days before the hearing, responses are due 10 days after service of the motion, and replies 5 days after the response. Add 3 business days after service by mail, commerc","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings and motions","Arkansas · civil rules","Only a complaint, answer, counterclaim reply, cross-claim answer, third-party complaint, and third-party answer are allowed. Every written motion, response, and reply must include a brief supporting statement of facts, law, and citations, with affidavits served at the same time, or it may be struck; the court need not grant an unopposed motion. Dispositive-motion submission follows Administrative Order 3(2)(B), and o","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","Arkansas · civil rules","A claim must state, in ordinary and concise language, “facts” showing jurisdiction, venue, and entitlement to relief. A demand for unliquidated damages that states no amount limits recovery to less than the federal diversity amount unless the demand shows more is sought. The affirmative-defense list includes comparative fault and the workers’ compensation exclusive remedy, and averments other than damages are admitte","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","Arkansas · civil rules","Capacity is denied by specific negative averment; fraud, mistake, duress, and undue influence must be pleaded with particularity. Subdivision (h) requires a defendant who wants a jury to allocate fault to a nonparty under Ark. Code Ann. § 16-61-202(c) to identify the nonparty and plead facts showing fault in the first responsive pleading (or an amendment once known); the plaintiff then has 30 days to amend to sue the","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","Arkansas · civil rules","Caption with court, title, case number, and designation; the complaint names all parties. Pleadings must show the signing attorney’s name, bar number, mailing address, telephone, fax, and e-mail. Averments go in numbered paragraphs, and a copy of any written instrument on which a claim or defense is based must be attached unless good cause is shown.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing; sanctions","Arkansas · civil rules","Every paper is signed by an attorney of record (except limited-scope drafting under Rule 87) and certifies proper purpose, legal support, and factual support. The certificate also covers (b)(5), requiring a party whose claim or defense needs expert proof to have consulted an expert who finds a reasonable basis, and (b)(6), redaction of confidential information. Sanctions are mandatory when a violation is found; a san","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections","Arkansas · civil rules","Answer within 30 days after service; 30 days from first publication or posting after a warning order; 60 days for a defendant incarcerated in Arkansas; 30 days for counterclaims and cross-claims; 30 days after notice of a federal remand order is filed. After a motion is denied or a more definite statement is served, plead within 10 days. Rule 12(b)(6) tests “failure to state facts,” and Arkansas adds 12(b)(8) (the Re","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and cross-claim","Arkansas · civil rules","Counterclaims arising from the same transaction or occurrence are compulsory, with the usual exceptions for claims in another pending action or where the opposing party used attachment. Permissive counterclaims and cross-claims against co-parties follow the federal pattern; an omitted counterclaim may be added by amendment under Rule 15.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","Arkansas · civil rules","A defendant may implead a person who is or may be liable to it without leave if the third-party complaint is filed within 10 days after the answer; otherwise leave is needed on notice to all parties. A plaintiff facing a counterclaim may implead on the same terms.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Arkansas · civil rules","A party may amend at any time without leave, except to add Rule 12(h)(1) defenses, but the court may strike the amendment or grant a continuance on motion for prejudice or undue delay. Respond within the remaining time or 20 days after service of the amendment, whichever is longer. Relation back for a changed party requires notice and knowledge within the Rule 4(i) service period (120 days); supplemental pleadings al","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 16. Pretrial procedure","Arkansas · civil rules","The court may hold a pretrial conference on simplifying issues, amendments, admissions, limiting experts, referral to a master, and settlement or mediation under Ark. Code Ann. § 16-7-202, and enters an order that controls unless modified to prevent manifest injustice.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 17. Parties plaintiff and defendant","Arkansas · civil rules","Actions are prosecuted by the real party in interest, with a reasonable time after objection for ratification, joinder, or substitution. A guardian sues or defends for a minor or incompetent person; otherwise a next friend or guardian ad litem acts, and no judgment may be rendered against a minor or incompetent person until a guardian or guardian ad litem has defended.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims and remedies","Arkansas · civil rules","A party may join as many claims as it has against an opponent, subject to Rule 13(a). Any claim may be severed, and the court may transfer a claim to another division in the interest of justice; a money claim and a fraudulent-conveyance claim may be joined before judgment on the money claim.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 19. Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","Arkansas · civil rules","Persons needed for complete relief or with an interest that could be impaired must be joined if subject to service; if joinder is not feasible, the court weighs prejudice, shaping of relief, adequacy of a judgment, and the plaintiff’s alternative remedy to decide whether to proceed or dismiss. Subject to Rule 23.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","Arkansas · civil rules","Plaintiffs or defendants may be joined if claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact; the court may order separate trials to prevent embarrassment, delay, or expense.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder","Arkansas · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped by order at any stage on just terms, and claims may be severed.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","Arkansas · civil rules","Claimants exposing the plaintiff to multiple liability may be required to interplead, even if claims lack common origin. A disinterested stakeholder who deposits the money or property in the registry is discharged and may be awarded reasonable litigation expenses, including attorney’s fees.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","Arkansas · civil rules","Requires numerosity, commonality, typicality, and adequacy, plus a finding that common questions predominate and a class action is superior; Arkansas uses this single predominance-and-superiority test for every class. Notice in money-relief classes must be the best practicable; costs of notice fall on the representatives but may be shifted on settlement or substantial success. Settlements, dismissals, and compromises","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 23.1. Actions by shareholders","Arkansas · civil rules","Derivative complaints must be verified, allege contemporaneous ownership, and plead with particularity the demand made on directors (and members, if necessary) or why none was made; dismissal or compromise requires court approval and notice.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 23.2. Actions relating to unincorporated associations","Arkansas · civil rules","Members may sue or be sued as a class through named representatives only if they will fairly and adequately protect the association and its members; Rule 23(d) and (e) procedures apply.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","Arkansas · civil rules","Intervention of right on an unconditional statutory right or an impaired, inadequately represented interest; permissive intervention on a conditional statutory right or common question, including by agencies administering a relied-upon statute. The motion must attach the pleading, and the court may require notice to the Attorney General when a state statute’s constitutionality is challenged.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","Arkansas · civil rules","After death, substitution must be moved within 90 days after the death is suggested on the record by service of a statement, or the action may be dismissed as to the decedent. The court may appoint a special administrator only for the litigation if there is no general personal representative. Guardians, transferees, and successor public officers may be substituted; the rule does not revive claims barred by limitation","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 26. General provisions governing discovery","Arkansas · civil rules","Scope is any nonprivileged matter relevant to a claim or defense and proportional to the needs of the case. The rule contains no initial-disclosure or discovery-conference requirement and no expert-report requirement; expert discovery is by interrogatory and deposition of testifying experts. Inadvertent production is presumed not to waive privilege if the producing party gives notice within 14 calendar days of discov","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 26.1. Electronic discovery","Arkansas · civil rules","An optional rule that applies only if the parties agree or the court orders it on motion for good cause, made within 120 days after the complaint is filed. The parties confer within 30 days and report a plan within 14 days after the conference. It covers preservation, form of production, inaccessible sources (burden on the objecting party), cost allocation, and a safe harbor for information lost through routine good-","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions before action or pending appeal","Arkansas · civil rules","A verified petition in the county of an expected adverse party’s residence; notice must be served at least 20 days before the hearing in the manner of a summons. Depositions may also be taken pending appeal on motion in the circuit court.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Arkansas · civil rules","Before an officer authorized to administer oaths or a court appointee; abroad on notice, by commission, by treaty, or by letter of request. Arkansas adds rules for foreign proceedings seeking Arkansas evidence and bars preferred-provider arrangements and any restriction on an attorney’s choice of court reporter.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations regarding discovery","Arkansas · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, parties may stipulate in writing to deposition arrangements and to modify other discovery procedures.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions upon oral examination","Arkansas · civil rules","The plaintiff needs leave to depose within 30 days after service on any defendant unless the defendant has sought discovery or special notice is given that the witness is leaving the state or country. The text sets no limit on the number or length of depositions (the Reporter’s Notes say the federal limit on the number of depositions was not adopted), but the court may limit time by order. Objections must be concise,","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions upon written questions","Arkansas · civil rules","No leave needed except for prisoners or early plaintiff depositions. Cross questions within 14 days after notice, redirect within 7 days after cross, and recross within 7 days after redirect.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 32. Use of depositions in court proceedings","Arkansas · civil rules","A deposition may be used against a party present or noticed: by any party to impeach; by an adverse party for any purpose if the deponent was a party or its officer, director, managing agent, or designee; and for any purpose if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or out of state, unable to attend, or cannot be subpoenaed. A deposition is not usable against a party who got less than 11 days’ notice and promp","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories to parties","Arkansas · civil rules","Answers or objections are due within 30 days after service, but a defendant gets the later of 30 days after service or 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. The text sets no numeric limit on interrogatories. Blanket objections are not recognized, grounds must be stated with particularity, and unstated grounds are waived; each interrogatory must be repeated before its answer.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 34. Production of documents and entry on land","Arkansas · civil rules","Responses are due in 30 days (a defendant gets the later of 30 days or 45 days after service of process). Objections must be specific and particular, blanket objections are not recognized, and an objection must say whether responsive materials are being withheld. Documents are produced as kept or organized by request category.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examinations","Arkansas · civil rules","On motion for good cause, by a physician or psychologist. Under subdivision (c), a party relying on a physical, mental, or emotional condition must sign a medical-records authorization within 30 days after request, and informal ex parte contact with another party’s physician or psychotherapist is prohibited without that party’s express consent.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","Arkansas · civil rules","Matters are admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days (a defendant gets the later of 30 days or 45 days after service of process). Requests must be in a separate, titled document not combined with other discovery; each request must be repeated before the answer; blanket objections are not recognized. An attorney’s signature on answers counts as the attorney’s oath and authority to bind the client.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to make discovery; sanctions","Arkansas · civil rules","Motions to compel require a good-faith conference statement; expenses are awarded to the winner unless the position was substantially justified. Failure to obey an order allows issue-establishing, preclusion, striking, dismissal, default, and contempt sanctions. Arkansas adds (e), sanctions for prejudicial failure to supplement under Rule 26(e), and (f), no expenses or fees against the State except as a statute allow","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury trial of right","Arkansas · civil rules","A jury must be demanded in writing at any time after commencement and no later than 20 days before the trial date; others may demand on remaining issues within 10 days. Failure to demand waives the jury, and a demand cannot be withdrawn without the parties’ consent.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","Arkansas · civil rules","Issues demanded are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or no jury right exists; otherwise the court tries them but may order a jury trial on motion. Advisory juries and consent juries are allowed.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 40. Trial settings and continuances","Arkansas · civil rules","Cases are set on request after issues are joined, or by the court. Continuances require a motion and good cause. Proceedings are stayed from 15 days before through at least 30 days after a General Assembly session when a party, an attorney, or certain officers are legislators, and are reset on legislative-committee meeting days if the attorney-member asks at least 3 days ahead.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","Arkansas · civil rules","A plaintiff may take a voluntary nonsuit as of right any time before final submission to the jury or court, effective only on entry of an order (the Reporter’s Notes say Rule 41 rejects the federal limits and follows prior Arkansas law). A second voluntary dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits unless all parties stipulate otherwise. After 12 months with no action of record, the court m","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials","Arkansas · civil rules","The court may consolidate actions sharing common questions and may order separate trials. In jury cases seeking punitive damages, on any party’s motion and if the evidence warrants, the trial must be bifurcated before the same jury, and evidence of the defendant’s financial condition is excluded from the first phase unless relevant to another issue.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 43. Taking of testimony","Arkansas · civil rules","Testimony is taken in open court unless the rules (including Rule 88 on virtual and blended proceedings) or law provide otherwise; affirmation may replace an oath; motions may be heard on affidavits; the court may appoint and pay interpreters, with costs taxable.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 44. Proof of official record","Arkansas · civil rules","Domestic records by official publication or attested copy with a custodian certificate; foreign records by attested copy with final certification (or treaty certification); a certificate of no record proves absence; other methods allowed by law remain available.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 44.1. Determination of foreign law","Arkansas · civil rules","A party raising the law of another jurisdiction (including another state) must give notice in a pleading or other reasonable written notice. The court decides it, may consider any relevant source, and the ruling is reviewed as a question of law.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","Arkansas · civil rules","The clerk, or an attorney “admitted to practice in this State” who is counsel of record, may issue subpoenas; the text does not extend attorney-issued subpoenas to lawyers appearing pro hac vice, so have Arkansas co-counsel sign them. A documents-only subpoena must be served on all other parties by e-mail, fax, or hand delivery at least 3 business days before it is served on the recipient, and all produced material m","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 45.1. Subpoena for interstate depositions and discovery","Arkansas · civil rules","A party in an out-of-state case submits the foreign subpoena to the circuit clerk in the county where discovery is sought; the clerk issues an Arkansas subpoena without opening a case or charging more than the statutory fee, and this is not an appearance. Objections are due within 10 days after service; enforcement, quash, and protective-order motions are filed in that county, which then opens a case and charges the ","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 46. Exceptions unnecessary","Arkansas · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary; making the desired ruling or objection and its grounds known at the time preserves the issue.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 47. Jurors","Arkansas · civil rules","The court either lets counsel examine the venire or examines it itself and allows supplemental questioning. Up to 2 alternates may be seated, with 1 extra peremptory challenge per side usable only on alternates.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 48. Number of jurors; verdict","Arkansas · civil rules","A civil verdict needs agreement of 9 of 12 jurors (the Reporter’s Notes say Rule 48 follows prior Arkansas law, not FRCP 48). The parties may stipulate to fewer jurors and a stated majority. All consenting jurors sign a non-unanimous verdict; the foreperson signs a unanimous one.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 49. Verdicts and interrogatories","Arkansas · civil rules","General verdicts, general verdicts with interrogatories, and special verdicts follow the federal pattern. Subdivision (c) adds allocation of fault in personal-injury, medical-injury, wrongful-death, and property-damage cases among all persons, including nonparties, but a nonparty’s fault may be assessed only if the nonparty settled or Rule 9(h) notice was given, and the defendant made a prima facie case.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 50. Directed verdict and JNOV","Arkansas · civil rules","Arkansas keeps the “directed verdict” and “judgment notwithstanding the verdict” labels. A JNOV motion is due within 10 days after entry of judgment and it is deemed denied if not ruled on within 30 days of filing. A party without the burden of proof must move for a directed verdict at the close of all the evidence to preserve a sufficiency challenge in a jury case; a party opposing a JNOV that was granted may move f","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 51. Instructions; objections","Arkansas · civil rules","Requested instructions are submitted at the close of evidence or earlier as directed; the court instructs before closing argument. To preserve error, object before or when the instruction is given, stating the matter and grounds, and to complain of a failure to instruct, a party must have proffered an instruction on that issue.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings by the court","Arkansas · civil rules","In nonjury cases the court makes special findings only if a party requests them before entry of judgment. Findings are set aside only if clearly erroneous (clearly against the preponderance of the evidence). Bench trials also allocate fault among parties and nonparties under (a)(2), on the same conditions as Rule 49(c). A motion to amend findings is due within 10 days after entry of judgment and is deemed denied afte","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters","Arkansas · civil rules","Reference is the exception and only where no jury right exists or it has been waived; outside accounts and damage computations, an exceptional condition must be shown. The court accepts a master’s findings unless clearly erroneous; objections are due within 20 days after notice of filing the report.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgments; costs","Arkansas · civil rules","A partial judgment is final only with an executed Rule 54(b) certificate stating specific factual findings that there is no just reason for delay; without it, the order is not appealable. A motion for a certificate must be filed within 14 days after entry; it is deemed denied if not entered within 30 days of the motion, and no certificate may be entered more than 45 days after the judgment or 30 days after the motion","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 55. Default","Arkansas · civil rules","Default judgment is entered by the court; the rule provides no clerk’s default (the Reporter’s Notes say Rule 55 varies substantially from FRCP 55). A party that has appeared gets written notice at least 3 days before the hearing. A default may be set aside for mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect, voidness, fraud, or any other reason justifying relief, but except for a void judgment the movant must sho","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","Arkansas · civil rules","A claimant may move after 20 days from commencement; any summary-judgment motion must be filed at least 45 days before a scheduled trial absent leave for good cause. The response is due 21 days after service of the motion and the reply 14 days after the response; no supplemental materials after the reply without leave; any hearing must be at least 7 days after the reply deadline. The standard is “no genuine issue as ","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments","Arkansas · civil rules","Actions under Ark. Code Ann. §§ 16-111-101 to -111 follow these rules, with jury rights under Rules 38 and 39; another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the court may advance the case.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 58. Entry of judgment","Arkansas · civil rules","Every judgment or decree must be on a separate document and is effective only when entered as provided in Administrative Order No. 2, which makes entry the clerk’s file-stamp of date, time, and “filed.” The court may have the prevailing party prepare the form and may enter it without opposing counsel’s consent; costs taxation does not delay entry.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 59. New trials","Arkansas · civil rules","Eight listed grounds, including verdict clearly contrary to the preponderance of the evidence and newly discovered evidence. The motion is due within 10 days after entry of judgment, is deemed denied if not ruled on within 30 days of filing, and grounds (a)(2), (3), and (7) need supporting affidavits; opposing affidavits are due 10 days after service, extendable up to 20 more days. The court may order a new trial on ","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from judgment, decree, or order","Arkansas · civil rules","Within 90 days after filing with the clerk, the court may modify or vacate a judgment for any reason to correct errors or prevent a miscarriage of justice. Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time. After 90 days, only listed grounds apply: newly discovered grounds (within 1 year after discovery or judgment, whichever is earlier), constructively summoned defendants (within 2 years after the judgment or 1 year af","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","Arkansas · civil rules","Errors that do not affect substantial rights are disregarded.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","Arkansas · civil rules","No execution for 10 days after entry unless ordered; injunction and receivership judgments are not automatically stayed. The court may stay pending post-trial motions; a supersedeas bond, given with or after the notice of appeal, stays the judgment (bond terms and the $25 million cap are in Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 8). The State and its agencies need no bond.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 63. Disability of a judge","Arkansas · civil rules","If the trial judge cannot perform post-verdict or post-findings duties, another judge sitting in or assigned to that court may perform them or grant a new trial if unable to do so.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 64. Addition and withdrawal of counsel","Arkansas · civil rules","New counsel must promptly enter an appearance with the clerk and notify the court and opposing counsel. Withdrawal requires court permission for good cause on a motion showing steps to avoid prejudice, readiness to return papers and property, and refund of any unearned fee, except limited-scope withdrawal under Rule 87.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions and temporary restraining orders","Arkansas · civil rules","Preliminary injunctions only on notice; a TRO without notice needs specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint and an attorney certification of notice efforts, expires after no more than 14 days unless extended once for good cause or by consent, and can be challenged on 2 days’ notice. Security is required for any TRO or preliminary injunction, but the State, its officers, and agencies are exempt. Every order m","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 65.1. Security; proceedings against sureties","Arkansas · civil rules","Sureties submit to the court’s jurisdiction and appoint the clerk as agent; liability may be enforced by motion.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers","Arkansas · civil rules","Circuit courts may appoint receivers when necessary; the receiver gives bond and takes an oath, reports every 6 months or as directed, may hire professionals with approval, and may be removed for good cause. An action with a receiver cannot be dismissed without a court order.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit in court","Arkansas · civil rules","By leave and on notice, a party may deposit money or deliverable things; withdrawal only on express written order to the person the court finds entitled.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of judgment","Arkansas · civil rules","A defending party may serve an offer more than 10 days before trial; acceptance must be served within 10 days. If the offeree’s final judgment, exclusive of interest from the date of the offer, is not more favorable, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer, and “costs” means reasonable litigation expenses excluding attorney’s fees (broader than taxable costs).","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution discovery","Arkansas · civil rules","A judgment creditor or successor of record may take discovery from any person, including the debtor, under these rules. The rule does not itself set execution procedure.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 70. Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","Arkansas · civil rules","If a party fails to perform a directed act, the court may appoint someone to do it at that party’s cost, issue attachment or sequestration, hold the party in contempt, or enter a judgment divesting and vesting title to property within its jurisdiction.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 71. Process for or against nonparties","Arkansas · civil rules","A nonparty may enforce an order in its favor, and be compelled to obey an enforceable order, by the same process as a party.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 72. Suits in forma pauperis","Arkansas · civil rules","An indigent person petitions with a verified affidavit in the prescribed form and a copy of the complaint; the court finds indigency (it may consult federal poverty guidelines) and, if there is a colorable cause of action, waives clerk’s fees. No action for slander, libel, or malicious prosecution may be brought in forma pauperis.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 73–76. (not in compilation)","Arkansas · civil rules","Rules 73 through 76 do not appear in the October 1, 2026 compilation collected from the judiciary’s website; no rule text exists to summarize.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 77. Courts and clerks","Arkansas · civil rules","Courts are deemed open for filings and process during clerk’s office business hours; trials and hearings are public except as provided by law (for example, Ark. Code Ann. § 16-13-222). The clerk’s office is open business hours except weekends and legal holidays, and the clerk may grant routine process motions subject to the court’s review.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 78. Motion day and hearings on motions","Arkansas · civil rules","Courts set regular motion times where practicable. A hearing may be held only after the reply time expires; unless counsel requests or the court orders a hearing, it is waived and the court may rule on the papers. Election-related mandamus or prohibition petitions must be set for hearing no sooner than 2 and no later than 7 days after filing.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 79. [Abolished]","Arkansas · civil rules","Marked “[Abolished]” in the compilation.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 80. Admissibility of testimony at prior trial","Arkansas · civil rules","When admissible, testimony from a former trial between the same parties or privies on the same issue may be proved by a certified transcript.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 81. Applicability of rules","Arkansas · civil rules","The rules apply to all civil proceedings in circuit court except where a statute creating a right, remedy, or proceeding specifically provides a different procedure; they also apply to civil appeals tried de novo in a court of record. Where no procedure is prescribed, the court proceeds in any lawful manner.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 82. Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","Arkansas · civil rules","The rules do not extend or limit circuit-court jurisdiction or venue.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 83. [Abolished]","Arkansas · civil rules","Marked “[Abolished]” in the compilation. The Reporter’s Notes to Rule 30 state that local rules are not permissible in Arkansas.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 84. Uniform paper size","Arkansas · civil rules","All papers are on 8½ × 11 inch paper.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 85. Title","Arkansas · civil rules","Cited as the Arkansas Rules of Civil Procedure (ARCP).","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 86. Effective date","Arkansas · civil rules","The rules took effect July 1, 1979.","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 87. Limited scope representation","Arkansas · civil rules","Consistent with Ark. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.2(c), a lawyer may limit an appearance by filing and serving a notice of limited scope representation. A lawyer who only drafts papers for a self-represented person need not sign them but must add the notation that the document was prepared with the assistance of a named licensed Arkansas lawyer under Rule 1.2(c), and may rely on the client’s facts unless the lawyer has reason","#st-ar-rules"],["Rule 88. Virtual and blended court proceedings","Arkansas · civil rules","Hearings and nonjury trials may be fully virtual or blended if the court finds rights are protected, a record can be kept, evidence procedures and technology are adequate, and the public has access (a virtual hearing needs a courtroom notice posted at least 1 hour before). Jury trials may be blended only for specific witnesses or the court reporter; parties, counsel, judge, and jury must be in the courtroom. Requests","#st-ar-rules"],["Appeals in Arkansas: 30 days from the clerk’s file-stamp, and a short fuse on post-trial motions","Arkansas · appeals","Civil appeals go to the Arkansas Court of Appeals unless Supreme Court Rule 1-2(a) sends them to the Supreme Court, which can also reassign cases and reviews Court of Appeals decisions only by discretionary petition for review. An appeal is taken by filing a notice of appeal with the circuit clerk within 30 days after the judgment is entered, and a judgment is entered when the clerk file-stamps it.\nPost-trial motions","#st-ar-appeals"],["Default court is the Court of Appeals","Arkansas · appeals · Which court, and what is appealable","Every appeal is filed in the Court of Appeals except cases on the Supreme Court’s list: interpretation of the Arkansas Constitution, death or life sentences, writs against state, county, or municipal officials or circuit courts, elections, attorney and judicial discipline, second or later appeals after a Supreme Court decision, and appeals required by law. The Supreme Court may reassign cases involving first impressi","#st-ar-appeals"],["Final judgments and the listed interlocutory orders","Arkansas · appeals · Which court, and what is appealable","Appeal lies from a final judgment, an order that ends the action, and listed orders: grant or refusal of a new trial; an order striking an answer or pleading; orders on attachment or garnishment; injunction and receivership orders; an order disqualifying an attorney; an order granting or denying class certification; denial of a motion to dismiss or for summary judgment based on sovereign immunity or a government offi","#st-ar-appeals"],["Partial judgments need a Rule 54(b) certificate with findings","Arkansas · appeals · Which court, and what is appealable","An order resolving fewer than all claims or parties is not final unless the circuit court executes the Rule 54(b) certificate, stating specific factual findings that there is no just reason for delay. A party wanting a certificate must move within 14 days after entry; the motion is deemed denied if no certificate is entered within 30 days, and no certificate may be entered more than 45 days after the judgment or 30 d","#st-ar-appeals"],["Notice of appeal must abandon pending unresolved claims","Arkansas · appeals · Which court, and what is appealable","The notice must state that the appellant abandons any pending but unresolved claim, and that statement operates as a dismissal with prejudice as of the date the appealed order was entered. The statement is not required for interlocutory appeals under Rule 2(a)(2)–(13), juvenile appeals, or Rule 54(b) partial judgments. Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 3(e)(vi)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Discretionary interlocutory appeal of privilege discovery orders","Arkansas · appeals · Which court, and what is appealable","The Supreme Court may allow an appeal from an order denying a protective order, compelling production, or refusing to quash a subpoena when the objection is a privilege or opinion work product. The petition is due within 14 calendar days after entry, is limited to 10 pages plus an addendum, and must address six factors; the circuit court’s order must contain findings on the same factors. A response is due within 10 c","#st-ar-appeals"],["Extraordinary writs","Arkansas · appeals · Which court, and what is appealable","Prohibition, mandamus, and certiorari petitions are filed in the Supreme Court with the certified record, and are not available where appeal is an adequate remedy. Name the other parties, not the judge, as respondents, and give the judge a copy. Responses are due in 10 calendar days; petitions and responses are limited to 15 pages excluding the addendum. Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 6-1(a), (e)","#st-ar-appeals"],["30 days from entry, and entry means the clerk’s file-stamp","Arkansas · appeals · Notice of appeal and cross-appeal","File the notice of appeal with the circuit clerk (or the county clerk, where that clerk serves as ex officio circuit clerk) within 30 days after entry of the judgment or the Rule 54(b) certificate. A judgment is entered when the clerk stamps it with the date, time, and “filed,” regardless of when it is recorded. A judge may make an order effective at once by signing it and marking it “filed in open court” or “filed b","#st-ar-appeals"],["Required contents","Arkansas · appeals · Notice of appeal and cross-appeal","The notice must name the appealing parties, designate the order appealed, designate the record contents, state that the transcript has been ordered and paid for under Ark. Code Ann. § 16-13-510(c), state whether the appeal goes to the Court of Appeals or the Supreme Court (citing the Rule 1-2(a) ground), and include the abandonment statement. If less than the full record is designated, serve a statement of the points","#st-ar-appeals"],["Cross-appeal","Arkansas · appeals · Notice of appeal and cross-appeal","File a notice of cross-appeal with the circuit clerk within the later of 10 days after the notice of appeal is filed or 30 days after entry of the order. An appellee who wants more of the record designates it within 10 days after the notice of appeal is filed. Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 3(d), 4(a)(2), 6(b)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Failure to receive notice of the judgment","Arkansas · appeals · Notice of appeal and cross-appeal","If a party shows it did not receive notice of the judgment, counsel was diligent, and no one is prejudiced, the circuit court must extend the appeal time by 14 days from entry of the extension order, but only on a motion filed within 180 days after entry of the judgment. Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(3)","#st-ar-appeals"],["10 days to file; 30 days to rule; then the clock runs","Arkansas · appeals · Post-trial motions and the deemed-denied trap","A timely JNOV motion, motion to amend findings, new-trial motion, or any other motion to vacate, alter, or amend filed within 10 days after entry extends the appeal time for all parties. The notice of appeal is due 30 days after the order disposing of the last motion. But if the court does not rule within 30 days after the motion is filed, it is deemed denied on the 30th day and the 30-day appeal period starts then, ","#st-ar-appeals"],["Notices filed while a motion is pending","Arkansas · appeals · Post-trial motions and the deemed-denied trap","A notice of appeal filed before the motion is decided is treated as filed the day after the disposing order or the deemed-denial date, and it covers the underlying judgment. To also challenge the ruling on the motion, amend the notice within 30 days; no extra fee. Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(2)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Rule 60 relief is separate","Arkansas · appeals · Post-trial motions and the deemed-denied trap","Within 90 days after the judgment is filed, the circuit court may vacate or modify it to correct errors or prevent a miscarriage of justice; after 90 days, only the grounds listed in Rule 60(c) apply. A Rule 60 motion does not affect finality or suspend the judgment. A misprision of the clerk (including a judgment entered before the answer was due) is not a ground for appeal until relief is sought in the circuit cour","#st-ar-appeals"],["New-trial motion not required to preserve error","Arkansas · appeals · Post-trial motions and the deemed-denied trap","A party that has preserved an error need not move for a new trial to raise it on appeal. But in a jury case, a party without the burden of proof must move for a directed verdict at the close of all the evidence to challenge sufficiency on appeal. Ark. R. Civ. P. 59(f), 50(e)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Lodge within 90 days of the first notice of appeal","Arkansas · appeals · Record on appeal","The appellant files the certified record with the Clerk of the Supreme Court within 90 days after the first notice of appeal; for injunction or receivership appeals under Rule 2(a)(6)–(7), within 30 days. The circuit court may extend the time, before it expires, on the appellant’s motion and specific findings (including that the transcript was timely ordered and paid for), but never beyond 7 months from the first not","#st-ar-appeals"],["Electronic record format","Arkansas · appeals · Record on appeal","The record is a searchable, bookmarked PDF whose page numbers match the record pages, with the transcript as a separate file and files under 30 megabytes each. The appellant transmits it electronically after certification. Cite the record as (RP page) for the clerk’s portion and (RT page) for the transcript. Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 7(b)–(c) (electronic-record version); Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 4-2(f)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Supersedeas and the $25 million cap","Arkansas · appeals · Record on appeal","A stay requires a supersedeas, issued by the circuit clerk (or the appellate clerk once the record is lodged) on an approved bond. No bond in a civil action may exceed $25,000,000 regardless of the judgment, unless the court finds by a preponderance that the appellant is dissipating assets to evade payment. Execution is automatically stayed only for 10 days after entry; injunction and receivership judgments are not s","#st-ar-appeals"],["Schedule runs from lodging the record","Arkansas · appeals · Briefing","Appellant’s brief: 40 days after the record is lodged. Appellee’s brief: 30 days after the appellant’s brief. Reply: 15 days after the appellee’s brief; a cross-appellant’s reply 15 days after the cross-appellee’s brief. The Clerk may grant one 7-calendar-day extension on oral or electronic request, which must be confirmed by letter; further extensions need a written motion showing good cause, and stipulations are no","#st-ar-appeals"],["Word limits and required contents","Arkansas · appeals · Briefing","Appellant and appellee briefs: 8,600 words; reply: 2,875; appellee/cross-appellant: 14,325; reply/cross-appellee: 11,475. Only the jurisdictional statement, statement of the case and facts, argument, and request for relief count. Each brief needs a cover, table of contents, points on appeal, table of authorities, jurisdictional statement (finality, timeliness dates, and which court under Rule 1-2), statement of the c","#st-ar-appeals"],["Format","Arkansas · appeals · Briefing","File a single word-searchable PDF converted from the word processor (not scanned), double-spaced, at least 14-point proportionally spaced serif type, one-inch margins (two inches at the top of the cover), paginated with the cover as page one and bookmarked for each section. Noncompliant briefs are rejected; a substantially compliant brief is marked “tendered” with 7 days to fix. Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 4-1, 4-4(g)(1)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Oral argument by letter request","Arkansas · appeals · Briefing","Request oral argument by a separate letter filed with the party’s initial or reply brief. It is allowed on request unless the appeal is frivolous, the issue has been authoritatively decided, or argument would not help. Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 5-1(a)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Citing opinions","Arkansas · appeals · Briefing","Opinions issued after July 1, 2009 are precedent and may be cited; earlier opinions not designated for publication may not be cited except in related litigation. Opinions since February 14, 2009 use the public-domain form, for example 2010 Ark. 171 or 2010 Ark. App. 745, with pinpoint cites and the parallel regional-reporter or database cite. Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 5-2(b)–(d)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Motions","Arkansas · appeals · Motions, rehearing, and Supreme Court review","Motions, petitions, and responses are filed electronically (except case-initiating filings, pro se filers, and those with special needs) and are limited to 10 double-spaced pages. Responses are due within 10 calendar days. A motion to reconsider an order on a motion is due within 18 calendar days. Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-1(a), (d), (g), (h)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing: 18 calendar days, one shot","Arkansas · appeals · Motions, rehearing, and Supreme Court review","File within 18 calendar days after the decision, with a supporting brief, proof of service, and a certificate that it is not for delay; 10 pages maximum. The response is due the following Monday (Supreme Court) or Wednesday (Court of Appeals) or within 7 calendar days, whichever is later. Only one petition per party; no oral argument; new counsel may not be substituted for the purpose. A fact claimed to be overlooked","#st-ar-appeals"],["Petition for review of a Court of Appeals decision","Arkansas · appeals · Motions, rehearing, and Supreme Court review","There is no appeal of right from the Court of Appeals. A petition for review is due within 10 calendar days after the Court of Appeals’ rehearing period ends, is limited to 3 pages, and must allege a tie vote, a conflict with a published opinion, or error tied to a Rule 1-2(b) ground; briefs are not accepted. A response is due within 10 calendar days. If review is granted, a party may request oral argument and supple","#st-ar-appeals"],["Mandate","Arkansas · appeals · Motions, rehearing, and Supreme Court review","The mandate issues when the decision is final, meaning after the rehearing or review period, and not within 18 calendar days absent leave or stipulation. A stay of mandate pending a U.S. Supreme Court certiorari petition lasts up to 90 days unless extended. Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 5-3","#st-ar-appeals"],["Certified questions","Arkansas · appeals · Motions, rehearing, and Supreme Court review","The Arkansas Supreme Court may answer questions of Arkansas law certified by a federal court; it decides whether to accept within 30 days of the certification order, and inaction for 30 days is a declination. Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 6-8(a)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Appellate filings are electronic","Arkansas · appeals · E-filing","Briefs, motions, and petitions are filed through the Administrative Office of the Courts’ e-filing system with no paper copies; pro se filers and persons with disabilities may file on paper. The e-filing fee in the Supreme Court or Court of Appeals is $20.00 for civil actions. An appellate e-filing is considered filed when transmission is complete, subject to the clerk’s review and acceptance, and anything received b","#st-ar-appeals"],["Frivolous appeals","Arkansas · appeals · E-filing","The appellate courts must sanction frivolous appeals, improper filings, and delay, with sanctions including dismissal, fees, penalties, and damages. A sanctions response is due within 21 days. Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 11","#st-ar-appeals"],["Notice of appeal","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry (clerk’s file-stamp) of the judgment or order, or entry of the Rule 54(b) certificate.\nFile with the circuit clerk. Must include the abandonment statement for final orders. Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(a)(1), 4(d); Admin. Order No. 2(b)(2)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Notice of cross-appeal","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the notice of appeal (or 30 days from entry of the order, whichever is later).\nUse the later of the two dates. Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(a)(2)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Post-trial motions (JNOV, amend findings, new trial, alter or amend)","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of judgment.\nDeemed denied if not ruled on within 30 days after filing. Ark. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b); Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(1)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Notice of appeal after a post-trial motion","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order deciding the last motion, or the deemed-denial date (30th day after filing).\nThe deemed-denial date runs automatically. Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(1)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Amend notice to appeal the post-trial ruling","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from disposition or deemed denial of the motion.\nNo additional fee. Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(2)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Motion to extend appeal time for non-receipt of notice of judgment","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","180 days from entry of the judgment.\nRelief is 14 days from entry of the extension order. Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(3)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Rule 54(b) certificate motion","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of the partial judgment.\nDeemed denied at 30 days; no certificate later than 45 days after judgment or 30 days after the motion, whichever is later. Ark. R. Civ. P. 54(b)(2)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Petition for permission to appeal privilege discovery order","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of the order.\nCalendar days; 10-page limit; response in 10 calendar days. Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 2(f)(1)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Appellee’s designation of additional record","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the notice of appeal. Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 6(b)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Lodge the record","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","90 days from filing of the first notice of appeal.\n30 days for injunction and receivership appeals; circuit court extensions capped at 7 months from the first notice. Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 5(a)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from lodging of the record.\nOne 7-day clerk extension available. Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 4-4(b)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the appellant’s brief. Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 4-4(c)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Reply brief","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the appellee’s brief. Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 4-4(d)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Response to a motion or petition","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the motion or petition.\nCalendar days. Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-1(d)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Motion to reconsider an order on a motion","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","18 days from date of the order.\nCalendar days. Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-1(g)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","18 days from date of the decision.\nCalendar days; 10-page limit; one petition only. Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-3(a)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Petition for review in the Supreme Court","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from end of the Court of Appeals rehearing period.\nCalendar days; 3-page limit. An early petition is held as tendered. Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-4(a)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Response to petition for review","Arkansas · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing (or deemed filing) of the petition.\nCalendar days. Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-4(e)","#st-ar-appeals"],["Colorado Supreme Court","Colorado · courts","Court of last resort. Reviews the Court of Appeals, and district-court rulings on appeals from county court, by discretionary certiorari (C.A.R. 51–55; C.R.S. § 13-6-310(4)), and hears original proceedings under C.A.R. 21. It also writes the court rules by numbered “Rule Change” orders and regulates lawyer admission (C.R.C.P. 201–227) and discipline. The court sits in three terms a year beginning in September, Januar","#st-co"],["Colorado Court of Appeals","Colorado · courts","Twenty-two judges (C.R.S. § 13-4-103). Hears appeals as of right from final district-court judgments (C.A.R. 1, 3, 4), certified interlocutory appeals of controlling and unresolved questions of law (C.R.S. § 13-4-102.1; C.A.R. 4.2), and appeals from anti-SLAPP rulings (C.R.S. §§ 13-4-102.2, 13-20-1101(7)).","#st-co"],["District courts (23 judicial districts), plus the Denver Probate and Denver Juvenile Courts and the water courts","Colorado · courts","General-jurisdiction trial courts; the state is divided into twenty-three judicial districts (C.R.S. § 13-5-101). The Colorado Rules of Civil Procedure govern district courts and the juvenile and probate courts of the City and County of Denver (C.R.C.P. 1(a)). Water-court decrees are appealed under C.A.R. 1(a)(2) and 1(e).","#st-co"],["County courts (including small claims)","Colorado · courts","County courts share jurisdiction with district courts over civil claims up to $25,000 and over eviction cases, with judgments capped at $25,000 (C.R.S. § 13-6-104). Small claims courts hear claims up to $7,500 (C.R.S. § 13-6-403). County-court civil procedure is in C.R.C.P. Chapter 25 (Rules 301–411). Appeals from county court go to the district court on the record, then to the Supreme Court only by certiorari (C.R.S","#st-co"],["Statute: C.R.S. § 13-20-1101 (special motion to dismiss), effective July 1, 2019","Colorado · anti-SLAPP","Colorado’s anti-SLAPP law is C.R.S. § 13-20-1101, added by H.B. 19-1324 effective July 1, 2019. It protects any “act in furtherance of a person’s right of petition or free speech … in connection with a public issue.” That includes statements made before, or about an issue under review by, a legislative, executive, judicial, or other official proceeding; statements in a public place or public forum on an issue of publ","#st-co-slapp"],["Standard: the plaintiff must show a reasonable likelihood of prevailing","Colorado · anti-SLAPP","A covered claim is dismissed on a special motion unless the plaintiff establishes a reasonable likelihood of prevailing on the claim. The court considers the pleadings and the supporting and opposing affidavits. If the plaintiff meets the standard, neither the ruling nor the fact that it was made is admissible later in the case or in any later proceeding, and it does not change any burden of proof. C.R.S. § 13-20-110","#st-co-slapp"],["Deadline 63 days after service; hearing within 28 days; discovery stayed","Colorado · anti-SLAPP","File the special motion within 63 days after service of the complaint, or later if the court allows it on terms. The hearing must be set no more than 28 days after service of the motion unless the court’s docket requires a later date. All discovery is stayed when notice of the motion is filed and remains stayed until notice of entry of the order ruling on it. On noticed motion and good cause, the court may allow spec","#st-co-slapp"],["Fees: mandatory for a winning defendant; for a plaintiff only if the motion was frivolous","Colorado · anti-SLAPP","A defendant who wins the special motion is entitled to attorney fees and costs. A plaintiff who defeats the motion gets costs and reasonable fees only if the court finds the motion frivolous or solely intended to cause unnecessary delay, under C.R.S. title 13, article 17, part 1. A winning defendant does not get fees under this section if the claim was brought under the Open Meetings Law (part 4 of article 6 of title","#st-co-slapp"],["Exemptions: government enforcement, public-interest suits, and commercial speech","Colorado · anti-SLAPP","The statute does not apply to (1) enforcement actions by the state or a subdivision, or actions to protect against an imminent threat to health or safety; (2) actions brought solely in the public interest that meet three listed conditions; or (3) claims against a seller or lessor of goods or services based on factual statements about its own or a competitor’s business made to sell, or made to actual or potential buye","#st-co-slapp"],["Appeal goes to the Court of Appeals, but read Hinds v. Foreman (2026)","Colorado · anti-SLAPP","An order granting or denying the special motion is appealable to the Court of Appeals under C.R.S. § 13-4-102.2, except when the trial court denies the motion because the case is exempt under subsection (8). The 2026 C.R.S. editor’s note reports that Hinds v. Foreman, 2026 CO 9, held § 13-20-1101(7) and § 13-4-102.2 unconstitutional to the extent they let the Court of Appeals review final judgments, because that conf","#st-co-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: verified motion in the trial court, plus a copy and fee to the Office of Attorney Registration","Colorado · out of state","An out-of-state attorney may appear in a particular matter in any state court of record. Before appearing, the attorney must (i) file a verified motion with the trial court, (ii) designate an associate attorney admitted and licensed in Colorado, (iii) file a copy of the motion with the Clerk of the Supreme Court’s Office of Attorney Registration at the same time, (iv) pay the required fee, and (v) get the trial court","#st-co-oos"],["Colorado associate counsel must attend every appearance","Colorado · out of state","The Colorado associate attorney’s name and address must appear on every paper the out-of-state attorney serves and files. The associate must appear in person and, unless excused, stay in attendance with the out-of-state attorney at every pro hac vice appearance. A tribal attorney in an Indian Child Welfare Act child-custody proceeding is exempt from both the associate and fee requirements if the tribe has asked to pa","#st-co-oos"],["Fee amount and caps on the number of admissions: not verified","Colorado · out of state","Rule 205.3 refers to “the required fee” without stating it in the collected text. Subsections (4) and (5) were not in any collected amendment, so whether Colorado caps repeat pro hac vice appearances is unverified. The Office of Attorney Admissions website, which posts the current admission rules and the pro hac vice application, refused automated access. Check the fee and any limits there before filing. C.R.C.P. 205","#st-co-oos"],["Appeals: a short update if you were admitted below; a new motion if not","Colorado · out of state","If the attorney already has permission in the same action in a Colorado trial court, appearing in the appellate court only requires an updated affidavit filed with the Office of Attorney Registration, with a copy filed in the appeal. There is no new fee. Otherwise, the attorney files a motion and affidavit with the appellate court clerk, sends a copy to Attorney Registration, and pays the fee as in a trial court. C.R","#st-co-oos"],["Pro hac vice lawyers are bound by Colorado’s rules, but some regulatory duties are waived","Colorado · out of state","An attorney admitted under Rule 205.3 is subject to the Colorado Rules of Professional Conduct, except the Colo. RPC 1.15A–1.15E duties to keep business and trust accounts at a financial institution doing business in Colorado. The attorney is subject to the C.R.C.P., except C.R.C.P. 227 (registration fees) and C.R.C.P. 250 (mandatory CLE). A lawyer domiciled in Colorado and authorized to practice pending admission un","#st-co-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances and ghostwriting are authorized, with disclosure","Colorado · out of state","Under C.R.C.P. 11(b), a lawyer may (1) enter a limited appearance for a self-represented party by filing a notice before or with the covered part of the case, and withdraw without leave by filing a notice of completion; (2) ghostwrite, but every document drafted must show the lawyer’s name, address, phone number, email address, and Colorado registration number, and the party must attach the lawyer’s signed disclosure","#st-co-oos"],["E-filing: mandatory for Colorado-licensed lawyers in the appellate courts","Colorado · out of state","All attorneys licensed in Colorado must use the e-filing system in the Supreme Court and Court of Appeals. A document transmitted by 11:59 p.m. Colorado time is filed (and, if e-served, served) that day. If an attorney files on paper after being told to e-file, the court may charge $50 per document to scan it. Self-represented parties are excluded from mandatory e-filing.\nIn the trial courts, C.R.C.P. 121 § 1-26 runs","#st-co-oos"],["Withdrawal needs a motion and a 14-day objection window","Colorado · out of state","Leave to withdraw is granted only after a motion is filed and served on the client and other parties, and either everyone consents in writing or 14 days pass without objection. The motion must advise the client of the right to object within 14 days. In the appellate courts, the client and opposing parties likewise have 14 days to object. C.R.C.P. 121 § 1-1 (as amended by Rule Change 2011-18); C.A.R. 5 (Rule Change 20","#st-co-oos"],["Governing code: Colorado Rules of Professional Conduct (2008 restatement, amended through 2026)","Colorado · ethics","Colorado repealed and readopted its Rules of Professional Conduct effective January 1, 2008, in ABA Model Rule numbering, and amends them by Supreme Court Rule Change orders. The latest collected orders include 2025(29) (Rule 1.5 flat fees, effective January 1, 2026), 2026(02) (Scope [20A] and Rule 1.1 comments on technology and AI), and 2026(14) (Rule 6.5, September 2026). Colorado-only rules include Rules 1.15A–1.1","#st-co-ethics"],["Fees: written fee and scope terms, no referral fees, no nonrefundable fees","Colorado · ethics","Rule 1.5 departs from the ABA text in several ways. (b) The basis or rate of the fee and expenses, and the scope of the representation, must be communicated in writing before or within a reasonable time after starting, with limited exceptions for regularly represented clients; changes must be communicated promptly in writing. (c) Contingent fees must meet C.R.C.P. Chapter 23.3. (e) Referral fees are prohibited. (f) F","#st-co-ethics"],["No threatening criminal, administrative, or disciplinary charges for civil advantage","Colorado · ethics","Colorado keeps a rule the ABA Model Rules do not have. A lawyer may not threaten criminal, administrative, or disciplinary charges to gain an advantage in a civil matter, or present such charges solely for that purpose. Telling someone in a civil matter that the lawyer reasonably believes their conduct may violate criminal, administrative, or disciplinary law is not a violation, and comment [7] gives factors for tell","#st-co-ethics"],["Misconduct rule is broader: bias, harm to others, and an investigation exception","Colorado · ethics","Rule 8.4(g) makes it misconduct, in representing a client, to engage in conduct that shows or appeals to bias based on race, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, religion, national origin, ethnicity, disability, age, or socioeconomic status toward anyone in the legal process. Rule 8.4(h) bars conduct that directly, intentionally, and wrongfully harms others and reflects adversely on fitness. Rule 8","#st-co-ethics"],["Lateral screening is unavailable if the lawyer substantially participated","Colorado · ethics","A firm hiring a lawyer disqualified under Rule 1.9 avoids imputation only if (1) the matter is not one in which that lawyer substantially participated, (2) the lawyer is timely screened and gets no part of the fee, and (3) prompt written notice describing the prior work and the screen goes to the affected former clients and their lawyers. Colo. RPC 1.10(e) (Rule Change 2007-07; rule text unchanged by Rule Change 2025","#st-co-ethics"],["Marijuana, natural medicine, and AI comments","Colorado · ethics","Comment [14] to Rule 1.2 lets a lawyer counsel and assist a client with conduct the lawyer reasonably believes is permitted by Colo. Const. art. XVIII, §§ 14 and 16 and the Natural Medicine Act of 2022, with advice on federal law. Scope [20A] and Rule 1.1 comments [8]–[9] (2026) say technology, including artificial intelligence, does not reduce a lawyer’s duties, and list the Rules that AI use can implicate. Colo. RP","#st-co-ethics"],["Notice of appeal is filed in the appellate court, 49 days after entry","Colorado · land mine","Unlike federal practice, the notice goes to the clerk of the appellate court, with an advisory copy served on the trial court within the same time. Civil deadline: 49 days after entry of the judgment or order. If notice of entry was mailed or e-served, the time runs from that date. C.A.R. 3(a), 4(a)(1), (5)","#st-co-unique"],["Post-trial motions: 14 days, and silence denies them at 63 days","Colorado · land mine","A C.R.C.P. 59 motion is due 14 days after entry of judgment. Any extension must be requested within those 14 days. If the court does not rule within 63 days of the last motion, the motion is deemed denied and the 49-day appeal clock starts that day without any order. C.R.C.P. 59(a), (j); C.A.R. 4(a)(3)","#st-co-unique"],["A suit can start by service, but the complaint must follow within 14 days","Colorado · land mine","An action may be commenced by serving a summons and complaint before filing. The complaint must then be filed within 14 days, or the service is void without notice and fees may be taxed against the plaintiff or counsel. The defendant waives the requirement by responding without reserving it. C.R.C.P. 3(a)","#st-co-unique"],["No punitive-damages claim in the complaint","Colorado · land mine","An exemplary-damages claim may not be included in any initial claim for relief. It can be added by amendment only after Rule 26 initial disclosures are exchanged and the plaintiff shows prima facie proof of a triable issue. Exemplary damages are capped at the amount of actual damages awarded, subject to the statute’s exceptions. C.R.S. § 13-21-102(1)(a), (1.5)(a)","#st-co-unique"],["Losing a tort case on a Rule 12(b) motion shifts the defense fees","Colorado · land mine","When a tort action for death or injury to person or property is dismissed before trial on a defendant’s C.R.C.P. 12(b) motion, the defendant gets a judgment for reasonable attorney fees. This does not apply if the motion is converted to summary judgment, or to a good-faith claim pleaded as seeking to change or establish the law and identifying the precedent at issue. C.R.S. § 13-17-201(1)–(2)","#st-co-unique"],["Professional-negligence claims need a certificate of review in 60 days","Colorado · land mine","In a damages action based on a licensed professional’s alleged negligence, the plaintiff’s lawyer must file a certificate of review for each professional named, and for any firm that employed one, within 60 days after service. The certificate declares that an expert was consulted and concluded the claim does not lack substantial justification. C.R.S. § 13-20-602(1), (3)","#st-co-unique"],["Rule 16 runs on the “at issue” date, not a scheduling conference","Colorado · land mine","No later than 14 days after the case is at issue, lead counsel must confer. Rule 26(a)(1) disclosures are due 28 days after the at-issue date. A proposed Case Management Order, in editable format, is due 42 days after the at-issue date and at least 7 days before the case management conference. Formal discovery generally cannot begin until the Case Management Order is served. Plaintiff’s counsel is the “responsible at","#st-co-unique"],["Tight presumptive discovery limits","Colorado · land mine","Without a court order for good cause and proportionality, each party gets one deposition of each adverse party plus two other persons (experts excluded), 30 single-question interrogatories, 20 document requests, and 20 requests for admission (plus up to 50 genuineness-of-document requests) per adverse party. Non-expert depositions are limited to 6 hours, and retained-expert depositions also to 6 hours. C.R.C.P. 26(b)","#st-co-unique"],["Expert disclosures count back from trial, and testimony is limited to the report","Colorado · land mine","Unless the Case Management Order says otherwise, the claiming party’s expert disclosures are due 126 days before trial. The defending party’s are due 28 days later, but no earlier than 98 days before trial if the claimant disclosed early. Rebuttal is due 77 days before trial. A retained expert’s signed report must include all opinions, sources, the fee agreement, and an itemization of fees (supplemented 14 days befor","#st-co-unique"],["Pretrial-motion cutoffs: 91 days for summary judgment, 70 for C.R.E. 702","Colorado · land mine","Summary-judgment motions and motions to determine a question of law are due no later than 91 days before trial. C.R.E. 702 challenges to experts are due 70 days before trial, and other pretrial motions, including motions in limine, 35 days before trial, unless the court orders otherwise. C.R.C.P. 16(c); 56(h) (Rule Change 2025(12), eff. Jan. 1, 2026)","#st-co-unique"],["Every motion must certify conferral, and briefs are page-limited","Colorado · land mine","Before filing almost any motion, counsel must confer with opposing counsel and self-represented parties, and the motion must open with a conferral certification. Legal authority goes in the motion itself, not a separate brief. Limits: 15 pages for motions and responses and 10 for replies; 25 and 15 pages for Rule 12(b)(1)–(2) and Rule 56 motions. Responses are due in 21 days (14 if filed within 42 days of trial). Rep","#st-co-unique"],["Untimely or missing disclosure means exclusion","Colorado · land mine","A party that fails without substantial justification to make a Rule 26(a) or 26(e) disclosure cannot use the undisclosed evidence at trial or on a Rule 56 motion. The 2015 amendment frames the exception as a failure that has not caused and will not cause significant harm, or preclusion that would be disproportionate to that harm, in place of the older “harmless” wording. The collected text is a redline only, so confi","#st-co-unique"],["No peremptory challenge found; C.R.C.P. 97 (change of judge) not collected","Colorado · judges","None of the collected texts gives a party a right to remove a trial judge without cause. C.R.C.P. 97 governs motions to change or disqualify a judge, but its text was not in any collected rule-change order, so its timing and affidavit requirements are not stated here. Read the current rule before filing. C.R.C.P. 97 (not collected)","#st-co-unique"],["Statutory disqualification unless all parties consent","Colorado · judges","A judge may not act in a case in which the judge is a party or has an interest, is related to either party within the third degree by blood or marriage, or has been attorney or counsel for either party, unless all parties consent. C.R.S. § 13-1-122","#st-co-unique"],["Successor judge after trial may order a new trial","Colorado · judges","If the judge who tried a case cannot finish post-verdict or post-findings duties, a successor judge is appointed. The successor may decide some duties cannot be performed without having presided, and may order a new trial on some or all issues after notice and an opportunity to be heard, stating the grounds on the record. C.R.C.P. 63 (Rule Change 2026(01), effective February 1, 2026)","#st-co-unique"],["Parties can jointly hire an appointed (private) judge","Colorado · judges","By joint motion of all parties, filed with the Supreme Court and copied to the originating court, the parties may ask for a case-specific appointed judge under C.R.S. § 13-3-111. The motion must state the judge’s name and registration number and the agreed rate of pay, and include the judge’s agreement to be bound by the Code of Judicial Conduct as it applies to appointed judges. C.R.C.P. 122(c) (Corrective Order, Ru","#st-co-unique"],["Recusal standard under the Code of Judicial Conduct: not collected","Colorado · judges","Colorado’s Code of Judicial Conduct (Rule 2.11 in the ABA numbering) was not collected; only a 2026 amendment to its Application section appeared in the orders. Do not rely on this page for the recusal standard. Colo. Code of Judicial Conduct (not collected)","#st-co-unique"],["Colorado constitutional speech protection: not established here","Colorado · speech","The Colorado Constitution has its own free-speech guarantee, but neither the Constitution nor case law construing it was collected. Whether Colorado courts read it more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked. The verified speech-specific protection is the anti-SLAPP statute, C.R.S. § 13-20-1101. Colo. Const. art. II, § 10 (not in collected texts)","#st-co-unique"],["Simplified Procedure is the default for cases of $100,000 or less","Colorado · unique rules","Every district-court civil case falls under C.R.C.P. 16.1 Simplified Procedure unless it is excluded by type or the Civil Cover Sheet certifies under C.R.C.P. 11 that a party’s claims against another party are reasonably believed to exceed $100,000. A party can also move for exclusion for good cause within 42 days after the case is at issue. Under 16.1, recovery against any one party is capped at $100,000 including f","#st-co-unique"],["County-court appeals stop at the district court","Colorado · unique rules","A final county-court judgment is appealed to the district court for that judicial district, which reviews the record. The district court may affirm, reverse, remand, modify, or order a trial de novo. Further review is only by Supreme Court certiorari, not by the Court of Appeals. C.R.S. § 13-6-310(1), (2), (4)","#st-co-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope of rules","Colorado · civil rules","The rules govern civil cases in the Supreme Court, Court of Appeals, district courts, and the Denver juvenile and probate courts. County courts use Chapter 25 (Rules 301–411), and the probate and juvenile chapters control over the general rules when they conflict. Since 2015 the rules must be construed, administered, and employed by the court and the parties to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination o","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencement of action","Colorado · civil rules","An action starts by filing a complaint or by serving a summons and complaint. If the case starts by service, the complaint must be filed within 14 days or the service is void, and the court may tax expenses and fees against the plaintiff. The defendant waives the 14-day requirement by responding without reserving it. Unlike the federal rule, a case can begin before anything is filed. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 4. Process","Colorado · civil rules","The summons may be signed and issued by the clerk or by the plaintiff’s attorney, and must name the court and county, state the response time, warn of default, and give the plaintiff’s attorney’s name, address, and registration number. In eviction cases a represented plaintiff’s attorney must issue the summons, it must carry the case number, and it must be served with the eviction complaint form and blank answer and ","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 6. Time","Colorado · civil rules","Since 2012 most periods are multiples of 7 days (14, 21, 28, 35, 42, 49, 63, 91, 182). Legal holidays include Juneteenth and Frances Cabrini Day (first Monday in October). The court may extend a period on request made before it expires, or afterward for excusable neglect. It may not extend Rule 60(b) times, and may extend Rule 59 times only as Rule 59 allows. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2019-01, 2022-11]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","Colorado · civil rules","Rule 8(c) lists the affirmative defenses that must be pleaded, as in the federal rule, and also requires mitigating circumstances that reduce damages to be pleaded affirmatively. A 2015 committee comment says pleading every listed defense without a factual basis violates C.R.C.P. 11. The comment to Rule 54 notes that Rule 8(a) bars stating a dollar amount in the prayer for relief. [Rule Changes 2015-05, 2020-01]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings and documents","Colorado · civil rules","Documents, including e-filed ones, must meet the paper, format, and spacing standards of Rule 10(d), and motions and briefs must also comply with Practice Standard § 1-15. [Rule Change 2024(03)]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing of pleadings; limited legal services","Colorado · civil rules","Rule 11(a) is the signature certification, quoted in the 2015 comment to Rule 12: the pleading is well grounded in fact, warranted by law, and not filed for an improper purpose. Unlike the federal rule, Rule 11(b) authorizes limited-scope work: (1) a notice of limited appearance, ending without leave by notice of completion; (2) ghostwriting, if the document lists the drafting lawyer’s name, contact details, and regi","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections; motions","Colorado · civil rules","The answer is due 21 days after service of the summons and complaint unless a rule or statute says otherwise, or 35 days if served outside Colorado or by publication. If a Rule 12 motion is denied or postponed, the answer is due 14 days after notice of the ruling. A motion for a more definite statement must be made within the answer period and obeyed within 14 days. A Rule 12(b) dismissal of a tort action for injury ","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","Colorado · civil rules","A defending party may file a third-party complaint without leave within 14 days after serving its original answer; after that, leave is needed on motion and notice. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Colorado · civil rules","A party may amend once as of course before a responsive pleading is filed. If no responsive pleading is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar, the party may amend within 21 days after filing. Otherwise amendment needs leave, freely given, or written consent. The Case Management Order sets the deadline for amendments and joinder (presumptively no later than 105 days after the case is at issue). Exemplary","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 16. Case management and trial management","Colorado · civil rules","Very different from the federal rule. The parties, not the judge, drive a presumptive schedule keyed to the “at issue” date: all parties served and all pleadings filed or defaults entered. Lead counsel confer within 14 days. A proposed Case Management Order (form JDF 622) is filed in editable form 42 days after the at-issue date and at least 7 days before the case management conference, which is held no later than 49","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 16.1. Simplified procedure for civil actions","Colorado · civil rules","The default track for district-court civil cases unless excluded by case type or by a Civil Cover Sheet certification that a party’s claims against another party exceed $100,000; a party may also move to exclude for good cause within 42 days after the case is at issue. Parties may stipulate into it within 42 days. Recovery against any one party is capped at $100,000 including fees, penalties, and punitive damages, bu","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 16.2. Domestic relations case management","Colorado · civil rules","Court-facilitated management of domestic relations cases, with mandatory financial disclosures (sworn financial statement), expert reports 56 days and rebuttal reports 21 days before hearing, and discovery completed 28 days before hearing. Amended in 2024 and April 2026; details beyond the 2012 time changes were not reviewed. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2024(03), 2026(11)]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 16.5. Pretrial procedure in eviction cases","Colorado · civil rules","Special pretrial procedure for forcible entry and detainer (eviction) proceedings, amended in 2024; details not reviewed. [Rule Change 2024(08)]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 17. Parties; capacity","Colorado · civil rules","A partnership or unincorporated association may sue or be sued in its common name. Both parents, or the surviving parent, may sue for the injury or death of a child and share equally, and a guardian may sue for injury or death of a ward. [Rule Change 2017-01]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","Colorado · civil rules","Appeals of written, signed, dated orders granting or denying class certification may be allowed under C.R.S. § 13-20-901 (see C.A.R. 3.3). Unlike the federal rule, any order, judgment, or settlement that leaves residual funds must send at least 50% of them to the Colorado Lawyer Trust Account Foundation (COLTAF). [Rule Changes 2016-01, 2022-11]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","Colorado · civil rules","After a suggestion of death is served, a motion to substitute must be made within 91 days, or the action is dismissed as to the deceased party. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 26. Disclosure and discovery","Colorado · civil rules","Mandatory Rule 26(a)(1) disclosures are due 28 days after the case is at issue. They cover witnesses and documents relevant to the claims and defenses of any party, whether or not supportive, damages computations, and insurance. Disclosures cannot be objected to until the case management conference. Scope is relevance plus proportionality (2015). Presumptive limits per adverse party: one deposition of each adverse pa","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions before action or pending appeal","Colorado · civil rules","Personal service of the petition must be made at least 14 days before testimony is taken. A court may allow depositions to perpetuate testimony while an appeal is pending or within 35 days after judgment. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions upon oral examination","Colorado · civil rules","A deposition of anyone other than a disclosed retained expert is limited to one day of 6 hours (the federal limit is 7), unless the court allows otherwise or the parties stipulate. Objections must be concise and non-suggestive, and instructions not to answer are allowed only to preserve privilege, enforce a court limit, or present a motion. The deponent has 35 days to review and sign. A Rule 121 practice standard req","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions upon written questions","Colorado · civil rules","Cross questions are due 21 days after notice and direct questions, redirect 14 days after cross, and re-cross 7 days after redirect. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 32. Use of depositions","Colorado · civil rules","Objections to the form of written questions are waived unless served within the time for the next round of questions, and within 7 days after the last questions. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories","Colorado · civil rules","Answers and objections are due 35 days after service (the federal period is 30). Pattern interrogatories appear in Form 20. Interrogatories are limited to 30 per adverse party under Rule 26(b)(2). [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2017-01]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 34. Production of documents and things","Colorado · civil rules","A written response is due 35 days after service. Requests are limited to 20 per adverse party under Rule 26(b)(2)(D). [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2015-05]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","Colorado · civil rules","A matter is admitted unless answered or objected to within 35 days after service, or another time set by the court or agreed in writing under Rule 29. Requests are limited to 20 per adverse party plus 50 document-genuineness requests. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to disclose or cooperate; sanctions","Colorado · civil rules","Motions to compel need a good-faith conferral certificate, and fees are awarded unless the conduct was substantially justified or an award would be unjust. A party that fails without substantial justification to make Rule 26(a) or (e) disclosures cannot use the undisclosed evidence at trial or on summary judgment. The 2015 redline frames the exception as no significant harm or disproportionate preclusion (confirm cur","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury trial demand","Colorado · civil rules","A jury demand must be filed and served no later than 14 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue, or 14 days after a demand for trial de novo in mandatory-arbitration cases, and the jury fee must be paid when the demand is filed. A 2012 text; check for later amendments. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","Colorado · civil rules","A defendant may move to dismiss for failure to prosecute or to comply with the rules or orders. In a bench trial, a defendant may move for dismissal after the plaintiff rests, and the court may weigh the facts and enter judgment with findings under Rule 52. [Rule Change 2017-01]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 42.1. Consolidated multidistrict litigation","Colorado · civil rules","A state panel may transfer related cases to one district. A transfer motion is not entertained within 91 days before a trial date absent good cause. Responses to a show-cause order are due within 14 days, and replies within 7 days. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2022-11]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 43. Evidence; absentee testimony","Colorado · civil rules","A party may request testimony by phone or other remote means by written motion or stipulation filed as soon as the need is known, with copies of all documents to be used. The court decides whether it serves the interest of justice under listed factors. Remote appearances in residential evictions follow C.R.S. § 13-40-113.5. [Rule Change 2025(21), effective December 1, 2025]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","Colorado · civil rules","Service requires tendering one day’s attendance fee and mileage. A Colorado resident can be compelled to attend a deposition only in the county where the resident lives, works, or does business in person, or where the court orders. A nonresident can be compelled to attend only within 40 miles of where the subpoena was served in Colorado, or in the county where the nonresident lives, works, or does business. Written o","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 47. Jurors","Colorado · civil rules","Jurors must be allowed to submit written questions for witnesses. The court reviews each question with counsel outside the jury’s hearing and may refuse or modify it for good cause. [Rule Change 2014-14]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings by the court","Colorado · civil rules","In bench trials and in rulings on interlocutory injunctions, the court must find the facts specially and state its conclusions of law separately. Findings are not set aside unless clearly erroneous, and no request or objection is needed to preserve review. [Rule Change 2017-06]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters","Colorado · civil rules","The master sets a first meeting within 21 days after the order of reference, and parties have 14 days after notice of the report to object. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgments; costs","Colorado · civil rules","Since 2015, reasonable costs go to the prevailing party, considering the needs and complexity of the case and the amount in controversy. Costs against the state are limited by law. Under Rule 54(c), relief is not limited by an amount stated in the prayer, because Rule 8(a) bars stating one. A judgment-revival notice requires a response within 14 days. Fee and cost hearing procedure is in Practice Standard § 1-22. [Ru","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 55. Default","Colorado · civil rules","A party in default that has appeared must be served with written notice of the default-judgment application at least 7 days before the hearing. Practice Standard § 1-14 lists the documents a default motion needs, including proof of valid service and a Servicemembers Civil Relief Act showing. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2017-01, 2022-11, 2024(13)]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment; questions of law","Colorado · civil rules","A claimant may move after 21 days from commencement. Unless the court orders otherwise, summary-judgment motions are due no later than 91 days before trial. Since January 1, 2026, a motion to determine a question of law under Rule 56(h) is also due no later than 91 days before trial, instead of at any time after the last pleading. Briefs are limited to 25 pages (reply 15), with 14 days for the reply (Practice Standar","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments","Colorado · civil rules","When a pleading or motion challenges a statute or ordinance as invalid or unconstitutional, notice to the state or municipality must be served under Rule 5(b) within 21 days after the challenge is filed. [Rule Change 2019-01]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 58. Entry of judgment","Colorado · civil rules","The court signs a written judgment, and the date of signing is the effective date of entry. If a party is absent at signing, the court must immediately mail or e-serve a copy, and under C.A.R. 4(a)(5) the appeal time runs from that mailing or e-service. Entry is not delayed for costs. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 59. Motions for post-trial relief","Colorado · civil rules","A combined post-trial motion (new trial, JNOV, amended findings, amended judgment) is due 14 days after entry of judgment. Extensions may be granted only if requested within the 14 days. Opposing affidavits are due 21 days after service. The court must rule within 63 days after the last motion, or the motion is deemed denied and appeal time runs from then. This differs from federal Rules 50, 52, and 59 (28 days, no a","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from judgment","Colorado · civil rules","Relief for mistake, excusable neglect, or newly discovered evidence (reasons (1)–(2)) must be sought within 182 days after the judgment or order, compared with one year under the federal rule. Other grounds require a reasonable time. Independent actions and fraud-on-the-court relief are preserved. [Rule Change 2017-01]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","Colorado · civil rules","Execution is automatically stayed for 14 days after entry of judgment, except judgments in injunction or receivership actions. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 63. Inability of a judge to serve","Colorado · civil rules","If the trial judge cannot complete duties after verdict or findings, a successor judge is appointed. The successor may order a new trial on some or all issues after notice and a chance to be heard, stating the grounds on the record. [Rule Change 2026(01), effective February 1, 2026]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions","Colorado · civil rules","A temporary restraining order without notice expires on the date the court sets, no later than 14 days after entry. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution and post-judgment proceedings","Colorado · civil rules","A judgment debtor served with written interrogatories must appear before the clerk to sign and file sworn answers within 21 days. If the debtor fails, the creditor may move for an order compelling answers. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 84. Forms","Colorado · civil rules","The official forms in the appendix to Chapters 1–17A, such as the JDF civil cover sheet, case management order, and garnishment forms. Amended June 28, 2024. [Rule Change 2024(12)]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 98. Place of trial (venue)","Colorado · civil rules","A motion to change venue may be filed within the time for a Rule 12(b) motion. If it is denied, the responsive pleading is due 14 days later. If it is granted, the responsive pleading is due 14 days after the case is docketed in the new court. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 100. Contested elections","Colorado · civil rules","Contests of elections for presidential elector or judicial office start with a statement of contest within 35 days after the canvass and a complaint within 35 days after that. Amended in 2024. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2024(17)]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 102. Attachment","Colorado · civil rules","Prejudgment attachment. The sheriff returns the writ within 21 days, no final judgment enters until 35 days after levy, and a traverse is heard within 7 days. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 103. Garnishment","Colorado · civil rules","Writs of garnishment, including continuing garnishment of earnings, with mandatory forms and exemption notices. Garnishees answer and pay within set 7–14 day windows, and judgment debtors object or claim exemptions on the forms. Amended many times, most recently in 2020 and 2024 (forms). [Rule Changes 2017-01, 2020-28, 2024(03), 2024(13)]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 104. Replevin","Colorado · civil rules","A hearing on an order to show cause for possession is set no more than 14 days after issuance, with service at least 7 days before, and the sheriff returns the order within 21 days. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 105. Actions concerning real estate","Colorado · civil rules","Quiet-title procedure. A plaintiff who, 21 days or more before suit, requests a quitclaim deed and tenders $20 can recover costs if it is refused. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 106. Remedial writs; review of governmental action","Colorado · civil rules","Rule 106(a)(4) review of a quasi-judicial decision must be filed within 28 days after the final decision unless a statute provides otherwise. The opening brief is due 42 days after the record is filed, the answer brief 35 days later, and the reply 14 days later. This replaces certiorari, mandamus, and similar writs. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2020-26]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 106.5. Review of prison disciplinary hearings","Colorado · civil rules","Judicial review of correctional-facility quasi-judicial hearings, with a briefing schedule of 42/35/14 days. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2020-13]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 107. Contempt","Colorado · civil rules","A contempt citation, with the motion, affidavit, and order, must be served personally at least 21 days before the appearance date. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 120. Orders authorizing sales under powers (foreclosure)","Colorado · civil rules","Rule 120 hearings on a motion authorizing a foreclosure sale are set 21–35 days after the motion is filed. Notice is served at least 14 days before the hearing, and responses are due at least 7 days before. [Rule Change 2011-18]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 121. Statewide practice standards","Colorado · civil rules","Colorado’s uniform local rules, organized by section. § 1-1: entry of appearance, limited appearance (§ 1-1(5)), and withdrawal on 14 days’ notice. § 1-9: notice of related cases with the first pleading or Rule 12 motion. 7 days’ notice for depositions. § 1-14: default judgments. § 1-15: motions, covering conferral certification, page limits (15/10, or 25/15 for Rule 12(b)(1)–(2) and Rule 56 motions), 21-day response","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 122. Appointed judges","Colorado · civil rules","With all parties’ joint motion filed in the Supreme Court and approved by the appointed judge, the case may be assigned to a privately compensated appointed judge under C.R.S. § 13-3-111. The motion states the judge’s registration number and pay and includes the judge’s agreement to be bound by the Code of Judicial Conduct. [Rule Change 2025(04)]","#st-co-rules"],["Rule 205.3. Pro hac vice (out-of-state attorney)","Colorado · civil rules","Admission rule in C.R.C.P. Chapter 18. The out-of-state lawyer files a verified motion in the trial court, sends a copy and the fee to the Office of Attorney Registration, and associates Colorado counsel, who must attend all appearances unless excused. The attorney is then subject to the Colo. RPC, except the trust-account rules 1.15A–E. [Rule Changes 2019-03, 2021-04, 2022-14]","#st-co-rules"],["Appeals in Colorado: file in the appellate court within 49 days, and watch Rule 59(j)","Colorado · appeals","Final district-court judgments go to the Colorado Court of Appeals as of right. The Supreme Court reviews by certiorari, plus original proceedings under C.A.R. 21. County-court judgments go first to the district court (C.R.S. § 13-6-310).\nTwo features surprise lawyers trained on the federal rules. First, the notice of appeal is filed with the clerk of the appellate court, not the trial court; the trial court gets onl","#st-co-appeals"],["Notice of appeal goes to the appellate court; advisory copy to the trial court","Colorado · appeals · Starting the appeal","File the notice of appeal with the clerk of the appellate court within the C.A.R. 4 deadline, and serve an advisory copy on the lower court within the same time. Once the notice is filed, the appellate court has exclusive jurisdiction over the appeal. Missing any step other than timely filing the notice does not affect validity, though the court may act, including by dismissal. C.A.R. 3(a)","#st-co-appeals"],["49 days from entry; mailing or e-service of notice of entry restarts the count","Colorado · appeals · Starting the appeal","In civil cases the notice is due within 49 days after entry of the judgment, decree, or order. A judgment is “entered” when entered under C.R.C.P. 58, which makes the signing date the effective date of entry. If notice of entry was mailed or e-served to the parties, the 49 days run from the mailing or e-service. Agency-review appeals from district court also need the separate 49-day notice of intent required by C.R.S","#st-co-appeals"],["What the civil notice of appeal must contain","Colorado · appeals · Starting the appeal","The notice must include a caption under C.A.R. 32(d) and a short description of the case (the controversy in no more than one page, and what is appealed with the basis for jurisdiction). It must also state whether the order resolved all issues including attorney fees and costs; whether it was certified under C.R.C.P. 54(b); the entry and mailing dates; any post-trial extensions, filings, and denials (including deemed","#st-co-appeals"],["Cross-appeals and joint appeals","Colorado · appeals · Starting the appeal","If one party timely files a notice of appeal, any other party may file one within 14 days after the first notice was filed, or within the original period if that ends later. Parties may file a joint notice and proceed as a single appellant. The court may consolidate separate appeals. C.A.R. 4(a)(2); 3(c)","#st-co-appeals"],["Extension only for excusable neglect, and only 35 days","Colorado · appeals · Starting the appeal","On a showing of excusable neglect, the appellate court (not the trial court) may extend the time to file the notice of appeal by up to 35 days beyond the 49-day deadline. The request may be made before or after the deadline passes. C.A.R. 4(a)(4)","#st-co-appeals"],["A timely Rule 59 motion stops the clock for everyone","Colorado · appeals · Post-trial motions and tolling","When any party timely files a C.R.C.P. 59 motion, the appeal time stops for all parties. It runs again from the order disposing of the last timely Rule 59 motion, or from the date the motion is deemed denied under Rule 59(j). The trial court keeps jurisdiction to decide a timely Rule 59 motion even after a notice of appeal, and appellate proceedings are stayed meanwhile. C.A.R. 4(a)(3)","#st-co-appeals"],["Rule 59 deadlines: 14 days to file, 63 days to decide","Colorado · appeals · Post-trial motions and tolling","Post-trial motions (new trial, judgment notwithstanding the verdict, amended findings, amended judgment) are due within 14 days of entry of judgment. A longer time is allowed only if requested within that 14-day period. The court must decide within 63 days after the last such motion is filed. An undecided motion is then deemed denied for all purposes, including C.A.R. 4(a), and the appeal time starts that day. C.R.C.","#st-co-appeals"],["Certified questions of law (C.A.R. 4.2): two 14-day steps","Colorado · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","The Court of Appeals may, in its discretion, allow an interlocutory appeal from a district court or the Denver Probate Court if the trial court certifies that immediate review may promote a more orderly disposition or end the case, and the order involves a controlling and unresolved question of law. “Unresolved” means not decided by the Colorado Supreme Court or a published Court of Appeals opinion (or, for federal l","#st-co-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP rulings: appeal to the Court of Appeals","Colorado · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","An order granting or denying a special motion to dismiss under C.R.S. § 13-20-1101 is appealable to the Court of Appeals, except a denial based on the statutory exemptions. The 2026 C.R.S. editor’s note reports that Hinds v. Foreman, 2026 CO 9, held these provisions unconstitutional to the extent they let the Court of Appeals review final judgments, in conflict with Colo. Const. art. VI, § 17 and the rule that county","#st-co-appeals"],["Class-certification orders","Colorado · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","C.A.R. 3.3 governs appeals of orders granting or denying class certification. The collected orders restate only parts of it, such as a joint brief limited to 9,500 words, so read the full current rule before relying on it. C.A.R. 3.3 (Rule Changes 2023-05, 2024(11))","#st-co-appeals"],["Dependency-and-neglect and mental-health appeals run on 21 days","Colorado · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","In dependency or neglect cases, file the combined Notice of Appeal and Designation of Transcripts (JDF 1941) in the Court of Appeals within 21 days after entry, with an advisory copy to the trial court clerk. A cross-appeal is due within 7 days after the notice or within the 21 days, whichever is later. The record is due 42 days after the filing. Mental-health appeals under C.A.R. 3.5 also use 21 days for the notice.","#st-co-appeals"],["County-court judgments go to the district court","Colorado · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","Appeals from final county-court judgments go to the district court for the district, on the county-court record. Further review is by Supreme Court certiorari only. The appeal deadline in C.R.C.P. 411 was not in the collected text. C.R.S. § 13-6-310(1), (4); C.R.C.P. 411 (not collected)","#st-co-appeals"],["Record due 63 days after the notice of appeal","Colorado · appeals · Record and briefing","The record must be transmitted to the appellate court within 63 days after the notice of appeal is filed, unless the appellate court shortens or extends the time. If a court reporter’s affidavit blames the designating party’s failure to arrange payment for transcripts, that party must respond within 7 days. C.A.R. 10 (Rule Change 2023-05)","#st-co-appeals"],["Briefing schedule: 42 / 35 / 21 days","Colorado · appeals · Record and briefing","The opening brief is due 42 days after the record is filed, the answer brief 35 days after service of the opening brief, and the reply brief 21 days after service of the answer brief. In cross-appeals, the opening brief is due 42 days after the record, and each later combined brief (opening-answer, answer-reply) is due 35 days after service of the brief before it. The cross-appellant’s reply is due 21 days after the ","#st-co-appeals"],["Word limits: 9,500 principal, 5,700 reply","Colorado · appeals · Record and briefing","Opening and answer briefs may not exceed 9,500 words, and reply briefs 5,700. In cross-appeals, the opening, opening-answer, and answer-reply briefs get 9,500 words and the appellee’s reply 5,700. Headings, footnotes, and quotations count. The caption, tables, certificates, and signature block do not. A self-represented party without a word processor may file 30 pages (18 for a reply). A motion to exceed the limit mu","#st-co-appeals"],["Amicus briefs","Colorado · appeals · Record and briefing","An amicus supporting neither party files no later than 7 days after the appellant’s opening brief. The collected forms state an amicus word limit of 4,750 words, tied to C.A.R. 29(d). C.A.R. 29 (Rule Changes 2015-06, 2022-05); Form 6A","#st-co-appeals"],["Request fees in the principal brief","Colorado · appeals · Record and briefing","If attorney fees are recoverable for the appeal, the principal brief must specifically request them and explain the legal and factual basis. Citing C.A.R. 39.1 or a statute alone is not enough. Opposition goes in the answer or reply brief. The court may decide the amount or remand it. C.A.R. 39.1","#st-co-appeals"],["Oral argument must be requested","Colorado · appeals · Record and briefing","A request for oral argument must be a separate document titled “request for oral argument,” filed no later than 7 days after briefs close. C.A.R. 34 (Rule Change 2015-06)","#st-co-appeals"],["Rehearing: 14 days, 1,900 words","Colorado · appeals · Rehearing, mandate, and costs","A petition for rehearing may be filed within 14 days after entry of judgment, unless the time is changed by order. It may not exceed 1,900 words, excluding material not counted under C.A.R. 28(g)(1), unless the court permits. C.A.R. 40 (Rule Change 2016-05)","#st-co-appeals"],["Mandate timing","Colorado · appeals · Rehearing, mandate, and costs","The Court of Appeals mandate issues no earlier than 42 days after entry of judgment, or 28 days after denial of rehearing. In workers’ compensation and unemployment cases, it issues 28 days after judgment or 14 days after denial of rehearing, whichever is later. The Supreme Court mandate issues no earlier than 14 days after judgment. A stay pending a certiorari petition may not exceed 90 days unless extended for good","#st-co-appeals"],["Certiorari: 42 days, or 28 days after rehearing","Colorado · appeals · Supreme Court review","A petition for writ of certiorari is due within 42 days after entry of the final judgment on appeal, by opinion or by order, if no rehearing petition is filed. If rehearing is sought, the deadline is 28 days after denial of rehearing, or 28 days after a new or modified opinion issued on granted rehearing. The petitioner pays a $225 docket fee when filing the petition or a motion for extension. Mental-health (C.A.R. 3","#st-co-appeals"],["Opposition 14 days; reply 7 days","Colorado · appeals · Supreme Court review","The respondent’s opposition brief is due within 14 days after service of the petition, and the petitioner’s opposition to a cross-petition within 14 days after service of the cross-petition. Any reply is due within 7 days after service of the opposition. C.A.R. 53 (Rule Change 2023-05)","#st-co-appeals"],["Original proceedings (C.A.R. 21)","Colorado · appeals · Supreme Court review","Original proceedings in the Supreme Court are brought by petition under C.A.R. 21. The petitioner pays a $225 docket fee and must comply with C.A.R. 12. C.A.R. 21 was amended in 2023, 2024, 2025, and 2026. Its current filing requirements were not reviewed in full here. C.A.R. 21 (Rule Changes 2023(13), 2024(11), 2025(11), 2026(05))","#st-co-appeals"],["Mandatory for Colorado-licensed attorneys","Colorado · appeals · E-filing","Attorneys licensed in Colorado must e-file in the Supreme Court and Court of Appeals. A document transmitted by 11:59 p.m. Colorado time is filed that day. Documents e-filed are served by e-service, also effective the day transmitted by 11:59 p.m. Keep the signed copy for two years after final resolution, including appeals. A paper filing by an attorney who has been told to e-file can cost $50 per document. Self-repr","#st-co-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (civil)","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","49 days from entry of the judgment or order (or mailing/e-service of notice of entry).\nFiled with the appellate court; advisory copy to the trial court within the same period. C.A.R. 4(a)(1), (5)","#st-co-appeals"],["Notice of appeal by another party (cross-appeal)","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or the original 49 days, if later). C.A.R. 4(a)(2)","#st-co-appeals"],["Extension of time for notice of appeal","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","35 days from expiration of the 49-day period.\nExcusable neglect required; granted by the appellate court. C.A.R. 4(a)(4)","#st-co-appeals"],["Post-trial (Rule 59) motion","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment.\nExtension only if requested within the 14 days. Tolls the appeal time for all parties (C.A.R. 4(a)(3)). C.R.C.P. 59(a)","#st-co-appeals"],["Rule 59 motion deemed denied","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","63 days from filing of the last post-trial motion.\nThe 49-day appeal clock starts on the deemed-denial date with no order. C.R.C.P. 59(j)","#st-co-appeals"],["Motion to certify interlocutory appeal (C.A.R. 4.2)","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from date of the order to be appealed.\nOr a stipulation of all parties. C.A.R. 4.2(c)","#st-co-appeals"],["Petition to appeal after certification","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from trial court’s certification.\nFiled in the Court of Appeals, with an advisory copy to the trial court clerk. C.A.R. 4.2(d)","#st-co-appeals"],["Notice of appeal, dependency or neglect","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from entry of the judgment, decree, or order.\nCombined notice and designation of transcripts (JDF 1941). C.A.R. 3.4","#st-co-appeals"],["Record on appeal transmitted","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","63 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nAppellate court may shorten or extend. C.A.R. 10","#st-co-appeals"],["Opening brief","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","42 days from filing of the record.\n9,500 words (C.A.R. 28(g)). C.A.R. 31","#st-co-appeals"],["Answer brief","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","35 days from service of the opening brief.\n9,500 words. C.A.R. 31","#st-co-appeals"],["Reply brief","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from service of the answer brief.\n5,700 words. C.A.R. 31","#st-co-appeals"],["Amicus brief not supporting either party","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from filing of the appellant’s opening brief. C.A.R. 29","#st-co-appeals"],["Request for oral argument","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from close of briefing.\nSeparate document. C.A.R. 34","#st-co-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment.\n1,900 words. C.A.R. 40","#st-co-appeals"],["Petition for writ of certiorari (no rehearing)","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","42 days from entry of the final judgment on appeal.\n$225 docket fee (C.A.R. 51). C.A.R. 52","#st-co-appeals"],["Petition for writ of certiorari (after rehearing)","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from denial of rehearing, or a new or modified opinion on granted rehearing. C.A.R. 52 (Rule Change 2026(05))","#st-co-appeals"],["Opposition to certiorari petition","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the petition. C.A.R. 53","#st-co-appeals"],["Reply on certiorari","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from service of the opposition brief. C.A.R. 53","#st-co-appeals"],["Court of Appeals mandate issues (earliest)","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","42 days from entry of judgment.\nOr 28 days after denial of rehearing. C.A.R. 41","#st-co-appeals"],["Bill of costs","Colorado · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of the appellate mandate. C.A.R. 41; 39(c)(2)","#st-co-appeals"],["District of Columbia Court of Appeals","District of Columbia · courts","The local court of last resort (not the federal D.C. Circuit). Hears appeals as of right from final orders and judgments of the Superior Court and from listed interlocutory orders (injunctions, receivers and guardians, orders changing possession of property). It also makes the rules for bar admission and discipline, and lawyers admitted to its bar may practice in the D.C. courts.","#st-dc"],["None","District of Columbia · courts","D.C. has no intermediate appellate court. Appeals from the Superior Court go straight to the D.C. Court of Appeals.","#st-dc"],["Superior Court of the District of Columbia","District of Columbia · courts","The single local trial court of general jurisdiction, organized in divisions (including Civil, Criminal, Family Court, and Probate). It follows the Federal Rules of Civil and Criminal Procedure unless it adopts rules that modify them, and any modifying rule takes effect only after the Court of Appeals approves it.","#st-dc"],["Small Claims and Conciliation Branch (part of Superior Court)","District of Columbia · courts","There is no separate lower court. Small claims are heard in a branch of the Superior Court. Review of a small-claims judgment is not an appeal of right: you must file an application for allowance of an appeal in the Court of Appeals.","#st-dc"],["Statute and scope: claims arising from advocacy on issues of public interest","District of Columbia · anti-SLAPP","The Anti-SLAPP Act (D.C. Law 18-351, effective March 31, 2011) lets a party file a special motion to dismiss any claim arising from an ‘act in furtherance of the right of advocacy on issues of public interest.’\nThat covers (1) written or oral statements made in connection with an issue under consideration by a legislative, executive, or judicial body or other official proceeding, (2) statements in a place open to the","#st-dc-slapp"],["Deadline: 45 days after service of the claim","District of Columbia · anti-SLAPP","The special motion to dismiss must be filed within 45 days after service of the claim. The clock runs from service, not from filing. For a counterclaim or cross-claim, count from service of that pleading. D.C. Code § 16-5502(a)","#st-dc-slapp"],["Two-step test: prima facie showing, then ‘likely to succeed on the merits’","District of Columbia · anti-SLAPP","Step 1: the movant makes a prima facie showing that the claim arises from protected advocacy on an issue of public interest.\nStep 2: the motion must then be granted unless the plaintiff (the responding party) shows that the claim is likely to succeed on the merits. If the plaintiff makes that showing, the motion is denied.\nIf the motion is granted, dismissal is with prejudice. D.C. Code § 16-5502(b), (d)","#st-dc-slapp"],["Discovery stays automatically; the court may allow targeted discovery","District of Columbia · anti-SLAPP","Filing the motion stays discovery on the claim until the motion is decided. The court may order specified discovery if it appears likely that targeted discovery will let the plaintiff defeat the motion and it will not be unduly burdensome. The court can make the plaintiff pay the defendant’s expenses of responding to that discovery.\nThe court must hold an expedited hearing and rule as soon as practicable after it. D.","#st-dc-slapp"],["Special motion to quash protects anonymous speakers","District of Columbia · anti-SLAPP","A person whose personal identifying information is sought by a discovery order, request, or subpoena, in connection with a claim arising from protected advocacy, may file a special motion to quash. The same two-step test applies: a prima facie showing by the movant, then the motion is granted unless the party seeking the identity shows that the underlying claim is likely to succeed on the merits. D.C. Code § 16-5503","#st-dc-slapp"],["Fees: discretionary, and available after a partial win","District of Columbia · anti-SLAPP","The court ‘may’ (not must) award costs of litigation, including reasonable attorney fees, to a moving party who prevails in whole or in part on a special motion to dismiss or quash. The responding party can recover fees only if the court finds the motion frivolous or solely intended to cause unnecessary delay. D.C. Code § 16-5504","#st-dc-slapp"],["Exemptions: commercial speech and claims by the District government","District of Columbia · anti-SLAPP","The Act does not apply to (1) claims against a person primarily engaged in selling or leasing goods or services, when the statement was a representation of fact made to promote or complete sales or leases and the intended audience was an actual or potential buyer or customer, or (2) any claim brought by the District government, including D.C. public charter schools.\nThe government exemption was added by D.C. Law 24-3","#st-dc-slapp"],["Federal court: the special motion is not available in D.D.C. (case law, not verified)","District of Columbia · anti-SLAPP","The federal D.C. Circuit held that the special motion to dismiss does not apply in federal court in diversity cases, because it conflicts with Federal Rules 12 and 56 (Abbas v. Foreign Policy Group, LLC, 783 F.3d 1328 (D.C. Cir. 2015)). A defendant sued in D.C. Superior Court should weigh this before removing. The collected texts contain no case law, so this was not checked against them. Abbas v. Foreign Policy Grp.,","#st-dc-slapp"],["D.C. Court of Appeals: what ‘likely to succeed’ means, and appeal of a denial (case law, not verified)","District of Columbia · anti-SLAPP","The D.C. Court of Appeals has read ‘likely to succeed on the merits’ to require the plaintiff to present evidence on which a reasonable jury, properly instructed, could find for the plaintiff, a standard close to summary judgment (Competitive Enterprise Institute v. Mann, 150 A.3d 1213 (D.C. 2016)). It has also held that an order denying a special motion to dismiss is immediately appealable under the collateral order","#st-dc-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: no more than 5 cases in a calendar year","District of Columbia · out of state","To be eligible for pro hac vice admission, you must not have applied in more than five cases in the D.C. courts within the current calendar year. You must also be a member in good standing of the highest court of the state where you are admitted, have no pending disciplinary complaints, and never have been suspended or disbarred for disciplinary reasons.\nFee: $100 per application, paid through the Committee on Admiss","#st-dc-oos"],["Pro hac vice is closed to anyone who practices from D.C.","District of Columbia · out of state","An eligibility condition is that you do not practice, or hold yourself out as practicing, law in the District of Columbia. A lawyer working from a D.C. office cannot use pro hac vice admission to cover D.C. court work. D.C. Court of Appeals Committee on Admissions, Pro Hac Vice information page","#st-dc-oos"],["Practicing from a D.C. office without D.C. admission: Rule 49 controls, and its exceptions are narrow (not verified)","District of Columbia · out of state","D.C.’s version of Rule 5.5 has no ABA-style multijurisdictional safe harbors. It says only that a lawyer may not practice where doing so violates that jurisdiction’s rules, and its comment sends readers to D.C. Court of Appeals Rule 49 for what a lawyer not admitted in D.C. may and may not do here.\nRule 49 itself was not collected (dccourts.gov blocked automated access). Its exceptions are commonly understood to incl","#st-dc-oos"],["Admission to the federal district court is not admission to the D.C. bar","District of Columbia · out of state","Members of the bar of the D.C. Court of Appeals may practice in the D.C. courts. Members of the federal district court bar were enrolled automatically only if they were in good standing on April 1, 1972. Today, admission to the U.S. District Court for D.C. does not let you appear in Superior Court. D.C. Code § 11-2501(b)-(c)","#st-dc-oos"],["Admission without examination: three routes, all through the admissions portal","District of Columbia · out of state","Motion (Rule 46(e)(3)(A)): member in good standing of a bar of a court of general jurisdiction in the United States, in active practice for at least 3 of the 5 years before applying. Fee $595 plus the NCBE investigation fee.\nUBE score transfer (Rule 46(d)(3)): UBE score of 266 or higher (or NextGen UBE 616 or higher), MPRE 75 or higher, and a J.D. or LL.B. from an ABA-approved school (or a non-ABA degree plus 26 cred","#st-dc-oos"],["Governing code and recent amendments","District of Columbia · ethics","The District of Columbia Rules of Professional Conduct, adopted by the D.C. Court of Appeals as Appendix A to the Rules Governing the District of Columbia Bar. The court amended Rules 1.6, 1.7, 1.16, and 5.6 on May 14, 2025, effective September 15, 2025. The Rules Governing the Bar were last amended January 10, 2024, effective March 4, 2024. Rules Governing the D.C. Bar, App. A; D.C. Bar notices of 2024 and 2025 amen","#st-dc-ethics"],["Rule 5.4(b): nonlawyer partners and owners are allowed, but not passive investors","District of Columbia · ethics","Unlike ABA Model Rule 5.4, D.C. lets a lawyer practice in a firm in which an individual nonlawyer holds a financial interest or managerial authority, if (1) the firm’s sole purpose is providing legal services, (2) every owner and manager agrees to follow the D.C. Rules, (3) the lawyer-owners take responsibility for the nonlawyers as if they were lawyers under Rule 5.1, and (4) all of this is in writing.\nThe nonlawyer","#st-dc-ethics"],["Rule 1.6: ‘confidences and secrets,’ with narrower and different exceptions","District of Columbia · ethics","D.C. protects ‘confidences’ (privileged information) and ‘secrets’ (other information from the representation that the client asked to keep private, or whose disclosure would embarrass or likely harm the client). It bars revealing them and also using them to the client’s disadvantage or for anyone else’s advantage.\nPermitted disclosure differs from the ABA rule: a lawyer may reveal what is reasonably necessary to pre","#st-dc-ethics"],["Rule 1.7: an unwaivable bar on adverse positions in the same matter","District of Columbia · ethics","D.C.’s conflicts rule is organized differently from the ABA’s. Rule 1.7(a) flatly forbids advancing two or more adverse positions in the same matter; consent cannot cure it. Rule 1.7(b) lists conflicts that informed consent can cure (with full disclosure and a reasonable belief that you can represent each client competently), including taking a position adverse to another client in a matter involving specific parties","#st-dc-ethics"],["Rule 1.8(d): financial help to litigation clients beyond costs","District of Columbia · ethics","During contemplated or pending litigation or administrative proceedings, a lawyer may pay litigation expenses and also ‘other financial assistance which is reasonably necessary to permit the client to institute or maintain’ the case. That second category is broader than ABA Model Rule 1.8(e). D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 1.8(d)","#st-dc-ethics"],["Rule 1.5(b): fee basis, scope, and expenses must be in writing for a new client","District of Columbia · ethics","If you have not regularly represented the client, you must give the client, in writing and before or within a reasonable time after starting, the basis or rate of the fee, the scope of the representation, and the expenses the client will pay. The ABA rule only prefers a writing. Contingent-fee agreements must be in writing, and splitting a fee with another firm needs written notice to the client and the client’s info","#st-dc-ethics"],["Rule 8.4(g): no threats of criminal or disciplinary charges to gain civil leverage","District of Columbia · ethics","It is misconduct to seek or threaten to seek criminal or disciplinary charges solely to obtain an advantage in a civil matter. The ABA Model Rules have no such provision. Demand letters in D.C. matters need care. D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 8.4(g)","#st-dc-ethics"],["Rule 1.10(b): lateral screening, with notice that can be filed under seal","District of Columbia · ethics","When a lateral lawyer brings a Rule 1.9 conflict, the new firm avoids imputation if the lawyer is screened, gets no part of the fee, and the firm and lawyer promptly give the former client written notice describing the screen. If a firm client asks in writing that the representation be kept confidential, the notice is prepared at the start and filed under seal with Disciplinary Counsel until the matter becomes public","#st-dc-ethics"],["Rule 1.15: D.C. IOLTA, account titles, and five-year records","District of Columbia · ethics","Trust funds go in an ‘approved depository’ under Bar Rule XI. Nominal or short-term funds must be held in D.C. IOLTA. Account titles must include the lawyer’s or firm’s name and ‘DC IOLTA Account’ or ‘IOLTA Account’ (other trust accounts: ‘Trust Account’ or ‘Escrow Account’). Keep complete records for five years after the representation ends. Advance fees stay client property until earned unless the client gives info","#st-dc-ethics"],["Rule 3.3: candor duties yield to Rule 1.6","District of Columbia · ethics","The duty to correct a false statement you made to a tribunal does not apply if correcting it would require disclosure that Rule 1.6 prohibits. When a criminal defendant client insists on testifying falsely and you cannot dissuade the client or withdraw, the client may testify in narrative form; you may not elicit or argue the false testimony. Remedial disclosure of a fraud on the tribunal is limited to what Rule 1.6(","#st-dc-ethics"],["Rule 4.2: you may contact an adverse organization’s non-party employees, with disclosures","District of Columbia · ethics","You may talk with a nonparty employee of an organization without the consent of its lawyer. If the organization is adverse, you must first tell the employee who you are and that you represent a party adverse to the employer. ‘Party’ includes anyone with authority to bind the organization on the matter. You may also contact government officials with authority to redress the client’s grievance, with the same disclosure","#st-dc-ethics"],["Other D.C. differences worth knowing","District of Columbia · ethics","Rule 1.3 requires zealous as well as diligent representation, and bars intentionally prejudicing a client. Rule 3.6 limits trial publicity only for a case being tried to a judge or jury and only for statements creating a ‘serious and imminent threat’ of material prejudice. Rule 9.1 bars employment discrimination by lawyers, including on family responsibility and sexual orientation. Rule 8.5(b)(2)(ii): for a lawyer li","#st-dc-ethics"],["Anti-SLAPP motion: 45 days from service","District of Columbia · land mine","The special motion to dismiss must be filed within 45 days after service of the claim. Filing it stays discovery automatically. Dismissal is with prejudice, and fees are discretionary, not mandatory. D.C. Code §§ 16-5502(a), (c), (d), 16-5504(a)","#st-dc-unique"],["Claims against the District die without a 6-month notice","District of Columbia · land mine","For unliquidated damages against the District, written notice to the Mayor within six months of the injury is a condition of suit, separate from the limitations period. Calendar it at intake. D.C. Code § 12-309","#st-dc-unique"],["One-year limitations for defamation, assault, false arrest, malicious prosecution","District of Columbia · land mine","These torts carry a one-year period, much shorter than the default three years for most other claims. D.C. Code § 12-301(4), (7)-(8)","#st-dc-unique"],["Pro hac vice: 5 cases a calendar year, and never from a D.C. office","District of Columbia · land mine","Applying in more than five D.C. cases in a calendar year makes you ineligible, and so does practicing or holding yourself out as practicing in D.C. Out-of-town lawyers who handle D.C. matters regularly need full admission. D.C. App. R. 49; Committee on Admissions, Pro Hac Vice information page","#st-dc-unique"],["D.D.C. admission does not get you into Superior Court","District of Columbia · land mine","Only members of the D.C. Court of Appeals bar may practice in the D.C. courts. Automatic enrollment of federal-court lawyers ended with those in good standing on April 1, 1972. D.C. Code § 11-2501(b)-(c)","#st-dc-unique"],["Certified interlocutory appeals: 10 days, not 30","District of Columbia · land mine","The application to the Court of Appeals is due within ten days after the certified ruling is issued or entered, and it does not stop the case below unless a judge orders a stay. D.C. Code § 11-721(d)","#st-dc-unique"],["Small-claims judgments: no appeal of right","District of Columbia · land mine","Review of a Small Claims and Conciliation Branch judgment is by application for allowance of an appeal, not by notice of appeal. D.C. Code § 11-721(c)","#st-dc-unique"],["Threatening criminal charges in a civil dispute is misconduct","District of Columbia · land mine","D.C. Rule 8.4(g) bars seeking or threatening criminal or disciplinary charges solely to gain an advantage in a civil matter. Lawyers trained on the ABA rules often miss this. D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 8.4(g)","#st-dc-unique"],["New client? Put the fee, scope, and expenses in writing","District of Columbia · land mine","D.C. requires a writing, not just a preference, for a client you have not regularly represented. D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 1.5(b)","#st-dc-unique"],["Nonlawyer ownership is allowed only for working professionals","District of Columbia · land mine","A D.C. firm may have nonlawyer partners who provide professional services, with written undertakings, but not outside investors. Do not read Rule 5.4(b) as permission for investment ownership. D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 5.4(b) & cmt. [8]","#st-dc-unique"],["Removal can cost you the anti-SLAPP motion (case law, not verified)","District of Columbia · land mine","The D.C. Circuit has held that the special motion to dismiss is not available in federal court. Weigh this before removing a speech case to D.D.C. Verify against Abbas v. Foreign Policy Group, 783 F.3d 1328 (D.C. Cir. 2015) and later cases; no case law was collected. Abbas, 783 F.3d 1328 (not in collected texts)","#st-dc-unique"],["Superior Court civil traps: rule text not collected","District of Columbia · land mine","The Superior Court Civil Rules could not be collected (dccourts.gov blocked automated access). Commonly cited D.C. departures from the Federal Rules include Rule 12-I(a) (seek the other parties’ consent before filing most nondispositive motions and say in the motion whether consent was given) and Rule 4’s deadline for filing proof of service. Read the current rules before relying on either. Super. Ct. Civ. R. 4, 12-I","#st-dc-unique"],["No peremptory challenge; disqualification for cause by affidavit (not verified)","District of Columbia · judges","D.C. has no peremptory change of judge. A party seeking disqualification in a Superior Court civil case proceeds under the court’s own disqualification rule (Civil Rule 63-I), which is commonly understood to require a timely affidavit stating the facts of bias, with a certificate of counsel that it is made in good faith. The civil rules were not collected (dccourts.gov blocked automated access), so check the rule’s t","#st-dc-unique"],["No state constitution; the First Amendment applies directly","District of Columbia · speech","D.C. is a federal district, not a state, and has no state-constitution free-speech clause to read more broadly than the First Amendment. Local speech protection comes mainly from the Anti-SLAPP Act (see that section). Not checked against the collected texts. U.S. Const. amend. I; D.C. Code §§ 16-5501 to 16-5505","#st-dc-unique"],["Suing the District: written notice to the Mayor within 6 months","District of Columbia · unique rules","An action against the District of Columbia for unliquidated damages to person or property cannot be maintained unless, within six months after the injury, the claimant or the claimant’s agent or lawyer gives the Mayor written notice of the approximate time, place, cause, and circumstances. A Metropolitan Police Department report made in regular course is sufficient notice. The rule does not apply to claims brought un","#st-dc-unique"],["Short limitations periods: 1 year for defamation and intentional torts","District of Columbia · unique rules","One year: libel, slander, assault, battery, mayhem, wounding, malicious prosecution, false arrest, false imprisonment, and statutory penalties. Three years: personal property, injury to real or personal property, simple contracts, and any claim with no other period. Twelve years: instruments under seal. Fifteen years: recovery of land. Contracts for the sale of goods follow UCC § 28:2-725 instead, and § 12-301 does n","#st-dc-unique"],["Superior Court follows the Federal Rules unless it modifies them","District of Columbia · unique rules","By statute, the Superior Court conducts business under the Federal Rules of Civil and Criminal Procedure unless it adopts modifying rules, and a modifying rule needs the D.C. Court of Appeals’ approval. That is why the Superior Court Civil Rules track the federal numbering. Do not assume a local rule matches the federal one: read the D.C. text. D.C. Code § 11-946","#st-dc-unique"],["Interlocutory appeal by certification: apply within 10 days","District of Columbia · unique rules","If a Superior Court judge states in writing that a civil ruling involves a controlling question of law with substantial ground for difference of opinion and that an immediate appeal may materially advance the case, the Court of Appeals may allow an appeal if you apply within ten days after the ruling is issued or entered. The application does not stay the trial court proceedings unless a judge orders a stay. D.C. Cod","#st-dc-unique"],["Supreme Court of Delaware","Delaware · courts","Delaware’s only appellate court; there is no intermediate court of appeals. It hears appeals directly from the Court of Chancery and the Superior Court, usually in panels of three Justices (a quorum en banc is 5). It also takes certified questions from Delaware trial courts, federal courts, other states’ highest courts and the SEC, and it regulates the bar, including pro hac vice, in-house counsel certificates and di","#st-de"],["Court of Chancery","Delaware · courts","A court of equity with no juries. It hears all matters in equity unless an adequate remedy exists at law in another Delaware court, and it is the main forum for corporate, LLC, partnership, trust, fiduciary-duty, and stockholder litigation. It can also hear technology disputes by consent, and Magistrates in Chancery handle many matters, subject to review by a judge. It has its own rules, distinct from the Superior Co","#st-de"],["Superior Court","Delaware · courts","The general-jurisdiction trial court for civil actions at law (with juries) and felonies, sitting in each county. Its Complex Commercial Litigation Division (CCLD) takes business cases with $1 million or more in controversy or an exclusive Delaware choice-of-court clause, under special rules (Rules 141–146, effective March 20, 2026). It also hears some appeals from lower courts and agencies.","#st-de"],["Family Court; Court of Common Pleas; Justice of the Peace Court","Delaware · courts","Family Court handles domestic relations and juvenile matters. The Court of Common Pleas handles civil claims within its statutory dollar limit, misdemeanors and some appeals. The Justice of the Peace Court handles small civil claims, landlord-tenant cases and minor criminal matters. Each has its own civil rules on courts.delaware.gov; they were not collected for this page.","#st-de"],["Delaware adopted the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (2025)","Delaware · anti-SLAPP","Delaware replaced its narrow 1992 anti-SLAPP law with UPEPA, codified at 10 Del. C. ch. 60. The new law applies to civil actions filed, and causes of action asserted, on or after September 15, 2025. Former §§ 8136–8138 were repealed the same day but still govern claims and motions in cases asserted before that date. 10 Del. C. §§ 6001, 6013, 6014; 85 Del. Laws c. 217","#st-de-slapp"],["Scope: broad, with UPEPA’s usual exclusions","Delaware · anti-SLAPP","UPEPA covers claims based on (1) communications in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative or other governmental proceeding; (2) communications on an issue under consideration in such a proceeding; or (3) the exercise of free speech, press, assembly, petition or association rights under the U.S. or Delaware Constitution on a matter of public concern.\nIt does not apply to claims against government units or ","#st-de-slapp"],["Procedure: 60-day motion, automatic stay, quick hearing","Delaware · anti-SLAPP","A party may file a special motion for expedited relief within 60 days after being served with the pleading, or later for good cause. Filing the motion stays all other proceedings between the movant and the responding party, including discovery. The court may allow limited discovery that is needed on a specific issue.\nThe hearing must be held within 60 days after filing, and the ruling must come within 60 days after t","#st-de-slapp"],["Fees mandatory; punitive damages possible","Delaware · anti-SLAPP","A prevailing movant must be awarded court costs, reasonable attorney’s fees and litigation expenses related to the motion. The responding party gets fees only if the motion was frivolous or filed solely for delay. The court must also award punitive damages to a prevailing movant who proves the claim was brought or continued to harass, intimidate, punish or maliciously inhibit speech, petition or association. A volunt","#st-de-slapp"],["Appeal: 10 days, as of right, with a stay","Delaware · anti-SLAPP","A movant may appeal as of right from an order denying the motion in whole or in part, but must file the appeal within 10 days after entry of the order, not the usual 30. While the appeal is pending, all proceedings between all parties are stayed. A fee motion is not stayed. 10 Del. C. §§ 6004(c), (e), 6009","#st-de-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: same structure in every court, filed by Delaware counsel","Delaware · out of state","Each court admits out-of-state lawyers by its own rule: Superior Court Civil Rule 90.1, Court of Chancery Rule 170 and Supreme Court Rule 71. In each, a Delaware lawyer files a written motion, separately in each court, no later than the applicant’s first appearance, and certifies that the applicant is reputable and competent.\nThe applicant certifies good standing elsewhere, agrees to be bound by the Delaware Lawyers’","#st-de-oos"],["Pro hac vice assessment and annual renewal","Delaware · out of state","The assessment was $375 in 2015 and $400 in 2016 and has been indexed to inflation each year since. The current dollar figure is not stated in the rules, so check the court’s fee schedule. If the case continues into a later year, the assessment renews annually. In the Superior Court, it is payable January 1 and delinquent after February 1, with no proration. In the Supreme Court, it is payable February 1 and delinque","#st-de-oos"],["Delaware counsel stays on the hook","Delaware · out of state","Delaware counsel must appear, sign or receive service of all papers, and attend all court proceedings unless excused. Attendance at depositions is required only if ordered. In the Supreme Court, every paper must be signed by an active Delaware Bar member who maintains a bona fide Delaware office, and that lawyer or a firm colleague must attend all proceedings. A mail drop or answering service is not an office. In Cha","#st-de-oos"],["E-filing is through File & ServeXpress, by Delaware lawyers","Delaware · out of state","The Superior Court, Court of Chancery and Supreme Court all use eFiling. Only a Delaware Bar member (or a self-represented party) may sign an eFiling, and a lawyer may let only firm staff or a retained service provider file on the lawyer’s behalf. Per-document technology surcharges are $1.25 in the Superior Court and Chancery and $1.50 in the Supreme Court. In Chancery, all civil filings must be e-filed by a Delaware","#st-de-oos"],["Temporary practice and in-house counsel (2025 amendments)","Delaware · out of state","DLRPC 5.5(c) allows temporary practice in Delaware on the usual ABA grounds. An in-house lawyer with an office or other systematic and continuous presence in Delaware may practice for the employer under Rule 5.5(d)(1) only with a Supreme Court Rule 55.1 Certificate. The application is due within 30 days after starting employment, with a $250 fee and an employer affidavit. Both rules were amended effective July 14, 20","#st-de-oos"],["Limited-scope representation","Delaware · out of state","DLRPC 1.2(c) permits a lawyer to limit the scope of a representation if the limitation is reasonable and the client gives informed consent. No court rule on limited-scope appearances was found in the collected Superior Court, Chancery or Supreme Court rules. Withdrawal in the Supreme Court requires a court order. DLRPC 1.2(c); Supr. Ct. R. 12(b)","#st-de-oos"],["Governing code: Delaware Lawyers’ Rules of Professional Conduct","Delaware · ethics","These rules follow the ABA Model Rules numbering, with Delaware additions (1.15A trust account overdraft notification, 1.17A dissolution of law firm) and deletions (2.2 and 3.10). Pro hac vice lawyers in every Delaware court must agree to be bound by them. The Supreme Court’s Principles of Professionalism for Delaware Lawyers are aspirational; they expressly may not be used as a basis for discipline or sanctions. DLR","#st-de-ethics"],["No ABA 8.4(g) anti-harassment rule","Delaware · ethics","DLRPC 8.4 has only paragraphs (a)–(f). It does not adopt ABA Model Rule 8.4(g) on harassment or discrimination in conduct related to the practice of law. DLRPC 8.4","#st-de-ethics"],["Advance fees must be refundable and held in trust","Delaware · ethics","DLRPC 1.5(f), which has no ABA counterpart, lets a lawyer require payment in advance only if the lawyer gives the client a written statement that the fee is refundable if not earned and states how it will be earned. Unearned fees must stay in the trust account, and the client gets a statement when funds are withdrawn. Fee-splitting between firms under 1.5(e) requires only written notice to the client, no objection, a","#st-de-ethics"],["Detailed trust-account and bookkeeping rules","Delaware · ethics","DLRPC 1.15 goes well beyond the ABA rule. It applies to any lawyer in private practice in Delaware, even one whose office is elsewhere. Required records include receipts and disbursement journals, client subsidiary ledgers, and monthly reconciliations. Trust accounts must carry the title “Rule 1.15A Attorney Trust Account” (or a listed variant), and a separate operating account is mandatory. Records must be kept for ","#st-de-ethics"],["Confidentiality tracks the ABA rule","Delaware · ethics","DLRPC 1.6(b) allows disclosure on the ABA grounds, including to prevent, mitigate or rectify a client crime or fraud that used the lawyer’s services, and to detect conflicts when a lawyer changes firms. Paragraph (c) requires reasonable efforts to prevent unauthorized disclosure or access. Disclosure is permissive, not mandatory. DLRPC 1.6","#st-de-ethics"],["Delaware has one appellate court and appeal times cannot be extended","Delaware · land mine","Civil appeals go straight to the Supreme Court. The notice of appeal is due within 30 days after the judgment is entered on the docket, and the Supreme Court cannot enlarge the time to appeal or cross-appeal. Interlocutory appeals need a certification application in the trial court within 10 days of the order, plus a notice of appeal in the Supreme Court within 30 days. Supr. Ct. R. 6(a)(i), 11(b), 42(c)(i), (d)(i); ","#st-de-unique"],["The 10-day anti-SLAPP appeal","Delaware · land mine","An order denying a UPEPA special motion must be appealed within 10 days after entry, not the usual 30. 10 Del. C. § 6009","#st-de-unique"],["Short post-judgment windows in both trial courts","Delaware · land mine","In both the Superior Court and the Court of Chancery, reargument is due within 5 days after the decision. Motions for a new trial or to alter or amend the judgment are due within 10 days, not the federal 28. Opposition to reargument is due within 5 days. Super. Ct. Civ. R. 59(b), (d)–(e); Ct. Ch. R. 59(a)–(c)","#st-de-unique"],["20 days to answer, not 21","Delaware · land mine","An answer is due 20 days after service in both the Superior Court and Chancery. After a Rule 12 motion is denied, the answer is due within 10 days. Super. Ct. Civ. R. 12(a); Ct. Ch. R. 12(a)","#st-de-unique"],["Verify Chancery pleadings","Delaware · land mine","Complaints, counterclaims, crossclaims, third-party complaints and their amendments must be verified by each filing party. The Register may reject a new-case filing that lacks verification. Ct. Ch. R. 3(c); Ct. Ch. eFiling Best Practices § I(11)","#st-de-unique"],["Amend or stand: dismissal with prejudice","Delaware · land mine","In Chancery, and now in the Superior Court’s CCLD, a plaintiff facing a 12(b)(6) motion (or a 23.1 motion, in Chancery) must amend before the response is due. If the plaintiff stands on the complaint and loses, the dismissal is with prejudice as to that plaintiff. Ct. Ch. R. 15(a)(5); Super. Ct. Civ. R. 143","#st-de-unique"],["Chancery filing deadline is 5:00 p.m.","Delaware · land mine","Except for new complaints, notices of appeal and papers in summary or expedited matters (due by midnight), Chancery filings made after 5:00 p.m. count as filed the next day. Ct. Ch. R. 5(d)","#st-de-unique"],["Short Delaware limitations periods and the borrowing statute","Delaware · land mine","Contract, statutory and most other personal actions carry a 3-year period. Personal injury, wrongful death and wage claims carry 2 years. For a claim that arose outside Delaware, the shorter of Delaware’s period or the period where the claim arose applies, unless the claim originally belonged to a Delaware resident. A written contract involving at least $100,000 may set a period of up to 20 years. 10 Del. C. §§ 8106(","#st-de-unique"],["Pro hac vice renewal dates differ by court","Delaware · land mine","A pro hac vice assessment renews every January 1 in the Superior Court (delinquent after February 1) but every February 1 in the Supreme Court (delinquent after March 1, with a $50 late assessment). Delaware counsel must return each court’s renewal notice. Super. Ct. Civ. R. 90.1(b)(vi); Supr. Ct. R. 71(b)(vi)","#st-de-unique"],["Chancery cross-references still use old numbers","Delaware · land mine","Chancery rewrote and renumbered many rules in 2023–2026. For example, old Rule 15(aa) is now 15(a)(5), the e-filing rules 79.1 and 79.2 are gone, and the brief rule 171 moved to Rules 7 and 10. Yet the 2023 eFiling guide and some internal cross-references (such as Rule 144(f) citing ‘Rule 59(f)’) still use the old numbers. Check the current text. Ct. Ch. R. 7, 10, 15(a)(5), 59(c), 144(f), 171","#st-de-unique"],["Modified comparative negligence (not greater than)","Delaware · land mine","A plaintiff whose negligence is not greater than the combined negligence of the defendants may recover, but damages are reduced in proportion to the plaintiff’s share of fault. 10 Del. C. § 8132","#st-de-unique"],["No peremptory substitution of judge","Delaware · judges","None of the collected Superior Court, Chancery or Supreme Court rules gives a party a right to strike or substitute a judge without cause. The Superior Court’s President Judge, as Assignment Judge, may designate a judge for all proceedings in a case on motion for good cause. In the Supreme Court, a disqualified Justice is replaced by the next available Justice, and retired Justices or constitutional judges may be ass","#st-de-unique"],["Recusal under the Judges’ Code of Judicial Conduct","Delaware · judges","Disqualification is governed by Rule 2.11 of the Delaware Judges’ Code of Judicial Conduct. The Code itself was not collected. A July 14, 2025 order amended Rule 2.11(A)(4). It requires disqualification where impartiality might reasonably be questioned, including where the judge was associated within the preceding year with a firm or lawyer acting as counsel in the proceeding. A new comment says a former government l","#st-de-unique"],["State constitutional speech protection not checked","Delaware · speech","UPEPA protects speech, press, assembly, petition and association rights ‘guaranteed by the United States Constitution or Delaware’s Constitution,’ so the Delaware Constitution matters to its scope. The text of the Delaware Constitution (art. I, § 5) was not collected, and whether Delaware courts read it more broadly than the First Amendment was not verified. 10 Del. C. § 6002(b)(3); Del. Const. art. I, § 5 (not colle","#st-de-unique"],["Choosing the right court: Chancery (equity) vs. Superior (law)","Delaware · unique rules","The Court of Chancery has jurisdiction over all matters in equity but not over any matter where a sufficient remedy exists at law or by statute in another Delaware court. Damages-only contract and tort claims therefore belong in the Superior Court, where juries are available, unless equity jurisdiction attaches. Chancery may also hear technology disputes by consent, with no jury or punitive damages; monetary-only dis","#st-de-unique"],["Superior Court Complex Commercial Litigation Division (2026 rules)","Delaware · unique rules","Effective March 20, 2026, CCLD cases follow Superior Court Civil Rules 141–146. These rules borrow several Chancery features: a confidential-filing regime with tight public-version deadlines, an amend-or-stand rule on 12(b)(6) motions, and word limits (500 words for a motion with a brief; 3,000 for other motions and oppositions; 2,000 for replies). Discovery motions also require Delaware counsel on both sides to have","#st-de-unique"],["Superior Court: litigation-funding discovery (Sept. 30, 2026)","Delaware · unique rules","New Superior Court Civil Rule 26(b)(3) lets a party discover only four things about a litigation-funding agreement: whether it exists, who the parties are, how it meets the rule’s definition, and what aspects of the litigation the funder may control. The agreement itself and the funder’s work product are generally protected, and the agreement is inadmissible at trial. Super. Ct. Civ. R. 26(b)(3) (Order of Sept. 30, 2","#st-de-unique"],["Verified pleadings","Delaware · unique rules","Complaints, counterclaims, crossclaims, third-party complaints, and any amendments or supplements to them must be verified under oath or by affirmation by each filing party, and an authorized person must verify for an entity. The Register’s eFiling guidance treats a missing notarized verification from each plaintiff as grounds to reject the new-case filing, and requires the verification to be uploaded separately as a","#st-de-unique"],["Supplemental information sheet and deposits","Delaware · unique rules","Every complaint must be accompanied by a completed supplemental information sheet in the Court’s form. The eFiling guidance says the form should be one page, uploaded separately, with every section completed or marked N/A; no new form is needed for an amended complaint. Filing also requires an initial nonrefundable deposit (with listed fiduciary-type exceptions), a per-filing technology surcharge, and a supplemental ","#st-de-unique"],["Expedited and summary proceedings","Delaware · unique rules","The rules contain no stand-alone “motion to expedite” rule, but expedition changes several deadlines. In a summary proceeding or an action the Court has ordered expedited, papers may be filed and served until midnight (rather than 5:00 p.m.), the 30-day automatic stay of judgments does not apply, and exceptions to a Magistrate’s report must be noticed within three days. The eFiling guidance allows a proposed order on","#st-de-unique"],["Confidential filings and public versions","Delaware · unique rules","A paper may be filed confidentially only if it contains Confidential Information, and a filer must file confidentially if it believes another person would contend the paper contains Confidential Information. After a confidential filing of an original complaint, notice to interested persons is due by 3:00 p.m. the next day, and redaction designations are due by 3:00 p.m. on the third day. For other filings, notice is ","#st-de-unique"],["Motions, briefs, and letters have word limits","Delaware · unique rules","Former Rule 171 was repealed in 2023 and its content moved to Rules 7 and 10. A motion supported by a brief may not exceed 500 words. A motion without a brief, and the opposition to it, may not exceed 3,000 words each, and the reply may not exceed 2,000. Opening and answering briefs are capped at 14,000 words, reply briefs at 8,000, and letters at 1,000. The signature block must state the applicable word limit and th","#st-de-unique"],["Format, letters, and compendia","Delaware · unique rules","Filings must use Times New Roman 14-point (13-point footnotes), Century 12-point, or Century Schoolbook 12-point, with at least double line spacing and 1-inch margins. Citations follow the Blue Book as modified by Delaware’s Yellow Book. A letter may not seek relief without the Court’s authorization, and any letter from a lawyer must be signed by a Delaware lawyer. A party may submit a compendium of authorities, and ","#st-de-unique"],["Amend-or-stand on motions to dismiss","Delaware · unique rules","Former Rule 15(aa) is now Rule 15(a)(5). A plaintiff facing a Rule 12(b)(6) or 23.1 motion to dismiss must amend, or seek leave to amend, before its response to the motion is due, or within 30 days after a transfer from another court. If the plaintiff instead stands on the complaint and loses, the dismissal is with prejudice as to the named plaintiff, unless the Court finds good cause to dismiss without prejudice. An","#st-de-unique"],["Class actions: affidavits, settlements, fees","Delaware · unique rules","A proposed representative party must file an affidavit within 10 days after filing a complaint, a motion to intervene, or a motion for appointment, swearing that the person has received no compensation for serving beyond what the rule permits. A class action may be dismissed or settled only with Court approval after notice, with a further representative affidavit and the definitive settlement agreement. Approval of a","#st-de-unique"],["Derivative actions (Rule 23.1)","Delaware · unique rules","A derivative complaint must state with particularity any effort to obtain the desired action from the entity and the reasons for not obtaining it or not making the effort, which is the rule-based hook for demand futility. It must also plead facts supporting standing under the law governing the entity. The derivative plaintiff must file a no-compensation affidavit within 10 days and must be represented by counsel. If ","#st-de-unique"],["Voluntary dismissal limits","Delaware · unique rules","A plaintiff may dismiss by notice before the adverse party serves an answer or summary-judgment motion. That right is subject to Rules 23 and 23.1, and a notice of dismissal is not effective when the complaint faces a motion to dismiss and the plaintiff has filed an answering brief rather than amending. A case with no action for 1 year may be dismissed after reasonable notice. Ct. Ch. R. 41(a)(1), 41(e)","#st-de-unique"],["Expense and fee awards","Delaware · unique rules","Rule 88 was rewritten effective June 1, 2026. A request for expenses, including attorney’s fees, may be made in a paper or by separate motion that quantifies the amount. A person moving to quantify an award must file an affidavit summarizing the work and, for each timekeeper, giving the title, hourly rate, hours, and amount. The affidavit must also summarize out-of-pocket costs by category and state that the applican","#st-de-unique"],["Magistrates in Chancery and exceptions","Delaware · unique rules","The Chancellor may assign an action or dispute to a Magistrate in Chancery, whose rulings are “Reports” subject to de novo review by a Reviewing Judge. Outside the civil miscellaneous docket, exceptions are taken only after the Final Report. In non-expedited matters, the notice of exceptions is due within 11 days, the opening brief 20 days after that, the answering brief 20 days later, and the reply 15 days later. In","#st-de-unique"],["No jury; equity-court structure","Delaware · unique rules","The jury-trial rules (Rules 38, 39, and 47–51) are omitted, as are Rule 52 and Rule 68 (offer of judgment), so trials are to the Court. A preliminary injunction or TRO request must be supported by an affidavit or a verified pleading. A TRO issued without notice expires within no more than 10 days unless extended for up to 30 more days, and the movant must post security. Ct. Ch. R. 38, 39, 47–52, 65(a)–(c), 68","#st-de-unique"],["2023–2026 rule rewrites and renumbering","Delaware · unique rules","The Court has rewritten its rules in tranches. Effective September 25, 2023, it amended Rules 7, 10, and 17–25, including 23, 23.1, and 23.2, and repealed Rule 171 with its content moving to Rules 7 and 10. Effective June 14, 2024 (order of May 31, 2024), it amended Rules 1–6, 5.1, 8–15, 23, 23.1, and 79 and omitted Rules 79.1 and 79.2, with e-filing now in Rules 5(c)–(e) and (j); the old Rule 15(aa) became 15(a)(5),","#st-de-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope and purpose of Rules","Delaware · civil rules","The Rules govern procedure in the Superior Court of Delaware, except as stated in Rule 81, and are to be construed, administered, and employed by the Court and the parties to secure the just, speedy and inexpensive determination of every proceeding.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","Delaware · civil rules","There is one form of action, the “civil action.”","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencement of action","Delaware · civil rules","An action starts by filing with the Prothonotary a complaint plus a praecipe directing the Prothonotary to issue the writ, with copies for each defendant and a Case Information Statement (CIS); an objection to the Related Cases listed on another party’s CIS is due within 10 days after the last responsive pleading, with responses within 5 days, and the Civil Administrative Judge may reassign the case with the Presiden","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 4. Process","Delaware · civil rules","The plaintiff prepares the writ for the Prothonotary’s signature and seal; once it is on the electronic docket, the plaintiff delivers the service documents to the sheriff or a specially appointed process server (special appointment requires a non est return, service after 10:00 p.m. or on a weekend/holiday, or another exigent reason, except freely for Secretary of State service under 10 Del. C. §§ 3104, 3112, 3114).","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 5. Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","Delaware · civil rules","Papers after the complaint are served on every party (on the attorney if represented) by delivery or mail, with mail service complete on mailing; garnishees answer within 20 days and the plaintiff may except within 10 days, with a $20 garnishee fee per garnishee; withdrawal of an appearance needs a court order unless Delaware counsel remains. Discovery requests and responses are served but not filed (a “Notice of Ser","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 6. Time","Delaware · civil rules","Exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a weekend, legal holiday, or day the Prothonotary’s office is closed; for periods under 11 days, intermediate Saturdays, Sundays and legal holidays are excluded (legal holidays are those set by statute or by the Governor or Chief Justice). The Court may enlarge time for cause before expiration, or after expiration for excusable neglect, but not the time un","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings allowed; form of motions","Delaware · civil rules","The pleadings are a complaint and answer, a reply to a counterclaim, an answer to a cross-claim, and third-party complaint and answer; the Court may order a reply to an answer. Motions must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state the grounds with particularity and the relief sought, be filed without a backer, and be signed under Rule 11; demurrers and pleas are abolished, and papers go on white paper","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","Delaware · civil rules","A claim needs a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment; defenses must be stated in short and plain terms with denials fairly meeting the averments, and listed affirmative defenses (including contributory negligence, injury by fellow servant, statute of limitations) must be pleaded. Averments not denied are admitted except as to damages; unlike the federal rule, Rule 8(dd) de","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","Delaware · civil rules","Capacity need not be alleged but must be challenged by specific negative averment; fraud, negligence and mistake must be pleaded with particularity (Delaware adds negligence to the federal list), while intent and knowledge may be averred generally. A pleading seeking unliquidated damages must not state an amount (except items of special damage), but on written request the pleader must serve a statement of the amount ","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","Delaware · civil rules","Pleadings need a caption with the court, title, file number and Rule 7(a) designation, numbered paragraphs, and separate counts where helpful; exhibits are part of the pleading. A complaint may not be filed under a pseudonym without prior court approval or an accompanying motion supported by an affidavit of specific facts showing why anonymity is necessary, which may be filed under seal.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing of pleadings, motions, and other papers: Representations to Court, sanctions","Delaware · civil rules","Every paper must be signed by at least 1 attorney of record (or the unrepresented party), certifying after reasonable inquiry that it has a proper purpose and legal and factual support. A sanctions motion must be served but not filed unless the challenged paper is not withdrawn or corrected within 21 days; sanctions are limited to what deters repetition, no monetary sanctions against a represented party for legal con","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections — When and how presented — By pleading or motion — Motion for judgment on pleadings","Delaware · civil rules","A defendant must serve an answer within 20 days after service of process and complaint (or 20 days after appearing before service), and answers to cross-claims and replies to counterclaims are also due in 20 days; if a Rule 12 motion is denied or deferred, the responsive pleading is due 10 days after notice of the ruling (or 10 days after a more definite statement is served). Special timing applies to certiorari (exc","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and cross-claim","Delaware · civil rules","A pleading must state as a counterclaim any claim against an opposing party arising from the same transaction or occurrence, with exceptions for claims already pending elsewhere or where the opposing party sued by attachment; other counterclaims are permissive and may exceed the opposing claim. Later-maturing and omitted counterclaims may be added with leave, cross-claims against coparties are allowed if transactiona","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","Delaware · civil rules","A defending party may serve a third-party complaint on someone who may be liable to it for the plaintiff’s claim, without leave if filed within 10 days after serving the original answer and otherwise on motion with notice. The third-party defendant may raise defenses and claims as provided, and a plaintiff facing a counterclaim may bring in a third party on the same terms.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Delaware · civil rules","A party may amend once as of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 20 days after service if no responsive pleading is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar; otherwise leave (freely given) or written consent is needed, and responses to amended pleadings are due within the time left or 10 days, whichever is longer. Rule 15(aa) requires the amended pleading to indicate plainly how it dif","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 16. Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","Delaware · civil rules","The Court may hold pretrial conferences and generally must enter a scheduling order setting deadlines for joinder, amendment, motions, discovery, and compulsory ADR (mediation by default if the parties cannot agree on a format); if the parties cannot agree on an ADR practitioner, they must jointly move within 30 days of the scheduling order for the Court to appoint one. ADR practitioners are immune absent bad faith, ","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 16.1. Mandatory non-binding arbitration","Delaware · civil rules","Civil actions seeking money damages (with only nominal nonmonetary claims) go to mandatory non-binding arbitration (MNA) when claimant’s counsel elects it on the Case Information Sheet, with the arbitrator’s authority capped at $50,000 exclusive of costs and interest; listed actions (class actions, Rule 81(a) matters, replevin, declaratory judgment, attachment, interpleader, summary proceedings, foreclosure, some in ","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 16.2. [Deleted]","Delaware · civil rules","Deleted; the text lists Rule 16.2 only as “[Deleted].”","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 17. Parties plaintiff and defendant; capacity","Delaware · civil rules","Actions must be prosecuted by the real party in interest, though fiduciaries and parties to contracts for another’s benefit may sue in their own names, and no dismissal occurs until a reasonable time is allowed for ratification, joinder or substitution. An infant or incompetent person may sue or defend through a representative, next friend or guardian ad litem, whom the Court must appoint if needed; subdivision (b) i","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims and remedies","Delaware · civil rules","A party asserting a claim may join as many claims, independent or alternative, as it has against an opposing party; subdivision (b) is omitted.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 19. Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","Delaware · civil rules","A person must be joined if feasible when complete relief is otherwise impossible or the person’s interest would be impaired or parties exposed to inconsistent obligations. If joinder is not feasible, the Court decides in equity and good conscience whether to proceed using four listed factors, and pleadings must name unjoined required persons and explain why.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","Delaware · civil rules","Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, when claims arise out of the same transaction, occurrence or series and share a common question of law or fact. The Court may order separate trials or other orders to prevent embarrassment, delay or prejudice.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","Delaware · civil rules","Misjoinder is not grounds for dismissal; parties may be dropped or added by court order at any stage on just terms, and any claim may be severed.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","Delaware · civil rules","Claimants exposing the plaintiff to double or multiple liability may be joined and required to interplead even if their claims are not of common origin, and a defendant may seek interpleader by cross-claim or counterclaim. The remedy supplements the statutory remedy, but statutory actions proceed under these Rules.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","Delaware · civil rules","A class action requires numerosity, commonality, typicality and adequacy, plus one of the (b)(1), (b)(2) or (b)(3) grounds; for (b)(3) classes the Court must give the best practicable notice, including the right to opt out by a specified date. The Court decides certification as soon as practicable and may manage the action by order; a class action may not be dismissed or compromised without court approval and notice ","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","Delaware · civil rules","Intervention is of right when a statute confers an unconditional right or the applicant’s interest may be impaired and is not adequately represented, and is permissive when a statute confers a conditional right or the applicant shares a common question of law or fact (state officers and agencies may intervene where a party relies on a statute or regulation they administer). The motion must be timely, served under Rul","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","Delaware · civil rules","On a party’s death, any party or the decedent’s successors or representatives may move to substitute; unless the motion is made within 90 days after the death is suggested on the record, the action is dismissed as to the decedent (except where an interlocutory appeal application has been made). The Rule also covers incompetency, transfers of interest, and automatic substitution of a successor when a public officer su","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 26. General provisions governing discovery","Delaware · civil rules","Parties may discover any non-privileged matter relevant to any party’s claim or defense and proportional to the needs of the case, including ESI, and insurance agreements are discoverable; trial-preparation materials require substantial need and undue hardship, expert discovery proceeds mainly by interrogatory, and draft expert reports and most attorney-expert communications are protected. Effective September 30, 202","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 27. Deposition before action or pending appeal","Delaware · civil rules","Omitted; the Delaware text has no counterpart to this rule.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Delaware · civil rules","Depositions in the United States are taken before an officer authorized to give oaths where the examination occurs or a court-appointed person; abroad, under a treaty or convention, letter of request, on notice before an authorized person, or before a commissioned person. A relative, employee, attorney or financially interested person may not serve as the officer.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","Delaware · civil rules","Parties may by written stipulation set deposition logistics and modify discovery procedures, but stipulations extending the response time under Rules 33, 34, and 36 need court approval.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions upon oral examination","Delaware · civil rules","Any party may depose any person after the action begins, but a plaintiff needs leave to depose within 30 days after serving any defendant unless the defendant has sought discovery or a special notice shows the witness is about to leave Delaware; notice must state the recording method, and organizations must designate witnesses under (b)(6); remote depositions are allowed by stipulation or order. From start to finish ","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions upon written questions","Delaware · civil rules","Any party may take a deposition on written questions served with a notice naming the deponent and officer. Cross questions are due within 14 days after service, redirect questions within 7 days after cross, and a further round within 7 days after redirect (the text of that last clause is garbled); the Court may enlarge or shorten these times.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 32. Use of depositions in court proceedings","Delaware · civil rules","Depositions may be used to impeach, an adverse party’s or its officer’s or designee’s deposition may be used for any purpose, and any witness’s deposition may be used if the witness is dead, outside Delaware (unless procured by the offeror), unable to attend, not subpoenable, or in exceptional circumstances. A deposition is not usable against a party who got less than 11 days’ notice and promptly moved for a protecti","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories to parties","Delaware · civil rules","Interrogatories may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on other parties with or after service of the summons; answers and objections are due within 30 days after service, except a defendant has 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Objections must be specific or are waived; attorney-signed answers to the required Form 30 interrogatories (Rules 3(h) and 5) must be followed by party-sworn a","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 34. Production of documents and things and entry upon land for inspection and other purposes","Delaware · civil rules","A party may request inspection and copying of documents, ESI and tangible things, or entry on land, describing items with reasonable particularity. Responses are due within 30 days (a defendant has 45 days after service of the summons and complaint); objections must be specific and state whether materials are being withheld; production may be of copies by the inspection date or other stated reasonable time, and ESI i","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examinations of persons","Delaware · civil rules","When a party’s mental or physical condition (including blood group) is in controversy, the Court may order an examination by a suitably licensed or certified examiner on motion for good cause, specifying time, place, manner, conditions and scope. The examined party may obtain the examiner’s detailed report but then must provide like reports and waives privilege as to other examiners of the same condition.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","Delaware · civil rules","A party may request admission of facts, the application of law to fact, or the genuineness of documents; each matter is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 30 days, but a defendant need not respond before 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Lack of knowledge is no excuse without reasonable inquiry, the requester may move to test the sufficiency of answers, and admissions are","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to make discovery: Sanctions","Delaware · civil rules","A party may move to compel answers, designations or production, and expenses generally follow the result; disobeying a discovery order can lead to facts being taken as established, preclusion, striking pleadings, dismissal, default, or contempt, plus mandatory expenses. If ESI that should have been preserved is lost and cannot be replaced, the Court may order curative measures on a finding of prejudice, but adverse i","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury trial of right","Delaware · civil rules","The jury right is preserved as it has existed; a party must serve a written jury demand no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue (if endorsed on a pleading, on the first page right after the caption), and other parties have 10 days to demand jury trial of additional issues. A party who does not demand a jury of 12 is deemed to consent to a jury of 6, and any other party has 10 da","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the Court","Delaware · civil rules","Cases with a 12-person demand are docketed “jury action (12)” and cases with a 6-person jury are docketed “jury action (6),” and are tried that way unless the parties stipulate to a bench trial or the Court finds no jury right; the Court may on motion order a 12-person jury despite no demand. Issues not demanded are tried by the Court, which may still order a jury on motion, use an advisory jury, or with consent try ","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 40. Assignment of judges; special juries; continuances","Delaware · civil rules","The President Judge is the Assignment Judge and may, on motion for good cause, designate a judge for all proceedings in a case. A special jury must be requested at or before the case is marked for trial; a continuance motion for an absent material witness must be filed promptly with an affidavit of the expected testimony, efforts to secure the witness, and when the absence became known, and may be refused if the othe","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","Delaware · civil rules","A plaintiff may voluntarily dismiss without order (except in replevin) by notice before an answer or summary judgment motion is served, or by stipulation of all appearing parties, normally without prejudice (a second notice dismissal operates as an adjudication on the merits); otherwise dismissal requires a court order. Involuntary dismissals (other than for jurisdiction, venue or Rule 19) are on the merits unless st","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation: Separate trials","Delaware · civil rules","The Court may consolidate or jointly try actions sharing a common question of law or fact and may order separate trials of claims or issues for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or for expedition and economy.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 42.1. Opening statements by attorneys","Delaware · civil rules","In jury trials the plaintiff’s attorney may open, and the defendant’s attorney may open either before any plaintiff testimony or after the plaintiff rests and before the defense case.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 43. Taking of testimony; conferences during trial","Delaware · civil rules","Testimony is taken in open court, though for good cause in compelling circumstances the Court may permit contemporaneous transmission from another location; sidebar and chambers conferences during trial are recorded unless the judge determines in advance that no evidentiary or substantive issues are involved. Affirmations may replace oaths, motions may be heard on affidavits, and the Court may appoint and fix compens","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 44. Proof of official records","Delaware · civil rules","Domestic official records may be proved by official publication or an attested copy with a custody certificate; foreign records by attested copy with a final certification (or, for good cause, without one), or as provided by treaty. A certified statement of diligent search proves the absence of a record, and other lawful methods remain available.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 44.1. Determination of foreign law","Delaware · civil rules","A party raising foreign law must give notice in its pleadings or other reasonable written notice; the Court may consider any relevant source, admissible or not, and its determination is a ruling on a question of law.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","Delaware · civil rules","Subpoenas issue from the county where the action is pending, are signed in blank by the Prothonotary or issued and signed by a Delaware attorney, must recite subdivisions (c)–(e), and may be served by the Sheriff or any nonparty at least 18 years old, with prior notice to parties of document production. A person commanded to produce may serve written objections within 14 days after service (or before compliance if so","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 46. Exceptions unnecessary","Delaware · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary; it is enough to make known the desired action or objection and its grounds when the ruling is sought or made, and a party with no chance to object is not prejudiced.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 47. Jurors","Delaware · civil rules","The Court conducts voir dire, and attorneys’ proposed questions must be submitted in writing before jury drawing begins. Each party gets 3 peremptory challenges (multiple plaintiffs or defendants may be treated as one party, and the Court may allow more for good cause); up to 6 alternates may be seated, with 1, 2 or 3 extra peremptories for 1–2, 3–4 or 5–6 alternates, usable only against alternates.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 48. Juries of less than 12 — Majority verdict","Delaware · civil rules","The parties may stipulate to a jury of fewer than 12 or to a verdict by a stated majority.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 49. Special verdicts and interrogatories","Delaware · civil rules","The Court may require a special verdict on each fact issue, and an omitted issue is waived unless a party demands its submission before the jury retires. The Court may also submit interrogatories with a general verdict and, if the answers and verdict conflict, enter judgment on consistent answers, return the jury, or order a new trial.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 50. Judgment as a matter of law in actions tried by jury; alternative motion for new trial; conditional rulings","Delaware · civil rules","A motion for judgment as a matter of law may be made before the case goes to the jury, specifying the judgment sought and its legal and factual basis. A denied motion may be renewed by service and filing no later than 10 days after entry of judgment, with or alternatively to a Rule 59 new-trial motion, and the losing party on a granted motion may move for a new trial within 10 days after entry of judgment; the Court ","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 51. Instructions to jury; objection","Delaware · civil rules","Parties may file written requested instructions at the close of evidence or earlier as directed, and the Court must tell counsel its rulings before closing arguments. An instruction error is preserved only by an objection stating the matter and grounds, made before or at the time the Court sets immediately after the jury retires, outside the jury’s hearing.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings by the Court","Delaware · civil rules","Omitted; the Delaware text contains no Rule 52 (Rule 6(b) also lists Rule 52(b) as omitted).","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters","Delaware · civil rules","Omitted from this part of the Rules; masters in Superior Court are addressed in Rules 113–123.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgment; costs","Delaware · civil rules","A judgment includes any appealable order; in multi-claim or multi-party cases, partial final judgment requires an express determination of no just reason for delay and an express direction for entry. Costs are allowed to the prevailing party on application within 10 days of entry of final judgment; court-reporter, video, and deposition witness and expert fees are taxable only if the deposition is introduced in eviden","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 54.1. Fees for the prothonotary","Delaware · civil rules","Repealed, effective July 1, 1988.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 55. Default judgments","Delaware · civil rules","When a party fails to appear, plead or defend, the Prothonotary enters default judgment on a sum-certain claim on written direction with a statement of principal (not exceeding the complaint’s demand), credits and interest and an affidavit that the defendant is not an infant or incompetent, is in default, and owes the amount. Other defaults require application to the Court, with at least 3 days’ written notice to a p","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","Delaware · civil rules","A claimant may move for summary judgment after 20 days from commencement (or after the opponent moves) and before the case is marked for trial, and a defending party may move at any time, though the Court may allow later motions after a pretrial conference. The motion must be served at least 10 days before the hearing, judgment is granted if there is no genuine issue of material fact and the movant is entitled to jud","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments","Delaware · civil rules","Declaratory judgment actions under Delaware’s statute follow these Rules, with jury rights under Rules 38 and 39; another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the Court may order a speedy hearing and advance the case on the calendar.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 58. Entry of judgment","Delaware · civil rules","Subject to Rule 54(b), the Prothonotary enters judgment at once on a general verdict or a decision for a sum certain, costs, or denial of all relief, while other relief, special verdicts and verdicts with interrogatories need the Court to approve the form first. A judgment is effective only when set forth and entered, entry is not delayed for costs, and attorneys may submit forms of judgment only when the Court direc","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 58A. Entry of judgment by confession and execution thereon","Delaware · civil rules","Transferred; see Rule 58.1.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 58B. Entry of judgment by confession in open court","Delaware · civil rules","Transferred; see Rule 58.2.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 58C. Judgments by confession entered prior to July 9, 1971","Delaware · civil rules","Transferred; see Rule 58.3.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 58.1. Entry of judgment by confession and execution thereon","Delaware · civil rules","To confess judgment under 10 Del. C. § 2306, the plaintiff lodges a praecipe in the prescribed form, the original instrument with copies, any nonresident affidavit, and § 2306(b) notice letters with certified-mail envelopes; the Prothonotary dockets the judgment tentatively and mails each debtor notice that it may object on the second motion day after mailing (or by publication once a week for 2 weeks if mail is unde","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 58.2. Entry of judgment by confession in open court","Delaware · civil rules","An obligee may obtain a confessed judgment in open court on a motion day, appearing with the obligor before a court reporter, by filing the Rule 58.1 praecipe and the original instrument and proving the obligation’s genuineness, the obligor’s identity and signature, and an effective waiver of constitutional rights. The judgment is final like one after trial, and execution follows Rule 58.1(h).","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 58.3. Judgments by confession entered prior to July 9, 1971","Delaware · civil rules","Before the first execution on a confessed judgment entered before July 9, 1971, the creditor files a praecipe and § 2306(f) notice letters, which the Prothonotary mails (or publishes once a week for 2 weeks); the debtor may object on the second motion day after mailing. If the debtor contests, the creditor must file a complaint within 10 days alleging an effective waiver and the debt, and the judgment is stricken, su","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 59. New trials and rearguments","Delaware · civil rules","A new-trial motion must be served and filed no later than 10 days after entry of judgment (or after the verdict if entry was deferred) with a brief and any affidavits; the opponent has 10 days to answer, and with affidavits reply papers are due 10 days later, each extendable once by up to 10 days by the Court or stipulation. The Court may order a new trial on its own within 10 days after entry of judgment, and a moti","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from judgment or order","Delaware · civil rules","Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time. On motion and on just terms the Court may relieve a party from a final judgment for mistake or excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, fraud or misconduct, a void or satisfied judgment, or any other justifying reason; the motion does not suspend the judgment, independent actions and relief for fraud on the court remain available, and old writs such as coram nobis ","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","Delaware · civil rules","Errors in evidence rulings or other acts are not grounds for a new trial or disturbing a judgment unless refusing relief would be inconsistent with substantial justice, and the Court must disregard errors that do not affect substantial rights.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 62. Stays by trial court and on appeal","Delaware · civil rules","No execution or enforcement may occur until 10 days after entry of judgment (FRCP 62 now allows 30 days), and the Court may stay enforcement pending Rule 50, 59, or 60 motions on security. On appeals from a lower court to Superior Court, a stay may require a supersedeas bond or cash deposit covering the judgment plus interest and costs; stays pending appeal to the Supreme Court are governed by Article IV, § 24 of the","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 63. Inability of a judge to proceed","Delaware · civil rules","If a judge cannot continue a trial or hearing, another judge may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record and finding no prejudice; in a bench trial the successor must, on request, recall any available material disputed witness.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 64. Seizure of persons or property","Delaware · civil rules","Statutory seizure remedies to compel appearance or secure satisfaction of a judgment are available during the action. On a defendant’s motion for discharge on common bail the Court sets a hearing, and the defendant is discharged unless the plaintiff files an affidavit to hold to special bail; reasonable notice of justification is required when the Prothonotary takes special bail.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 64.1. Orders for hearing or rules to show cause","Delaware · civil rules","Except where a statute requires a rule to show cause, matters formerly brought that way are started by motion after a complaint or petition is filed, may be presented ex parte as to nonappearing persons, and the order sets the hearing and notice deadlines and is served like a summons (publication allowed on a showing). A rule to show cause, used only where a statute requires it, must state the return date and whether","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions","Delaware · civil rules","Omitted; the Delaware text has no Rule 65.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 65.1. Security: Proceedings against sureties","Delaware · civil rules","A surety on a bond or undertaking submits to the Court’s jurisdiction and appoints the Prothonotary as agent for service, and its liability may be enforced by motion without an independent action, with the Prothonotary mailing copies to known sureties.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers appointed by federal courts","Delaware · civil rules","Omitted; the Delaware text has no Rule 66.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit in Court","Delaware · civil rules","In actions for money or a deliverable thing, a party may, on notice and with leave of Court, deposit all or part of it with the Court and must serve the order on the Prothonotary. Money is withdrawn only by court order and is held in an interest-bearing account or court-approved instrument.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of judgment","Delaware · civil rules","A defending party may serve an offer of judgment more than 10 days before trial; the offeree has 10 days to accept in writing, after which judgment is entered. An unaccepted offer is deemed withdrawn and inadmissible except on costs, and an offeree who does not obtain a more favorable judgment must pay costs incurred after the offer; after liability is determined, an offer may be made at least 10 days before the dama","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution","Delaware · civil rules","Execution may issue at any time while the judgment remains a lien on the debtor’s real property, the fictitious writ of vice comes is abolished, and the creditor may take discovery in aid of execution; execution on confessed judgments follows Rules 58.1–58.3. Security for a stay of execution after certain affidavit-of-defense defaults must be given within 20 days after judgment, payable in six months; sheriff’s sale ","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 70. Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","Delaware · civil rules","Omitted; the Delaware text has no Rule 70.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 71. Process in behalf of and against persons not parties","Delaware · civil rules","Omitted; the Delaware text has no Rule 71.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 71A. Condemnation of property","Delaware · civil rules","Transferred; see Rule 71.1.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 71.1. Condemnation of property","Delaware · civil rules","In condemnation by a public agency (state, county, municipality or school district), an order of possession under 10 Del. C. § 6110(a) is entered forthwith on 10 days’ written notice and an affidavit of necessity from the agency’s chief administrative officer, unless the owner shows good cause by affidavits, depositions or verified answer, with any hearing held without delay. The owner bears the burden of overcoming ","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 71.2. Medical negligence","Delaware · civil rules","Repealed, effective August 13, 2025 (by the order effective September 1, 2025).","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 71.3. Forfeitures pursuant to 16 Del. C. § 4784","Delaware · civil rules","The State must give notice of a drug-related seizure within 60 days (also by first-class mail to any incarcerated interest holder), stating that claimants may petition for return within 45 days after the notice. After 45 days from the last notice, the State may apply under oath for forfeiture in rem; a timely sworn petition for return must be served on the Attorney General and is tried without a jury within 90 days u","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 72. Appeals from certain commissioners, boards and courts","Delaware · civil rules","For appeals on the record from boards, commissions, personnel hearing officers and courts, the notice of appeal is filed within the statutory time or, if none, 15 days after the final order, and copies are mailed to all parties with a certificate; an Industrial Accident Board claimant accepting part of an award must say so. The Prothonotary issues a citation requiring the agency to send the certified record within 20","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 72.1. Expedited procedure for appeals on the record","Delaware · civil rules","Deleted.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 73. Preparation of transcript on appeal to the Supreme Court","Delaware · civil rules","On appeals under Supreme Court Rule 7, counsel must follow Supreme Court Rule 9(e)(ii)–(iv) on designating the record, court reporters must follow Rule 9(e) on transcripts, and unrecorded or stipulated proceedings are handled under Supreme Court Rule 9(g).","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 74. Interlocutory appeals to the Supreme Court","Delaware · civil rules","Interlocutory appeals from Superior Court go forward on the terms and procedures of Supreme Court Rule 42.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 75. Certification of questions of law to the Supreme Court","Delaware · civil rules","Certification of questions of law follows Supreme Court Rule 41; a petition asking Superior Court to certify must set out the facts and issues clearly and attach a proposed certification substantially in the form of Supreme Court Official Form K. If certification is ordered, the Prothonotary files 6 certified copies with the Clerk of the Supreme Court within 5 days.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 76. Supreme Court mandate","Delaware · civil rules","When the Supreme Court reverses or modifies a judgment or further proceedings are needed, prevailing counsel prepares and submits an appropriate order, timed on remand so the Superior Court can comply with Supreme Court Rule 19(c).","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 77. Superior Courts; Prothonotaries, records and exhibits, fees","Delaware · civil rules","The Court is always open for business, merits trials are in open court, the Prothonotary mails notice of every order or judgment (lack of notice does not extend appeal time), notifies the Bar of rule amendments within 10 days, and keeps dockets (electronically if desired); exhibits must be removed after the appeal period and may be disposed of if not removed within 15 days of notice, and court reporters keep notes fo","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 78. Motion days; arguments","Delaware · civil rules","Unless the Court orders otherwise, New Castle County motions not before an assigned judge are heard Fridays at 1:30 p.m., Kent County motions Fridays at 11 a.m. (Commissioner motions Thursdays at 2:00 p.m.), and Sussex County motions on the 1st and 3rd Fridays at 11:00 a.m. Motions and responses may not exceed 6 pages, responses are due no later than 4 days before the hearing, and papers must be double-spaced in Time","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 79. Books and records kept by the Prothonotary","Delaware · civil rules","The Prothonotary keeps a docket of all civil actions with consecutive file numbers and chronological entries for every filing, process, order and judgment. Entries for judicial action must show the date and judge for arguments, trials (with elapsed trial time), verdicts and opinions (noting if decided without argument), plus other records the judges or the court administrator require.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 79.1. Electronic filing","Delaware · civil rules","The President Judge designates which civil cases or categories are eFile cases and sets the administrative procedures, and a technology surcharge of $1.25 per document is assessed in each eFile case to run the system. A Delaware lawyer may authorize only firm employees or a retained service provider to eFile for the lawyer, no one may use another’s password, an eFiling by or under a lawyer’s authorization counts as t","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 80. Seal","Delaware · civil rules","The Court’s seal reads “SUPERIOR COURT” on the upper arc and “STATE OF DELAWARE” on the lower arc around arms like those on the Great Seal of the State, and the Prothonotary may affix it to appropriate documents.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 81. Applicability in special proceedings","Delaware · civil rules","In listed special matters—boundaries, change of name, condemnation, contested elections, ditches, dower, drainage, ejectment, escheat, ground rent, habeas corpus, insolvency, mandamus, mills, prohibition, quo warranto, satisfaction of mortgages and judgments, waste, and wrecks—procedure follows these Rules so far as practicable and consistent with statute, and otherwise prior practice; subdivisions (b) and (c) are re","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 82. Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","Delaware · civil rules","The Rules do not extend or limit the Superior Court’s jurisdiction or affect venue.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 83. Rules by district courts","Delaware · civil rules","Omitted.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 84. Forms","Delaware · civil rules","Omitted (court forms are posted at courts.delaware.gov/forms, per the Appendix of Forms note).","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 85. Title","Delaware · civil rules","The rules may be cited as the Superior Court Rules of Civil Procedure.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 86. Effective date","Delaware · civil rules","The Rules took effect January 1, 1948, and the 1965 and 1991 amendment packages took effect January 1, 1965 and January 1, 1991; each applies to new actions and to further proceedings in pending actions unless the Court finds application infeasible or unjust.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 87. Assignment of causes to Family Court","Delaware · civil rules","Transfers of causes to the Family Court are made by a Superior Court judge’s certificate naming the parties, the nature of the matter, and the issues to be decided, and directing the Prothonotary to deliver the certificate and designated pleadings and exhibits to the Family Court clerk; subdivisions (b) and (c) are repealed.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 87.1. Assignment of cases to the Court of Common Pleas","Delaware · civil rules","Transfers to the Court of Common Pleas under 10 Del. C. ch. 13 are made by a certificate signed by a Superior Court judge that includes the caption and directs the Prothonotary to deliver it with designated pleadings and exhibits.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 88. Allowance of attorney’s fee","Delaware · civil rules","Before awarding fees to an appointed attorney or an attorney for a party without funds, the Court requires an affidavit or letter stating any amounts received or to be received from other sources.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 89. Appointment of counsel for State officers and employees","Delaware · civil rules","Appointment of counsel for State officials and employees under 60 Del. Laws c. 474 is governed by Supreme Court Rule 68.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 90. Attorneys","Delaware · civil rules","Except under Rule 90.1, only members of the Delaware Supreme Court Bar who maintain a Delaware office for the practice of law may practice in Superior Court. No attorney may withdraw without court order, withdrawal is ordinarily not grounds for delay, and agreements between attorneys are enforced only if written and filed or stated on the record.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 90.1. Admission pro hac vice","Delaware · civil rules","Out-of-state attorneys may be admitted in the Court’s discretion only on written motion by a Delaware Bar member with a Delaware office (“Delaware Counsel”), filed separately in each court and no later than the applicant’s first appearance, with Delaware Counsel certifying that the applicant is reputable and competent. The applicant certifies good standing elsewhere, being bound by the Delaware Lawyers’ Rules of Prof","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 91-99. [Omitted]","Delaware · civil rules","Rules 91 through 99 are listed as omitted.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 100. Divorce and annulment","Delaware · civil rules","Repealed, effective October 23, 1972.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 101. Process","Delaware · civil rules","Repealed, effective October 15, 1980.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 102. Sheriffs to whom writs shall be issued","Delaware · civil rules","Repealed, effective October 23, 1972.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 103. Mailing of petition","Delaware · civil rules","Repealed, effective October 23, 1972.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 104. Answer in contested divorce","Delaware · civil rules","Repealed, effective October 23, 1972.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 104A. Regular trial days","Delaware · civil rules","Transferred.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 104.1. Regular trial days","Delaware · civil rules","Repealed, effective October 15, 1980.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 105. Necessity of payment of costs","Delaware · civil rules","Repealed, effective October 15, 1980.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 106. Removal of causes from Court of Common Pleas","Delaware · civil rules","A case removed from the Court of Common Pleas proceeds without further process as though a complaint had been served on the date the original process was served.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 107. Briefs","Delaware · civil rules","File the original and a copy of every brief with the Prothonotary (extra copies if more than one judge sits), double-spaced in Times New Roman 14-point with 14-point single-spaced footnotes and two spaces between sentences, on roughly 8½″ x 11″ paper bound on the left, with a cover, tables of contents and citations, a statement of the case (first brief), questions involved, and argument; citations follow the Bluebook","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 108. Sureties","Delaware · civil rules","Each surety company must file, every January, a power of attorney in each county where it does business before the Court will accept it as surety. No attorney or court officer, nor their agents, employees, immediate family, or controlled corporations, may serve as special bail or surety in a case in the Court, except where they are the principal on the bond.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 109. Board of Canvass proceedings","Delaware · civil rules","A complaint asking the Board of Canvass to act under 15 Del. C. § 5702 must be sworn and filed by 12:00 noon on the day the Board convenes (unless the Board extends the time) and state the candidate, office, districts, the fraud or mistake with particularity, the number of votes affected and whether it changes the result, and the basis of knowledge. It may be withdrawn only with the Board’s permission.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 110. Condemnation cases; designation of proposed commissioners and formation of calendar","Delaware · civil rules","Unless ordered otherwise, within 30 days after filing a condemnation complaint the plaintiff submits a blank order for designating proposed commissioners under 10 Del. C. § 6108, and striking must occur no later than 20 days after the order. Once commissioners are struck, the case goes on the condemnation trial calendar for the next term.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 111. Proceeding for an order for the destruction of material in connection with a public health emergency","Delaware · civil rules","Petitions to destroy material or property under 16 Del. C. § 508(c) or 20 Del. C. § 3134 are filed in the county where it is located, after scheduling a hearing that allows at least 5 days’ notice. The verified petition must describe the petitioner’s authority, the property, the public-health need, and all owners and interested persons, with an affidavit of notice and a proposed order; the Court may grant final or in","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 112. Proceedings in forma pauperis","Delaware · civil rules","A party wishing to proceed without prepaying fees must apply with an affidavit, and the Court may waive fees or set a payment schedule, with accrued costs recovered from any later judgment or settlement. If the application is denied, the notice must state the filing fee and a payment date not less than 15 days from the notice to avoid dismissal.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 113. Appointment; removal","Delaware · civil rules","The Court may appoint a Master pro hac vice in any pending case; the appointment is effective when the Court signs the order, and the Master may be removed at the Court’s pleasure.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 114. Duties and powers","Delaware · civil rules","The Master regulates hearings under the order of reference and may administer oaths, examine parties and witnesses, compel attendance and documents, have evidence taken down or taken by commission or deposition, grant adjournments and extensions, and take other necessary steps, all subject to the Court’s revision and control.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 115. Witnesses; documents","Delaware · civil rules","Witnesses, including parties, are summoned before the Master by Prothonotary-issued subpoena to testify or produce documents; disobedience is contempt certified by the Master and enforced by attachment, and witnesses receive the same compensation as for court attendance.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 116. Testimony taken and certified","Delaware · civil rules","Oral testimony before a Master is taken by a stenographer the Master selects and swears, the Master certifies that the testimony was taken in the Master’s presence and is accurate, and the Master fixes the stenographer’s pay subject to court approval.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 117. Objections to testimony; hearing thereon","Delaware · civil rules","The Master rules on competency and admissibility, witnesses must answer as if directed by the Court, irrelevant questions are not answered, and exceptions are unnecessary but grounds must be stated specifically. After proofs close and before the report, a party may bring overruled objections (or exclusions of evidence) to the Court, which may direct further testimony or disregard of testimony.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 118. Persons who may be examined; burden of proof on exceptions to claim","Delaware · civil rules","The Master may examine any party, creditor or claimant orally or by written interrogatories and compel attendance by subpoena. When exceptions are taken to claims against a fund, the claimant bears the burden of proving the claim as filed and may not prove unlisted items without the Master’s order for good cause.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 119. Time for taking testimony","Delaware · civil rules","If the order of reference fixes dates for starting testimony and closing proofs, the Master cannot extend them; otherwise the Master sets a time and place promptly on reasonable notice and proceeds diligently, and any party may ask the Court to order the Master to speed the proceedings and explain delays.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 120. Limiting time for taking testimony","Delaware · civil rules","The Master may set a deadline for each party to close its proofs, extendable for good cause, and then report on the evidence submitted without waiting for a party who misses the deadline.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 121. Minutes of proceedings to be kept","Delaware · civil rules","The Master keeps minutes of all proceedings, noting attorney appearances, adjournments, hearing times and other matters showing how the reference was executed.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 122. Master’s report; action by Court","Delaware · civil rules","The Master circulates a draft report, hears written exceptions within the time the Master sets, and files a final report; exceptions to the final report are due within 10 days after notice of its filing is mailed and are limited to disallowed draft exceptions and changes from the draft. Any party may request a hearing, and the Court reviews the report de novo, on the record in whole or in part.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 123. Inspection of documents","Delaware · civil rules","When documents are ordered or subpoenaed to be produced before a Master, the Master decides which must be produced, when and how long they stay in the Master’s office, or directs inspection by the parties at a time, place and manner the Master sets.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 124. Scope of rules","Delaware · civil rules","Rules 124–131 govern Summary Proceedings for Commercial Disputes, available by written consent in matters within Superior Court jurisdiction (excluding personal, physical or mental injury claims) where the amount in controversy exceeds $100,000 as to at least one party, exclusive of interest and costs, and at least one party is a Delaware citizen or entity. No punitive damages or jury trial are available; the Court m","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 125. Commencement of action","Delaware · civil rules","A Summary Proceeding starts by filing and serving a complaint, sent by next-day delivery to the agreed notice person or each defendant’s principal place of business or residence (plaintiff supplies prepaid envelopes and files proof of receipt), stating prominently on page one that Summary Proceedings are requested, the amount in controversy, the Delaware party, and the defendant’s consent, with the consent document a","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 125.1. Summary proceedings for Commercial Disputes Panel","Delaware · civil rules","The President Judge assigns Summary Proceedings to a judge on the Summary Proceedings for Commercial Disputes Panel, appointed by the President Judge from the Superior Court judges, and the Prothonotary keeps a public list of Panel judges.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 126. Responses to the complaint","Delaware · civil rules","The answer and any compulsory counterclaims are due within 30 days after service, replies to counterclaims within 20 days (each with a list of persons consulted), and cross-claims, permissive counterclaims and third-party claims require all parties’ agreement. A motion to dismiss with brief is due within 30 days (20 days for counterclaims), the answering brief within 15 days, and the reply within 10 days, with openin","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 127. Depositions and discovery","Delaware · civil rules","The plaintiff must serve trial documents, witness lists and persons consulted within 7 days after the answer, and the defendant must reciprocate within 30 days. Each party may serve up to 10 interrogatories and 10 requests for admission (subparts count separately) and document requests within 30 days after the last answer, with interrogatory and admission responses due in 20 days and document responses with productio","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 128. Summary judgment","Delaware · civil rules","No summary judgment motions are allowed in Summary Proceedings.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 129. Optional briefing procedure","Delaware · civil rules","If the parties notify the Court within 7 days after discovery closes that they will forgo trial witnesses, the plaintiff’s brief is due 30 days after the close of discovery, the answering brief 30 days after service, and the reply 15 days after that, citing the record. Affiants must be identified before discovery closes and produced for deposition on request within two weeks after the date discovery would otherwise c","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 130. Trial","Delaware · civil rules","If witnesses are waived, trial is oral argument (or submission on briefs) within one week after briefing closes; otherwise trial begins 30 to 60 days after discovery closes, an agreed pretrial order is due within 30 days after discovery closes, trial is limited to 5 days, and post-trial briefs of up to 50 pages with proposed findings are due within 10 days after trial. The Court decides or gives an estimate within 30","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 131. Application to Court for modification of Rules","Delaware · civil rules","The Summary Proceedings rules, except Rules 129 and 130, may be modified by agreement of all parties with court approval.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 132. Commissioners","Delaware · civil rules","Commissioners may administer oaths, serve as special masters, decide non-dispositive matters, and hear dispositive matters (including Title 16 ch. 50 mental hearings, judgment on the pleadings, summary judgment, and dismissal motions) and propose findings and recommendations to a judge. Objections are due within 10 days after the Commissioner’s order or proposed findings are filed, with responses due 10 days after se","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 133. Single-transaction guardianship; settlement of tort claims for disabled person","Delaware · civil rules","In settling a tort claim for a person with a disability (including a minor), the Court may approve the settlement and disbursements and, for minors, place net funds of a gross settlement of $25,000 or less in a UTMA account, or for larger settlements in a court-approved annuity or structured instrument (with up to $25,000 in a UTMA account), without appointing a property guardian; a guardian is appointed only on good","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 134. Scope of Rules for Mediation","Delaware · civil rules","Rules 134–136 govern court mediation of business disputes under 10 Del. C. § 546, eligible for purely monetary claims only where the amount in controversy exceeds $100,000; the mediator is a sitting judge or commissioner, and parties may change the rules with the mediator’s consent. The rule supplies model consent language for mediation in Superior Court under § 546.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 135. Commencement of Mediation","Delaware · civil rules","Mediation starts with a petition signed by Delaware counsel (as defined in Rule 90.1(a)) and the filing fee, which the Prothonotary sends by next-day delivery; it must state the issues, all parties’ consent, subject-matter jurisdiction, the business-entity and Delaware-connection requirements, that no party is a consumer, and the amount in controversy for monetary claims. The petition is confidential and not publicly","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 136. Mediation Conference","Delaware · civil rules","A representative with settlement authority and Delaware counsel for each party must attend; conferences are private, mediation communications and files are confidential (with listed exceptions), and mediators cannot be compelled to testify and are immune absent bad faith or willful misconduct. Any agreement is written and signed by the parties and mediator; if no agreement is reached the mediator terminates the media","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 137. Arbitration","Delaware · civil rules","Rules 137–139 govern voluntary, binding court arbitration of business disputes under 10 Del. C. § 546, eligible for purely monetary claims only where the amount in controversy exceeds $100,000, before a sitting judge or commissioner who (absent agreement) did not mediate the dispute. Superior Court Rules 26–37 apply unless modified, and the rule defines the preliminary conference, preliminary hearing, scheduling orde","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 138. Commencement of Arbitration","Delaware · civil rules","Arbitration starts with a petition signed by Delaware counsel and the filing fee, sent by the Prothonotary by next-business-day delivery, stating the dispute, parties, claims, remedy, consent, jurisdiction, the business-entity and Delaware-connection requirements, that no party is a consumer, and the amount for monetary claims. The petition is confidential unless appealed; the preliminary conference occurs within 10 ","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 139. Arbitration Hearing","Delaware · civil rules","A representative with authority and Delaware counsel for each party must attend; hearings are private, communications and files confidential (with listed exceptions), and arbitrators immune absent bad faith or willful misconduct. Parties may switch to court mediation or seek the arbitrator’s settlement help at any stage; the arbitrator may grant any just and equitable relief within the parties’ agreement, including i","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 140. Historical Society of the Superior Court of Delaware","Delaware · civil rules","Establishes the Historical Society of the Superior Court of Delaware to preserve and educate about the Court’s history, its members and its administration of justice, made up of the sitting judges and other bench and bar members designated by the President Judge with the concurrence of a majority of the judges.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 141. Scope of Complex Commercial Division Rules","Delaware · civil rules","Effective March 20, 2026, Rules 141–146 govern cases in the Complex Commercial Litigation Division (CCLD), which takes cases with a claim of $1 million or more, cases involving an exclusive choice-of-court agreement or a judgment based on one, or cases designated by the President Judge; personal-injury, mortgage foreclosure, mechanics’ lien, and condemnation cases, and choice-of-court cases involving consumers or emp","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 142. Public Access to Documents Filed with the Court in CCLD Actions","Delaware · civil rules","Replacing Rule 5(g) in CCLD cases, filed documents are public unless they contain Confidential Information (confidentially maintained, not public, would cause particularized harm outweighing the public interest, such as trade secrets, medical records, Social Security and account numbers, birth dates and minors’ names), which may be filed as a Confidential Filing with a prescribed cover page and footer. The filer must","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 143. Amended and Supplemental Pleadings in CCLD","Delaware · civil rules","In CCLD cases a party may amend once as of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 20 days after service if no response is required and the case is not set for trial; otherwise written consent or leave (freely given) is needed, the amended pleading must be filed with a document showing how it differs, and amendments do not affect others’ counterclaims, crossclaims or third-party claims. A plaintiff f","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 144. Additional Procedures for Filing Discovery Motions in CCLD","Delaware · civil rules","In CCLD cases, motions under Rules 26(c), 26(d) and 37 (except those during an oral deposition) must include counsel’s certification of a reasonable effort to agree, detailing dates, time spent and method, which must include oral communication between Delaware counsel for the moving and opposing parties; Rule 37(d) motions are exempt. Failure to certify may result in dismissal of the motion unless the Court orders ot","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 145. CCLD Motions","Delaware · civil rules","CCLD requests for orders are made by written motion (unless at a hearing or trial) with numbered paragraphs and, preferably, a proposed order. A motion supported by an opening brief is limited to 500 words, and briefs are permitted only with motions under Rules 12, 41 and 56 (briefed under Rule 146); other motions and oppositions are limited to 3,000 words and replies to 2,000 words, excluding caption, title, signatu","#st-de-rules"],["Rule 146. CCLD Briefs","Delaware · civil rules","Rule 107 governs the form of CCLD briefs. Unless the parties agree in writing or the Court orders otherwise, the responsive brief is due within 30 days after the opening brief and the reply within 15 days after the response, and any party may request a hearing after the opening brief is filed, which the Court may grant or decide without.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 1. Court of Chancery: Scope and Purpose","Delaware · civil rules","The rules govern Chancery procedure and are construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every proceeding.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 3. Court of Chancery: Commencing an Action; Deposits, Fees","Delaware · civil rules","An action starts with a complaint plus a supplemental information sheet; listed pleadings must be verified; deposits, the technology surcharge, and the security fee apply.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 4. Court of Chancery: Process","Delaware · civil rules","Covers summonses and service, including the statement required for consent-statute service (6 Del. C. §§ 15-114, 17-109, 18-109; 10 Del. C. § 3114) and seizure orders under 10 Del. C. § 365.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 5. Court of Chancery: Service and Filing; Appearance and Withdrawal","Delaware · civil rules","Sets electronic service and filing, the 5:00 p.m. and midnight deadlines, notices of discovery service, appearances, withdrawal, and redaction of personal identifiers.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 5.1. Court of Chancery: Public Access; Confidential Filings","Delaware · civil rules","Defines Confidential Information and sets the confidential filing, notice, designation, public-version, challenge, and 3-year expiration procedures.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 6. Court of Chancery: Computing and Extending Time","Delaware · civil rules","FRCP-style counting that excludes weekends and holidays for periods under 11 days, adds 3 days for mail-only service, and bars extending Rule 59 deadlines.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 7. Court of Chancery: Pleadings; Motions; Briefs; Letters; Compendia","Delaware · civil rules","Lists the permitted pleadings, requires answers to repeat each allegation, sets the motion, brief, and letter word limits, and authorizes compendia.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 10. Court of Chancery: Caption; Form of Filings","Delaware · civil rules","Sets the caption, typeface, spacing, margins, and Blue Book/Yellow Book citation rules, and requires a word-count certification in the signature block.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 11. Court of Chancery: Signing Pleadings; Sanctions","Delaware · civil rules","FRCP 11-style certification and sanctions; a sanctions motion is served but may not be filed if the challenged paper is withdrawn or corrected within 21 days after service (or another time the Court sets).","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 12. Court of Chancery: Defenses and Objections","Delaware · civil rules","An answer is due within 20 days after service (10 days after the Court acts on a Rule 12 motion); a motion for a more definite statement is answered within 10 days and a motion to strike is due within 20 days; lists the Rule 12(b) defenses.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 15. Court of Chancery: Amended and Supplemental Pleadings","Delaware · civil rules","Amendment as of course or by leave; a blackline is required; Rule 15(a)(5) is the amend-or-stand rule (formerly 15(aa)); relation back uses a 120-day notice period.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 17. Court of Chancery: Real Party in Interest; Capacity","Delaware · civil rules","Real party in interest and capacity to sue (amended effective Sept. 25, 2023).","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 23. Court of Chancery: Class Actions","Delaware · civil rules","Certification prerequisites and types, the 10-day representative affidavit, class counsel, Court-approved settlements and dismissals, and fee affidavits.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 23.1. Court of Chancery: Derivative Actions","Delaware · civil rules","Particularized pleading of demand efforts or excuse, standing, the 10-day affidavit, counsel selection, settlement approval, and fee affidavits.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 23.2. Court of Chancery: Unincorporated Associations","Delaware · civil rules","Suits by or against association members through a representative; requires Court approval of dismissals and settlements.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 40. Court of Chancery: Call of Calendar; Continuances","Delaware · civil rules","Annual calendar call to review delay; continuance motions for an absent witness need an affidavit.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 41. Court of Chancery: Dismissal of Actions","Delaware · civil rules","Voluntary dismissal is limited by Rules 15(a)(5), 23, and 23.1; covers involuntary dismissal and dismissal after 1 year of inaction.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 45. Court of Chancery: Subpoena","Delaware · civil rules","Subpoenas for testimony and documents; returns are e-filed as the “Subpoena” document type.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 54. Court of Chancery: Judgment; Costs","Delaware · civil rules","Defines judgment, provides for Rule 54(b) partial judgments, and awards costs to the prevailing party (rewritten effective June 1, 2026).","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 55. Court of Chancery: Default; Default Judgment","Delaware · civil rules","Default admits the allegations; 10 days’ notice of the application is required if the defaulting party has appeared.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 56. Court of Chancery: Summary Judgment","Delaware · civil rules","A claimant may move after 20 days; cross-motions without a disputed fact may be deemed a stipulation for decision on the record.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 57. Court of Chancery: Declaratory Judgments","Delaware · civil rules","Procedure under 10 Del. C. § 6501; the Court may order a speedy hearing.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 58. Court of Chancery: Form of Judgment","Delaware · civil rules","Except under Rule 54(b), a final order constitutes the judgment.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 59. Court of Chancery: New Trial; Alter or Amend; Reargument","Delaware · civil rules","10 days for a new trial or to alter or amend; 5 days for reargument or clarification; a timely motion suspends finality.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 60. Court of Chancery: Corrections; Relief from Judgment","Delaware · civil rules","Corrects clerical errors (with Supreme Court leave once an appeal is docketed) and gives FRCP 60(b)-style relief; the text says only that a Rule 60(b) motion must be made “within a reasonable time” (no separate one-year cap appears), and the motion does not affect finality.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 62. Court of Chancery: Stay Pending Appeal","Delaware · civil rules","30-day automatic stay with exceptions; bonded stay of money judgments; Supreme Court Rules and Del. Const. art. IV, § 24 apply.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 65. Court of Chancery: Injunctions and Restraining Orders","Delaware · civil rules","Requires an affidavit or verified pleading; a TRO issued without notice lasts up to 10 days, extendable by 30; security is required.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 67. Court of Chancery: Deposit in Court","Delaware · civil rules","Funds go to a Delaware-present bank with at least $50 million in capital and surplus, in an interest-bearing account.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 69. Court of Chancery: Execution","Delaware · civil rules","Writs of execution in Superior Court form, executed by the sheriff, plus discovery in aid of execution.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 70. Court of Chancery: Enforcing an Order for a Specific Act","Delaware · civil rules","Substitute performance, vesting of title, writs, and contempt.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 72. Court of Chancery: Appeals, Certifications and Mandates","Delaware · civil rules","Motions to certify interlocutory appeals or questions of law to the Supreme Court; handling of the mandate.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 77. Court of Chancery: Court and Register Open; Notice of Orders","Delaware · civil rules","Court is always open for filing; lack of service of an order does not extend appeal time; remote trials are allowed.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 79. Court of Chancery: The Docket","Delaware · civil rules","Electronic docket and entries for judicial actions; Rules 79.1 and 79.2 (old e-filing rules) are omitted.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 81. Court of Chancery: Corporate Election by Magistrate","Delaware · civil rules","A Magistrate may run a director election, with a stockholder list 10 days before and notice at least 20 days before.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 83. Court of Chancery: In Forma Pauperis","Delaware · civil rules","Affidavit under 10 Del. C. §§ 8802–8804; after a denial, at least 15 days to pay.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 86. Court of Chancery: Effective Date of Amendments","Delaware · civil rules","Amendments apply to new cases and to pending cases unless infeasible or unjust.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 88. Court of Chancery: Expense Awards","Delaware · civil rules","A motion to quantify a fee or expense award requires a detailed affidavit of rates, hours, and costs.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 90. Court of Chancery: Civil Miscellaneous Access","Delaware · civil rules","Guardianship and other C.M. matters are confidential; responses to access motions are due within 11 days.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 91. Court of Chancery: Technology Disputes at Law","Delaware · civil rules","Monetary-only technology disputes are heard only if the amount in controversy exceeds $1 million.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 92. Court of Chancery: Consent to Litigate","Delaware · civil rules","Prescribed consent language for 10 Del. C. § 346 technology litigation, with jury waiver.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 93. Court of Chancery: Business and Technology Mediation","Delaware · civil rules","Mediation under 10 Del. C. §§ 346–347; monetary-only claims must exceed $1 million.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 144. Court of Chancery: Magistrate in Chancery Reports","Delaware · civil rules","Reports, draft reports, de novo review, the 11-day/3-day exceptions schedules, and §§ 350–351 stipulations.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 170. Court of Chancery: Attorneys","Delaware · civil rules","Admission, pro hac vice motion by Delaware counsel, the applicant’s certification and assessment, and Delaware counsel’s duties.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 171. Court of Chancery: Briefs","Delaware · civil rules","Repealed effective Sept. 25, 2023; content transferred to Rules 7 and 10.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 171A. Court of Chancery: CD-ROM Briefs","Delaware · civil rules","Optional hyperlinked CD-ROM briefs filed within 10 days after the official brief.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 173. Court of Chancery: Application for Argument or Trial; Emergencies","Delaware · civil rules","Hearing requests go to the assigned judge; emergencies go first to the assigned judge, then to the Chancellor.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 174. Court of Chancery: Mediation","Delaware · civil rules","Court-annexed voluntary mediation before judicial or non-judicial mediators.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 178A. Court of Chancery: Petition Contrary to Advance Health-Care Directive","Delaware · civil rules","Revised effective June 1, 2026: verified petition, two physicians’ affidavits, and a 13-day notice and objection period.","#st-de-rules"],["Rule Ch. R. 180-B. Court of Chancery: Powers and Duties of Guardian","Delaware · civil rules","Revised effective June 1, 2026: covers Chapter 39A guardians; annual status report due the first business day of the appointment quarter.","#st-de-rules"],["Appeals in Delaware: one appellate court, short fuses, no extensions","Delaware · appeals","Delaware has no intermediate appellate court. Final judgments of the Court of Chancery and the Superior Court go directly to the Supreme Court of Delaware, which hears most cases in panels of three Justices. The notice of appeal is due within 30 days after the judgment is entered on the docket, and the Supreme Court cannot extend that time. Interlocutory appeals require a certification application in the trial court ","#st-de-appeals"],["Notice of appeal: 30 days from docket entry","Delaware · appeals · Starting the appeal","In a civil case, file the notice of appeal with the Clerk of the Supreme Court within 30 days after the judgment, order or decree is entered on the docket. The statutes measure the time from the date of the judgment of the Court of Chancery or Superior Court. The time to appeal or cross-appeal cannot be enlarged. The notice must name the court and judge below, the parties and their counsel, and the order appealed fro","#st-de-appeals"],["Filing fee, disclosure statement, e-filing","Delaware · appeals · Starting the appeal","The filing fee is $500 plus a $10 Court Security Assessment, and it is nonrefundable. Each party must file a Disclosure of Corporate Affiliations and Financial Interest (Form P) within 15 days after notice that the appeal has been docketed. Appeals of cases e-filed below are e-filed in the Supreme Court, with a $1.50 per-document technology surcharge, and an e-filing counts as the lawyer’s signature. Supr. Ct. R. 7(g","#st-de-appeals"],["Delaware counsel must sign and attend","Delaware · appeals · Starting the appeal","Except for self-represented parties, every paper must be signed by an active Delaware Bar member who keeps a bona fide office in Delaware, and that lawyer or a firm colleague must attend all proceedings. Out-of-state counsel must be admitted pro hac vice under Rule 71; the motion is filed by Delaware counsel no later than the first appearance. Supr. Ct. R. 12(a), (d), 71","#st-de-appeals"],["Only issues raised below","Delaware · appeals · Starting the appeal","Only questions fairly presented to the trial court may be raised on appeal, although the Court may consider others when the interests of justice require. Supr. Ct. R. 8","#st-de-appeals"],["Rule 59 motions delay finality; Rule 60 motions do not","Delaware · appeals · Tolling motions in the trial court","In Chancery, a timely motion for a new trial, to alter or amend, or for reargument makes the judgment not final until the Court rules. A Rule 60(b) motion does not affect finality in either trial court. The Supreme Court rules do not themselves list tolling motions; Rule 7(c)(3) only requires a late notice of appeal to explain why the time was tolled. File any Rule 59 motion within its own short deadline. Ct. Ch. R. ","#st-de-appeals"],["Trial-court deadlines are short","Delaware · appeals · Tolling motions in the trial court","In the Superior Court, a new-trial motion and a motion to alter or amend are due within 10 days after entry of judgment, and reargument within 5 days after the decision is filed. In Chancery, a new-trial or alter-or-amend motion is due within 10 days, and reargument or clarification within 5 days after the ruling, with no reply. Neither court can extend these times except as Rule 59 allows. Super. Ct. Civ. R. 6(b), 5","#st-de-appeals"],["15 days after the first notice, or 30 days after judgment, whichever is later","Delaware · appeals · Cross-appeals","Any party may cross-appeal from any appealable judgment or order. In a civil case, a cross-appeal is timely if filed within 15 days after the first notice of appeal was filed, or within 30 days after entry of the judgment, whichever is later. In the briefing, the party who first filed a notice of appeal is treated as the appellant. Supr. Ct. R. 6(b)(i), 7(b), 15(a)(iv); 10 Del. C. § 149","#st-de-appeals"],["Briefing schedule","Delaware · appeals · Record and briefing","If no transcript is designated, the opening brief and appendix are due 45 days after the notice of appeal; otherwise they are due 30 days after the record is filed. The answering brief is due 30 days after service of the opening brief. The reply is due 15 days later, or 30 days if it also answers a cross-appeal; the cross-appellee’s reply on the cross-appeal is due 10 days after that. Other argumentative filings requ","#st-de-appeals"],["Extensions are disfavored and strictly limited","Delaware · appeals · Record and briefing","A motion to extend must be docketed at least 5 days before the due date and state the opponent’s position. The Clerk may grant an unopposed first extension of 3 days. Further extensions require exceptional circumstances (serious illness, a death in the immediate family, an act of God, an emergency, or similar) and are capped at 15 days total for opening and answering briefs and 10 days for replies. Late briefs will n","#st-de-appeals"],["Word limits and format","Delaware · appeals · Record and briefing","Opening and answering briefs are limited to 10,000 words and replies to 5,500. With a cross-appeal, the appellee’s combined answering/opening brief may run 14,000 words, the appellant’s reply/answering brief 10,000, and the cross-appeal reply 5,500. A certificate of compliance stating the word count is required. Text, including footnotes, must be in Times New Roman 14-point, double-spaced, with 1-inch margins; footno","#st-de-appeals"],["Argument","Delaware · appeals · Record and briefing","Oral argument is held only in cases the Court designates; other cases are submitted on the briefs. Each side gets 20 minutes before a panel and 25 minutes before the Court en banc. A copy of any case decided after briefing must be sent to the Court and opponents at least 48 hours before argument. Supr. Ct. R. 16(a), (f), (g)","#st-de-appeals"],["Reargument: 15 days","Delaware · appeals · Rehearing and further review","A motion for reargument may be filed within 15 days after the opinion or order is filed. It must state its grounds succinctly and carry a good-faith certificate. No answer is allowed unless the Court requests one, and there is no oral argument. No reargument is available when the mandate issues forthwith, or from orders under Rules 41 and 42. Supr. Ct. R. 18","#st-de-appeals"],["Rehearing en banc after a panel decision","Delaware · appeals · Rehearing and further review","A unanimous three-Justice panel decision is the decision of the Court. A motion for rehearing en banc may be filed within 15 days, unless the panel ordered the mandate to issue forthwith. Grounds are a question of exceptional importance, the need for uniformity, or a prior decision that should be reconsidered. A non-unanimous panel automatically sends the case to the Court en banc. Supr. Ct. R. 4(d)–(f)","#st-de-appeals"],["No further state review; mandate","Delaware · appeals · Rehearing and further review","The Supreme Court is the court of last resort, so there is no further state review. The mandate issues when the time for reargument or rehearing expires, or after the Court rules on such a motion. Supr. Ct. R. 19(a)","#st-de-appeals"],["Certified questions","Delaware · appeals · Rehearing and further review","Delaware trial courts, federal courts, other states’ highest courts and the SEC may certify questions of law before final judgment. The Court accepts a question only for important and urgent reasons and only if the material facts are undisputed. Supr. Ct. R. 41","#st-de-appeals"],["Two-step certification under Rule 42","Delaware · appeals · Interlocutory appeals","First, apply to the trial court for certification within 10 days after entry of the order. The opponent responds within 10 days, and the trial court rules within 10 days after the response, or within 20 days of the application if there is no response. Second, file the notice of appeal in the Supreme Court within 30 days after entry of the order, even if the trial court has not yet ruled, followed by a supplementary n","#st-de-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP denial: appeal as of right in 10 days","Delaware · appeals · Interlocutory appeals","An order denying a UPEPA special motion in whole or in part is appealable as of right by the movant, but the appeal must be filed within 10 days after the order is entered. All proceedings are stayed while that appeal is pending. 10 Del. C. §§ 6004(c), 6009","#st-de-appeals"],["Partial judgments","Delaware · appeals · Interlocutory appeals","A judgment on fewer than all claims or parties is appealable only if the trial court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay. Ct. Ch. R. 54(b); Super. Ct. Civ. R. 54(b)","#st-de-appeals"],["Automatic stays below are short","Delaware · appeals · Stays pending appeal","Execution is stayed for 10 days after judgment in the Superior Court. In Chancery, it is stayed for 30 days, except for expedited or summary proceedings, equitable relief and Rule 54(b) judgments. A longer stay pending appeal requires security under Article IV, § 24 of the Delaware Constitution, with applications processed under Supreme Court Rule 32. Super. Ct. Civ. R. 62; Ct. Ch. R. 62(a)–(e); Supr. Ct. R. 32, 42(e","#st-de-appeals"],["What is a final, appealable judgment","Delaware · appeals · Appeals from the Court of Chancery","A “judgment” includes any order from which an appeal lies, and except under Rule 54(b) a final order is the judgment. A partial judgment on fewer than all claims or parties requires an express determination that there is no just reason for delay. Otherwise, such an order can be revised at any time before final judgment. Ct. Ch. R. 54(a)–(b), 58","#st-de-appeals"],["Interlocutory appeal and certified questions","Delaware · appeals · Appeals from the Court of Chancery","A party may move in the Court of Chancery to certify a question of law or an interlocutory appeal to the Delaware Supreme Court. The motion must comply with the applicable Supreme Court rules (Supreme Court Rule 42 for interlocutory appeals) and include a proposed form of order. The appeal itself is filed in the Supreme Court, and the eFiling guide notes a $500 Register appeal fee that must be paid before the record ","#st-de-appeals"],["Post-judgment motions","Delaware · appeals · Appeals from the Court of Chancery","A motion for a new trial or other post-trial action, or to alter or amend the judgment, is due no later than 10 days after entry of judgment. Reargument or clarification must be sought within five days after the ruling, with any opposition due within five days of the motion. A timely Rule 59 motion keeps the judgment from being final until the Court rules on it, while a Rule 60(b) motion does not affect finality. Ct.","#st-de-appeals"],["Stays pending appeal","Delaware · appeals · Appeals from the Court of Chancery","Execution on a judgment is automatically stayed for 30 days after entry, except in expedited or summary proceedings, orders awarding equitable relief, and Rule 54(b) judgments. A money judgment is stayed once the Court approves a bond or other security. Applications for a stay or injunction pending appeal must comply with the Supreme Court Rules and, for security, Article IV, § 24 of the Delaware Constitution. Ct. Ch","#st-de-appeals"],["Appeal timing traps","Delaware · appeals · Appeals from the Court of Chancery","Not receiving a copy of an order does not extend the time to appeal. Once an appeal is docketed, clerical mistakes may be corrected only with the Supreme Court’s leave. Ct. Ch. R. 77(d)(2), 60(a)","#st-de-appeals"],["Magistrate decisions and appeal waivers","Delaware · appeals · Appeals from the Court of Chancery","The parties may stipulate under 10 Del. C. § 350 to final adjudication by a Magistrate in Chancery, which is appealed like a decision of the Chancellor or a Vice Chancellor. Under § 351, they may instead stipulate that the Magistrate’s Report is final and not subject to appeal. Otherwise, Reports become orders of the Court only through adoption or the exceptions process. Ct. Ch. R. 144(c)(2), 144(g)","#st-de-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (civil, final judgment)","Delaware · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the judgment on the docket.\nCannot be enlarged (Supr. Ct. R. 11(b)). Supr. Ct. R. 6(a)(i); 10 Del. C. §§ 145, 148","#st-de-appeals"],["Cross-appeal","Delaware · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or 30 days from judgment, whichever is later). Supr. Ct. R. 6(b)(i); 10 Del. C. § 149","#st-de-appeals"],["Challenge to a Delaware Rapid Arbitration Act final award","Delaware · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from issuance of the final award.\nCross-challenge: 7 days after the first notice is served, or 15 days after the award, whichever is later. Supr. Ct. R. 6(a)(ii)","#st-de-appeals"],["Application to certify an interlocutory appeal (trial court)","Delaware · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of the interlocutory order.\nThe trial court may allow longer for good cause. Supr. Ct. R. 42(c)(i)","#st-de-appeals"],["Notice of interlocutory appeal (Supreme Court)","Delaware · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the interlocutory order.\nFile even if the trial court has not ruled on certification. Supr. Ct. R. 42(d)(i); 10 Del. C. § 143","#st-de-appeals"],["Appeal from denial of anti-SLAPP (UPEPA) motion","Delaware · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of the order.\nApplies to actions filed or claims asserted on or after Sept. 15, 2025. 10 Del. C. § 6009","#st-de-appeals"],["Disclosure of corporate affiliations (Form P)","Delaware · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from notice of docketing of the appeal. Supr. Ct. R. 7(g)","#st-de-appeals"],["Opening brief and appendix","Delaware · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the record (or 45 days after the notice of appeal if no transcript is designated). Supr. Ct. R. 15(a)(i)","#st-de-appeals"],["Answering brief","Delaware · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the opening brief. Supr. Ct. R. 15(a)(ii)","#st-de-appeals"],["Reply brief","Delaware · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from service of the answering brief.\n30 days if the reply also answers a cross-appeal; cross-appellee’s reply 10 days after that. Supr. Ct. R. 15(a)(iii)","#st-de-appeals"],["Motion to extend a briefing deadline","Delaware · appeals · Deadlines","5 days from before the due date (must be docketed at least 5 days in advance).\nCounted backward from the due date. Supr. Ct. R. 15(b)(i)","#st-de-appeals"],["Motion for reargument (Supreme Court)","Delaware · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the opinion or order. Supr. Ct. R. 18","#st-de-appeals"],["Motion for rehearing en banc","Delaware · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the panel’s opinion or order.\nNot available if the panel ordered the mandate to issue forthwith. Supr. Ct. R. 4(f)","#st-de-appeals"],["Reargument in the trial court (Superior Court or Chancery)","Delaware · appeals · Deadlines","5 days from filing of the decision. Super. Ct. Civ. R. 59(e); Ct. Ch. R. 59(c)","#st-de-appeals"],["New trial or motion to alter or amend (trial court)","Delaware · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of judgment.\nNot the federal 28 days. Super. Ct. Civ. R. 59(b), (d); Ct. Ch. R. 59(a)–(b)","#st-de-appeals"],["Supreme Court of Georgia","Georgia · courts","Court of last resort. Hears direct appeals in cases within its constitutional jurisdiction (for example, constitutional questions raised and ruled on below, death-penalty cases, election contests), reviews Court of Appeals decisions by discretionary certiorari, answers certified questions from federal and other state courts, and regulates admission, discipline, and pro hac vice in its own court. Files through the man","#st-ga"],["Court of Appeals of Georgia","Georgia · courts","Hears appeals and applications for appeal not within the Supreme Court’s jurisdiction; misfiled appeals and applications are transferred between the two courts. Sits in divisions of three judges, with en banc review by rule. Three terms a year (December, April, August); a case must be decided by the end of the term after the one to which it is docketed. Files through the mandatory eFast system.","#st-ga"],["Superior Courts","Georgia · courts","General-jurisdiction trial courts, organized by judicial circuit (one or more counties). Governed statewide by the Uniform Superior Court Rules (USCR); local rules were repealed and courts may add only standing orders that do not conflict, with actual notice to the parties.","#st-ga"],["State Courts","Georgia · courts","County-level courts of limited jurisdiction (civil cases and misdemeanors) in counties that have them. Governed by the Uniform State Court Rules, which largely track the USCR (the USCR text cross-refers to State Court Rules 6.2, 8.3, 36.1 and others where they differ).","#st-ga"],["Georgia State-wide Business Court","Georgia · courts","Statewide court for complex business cases with its own rules (posted by the Supreme Court as the State-wide Business Court Rules, June 11, 2026 version) and its own pro hac vice rule (Rule 4-4). Its rules were not collected.","#st-ga"],["Probate, Juvenile, Magistrate, and Municipal Courts","Georgia · courts","Each class has its own uniform rules posted by the Supreme Court. Pro hac vice in magistrate court is under Uniform Magistrate Court Rule 7.5 and in probate court under Uniform Probate Court Rule 3.3.3 (per the State Bar’s pro hac vice page; those rule texts were not collected).","#st-ga"],["Georgia has an anti-SLAPP statute: O.C.G.A. § 9-11-11.1","Georgia · anti-SLAPP","The official 2016 legislative summary describes Act 420 (HB 513), effective July 1, 2016, as revising the procedure for claims arising from acts that could be construed as in furtherance of the right of free speech, allowing a motion to strike, revising a definition, adding exceptions, and allowing a direct appeal from an order or judgment on such a claim or motion. It amended O.C.G.A. §§ 5-6-34, 9-11-11.1, and 51-5-","#st-ga-slapp"],["Procedure, deadlines, discovery stay, and fees: confirm in the code","Georgia · anti-SLAPP","Not verified from collected text. Practitioners commonly describe § 9-11-11.1 as requiring the special motion to strike to be filed within a short period after service of the complaint, staying discovery while the motion is pending, requiring a prompt hearing, and awarding attorney’s fees to a prevailing moving defendant. Treat every one of those points (and any day count) as unconfirmed until checked against the cur","#st-ga-slapp"],["Appeal: direct appeal of anti-SLAPP rulings","Georgia · anti-SLAPP","Per the 2016 summary, the Act amended O.C.G.A. § 5-6-34 to allow a direct appeal of an order or judgment on an anti-SLAPP claim or motion, so no certificate of immediate review or interlocutory application should be needed. Confirm the current wording of § 5-6-34, which was amended again in 2025 (HB 176, SB 298, HB 155). O.C.G.A. § 5-6-34 (as amended by 2016 Act 420); 2025 Legislative Counsel summary","#st-ga-slapp"],["Pro hac vice in superior, state, and magistrate courts: USCR 4.4 and a Georgia sponsor of record","Georgia · out of state","An eligible “Domestic Lawyer” (admitted elsewhere in the U.S., not disbarred or suspended anywhere) or “Foreign Lawyer” files a verified application with the court where the case is pending, serves it on all parties who have appeared and on the State Bar’s Office of General Counsel, and includes proof of service. The court may grant it summarily if no one objects; the Office of General Counsel or a party may object w","#st-ga-oos"],["Pro hac vice fees: $75 per application, $200 per year, January 15 deadline","Georgia · out of state","Superior court: a non-refundable $75 fee for each application, payable to the State Bar of Georgia when filed, plus an annual fee of $200 (no matter how many Georgia admissions) on first admission and by January 15 of each later year while admitted. A pro bono exemption applies to lawyers who charge no fee and work with a pro bono or nonprofit legal services program in a civil case, or for an indigent defendant in a ","#st-ga-oos"],["Grounds to deny: “regular practice” and prior appearances without approval","Georgia · out of state","Applications ordinarily should be granted unless admission may harm the administration of justice or other parties’ interests, the client may be inadequately represented, the applicant appears so often that it amounts to regular practice in Georgia, or the applicant previously filed or appeared in a Georgia court without approval under the USCR. Admission may be revoked on the same grounds. Eligibility also requires ","#st-ga-oos"],["Supreme Court of Georgia: apply to the State Bar first, with local counsel","Georgia · out of state","A non-resident lawyer admitted in the highest court of another state, territory, or D.C. must first file a pro hac vice application with the State Bar. An application must also be filed in the Supreme Court (if the Bar has not yet approved, contemporaneously with the first filing). It must be served on all parties and include a certificate of good standing from every admitting jurisdiction issued no more than 30 days","#st-ga-oos"],["Court of Appeals: separate application and a $200 check to the Georgia Bar Foundation","Georgia · out of state","An out-of-state lawyer must file a pro hac vice application before appearing in a single case, served on all parties, with a current certificate of good standing from the home jurisdiction’s highest court, business address, email, phone, and the party represented. The application may be made by the lawyer or by a member of the Court of Appeals bar. Each application requires a $200 check payable to “IOLTA/Georgia Bar ","#st-ga-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances must be filed as a “Limited Appearance”","Georgia · out of state","No attorney may undertake limited representation in superior court without first filing a separate entry titled “Limited Appearance” that complies with USCR 4.2 and states the limited purpose and duration. Within 5 days after the limited work ends, file a notice that it has ended (with the client’s last known mailing and email address) and serve the client, opposing counsel or parties, and the assigned judge. Without","#st-ga-oos"],["Entry of appearance: bar number and State-Bar-registered email on every pleading","Georgia · out of state","No attorney may appear in superior court until filing a signed entry of appearance or signed pleading stating the case style and number, the party represented, and the attorney’s name, State Bar number, office address, phone, fax, and the email address registered with the State Bar of Georgia. Within 48 hours after being retained, mail to the court and opposing counsel or file the entry of appearance (late filing doe","#st-ga-oos"],["E-filing access","Georgia · out of state","Superior courts: e-filing is available when required by law and otherwise where adopted under Judicial Council standards; registered filers who opt into electronic service are deemed served on filing; a filing is presumed filed when the e-filing service provider receives it and confirms receipt. Statewide mandatory superior-court e-filing is statutory (O.C.G.A. § 15-6-11, not collected).\nAppellate courts: e-filing is","#st-ga-oos"],["Temporary practice without admission (Rule 5.5)","Georgia · out of state","A Domestic Lawyer not disbarred or suspended may provide temporary services in Georgia in association with an actively participating Georgia lawyer, in connection with a proceeding where the lawyer is or expects to be authorized to appear, in connection with arbitration or mediation related to the home practice (unless the forum requires pro hac vice), or otherwise arising from the home practice. In-house work for an","#st-ga-oos"],["Full admission: on motion (reciprocity) or the Attorneys’ Examination","Georgia · out of state","Admission on motion without exam requires, among other things, an ABA-approved J.D. or LL.B., admission by exam in a U.S. jurisdiction that has reciprocity with Georgia (with the stricter rules of that jurisdiction applied to its applicants), active practice for five of the seven years before applying, good standing everywhere admitted, no prior Georgia exam failure or fitness denial, and fitness certification. Fees:","#st-ga-oos"],["Governing code: Georgia Rules of Professional Conduct, Bar Rule 4-102(d), each with a stated maximum penalty","Georgia · ethics","Georgia’s rules are published as part of Bar Rule 4-102 and follow the ABA Model Rules numbering, but each rule ends with its own maximum penalty (for example, disbarment for Rule 1.6, public reprimand for Rule 1.5). Levels of discipline run from formal letter of admonition and confidential reprimand to State Disciplinary Review Board reprimand, public reprimand (administered by a superior court judge in open court),","#st-ga-ethics"],["Confidentiality is broader, and disclosure exceptions differ (Rule 1.6)","Georgia · ethics","Rule 1.6(a) protects “all information gained in the professional relationship,” expressly including information the client asked to keep private or whose disclosure would be embarrassing or likely detrimental. Permissive disclosure covers preventing harm or substantial financial loss from client or third-party criminal conduct, and serious injury or death. If the client has already acted, disclosure is allowed only i","#st-ga-ethics"],["No screening cure for lateral hires (Rule 1.10)","Georgia · ethics","Georgia Rule 1.10(a) imputes a lawyer’s conflicts under Rules 1.7, 1.8(c), 1.9, and 2.2 to everyone in the firm, with no paragraph allowing a screen to avoid imputation of a lateral lawyer’s former-client conflict (unlike ABA Model Rule 1.10(a)(2)). The only listed cure is client waiver under Rule 1.7. Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 1.10(a)–(c)","#st-ga-ethics"],["Prohibited transactions: related-lawyer rule kept; no humanitarian-aid exception","Georgia · ethics","Rule 1.8(e) allows advancing costs (repayment contingent on outcome) or paying costs for an indigent client, and nothing else; there is no exception for modest living expenses for pro bono clients. Rule 1.8(i) still bars a lawyer from representing a client directly adverse to someone the lawyer knows is represented by the lawyer’s parent, grandparent, child, grandchild, sibling, or spouse without informed consent (pe","#st-ga-ethics"],["Fee arbitration clauses must be separately initialed (Rule 1.5(b))","Georgia · ethics","A lawyer may include an agreement to arbitrate fee disputes only with the client’s informed consent in a writing signed by the client; the clause and the lawyer’s disclosures must be a separate paragraph, in a font at least as large as the rest of the contract, initialed by both client and lawyer. Contingent-fee agreements must be in writing and say whether expenses come out before or after the fee; at the end the la","#st-ga-ethics"],["Rule 3.4 differs from the ABA text","Georgia · ethics","Georgia reserves the slots for ABA Rule 3.4(c) (knowingly disobeying a tribunal rule), (d) (frivolous discovery), and (e) (improper trial references). It adds a ban on using evidence-gathering methods that violate the opposing party’s or counsel’s legal rights, and on presenting or threatening criminal charges solely to gain an advantage in a civil matter. Maximum penalty: disbarment. Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 3.4","#st-ga-ethics"],["Misconduct: felony conviction is itself a violation; no ABA 8.4(d) or (g) paragraph","Georgia · ethics","Rule 8.4(a) makes it misconduct to be convicted of a felony, or of a moral-turpitude misdemeanor related to fitness; to fail to pay within ten days a final judgment for money the lawyer collected as a lawyer; and to commit a fitness-related criminal act the lawyer has admitted in judicio. First-offender probation and nolo pleas count as convictions. The Georgia text has no paragraph on conduct prejudicial to the admi","#st-ga-ethics"],["Reporting another lawyer’s misconduct is only a “should” (Rule 8.3)","Georgia · ethics","A lawyer who knows of another lawyer’s violation raising a substantial question about honesty or fitness “should” inform the appropriate authority, and the rule states that there is no disciplinary penalty for violating it. Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 8.3","#st-ga-ethics"],["Self-reporting within 60 days (Rule 9.1)","Georgia · ethics","Georgia lawyers must notify the State Bar within 60 days of admission in another jurisdiction, of any felony or fitness-related moral-turpitude misdemeanor conviction, and of discipline imposed by any other jurisdiction (state, federal, territorial, or foreign). Maximum penalty: public reprimand. Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 9.1","#st-ga-ethics"],["Solicitation: 30-day bar on written accident solicitation (Rule 7.3)","Georgia · ethics","Written solicitation about personal injury, wrongful death, or an accident or disaster involving the recipient or a relative is barred unless the accident or disaster happened more than 30 days earlier. Live person-to-person solicitation for pecuniary gain is barred except to lawyers, family, close personal or prior professional contacts, and routine business users of the services. Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 7.3(b)–(c)","#st-ga-ethics"],["Willful blindness counts (Rule 1.2(d)); trust accounts pay IOLTA interest to the Georgia Bar Foundation","Georgia · ethics","A lawyer may not “knowingly or with willful blindness” counsel or assist client crime or fraud. Nominal or short-term client funds go in an IOLTA account at an approved institution, with interest remitted at least quarterly to the Georgia Bar Foundation; other client funds go in an interest-bearing account for the client’s benefit. Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 1.2(d); 1.15(II)","#st-ga-ethics"],["Discovery must be finished within 6 months of the answer to use the court’s compulsory process","Georgia · land mine","Under USCR 5.1, discovery must be started promptly and completed within 6 months after the answer is filed (or 6 months starting 30 days after service if no timely answer) for a party to use the court’s compulsory process to compel it. The court may open, extend, reopen, or shorten the period, but ask before it closes. Motions to compel must quote or attach each request and response and certify a good-faith conferenc","#st-ga-unique"],["Responses to motions are due in 30 days, and there is no hearing unless you ask","Georgia · land mine","Responses are due 30 days after service of a motion (not 14). Civil motions, including summary judgment, are decided without oral hearing unless the court orders otherwise; for summary judgment, a separate “Request for Oral Hearing” must be filed with the motion or within 5 days after the response time. Summary-judgment motions need a separate statement of material facts, and the response needs its own statement of d","#st-ga-unique"],["Email the judge when you e-file a motion","Georgia · land mine","When a motion or response is e-filed, the filer must notify the opposing parties and the assigned judge (or the judge’s designee) by email contemporaneously and no later than 24 hours after e-filing. Every pre-trial motion must also include citations of authority and, for unstipulated facts, affidavits or record citations. USCR 6.1","#st-ga-unique"],["Recusal motions: 5 days from learning, 10 days before the hearing","Georgia · land mine","A superior court recusal motion must be filed and presented within 5 days after learning the grounds and at least 10 days before the hearing or trial, with a specific affidavit. Conclusory affidavits fail, and the motion cannot delay the proceeding. USCR 25.1–25.2","#st-ga-unique"],["Interlocutory appeal needs a trial-court certificate and a 10-day application, with no extensions","Georgia · land mine","Most non-final orders reach the appellate courts only by application after the trial court signs a certificate of immediate review. The application is due within 10 days after the certificate is entered (filed with the clerk). Neither appellate court grants extensions for interlocutory applications or responses, and if granted, a notice of appeal must be filed in the trial court within 10 days of the grant order. The","#st-ga-unique"],["Some final judgments are appealable only by discretionary application","Georgia · land mine","For case types listed in O.C.G.A. § 5-6-35 (statute not collected; divorce decrees are one example the rules mention), the appeal is by application filed in the appellate court within 30 days after the order is entered (7 days for dispossessory orders under O.C.G.A. § 44-7-56). An extension is possible only by motion filed on or before the due date. If granted, file the notice of appeal within 10 days. Ga. Ct. App. R","#st-ga-unique"],["Court of Appeals reconsideration motions are due by 4:30 p.m., not midnight","Georgia · land mine","Most e-filings in the Court of Appeals count as filed through 11:59 p.m., but a motion for reconsideration must be received by 4:30 p.m. within 10 days after the judgment; later filings count as received the next business day. Extensions only for providential cause by motion before the 10 days run. Mail-box and prison-mailbox rules do not apply. Ga. Ct. App. R. 4(b)–(d), 37(b), 46(a)","#st-ga-unique"],["Certiorari takes two filings in two courts","Georgia · land mine","To seek Supreme Court review of a Court of Appeals decision, first file a notice of intent to apply for certiorari in the Court of Appeals within 10 days after judgment (or after the ruling on reconsideration), then file the petition in the Supreme Court within 20 days after judgment or the reconsideration ruling, and file notice of the petition in the Court of Appeals the same day. A motion for permission to file a ","#st-ga-unique"],["Leaves of absence work in trial courts but not in the appellate courts","Georgia · land mine","In superior court, a lawyer can protect cases with a USCR 16.1 notice filed at least 30 days before a leave of 30 cumulative days or less; it stands granted unless opposing counsel objects or the court denies it within ten days. Longer leaves need a USCR 16.2 application. A leave never extends a deadline set by law or the court. The Court of Appeals and the Supreme Court do not recognize leaves of absence at all. USC","#st-ga-unique"],["Withdrawal requires a 20-day client notice with prescribed contents","Georgia · land mine","To withdraw in superior court, give the client written notice with the items listed in USCR 4.3(2) (including the client’s right to object within 20 days and the exact 20th day), file a notification certificate, and then request an order. The request is granted as of course only if made before a pretrial order is entered; the judge may refuse it if it would delay a trial on a published calendar, disrupt the court, or","#st-ga-unique"],["Pro hac vice: appearing before approval can sink later applications","Georgia · land mine","A court should deny pro hac vice if the applicant previously filed or appeared in a Georgia court without approval under the USCR, and frequent appearances can be treated as regular practice. The annual $200 fee is due January 15 for every year you remain admitted in any Georgia case. USCR 4.4 D.3(d)–(e), E.3; Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 5.5 (pro hac vice fee paragraph)","#st-ga-unique"],["Default judgments need a written service certificate","Georgia · land mine","A party seeking default judgment must attach to the proposed judgment a written certificate stating the date and type of service, whether proof of service was filed within 5 business days of service (or when it was filed), that no defensive pleading appears of record, and the defendant’s military status if required. USCR 15","#st-ga-unique"],["No peremptory strike of a superior court judge; recusal motion within 5 days with affidavit","Georgia · judges","The collected rules provide no peremptory substitution of judge. A motion to recuse or disqualify must be in writing with affidavits fully stating the facts, filed and presented no later than 5 days after the affiant first learned of the grounds and no later than 10 days before the hearing or trial, unless good cause is shown; it may not delay the proceeding. The affidavit must be definite and specific as to time, pl","#st-ga-unique"],["Recusal in the appellate courts","Georgia · judges","Court of Appeals: motion with affidavits within 20 days of docketing (absent good cause), decided on the papers by the other two judges of the division plus the presiding judge of the next division. Supreme Court: motion with affidavits no later than 5 days after learning of the grounds and 10 days before any oral argument; the challenged Justice decides, alone or after consulting the others. Ga. Ct. App. R. 44(a)–(e","#st-ga-unique"],["Cameras and recording in trial courts: a presumption of approval","Georgia · speech","Anyone (including media) seeking to record a superior court proceeding submits a request, at least 24 hours ahead where practicable. The court notifies the parties; a properly submitted request should generally be approved, and the judge may deny or limit it only after specific findings on listed factors. Parties, witnesses, and alleged victims waive objections not made in writing or on the record before the proceedi","#st-ga-unique"],["Sealing court files requires a finding that privacy harm clearly outweighs the public interest","Georgia · speech","All court records are public unless limited by law or the USCR 21 procedure. An order limiting access must specify what is limited, for how long, and why, and may issue only on a finding that harm to a person’s privacy clearly outweighs the public interest. Any party or nonparty opposing may demand a hearing within 30 days. Review is by interlocutory application to the appellate court. USCR 21, 21.1–21.5 (as amended ","#st-ga-unique"],["Constitutional speech protections: not addressed in collected text","Georgia · speech","Whether the Georgia Constitution’s speech clause is read more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked; no constitutional or case text was collected. Ga. Const. (not collected)","#st-ga-unique"],["Uniform rules, no local rules","Georgia · unique rules","All local rules expired; the USCR apply statewide and are not subject to local deviation. A judge may supersede a specific rule in a specific case only by an order explaining the necessity, served on the attorneys. Courts may adopt standing orders on matters the USCR do not address (for example, ADR, e-filing, registry payments), but only with actual notice to the parties; posting in the courthouse is not enough. USC","#st-ga-unique"],["Civil Practice Act is statutory and recently amended","Georgia · unique rules","Georgia’s rules of civil procedure are the Civil Practice Act, O.C.G.A. § 9-11-1 et seq., enacted by the General Assembly rather than adopted by the court. Official legislative summaries show 2025 amendments to §§ 9-11-12 and 9-11-41 (SB 68, effective April 21, 2025, on timing and procedure for answers, discovery, and dismissals) and § 9-11-26 (SB 69, litigation-funding discovery), and 2026 amendments to §§ 9-11-28 a","#st-ga-unique"],["Rule NOTE. What this file covers","Georgia · civil rules","Georgia’s civil procedure rules are the Civil Practice Act, a statute (O.C.G.A. § 9-11-1 et seq.), not court rules. No complete official free text of the Act was available (the state’s official code is hosted only by LexisNexis), so this file instead summarizes the Uniform Superior Court Rules (USCR), the statewide court rules that govern practice under the Act in superior courts, from the compilation dated November ","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 1. Preamble","Georgia · civil rules","The USCR are adopted under the courts’ inherent power and Ga. Const. Art. VI, Sec. IX, Par. I to secure speedy, efficient, and inexpensive resolution of cases. They yield to the Constitution and substantive law in any conflict and prevail over local practices statewide.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 1.1. Repeal of local rules","Georgia · civil rules","All local rules, internal operating procedures, and experimental rules of the superior courts expired December 31, 2010.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 1.2. Authority to deviate; standing orders and pilot projects","Georgia · civil rules","No deviation from the USCR is allowed. Courts may keep standing orders on internal processes that do not materially affect parties’ rights (case management, ADR programs, specialty courts, and similar), may adopt jury-excuse guidelines under O.C.G.A. § 15-12-10, and may run Supreme Court-approved pilot projects for one year (extendable one more). Standing orders on matters the USCR do not address (for example, ADR, e","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 1.3. Repeal of earlier rules","Georgia · civil rules","The old “Rules of the Superior Court” in former Ga. Code Ann. §§ 24-3301 to 24-3389 are repealed.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 1.4. Matters of statewide concern","Georgia · civil rules","The USCR apply statewide.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 1.5. Deviation","Georgia · civil rules","No local deviation. A judge may supersede a specific rule in a specific case only by an order explaining why, served on the attorneys.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 1.6. Amendments","Georgia · civil rules","A Council of Superior Court Judges committee recommends amendments to the Supreme Court; the State Bar and other uniform-rules committees get notice and a chance to comment.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 1.7. Publication","Georgia · civil rules","Rules and amendments are published in the Georgia Reports advance sheets and, unless stated otherwise, take effect on publication.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 2. Definitions","Georgia · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 2.1–2.6.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 2.1. Attorney","Georgia · civil rules","Includes anyone admitted to practice in the superior courts, anyone otherwise permitted by law to represent a party, and any pro se party.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 2.2–2.5. Judge, clerk, calendar clerk, assigned judge","Georgia · civil rules","“Judge” includes senior judges authorized to serve; “clerk” includes deputies; the calendar clerk schedules hearings and trials under the assigned judge’s supervision; the “assigned judge” is the judge to whom the case is assigned (or, in general-calendar circuits, the judge responsible at the time).","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 2.6. Filing","Georgia · civil rules","“Filing” includes paper or electronic submission to the clerk; e-filing is governed by Rule 36.16.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 3.1. Method of assignment","Georgia · civil rules","In multi-judge circuits the clerk assigns cases under a judge-approved plan that equalizes caseloads. The clerk has no discretion, may not reveal the assigned judge before filing when timing matters, and no one may try to influence assignment.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 3.2. Companion and related actions","Georgia · civil rules","Actions with substantially the same parties, subject matter, or facts, refiled actions, and derivative or companion actions go to the judge who has (or had) the original action, generally the one with the lower case number.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 3.3. Exclusive control; transfer to another judge","Georgia · civil rules","The assigned judge has exclusive control of the case; reassignment requires that judge’s written order (and the receiving judge’s consent). A judge who knows of facts requiring recusal under Rule 25 may not transfer the case instead.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 3.4. Local authority","Georgia · civil rules","Each multi-judge circuit sets its assignment method and publishes it to the local bar, the clerk, and the Supreme Court.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 4.1. Ex parte communications","Georgia · civil rules","Judges may not initiate or consider ex parte communications about pending or impending proceedings except as allowed by law or rule.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 4.2. Entry of appearance and pleadings","Georgia · civil rules","An attorney may not appear until filing a signed entry of appearance or signed pleading stating the case style and number, the party represented, and the attorney’s name, State Bar number, address, phone, fax, and State-Bar-registered email. Within 48 hours after being retained, mail the entry to the court and opposing counsel or file it (late filing does not bar the appearance). An entry of appearance is not an answ","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 4.3. Withdrawal","Georgia · civil rules","Withdrawal requires a written request to the judge stating that the client received written notice of intent to withdraw and 20 days passed without objection, or that the client consents. The notice must include the items listed in 4.3(2)(A)–(I) (court keeps jurisdiction, client’s duties, scheduled dates, corporate-representation warning, the right to object within 20 days and the exact 20th day), and counsel files a","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 4.4. Admission pro hac vice","Georgia · civil rules","An eligible out-of-state (Domestic) or foreign lawyer files a verified application with the court, served on all appearing parties and the State Bar’s Office of General Counsel, including the Appendix A information and the name of an active Georgia sponsor who will appear of record. Fees: $75 per application and $200 per year (due January 15 each later year), payable to the State Bar, waived for qualifying pro bono a","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 4.5. Firm appearances and withdrawals","Georgia · civil rules","An appearance or withdrawal by one lawyer in a firm or professional corporation covers the other lawyers in that firm.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 4.6. Notice of representation","Georgia · civil rules","New counsel must promptly notify the calendar clerk (and opposing counsel in civil cases; the district attorney in criminal cases) in writing of the representation and of any later change of representation or contact information.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 4.7. Use the assigned judge","Georgia · civil rules","Do not present a matter in a case assigned to another judge except under the most compelling circumstances, and then disclose the assignment and inform the assigned judge promptly.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 4.8. Notice of related cases","Georgia · civil rules","Counsel must immediately tell the judges involved about any related action pending or previously pending before a judge in the same circuit.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 4.9. Prior presentation to another judge","Georgia · civil rules","Before presenting a matter already presented to another judge, disclose that fact and the result.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 4.10. Settlements and dismissals","Georgia · civil rules","Notify the assigned judge (and calendar clerk where appropriate) immediately upon settlement or dismissal of a civil action.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 4.11. Duty to attend and remain","Georgia · civil rules","Subject to Rule 17, attorneys with calendared matters must be in court at the call and stay until excused, contacting the court daily during an ongoing calendar; failure is punishable by contempt.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 4.12. Binding authority","Georgia · civil rules","Attorneys of record have apparent authority to make agreements for their clients in civil actions, and oral agreements, if established, are enforceable.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 4.13. Limited appearances","Georgia · civil rules","Limited-scope representation requires first filing a separate “Limited Appearance” entry (complying with Rule 4.2) stating its purpose and duration, and, within 5 days after it ends, a notice that it has ended served on the client, opposing counsel or parties, and the judge. Otherwise counsel remains of record until withdrawal under Rule 4.3.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 5.1. Prompt completion of discovery","Georgia · civil rules","To use the court’s compulsory process, discovery must be commenced promptly and completed within 6 months after the answer is filed (or, if no answer within 30 days of service or any extended date, within 6 months starting 30 days after service). The court may open, extend, reopen, or shorten the period at any time. Federal practice has no comparable rule cutting off compulsory process at a fixed period after the ans","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 5.2. Filing discovery","Georgia · civil rules","Depositions and original discovery are not filed unless O.C.G.A. § 9-11-29.1(a)(1)–(5) requires it. A party serving interrogatories, document requests, admission requests, or responses files only a certificate identifying what was served, when, and on whom.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 5.3. Deposition duration","Georgia · civil rules","Unless the court orders or the parties stipulate otherwise, a deposition is limited to one day of seven hours; the court must allow more time if needed for a fair examination or if the deponent or others cause delay. Mirrors FRCP 30(d)(1).","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 5.4. Early planning conference and discovery plan","Georgia · civil rules","The parties may agree to, the judge may order, or a party may petition for an early planning discovery conference (in person, phone, or video) to discuss claims, settlement, preservation, and a discovery plan. An agreed plan (covering issues, schedule, ESI, preservation, production format, inaccessible sources) or a joint report of disagreements may be submitted within 14 days of the meeting, by plaintiff’s counsel u","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 5.5. Privilege","Georgia · civil rules","A party withholding information as privileged or work product must expressly claim it and describe what is withheld well enough for others to assess the claim (a privilege log in substance, like FRCP 26(b)(5)(A)). After inadvertent production, the producing party may give notice; the receiving party must return, sequester, or destroy it, not use it until resolved, retrieve copies it disclosed, and may submit it for i","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 6.1. Filing motions","Georgia · civil rules","Every pre-trial civil motion (unless consented to by all parties) must include citations of authority and, for unstipulated facts, affidavits or record citations. When a motion or response is e-filed, the filer must email the opposing parties and the assigned judge or designee contemporaneously and no later than 24 hours after e-filing.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 6.2. Responses to motions","Georgia · civil rules","Unless the judge orders otherwise or law provides, a party opposing a motion must serve and file its response, with authorities and affidavits or record citations for unstipulated facts, within 30 days after service of the motion. (State courts have their own Rule 6.2.)","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 6.3. Hearings","Georgia · civil rules","Civil motions, including summary judgment, are decided without oral hearing unless the court orders otherwise, except motions for new trial and for judgment notwithstanding the verdict. Oral argument on summary judgment is allowed on a separate “Request for Oral Hearing” filed with the motion or within 5 days after the response time expires.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 6.4. Motions to compel","Georgia · civil rules","A motion to compel under O.C.G.A. § 9-11-37 must quote or attach each disputed request, state the objection or response claimed insufficient and its grounds, and give reasons. Before filing any discovery motion (including to quash or for a protective order) counsel must confer in good faith and file a certificate that the conference occurred and failed. Objections must be specific, not general.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 6.5. Summary judgment statements","Georgia · civil rules","A summary-judgment motion under the Civil Practice Act must attach a separate, short statement of each theory of recovery and each material fact the movant says is undisputed; the response must include a separate statement of each material fact claimed to be genuinely disputed. Comparable in function to the separate statements many federal local rules require; FRCP 56 itself requires record citations instead.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 6.6. Timing of summary judgment motions","Georgia · civil rules","File early enough not to delay trial; no trial will be continued because a summary-judgment motion was filed late. (The Act’s own timing provisions, O.C.G.A. § 9-11-56, were not collected.)","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 6.7. Emergency motions","Georgia · civil rules","On written notice and good cause, the assigned judge may shorten or waive time limits for emergency motions (but not summary judgment) or hear them immediately; the motion must explain in detail why expedition is needed.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 7.1. Civil pretrial conferences","Georgia · civil rules","The judge may set a pretrial conference on motion or sua sponte, considering O.C.G.A. § 9-11-16 issues. The attorneys who will try the case must attend (another attorney of record only with consent and full authority), and each side presents a written proposed pretrial order. Failure to attend or submit one allows removal from the trial calendar, entry of the court’s own order, or other sanctions short of dismissal w","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 7.2. Civil pretrial order form","Georgia · civil rules","The proposed pretrial order follows the prescribed form: trial counsel, time estimate, pending motions, discovery completed, contentions, jury issues, specifications of negligence, contract terms, damages, stipulations, exhibit lists (authenticity stipulated unless noted), witness will-call and may-call lists, deposition designations, verdict forms, and settlement. Once entered it supersedes the pleadings, which may ","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 7.3. Interpreters","Georgia · civil rules","A party must file notice of the need for a qualified interpreter within a reasonable time, at least 5 days before the proceeding where practicable, naming the participants, length, and language. The court appoints a licensed interpreter at its expense; late notice can result in costs, and cancellations require 24 hours’ notice or the party may bear the interpreter’s expenses.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 7.4. Criminal pretrial conferences","Georgia · civil rules","In criminal cases, pending motions, special pleas, and demurrers not called to the judge’s attention at the pretrial conference are deemed abandoned unless the judge allows later hearing for good cause.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 8.1. Scheduling trials","Georgia · civil rules","The assigned judge alone sets hearings and trials and publishes calendars, and is responsible for keeping cases from languishing.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 8.2. Ready list","Georgia · civil rules","Cases ready under O.C.G.A. § 9-11-40 go on a ready list, placed there by the judge on notice or by a party after the pretrial order, generally in filing-date order with statutory priorities.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 8.3. Trial calendar","Georgia · civil rules","The calendar clerk publishes the trial calendar from the ready list at least 20 days before the term, and may email it to attorneys at their State-Bar-registered addresses (pro se parties by regular mail).","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 8.4. Trial date","Georgia · civil rules","Parties in the first 10 cases on a published calendar must appear ready on the stated date; others must be ready but may arrange a specific time or await a call on reasonable notice.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 8.5. Continuances","Georgia · civil rules","Continuances are not granted merely by agreement of counsel. A first continuance motion made within 5 days of the trial week without statutory grounds or good cause can bring a penalty of up to $50.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 8.6. Special settings","Georgia · civil rules","Special settings for jury trial are not favored.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 9.1. Virtual proceedings: scope","Georgia · civil rules","Any proceeding may be virtual with the parties’ consent and the court’s agreement. Without consent, all civil matters except trials may be virtual, including depositions, defaults, damages hearings, motions, and pre- and post-trial motions; in criminal cases most non-trial matters may be virtual unless law requires in-person proceedings. Effective for events on or after March 1, 2023.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 9.2. Virtual proceedings: conduct","Georgia · civil rules","The judge may set logistics and allocate costs; privileged attorney-client communication must be protected; records are made as for in-person proceedings. Witnesses may testify by video in civil matters at the judge’s discretion; in criminal matters an objection to video testimony is sustained but operates as a continuance motion. Virtual events that would be open court must be publicly accessible, with notice and a ","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 10.1. Voir dire","Georgia · civil rules","Questions under O.C.G.A. § 15-12-133 are allowed, but form, time, and number are within the court’s discretion; hypotheticals are discouraged, and questions seeking prejudgment or legal opinions are improper. Objections to voir dire procedure must be raised promptly or are waived.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 10.2. Opening statements","Georgia · civil rules","All parties may open, limited to expected proof by admissible evidence or its lack; the court may limit time (amended Nov. 5, 2026). In criminal cases the defense may open after the State’s opening or after the State’s evidence.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 10.3. Requests to charge","Georgia · civil rules","Requests to charge must be numbered consecutively on separate sheets and submitted in duplicate at the start of trial (unless the pretrial order says otherwise); additional requests may cover unanticipated points.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 10.4. Excusals from courtroom","Georgia · civil rules","Only the judge may excuse a party, witness, or counsel from the courtroom during a proceeding.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 11. Selection of juries","Georgia · civil rules","After voir dire, parties may request 15 minutes to prepare for selection; after warning, the court may limit each peremptory challenge to not less than 1 minute, and an unexercised challenge is forfeited.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 12. Voluntary dismissal after jury empaneled","Georgia · civil rules","If a civil action is voluntarily dismissed after the jury is empaneled (other than by a final settlement dictated into the record), all court costs, including juror fees for every panel used, are taxed to the dismissing party. (Statutory voluntary-dismissal rules in O.C.G.A. § 9-11-41 were amended in 2025 and were not collected.)","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 13.1. Argument time limits","Georgia · civil rules","Closing arguments: 2 hours per side in civil cases (30 minutes in appeals from magistrate court); criminal limits range from 30 minutes (misdemeanor) to 2 hours (death or life cases).","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 13.2–13.4. Extensions, number of arguments, conclusion","Georgia · civil rules","Extensions must be sought, with reasons, before arguments begin. No more than two attorneys argue per party without leave, and only one in conclusion. In civil cases the plaintiff opens and concludes, unless the defendant introduces no evidence or admits a prima facie case.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 14. Dismissal for failure to respond to call","Georgia · civil rules","The court may dismiss a civil action, or a party’s pleading, without prejudice for failure to respond properly to the call of the case, and may hold an attorney in contempt for failing to appear without legal excuse.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 15. Default judgments","Georgia · civil rules","A party seeking default judgment must attach to the proposed judgment a written certificate stating the date and type of service, whether proof of service was filed within 5 business days of service (or when), that no defensive pleading is of record, and the defendant’s military status if required.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 15.1. Garnishments","Georgia · civil rules","Under O.C.G.A. § 18-4-61(5), the clerk may supervise initiation of garnishments and accept the affidavit if it is on personal knowledge, complete, and on proper forms; questionable matters go to the judge.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 16.1. Leave of absence, 30 days or less","Georgia · civil rules","An attorney may protect cases not on a published calendar or noticed for hearing by filing with the clerk, at least 30 days before the leave, a notice listing the cases, reason, and duration, copied to the judge and opposing counsel. It stands granted unless opposing counsel objects or the court denies it within ten days.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 16.2. Longer or late leaves","Georgia · civil rules","Leaves over 30 cumulative days, for cases already calendared or noticed, or not meeting Rule 16.1 timing require a written application filed and served at least ten days before submission to the judge (waivable by written consent); the court has discretion.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 16.3–16.4. Effect and denial of leave","Georgia · civil rules","A granted leave relieves counsel of trials, hearings, and depositions in the listed matters, but does not extend any deadline set by law or the court; a new request is needed if counsel later files a criminal speedy-trial demand. Nonconforming applications are denied; the rule includes a notice form.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 17.1. Conflicts between courts","Georgia · civil rules","A conflict exists only if the attorney is lead counsel (or subpoenaed) in two or more matters and certifies others cannot handle them. Give written notice, ideally received at least seven days before the conflict, to opposing counsel, each clerk, and each judge, with a proposed resolution. Priority: felony criminal, JQC, and habeas over civil (speedy-trial demands first); jury over non-jury; among non-jury, dependenc","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 17.2. Attorneys serving as part-time judges","Georgia · civil rules","Civil judges should promptly resolve conflicts from an attorney’s service as a part-time judge, mindful of juvenile time limits, but should not continue civil matters involving child safety or temporary support for that reason.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 18.1–18.8. Senior judges","Georgia · civil rules","Procedures for chief judges to request assistance from other judges and senior judges (disqualification, disability, or caseload), certificates of need, emergency appointments, residency, and senior judges’ election to practice law instead of serving.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 19.1. Civil transfer for lack of jurisdiction or venue","Georgia · civil rules","A timely motion that jurisdiction is lacking or venue improper (or the court’s own finding of no subject-matter jurisdiction) is treated as a motion to transfer to a proper Georgia court; the movant names that court. Serve written motions at least 10 days before hearing; opponents respond in writing within 10 days of service. The transfer order states the action is dismissed without prejudice unless the plaintiff pay","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 19.2–19.3. Criminal transfers; election contests","Georgia · civil rules","The judge granting a criminal venue change continues to preside. Election-contest venue may move to any county, with costs borne by the transferor county.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 19.4. Civil removal to defendant’s home county","Georgia · civil rules","Subject to O.C.G.A. § 14-2-510(b)(4), a tort defendant may file a notice of removal within 45 days after service of the summons, moving the case to the county of its principal place of business; the defendant must pay transfer costs within 20 days of the cost bill or the removal stands dismissed. The plaintiff may move to remand within 45 days after notice from the receiving clerk; prior orders may be reviewed on mot","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 20. Peremptory calendar","Georgia · civil rules","The judge may publish a list of civil cases with expired discovery, requiring parties to announce readiness; failure to appear or respond can lead to dismissal without prejudice of the plaintiff’s action or the defendant’s answer, counterclaim, or cross-claim.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 21. Limitation of access to court files","Georgia · civil rules","All court records are public unless access is limited by law or this procedure (amended Nov. 5, 2026).","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 21.1–21.3. Sealing motions, finding of harm, ex parte orders","Georgia · civil rules","On motion or sua sponte the court may limit access by an order specifying the part, nature, duration, and reason; any party or nonparty opposing may request a hearing within 30 days. Sealing requires a finding that privacy harm clearly outweighs the public interest. Temporary ex parte limitation only under compelling circumstances.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 21.4–21.5. Review and amendment of sealing orders","Georgia · civil rules","Sealing orders are reviewable by interlocutory application to the appellate court, and may be amended on notice by the issuing court or appellate court; a hearing within 30 days must be held if requested in a motion to amend or vacate.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 21.6. Redaction and filing under seal","Georgia · civil rules","Protected identifiers are as defined in O.C.G.A. § 9-11-7.1. To file under seal, first file a redacted public version, then submit the unredacted version and a proposed sealing order directly to the court. Protective-order identifiers go on a separate page the clerk seals.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 22. Electronic devices and recording of proceedings","Georgia · civil rules","Governs phones and devices in courtrooms and requests to record proceedings. Recording requests should be made at least 24 hours ahead where practicable and generally approved; denial or limitation requires specific findings on listed factors, and objections not raised before the proceeding are waived.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 22.1. Audiovisual media used in trials","Georgia · civil rules","Media exhibits should be tendered in commonly playable formats on a clean USB drive with any needed player or codec; the judge may require advance delivery.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 23. Withdrawal of funds from court registry","Georgia · civil rules","An order paying out registry funds (except garnishments) must be accompanied by counsel’s certificate that all claimants of record consent in writing (and, in condemnation, that taxes and assessments are provided for).","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 24.1. Domestic relations: scope","Georgia · civil rules","Covers divorce, alimony, equitable division, custody, support, legitimation, paternity, related adoption and termination matters, contempt and modification, Family Violence Act actions, and foreign support judgments.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 24.2–24.2A. Financial affidavits and worksheets","Georgia · civil rules","At least 5 days before any temporary or final hearing on support, alimony, property division, or fees, each party serves a financial affidavit in the prescribed form, plus Child Support Commission worksheets and schedules in support cases. Monthly figures use 4.35 weeks per month and 174 hours per month.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 24.3–24.5. Acknowledgments, notice, and witnesses","Georgia · civil rules","Acknowledgments of service and consents must be witnessed by an attesting officer or counsel. At temporary hearings each side may present the parties and one other live witness; other testimony is by deposition or affidavit served at least 24 hours before. Children do not testify at temporary hearings without leave.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 24.6–24.7. Uncontested and contested divorce","Georgia · civil rules","By written consent a divorce may be granted 31 days after service or acknowledgment; if unanswered, 46 days after service; if served by publication, 61 days after first publication. No decree issues in a contested case until all contestable issues are resolved.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 24.8–24.12. Parenting seminars, GALs, parenting plans, income withholding","Georgia · civil rules","Circuits may require a seminar of up to four hours on divorce’s effects on children. Rules govern guardian ad litem appointment and duties, mandatory parenting plans (O.C.G.A. § 19-9-1(b) content, in the prescribed form) in custody cases, and a separate income withholding order in child-support cases unless an exception applies.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 25.1. Recusal motions: timing","Georgia · civil rules","Motions to recuse must be written, supported by affidavits, and filed and presented no later than 5 days after learning of the grounds and 10 days before the hearing or trial at issue, unless good cause is shown; they may not delay the proceeding. Georgia has no peremptory challenge to a judge in these rules.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 25.2. Affidavit","Georgia · civil rules","The affidavit must state specific facts (time, place, persons, circumstances) of extra-judicial conduct showing bias or prejudice; conclusions and opinions are insufficient.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 25.3. Duty of the trial judge","Georgia · civil rules","The challenged judge stops acting on the merits and immediately decides only timeliness and legal sufficiency, assuming the facts true; if recusal would be authorized, another judge hears the motion, the allegations stand denied, and the challenged judge may not otherwise oppose (amended April 9, 2026). Guided by Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 25.4–25.7. Selection of judge; findings; voluntary recusal","Georgia · civil rules","The hearing judge is chosen by the district administrative judge, the other judge in a two-judge circuit, or the circuit’s random method (never by the challenged judge). The assigned judge may rule on affidavits or hold a hearing and must make written findings. A voluntary recusal is not an admission.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 26–35. Criminal procedure rules","Georgia · civil rules","Rules 26–35 govern criminal matters: first appearance within 48 hours of a warrantless arrest (72 with a warrant) and commitment hearings (26), pretrial release programs (27), jail census (28), indigent-defense appointment (29), arraignment (30), criminal motions and notices due at least 10 days before trial for certain defenses and child-hearsay statements (31), criminal trial calendars (32), guilty pleas and plea a","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 36.1–36.4. Document preparation, docketing, caption, signatures","Georgia · civil rules","Filings should be on 8½ x 11 white paper, one side, with a caption stating the exact nature of the pleading. Proposed orders and judgments must show the preparer’s name, bar number, address, phone, fax, and email; avoid separate signature pages, and any separate signature page must identify the parties, case number, and document.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 36.5–36.9. Originals, minutes, transcripts, file categories, case numbers","Georgia · civil rules","Originals stay with the clerk (attorneys may carry the file to the judge for a hearing). Case numbers follow the sequence year–type–number–judge.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 36.10–36.12. Civil filing requirements","Georgia · civil rules","Complaints are filed only with the filing fee and sheriff’s fee (or pauper’s affidavit), a civil case initiation form, service copies, and any required forms; dispositions require a civil case disposition form. Returns of service go on the clerk’s form. Advance costs are the minimum costs.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 36.13–36.15. Criminal filing and costs","Georgia · civil rules","Requirements for indictments, accusations, no bills, criminal-history data, and minimum criminal costs of $100.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 36.16. Electronic filing","Georgia · civil rules","E-filing is available when required by law or adopted under Judicial Council standards. Registered filers may e-file anything except sealed, in camera, or restricted documents (original depositions may be e-filed). An e-filed document is deemed signed by the filer, presumed filed when the provider receives and confirms it, and deemed served on parties who registered for e-service. Courts grant relief (nunc pro tunc f","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 36.17. Sensitive information","Georgia · civil rules","Under O.C.G.A. § 9-11-7.1, filings may include only the last four digits of Social Security, taxpayer ID, and financial account numbers and the year of birth. Counsel and parties alone are responsible for redaction; the clerk does not check.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 36.18. Electronic signatures of judges","Georgia · civil rules","Judges may sign orders electronically, using secure, auditable, non-editable methods with date, time, and case number where practicable.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 36.19. Digital recording of proceedings","Georgia · civil rules","Rules for digital recording systems authorized by O.C.G.A. §§ 5-6-41, 9-14-50, and 15-14-1: qualified digital monitors, storage, and transcripts prepared only by certified court reporters with a required certification (adopted Feb. 26, 2026). Does not apply to depositions.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 37. Court administrators","Georgia · civil rules","District and local court administrators perform administrative duties assigned by the courts.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 38. Filing of remittitur and judgment","Georgia · civil rules","On receiving an appellate remittitur, the clerk files the notice of appeal, remittitur, and index with the original action.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 39. Docketing and indexing","Georgia · civil rules","Clerks maintain civil, criminal, lis pendens, general execution, and adoption dockets and use the required civil case initiation and disposition forms.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 40. Computer applications","Georgia · civil rules","Computerized records must use the same data elements and security as manual systems.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 41.1–41.2. Motions for new trial","Georgia · civil rules","Motions for new trial must be heard and decided as promptly as possible. In criminal cases the court holds a status conference within 120 days after sentencing and about every 180 days after, and issues a show-cause notice to the reporter if the transcript is not filed within 120 days.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 41.3. Transcript costs","Georgia · civil rules","Except for pauper cases, an attorney who files a motion for new trial or a notice of appeal designating a transcript is personally responsible for the court reporter’s fee, and the filing certifies the transcript has been ordered; filing before ordering it can bring discipline.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 41.4. Transmission of the record","Georgia · civil rules","On a notice of appeal the clerk compiles and transmits the record under O.C.G.A. § 5-6-43; failure within 60 days of the statutory deadlines can bring a show-cause hearing for the clerk.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 42. Special counsel","Georgia · civil rules","Private special prosecutors retained by victims’ families may not participate in prosecutions; the court may appoint counsel to assist retained defense counsel.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 43. Mandatory continuing judicial education","Georgia · civil rules","Education requirements, administration, sanctions, and exemptions for superior court judges.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 44. Habeas corpus in death-sentence cases","Georgia · civil rules","Procedures for first state habeas petitions by death-sentenced petitioners (O.C.G.A. § 9-14-47.1): clerk service on the Council within ten days, judicial assignment, answer, scheduling, motions, discovery, hearing, transcript, briefing, and ruling.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 45. Court security and emergency operations","Georgia · civil rules","Each circuit must have a confidential courthouse security plan (sheriff with chief judge, reviewed annually) and a judicial emergency operations plan.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 46. Special masters","Georgia · civil rules","Unless a statute provides otherwise, the court may appoint a master on motion or sua sponte for consented duties, pretrial and post-trial matters, technical subjects such as e-discovery, compliance monitoring, accountings, out-of-state depositions, or (in exceptional conditions) non-jury fact-finding, after notice and a chance to be heard. A party may move to reject or modify a master’s order, report, or recommendati","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 47. Uncontested agency adoptions","Georgia · civil rules","The judge should hold the final hearing on an agency adoption (O.C.G.A. § 19-8-4) within fifteen days after counsel certifies all statutory requirements are met, and may hear it in any county of the circuit with consent.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 48. Weapons carry license inquiry","Georgia · civil rules","At sentencing for listed offenses and in other listed situations, the judge must ask whether the person holds a weapons carry license, and the clerk notifies the issuing probate court within 10 days.","#st-ga-rules"],["Rule 49. Emergency dispossessory (CARES Act)","Georgia · civil rules","For residential nonpayment dispossessories filed before August 25, 2020, landlords had to file a verification on CARES Act coverage and, for covered properties, give the 30-day federal notice. Historical; retained in the compilation.","#st-ga-rules"],["Appeals in Georgia: direct appeal, or application first?","Georgia · appeals","Georgia has two appellate courts with divided jurisdiction: the Supreme Court of Georgia and the Court of Appeals of Georgia. Every appellant’s brief and every application must open with a jurisdictional statement explaining why this court (and not the other) has jurisdiction and why the appeal is timely; misfiled cases are transferred.\nThe first question is always whether you may file a notice of appeal in the trial","#st-ga-appeals"],["Jurisdictional statement required in both courts","Georgia · appeals · Which court","An appellant’s brief must state (i) the basis for appellate jurisdiction (for example, O.C.G.A. §§ 5-6-34, 5-6-35), (ii) why this court rather than the other has jurisdiction (Ga. Const. Art. VI, Sec. VI, Pars. II–III; O.C.G.A. § 15-3-3.1), and (iii) the filing dates showing timeliness (O.C.G.A. §§ 5-6-38, 5-6-39). In the Supreme Court, a party claiming jurisdiction based on a constitutional question must cite where ","#st-ga-appeals"],["Transfers between the courts","Georgia · appeals · Which court","An appeal or application filed in the wrong court is transferred by order. In the Supreme Court a transferred case proceeds as if originally filed there; parties may elect within two business days of docketing to rely on briefs already filed in the Court of Appeals, and must renew any oral-argument request within five days. No second filing fee if it was paid in the Court of Appeals. Ga. Ct. App. R. 11(b)–(c), 32; Ga","#st-ga-appeals"],["Election contests in the Supreme Court","Georgia · appeals · Which court","Emergency motions, motions to expedite, and applications in election-contest cases must include the basis for the challenge, a jurisdictional statement (citing, e.g., O.C.G.A. §§ 21-2-5(e), 21-2-528 for timeliness), an enumeration of errors, the steps taken to expedite the case below, and a standing explanation. Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 29","#st-ga-appeals"],["Notice of appeal in the trial court (statute)","Georgia · appeals · Starting a direct appeal","A direct appeal starts with a notice of appeal filed in the trial court under O.C.G.A. §§ 5-6-37 and 5-6-38. The statutory filing period (commonly 30 days after entry of judgment, extended by a timely motion for new trial or similar motion) was not verified from collected text. The notice must show service on all opposing counsel or parties by mail, personal, or electronic service, with names and full mailing address","#st-ga-appeals"],["One extension of the notice-of-appeal deadline, requested before it runs","Georgia · appeals · Starting a direct appeal","Under O.C.G.A. § 5-6-39, only one extension may be granted, it cannot exceed the original period, and the request must be made before the original period expires. A request may be filed in the Court of Appeals only after a bona fide effort to get the extension from the trial court, explaining why it could not be obtained, with a stamped “filed” copy of the order and the filing fee. Ga. Ct. App. R. 16(a)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Transcript costs and record transmission","Georgia · appeals · Starting a direct appeal","An attorney who files a motion for new trial or a notice of appeal designating a transcript is personally responsible for paying the court reporter, and the filing certifies that the transcript has been ordered. The trial clerk compiles and transmits the record under O.C.G.A. § 5-6-43. Original evidence is not sent: in the Court of Appeals, move that court for an order; in the Supreme Court, include a description or ","#st-ga-appeals"],["Objections to the record must be made below","Georgia · appeals · Starting a direct appeal","The appellee waives objections to the appellant’s failure to comply with the Appellate Practice Act on filing the transcript or transmitting the record unless the objection was made and ruled on in the trial court before transmittal. Ga. Ct. App. R. 20; Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 74","#st-ga-appeals"],["10 days after the certificate of immediate review, no extensions","Georgia · appeals · Interlocutory appeals (certificate plus application)","The application must be filed in the appellate court within 10 days after entry (filing with the clerk) of the trial court’s order granting the certificate of immediate review. It must include a jurisdictional statement and stamped “filed” copies of the order and the certificate, both bearing the judge’s actual signature (no conformed or stamped signatures unless that court’s official practice is electronic signing).","#st-ga-appeals"],["Standard for granting","Georgia · appeals · Interlocutory appeals (certificate plus application)","Granted only when the issue appears dispositive, the order appears erroneous and will probably cause substantial error at trial (Court of Appeals adds: or will adversely affect the appellant’s rights until final judgment, in which case the appeal is expedited), or precedent is desirable. The applicant bears the burden. Ga. Ct. App. R. 30(b); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 31","#st-ga-appeals"],["What to attach; 100-page exhibit cap in the Court of Appeals","Georgia · appeals · Interlocutory appeals (certificate plus application)","Attach the motion that produced the order and any responses, and enough of the record to show the issues in context; insufficient material means denial. In the Court of Appeals, e-filed exhibits are capped at 100 pages total (excluding the brief, index, order, certificate, the underlying motion and responses, and transcripts) unless counsel files a signed good-faith certificate; unnecessary exhibits can draw a sancti","#st-ga-appeals"],["Responses, grant, and notice of appeal","Georgia · appeals · Interlocutory appeals (certificate plus application)","Responses are due within 10 days of docketing (encouraged, not required unless ordered). If the application is granted, file a notice of appeal in the trial court within 10 days of the grant order. No pleadings are accepted more than 30 days after the order granting, denying, or dismissing the application or the reconsideration motion. Ga. Ct. App. R. 30(i)–(k); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 30","#st-ga-appeals"],["Direct appeals for anti-SLAPP and certain immunity rulings","Georgia · appeals · Interlocutory appeals (certificate plus application)","Official summaries say O.C.G.A. § 5-6-34 was amended in 2016 to allow a direct appeal of orders on anti-SLAPP claims or motions, and in 2025 (SB 298, effective July 1, 2025) to provide for direct appeals relating to the grant, denial, or refusal to rule on certain immunities and privileges. The amended statute was not collected; check whether your order qualifies before relying on a direct appeal. O.C.G.A. § 5-6-34 (","#st-ga-appeals"],["30 days from entry; 7 days for dispossessory","Georgia · appeals · Discretionary applications","For final judgments in case types covered by O.C.G.A. § 5-6-35, file an application in the appellate court within 30 days after entry of the order (7 days for dispossessory orders under O.C.G.A. § 44-7-56). Include a jurisdictional statement, enumeration of errors, and a stamped “filed” copy of the order with the judge’s actual signature. An extension is possible only by a Rule 40(b) motion filed on or before the due","#st-ga-appeals"],["Standards for granting","Georgia · appeals · Discretionary applications","Court of Appeals: reversible error appears to exist; precedent is desirable; further development of the common law (particularly in divorce cases) is desirable; or the application seeks review of a final, timely divorce decree with possible merit (the last two grounds need a counsel certificate of good faith, and a frivolous application can bring a sanction up to $10,000). Supreme Court: reversible error appears to e","#st-ga-appeals"],["Supersedeas effect, response, and notice of appeal","Georgia · appeals · Discretionary applications","Filing a discretionary application acts as supersedeas to the same extent as a notice of appeal (O.C.G.A. § 5-6-35(h)). Responses are due within 10 days of docketing. If granted, file the notice of appeal within 10 days of the grant order. The same 100-page exhibit cap and 30-day late-filing bar apply as for interlocutory applications. Ga. Ct. App. R. 31(g), (i)–(k), 40(a); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 33","#st-ga-appeals"],["2025 amendments","Georgia · appeals · Discretionary applications","Official summaries report 2025 amendments to §§ 5-6-34 and 5-6-35 (HB 176: direct appeals from guilty pleas now by application, and out-of-time motion for new trial or notice of appeal procedure in new § 5-6-39.1; HB 155: appellate procedures for zoning and quasi-judicial land-use decisions, Part I effective Jan. 1, 2026). Statute text not collected. 2025 Acts 252 (HB 176), 128 (HB 155) (Legislative Counsel summary)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Court of Appeals: 20 / 40 / 20","Georgia · appeals · Briefing schedule","Appellant’s brief and enumeration of errors: within 20 days after docketing (failure can mean dismissal and contempt). Appellee: within 40 days after docketing or 20 days after the appellant’s brief, whichever is later; optional except for the State in criminal cases. Reply: within 20 days after the appellee’s brief; only one reply. “Other parties” may file one brief by the appellee’s deadline. Extension motions must","#st-ga-appeals"],["Supreme Court: 20 / 40 / 50, including cross-appeals","Georgia · appeals · Briefing schedule","Appellant’s principal brief within 20 days after docketing; appellee within 40 days after docketing or 20 days after the appellant’s brief, whichever is later; reply within 50 days after docketing or 10 days after the response, whichever is later. Cross-appeals run on the same schedule from the cross-appeal’s docketing. After certiorari is granted, the same schedule runs from the grant order and briefs address only t","#st-ga-appeals"],["Amicus briefs","Georgia · appeals · Briefing schedule","Without leave: within 10 days after the supported party’s brief is due (supporting neither party: 10 days after the response or reply is due); for applications in the Court of Appeals, within 5 days after the supported party’s application or response is due. Later only with leave, by motion attaching the brief. Amici cannot move for reconsideration. Supreme Court amicus briefs are limited to 7,000 words. Ga. Ct. App.","#st-ga-appeals"],["No letter briefs; supplemental briefs only with leave","Georgia · appeals · Briefing schedule","New authority after briefing must come in as a supplemental brief on motion with the proposed brief attached. In the Supreme Court, a granted supplemental brief is due within 5 days of approval and any response within 5 days after it is filed. Supplemental briefs cannot add enumerations of error. Ga. Ct. App. R. 27; Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 24","#st-ga-appeals"],["Court of Appeals limits","Georgia · appeals · Length, format, and content","Principal briefs: 8,400 words (civil) or 14,000 words (criminal). Reply briefs, supplemental briefs, emergency motions, motions for reconsideration and responses, and other motions: 4,200 words. Paper filers: 30 pages civil, 50 criminal, 15 for replies and motions. Include the certificate “This submission does not exceed the word count limit imposed by Rule 24.” Format: 14-point serif proportional font, double-spaced","#st-ga-appeals"],["Supreme Court limits","Georgia · appeals · Length, format, and content","Principal briefs: 10,000 words; reply briefs: 7,000; petitions for certiorari, applications, motions, and responses: 7,000; motions for reconsideration: 5,000; death-penalty principal briefs: 20,000. Paper limits are 30, 20, 20, and 10 pages respectively. Certify “This submission does not exceed the word-count limit imposed by Rule 20.” Format: serif font no smaller than 13-point Century Schoolbook (footnotes 11-poin","#st-ga-appeals"],["Enumerations not argued are abandoned","Georgia · appeals · Length, format, and content","Each enumerated error must be supported by argument, citation of authority, and specific citation to the record (electronic record cites as volume and PDF page, e.g., V2-46); otherwise it may be deemed abandoned and the court will not search the record. Georgia cases must be cited to the official reporters with volume and page. Ga. Ct. App. R. 24(d), 25(d); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 22","#st-ga-appeals"],["Certificate of Interested Persons in the Supreme Court","Georgia · appeals · Length, format, and content","With the first filing (including an extension motion), the appellant, applicant, petitioner, or movant must file a separate Certificate of Interested Persons listing every party ever named, every attorney who appeared below, and every witness called at any trial or hearing. The appellee must supplement it. Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 26.1","#st-ga-appeals"],["Court of Appeals","Georgia · appeals · Oral argument","Argument is only by grant of a request filed as a motion within 20 days after docketing (a briefing extension does not extend it), stating with particularity how argument will aid decision and the other side’s position. 15 minutes per side. No argument on applications or motions. Remote appearance by request, ideally at least 7 days before argument. Ga. Ct. App. R. 28(a), (d)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Supreme Court","Georgia · appeals · Oral argument","Mandatory in granted certiorari, death-penalty, interim-review, and certified-question cases; otherwise by request filed within 20 days after docketing (appellee: within 10 days after the appellant’s brief), up to 3,000 words, stating specific reasons. 20 minutes per side (30 in death-penalty appeals); the appellant may reserve up to 5 minutes for rebuttal by notice two weeks ahead. Remote argument only in exceptiona","#st-ga-appeals"],["Court of Appeals: 10 days, by 4:30 p.m.","Georgia · appeals · Reconsideration (rehearing)","File within 10 days from the judgment or dismissal, received by 4:30 p.m. (later filings count as the next business day). Granted only if the Court overlooked a material fact, statute, or controlling decision requiring a different result, or misapplied the law. No second motion without permission; no oral argument; 4,200 words. Ga. Ct. App. R. 37(a)–(e), (h)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Supreme Court: 10 days","Georgia · appeals · Reconsideration (rehearing)","Must be received or e-filed within 10 days from the ruling, attaching the ruling; no prison-mailbox rule. 5,000 words. No second motion without permission. A motion to stay the remittitur to seek U.S. Supreme Court review must be filed with the reconsideration motion or within the time to file one. Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 13(3), 27, 61","#st-ga-appeals"],["Notice of intent, then petition","Georgia · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court of Georgia (certiorari)","File a notice of intent to apply for certiorari in the Court of Appeals within 10 days after judgment or the ruling on reconsideration (reconsideration is not a prerequisite). File the petition in the Supreme Court within 20 days after judgment or the reconsideration ruling, with the filing fee and a copy of the Court of Appeals decision and any reconsideration order, and file notice of the petition in the Court of A","#st-ga-appeals"],["Standard and form","Georgia · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court of Georgia (certiorari)","Granted only in cases of great concern, gravity, or importance to the public, such as conflicts in the case law, precedent that warrants reconsideration, or an important recurring question of state law; generally not to correct error in factual findings or the application of a correct rule. Questions presented go first, before the tables. Considered without oral argument. Responses within 20 days (failure to respond ","#st-ga-appeals"],["Remittitur","Georgia · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court of Georgia (certiorari)","Court of Appeals: remittitur goes to the trial court after 10 days from judgment unless reconsideration or a notice of intent to seek certiorari is filed. Supreme Court: after 10 days following judgment or denial of reconsideration. The trial clerk files the remittitur with the original action. Ga. Ct. App. R. 39; Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 60; USCR 38","#st-ga-appeals"],["Supersedeas","Georgia · appeals · Stays, emergencies, and sanctions","In civil cases, a notice of appeal serves as supersedeas once the appellant pays all trial-court costs; on the appellee’s motion, the trial court may require a supersedeas bond. In the Supreme Court, a supersedeas motion must say whether a stay was sought in the trial court, include the order and stamped notice of appeal, and explain the urgency. Ga. Ct. App. R. 40(a); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 9","#st-ga-appeals"],["Emergency motions","Georgia · appeals · Stays, emergencies, and sanctions","Used sparingly to preserve jurisdiction or prevent mootness. Must explain why an order is needed and time-sensitive, attach stamped copies of the order and any notice of appeal, show prior service, and include the filing fee if the case is not yet docketed. Ga. Ct. App. R. 40(b); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 26(3)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Frivolous-appeal penalties","Georgia · appeals · Stays, emergencies, and sanctions","Court of Appeals: up to $10,000 against a party or counsel in a civil case, entered as a money judgment. Supreme Court: up to $2,500, plus penalties for appeals taken for delay under O.C.G.A. § 5-6-6. Discourteous personal remarks about judges or counsel are forbidden. Ga. Ct. App. R. 7(e), 10; Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 6, 7","#st-ga-appeals"],["Mandatory e-filing; filing times","Georgia · appeals · Filing, fees, and service","Attorneys must e-file through eFast (Court of Appeals) or SCED (Supreme Court) unless exempted. E-filings count as filed through 11:59 p.m. if they comply with the rules, except Court of Appeals reconsideration motions (4:30 p.m.). Noncompliant filings are rejected and count as filed only when resubmitted in compliance. Keep eFast/SCED contact information current; failure to receive notice is no excuse. Ga. Ct. App. ","#st-ga-appeals"],["Fees","Georgia · appeals · Filing, fees, and service","$300 in civil cases and $80 in criminal cases, due with the application or, in direct appeals, with the appellant’s brief; the clerk will not accept the filing without it (or an approved indigence affidavit). No additional fee for the brief after a granted application. Court of Appeals admission: $30 (special expedited admission $200 extra). Ga. Ct. App. R. 5, 9(a)–(b); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 4(2), 5","#st-ga-appeals"],["Service is separate from e-filing","Georgia · appeals · Filing, fees, and service","Serve by mail, personal service, or email, and say which in the certificate of service. Email service requires a prior agreement and a specific certification, and the certificate must list each recipient’s full name, email, phone, and mailing address. Filing in the e-filing system is not service. Documents without a proper certificate are not accepted. Ga. Ct. App. R. 6; Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 14","#st-ga-appeals"],["What counts as binding Court of Appeals precedent","Georgia · appeals · Precedent and decisions","Since August 1, 2020, a published opinion in which a majority of the judges fully concur is binding. Earlier division opinions bind only if all three judges fully concurred; otherwise they are “physical precedent” (persuasive only) and must be marked as such when cited. Unreported decisions and Rule 36 affirmances without opinion are not precedent. A division can overrule precedent only unanimously after consulting t","#st-ga-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (direct appeal)","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the appealable judgment or order.\nNot verified from collected text; confirm in the code, including tolling by motion for new trial, motion in arrest of judgment, or motion for judgment notwithstanding the verdict. O.C.G.A. § 5-6-38 (statute not collected)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Extension of notice-of-appeal deadline","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","\nOnly one extension, not longer than the original period; try the trial court first. Ga. Ct. App. R. 16(a); O.C.G.A. § 5-6-39","#st-ga-appeals"],["Interlocutory application","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of the order granting the certificate of immediate review.\nNo extensions. The time for the trial court to sign the certificate is in O.C.G.A. § 5-6-34(b) (not collected). Ga. Ct. App. R. 30(a); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 30","#st-ga-appeals"],["Response to interlocutory or discretionary application","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from docketing of the application.\nOptional unless ordered; no extensions in the Court of Appeals. Ga. Ct. App. R. 30(i), 31(i); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 30, 33","#st-ga-appeals"],["Discretionary application","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry (filing) of the trial court order.\n7 days for dispossessory orders (O.C.G.A. § 44-7-56). Extension only by motion filed on or before the due date. Ga. Ct. App. R. 31(a)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Notice of appeal after an application is granted","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from order granting the application.\nFile in the trial court. Ga. Ct. App. R. 30(j), 31(j)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief (Court of Appeals)","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from docketing.\nExtension motion must be filed before the due date. Ga. Ct. App. R. 23(a)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief (Court of Appeals)","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from docketing, or 20 days after appellant’s brief if later.\nRequired of the State in criminal appeals. Ga. Ct. App. R. 23(b)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Reply brief (Court of Appeals)","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from filing of appellee’s brief.\nOne reply only; 4,200 words. Ga. Ct. App. R. 23(c)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief (Supreme Court)","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from docketing (or order granting certiorari).\nSame for cross-appellant from cross-appeal docketing. Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 10(1)(a), 45","#st-ga-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief (Supreme Court)","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from docketing, or 20 days after appellant’s brief if later. Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 10(1)(b)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Reply brief (Supreme Court)","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","50 days from docketing, or 10 days after the response brief if later.\n7,000 words. Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 10(2)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Oral argument request (Court of Appeals)","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from docketing.\nNot extended by a brief extension. Ga. Ct. App. R. 28(a)(2)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Oral argument request (Supreme Court, appellant)","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from docketing.\nAppellee: within 10 days after appellant’s brief is filed. Renew within 5 days after a transfer. Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 51(1)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Motion for reconsideration (Court of Appeals)","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from judgment or dismissal.\nMust be received by 4:30 p.m. Ga. Ct. App. R. 37(b)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Motion for reconsideration (Supreme Court)","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from the ruling.\nMust be received or e-filed within the 10 days; no mailbox rule. Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 13(3), 27(2)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Notice of intent to apply for certiorari (filed in Court of Appeals)","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from judgment, or order ruling on reconsideration. Ga. Ct. App. R. 38(a)(1)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Petition for certiorari (filed in Supreme Court)","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from judgment, or disposition of reconsideration motion.\nFile notice of the petition in the Court of Appeals the same day. Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 38(2)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Response to certiorari petition","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from filing of the petition.\nReply within 10 days after the response (Rule 42.1). Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 42","#st-ga-appeals"],["Notice of intent to seek U.S. Supreme Court certiorari (filed in Court of Appeals)","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from supreme Court of Georgia’s denial of certiorari.\nGenerally does not stay the remittitur. Ga. Ct. App. R. 38(b)(1)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Recusal motion (Court of Appeals)","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from docketing.\nUnless good cause shown. Ga. Ct. App. R. 44(a)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Recusal motion (Supreme Court)","Georgia · appeals · Deadlines","5 days from learning of the grounds.\nAnd no later than 10 days before oral argument. Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 26(2)","#st-ga-appeals"],["Supreme Court of Guam","Guam · courts","Guam’s highest court and its only appellate court: there is no intermediate court of appeals. Three full-time justices (a Chief Justice and two Associate Justices), plus Designated Justices appointed to sit when needed. Hears appeals from final judgments of the Superior Court, discretionary interlocutory appeals, original writs (mandamus, prohibition and similar), certified questions from the Superior Court, and decl","#st-gu"],["Superior Court of Guam","Guam · courts","The single, island-wide court of general jurisdiction for civil, criminal, family, juvenile and probate matters: a Presiding Judge and six other judges by statute (7 GCA §§ 3105, 4101). Also runs traffic court and treatment courts (drug, mental health, veterans, reentry). Hears appeals from the Traffic Division (fines over $150, de novo, notice within 5 court days) and from the Small Claims Division (7 GCA § 4102).","#st-gu"],["Small Claims Division of the Superior Court","Guam · courts","Claims, counterclaims and cross-claims of $10,000 or less; no attorney’s fees may be awarded. A defendant facing a claim over $5,000 who applies in time is entitled to transfer the case to the regular civil docket (7 GCA §§ 4202–4203).","#st-gu"],["District Court of Guam","Guam · courts","A separate federal territorial court with its own rules and admission, covered elsewhere in Consi. Guam’s pro hac vice rule says it does not govern federal courts or agencies unless they adopt it (GRGAPL 8.01(a)(5)).","#st-gu"],["Citizen Participation in Government Act of 1998 (7 GCA §§ 17101–17109)","Guam · anti-SLAPP","Guam has an anti-SLAPP statute. It was enacted as uncodified P.L. 24-297 (Dec. 3, 1998) and codified by the Compiler as Chapter 17 of Title 7. Its core is an immunity: acts in furtherance of the constitutional right to petition, “including seeking relief, influencing action, informing, communicating and otherwise participating in the processes of government,” are immune from liability regardless of intent or purpose,","#st-gu-slapp"],["Procedure: summary-judgment motion, discovery stay, clear-and-convincing burden on the plaintiff","Guam · anti-SLAPP","The Act applies to any motion to dispose of a claim on the ground that it is based on, relates to, or responds to the movant’s protected petitioning (§ 17105). “Motion” is broad: a motion to dismiss, for summary judgment, for judgment on the pleadings, to strike, or any other filing to dispose of a claim (§ 17103(d)). Once filed:\n• the motion is treated as one for summary judgment, heard on the time frame used for pr","#st-gu-slapp"],["Mandatory fees and sanctions, plus a damages claim (SLAPP-back)","Guam · anti-SLAPP","A moving party who wins dismissal must be awarded, “without regards to any limit under Guam law,” the costs of litigation including reasonable attorney and expert witness fees incurred on the motion. The court must also impose sanctions on the responding party, its attorneys or law firms sufficient to deter repetition (§ 17106(g)).\nSeparately, a person harmed by a claim filed in violation of § 17104 may sue the perso","#st-gu-slapp"],["Appeal: expedited appeal of a denial, or of a failure to rule promptly","Guam · anti-SLAPP","The moving party has a right to an expedited appeal from an order denying the motion, or from the trial court’s failure to rule on it in expedited fashion (§ 17106(a)(2)). Discovery stays suspended during the appeal (§ 17106(b)). The collected Guam Rules of Appellate Procedure (2014 compilation) contain no rule specific to these appeals, so the notice of appeal timing in GRAP 4(a) (30 days from entry) is the safe ass","#st-gu-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: verified application in each court, Guam sponsor of record, $1,250 fee","Guam · out of state","An out-of-state lawyer (admitted in a U.S. state, territory, commonwealth or D.C., and not disbarred or suspended anywhere) who does not live or regularly work in Guam may be admitted for a particular case. Steps:\n• File a verified application with the court where the case is pending. For a Guam agency proceeding, file with the Supreme Court of Guam.\n• Serve it on all parties who have appeared and on the Office of Re","#st-gu-oos"],["Standards, objections and discipline","Guam · out of state","An application “ordinarily should be granted.” A court may deny or revoke admission if it may harm the prompt and fair administration of justice or other parties’ interests, if the client may be inadequately represented, or if the applicant appears so often that it amounts to regular practice in Guam. There is no numeric cap; frequency is judged case by case.\nThe Office of Regulation Counsel or any party may object w","#st-gu-oos"],["Temporary practice without admission (Rule 8.02 and GRPC 5.5)","Guam · out of state","Without pro hac vice admission, an out-of-state lawyer may: consult with a Guam lawyer about that lawyer’s client; consult with a potential client in Guam about possible retention; prepare for a Guam case while reasonably believing they are eligible for pro hac vice; work in Guam on a case pending or anticipated elsewhere; serve a Guam client from outside Guam; and handle arbitration, mediation or other ADR anywhere.","#st-gu-oos"],["Full admission: Guam Bar membership required; UBE transfer still needs the local exam portion","Guam · out of state","No one may practice law in Guam unless a member in good standing of the Guam Bar Association, an integrated bar. Guam administers the NextGen UBE (first given July 2026). A transferred Legacy UBE score of 262+ or NextGen UBE score of 612+, earned within the prior 3 years, is accepted, but the applicant must still pass the local-question portion of the Guam exam and meet character and MPRE requirements. CNMI lawyers a","#st-gu-oos"],["E-filing access: register only after pro hac vice is granted; 11:59 p.m. Chamorro Standard Time","Guam · out of state","Lawyers admitted in Guam, pro hac vice lawyers and self-represented litigants may register for the Judiciary’s electronic document management system. A pro hac vice lawyer must be admitted first, then register, and gets remote access only to the cases where admitted. The Superior Court eFile page says e-filing covers civil cases and is being expanded to other case types. The rules read as permissive (documents the fi","#st-gu-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances","Guam · out of state","A lawyer whose role is limited to one or more matters files a document titled “Limited Appearance.” The lawyer then receives e-service until filing a notice of completion of limited appearance. The client keeps receiving service throughout. GRPC 1.2(c) allows limiting the scope of representation if the limitation is reasonable and the client gives informed consent. EFR 3.22; GRPC 1.2(c)","#st-gu-oos"],["Off-island counsel and fax filing in the Superior Court","Guam · out of state","GRCP 5(f) still governs fax filing. An off-island attorney who has local counsel must fax the document to local counsel, who files a plain-paper copy. An attorney who is not required to have local counsel may fax-file only in emergencies. Faxes received after 5:00 p.m. on the due date are untimely, and the originals must reach the clerk within 14 days. GRCP 5(f)(1)–(3), (9)","#st-gu-oos"],["Governing code: Guam Rules of Professional Conduct, based on the 2002 ABA Model Rules","Guam · ethics","The Supreme Court adopted the GRPC by Promulgation Order No. 04-002 (Sept. 29, 2003), drawing from the 2002 ABA Model Rules. The only amendment since is Rule 1.2(d) (2019). The official text has the Preamble and Scope but no rule-by-rule Comments. As a result, ABA changes made after 2002 are generally not in Guam’s rules. That includes the 2003 confidentiality exceptions, the 2009 lateral screening rule, the 2012 tec","#st-gu-ethics"],["Rule 1.6: no crime-fraud or conflict-check exceptions, no stated duty to safeguard data","Guam · ethics","Guam’s Rule 1.6(b) allows disclosure only to prevent reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm, to get ethics advice, for lawyer–client controversies and defenses, and to comply with law or a court order. It lacks the ABA exceptions for preventing, mitigating or rectifying client crime or fraud causing substantial financial injury that used the lawyer’s services (ABA 1.6(b)(2)–(3)). It also lacks the except","#st-gu-ethics"],["Rule 1.2(d): cannabis advice expressly allowed, with a federal-law warning","Guam · ethics","A lawyer may counsel or assist a client regarding Guam’s marijuana and cannabis-related laws. Where Guam law differs from federal law, the lawyer must also advise the client on federal law and policy. GRPC 1.2(d) (PRM 19-001-01, Nov. 8, 2019)","#st-gu-ethics"],["Rule 1.10: no screening cures a lateral lawyer’s conflict","Guam · ethics","Guam’s Rule 1.10(a) imputes a conflict under Rules 1.7 or 1.9 to every lawyer in the firm. The only exception is a purely personal-interest conflict. There is no ABA-style 1.10(a)(2) exception for screening a laterally hired lawyer with written notice, so a lateral’s former-client conflict disqualifies the firm unless the affected client waives under Rule 1.7. GRPC 1.10(a), (c)","#st-gu-ethics"],["Rule 1.8(e): no humanitarian gifts to indigent litigation clients","Guam · ethics","Financial assistance to a client in litigation is limited to advancing court costs and litigation expenses (repayment may be contingent on outcome) and paying those costs for an indigent client. The 2020 ABA exception for modest gifts for food, rent, transportation or medicine to indigent pro bono clients is not in Guam’s rule. GRPC 1.8(e)","#st-gu-ethics"],["Advertising and misconduct rules are the pre-2018 versions; no Rule 8.4(g)","Guam · ethics","Guam keeps the older ABA advertising scheme:\n• Rule 7.2: a lawyer may pay only for ads, legal service plans or qualified referral services, and a law practice purchase. Every ad must name at least one responsible lawyer or firm and its office address.\n• Rule 7.3: bans in-person, live telephone and real-time electronic solicitation for pecuniary gain, except of lawyers and family, close personal or prior professional ","#st-gu-ethics"],["Trust accounts: records for five years","Guam · ethics","Client and third-party funds go in a separate account maintained where the lawyer’s office is, unless the client or third person consents to another location. Complete records must be kept for five years after the representation ends (the official text still shows “[five years]” in brackets, copied from the ABA model). Advance fees go into the trust account until earned. GRPC 1.15(a), (c)","#st-gu-ethics"],["Post-judgment motions: 10 days, counted on the calendar since October 2023","Guam · land mine","Renewed judgment as a matter of law, amended findings, new trial, and motions to alter or amend the judgment are all due no later than 10 days after entry of judgment. Federal practice allows 28 days. The court cannot extend these deadlines. Since PRM 06-006-24 (Oct. 19, 2023), GRCP 6(a) counts every day, including weekends and holidays, so the 10 days are calendar days. A Rule 60 motion tolls the appeal time only if","#st-gu-unique"],["Statement of jurisdiction within 10 days of the notice of appeal, or the appeal is dismissed","Guam · land mine","Within 10 days after filing the notice of appeal, the appellant must submit a statement of jurisdiction to the Supreme Court. It must give the basis for finality or appealability, the statutory basis for jurisdiction, and the docket-entry date, with a legible copy of the judgment showing its entry date. If it is not filed, or if it shows no final appealable judgment has been entered, the appeal is dismissed for lack ","#st-gu-unique"],["Two clocks: the Supreme Court skips weekends for short periods, the Superior Court does not","Guam · land mine","GRAP 11(a)(1)(B) still excludes Saturdays, Sundays and legal holidays from periods shorter than 11 days, unless stated as calendar days. GRCP 6(a), as amended in 2023, counts every day. A 10-day deadline in the Supreme Court can therefore end days later than a 10-day deadline in the Superior Court. GRAP adds 3 calendar days after service, including electronic service. GRCP adds 3 days after service by mail, clerk-lea","#st-gu-unique"],["Answer in 20 days; Government of Guam gets 60; serve within 90","Guam · land mine","A defendant must answer within 20 days after service of the summons and complaint. The Government of Guam and its officers or agencies get 60 days after service on the Attorney General or agency counsel. After a ruling on a Rule 12 motion, the responsive pleading is due within 10 days. A plaintiff must serve within 90 days after filing; this was 180 days before October 19, 2023. A waiver request must allow 30 days (6","#st-gu-unique"],["Motion briefing runs from the motion’s filing date, and oral argument must be requested","Guam · land mine","In Superior Court civil cases, an opposition is due 28 days after the motion is filed, not after service. The reply is due 42 days after the motion is filed. Parties may extend each by stipulation by no more than 7 days, and late papers are not considered without leave. Memoranda are limited to 20 pages and replies to 10. Request oral argument on the notice of motion or Form 2 within 7 days after the last paper is du","#st-gu-unique"],["One set of interrogatories, 50 questions; discovery motions need a joint stipulation","Guam · land mine","Without leave, each party may serve only one set of interrogatories and one set of requests for admission on any other party. Each set is capped at 50 including every subpart. Answers must quote each question in full. Before any discovery motion, counsel must meet and confer. If disputes remain, they must file with the motion a written stipulation setting out each issue verbatim with each side’s contentions; otherwis","#st-gu-unique"],["Jury demand within 10 days after the last pleading","Guam · land mine","A jury must be demanded in writing and served no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue. Failure to serve and file the demand waives a jury. GRCP 6(a) now counts those 10 days on the calendar. GRCP 38(b), (d); GRCP 6(a)","#st-gu-unique"],["Appeal mechanics: file in the Superior Court and pay the Supreme Court’s fee at the same time","Guam · land mine","The notice of appeal is filed with the Clerk of the Superior Court within 30 days after entry of the judgment or order. The appellant must also send the Supreme Court a file-stamped copy. The non-refundable Supreme Court docket fee ($375 on the posted schedule) is paid to the Clerk of the Supreme Court when the notice is filed below, and a cross-appellant pays its own fee. A second party may appeal within 14 days aft","#st-gu-unique"],["Pro hac vice does not follow the case up on appeal","Guam · land mine","Admission in the Superior Court does not carry over to the Supreme Court. File a new verified application there; no second fee is charged for the same lawyer in the same case. Your Guam sponsor is the lawyer of record throughout. GRGAPL 8.01(e)(2), App. A(9)","#st-gu-unique"],["Judge challenge must come before the first fact hearing","Guam · land mine","No peremptory challenge exists. A verified for-cause statement must be filed at the earliest practicable opportunity after you learn of the ground, and in all events before the hearing of any issue of fact starts. Waiting until after an adverse ruling at trial forfeits the challenge. 7 GCA § 6107(a)–(b)","#st-gu-unique"],["Appellate briefs: 14-point type, colored covers, excerpts of record in every case","Guam · land mine","Proportional fonts must be 14-point or larger, with serifs in the text. Page and word limits:\n• Opening and answering briefs: 30 pages or 14,000 words.\n• Reply briefs: 15 pages or half the word count.\n• In cross-appeals, the appellee’s principal and response brief may run 35 pages or 16,500 words.\nThe court disfavors motions to exceed the limits; file one at least 14 days before the brief is due (7 days for a reply).","#st-gu-unique"],["Summary judgment: 30 days after discovery closes, with a statement of undisputed facts","Guam · land mine","Unless the court orders otherwise, summary judgment motions may be filed up to 30 days after the close of all discovery. A motion must include a statement of the issues and a separate “Statement of Undisputed Material Facts” with record citations. The opposing party must respond to that statement. GRCP 56(b); CVR 56.1","#st-gu-unique"],["No peremptory challenge of a judge; for-cause disqualification only","Guam · judges","Guam has no peremptory (no-cause) judge challenge. The 1985 drafters considered and rejected California’s affidavit-of-prejudice procedure (Cal. Code Civ. Proc. § 170.6), fearing the bar would make “de facto assignments of judges.” Grounds track 28 U.S.C. § 455. A judge must step aside where impartiality might reasonably be questioned, and for personal bias, prior involvement as a lawyer or government counsel, financ","#st-gu-unique"],["Disqualification procedure: verified statement before any fact hearing; 10 days to answer","Guam · judges","If the judge does not declare a disqualification, a party who has appeared may file and serve a written, verified statement of the facts. It must be filed at the earliest practicable opportunity after discovering the facts, and in any event before the hearing of any issue of fact begins. Serve it on every party and on the judge.\nThe judge has 10 days to consent in writing or file a verified answer. Failing to answer ","#st-gu-unique"],["Petitioning activity has statutory immunity regardless of motive","Guam · speech","The Citizen Participation in Government Act makes acts in furtherance of the right to petition (seeking relief, influencing action, informing and communicating with government or the electorate) immune from liability “regardless of intent or purpose.” The only exception is conduct not aimed at procuring any government or electoral action, result or outcome. This is a substantive defense, not only a procedural motion,","#st-gu-unique"],["Organic Act bill of rights: not reviewed","Guam · speech","Guam has no constitution of its own. Its civil-rights guarantees, including speech and petition, are in the federal Organic Act of Guam (48 U.S.C. § 1421b). The Organic Act text and Guam case law were not in the collected sources, so whether Guam courts read these protections more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked. 48 U.S.C. § 1421b (not in collected texts)","#st-gu-unique"],["Governor and Legislature can ask the Supreme Court for declaratory judgments","Guam · unique rules","The Governor (in writing) or the Legislature (by resolution) may ask the Supreme Court for a declaratory judgment on the interpretation of any law within the Guam courts’ jurisdiction and on questions about their own powers and duties. The Court may act only on matters of great public interest where normal process would cause undue delay. Private parties cannot use this procedure, but interested parties must be allow","#st-gu-unique"],["Evenly divided panel affirms; unpublished memorandum opinions cannot be cited","Guam · unique rules","If a Supreme Court panel is evenly divided, the decision appealed from is sustained. Opinions are published unless designated otherwise. Memorandum opinions are never published, and unpublished opinions may not be cited except for law of the case, res judicata, collateral estoppel, or a criminal case involving the same respondent. 7 GCA § 3108(c)(1); GRAP 27(b)(1)","#st-gu-unique"],["Court boxes: service is complete when the court drops a paper in your box","Guam · unique rules","Every active Guam Bar member practicing in Guam with a Guam office is assigned a box at the Superior Court Clerk’s office. The court serves orders, judgments and notices by depositing them in the box, and service is complete on deposit. Check the box: deadlines can run from that deposit. Only the court may serve this way. GRCP 5(g); GRAP 10(c)","#st-gu-unique"],["Bluebook citations; supply copies of hard-to-find authority","Guam · unique rules","Citations must follow the most recent edition of The Bluebook. A party must give the court a copy of any authority it cites that is not available in the Guam Law Library or a generally recognized legal research database (for example, decisions of other Pacific island courts). GR 4.1","#st-gu-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope of rules","Guam · civil rules","The GRCP govern civil actions in the Superior Court of Guam. Most rules are taken from the 2003 Federal Rules of Civil Procedure (before the 2007 restyling and the 2009 time-computation changes), with Guam substitutions. Several rules (4, 6, 16, 47, 48, 55, 56) were updated in 2014, 2022 and 2023 to later federal text.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","Guam · civil rules","There is one form of action, the civil action. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencement of action","Guam · civil rules","A civil action is commenced by filing a complaint. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 4. Summons","Guam · civil rules","Restyled to the 2021 federal text by PRM 06-006-24 (Oct. 19, 2023). Any non-party 18 or older may serve. A waiver request must give the defendant at least 30 days to return it (60 days if outside Guam). A defendant who waives has 60 days from the request to answer (90 if outside Guam). A defendant in Guam or a U.S. jurisdiction who refuses to waive without good cause pays the later service costs and fees. Service on ","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 4.1. Service of other process","Guam · civil rules","Process other than a summons or subpoena is served by the marshal, a deputy marshal, or a specially appointed person, with proof under Rule 4(l). Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 5. Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","Guam · civil rules","Service on a represented party is made on the attorney: by hand, by leaving at the office or home, by mail (complete on mailing), or by consented electronic means (complete on transmission). Discovery papers are not filed unless ordered or used. Guam additions: Rule 5(f) allows fax filing on conditions. An off-island attorney with local counsel faxes to local counsel, who files the paper. Faxes after 5:00 p.m. on the","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 6. Time","Guam · civil rules","Amended by PRM 06-006-24 (Oct. 19, 2023). Every day now counts, including weekends and holidays. If the last day is a weekend, holiday, or a day the clerk’s office is inaccessible, the period runs to the next business day. Legal holidays include those in 1 GCA § 1000(a)–(b) and others declared by U.S. or Guam authorities. Before 2023, periods under 11 days excluded weekends and holidays. The court may extend time for","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings allowed; form of motions","Guam · civil rules","Allowed pleadings: complaint, answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a cross-claim, and third-party complaint and answer. A reply to an answer is allowed only if ordered. Motions must be in writing with particular grounds. Demurrers and pleas are abolished. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","Guam · civil rules","Short and plain statement of jurisdiction, claim and relief demanded. Denials must fairly meet the averments. Affirmative defenses must be pleaded, and the list still includes “discharge in bankruptcy.” Averments not denied are admitted. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","Guam · civil rules","Fraud or mistake must be pleaded with particularity; conditions of mind may be pleaded generally. Special damages must be specifically stated. The admiralty subdivision (h) is omitted. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","Guam · civil rules","Caption, numbered paragraphs, separate counts, and adoption by reference; exhibits are part of the pleading. Follows the 2003 federal rule. See also GR 5.1 (12-point minimum, title 3 inches from top) and CVR 10.1 (separate “jurisdiction” paragraph).","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing; representations to court; sanctions","Guam · civil rules","Every paper must be signed, which certifies a proper purpose and support in law and fact. Safe harbor: a sanctions motion may not be filed until 21 days after service, and only if the paper is not withdrawn or corrected. Monetary sanctions are not allowed against a represented party for frivolous legal contentions. The rule does not apply to discovery. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections; motion for judgment on the pleadings","Guam · civil rules","Answer within 20 days after service (federal: 21). Cross-claim answers and counterclaim replies are also due within 20 days. The Government of Guam and its officers or agencies have 60 days after service on the Attorney General or agency counsel. After denial or postponement of a Rule 12 motion, the responsive pleading is due within 10 days, and within 10 days after a more definite statement is served. Motions to str","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and cross-claim","Guam · civil rules","Compulsory counterclaims arise from the same transaction or occurrence; permissive counterclaims are allowed. Counterclaims against the Government of Guam are not enlarged beyond existing law. An omitted counterclaim may be added by leave. Cross-claims against co-parties are allowed. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","Guam · civil rules","A defendant may implead without leave if the third-party complaint is filed within 10 days after serving the original answer (federal: 14), and otherwise by motion. A plaintiff facing a counterclaim may implead likewise. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Guam · civil rules","One amendment as of course before a responsive pleading is served (or within 20 days if no response is permitted). After that, leave is freely given or written consent is needed. Respond to an amended pleading within the time left or 10 days, whichever is longer. Relation back follows the 2003 federal text; delivery to the Attorney General of Guam satisfies notice for Guam defendants. CVR 15.1 requires reproducing th","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 16. Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","Guam · civil rules","Adopted from the 2007 federal rule by PRM 06-006-16 (July 29, 2014). The judge issues a scheduling order after the Rule 26(f) report or a conference, within the earlier of 120 days after any defendant is served or 90 days after any defendant appears. It must limit time to join parties, amend, complete discovery and file motions. The schedule is modified only for good cause. The final pretrial order is modified only t","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 17. Parties plaintiff and defendant; capacity","Guam · civil rules","Action is prosecuted in the name of the real party in interest; when a Guam statute so provides, it is brought in the name of the Government of Guam. Capacity and representation of minors and incompetents. Follows the 2003 federal rule and GRCP 17 (1990).","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims and remedies","Guam · civil rules","A party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 19. Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","Guam · civil rules","Required joinder where complete relief or protection of interests requires it; equity-and-good-conscience analysis when joinder is not feasible; the pleading must name absent persons and say why they are not joined. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","Guam · civil rules","Parties may join or be joined when claims arise from the same transaction or occurrence with a common question. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and non-joinder of parties","Guam · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped and claims severed on just terms. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","Guam · civil rules","Claimants exposing the plaintiff to double or multiple liability may be required to interplead, even if their claims lack a common origin; available by cross-claim or counterclaim. Taken from GRCP 22 (1990); no statutory-interpleader counterpart.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","Guam · civil rules","Prerequisites, types, notice, and court approval of dismissal or compromise follow the 2003 federal rule. Guam’s 23(f): the Supreme Court may permit an appeal from an order granting or denying certification if application is made within 10 days after entry (federal: 14). The appeal does not stay proceedings unless ordered.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 23.1. Derivative actions by shareholders","Guam · civil rules","Verified complaint, contemporaneous-ownership and demand allegations, adequacy of representation, and court approval of dismissal or settlement. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 23.2. Actions relating to unincorporated associations","Guam · civil rules","Members may sue or be sued as representatives of the association if they fairly and adequately protect its interests. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","Guam · civil rules","Intervention of right and permissive intervention on timely application. When the validity of a Guam Legislature act affecting the public interest is questioned in a case where the government is not a party, the court must notify the Attorney General. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","Guam · civil rules","After death is suggested on the record, a motion to substitute must be made within 90 days or the action is dismissed as to the deceased party. Also covers incompetency, transfer of interest, and successors of public officers. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 26. General provisions governing discovery; duty of disclosure","Guam · civil rules","Based on the 2003 federal rule. Initial disclosures are due within 14 days after the 26(f) conference, with Guam’s own list of 20 exempt proceedings. Expert disclosures are due at least 90 days before trial (rebuttal within 30 days). Pretrial disclosures are due 30 days before trial, with objections 14 days later. The 26(f) conference must be held at least 21 days before the scheduling conference, and the report is d","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions before action or pending appeal","Guam · civil rules","A verified petition to perpetuate testimony; notice must be served at least 20 days before the hearing. Also covers depositions pending appeal. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Guam · civil rules","Within Guam or any U.S. jurisdiction, before an officer authorized by local law or appointed by the court. Abroad, by treaty, notice, commission or letter rogatory. Interested persons are disqualified. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","Guam · civil rules","Parties may stipulate to deposition and discovery procedures, except extensions that would interfere with court-set deadlines. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions upon oral examination","Guam · civil rules","Leave is required to exceed 10 depositions per side, to depose someone twice, or to depose early (unless the witness is about to leave Guam). Depositions are limited to one day of 7 hours (unless an interpreter is involved). The deponent has 30 days after notice to review and sign the transcript. Follows the 2003 federal rule. Under CVR 30.1, the original transcript goes to the noticing attorney.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions upon written questions","Guam · civil rules","Cross questions are due within 14 days after notice; redirect within 7 days; recross within 7 days. The same 10-deposition limit applies. Follows the 2003 federal rule (pre-2009 periods).","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 32. Use of depositions in court proceedings","Guam · civil rules","A deposition may be used for any purpose if the witness is dead, “outside of Guam” (the federal rule uses 100 miles), unable to attend, or not subpoenable, or in exceptional circumstances. It may not be used against a party who got less than 11 days’ notice and promptly moved for a protective order. Objections and waiver rules follow the 2003 federal text.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories to parties","Guam · civil rules","Up to 50 interrogatories including discrete subparts without leave (federal: 25). Answers or objections are due within 30 days, and objections must be specific. CVR 33.1 further limits each party to one set per opposing party and requires quoting each question in full in the answer.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 34. Production of documents and things; entry upon land","Guam · civil rules","Requests must describe items with reasonable particularity. A written response is due within 30 days. Documents are produced as kept or organized by request category. Non-parties are reached by Rule 45 subpoena. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examinations","Guam · civil rules","The court may order an examination for good cause when a condition is in controversy; the report must be exchanged on request. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","Guam · civil rules","A matter is admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days after service. CVR 33.1 limits each party to one set per opposing party, capped at 50 requests including subparts. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to make disclosure or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","Guam · civil rules","Motions to compel, sanctions for violating orders, preclusion for failure to disclose, sanctions for failure to attend a deposition or respond, and sanctions for failing to help frame a discovery plan. Subdivisions (e) and (f) are omitted. CVR 37.1 requires a meet-and-confer and a joint written stipulation of the disputed issues before any discovery motion. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury trial of right","Guam · civil rules","The jury right under Guam law and the Organic Act is preserved. A written demand must be served no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading on the issue (federal: 14). Another party may demand other issues within 10 days. Failure to demand is a waiver.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","Guam · civil rules","Jury trial on demanded issues unless the parties consent otherwise or no jury right exists under the Organic Act or Guam law. The court may use an advisory jury. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 40. Assignment of cases for trial","Guam · civil rules","Cases are calendared as provided by court rule, the Rules of the Superior Court, or Supreme Court administrative rule; statutory preferences apply. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","Guam · civil rules","The plaintiff may dismiss without order before an answer or summary-judgment motion is served, or by stipulation of all parties who have appeared. A second notice dismissal operates as an adjudication on the merits, counting a prior dismissal in any U.S. state, commonwealth or territory court. Involuntary dismissal and costs of a previously dismissed action follow federal practice. CVR 41.1: cases with no action for ","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials","Guam · civil rules","Consolidation of actions with common questions; separate trials preserving the jury right under the Organic Act and Guam law. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 43. Taking of testimony","Guam · civil rules","Testimony is taken in open court unless Guam law, these rules, or Title 6 GCA (Evidence) provide otherwise. Remote testimony is allowed for good cause in compelling circumstances. Motions may be heard on affidavits. The court may appoint interpreters. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 44. Proof of official record","Guam · civil rules","Authentication of domestic and foreign official records by official publication or attested copy with certificate. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 44.1. Determination of foreign law","Guam · civil rules","A party raising foreign law must give written notice. The court may consider any relevant source, including materials not admissible under Title 6 GCA (Evidence). The ruling is treated as a ruling on a question of law. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","Guam · civil rules","Drawn from the 2003 federal rule and the 2005 Nevada rules. The clerk issues subpoenas signed in blank, or attorneys may sign them. Any non-party 18 or older may serve, tendering one day’s fees and mileage (none for Government of Guam subpoenas). Subpoenas may be served anywhere in Guam. Written objections to production are due within 14 days after service, or before the compliance date if sooner. Parties must get pr","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 46. Exceptions unnecessary","Guam · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary; making known the action desired and the grounds is enough. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 47. Selecting jurors","Guam · civil rules","Restyled to the 2021 federal text by PRM 06-006-24 (Oct. 19, 2023). The court or the parties may examine jurors. Peremptory challenges are allowed in the number set by 7 GCA § 22120 (not collected). The court may excuse a juror for good cause during trial or deliberation. The former alternate-juror subdivision was removed.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 48. Number of jurors; verdict; polling","Guam · civil rules","Restyled to the 2021 federal text by PRM 06-006-24. A jury begins with at least 6 and no more than 12 members. Unless the parties stipulate otherwise, the verdict must be unanimous from at least 6 jurors. Polling is required on a party’s request.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 49. Special verdicts and interrogatories","Guam · civil rules","Special verdicts and general verdicts with interrogatories; rules for inconsistent answers. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 50. Judgment as a matter of law; alternative new-trial motion","Guam · civil rules","A JMOL motion may be made before submission to the jury. A renewed motion is due no later than 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28), and cannot be extended. A new-trial motion by a party against whom JMOL is entered is also due within 10 days. Conditional new-trial rulings follow the federal rule. Since Oct. 19, 2023, GRCP 6(a) counts these as calendar days.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 51. Instructions to jury; objections; preserving a claim of error","Guam · civil rules","Requests for instructions, the court’s duty to disclose proposed instructions, on-the-record objections before instructions are given, and plain-error review. Follows the 2003 federal rule. CVR 51.1 governs submission of proposed instructions, voir dire questions and verdict forms.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings by the court; judgment on partial findings","Guam · civil rules","In non-jury trials, the court finds facts specially and states conclusions of law; findings are set aside only if clearly erroneous. A motion to amend findings is due no later than 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28). Judgment on partial findings is allowed. Follows the 2003 federal rule. CVR 52.1 governs proposed findings.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters","Guam · civil rules","Appointment by consent, for exceptional conditions, or for matters a judge cannot timely address, with a disqualification affidavit under 28 U.S.C. § 455. Objections to a master’s order, report or recommendations are due within 20 days after service. Findings are reviewed de novo unless the parties stipulate otherwise. Follows the 2003 federal rule, including the magistrate-judge reference.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgments; costs","Guam · civil rules","Rule 54(b) certification of partial final judgment. A default judgment may not exceed the demand. Costs go to the prevailing party, but against the Government of Guam only as permitted by law. The clerk taxes costs on 1 day’s notice, with review on motion within 5 days. A fee motion is due no later than 14 days after entry of judgment (also CVR 54.3). Follows the 2003 federal rule. CVR 54.1 governs the bill of costs.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 55. Default; default judgment","Guam · civil rules","Restyled to the 2021 federal text by PRM 06-006-24. The clerk enters default and may enter judgment for a sum certain against a non-appearing, competent defendant. Otherwise apply to the court. A party who has appeared must get written notice at least 7 days before the hearing (it was 3 days). Default is set aside for good cause, and a default judgment under Rule 60(b). A default judgment against the Government of Gu","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","Guam · civil rules","Rewritten to the 2010 and later federal text by PRM 06-006-18-01 (July 18, 2022). Standard: no genuine dispute of material fact. Unless the court orders otherwise, a motion may be filed until 30 days after the close of all discovery. Materials must be cited. Covers unavailable facts (56(d)), judgment independent of the motion (56(f)), and bad-faith declarations (56(h)). CVR 56.1 requires a statement of issues and a “","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments","Guam · civil rules","Procedure follows these rules; jury rights are preserved. Another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and a speedy hearing may be ordered. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 58. Entry of judgment","Guam · civil rules","Every judgment must be on a separate document, except orders on Rule 50(b), 52(b), 54 (fees), 59 and 60 motions. The clerk enters simple judgments; the court approves others. Entry occurs on docketing plus the earlier of the separate document or 150 days. A timely fee motion may be given Rule 59 tolling effect for appeal under GRAP 4. Follows the post-2002 federal text. CVR 58.1 requires following Official Forms 31 o","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 59. New trials; amendment of judgments","Guam · civil rules","New-trial grounds are those historically recognized in Guam’s courts. A new-trial motion is due no later than 10 days after entry of judgment, and so is a motion to alter or amend (federal: 28 for both). Opposing affidavits are due within 10 days, extendable up to 20. The court may order a new trial on its own within 10 days. None of these times may be extended (Rule 6(b)(2)). Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from judgment or order","Guam · civil rules","Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time; after an appeal is docketed, only with leave of the appellate court. Rule 60(b) grounds (mistake, new evidence, fraud, void, satisfied, any other reason) must be raised within a reasonable time, and not more than 1 year for reasons (1)–(3). Independent actions are preserved. To toll the appeal time under GRAP 4(a)(4), file within 10 days after judgment. Follows the 2003","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","Guam · civil rules","Errors not affecting substantial rights are disregarded. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","Guam · civil rules","Execution is automatically stayed for 10 days after entry (federal: 30), except for injunction and receivership judgments. Stays are available pending post-judgment motions. A supersedeas bond stays the judgment when the court approves it. The Government of Guam need not post a bond. Appellate power to stay is preserved. Follows the 2003 federal rule (pre-2018 text).","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 63. Inability of a judge to proceed","Guam · civil rules","A successor judge may continue a trial or hearing after certifying familiarity with the record. In bench trials, material disputed witnesses must be recalled on request. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 64. Seizure of person or property","Guam · civil rules","Pre-judgment seizure remedies are available as provided by the law of Guam. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions","Guam · civil rules","A preliminary injunction requires notice. A TRO without notice requires an affidavit or verified complaint showing immediate irreparable injury and an attorney certification of notice efforts. It expires within the time fixed, not over 10 days, extendable once for a like period. The adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice. Security is required, except from the Government of Guam and from parties in domes","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 65.1. Security: proceedings against sureties","Guam · civil rules","Sureties submit to the court’s jurisdiction and appoint the clerk as agent for service; liability is enforced by motion. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers appointed by the courts","Guam · civil rules","Administration of receiverships follows past Guam practice or Supreme Court rules. A receivership action may be dismissed only by court order. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit in court","Guam · civil rules","Money or things in dispute may be deposited with the court on notice and by leave. The depositing party serves the order on the clerk. Money must be placed in an interest-bearing account or instrument approved by the court (a Guam addition). Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of judgment","Guam · civil rules","A defending party may serve an offer more than 10 days before trial. The offeree has 10 days to accept in writing. If the final judgment is not more favorable than the offer, the offeree pays the costs incurred after the offer. A post-liability offer must be made at least 10 days before the damages hearing. Follows the 2003 federal rule (federal now: 14 days).","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution and garnishment","Guam · civil rules","Guam’s own rule (GRCP 69 (1990), amended July 19, 2010). A writ of execution is optional; enforcement may also follow the GCA. The clerk may disallow needless costs where multiple writs issue. A claimed wage exemption must be set for hearing not less than 5 days later. If the plaintiff does not join issue on a garnishee’s answer within 5 days after notice, the garnishee gets judgment. Garnishee examinations require l","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 70. Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","Guam · civil rules","If a party fails to perform as directed, the court may direct another to perform at that party’s cost, hold the party in contempt, or vest title to property in Guam by judgment. Former 70(a) on repossession deficiencies was deleted, and the subject is left to the Guam UCC (Title 13 GCA). Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 71. Process in behalf of and against persons not parties","Guam · civil rules","Orders in favor of or against non-parties are enforceable as if they were parties. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 71A. Condemnation of property","Guam · civil rules","Eminent-domain procedure follows the 2003 federal rule. The notice tells defendants to serve an answer within 20 days. Defendants who live in Guam at a known address are served personally under Rule 4(c)–(d). Otherwise, on an attorney’s certificate, service is by publication in a Guam newspaper of general circulation once a week for at least 3 successive weeks, with mailing to any known address before the last public","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 72. Vacant","Guam · civil rules","The federal magistrate-judge rule has no Superior Court counterpart.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 73. Vacant","Guam · civil rules","See note to Rule 72.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 74. Vacant","Guam · civil rules","See note to Rule 72.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 75. Vacant","Guam · civil rules","See note to Rule 72.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 76. Vacant","Guam · civil rules","See note to Rule 72.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 77. Courts and clerks","Guam · civil rules","The courts are always open for filing. Trials are in open court; other acts may be done in chambers anywhere in Guam. The clerk’s office is closed weekends and holidays. Guam additions let the clerk grant certain orders without the judge: consent satisfactions, Rule 58 and default judgments, judgment-debtor examination orders and OSCs under 7 GCA §§ 23201, 23207, fee waivers, and stipulated or confessed judgments. Th","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 78. Motions","Guam · civil rules","Each judge sets regular motion days and may decide motions on written submissions without oral hearing. Follows the 2003 federal rule. CVR 7.1 controls briefing: opposition 28 days after the motion is filed, reply 42 days after filing, 20-page and 10-page limits, and a request for oral argument within 7 days after the last paper.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 79. Books and records kept by the clerk","Guam · civil rules","The clerk keeps the civil docket and other records in the form the Presiding Judge prescribes with Judicial Council approval. Entries of orders and judgments show their date of entry. Follows the 2003 federal rule and GRCP 79 (1990).","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 80. Stenographer; transcript as evidence","Guam · civil rules","A certified transcript of testimony may be used to prove that testimony where relevant. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 81. Vacant","Guam · civil rules","No applicability-in-general rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 82. Jurisdiction unaffected","Guam · civil rules","The rules do not extend or limit the Superior Court’s jurisdiction.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 83. Vacant","Guam · civil rules","Omitted because local practice rules are the Rules of the Superior Court (the CVR and GR).","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 84. Forms","Guam · civil rules","The appendix forms suffice under the rules. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 85. Title","Guam · civil rules","The rules may be cited as the Guam Rules of Civil Procedure (GRCP).","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 86. Effective date","Guam · civil rules","The revised rules took effect June 1, 2007. They apply to pending cases unless that would be infeasible or unjust (Preamble).","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 87. Vacant","Guam · civil rules","Now Rule 85.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 88. Vacant","Guam · civil rules","Now Rule 86.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 89. Vacant","Guam · civil rules","Formerly listed Code of Civil Procedure sections superseded by the GRCP; the list was dropped.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 90. Fee exemption for Government of Guam","Guam · civil rules","No filing fees or costs (except reporter transcript fees) are charged to a Government of Guam agency funded by annual General Fund appropriation. The agency must file a Certificate of Waiver (Form 35) signed under penalty of perjury.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 91. Omitted","Guam · civil rules","Fees are now set by the Judicial Council, not the GRCP.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 92. Omitted","Guam · civil rules","Small-claims rules are now in the Miscellaneous Rules.","#st-gu-rules"],["Rule 93. Omitted","Guam · civil rules","Records-retention rules are now in the Miscellaneous Rules.","#st-gu-rules"],["Appeals in Guam: one appellate court, a 30-day notice, and a 10-day jurisdiction statement","Guam · appeals","Guam has no intermediate appellate court. Every appeal from the Superior Court goes directly to the three-justice Supreme Court of Guam, and Supreme Court decisions can be reviewed by the U.S. Supreme Court on certiorari (GRAP 27(c)(4)(B), 27(d)). GRAP is modeled on the federal appellate rules (with Ninth Circuit features such as excerpts of record), so the structure will feel familiar. The traps are local:\n• The not","#st-gu-appeals"],["Final judgments, plus interlocutory orders made appealable by statute","Guam · appeals · What is appealable","Appellate review is available only after final judgment in the Superior Court, except where a law provides for immediate review. 7 GCA § 25102 makes these civil orders appealable:\n• orders after an appealable judgment;\n• orders quashing service of summons;\n• orders granting a new trial or denying judgment notwithstanding the verdict;\n• orders discharging or refusing to discharge an attachment;\n• orders granting, diss","#st-gu-appeals"],["Discretionary interlocutory appeal by petition","Guam · appeals · What is appealable","Other interlocutory orders can be reviewed only at the Supreme Court’s discretion, where resolving the question of law will (1) materially advance the end of the litigation or clarify further proceedings, (2) protect a party from substantial and irreparable injury, or (3) clarify issues of general importance in the administration of justice. File a verified petition for permission to appeal with the Supreme Court Cle","#st-gu-appeals"],["Class certification and anti-SLAPP orders","Guam · appeals · What is appealable","The Supreme Court may permit an appeal from an order granting or denying class certification if application is made within 10 days after entry. The appeal does not stay proceedings unless ordered. A party whose anti-SLAPP motion under the Citizen Participation in Government Act is denied, or not ruled on expeditiously, has a statutory right to an expedited appeal, and discovery stays suspended meanwhile. GRAP sets no","#st-gu-appeals"],["Writs of mandamus and prohibition","Guam · appeals · What is appealable","A petition for a writ directed to the trial court is titled “In re (petitioner)” and must be verified. It states the relief sought, the issues, the facts and the reasons the writ should issue, with the essential order and record attached. Serve all parties and give a copy to the trial judge. Name the Superior Court as respondent, not the judge by name, and list each real party in interest. Writ petitions are limited ","#st-gu-appeals"],["30 days from entry; file in the Superior Court","Guam · appeals · Notice of appeal and tolling","In a civil case, the notice of appeal must be filed with the Superior Court within 30 days after the judgment or order is entered. Give the Superior Court Clerk enough copies for service, and submit a file-stamped copy to the Supreme Court. A notice filed after the decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed on the date of entry. A notice mistakenly filed in the Supreme Court is forwarded and treated a","#st-gu-appeals"],["Entry of judgment: separate document, or 150 days","Guam · appeals · Notice of appeal and tolling","Where a separate document is required, the judgment is “entered” when it is on the docket and either set out on a separate document or 150 days have run from docketing, whichever comes first. Orders on Rule 50(b), 52(b), 54 fee, 59 and 60 motions need no separate document. GRAP 4(a)(7); GRCP 58(a)–(b)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Tolling motions: 10 days after judgment","Guam · appeals · Notice of appeal and tolling","A timely motion under GRCP 50(b), 52(b), 59 (new trial or alter or amend), or 60 restarts the appeal time for all parties from entry of the order disposing of the last such motion. A Rule 60 motion counts only if filed within 10 days after judgment. A fee motion under Rule 54(d)(2) counts only if the court orders it to under Rule 58(c). Each of those GRCP motions must be filed no later than 10 days after entry of jud","#st-gu-appeals"],["Cross-appeals and other parties: 14 days after the first notice","Guam · appeals · Notice of appeal and tolling","If one party files a timely notice, any other party may file within 14 days after the first notice was filed, or within the regular 30 days, whichever ends later. The second party to appeal is the cross-appellant and must pay its own docket fee when filing its notice. Parties may file a joint notice, and appeals may be consolidated. GRAP 3(b), 3(e)(2), 4(a)(3), 8(a)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Extending or reopening the time to appeal","Guam · appeals · Notice of appeal and tolling","The Superior Court may extend the time if a party moves no later than 14 days after the 30-day period expires and shows excusable neglect or good cause. Note: the rule text also refers to motions filed “during the thirty (30) days after” expiry, an internal inconsistency; move within 14 days to be safe. No extension may exceed 30 days after the original deadline or 10 days after the order granting it, whichever is la","#st-gu-appeals"],["Statement of jurisdiction within 10 days, or dismissal","Guam · appeals · Notice of appeal and tolling","Within 10 days after filing the notice of appeal, the appellant must submit a statement of jurisdiction. It sets out why the judgment is final or otherwise appealable, the statutory basis of jurisdiction, and the docket date of the judgment, with a legible copy of the judgment showing the entry date. If no statement is filed, or if it shows that no final appealable judgment is on the docket, the case “shall be dismis","#st-gu-appeals"],["Docket fee due when the notice is filed","Guam · appeals · Fees, record and docketing","When filing the notice of appeal in the Superior Court, the appellant must pay the required non-refundable docket fee to the Clerk of the Supreme Court. The Supreme Court’s posted schedule (January 1, 2014) lists $375 for new appeals. The attorney who filed the notice must file a representation statement within 10 days, naming the parties represented and all other parties and counsel. GRAP 8(a)–(b); Supreme Court of ","#st-gu-appeals"],["Transcript: order within 10 days","Guam · appeals · Fees, record and docketing","Within 10 days after the notice of appeal (or after the last tolling motion is decided), the appellant must either order the needed transcript in writing, filing the order with the Superior Court Clerk and copying the Supreme Court Clerk, or certify that none will be ordered. If you will argue that a finding lacks evidentiary support, you must include all relevant evidence. For a partial transcript, serve a statement","#st-gu-appeals"],["Record is “filed” when the certificate of record is filed","Guam · appeals · Fees, record and docketing","The record stays in the Superior Court. When the transcript is filed, or once the clerk learns that none will be ordered, the Superior Court Clerk files a certificate of record with the Supreme Court. The record is then considered filed, and the clerk notifies the parties of the briefing schedule. The opening-brief clock runs from this date. GRAP 7.1(c)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Schedule: 40 / 30 / 14 days","Guam · appeals · Briefing, length and form","Opening brief: 40 days after the record is filed. Answering brief: 30 days after service of the opening brief. Reply: 14 days after service of the answering brief, and, absent good cause, at least 14 days before argument. In cross-appeals, the appellant’s response-and-reply brief is due 30 days after the appellee’s principal and response brief, and the appellee’s reply is due 14 days later (at least 3 days before arg","#st-gu-appeals"],["Extensions: one 14-day clerk extension, then written motion","Guam · appeals · Briefing, length and form","For good cause, the Clerk may grant one oral (even telephone) extension of up to 14 days on prior notice to the other side. After that, only a written motion showing extraordinary and compelling circumstances will work. Any other extension needs a written motion filed at least 5 days before the due date, with an affidavit covering the due date, prior extensions, reasons, the time needed, and assurance of filing. The ","#st-gu-appeals"],["Length and form","Guam · appeals · Briefing, length and form","A principal brief may be 30 pages or 14,000 words (1,300 monospaced lines). A reply may be 15 pages or half the words. In cross-appeals, the appellee’s principal and response brief may be 35 pages or 16,500 words. Include a certificate of compliance when relying on word count. The court disfavors motions to exceed the limits; file one at least 14 days before the brief is due (7 for a reply), with a detailed affidavit","#st-gu-appeals"],["Excerpts of record are mandatory","Guam · appeals · Briefing, length and form","Every appeal requires excerpts of record: 3 copies, bound separately, filed with the opening brief, and 1 served on each party. They must include at least the notice of appeal, the trial docket sheet, and the judgment or order appealed. Volumes over 300 pages must be split. GRAP 15(a)–(c)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Motions and amicus briefs","Guam · appeals · Briefing, length and form","A response to a motion is due 7 days after service. A party that does not oppose must file a statement of non-opposition within 7 days. A reply is due 5 days after the response. Motions and responses are limited to 20 pages, replies to 10. Emergency motions need “Emergency” in the title and an affidavit, and you must notify the Clerk early. The Government of Guam may file amicus briefs without leave; others need cons","#st-gu-appeals"],["Oral argument is the default","Guam · appeals · Argument, decision, rehearing and further review","Oral argument must be allowed unless a three-justice panel unanimously finds the appeal frivolous, the issue authoritatively decided, or argument unnecessary. Each side gets 30 minutes, including questions. Requests for more time go by letter to the Clerk within 7 days of the argument date. A decision needs a majority of the panel, and an evenly divided panel leaves the decision below in place. GRAP 19(a), (c); 7 GCA","#st-gu-appeals"],["Rehearing: 14 days (45 if the Government of Guam is a party)","Guam · appeals · Argument, decision, rehearing and further review","A petition for rehearing is due within 14 days after entry of judgment. In a civil case where the Government of Guam or its officer or agency is a party, any party has 45 days. The petition must state with particularity the points overlooked or misapprehended. An issue not previously briefed cannot be raised for the first time. Limit: 15 pages, orange cover. No answer is allowed unless the Court requests one, and reh","#st-gu-appeals"],["Mandate, costs and interest","Guam · appeals · Argument, decision, rehearing and further review","The mandate issues 7 calendar days after the rehearing time expires, or 7 days after denial of a timely rehearing petition or stay motion. A bill of costs is due within 14 days after entry of judgment, and objections within 10 days after service. After a remand, the Supreme Court keeps no jurisdiction, so review of the proceedings on remand needs a new notice of appeal. Frivolous appeals can draw just damages and sin","#st-gu-appeals"],["Review by the U.S. Supreme Court","Guam · appeals · Argument, decision, rehearing and further review","A party may move to stay the mandate pending a petition for certiorari to the Supreme Court of the United States. The motion must show a substantial question and good cause. The stay is limited to 90 days unless extended or unless the petition is filed and the Clerk notified within that period. File a copy of any certiorari petition with the Supreme Court of Guam. The federal statute authorizing certiorari (in the Or","#st-gu-appeals"],["GRAP excludes weekends from periods under 11 days","Guam · appeals · Computing time, stays and e-filing","Unlike the 2023 GRCP, GRAP 11 still excludes intermediate Saturdays, Sundays and legal holidays (as defined in 1 GCA § 1000) when a period is shorter than 11 days, unless stated as calendar days. Add 3 calendar days after service unless the paper is delivered on the date of service, and electronic service does not count as delivered that day. The last day for electronic filing ends at midnight; EFR 3.11 sets 11:59 p.","#st-gu-appeals"],["Stays and supersedeas","Guam · appeals · Computing time, stays and e-filing","Move first in the Superior Court for a stay, approval of a supersedeas bond, or an injunction pending appeal. A motion in the Supreme Court must show that moving below was impracticable or was denied. Execution is automatically stayed for 10 days after entry of judgment, except injunction and receivership judgments. A supersedeas bond stays the judgment once approved. The Government of Guam needs no bond. GRAP 12(a);","#st-gu-appeals"],["E-filing in the Supreme Court","Guam · appeals · Computing time, stays and e-filing","Lawyers admitted in Guam, pro hac vice lawyers, and self-represented litigants may register for the Judiciary’s electronic system; one registration covers both courts. The Supreme Court may convert a pending case to e-filing. A system failure never excuses a missed jurisdictional deadline, so file by any available means. Out-of-state counsel must be admitted pro hac vice in the Supreme Court separately from the Super","#st-gu-appeals"],["Notice of appeal, civil (filed in Superior Court)","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the judgment or order appealed from.\nJurisdictional. Pay the Supreme Court docket fee ($375 on posted schedule) at filing (GRAP 8(a)). GRAP 4(a)(1), 3(a)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Notice of appeal by another party / cross-appeal","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or the regular 30 days, if later).\nCross-appellant pays its own docket fee. GRAP 4(a)(3)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Statement of jurisdiction","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nMandatory dismissal if not filed. GRAP 11 excludes weekends and holidays from periods under 11 days. GRAP 4.1(a)–(b)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Order transcript or certify none; representation statement","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the notice of appeal (transcript: or decision on last tolling motion, if later). GRAP 7(b)(1), 8(b)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Post-judgment motions (GRCP 50(b), 52(b), 59 new trial or alter/amend)","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended. Calendar days since Oct. 19, 2023 (GRCP 6(a)). Timely motion restarts appeal time (GRAP 4(a)(4)). GRCP 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e); GRCP 6(b)(2)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Rule 60 motion that tolls appeal time","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of judgment.\nLater Rule 60 motions (up to 1 year for reasons 1–3) do not toll. GRAP 4(a)(4)(A)(vi)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Attorney’s fee motion","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment.\nTolls appeal time only if the court so orders under GRCP 58(c). GRCP 54(d)(2)(B); CVR 54.3","#st-gu-appeals"],["Motion to extend time to appeal","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from expiration of the 30-day appeal period.\nExcusable neglect or good cause. Extension capped at 30 days past the deadline or 10 days after the order, whichever is later. GRAP 4(a)(5)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Motion to reopen time to appeal","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","180 days from entry of judgment (or 7 days after receiving notice, if earlier).\nOnly if notice of entry was not received within 21 days. Reopened window is 14 days. GRAP 4(a)(6)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Petition for permission to appeal (interlocutory)","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order (unless the authorizing statute or rule sets another time).\nAnswer or cross-petition 7 days after service. 15-page limit. GRAP 4.2(a)(2), 4(a)(1); 7 GCA § 3108(b)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Application to appeal class-certification order","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of the order.\nDiscretionary. No automatic stay. GRCP 23(f)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Opening brief and excerpts of record","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from filing of the record (certificate of record). GRAP 17(a), 13.2(f)(1), 15(b), 7.1(c)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Answering brief","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the opening brief. GRAP 17(a), 13.2(f)(2)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Reply brief","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the answering brief.\nAlso at least 14 days before argument absent good cause. In a cross-appeal the appellant’s response-and-reply is due in 30 days and the appellee’s reply in 14 (GRAP 13.2(f)(3)–(4)). GRAP 17(a)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Motion to exceed page or word limits","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from before the brief’s due date (7 days for a reply).\nGranted only for extraordinary and compelling reasons. GRAP 16(a)(7)(C)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Written motion to extend a brief deadline","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","5 days from before the brief’s due date.\nOne oral clerk extension of up to 14 days is available first (GRAP 17(c)(1)). GRAP 17(c)(2)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Response to a motion","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from service of the motion.\nNon-opposition statement due in the same 7 days. Reply due 5 days after the response. GRAP 6(a)(3)(A)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Amicus brief","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from filing of the supported party’s principal brief. GRAP 14(e)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing (panel or en banc)","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment.\n45 days in a civil case where the Government of Guam or its officer or agency is a party. GRAP 30(a)(1), 29(c)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Issuance of mandate","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from expiration of rehearing time or denial of rehearing or stay motion.\nCalendar days. GRAP 27(c)(2)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Bill of costs","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment.\nObjections within 10 days after service. GRAP 22(d)(1)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Stay of mandate pending U.S. Supreme Court certiorari","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","90 days from grant of the stay.\nContinues if the petition is filed and the Clerk notified within the stay. GRAP 27(c)(4)(B)(ii)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Criminal appeal by defendant (for reference)","Guam · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of judgment or order (or government’s notice, if later).\nGovernment: 30 days (GRAP 4(b)(1)(B)). GRAP 4(b)(1)(A)","#st-gu-appeals"],["Supreme Court of Hawai‘i","Hawaii · courts","Court of last resort. Reviews Intermediate Court of Appeals decisions only by discretionary writ of certiorari, and takes cases directly from the ICA docket by transfer (mandatory for questions of imperative or fundamental public importance, rulings invalidating a statute or constitutional amendment, and life-without-parole sentences; discretionary for novel questions or conflicting decisions). Admits and disciplines","#st-hi"],["Intermediate Court of Appeals (ICA)","Hawaii · courts","Every appeal allowed by law from a court or agency goes first to the ICA, subject to transfer to the Supreme Court or later certiorari review. Decides cases in panels; a motions panel handles defaults and motions before a case is submitted on the merits.","#st-hi"],["Circuit Courts","Hawaii · courts","General-jurisdiction trial courts organized by judicial circuit, hearing civil actions (including all jury trials), felonies, and probate, trust, and guardianship matters. Civil practice is governed by the Hawai‘i Rules of Civil Procedure plus the Rules of the Circuit Courts. The Family Court is a division of the circuit court.","#st-hi"],["District Courts (including Small Claims Division)","Hawaii · courts","Civil cases up to $40,000 (exclusive up to $10,000, not counting fees, interest, and costs), summary possession, and specific performance up to $20,000. Bench trials only: a timely jury demand transfers the case to circuit court. District courts cannot hear defamation, libel, slander, malicious prosecution, false imprisonment, or cases where title to real estate is in question.","#st-hi"],["Land Court and Tax Appeal Court","Hawaii · courts","Separate courts with their own rules; final judgments of the land court are appealed to the ICA like circuit court judgments.","#st-hi"],["Hawaii Public Expression Protection Act (UPEPA)","Hawaii · anti-SLAPP","Hawai‘i adopted the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act as HRS chapter 634G (L 2022, c 96). The older anti-SLAPP chapter, HRS ch. 634F (Citizen Participation in Government), was repealed by the same act. The effective date of Act 96 is not shown in the collected text, so check it for claims based on conduct around 2022. HRS ch. 634G; HRS ch. 634F (repealed, L 2022, c 96, §3)","#st-hi-slapp"],["Scope: three kinds of protected activity, three exclusions","Hawaii · anti-SLAPP","Covers claims based on (1) communication in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding; (2) communication on an issue under consideration in such a proceeding; or (3) exercise of free speech, press, assembly, petition, or association rights under the U.S. or Hawai‘i Constitution on a matter of public concern. Excluded: claims against a governmental unit or its employee acting","#st-hi-slapp"],["Special motion to dismiss: 60 days from service, and everything stops","Hawaii · anti-SLAPP","File no later than 60 days after service of the pleading asserting the claim, or later for good cause, notwithstanding court rules. Filing automatically stays all other proceedings between the moving and responding parties, including discovery and pending motions; the court may also stay related proceedings involving other parties. The stay lasts until the order and the time to appeal it expire. Limited discovery is ","#st-hi-slapp"],["Hearing within 60 days; ruling within 60 days after hearing","Hawaii · anti-SLAPP","The court must hear the motion no later than 60 days after filing, unless it orders later for allowed discovery (then within 60 days after the discovery order) or other good cause, and must rule no later than 60 days after the hearing. The court considers the pleadings, motion papers, and any evidence admissible on summary judgment under the Hawai‘i Rules of Civil Procedure. HRS §§ 634G-4, 634G-5, 634G-7","#st-hi-slapp"],["Three-step test; dismissal is with prejudice","Hawaii · anti-SLAPP","The court must dismiss with prejudice if the movant shows the chapter applies, the responding party fails to show an exclusion, and either the responding party fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of material fact. A voluntary dismissal without prejudice does not defeat the movant’s right to a ruling and fees; a voluntary dis","#st-hi-slapp"],["Mandatory fees for a winning movant","Hawaii · anti-SLAPP","The court ‘shall award’ costs, reasonable attorney’s fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion to a prevailing movant. A prevailing responding party gets them only if the court finds the motion frivolous or filed solely to delay. HRS § 634G-9","#st-hi-slapp"],["Immediate appeal of a denial, 30 days, with a full stay","Hawaii · anti-SLAPP","A moving party may appeal as of right within 30 days from an order denying the motion in whole or in part. While that appeal is pending, all proceedings between all parties are stayed. The Act must be construed liberally to protect speech, press, assembly, petition, and association rights, with attention to uniformity among enacting states. HRS §§ 634G-3(d), 634G-8, 634G-10, 634G-11","#st-hi-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: discretionary, through local counsel only","Hawaii · out of state","A lawyer actively licensed by the highest court of any U.S. state, territory, or D.C. may associate with a Hawai‘i bar member (local counsel) in a specific case, at the discretion of the presiding judge. Local counsel must file the petition or motion in the court where the case is pending and send a copy to the Office of Disciplinary Counsel. Admission carries over to an appeal or remand in the same case without a ne","#st-hi-oos"],["What the papers must show, including Hawai‘i tax registration","Hawaii · out of state","Local counsel’s declaration must state an address for service, that local counsel is attorney of record responsible for all phases of the case, a DCCA certificate of good standing for local counsel’s employer dated within 10 days (or an exemption explanation), and a Hawai‘i general excise tax license number with a promise to pay state income and GET (or why that does not apply). The applicant’s declaration must list ","#st-hi-oos"],["Repeat appearances need ‘special circumstances’","Hawaii · out of state","If the applicant has made more than one appearance as counsel in Hawai‘i in the preceding five years, the declaration must explain the special circumstances that justify another admission. RSCH Rule 1.9(b)(2)(E)","#st-hi-oos"],["Annual fees: pay within 10 days or the order dies","Hawaii · out of state","For each year the order is in effect, the attorney pays the Hawai‘i State Bar an annual Disciplinary Board fee and an annual Lawyers’ Fund for Client Protection fee (one set of fees covers all cases), plus any Bar registration fee. Proof of payment must be filed in the case within 10 days after the order and within 10 days after each December payment. Missing the first 10-day deadline voids the order (a new motion is","#st-hi-oos"],["Local counsel signs everything and must be ready to try the case","Hawaii · out of state","Local counsel must sign all pleadings, motions, briefs, and other documents, participate actively in all phases, and be prepared to go forward. Service on local counsel is service on pro hac vice counsel and the client. Local counsel must send the order to the Bar and ODC and notify them when the engagement ends, the case closes, or the order lapses. RSCH Rule 1.9(d)–(e)","#st-hi-oos"],["Pro hac vice counsel cannot e-file","Hawaii · out of state","Attorneys must register for and file through the Judiciary Electronic Filing System (JEFS), but a lawyer admitted pro hac vice may not register as a JEFS user, because all filings must be made by local counsel. HEFSR Rule 4.1(a); HRCP Rule 1.1; HRAP Rule 25(a)","#st-hi-oos"],["Arbitration in Hawai‘i also needs approval","Hawaii · out of state","An out-of-state lawyer representing a party in a Hawai‘i arbitration over a Hawai‘i-related dispute must associate with a Hawai‘i lawyer and get written approval from the arbitrator(s) on a petition served on all parties, with declarations similar to court pro hac vice (including that the lawyer is not a Hawai‘i resident and not regularly practicing there). The same annual fees and 10-day rule apply. Collective-barga","#st-hi-oos"],["Unauthorized practice is a statutory offense","Hawaii · out of state","Appearing without pro hac vice approval exposes the lawyer to Supreme Court discipline and referral for violating HRS § 605-14, which makes it unlawful to practice law unless licensed or authorized by a court, agency, or statute. RSCH Rules 1.9(f), 1.9A(f); HRS § 605-14","#st-hi-oos"],["No admission on motion in the collected rules","Hawaii · out of state","Admission to the Hawai‘i bar requires passing the Hawai‘i Bar Examination (held in Honolulu) and the MPRE; the collected rules contain no admission-by-motion path. A lawyer who did not attend an ABA-accredited school may sit for the exam after actively practicing five of the last six years in another U.S. jurisdiction. Limited admissions exist for military judge advocates, legal-services lawyers, and uniformed-servic","#st-hi-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances are formalized","Hawaii · out of state","A lawyer giving limited-scope help may file a Notice of Limited Appearance (Form 32-A) and, once the agreed work is done, a Notice of Withdrawal (Form 32-B); the client has 14 days to object. Withdrawing early, or without having filed the notice, requires a Rule 25.1 motion. A ghostwritten filing need not name the lawyer but must state on its first page: “This document was prepared with the assistance of an attorney.","#st-hi-oos"],["Governing code: older Model Rules language","Hawaii · ethics","The Hawai‘i Rules of Professional Conduct (Exhibit A to the Supreme Court rules) follow the ABA Model Rules’ numbering but often keep pre-2002 wording, such as ‘consent after consultation’ instead of ‘informed consent’ (for example in Rules 1.2(c), 1.6(a), 1.7(b)(4), 1.8). The latest amendments took effect January 1, 2026. Pro hac vice applicants must also promise to follow the Guidelines of Professional Courtesy and","#st-hi-ethics"],["Fees: no ‘non-refundable’ fees; written terms required","Hawaii · ethics","The scope and basis of the fee must be communicated in writing (except for regular clients on the same basis, or total cost of $250 or less). Advance fees are presumed unearned and go into trust, and no agreement may call a fee non-refundable or earned on receipt. A flat fee over $1,500 requires a signed written agreement listing six items, including milestones or an hourly rate for earning the fee and a refund of an","#st-hi-ethics"],["Confidentiality: mandatory disclosure of client fraud that used your services","Hawaii · ethics","Beyond the ABA permissive exceptions, Hawai‘i requires (‘shall reveal’) disclosure of information that clearly establishes a client’s criminal or fraudulent act, done using the lawyer’s services, that caused substantial financial or property injury, to the extent needed to rectify it. Government lawyers may also disclose to prevent or rectify criminal or illegal acts by public officials or agencies harmful to the pub","#st-hi-ethics"],["Conflicts: older 1.8 provisions","Hawaii · ethics","There is no ABA-style exception for modest humanitarian gifts to indigent litigants; a lawyer may only advance costs or pay them for an indigent client. A lawyer related to opposing counsel as parent, child, sibling, spouse, or domestic partner needs the client’s consent after consultation. Aggregate settlements need each client’s signed written consent. HRPC Rule 1.8(e), (g), (k)","#st-hi-ethics"],["Lateral screening is narrower than the ABA rule","Hawaii · ethics","A lateral lawyer’s conflict from a former firm’s matter is not imputed only if the lawyer did not participate in the matter and has no confidential information about it, is timely screened and gets no part of the fee, and written notice goes to the former client. HRPC Rule 1.10(c)","#st-hi-ethics"],["Intermediary rule retained","Hawaii · ethics","Hawai‘i keeps Rule 2.2 (lawyer as intermediary between clients), which the ABA deleted. Conflicts under Rule 2.2 are imputed to the whole firm. HRPC Rules 2.2, 1.10(a)","#st-hi-ethics"],["No multijurisdictional-practice safe harbor","Hawaii · ethics","Hawai‘i Rule 5.5 has no counterpart to ABA Rule 5.5(c)–(d): there is no rule-based permission for temporary practice by out-of-state lawyers or for in-house counsel. It instead bars letting a suspended or disbarred person in the office have contact with clients. HRPC Rule 5.5; HRS § 605-14","#st-hi-ethics"],["Misconduct: no ‘prejudicial to justice’ catch-all; sexual harassment rule","Hawaii · ethics","Rule 8.4(d) is ‘Reserved’, so there is no ABA-style catch-all for conduct prejudicial to the administration of justice. Hawai‘i adds failure to cooperate with an ethics investigation (8.4(g)) and bars sexual harassment in a professional capacity (8.4(h)), but has no general anti-discrimination rule like ABA 8.4(g). HRPC Rule 8.4","#st-hi-ethics"],["Trust accounts, solicitation, and pro bono","Hawaii · ethics","Client funds must be held in a separate account in Hawai‘i, with records kept for 6 years after the representation ends. Written solicitation about a personal-injury or wrongful-death claim is barred until more than 30 days after the injury or death. Pro bono goal is 50 hours a year, or a $500 contribution to the Hawai‘i Justice Foundation or a legal-services provider instead. Rules 7.4 and 7.5 (fields of practice, f","#st-hi-ethics"],["Answer in 20 days, not 21","Hawaii · land mine","An answer is due 20 days after service of the summons and complaint. After a Rule 12 motion is denied, the answer is due in 10 days. There is no waiver-of-service procedure that extends the deadline. HRCP Rules 4, 12(a)","#st-hi-unique"],["Post-trial motions are due in 10 days","Hawaii · land mine","Renewed judgment-as-a-matter-of-law, amended-findings, and new-trial/alter-or-amend motions must be filed no later than 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28). The court cannot extend these deadlines. HRCP Rules 6(b), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)","#st-hi-unique"],["The 90-day deemed-denial trap on tolling motions","Hawaii · land mine","A timely post-judgment tolling motion extends the appeal time, but if the court does not enter an order within 90 days the motion is denied by operation of the rule. Under the current rule, the appeal clock runs from the clerk’s notice of that denial. From January 1, 2027, with no filed written order within 90 days, the notice of appeal is due 120 days after the motion was filed, and a Rule 60(b) motion tolls only if","#st-hi-unique"],["Motion practice runs on 18/8/3 days and page limits","Hawaii · land mine","Hearing motions must be filed and served at least 18 days before the hearing, oppositions at least 8 days, and replies at least 3 days. Mail service adds 2 days, not 3. Memoranda are capped at 20 pages (replies 10), and memoranda over 15 pages need tables of contents and authorities. HRCP Rule 6(d)–(e); RCCH Rules 7(b), 7.1, 8","#st-hi-unique"],["Dispositive motions die 50 days before trial","Hawaii · land mine","Motions seeking judgment or dismissal of any claim, including summary judgment, must be filed no later than 50 days before the assigned trial date unless the court allows otherwise for good cause. RCCH Rule 7(f); HRCP Rule 56(b)","#st-hi-unique"],["Serve within 6 months or risk dismissal","Hawaii · land mine","HRCP has no 90-day service limit, but the court may dismiss any action or claim on its own, with written notice, if no service is made within 6 months after filing. A motion to reinstate must be filed within 10 days of the dismissal order. RCCH Rule 28","#st-hi-unique"],["Jury demand within 10 days","Hawaii · land mine","Serve a written jury demand no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue (federal: 14) and file it, or the jury right is waived. In district court, a jury demand sends the case to circuit court. HRCP Rule 38(b), (d); HRS § 604-5(b)","#st-hi-unique"],["Don’t file defamation in district court","Hawaii · land mine","District courts lack jurisdiction over libel, slander, defamation, malicious prosecution, false imprisonment, and real-property title disputes, whatever the amount. HRS § 604-5(d)","#st-hi-unique"],["No temporary-practice safe harbor and no e-filing account","Hawaii · land mine","Hawai‘i Rule 5.5 has no ABA 5.5(c) temporary-practice exception, so out-of-state lawyers need pro hac vice even for limited court work. Pro hac vice counsel cannot register for JEFS, and local counsel must sign and file everything. HRPC Rule 5.5; RSCH Rule 1.9(d); HEFSR Rule 4.1(a)","#st-hi-unique"],["Pay the pro hac vice fees within 10 days","Hawaii · land mine","If the annual Disciplinary Board and Lawyers’ Fund fees are not paid, with proof filed, within 10 days after the order granting pro hac vice, the order is no longer valid and a new motion must be filed. Unpaid December renewals bring automatic suspension. RSCH Rule 1.9(a)(2)–(3)","#st-hi-unique"],["‘Non-refundable’ retainers are forbidden","Hawaii · land mine","No fee agreement may describe a fee as non-refundable or earned on receipt. Advance fees are presumed unearned and must be held in trust, and flat fees over $1,500 need a signed agreement with refund terms. HRPC Rule 1.5(b)–(c)","#st-hi-unique"],["Appellate briefs are limited by pages, with strict points of error","Hawaii · land mine","Opening and answering briefs are capped at 35 pages and replies at 10, in 12-point type. Each point of error must say where in the record the error occurred and where it was objected to (quoting the evidence, instruction, or finding at issue); nonconforming points are disregarded. Hawai‘i cases since statehood must be cited to both the state and regional reporters. HRAP Rules 28(a), 28(b)(1), (4), 32(b)","#st-hi-unique"],["No peremptory challenge of a judge","Hawaii · judges","The collected rules and statutes give no right to remove a trial judge without cause. Disqualification requires a reason. HRS § 601-7; HRCP (no provision)","#st-hi-unique"],["Affidavit of bias: one per case, before the hearing","Hawaii · judges","A party may file an affidavit stating facts and reasons showing the judge’s personal bias or prejudice for or against a party. It must be filed before the trial or hearing (or good cause shown for lateness), only one affidavit is allowed per party per case, and it must come with counsel’s certificate of good faith. A judge is also disqualified if a relative within the third degree is counsel or a party, the judge has","#st-hi-unique"],["Appellate recusal motion: 10 days","Hawaii · judges","A motion to disqualify or recuse an ICA judge or Supreme Court justice is due within 10 days after the appellate case is opened or after new information is discovered, or the objection is waived absent good cause. It must rest on Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11 grounds, be supported by a declaration of facts, and certify good faith; disagreement with past rulings is not enough. HRAP Rule 5","#st-hi-unique"],["Anti-SLAPP protects Hawai‘i constitutional rights too","Hawaii · speech","Chapter 634G covers speech, press, assembly, petition, and association rights ‘guaranteed by the United States Constitution or the Hawaii State Constitution’ and must be construed liberally to protect them. HRS §§ 634G-2(a)(3), 634G-10","#st-hi-unique"],["Hawai‘i Constitution speech provisions not collected","Hawaii · speech","Whether the Hawai‘i Constitution’s speech guarantees are read more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked against collected text. n/a","#st-hi-unique"],["Two-step trial setting with hard pretrial cutoffs","Hawaii · unique rules","Within 14 days after any defendant is served or appears, the plaintiff must file a notice requesting a scheduling conference; failure can lead to dismissal. The judge then issues a scheduling order within the earlier of 90 days after service or 60 days after appearance. By default discovery closes 60 days before trial; plaintiffs must name all witnesses 60 days before the discovery cutoff and defendants 30 days befor","#st-hi-unique"],["Expedited track by agreement","Hawaii · unique rules","By agreement, cases can go on an expedited track to trial within 9 months of the scheduling conference, with 4 depositions (16 hours total) and 35 combined written discovery requests. Other cases are set for trial within 12 months (up to 18 on request). HRCP Rule 16.1","#st-hi-unique"],["Unpublished appellate decisions can be cited","Hawaii · unique rules","Dispositions filed on or after July 1, 2008 may be cited for persuasive value. A copy of any cited unpublished disposition must be attached to the brief. HRAP Rule 35(c)(2) (amended July 21, 2026)","#st-hi-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope, interpretation, e-filing, automation","Hawaii · civil rules","The rules govern all civil suits, at law or in equity, in the circuit courts (exceptions in Rule 81) and are construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action. They are read together with the Hawai‘i Electronic Filing and Service Rules (HEFSR), the Hawai‘i Court Records Rules, and the Rules of the Circuit Courts; documents filed and notices given under the HEFSR satisfy every filing,","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 1.1. Registration required (JEFS)","Hawaii · civil rules","Unless exempted by the court, every attorney representing a party in a case kept in the Judiciary Information Management System (JIMS) must register as a Judiciary Electronic Filing and Service System (JEFS) user and file all documents electronically. A self-represented individual party may register as a JEFS user for that party’s own case. No federal counterpart.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","Hawaii · civil rules","There is one form of action, the “civil action.”","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencement of action","Hawaii · civil rules","A civil action is commenced by filing a complaint with the court.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 3.1. Civil information sheet; additional claims; transfers from district court","Hawaii · civil rules","Every initial complaint must be filed with a fully completed civil information sheet (Form 2-A), and every later affirmative pleading with an additional claims information sheet (Form 2-B). In a case transferred from district court, the plaintiff must file the civil information sheet within 7 days after the notice of docketing is filed. No federal counterpart (compare the federal civil cover sheet, which is a local r","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 4. Process","Hawaii · civil rules","The clerk issues the summons when the complaint is filed; it must warn of default and bar personal delivery between 10:00 p.m. and 6:00 a.m. on premises not open to the public unless a judge permits it in writing on the summons. Service may be made anywhere in the State by the sheriff or deputy, a court-appointed person, any nonparty at least 18 years old, or (within a county) the police chief or authorized subordina","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 5. Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","Hawaii · civil rules","Every pleading after the complaint, discovery paper, written motion (other than ex parte), notice, brief, offer of judgment, and similar paper must be served on every party, but parties in default for failure to appear need be served only with new or additional claims (served like a summons). Service on a represented party goes to the attorney, by delivery, mail to the last known address, or leaving it with the clerk","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 6. Time","Hawaii · civil rules","Computation excludes the trigger day and includes the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday, or holiday (holidays are those designated under HRS § 8-1); for periods under 7 days, intermediate weekends and holidays are excluded. The court may enlarge time for cause before expiration, or after expiration for excusable neglect, but not the times under Rules 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (d), (e), 60(b), or HRAP 4(a) except as","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings allowed; form of motions","Hawaii · civil rules","The allowed pleadings are a complaint and answer, a reply to a counterclaim denominated as such, an answer to a cross-claim, a third-party complaint and third-party answer; the court may order a reply to an answer. Motions must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state the grounds with particularity and the relief sought, and be signed under Rule 11. Demurrers, pleas, and exceptions for insufficiency a","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","Hawaii · civil rules","A claim needs a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment; alternative or different types of relief may be demanded. Denials must fairly meet the substance of the averments, lack of knowledge has the effect of a denial, and the listed affirmative defenses (accord and satisfaction through waiver, including injury by fellow servant) must be pleaded. Averments other than damages a","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","Hawaii · civil rules","Capacity need not be pleaded, but a challenge to capacity or legal existence must be made by specific negative averment. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity, while malice, intent, and knowledge may be averred generally; denial of conditions precedent must be specific and particular. Averments of time and place are material, and special damages must be specifically stated.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","Hawaii · civil rules","The caption names the court, title of the action, file number, and Rule 7(a) designation; the complaint names all parties. The first page must have either a 3-inch top margin or a 3-inch-by-3-inch space in the top right corner for an electronic file stamp, a requirement not in the federal rule. Averments go in numbered paragraphs, and an exhibit to a pleading is part of it for all purposes.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing; representations to the court; sanctions","Hawaii · civil rules","Every paper must be signed by at least one attorney of record (or by the self-represented party) and list address and telephone number; JEFS or JIMS filings are signed under HEFSR Rule 5, and documents an attorney prepares for an unrepresented party must comply with Rule 11.1(c). Presenting a paper certifies proper purpose, legal warrant, and evidentiary support after reasonable inquiry. A sanctions motion must be ma","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 11.1. Limited appearance and withdrawal","Hawaii · civil rules","An attorney giving limited-scope help under HRPC Rule 1.2 may file a “Notice of Limited Appearance” (Form 32-A) and may submit in camera an “Agreement and Consent to Limited Representation” (Form 32); filing outside the agreed scope extends the notice. After finishing the agreed services the attorney may file a Notice of Withdrawal of Limited Appearance; the client may object within 14 days, which triggers a hearing,","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections; motion for judgment on the pleadings","Hawaii · civil rules","An answer is due 20 days after service of the summons and complaint (not 21 as in the federal rule), and an answer to a cross-claim or reply to a counterclaim is due within 20 days; after a Rule 12 motion is denied or deferred, or a more definite statement is served, the responsive pleading is due within 10 days. The seven Rule 12(b) defenses may be raised by pre-answer motion, and a 12(b)(6) or 12(c) motion that rel","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and cross-claim","Hawaii · civil rules","A counterclaim arising from the same transaction or occurrence is compulsory unless it is already the subject of another pending action or the opposing party sued by attachment without personal jurisdiction; other counterclaims are permissive and may exceed or differ from the opposing claim. The rules do not enlarge the right to counterclaim against the State or a county. Counterclaims that mature later may be added ","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","Hawaii · civil rules","A defending party may serve a third-party complaint on a nonparty who is or may be liable to it for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim without leave if it files the third-party complaint within 10 days after serving its original answer (federal practice allows 14); otherwise leave on motion with notice to all parties is required. The third-party defendant answers under Rules 12 and 13, may assert the third-party pl","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Hawaii · civil rules","A party may amend once as of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 20 days after service if no responsive pleading is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar; otherwise only with leave (freely given) or written consent. A motion or stipulation to amend must attach the full proposed amended pleading in Ramseyer format (additions underscored, deletions bracketed and stricken) without inco","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 16. Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","Hawaii · civil rules","Under the version effective January 1, 2022, within the earlier of 14 days after any defendant is served or appears, the plaintiff must file a notice requesting a scheduling conference (Form 33) and serve the resulting order or notice; lead counsel or the party must attend. Unless the case is exempt under the Rules of the Circuit Courts, the court must issue a scheduling order within the earlier of 90 days after any ","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 16.1. Expedited or non-expedited track; discovery limits; trial setting","Hawaii · civil rules","By agreement of the parties, the court may assign a non-exempt case to an expedited track through the scheduling order for cases that can be resolved within 9 months of the scheduling conference; foreclosure, Court Annexed Arbitration Program cases (HRS § 601-20), agency appeals, consumer debt collection, quiet title, and asbestos cases are exempt. Expedited-track parties are limited to 4 oral depositions totaling 16","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 16.2. Appearance by telephone or videoconference","Hawaii · civil rules","Absent good cause, the court must allow parties or counsel to appear remotely for scheduling and trial-setting conferences, status conferences, uncontested motions, and other approved matters, and may require in-person appearance midway. Remote appearance is presumptively not allowed for trials, evidentiary hearings, contested or dispositive motions, and settlement conferences unless permitted. A party granted remote","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 17. Parties plaintiff and defendant; capacity","Hawaii · civil rules","Actions must be prosecuted by the real party in interest, with reasonable time allowed after objection for ratification, joinder, or substitution; subdivision (b) is reserved (the text has no capacity-by-domicile provision like the federal rule). A guardian may sue or defend for an infant or incompetent, who otherwise sues by next friend or guardian ad litem. Unlike the federal rule, Rule 17(d) authorizes suing an un","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims and remedies","Hawaii · civil rules","A party may join as many claims, legal or equitable, as it has against an opposing party. Claims ordinarily cognizable only after another is concluded may be joined, so a plaintiff may seek money and set aside a fraudulent conveyance without first obtaining a money judgment.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 19. Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","Hawaii · civil rules","A person subject to service must be joined if complete relief is otherwise impossible or the person’s interest would be impaired or would expose parties to inconsistent obligations; a refusing plaintiff may be made a defendant or involuntary plaintiff. If joinder is not feasible, the court weighs four equity-and-good-conscience factors to decide whether to proceed or dismiss. Pleadings must name nonjoined needed pers","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","Hawaii · civil rules","Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, when claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact. Judgment may be given for or against fewer than all, and the court may order separate trials or other orders to prevent embarrassment, delay, or expense.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","Hawaii · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be dropped or added by court order at any stage on just terms, and any claim may be severed.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","Hawaii · civil rules","Claimants against the plaintiff may be joined and required to interplead when the plaintiff is or may be exposed to double or multiple liability, even if their claims lack a common origin or the plaintiff denies liability; a defendant may obtain interpleader by cross-claim or counterclaim. The text has no reference to statutory interpleader.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","Hawaii · civil rules","Class certification requires numerosity, commonality, typicality, and adequacy plus one of the three Rule 23(b) categories, decided by order as soon as practicable; (b)(3) classes get the best practicable notice and a right to opt out by a specified date. A class action may not be dismissed or compromised without court approval and notice to all members. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 23, the text has no interlocutory-appeal","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 23.1. Derivative actions by shareholders","Hawaii · civil rules","A shareholder or member suing to enforce a right of a corporation or unincorporated association must file a verified complaint alleging contemporaneous ownership (or devolution by operation of law) and, with particularity, the demand made on the directors and shareholders or members and why it failed or was not made. The action fails if the plaintiff does not fairly and adequately represent similarly situated holders","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 23.2. Actions relating to unincorporated associations","Hawaii · civil rules","An action by or against association members as a class through named representatives may proceed only if the representatives will fairly and adequately protect the association’s and members’ interests; Rule 23(d) orders and Rule 23(e) dismissal and compromise procedures apply. The rule does not bar actions by or against an association under a statute.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","Hawaii · civil rules","On timely application, intervention is of right when a statute confers an unconditional right or the applicant’s interest may be impaired and is not adequately represented, and permissive when a statute confers a conditional right or a common question exists; State or county officers and agencies may intervene when a party relies on a law or regulation they administer. The motion must be served under Rule 5 with a pl","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","Hawaii · civil rules","If a party dies and the claim survives, a motion to substitute (served on parties under Rule 5 and on nonparties like a summons) must be made within 120 days after death is suggested on the record by a served statement, or the action is dismissed as to the deceased party; the federal period is 90 days. Substitution is also available for incompetency and transfer of interest, and a public officer’s successor is automa","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 25.1. Withdrawal, substitution, and appearance of counsel","Hawaii · civil rules","Except as RCCH Rule 10(c) provides, withdrawal or substitution of counsel in circuit court is effective only with court approval, subject to HRPC Rule 1.16. A withdrawal-and-substitution must cite authority, bear the signatures of the withdrawing and substituting attorneys and the client, show the trial date, and include an “APPROVED AND SO ORDERED” signature line; a motion to withdraw must be served on the client, c","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 26. General provisions governing discovery","Hawaii · civil rules","Under the version effective January 1, 2022, parties must make initial disclosures (trial witnesses other than experts, supporting documents, damages computations, and insurance declarations pages and any reservation-of-rights letters) at or within 14 days after the Rule 26(f) conference, or within 30 days after being served or joined later; foreclosure, Court Annexed Arbitration Program, agency appeal, consumer debt","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions before action or pending appeal","Hawaii · civil rules","A person expecting to be a party to an action cognizable in a Hawai‘i court may file a verified petition to perpetuate testimony in the circuit court of the circuit where any expected adverse party resides, with notice served at least 20 days before the hearing in the manner of Rule 4(d); the court appoints an attorney for unserved adverse parties. While an appeal is pending or the time to appeal has not expired, the","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Hawaii · civil rules","In the United States, depositions are taken before an officer authorized by Hawai‘i, federal, or local law, or a person appointed by the court; abroad, under a treaty or convention, a letter of request, on notice before an authorized person, or before a commissioned person. A deposition may not be taken before a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or of counsel, or anyone financially interested in the action.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","Hawaii · civil rules","Unless the court directs otherwise, the parties may stipulate in writing to deposition logistics and to modify other discovery procedures or limits. Two exceptions: the expedited-track limits in Rule 16.1(c) may be changed only as that rule provides, and stipulations extending the response times under Rules 33, 34, and 36 need court approval if they would interfere with any time set for completing discovery, hearing ","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions upon oral examination","Hawaii · civil rules","Any party may depose any person after the action begins; leave is needed for a plaintiff’s deposition within 30 days after service on any defendant (unless the defendant has sought discovery or the notice certifies the deponent is about to leave the State or the United States or go to sea), for a prisoner, for more than 10 depositions per side under Rules 30 and 31, or to re-depose someone, absent written stipulation","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions upon written questions","Hawaii · civil rules","A party may depose any person on written questions without leave except for a prisoner, more than 10 depositions per side, or a repeat deposition. Cross questions are due within 14 days after the notice and questions are served, redirect within 7 days after cross, and recross within 7 days after redirect; the court may enlarge or shorten these times. The officer takes the answers under Rule 30(c), (e), and (f), and t","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 32. Use of depositions in court proceedings","Hawaii · civil rules","A deposition may be used against a party present, represented, or given reasonable notice: for impeachment or as the Hawai‘i Rules of Evidence permit, by an adverse party for any purpose if the deponent was a party or its officer, director, managing agent, or designee, and by any party if the witness is dead, unable to attend, could not be subpoenaed, exceptional circumstances exist, or—unlike the federal 100-mile te","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories to parties","Hawaii · civil rules","Without leave or stipulation, a party may serve up to 60 interrogatories, counting subparts as separate questions (the federal limit is 25). Answers and objections are due within 30 days after service, except that a defendant may respond within 45 days after service of the summons and complaint; objections must be specific or are waived absent good cause. Contention interrogatories are allowed, and business records m","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 34. Production of documents, electronically stored information, and things; entry on land","Hawaii · civil rules","A party may request inspection, copying, testing, or sampling of designated documents, ESI, and tangible things in the responding party’s possession, custody, or control, or entry on land. The written response is due within 30 days after service, or for a defendant within 45 days after service of the summons and complaint, and must state objections with reasons and permit inspection of the rest. Documents are produce","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examination of persons","Hawaii · civil rules","When a party’s (or a person in its custody or control’s) mental or physical condition, including blood group, is in controversy, the court may order an examination by a suitably licensed or certified examiner on motion for good cause and notice, specifying time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner. On request, the party causing the examination must provide the detailed report and earlier reports of the sam","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","Hawaii · civil rules","A party may request admission of facts, opinions of fact, application of law to fact, and genuineness of documents (with copies attached). A matter is admitted unless a signed written answer or objection is served within 30 days after service, but a defendant need not respond before 45 days after service of the summons and complaint unless the court shortens the time; lack of knowledge is no excuse without reasonable","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to make or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","Hawaii · civil rules","A motion to compel (filed in the court where the action is pending, or for a deposition in the circuit where it is taken; for a nonparty, in the circuit of the deposition) must include a good-faith conferral certification; evasive or incomplete answers count as failures, and expenses including attorney’s fees follow the result unless the position was substantially justified. Disobeying a discovery order (including a ","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury trial of right","Hawaii · civil rules","The constitutional and statutory jury right is preserved. A party must serve a written jury demand no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue (the federal period is 14 days) and file it under Rule 5(d); another party may demand jury trial of other issues within 10 days after service of a partial demand. On a statutory de novo appeal to circuit court, the jury demand goes in the not","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","Hawaii · civil rules","Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate in writing or on the record to a bench trial or the court finds no jury right exists. Undemanded issues are tried to the court, though the court may order a jury on motion; an advisory jury or a consent jury with binding effect is available in nonjury cases.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 40. Assignment of cases for trial","Hawaii · civil rules","The circuit courts provide by rule for placing actions on the trial calendar, with or without a party’s request, giving precedence to actions entitled to it by statute.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","Hawaii · civil rules","A plaintiff may dismiss without order by notice filed before the return date under Rule 12(a) or before service of an answer or summary judgment motion, whichever comes first, or by stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared, in the form required by Rule 41.1; dismissal is without prejudice unless stated, but a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits. Otherwise dis","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 41.1. Documents dismissing an action or part of it","Hawaii · civil rules","Notices and stipulations of dismissal under Rules 41(a)(1)(A), 41(a)(1)(B), and 41(c) must be presented before filing to the assigned judge (including in Court-Annexed Arbitration Program cases) or, if no judge is assigned, to the Legal Documents Branch/Section. Each must show below the title the judge (or “none”) and trial date (or “none”), cite the specific Rule 41 subsections, and for a Rule 41(a)(1)(A) notice sta","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials","Hawaii · civil rules","Actions with a common question of law or fact may be joined for hearing or trial or consolidated. The court may order separate trials of claims or issues for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or for expedition and economy, preserving the jury right.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 43. Taking of testimony","Hawaii · civil rules","Trial testimony is taken orally in open court unless the Hawai‘i Rules of Evidence, a statute, or other Hawai‘i Supreme Court rules provide otherwise; the text does not include the federal provision on remote testimony by contemporaneous transmission. The court may schedule all expert testimony during the same phase of trial; offers of proof may be made when an objection is sustained, affirmation may replace oath, mo","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 44. Proof of official record","Hawaii · civil rules","A domestic official record may be proved by official publication or a copy attested by the custodian with a certificate of custody from a judge of a court of record or a sealed public officer; foreign records require attestation and a final certification by a U.S. or foreign diplomatic or consular official, which the court may excuse for good cause or a treaty may make unnecessary. A certified statement of diligent s","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 44.1. Determination of foreign law","Hawaii · civil rules","A party intending to raise foreign law must give notice by pleading or other reasonable written notice. The court may consider any relevant material or source, whether or not admissible under the Hawai‘i Rules of Evidence, and its determination is a ruling on a question of law.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","Hawaii · civil rules","The clerk of the circuit court where the action is pending issues subpoenas under seal, signed but otherwise blank, for the requesting party to complete; a subpoena may require production of documents, ESI, or things, and the court may quash or modify an unreasonable and oppressive subpoena on motion made by the compliance time or condition denial on advance payment of production costs. Subpoenas may be served anywhe","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 46. Exceptions unnecessary","Hawaii · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary; it is enough to make known the desired action or objection and its grounds when the ruling is made or sought, and a party with no opportunity to object is not prejudiced.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 47. Jurors","Hawaii · civil rules","At the court’s discretion each party may give a brief “mini-opening statement” to the panel before jury selection, and the court must permit the parties or their attorneys to examine prospective jurors (or to supplement the court’s examination), unlike the federal rule, which leaves attorney voir dire to the court’s discretion. Up to 6 alternate jurors may be seated, with 1 extra peremptory per side for 1–2 alternate","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 48. Juries of less than twelve; majority verdict","Hawaii · civil rules","The parties may stipulate to a jury of fewer than 12 or to a verdict or finding by a stated majority (see HRS § 635-20). Unlike the federal rule, the text does not set a minimum of 6 jurors or require unanimity absent stipulation.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 49. Special verdicts and interrogatories","Hawaii · civil rules","The court may require a special verdict with written findings on each fact issue; an omitted issue is waived unless a party demands its submission before the jury retires, and otherwise the court may find it or is deemed to have found consistently with the judgment. With a general verdict and interrogatories, harmonious answers yield judgment under Rule 58, consistent answers inconsistent with the verdict may support","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 50. Judgment as a matter of law; new trial; conditional rulings","Hawaii · civil rules","A motion for judgment as a matter of law may be made any time before submission to the jury and must specify the judgment sought and its legal and factual basis. A renewed motion must be filed no later than 10 days after entry of judgment (the federal period is 28 days), alone or with a Rule 59 new-trial request, and the court may let the judgment stand, order a new trial, or direct judgment. If the renewed motion is","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 51. Instructions to jury","Hawaii · civil rules","The court may pre-instruct the jury on elements and defenses before evidence; parties file written requests at the close of evidence or earlier as directed, and the court marks each “given,” “refused,” or modified, with unobjected instructions marked “given by agreement” and not later challengeable. The court may revise and combine instructions in writing after hearing counsel, may not orally qualify, modify, or expl","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings by the court","Hawaii · civil rules","In bench trials and with advisory juries, and when ruling on interlocutory injunctions, the court must find facts specially and state conclusions of law separately; findings are reviewed for clear error, and findings are unnecessary on Rule 12 or 56 motions. A motion to amend findings or the judgment must be made no later than 10 days after entry of judgment (the federal period is 28 days) and may be joined with a Ru","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters","Hawaii · civil rules","The court may appoint a special master (including a referee, auditor, examiner, or assessor), fix compensation charged to the parties or a fund, and the master may get execution against a party that fails to pay. Reference is the exception: in jury cases only when issues are complicated, and in bench cases only for accounts, difficult damages computations, or an exceptional condition. The order of reference may limit","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgments; costs; attorneys’ fees","Hawaii · civil rules","“Judgment” includes a decree and any appealable order; with multiple claims or parties, the court may enter final judgment on fewer than all only upon an express determination of no just reason for delay and an express direction for entry. Default judgments may not differ in kind or exceed the demand, but other judgments grant all relief to which the party is entitled. Costs go to the prevailing party as of course (a","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 55. Default","Hawaii · civil rules","The clerk enters default when a party fails to plead or otherwise defend. The clerk may enter judgment for a sum certain on affidavit against a defendant defaulted for failure to appear who is not an infant or incompetent; otherwise the court enters judgment, and a party that has appeared must receive written notice of the application at least 3 days before the hearing (the federal period is 7 days). Default may be s","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","Hawaii · civil rules","A claimant may move after 20 days from commencement (or after the adverse party moves), and a defending party at any time, but any summary judgment motion must be filed and served no less than 50 days before trial unless the court permits otherwise for good cause. The motion must be filed and served at least 18 days before the hearing, opposition at least 8 days before, and any reply at least 3 days before; judgment ","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments","Hawaii · civil rules","Declaratory judgment procedure under statute follows these rules, with jury rights under Rules 38 and 39; another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, except that declaratory relief is not available in any controversy about taxes. The court may order a speedy hearing and advance the case.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 58. Entry of judgment","Hawaii · civil rules","Unless the court directs otherwise, and subject to Rule 54 and RCCH Rule 23, the prevailing party prepares and submits the proposed judgment. Filing the judgment in the clerk’s office is its entry, and it is not effective before entry; entry is not delayed for taxing costs, and every judgment must be on a separate document. The text has no counterpart to the federal rule deeming judgment entered 150 days after docket","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 59. New trials; altering or amending judgment","Hawaii · civil rules","A new trial may be granted on the grounds historically recognized in Hawai‘i actions at law (jury cases) or for rehearing in equity (bench cases). A new-trial motion, and any motion to alter or amend the judgment, must be filed no later than 10 days after entry of judgment (the federal period is 28 days); opposing affidavits are due 10 days after service, extendable up to 20 days by the court for good cause or writte","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from judgment or order","Hawaii · civil rules","Clerical mistakes may be corrected any time, but once an appeal is docketed only with leave of the appellate court. Relief from a final judgment, order, or proceeding is available for the six familiar reasons (mistake or excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, fraud, voidness, satisfaction or changed prospective equity, any other reason); the motion must be made within a reasonable time and, for the first three","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","Hawaii · civil rules","No error in admitting or excluding evidence or in any ruling is a ground for a new trial or for disturbing a judgment unless refusing relief would be inconsistent with substantial justice; the court must disregard errors that do not affect substantial rights.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","Hawaii · civil rules","No execution or enforcement may issue until 10 days after entry of judgment (the federal automatic stay is 30 days), but judgments in injunction or receivership actions and accounting orders are not stayed unless the court orders otherwise. The court may stay enforcement pending Rule 50, 52(b), 59, or 60 motions on conditions securing the adverse party, may modify or grant injunctions pending appeal, and the appellan","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 63. Inability of a judge to proceed","Hawaii · civil rules","If a judge cannot continue a commenced trial or hearing, another judge may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record and finding no prejudice. In a nonjury trial or hearing, the successor must, on request, recall any available witness whose testimony is material and disputed, and may recall others.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 64. Seizure of person or property","Hawaii · civil rules","All Hawai‘i-law remedies for seizing a person or property to secure satisfaction of the eventual judgment are available at the start of and during an action, including arrest, attachment, garnishment, replevin, and sequestration.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions","Hawaii · civil rules","No preliminary injunction issues without notice, and the court may consolidate the hearing with trial on the merits while preserving jury rights. An ex parte temporary restraining order requires specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint showing immediate irreparable injury and the attorney’s written certification of notice efforts; it must state the date and hour, define the injury, and expire within the time","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 65.1. Security: proceedings against sureties","Hawaii · civil rules","A surety on a bond or undertaking given under these rules submits to the court’s jurisdiction and irrevocably appoints the clerk as its agent for service. Liability may be enforced by motion without an independent action, with the motion served on the clerk, who mails copies to sureties with known addresses.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers appointed by courts","Hawaii · civil rules","An action in which a receiver has been appointed may not be dismissed except by court order. Administration of estates by receivers follows prior practice; otherwise these rules govern actions seeking a receiver or brought by or against one.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit in court","Hawaii · civil rules","In an action seeking money or another deliverable thing, a party may, with notice to every other party and leave of court, deposit all or part of it with the court; deposit and withdrawal follow court orders.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of settlement or judgment","Hawaii · civil rules","More than 10 days before trial begins, any party (not only a defending party, as in the federal rule) may serve an offer of settlement or an offer to allow judgment against either party, with costs then accrued. Acceptance must be served within 10 days after service of the offer, after which the clerk enters dismissal or judgment; if the offeree’s final judgment is not more favorable than the offer, the offeree pays ","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution","Hawaii · civil rules","Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution unless the court directs otherwise, with procedure under State law. The judgment creditor, or a successor in interest of record, may obtain discovery from any person, including the judgment debtor, by deposition procedures.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 70. Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","Hawaii · civil rules","If a party fails to perform an act required by judgment (such as conveying land or delivering documents), the court may have another appointed person do it at the party’s cost with the same effect, the clerk issues writs of attachment or sequestration on application, and contempt is available. For property within the State the court may enter a judgment divesting and vesting title, and a party awarded possession gets","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 71. Process in behalf of and against persons not parties","Hawaii · civil rules","A nonparty in whose favor an order is made may enforce it by the same process as a party, and a nonparty against whom an order may lawfully be enforced is subject to the same process as a party.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 72. Appeal to a circuit court","Hawaii · civil rules","Where a statute allows circuit-court review of a decision of a non-court governmental official or body, the appeal is by notice of appeal filed in the circuit court within 30 days after the appellant is notified of the decision as the statute provides; a mailed notice is timely if postmarked within the period and received within 5 days after the postmark, and a certified copy must be served promptly on each appellee.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 73–76. Deleted","Hawaii · civil rules","Rules 73 through 76 are deleted.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 77. Circuit courts and clerks","Hawaii · civil rules","Circuit courts are always open for filing, and documents filed through JEFS or JIMS are deemed filed with the clerk; trials are in open court, while other proceedings may be in chambers anywhere, but no non-ex parte hearing may be held outside the circuit without all affected parties’ consent. The clerk’s office is open business hours except weekends and holidays and may grant process, defaults, and default judgments","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 78. Motion day","Hawaii · civil rules","Each circuit court sets regular times and places for hearing motions requiring notice, unless local conditions make that impracticable, and the judge may make orders advancing and conducting actions at any time. The court may by rule or order decide motions without oral hearing on brief written statements.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 79. Books and records kept by the clerk","Hawaii · civil rules","The clerk keeps a civil docket in the form the supreme court prescribes, with consecutive file numbers and chronological notation of filings, process, orders, verdicts, and judgments, marking jury actions “jury.” The clerk also keeps copies of final judgments, appealable orders, and orders affecting title to or liens on property, indices, trial calendars distinguishing jury and court actions, and other records the su","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 80. Stenographic report or transcript as evidence","Hawaii · civil rules","Subdivisions (a) and (b) are reserved. When stenographically reported testimony from a trial or hearing is admissible at a later trial, it may be proved by a transcript certified by the reporter.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 81. Applicability","Hawaii · civil rules","The rules do not apply (unless a rule says otherwise) to probate (HRS ch. 560), guardianship (ch. 551), ex parte trustee and guardian accounts (ch. 554), family court, pre-judgment arbitration applications under ch. 658A, habeas corpus (ch. 660), writs to inferior courts under § 603-21.7(b), bond forfeiture under § 709-51, and corporate meeting calls under § 416-81; they apply, except where inconsistent with specific","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 81.1. Mandamus","Hawaii · civil rules","The writ of mandamus is abolished in the circuit courts except when directed to a court of inferior jurisdiction; equivalent relief is obtained by appropriate action or motion, and the court may shorten time limits in mandamus-type actions.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 82. Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","Hawaii · civil rules","The rules do not extend or limit the circuit courts’ jurisdiction or venue.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 83. Rules by circuit courts","Hawaii · civil rules","Each circuit court may recommend rules consistent with these rules, which take effect when promulgated by the supreme court and are made available to every licensed attorney; in matters not covered by rule, circuit courts may regulate practice consistently with these rules. This differs from the federal rule, under which district courts adopt local rules themselves.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 84. Forms","Hawaii · civil rules","The forms in the Appendix of Forms (Forms 1 through 36, including the civil information sheets, publication summons, limited-appearance forms, and scheduling-conference forms) are sufficient under the rules and illustrate the intended simplicity and brevity. Unlike federal Rule 84, which was abrogated in 2015, the Hawai‘i rule and its forms remain in effect.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 85. Title","Hawaii · civil rules","The rules may be cited as the Hawai‘i Rules of Civil Procedure.","#st-hi-rules"],["Rule 86. Reserved","Hawaii · civil rules","Deleted April 7, 1980, effective April 7, 1980.","#st-hi-rules"],["Appeals in Hawai‘i: everything starts at the ICA, and finality is strict","Hawaii · appeals","Almost every civil appeal goes first to the Intermediate Court of Appeals (ICA). The Supreme Court takes cases from the ICA by transfer before decision, or reviews ICA decisions by discretionary certiorari. The notice of appeal is due 30 days after entry of an appealable judgment or order, and no appellate court can enlarge the Rule 4 deadlines. Two Hawai‘i features catch newcomers. The 10-day post-judgment motions h","#st-hi-appeals"],["ICA hears all appeals first","Hawaii · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","Appeals are allowed from all final judgments, orders, and decrees of the circuit, district, and land courts to the ICA, which also issues writs in aid of its jurisdiction. The Supreme Court reaches the case by transfer or certiorari. HRS §§ 641-1(a), 602-57","#st-hi-appeals"],["Separate, complete judgment","Hawaii · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","Entry means filing in the clerk’s office, and every judgment must be set out in a separate document. With multiple claims or parties, a judgment on fewer than all is final only with an express determination that there is no just reason for delay and an express direction for entry. In circuit court the prevailing party must prepare the proposed judgment within 10 days after the decision; objections to form are due wit","#st-hi-appeals"],["Prove jurisdiction early","Hawaii · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","Within 10 days after the record on appeal is filed, each appellant and cross-appellant must file a statement of jurisdiction (10 pages maximum). In multi-claim or multi-party cases it must show, with record cites, how each claim against each party was resolved or that the judgment was certified, with the order attached. Failure can lead to dismissal. HRAP Rule 12.1","#st-hi-appeals"],["Interlocutory appeals need the circuit court’s permission","Hawaii · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","A circuit court may allow an appeal from an order denying a motion to dismiss or any interlocutory order if it would speed the end of the litigation, and its refusal cannot be reviewed. The motion for leave is due within 30 days after entry of the order; if granted, the notice of appeal is due within 30 days after the order granting leave. HRS § 641-1(b); HRAP Rule 4(a)(1)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP denials are immediately appealable","Hawaii · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","A party whose special motion to dismiss under the Hawaii Public Expression Protection Act is denied in whole or part may appeal as of right within 30 days, and all proceedings are stayed during the appeal. HRS §§ 634G-8, 634G-3(d)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Extraordinary writs go to the Supreme Court","Hawaii · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","Petitions for mandamus or prohibition directed to a judge are filed with the appellate clerk, served on the judge, all parties, and the attorney general, and submitted to the Supreme Court to decide whether to entertain them. HRAP Rule 21(a)","#st-hi-appeals"],["30 days; file in the appellate court through JEFS","Hawaii · appeals · Notice of appeal and tolling","The civil notice of appeal is due within 30 days after entry of the judgment or appealable order. Attorneys who are JEFS users file it electronically with the appellate court; self-represented and exempt filers file it with the clerk of the court appealed from. A mailed notice is timely if postmarked in time and received within 5 days. A notice filed after the decision is announced but before entry is treated as file","#st-hi-appeals"],["Serve the notice and file proof within 7 days","Hawaii · appeals · Notice of appeal and tolling","The appellant serves a filed copy of the notice on every other party and files proof of service with the appellate court within 7 days. Missing any step other than the timely notice does not void the appeal but may bring sanctions, including dismissal. HRAP Rule 3(a), (e)(1)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Tolling motions and the 90-day rule (current)","Hawaii · appeals · Notice of appeal and tolling","A timely motion for judgment as a matter of law, amended findings, new trial, reconsideration or alter/amend, or attorney’s fees or costs extends the appeal time for all parties until 30 days after the order deciding it. The court must decide within 90 days. If it does not, within 5 days after the 90th day the clerk notifies the parties that the motion is denied and later orders are void, and the appeal time runs fro","#st-hi-appeals"],["Tolling rule from January 1, 2027","Hawaii · appeals · Notice of appeal and tolling","The amended rule lists the tolling motions by rule: HRCP 50(b), 52(b), 59; Rule 60(b) only if filed within 30 days of judgment; probate Rule 36(b) within 30 days; HRCP 54 fee or cost motions only if filed within 14 days; and agency motions. A filed written order within 90 days starts a new 30-day period. With no filed written order within 90 days, the motion is denied, later orders are void, and the notice of appeal ","#st-hi-appeals"],["Extensions only from the trial court, capped at 30 days","Hawaii · appeals · Notice of appeal and tolling","The court or agency appealed from may extend the time for good cause on a motion filed within the original period, or for excusable neglect on a motion filed within 30 days after it expires. No extension may exceed 30 days past the original deadline. No court may change Rule 4’s jurisdictional requirements, and default in filing a timely notice of appeal cannot be excused. HRAP Rules 4(a)(4), 26(b), 26(e)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Cross-appeals","Hawaii · appeals · Notice of appeal and tolling","Any party may cross-appeal within 14 days after the notice of appeal is served on it, or within the time for the notice of appeal, whichever is later. The cross-appeal survives dismissal of the main appeal, and separate opening and answering briefs are filed on it. HRAP Rules 4.1(b)(1), 4.1(d), 28(h)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Transcripts and findings within 10 days","Hawaii · appeals · Record, briefing, and form","Within 10 days after filing the notice of appeal, request the transcripts needed, or certify that none are needed. If the trial court must enter findings once an appeal is filed, request them in that court within 10 days, and the judge must enter them within 28 days. The clerk below files the record within 60 days after the notice of appeal. HRAP Rules 10(b)(1)(A), 10(f), 11(b)(1)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Briefing schedule","Hawaii · appeals · Record, briefing, and form","Opening brief: 40 days after the record on appeal is filed. Answering brief: 40 days after service of the opening brief, or after docketing, whichever is later. Reply: 14 days after service of the answering brief; if none will be filed, notify the clerk before the deadline. Mail service adds 2 days. HRAP Rules 28(b)–(d), 26(c)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Extensions of briefing time","Hawaii · appeals · Record, briefing, and form","The appellate clerk grants one extension on a timely request (received within the original time): up to 30 days for an opening or answering brief, 10 days for a reply. Further extensions require a motion filed at least 5 days before the due date and good cause. A pending request does not toll the deadline. HRAP Rule 29","#st-hi-appeals"],["Page limits, not word limits","Hawaii · appeals · Record, briefing, and form","Opening and answering briefs are limited to 35 pages and replies to 10 pages, excluding indexes, appendices, and the statement of related cases. Use 12-point type (Times New Roman, Courier New, or Arial qualify), double or 1.5 spacing, and 1-inch margins. Footnotes must be in the same size, and condensing print is prohibited. HRAP Rules 28(a), 32(a)–(b)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Opening brief contents are enforced","Hawaii · appeals · Record, briefing, and form","The opening brief must include numbered points of error stating the error, where it occurred in the record, and where it was objected to, with required quotations for evidence, instruction, and finding points. It also needs a separate standard-of-review section, record cites by JEFS/JIMS docket number and electronic page, the challenged rulings appended, and a statement of related cases. Points not presented this way","#st-hi-appeals"],["Late or nonconforming briefs","Hawaii · appeals · Record, briefing, and form","An appeal may be dismissed, or a brief stricken, for late or nonconforming briefs. An appellee’s default may lead the court to accept the appellant’s statement of facts as true. Once a brief is filed, the court must give notice and a chance to cure first. HRAP Rule 30","#st-hi-appeals"],["Citing unpublished decisions","Hawaii · appeals · Record, briefing, and form","Memorandum opinions and unpublished orders filed on or after July 1, 2008 may be cited for persuasive value, with a copy appended. Earlier ones generally may not be cited. HRAP Rule 35(c) (amended July 21, 2026)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Appellate mediation","Hawaii · appeals · Record, briefing, and form","If an appeal is selected for the Hawai‘i Appellate Mediation Program, participation is required, and transcript, record, jurisdiction-statement, and briefing deadlines are stayed until the clerk returns the case to the docket. HRAP Rules 3.1(e), 33(a)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Stay: ask the trial court first","Hawaii · appeals · Stays and costs","Stays, supersedeas bonds, and injunctions pending appeal are normally sought first in the court appealed from. An appellate motion must show that asking below was impracticable, or was denied with reasons. Execution is automatically stayed for only 10 days after entry of judgment, and a supersedeas bond stays enforcement once approved; the State and counties need no bond. HRAP Rule 8(a); HRCP Rule 62(a), (d)–(e)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Fees and costs on appeal","Hawaii · appeals · Stays and costs","Request fees and costs with an itemized, verified bill (Form 8 for non-indigent requests) and a statement of authority no later than 14 days after the reconsideration period expires or reconsideration is decided. Objections are due in 10 days and replies in 7. Copying costs are capped at 20¢ per page. HRAP Rule 39(c)–(d)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Reconsideration: 10 days, one motion","Hawaii · appeals · Rehearing and Supreme Court review","A motion for reconsideration is due within 10 days after the opinion, order, or ruling. It must state with particularity what was overlooked or misapprehended and include a good-faith declaration. No response unless requested. If the court does not act within 10 days, the motion is rejected, and only one motion is allowed per party. HRAP Rule 40","#st-hi-appeals"],["Certiorari: 30 days plus an automatic 30","Hawaii · appeals · Rehearing and Supreme Court review","Apply for a writ of certiorari within 30 days after the ICA’s judgment on appeal or dismissal order. A written request filed within that 30 days gets an automatic 30-day extension; an untimely request extends nothing. The application is limited to 20 pages, a response (optional) to 20 pages within 15 days (automatic 15-day extension on timely request), and a reply to 5 pages within 7 days. Grounds are grave errors of","#st-hi-appeals"],["Transfer to the Supreme Court before ICA decision","Hawaii · appeals · Rehearing and Supreme Court review","Any party may apply to transfer a case from the ICA no earlier than 10 days after the record is filed and no later than 20 days after the last brief is or could have been filed. The explanation is limited to 10 pages, and responses are due in 10 days. Transfer is mandatory for questions of imperative or fundamental public importance, rulings invalidating a constitutional amendment or holding a statute, ordinance, or ","#st-hi-appeals"],["When the ICA judgment takes effect","Hawaii · appeals · Rehearing and Supreme Court review","The ICA judgment becomes effective on the 31st day after entry if no certiorari application is filed (or when an extension expires). A timely application stays it until the Supreme Court rejects, dismisses, or affirms. Reconsideration, transfer, or certiorari is not required to exhaust state remedies. HRAP Rules 36(c), 41, 40.3","#st-hi-appeals"],["JEFS is mandatory for attorneys","Hawaii · appeals · E-filing and counsel","Unless excused, every attorney appearing in the appellate courts must register as a JEFS user and file everything through JEFS. Filing is complete when the Notice of Electronic Filing issues, and the NEF time stamp controls. Each PDF document and exhibit is limited to 10 MB. Pro hac vice counsel cannot register, so local counsel files. HRAP Rule 25(a); HEFSR Rules 2.2, 2.3, 3.1, 3.3, 4.1(a)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Withdrawal and substitution of appellate counsel","Hawaii · appeals · E-filing and counsel","Withdrawal requires a motion served on the client, but a client-signed notice of withdrawal and substitution works without a motion. Since July 14, 2026, a firm may also file a notice of disaffiliation or staffing change naming continuing counsel and showing the client was informed. HRAP Rule 50 (amended July 14, 2026)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Recusal of appellate judges: 10 days","Hawaii · appeals · E-filing and counsel","A motion to disqualify an appellate judge or justice is due within 10 days after the appellate case is opened or after newly discovered information, or the objection is waived absent good cause. HRAP Rule 5(b)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (civil)","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry (filing) of the judgment or appealable order.\nJurisdictional. Mailed notice timely if postmarked in time and received within 5 days. File the Civil Appeal Docketing Statement with it. HRAP 4(a)(1)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Proof of service of notice of appeal","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from filing of the notice of appeal. HRAP 3(e)(1)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Motion for leave for interlocutory appeal (circuit court)","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the interlocutory order.\nIf granted, the notice of appeal is due 30 days after entry of the order granting leave. HRAP 4(a)(1); HRS § 641-1(b)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Renewed JMOL / amend findings / new trial / alter or amend","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended (HRCP 6(b)). A timely motion tolls the appeal time. HRCP 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Notice of appeal after a tolling motion","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order deciding the motion (or the clerk’s deemed-denial notice).\nThe court must rule within 90 days of the motion; otherwise it is deemed denied. From Jan. 1, 2027: 30 days after a filed written order entered within 90 days, otherwise 120 days after the motion was filed. HRAP 4(a)(3) (current)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Extension of time for notice of appeal","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from expiration of the original appeal period.\nMotion in the court appealed from: good cause if filed before expiry, excusable neglect if filed within 30 days after. Extension capped at 30 days past the original deadline. HRAP 4(a)(4)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Cross-appeal","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the notice of appeal on the cross-appellant.\nOr within the time for the notice of appeal, whichever is later. HRAP 4.1(b)(1)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Transcript request (or certificate that none needed)","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the notice of appeal. HRAP 10(b)(1)(A)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Request for findings of fact/conclusions of law (where required after appeal)","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nThe judge must enter them within 28 days of the request. HRAP 10(f)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Record on appeal filed by clerk below","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from filing of the notice of appeal. HRAP 11(b)(1)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Statement of jurisdiction","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the record on appeal.\n10-page limit. Appellees contesting jurisdiction file within the same period. HRAP 12.1(a)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Opening brief","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from filing of the record on appeal.\n35-page limit. One clerk extension of up to 30 days on a timely request (HRAP 29(a)). HRAP 28(b)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Answering brief","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from service of the opening brief (or docketing, if later).\n35-page limit. One clerk extension of up to 30 days. HRAP 28(c)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Reply brief","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the answering brief.\n10-page limit. One clerk extension of up to 10 days. If not filing, notify the clerk before the deadline. HRAP 28(d)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Motion for further brief extension","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","5 days from before the brief’s due date (at least).\nGood cause required. Does not toll the deadline. HRAP 29(b)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Motion to disqualify appellate judge/justice","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from opening of the appellate proceeding or discovery of new information.\nOtherwise waived absent good cause. HRAP 5(b)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Motion for reconsideration (appellate)","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the opinion, dispositional order, or ruling.\nIf the court does not act within 10 days, the motion is rejected. One motion per party. HRAP 40(a)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Request for appellate fees and costs","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from expiration of the reconsideration period or decision on reconsideration.\nObjections due in 10 days, replies in 7 (HRAP 39(d)(4)). HRAP 39(d)(2)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Application for writ of certiorari","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the ICA judgment on appeal or dismissal order.\nA written request filed within the 30 days gets an automatic 30-day extension. 20-page limit. HRAP 40.1(a)(1); HRS § 602-59(c)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Response to certiorari application","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the application.\nOptional. Automatic 15-day extension on timely request. Reply within 7 days, 5 pages. HRAP 40.1(e)(1)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Supreme Court action on certiorari","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from filing of the application.\nNo acceptance within 60 days = rejection. HRAP 40.1(g)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Application for transfer to Supreme Court (latest)","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from filing (or due date) of the last brief.\nNot earlier than 10 days after the record is filed. Response 10 days. Decision within 45 days. No extensions. HRAP 40.2(a)(2)","#st-hi-appeals"],["Appeal from denial of anti-SLAPP motion","Hawaii · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from order denying the special motion to dismiss.\nAs of right. All proceedings are stayed pending appeal (HRS § 634G-3(d)). HRS § 634G-8","#st-hi-appeals"],["Iowa Supreme Court","Iowa · courts","Every appeal is filed in the Supreme Court. It keeps cases raising substantial constitutional questions, conflicts, first-impression issues, urgent issues of broad public importance, and lawyer discipline, and transfers the rest to the Court of Appeals. It ordinarily decides cases en banc. It reviews Court of Appeals decisions only on a discretionary application for further review. It also answers certified questions","#st-ia"],["Iowa Court of Appeals","Iowa · courts","Decides only the cases the Supreme Court transfers to it, which may sit in panels. It decides which of its opinions to publish. Unpublished opinions may be cited as persuasive but are not controlling.","#st-ia"],["Iowa District Court","Iowa · courts","The single general-jurisdiction trial court, organized into eight judicial districts. District judges, district associate judges, associate juvenile judges, and associate probate judges hear civil, criminal, juvenile, probate, and small-claims matters. Small claims are appealed to a district judge, not to the appellate courts (Iowa R. App. P. 6.105).","#st-ia"],["Iowa Business Court","Iowa · courts","A business court created by statute (Iowa Code § 602.6801, Senate File 639). Its judges were appointed effective September 1, 2026, and visiting judges may be assigned. It follows an earlier Iowa Business Specialty Court that operated under supreme court memoranda of operation. Eligibility and transfer rules were not collected; check the statute and current orders.","#st-ia"],["Iowa adopted the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act in 2025","Iowa · anti-SLAPP","Iowa Code chapter 652 (2025 Iowa Acts ch. 93) applies to civil actions filed on or after July 1, 2025. It covers claims based on: (a) communications in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding; (b) communications on an issue under consideration or review in such a proceeding; or (c) exercise of the federal or Iowa rights of free speech, press, assembly, petition, or associa","#st-ia-slapp"],["Only three exclusions","Iowa · anti-SLAPP","The act does not apply to claims (a) against a governmental unit, or its employee or agent acting or purporting to act officially; (b) by a governmental unit or its official enforcing a law against an imminent threat to public health or safety; or (c) against a person primarily engaged in selling or leasing goods or services, where the claim arises from a communication related to that sale or lease. ‘Goods or service","#st-ia-slapp"],["Procedure: special motion within 60 days; automatic stay","Iowa · anti-SLAPP","File a special motion for expedited relief within 60 days after being served with the pleading asserting the claim, or later for good cause. Filing stays all other proceedings between the moving and responding parties, including discovery, until the ruling and the time to appeal it expire. The court may allow limited, specific discovery and may hear unrelated motions or emergency injunction motions.\nThe hearing must ","#st-ia-slapp"],["Standard: three-step test, dismissal with prejudice","Iowa · anti-SLAPP","The court considers the pleadings, the motion, any reply or response, and evidence that could be considered on summary judgment under Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.981. It must dismiss with prejudice if: (1) the movant shows the act applies; (2) the responding party fails to show an exclusion applies; and (3) either the responding party fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows failure ","#st-ia-slapp"],["Fees are mandatory","Iowa · anti-SLAPP","The court shall award court costs, reasonable attorney fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion to a moving party who prevails. A responding party gets them only if it prevails and the court finds the motion frivolous or filed solely to delay. A fee motion is not subject to the stay. Iowa Code §§ 652.4(5), 652.10","#st-ia-slapp"],["Immediate appeal within 30 days, with a stay","Iowa · anti-SLAPP","A moving party may appeal as of right from an order denying the motion in whole or in part, within 30 days after entry. While the appeal is pending, all proceedings between all parties are stayed. Under the appellate rules, an appeal authorized by statute is taken by notice of appeal that names the statute. Iowa Code §§ 652.4(3), 652.9; Iowa R. App. P. 6.108","#st-ia-slapp"],["Pro hac vice requires Iowa co-counsel who actively participates","Iowa · out of state","A court may admit an eligible out-of-state lawyer for a particular proceeding only if the lawyer appears with an Iowa lawyer. The Iowa lawyer must appear of record, actively participate, accept service for the out-of-state lawyer (Iowa Code § 602.10111), and tell the client of any disagreement in judgment. Agency proceedings also require pro hac vice admission, even where nonlawyers may appear. Admission covers only ","#st-ia-oos"],["$250 registration fee, good for 5 years","Iowa · out of state","Before applying, the out-of-state lawyer must register with the Office of Professional Regulation and pay $250 to the client security trust fund. Registration lets the lawyer file pro hac vice applications in any Iowa court or agency for 5 years from the registration date; after that, register and pay again. Iowa Ct. R. 31.14(5)(r), 31.14(11)","#st-ia-oos"],["Verified application, served on all parties; five-case limit","Iowa · out of state","The verified application (Form 1 for district court, Form 2 for the supreme court) must be served on all parties who have appeared, with proof of service. It must disclose, among other things, any discipline or contempt and sanctions in the last 5 years, every Iowa pro hac vice application in the last 2 years, and the sponsoring Iowa lawyer’s name and personal identification number.\nThe application ordinarily should ","#st-ia-oos"],["E-filing: the Iowa lawyer files the application; access is case-limited","Iowa · out of state","Under the electronic rules, the Iowa lawyer appearing with the out-of-state lawyer must file the pro hac vice application. Only after it is granted does the out-of-state lawyer register for EDMS and enter an appearance. E-filing is then mandatory for pro hac vice counsel, at trial and on appeal. Pro hac vice lawyers can view remotely only the documents in the cases where they are admitted. Iowa R. Elec. P. 16.302(1),","#st-ia-oos"],["Appearing without admission is unauthorized practice","Iowa · out of state","An out-of-state lawyer who appears before an Iowa court, or before an agency where practice is limited to lawyers, without pro hac vice admission is engaged in the unauthorized practice of law. Work reasonably related to a proceeding in which the lawyer reasonably expects to be admitted is permitted. The lawyer submits to Iowa discipline from the moment the application is filed. Iowa Ct. R. 31.14(9); Iowa R. of Prof’","#st-ia-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances are allowed by notice","Iowa · out of state","A lawyer may appear for one or more specific proceedings by filing and serving a notice of limited appearance before or at the proceeding. The lawyer must tell the court of the limit at the start of any hearing, and exits without leave by filing a notice of completion of limited appearance. Service on a limited-appearance lawyer is valid only for those proceedings.\nPapers ghostwritten for a self-represented party mus","#st-ia-oos"],["Admission on motion: 5 of the last 7 years","Iowa · out of state","A lawyer licensed elsewhere may be admitted without examination after regularly practicing (at least 1,000 hours a year) for 5 of the 7 years before applying. Apply through the NCBE character and fitness process, paying a nonrefundable $900 fee to the Office of Professional Regulation plus the NCBE investigation fee. Lawyers who failed an Iowa bar exam within 5 years, or failed five or more bar exams, are ineligible.","#st-ia-oos"],["Governing code","Iowa · ethics","The Iowa Rules of Professional Conduct are Iowa Court Rules chapter 32, cited ‘Iowa R. of Prof’l Conduct 32:x.x.’ The collected chapter is headed December 2020; it was not amended in the 2025–2026 orders listed by the Iowa Judicial Branch. The rules track the ABA Model Rules, with the Iowa differences below. Trust accounts are governed by chapter 45, and chapter 33 sets out Standards for Professional Conduct. Iowa R.","#st-ia-ethics"],["Mandatory disclosure to prevent imminent death or serious harm","Iowa · ethics","In addition to the ABA-style permissive exceptions, Iowa requires a lawyer to reveal client information to the extent the lawyer reasonably believes necessary to prevent imminent death or substantial bodily harm. Iowa R. of Prof’l Conduct 32:1.6(c)","#st-ia-ethics"],["Sexual relations and related-lawyer conflicts","Iowa · ethics","The ban on sexual relations reaches a client or a client’s representative. Exceptions: a spouse, or a relationship that predates the representation. Even then, the lawyer should withdraw if the representation may be impaired. Iowa also keeps a ‘related lawyer’ rule. A lawyer may not oppose a person represented by a parent, child, sibling, spouse, cohabiting partner, or other familial or romantic relation without the ","#st-ia-ethics"],["Trust records kept 6 years; limited scope in writing","Iowa · ethics","Trust account records must be preserved for six years after the representation ends, and chapter 45 governs client trust accounts. A limited-scope agreement must generally be confirmed in writing, with exceptions for telephone-only advice, nonprofit or court-annexed programs, and limited court appointments. A signed writing creates a presumption that the representation is limited to what it describes. A person receiv","#st-ia-ethics"],["Trial publicity: presumption-of-innocence statement","Iowa · ethics","Any permitted public statement that a defendant will be or has been charged with a crime must also explain that a charge is merely an accusation and the defendant is presumed innocent until proven guilty. Iowa R. of Prof’l Conduct 32:3.6(e)","#st-ia-ethics"],["Reporting and discrimination rules","Iowa · ethics","A lawyer who knows another lawyer violated the Iowa rules must report it. The text has no ‘substantial question as to honesty, trustworthiness or fitness’ qualifier. Information protected by Rule 32:1.6, Iowa Code § 622.10, or an approved lawyers assistance program is exempt. It is misconduct to engage in sexual harassment or other unlawful discrimination in the practice of law, or to knowingly permit staff or agents","#st-ia-ethics"],["No default without a 10-day mailed notice of intent","Iowa · land mine","Before applying for a default, mail written notice of intent to the defaulting party and to any lawyer you know represents it, at least 10 days before filing the application. The days run from mailing. The application must certify the notice and attach a copy (Form 10). Exceptions: small claims, eviction (forcible entry and detainer), juvenile cases, and service by publication. Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.972(2)–(4)","#st-ia-unique"],["Do not plead a dollar amount; say ‘law’ or ‘equity’","Iowa · land mine","Except in small claims and liquidated-damages cases, a pleading may not state the specific amount of money damages sought. It states only whether the amount meets jurisdictional requirements. The petition must also state whether the action is at law or in equity, which affects trial and appellate review (equity cases are reviewed de novo). Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.403(1)–(2); Iowa R. App. P. 6.907","#st-ia-unique"],["Post-trial motions are due 15 days after the decision is filed","Iowa · land mine","Not 28 days after judgment as in federal court. Motions for new trial and JNOV, and a Rule 1.904(2) motion to amend findings, are due 15 days after the verdict, report, or decision is filed. The court may extend by at most 30 days, for good cause and not ex parte. Only a timely motion restarts the appeal clock, and a second such motion by the same party does not. Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.904(2), 1.1007; Iowa R. App. P. 6.10","#st-ia-unique"],["Appeal: 30 days, filed in the district court, then 7-day follow-ups","Iowa · land mine","The notice of appeal goes to the district court clerk, with an informational copy to the supreme court, within 30 days after the judgment is filed. The appellate courts cannot extend this. Within 7 more days you must pay the $150 fee and file and serve the combined certificate ordering the transcript. Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(1)(b), 6.102(3), 6.804(1), 6.1003(1)","#st-ia-unique"],["Further review: 20 days, and rehearing does not stop the clock","Iowa · land mine","An application for supreme court review of a Court of Appeals decision is due 20 days after the decision is filed, and the supreme court cannot extend it. A petition for rehearing in the Court of Appeals (due in 7 days) does not toll the 20 days, so file both if needed. Iowa R. App. P. 6.1003(1)(e), 6.1103(1)(a), 6.1204(1)–(2)","#st-ia-unique"],["Preserve error and say how in the brief","Iowa · land mine","Each argument must open with a statement of how the issue was preserved, with record cites to where it was raised and decided below. Citing the notice of appeal is not enough. Uncited issues may be deemed waived. Each missed appellate deadline brings a $150 penalty that the lawyer pays personally. Iowa R. App. P. 6.903(2)(a)(8), 6.1202(2)","#st-ia-unique"],["Serve within 90 days of filing","Iowa · land mine","If the original notice is not served within 90 days after the petition is filed, the court must dismiss without prejudice as to that defendant, or direct another time or manner of service, unless good cause is shown. Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.302(5)","#st-ia-unique"],["Old cases are swept every year","Iowa · land mine","A case whose petition was filed more than one year before July 15 must be tried before January 1 of the next year, or it is dismissed without prejudice at the plaintiff’s cost. The clerk sends notice by August 15. A continuance requires a court order; a stipulation alone does not work. Reinstatement must be sought within six months. Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.944(2), (5)–(6)","#st-ia-unique"],["$1,000 late-settlement fee","Iowa · land mine","Waiving a jury or settling later than two full working days before a scheduled jury trial, or settling during trial, brings a $1,000 fee assessed as court costs. The court may not waive it or grant a continuance to avoid it. Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.909","#st-ia-unique"],["Only 60 days to set aside a default judgment","Iowa · land mine","A motion to set aside a default or default judgment for mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect, or unavoidable casualty must be filed promptly after the grounds are discovered, and never more than 60 days after entry of the judgment. Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.977","#st-ia-unique"],["Discovery conference within 21 days of the first answer, even with a motion pending","Iowa · land mine","The parties must confer no later than 21 days after any defendant answers or appears. A pending pre-answer motion does not suspend the conference or initial disclosures. Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.507(1)","#st-ia-unique"],["Pro hac vice is case-by-case and filed by Iowa counsel","Iowa · land mine","Register and pay $250 with the Office of Professional Regulation first. Your Iowa co-counsel files the application electronically, and you can e-file only after it is granted. Admission does not carry over to the appeal: apply again in each later district or appellate proceeding. After five admissions in two years, you must show good cause. Iowa Ct. R. 31.14(2)(c), 31.14(7)(d), 31.14(11); Iowa R. Elec. P. 16.304(1)(b","#st-ia-unique"],["No peremptory change of judge in civil cases","Iowa · judges","The collected Iowa Rules of Civil Procedure and Rules of Appellate Procedure contain no procedure for removing a judge without cause. Seek disqualification for cause under the Code of Judicial Conduct. Iowa R. Civ. P. ch. 1; Iowa R. App. P. ch. 6 (searched)","#st-ia-unique"],["Disqualification for cause under the Code of Judicial Conduct","Iowa · judges","A judge must step aside whenever the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned. Listed grounds include personal bias or knowledge of disputed facts; a relative within the third degree as party, lawyer, interested person, or likely material witness; family economic interests; prior involvement as lawyer, government lawyer, witness, or judge in another court; and public statements committing the judge to a re","#st-ia-unique"],["Anti-SLAPP act read broadly","Iowa · speech","Chapter 652 must be broadly construed and applied to protect speech, press, assembly, petition, and association, and applied uniformly with other states that enact the uniform act. Iowa Code §§ 652.11, 652.12","#st-ia-unique"],["Cameras in court: allowed with authorization; object 3 days ahead","Iowa · speech","Expanded news media coverage is permitted with the judicial officer’s prior authorization, unless it would materially interfere with a fair trial. Requests go through the media coordinator, who gives at least 7 days’ notice. A party must file a written objection at least 3 days before the proceeding, and lawyers must tell their witnesses of their right to object.\nCoverage of jury selection is prohibited. Coverage is ","#st-ia-unique"],["Expedited civil actions up to $75,000","Iowa · unique rules","A plaintiff whose only claim is for money and whose claims against any one party total $75,000 or less may elect the expedited track. The election caps recovery at that amount and brings streamlined procedures: discovery completed 60 days before trial, trial within one year of filing, a 6-person jury (3 strikes per side; 5 of 6 may decide after 3 hours of deliberation), and no more than 6 hours per side for jury sele","#st-ia-unique"],["Motion practice","Iowa · unique rules","Unless a rule or order says otherwise, a resistance is due 10 days after service of a motion. A reply is due 7 days after the resistance, or before the hearing if earlier, and is limited to new authority or unanticipated matters. Legal briefs are served (with a copy delivered to the presiding judge) but not filed, except on summary judgment or by order; file a certificate of compliance. The court must rule within 30 ","#st-ia-unique"],["Redact protected information","Iowa · unique rules","Do not put Social Security numbers, financial account numbers, dates of birth, names of minor children, taxpayer or personal identification numbers, or other unique identifiers in public filings. Omit them, use only a portion (such as initials or the birth year), or put them on the separate protected information form. Sanctions apply. Iowa R. Elec. P. 16.602–16.606, 16.609","#st-ia-unique"],["Rule 1.101. Applicability; statutes affected","Iowa · civil rules","The chapter governs practice and procedure in all Iowa courts, except where the rules themselves provide otherwise or unaffected statutes prescribe a different procedure for particular courts or cases.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.201. Real party in interest","Iowa · civil rules","Every action must be prosecuted by the real party in interest, though executors, administrators, conservators, guardians, express trustees, contracting parties for another’s benefit, and statutorily authorized parties may sue in their own names. No dismissal for lack of the real party until a reasonable time after objection has been allowed for ratification, joinder, or substitution, which then relates back.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.202. Public bond","Iowa · civil rules","When a bond or other instrument given to the state, a county, school, or other municipal corporation, or an officer is meant to secure the public or particular individuals, anyone it was meant to protect who was injured by its breach may sue on it in that person’s own name, unless otherwise provided.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.203. Partnerships","Iowa · civil rules","Partnerships may sue and be sued as such, or, where law permits, any or all partners may be sued with or without the firm. A judgment against the partnership reaches partnership property and the property of partners served or appearing; a new action lies against partners not served or appearing, and the court may order absent partners brought in.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.204. Foreign corporations","Iowa · civil rules","Foreign corporations may sue and be sued in their corporate name unless a statute prohibits it.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.205. Assignees; exception","Iowa · civil rules","Outside the Uniform Commercial Code, an assignee of a claim takes it subject to any defense, counterclaim, or claim existing against the assignor in favor of the pleading party, whether or not matured, if matured when pleaded.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.206. Injury or death of a minor","Iowa · civil rules","A parent or parents may sue for the expenses and actual loss of services, companionship, and society resulting from injury to or death of a minor child.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.207. Actions by and against state","Iowa · civil rules","The state may sue the same way an individual can, and no security may be required of it.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.208. Married persons","Iowa · civil rules","A married person may sue or be sued without joining the spouse. If both are sued, each may defend, and if one fails to defend the other may defend for both.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.209. Desertion of family","Iowa · civil rules","When a spouse deserts the family, the other spouse may prosecute or defend any action either could have, with the same powers and rights.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.210. Minors; incompetents","Iowa · civil rules","An action for a minor or an adjudged incompetent is brought by the conservator, or if none the guardian; otherwise a minor sues by next friend and an incompetent by a court-appointed conservator or guardian. The court may dismiss the action or substitute the representative if that is in the person’s best interest.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.211. Defense by incompetent, prisoner, etc.","Iowa · civil rules","No judgment without a defense may be entered against a minor, a person confined in a penitentiary, reformatory, or state mental hospital, an adjudged incompetent, or a party whose physician certifies the party appears mentally incapable of defending. The defense is by guardian ad litem, though the ward’s conservator (or guardian) or the attorney for a competent party may defend unless the fiduciary brought the case o","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.212. Guardian ad litem","Iowa · civil rules","If a served party appears subject to rule 1.211, the court may appoint or substitute a guardian ad litem. The ward (if competent), a minor over 14, the conservator or guardian, or if none any friend or party may apply.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.221. Substitution at death; limitation","Iowa · civil rules","Substitution of legal representatives or successors of a deceased party, where a statute permits it, must be ordered within two years after the death. If the claim survives entirely to existing parties, the case continues without substitution. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 25(a)’s 90 days after a statement of death, the Iowa clock is a flat two years from death, and the order (not just the motion) must come within it.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.222. Transfer of interest","Iowa · civil rules","Transfer of an interest in a pending action does not abate it but may justify bringing in new parties.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.223. Incapacity pending action","Iowa · civil rules","If a party is adjudged incompetent, confined in a state mental hospital, or certified by a physician as apparently mentally incapable during the case, the conservator or guardian must be joined, or if none the court must appoint a guardian ad litem.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.224. Nonabatement in case of guardianship","Iowa · civil rules","A pending action does not abate when a conservatorship or guardianship ends; the successor fiduciary, former ward, or the ward’s personal representative is substituted or joined, and if no one applies the court may appoint a personal representative for a deceased party.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.225. Majority of minor","Iowa · civil rules","A minor party who reaches majority continues as a party in that person’s own right.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.226. Officers; representatives","Iowa · civil rules","When a public official or other representative party leaves that capacity during the suit, the court may order the successor brought in and substituted.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.227. Notice to substituted party","Iowa · civil rules","The substitution order fixes the time to appear and the notice to be given; substitution of a deceased party’s legal representative requires notice served like an original notice.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.231. Actions joined","Iowa · civil rules","A single plaintiff may join as many legal or equitable, independent or alternative causes of action as the plaintiff has against a single defendant.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.232. Multiple plaintiffs","Iowa · civil rules","Any number of persons may join as plaintiffs when they claim relief arising from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and a common question of law or fact is involved, and they may join any related causes of action sharing a common question.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.233. Permissive joinder of defendants","Iowa · civil rules","Defendants may be joined when relief is asserted against them jointly, severally, or alternatively arising from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and a common question of law or fact is involved.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.234. Necessary parties; joinder","Iowa · civil rules","Persons with a joint interest must be joined on the same side (a reluctant co-plaintiff may be made a defendant), except in class actions and as Iowa Code sections 613.1 and 613.2 allow. A party is indispensable if its interest is not severable and its absence prevents any judgment, or if its interest would be inequitably affected; the court must order indispensable parties brought in and must add other needed partie","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.235. Parties partly interested","Iowa · civil rules","A party need not be interested in all the relief demanded, and judgment may be given for or against parties according to their respective rights and liabilities.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.236. Remedy for misjoinder","Iowa · civil rules","Misjoinder of parties is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be dropped, realigned, or severed at any stage on just terms (the comment notes it tracks Fed. R. Civ. P. 21). The only remedy for misjoinder of causes of action is a motion, on which the court dockets causes separately or strikes them, always keeping at least one in the original case, and the pleader may first withdraw the challenged causes.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.237. Dependent remedies joined","Iowa · civil rules","A claim formerly allowed only after another action concluded may be joined with that action, but an action against an indemnitor or insurer may not be joined with one against the insured or indemnified party unless a statute allows it.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.241. Compulsory counterclaims","Iowa · civil rules","A pleading must contain a counterclaim for every matured claim against an opposing party arising from the same transaction or occurrence and not the subject of a pending action, unless adjudication would require indispensable parties beyond the court’s jurisdiction. A final judgment on the merits bars an unpleaded compulsory counterclaim.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.242. Permissive counterclaims","Iowa · civil rules","Unless a rule or statute prohibits it, a party may counterclaim on any claim held when the action was commenced and matured when pleaded. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 13(b), the claim must have been held at commencement.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.243. Joinder of counterclaims","Iowa · civil rules","A counterclaimant may join multiple claims to the same extent a plaintiff may under rules 1.231 and 1.232.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.244. Counterclaim not limited","Iowa · civil rules","A counterclaim may diminish or defeat the opposing claim and may seek relief greater than or different from that sought by the opponent.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.245. Cross-claim against coparty","Iowa · civil rules","A pleading may assert a cross-claim against a coparty arising from the transaction or occurrence of the original action or a counterclaim, or relating to property at issue, including a claim that the coparty is liable to the cross-claimant for all or part of the claim against it.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.246. Third-party practice","Iowa · civil rules","A defending party may file a cross-petition and serve an original notice on a nonparty who is or may be liable for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim, without leave if filed within 10 days after filing the original answer and otherwise by motion on notice. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 14(a)(1), the no-leave window runs from the filing of the answer and the third-party pleading is called a cross-petition; the rest (defens","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.251. Right of interpleader","Iowa · civil rules","A person exposed to multiple liability or vexatious litigation from several claims to the same thing may bring an equitable interpleader action against all claimants, whose claims need not share a common origin or be identical, and may dispute liability in whole or in part.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.252. By defendants","Iowa · civil rules","A defendant exposed to similar liability may obtain interpleader by counterclaim or cross-petition; claimants not before the court may be brought in, and on their default after service the court may bar their claims.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.253. Deposit; discharge","Iowa · civil rules","If the party initiating interpleader admits liability for, or nonownership of, the property or amount, the court may order it deposited or secured by bond and, after hearing, discharge the depositor before deciding the competing claims.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.254. Substitution of claimant","Iowa · civil rules","When a defendant seeks interpleader involving a third person, that person may appear and be substituted, and the original defendant may be discharged on complying with rule 1.253.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.255. Injunction","Iowa · civil rules","After the petition and returns of original notices are filed in interpleader, the court may enjoin all parties before it from starting or prosecuting other suits over the subject until further order.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.256. Costs","Iowa · civil rules","Costs may be taxed against the unsuccessful claimant in favor of the successful claimant and the party who initiated the interpleader.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.257. Sheriff or officer; creditor","Iowa · civil rules","When an officer is sued over personal property taken under a writ, the writ may be filed with the officer’s affidavit, and the plaintiff must then join the attaching or execution creditor (or the creditor may join on application); any judgment against both must be satisfied first from the creditor’s property.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.261. Commencement of a class action","Iowa · civil rules","One or more class members may sue or be sued as representatives if the class is so numerous or so constituted that joinder is impracticable and there is a common question of law or fact. Iowa follows the Uniform Class Actions Rules model rather than Fed. R. Civ. P. 23.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.262. Certification of class action","Iowa · civil rules","Unless the court defers it, the court must hold a hearing as soon as practicable after commencement and certify or refuse to certify the class. Certification requires that rule 1.261 is met, a class action is permitted for fair and efficient adjudication, and the representatives will fairly and adequately protect the class; the court may certify by claim, issue, or form of relief and create subclasses.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.263. Criteria considered","Iowa · civil rules","In deciding whether a class action is fair and efficient the court weighs 13 listed factors (a through m), including common interest, risk of inconsistent adjudications, predominance, superiority, management and conflict-of-laws difficulties, and whether individual claims are too small to pursue alone. Adequacy requires findings that class counsel is adequate, the representatives have no conflict, and they have or ca","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.264. Order on certification","Iowa · civil rules","The certification order must describe the class, the relief sought, any claim or issue limitation, and any subclasses, and both a grant and a refusal must state reasons and findings on the rule 1.263(1) factors. An order certifying or refusing to certify is appealable as of right (unlike the discretionary Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(f) petition), and refusal does not end the action but bars class treatment.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.265. Amendment of certification order","Iowa · civil rules","The court may amend the certification order any time before judgment on the merits (add subclasses, eliminate members, limit claims or issues, change relief), with notice as directed if notice has already gone out, and must state reasons. An amending order is appealable; an order denying a nonrepresentative defendant-class member’s motion to amend is appealable only if the court certifies it for immediate appeal.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.266. Notice of action","Iowa · civil rules","After certification the court must, after a hearing, order notice to the class containing eight listed items, including the right to opt out by a set date and that the judgment binds all who do not. Any member whose potential recovery or liability is estimated to exceed $100 must get personal or mailed notice if identifiable with reasonable diligence; others get notice reasonably calculated to inform them. The plaint","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.267. Exclusion","Iowa · civil rules","A plaintiff-class member may opt out unless the member is a representative, the certification order contains a finding under rule 1.263(1)(a), (b), or (c), or a rule 1.270 counterclaim is pending against the member or the member’s class or subclass; a timely election excludes the member from the judgment. Members of a defendant class may not opt out.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.268. Conduct of action","Iowa · civil rules","The court may issue orders controlling the course of proceedings, requiring notice to members of steps in the case, the proposed judgment, or chances to participate, imposing conditions on representatives or intervenors, inviting the attorney general to address adequacy, and ensuring adequate representation and competent class counsel. A nonrepresentative member may appear through separate counsel.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.269. Discovery by or against class members","Iowa · civil rules","Discovery against a class member who is not a representative party or has not appeared requires a court order, considering timing, subject, duplication, and burden. Representatives and appearing members are subject to ordinary party discovery.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.270. Counterclaims","Iowa · civil rules","A defendant may counterclaim against a plaintiff class on any claim the court certifies as a class action, and with leave against a member or subclass; any counterclaim against a plaintiff class must be asserted before class notice is given. The court may stay distribution to a member the losing party is diligently suing elsewhere, a defendant class may counterclaim, a counterclaiming member is treated as a plaintiff","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.271. Dismissal or compromise","Iowa · civil rules","Unless certification was refused, a class action may not be voluntarily dismissed, involuntarily dismissed without a merits ruling, or compromised without court approval after a hearing. Notice of the hearing must go to all members of a certified class (and may be ordered before certification) and must disclose payments, effects, agreements, alternatives considered, and other reasons; the court may approve, deny, or ","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.272. Effect of judgment on class","Iowa · civil rules","In a certified class action where notice was given under rule 1.266 or 1.271, the judgment on the certified claims or issues binds every member who did not file an exclusion election, and the judgment must name or describe the members bound.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.273. Costs","Iowa · civil rules","Only representatives and individually appearing members are liable for costs assessed against a plaintiff class; the court apportions costs assessed against a defendant class, and advanced notice expenses are taxable as costs for the prevailing party.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.274. Relief afforded","Iowa · civil rules","The court may award any relief consistent with the certification order, in a lump sum or installments, but statutory minimum damages may not be recovered in a class action. For money judgments the rule sets a distribution procedure, with identification costs paid from the fund and undistributed amounts going, after hearing and weighing six listed factors, to states as unclaimed property, to the defendant (with possib","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.275. Attorney’s fees","Iowa · civil rules","Class counsel fees are subject to court control. Only representatives and individually appearing members are liable for fees owed by a plaintiff class; the court may award fees from a divisible recovery, and for declaratory or equitable relief may shift fees to the adversary if permitted by law in similar cases or if an important public interest was vindicated, weighing six listed factors including the Iowa Rules of ","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.276. Arrangements for attorney’s fees and expenses","Iowa · civil rules","Before the certification hearing, or when directed, the representatives and their counsel must file a statement of any amounts paid or promised for services or expenses and their sources, and copies or summaries of fee agreements and fee-sharing agreements with outsiders, supplemented promptly. If costs cannot otherwise be met, the court may authorize and control contributions from class members and advances by attor","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.277. Statute of limitations","Iowa · civil rules","Filing a class action tolls limitations for all class members. The statute resumes running against a member on that member’s exclusion election, an order removing the member from the class, an order refusing certification (except for representatives), or dismissal without a merits adjudication.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.278. Virtual representation","Iowa · civil rules","When all living members of a class that may grow by later births are parties to an action affecting specific property, those later born are deemed parties and bound by the decree.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.279. Shareholder’s actions","Iowa · civil rules","Shareholders of an incorporated or unincorporated association suing to enforce its rights must support the petition by affidavit and allege their efforts to get the directors, trustees, or other shareholders to act, or a sufficient reason for not making the effort.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.281. Expedited civil actions","Iowa · civil rules","Plaintiffs whose only relief is money and whose claims by or against any one party total $75,000 or less (counting penalties, prefiling interest, and attorney fees, but not post-filing prejudgment interest, postjudgment interest, or costs) may elect expedited treatment by filing a supreme-court-approved certification signed by all plaintiffs and counsel before the rule 1.507(1) discovery-conference deadline; it does ","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.301. Commencement of actions; tolling; cover sheet","Iowa · civil rules","A civil action is commenced for all purposes, including statutes of limitations, by filing a petition. Every civil petition except small claims, probate, and mental-health commitment must be accompanied by a civil cover sheet, which has no legal effect.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.302. Original notice; form, issuance and service","Iowa · civil rules","The summons is called the “original notice”; it must state the court, parties, plaintiff’s counsel contact information, the petition filing date, the response time, the warning that default judgment may be rendered, and the ADA compliance notice, must be signed by the clerk under seal, and is served with a copy of the petition (except publication, where the notice alone, with a general statement of the claim, is publ","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.303. Time for motion or answer to petition","Iowa · civil rules","A defendant must serve a motion or answer within 20 days after service of the original notice and petition, and file it within a reasonable time afterward, unless a specific Iowa statute sets another time. A party served under a court order responds by the date fixed; a party served by publication responds by the date in the published notice, at least 20 days after the last publication; and a party served by mail und","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.304. Response of garnishee","Iowa · civil rules","The officer serving a writ of attachment or execution garnishes the persons the plaintiff directs by a notice served like an original notice, forbidding payment to the defendant and requiring the garnishee to hold the defendant’s property. Unless statute requires answers to be taken otherwise, the notice cites the garnishee to appear at least 10 days after service and answer interrogatories, on pain of liability for ","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.305. Personal service","Iowa · civil rules","Lists 14 methods of personal service: on an adult by signed, dated acknowledgment, personal delivery, or delivery at the dwelling to a resident at least 18 years old (with rules for rooming houses, hotels, and apartment buildings, and service on a spouse elsewhere if probable cause shows the spouse lives there); on minors and incompetents through conservators, guardians, parents, spouses, or custodians aged 18 or mor","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.306. Alternate method of service","Iowa · civil rules","Iowa’s long-arm rule: any corporation, individual, personal representative, partnership, or association with the necessary minimum contacts is subject to Iowa jurisdiction to the full extent of the U.S. Constitution. Service may be made under rule 1.305 in or out of state or, if that cannot be done, in any due-process-compliant manner the court orders, without limiting service methods permitted by statute.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.307. Member of general assembly","Iowa · civil rules","No legislator may be required to move or answer in any civil action in an Iowa court while the general assembly is in session.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.308. Returns of service","Iowa · civil rules","Sheriffs and deputies (in their own or a contiguous county) and other peace officers, bailiffs, or marshals (in their own territory) may make unsworn returns; all other returns require the server’s affidavit, and private-server fees up to the sheriff’s statutory rate are taxable as costs. The return must state time, manner, and place and the person served (with facts showing compliance for substitute service); proof ","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.309. Amendment of process or proof of service","Iowa · civil rules","The court may allow process or proof of service to be amended at any time on just terms unless it clearly appears that material prejudice to the served party’s substantial rights would result.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.310. Service by publication; what cases","Iowa · civil rules","After an affidavit that personal service cannot be had in Iowa, the original notice may be published only in the 12 listed kinds of cases, including actions to recover or partition property, foreclose liens, compel specific performance of a real estate contract, contest or construe a will against a nonresident or unknown party, reach a nonresident’s Iowa property by provisional remedy, affect property in Iowa, reach ","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.311. Known defendants","Iowa · civil rules","When a known party is served by publication, a copy of the notice must also be sent by ordinary mail to the last-known address at least 20 days before the response date, unless an affidavit states that no address is known after diligent inquiry. The mailing affidavit (or no-address affidavit) must be filed before judgment, and the court must find that the address used was the last known or that none was known.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.312. Unknown defendants, respondents, or other parties","Iowa · civil rules","An original notice to unknown parties is directed to the unknown claimants of the described property and otherwise complies with rule 1.302.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.313. How published","Iowa · civil rules","After the petition is filed, the original notice is published once a week for three consecutive weeks in a newspaper of general circulation published in the county of filing, chosen by the plaintiff.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.314. Proof of publication","Iowa · civil rules","Before default is taken, proof of publication sworn by the publisher or a newspaper employee must be filed.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.315. Actual service","Iowa · civil rules","Personal service under rule 1.305, in or out of Iowa, makes publication unnecessary.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.401. Allowable pleadings","Iowa · civil rules","The pleadings are a petition and answer, a reply to a counterclaim so designated, an answer to a cross-claim, a cross-petition when a new party is brought in under rule 1.246, and an answer to a cross-petition. Iowa calls the complaint a “petition” and a third-party complaint a “cross-petition.”","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.402. General rules of pleading","Iowa · civil rules","Pleadings are judged by the rules and construed to secure a just, speedy, and inexpensive decision on the merits; averments must be simple, concise, and direct, alternative and inconsistent claims or defenses are allowed, and motions are not pleadings. The court may order prolix or confused pleadings recast. A party may amend once as of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 20 days after service if","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.403. Claims for relief","Iowa · civil rules","A claim needs a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for the type of relief sought. Except in small claims and purely liquidated-damages cases, the pleading must not state a specific dollar amount, only whether jurisdictional amount requirements are met; amounts are obtained in discovery. The petition must state whether it is at law or in equity.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.404. Appearances","Iowa · civil rules","An appearing attorney must identify the attorney or attorneys in charge, not merely the firm, and is then entitled to service under rule 1.442. An appearance alone does not prevent default or other orders, but any party who has appeared must get notice and an opportunity to respond to a motion for default judgment under rule 1.973(2). Limited-scope appearances under Iowa R. Prof’l Conduct 32:1.2(c) must be noticed in","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.405. Answer","Iowa · civil rules","The answer must state on whose behalf it is filed, specifically admit or deny each allegation or paragraph (lack of information is a permitted denial), plead additional defensive facts, and put any counterclaim in a separate division; answers for wards must address service and deny all prejudicial material allegations. Facts not denied are admitted, except allegations of value or amount of damage, averments needing n","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.406. Reply","Iowa · civil rules","The court may order a reply to an answer or to an answer to a cross-petition.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.407. Interventions","Iowa · civil rules","On timely application, intervention is of right when a statute gives an unconditional right or when the applicant has an interest the action may impair that existing parties do not adequately represent, and is permissive for a conditional statutory right, a common question of law or fact, or a government officer or agency administering a statute or regulation relied on, considering undue delay or prejudice. The movan","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.411. Caption and signature","Iowa · civil rules","Every appearance, notice, motion, or pleading must carry a caption naming the court, parties, and instrument and the filer’s signature, personal identification number, address, phone, and, if available, fax and e-mail; first papers must include each named party’s identification number if available, and the petition caption must state law or equity. Social security numbers go on a separately filed confidential informa","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.412. Paragraphs; separate statements","Iowa · civil rules","Averments must be in numbered paragraphs each limited, as far as practicable, to a single set of circumstances, and each claim on a separate transaction and each defense other than denials must be in a separate count or defense when that aids clarity.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.413. Verification abolished; affidavits; certification","Iowa · civil rules","Pleadings need not be verified unless a statute requires it. Counsel’s signature certifies reading, reasonable inquiry, factual and legal grounding, and no improper purpose; unsigned papers are stricken unless promptly signed, and a violation requires (“shall”) an appropriate sanction against the signer, the represented party, or both, which may include expenses and fees. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 11, there is no 21-day","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.414. Supplemental pleadings","Iowa · civil rules","With leave on reasonable notice and just terms, or the adversary’s written consent, a party may serve and file a supplemental pleading about later events, even if the original pleading is defective; no response is required unless ordered.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.415. Judicial notice; statutes","Iowa · civil rules","Judicially noticed matters, including Iowa statutes, need not be pleaded. A pleading relying on another jurisdiction’s statute must identify it plainly, and the court must then take judicial notice of it.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.416. Negligence; mitigation","Iowa · civil rules","In negligence actions by an employee against an employer or a passenger against a common carrier, the plaintiff need not plead or prove freedom from contributory negligence, but the defendant may plead and prove it in mitigation of damages.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.417. Permissible conclusions; denials","Iowa · civil rules","Capacity, corporate authority to sue or do business in Iowa, performance of conditions precedent, and judgments of special-jurisdiction tribunals may be pleaded as conclusions. A denial of such an averment must state the facts relied on, not merely contradict it.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.418. Contract","Iowa · civil rules","Every pleading referring to a contract must state whether it is written or oral.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.419. Defenses to be specially pleaded","Iowa · civil rules","Defenses that a contract or writing is void or voidable or was delivered in escrow, and any matter of justification, excuse, release, discharge, or confession and avoidance, must be specially pleaded.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.420. Account; bill of particulars; denial","Iowa · civil rules","A pleading on an account must include a bill of particulars by consecutively numbered items, which defines and limits the proof. A responding pleading must specify the items denied, and unspecified items are deemed admitted.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.421. Defenses; how raised; consolidation; waiver","Iowa · civil rules","Every defense must be asserted in the responsive pleading or in an amendment to the answer made within 20 days after the answer is served, or at trial if no response is required. Six matters may be raised by pre-answer motion: subject-matter jurisdiction, personal jurisdiction, insufficient original notice or service, to recast or strike, for a more specific statement, and failure to state a claim; improper venue und","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.422. Protected information","Iowa · civil rules","Counsel and parties, not the clerk, must omit or redact social security numbers, financial account numbers, personal identification numbers, and other unique identifiers from filings unless material or required by law (using only the last four digits of a social security number and incomplete account numbers when needed), including in initially confidential records that later become public. Filers may also redact 11 ","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.423. Limited representation pleadings and papers","Iowa · civil rules","A pro se party’s paper drafted with a limited-scope attorney’s help must say so before the signature line and list the attorney’s (or the nonprofit program’s) name and contact information, but the attorney does not sign it. The drafting attorney must ensure the paper is well grounded and not improper, may rely on the client’s facts unless there is reason to doubt them, and is not thereby appearing or entitled to serv","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.431. Motion practice; generally","Iowa · civil rules","A motion is not a pleading but is subject to rule 1.413(1) certification and must be captioned, signed, and state its specific points, with an optional concise brief. Unless otherwise ordered or provided, a written resistance is due within 10 days after service of the motion, and a reply limited to new authority or unanticipated matters is due within seven days after the resistance or before the hearing, whichever is","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.432. Failure to move; effect of overruling motion","Iowa · civil rules","A pleading is not held sufficient merely because no one moved against it, and a party whose motion was overruled does not waive the error by pleading over and may keep challenging the pleading’s sufficiency.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.433. Motion for more specific statement","Iowa · civil rules","A party may move for a more specific statement only of matter too indefinite to permit a response, pointing out the defect and the particulars desired.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.434. Motion to strike","Iowa · civil rules","Improper or unnecessary matter in a pleading may be stricken on the adverse party’s motion.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.435. Motion days; submission of pretrial motions","Iowa · civil rules","Each district’s chief judge sets monthly motion days in each county, when all pretrial motions on file 14 days or more are deemed submitted unless a hearing is set or another time is fixed by rule, statute, or order for good cause; served or appearing parties are charged with notice of motion day. The court may rule earlier to avoid delay and may set other hearing times to expedite, not delay.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.441. Time to move or plead","Iowa · civil rules","Motions attacking a pleading must be served before responding, or within 20 days after service if no response is required. The answer is due under rule 1.303; answers to cross-claims and replies to counterclaims are due 20 days after service (or 20 days after an order requiring a reply). After a motion is decided in a way requiring further pleading, the pleading is due 10 days after notice of the ruling; responses to","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.442. Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","Iowa · civil rules","Every filed paper, later pleading, discovery paper, written motion, notice, appearance, demand, and offer of judgment must be served on each party, but not on defaulted parties except for new claims, which require original-notice service under rule 1.305. Service on a represented party is on the attorney (limited-appearance counsel only for the covered proceedings) by delivery, mail (complete on mailing), or fax, or ","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.443. Enlargement; additional time after service","Iowa · civil rules","For cause the court may enlarge a period, with or without motion, if asked before it expires, or after expiration on motion for excusable neglect, but it may not extend the times for exceptions, judgment notwithstanding the verdict, or new-trial motions (rules 1.1001, 1.1003, 1.1004) except as rule 1.1007 allows. Three days are added when a paper triggering a deadline is served by mail, e-mail, or fax, but not when t","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.444. Pleading over; election to stand","Iowa · civil rules","When a ruling requires or permits further pleading, the clerk mails or delivers notice to counsel, and unless the ruling says otherwise the party must file within 10 days after that mailing or delivery. A party who does not plead within that time is deemed to elect to stand on the record, and the ruling becomes a final adjudication in the trial court without further judgment as to the issues it disposes of; this can ","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.451. Specific rulings required","Iowa · civil rules","A motion or other matter with separate grounds or parts must be ruled on separately as to each, not sustained generally.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.452. Order defined","Iowa · civil rules","Every written direction of the court not included in the judgment or decree is an order.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.453. When and how entered","Iowa · civil rules","A judge may enter judgments, orders, or decrees any time after submission, effective when filed with the clerk (or as rule 1.442(5) allows), and the clerk must promptly send notice or a copy to each appearing party or one of its attorneys, including by e-mail to the address provided.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.455. Preliminary determination","Iowa · civil rules","On any party’s application, a motion for judgment on the pleadings under rule 1.954 and the defenses of lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction, improper venue, insufficient process, insufficient service, failure to state a claim, and failure to join a party under rule 1.234 must be decided before trial unless the court defers them to trial.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.456. Cross-petition, cross-claim, counterclaim; judgment","Iowa · civil rules","Judgment on the original claim is not delayed by a pending cross-petition, cross-claim, or counterclaim if it can be entered without prejudice to the rights at issue in them.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.457. Amending to conform to the evidence","Iowa · civil rules","Issues tried by express or implied consent are treated as if pleaded, and amendments to conform may be made at any time, even after judgment, though failing to amend does not affect the result. When evidence is objected to as outside the pleadings, the court must freely allow amendment unless the objector shows prejudice, and may grant a continuance.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.458. Special action; proper remedy awarded","Iowa · civil rules","In mandamus, certiorari, appeals to the district court, or actions for specific equitable relief, if the facts pleaded and proved show entitlement to a different remedy, the court must let the petitioner amend to request it on terms, and may award it.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.500. Duty to disclose; required disclosures","Iowa · civil rules","Without a request, parties must disclose witnesses with contact information and subjects, copies of documents and ESI they may use (or a good-cause statement and description), a computation of each damages category (not exact noneconomic amounts), and insurance declarations pages (plus the policy and coverage letters if coverage is contested); personal-injury claimants must also give date of birth, Medicare number, a","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.501. Discovery methods","Iowa · civil rules","Beyond rule 1.500 disclosures and subject to rule 1.505 timing, discovery is by depositions on oral or written questions, interrogatories, production or entry, physical and mental examinations, and requests for admission, liberally construed to give access to all relevant facts. Discovery must be in good faith with responses meeting the substance of requests, and every discovery motion must certify, with date and tim","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.502. Discovery materials not filed","Iowa · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, depositions, notices, interrogatories, production requests, requests for admission, responses, produced materials, and objections are not filed. A rule 1.517 motion attacking the sufficiency of a response must attach the request and response or may be denied; perpetuation depositions under rules 1.721 to 1.728 are excepted.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.503. Scope of discovery","Iowa · civil rules","Discovery extends to any nonprivileged matter relevant to the subject matter of the action, including information reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence, with “documents” including ESI, but always subject to the proportionality limits of rule 1.503(8). Unlike current Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(1), Iowa’s text retains the “subject matter” and “reasonably calculated” formulation, while adding the federal propo","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.504. Protective orders","Iowa · civil rules","For good cause, on motion of a party or affected person, the court where the action is pending (or, for depositions, where the deposition is taken) may issue any order justice requires to prevent annoyance, embarrassment, oppression, or undue burden or expense, including the eight listed forms, and must limit discovery under rule 1.503(8). ESI from sources not reasonably accessible need not be produced unless the req","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.505. Timing and sequence of discovery","Iowa · civil rules","No party may seek discovery from any source before the rule 1.507 conference, except in proceedings exempt from initial disclosures or as authorized by rule, stipulation, or order; in domestic relations cases discovery waits for the rule 1.500(1)(d) disclosures. The moratorium applies to actions commenced on or after January 1, 2015, and discovery methods may otherwise be used in any sequence without one party’s disc","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.506. Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","Iowa · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, the parties may by written stipulation provide for depositions before any qualified person at any time, place, notice, or manner and may modify other discovery procedures.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.507. Discovery conference of the parties","Iowa · civil rules","Except in exempt proceedings, domestic relations cases, or as ordered, the parties must confer as soon as practicable and no later than 21 days after any defendant has answered or appeared (or 21 days after a small-claims transfer is docketed); the plaintiff must notify all parties of the deadline, and a pre-answer motion does not suspend the conference or initial disclosures. The parties must discuss claims, settlem","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.508. Discovery of experts","Iowa · civil rules","A party may depose any expert identified to testify, but only after any required rule 1.500(2)(b) report is provided, and may obtain expert reports, models, and materials, including consultant material that forms a basis of a testifying expert’s opinions; draft reports and most attorney-expert communications are protected as in Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(4). A nontestifying consultant is discoverable only under rule 1.516","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.509. Interrogatories to parties","Iowa · civil rules","Except in small claims, any party may serve interrogatories, which must be provided in electronic word-processing format, and answers and objections are due within 30 days after service, with each interrogatory set out immediately before its answer (failure counts as a failure to answer), answers signed under oath, and specific objections signed by counsel; unstated grounds are waived. A party may not serve more than","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.510. Requests for admission","Iowa · civil rules","A party may serve requests to admit facts, opinions of fact, application of law to fact, and genuineness of documents, but no more than 30 per party without agreement or leave on a written motion. Matters are admitted unless a signed answer or objection is served within 30 days after service (a defendant need not respond before 60 days after service of the original notice unless the court shortens it); denials must f","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.511. Effect of admission","Iowa · civil rules","A rule 1.510 admission is conclusively established for the pending action unless the court on motion allows withdrawal or amendment, which it may do when that serves presentation of the merits and the requesting party fails to show prejudice, subject to rule 1.604. Admissions are only evidentiary admissions in other proceedings.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.512. Production of documents, electronically stored information, and things; entry upon land for inspection and other purposes","Iowa · civil rules","A party may request inspection, copying, testing, or sampling of designated documents, ESI, and tangible things in another party’s possession, custody, or control, or entry on land, described with reasonable particularity and with a reasonable time, place, and manner. A written response is due within 30 days after service, with specific objections stating whether anything is being withheld (unstated grounds are waive","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.514. Action for production or entry against persons not parties","Iowa · civil rules","Rule 1.512 does not preclude an independent action against a nonparty for production of documents and things or permission to enter land.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.515. Physical and mental examination of persons","Iowa · civil rules","When the mental or physical condition (including blood group) of a party or a person in a party’s custody or legal control is in controversy, the court may, only on motion for good cause and notice to the person and all parties, order an examination by a health care practitioner (not only a physician), specifying time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.516. Report of health care practitioner","Iowa · civil rules","On request of the examined person or the party ordered, the party causing the examination must deliver the examiner’s detailed report and earlier reports on the same condition, and must then receive like reports from the other side; an examiner who will not report may be excluded at trial. Requesting the report or deposing the examiner waives privilege as to every other examiner of the same condition in that or any a","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.517. Consequences of failure to make disclosures or discovery","Iowa · civil rules","A party may move to compel disclosures, deposition answers, entity designations, interrogatory answers, or production (evasive or incomplete answers count as failures), and an unresisted motion may be granted without hearing once the resistance time expires; every order granting such a motion must warn that noncompliance may bring sanctions, and the clerk serves it on counsel and the party. Expenses and fees shift to","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.601. Pretrial calendar","Iowa · civil rules","The court may provide for a pretrial calendar in any county, covering all actions or limited to jury or nonjury cases.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.602. Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","Iowa · civil rules","The court may order attorneys and unrepresented parties to pretrial conferences to expedite disposition, manage the case, and facilitate settlement. On application or its own motion (except in exempt categories) the court must enter a scheduling order setting deadlines for joinder, expert designation, discovery, amendment, and motions, may add ESI, privilege, complex-case, and conference provisions, and may modify th","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.603. Pretrial conference; record","Iowa · civil rules","On request of any interested counsel or the court, the reporter must record all or any designated part of a pretrial conference.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.604. Pretrial orders","Iowa · civil rules","After any rule 1.602 conference the court enters an order reciting the action taken, which controls the case unless modified. The order after the final pretrial conference must follow rule 1.1901 Form 6 and may be modified only to prevent manifest injustice.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.701. Depositions upon oral examination","Iowa · civil rules","Any party may depose any person without leave, compelling attendance by rule 1.715 subpoena, except that leave is needed (absent stipulation) to depose before the rule 1.505(1) moratorium ends, to depose a person already deposed in the case, or to depose a prisoner; a plaintiff may depose early without leave on a certified notice that the witness is about to leave the state and will be unavailable unless deposed befo","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.702. Depositions in small claims","Iowa · civil rules","In small claims, no deposition may be taken without leave of court obtained on notice and a showing of just cause, on just terms.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.703. Deposition notice to parties in default","Iowa · civil rules","A party who must prove up a judgment against a defaulted party may take depositions after serving notice on the defaulted party’s attorney of record, or on the defaulted party if unrepresented; defaulted parties are not otherwise entitled to deposition notice.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.704. Use of depositions","Iowa · civil rules","Admissible parts of a deposition may be used at trial, interlocutory hearings, or motion hearings against any party who appeared, stipulated, or had notice: to impeach; for any purpose if the deponent was an adverse party or an adverse entity’s officer, partner, or managing agent; for any purpose if the witness could not be subpoenaed, is out of state (not procured by the offeror), dead, or unable to testify; for any","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.705. Effect of taking or using depositions","Iowa · civil rules","If part of a deposition is offered, others may require the relevant remainder and offer other relevant parts. Taking a deposition, or using it only to impeach or as an adverse party’s, does not make the deponent the offeror’s witness, but using it for another purpose does, though the offeror may still contradict it.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.706. Substituted parties; successive actions","Iowa · civil rules","Substitution does not prevent using depositions already taken, and depositions from a dismissed action may be used in a later action on the same subject between the same parties or their representatives or successors.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.707. Notice for oral deposition","Iowa · civil rules","Reasonable written notice to every party must state the time, place, and the deponent’s name and address or an identifying description, and must include any subpoena duces tecum designation; a notice to a party deponent may include a rule 1.512 production request. No subpoena is needed for a party or an entity party’s officer, partner, or managing agent, and an organization may be noticed or subpoenaed with matters d","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.708. Conduct of oral deposition","Iowa · civil rules","Examination proceeds as at trial before an officer who swears the witness and records the testimony (transcribed on request); objections are noted and evidence taken subject to them, must be concise, nonargumentative, and nonsuggestive, and instructions not to answer are allowed only to preserve privilege, enforce a court limitation, or present a motion to terminate or limit. Parties may instead submit sealed written","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.709. Reading and signing depositions","Iowa · civil rules","A deposition reported by an official court reporter or Iowa certified shorthand reporter need not be read or signed by the deponent. In other cases the transcript is submitted to the witness for review, changes are entered with reasons, and the witness signs unless waived or unable; if not signed within 30 days after submission, the officer signs and notes why, and the deposition may be used unless suppressed under r","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.710. Depositions on written interrogatories","Iowa · civil rules","A party may take a deposition on written questions after serving all nondefaulted parties with the questions and a notice naming the officer and deponents. Cross-interrogatories are due within 10 days after the notice, redirect within five days after that, and recross within three days after that, subject to court adjustment for good cause; within the cross-interrogatory period any party may instead elect to cross-ex","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.711. Answers to interrogatories","Iowa · civil rules","The party taking a written deposition promptly sends the notice and all questions to the officer, who takes the answers and completes the deposition under rules 1.708 and 1.709, though not necessarily stenographically.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.712. Certification and return; copies","Iowa · civil rules","The officer certifies that the witness was sworn and the record is true; documents produced are marked and annexed on request, though copies may be substituted after verification or originals returned after marking and inspection, and any party may move to have originals filed. The officer must furnish copies to any party or the deponent on payment of reasonable charges.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.713. Before whom taken","Iowa · civil rules","The officer may not be a party, financially interested, a party’s attorney or employee, or related within the fourth degree to a party, an attorney, or their employees, and may not enter reporting agreements that take control of the original transcript before certification, give one party special terms not offered to all, or give any party an exclusive advantage. Domestic depositions may be taken before anyone author","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.714. Letters rogatory","Iowa · civil rules","A commission or letters rogatory for a foreign deposition issue only when convenient or necessary, on application and notice and on just terms, specifying the officer by name or title, and may be addressed to the appropriate judicial authority of the country.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.715. Deposition subpoena","Iowa · civil rules","On a party’s application or proof of service of a deposition notice, the clerk where the action is pending issues subpoenas for the persons named, and subpoenas may also issue by statute or rule 1.1701. No Iowa resident may be subpoenaed to attend more than 50 miles from where the person resides, is employed, or transacts business in person.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.716. Costs of taking deposition","Iowa · civil rules","The deposing party pays the costs of a deposition and cannot use it in evidence until they are paid; the judgment taxes against the loser only the portion necessarily incurred for testimony offered and admitted at trial.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.717. Irregularities and objections","Iowa · civil rules","Objections to a deposition notice are waived unless promptly served in writing; objections to the officer’s qualifications unless made before the deposition begins or when discoverable; objections to the form of written interrogatories unless served within the time for the next round (three days for the last); and curable errors in conduct, oath, or form of question or answer unless seasonably made at the deposition.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.721. Common law preserved","Iowa · civil rules","Rules 1.722 through 1.728 do not limit the court’s common-law power to entertain actions to perpetuate testimony.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.722. Application before action","Iowa · civil rules","An application to perpetuate testimony before suit is filed in the court where the action might be brought, captioned in the applicant’s name, supported by affidavit, and must show five things: the applicant expects to be a party to an Iowa action that cannot yet be brought, its subject and the applicant’s interest, the facts to be shown and reasons for perpetuating, the expected adverse parties, and each deponent’s ","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.723. Notice of application","Iowa · civil rules","The applicant must serve each expected adverse party with notice of the hearing and a copy of the application, in the manner of an original notice other than publication, at least 20 days before the hearing; if diligent service fails, the court may order publication or other service, or on extraordinary circumstances a hearing on less than 20 days’ notice.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.724. Guardian ad litem","Iowa · civil rules","Before the hearing the court must appoint an attorney as guardian ad litem for anyone under legal disability or not personally served, to cross-examine at any ordered deposition; without that appointment the deposition is inadmissible against that person later.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.725. Order allowing application","Iowa · civil rules","If the application is not for discovery, its allowance may prevent future delay or failure of justice, and the applicant cannot bring the action, the court must order the testimony perpetuated, designating deponents, subject matter, time, place, officer, and whether oral or written.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.726. Taking and filing testimony","Iowa · civil rules","Perpetuation depositions are taken as ordered and otherwise under rules 1.708 to 1.713 and 1.717, and unless the court enlarges the time must be filed within 30 days after the date fixed for taking them or they cannot later be received in evidence.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.727. Limitations on use","Iowa · civil rules","In a later action involving an expected adverse party named and served under rule 1.723, or that party’s privies or successors, any party may use the perpetuation deposition or a certified copy if the deponent is dead, mentally ill, or unavailable.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.728. Perpetuating testimony pending appeal","Iowa · civil rules","During the time to appeal or while an appeal is pending, the trial court may on motion (stating deponents, expected testimony, and reasons) allow testimony to be perpetuated for possible further proceedings if needed to avoid a failure or delay of justice and not for discovery; the depositions are then treated as if taken while the case was pending.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.801. Grounds for change","Iowa · civil rules","The place of trial may be changed on motion if the county is a party and an adverse party has demanded a jury on a jury-triable issue; if the trial judge is directly interested or related within the fourth degree to a party; for prejudice of the judge or county inhabitants or an adverse party’s undue influence, shown by affidavits of the movant and three disinterested, unrelated persons, with counter-affidavits allow","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.802. Limitations","Iowa · civil rules","No change of venue is allowed in an appeal from small claims; for county prejudice in a bench trial (except prejudice of the judge); before the issues are made up (unless the objection is to the judge); after a continuance (except for new or unknown causes); or after one change, for causes then existing and reasonably ascertainable. No party may have more than two changes.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.803. Subsequent change","Iowa · civil rules","After a trial following a venue change ends in a hung jury or a new trial is granted, the court may allow another change under rule 1.801(1)–(4), subject to rule 1.802.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.804. Of whole case","Iowa · civil rules","A change may be granted on one coparty’s motion, and the whole case is then transferred unless separate trials are ordered under rule 1.914.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.805. Where tried","Iowa · civil rules","Except for fraud-in-contract transfers, the case goes to a convenient county in the same judicial district, or another district if the ground applies to all its counties; if the ground concerns only the judge, the court may instead obtain another judge, or the supreme court may designate one.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.806. Costs","Iowa · civil rules","Except for changes by agreement or for fraud in contract, the order designates the costs of the change, which the movant must pay before it is perfected; failure to pay within 10 days after the order waives the change of venue.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.807. Transferring cause","Iowa · civil rules","Once a change is ordered and costs paid, the clerk sends a transcript and original papers to the new court, keeping an authenticated copy, and the case is docketed there without fee.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.808. Action brought in wrong county","Iowa · civil rules","An action filed in the wrong county may proceed there unless a defendant moves before answering for transfer to the proper county; the court must then transfer at the plaintiff’s cost, which may include the defendant’s expenses and attorney fees for attending in the wrong county. If those costs are not paid within 20 days after the transfer order, the action must be dismissed. Unlike federal practice, wrong venue is ","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.901. Trials and issues","Iowa · civil rules","A trial is a judicial examination of issues of law or fact. An issue of fact arises when a material factual allegation is denied by pleading or by operation of law; all other issues are issues of law and must be tried first.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.902. Demand for jury trial","Iowa · civil rules","Jury trial is waived unless a written demand is made no later than 10 days after the last pleading directed to the issue; a demand may be in a pleading (noted in the caption) or filed separately and served, and once filed may not be withdrawn without consent of all nondefaulted parties. An unlimited demand covers all jury-triable issues; after a limited demand, another party may demand other issues within 10 days or ","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.903. Trial of issues; reporting","Iowa · civil rules","Issues are tried to the court unless a jury is demanded, and demanded issues go to a jury unless there is no right to one or all appearing parties waive it in writing or orally in open court. Unless waived, all trial proceedings must be reported, including the 10 listed categories (court comments, jury selection, openings, testimony, exhibits, motions, closings, verdict); the reporter files a memorandum on rule 1.190","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.904. Findings by court","Iowa · civil rules","In a nonjury trial the court must make written findings of fact and separate conclusions of law, no request is needed, and sufficiency of the evidence may be challenged on appeal without objection below. A motion to reconsider, enlarge, or amend findings and conclusions must be joined with or filed within the time for a new-trial motion (under rule 1.1007, 15 days after the verdict, report, or decision is filed), and","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.905. Exceptions unnecessary","Iowa · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary once a matter has been raised by objection, motion, or otherwise and the court has ruled.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.906. Civil trial-setting conference","Iowa · civil rules","Except in domestic relations, the clerk must send notice of a trial-setting conference (Iowa Ct. R. 23.5 Form 1) to nondefaulted parties no later than 21 days after any defendant answers or appears, setting the conference between 35 and 50 days after that answer or appearance. The parties must obtain a timely conference whether or not they receive notice, and lack of notice is no excuse from dismissal under rule 1.94","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.907. Trial assignments","Iowa · civil rules","The court (or a delegated court administrator) may assign a case for trial at the end of a scheduling, pretrial, or trial-setting conference, by agreement of all parties, or on its own motion after consulting counsel; dissolution and small claims trials may be set without consultation, subject to rescheduling for conflicts. At least twice each month the clerk presents to a judge each small claims appeal taken more th","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.908. Duty to notify court","Iowa · civil rules","Attorneys or self-represented parties must notify the court immediately when a case assigned for trial settles. An attorney actually engaged in trial elsewhere when the case is reached must tell the court, which may hold the case in abeyance, and must notify the court and stand ready as soon as the other engagement ends.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.909. Fee for late settlement of jury trial","Iowa · civil rules","If a party waives a jury or gives notice of settlement later than two full working days before a civil jury trial is scheduled or reached (whichever is later), or the case settles during trial, a $1,000 fee is assessed as court costs. The court may not waive it or grant a continuance to avoid it, and the money goes to the state general fund.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.910. Motions for continuance","Iowa · civil rules","A continuance motion must be filed without delay once the grounds are known and may be amended only to fix clerical errors. No case assigned for trial may be continued ex parte, and every continuance motion in a case set for trial must be signed by counsel and approved in writing by the represented client unless the court waives that approval.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.911. Causes for continuance","Iowa · civil rules","A continuance may be granted for any cause not due to the movant’s fault or negligence if substantial justice will be better served, and must be granted if all parties agree and the court approves. A motion based on absent evidence needs an affidavit giving the witness’s name and residence (or diligence to find them), diligent efforts and grounds to expect the testimony by a specified date, and the particular facts e","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.912. Objections; ruling; costs","Iowa · civil rules","The opponent may file specific written objections to a continuance motion at once or within a reasonable time allowed. Where defenses are distinct, the case may be continued as to fewer than all defendants, and every continuance is at the movant’s cost unless ordered otherwise.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.913. Consolidation","Iowa · civil rules","Unless a party shows prejudice, the court may consolidate actions involving common questions of law or fact or order a joint trial of any issues, with orders to avoid unnecessary cost or delay.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.914. Separate trials","Iowa · civil rules","For convenience or to avoid prejudice, the court may order separate trials of any claim, counterclaim, cross-claim, cross-petition, or issue, and may sever any claim against a party.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.915. Impaneling jury","Iowa · civil rules","Sixteen jurors are drawn (by ballot or computer), sworn, and examined by the parties and the court; the court decides all challenges for cause. A challenge to the panel must be in writing before any juror is sworn and can rest only on a material departure from statutory requirements, and individual challenges must come before the jury is sworn, on 12 listed grounds including felony conviction without restored rights,","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.916. Saturday a religious day","Iowa · civil rules","Before final submission, no juror whose faith requires observing Saturday as a religious day can be compelled to attend that day.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.917. Juror incapacity; minimum number of jurors","Iowa · civil rules","If a juror becomes unable to act or is disqualified before the jury retires, the rest continue; if more than two jurors drop out before verdict (leaving fewer than six), the court must declare a mistrial.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.918. Returning ballots to box","Iowa · civil rules","When the jury is sworn, ballots of absent or excused jurors return to the box at once, and ballots of sworn jurors are set aside and returned when that jury is discharged.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.919. Procedure after jury sworn","Iowa · civil rules","The party with the burden on the whole action may make an opening statement, followed by the other party; the burdened party then presents evidence, followed by the adverse party, and then only rebuttal unless the court allows otherwise. Only one counsel per side may examine a given witness unless the court permits.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.920. Further testimony for mistake","Iowa · civil rules","Before final submission, the court may allow further testimony to correct an evident oversight or mistake, on just terms.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.921. Adjournments","Iowa · civil rules","After trial begins, the court may adjourn it for such time and on such conditions as justice requires.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.922. View","Iowa · civil rules","The court may order an officer to take the jury as a body to view property or a place where a material fact occurred, and no one else may speak to them about the case during the view.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.923. Arguments","Iowa · civil rules","The parties may submit or argue the case; the party with the burden opens and closes, must disclose all relied-on points in opening, and if the close raises new material points the adversary may reply, closing argument. A party waiving opening is limited in closing to reply. The court may limit argument time to itself but not jury argument time.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.924. Instructions","Iowa · civil rules","The court must give written, consecutively numbered instructions on all material issues, read without comment (oral only if the parties agree). Parties may file written requests at the close of evidence or earlier if the court sets a time; the court gives a preliminary draft on controversial issues and then final instructions before jury argument, and all objections must be made in writing or dictated into the record","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.925. Additional instructions","Iowa · civil rules","During deliberations the court may give further written instructions in counsel’s presence or after notice; they are filed as part of the record, and objections are made in the motion for new trial.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.926. Materials available to jurors","Iowa · civil rules","Jurors may take notes on court-provided materials and must be told the notes are not evidence and will be destroyed after deliberations. Jurors take their notes and all exhibits into deliberations unless ordered otherwise, but depositions go only if all evidence is in writing and none was stricken.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.927. Separation and deliberation of jury","Iowa · civil rules","A sworn jury may separate only by court order with admonitions not to discuss the case or form opinions. After submission the jury is kept together by an officer until verdict or discharge, except as the court permits overnight, weekends, holidays, or emergencies, and the officer may communicate only to ask whether they have agreed.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.928. Discharge; retrial","Iowa · civil rules","The court may discharge a jury for accident or calamity, by consent, when a continuance follows an amendment, or when it is clear the jurors cannot agree, and the case is retried immediately or later as directed.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.929. Court open for verdict","Iowa · civil rules","While the jury is out, the court may adjourn other business but stays open for every purpose connected with the case until verdict or discharge.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.930. Food and lodging","Iowa · civil rules","The court may order food and lodging at state expense for a jury kept together.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.931. Rendering verdict and answering interrogatories","Iowa · civil rules","The parties may stipulate before return that a stated majority may decide; otherwise verdicts and answers must be unanimous, except that all jurors but one may decide after at least six hours of deliberation following submission. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 48(b), which requires unanimity absent stipulation, Iowa allows a 7-of-8 verdict after six hours. The finding is read in court, any party may require a poll, and the j","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.932. Form and entry of verdicts","Iowa · civil rules","Verdicts and answers must be in writing, signed by the jury’s foreperson if unanimous or by all concurring jurors if not, are sufficient if they express the jury’s intent, and are filed and entered after being put in form by the court if needed.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.933. Special verdicts","Iowa · civil rules","The court may require a special verdict on each fact issue by written questions or proposed findings, with instructions enabling a finding on each. A party who does not demand submission of an omitted fact issue before the jury retires waives jury trial on it, and the court may find it or it is deemed found consistent with the judgment; comparative-fault interrogatories under Iowa Code chapter 668 are treated as spec","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.934. Interrogatories","Iowa · civil rules","With a general verdict the court may, and on any party’s request must, submit written fact interrogatories, shown to adverse counsel before argument. If answers are consistent with each other but not the verdict, the court may enter judgment on the answers, order a new trial, or send the jury back; if the answers are inconsistent with each other and the verdict, it must send the jury back or order a new trial. Unlike","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.935. Reference to master","Iowa · civil rules","A master includes a referee, auditor, or examiner, and may be appointed only on a showing of exceptional conditions and only for issues not tried to a jury; the clerk gives the master the appointment order.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.936. Compensation","Iowa · civil rules","The court fixes the master’s pay and orders it paid by parties or from a fund as just, enforceable by execution on the master’s demand; the master may not hold reports as security.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.937. Powers","Iowa · civil rules","The order may limit the master’s powers, issues, and deadlines or confine the master to taking evidence; otherwise the master regulates proceedings, administers oaths, compels witnesses and evidence, rules on admissibility, makes a record of excluded evidence on request, and may appoint a reporter whose fees the requesting party advances.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.938. Speedy hearing","Iowa · civil rules","The master must notify the parties of a first meeting within 20 days after appointment or as ordered, may proceed ex parte or adjourn if a party fails to appear, must act diligently, and may be ordered by the court to expedite or report.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.939. Witnesses","Iowa · civil rules","Parties may subpoena witnesses before a master as for trial, and a witness who fails to appear or testify without good cause faces the same consequences as in court.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.940. Accounts","Iowa · civil rules","The master may prescribe the form for accounts, receive a testifying CPA’s statement, and, on objection or insufficiency, require a different form or proof by testimony or written interrogatories of the accounting parties.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.941. Filing report","Iowa · civil rules","The master files the original exhibits, any transcript or else a summary, and a report with findings and conclusions if ordered, and may first circulate a draft to counsel.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.942. Disposition","Iowa · civil rules","The clerk mails notice of the report’s filing to counsel, and any party may file written objections within 10 days after mailing unless the court enlarges the time. The court must accept the master’s fact findings unless clearly erroneous and may adopt, reject, modify, or recommit the report; if the parties stipulated that findings are final, only questions of law remain.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.943. Voluntary dismissal","Iowa · civil rules","A party may dismiss its own petition, counterclaim, cross-claim, cross-petition, or petition of intervention without court order at any time up until 10 days before trial is scheduled to begin; after that only with court consent on terms, and with the consent of a counterclaimant unless the counterclaim can stand independently. A dismissal is without prejudice unless stated otherwise, but a second dismissal of an act","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.944. Uniform rule for dismissal for want of prosecution","Iowa · civil rules","Iowa’s policy is that civil cases be tried within one year after filing. Every case whose petition was filed more than one year before July 15 of a year must be tried before January 1 of the next year; the clerk sends notice before August 15, and unless satisfactory reasons are shown by application, ruled on after notice and not ex parte, the case is dismissed without prejudice at the plaintiff’s cost. Exceptions (wi","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.945. Involuntary dismissal","Iowa · civil rules","A party may move to dismiss a claim or for another order when the claimant fails to comply with the rules or a court order. After a party rests, the adversary may move to dismiss for failure to show a right to relief without waiving the right to offer evidence.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.946. Effect of dismissal","Iowa · civil rules","Dismissals not governed by rule 1.943 and not for lack of jurisdiction or improper venue operate as adjudications on the merits unless they say otherwise.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.947. Costs of previously dismissed action","Iowa · civil rules","When a plaintiff sues again on a claim previously dismissed against the same defendant in any state or federal court, the court may stay the new suit until the costs of the prior action are paid.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.951. Judgment defined","Iowa · civil rules","Every final adjudication of any of the parties’ rights in an action is a judgment.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.952. Partial judgment","Iowa · civil rules","A party who succeeds only in part may have judgment expressly for the successful part and against it on the rest.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.953. As to some parties only","Iowa · civil rules","In a multiparty action the court may render judgment for or against some parties only, whenever the prevailing party would have been entitled to it had that party been alone or a several judgment is proper, leaving the action to continue as to the others.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.954. Judgment on the pleadings","Iowa · civil rules","After the pleadings, a party may move for judgment on the pleadings.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.955. On verdict","Iowa · civil rules","The clerk must immediately enter judgment on a filed verdict unless it is special or the court has reserved the case for further argument or consideration.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.956. Principal and surety; order of liability","Iowa · civil rules","A judgment against a principal and surety must recite the order of their liability, and “surety” includes anyone whose liability is secondary to another’s.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.957. On claim and counterclaim","Iowa · civil rules","A claim and counterclaim may not be set off against each other unless both parties agree or a statute requires it. If either side’s obligation is likely uncollectible, the court may order both to pay into court for distribution, deciding separate set-off issues independently and treating payments as if made between the parties.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.959. Entry","Iowa · civil rules","All judgments and orders must be entered on the court record and clearly specify the relief granted or order made.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.960. Taxation of costs","Iowa · civil rules","If the court fails to assess costs on disposition, the clerk notifies the judicial officer, and if no assessment is made within 10 days after that notice, the clerk enters judgment for costs against the party who initiated the action.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.961. Notes surrendered","Iowa · civil rules","Unless the court specially orders otherwise, the clerk may not enter a judgment on a note or other written evidence of debt until the original is filed with the clerk for cancellation.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.962. Affidavit of identity","Iowa · civil rules","The clerk may not enter a personal judgment until the creditor or its agent or attorney files an affidavit of the debtor’s full name, occupation, and residence (with street residence and business addresses if the debtor lives in an incorporated place of more than 5,000 people), though a judgment entered without it is not invalid.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.971. Default defined","Iowa · civil rules","A party is in default when it fails to serve, and within a reasonable time file, a motion or answer as required by rule 1.303 or 1.304; withdraws a pleading without leave to replead; fails to appear for trial; fails to comply with any court order; or does any act permitting default under a rule or statute.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.972. Procedure for entry of default","Iowa · civil rules","The clerk enters defaults for failure to plead or withdrawal of a pleading against parties not under legal disability or imprisoned, without court order; all other defaults are entered by the court. The written application must certify that written notice of intent to seek default (substantially as rule 1.1901 Form 10) was mailed by ordinary mail to the defaulting party’s last known address, and to any attorney known","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.973. Judgment on default","Iowa · civil rules","For a sum certain or computable sum, the clerk on request and affidavit that the amount is due enters judgment for that amount and costs. In all cases the court, on the prevailing party’s motion, orders the judgment to which it is entitled, after notice and opportunity to respond for any party who has appeared, and may (and on demand of a nondefaulted party must) take evidence, refer to a master, or submit to a jury ","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.974. Notice of default in certain cases","Iowa · civil rules","When a default judgment (other than in rem) is taken against a party served by substituted service on another person under rule 1.305(1), the clerk must immediately mail written notice to that party’s last known address or the service address and record the mailing, though failure to do so does not invalidate the judgment.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.975. On published service","Iowa · civil rules","No personal judgment may be entered against a person served only by publication, or publication and mailing under rule 1.311, unless that person has appeared.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.976. Relief in other cases","Iowa · civil rules","A default judgment may award any relief consistent with and within the issues of the petition, but if the defaulting party never appeared it cannot exceed what was demanded.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.977. Setting aside default","Iowa · civil rules","On motion, for good cause, on terms, and not ex parte, the court may set aside a default or default judgment for mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect, or unavoidable casualty. The motion must be filed promptly after the grounds are discovered and no more than 60 days after entry of the judgment, and filing does not affect the judgment’s finality or operation. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 55(c) and 60(b), ther","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.981. Summary judgment: on what claims","Iowa · civil rules","A claimant may move for summary judgment on all or part of a claim at any time after the appearance day or after the adverse party moves, and a defending party at any time, with or without affidavits. Unless the court orders otherwise, the motion must be filed at least 60 days before the trial date, a resistance (with a statement of disputed facts, a memorandum, and any affidavits) is due within 15 days after service","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.982. On motion in other cases","Iowa · civil rules","Judgment may be obtained by motion by sureties against principals or cosureties for amounts paid, by clients against attorneys, by execution plaintiffs against sheriffs or officers for money or property collected, and where statutes authorize. Contribution based on comparative fault may be obtained by motion only on fact findings already made in that action and only by or against persons who were parties when the fin","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.983. Summary judgment: procedure","Iowa · civil rules","A rule 1.982 motion in a pending action follows rule 1.981 procedure; otherwise it must be served with notice of the hearing at least 10 days before the hearing, and the court hears it without further pleadings and enters judgment.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1001. Bill of exceptions","Iowa · civil rules","A bill of exceptions is needed only to show material parts of the record not reflected in the court files, entries, or certified reporter’s notes. No more than five supporting affidavits may be filed with it, and up to five controverting affidavits within seven days after, extendable for good cause; the judge must sign a bill that fairly presents the facts, and if the judge refuses, counsel’s certification plus the w","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1002. New trial defined","Iowa · civil rules","A new trial is a reexamination in the same court of any fact issue or part of one after a verdict, master’s report, or court decision.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1003. Judgment notwithstanding verdict","Iowa · civil rules","On motion a party may get judgment despite an adverse verdict or a jury’s failure to return one if the adverse pleadings omit a material fact necessary to a complete claim or defense (specified in the motion), or if the movant moved for and was entitled to a directed verdict at the close of all the evidence, in which case the court may grant a new trial or enter judgment as if it had directed the verdict. Unlike Fed.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1004. New trial","Iowa · civil rules","On motion, an aggrieved party may have a verdict, decision, or report vacated in whole or part and a new trial granted for nine listed causes that materially affected substantial rights: irregularity or abuse of discretion preventing a fair trial, jury or prevailing-party misconduct, unguardable accident or surprise, damages influenced by passion or prejudice, error in the amount in contract or property actions, insu","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1005. Motion; affidavits","Iowa · civil rules","Motions for judgment notwithstanding the verdict and new trial must be in writing, and those based on misconduct, accident or surprise, or newly discovered evidence may be supported and opposed by affidavits and heard under rule 1.431(6).","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1006. Stay","Iowa · civil rules","When a timely motion for judgment notwithstanding the verdict or new trial, or a rule 1.1012 petition, is filed, the court may in its discretion stay further proceedings and enforcement on terms pending disposition.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1007. Time for motions and exceptions","Iowa · civil rules","Motions for judgment notwithstanding the verdict and for new trial, and bills of exception, must be filed within 15 days after the verdict, report, or decision is filed with the clerk (or the hung jury is discharged), unless the court for good cause and not ex parte grants additional time of no more than 30 days. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(b) and 59’s 28 days after entry of judgment, the Iowa clock is 15 days from fil","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1008. Conditional rulings on grant of motion","Iowa · civil rules","Filing one post-trial motion does not waive others. A party whose verdict is set aside by judgment notwithstanding the verdict may move for a new trial within 15 days after that judgment is entered, and a court granting judgment notwithstanding the verdict must also conditionally rule on any new-trial motion and state grounds, with the conditional ruling governing what happens if the judgment is reversed on appeal.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1009. Issues tried by consent; amendment","Iowa · civil rules","In ruling on post-trial motions, the court treats issues actually tried by express or implied consent as pleaded, and parties may amend to conform, though failure to amend does not affect the result.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1010. Conditional new trial","Iowa · civil rules","The court may let a party avoid a new trial by accepting terms, such as remittitur or additur. Consent to a reduced, modified, or increased judgment must be filed with the district court clerk no later than seven days before the appeal deadline under Iowa R. App. P. 6.101 if imposed by the district court, or within 30 days after procedendo is filed if imposed by an appellate court, or the new trial is automatically o","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1011. Retrial after published notice","Iowa · civil rules","Except in dissolution and annulment cases, a defendant who did not appear and was served only by publication (or publication and mailing) may apply for retrial within six months after entry of judgment and, on posting security for costs, is entitled to defend as if no judgment existed. After retrial the court may confirm, modify, or vacate the judgment and order restitution.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1012. Grounds for vacating or modifying judgment","Iowa · civil rules","On a timely petition under rule 1.1013, the court may correct, vacate, or modify a final judgment or order, or grant a new trial, for six grounds: clerk’s mistake, neglect, or omission; irregularity or fraud in obtaining it; erroneous proceedings against a minor or person of unsound mind not shown in the record; a party’s death before entry without substitution; unavoidable casualty or misfortune preventing prosecuti","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1013. Procedure for vacating or modifying judgment","Iowa · civil rules","The petition requires the filing fee under Iowa Code section 602.8105(1)(a) (or section 631.6(1)(a) in small claims), must be filed and served in the original action within one year after entry of the judgment or order, must state the grounds (and for a new trial, why they could not be found in time for rules 1.977 or 1.1004), must allege a meritorious claim or defense if the original pleadings did not, and must be s","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1014. Disposition of exhibits","Iowa · civil rules","One year after final determination, the clerk may destroy filed exhibits after written notice to counsel that they will be destroyed unless receipted for within 60 days; the clerk may destroy all trial exhibits without notice two years after final determination.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1015. Titles and liens protected","Iowa · civil rules","A good-faith purchaser’s title to property sold under the original judgment is not affected by proceedings under rules 1.1011 to 1.1013, and liens and security obtained under a judgment that is merely modified carry over to the modified judgment.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1016. Judgment discharged on motion","Iowa · civil rules","When matters discharging a judgment arise after its entry, the defendant or any interested person may move to have it discharged in whole or in part.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1017. Fraudulent assignment; motion","Iowa · civil rules","On motion, the court may inquire into the assignment of a judgment or its entry for a party’s use and cancel or strike it in whole or part if inequitable, fraudulent, or in bad faith.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1018. Execution; duty of officer","Iowa · civil rules","An officer receiving an execution must act diligently, levying on debtor property likely to bring as nearly the exact amount as practicable (with successive levies if needed), collecting or selling things in action (suing in the officer’s own name if necessary), and selling or garnishing enough to satisfy the writ, paying proceeds less costs to the clerk.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1019. Endorsement","Iowa · civil rules","The officer must endorse on the execution the day and hour received, each levy, sale, or other act with its date, and the date and amount of each payment, at the time of the act, though a late endorsement does not impair a levy or sale.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1020. Levy on personalty","Iowa · civil rules","A levy on personal property under attachment or general execution creates no lien until the officer either takes possession and appends a signed, dated, detailed description to the writ, or, on the creditor’s written request, views the property, appends a detailed inventory and a statement of case, parties, amount, location, possessor, and debtor’s address, and files a certified transcript with the secretary of state","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1101. Declaratory judgments permitted","Iowa · civil rules","Courts of record may declare rights, status, and other legal relations whether or not further relief could be claimed, in affirmative or negative form with the effect of a final decree; another available remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the specific instances in rules 1.1102 to 1.1104 do not limit this power.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1102. Construing contracts, etc.","Iowa · civil rules","Anyone interested in an oral or written contract or will, or whose rights are affected by a statute, ordinance, rule, regulation, contract, or franchise, may obtain a determination of its construction or validity and a declaration of rights under it.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1103. Before or after breach","Iowa · civil rules","A contract may be construed before or after a breach.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1104. Fiduciaries, beneficiaries and others","Iowa · civil rules","Persons interested as or through fiduciaries, creditors, devisees, legatees, heirs, next of kin, or trust beneficiaries in a trust or an estate of a decedent, insolvent, minor, or ward may obtain a declaration to ascertain a class of creditors or heirs, to direct fiduciaries to act or refrain, or to resolve administration questions including construction of wills and other writings.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1105. Discretionary","Iowa · civil rules","The court may refuse a declaratory judgment that would not end the uncertainty or controversy.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1106. Supplemental relief","Iowa · civil rules","Further relief based on a declaratory judgment may be sought by petition in the original case, and if sufficient the court orders adverse parties whose rights were adjudicated to show cause, on reasonable notice, why it should not be granted.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1107. Review","Iowa · civil rules","Declaratory orders, judgments, and decrees are reviewable like other judgments.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1108. Jury trial","Iowa · civil rules","The declaratory judgment rules neither abridge nor extend the right to jury trial.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1109. “Person”","Iowa · civil rules","For declaratory judgments, “person” includes any individual or entity capable of suing or being sued under Iowa law.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1301. Quo warranto: for what causes","Iowa · civil rules","A civil action in the nature of quo warranto, tried in equity, may be brought in the state’s name against anyone unlawfully holding a public office, franchise, or Iowa corporate office; a public officer whose act works a forfeiture of office; anyone acting as an Iowa corporation without authority; a corporation exceeding its powers or forfeiting its privileges; or a holder of a state patent, permit, certificate, or l","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1302. By whom brought","Iowa · civil rules","The county attorney may bring quo warranto and must when directed by the governor, general assembly, or supreme or district court, with the attorney general acting if the county attorney may be a defendant. If the county attorney refuses a citizen’s demand, the attorney general may sue, or the citizen may obtain leave and, after posting a court-fixed cost bond, prosecute the action.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1303. No joinder or counterclaim","Iowa · civil rules","No other claim may be joined in a quo warranto action, and no counterclaim is allowed.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1304. Petition","Iowa · civil rules","The quo warranto petition must state its grounds and name others claiming the office, franchise, or right, who may be made parties.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1305. Judgment","Iowa · civil rules","The judgment must determine all parties’ rights, including who is entitled to any disputed office, remove or forfeit unlawfully held offices, franchises, or privileges (including a corporation’s forfeited privileges), and prohibit further exercise of powers improperly used where forfeiture is not warranted.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1306. Costs","Iowa · civil rules","Judgment against a defendant or intervenor includes costs, assessed against the individuals acting for a pretended corporation; if the action fails the court may tax costs to a private relator, otherwise they are paid as in criminal cases.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1307. Corporation dissolved","Iowa · civil rules","If the judgment dissolves a corporation, the court must enter appropriate dissolution orders under the governing statutes.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1401. Certiorari petition","Iowa · civil rules","A party may bring certiorari when a statute authorizes it or when an inferior tribunal, board, or officer exercising judicial functions, or a judicial magistrate, exceeded its jurisdiction or otherwise acted illegally.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1402. Certiorari: procedure","Iowa · civil rules","The petition is captioned with the petitioner as plaintiff against the tribunal, board, or officer, proceeds by ordinary proceedings, and must be filed within 30 days after the challenged illegal act. The reviewing court may extend the time only if the delay resulted from the tribunal’s failure to notify the petitioner, on a motion filed within 90 days after the decision, supported only by portions of the record and ","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1403. Other remedies","Iowa · civil rules","Certiorari is not denied because another plain, speedy, or adequate remedy exists, but unless a statute provides otherwise relief is strictly limited to questions of jurisdiction and legality.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1404. The writ","Iowa · civil rules","A district judge may order a writ to an inferior tribunal, board, officer, or magistrate, issued by the clerk under seal, commanding certification of the pertinent records and proceedings and the facts at a specified time and place.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1405. Stay, bond","Iowa · civil rules","The court may stay the original proceedings even if no stay is requested, and may condition a requested stay on a bond with security for costs as it prescribes.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1406. Notice of issuing writ","Iowa · civil rules","The court may issue the writ without notice when the petition is filed or set a hearing on reasonable notice, but must set a noticed hearing first if the petition precedes a final order below or a stay is sought; the hearing is confined to the petition’s sufficiency, what is to be certified, and any bond.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1407. Service of writ","Iowa · civil rules","A sheriff or deputy serves the writ by delivering the original (unless service is accepted), on the magistrate or that court’s clerk, or a board’s secretary, clerk, or member, and returns a copy with the return of service to the issuing clerk.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1408. Return to writ, by whom","Iowa · civil rules","The return is made and signed by the magistrate whose decision is challenged if practicable (otherwise that court’s clerk), by the officer to whom the writ issued, or by a board’s presiding officer, clerk, or secretary.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1409. Defective return","Iowa · civil rules","If the return is defective, the court may order a further return on its own or a party’s motion and enforce the writ or order by attachment or contempt.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1410. Certiorari: hearing","Iowa · civil rules","After a full return, the court sets a hearing and may receive transcripts, recordings, and explanatory evidence, considered only on the legality of the proceedings or sufficiency of the evidence below unless a statute provides otherwise.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1411. Certiorari: judgment","Iowa · civil rules","Unless a statute provides otherwise, the judgment only annuls or sustains the writ in whole or part to the extent the proceedings were illegal or beyond jurisdiction, prescribes how the parties may proceed, and may not substitute a different decree or order.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1412. Certiorari: appeal","Iowa · civil rules","Appeals from district court certiorari rulings follow the ordinary civil appellate rules, but review is discretionary when the district court ruling was itself a discretionary review of another tribunal.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1501. Independent or auxiliary remedy","Iowa · civil rules","An injunction may be sought as an independent equitable action or as auxiliary relief in any action, with damages or other relief in the same case, and may be granted in the judgment or earlier by order as a temporary injunction.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1502. Temporary; when allowed","Iowa · civil rules","A temporary injunction may issue when the petition, supported by affidavit, shows entitlement to relief restraining an act that would greatly or irreparably injure the plaintiff; when during the case a party is doing or threatening an act violating the other’s rights in the subject that would make the judgment ineffectual; or when a statute specially authorizes it.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1503. Endorsing refusal","Iowa · civil rules","A court or supreme court justice refusing a temporary injunction must endorse the refusal on the petition.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1504. Statement re prior presentation","Iowa · civil rules","A petition for a temporary injunction must state, or counsel must certify on it, whether the same relief has previously been presented to and refused by any court or justice, and if so by whom and when.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1505. Place for filing","Iowa · civil rules","A temporary-injunction request must be filed in the county where the action is or will be pending.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1506. By whom granted","Iowa · civil rules","A temporary injunction may be granted by a judge of the district where the action is or will be pending, or by the supreme court or a justice of it.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1507. Injunctions: notice","Iowa · civil rules","The court may require notice and hearing before a temporary injunction, and an applicant seeking one without notice must have counsel certify in writing the efforts made to notify the adversary or its attorney or why notice should not be required. Notice and hearing are mandatory for temporary injunctions or stays of agency action under Iowa Code section 17A.19(5) and to halt a corporation’s ordinary business, a stat","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1508. Injunctions: bond","Iowa · civil rules","Before a temporary injunction issues, the order must require a bond in a stated penalty of 125 percent of the probable liability, with clerk-approved sureties, conditioned to pay damages caused by the injunction; in dissolution, separate maintenance, annulment, and domestic abuse cases the court may waive the bond or set any reasonable amount. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(c), the amount is fixed by formula at 125 percen","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1509. Hearing to dissolve temporary injunction","Iowa · civil rules","A party enjoined without notice may at any time move the court where the action is pending to dissolve, vacate, or modify the injunction, and a hearing must be held within 10 days after the motion is filed.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1510. Enjoining proceedings or judgment; venue; bond","Iowa · civil rules","An action to enjoin proceedings in a civil case or on a judgment or final order must be brought in the county and court where they are pending or were obtained (or, for a supreme court judgment, the court appealed from), and any bond must also be conditioned to pay or comply with the judgment or any judgment recovered on the enjoined claim.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1511. Violation as contempt","Iowa · civil rules","Violating any temporary or permanent injunction is contempt and punishable accordingly.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1601. Applicability of rules","Iowa · civil rules","The civil procedure rules apply to judicial review of agency action under Iowa Code chapter 17A except where inconsistent with that Act or this division.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1602. Time for motion or answer","Iowa · civil rules","In judicial review under Iowa Code section 17A.19(2), the respondent must serve a motion or answer on the petitioner and all others required to be served within 20 days after personal service or mailing of the petition, and file it within a reasonable time thereafter.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1603. Contested case proceedings; intervention; schedule","Iowa · civil rules","In judicial review of a contested case under Iowa Code section 17A.19, an intervenor may side with either party or claim adversely to both, and the court must set a schedule on any party’s request and may on its own.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1701. Subpoena","Iowa · civil rules","A subpoena must state the court, title, and docket number, command attendance, production of documents, ESI, or things, or inspection at a stated time and place, state the recording method for depositions, and set out the text of rules 1.1701(4) and (5); the clerk issues signed blank subpoenas on oral or written request, and Iowa-authorized attorneys may issue them. Any nonparty at least 18 years old may serve within","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1702. Uniform interstate depositions and discovery","Iowa · civil rules","A party to an action in another state may obtain an Iowa subpoena by submitting the foreign subpoena to the clerk in the county where discovery will occur, who issues a signed blank subpoena (opening a file for a $50 fee), or by having an Iowa-authorized attorney issue it, which is not an appearance. The subpoena must comply with rule 1.1701 using the Iowa county court and the foreign caption and docket number, list ","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1801. Computing time; holidays","Iowa · civil rules","Time under the rules is computed under Iowa Code section 4.1(34). The rule itself sets no counting method, so the statute governs exclusion of the first day and treatment of weekends and holidays.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1802. Death, retirement or disability of judge","Iowa · civil rules","If a presiding judge dies or becomes disabled during a proceeding or while post-trial motions are pending, another judge of the district may proceed if able, or order a continuance, mistrial, or new trial. If the judge had a bench case or motion under advisement, another district judge or one appointed by the chief justice may decide it after reviewing the transcript or reargument, or order a new trial or recall of w","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1803. Appeal to district court from administrative body","Iowa · civil rules","When a statute allows appeal to district court from an officer, body, or board but does not provide how issues are framed, the appellant must file a petition within 10 days after perfecting the appeal (or as the court sets), the appellee must move or answer within 20 days after that (or as the court sets), and ordinary district court procedure then applies.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1804. Effect of notice by posting","Iowa · civil rules","Notice by posting has no legal effect unless a statute expressly authorizes it.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1805. General provisions, comments and footnotes","Iowa · civil rules","Tense, gender, and number each include the others; rule headings do not affect meaning; and source notes, comments, and footnotes are for convenience only and are not part of the rules.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1806. Rules by trial courts","Iowa · civil rules","Each district court, by majority of its district judges, may adopt local practice and administration rules consistent with these rules, subject to the supreme court’s prior approval.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1807. Purpose of administrative rules","Iowa · civil rules","Court administration rules exist to administer justice in an orderly, efficient, and effective manner under the highest standards of justice and judicial service.","#st-ia-rules"],["Rule 1.1901. Forms","Iowa · civil rules","The forms in the appendix are sufficient under the rules. They are: Form 1 original notice for personal service; Form 2 original notice against a nonresident motor vehicle owner or operator under Iowa Code section 321.500; Form 3 original notice against a foreign corporation or nonresident under Iowa Code section 617.3; Form 4 original notice for publication; Form 5 directions for service; Form 6 final pretrial order","#st-ia-rules"],["Appeals in Iowa: one notice to the supreme court, 30 days, no extensions","Iowa · appeals","Every Iowa appeal is filed in the Iowa Supreme Court, even though most cases are decided by the Court of Appeals. You file the notice of appeal with the clerk of the district court within 30 days after the final order or judgment is filed, and file an informational copy with the supreme court. The supreme court then keeps the case or transfers it to the Court of Appeals under published criteria. A party unhappy with ","#st-ia-appeals"],["Notice of appeal: 30 days from filing of the final order or judgment","Iowa · appeals · Starting the appeal","File the notice with the clerk of the district court where the order was entered, and file an informational copy with the clerk of the supreme court, within 30 days after the final order or judgment is filed. In juvenile (Iowa Code chapter 232) child-in-need-of-assistance and termination cases the time is 15 days, and the client must sign the notice too.\nThe notice must name the parties appealing and the order appeal","#st-ia-appeals"],["Fee and combined certificate within 7 days","Iowa · appeals · Starting the appeal","Within 7 days after filing the notice of appeal, pay the $150 filing fee to the clerk of the supreme court (or move to waive or defer it). Within the same 7 days, file the combined certificate (Form 2) with both clerks and serve it on each court reporter. In it you certify that you ordered the transcript, say which parts you ordered and the issues you plan to raise, and state whether expedited deadlines apply. Iowa R","#st-ia-appeals"],["Deadlines to start a case cannot be extended","Iowa · appeals · Starting the appeal","The supreme court may not extend the deadline for a notice of appeal, an interlocutory application, an application for discretionary review, or a certiorari petition. The one exception is when the district court clerk failed to notify the party of the ruling. Then a motion must be filed within 60 days after the original deadline expired, supported by affidavits, and any extension runs no more than 30 days from the or","#st-ia-appeals"],["Safety valves: early notice, service, wrong form","Iowa · appeals · Starting the appeal","A notice filed before the order is filed is timely if the order is filed within 30 days after the notice. The time to file a notice of appeal is tolled when the notice is served, if it is filed within a reasonable time. A case started by the wrong form of review (appeal instead of certiorari, for example) is not dismissed; it proceeds as if the proper form had been used. That rule does not extend any filing time. Iow","#st-ia-appeals"],["What is final and when to appeal partial rulings","Iowa · appeals · Starting the appeal","Final orders and judgments are appealable as of right. An order granting or denying a new trial is final. An order setting aside a default is final only in dissolution or annulment cases. An order disposing of some but not all parties or issues may be appealed within the time for appealing the judgment that finally disposes of everything, even if the claims are severable. A post-judgment attorney-fee ruling is separa","#st-ia-appeals"],["Rule 1.904(2) and post-trial motions restart the 30 days","Iowa · appeals · Tolling and post-decision motions","If a motion under Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.904(2) (to reconsider, enlarge, or amend) or 1.1007 (new trial, judgment notwithstanding the verdict) is timely filed, the notice of appeal is due 30 days after the ruling on that motion. A motion counts as timely if it is filed by the deadline and asks the court to reconsider, enlarge, or amend; whether it was ‘proper’ does not matter. A second such motion by the same party does n","#st-ia-appeals"],["Post-trial motions are due in 15 days","Iowa · appeals · Tolling and post-decision motions","Motions for new trial and for judgment notwithstanding the verdict must be filed within 15 days after the verdict, report, or decision is filed with the clerk. The court may grant up to 30 more days, for good cause and not ex parte. A Rule 1.904(2) motion must be joined with, or filed within the time for, a new-trial motion. Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.904(2), 1.1007","#st-ia-appeals"],["Cross-appeal: the 30-day limit or 10 days after the notice, whichever is later","Iowa · appeals · Cross-appeals","File the notice of cross-appeal in the district court, with an informational copy to the supreme court, within the 30-day appeal period or within 10 days after the notice of appeal was filed, whichever is later. In chapter 232 juvenile cases the base period is 15 days. A cross-appeal also requires the $150 fee. Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(2), 6.703(1)(a)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Briefing with a cross-appeal","Iowa · appeals · Cross-appeals","The appellee may file a combined appellee/cross-appellant brief. The appellant/cross-appellee must then respond within 21 days, by reply brief or by a statement waiving further briefing. The appellee/cross-appellant may reply to that within 14 days. Iowa R. App. P. 6.901(1)(b)–(c), 6.903(5)–(6)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Transcript deadlines run from the combined certificate","Iowa · appeals · Record and transcripts","The court reporter must file the transcript within 40 days after service of the combined certificate in ordinary cases (20 days for guilty pleas and sentencing; 30 days in chapter 232 cases). The clerk of the supreme court sends a briefing-deadline notice once all ordered transcripts are filed. That notice starts the appellant’s brief clock. Iowa R. App. P. 6.803, 6.803(6)","#st-ia-appeals"],["No appendix: attach the rulings to the brief","Iowa · appeals · Record and transcripts","The appendix process was eliminated. The orders and judgments appealed from are attached to the appellant’s brief instead. In exceptional cases a party may ask leave to file an appendix. Iowa R. App. P. 6.903(2)(b); comment to reserved rule 6.905","#st-ia-appeals"],["Schedule: 50 / 30 / 21 days","Iowa · appeals · Briefs","Appellant’s brief: 50 days after the clerk’s briefing-deadline notice. Appellee’s brief (or a statement waiving it): 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief. Appellant’s reply: 21 days after service of the appellee’s brief. Each brief may be amended once without leave: the opening brief within 15 days after service, the appellee’s within 10 days, and a reply within 7 days. Iowa R. App. P. 6.901(1), 6.901(8)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Expedited cases: half the time, no extensions","Iowa · appeals · Briefs","Custody, physical care and visitation, adoption, chapter 600A and chapter 232 termination and CINA cases, guilty-plea and sentencing-only criminal appeals, juvenile placement, lawyer discipline, involuntary commitments, and certified questions are expedited. Briefing times (other than replies) are cut in half. Replies are due 15 days after the appellee’s brief (7 days for a cross-appellant’s reply). The court will no","#st-ia-appeals"],["Length and typeface","Iowa · appeals · Briefs","A brief may contain up to 13,000 words in a proportionally spaced typeface (14-point or larger, with serifs, including footnotes), or 1,300 lines in a monospaced typeface. A reply brief gets half that. A handwritten brief is limited to 50 pages, or 25 for a reply. Headings, footnotes, and quotations count. File the Form 7 certificate of compliance. Page numbers go bottom center, starting with the cover as page one, w","#st-ia-appeals"],["Required contents: routing statement and error preservation","Iowa · appeals · Briefs","The appellant’s brief must include a routing statement saying whether the supreme court should retain the case or transfer it to the Court of Appeals under Rule 6.1101. Each argument division must begin by stating how the issue was preserved, with record cites showing where it was raised and decided below; citing the notice of appeal does not satisfy this. Next comes the scope and standard of review. Failure to cite ","#st-ia-appeals"],["Citations, including unpublished opinions","Iowa · appeals · Briefs","Cite Iowa cases to the North Western Reporter with a pin cite. Unpublished opinions are not controlling authority but may be cited as persuasive. Give the docket number and an electronic-database cite; if the opinion is not in a readily accessible database, you must file and serve a copy. Iowa R. App. P. 6.904(2)(a)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Supersedeas bond","Iowa · appeals · Stays, bonds, and motions","An appeal does not stay the judgment unless a supersedeas bond is filed and approved in the district court, or the court orders a stay. For a money judgment the bond may not exceed 110% of the judgment, unless the district court sets it higher under Iowa Code section 625A.9(2)(a), and it is subject to the statutory maximum in section 625A.9(2)(b). In other cases the bond must hold the appellee harmless, and it may no","#st-ia-appeals"],["Motions: 14 days to resist, 3 to reply","Iowa · appeals · Stays, bonds, and motions","A resistance is due within 14 days after service of a motion, and a reply within 3 days after service of the resistance. Attachments are limited to 25 pages unless the court orders otherwise. A party adversely affected by a ruling made without awaiting a resistance may ask for review within 10 days. Iowa R. App. P. 6.1002","#st-ia-appeals"],["Missed deadlines cost the lawyer $150","Iowa · appeals · Stays, bonds, and motions","When an appellant misses a deadline, the clerk issues a default notice: cure within 15 days or the appeal may be dismissed. An appellee who misses the brief deadline loses oral argument unless the brief is filed within 15 days of the notice. Each default notice sent to a lawyer carries a $150 penalty that the lawyer must pay personally, not charge to the client. A dismissal for a represented appellant’s default is re","#st-ia-appeals"],["Extensions of other deadlines","Iowa · appeals · Stays, bonds, and motions","Nonjurisdictional deadlines may be shortened or extended for good cause, on motion or by the court on its own. Examples of good cause: illness of counsel, unusual and compelling unavailability, a missing transcript, or a real chance of settlement. In expedited cases the motion must say so. Iowa R. App. P. 6.1003(2)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Ask for oral argument in the brief","Iowa · appeals · Argument, decision, and rehearing","A request for oral argument must be made in the party’s brief; otherwise argument will ordinarily not be granted. The court denies argument that is unlikely to help it. Iowa R. App. P. 6.903(2)(a)(10), 6.908(1)–(2)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Scope of review","Iowa · appeals · Argument, decision, and rehearing","Review in equity cases is de novo. In all other cases the appellate courts correct errors at law. Iowa R. App. P. 6.907","#st-ia-appeals"],["Rehearing in the Court of Appeals: 7 days, and it does not stop the further-review clock","Iowa · appeals · Argument, decision, and rehearing","File within 7 days after the Court of Appeals decision. A petition not granted or denied within 7 days is deemed denied. No response is received unless requested, and there is no oral argument. The 20-day period for further review keeps running, so a party may file both. The supreme court may stay a pending further-review application while rehearing is pending. The petition is limited to one-fifth of the brief length","#st-ia-appeals"],["Rehearing in the supreme court: 14 days","Iowa · appeals · Argument, decision, and rehearing","File within 14 days after the supreme court opinion. No response is allowed unless the court asks for one, and the court ordinarily will not grant rehearing without asking. No rehearing may be sought from an order denying further review. The petition is limited to one-fifth of the brief length. Iowa R. App. P. 6.1205","#st-ia-appeals"],["Routing: retained or transferred","Iowa · appeals · Review in the Iowa Supreme Court","The supreme court ordinarily keeps cases raising substantial constitutional questions about a statute or rule, conflicts in published decisions, substantial issues of first impression, urgent issues of broad public importance, lawyer discipline, and the announcing or changing of legal principles. Cases that apply existing principles, or that suit summary disposition, ordinarily go to the Court of Appeals. Iowa R. App","#st-ia-appeals"],["Further review: 20 days, discretionary, no extensions","Iowa · appeals · Review in the Iowa Supreme Court","Apply within 20 days after the Court of Appeals decision is filed (10 days in chapter 232 juvenile cases). Neither court may extend this, except that the Court of Appeals must extend it when the clerk failed to notify the party of the decision. Further review is discretionary and ‘will not be granted in normal circumstances.’ Grounds include conflicting decisions, important unsettled questions, changing legal princip","#st-ia-appeals"],["Interlocutory appeal by permission: 30 days","Iowa · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","Apply to the supreme court within 30 days after entry of the ruling, or within 30 days after the ruling on a timely Rule 1.904(2) motion. The court grants permission only if the ruling involves substantial rights and will materially affect the final decision, and if deciding its correctness before trial will better serve the interests of justice. A grant stays the trial court. The fee is $100, plus $50 more if grante","#st-ia-appeals"],["Certiorari and discretionary review: 30 days","Iowa · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","An original certiorari action claiming that a district judge, district associate judge, associate juvenile judge, or associate probate judge exceeded jurisdiction or acted illegally must be filed in the supreme court within 30 days after the decision (or after a ruling on a timely 1.904(2) or 1.1007 motion). Applications for discretionary review of orders that a statute makes reviewable only by permission are also du","#st-ia-appeals"],["Appeals of right created by statute or rule","Iowa · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","When a statute or rule grants an appeal as of right from something other than a final judgment, use a notice of appeal and name the statute or rule that authorizes it. Examples given in the comment: qualified immunity (Iowa Code § 670.4A(4)), arbitration (§ 679A.17), and class certification (Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.264(3)). An order denying, in whole or in part, an anti-SLAPP motion under Iowa’s Uniform Public Expression P","#st-ia-appeals"],["Small claims go to a district judge, not the appellate courts","Iowa · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","A case tried as a small claim (except one involving an interest in real estate) cannot be appealed to the supreme court. It is appealed to the district court under Iowa Code section 631.13, and further review is only by discretionary review under section 631.16 and Rule 6.106. Iowa R. App. P. 6.105","#st-ia-appeals"],["Certified questions","Iowa · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","Federal and other courts may certify questions of Iowa law to the supreme court under Iowa Code chapter 684A. Certified-question cases are on the expedited track. Iowa R. App. P. 6.301–6.305, 6.902(1)(j)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Electronic filing is mandatory, including for pro hac vice counsel","Iowa · appeals · E-filing and time computation","All Iowa lawyers, all lawyers admitted pro hac vice, and self-represented parties must register for and use the Electronic Document Management System (EDMS) in appellate cases. The Rules of Electronic Procedure, including the privacy and redaction rules, apply on appeal. Iowa R. App. P. 6.1(1)–(2); Iowa R. Elec. P. 16.302(1)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Filed by midnight; technical trouble is no excuse","Iowa · appeals · E-filing and time computation","A document is timely if filed before midnight on the due date. The electronic file stamp is the official filing time. A technical problem never excuses a jurisdictional deadline. For a nonjurisdictional deadline, file by the soonest available means and ask the court to treat the filing as timely. Iowa R. Elec. P. 16.309(1)–(2)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (final order or judgment)","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the final order or judgment.\nFiled in the district court with an informational copy to the supreme court. Not extendable except for the clerk’s failure to give notice (Rule 6.101(5)). Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(1)(b)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Notice of appeal after a timely Rule 1.904(2) or 1.1007 motion","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the ruling on the motion.\nA successive motion by the same party does not count unless it addresses only a modification. Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(1)(b)–(c)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Notice of appeal in chapter 232 CINA or termination cases","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the order or judgment.\nAmendments effective January 1, 2027 change juvenile appeal rules; check the new text. Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(1)(a)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Notice of cross-appeal","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the final order, or 10 days after the notice of appeal, whichever is later.\n15-day base period in chapter 232 cases. Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(2)(b)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Motion to extend appeal time for lack of clerk notice","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from expiration of the original appeal deadline.\nAny extension granted runs no more than 30 days after the order granting it. Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(5)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Filing fee and combined certificate","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\n$150 fee. Certificate to both clerks and to court reporters. Iowa R. App. P. 6.102(3), 6.804(1)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Transcript filed by court reporter","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from service of the combined certificate.\n20 days for guilty pleas and sentencing; 30 days in chapter 232 cases. Iowa R. App. P. 6.803","#st-ia-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","50 days from clerk’s notice of briefing deadline.\nHalved in expedited cases (Rule 6.902). Iowa R. App. P. 6.901(1)(a)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the appellant’s brief.\nOr a written statement waiving the brief. Iowa R. App. P. 6.901(1)(b)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Appellant’s reply brief","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\nWith a cross-appeal the appellant must reply or file a waiver; the cross-appellant then has 14 days to reply. Iowa R. App. P. 6.901(1)(c)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Resistance to a motion","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the motion.\nReply within 3 days after the resistance. Iowa R. App. P. 6.1002(2)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Cure of default after clerk’s notice","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from issuance of the default notice.\n$150 penalty to the lawyer for each default notice. Iowa R. App. P. 6.1202(1)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Application for interlocutory appeal","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the ruling (or of the ruling on a timely 1.904(2) motion).\n15 days in chapter 232 cases. Iowa R. App. P. 6.104(1)(c)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Petition for writ of certiorari","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the challenged decision.\nRuns from the ruling on a timely 1.904(2) or 1.1007 motion. Iowa R. App. P. 6.107(1)(b)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Application for discretionary review","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the ruling, order, or judgment. Iowa R. App. P. 6.106(1)(b)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing in the Court of Appeals","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from filing of the Court of Appeals decision.\nDoes not toll the 20-day further-review period. Iowa R. App. P. 6.1204(2)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Application for further review","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from filing of the Court of Appeals decision.\n10 days in chapter 232 cases. Not extendable by the supreme court. Iowa R. App. P. 6.1103(1)(a)(2)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Resistance to application for further review","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from service of the application.\nNo replies. Not permitted in chapter 232 cases unless requested. Iowa R. App. P. 6.1103(2)(b)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing in the supreme court","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the supreme court opinion.\nUnless the court shortens or enlarges the time. Iowa R. App. P. 6.1205(1)","#st-ia-appeals"],["Appeal from denial of anti-SLAPP motion","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order.\nMoving party only; proceedings are stayed during the appeal (§ 652.4(3)). Iowa Code § 652.9","#st-ia-appeals"],["Motion for new trial or JNOV (trial court)","Iowa · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the verdict, report, or decision.\nCourt may add up to 30 days for good cause, not ex parte. Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.1007","#st-ia-appeals"],["Idaho Supreme Court","Idaho · courts","Court of last resort, with five justices who choose a chief justice from among themselves. Every appeal is filed as an appeal to the Supreme Court; the Court keeps some and assigns others to the Court of Appeals under I.A.R. 108. Hears appeals from district courts, the Public Utilities Commission, and the Industrial Commission (workers’ compensation), reviews Court of Appeals decisions on petition for review, issues ","#st-id"],["Idaho Court of Appeals","Idaho · courts","Four judges sitting in panels of three. Hears only the cases the Supreme Court assigns to it. All filings after the notice of appeal go to the Clerk of the Supreme Court, who is also the Clerk of the Court of Appeals, under the Supreme Court caption and number.","#st-id"],["District Courts (seven judicial districts)","Idaho · courts","General-jurisdiction trial courts. Hear felonies, civil actions over $10,000, appeals from the magistrate division, and judicial review of agency decisions. One set of civil rules applies statewide in district and magistrate courts.","#st-id"],["Magistrate Division of the District Courts","Idaho · courts","Part of the district court, not a separate court. Magistrate judges hear civil actions up to $10,000, small claims, evictions, probate, guardianships, family law (under the separate Idaho Rules of Family Law Procedure), juvenile cases, misdemeanors, infractions, and felony preliminary hearings. Most magistrate decisions are appealed to the district judge first (I.R.C.P. 83), not to the Supreme Court.","#st-id"],["Statute: Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (effective January 1, 2026)","Idaho · anti-SLAPP","Idaho adopted the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (UPEPA) in 2025, effective January 1, 2026. It applies to civil actions filed, and causes of action asserted, on or after January 1, 2026; claims asserted earlier are not affected. Before this Act, the Idaho Code chapter list shows no general anti-SLAPP statute. Idaho Code §§ 6-3901, 6-3913, 6-3914 (2025 Idaho Sess. Laws ch. 17, § 1)","#st-id-slapp"],["Scope: three kinds of protected activity","Idaho · anti-SLAPP","Covers any cause of action (including a cross-claim, counterclaim, or third-party claim) against a person based on: (a) communication in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding; (b) communication on an issue under consideration or review in such a proceeding; or (c) exercise of freedom of speech or press, assembly, petition, or association under the U.S. or Idaho Constitut","#st-id-slapp"],["Procedure: 60 days to file, automatic stay, 60-day hearing and ruling","Idaho · anti-SLAPP","File a special motion for expedited relief within 60 days after being served with the pleading that asserts the claim, or later for good cause. Filing automatically stays all other proceedings between the movant and the responding party, including discovery and pending hearings or motions, until the court rules and the time to appeal runs; on motion, the court may also stay related matters involving other parties. Li","#st-id-slapp"],["Standard: dismissal with prejudice","Idaho · anti-SLAPP","The court considers the pleadings, the motion papers, and any evidence that could be considered on summary judgment. It must dismiss with prejudice if the movant shows the Act applies, the responding party fails to show an exclusion, and either the responding party fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of material fact. A volu","#st-id-slapp"],["Fees: mandatory for a prevailing movant","Idaho · anti-SLAPP","The court must award court costs, reasonable attorney’s fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion to a prevailing movant, and to a prevailing responding party only if the court finds the motion frivolous or filed solely to delay. Idaho Code § 6-3910","#st-id-slapp"],["Appeal: as of right within 42 days, with a full stay","Idaho · anti-SLAPP","A moving party may appeal as of right from an order denying the motion in whole or in part, within 42 days after entry of the order “pursuant to applicable court rule.” While the appeal is pending, all proceedings between all parties are stayed. The Idaho Appellate Rules collected on November 10, 2026 do not mention the Act; the appeal would rest on I.A.R. 11(a)(8) (orders made appealable by statute), and the notice ","#st-id-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: motion in each case, $625 to the Bar, Idaho local counsel","Idaho · out of state","A lawyer who is not an Idaho Bar member and not an Idaho resident may appear in an Idaho case by court order if active and in good standing in another U.S. jurisdiction and currently maintaining an ongoing law practice in another jurisdiction. File a motion with the court that designates Idaho local counsel (with address and phone), includes local counsel’s written consent, and identifies the home bar and whether it ","#st-id-oos"],["Local counsel must attend everything","Idaho · out of state","Local counsel is an active Idaho Bar member with whom the court and opposing counsel can readily communicate, and must personally appear with the pro hac vice lawyer on all matters before the court unless specifically excused by the trial judge. Every filing that names the pro hac vice lawyer must give that lawyer’s current office address in the home jurisdiction. I.B.C.R. 227(b), (f)","#st-id-oos"],["No numerical cap, except reciprocity","Idaho · out of state","Idaho places no limit on the number of pro hac vice admissions, except that a lawyer applying on a license from a jurisdiction that caps admissions of Idaho lawyers faces the same cap in Idaho. Agencies may admit out-of-state lawyers using the same standards. The applicant consents to disciplinary jurisdiction of the court and the Bar and commits to follow the Idaho Standards for Civility in Professional Conduct. I.B","#st-id-oos"],["Appeals: separate motion to argue, 14 days ahead, unless already admitted","Idaho · out of state","To argue before the Supreme Court, a licensed Idaho lawyer must move at least 14 days before the hearing or argument, certifying the out-of-state lawyer’s good standing, substantially in the I.B.C.R. 227(j) form. A lawyer already admitted pro hac vice in the case may continue in the Supreme Court without a new order. Every notice of appeal, petition, motion, and brief must be signed by at least one licensed Idaho att","#st-id-oos"],["Practicing without pro hac vice: Rule 5.5","Idaho · out of state","Idaho’s Rule 5.5(b) lets a lawyer admitted elsewhere provide services in Idaho that are authorized by pro hac vice admission under I.B.C.R. 227, are for an in-house employer, are reasonably related to a representation in the lawyer’s home jurisdiction, or are done in association with Idaho counsel who actively participates. The text has no separate ABA-style temporary-practice list, and its comments bar soliciting Id","#st-id-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances: only a pro bono notice in civil court","Idaho · out of state","Rule 1.2(c) allows a reasonable limited-scope representation with informed consent (writing encouraged, not required). The civil rules provide a court procedure only for unpaid help: an attorney may file a notice of limited pro bono appearance for a self-represented litigant, act only on the listed matters, and end the appearance by a notice of completion without leave of court. Otherwise, withdrawal requires leave o","#st-id-oos"],["E-filing: mandatory for attorneys through iCourt File and Serve","Idaho · out of state","Attorneys must file electronically under the Idaho Rules for Electronic Filing and Service, which override other procedural rules on filing, format, and service. Documents must be text-searchable PDF (or PDF/A), 50 MB per file at most. A filing submitted before midnight in the court’s time zone counts for that day, and the system timestamp controls, even if the filing is placed in an error queue. Service through the ","#st-id-oos"],["Governing code: Idaho Rules of Professional Conduct","Idaho · ethics","The Idaho Rules of Professional Conduct took effect November 1, 1986 and were revised effective July 1, 2004 after an Ethics 2000 review. They are based largely on the ABA Model Rules, with Idaho variations, and the Supreme Court also adopted the comments. The Bar’s posted version is labeled current as of March 9, 2026; the text itself shows no 2024-2026 amendment dates. Idaho R. Prof. Conduct (isb.idaho.gov version ","#st-id-ethics"],["Confidentiality: may reveal intent to commit any crime","Idaho · ethics","Rule 1.6(b)(1) permits (but does not require) disclosure to prevent the client from committing a crime, including disclosure of a stated intention to commit any crime, not just crimes involving death or bodily harm. The Idaho comment says this differs from the ABA Model Rule. Idaho R. Prof. Conduct 1.6(b)(1) & cmt. [6]","#st-id-ethics"],["Client files: a separate Rule 1.16A","Idaho · ethics","Idaho has a separate client-file rule. The lawyer must make the file available to the client or former client. The file includes the lawyer’s notes whether or not they are work product. Copying may be charged only at actual cost. The file may be withheld for unpaid fees only if that will not cause imminent prejudice, and it may not be destroyed while litigation, an investigation, or discipline is pending or anticipat","#st-id-ethics"],["Fees: free accounting on request; no contingent fees in divorce or criminal cases","Idaho · ethics","On reasonable request, a lawyer must provide, without charge, an accounting of fees and costs that itemizes charges and describes the services and who performed them. The fee basis should preferably be in writing; contingent fee agreements must be in a writing signed by the client. Advance fees go into trust and are withdrawn only as earned; the text has no “nonrefundable fee” provision. Idaho R. Prof. Conduct 1.5(b)","#st-id-ethics"],["Threatening criminal charges for civil advantage is barred","Idaho · ethics","Rule 4.4(a) forbids presenting, participating in presenting, or threatening criminal charges solely to gain advantage in a civil matter. It also forbids means meant to embarrass or burden others, including engendering bias based on gender, race, religion, national origin, or sexual preference. The comment calls the criminal-charge rule Idaho’s “more traditional view,” abandoned in most jurisdictions. Idaho R. Prof. C","#st-id-ethics"],["No ABA 8.4(g); settlement conditioned on dropping a bar complaint is misconduct","Idaho · ethics","Rule 8.4 has no harassment or discrimination paragraph like ABA Model Rule 8.4(g). Knowingly showing bias in representing a client is addressed under 8.4(d) through a comment. A comment also says conditioning a civil settlement on someone not filing, or withdrawing, a disciplinary complaint is prejudicial to the administration of justice. Idaho R. Prof. Conduct 8.4 & cmts. [3], [6]","#st-id-ethics"],["Advertising: older structure kept","Idaho · ethics","Idaho keeps separate Rules 7.4 (communicating fields of practice; a specialist claim needs certification by a Bar-approved organization) and 7.5 (firm names). It also keeps a 2-year advertising record requirement and the “Advertising Material” label on written solicitations. Rule 7.6 bars accepting government legal work or judicial appointments obtained through political contributions. Idaho R. Prof. Conduct 7.1(c), ","#st-id-ethics"],["Sale of a practice: partial sales allowed","Idaho · ethics","Idaho does not require selling the entire practice or leaving practice; the seller need only stop practicing in the area of law sold. The comment says this differs from the ABA Model Rule. Client consent is presumed if the client does not object within 90 days of notice. Idaho R. Prof. Conduct 1.17(a), (c) & cmt. [2]","#st-id-ethics"],["Financial assistance, screening, trust records","Idaho · ethics","Rule 1.8(e) allows only advancing costs and paying costs for indigent clients; the text has no exception for modest gifts to pro bono clients. Lateral conflicts may be screened with written notice to the former client. Trust account records must be kept for 5 years, and trust funds must be in an account in the state where the lawyer’s office is located unless the client consents otherwise. Pro bono is aspirational at","#st-id-ethics"],["Civility standards bind pro hac vice lawyers","Idaho · ethics","Pro hac vice applicants commit to follow the Idaho Standards for Civility in Professional Conduct, and the form motion recites this commitment. The Standards themselves were not collected. I.B.C.R. 227(d)(2), (j)","#st-id-ethics"],["Disqualification without cause can expire within 7 days","Idaho · land mine","The peremptory motion is due 7 days after service of the first notice setting a status conference, pretrial conference, trial, or contested motion hearing, if that comes before the 21-day pleading deadline. It is lost once the judge presides over any such proceeding. Each party gets only one in the case. I.R.C.P. 40(a)(1), (4)","#st-id-unique"],["Notice of appeal: 42 days from the file stamp, filed in the trial court, jurisdictional","Idaho · land mine","Appeal by filing a notice with the district court clerk within 42 days from the clerk’s filing stamp on the judgment, not 30 days from entry or service. It is not filed until received, it cannot be extended, and a late notice means automatic dismissal. Rule 60 motions and cost or fee motions do not restart the clock. I.A.R. 14(a), 20, 21, 46","#st-id-unique"],["Post-judgment motions are due in 14 days, not 28","Idaho · land mine","Motions for new trial, to alter or amend, to amend findings, and for judgment notwithstanding the verdict are all due within 14 days after entry of judgment. A motion to reconsider an interlocutory order must come before, or within 14 days after, final judgment. The court cannot extend these times. I.R.C.P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), 11.2(b), 2.2(b)","#st-id-unique"],["A judgment must be a bare separate document","Idaho · land mine","A judgment must be titled “Judgment” or “Decree,” begin “JUDGMENT IS ENTERED AS FOLLOWS:”, and contain no recitals, reasoning, or findings. It is final only if it resolves all claims as to all parties (except costs and fees) or carries a Rule 54(b) certificate. A nonconforming order may not start the appeal clock. I.R.C.P. 54(a)-(b), 58; I.A.R. 11(a)(1), (3)","#st-id-unique"],["Costs and fees memorandum due 14 days after judgment, or waived","Idaho · land mine","File and serve the memorandum of costs and attorney fees, with an attorney affidavit, no later than 14 days after entry of judgment. Objections are due 14 days after service. On appeal, fees must be claimed as an issue in your first brief, and the costs memorandum is due 14 days after the opinion. I.R.C.P. 54(d)(4)-(5), (e)(5); I.A.R. 40(c), 41(a), (d)","#st-id-unique"],["No automatic 30-day stay of judgment, and only 14 days after appeal","Idaho · land mine","Unlike the federal rule, Idaho has no automatic stay after entry of judgment; execution may issue at once unless the court orders otherwise. Filing a civil notice of appeal stays proceedings and execution for only 14 days. A stay of a money judgment needs a bond or cash deposit of the judgment plus 36%. I.R.C.P. 62(a); I.A.R. 13(a), 13(b)(15)","#st-id-unique"],["Motion practice runs on a 14/7/2-day schedule tied to the hearing","Idaho · land mine","A motion, supporting affidavits and briefs, and the notice of hearing must be filed and served so they are received at least 14 days before the hearing. Opposition is due at least 7 days before, and a reply at least 2 days before. The motion must say whether oral argument is wanted and whether a brief will be filed. Summary judgment instead runs 28/14/7 days before the hearing, and the motion must be filed at least 9","#st-id-unique"],["Do not plead punitive damages","Idaho · land mine","A complaint with a prayer for punitive damages violates the statute. Move to amend later, after showing at a hearing a reasonable likelihood of proving punitive damages at trial. Idaho Code § 6-1604(2)","#st-id-unique"],["Fee demand letters before suing on small claims","Idaho · land mine","To recover fees in actions pleading $35,000 or less, the plaintiff must send a written demand at least 10 days before filing. For personal injury, the demand and an itemized statement of claim with records must reach the insurer (or the defendant) at least 60 days before filing, and new injuries or damage items later waive fees. Idaho Code § 12-120(1), (4)","#st-id-unique"],["Do not contact the other side’s retained expert","Idaho · land mine","Idaho’s discovery rule bars contacting a retained expert disclosed by another party without that party’s permission or a court order. Discovery scope still uses “reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence,” and the text contains no mandatory initial-disclosure requirement. I.R.C.P. 26(b)(1), 26(b)(4)","#st-id-unique"],["Every caption needs an Idaho Bar number, and appellate papers need an Idaho signer","Idaho · land mine","The attorney of record’s name, address, phone, email, and current Idaho State Bar number must appear above the court’s title on the first page. In the appellate courts, at least one licensed Idaho attorney of record must sign every notice, motion, petition, and brief. Pro hac vice local counsel must attend all court proceedings unless excused. I.R.C.P. 2(a)(4); I.A.R. 11.2(a); I.B.C.R. 227(b)(2)","#st-id-unique"],["Rule 60(b) has a 6-month outer limit","Idaho · land mine","A motion for relief from judgment for mistake, excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, or fraud must be made within a reasonable time and no more than 6 months after entry of the judgment. The federal rule allows up to one year. I.R.C.P. 60(c)(1)","#st-id-unique"],["Disqualification without cause: one per party, short deadline","Idaho · judges","In every civil action and petition for judicial review, each party may file one motion to disqualify the judge without stating grounds. The deadline is whichever comes first: 7 days after service of a notice or order setting a status conference, pretrial conference, trial, or the first contested motion hearing, or 21 days after service or receipt of a pleading or order showing who the presiding judge will be. In any ","#st-id-unique"],["Where it does not apply","Idaho · judges","No disqualification without cause of a judge acting in an appellate capacity (unless the appeal is a trial de novo), a post-conviction judge who entered the challenged judgment, or a judge the Supreme Court appointed to a specific civil action. If the motions are used to delay or are filed so often that they impede the administration of justice, the Administrative Director of Courts reviews the misuse. I.R.C.P. 40(a)","#st-id-unique"],["Disqualification for cause, at any time","Idaho · judges","A party may move at any time to disqualify a judge who is a party or interested, is related to a party within the third degree, was counsel for a party, or is biased or prejudiced for or against a party or the subject matter. The motion needs an affidavit stating the specific grounds and facts. The challenged judge decides it after notice and hearing. Once a disqualification motion is filed, the judge may act only to","#st-id-unique"],["UPEPA: broad construction for state and federal speech rights","Idaho · speech","Idaho’s anti-SLAPP statute protects speech, press, assembly, petition, and association rights “guaranteed by the constitution of the United States or the constitution of the state of Idaho” and directs that it be broadly construed and applied to protect those rights, with an eye to uniformity among enacting states. Idaho Code §§ 6-3902(2)(c), 6-3911, 6-3912","#st-id-unique"],["Whether the Idaho Constitution protects speech more broadly: not checked","Idaho · speech","The collected texts do not include the Idaho Constitution or case law, so whether Idaho courts read the state free-speech guarantee more broadly than the First Amendment was not verified. Idaho Const. (not in collected texts)","#st-id-unique"],["Statutory fee-shifting is common, and you must ask for it in time","Idaho · unique rules","Idaho Code § 12-120 gives the prevailing party attorney’s fees in actions pleading $35,000 or less (after a written demand at least 10 days before suit), in personal injury claims of $35,000 or less (after a demand and statement of claim served at least 60 days before suit), and in any commercial transaction or action on an account, note, or contract for goods or services. Section 12-121 allows fees for a case brough","#st-id-unique"],["Offer of judgment compares an “adjusted award”","Idaho · unique rules","Idaho Rule 68 lets a defending party serve an offer at least 14 days before trial, with 14 days to accept. For money claims, the comparison is to an “adjusted award” (the verdict plus the offeree’s pre-offer costs and fees). If the adjusted award is less than the offer, the offeree pays the offeror’s post-offer costs. That is a different calculation from the federal rule. I.R.C.P. 68","#st-id-unique"],["Punitive damages cannot be pleaded at the outset","Idaho · unique rules","No complaint may pray for punitive damages. A party must move to amend before trial and, after a hearing, show a reasonable likelihood of proving facts at trial that support punitive damages. At trial the standard is clear and convincing evidence of oppressive, fraudulent, malicious, or outrageous conduct. Idaho Code § 6-1604(1)-(2)","#st-id-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope of rules; district court rules","Idaho · civil rules","The rules apply uniformly in district courts and magistrate’s divisions in all civil actions, proceedings and appeals, except small claims (governed only as the Small Claims Rules provide), certain mental-hospitalization and neurocognitive-treatment proceedings (governed by ICAR) and family law proceedings (governed by the IRFLP); they are to be construed to secure the just, speedy and inexpensive determination of ev","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 1.1. Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","Idaho · civil rules","The rules do not extend or limit the jurisdiction of any Idaho court or the venue of actions.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 1.2. Jurisdictional amounts for assignment to magistrates; counterclaims and cross-claims","Idaho · civil rules","The jurisdictional amounts in Idaho Code sections 1-2208 and 1-2210 or the ICAR exclude interest, costs, punitive damages and attorney fees, and the amounts of all counts are added together, though the assigned judge may decide by written order the actual amount in controversy where several counts seek the same or similar relief. If a counterclaim or cross-claim filed in the magistrate’s division exceeds the magistra","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 1.3. Objection to assignment to magistrates","Idaho · civil rules","Any irregularity in assigning a case to a magistrate, and any objection to the propriety of the assignment, is waived unless a written objection is filed before the trial or hearing begins. An order or judgment is not void or open to collateral attack because it was rendered under an improper assignment to a magistrate.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 2. Form of documents; caption; name of parties; language; abbreviation; and numbers","Idaho · civil rules","Filed documents must be black ink on 8½” by 11” white paper (prisoners and approved forms may be hand-printed), with a caption, the title of the court starting at least 3 inches from the top, and attorney (with Idaho bar number) or self-represented party contact information starting at least 1.2 inches from the top. Body text must be double or 1½ spaced, at least 11-point, with margins of at least 1.2 inches at the t","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 2.1. Electronic signatures","Idaho · civil rules","An electronic signature may be used on any document transmitted electronically, and a notary’s seal may be in electronic form.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 2.2. Computing and extending time","Idaho · civil rules","In computing a period stated in days or longer, exclude the trigger day, count every day including weekends and legal holidays, and include the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday or legal holiday, in which case the period runs to the next day that is not one; if the clerk’s office is inaccessible, filing time extends to the first accessible day. The court may extend time for good cause, with or without motion i","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 2.3. Serving notice of an order or judgment","Idaho · civil rules","The prevailing party (or the party the court designates) must serve a proposed order or judgment on each party and give the clerk enough copies and stamped, addressed envelopes for service unless the court orders otherwise. Immediately after entry, the clerk must serve the order or judgment, bearing the filing stamp, on every party by mail, email or delivery and note the mailing in the record; mailing is sufficient n","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 2.4. Setting hearings by court","Idaho · civil rules","The court on its own initiative may notice for hearing any pending motion, trial or proceeding by giving notice to all parties as these rules require.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 2.5. Stipulations not binding on court; continuance of trial or hearing","Idaho · civil rules","Parties may stipulate to any procedural matter, including vacating or continuing a hearing or trial, but the stipulation is treated as a joint motion and does not bind the court, which may approve or disapprove it as it would a motion. The court may, by oral or written notice, limit the time within which a motion or stipulation to vacate or continue a hearing or trial must be made.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 2.6. Privacy protection for filings made with the court","Idaho · civil rules","The filer is responsible for omitting or redacting personal data identifiers before filing (the clerk does not review), and identifiers should be left out unless required by the court, statute or rule or material to the proceedings. When they must be included, redact to the last four digits for Social Security numbers, financial account numbers, driver’s license and state ID numbers, and employer or taxpayer ID numbe","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 2.7. Declarations","Idaho · civil rules","Where the rules require or permit a statement under oath or affirmation, it may be made as provided in Idaho Code section 9-1406, and an affidavit includes a written certification or declaration made that way.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 2.8. Unsworn foreign declarations","Idaho · civil rules","This is Idaho’s version of the Uniform Unsworn Foreign Declarations Act: an unsworn declaration made under penalty of perjury by a declarant physically outside the boundaries of the United States has the same effect as a sworn declaration wherever Idaho law requires or permits one, if it is in substantially the form in Appendix B and in any medium Idaho law requires for sworn declarations. It does not apply to deposi","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 2.9. Lost papers","Idaho · civil rules","If an original pleading or paper is lost, the court may authorize a copy to be filed and used instead of the original.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 2.10. Reclaiming exhibits, documents or property","Idaho · civil rules","Any party or interested person may apply to the trial court for return of exhibits, documents or property displayed or considered in the action, but only after the latest of the expiration of the time for appeal, the determination of an appeal, or the determination of any proceeding following an appeal and expiration of the time to appeal from that determination. The court may grant the application on appropriate con","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencement of action","Idaho · civil rules","There is one form of action, the civil action, commenced by filing a complaint, petition or application; filing parties are designated plaintiff or petitioner and opposing parties defendant or respondent. Except for governmental entities as defined in Idaho Code section 6-902(3) and the listed case types, every party must file a Supreme Court approved case information sheet (exempt from disclosure) with its first app","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 4. Summons","Idaho · civil rules","The summons must contain the items listed in the rule (court, case number, parties, county, clerk and plaintiff’s attorney contact information, the time to appear and defend, default warning, clerk’s signature and court seal) in substantially the Appendix B form, and the clerk issues one for each defendant on or after filing. If a defendant is not served within 182 days after the complaint is filed, the court, on mot","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 4.1. General or special appearance","Idaho · civil rules","A voluntary appearance or service of a pleading submits the party to personal jurisdiction, except that a Rule 12(b)(2), (4) or (5) motion, a Rule 40(a) or (b) motion, a motion to extend time to answer or appear, joinder of other defenses in such a motion, responding to discovery or motions after filing such a motion, and pleading further after denial of such a motion are not voluntary appearances. A document called ","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 5. Serving and filing pleadings and other papers","Idaho · civil rules","Orders requiring service, later pleadings, discovery papers, written motions (except ex parte), and notices, appearances, demands and offers of judgment must be served on every party (not on a party in default for failing to appear, unless a new claim is asserted, which must be served under Rule 4), and service on a represented party is made on the attorney. Service may be made by hand, by leaving at the office or ab","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 5.1. Attorney legislators","Idaho · civil rules","An attorney serving as a legislator during a general or special session need not appear at any trial or proceeding, and the time to file any paper is extended for 10 days after adjournment, without tolling any statutory limitation period. On a motion supported by affidavit, the court may order ex parte that the attorney-legislator appear or arrange substitute counsel if it finds an emergency, undue prejudice to the p","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 6. Reserved","Idaho · civil rules","Reserved. Idaho puts time computation in Rule 2.2, not Rule 6.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings allowed; form of motions and other papers","Idaho · civil rules","Only these pleadings are allowed: a complaint, an answer to a complaint, an answer to a counterclaim, an answer to a cross claim, a third party complaint, an answer to a third party complaint, and a reply to an answer if the court orders one. A motion must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state its grounds with particularity including the rule number, and state the relief sought; a proposed order mu","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 7.1. Evidence on a motion","Idaho · civil rules","When a motion relies on facts outside the record, the court may hear it on affidavits or wholly or partly on oral testimony or depositions.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 7.2. Hearings by telephone or video teleconference","Idaho · civil rules","The court may hear any motion by phone or video, except summary judgment motions unless the parties stipulate; evidentiary hearings by phone or video only when no oral testimony is taken (video testimony allowed if the parties stipulate); and any other pretrial matter. Minutes must be filed and the hearing audio recorded, and a prevailing party may recover the conferencing cost as a discretionary cost.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 7.3. Video teleconferencing for mental commitment hearings","Idaho · civil rules","Initial and continuing involuntary commitment hearings may be conducted by video teleconference if the proposed patient is visible and audible to the court and those in the courtroom, can consult privately with counsel if represented, and the court, patient, counsel and any testifying witness can all see, hear and communicate with each other simultaneously. The audio must be recorded and minutes of the hearing prepar","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","Idaho · civil rules","A claim for relief must contain a short and plain statement of the grounds for jurisdiction (unless already established), a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief, and a demand for relief, which may be alternative or of different types. A responding party must state its defenses and admit or deny each allegation (by general denial, specific denial, or denial of all except those admitted, with a state","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","Idaho · civil rules","A pleading need not allege capacity to sue or be sued, representative authority or legal existence of an association unless needed to show jurisdiction, and these issues are raised by specific denial; fraud, mistake and violation of civil or constitutional rights must be pleaded with particularity, while malice, intent and knowledge may be alleged generally. Conditions precedent may be alleged generally but must be d","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","Idaho · civil rules","Every pleading must comply with Rule 2 and carry a Rule 7(a) designation, state claims and defenses in numbered paragraphs each limited to a single set of circumstances, and put each claim on a separate transaction or occurrence, and each defense other than a denial, in a separate count or defense where that promotes clarity. Statements may be adopted by reference, a written instrument attached as an exhibit is part ","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 10.1. Filing fee waiver","Idaho · civil rules","The filing fee in Appendix A must be paid before filing a pleading or motion listed in the fee schedule. The court may waive it on a party’s verified application (no fee for the application), and the fee is automatically waived where the party is represented by a volunteer attorney through the Idaho Law Foundation Volunteer Lawyers Program, the University of Idaho Legal Aid Clinic, the Idaho Legal Aid Program, or a p","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing pleadings, motions, and other papers; representations to the court; sanctions","Idaho · civil rules","Every paper must be signed by at least one Idaho-licensed attorney of record (or by an unrepresented party) with the signer’s address, email and phone number, pleadings need not be verified unless a rule or statute says so, and the court must strike an unsigned paper unless the omission is promptly corrected. By presenting a paper, the signer certifies after reasonable inquiry that it is not for an improper purpose a","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 11.1. Verification","Idaho · civil rules","A verification of a pleading must be a written statement or declaration by a party or the party’s attorney of record, sworn or affirmed before an officer authorized under Rule 28 or complying with Idaho Code section 9-1406, that the affiant believes the facts stated are true, unless verification on personal knowledge is required. For a corporation an officer may verify, and for a partnership or unincorporated associa","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 11.2. Successive applications for orders or writs; motions for reconsideration","Idaho · civil rules","If an application for an order or writ is denied in whole or in part, neither the party nor the attorney may apply to another judge except by appeal; an order obtained in violation must be immediately vacated by the issuing judge and the court must sanction the party and attorney, including costs and attorney fees. A second application for a constitutional writ is allowed if the first application and ruling are discl","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 11.3. Substitution and withdrawal of attorneys","Idaho · civil rules","An attorney may be substituted by filing written notice signed by both the new and withdrawing attorney, and substitution must not delay proceedings except for good cause. Otherwise withdrawal requires leave of court on a noticed motion set for hearing and giving the client’s last known address; the court may grant it for good cause on conditions that prevent delay or prejudice, and after final judgment the attorney ","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 11.4. Limited pro bono appearance","Idaho · civil rules","Under Idaho Rule of Professional Conduct 1.2(c), an attorney may give limited unpaid assistance to a self-represented litigant by filing and serving a notice of limited appearance specifying the matters undertaken. The attorney acts only on those matters, service on the attorney is valid only for those proceedings, and when they conclude the attorney files a notice of completion of limited appearance, which ends the ","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections: when and how presented; motion for judgment on the pleadings; consolidating motions; waiving defenses; hearings before trial","Idaho · civil rules","Unless another time is set by rule or statute, an answer is due within 21 days after service of the summons and complaint, an answer to a counterclaim or crossclaim is due within 21 days after service of that pleading, and a reply ordered by the court is due 21 days after service of the order unless the court sets another time; if the court denies a Rule 12 motion or postpones it until trial, or grants a motion for a","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaims and crossclaims","Idaho · civil rules","A pleading must state as a compulsory counterclaim any claim against an opposing party arising from the same transaction or occurrence that does not require adding a party over whom the court lacks jurisdiction, subject to the stated exceptions, and may state any other claim as a permissive counterclaim. A counterclaim may seek more or different relief than the opposing party’s claim, the rules do not expand the righ","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","Idaho · civil rules","A defending party may serve a summons and complaint on a nonparty who is or may be liable to it for all or part of the claim, but needs leave of court by motion if the third-party complaint is filed more than 14 days after serving its original answer. The third-party defendant must assert Rule 12 defenses and Rule 13(a) counterclaims against the third-party plaintiff, may assert permissive counterclaims, crossclaims,","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Idaho · civil rules","A party may amend once as a matter of right within 21 days after serving the pleading or, if a responsive pleading is required, within 21 days after service of a responsive pleading or of a Rule 12(b), (e) or (f) motion, whichever is earlier; otherwise amendment requires the opposing party’s written consent or leave of court, which should be freely given when justice requires. Unless the court orders otherwise, a res","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 16. Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","Idaho · civil rules","Within 30 days after an answer or notice of appearance is filed (or within 90 days after the complaint is filed, if a defendant has been served but has not appeared), the court must act to get a scheduling order entered, such as by setting a scheduling conference or requesting trial dates. The order covers trial and pretrial dates, deadlines for joining parties, amending pleadings, dispositive motions, discovery and ","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 17. Plaintiff and defendant; capacity","Idaho · civil rules","An action must be prosecuted by the real party in interest, though executors, administrators, personal representatives, guardians, bailees, express-trust trustees, parties holding a contract made for another’s benefit, and statutorily authorized parties may sue in their own names. The court may not dismiss for failure to name the real party in interest until, after an objection, a reasonable time has been allowed for","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims","Idaho · civil rules","A party asserting a claim, counterclaim, cross-claim, or third-party claim may join as many independent or alternative claims as it has against an opposing party. Contingent claims may be joined, and a plaintiff may, for example, plead a money claim and a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance without first obtaining a money judgment.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 19. Required joinder of parties","Idaho · civil rules","A person subject to service must be joined if the court cannot give complete relief without the person, or if the person claims an interest that disposing of the action could impair or that leaves an existing party at risk of double or inconsistent obligations. The court must order joinder of a required person; if joinder is not feasible, the court weighs listed equity factors (prejudice, ways to lessen it, adequacy ","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 19.1. Motor vehicle operator","Idaho · civil rules","In an action against a vehicle owner under Idaho Code section 49-2417, the operator whose negligence is imputed to the owner must be made a defendant if the operator can be personally served in Idaho. This is an Idaho-specific rule with no federal counterpart.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","Idaho · civil rules","Persons may join as plaintiffs, or be joined as defendants, if the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series of them and a common question of law or fact will arise. A party need not be interested in all the relief demanded, and the court may enter judgment according to each party’s rights or liabilities. The court may issue protective orders, including separate trials, and may direct final judgme","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","Idaho · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal. On motion or on its own, the court may at any time, on just terms, add or drop a party and may sever any claim against a party.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","Idaho · civil rules","A plaintiff exposed to double or multiple liability may join the competing claimants as defendants and require them to interplead, even if their claims lack a common origin or the plaintiff denies liability. A defendant facing similar liability may interplead through a cross-claim or counterclaim. The rule supplements, and does not limit, Rule 20 joinder.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 23. Reserved","Idaho · civil rules","Reserved. Idaho’s class-action rule is Rule 77, not Rule 23.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","Idaho · civil rules","On timely motion, the court must allow intervention by anyone given an unconditional right by an Idaho statute, or who claims an interest in the property or transaction that disposition may impair, unless existing parties adequately represent it. The court may allow permissive intervention for a conditional statutory right, a claim or defense sharing a common question with the main action, or a federal or state agenc","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","Idaho · civil rules","If a party dies and the claim survives, the court may order substitution on motion by any party or the decedent’s successor or representative; if no motion is made within a reasonable time, the action by or against the decedent may be dismissed. The action continues among the remaining parties if the right survives only to or against them. The substitution motion and notice of hearing go to parties under Rule 5 and t","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 26. General provisions governing discovery","Idaho · civil rules","Discovery methods are depositions, interrogatories, document and ESI production, entry on land, physical and mental examinations, and requests for admission. The scope is any nonprivileged matter relevant to a claim or defense and, for good cause, any matter relevant to the subject matter of the action; Idaho keeps the “reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence” language, and the text cont","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions to perpetuate testimony","Idaho · civil rules","Before suit, a person may file a verified petition in the district court where an expected adverse party resides to depose named persons, and must serve each expected adverse party at least 21 days before the hearing; the court may order service by publication if service cannot be made and must appoint an attorney for unserved persons. If it finds perpetuation may prevent a failure or delay of justice, the court issu","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Idaho · civil rules","In Idaho, a deposition is taken before a person authorized to administer oaths; elsewhere in the United States, before an officer authorized by Idaho, federal, or local law, or a person appointed by the court. In a foreign country, it may be taken before a U.S. diplomatic or consular officer, an officer authorized under Idaho or federal law, or a court-appointed person, and a commission may issue. Depositions of arme","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations about discovery procedure","Idaho · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, the parties may stipulate that a deposition be taken before any person, at any time or place, on any notice, and in the manner specified, and may modify other discovery procedures. A stipulation extending time for any form of discovery needs court approval if it would interfere with the trial date or if another court order requires approval.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions by oral examination","Idaho · civil rules","A party may depose any person without leave, except that leave is needed if the deponent is in prison or if the plaintiff seeks the deposition before 30 days have passed since service on the defendant (unless the defendant has noticed a deposition or sought discovery, or the plaintiff certifies the deponent will leave the district for more than 100 miles from the place of trial or leave the country before then). Reas","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 30.1. Audio-visual recording of depositions","Idaho · civil rules","Upon notice, a deposition may be recorded by audio-visual means but must also be recorded stenographically; the noticing party bears the recording and transcription costs, and both records are official. The notice and subpoena must say the deposition will be audio-visually recorded, and the rule sets on-camera opening and closing statements, an on-camera oath, announcement of media units, and a time index. The record","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions by written questions","Idaho · civil rules","A party may depose any person by written questions without leave, except that leave is required (and must be granted consistent with Rule 26(b)(1)(C)) if the deponent has already been deposed without stipulation or is in prison. The notice and questions go to every party, naming the deponent and the officer. Other parties’ questions are due within set times: cross-questions within 14 days after service of the notice ","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 32. Using depositions in court proceedings","Idaho · civil rules","A deposition may be used against a party who was present, represented, or given reasonable notice, to the extent admissible under the Idaho Rules of Evidence. Depositions may be used to impeach, an adverse party may use a party’s or its officer’s, director’s, managing agent’s, or designee’s deposition for any purpose, and any deposition may be used if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or outside Idaho, un","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories to parties","Idaho · civil rules","Unless otherwise stipulated or court-ordered for good cause, a party may serve no more than 40 interrogatories on any other party, including all discrete subparts. They may be served on the plaintiff after the suit is commenced and on any other party with or after the summons and complaint, and must be answered under oath within 30 days after service (shorter or longer by stipulation or order), with objections stated","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 34. Producing documents, electronically stored information, and tangible things, or entering onto land, for inspection and other purposes","Idaho · civil rules","A party may request inspection, copying, or testing of documents, ESI, and tangible things, or entry onto land, within the scope of Rule 26(b); requests may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on other parties with or after the summons and complaint. The request must describe items with reasonable particularity and specify a reasonable time, place, and manner. The response is due in writing within 30 da","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examinations","Idaho · civil rules","On motion for good cause and notice to all parties and the person to be examined, the court may order a party whose physical or mental condition (including blood group) is in controversy to be examined by a suitably certified examiner, licensed physician, or qualified mental health professional under Idaho Code section 6-1901, excluding nurses. The order must specify time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examin","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","Idaho · civil rules","A party may serve written requests to admit facts, application of law to fact, opinions about either, and genuineness of documents (with a copy attached unless already furnished); they may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on other parties with or after the summons and complaint. A matter is admitted unless the responding party serves a signed written answer or objection within 30 days after service (","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to cooperate in discovery; sanctions","Idaho · civil rules","A party may move to compel disclosure or discovery after certifying a good-faith attempt to confer; evasive or incomplete responses count as failures, and the court must award reasonable expenses including attorney’s fees to the prevailing side on a motion unless the motion was premature, the opposition was substantially justified, or an award would be unjust. For violating a discovery order, the court may establish ","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 37.1. Mediation of civil cases","Idaho · civil rules","All civil cases may be referred to mediation, in the court’s discretion on a party’s motion, at a Rule 16 conference, on a Rule 16(b) trial-setting request if all parties agree, or at any other time on 7 days’ notice. The parties must select a mediator within 28 days of the mediation order or the court will appoint one from the district’s list, and the first session must occur within 42 days after the parties report ","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury trial","Idaho · civil rules","A party may demand a jury on any issue triable of right by a jury by serving a written demand (which may be in a pleading) no later than 14 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue, and filing it under Rule 5(d). The demand must state whether the party will stipulate to a jury of fewer than 12 but at least 6. If a party demands a jury on only some issues, any other party may demand a jury on othe","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","Idaho · civil rules","Once a jury is demanded under Rule 38, the action is designated a jury action and all demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate to a nonjury trial or the court finds there is no jury right on those issues. Issues without a proper demand are tried by the court, though the court may order a jury on motion; the court may also use an advisory jury or, with the parties’ consent, a binding jury in ac","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 40. Disqualification","Idaho · civil rules","Disqualification without cause (40(a)): in all civil actions and petitions for judicial review, each party may file one motion to disqualify the judge without stating grounds. The motion is due the earlier of 7 days after service of a notice or order setting a status conference, pretrial conference, trial, or the first contested motion hearing, or 21 days after service or receipt of a pleading or order showing who th","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 40.1. Change of venue","Idaho · civil rules","A judge may change venue only on a party’s motion. Venue may be changed in the court’s discretion by statute or on affidavit or other proof that an impartial trial cannot be had in the county or that witness convenience and the ends of justice would be promoted; it must be changed if the county designated in the complaint is improper, on a motion made no later than 14 days after the moving party files a responsive pl","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","Idaho · civil rules","A plaintiff may dismiss without a court order by filing a notice of dismissal before the opposing party serves an answer or a summary judgment motion, or a stipulation signed by all appearing parties; the dismissal is without prejudice unless stated otherwise, but a notice operates as a merits adjudication if the plaintiff previously dismissed a state or federal action on the same claim. Otherwise dismissal at the pl","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials","Idaho · civil rules","If actions before the court share a common question of law or fact, the court may join them for hearing or trial, consolidate them, or issue other orders to avoid unnecessary cost or delay. For convenience, to avoid prejudice, or to expedite, the court may order separate trials of issues or claims, preserving any jury right.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 42.1. Trials and hearings in open court; where held","Idaho · civil rules","Trials on the merits must be in open court (witnesses may be excluded as the Idaho Rules of Evidence allow) and, so far as convenient, in a regular courtroom, though all trials, hearings, judgments, and orders are deemed done in open court wherever held. A hearing other than a trial or evidentiary hearing may be held outside the county where the action was filed or transferred; a trial or evidentiary hearing may be h","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 43. Taking testimony","Idaho · civil rules","Trial testimony is taken in open court unless a statute or rule provides otherwise, though for good cause in compelling circumstances and with safeguards the court may allow contemporaneous transmission from another location; an affirmation may replace an oath. A party needing an interpreter under Idaho Court Administrative Rule 52 for itself or a witness must notify the court at least 14 days before the proceeding (","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 44. Judicial notice of facts and foreign law","Idaho · civil rules","The court must take judicial notice as provided by law, and when it notices an adjudicative fact it instructs the jury under Idaho Rule of Evidence 201. A party who wants the court to notice another state’s statutes or laws must submit a memorandum citing the foreign law to the court and opposing counsel at least 14 days before the trial or hearing (the court may deny the request if not submitted), and opposing couns","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoenas","Idaho · civil rules","A subpoena must be substantially in the Appendix B form, issue from the court where the action is pending, and be issued by the clerk on request or signed by an Idaho-licensed attorney. It may be served by anyone at least 18 years old who is not a party, with fees for 1 day’s attendance and mileage tendered if requested (not required for State subpoenas), and a party can be served through its attorney of record for a","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 46. Objecting to a ruling or order","Idaho · civil rules","A formal exception to a ruling or order is unnecessary; when the ruling is requested or made, a party need only state the action it wants the court to take, or objects to, and the grounds. Failing to object does not prejudice a party who had no opportunity to do so when the ruling was made.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 47. Jury procedure","Idaho · civil rules","Juror questionnaire answers are confidential and released only by court order, though the clerk may give copies to attorneys or pro se parties for trial preparation. The rule covers roll call, the oath to the panel, random selection, optional opening statements before voir dire, voir dire (conducted first by the court, then plaintiff, defendant, and other parties), and a struck-jury option. Challenges for cause are u","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 48. Mistrial","Idaho · civil rules","After trial begins and before a verdict, the court may declare a mistrial on its own motion or a party’s motion if an occurrence at trial has prevented a fair trial. If deliberate misconduct of a party or attorney caused the mistrial, the court may require that party, attorney, or both to pay the opposing parties’ reasonable expenses, including attorney fees.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 49. Verdicts","Idaho · civil rules","The court may require a special verdict (written findings on each fact issue, by written questions, forms, or other method), with instructions sufficient for the jury to answer. A party waives a jury on any fact issue not submitted unless it demands submission before the jury retires; if the court makes no finding it is deemed to have found consistently with its judgment. The court may instead use a general verdict w","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 50. Dispositive trial motions","Idaho · civil rules","A motion for a directed verdict must state specific grounds; making one at the close of an opponent’s evidence does not require reserving the right to present evidence, and an unsuccessful motion does not waive a jury even if all parties move. A motion for judgment notwithstanding the verdict must be served within 14 days after entry of judgment (or within 14 days after discharge of the jury if no verdict was returne","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 51. Jury instructions","Idaho · civil rules","Any party may file written requested instructions no later than 7 days before a jury trial begins, and they must be served on and received by all parties at least 7 days before trial; the court need not consider late requests, but may allow requests up to the close of the evidence for matters that could not have been anticipated or were overlooked. An original and a copy are submitted, with the legal citation only on","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings and conclusions by the court","Idaho · civil rules","In an action tried without a jury or with an advisory jury, the court must find the facts specially and state conclusions of law separately, on the record or in an opinion or memorandum, and judgment is entered under Rule 58; findings are also required for granting or refusing an interlocutory injunction. Findings are not required for default judgments or when ruling on show cause hearings, Rule 12 or 56 motions, or ","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters","Idaho · civil rules","A court may appoint a master (including a referee, commissioner, auditor, or examiner), but in a jury trial only if the issues are complicated, and in a nonjury trial only for an accounting or on a showing of an exceptional condition. Any party may move to disqualify a master within 14 days after receiving notice of the appointment, and the master must hold the first meeting within 21 days after the appointing order ","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgments; costs","Idaho · civil rules","A judgment is a separate document titled “Judgment” or “Decree” beginning with the words “JUDGMENT IS ENTERED AS FOLLOWS:” and stating the relief without recitals, reasoning, or findings; it is final only if entered on all claims except costs and fees as to all parties, or if it is a partial judgment certified final under Rule 54(b) on an express finding of no just reason for delay. Costs as a matter of right to the ","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 55. Default; default judgment","Idaho · civil rules","When a party against whom affirmative relief is sought fails to plead or defend, the court orders entry of default, and a party who has appeared must get 3 days’ written notice of the application. Default cannot be entered before the time to appear or defend expires, unless the party waives that time under oath and the court shortens it for good cause. A default judgment for a sum certain may be entered on the claima","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","Idaho · civil rules","The court must grant summary judgment if the movant shows no genuine dispute of material fact and entitlement to judgment as a matter of law. A motion may be filed any time after 21 days from service of process on the adverse party (or that party’s appearance) or after the adverse party serves its own motion, but must be filed at least 90 days before the trial date or within 7 days of the order setting trial, whichev","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments","Idaho · civil rules","The rules govern procedure for declaratory judgments under state statutes, and Rules 38 and 39 govern jury demands; another adequate remedy does not bar a declaratory judgment, and the court may order a speedy hearing. A party seeking a declaratory judgment on insurance policy coverage must give notice of the action to any person known to have a claim against the insured relating to the incident.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 58. Entry of judgment","Idaho · civil rules","Every judgment and amended judgment must be a separate document as required by Rule 54(a). Filing the judgment under Rule 5(d) or the clerk’s filing stamp constitutes entry, the judgment is not effective before entry, and entry must not be delayed for the taxing of costs.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 58.1. Satisfaction of judgment","Idaho · civil rules","On full payment of a judgment, the party in whose favor it was rendered must file a satisfaction of judgment in the court that entered it and record it in every county where the judgment or an abstract of it is recorded. The satisfaction must be signed by that party or the party’s attorney.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 58.2. Certificate of adoption","Idaho · civil rules","The court must not enter a decree of adoption until the adopting parents give the court a completed certificate of adoption on the form furnished by the department of vital statistics.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 59. New trial; altering or amending a judgment","Idaho · civil rules","The court may grant a new trial on all or some issues for listed grounds, including irregularity, jury misconduct, accident or surprise, newly discovered evidence, excessive or inadequate damages given under passion or prejudice, insufficient evidence, and legal error; motions on the first five grounds need a supporting affidavit and those on insufficiency or legal error must state factual grounds with particularity.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 59.1. Additurs or remittiturs instead of new trial","Idaho · civil rules","When a court conditionally grants or denies a new trial subject to an additur or remittitur, the party to whom it is directed has 42 days from entry of the order to accept or reject it, and filing a notice of appeal is neither acceptance nor rejection and defers that decision until the appeal is decided. If the conditional new trial order is affirmed on appeal, that party has 14 days from issuance of the appellate re","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from a judgment or order","Idaho · civil rules","The court may correct clerical mistakes and oversights or omissions in a judgment, order, or record on motion or on its own, with or without notice, but once an appeal is docketed and pending only with the appellate court’s leave. On motion and just terms, the court may relieve a party from a final judgment for mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect; newly discovered evidence that could not have been f","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","Idaho · civil rules","Unless justice requires otherwise, no error in admitting or excluding evidence or any other error by the court or a party is grounds for a new trial, setting aside a verdict, or vacating, modifying, or otherwise disturbing a judgment or order. At every stage the court must disregard errors and defects that do not affect any party’s substantial rights.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","Idaho · civil rules","Unlike the federal rule, Idaho has no automatic stay on entry of judgment: execution and other enforcement may issue immediately unless the court, in its discretion and on conditions securing the adverse party, directs otherwise. Judgments in injunction, writ of mandate, and receivership actions are not stayed after entry or during appeal unless the court orders otherwise, and subdivision (c) governs suspending, modi","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 63. Judge’s inability to proceed","Idaho · civil rules","If the trial judge cannot perform required duties after a verdict is returned or findings of fact and conclusions of law are filed, because of death, sickness, or other disability, any other judge regularly sitting in or assigned to the court where the action was tried may perform them. If that judge is satisfied the trial judge cannot perform the duties, the judge may grant a new trial.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 64. Seizing a person or property","Idaho · civil rules","At the start of and during an action, all remedies for seizing a person or property to secure satisfaction of a potential judgment are available under the circumstances and in the manner provided by law.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions and restraining orders","Idaho · civil rules","A preliminary injunction may issue only on notice to the adverse party, and the court may advance and consolidate the trial on the merits with the hearing while preserving any jury right. A temporary restraining order may issue without notice only if an affidavit or verified complaint clearly shows immediate and irreparable injury before the adverse party can be heard and the movant or attorney certifies in writing e","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 66. Sureties on bond","Idaho · civil rules","A bond or undertaking required by statute or the rules must follow the form and surety justification requirements of chapter 6 of title 12, Idaho Code. An attorney of record, or a member or associate of a firm of record, cannot serve as surety. Each surety submits to the court’s jurisdiction and appoints the court clerk as agent for service, and liability may be enforced on motion without an independent action, with ","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit into court","Idaho · civil rules","When relief sought includes a money judgment or disposition of money or a deliverable thing, a party may, on notice to every other party and with leave of court, deposit all or part of it with the court (whether or not the party claims it) and must deliver a copy of the order permitting the deposit to the clerk. On motion, the court may order a party holding money or a thing that is held as trustee for, or belongs or","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of judgment","Idaho · civil rules","At least 14 days before trial, a party defending against a claim may serve (but not file) an offer to allow judgment on specified terms, deemed to include all costs and fees accrued; if the opposing party serves written acceptance within 14 days after service, either party may file the offer, acceptance, and proof of service, and the clerk enters judgment for the offer amount without costs. An unaccepted offer is wit","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution","Idaho · civil rules","An appealable final judgment (or a Rule 54(b) partial judgment) or court order for payment of money is enforced by writ of execution unless the court directs otherwise. A writ may not issue for more than the judgment’s face amount plus court-approved costs and fees without an affidavit of the party or attorney verifying the computation, and the clerk may rely on that affidavit. After service the sheriff must return t","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 69.1. Claim of exemptions; interrogatories submitted to garnishee; forms","Idaho · civil rules","The forms for notice of exemptions, instructions to debtors and third parties, claim of exemptions or third-party claim, and interrogatories submitted to a garnishee must be in substantially the form found in Appendix B. This rule has no federal counterpart.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 69.2. Deposition in aid of enforcing judgment","Idaho · civil rules","After return of a writ of execution that does not fully satisfy a judgment debt, the judgment creditor may move to depose the judgment debtor and require production of documents and things bearing on ability to pay, and the presiding judge shall order the debtor to appear and produce what the judge considers appropriate. If the debtor resides in the county where the judgment was obtained, the deposition may be held a","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 70. Enforcing a judgment for a specific act","Idaho · civil rules","If a party fails to comply with a judgment to convey land, deliver a deed or document, or perform another specific act within the time specified, the court may order another person appointed by the court to do it at the disobedient party’s expense, with the same effect as if the party had done it. If property is within the district, the court may instead enter a judgment divesting title and vesting it in others, with","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 71. Enforcing relief for or against a nonparty","Idaho · civil rules","When an order grants relief for a nonparty or may be enforced against a nonparty, the procedure for enforcing it is the same as for a party.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 72. Order to show cause","Idaho · civil rules","An application for an order to show cause must be by verified complaint or accompanied by an affidavit stating the facts and grounds. If the application makes a prima facie showing for an order commanding specific acts, forbearance, or payment of money, the court must issue an order to show cause, which must be served on the party or the party’s attorney of record at least 7 days before the hearing in the same manner","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 73. Receivers","Idaho · civil rules","These rules govern an action in which appointment of a receiver is sought or a receiver sues or is sued. Appointment and administration of estates by receivers or similar officers must follow Idaho Code, and an action in which a receiver has been appointed may be dismissed only by court order.","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 74. Actions for writ of mandate or prohibition","Idaho · civil rules","The Rules of Civil Procedure apply to actions for a writ of mandate or prohibition, and the rule defines writ of mandate, writ of prohibition, alternative writ, and peremptory writ. A court may issue an alternative writ on a verified complaint or affidavit showing grounds when the petition asks for one first; copies of the summons, petition, affidavits, and alternative writ must be served on the defendant at least 14","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 75. Contempt","Idaho · civil rules","This rule governs contempt proceedings in or apart from a civil lawsuit, but not contempt charged under Idaho Code section 18-1801 or other criminal statutes, and it defines summary and nonsummary proceedings and civil (conditional) and criminal (unconditional) sanctions. A summary proceeding may be used only for contempt in the court’s presence, after notice (which may be oral) and a brief chance to be heard, follow","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 76. Small Lawsuit Resolution Act procedure","Idaho · civil rules","This Idaho-specific rule applies only when a party initiates the Small Lawsuit Resolution Act (Idaho Code section 7-1501 et seq.), with the Rules of Civil Procedure otherwise applying. The Act’s dollar limit applies separately to each party and excludes costs and attorney fees, and the complaint must state the claim does not exceed the limit. A party starts the process by filing a “Notice of Initiation of Proceedings","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 77. Class actions","Idaho · civil rules","Class suits require numerosity, common questions, typicality, and adequate representation, and must also fit a (b)(1), (b)(2), or (b)(3) category (the latter requiring predominance and superiority). The court must decide certification at an early practicable time and may alter the order before final judgment; (b)(3) classes require the best practicable notice with individual notice to identifiable members, including ","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 78. Derivative actions","Idaho · civil rules","This rule applies when shareholders or members of a corporation or unincorporated association sue to enforce a right the entity failed to enforce, and the action cannot be maintained if the plaintiff does not fairly and adequately represent similarly situated shareholders or members. The complaint must be verified, allege that the plaintiff held the shares or membership when the transaction occurred (or received them","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 79. Action relating to unincorporated associations","Idaho · civil rules","This rule applies to actions brought by or against members of an unincorporated association as a class through named representative parties, which may be maintained only if those parties will fairly and adequately protect the interests of the association and its members. The court may issue orders corresponding to those under Rule 77(d), and settlement, voluntary dismissal, or compromise must follow the procedure in ","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 83. Appeals from decisions of magistrate judges","Idaho · civil rules","Appeals from magistrate court go to the Supreme Court as of right for final judgments on termination of parental rights or adoption petitions, by permission in specified custody, Child Protective Act, and guardianship matters, and when a magistrate is sitting as a district court; other final judgments and listed orders go to the district court. A notice of appeal must be filed with the district court clerk within 42 ","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 83.1. Appeal by permission from interlocutory orders of magistrate judges","Idaho · civil rules","Adopted effective July 1, 2024. Move in the magistrate court within 14 days after entry of the interlocutory order (controlling question of law, substantial ground for difference of opinion, immediate appeal may materially advance the case); the magistrate rules within 14 days after the hearing. Any party may then move the district court within 14 days after that ruling, or if the magistrate has not ruled within 21 d","#st-id-rules"],["Rule 84. Judicial review of agency actions by the district court","Idaho · civil rules","This rule governs district court judicial review of state agency and local government actions when a statute authorizes review and supplies no procedure or standard of its own. The petition must be filed within 28 days after the agency action is ripe for review unless a statute says otherwise (time restarts after a decision on a timely reconsideration motion), cross-petitions are due within 14 days of service or with","#st-id-rules"],["Appeals in Idaho: 42 days, file in the trial court, and the Supreme Court decides who hears it","Idaho · appeals","Every appeal from a district court is an appeal to the Idaho Supreme Court. File the notice of appeal with the district court clerk within 42 days of the clerk’s filing stamp on the judgment. That deadline is jurisdictional and cannot be extended. The Supreme Court then keeps the case or assigns it to the four-judge Court of Appeals. A Court of Appeals decision can be taken to the Supreme Court only by a discretionar","#st-id-appeals"],["All appeals are filed as Supreme Court appeals","Idaho · appeals · Which court","A party aggrieved by an appealable district court judgment or order, or a decision of the Public Utilities Commission or Industrial Commission, appeals to the Supreme Court. The Supreme Court decides which cases to assign to the Court of Appeals. It never assigns original proceedings, capital appeals, Industrial Commission or Public Utilities Commission appeals, or Bar and Judicial Council matters. It ordinarily keep","#st-id-appeals"],["Magistrate decisions: usually to the district judge first","Idaho · appeals · Which court","Final magistrate judgments and listed orders in civil cases are appealed to the district court within 42 days of the file-stamped judgment; a timely motion for JNOV, to amend findings, to alter or amend, or for new trial restarts the time. The district court’s decision can then go to the Supreme Court as of right. Direct appeals from a magistrate to the Supreme Court are limited to final judgments on termination of p","#st-id-appeals"],["Appeal filed in the wrong court can be transferred (new July 1, 2026)","Idaho · appeals · Which court","A timely notice of appeal filed in the wrong court (Supreme Court instead of district court, or the reverse) may be transferred, and it is deemed filed in the receiving court on the original filing date. No new filing fee is due except to cure a deficiency. I.A.R. 11.3","#st-id-appeals"],["Appeals as of right in civil cases","Idaho · appeals · What is appealable","Final judgments under I.R.C.P. 54(a); district court decisions on appeals from magistrates; Rule 54(b)-certified partial judgments; contempt orders; orders granting or refusing a new trial; orders on JNOV motions; orders after final judgment (except an order granting a motion to set aside or vacate the judgment); any order made appealable by statute; and vexatious-litigant designations. Attach a copy of the judgment ","#st-id-appeals"],["The judgment must be a bare separate document","Idaho · appeals · What is appealable","A “judgment” is a separate document titled “Judgment” or “Decree” that begins “JUDGMENT IS ENTERED AS FOLLOWS:” and states the relief without recitals, reasoning, or findings. It is final only if it resolves all claims as to all parties (except costs and fees) or is certified under Rule 54(b). The clerk’s filing stamp is entry. I.R.C.P. 54(a)-(b), 58; I.A.R. 11(a)(1), (3)","#st-id-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP denials: appeal as of right by statute","Idaho · appeals · What is appealable","Under the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (claims filed or asserted on or after January 1, 2026), a moving party may appeal as of right from an order denying the special motion in whole or part, within 42 days after entry of the order. Proceedings between all parties are stayed during the appeal. The Appellate Rules do not mention the Act; rely on I.A.R. 11(a)(8) and cite § 6-3909 in the notice’s jurisdictio","#st-id-appeals"],["Notice of appeal: 42 days from the file stamp, filed in the trial court","Idaho · appeals · Starting the appeal","File the notice with the clerk of the district court within 42 days from the clerk’s filing stamp on the judgment or order. Electronic filing under the Idaho Rules for Electronic Filing and Service counts as physical filing, but the notice is not filed until received; mailing is not enough. Late filing is jurisdictional, causes automatic dismissal, and cannot be cured by extension. A premature notice becomes valid wh","#st-id-appeals"],["Contents, fees, and transcript designation","Idaho · appeals · Starting the appeal","The notice must include the parties and counsel (with email addresses), the judgment appealed (copy attached), a preliminary statement of issues, a jurisdictional statement, designations of the transcript and any added record items and exhibits, and certifications of service on the reporter and payment of the estimated transcript, record, and filing fees. The notice brings up earlier interlocutory orders. Filing fee:","#st-id-appeals"],["Cross-appeal","Idaho · appeals · Starting the appeal","File a notice of cross-appeal in the district court within the 42-day period for the judgment, or within 21 days after the original notice of appeal was filed, whichever is later. It is also jurisdictional. A respondent seeking no affirmative relief may raise additional issues in its brief without cross-appealing. I.A.R. 11(g), 15, 21, 35(b)(4)","#st-id-appeals"],["Record and transcript","Idaho · appeals · Starting the appeal","The clerk’s record is due within 28 days after the notice is filed. A transcript estimated under 100 pages is due 30 days after the reporter is notified, and 100 to 500 pages in 63 days. Respondents may request additions within 14 days. After service of the record and transcript, parties have 28 days to object, or they are settled. The clerk then files them with the Supreme Court within 7 days, and briefing runs from","#st-id-appeals"],["What restarts the clock","Idaho · appeals · Tolling and post-judgment motions","A timely motion that, if granted, could affect any findings, conclusions, or the judgment terminates the appeal period; a new 42 days runs from the file stamp on the order deciding it. Rule 60 motions and motions about costs or attorney fees do not. I.A.R. 14(a)","#st-id-appeals"],["Post-judgment motions are due in 14 days","Idaho · appeals · Tolling and post-judgment motions","JNOV, amended findings, new trial, and motions to alter or amend are each due within 14 days after entry of judgment. A motion to reconsider an interlocutory order may be filed any time before, or within 14 days after, final judgment. The trial court cannot extend these times. A Rule 60(b) motion for mistake, new evidence, or fraud has a 6-month outer limit. I.R.C.P. 2.2(b)(3), 11.2(b), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), 60(c","#st-id-appeals"],["Trial court keeps limited jurisdiction during the appeal","Idaho · appeals · Tolling and post-judgment motions","While the appeal is pending, the district court may still decide post-trial and Rule 60 motions, tax trial costs and fees, set stays and bonds, issue Rule 54(b) certificates, and settle the transcript. I.A.R. 13(b)","#st-id-appeals"],["Only a 14-day automatic stay","Idaho · appeals · Stays","Idaho has no automatic stay on entry of judgment; enforcement may issue at once unless the court orders otherwise. Filing a civil notice of appeal or cross-appeal stays proceedings and execution for 14 days (not civil protection orders). Any further stay requires an order of the district court or Supreme Court, and you must apply to the district court first. I.R.C.P. 62(a); I.A.R. 13(a), 13(g)","#st-id-appeals"],["Money judgments: judgment plus 36%","Idaho · appeals · Stays","A money judgment is stayed by a cash deposit or surety bond equal to the judgment plus 36%, or by a written agreement not to execute. Objections to bond sufficiency are waived unless made by motion within 21 days. The bond requirement is waived for punitive damages above $1,000,000 and may be waived for good cause. Other stays require a likelihood of success and of irreparable harm. I.A.R. 13(b)(8), (14), (15), 13(g)","#st-id-appeals"],["Due dates","Idaho · appeals · Briefing","Appellant: 35 days after the transcript and record are filed with the Supreme Court. Respondent (and cross-appellant, in a joint brief): 28 days after service of the appellant’s brief. Cross-respondent: 28 days after the cross-appellant’s brief. Reply: 21 days after service of the respondent’s brief. The Clerk notifies counsel of the due dates. Extensions are disfavored and must be sought by the due date with an affi","#st-id-appeals"],["Word limits and format","Idaho · appeals · Briefing","First brief: 14,000 words; reply: 7,000 words (headings, footnotes, and quotations count; tables, certificates, and appendices do not). A certificate stating the word count goes immediately before the signature. Briefs must be 14-point or larger Times New Roman or Century Schoolbook, double-spaced with 1-inch margins and bottom page numbers. Record citations go in the text, not footnotes. File only the original, elec","#st-id-appeals"],["Fees on appeal must be claimed in your first brief","Idaho · appeals · Briefing","A party seeking attorney fees on appeal must list the claim and its legal basis among the issues in its first brief, or it is waived unless the Court allows a later claim. If fees are awarded, file the claim with the memorandum of costs within 14 days after the opinion; a late costs memorandum waives costs. I.A.R. 35(a)(5), (b)(5), 40(c), 41(a), (d)","#st-id-appeals"],["Oral argument and motions","Idaho · appeals · Briefing","Oral argument is held in all appeals unless waived by stipulation with Court approval, or unless the Court orders submission on the briefs; an objection to that order is due within 21 days. Each side gets 30 minutes. Responses to motions are due within 14 days. A motion to dismiss for rule violations must be filed at least 21 days before argument; a motion to dismiss for a late notice of appeal may be filed at any ti","#st-id-appeals"],["Rehearing: 21 days, brief 14 days later","Idaho · appeals · Decision, rehearing, and Supreme Court review","A petition for rehearing must be physically filed with the Clerk of the Supreme Court within 21 days after the opinion is filed. The filing fee is $71. The supporting brief follows within 14 days after the petition. The deadline is jurisdictional and cannot be extended. The same rule applies to Court of Appeals decisions. I.A.R. 21, 23(a)(7), 42(a)-(b), 46, 116","#st-id-appeals"],["Petition for review of a Court of Appeals decision","Idaho · appeals · Decision, rehearing, and Supreme Court review","File within 21 days after the Court of Appeals opinion or order, after an order denying rehearing, or after an opinion on rehearing. A supporting brief limited to the review criteria is due with the petition or within 14 days. There is no fee. Review is discretionary and needs a majority of the justices, based on factors like an unsettled question, conflict with precedent, or a departure from usual procedure. The Cou","#st-id-appeals"],["Finality and remittitur","Idaho · appeals · Decision, rehearing, and Supreme Court review","Opinions become final 21 days after the latest announcement of the opinion, an opinion on rehearing, or a modified opinion; the Clerk then issues the remittitur. A surety must pay within 30 days after the remittitur is filed. I.A.R. 38, 122, 13(b)(15)","#st-id-appeals"],["Permissive appeal (I.A.R. 12)","Idaho · appeals · Interlocutory appeals","For an interlocutory district court order involving a controlling question of law with substantial grounds for difference of opinion, where an immediate appeal may materially advance the litigation: move in the district court within 14 days after entry of the order. The court rules within 14 days after the hearing. Any party may then move in the Supreme Court within 14 days after that ruling, or if the court has not ","#st-id-appeals"],["Magistrate interlocutory orders go to the district court","Idaho · appeals · Interlocutory appeals","A parallel procedure (14 days to the magistrate, then 14 days to the district court) applies to interlocutory magistrate orders, and a district court denial is final. I.R.C.P. 83.1","#st-id-appeals"],["Electronic filing","Idaho · appeals · Filing mechanics and out-of-state counsel","Briefs, original petitions, motions, and memoranda of costs must be filed electronically with the Supreme Court. Transcripts and records are electronic. Counsel must provide an email address for service and keep a current email address on file with the Idaho State Bar. I.R.E.F.S. 20, 21; I.A.R. 47(d)","#st-id-appeals"],["Out-of-state lawyers","Idaho · appeals · Filing mechanics and out-of-state counsel","A licensed Idaho attorney must move at least 14 days before argument for an out-of-state lawyer to argue, substantially in the I.B.C.R. 227(j) form. This is unnecessary if the lawyer was already admitted pro hac vice in the case. Every appellate paper must be signed by at least one licensed Idaho attorney of record. I.A.R. 9, 11.2(a); I.B.C.R. 227","#st-id-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (civil, as of right)","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","42 days from clerk’s filing stamp on the judgment or order.\nJurisdictional; filed in the district court; no extension (I.A.R. 46). Restarts after a timely motion that could affect findings or the judgment (not Rule 60 or costs/fees motions). I.A.R. 14(a), 21","#st-id-appeals"],["Notice of cross-appeal","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","42 days from filing stamp on the judgment, or 21 days after the notice of appeal, whichever is later.\nJurisdictional. I.A.R. 15(b), 21","#st-id-appeals"],["Notice of appeal, order denying anti-SLAPP (UPEPA) motion","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","42 days from entry of the order denying the § 6-3903 motion in whole or part.\nAppeal of right for the moving party; all proceedings stayed during the appeal (§ 6-3904(3)). Idaho Code § 6-3909; I.A.R. 11(a)(8)","#st-id-appeals"],["Appeal from magistrate to district court","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","42 days from file-stamped magistrate judgment or order.\nJurisdictional; cross-appeal within 14 days after service of a notice filed more than 28 days after entry. I.R.C.P. 83","#st-id-appeals"],["Motion for new trial, to alter or amend, to amend findings, or JNOV","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended (I.R.C.P. 2.2(b)(3)). I.R.C.P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)","#st-id-appeals"],["Motion for permission to appeal (district court)","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of the interlocutory order.\nThen 14 days from the ruling (or after 21 days with no ruling) to move in the Supreme Court (I.A.R. 12(c)). I.A.R. 12(b)","#st-id-appeals"],["Automatic stay after notice of appeal","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the notice of appeal or cross-appeal.\nFurther stays only by court order; apply to district court first. I.A.R. 13(a)","#st-id-appeals"],["Objection to supersedeas bond","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from filing of the bond or cash deposit.\nWaived if not made by motion in time; bond is judgment plus 36%. I.A.R. 13(b)(15)","#st-id-appeals"],["Respondent’s request for added transcript or record","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the notice of appeal (or amended notice). I.A.R. 19","#st-id-appeals"],["Objections to transcript and record","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from service of the transcript and record.\nSettled if no objection. I.A.R. 29(a)","#st-id-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","35 days from filing of the transcript and record with the Supreme Court.\n14,000 words. I.A.R. 34(c)","#st-id-appeals"],["Respondent’s brief","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from service of appellant’s brief.\nCombined with any cross-appellant’s brief. I.A.R. 34(c)","#st-id-appeals"],["Reply brief","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from service of respondent’s brief.\n7,000 words. I.A.R. 34(c)","#st-id-appeals"],["Response to a motion","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the motion. I.A.R. 32(d)","#st-id-appeals"],["Out-of-state counsel motion to argue","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from before the hearing or argument (at least).\nNot needed if already admitted pro hac vice in the case. I.A.R. 9","#st-id-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from filing of the opinion.\nJurisdictional; $71 fee; supporting brief within 14 days after the petition. I.A.R. 42(a)","#st-id-appeals"],["Petition for review (Court of Appeals decision)","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from announcement of the opinion, order denying rehearing, or opinion on rehearing.\nBrief with the petition or within 14 days; no fee. I.A.R. 118(a)","#st-id-appeals"],["Memorandum of costs and attorney fee claim on appeal","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing and announcement of the opinion.\nLate memorandum waives costs; objections within 14 days of service. I.A.R. 40(c), 41(d)","#st-id-appeals"],["Opinion final; remittitur issues","Idaho · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from last announcement of the opinion (or opinion on rehearing or modified opinion). I.A.R. 38(b)","#st-id-appeals"],["Supreme Court of Illinois","Illinois · courts","Court of last resort. Writes the Supreme Court Rules, which govern civil and appellate procedure alongside the Code of Civil Procedure, and controls bar admission and lawyer discipline (through the Attorney Registration and Disciplinary Commission). Hears most cases by discretionary petition for leave to appeal (Rule 315), and takes direct appeals when a circuit court holds a statute invalid (Rule 302(a)).","#st-il"],["Appellate Court of Illinois (five districts)","Illinois · courts","Five districts sitting in Chicago (First), Elgin (Second), Ottawa (Third), Springfield (Fourth), and Mount Vernon (Fifth), deciding in three-judge panels. A five-judge Workers’ Compensation Commission division reviews those cases. Hears appeals of right from final circuit court judgments and interlocutory appeals under Rules 306–308 (Rules 22, 301).","#st-il"],["Circuit Courts (judicial circuits)","Illinois · courts","Single, unified general-jurisdiction trial court organized by judicial circuits and counties, staffed by circuit judges and associate judges (Rule 295). Cases are designated by division in the caption, e.g., ‘at law,’ ‘in chancery,’ ‘small claim’ (Rule 132).","#st-il"],["No separate limited-jurisdiction courts","Illinois · courts","Small claims (money claims up to $10,000) are a track within the circuit court with relaxed procedure (Rules 281–288). Circuits approved by the Supreme Court run court-annexed mandatory arbitration for money claims under a limit set for each circuit (Rules 86–95).","#st-il"],["Statute and scope: Citizen Participation Act (amended 2025)","Illinois · anti-SLAPP","Illinois’s anti-SLAPP law is the Citizen Participation Act, 735 ILCS 110. It applies to any motion to dispose of a claim that is based on, relates to, or responds to the movant’s acts in furtherance of petition, speech, association, or participation in government, including freedom of the press. ‘Motion’ covers motions to dismiss, for summary judgment, or to strike, and ‘claim’ includes counterclaims and cross-claims","#st-il-slapp"],["Procedure: 90-day hearing, automatic stay, discovery suspended","Illinois · anti-SLAPP","A hearing and decision must occur within 90 days after notice of the motion is given to the respondent. Discovery is suspended pending decision, except discovery the court allows for good cause on whether the movant’s acts are immunized or in furtherance of immunized acts.\nFor actions commenced on or after Jan. 1, 2026, filing the motion stays all other proceedings between the moving and responding parties, including","#st-il-slapp"],["Standard: plaintiff must show clear and convincing evidence","Illinois · anti-SLAPP","The court must grant the motion and dismiss unless the responding party produces clear and convincing evidence that the movant’s acts are not immunized, or are not in furtherance of immunized acts. The burden sits on the plaintiff (the responding party) at a heightened standard. 735 ILCS 110/20(c)","#st-il-slapp"],["Fees: mandatory for a winning movant","Illinois · anti-SLAPP","The court must award a prevailing movant reasonable attorney’s fees and costs incurred in connection with the motion. A prevailing respondent gets fees only if the court finds the motion frivolous or filed solely to delay. 735 ILCS 110/25","#st-il-slapp"],["Appeal: denial reviewable by petition within 30 days","Illinois · anti-SLAPP","An order denying a motion to dispose under the Act may be reviewed by petition for leave to appeal under Supreme Court Rule 306(a)(9), filed with a supporting record within 30 days. The appellate court must expedite any appeal from a denial, or from the trial court’s failure to rule within 90 days. Once a party appeals a ruling on the motion, all proceedings between all parties are stayed until the appeal ends (actio","#st-il-slapp"],["Rule 707: out-of-state lawyers appear by filing a Statement, not by motion","Illinois · out of state","An eligible out-of-state attorney may appear in an Illinois court case, court-annexed ADR, or an Illinois agency proceeding without any court order. File a verified Statement, and an active Illinois attorney must file an appearance in association.\nServe the Statement on the ARDC Administrator, Illinois co-counsel, the client, and all parties. It must list offices and contact details; parties represented; every Rule 7","#st-il-oos"],["Fee: $250 per proceeding, plus annual ARDC registration","Illinois · out of state","Pay a nonrefundable $250 per proceeding to the ARDC Administrator when serving the Statement. Exempt: lawyers appointed for indigent parties, nonprofit legal-services lawyers in civil cases for program clients, Rule 718 disaster-relief lawyers, and U.S. Department of Justice lawyers representing the United States.\nThe lawyer must also register with the ARDC within 30 days of filing the Statement, if not already regis","#st-il-oos"],["Volume limit: no more than five other proceedings a year","Illinois · out of state","Eligibility is lost if the attorney has already appeared in more than five other proceedings under Rule 707 in the calendar year the Statement is filed. An appeal or review of a proceeding before a different tribunal counts as a separate proceeding and needs its own Statement and fee. R. 707(b)(2), (c)","#st-il-oos"],["Local counsel must actively participate; permission can be terminated","Illinois · out of state","The Illinois lawyer must stay of record and actively participate (Rule 5.5(c)(1)). The Supreme Court, the chief judge, or the trial court may terminate permission for lost qualifications, misconduct, inaccurate Statements, unpaid fees, failure to respond to the Administrator, or the absence of active local counsel.\nNew information about admissions, Illinois presence, or local counsel, plus any discipline or convictio","#st-il-oos"],["Temporary practice without a proceeding: Rule 5.5","Illinois · out of state","A lawyer admitted elsewhere in the U.S. and not suspended may provide temporary services in Illinois in association with an actively participating Illinois lawyer; for a pending or expected proceeding; in ADR reasonably related to home-state practice when the forum does not require pro hac vice; or for other matters arising from home-state practice. Establishing a systematic and continuous presence or holding out as ","#st-il-oos"],["Admission on motion and other limited admissions","Illinois · out of state","Admission on motion (Rule 705) requires at least 3 years’ licensure and active, continuous, lawful practice for 3 of the 5 years before applying. That means at least 80 hours a month and 1,000 hours a year during 36 of the last 60 months. Lawyers licensed fewer than 15 years must have passed the MPRE, and character-and-fitness certification and good standing everywhere are required.\nOther routes include a transferred","#st-il-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances are expressly allowed","Illinois · out of state","Under a written limited-scope agreement (Rule 1.2(c)), a lawyer may file a statewide-form Notice of Limited Scope Appearance for specific aspects of a civil case, and may file more than one. When done, the lawyer withdraws by filing a Notice of Completion, in open court or by serving it with an Objection form. If the client objects within 21 days, withdrawal is denied only on clear and convincing proof the work is in","#st-il-oos"],["E-filing is mandatory statewide; e-mail service is the default","Illinois · out of state","All civil documents in all courts must be e-filed through a Supreme Court-approved system unless exempt (original wills cannot be e-filed). A filing is timely if submitted before midnight in the court’s time zone on the due date. If the clerk rejects a filing, or the system fails, move within 5 court days to have the corrected document deemed filed on the original date.\nAttorneys must designate a primary e-mail addre","#st-il-oos"],["Attorney-issued subpoenas need an Illinois-admitted lawyer","Illinois · out of state","Deposition subpoenas may be issued by the clerk or by ‘an attorney admitted to practice in the State of Illinois who is currently counsel of record.’ The rule does not say whether a Rule 707 lawyer qualifies, so have Illinois co-counsel or the clerk issue them. Subpoenas for out-of-state cases go through Rule 17 (Uniform Interstate Depositions and Discovery Act). R. 204(a)(1), (b)(2)","#st-il-oos"],["Governing code: Illinois Rules of Professional Conduct of 2010","Illinois · ethics","Article VIII of the Supreme Court Rules, adopted July 1, 2009, effective January 1, 2010, and amended many times since (e.g., Rule 1.6 in 2025, Rule 8.4 in 2024). It follows the ABA Model Rules numbering with the Illinois differences below. Discipline is handled by the ARDC under Rules 751–780. Ill. S. Ct. Rules, art. VIII; R. 751","#st-il-ethics"],["Confidentiality: disclosure to prevent death or serious harm is mandatory","Illinois · ethics","Rule 1.6(c) requires (not just permits) disclosure to the extent reasonably necessary to prevent reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm. Rule 1.6(b)(1) also permits disclosure to prevent the client from committing any crime, broader than the ABA’s permissive exceptions. Information shared in approved lawyers’ assistance, intermediary, or lawyer-connection (ICS) programs is treated as confidential client ","#st-il-ethics"],["Fees: nonrefundable retainers banned; detailed retainer types","Illinois · ethics","Nonrefundable fees and retainers are prohibited, as are agreements restricting the right to fire the lawyer or to get a refund. Rule 1.5(d) defines fixed fees (not deposited in trust), engagement retainers, security retainers (client property; must go in trust), and special purpose retainers (writing required, with five listed disclosures and an offer of a security retainer).\nFee splitting with another firm is allowe","#st-il-ethics"],["Duty to report is narrower, plus self-reporting of outside discipline","Illinois · ethics","A lawyer must report another lawyer only for known criminal acts reflecting on fitness (8.4(b)) or dishonesty, fraud, deceit, or misrepresentation (8.4(c)), not for every violation raising a substantial question of fitness. A lawyer disciplined, or transferred to disability inactive status, by any body other than the Illinois ARDC must report it to the ARDC. Ill. R. Prof’l Conduct 8.3(a), (d)","#st-il-ethics"],["Misconduct: extra Illinois prohibitions","Illinois · ethics","Illinois adds these items to Rule 8.4:\n• threatening criminal or disciplinary charges to gain an advantage in a civil matter (8.4(g));\n• agreements limiting a client’s right to complain to the ARDC (8.4(h));\n• bad-faith avoidance of government-guaranteed student loans (8.4(i));\n• gifts to judges beyond what the Code of Judicial Conduct allows, and judicial campaign contributions other than by check to a political com","#st-il-ethics"],["No Rule 6.1 pro bono rule; lateral screening","Illinois · ethics","Illinois has no counterpart to ABA Model Rule 6.1. The Preamble says pro bono is a responsibility but not a disciplinary standard. Lateral-hire conflicts under Rule 1.9 can be cured by timely screening and no fee share (1.10(e)); the Illinois text has no written-notice-to-former-client requirement. Ill. R. Prof’l Conduct, Preamble cmt. [6B]; R. 1.10(e)","#st-il-ethics"],["Requests to admit: 28 days, sworn, or the facts are deemed true","Illinois · land mine","Rule 216 requests are admitted unless within 28 days you serve a sworn denial (or detailed reasons) or written objections. An unsworn lawyer letter does not count. The request must be a separate document with the boldface warning; up to 30 requests, subparts counted. Ill. S. Ct. R. 216(c), (f), (g)","#st-il-unique"],["Filing anything before a 2-301 motion waives personal jurisdiction","Illinois · land mine","An objection to personal jurisdiction must come before any other pleading or motion; filing something else first waives it prospectively. The only exceptions are an extension motion, or a 2-1301/2-1401 motion (then object within 60 days of its disposition, or combine the two). Losing the objection and then participating also waives error unless the ground is non-amenability to process. 735 ILCS 5/2-301(a), (a-6), (c)","#st-il-unique"],["Substitution of judge as of right disappears after a substantial ruling","Illinois · land mine","You get one no-cause substitution, but only if you move before trial or hearing begins and before the judge rules on any substantial issue. Decide on day one. 735 ILCS 5/2-1001(a)(2)(ii)","#st-il-unique"],["Partial judgments are not appealable without a 304(a) finding","Illinois · land mine","An order resolving fewer than all claims or parties is neither appealable nor enforceable without an express written finding of ‘no just reason for delaying either enforcement or appeal or both.’ With the finding, the 30-day clock starts on the finding date, so waiting can forfeit the appeal. A pending Rule 137 claim also blocks finality. Ill. S. Ct. R. 304(a); R. 303(a)(1)","#st-il-unique"],["Only one postjudgment motion tolls; reconsideration of the ruling does not","Illinois · land mine","Each party gets one postjudgment motion per final judgment within 30 days. A motion to reconsider the denial of a postjudgment motion does not extend the 30 days to appeal. Ill. S. Ct. R. 274; R. 303(a)(2); 735 ILCS 5/2-1202(c), 2-1203(a)","#st-il-unique"],["Witness disclosure limits trial testimony","Illinois · land mine","On direct examination a witness may testify only to what was disclosed in Rule 213(f) answers or in a discovery deposition. Controlled experts need full opinions, bases, qualifications, and reports disclosed, and evidence-deposition material not previously disclosed is excluded on objection. Ill. S. Ct. R. 213(f), (g)","#st-il-unique"],["Discovery vs. evidence depositions","Illinois · land mine","Unless the notice says ‘evidence deposition,’ it is a discovery deposition only, usable at trial essentially for impeachment, admissions, or affidavit purposes. Discovery depositions are capped at 3 hours, and nonparty treating physicians cannot be deposed without agreement plus consent or a court-ordered subpoena. Ill. S. Ct. R. 202, 206(d), 212(a), 204(c)","#st-il-unique"],["Jury demand timing","Illinois · land mine","A plaintiff must file a jury demand when the action is commenced, and a defendant no later than filing the answer, or the jury is waived. Small claims demands are due at filing (plaintiff) or by the appearance date (defendant). 735 ILCS 5/2-1105(a); Ill. S. Ct. R. 285","#st-il-unique"],["Silence is admission in an Illinois answer","Illinois · land mine","Every allegation except damages that is not explicitly denied is admitted. Pleading insufficient knowledge requires an affidavit. ‘Neither admits nor denies’ answers are risky. 735 ILCS 5/2-610(b)","#st-il-unique"],["Serve diligently, even within the limitations period","Illinois · land mine","A defendant may be dismissed for lack of reasonable diligence in service; if the lack of diligence occurs after limitations runs, the dismissal is with prejudice. Courts weigh diligence in earlier dismissed or refiled cases too. Ill. S. Ct. R. 103(b)","#st-il-unique"],["Combined motions must be split into labeled parts","Illinois · land mine","Combining 2-615, 2-619, and 2-1005 arguments in one undifferentiated motion violates 2-619.1. Each part must name its section and grounds, and 2-619/2-1005 affidavits must meet Rule 191(a). 735 ILCS 5/2-619.1; Ill. S. Ct. R. 191(a)","#st-il-unique"],["Rule 707 limits: five proceedings a year, and the appeal is a new one","Illinois · land mine","An out-of-state lawyer becomes ineligible after more than five other Rule 707 appearances in a calendar year. Each appeal or agency review counts as a separate proceeding requiring a new Statement and a $250 fee. Trial counsel’s appearance ends when the notice of appeal is filed. Ill. S. Ct. R. 707(b)(2), (c), (f); R. 13(c)(8)","#st-il-unique"],["One substitution of judge as of right, but only before a substantial ruling","Illinois · judges","Each party gets one substitution of judge without cause, by motion. It must be granted if presented before trial or hearing begins and before the judge has ruled on any substantial issue (or by consent).\nA party who had not yet appeared (and was not in default) is not barred by rulings made before its appearance. Move at the first opportunity: once the judge rules on something substantial, the right is gone. 735 ILCS","#st-il-unique"],["Substitution for cause: verified petition heard by another judge","Illinois · judges","There is no limit on for-cause petitions. The petition must state the specific cause and be verified by the applicant’s affidavit, and a different judge hears it as soon as possible. The challenged judge may submit an affidavit but need not testify. If allowed, the case is reassigned; if denied, it returns to the same judge.\nA judge who is a party, interested, a material witness, related to a party, or former counsel","#st-il-unique"],["Judicial disqualification under the 2023 Code of Judicial Conduct","Illinois · judges","Separately, a judge must disqualify whenever impartiality might reasonably be questioned. Examples include personal bias or knowledge of disputed facts; a spouse, domestic partner, or relative within the third degree as party, lawyer, interested person, or likely material witness; or a family economic interest in the matter. Ill. Code of Judicial Conduct of 2023, Rule 2.11(A) (Ill. S. Ct. Rules, art. XI)","#st-il-unique"],["Small claims: substitution motion allowed without leave","Illinois · judges","In small claims cases most motions need leave of court, but motions under 2-619 and 2-1001 (substitution of judge) may be filed without it. Ill. S. Ct. R. 287(b)","#st-il-unique"],["Ill. Const. art. I, § 4: not established here","Illinois · speech","The Illinois Constitution has its own free speech and press guarantee, but the collected texts do not include the Constitution or case law. Whether Illinois courts read it more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked. Ill. Const. 1970, art. I, § 4 (not in collected texts)","#st-il-unique"],["Statutory protection: Citizen Participation Act covers the press","Illinois · speech","The amended Citizen Participation Act expressly protects the press when it reports, opines, or investigates on matters of public concern. It also calls for the ‘utmost protection’ of petition, speech, association, and government participation, and says the Act should be construed broadly. 735 ILCS 110/5, 110/15","#st-il-unique"],["Key Code of Civil Procedure motions: 2-615, 2-619, 2-619.1","Illinois · unique rules","Section 2-615 motions attack legal sufficiency on the face of the pleading and must specify the defects; they also cover motions to strike, make more definite, and for judgment on the pleadings. Section 2-619 motions, filed within the time to plead, raise listed defenses outside the pleading (lack of subject-matter jurisdiction, capacity, another pending action, prior judgment, limitations, release, statute of frauds","#st-il-unique"],["Pleadings: specific denials, attach the instrument","Illinois · unique rules","Every allegation not explicitly denied is admitted, except damages, unless the answer claims lack of knowledge supported by an affidavit. A claim or defense founded on a written instrument must attach it (or recite it) unless an affidavit shows it is not accessible. 735 ILCS 5/2-610(b), 2-606","#st-il-unique"],["Postjudgment motions and relief from judgments","Illinois · unique rules","Post-trial motions in jury cases (single motion) and motions after judgment in nonjury cases are due within 30 days of judgment and stay enforcement. A 2-1203 motion does not stay an injunction or declaratory judgment without a separate order. After 30 days, relief requires a 2-1401 petition, generally within 2 years. 735 ILCS 5/2-1202, 2-1203, 2-1401","#st-il-unique"],["Rule 23 orders can be cited (persuasively) since 2021","Illinois · unique rules","Nonprecedential Appellate Court Rule 23(b) orders entered on or after January 1, 2021 may be cited for persuasive purposes; older ones and summary orders may be cited only for res judicata, collateral estoppel, double jeopardy, or law of the case. Ill. S. Ct. R. 23(e)(1)","#st-il-unique"],["Cases up to $50,000: affidavit, mandatory disclosures, limited discovery","Illinois · unique rules","Every money-damages complaint must attach an affidavit stating whether damages exceed $50,000. If not, Rule 222 applies: written initial disclosures (within 120 days after the responsive pleading absent a local rule) with exclusion of undisclosed evidence, 30 interrogatories, 3-hour party/expert depositions, and a $50,000 cap on any judgment. Ill. S. Ct. R. 222(a)–(g)","#st-il-unique"],["Remote appearances are built into the rules","Illinois · unique rules","Remote appearance by phone or video is generally equivalent to in-person under Rule 45. Remote testimony needs good cause and safeguards (Rule 241), and remote depositions are allowed on notice (Rule 206(h)). Ill. S. Ct. R. 45(a), 241, 206(h)","#st-il-unique"],["Rule 101. Summons – form and issuance","Illinois · civil rules","Summons issues under the court’s seal and must carry the statewide notice telling defendants to file an Appearance and Answer/Response and how to e-file. In money cases up to $50,000 (or arbitration cases with a local fixed date) the summons sets a return day 40–61 days after issuance; in eviction and replevin of personal property 7–40 days; when serving under 735 ILCS 5/2-208, 40–60 days with no default until 30 day","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 102. Service of summons and complaint; return","Illinois · civil rules","A 30-day summons cannot be served more than 30 days after its date; a return-day summons must be served at least 21 days (money cases) or 3 days (eviction/2-208 cases) before the appearance date. On a 735 ILCS 5/2-203.1 motion the court may order service by social media direct message, e-mail, or text if the defendant can receive it, with a supporting affidavit, prescribed message wording, a follow-up mailing within ","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 102.1. Service in protective-order proceedings","Illinois · civil rules","A respondent who appears remotely in a protective-order case is personally served, and is ‘present’ when the order issues, if the order’s operative terms are read in open court; the clerk records the service and sends a copy.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 103. Alias summons; dismissal for lack of diligence","Illinois · civil rules","The clerk issues alias summonses on request. If the plaintiff fails to use reasonable diligence to serve a defendant before limitations runs, the action may be dismissed without prejudice; if the lack of diligence occurs after limitations has run, the dismissal is with prejudice as to that defendant (but does not bar vicarious-liability claims against others). The court looks at the totality, including diligence in a","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 104. Service of pleadings and papers; filing","Illinois · civil rules","Every summons must have the complaint attached. Later pleadings, motions, and documents are filed with a certificate or proof of service on all parties who have appeared and are not in default. Failure to serve does not affect jurisdiction; the offending party pays the cost of the aggrieved party getting the document from the clerk.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 105. New or additional relief against defaulted parties – notice","Illinois · civil rules","A party in default must get notice, with the new pleading attached, of any new or additional relief sought, warning that a default may be taken unless the party appears within 30 days after service, receipt of certified mail, or first publication. Notice may be served by any summons method, by certified or registered mail (restricted delivery for individuals, complete on receipt), or by publication where publication ","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 106. Notice of petitions under 2-1401, 2-1601, and 12-183(g)","Illinois · civil rules","Petitions for relief from or revival of judgments are noticed by the Rule 105 methods, not by ordinary service on counsel.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 107. Notice of hearing for an order of replevin","Illinois · civil rules","Notice of a replevin hearing follows the Article II form and must be served at least 5 days before the hearing, by summons methods or by Rule 284 certified mail.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 108. Notice to heirs and legatees on admission or denial of a will","Illinois · civil rules","Notices under section 6-10 of the Probate Act must include the prescribed explanation-of-rights forms (Forms 1–4) when a will, including a foreign will proved by copy, is admitted or denied.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 109. Reserved","Illinois · civil rules","Reserved; no rule text.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 110. Explanation of rights in independent administration","Illinois · civil rules","Notices under the Probate Act when independent administration is granted must enclose the prescribed explanation of rights and petition-to-terminate form.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 113. Mortgage foreclosure practice","Illinois · civil rules","For foreclosures filed on or after May 1, 2013, the note with all indorsements and allonges must be attached to the complaint, and any default or foreclosure-judgment motion needs a detailed prove-up affidavit (affiant’s basis, records reviewed, software used). Plaintiff’s counsel prepares a notice of default within 2 business days after default for the clerk to mail; notice of sale goes out at least 10 business days","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 114. Loss mitigation affidavit","Illinois · civil rules","Where a mortgagor has appeared, the plaintiff must comply with any applicable loss-mitigation program and file an affidavit describing it before or when moving for a foreclosure judgment; the court may stay or deny judgment for noncompliance.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 131. Form of documents","Illinois · civil rules","Documents must be legible and captioned in the court and cause, plaintiff first. Attorneys must show name, business address, telephone, and e-mail, designating one primary and up to two secondary e-mail addresses; self-represented litigants with e-mail must designate one address for Rule 11 service.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 132. Designation of cases","Illinois · civil rules","The initiating document’s caption must state the division (‘at law,’ ‘in chancery,’ ‘small claim,’ etc.); a wrong designation does not affect jurisdiction.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 133. Pleading statutory duty, judgments, conditions precedent","Illinois · civil rules","Cite the statute when alleging breach of a statutory duty; a judgment may be pleaded generally by date and nature; performance of conditions precedent may be alleged generally, but a denial must state the facts of nonperformance.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 134. Incorporation by reference","Illinois · civil rules","Facts adequately stated once may be incorporated by reference elsewhere in the pleading or in other pleadings.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 135. Pleading equitable matters","Illinois · civil rules","Equitable matters may be pleaded as a single equitable cause of action without counts; when legal and equitable actions are joined and treated as separate, plead them in counts marked ‘separate action at law’ and ‘separate action in chancery.’","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 136. Denials","Illinois · civil rules","A pleader may deny all allegations of a paragraph (or all not specifically admitted) without restating them. Unless ordered, no response to a reply is required and new matter in a reply is deemed denied.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 137. Signing of pleadings; sanctions","Illinois · civil rules","An attorney’s signature certifies reasonable inquiry, legal warrant, and proper purpose, as in Fed. R. Civ. P. 11, and pleadings need not be verified unless a rule or statute says so. Unlike Rule 11 there is no 21-day safe harbor in the text; a sanctions motion must be filed within 30 days after final judgment (or after ruling on a timely post-judgment motion) in the same case, and the judge must explain any sanction","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 138. Personal identity information","Illinois · civil rules","Do not file full Social Security or taxpayer numbers, driver’s license, financial account, or debit/credit card numbers; only the last four digits are allowed. When full numbers are required, file them in a separate ‘Notice of Confidential Information Within Court Filing,’ which is impounded. Anyone may move to redact a noncompliant filing, and willful violations can draw fees.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 139. Eviction practice","Illinois · civil rules","For eviction cases filed on or after July 17, 2020, the complaint must attach the eviction notice or demand with proof of service (or a form affidavit), and, for actions on a written lease under 9-210, the lease or relevant portions (or a form affidavit).","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 181. Appearances, answers, and motions – timing","Illinois · civil rules","On a 30-day summons, the 30 days run from the day the summons is left with the person served (not any later mailing); appearing by motion within that time extends the answer date to whatever the court sets. On a return-day summons in a money case, a defendant who appears in writing without answering gets 10 days after the return day to answer or move. In eviction cases no answer is needed unless ordered, and the comp","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 182. Time for replies, answers to counterclaims, and other motions","Illinois · civil rules","Replies are due 21 days after the last day allowed for the answer; answers or motions to counterclaims are due 21 days after the last day allowed for the counterclaim; a motion attacking any pleading other than the complaint is due 21 days after the last day allowed for that pleading.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 183. Extensions of time","Illinois · civil rules","For good cause shown on motion with notice, the court may extend any rule deadline, before or after it expires.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 184. Hearings on motions","Illinois · civil rules","A rule or statutory deadline for filing a motion does not require the motion to be heard within that time; either party may call it up before or after the filing period.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 187. Forum non conveniens motions","Illinois · civil rules","A motion to dismiss or transfer for forum non conveniens must be filed no later than 90 days after the last day allowed for the movant’s answer, and hearings should allow time for discovery on the facts. Dismissal is conditioned on the defendant accepting service and waiving limitations if the plaintiff refiles in the other forum within 6 months; if the other forum refuses jurisdiction, the plaintiff may reinstate wi","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 191. Affidavits for summary judgment, 2-619, and 2-301 motions","Illinois · civil rules","Summary judgment (2-1005) and 2-619 motions must be filed before any dispositive-motion cutoff. Supporting and opposing affidavits, and affidavits on a 2-301 jurisdiction motion, must be on personal knowledge, state specific admissible facts (not conclusions), attach sworn or certified copies of documents relied on, and show the affiant is competent. Rule 191(b) lets a party who cannot get key affidavits name the wit","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 192. Summary judgment on fewer than all issues","Illinois · civil rules","When summary judgment does not resolve the whole case, the court may postpone entry of judgment, enter it, or enter it and stay enforcement; a smaller cross-demand may be netted against the movant’s demand.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 201. General discovery provisions","Illinois · civil rules","Discovery reaches ‘full disclosure’ of any matter relevant to the subject matter of the action, a broader test than the federal claim-or-defense-and-proportionality standard, though proportionality is a factor for protective orders (201(c)(3)). Work product is protected only for counsel’s theories, mental impressions, and litigation plans; consultants are discoverable only on exceptional circumstances. No discovery b","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 202. Purposes of depositions","Illinois · civil rules","Every deposition notice must say whether it is a discovery or an evidence deposition; if it does not, it is a discovery deposition only. Discovery and evidence depositions of the same witness are taken separately unless stipulated or ordered, and no discovery deposition is allowed if an evidence deposition is taken within 21 days of trial.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 203. Where depositions may be taken","Illinois · civil rules","Unless agreed, in the county where the deponent resides, works, or does business (a plaintiff in the county of suit), but the court may order a party or its officer or employee to appear elsewhere. A remote deposition is deemed taken where the deponent is.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 204. Compelling a deponent’s appearance","Illinois · civil rules","Subpoenas are issued by the clerk or by an attorney admitted in Illinois who is counsel of record; mail service is proved by return receipt at least 7 days before appearance with fee and mileage enclosed. Notice alone compels a party or its current officer, director, or employee. A records-only deposition requires serving copies on all parties at least 3 days before, and the subpoena must be attached to the notice an","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 205. Persons before whom depositions are taken","Illinois · civil rules","Depositions are taken before an officer authorized to administer oaths (or a U.S. consular officer abroad) or a court appointee; commissions and letters rogatory are optional; no relative, attorney, or financially interested person may preside.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 206. Method of taking oral depositions","Illinois · civil rules","Notice must give reasonable advance warning, state discovery or evidence purpose, and disclose any audio-visual recording; an entity may be named with topics and must designate witnesses (as in Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(b)(6)). A discovery deposition is limited to 3 hours (the federal limit is 7) absent stipulation or court order; objections must be concise; no depositions on weekends or court holidays without agreement; re","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 207. Signing and filing depositions","Illinois · civil rules","Unless signature is waived, the deponent may review the transcript and correct only reporting or transcription errors, not substance; if the deponent does not review within 28 days after notice, the deposition may be used as if signed. Transcripts are not filed as a matter of course.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 208. Fees and charges; copies","Illinois · civil rules","The party taking the deposition pays the witness, officer, and attendance charges; the party ordering transcription pays for it; the court may tax these as costs. Each party pays its own Rule 213(f)(3) controlled expert’s fees unless manifest injustice would result.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 209. Failure to attend or to serve subpoena","Illinois · civil rules","A party who notices a deposition and then fails to attend, or fails to subpoena the deponent, may be ordered to pay the expenses and fees of a party who showed up; remote attendance counts.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 210. Depositions on written questions","Illinois · civil rules","Cross-questions are due within 14 days after service of the direct questions, redirect within 7 days, and re-cross within 7 days; the officer takes the answers with no party or lawyer present.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 211. Errors and irregularities in depositions","Illinois · civil rules","Objections to the notice must be promptly served in writing; objections to the officer’s qualification must be made before or promptly after discovery; objections to form and curable errors are waived unless made at the deposition; objections to the form of written questions are due within the time for the next round (7 days for the last).","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 212. Use of depositions","Illinois · civil rules","A discovery deposition may be used only to impeach, as a party admission, under a hearsay exception, as an affidavit, or, with notice, as evidence if the deponent (not a controlled expert) is dead or infirm and no evidence deposition was taken. An evidence deposition of a physician may be used regardless of availability; other evidence depositions may be used if the witness is dead, ill, imprisoned, out of the county","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 213. Interrogatories; witness disclosure","Illinois · civil rules","No more than 30 interrogatories including subparts (the federal limit is 25), answered under oath within 28 days. Rule 213(f) requires disclosure of lay witnesses, independent experts, and controlled experts (with opinions, bases, qualifications, and reports) on interrogatory, and 213(g) limits direct testimony at trial to what was disclosed in answers or a discovery deposition. There is a continuing duty to suppleme","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 214. Production of documents and inspection","Illinois · civil rules","Requests may seek documents, ESI, tangible things, or entry onto land, with a response time of at least 28 days (federal: 30). The responding party must produce or object, may object on proportionality grounds, and must serve an affidavit stating whether production is complete; a party claiming the items are not in its control may be examined in court or by deposition. Duty to supplement continues.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 215. Physical and mental examinations","Illinois · civil rules","On motion when a condition is in controversy, the court may order an examination by a licensed professional, with the requesting party paying the examiner, lost earnings, and expenses. The examiner’s report must be delivered within 21 days after the exam or the examiner’s findings and testimony are excluded (except at the examinee’s instance). The court may also order an impartial examination by a panel physician cho","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 216. Requests to admit","Illinois · civil rules","Each fact or document’s genuineness is admitted unless within 28 days the party serves a sworn denial or detailed reasons, or written objections. Limit of 30 requests, each subpart counted, served in a separate document bearing a boldface 12-point warning that failure to respond within 28 days means the facts are deemed true. Admissions bind only in the pending case and any 13-217 refiling; a public-records notice is","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 217. Depositions to perpetuate testimony","Illinois · civil rules","Before suit, a verified petition (with notice served at least 21 days before the hearing) may obtain an order to perpetuate testimony; while an appeal is pending or the time to appeal runs, the trial court may allow perpetuation depositions for possible further proceedings.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 218. Pretrial procedure and case management","Illinois · civil rules","Unless a local rule approved by the Supreme Court provides otherwise, the court must hold an initial case management conference within 35 days after the parties are at issue and no later than 182 days after the complaint is filed. The resulting order controls the case, and disclosure and discovery deadlines must be set so discovery closes at least 60 days before the anticipated trial date unless the parties agree oth","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 219. Discovery sanctions","Illinois · civil rules","Fees are mandatory on a motion to compel unless the refusal (or the motion) was substantially justified; a party who denies a request to admit that is later proved pays the cost of proof unless the denial had good reason. Sanctions for unreasonable noncompliance include stays, debarring pleadings or claims, barring witnesses, default or dismissal, judgment interest for pretrial delay, fees, and monetary penalties for","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 222. Limited and simplified discovery (cases up to $50,000)","Illinois · civil rules","Applies to mandatory-arbitration cases and money-damages claims up to $50,000 (not small claims, family, or equitable cases); every money-damages complaint must attach an affidavit stating whether damages sought exceed $50,000, and any judgment above that is reduced to $50,000. Absent a local rule, parties must make detailed written initial disclosures within 120 days after the responsive pleading, with an affidavit ","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 223. Reserved","Illinois · civil rules","Reserved; no rule text.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 224. Pre-suit discovery to identify responsible persons","Illinois · civil rules","A verified petition in an independent action may seek discovery solely to identify persons who may be liable in damages, served with a special summons. The order is limited to identification, expires automatically 60 days after issuance unless extended, and the petitioner pays the respondent’s reasonable compliance costs. There is no federal counterpart.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 231. Motions for continuance","Illinois · civil rules","A continuance for absent evidence requires an affidavit showing diligence, the specific facts, the witness’s residence, and that the evidence can be obtained; it is denied if the opponent will admit the affidavit as the witness’s testimony. Military service in wartime and General Assembly membership during session are grounds; a continuance sought after the case is reached for trial needs a sufficient excuse.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 232. Trial of equitable and legal matters","Illinois · civil rules","A single equitable cause is tried as in equity. Where legal and equitable matters are joined, the court decides severability, and properly demanded legal issues go to the jury while equitable issues are heard by the court.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 233. Order of proceeding","Illinois · civil rules","Parties proceed at every stage (jury selection, openings, evidence, closings) in the order they appear in the pleadings unless agreed or ordered.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 234. Voir dire","Illinois · civil rules","The judge conducts voir dire and must permit the parties to supplement it with direct questioning for a reasonable time; questions may not concern law or instructions.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 235. Opening statements","Illinois · civil rules","The plaintiff may open as soon as the jury is impaneled and the defendant immediately after; any other timing is discretionary.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 236. Business records","Illinois · civil rules","Records made in the regular course of business at or near the time are admissible, with lack of personal knowledge going to weight; police accident reports are expressly excluded from this rule.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 237. Compelling witnesses at trial","Illinois · civil rules","Subpoenaed witnesses must appear if fees and mileage are tendered (mail service proved by receipt at least 7 days before). A party, or its current officer, director, or employee, can be required to appear at trial (in person or remotely) and bring original documents simply by serving a Rule 237(b) notice on the party; noncompliance can draw any Rule 219(c) sanction.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 238. Impeachment; hostile witnesses","Illinois · civil rules","Any party may impeach a witness, including its own; a hostile or unwilling witness may be examined as if on cross.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 239. Jury instructions","Illinois · civil rules","Illinois Pattern Jury Instructions (IPI) must be used when applicable unless the court finds the IPI inaccurate; non-IPI instructions must be simple, brief, and impartial. Each tendered instruction must be marked ‘IPI No. __,’ ‘IPI No. __ Modified,’ or ‘Not in IPI’; objections must be specific; the court may instruct before openings and must instruct after closings, and jurors may not get written copies before closin","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 240. Directed verdicts","Illinois · civil rules","An order directing a verdict takes effect without the jury’s assent.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 241. Remote testimony in civil trials and evidentiary hearings","Illinois · civil rules","The judge may allow testimony by video conference for good cause with safeguards (telephone only in compelling circumstances) and may allow nontestifying participants to appear remotely; arbitrators may do so by agreement. The judge may allocate costs so they are not a barrier to access.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 242. Remote jury selection","Illinois · civil rules","Remote jury selection by video is allowed with all parties’ consent, or without consent if the judge finds a compelling circumstance, consistent with constitutional rights and public access.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 243. Juror questions to witnesses","Illinois · civil rules","The court may let jurors submit written questions after counsel’s questioning; counsel may object outside the jury’s presence, the court asks approved questions, and counsel get limited follow-up. Rule 213(g) disclosure limits apply to juror questions.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 271. Orders on motions","Illinois · civil rules","Unless the court directs otherwise, the prevailing party’s attorney prepares and presents the order; orders may be prepared and signed electronically.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 272. When judgment is entered","Illinois · civil rules","If the judge requires a written judgment to be signed (or a local rule requires a draft order), the judgment is final only when the signed judgment is filed; otherwise it is entered when entered of record. This date starts the appeal clock.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 273. Effect of involuntary dismissal","Illinois · civil rules","Unless the order or a statute says otherwise, an involuntary dismissal (other than for lack of jurisdiction, improper venue, or failure to join an indispensable party) is an adjudication on the merits.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 274. One postjudgment motion","Illinois · civil rules","A party may file only one postjudgment motion directed at a final judgment, within 30 days or any extension; a new or modified judgment allows one motion directed at it. Each timely motion tolls finality and appealability until decided. A pending Rule 137 claim does not extend the time for postjudgment motions but may delay appealability under Rule 303(a)(1).","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 275. Reserved","Illinois · civil rules","Reserved; no rule text.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 276. Opening a judgment by confession","Illinois · civil rules","The motion must be supported by a Rule 191 affidavit and a verified proposed answer; if a prima facie meritorious defense and diligence are shown, the judgment is opened (in whole or part) and the original judgment stands as security. Trial is to the court unless a jury is demanded and paid for when the opening order is entered.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 277. Citation to discover assets","Illinois · civil rules","A citation proceeding under 735 ILCS 5/2-1402 may start immediately after a money judgment, in the court of entry or any court where the judgment is filed, against the debtor or any third party; examination is at least 5 days after service, and written interrogatory answers from a third party are due within 3 days of service. A later citation against the debtor needs leave of court on affidavit. The proceeding and it","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 278. Exemption claims in enforcement proceedings","Illinois · civil rules","A judgment debtor may claim statutory exemptions orally on the return date or by an expedited motion that is granted automatically unless the creditor objects within 5 business days (otherwise a hearing within 7 business days). Agreed orders must be ruled on within 2 business days; exemptions are liberally construed, may be claimed repeatedly, and the 12-1001(b) wildcard applies collectively across accounts.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 279. Reserved","Illinois · civil rules","Reserved; no rule text.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 280. Credit card and debt buyer actions – applicability","Illinois · civil rules","Part I applies to any complaint with a claim originating from a credit card or by a debt buyer collecting a consumer debt.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 280.1. Definitions","Illinois · civil rules","Defines affidavit, assignment, charge-off balance and date, consumer debt, credit card, debt buyer, payment, principal, and related terms for Part I.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 280.2. Complaint requirements","Illinois · civil rules","The complaint must print the signer’s name, attach the Supreme Court’s Credit Card or Debt Buyer Collection Affidavit signed by the plaintiff or its agent (not plaintiff’s attorney), attach the contract or an account document (a charge-off statement suffices for revolving credit), and state that suit is within the limitations period.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 280.3. Continuance or voluntary dismissal on trial date","Illinois · civil rules","Absent a properly noticed written motion, a trial-day motion to continue or voluntarily dismiss is denied and the case is tried, unless all parties knowingly consent and justice is served or the court cannot proceed.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 280.4. Consequences of noncompliance","Illinois · civil rules","If the plaintiff does not comply with Part I, no default judgment may be entered and the complaint may be dismissed.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 280.5. Identity theft defense","Illinois · civil rules","A defendant claiming identity theft serves the Attorney General’s and the Supreme Court’s identity-theft affidavits; within 90 days the case must be dismissed unless the plaintiff files an affidavit showing the defendant is responsible.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 281. Definition of small claim","Illinois · civil rules","A small claim is a tort or contract action for money not over $10,000, exclusive of interest and costs.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 282. Small claims – commencement; corporations","Illinois · civil rules","A short, simple complaint with names, addresses, and the nature and amount of the claim (attaching any written instrument) starts the case. A corporation may sue in small claims only through counsel, but may defend through an officer, director, manager, or supervisor.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 283. Small claims summons","Illinois · civil rules","The summons sets a return day 40–61 days after issuance and follows the Rule 101(b) form.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 284. Small claims service by certified or registered mail","Illinois · civil rules","Unless a circuit rule says otherwise, the plaintiff may pay the clerk $2 plus postage to mail the summons and complaint, restricted delivery for individuals; a receipt showing delivery at least 21 days before the return day is proof of service.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 285. Small claims jury demands","Illinois · civil rules","Trial is to the court unless the plaintiff demands a jury when filing or the defendant demands one by the appearance date. The jury is 6 unless a party demands 12; fees are $12.50 for 6 and $25 for 12.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 286. Small claims appearance, answer, and informal hearings","Illinois · civil rules","The defendant must attend the first court date in person or remotely but need not file a written appearance or answer unless ordered; the complaint is deemed denied. The court may hold an informal hearing, relaxing procedure and evidence rules except hearsay and privilege, and must explain its ruling.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 287. Small claims discovery and motions","Illinois · civil rules","No depositions, interrogatories, other discovery, or requests to admit without leave of court; no motions without leave except under 735 ILCS 5/2-619 and 2-1001 (substitution of judge).","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 288. Installment payment of small claims judgments","Illinois · civil rules","The court may order payment by a date or in installments and stay enforcement meanwhile, but installments may not run more than 3 years.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 289. Service in confession or collection proceedings up to $10,000","Illinois · civil rules","Process to confirm a confessed judgment or collect a judgment of $10,000 or less may be served by Rule 284 certified mail.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 290. Reserved","Illinois · civil rules","Reserved; no rule text.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 291. Administrative Review Law proceedings","Illinois · civil rules","The clerk serves the Article II-form summons by registered or certified mail and files a certificate within 5 days of mailing; defendants appear within 35 days after the summons date. The agency’s answer is the administrative record, which goes into the record on appeal unless reduced by stipulation or order.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 292. Summons to review Workers’ Compensation Commission orders","Illinois · civil rules","On a written request for review, the circuit clerk issues the Article II-form summons to the Commission and all parties in interest.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 293. Jury trial in involuntary admission","Illinois · civil rules","A requested jury trial must begin within 30 days; continuances may not exceed 15 days except at the respondent’s request.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 294. Collaborative process lawyers","Illinois · civil rules","A collaborative process lawyer (and the whole firm, without screening or waiver) is disqualified from representing a party before a tribunal in the related matter, and must withdraw if the process fails, except for procedural filings, approval of the agreement, or emergency protective orders.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 295. Matters assignable to associate judges","Illinois · civil rules","The chief judge (or a designated circuit judge) may assign associate judges any suitable matter, including felony trials.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 296. Restraints in mental health proceedings","Illinois · civil rules","A respondent in involuntary admission, medication, or judicial admission proceedings may not be restrained unless the court first finds manifest necessity at a separate hearing on the record; only the least restrictive restraint is allowed.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 297. Reserved","Illinois · civil rules","Reserved; no rule text.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 298. Application for waiver of court fees","Illinois · civil rules","Fee-waiver applications use the statewide form under 735 ILCS 5/5-105, cost nothing to file, and are decided on the papers; if information is incomplete the court may not deny outright but must allow amendment or a hearing within 30 days of filing. Clients of civil legal services providers or court-sponsored pro bono programs proceed without fees on counsel’s certification.","#st-il-rules"],["Rule 299. Compensation of appointed counsel for indigent parties","Illinois · civil rules","Appointed counsel get a reasonable fee, at a local-rule rate of not less than $150 per hour, capped at $750 for misdemeanors and $10,000 for felonies and listed termination-of-parental-rights and commitment matters, unless the court finds exceptional circumstances and the chief judge approves in advance.","#st-il-rules"],["Appeals in Illinois: 30 days, one notice, and a Supreme Court rule for almost everything","Illinois · appeals","Civil appeals go from the circuit court to the Appellate Court (five districts) and then, almost always by discretionary petition for leave to appeal, to the Illinois Supreme Court. The procedure is set by Supreme Court Rules 301–384, not by a statute or a separate rules of appellate procedure. Every final judgment is appealable as of right, and the only jurisdictional step is a timely notice of appeal filed in the c","#st-il-appeals"],["Final judgments are appealable as of right; notice of appeal is the only jurisdictional step","Illinois · appeals · Starting the appeal","Every final judgment of a circuit court in a civil case is appealable as of right. The appeal begins by filing a notice of appeal in the circuit court; no other step is jurisdictional, and the appeal is a continuation of the proceeding. The circuit clerk forwards the notice to the reviewing court within 5 days. Ill. S. Ct. R. 301; R. 303(a)(4)","#st-il-appeals"],["30 days from judgment, or from the order deciding the last postjudgment motion","Illinois · appeals · Starting the appeal","File the notice within 30 days after entry of the final judgment or, if a timely postjudgment motion is filed, within 30 days after entry of the order disposing of the last pending postjudgment motion. A notice filed after the decision is announced but before entry counts as filed on entry. Any attorney may file the notice, even one who has not appeared in the circuit court. R. 303(a)(1)","#st-il-appeals"],["When a judgment is ‘entered’","Illinois · appeals · Starting the appeal","If the judge requires a signed written judgment (or a local rule requires a draft order), the judgment is final only when the signed judgment is filed; otherwise it is entered when entered of record. R. 272","#st-il-appeals"],["Contents, amendment, and service of the notice","Illinois · appeals · Starting the appeal","Caption ‘Appeal to the ___ Court / From the Circuit Court of ___,’ designate it as notice of appeal, joining prior appeal, separate appeal, or cross-appeal, and specify the judgment or parts appealed and relief sought. It may be amended without leave within the original 30 days; later only by motion in the reviewing court. Within 7 days file a notice of filing in the reviewing court and serve the notice on every part","#st-il-appeals"],["Late notice of appeal: one extra 30-day window","Illinois · appeals · Starting the appeal","Within 30 days after the time to appeal expires, a party may move in the reviewing court for leave to file a late notice, showing reasonable excuse and attaching the proposed notice and filing fee. Other parties then have 10 days from the order to join, appeal separately, or cross-appeal. R. 303(d)","#st-il-appeals"],["Cross-appeals and separate appeals","Illinois · appeals · Starting the appeal","After a timely notice is served, any other party may join, appeal separately, or cross-appeal within 10 days after service of the notice, or within 30 days after the judgment or the order deciding the last postjudgment motion, whichever is later. R. 303(a)(3)","#st-il-appeals"],["Direct appeals to the Supreme Court","Illinois · appeals · Starting the appeal","Appeals go straight to the Supreme Court when a circuit court holds a U.S. or Illinois statute invalid (not counting federal preemption rulings), with the Rule 18 findings attached to the notice. The Supreme Court may also take a case directly when the public interest requires prompt decision. R. 302(a), (b); R. 303(b)(3)","#st-il-appeals"],["Partial judgments need a Rule 304(a) finding","Illinois · appeals · Finality and postjudgment motions","When there are multiple parties or claims, a judgment disposing of fewer than all is appealable only if the trial court makes an express written finding that there is no just reason to delay enforcement or appeal or both. The finding’s entry date starts the 30 days. Without it, the order is not appealable or enforceable and may be revised anytime. R. 304(a)","#st-il-appeals"],["Orders appealable without a finding","Illinois · appeals · Finality and postjudgment motions","Rule 304(b) lists orders immediately appealable without a 304(a) finding, under the Rule 303 timing: estate and guardianship orders finally determining a right or status, receivership and similar orders, rulings on 2-1401 petitions, final orders in 2-1402 citation proceedings, contempt orders imposing a penalty, and custody or parental-responsibility judgments. R. 304(b)(1)–(6)","#st-il-appeals"],["Pending sanctions claim blocks finality","Illinois · appeals · Finality and postjudgment motions","A judgment is not final and appealable while a Rule 137 claim remains pending unless the court enters a Rule 304(a) finding. A pending Rule 137 claim does not extend the time for postjudgment motions. R. 303(a)(1); R. 274","#st-il-appeals"],["Postjudgment motions: 30 days, and only one","Illinois · appeals · Finality and postjudgment motions","Jury cases: all post-trial relief (judgment n.o.v., new trial, renewed directed verdict) goes in a single post-trial motion within 30 days after judgment, stating the grounds specifically; failing to request a new trial waives it. Nonjury cases: a motion to rehear, retry, modify, or vacate within 30 days. Extensions must be granted within the 30 days. Each party gets one postjudgment motion per final judgment, and a ","#st-il-appeals"],["Motions to reconsider a postjudgment ruling do not toll","Illinois · appeals · Finality and postjudgment motions","No request to reconsider a ruling on a postjudgment motion tolls the time to appeal. A notice filed while a timely postjudgment motion is pending becomes effective when the motion is decided, but to challenge the ruling on the motion or an amended judgment you must file a new or amended notice within 30 days of that order. R. 303(a)(2)","#st-il-appeals"],["After 30 days: section 2-1401 petition","Illinois · appeals · Finality and postjudgment motions","Relief from a final judgment after 30 days requires a petition under 2-1401, filed in the same case (but a new proceeding), supported by affidavit, generally within 2 years after the judgment (excluding periods of disability, duress, or fraudulent concealment). The ruling is appealable under Rule 304(b)(3). 735 ILCS 5/2-1401(a)–(c); R. 304(b)(3); R. 106","#st-il-appeals"],["As of right: injunctions, receivers, and similar orders (Rule 307)","Illinois · appeals · Interlocutory appeals","Orders granting, modifying, refusing, dissolving, or refusing to dissolve an injunction; appointing or refusing a receiver; placing a mortgagee in possession; and several others may be appealed as of right. File a ‘Notice of Interlocutory Appeal’ and the Rule 328 supporting record within 30 days of the order. If the order was entered ex parte, first move to vacate; the 30 days run from denial or from 7 days after pre","#st-il-appeals"],["Temporary restraining orders: 2 days","Illinois · appeals · Interlocutory appeals","Review of a TRO ruling is by petition in the Appellate Court, plus a notice of interlocutory appeal in the circuit court, both within 2 days of the order, with a supporting record. Memoranda are limited to 15 pages or 4,500 words; the response is due 2 days later; no replies or extensions; the court decides within 5 business days without argument. R. 307(d)","#st-il-appeals"],["By permission: petition for leave to appeal (Rule 306)","Illinois · appeals · Interlocutory appeals","Listed orders may be reviewed by petition: new-trial grants; forum non conveniens rulings; denial of a motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction; certain venue transfers; custody orders; remands to an agency; orders disqualifying counsel; class certification rulings; and denials of a motion to dispose under the Citizen Participation Act. File the petition and Rule 328 supporting record within 30 days of the","#st-il-appeals"],["Certified questions (Rule 308)","Illinois · appeals · Interlocutory appeals","If the trial court certifies in writing that an interlocutory order involves a question of law with substantial ground for difference of opinion and that immediate appeal may materially advance the litigation, a party may apply to the Appellate Court within 30 days after the order or the certification, whichever is later. Answers are due 21 days after the application’s due date. The application does not stay the tria","#st-il-appeals"],["Money judgments: bond within the appeal time","Illinois · appeals · Stays and bonds","Enforcement of a money judgment is stayed if a timely notice of appeal is filed and an appeal bond or other approved security is presented, approved, and filed within the time to appeal (or extension). Security normally covers the judgment, costs, and anticipated interest; the court may approve less if full security is not reasonably available, with conditions against dissipating assets. Circuit court extensions may ","#st-il-appeals"],["Other judgments; reviewing-court stays","Illinois · appeals · Stays and bonds","Stays of nonmoney judgments and appealable orders are discretionary on terms, and a bond is required to protect an appellee’s property interest. Ask the circuit court first; a reviewing court motion must show that was impractical or unsuccessful and include a supporting record. Public bodies may be stayed without bond. R. 305(b), (d), (i)","#st-il-appeals"],["No stay, no protection against third-party buyers","Illinois · appeals · Stays and bonds","If a stay is not perfected within the appeal time, reversal does not affect property rights acquired by nonparties after the judgment became final and before the stay. R. 305(k)","#st-il-appeals"],["Docketing statement in 14 days","Illinois · appeals · Docketing statement and record","Appellants and cross-appellants file a docketing statement within 14 days after the notice of appeal (7 days for Rule 307 appeals; with the petition for Rule 306/308 appeals), with the filing fee and their written requests to the circuit clerk and court reporters for the record. The appellee may respond within 7 days. R. 312(a), (b)","#st-il-appeals"],["Report of proceedings","Illinois · appeals · Docketing statement and record","Within the docketing-statement time, ask court reporting personnel in writing for the transcript you want; the appellee may designate more within 7 days, and the appellant must then request it or move to have the appellee advance the cost. Including unnecessary matter can be taxed against the designating party. R. 323(a)","#st-il-appeals"],["Record due 63 days after the notice of appeal","Illinois · appeals · Docketing statement and record","The record on appeal must be filed within 63 days after the (last) notice of appeal, or 14 days after an extended transcript deadline. Extensions require a motion before the deadline or within 35 days after it with a reasonable excuse. R. 326","#st-il-appeals"],["Schedule: 35 / 35 / 14","Illinois · appeals · Briefs","Appellant’s brief is due 35 days after the record is filed, appellee’s 35 days after the appellant’s due date, and the reply 14 days after the appellee’s due date. A cross-appellant files one combined appellee/cross-appellant brief; the appellant’s reply and cross-appeal answer is due 35 days later; the cross-reply 14 days after that. Extensions need a motion supported by affidavit or 1-109 certification. R. 343(a)–(","#st-il-appeals"],["Length: 50 pages or 15,000 words; reply 20 pages or 6,000","Illinois · appeals · Briefs","Opening and response briefs are limited to 50 pages or 15,000 words, replies to 20 pages or 6,000 words; cross-appeals get an extra 30 pages or 9,000 words. Covers, table of contents and points and authorities, certificates, and the Rule 342 appendix are excluded. A signed certificate of compliance is required. Motions for extra length are disfavored and due at least 10 days before the brief (5 for a reply), with an ","#st-il-appeals"],["Format and colored covers","Illinois · appeals · Briefs","Double-spaced 12-point text, 1½-inch left margin and 1-inch elsewhere, no condensed type. Even e-filed briefs use colored covers: appellant white, appellee light blue, reply light yellow, rehearing petition light green. R. 341(a), (d)","#st-il-appeals"],["Points not argued are forfeited","Illinois · appeals · Briefs","The appellant’s brief must state the standard of review for each issue, and the argument must contain contentions with citations to authority and the record. Points not argued are forfeited and cannot be raised in the reply, at oral argument, or on rehearing. The appendix must include the judgment, the notice of appeal, and a table of contents of the record. R. 341(h); R. 342","#st-il-appeals"],["Rule 23 orders: nonprecedential but citable since 2021","Illinois · appeals · Decisions, rehearing, and mandate","Appellate decisions are full opinions or Rule 23 written or summary orders. Rule 23 orders are not precedential (except for res judicata, double jeopardy, collateral estoppel, or law of the case), but a Rule 23(b) order entered on or after January 1, 2021 may be cited for persuasive purposes. R. 23(a)–(c), (e)(1)","#st-il-appeals"],["Rehearing: 21 days","Illinois · appeals · Decisions, rehearing, and mandate","A petition for rehearing is due within 21 days after the judgment, limited to 27 pages or 8,100 words, stating points overlooked or misapprehended without reargument. No answer is allowed unless requested; an answer is due 21 days after request or grant and a reply 14 days later. Only one rehearing round in the Appellate Court. R. 367(a)–(e)","#st-il-appeals"],["Mandate: no earlier than 35 days","Illinois · appeals · Decisions, rehearing, and mandate","The mandate issues no earlier than 35 days after judgment (or after denial of rehearing) unless the court orders otherwise; a timely rehearing petition stays it. R. 368(a)","#st-il-appeals"],["Petition for leave to appeal: 35 days","Illinois · appeals · Review in the Illinois Supreme Court","Review is discretionary, considering general importance, conflicts with the Supreme Court or another Appellate Court division, the need for supervisory authority, and finality. File within 35 days after the Appellate Court judgment, or after denial of a timely rehearing petition or entry of judgment on rehearing. For Rule 23 orders, a timely Rule 23(f) motion to publish restarts the 35 days. Extensions are allowed on","#st-il-appeals"],["Petition format and answer","Illinois · appeals · Review in the Illinois Supreme Court","Petition and answer are each limited to 20 pages or 6,000 words, follow the brief rules, and include the Appellate Court decision in an appendix. An answer is optional, due 21 days after the petition deadline; no reply. If leave is granted, the appellant must within 14 days elect to stand on the petition or file a brief, due 35 days after leave was allowed. R. 315(c), (d), (f), (h)","#st-il-appeals"],["Appeals as of right and cross-relief","Illinois · appeals · Review in the Illinois Supreme Court","An appeal as of right lies when the Appellate Court holds a statute invalid or a constitutional question first arises from its decision, by a ‘Petition for Appeal as a Matter of Right’ following Rule 315 procedure (leave may be sought in the alternative). Any appellee or coparty may get relief warranted by the record without its own petition. R. 317; R. 318(a)","#st-il-appeals"],["Mandatory e-filing; filed when received","Illinois · appeals · Filing, service, and fees","Civil documents, including in reviewing courts, must be e-filed through an approved system unless exempt. A filing is timely if submitted before midnight in the court’s time zone on the due date. In the reviewing court, documents count as filed when actually received by the clerk; only paper filers exempt from e-filing get the mailing date (within 3 business days). This rule also applies to postjudgment motions and n","#st-il-appeals"],["E-mail service and proof","Illinois · appeals · Filing, service, and fees","Serve documents by e-mail or through the e-filing provider to the addresses of record; service by e-mail is complete on transmission. Proof is the automated EFSP verification or a 1-109 certification. R. 11(c); R. 12(b), (c)","#st-il-appeals"],["Reviewing court fees","Illinois · appeals · Filing, service, and fees","Appellants and petitioners pay a $50 filing fee; other parties pay $30 on appearance or first filing; a nonparty (including an amicus seeking leave) pays $30. R. 313(a)","#st-il-appeals"],["Trial counsel’s appearance ends with the notice of appeal","Illinois · appeals · Filing, service, and fees","An attorney’s trial-court appearance ends when a notice of appeal is filed from the final judgment, or when the time to file it expires. An out-of-state lawyer appearing under Rule 707 needs a new Statement for the appeal, which is a separate proceeding. R. 13(c)(8); R. 707(c)","#st-il-appeals"],["Notice of appeal from final judgment","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of final judgment, or entry of order disposing of last pending timely postjudgment motion.\nJurisdictional. Filed in the circuit court. Ill. S. Ct. R. 303(a)(1)","#st-il-appeals"],["Motion for leave to file late notice of appeal","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from expiration of the time to file the notice of appeal.\nFiled in the reviewing court with reasonable excuse, proposed notice, and fee. R. 303(d)","#st-il-appeals"],["Cross-appeal, separate appeal, or joining appeal","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from service of another party’s timely notice of appeal (or 30 days from judgment / last postjudgment order, whichever is later).\nUse whichever period ends later. R. 303(a)(3)","#st-il-appeals"],["Serve notice of appeal and file notice of filing in reviewing court","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from filing of notice of appeal. R. 303(c)","#st-il-appeals"],["Post-trial motion (jury case)","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of judgment or discharge of jury without verdict.\nSingle motion; extensions must be granted within the 30 days. 735 ILCS 5/2-1202(c)","#st-il-appeals"],["Postjudgment motion (nonjury case)","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of judgment.\nOnly one postjudgment motion per judgment tolls the appeal time. 735 ILCS 5/2-1203(a); R. 274","#st-il-appeals"],["Rule 137 sanctions motion","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of final judgment, or ruling on timely postjudgment motion.\nA pending Rule 137 claim keeps the judgment non-final absent a 304(a) finding. R. 137(b)","#st-il-appeals"],["Section 2-1401 petition for relief from judgment","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","730 days from entry of the judgment.\nStatute says 2 years; excludes disability, duress, and fraudulent concealment periods. 735 ILCS 5/2-1401(c)","#st-il-appeals"],["Notice of interlocutory appeal as of right and Rule 328 supporting record","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the interlocutory order (or denial of motion to vacate an ex parte order). R. 307(a), (b)","#st-il-appeals"],["Petition to review TRO ruling (and notice in circuit court)","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","2 days from entry or denial of the TRO order.\nResponse due 2 days after petition; decision within 5 business days. R. 307(d)(1)","#st-il-appeals"],["Petition for leave to appeal interlocutory order (Rule 306)","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order.\nIncludes denial of Citizen Participation Act motion (306(a)(9)). Custody petitions under 306(a)(5): 14 days. R. 306(c)(1)","#st-il-appeals"],["Answer to Rule 306 petition","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from filing of the petition. R. 306(c)(2)","#st-il-appeals"],["Application for leave to appeal certified question","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of order or trial court’s Rule 308 statement, whichever is later.\nAnswer due 21 days after the application’s due date. R. 308(b)","#st-il-appeals"],["Bond extension limit in circuit court","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","45 days from aggregate of circuit court extensions for filing appeal bond.\nUnless parties stipulate otherwise. R. 305(c)","#st-il-appeals"],["Docketing statement (appeal as of right)","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of notice of appeal.\n7 days for Rule 307 appeals. R. 312(a)(1)","#st-il-appeals"],["Record on appeal","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","63 days from filing of the (last) notice of appeal.\nExtension motion before deadline or within 35 days after with reasonable excuse. R. 326","#st-il-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","35 days from filing of the record on appeal. R. 343(a)","#st-il-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","35 days from due date of appellant’s brief. R. 343(a)","#st-il-appeals"],["Reply brief","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from due date of appellee’s brief. R. 343(a)","#st-il-appeals"],["Interlocutory (Rule 307) appellant’s brief","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from filing of the Rule 328 supporting record.\nAppellee 7 days later; reply 7 days after that. R. 307(c)","#st-il-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from filing of the reviewing court’s judgment.\nExtensions disfavored. R. 367(a)","#st-il-appeals"],["Petition for leave to appeal to Supreme Court","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","35 days from appellate Court judgment, or denial of timely rehearing petition / judgment on rehearing.\nExtensions only in the most extreme and compelling circumstances. R. 315(b)(1)","#st-il-appeals"],["Answer to petition for leave to appeal","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from expiration of the time to file the petition.\nOptional; no reply allowed. R. 315(f)","#st-il-appeals"],["Election to stand on PLA or file brief","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from order allowing leave to appeal.\nAdditional brief due 35 days after leave allowed. R. 315(h)","#st-il-appeals"],["Mandate issues (earliest)","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","35 days from entry of judgment or order denying rehearing. R. 368(a)","#st-il-appeals"],["Motion for original submission date after e-filing rejection","Illinois · appeals · Deadlines","5 days from notice of rejection (court days).\nCourt days, not calendar days. R. 9(f)(1)","#st-il-appeals"],["Indiana Supreme Court","Indiana · courts","Has mandatory and exclusive jurisdiction over death-penalty appeals, appeals of final judgments declaring a state or federal statute unconstitutional, parental-consent-to-abortion waiver appeals, and mandate-of-funds appeals. Everything else reaches it by discretionary transfer from the Court of Appeals (App.R. 57) or review of the Tax Court (App.R. 63). It has exclusive control of bar admission (including temporary ","#st-in"],["Indiana Court of Appeals","Indiana · courts","Hears all appeals from final judgments of circuit, superior, probate, and county courts not reserved to the Supreme Court, interlocutory appeals under App.R. 14, and review of final agency decisions. It decides most cases by memorandum decision; only opinions meeting App.R. 65(A) criteria are published.","#st-in"],["Indiana Tax Court","Indiana · courts","Specialized court whose decisions are reviewed by the Supreme Court on a petition for review under App.R. 63, not by the Court of Appeals.","#st-in"],["Circuit, Superior, and Probate Courts","Indiana · courts","The general trial courts, organized by county. Any case may be filed in any county (T.R. 75(A)), but a party can force transfer to a county of ‘preferred venue.’ Special judges for these courts are chosen under T.R. 79 and local rules approved by the Supreme Court.","#st-in"],["City and town courts; Marion County small claims courts","Indiana · courts","Limited-jurisdiction courts with their own special-judge selection rule (T.R. 79.1). Small claims procedure is governed by separate Small Claims Rules, which were not collected.","#st-in"],["Statute: Ind. Code ch. 34-7-7 (1998)","Indiana · anti-SLAPP","Indiana’s anti-SLAPP law makes it a defense to a civil action that the act or omission complained of was (1) in furtherance of the person’s right of petition or free speech under the U.S. or Indiana Constitution in connection with a public issue or issue of public interest, and (2) taken in good faith and with a reasonable basis in law and fact. ‘Claim’ includes a lawsuit, cause of action, petition, complaint, cross-","#st-in-slapp"],["Procedure: motion to dismiss treated as summary judgment; 180-day outer limit","Indiana · anti-SLAPP","The defendant raises the defense by a motion to dismiss under the chapter, which must state with specificity the public issue or issue of public interest that prompted the act. The court must treat it as a summary-judgment motion, set a reasonable expedited period of no more than 180 days to rule, and set limits for discovery needed to respond. Filing the motion stays all discovery except discovery relevant to the mo","#st-in-slapp"],["Mandatory fees for a prevailing defendant; fees against a frivolous movant","Indiana · anti-SLAPP","A defendant who prevails on the motion is entitled to reasonable attorney’s fees and costs. If the court finds the motion frivolous or solely intended to cause unnecessary delay, the plaintiff is entitled to reasonable fees and costs to answer it. Ind. Code §§ 34-7-7-7, -8","#st-in-slapp"],["Appeal: only an express right if the court fails to rule in 30 days","Indiana · anti-SLAPP","The court must act within 30 days after the submission of the evidence discovered in the expedited period. If it does not, the movant may appeal based on the failure to rule. The chapter gives no express interlocutory appeal from an order denying the motion; absent another route, a denial would need trial-court certification and Court of Appeals acceptance under App.R. 14(B). Confirm current case law on appealability","#st-in-slapp"],["Temporary admission (pro hac vice): Admis. Disc. R. 3, § 2","Indiana · out of state","Any Indiana court may, in its discretion and for good cause, let a lawyer admitted in another U.S. state, territory, or D.C. appear in a particular case. Conditions: (1) an Indiana lawyer has appeared and agreed to act as co-counsel; (2) the applicant is not an Indiana resident and is not regularly employed or regularly engaged in business or professional activities in Indiana; (3) the applicant pays a $300 case-spec","#st-in-oos"],["What the verified petition must say; repeat appearances","Indiana · out of state","The petition lists contact and firm information, every jurisdiction of admission with dates and numbers, good standing, any past discipline (with reasons to admit anyway), any pending disciplinary proceeding (with a continuing duty to report), every Indiana case in the last 5 years in which the lawyer appeared by temporary admission, an agreement to be bound by the Indiana Rules of Professional Conduct and to submit ","#st-in-oos"],["Local counsel duties; each lawyer pays for each case","Indiana · out of state","Indiana co-counsel must sign all briefs, papers, and pleadings and is jointly responsible for them; the signature certifies good ground and no improper purpose. Local counsel need not attend proceedings unless the trial court orders it. A foreign lawyer who fails to register or pay is subject to Indiana discipline, and so is local counsel if the admitted lawyer fails to pay or comply. The fee is per case, and each la","#st-in-oos"],["Temporary and limited-scope appearances in trial courts","Indiana · out of state","A lawyer representing a party on a temporary or limited-scope basis files a notice of temporary or limited representation giving the appearance information and describing the end date or scope; the court need not act on it. The lawyer files a notice of completion when done. Every appearance form must include the attorney’s Indiana attorney number, so get the temporary admission number first. Ind. T.R. 3.1(A)(2), (I)","#st-in-oos"],["Other paths: admission without examination, business counsel, temporary license","Indiana · out of state","A lawyer actively practicing at least 3 of the last 5 years (1,000 hours a year for most categories) may be admitted without examination. In-house lawyers who establish an Indiana presence for an employer that is not a law practice may obtain a business counsel license, which ends if the employment moves out of Indiana or ends (unless new qualifying employment begins within 3 months). Rule 6.1 temporary licenses (up ","#st-in-oos"],["Multijurisdictional practice without admission","Indiana · out of state","Indiana’s Rule 5.5 follows the ABA model: temporary services in association with an actively participating Indiana lawyer, services related to a proceeding in which the lawyer is or expects to be authorized to appear, ADR-related services, and services arising out of home-state practice are allowed, as are services to the lawyer’s employer if there is no systematic presence and the forum does not require temporary ad","#st-in-oos"],["Mandatory e-filing for lawyers (IEFS)","Indiana · out of state","Lawyers must e-file in trial courts through the Indiana E-Filing System (IEFS) after signing a user agreement with an approved e-filing service provider; an exemption requires a petition in each pending case, granted only for good cause. Unrepresented litigants are encouraged but not required to e-file. A filing counts for a day only if completed before midnight in the time zone of the court where the case is filed. ","#st-in-oos"],["Governing code: Indiana Rules of Professional Conduct","Indiana · ethics","Indiana’s rules are based on the ABA Model Rules (adopted effective January 1, 1987; the collected compilation is updated effective October 1, 2024). The Supreme Court’s Disciplinary Commission enforces them under Admis. Disc. R. 23. The differences below come from the rule text. Ind. Prof. Cond. R. (preamble and Rules 1.0–8.5); Ind. Admis. Disc. R. 23","#st-in-ethics"],["Rule 2.2 ‘Intermediary’ is still in force","Indiana · ethics","The ABA deleted Model Rule 2.2 in 2002, but Indiana keeps it. A lawyer may act as intermediary between clients after consulting each client about the risks, advantages, and effect on privilege and getting each client’s consent, if the lawyer reasonably believes the matter can be resolved compatibly with each client’s best interests and handled impartially. The lawyer must withdraw if any client asks or a condition fa","#st-in-ethics"],["Rule 8.4(g): bias in a professional capacity is misconduct","Indiana · ethics","It is misconduct to engage, in a professional capacity, in conduct manifesting bias or prejudice by words or conduct based on race, gender, religion, national origin, disability, sexual orientation, age, socioeconomic status, or similar factors. Legitimate advocacy is excepted, and a trial judge’s finding of discriminatory peremptory strikes does not by itself establish a violation. This is Indiana’s own wording, not","#st-in-ethics"],["Targeted solicitation: ‘Advertising Material,’ filing with the Disciplinary Commission, $50 fee, 30-day accident ban","Indiana · ethics","Written, recorded, or electronic solicitations to a person known to need help in a particular matter must be labeled ‘Advertising Material’ (on the envelope and at the start, or at the beginning and end of a recording), and a copy must be filed with the Disciplinary Commission at or before dissemination with a $50 fee; each changed version must be filed too. The lawyer must keep a recipient list for at least 1 year. ","#st-in-ethics"],["Advertising records kept 6 years","Indiana · ethics","Every advertising communication must name at least one responsible lawyer or firm with an office address, and a copy or recording must be kept for 6 years after dissemination. Ind. Prof. Cond. R. 7.2(c)","#st-in-ethics"],["Contingent fees: post-judgment domestic collection allowed","Indiana · ethics","Contingent fee agreements must be in a writing signed by the client stating the percentages for settlement, trial, and appeal and how expenses are handled, with a closing statement at the end. Contingent fees in domestic relations matters are barred, but Indiana expressly allows one for a post-judgment collection action if the client is told in writing about alternative collection measures. Ind. Prof. Cond. R. 1.5(c)","#st-in-ethics"],["Confidentiality: added disability exception","Indiana · ethics","Rule 1.6(b) tracks the ABA exceptions (death or bodily harm, client crime or fraud using the lawyer’s services, ethics advice, self-defense, other law or court order). Indiana adds Rule 1.6(c), permitting disclosure of client names and files as needed by the person managing the files of a disabled lawyer or by an appointed guardian or conservator of the client files. Ind. Prof. Cond. R. 1.6(b)–(c)","#st-in-ethics"],["The notice of appeal is filed with the appellate Clerk, not the trial court","Indiana · land mine","The notice of appeal is filed with the Clerk of the Indiana Supreme Court, Court of Appeals and Tax Court within 30 days after the final judgment is noted in the Chronological Case Summary, with the $250 fee. A late notice forfeits the appeal (except under Post-Conviction Rule 2). Ind. App. R. 2(D), 9(A)(1), (A)(5), 9(E)","#st-in-unique"],["Motion to correct error: deemed denial starts the clock","Indiana · land mine","After a timely motion to correct error, the notice of appeal is due 30 days after the ruling or 30 days after the motion is deemed denied under T.R. 53.3, whichever comes first. Do not wait for a written ruling after the deemed-denial date passes. Ind. App. R. 9(A)(1); Ind. T.R. 53.3(A)","#st-in-unique"],["Some issues must be raised in a motion to correct error","Indiana · land mine","A motion to correct error is generally optional, but it is required to raise newly discovered evidence (including jury misconduct) or a claim that a jury verdict is excessive or inadequate. It is due 30 days after entry of final judgment is noted in the CCS, and that time cannot be extended. Ind. T.R. 59(A), (C); Ind. T.R. 6(C)","#st-in-unique"],["Money-payment orders are appealable now, or the chance may be lost","Indiana · land mine","Interlocutory orders for the payment of money, to compel execution of a document or delivery of securities, for sale or delivery of real property, on preliminary injunctions and receivers, and transferring or refusing to transfer venue under T.R. 75 are appealable as of right within 30 days after the order is noted in the CCS. Other interlocutory orders need a motion for trial-court certification within 30 days and a","#st-in-unique"],["Change-of-judge window is short and one-time","Indiana · land mine","The automatic change of judge must be sought within 10 days after the issues are first closed on the merits (typically the answer or reply). Miss it and you need a personally verified motion showing newly discovered grounds; each party gets only one change of judge and one change of county in the case. Ind. T.R. 76(B)–(D)","#st-in-unique"],["Automatic 30-day extension to answer, but only if noticed on time","Indiana · land mine","A party may get one automatic 30-day enlargement to respond to a complaint or other pleading by filing a notice stating the original and new due dates on or before the original due date. Responses to pleadings and motions are otherwise due 20 days after service, replies 14 days after the response, and 3 days are added only for service by U.S. mail. Ind. T.R. 6(B), (D), (G)","#st-in-unique"],["Midnight deadline in the court’s time zone","Indiana · land mine","Indiana has counties in two time zones, and an e-filing is timely only if completed before midnight in the time zone of the court where the case is filed (or pending, on appeal). Ind. T.R. 87(H); Ind. App. R. 68(I)","#st-in-unique"],["No extensions for rehearing or transfer petitions","Indiana · land mine","A petition for rehearing is due 30 days after the decision and a petition to transfer 45 days after the decision (or 30 days after a rehearing ruling). No extension is allowed, and the 3-day mail extension does not apply. Ind. App. R. 35(C), 54(B), 57(C)","#st-in-unique"],["Pre-2023 memorandum decisions are not citable","Indiana · land mine","Unpublished Court of Appeals memorandum decisions issued before January 1, 2023 may not be cited to any court except for res judicata, collateral estoppel, or law of the case. Those issued on or after January 1, 2023 may be cited for persuasive value. A party may move to publish within 15 days of the decision. Ind. App. R. 65(B), (D)","#st-in-unique"],["Summary judgment lives or dies on ‘designation’","Indiana · land mine","The opponent has 30 days to respond, each side must specifically designate the evidence it relies on, and the court considers only designated material. A summary-judgment ruling will not be reversed on a fact issue that was not specifically designated to the trial court. Ind. T.R. 56(C), (H)","#st-in-unique"],["Pro hac vice: $300 per case, good-standing certificates under 30 days old, residency bar","Indiana · land mine","Temporary admission requires Indiana co-counsel who signs everything, a $300 per-case fee paid before filing the petition, certificates of good standing from every admitting state issued within 30 days, and the applicant may not be an Indiana resident or regularly practicing or employed in Indiana. Ind. Admis. Disc. R. 3, § 2(a)–(b)","#st-in-unique"],["Discovery motions need a recited conference","Indiana · land mine","Before filing a discovery motion, a party must make a reasonable effort to resolve the dispute and state in the motion the date, time, and place of that effort and who took part, or the court may deny the motion. Discovery requests must also be served in an editable word-processing format. Ind. T.R. 26(F), (A)(1)","#st-in-unique"],["One automatic change of judge per party, then T.R. 79 special-judge selection","Indiana · judges","In a civil case each party gets one change of judge, granted on an unverified motion that states no grounds. The motion is due within 10 days after the issues are first closed on the merits (30 days after filing where no responsive pleading closes the issues, and in probate, receivership, and remonstrance matters; 10 days after a new-trial order, remand, or transfer to a new case number), or later only on a motion pe","#st-in-unique"],["No separate speech rule collected","Indiana · speech","Beyond the anti-SLAPP statute (Ind. Code ch. 34-7-7), the collected texts contain no Indiana-specific speech protection to summarize. The Indiana Constitution’s free-speech clause and Indiana defamation statutes (Ind. Code art. 34-15) were not collected. None collected","#st-in-unique"],["The ‘lazy judge’ rule and deemed denials","Indiana · unique rules","If a trial court fails for 30 days to set a motion for hearing, or fails to rule within 30 days after the hearing (or after filing if no hearing is required), a party may file a praecipe to withdraw submission and have a special judge appointed. The judge may extend once by 30 days by a CCS entry made before the deadline. Motions to correct error are deemed denied if not set within 45 days, or not ruled on within 30 ","#st-in-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope of the rules","Indiana · civil rules","The rules govern procedure in all Indiana courts in all civil suits, whether at law, in equity, or of statutory origin, and must be construed to secure the just, speedy and inexpensive determination of every action. Courts may adopt local and administrative district rules consistent with Rule 81.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","Indiana · civil rules","There is one form of action, the “civil action.” A civil action is not merged in a public offense or public remedy and may be pursued independently of, and in addition to, any criminal punishment or public relief.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencement of an action","Indiana · civil rules","An action is commenced by filing a complaint (or equivalent pleading required by statute), paying the filing fee or filing an order waiving it, and, where service is required, furnishing the clerk enough copies of the complaint and summons. Unlike FRCP 3, payment of the fee (or a waiver) and delivery of service copies to the clerk are part of commencement.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 3.1. Appearance","Indiana · civil rules","Indiana requires an appearance form, with no FRCP counterpart: the initiating party (or counsel) files it when the action is commenced, responding parties when they first appear, and intervenors with their first filing, listing contact information, attorney number, case type, whether service by FAX or e-mail is accepted, related cases, and other items (Social Security numbers of family members in support-related case","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4. Process","Indiana · civil rules","The court gets jurisdiction over a person who commences or joins the action, is served with summons, enters an appearance, or is otherwise subject to its power. When the complaint is filed, the party furnishes copies of the complaint and summons to the clerk, who signs, seals and issues the summons (with any praecipe); the summons must state the response time and warn of default. The party may designate the manner of","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4.1. Summons: service on individuals","Indiana · civil rules","Service on an individual (including one acting in a representative capacity) is by (1) registered or certified mail or other public means with a return receipt requested and returned, sent to the residence, place of business or employment, (2) delivering a copy personally, (3) leaving a copy at the dwelling house or usual place of abode, or (4) serving an agent under a rule, statute or valid agreement. When copy is l","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4.2. Summons: service upon infant or incompetents","Indiana · civil rules","Service on an infant is made on the next friend or guardian ad litem in the same action, otherwise on a court-appointed representative who can be served in Indiana, otherwise on a parent with custody or a person standing in place of a parent; the infant must also be served if 14 or older, and is served directly if none of these is possible. Service on an incompetent follows the same order, except that absent a repres","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4.3. Summons: service upon institutionalized persons","Indiana · civil rules","Service on a person imprisoned or restrained in an institution is made by delivering or mailing the summons and complaint to the official in charge. The official must immediately deliver the papers to the person, allow the person to arrange counsel, and state on the return whether the person received the summons and had an opportunity to retain counsel.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4.4. Service upon persons in actions for acts done in this state or having an effect in this state","Indiana · civil rules","Indiana’s long-arm rule subjects nonresidents, departed residents, and persons of unknown residence to jurisdiction for claims arising from eight listed acts (including doing business in Indiana, causing injury by acts in or affecting Indiana, owning Indiana real property, insuring Indiana risks, living in a marital relationship in Indiana for support and property obligations, and abuse or protective-order violations","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4.5. Summons: service upon resident who cannot be found or served within the state","Indiana · civil rules","When an Indiana resident cannot be served personally or by agent in Indiana and cannot be found, has concealed their whereabouts, or has left the state, summons may be served in the manner provided for in rem actions under Rule 4.9.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4.6. Service upon organizations","Indiana · civil rules","Service on an organization is made on an executive officer or an appointed or statutory agent; on a partnership, a general partner; on a state governmental organization, its executive officer and also the Attorney General; and on a local governmental organization, its executive and its attorney (plus the representative personally when named individually). Service is made in the manner used for individuals but must no","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4.7. Summons: service upon agent named by statute or agreement","Indiana · civil rules","When an agent is designated by statute or valid agreement to receive service, a governmental agent is served under Rule 4.10 and any other agent under Rule 4.1 or 4.6. If the agent cannot be served because no address was furnished or the agent’s whereabouts in Indiana are unknown, the principal is deemed to have appointed the Secretary of State, who may be served under Rule 4.10.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4.8. Summons: service of pleadings or summons on Attorney General","Indiana · civil rules","Service of a summons, complaint or any pleading on the Attorney General is made personally on the Attorney General, a deputy or a clerk at the office, or by mail or other public means to that office under Rule 4.1(A)(1) and, as applicable, Rule 4.11.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4.9. Summons: in rem actions","Indiana · civil rules","In an action involving a res located in Indiana, the court may enter judgment to the extent of its jurisdiction over the res. Service may be made by summons on the person or agent under these rules, by service outside Indiana under Rule 4.1 or other rules, or by publication under Rule 4.13 (inside or outside Indiana).","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4.10. Summons: service upon Secretary of State or other governmental agent","Indiana · civil rules","When the Secretary of State or another governmental officer is served as agent, the party must request that service in the praecipe, state the person’s recorded or last known address (or that none is known), and pay any statutory fee, and the clerk forwards copies to the agent. The agent must promptly send the summons and complaint by registered or certified mail or other public means with written acknowledgment of r","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4.11. Summons: registered or certified mail","Indiana · civil rules","When service by registered or certified mail or other public means with a return receipt is authorized, the clerk (or a Rule 4.10 governmental agent) mails the summons and complaint to the address on the summons or supplied by the party. The return shows the date and place of mailing and the return receipt, including whether and by whom it was accepted, and is filed as part of the record; if the clerk’s mailing comes","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4.12. Summons: service by sheriff or other officer","Indiana · civil rules","Personal and abode service under Rule 4.1 is made by the sheriff, a deputy, or a person appointed by the court; service by an unauthorized person is still effective, but that person must prove it as a witness or by deposition with no expenses taxed as costs. A sheriff, deputy, or full-time state or municipal police officer may agree to serve summons in any Indiana county (but not by agreement with the sheriff in the ","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4.13. Summons: service by publication","Indiana · civil rules","Where publication is permitted, the party requests it in the praecipe with affidavits of diligent search showing the defendant cannot be found, has concealed their whereabouts, or has left the state, and one summons may name all persons served. The summons must describe the suit and any res and state that the defendant must respond within 30 days after the last publication or face default. It is published 3 times, th","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4.14. Service under special order of court","Indiana · civil rules","On a verified motion showing that prior attempts at service under the rules have failed, the court may order service in any other manner reasonably calculated to give the defendant actual knowledge of the proceedings and an opportunity to be heard. The rule expressly allows social media, email, or other technology.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4.15. Summons: proof of service—return—amendments—defects","Indiana · civil rules","The server must promptly sign and deliver a return to the clerk stating the time, place and manner of service or, if service failed, how it was thwarted; the return, praecipe and affidavits become part of the record, and the clerk’s filing and issuance dates are evidence without further authentication. A signed written admission of service stating the date and place is evidence of proper service. The court may allow ","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4.16. Summons: duties of persons to aid in service","Indiana · civil rules","Everyone being served must cooperate, accept service, and acknowledge personal service in writing; offering or tendering the papers and telling the person they are being served is adequate, and a person who refuses tender may not later challenge that service. A person who accepts service for another must promptly deliver the papers or notify that person, or within a reasonable time notify the clerk or server in writi","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 4.17. Summons: certain proceedings excepted","Indiana · civil rules","Rules 4 through 4.16 do not replace special statutory or rule-based methods of service or notice in proceedings such as decedents’ estates, guardianships, receiverships, and assignments for the benefit of creditors.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 5. Service and filing of pleadings, documents, and other papers","Indiana · civil rules","Every party and any special judge must be served with later pleadings, written non-ex parte motions, briefs, discovery papers, notices, appearances, offers of judgment and similar papers; parties in default for failure to appear need not be served, except that new claims against them must be served as a summons under Rule 4. Service on a represented party is on the attorney, by delivery (refusing a tendered document ","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 6. Time","Indiana · civil rules","Periods exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday, legal holiday, or day the office is closed, and for periods of less than 7 days intermediate weekends, holidays and closed days are excluded. A party may get one automatic 30-day enlargement to respond to a complaint or other pleading by filing a notice, on or before the original due date, stating the original and enlarged due d","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings and motions","Indiana · civil rules","The only pleadings are a complaint and answer, a counterclaim and reply, a cross-claim and answer, and a third-party complaint and answer. Applications for orders must be by written motion (unless made at a hearing or trial) stating the grounds and relief and accompanied by a separate proposed order, and a hearing must be requested by separate written motion. A motion for continuance must be filed as soon as the caus","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","Indiana · civil rules","A claim requires a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for relief, but a complaint for personal injury, death, or punitive damages may not state any dollar amount. Denials may be general (subject to Rules 11 and 11.1) or specific, a lack of knowledge operates as a denial, and averments other than damages are admitted if not denied. Indiana’s list of affirmative defenses, which the ple","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","Indiana · civil rules","Capacity, authority, and legal existence need not be averred, and lack of them must be pleaded as an affirmative defense by the person asserting it. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with specificity while conditions of mind may be averred generally; performance of conditions precedent may be averred generally, but a denial must be specific and particular. Special damages must be specifically stated, and a default ju","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 9.1. Pleading and proof of contributory negligence, assumed risk, res ipsa loquitur, consideration, bona fide purchaser, matters of judicial notice—answer of distraint","Indiana · civil rules","The defendant bears the burden of pleading and proving contributory negligence, assumption of risk or incurred risk, and may plead them by denial. Res ipsa loquitur may be pleaded generally; lack of consideration for a written contract or release is an affirmative defense; and bona fide purchaser status must be pleaded and proved by the party asserting it, though once value and any recording or perfection are shown, ","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 9.2. Pleading and proof of written instruments","Indiana · civil rules","A pleading founded on a written instrument must include or attach the original or a copy; a claim on a written contract must attach a copy (unless not in the plaintiff’s custody), and a claim on an account must attach an Affidavit of Debt substantially like Appendix A-2. A non-original creditor suing on a consumer debt (other than a mortgage foreclosure) must also attach the signed original contract (or documents gen","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings, motions, memoranda, and briefs","Indiana · civil rules","Counsel’s filings must be on 8 1/2 by 11 inch pages (white paper, one side only, when filed conventionally), in black type, using one of the listed fonts at 12-point or larger in text and footnotes, double-spaced (with single-spaced footnotes, tables and block quotes separated by at least 4-point spaces), numbered consecutively at the bottom starting with 1, with margins of at least one inch. Every pleading needs a c","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 11. Parties represented by an attorney: signing, certification, and verification","Indiana · civil rules","Every submission by a represented party must be signed by at least one attorney of record with address, telephone number and attorney number, and need not be verified unless a rule requires. The signature certifies that the attorney read the filing, that factual contentions and denials have or will likely have evidentiary support, that legal contentions are warranted by existing law or a nonfrivolous argument, that a","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 11.1. Parties not represented by an attorney: signing, certification, and verification","Indiana · civil rules","A self-represented party must sign every submission and provide a mailing address, telephone number, and email address if available, except information protected by the Rules on Access to Court Records or replaceable by a public mailing address. The signature carries the same certifications as Rule 11, including that cited authority is authentic, and applies to filings prepared with artificial intelligence or other r","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 11.2. Abusive litigation","Indiana · civil rules","On motion or its own initiative, and after considering the filer’s litigation history, the court may find a filer to be an abusive litigant, based on conduct such as frivolous or repetitive claims, bad-faith litigation, citing fictitious legal authority, or egregious rule violations. The court may then impose tailored restrictions, including requiring sworn affidavits with filings, lists of prior related cases, expla","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses, objections, and motions on the pleadings","Indiana · civil rules","Defenses may be made by motion for lack of subject matter or personal jurisdiction, incorrect venue under Rule 75, insufficiency of process or service, failure to state a claim (including failure to name the real party in interest), failure to join a party under Rule 19, and, unlike the FRCP, the same action pending in another Indiana state court; such a motion must be made before pleading or within 20 days after ser","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and cross-claim","Indiana · civil rules","A pleading must state as a counterclaim any claim against an opposing party arising from the same transaction or occurrence, unless it was already pending in another action or the opposing party sued by attachment without personal jurisdiction, and may state any other claim as a permissive counterclaim, which may exceed or differ from the opposing claim; cross-claims may be asserted against any co-party. After-acquir","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","Indiana · civil rules","A defending party may bring in a nonparty who is or may be liable to it for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim, but must file the third-party complaint with its original answer or later by leave of court for good cause. The third-party defendant may assert defenses, counterclaims and cross-claims against the third-party plaintiff, other parties and the plaintiff, the plaintiff may assert claims against it, and a pl","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Indiana · civil rules","A party may amend once as a matter of course before a responsive pleading is served or, if no response is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar, within 30 days after service; otherwise amendment requires leave (freely given when justice requires) or written consent, and a response to an amended pleading is due within the time remaining on the original or 20 days after service, whichever is longer. Issue","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 16. Pre-trial procedure: formulating issues","Indiana · civil rules","In any non-criminal case the court may, and on any party’s motion must, hold a pre-trial conference on issues such as simplification, amendments, admissions, limits on expert witnesses, witness exchange, discovery orders, alternative dispute resolution, and dispositive motion deadlines. The clerk gives at least 30 days’ notice, at least 1 trial attorney per party must attend, and at least 10 days before the conferenc","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 17. Parties plaintiff and defendant—capacity","Indiana · civil rules","Actions must be prosecuted by the real party in interest, though executors, guardians, trustees and similar representatives may sue in their own names, and no dismissal may occur until a reasonable time after objection has been allowed for ratification, joinder or substitution. Capacity is governed by Indiana law, and partnerships and unincorporated associations may sue or be sued in their common names, but a money j","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 17.1. Parties: state as party—Attorney General","Indiana · civil rules","When a pleading in a real property action (such as foreclosure, sale, partition, or quiet title) shows the State of Indiana claims a lien or interest, the state may be made a defendant, is bound by the judgment, and is served through the Attorney General under Rule 4.8. The Attorney General must defend and may designate the local prosecuting attorney as deputy, after which Rule 5 service goes to that prosecutor, and ","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims and remedies","Indiana · civil rules","A party asserting any claim may join as many legal, equitable, or statutory claims, independent or alternative, as it has against an opposing party. Claims that once had to await the outcome of another claim may be joined in one action, and a plaintiff may seek money and to set aside a fraudulent conveyance without first obtaining a money judgment.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 19. Joinder of person needed for just adjudication","Indiana · civil rules","A person subject to service must be joined if complete relief cannot be given without them or if their absence may impair their interest or expose existing parties to inconsistent obligations, and the court must order joinder (a refusing plaintiff may be made a defendant). If joinder is not made, the court weighs prejudice, ways to lessen it, adequacy of a judgment, and the plaintiff’s alternative remedies to decide ","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","Indiana · civil rules","Persons may join as plaintiffs, or be joined as defendants, when the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence or series and share a common question of law or fact, and judgment may be given for or against fewer than all. Unwilling plaintiffs may be joined as defendants, and a defendant may bring in anyone who could be joined by alleging their interest. The court may order separate trials or other protective","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and non-joinder of parties; venue and jurisdiction over the subject-matter","Indiana · civil rules","Misjoinder or non-joinder is not grounds for dismissal; the court may add or drop parties at any stage on just terms, sever claims, and allow misnomers to be corrected under Rule 15 at any time. If the court has venue or authority over any claim between the parties, it has venue and authority over all claims and persons joined, impleaded, or brought in by intervention, interpleader, counterclaim or cross-claim. It ma","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","Indiana · civil rules","A plaintiff or defendant exposed to double or multiple liability may require adverse claimants to interplead even if their claims lack a common origin or the party denies liability, and the rule extends but does not reduce statutory interpleader. The complaint or answer must admit or describe the asserted liability, allege the risk of multiple liability, and pray that claimants litigate among themselves; it may show ","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","Indiana · civil rules","A class action requires numerosity, common questions, typicality, and adequate representation, plus one of the three familiar categories (risk of inconsistent or dispositive individual adjudications, generally applicable injunctive or declaratory relief, or predominance and superiority). The court decides certification as soon as practicable, (B)(3) members must receive the best practicable notice and may opt out, th","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 23.1. Derivative actions by shareholders","Indiana · civil rules","A shareholder or member derivative complaint must be verified and allege that the plaintiff held the interest at the time of the transaction (or acquired it by operation of law) and, with particularity, the efforts made to obtain action from the directors or comparable authority and why they failed or were not made. The action cannot proceed if the plaintiff does not fairly and adequately represent similarly situated","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 23.2. Actions relating to unincorporated associations","Indiana · civil rules","Besides suing or being sued in its common name under Rule 17(E), an unincorporated association’s members may be sued as a class through named representative members if they will fairly and adequately protect the association’s and members’ interests. The court may issue orders like those under Rule 23(D), and dismissal or compromise follows Rule 23(E).","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","Indiana · civil rules","On timely motion, intervention is of right when a statute gives an unconditional right or the applicant has an interest in the property, fund or transaction that may be impaired and is not adequately represented. Permissive intervention is allowed when a statute gives a conditional right, the applicant’s claim or defense shares a common question of law or fact, or a governmental unit administers a statute or order re","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","Indiana · civil rules","If a party dies and the claim survives, the court may order substitution on motion by the court, any party, or the decedent’s successors or representatives, served on parties under Rule 5 and on nonparties as a summons under Rule 4, before or after judgment, with no fixed deadline stated in the rule; if the right survives only to or against remaining parties, the death is suggested on the record and the case proceeds","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 26. General provisions governing discovery","Indiana · civil rules","Discovery may be had by depositions, interrogatories, production and entry requests, physical and mental examinations, and requests for admission, covering any non-privileged matter relevant to any party’s claim or defense (inadmissible information is discoverable if reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence), and the court must limit cumulative or overly burdensome discovery; unlike the FRCP, the rule has","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions before action or pending appeal","Indiana · civil rules","A person expecting to be a party to an action may file a verified petition to perpetuate testimony, and must serve notice of the hearing on each expected adverse party at least 20 days before the hearing in the manner provided for a summons. While an appeal is pending, or before one is taken while the time has not expired, the trial court may allow depositions to perpetuate testimony for use in later proceedings. Fil","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken; discovery across state lines; before administrative agencies; and after judgment","Indiana · civil rules","Depositions in the United States are taken before an officer authorized to administer oaths or a court-appointed person, and abroad by notice, commission, or letter rogatory; the officer may not be a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or have a financial interest. Indiana courts may order discovery for use by tribunals outside the state. Parties to adjudicatory hearings before administrative agencies may use ","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","Indiana · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, the parties may by written stipulation provide that depositions be taken before any person, at any time or place, on any notice, and in any manner, and may modify the procedures for other discovery methods.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions upon oral examination","Indiana · civil rules","Any party may depose any person after the action begins, but the plaintiff needs leave of court to take a deposition within 20 days after service of the summons and complaint, unless the defendant has already sought discovery or the plaintiff gives a signed special notice that the witness is leaving the state or will become unavailable. For an organization deposition, the parties must confer in good faith about the t","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 31. Deposition of witnesses upon written questions","Indiana · civil rules","Any party may depose any person on written questions served on all parties with a notice. Cross questions may be served within 20 days after the notice, redirect questions within 10 days after cross questions, and recross questions within 10 days after redirect questions, and the court may enlarge or shorten these times. The officer takes the answers and prepares and certifies the deposition as under T.R. 30.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 32. Use of depositions in court proceedings","Indiana · civil rules","A deposition may be used to impeach the deponent, and a party’s (or a party organization’s officer’s or designee’s) deposition may be used by an adverse party for any purpose. Any witness’s deposition may be used for any purpose if the witness is dead, outside the state (unlike the FRCP’s 100-mile test), unable to attend due to age, sickness, infirmity, or imprisonment, cannot be subpoenaed, in exceptional circumstan","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories to parties","Indiana · civil rules","Any party may serve interrogatories on another party, on a defendant with or after the summons and complaint, and the text sets no numerical limit (unlike the FRCP’s 25). Answers are due within the period stated in the interrogatories, not less than 30 days, each answer or objection must restate the interrogatory immediately before it, and general objections are not allowed and have no effect. A party may object to a","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 34. Production of documents, electronically stored information, and things; entry upon land","Indiana · civil rules","A party may request production of documents, ESI, and tangible things or entry upon land, and the request may be served on a defendant with or after the summons and complaint. The response is due within the period stated in the request, not less than 30 days, general objections are barred, and each objection must say whether materials are being withheld. A request to a non-party must be served by subpoena, and a copy","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examination of persons","Indiana · civil rules","When a party’s mental or physical condition (including blood group), or that of a person in its custody or legal control, is in controversy, the court may on motion for good cause order an examination by a suitably licensed or certified examiner. On request, the party causing the examination must provide the examiner’s detailed report, and is then entitled to like reports from the examined party. Requesting the repor","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","Indiana · civil rules","Requests for admission may be served, without leave, no earlier than 10 days after the issues are first closed on the merits, or 30 days after the action begins if no responsive pleading is required, and must be in a separate document from any other pleading or discovery. A matter is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within the period stated in the request, not less than 30 days, and no motion t","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to make or cooperate in discovery: sanctions","Indiana · civil rules","A party may move to compel discovery, and the loser of the motion must ordinarily pay the winner’s reasonable expenses, including attorney’s fees, unless the position was substantially justified. Failure to obey a discovery order may lead to facts being taken as established, exclusion of evidence, striking of pleadings, dismissal, default, or contempt, plus expenses. For lost ESI that should have been preserved and c","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury trial of right","Indiana · civil rules","Issues in causes that were exclusively equitable before June 18, 1852, are tried by the court, while other issues remain triable by jury as before. A party must file and serve a written jury demand no later than 10 days after the first responsive pleading to the complaint, counterclaim, cross-claim, or other claim (or within 10 days after that pleading would have been due). A late demand may be granted only on the wr","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","Indiana · civil rules","Issues on which a jury has been demanded are tried by jury unless the parties stipulate to a bench trial before evidence is admitted, or later if the court allows. On issues without a proper jury demand or jury right, the court may use an advisory jury, or with both sides’ consent may give the verdict binding effect. Findings of fact are not required to the extent the judge’s decision follows the verdict of a properl","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 40. Assignment of cases for trial","Indiana · civil rules","Each trial court must adopt a rule for placing cases on the trial calendar, with precedence for actions entitled to it by statute, including hearings on temporary restraining orders, injunctions, and receiverships. The rule has no subdivision (B).","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","Indiana · civil rules","A plaintiff may dismiss without a court order by notice before the adverse party serves an answer or a motion for summary judgment, or by a stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared, and a second notice of dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits. In a bench trial, after the party with the burden rests, the opposing party may move for involuntary dismissal on the weight of the e","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials","Indiana · civil rules","The court may consolidate actions with common questions of law or fact, order separate trials, and submit a case to the jury in stages, such as bifurcating compensatory and punitive damages. Unlike the FRCP, actions with common questions pending in different Indiana courts may be consolidated for discovery and pretrial proceedings by motion in the court with the earliest-filed action, and are then returned unless the","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 43. Evidence","Indiana · civil rules","Testimony is taken in open court unless state law or Supreme Court rules provide otherwise, and motions on facts outside the record may be heard on affidavits, testimony, or depositions. The court may appoint and set compensation for an interpreter, in compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act. Unless the court directs otherwise, the party with the burden states its case first, then the adverse party, and e","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 44. Proof of official record","Indiana · civil rules","Proof of official records is governed by the Indiana Rules of Evidence.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","Indiana · civil rules","The clerk issues signed and sealed blank subpoenas, and an Indiana attorney who has appeared for a party may also issue and sign subpoenas, which may be served by the sheriff, a party, or any person. A resident may be required to attend a deposition only in the county of residence, employment, or business, and a non-resident only in the county of service or within 40 miles of the place of service, unless the court or","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 46. Exceptions unnecessary","Indiana · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary; it is enough to make known to the court the action desired or the objection and its grounds when the ruling is made or sought. A party who had no opportunity to object is not prejudiced by the lack of objection.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 47. Jurors and peremptory challenges","Indiana · civil rules","Civil juries have 6 members, and the court may seat up to 3 alternate jurors. Each side has 3 peremptory challenges, plus 1 more if 1 or 2 alternates are seated or 2 more if 3 alternates are seated, and the extra challenges may be used only against alternates. Unlike the FRCP, the court must allow the parties or their attorneys to question prospective jurors, though it may set an advance time limit and must liberally","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 48. Juries of less than six; majority verdict","Indiana · civil rules","The parties may stipulate, before the jury is selected, to a jury of fewer than 6 members, or, before the verdict is announced, that a verdict of a stated majority of jurors will be the verdict of the jury.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 49. Special verdicts and interrogatories","Indiana · civil rules","Special verdicts and interrogatories to the jury are abolished, unlike FRCP 49.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 50. Judgment on the evidence (directed verdict)","Indiana · civil rules","Indiana’s ‘judgment on the evidence’ replaces both the directed verdict and the JNOV: when issues are not supported by sufficient evidence or a verdict is clearly erroneous, the court must withdraw the issues from the jury or enter judgment notwithstanding the verdict. A party may move after the opponent rests, after the evidence on an issue or all evidence closes, or in a motion to correct error, and the court may a","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 51. Instructions to jury; objections; requests","Indiana · civil rules","After the jury is sworn, the court gives preliminary instructions under Jury Rule 20, and the parties may object outside the jury’s presence; final instructions follow Jury Rule 26. Each party may tender no more than 10 requested instructions, including pattern instructions, each limited to 1 legal principle, unless the court allows more for good cause. A party must object to an instruction before the jury retires, s","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings by the court","Indiana · civil rules","In bench trials or trials with an advisory jury, the court must make special findings of fact and conclusions on its own motion or on a party’s written request filed before evidence is admitted, and must make them without request in preliminary injunction rulings, agency reviews, and where a rule or statute requires. Findings and judgment are set aside on appeal only if clearly erroneous. The court may amend findings","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 53. Court-appointed neutrals","Indiana · civil rules","The court may appoint a court-appointed neutral (such as an attorney, senior judge, referee, auditor, examiner, commissioner, or other skilled person) if all parties consent, or without consent after notice and a hearing if it will materially help resolve the case and the costs are proportionate and not unduly burdensome; an attorney neutral must be licensed and in good standing in Indiana. Unless the order says othe","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 53.1. Failure to rule on motion","Indiana · civil rules","Indiana’s ‘lazy judge’ rule: if a court fails for 30 days to set a motion for hearing, or fails to rule within 30 days after hearing (or 30 days after filing if no hearing is required), a party may file a praecipe with notice to the Chief Administrative Officer of the Indiana Office of Judicial Administration to withdraw submission from the judge and have the Supreme Court appoint a special judge. ADR periods and pos","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 53.2. Time for holding issue under advisement; delay of entering a judgment","Indiana · civil rules","If a cause tried to the court (including a post-conviction petition) is taken under advisement and the judge fails to decide any issue of law or fact within 90 days, submission may be withdrawn and the case sent to the Supreme Court for appointment of a special judge. ADR and post-hearing submission periods are excluded, the parties may agree on the record that the limit does not apply, and extensions, withdrawal pro","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 53.3. Motion to correct error: time limitation for ruling","Indiana · civil rules","A motion to correct error (Indiana’s post-trial motion under T.R. 59) is deemed denied if the court fails for 45 days to set it for hearing, or fails to rule within 30 days after the hearing or 45 days after filing if no hearing is required. A notice of appeal must then be filed under Appellate Rule 9(A) within 30 days after the deemed denial. The judge may extend the ruling time once by up to 30 days by a written CC","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 53.4. Repetitive motions and motions to reconsider; automatic denial","Indiana · civil rules","No hearing is required on a repetitive motion or a motion to reconsider, and such a motion does not delay the trial or extend any other deadline. Unless ruled on within 5 days, it is deemed denied without notice. The rule does not apply to an original motion for judgment on the evidence after the jury is discharged, a T.R. 52(B) motion to amend findings, an original motion to correct error, a T.R. 60 motion for relie","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 53.5. [Vacated]","Indiana · civil rules","Vacated effective January 1, 2025.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgment; costs","Indiana · civil rules","A “judgment” includes a decree and any appealable order; in a case with multiple claims or parties, a judgment on fewer than all is final only if the court in writing expressly determines there is no just reason for delay and expressly directs entry of judgment. A default judgment may not differ in kind from or exceed the amount demanded, but other final judgments grant all relief the prevailing party is entitled to.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 55. Default","Indiana · civil rules","A party who fails to plead or otherwise comply with the rules may be defaulted by the court, and the party seeking a default judgment must apply to the court; no default judgment may be entered against a known infant or incompetent unless represented by a general guardian or similar representative who has appeared, or against a person protected by the Servicemembers Civil Relief Act unless the Act is satisfied. A def","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","Indiana · civil rules","A claimant may move for summary judgment any time after 20 days from commencement of the action (or after the adverse party moves), and a defending party may move at any time; the adverse party has 30 days after service of the motion to respond, and on a motion made no later than 10 days after the response was filed or due the court must hold a hearing set at least 10 days after the response deadline. Each side must ","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments","Indiana · civil rules","Declaratory judgment actions follow these rules, with jury trial available as provided in Rules 38 and 39. Another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, no property right need be involved, affirmative relief may be granted once the right is established, and the court may order a speedy hearing and advance the case on its calendar.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 58. Entry and content of judgment","Indiana · civil rules","The court must promptly prepare and sign the judgment on a separate document (or on the same document as its findings or opinion), and the clerk enters it in the Record of Judgments and Orders and notes it in the Chronological Case Summary and Judgment Docket; entry is not delayed for taxing costs, and a judge who fails to act may be compelled by mandate. Except in small claims, a judgment must state the nature of th","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 59. Motion to correct error","Indiana · civil rules","Indiana uses a single motion to correct error in place of the federal motions for new trial and to alter or amend; it may be filed by any party or by the court, and must be filed within 30 days after entry of the final judgment is noted in the Chronological Case Summary. The motion is not a prerequisite to appeal except to raise newly discovered material evidence (including jury misconduct) that could be produced wit","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from judgment or order","Indiana · civil rules","Clerical mistakes may be corrected by the trial court at any time before the Notice of Completion of Clerk’s Record is filed, and afterward with leave of the court on appeal. Relief may be granted for (1) mistake, surprise, or excusable neglect, (2) grounds for a motion to correct error such as newly discovered evidence, (3) fraud or misconduct, (4) default against a party served only by publication without actual kn","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 60.5. Mandate of funds","Indiana · civil rules","A trial court (other than the Supreme Court or Court of Appeals) seeking to compel a municipality, political subdivision, or officer to appropriate or pay funds reasonably necessary for court operations must first meet with the mandated party and then issue an “Order for Mandate of Funds” to show cause, followed by a bench trial before a special judge appointed by the Supreme Court who is not a current or former regu","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","Indiana · civil rules","No error in admitting or excluding evidence, or in any ruling or act of the court or parties, is grounds for correcting error, setting aside a verdict, disturbing a judgment, or reversal unless refusing to act would be inconsistent with substantial justice. The court must disregard any error that does not affect the parties’ substantial rights.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","Indiana · civil rules","Execution may issue once the judgment is noted in the Chronological Case Summary, but the court may stay enforcement pending a motion to correct error, a Rule 50, 52, or 60 motion, or an appeal, and may suspend, modify, or grant injunctions or receivership orders during an appeal on terms it sets. No bond is needed to perfect an appeal, but enforcement is stayed on appeal only on approval of an appeal bond, an irrevo","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 63. Disability and unavailability of a judge","Indiana · civil rules","The judge who presided at a trial or evidentiary hearing should decide post-trial matters, but if that judge is unavailable through death, sickness, absence, or unwillingness, another judge of the circuit or assigned to the case may act and may grant a new trial in whole or part if unable to perform the duties. On a verified petition from the judge, or from any person alleging the judge is disabled or neglecting the ","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 63.1. Lis pendens notice of proceedings avoiding judgments; assignments and discharges","Indiana · civil rules","Post-judgment proceedings to avoid a judgment (under Rules 50, 52, 59, 60(B), or the appellate rules) and the tolling or extension of a limitations period are ineffective against a good-faith purchaser of land, or a purchaser or lien creditor of personal property, unless lis pendens notice is filed in the county lis pendens record (for land) or by a financing statement (for personal property); the notice must be sign","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 64. Seizure of person or property","Indiana · civil rules","All remedies for seizing persons or property to secure a judgment (arrest, attachment, garnishment, lis pendens, replevin, ejectment, sequestration, and the like) are available as provided by law; a writ of attachment, bench warrant, or body attachment may issue only after a rule to show cause is personally served on the judgment debtor (including certified mail signed by the debtor), a return of service is filed, an","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions","Indiana · civil rules","No preliminary injunction may issue without notice and an opportunity for a hearing, the court may consolidate the hearing with trial on the merits, and if no order is entered within 10 days after the hearing the matter becomes subject to Rule 53.1; if the assigned judge is unavailable, a party may apply by affidavit to another judge in the circuit or an adjoining circuit. A temporary restraining order without notice","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 65.1. Security: proceedings against sureties","Indiana · civil rules","A surety on any bond or undertaking given in a court proceeding submits to the court’s jurisdiction and irrevocably appoints the clerk as agent for service. The surety’s liability may be enforced by motion without a separate action, with the clerk mailing copies to sureties whose addresses are known; this applies to appeal bonds, enforced in the court to which the case returns.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers, assignees, and liquidators; claims","Indiana · civil rules","An action in which a receiver, assignee for the benefit of creditors, or other liquidator is appointed may not be dismissed without court order, and the court may (or on petition must) set a deadline for the debtor or its officers to file an itemized affidavit of all assets, liabilities, and known creditors, which the liquidator prepares if they do not. The officer must give notice of appointment by publication and m","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit in court; payment of judgment","Indiana · civil rules","Before judgment in an action involving money or a deliverable thing, a party may, on notice and with leave of court, deposit all or part of it with the court for later payment as the court directs. A money judgment may be paid to the judgment creditor or the creditor’s attorney, the sheriff holding execution, or the clerk, and the person receiving payment must furnish a signed, acknowledged statement of full or parti","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of judgment","Indiana · civil rules","A party defending against a claim may serve an offer of judgment, with costs then accrued, at any time more than 10 days before trial begins (the federal rule uses 14 days), and the offeree has 10 days after service to accept in writing, after which either party may file the offer and acceptance and the clerk enters judgment. An unaccepted offer is deemed withdrawn and inadmissible except on costs, and if the offeree","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution, proceedings supplemental, foreclosure sales","Indiana · civil rules","Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution, but real estate may not be sold until 6 months after the judgment or execution becomes a lien, and execution sales of real estate follow mortgage-foreclosure procedures without a right of redemption after sale; judgment and execution liens on after-acquired property share pro rata, and other judicial sales generally follow the procedure for sales in decedents’ estate","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 70. Judgment for specific acts; vesting title; recordation","Indiana · civil rules","If a party fails to perform an act ordered by judgment, such as conveying land or delivering documents, the court may have another person perform it at the disobedient party’s cost with the same effect, may issue attachment, assistance, or sequestration, may hold the party in contempt and award damages, or may simply enter a judgment divesting and vesting title. A judgment for possession entitles the party to a writ ","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 71. Process for and against nonparties","Indiana · civil rules","A nonparty in whose favor an order is made may enforce it by the same process as a party. A nonparty against whom an order may lawfully be enforced is subject to the same enforcement process as a party.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 72. Trial courts and clerks","Indiana · civil rules","Trial courts are always open for filing papers and issuing process, terms of court are not recognized, trials on the merits are held in open court, and other proceedings may be held in chambers anywhere, though no hearing other than ex parte may be held outside Indiana without all affected parties’ consent. The clerk’s office is open on business days, and the clerk must immediately serve a copy of every ruling, order","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 73. Hearing of motions","Indiana · civil rules","Each judge should set regular times and places for hearing motions, and may order the advancement and hearing of actions at any time on reasonable notice. The court may decide motions without oral hearing on brief written statements, or hold hearings by telephone conference or similar means.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 74. Recording proceedings; transcripts; audio recordings","Indiana · civil rules","Circuit, superior, probate, city, town, and Marion County Small Claims courts must audio-record all hearings and trials in all case types, including all testimony, rulings, and objections; stenography is allowed if the audio requirement is also met. A transcript for appeal is requested through the Notice of Appeal under the Appellate Rules, and transcripts or audio copies for other purposes are requested by written r","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 75. Venue requirements","Indiana · civil rules","Any case may be filed and decided in any court in any county, but on a timely Rule 12(B)(3) motion or pleading the court must transfer the case to a county or court of “preferred venue” selected by the first party properly moving; preferred venue includes, among 10 listed categories, the county where most individual defendants reside, where the land or chattels are located, where a vehicle accident occurred, where a ","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 76. Change of venue or judge in civil actions","Indiana · civil rules","Each party in a civil case is entitled to one change of judge, granted automatically on an unverified motion stating no grounds, with the new judge chosen under Rule 79; a change of venue from the county requires a verified motion showing the county is a party or that local prejudice makes a fair trial unlikely, and denial on fair-trial grounds is reviewed only for abuse of discretion. After venue change is granted, ","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 77. Court records","Indiana · civil rules","The circuit court clerk keeps the records for all circuit, superior, and probate courts in the county, including statutory records (lis pendens, judgment docket, execution docket, and others), the Chronological Case Summary (CCS), case files, the Record of Judgments and Orders (RJO), and indexes, in paper, microfilm, or electronic form. The judge must cause prompt CCS entries of all judicial events, dated the day the","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 78. Jurisdiction pending change from county","Indiana · civil rules","Once a change of venue to another county is ordered and any required costs are paid, either party may file a certified copy of the order in the receiving court, which then has full jurisdiction even before the transcript and papers arrive. The original court keeps jurisdiction to decide emergency matters between the filing of the venue motion and the order granting it.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 79. Special judge selection: circuit, superior, and probate courts","Indiana · civil rules","This rule is the exclusive method for selecting a special judge in circuit, superior, and probate court civil and juvenile cases after a change of judge under Rule 76 or a mandatory recusal (when the judge, the judge’s spouse, a relative within the third degree, or a household member is a party, a lawyer in the case, has an interest that could be substantially affected, or is otherwise disqualified under the Code of ","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 79.1. Special judge selection: city, town, and Marion County small claims courts","Indiana · civil rules","This rule is the exclusive method for selecting special judges in city, town, and Marion County small claims courts, and unlike Rule 76(B) a motion for change of judge in these courts must be verified and signed by the party, stating facts supporting the statutory basis. The parties may agree on an eligible special judge, or consent to selection by the presiding judge, and the selected judge has 20 days to accept jur","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 80. Procedure for amending rules","Indiana · civil rules","Proposed amendments to the Indiana Rules of Court are submitted to the Supreme Court’s Chief Administrative Officer in a Word-compatible format with added and deleted language marked, along with the form on the Court’s website, and are referred to the appropriate committee. The Rules Committee publishes proposals on or before January 2, April 1, July 1, and October 1 each year (and at other times as needed), accepts ","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 81. Local court rules","Indiana · civil rules","Courts and administrative districts may adopt local rules that neither conflict with nor duplicate the statewide rules, but may not regulate practice by standing orders. Proposed local rules must be noticed to the bar and public (including local bar association officers), sent to the county clerk and the Office of Judicial Administration for posting, and open for comment for at least 30 days on a uniform annual sched","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 81.1. Procedures for cases involving family or household members","Indiana · civil rules","Courts may use “Family Procedures” to coordinate and hear together cases involving family or household members (current or former spouses or domestic partners, those with a child in common, blood or adoptive relatives, and guardians, wards, custodians, or foster parents), keeping separate case numbers or using a common number before one judge, though guardianship cases may not be consolidated into a common number. Th","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 82. Forms","Indiana · civil rules","Forms adopted by the Supreme Court or the Coalition for Court Access are sufficient under the rules. They must be accepted for filing, subject to Rule 88.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 83. Definitions","Indiana · civil rules","Defines “court on appeal” (the Indiana Supreme Court or Court of Appeals), “executive” and “executive officer” of an organization (including, for service purposes, a personal secretary or employee entrusted with legal papers), “governmental organization,” “governmental representative,” “organization,” and “representative.” “Signature” or “signed” includes an electronic reproduction of a handwritten signature.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 84. [Vacated]","Indiana · civil rules","Vacated, effective July 15, 2021.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 85. [Vacated]","Indiana · civil rules","Vacated, effective February 1, 1996.","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 86. General electronic filing and electronic service","Indiana · civil rules","Defines the terms used in Rules 86-88, including the Indiana E-Filing System (IEFS), E-Filing (which excludes fax and email), E-Filing Service Providers, the Notice of Electronic Filing (NEF), and Users, who must sign a User Agreement with an approved provider. When filing the initial complaint, the User also files completed summonses, which the clerk dates, signs, seals, and returns for the User to serve (the clerk ","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 87. Electronic filing","Indiana · civil rules","An action must be commenced through the IEFS (unless exempt) by paying the filing fee (unless waived) and filing the complaint with the required summonses, and all documents must be e-filed unless the rules provide otherwise; an attorney may be exempted only by a petition filed in each pending case and granted for good cause, while unrepresented litigants are not required (though encouraged) to e-file until the Supre","#st-in-rules"],["Rule 88. Court and clerk electronic filing review","Indiana · civil rules","The clerk may reject an e-filed document only if the filing fee was not paid, the wrong case management system was selected, or the User requested rejection; a document that cannot be processed electronically is returned and may be cured within 3 business days, and other non-conforming documents are processed and then returned for correction. If the court finds an e-filed document non-conforming, it must issue an ord","#st-in-rules"],["Appeals in Indiana: file with the appellate Clerk in 30 days, then most cases end in the Court of Appeals","Indiana · appeals","Almost every civil appeal goes to the Court of Appeals; the Supreme Court hears only a short list of mandatory appeals and otherwise acts by discretionary transfer. The appeal starts with a Notice of Appeal filed with the Clerk of the Indiana Supreme Court, Court of Appeals and Tax Court (not the trial court clerk) within 30 days after the final judgment is noted in the Chronological Case Summary (CCS). A late notice","#st-in-appeals"],["What is a final judgment","Indiana · appeals · Getting into the Court of Appeals","A judgment is final if it disposes of all claims as to all parties; if the trial court in writing expressly finds no just reason for delay and expressly directs entry of judgment under T.R. 54(B) or 56(C) as to fewer than all claims or parties; if deemed final under T.R. 60(C); if it rules on a timely motion to correct error; or if otherwise deemed final by law. Ind. App. R. 2(H)","#st-in-appeals"],["Notice of Appeal: where, when, and the fee","Indiana · appeals · Getting into the Court of Appeals","File the Notice of Appeal with the appellate Clerk within 30 days after entry of the final judgment is noted in the CCS. Pay the $250 filing fee when the notice is filed (none for in forma pauperis or governmental appellants); the Clerk will not file other papers until it is paid. Unless the notice is timely, the right to appeal is forfeited except under Post-Conviction Rule 2. Ind. App. R. 9(A)(1), (A)(5), 9(E)","#st-in-appeals"],["Contents of the notice","Indiana · appeals · Getting into the Court of Appeals","The notice designates the order appealed and the basis for jurisdiction (final judgment, interlocutory appeal of right, accepted discretionary interlocutory appeal, or expedited appeal), directs assembly of the Clerk’s Record, and requests the Transcript; if you will argue a finding is unsupported by the evidence, request a Transcript of all the evidence. Attach the judgment (with findings and conclusions), the order","#st-in-appeals"],["Motion to correct error and the deemed-denial trap","Indiana · appeals · Getting into the Court of Appeals","A motion to correct error is due 30 days after entry of the final judgment is noted in the CCS and cannot be extended. If one is timely filed, the Notice of Appeal is due 30 days after the ruling is noted in the CCS or 30 days after it is deemed denied under T.R. 53.3, whichever comes first. It is deemed denied if not set for hearing within 45 days, or not ruled on within 30 days after the hearing or 45 days after fi","#st-in-appeals"],["When a motion to correct error is required","Indiana · appeals · Getting into the Court of Appeals","It is optional for most issues, but it is a prerequisite to appeal for newly discovered material evidence (including jury misconduct) and for a claim that a jury verdict is excessive or inadequate. Ind. T.R. 59(A)","#st-in-appeals"],["Cross-appeals and joint appeals","Indiana · appeals · Getting into the Court of Appeals","An appellee may cross-appeal without filing a notice of appeal by raising cross-appeal issues in the appellee’s brief. But if no other party appeals, a party must file its own Notice of Appeal to preserve its right. Parties entitled to appeal from the same judgment may file a joint notice and proceed as one appellant. Ind. App. R. 9(C), (D)","#st-in-appeals"],["Interlocutory appeals of right","Indiana · appeals · Getting into the Court of Appeals","These orders are appealable as of right by a Notice of Appeal filed within 30 days after the order is noted in the CCS: orders for the payment of money; to compel execution of a document; to compel delivery or assignment of securities, evidence of debt, documents, or things in action; for the sale or delivery of possession of real property; granting, refusing, dissolving, or refusing to dissolve a preliminary injunct","#st-in-appeals"],["Discretionary interlocutory appeals: two motions","Indiana · appeals · Getting into the Court of Appeals","Move the trial court to certify the order within 30 days after it is noted in the CCS (a belated motion needs good cause and a finding); responses are due in 15 days, and the motion is deemed denied if not set within 30 days or not ruled on within 30 days after hearing or filing. If certified, move the Court of Appeals to accept jurisdiction within 30 days after the certification is noted in the CCS (response in 15 d","#st-in-appeals"],["Interlocutory appeals do not stay the case","Indiana · appeals · Getting into the Court of Appeals","An interlocutory appeal does not stay trial-court proceedings unless the trial court or a Court of Appeals judge orders a stay, which may require a bond. Brief extensions in interlocutory appeals are disfavored, and a motion to shorten time is due within 10 days of the notice or acceptance motion. Ind. App. R. 14(G), (H)","#st-in-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP: appeal for failure to rule","Indiana · appeals · Getting into the Court of Appeals","Under the anti-SLAPP statute, if the trial court does not act on the motion to dismiss within 30 days after the submission of the evidence discovered in the expedited period, the movant may appeal based on the failure to rule. The statute itself gives no other interlocutory appeal. Ind. Code § 34-7-7-9(e)–(f)","#st-in-appeals"],["Stays pending appeal","Indiana · appeals · Getting into the Court of Appeals","An appeal does not stay the judgment unless a court orders it. You must first ask the trial court (or agency); the Court of Appeals considers a stay only after denial, a failure to rule within a reasonable time, or extraordinary circumstances, and the motion must include certified or verified copies of the order and ruling plus service and notice certificates. The court may set a bond under App.R. 18. Ind. App. R. 39","#st-in-appeals"],["Clerk’s Record and Transcript","Indiana · appeals · Record and briefing","The trial court clerk assembles the Clerk’s Record within 30 days after the Notice of Appeal and files a Notice of Completion of Clerk’s Record. The court reporter has 45 days after the Notice of Appeal to file the Transcript, and the clerk serves a Notice of Completion of Transcript within 5 days after it is filed. Within 10 days after the notice, a party must arrange payment of the balance of the transcript cost (a","#st-in-appeals"],["Appendices filed with the briefs","Indiana · appeals · Record and briefing","Each party files its own Appendix of the record parts needed for the appeal on or before the day its brief is filed; supplemental appendices are allowed without leave until the final reply brief. Leaving something out of an Appendix does not waive any issue. Ind. App. R. 49(A)–(B), 50","#st-in-appeals"],["Briefing schedule","Indiana · appeals · Record and briefing","Appellant’s brief: 30 days after service of the Notice of Completion of Clerk’s Record (if the Transcript is complete or none was requested) or otherwise of the Notice of Completion of Transcript; the 3-day mail extension does not apply to this deadline. Appellee’s brief: 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief. Reply: 15 days after the appellee’s brief (30 days if it is also the cross-appellee’s brief). Cross","#st-in-appeals"],["Length limits","Indiana · appeals · Record and briefing","Without a word count, briefs are limited to 30 pages (appellant and appellee), 15 pages (reply; amicus), or 30 pages (reply plus cross-appellee). With a word-count certificate, the limits are 14,000 words (appellant, appellee, reply plus cross-appellee), 7,000 words (reply, amicus), and 4,200 words for petitions for rehearing or transfer and responses (1,000 words for a transfer reply). Headings and footnotes count. ","#st-in-appeals"],["Extensions of time","Indiana · appeals · Record and briefing","A verified motion for extension must be filed at least 7 days before the deadline unless the movant was unaware of the facts, and never after the deadline; it must give the dates, the requested business-day due date, and the reason (identified conflicting litigation, complexity, or hardship). No extensions are allowed for rehearing or transfer petitions or responses, or in termination-of-parental-rights appeals; in w","#st-in-appeals"],["Computing time","Indiana · appeals · Record and briefing","Exclude the trigger day; if the last day is a Saturday, Sunday, legal holiday, or a day the Clerk’s office is closed, the period runs to the next business day. For periods under 7 days, exclude all non-business days. Service by mail or commercial carrier adds 3 calendar days to responses, but not to rehearing or transfer deadlines or when service is by E-Service. Ind. App. R. 25(A)–(C)","#st-in-appeals"],["Defective filings","Indiana · appeals · Record and briefing","A non-compliant document is stamped ‘received’ but not filed, and the Clerk sends a Notice of Defect. Most filers have 10 business days to cure (20 for unrepresented incarcerated persons); a timely cure is deemed filed as of the corrected filing and treated as timely. Ind. App. R. 23(D)","#st-in-appeals"],["Opinions and memorandum decisions","Indiana · appeals · After the Court of Appeals decides","Court of Appeals cases that do not meet the publication criteria are decided by memorandum decision. Memorandum decisions issued on or after January 1, 2023 may be cited for persuasive value; earlier ones may be cited only for res judicata, collateral estoppel, or law of the case. A party may move to publish within 15 days after the decision. Published Court of Appeals opinions bind all Indiana trial courts. Ind. App","#st-in-appeals"],["Rehearing","Indiana · appeals · After the Court of Appeals decides","A petition for rehearing may be sought from a published opinion, a memorandum decision, an order dismissing an appeal, or an order declining to authorize a successive post-conviction petition, but not from an order denying transfer. It is due 30 days after the decision with no extensions and no 3-day mail extension. No response is required unless the court asks (response due 15 days after service or the request), and","#st-in-appeals"],["Certification of the decision","Indiana · appeals · After the Court of Appeals decides","The trial court and parties may not act in reliance on an appellate decision until it is certified, which happens only after the time for rehearing, transfer, or review expires (or after the Supreme Court finally disposes of a granted transfer), unless all parties ask for earlier certification. Ind. App. R. 65(E)","#st-in-appeals"],["Mandatory jurisdiction","Indiana · appeals · Indiana Supreme Court","The Supreme Court has mandatory and exclusive jurisdiction over death-penalty appeals, appeals of final judgments declaring a state or federal statute unconstitutional in whole or part, parental-consent waiver appeals, and mandate-of-funds appeals. All other review is discretionary. Ind. App. R. 4(A)","#st-in-appeals"],["Petition to transfer","Indiana · appeals · Indiana Supreme Court","After an adverse Court of Appeals decision (opinion, memorandum decision, amendment of either, or dismissal order), file a petition to transfer within 45 days after the decision, or within 30 days after the ruling on rehearing if rehearing was sought, with a $125 fee (none for governmental or in forma pauperis parties). No extensions and no 3-day mail extension. Responses are due 20 days after service (or file a noti","#st-in-appeals"],["What the Court looks for","Indiana · appeals · Indiana Supreme Court","Transfer is discretionary. The listed considerations are a conflict among Court of Appeals decisions, a conflict with Supreme Court precedent, a conflict with U.S. Supreme Court or federal court of appeals decisions on an important federal question, an important undecided question of law or great public importance, Supreme Court precedent that needs reconsideration, and a significant departure from accepted law or pr","#st-in-appeals"],["Effect of granting or denying transfer","Indiana · appeals · Indiana Supreme Court","If transfer is granted, the Court of Appeals decision is vacated except parts the Supreme Court expressly adopts or summarily affirms, and the Supreme Court takes the whole appeal. Denial ends the litigation in the Supreme Court and has no other legal effect; rehearing of a denial is not allowed. An evenly divided Court denies transfer, or, after granting, reinstates the Court of Appeals decision. Ind. App. R. 58(A)–","#st-in-appeals"],["Emergency transfer before the Court of Appeals rules","Indiana · appeals · Indiana Supreme Court","In rare cases, on a verified motion showing a substantial question of law of great public importance and an emergency requiring speedy determination, the Supreme Court may take an appeal directly. Ind. App. R. 56(A)","#st-in-appeals"],["Mandatory e-filing in the appellate courts","Indiana · appeals · E-filing","Attorneys must file electronically through the Indiana E-Filing System (IEFS) under a user agreement with an e-filing service provider; an exemption requires a motion in each case, granted only for good cause. A document is filed at the time on the Notice of Electronic Filing, subject to payment of fees, and must be completed before midnight in the time zone of the court where the case is pending. E-Service on public","#st-in-appeals"],["Notice of Appeal (final judgment)","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the final judgment noted in the CCS.\nFiled with the appellate Clerk with a $250 fee. Untimely notice forfeits the appeal. Ind. App. R. 9(A)(1)","#st-in-appeals"],["Motion to correct error","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the final judgment noted in the CCS.\nCannot be extended (T.R. 6(C)). Opposing statement due 15 days after service. Ind. T.R. 59(C)","#st-in-appeals"],["Notice of Appeal after a motion to correct error","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from ruling noted in the CCS or deemed denial under T.R. 53.3, whichever is first.\nDeemed denied if not set within 45 days, or not ruled on within 30 days after hearing or 45 days after filing without a hearing. Ind. App. R. 9(A)(1); Ind. T.R. 53.3","#st-in-appeals"],["Notice of Appeal (agency judicial review in the Court of Appeals)","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from date of the agency order, ruling, or decision.\nApplies notwithstanding any statute to the contrary. Ind. App. R. 9(A)(3)","#st-in-appeals"],["Interlocutory appeal of right","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from notation of the interlocutory order in the CCS.\nIncludes orders for the payment of money and preliminary injunctions. Ind. App. R. 14(A)","#st-in-appeals"],["Motion to certify interlocutory order (trial court)","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from notation of the order in the CCS.\nResponse due 15 days after service; deemed denied if the court does not act within 30 days. Ind. App. R. 14(B)(1)(a)","#st-in-appeals"],["Motion asking the Court of Appeals to accept interlocutory appeal","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from notation of the trial court’s certification in the CCS.\nResponse due 15 days after service. Ind. App. R. 14(B)(2)(a)","#st-in-appeals"],["Notice of Appeal after acceptance of discretionary interlocutory appeal","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from court of Appeals order accepting jurisdiction. Ind. App. R. 14(B)(3), (C)(5)","#st-in-appeals"],["Class-certification interlocutory appeal motion","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from notation of the class-certification order in the CCS. Ind. App. R. 14(C)(1)","#st-in-appeals"],["Transcript payment agreement with the court reporter","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the Notice of Appeal.\nCourt reporter may require a 50% deposit. Ind. App. R. 9(H)","#st-in-appeals"],["Clerk’s Record assembled","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the Notice of Appeal. Ind. App. R. 10(B)","#st-in-appeals"],["Transcript filed by court reporter","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","45 days from filing of the Notice of Appeal.\nExtensions by the reporter’s motion under App.R. 35(A). Ind. App. R. 11(B)","#st-in-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the Notice of Completion of Clerk’s Record (if Transcript complete or not requested), otherwise of the Notice of Completion of Transcript.\nThe 3-day mail extension does not apply. Appendix due the same day (App.R. 49(A)). Ind. App. R. 45(B)(1)","#st-in-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the appellant’s brief. Ind. App. R. 45(B)(2)","#st-in-appeals"],["Reply brief","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\n30 days if it is also the cross-appellee’s brief. Ind. App. R. 45(B)(3)","#st-in-appeals"],["Cross-appellant’s reply brief","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from service of the appellant’s reply brief. Ind. App. R. 45(B)(4)","#st-in-appeals"],["Motion for extension of time","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from before the deadline expires (at least 7 days before).\nNever after the deadline; none for rehearing or transfer. Ind. App. R. 35(A)","#st-in-appeals"],["Motion for oversized brief","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from before the brief is due (at least 15 days before).\nState words requested, not pages. Ind. App. R. 44(B)","#st-in-appeals"],["Motion to publish a memorandum decision","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from entry of the decision. Ind. App. R. 65(B)","#st-in-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from the decision.\nNo extensions; mail extension does not apply. Ind. App. R. 54(B)","#st-in-appeals"],["Petition to transfer (no rehearing sought)","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","45 days from the adverse Court of Appeals decision.\n$125 fee (App.R. 56(B)). No extensions. Ind. App. R. 57(C)(1)","#st-in-appeals"],["Petition to transfer (after rehearing)","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from court of Appeals disposition of the petition for rehearing.\nNo extensions. Ind. App. R. 57(C)(2)","#st-in-appeals"],["Response to petition to transfer","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from service of the petition.\nMail extension may apply; no other extension. Ind. App. R. 57(D)","#st-in-appeals"],["Reply on transfer","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from service of the response. Ind. App. R. 57(E)","#st-in-appeals"],["Cure of a Notice of Defect","Indiana · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from date of the Notice of Defect (business days).\n20 business days for unrepresented incarcerated persons. Ind. App. R. 23(D)(1)(a)","#st-in-appeals"],["Kansas Supreme Court","Kansas · courts","At least seven justices; every case is heard by at least four, and a decision needs a majority of those sitting and at least four votes. Original jurisdiction in quo warranto, mandamus, and habeas corpus; appellate jurisdiction as provided by law, including direct appeals when a statute is held unconstitutional and discretionary review of Court of Appeals decisions. It has general administrative authority over all Ka","#st-ks"],["Kansas Court of Appeals","Kansas · courts","Fourteen judges (K.S.A. 20-3002), appointed under K.S.A. 20-3020. Hears most civil appeals from the district courts and some agency appeals; cases may be transferred to the Supreme Court on motion within 30 days or on certification (K.S.A. 20-3001, 20-3016 to 20-3018). The Clerk of the Supreme Court is also its clerk (K.S.A. 20-3015).","#st-ks"],["District Courts","Kansas · courts","A district court sits in each county with general original jurisdiction over civil and criminal matters (K.S.A. 20-301), organized into 31 judicial districts (K.S.A. 4-202 through 4-232). District judges serve four-year terms and are either elected or chosen by nonpartisan merit selection depending on the district’s vote (Kan. Const. art. 3, § 6). Civil procedure is statutory: the Code of Civil Procedure, K.S.A. chap","#st-ks"],["District magistrate judges; limited actions","Kansas · courts","District magistrate judges sit within the district court. Absent consent, they may not hear civil cases over $10,000 (other than unsecured contract debt for goods, services, or money), injunctions, class actions, declaratory judgments, real-estate title or foreclosure, and other listed matters. Appeals from a magistrate who is not a lawyer go to a district judge within 14 days (K.S.A. 20-302b; 60-2103a). Smaller civi","#st-ks"],["Statute: Public Speech Protection Act","Kansas · anti-SLAPP","K.S.A. 60-5320, enacted 2016 (L. 2016, ch. 58, eff. July 1, 2016). It protects the exercise of free speech (a communication in connection with a public issue or issue of public interest, defined to include health or safety, environmental, economic or community well-being, government, public officials or figures, and marketplace goods or services), the right to petition (broadly defined, including communications in or","#st-ks-slapp"],["Motion to strike: 60 days; hearing within 30 days","Kansas · anti-SLAPP","A party may move to strike a claim (including a counterclaim or cross-claim) that is based on, relates to, or responds to its protected activity. The motion may be filed within 60 days of service of the most recent complaint or later in the court’s discretion. A hearing must be held not more than 30 days after service of the motion. K.S.A. 60-5320(c)(1), (d)","#st-ks-slapp"],["Burden shifting","Kansas · anti-SLAPP","The movant must make a prima facie showing that the claim concerns its exercise of speech, petition, or association. The burden then shifts to the responding party to establish a likelihood of prevailing by presenting substantial competent evidence of a prima facie case; if it does, the motion is denied. The court considers the pleadings and supporting and opposing affidavits. A determination that the responding part","#st-ks-slapp"],["Discovery and proceedings stayed","Kansas · anti-SLAPP","Filing the motion stays all discovery, motions, and other pending hearings until the ruling, except specified and limited discovery the court allows for good cause. K.S.A. 60-5320(e)","#st-ks-slapp"],["Mandatory fees and sanctions","Kansas · anti-SLAPP","If the movant prevails, the court must award costs and reasonable attorney fees, without regard to state-law limits, plus additional relief, including sanctions on the responding party and its attorneys and law firms, needed to deter repetition. If the motion is frivolous or solely for delay, the court must award the responding party its fees and costs on the motion. A ruling against a government contractor must be s","#st-ks-slapp"],["Appeal: 14 days; mandamus if no ruling","Kansas · anti-SLAPP","The movant may file an interlocutory appeal from an order denying the motion if the notice of appeal is filed within 14 days after entry, or petition for mandamus if the trial court fails to rule in an expedited fashion. Trial-court proceedings are stayed pending the appeal. K.S.A. 60-5320(f)","#st-ks-slapp"],["Exemptions","Kansas · anti-SLAPP","Does not apply to enforcement actions brought by the Attorney General or a district or county attorney in the name of the State or a subdivision; claims against a person primarily selling or leasing goods or services over statements or conduct arising from a sale, lease, insurance product, or commercial transaction aimed at actual or potential customers (but this exemption does not reach claims based on dramatic, lit","#st-ks-slapp"],["Check case law before relying on the statute","Kansas · anti-SLAPP","The Revisor’s annotations list one appellate decision, holding that review of a ruling on the motion is de novo (Doe v. Kan. State Univ., 61 Kan. App. 2d 128 (2021)), and a 2024 law review article on federal collateral-order review of anti-SLAPP rulings. Later Kansas appellate decisions on the act’s scope or constitutionality, and whether federal courts in Kansas apply it, were not researched. K.S.A. 60-5320 (Revisor","#st-ks-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: Supreme Court Rule 116 (text not collected)","Kansas · out of state","Admission of out-of-state lawyers for a single case is governed by a Kansas Supreme Court rule (identified in the research brief as Rule 116). Its requirements, including any association with Kansas counsel, fees, and limits, could not be verified. The statute K.S.A. 7-103 gives the Supreme Court power to make rules for admission and discipline; the former statute on out-of-state attorneys, K.S.A. 7-104, is listed as","#st-ks-oos"],["Signature block must include e-mail and fax","Kansas · out of state","Every pleading, motion, and paper must be signed by at least one attorney of record and state the signer’s address, e-mail address, telephone number, and fax number; discovery papers need address, e-mail, and telephone. Unsigned papers are stricken unless promptly corrected. K.S.A. 60-211(a); 60-226(f)(1)","#st-ks-oos"],["Electronic filing and service are rule-driven","Kansas · out of state","District and appellate clerks must accept electronic filings to the extent provided by Supreme Court rule; electronic signatures and verifications that comply with the rules satisfy the code. A petition received electronically in compliance with Supreme Court rules counts as filed for commencement. Electronic service is allowed when authorized by Supreme Court or local rule. The e-filing rules themselves (who must e-","#st-ks-oos"],["Midnight deadline only for electronic or fax filing","Kansas · out of state","For electronic or fax filing, the last day ends at midnight in the court’s time zone; for other filing, when the clerk’s office is scheduled to close. Three extra days are added only after service by mail or by leaving the paper with the clerk, not after electronic or fax service. K.S.A. 60-206(a)(4), (d)","#st-ks-oos"],["Limited-scope representation","Kansas · out of state","No limited-scope appearance provision appears in chapter 60, article 2. Any Supreme Court rule on limited representation was not collected and should be checked. Kansas Supreme Court Rules not collected: kscourts.gov served a Cloudflare bot check on November 10, 2026","#st-ks-oos"],["Out-of-state subpoenas for Kansas discovery","Kansas · out of state","Kansas has adopted the Uniform Interstate Depositions and Discovery Act. Present the foreign subpoena to the clerk of the district court in the county where discovery will occur and pay the docket fee; the request is not an appearance. Motions to quash or enforce go to that county’s court. K.S.A. 60-228a","#st-ks-oos"],["Governing code: Kansas Rules of Professional Conduct (Rule 226)","Kansas · ethics","Kansas lawyer conduct is governed by the Kansas Rules of Professional Conduct adopted by the Supreme Court (identified in the research brief as Supreme Court Rule 226), under the Court’s rulemaking power over admission and discipline. The rule text was not collected, so no differences from the ABA Model Rules are stated here. K.S.A. 7-103; Kan. Sup. Ct. R. 226 (not collected). Kansas Supreme Court Rules not collected","#st-ks-ethics"],["Post-verdict juror contact is regulated by statute","Kansas · ethics","After a civil jury is discharged, parties, lawyers, and their representatives may discuss deliberations or the verdict with a juror immediately afterward only with the juror’s consent. A later contact must first tell the juror the case, the party represented, the subject, the juror’s absolute right not to talk, and the right to see any declaration filed. Unreasonable nonconsensual contact must be reported to the cour","#st-ks-ethics"],["Rule 11 analog has no safe harbor","Kansas · ethics","Sanctions for unwarranted pleadings, motions, or papers may be imposed after notice and an opportunity to respond, on the attorney, the law firm, or the party, with no 21-day withdrawal period like Fed. R. Civ. P. 11(c)(2). A sanctions motion must be filed no later than 14 days after entry of judgment. The State and its agencies are covered. K.S.A. 60-211(c), (e)","#st-ks-ethics"],["Mandatory conferral certifications","Kansas · ethics","Motions to compel, motions for protective orders, and motions for sanctions for failing to respond to discovery must certify good-faith conferral and describe the steps taken by all attorneys or unrepresented parties to resolve the dispute. K.S.A. 60-226(c)(1); 60-237(a)(1), (d)(1)(B)","#st-ks-ethics"],["Litigation funding disclosure","Kansas · ethics","Since July 1, 2025, a party that has a third-party litigation funding agreement must submit it to the court for in camera review, and deliver to all other parties a sworn statement about the funder, its control or approval rights, its access to confidential materials, its relationships with the opposing side or the court, the nature of its interest, and any funding from a foreign person of a country of concern, withi","#st-ks-ethics"],["Filing alone may not stop the limitations clock","Kansas · land mine","An action counts as commenced on the filing date only if service (or first publication) is made within 90 days after filing, extendable once by 30 days for good cause. Otherwise it is commenced only on the date of service. Relation back for a new defendant also depends on notice within the limitations period plus that service window. K.S.A. 60-203(a)-(b); 60-215(c)(3)","#st-ks-unique"],["Caption and damages pleading rules","Kansas · land mine","The petition must state below the caption that it is filed under chapter 60 (“Petition Pursuant to K.S.A. Chapter 60”). Except in contract cases, a demand over $75,000 must say only that damages exceed $75,000, and a demand of $75,000 or less must state the specific amount. Before taking a default on an “exceeds $75,000” petition, notify the defendant of the amount by return-receipt delivery at least 14 days before j","#st-ks-unique"],["Jury demand within 14 days or it is waived","Kansas · land mine","Serve a written jury demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue, and file it. A missing or unfiled demand waives jury trial, though the court may relieve an inadvertent waiver. K.S.A. 60-238(b), (d)","#st-ks-unique"],["No initial disclosures, but expert and funding disclosures are mandatory","Kansas · land mine","Kansas has no Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(1) initial disclosures. Expert disclosures (identity, subject, substance of facts and opinions, and for retained experts a summary of grounds) are due at least 90 days before trial absent an order, and are filed with the court. Litigation funding agreements must be submitted in camera and disclosed by sworn statement within 30 days after the case starts or the agreement is signed. ","#st-ks-unique"],["Nonparty deposition timing tied to the case management conference","Kansas · land mine","Without stipulation or leave, a nonparty may not be deposed before the case management conference (which must be held within 45 days after an answer, unless extended) unless the notice certifies with facts that the deponent will leave Kansas. If no conference is held within 45 days after the answer, the restriction lapses. K.S.A. 60-216(b)(2); 60-230(a)(2)(A)(ii)","#st-ks-unique"],["Document subpoenas need advance notice to all parties","Kansas · land mine","A subpoena for documents or inspection that is not tied to a deposition may be served only after notice is served on every party. A records-only subpoena under 60-245a requires 14 days’ advance notice with a copy of the proposed subpoena, and any party objection blocks issuance without a court order. K.S.A. 60-245(b); 60-245a(b)(1)(A)","#st-ks-unique"],["Change-of-judge window is 7 days","Kansas · land mine","In prejudgment matters, a motion for change of judge must be filed within 7 days after pretrial or after written notice of the assigned judge, whichever is later, and each party gets only one change. The motion itself must not state grounds; an affidavit with grounds follows immediately if the judge refuses. K.S.A. 20-311d(a); 20-311f","#st-ks-unique"],["Post-trial motions: 28 days, no extensions","Kansas · land mine","Renewed motions for judgment as a matter of law, motions to amend findings, new-trial motions, and motions to alter or amend must be filed within 28 days after entry of judgment, and the court has no power to extend those deadlines. K.S.A. 60-206(b)(2); 60-250(b); 60-252(b); 60-259(b), (f)","#st-ks-unique"],["The appeal clock starts at the filed journal entry, not the oral ruling","Kansas · land mine","A judgment is not effective until a journal entry or judgment form is signed and filed; the 30-day notice of appeal runs from that entry, and the notice is filed in the district court. The only statutory extension is up to 30 days for excusable neglect based on not learning of the entry. K.S.A. 60-258; 60-2103(a)","#st-ks-unique"],["Cross-appeal in 21 days","Kansas · land mine","An appellee who wants any ruling reviewed must file a notice of cross-appeal within 21 days after the notice of appeal was served on it and filed. K.S.A. 60-2103(h)","#st-ks-unique"],["Object to Court of Appeals jurisdiction within 30 days","Kansas · land mine","A motion to transfer a case from the Court of Appeals to the Supreme Court must be filed within 30 days after the notice of appeal is served on the appellee; failing to file waives any objection to Court of Appeals jurisdiction. K.S.A. 20-3017","#st-ks-unique"],["Anti-SLAPP motion: 60 days, and fees are mandatory","Kansas · land mine","The motion to strike may be filed within 60 days of service of the most recent complaint (later only in the court’s discretion), and a hearing must occur within 30 days after service of the motion. A prevailing movant must be awarded fees and costs, and sanctions may reach the opposing lawyers and law firms. An appeal from denial requires notice within 14 days. K.S.A. 60-5320(d), (f), (g)","#st-ks-unique"],["Change of judge: motion first, then affidavit","Kansas · judges","A party or attorney who believes the assigned judge cannot give a fair trial files a motion for change of judge that must not state its grounds; the judge hears it informally. If the judge declines to step aside, the party must immediately file an affidavit alleging a statutory ground: the judge was counsel in the case, is otherwise interested, is related to a party, is a material witness, or has personal bias, preju","#st-ks-unique"],["One change per party; 7-day window","Kansas · judges","No party gets more than one change of judge in an action. In prejudgment matters, the motion must be filed within 7 days after pretrial or after written notice of the judge assigned to hear the case, whichever is later; in post-judgment proceedings it may be filed at any time. This is a for-cause procedure, not a peremptory strike. K.S.A. 20-311f","#st-ks-unique"],["Automatic disqualification for close relatives","Kansas · judges","In any contested case, a district judge is disqualified if any party or attorney of record is the judge’s spouse, parent, child, grandchild, grandparent, brother, or sister, or the spouse of one of those relatives. K.S.A. 20-311","#st-ks-unique"],["90-day reporting on undecided matters","Kansas · judges","If a bench-tried case, motion, or objection is not decided within 90 days after final submission, the judge must file a written report with the Supreme Court explaining why. K.S.A. 60-252a","#st-ks-unique"],["Statutory, not constitutional, protections shown","Kansas · speech","The collected texts support the Public Speech Protection Act (K.S.A. 60-5320) and a libel pleading rule: in a libel or slander action it suffices to allege generally that defamatory matter was published concerning the plaintiff, and the defendant may plead both truth and mitigating circumstances. No Kansas Bill of Rights text or case law on free speech was collected, so no claim is made that Kansas protects speech mo","#st-ks-unique"],["Civil procedure is statutory","Kansas · unique rules","Kansas’s rules of civil procedure are article 2 of the Code of Civil Procedure, enacted and amended by the Legislature, restyled to follow the federal rules in 2010 (L. 2010, ch. 135). Expect statutory changes each session, such as the 2025 litigation-funding disclosure. Local rules adopted by a majority of a district’s judges must be consistent with the code. K.S.A. 60-101, 60-201, 60-267","#st-ks-unique"],["Modified comparative fault and several liability","Kansas · unique rules","A claimant recovers only if its negligence is less than that of the parties it claims against, with damages reduced proportionally; liability among defendants is several, by percentage of fault, determined by special verdict. On a defendant’s motion, others whose fault allegedly contributed must be joined, and crossclaims among co-parties arising from the occurrence are compulsory. K.S.A. 60-258a; 60-213(f)","#st-ks-unique"],["Ten of twelve jurors decide","Kansas · unique rules","With a 12-member civil jury, agreement of 10 jurors suffices for a verdict; smaller juries must be unanimous unless the parties stipulate otherwise. Each side has 3 peremptory challenges. K.S.A. 60-247(c)(2); 60-248(a), (g)","#st-ks-unique"],["Nonparty business-records subpoenas","Kansas · unique rules","Kansas has a records-only subpoena procedure: notice and a copy of the proposed subpoena go to all parties at least 14 days before issuance, a party objection stops issuance absent a court order, and the custodian may comply by mailing certified copies with a declaration within the earlier of the compliance date or 14 days. K.S.A. 60-245a","#st-ks-unique"],["Rule 60-201. Rules of civil procedure; citation; scope","Kansas · civil rules","Article 2 of the Kansas Code of Civil Procedure may be cited as the “rules of civil procedure” and governs all civil actions and proceedings in the district courts, except cases under the Code of Civil Procedure for Limited Actions (chapter 61). Unlike the federal rules, these are statutes enacted by the Legislature; K.S.A. 60-102 directs that the code be liberally construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensiv","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-202. One form of action","Kansas · civil rules","There is one form of action, the civil action.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-203. Commencement of action","Kansas · civil rules","An action is commenced on filing the petition only if service is obtained (or first publication is made) within 90 days after filing, extendable once by 30 days for good cause; otherwise it is commenced only on the date of service or first publication, which matters for the statute of limitations. If service is later held invalid for an irregularity, the filing date still counts if valid service follows within 90 day","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-204. Process, generally","Kansas · civil rules","The article 3 service methods are sufficient but do not displace other methods provided by law. Substantial compliance with a service method is valid service if the court finds the party served was made aware that an action that might affect the party was pending in a specified court.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-205. Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","Kansas · civil rules","Later pleadings, written motions, discovery papers required to be served, and notices, appearances, demands, and offers of judgment must be served on every party (not on a party in default for failure to appear, unless a new claim is asserted against it, which requires summons-type service). Service on a represented party goes to the attorney; it may be by hand, leaving at office or home, mail (complete on mailing), ","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-206. Time, computation and extension","Kansas · civil rules","Time is computed like Fed. R. Civ. P. 6: exclude the trigger day, count every day, and roll a weekend or legal holiday (including a day the Kansas Supreme Court orders observed) to the next business day; the last day ends at midnight for electronic or fax filing and at clerk’s-office closing otherwise. The court may extend time for good cause before expiration, or after expiration for excusable neglect, but may not e","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-207. Pleadings allowed; motions; form","Kansas · civil rules","Allowed pleadings are the petition (Kansas’s name for the complaint), answer, answer to a counterclaim or crossclaim, third-party petition and answer, and a reply only if the court orders one. A motion must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state its grounds with particularity, and state the relief sought. Kansas-specific: the petition must state immediately below the caption that it is filed under c","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-208. General rules of pleadings","Kansas · civil rules","Notice pleading as in Fed. R. Civ. P. 8: a short and plain statement of the claim and a demand for relief. Kansas-specific damages rule: except in contract actions, a pleading seeking more than $75,000 in money damages must state only that the amount exceeds $75,000, while a claim for $75,000 or less must state the specific amount. Denials, the effect of failing to deny, and a list of 17 affirmative defenses (includi","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-209. Pleading special matters","Kansas · civil rules","Capacity need not be alleged, but must be challenged by specific denial. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity; conditions of mind generally; conditions precedent generally (denials with particularity). Special damages must be specifically stated; an amended petition adding punitive damages under K.S.A. 60-3703 states only whether damages exceed $75,000. Kansas additions: a claim on a written instrumen","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-210. Form of pleadings","Kansas · civil rules","Every pleading needs a caption with the court’s name, a title, file number, and designation; the petition names all parties. Claims and defenses go in numbered paragraphs, separate counts where that promotes clarity, and exhibits are part of the pleading. A party’s name change after filing may be noted of record by filing a certified record or an affidavit or declaration, and the changed name is then used in the alte","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-211. Signing; representations; sanctions","Kansas · civil rules","Every paper must be signed by an attorney of record (or the unrepresented party) and state the signer’s address, e-mail address, telephone number, and fax number; unsigned papers are stricken unless promptly corrected. The certifications mirror Fed. R. Civ. P. 11(b). Key difference: Kansas has no 21-day safe harbor; after notice and an opportunity to respond, the court may sanction the attorney, firm, or party, and a","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-212. Defenses and objections","Kansas · civil rules","Answer within 21 days after service of summons and petition (or within the time in the publication notice, at least 41 days after first publication); answer a counterclaim or crossclaim within 21 days; a reply when ordered within 21 days. A motion under this section moves the deadline to 14 days after notice of the court’s ruling (or after service of a more definite statement). The seven Rule 12(b) defenses, judgment","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-213. Counterclaims and cross-claims","Kansas · civil rules","Compulsory and permissive counterclaims follow Fed. R. Civ. P. 13. Kansas differences: a claim connected to the opposing claim survives assignment, death, or the running of limitations, but only as an offset up to the opposing party’s award; and in a comparative-fault case under K.S.A. 60-258a, a crossclaim against a co-party arising from the same occurrence is compulsory. When a limited action is transferred or a di","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-214. Third-party practice","Kansas · civil rules","A defending party may serve a third-party petition on a nonparty who is or may be liable to it; leave of court is needed if filed more than 14 days after serving the original answer. The third-party defendant’s defenses and claims track Fed. R. Civ. P. 14 (and compulsory crossclaims under 60-213(f) apply). Kansas addition: a third-party plaintiff may execute on a judgment against the third-party defendant only to the","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-215. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Kansas · civil rules","Amend once as a matter of course within 21 days after serving the pleading, or, if a response is required, within 21 days after service of the responsive pleading or of a motion under 60-212(b), (e), or (f), whichever is earlier; otherwise with written consent or leave, freely given. Respond to an amended pleading within the time left on the original or 21 days, whichever is later. Amendments at and after trial, rela","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-216. Pretrial conferences; case management conference","Kansas · civil rules","On any party’s request the court must (or on its own may) hold pretrial conferences and a case management conference, which must be held within 45 days after an answer is filed unless extended; the conference covers issues, ADR, ESI, privilege agreements (including K.S.A. 60-426a), a discovery plan and deadline, and dates for motions, joinder, amendment, and trial. Kansas-specific: if a case management conference is ","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-217. Parties; capacity","Kansas · civil rules","Actions are prosecuted by the real party in interest (with the usual list of representatives who may sue in their own names), and dismissal for not doing so waits a reasonable time for ratification, joinder, or substitution. Kansas additions: a claim that accrued under another state’s law may be sued on by whoever may sue on it there, and a Kansas-appointed administrator or executor may bring a foreign wrongful-death","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-218. Joinder of claims; contingent claims","Kansas · civil rules","A party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party, including contingent claims, such as a money claim with a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance. Kansas addition: a plaintiff may plead a claim to set aside a release, settlement, or discharge of the original claim as fraudulent or wrongfully procured.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-219. Required joinder of parties","Kansas · civil rules","Required-party joinder and the equity-and-good-conscience factors when joinder is not feasible track Fed. R. Civ. P. 19, and a pleader must name unjoined required persons and explain why. Kansas addition: in an action affecting title to or a security interest in real property, a nominee of record for a beneficial owner (for example, a mortgage-registry nominee) who is subject to service must be joined.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-220. Permissive joinder of parties","Kansas · civil rules","Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, when claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact; the court may order separate trials or other protective orders. Same as Fed. R. Civ. P. 20.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-221. Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","Kansas · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may add or drop parties on just terms at any time and sever any claim.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-222. Interpleader","Kansas · civil rules","A plaintiff exposed to double or multiple liability may join claimants as defendants and require them to interplead, and a defendant may interplead by crossclaim or counterclaim. Kansas addition (disclaiming interpleader): a party holding money or property claimed by another may say so in its answer, deposit or deliver it as ordered, and be discharged; the court may order the claimant (served like a summons) to appea","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-223. Class actions","Kansas · civil rules","Prerequisites, the three types of class actions, certification, notice, judgment, subclasses, settlement approval, and class counsel and fees track Fed. R. Civ. P. 23 (in its pre-2018 form, without the newer settlement-notice and objector-payment provisions). Appeal of an order granting or denying certification is by permission of the Kansas Court of Appeals on application within 14 days after entry; it does not stay","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-223a. Derivative actions","Kansas · civil rules","A shareholder or member derivative petition must be verified, allege contemporaneous ownership and no collusion, and state with particularity the demand made (or why none was made). The plaintiff must fairly and adequately represent similarly situated holders; settlement or dismissal needs court approval and notice as the court orders.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-223b. Actions relating to unincorporated associations","Kansas · civil rules","An action by or against members of an unincorporated association as a class through named representatives may be maintained only if they will fairly and adequately protect the association and members; the 60-223(d) and (e) procedures apply.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-224. Intervention","Kansas · civil rules","Intervention of right and permissive intervention on timely motion track Fed. R. Civ. P. 24. Kansas additions: when a Kansas statute, ordinance, regulation, or constitutional provision is challenged in a case in which the State or subdivision is not a party, the court may notify the chief legal officer and permit intervention, and must permit the Attorney General to intervene on proper application when notice is requ","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-225. Substitution of parties","Kansas · civil rules","After a party’s death, the court must on motion order substitution if the claim survives; if no motion is made within a reasonable time after service of a statement noting the death, the action by or against the decedent must be dismissed. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 25(a)(1), there is no fixed 90-day period. Incapacity, transfer of interest, and automatic substitution of public officers follow the federal pattern; the at","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-226. Discovery","Kansas · civil rules","Scope is relevance and proportionality, as in Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(1). Major differences: Kansas has no initial-disclosure requirement like Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(1) and no Rule 26(f) conference. Expert disclosure must identify the expert, the subject matter, and the substance of facts and opinions, and for retained experts only a summary of the grounds for each opinion (not a full signed report), at least 90 days be","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-227. Perpetuation of testimony","Kansas · civil rules","Before suit, a verified petition may be filed in the county where an expected adverse party resides (for a will contest, the testator’s county of residence), served at least 21 days before the hearing (personally, by restricted mail, or as the court orders). If granted, the deposition may be used later against a party or someone with a similar interest and motive, generally only if the deponent is unavailable. Deposi","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-228. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Kansas · civil rules","In Kansas, a deposition must be taken before a person authorized to administer oaths and a Kansas Supreme Court certified court reporter. Outside Kansas, before an officer authorized there or a person the court appoints; a court may issue a commission. Foreign-country depositions may proceed by treaty, letter of request, notice, or commission. The officer may not be a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or cou","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-228a. Uniform interstate depositions and discovery act","Kansas · civil rules","To use an out-of-state subpoena in Kansas, submit the foreign subpoena to the clerk of the district court in the county where discovery will occur and pay the docket fee under K.S.A. 60-2001; the clerk issues a Kansas subpoena, opens a civil case number, and the request is not an appearance. The subpoena must list all counsel and unrepresented parties, is served under K.S.A. 60-303, and is governed by K.S.A. 60-245; ","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-229. Discovery procedure; stipulations","Kansas · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, parties may stipulate to deposition logistics and modify discovery procedures, but a stipulation extending discovery time needs court approval if it would interfere with the discovery-completion, motion-hearing, or trial dates.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-230. Depositions by oral examination","Kansas · civil rules","Any person may be deposed without leave except a second deposition, a prisoner, or (absent stipulation) a nonparty before the 60-216(b) case management conference unless the notice certifies with facts that the deponent will leave Kansas. Reasonable written notice is required; video may be added on notice; remote depositions by stipulation or order are deemed taken where the deponent answers. Objections must be conci","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-231. Depositions by written questions","Kansas · civil rules","Any person may be deposed by written questions on notice served on all parties (leave needed in the same situations as 60-230). Cross-questions are due 14 days after service of the notice and direct questions, redirect 14 days after cross, and recross 14 days after redirect; the court may change these times. The officer takes, certifies, and sends the deposition, and the noticing party notifies others of completion.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-232. Use of depositions in court proceedings","Kansas · civil rules","Depositions may be used against a party present, represented, or noticed, to the extent admissible as live testimony: to impeach, any deposition of an adverse party or its officer or designee for any purpose, and any witness’s deposition if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or outside Kansas (unless procured by the offering party), unable to attend because of age, illness, infirmity, or imprisonment, beyo","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-233. Interrogatories to parties","Kansas · civil rules","Interrogatories may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on any other party with or after service of process. Answers and objections are due within 30 days after service, but a defendant has at least 45 days after service of process; timing may be changed by stipulation or order. Objections must be specific or are waived; contention interrogatories are allowed; the business-records option applies. Kansas","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-234. Production of documents, ESI, tangible things, and entry onto land","Kansas · civil rules","Requests may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on any other party with or after service of process. A written response is due in 30 days, or for a defendant 45 days after service of process. Objections must be specific, state whether material is being withheld, and allow inspection of the rest; ESI form rules and production rules track Fed. R. Civ. P. 34. Nonparties are reached by subpoena under 60-24","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-235. Physical and mental examinations","Kansas · civil rules","On motion for good cause and notice, the court may order a party whose physical or mental condition (including blood group) is in controversy to be examined by a suitably licensed or certified examiner; the order sets time, place, scope, and the examiner, and the moving party advances expenses. The examined party may obtain the detailed report and reports of earlier exams, and must then provide its own like reports; ","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-236. Requests for admission","Kansas · civil rules","Requests may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on any other party with or after service of process. A matter is admitted unless a signed answer or objection is served within 30 days after service, or for a defendant 45 days after service of process. Answer, objection, sufficiency motion, and withdrawal rules track Fed. R. Civ. P. 36; admissions bind only in the pending action. No numerical limit appea","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-237. Compelling discovery; sanctions; failure to preserve ESI","Kansas · civil rules","A motion to compel requires a certification of good-faith conferral that describes the steps taken by all counsel or unrepresented parties; motions about nonparties go to the district where discovery is taken. Fee shifting, sanctions for disobeying orders (established facts, preclusion, striking, stay, dismissal, default, contempt), exclusion of undisclosed experts and supplementation, failure-to-admit costs, and san","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-238. Right of trial by jury; demand; waiver","Kansas · civil rules","The jury right under section 5 of the Kansas Bill of Rights or a statute is preserved. Serve a written jury demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue is served, and file it; other parties have 14 days after a partial demand to demand jury on other issues. Failure to serve and file waives jury trial, but the court may set aside a waiver in the interest of justice or when it was inadver","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-239. Trial by jury or by the court","Kansas · civil rules","A demanded jury case is docketed as such and tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or the court finds no jury right under the Kansas constitution or statutes. Issues not properly demanded are tried to the court, which may still order a jury on motion; advisory and consent juries are allowed (consent verdicts bind, except in actions against the State where a statute provides for nonjury trial).","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-240. Scheduling cases for trial; continuances","Kansas · civil rules","Each district court schedules trials by rule, giving priority to cases entitled to it by law. Continuances are for good cause; one for absent evidence is at the requesting party’s cost unless ordered otherwise. Kansas-specific: a continuance for an absent witness or evidence must be supported by an affidavit or declaration stating the witness, the expected testimony and its basis, and efforts to procure it; the court","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-241. Dismissal of actions","Kansas · civil rules","A plaintiff may dismiss without order by notice before an answer or summary-judgment motion is served, or by stipulation signed by all parties who appeared; a second notice dismissal of the same claim is an adjudication on the merits. Otherwise dismissal at plaintiff’s request is by court order. Involuntary dismissal for failure to prosecute or comply operates on the merits unless the order says otherwise (except for","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-242. Multicounty and multidistrict litigation","Kansas · civil rules","The court may consolidate or jointly try actions sharing a common question pending in counties of the same judicial district, and order separate trials (in the home county or another county of the district) while preserving jury rights. For related actions in different judicial districts, the Kansas Supreme Court, on request of a party or court, may transfer them to one county for consolidated discovery, pretrial pro","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-243. Testimony of witnesses; evidence","Kansas · civil rules","Trial testimony is taken in open court; remote contemporaneous testimony is allowed for good cause in compelling circumstances with safeguards. Kansas keeps specific rules on leading and calling an adverse party or its officer, director, or managing agent as on cross; cross-examination is limited to the subject matter of direct. Offers of proof on excluded evidence are made outside the jury’s hearing (in bench trials","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-244. Proof of records","Kansas · civil rules","Authentication and admissibility of official records and other documents are governed by article 4 of chapter 60 (the Kansas rules of evidence), not by this section.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-245. Subpoenas","Kansas · civil rules","Subpoenas are issued by the clerk under seal (blank on request) or by a judge, may be served anywhere in Kansas under K.S.A. 60-303, and must tender one day’s witness fee and mileage if attendance is required. Kansas-specific: before serving a subpoena for documents or inspection that is not tied to a deposition, notice must first be served on every party. Written objections to production are due before the earlier o","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-245a. Subpoena of nonparty business records","Kansas · civil rules","A Kansas procedure with no federal counterpart: a party may obtain copies of a nonparty’s business records by a records-only subpoena. At least 14 days before the subpoena issues, the party must serve all parties notice with a copy of the proposed subpoena; if any party objects before issuance, it issues only on court order. The custodian may comply within the earlier of the compliance date or 14 days by delivering t","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-246. Objections to rulings or orders","Kansas · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary; a party need only state the action it wants or objects to and the grounds. A party with no opportunity to object is not prejudiced.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-247. Jurors","Kansas · civil rules","Enough prospective jurors are called to leave 12 after challenges. The parties or counsel are entitled to conduct voir dire, and the court decides all challenges for cause. Each side gets 3 peremptory challenges (multiple plaintiffs or defendants count as one party unless the court finds a good-faith controversy among them and allows more), exercised without revealing to the juror which party challenged.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-248. Jury trial procedure","Kansas · civil rules","Civil juries have 12 members unless the parties stipulate to fewer or to a stated majority verdict. Key difference from federal practice: with a 12-person jury, 10 jurors’ agreement is enough for a verdict; other juries must be unanimous unless the parties stipulate. The section also covers jury views, keeping jurors together, admonitions on separation, written questions from the jury, discharge, polling, and alterna","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-248a. (Repealed)","Kansas · civil rules","Repealed by L. 2010, ch. 135, § 225.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-249. Special verdict; general verdict; written questions","Kansas · civil rules","The court may require a special verdict or a general verdict with written questions. An issue not submitted is waived unless submission is demanded before the jury retires, and the court may then make its own finding. Inconsistent answers are handled as in Fed. R. Civ. P. 49.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-249a. Itemized verdict, personal injury actions","Kansas · civil rules","In a personal-injury damages case, the factfinder must itemize noneconomic damages (pain and suffering, disability, disfigurement and accompanying mental anguish), medical expenses, and other economic losses, and must split each between damages to date and future damages. Instructions and the verdict form may mention only items on which evidence was introduced. No federal counterpart.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-250. Judgment as a matter of law; new trial","Kansas · civil rules","A motion for judgment as a matter of law may be made any time before submission to the jury. A renewed motion (with an optional alternative request for new trial) must be filed no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, or 28 days after discharge if the issue was not decided by the verdict. Kansas addition: in comparative-fault cases, the court must reserve ruling on a motion by a party joined under K.S.A. 60-258","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-251. Jury instructions; objections","Kansas · civil rules","Requests are filed at the close of evidence or earlier as ordered. The court must disclose proposed instructions and give an opportunity to object on the record outside the jury’s hearing before argument, and must instruct at the close of evidence before argument (unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 51, which allows instructing after argument). Objections must state the matter and grounds distinctly; unpreserved instruction error","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-252. Findings and conclusions by the court","Kansas · civil rules","In bench trials, advisory-jury trials, and (Kansas addition) when entering summary judgment, the court must find facts specially and state conclusions of law separately; also when granting or refusing an interlocutory injunction (except in divorce cases). Findings are reviewed for clear error. A motion to amend or add findings is due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment and cannot be extended. Judgment on pa","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-252a. Trial by the court; 90-day report","Kansas · civil rules","If a judgment or decision is not entered within 90 days after trial and final submission of a bench-tried case, or after submission of a motion or objection, the judge must file a written report with the Supreme Court explaining why. No federal counterpart.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-252b. Rules of supreme court","Kansas · civil rules","Directs the Supreme Court to adopt rules and require reports from district courts or clerks to enforce the 90-day reporting requirement of K.S.A. 60-252a.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-253. Trial by masters","Kansas · civil rules","Reference to a master (including a referee, auditor, commissioner, or examiner) is by consent, or otherwise only if the ends of justice will be measurably advanced, and in jury cases only for complex or voluminous accounts. The master’s first meeting must be set within 21 days after the order of reference; in nonjury cases findings are accepted unless clearly erroneous and objections are due within 14 days after noti","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-254. Judgment","Kansas · civil rules","A judgment is the final determination of the parties’ rights. Partial final judgment on fewer than all claims or parties requires an express determination of no just reason for delay; otherwise the order remains revisable. A default judgment may not exceed or differ from the demand. Kansas-specific: where the petition stated only that damages exceed $75,000, the party seeking default must notify the defaulting party ","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-255. Default","Kansas · civil rules","A party that fails to plead or otherwise defend is in default, and on request and showing of entitlement the court renders judgment (Kansas does not use a separate clerk’s entry of default). A party who has appeared must get written notice of the request at least 7 days before the hearing. Default against a minor or incapacitated person requires an appearing representative or guardian ad litem; against the State, its","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-256. Summary judgment","Kansas · civil rules","Unless a local rule or order differs, a motion may be filed any time until 30 days after the close of all discovery; the response is due 21 days after service of the motion or the date a responsive pleading is due, whichever is later; a reply is due 14 days after the response. Judgment is rendered if there is no genuine issue of material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. Partial adjudica","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-257. Declaratory judgment","Kansas · civil rules","Article 2 governs procedure for declaratory judgments under article 17 of chapter 60, with jury demands under 60-238 and 60-239. Another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the court may order a speedy hearing.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-258. Entry of judgment","Kansas · civil rules","No judgment is effective until a journal entry or judgment form is signed by the judge and filed with the clerk. When judgment is entered by judgment form, the clerk must serve it on all attorneys of record within 3 days (excluding weekends and legal holidays); failure to serve does not affect validity. This filing date starts post-judgment and appeal deadlines.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-258a. Comparative negligence","Kansas · civil rules","Kansas modified comparative fault: a claimant recovers only if its negligence is less than that of the parties against whom it claims (so 50% bars recovery), with damages reduced by its percentage; a decedent’s negligence is imputed in wrongful-death claims. The jury (or court) must return special findings of each party’s percentage and total damages. On motion of a defendant, any other person whose causal negligence","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-258b. (Repealed)","Kansas · civil rules","Repealed by L. 2010, ch. 135, § 225.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-259. New trial; motion to alter or amend judgment","Kansas · civil rules","Grounds include abuse of discretion, misconduct, accident or surprise, lack of opportunity to be heard, erroneous rulings or instructions, passion or prejudice, a verdict contrary to the evidence, newly discovered evidence, and corruption (which requires a new trial as of right with costs charged to the party who procured the verdict). A new-trial motion, and a motion to alter or amend, must be filed no later than 28","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-260. Relief from judgment or order","Kansas · civil rules","Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time, but after an appeal is docketed only with the appellate court’s leave. The six grounds for relief track Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b); the motion must be made within a reasonable time, and for mistake, newly discovered evidence, or fraud no more than one year after entry. The motion does not affect finality. Independent actions, relief under K.S.A. 60-309 for defendants served ","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-261. Harmless error","Kansas · civil rules","Errors that do not affect any party’s substantial rights must be disregarded at every stage.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-262. Stay of proceedings to enforce judgment","Kansas · civil rules","No execution or enforcement for 14 days after entry of judgment (Fed. R. Civ. P. 62(a) now uses 30 days); injunctions and receivership judgments are not automatically stayed. The court may stay enforcement pending post-trial motions on security terms. On appeal, a stay is obtained by supersedeas bond, effective on court approval of the bond; the State, its officers, and agencies need not post bond. The court may modi","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-263. Disability of judge","Kansas · civil rules","If the trial judge becomes unable to act after the verdict or after findings and conclusions are filed, another judge of the court may perform the remaining duties, or may grant a new trial if unable to perform them. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 63, the text does not provide for a successor judge to take over mid-trial by certifying familiarity with the record.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-264. Enforcing orders for and against a nonparty","Kansas · civil rules","An order granting relief for or enforceable against a nonparty is enforced by the same procedure as for a party.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-265. Applicability of article","Kansas · civil rules","Article 2 applies to civil actions in district court other than limited actions. Other district-court proceedings follow their own codes, but may borrow article 2 provisions when those codes are silent. When nothing in the article addresses a matter, the court must proceed in a just and equitable manner protecting all affected parties.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-266. Jurisdiction and venue not affected","Kansas · civil rules","Article 2 neither extends nor limits district-court jurisdiction or venue.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-267. Rules by district courts","Kansas · civil rules","A majority of the judges of a district court may adopt local rules consistent with article 2, furnished to the Supreme Court on adoption. Where the article is silent, district courts may regulate practice consistently with it and Supreme Court rules.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-268. (Repealed)","Kansas · civil rules","Repealed by L. 2017, ch. 75, § 10.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-269. (Repealed)","Kansas · civil rules","Repealed by L. 2005, ch. 101, § 19.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-270. Retaining original records until case closed","Kansas · civil rules","Parties or attorneys holding original deposition transcripts, discovery responses, and other original discovery materials must keep them until the case is closed (order terminating the case filed and all appeals ended or time expired, or judgment satisfied or barred under K.S.A. 60-2403), then may dispose of them unless a protective order, rule, statute, or agreement requires otherwise.","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-271. Acceptance of filings by electronic means","Kansas · civil rules","To the extent provided by Supreme Court rule, district and appellate court clerks must accept electronic filings of any document. Electronic signatures and verifications consistent with Supreme Court rules satisfy 60-211 and every other signing or verification requirement in the code. The Supreme Court’s electronic filing rules themselves were not collected (kscourts.gov blocked automated access).","#st-ks-rules"],["Rule 60-272. Contact with jurors after verdict","Kansas · civil rules","Added in 2018 and outside 60-201 to 60-271, but codified in article 2. Before discharge, the judge must tell jurors they have an absolute right to discuss or not discuss deliberations. Parties and counsel may talk to a juror right after discharge only with consent; a later contact must first disclose the case, the party represented, the subject, the juror’s right to refuse, and the juror’s right to see any declaratio","#st-ks-rules"],["Kansas appeals: 30 days from a filed journal entry, and most civil appeals go to the Court of Appeals first","Kansas · appeals","Civil appeals from the district court go to the Kansas Court of Appeals (14 judges) unless a statute requires a direct appeal to the Supreme Court, chiefly when a district court has held a Kansas or federal statute unconstitutional. The Supreme Court reviews Court of Appeals decisions mainly by discretionary petition for review, and it can also take cases by transfer.\nThe notice of appeal is filed with the clerk of t","#st-ks-appeals"],["Court of Appeals is the default","Kansas · appeals · Which court hears the appeal","The Court of Appeals hears appeals from district courts except cases reviewable in the district court and cases where a direct Supreme Court appeal is required by law, plus agency appeals where a statute sends them directly there. It may correct, modify, vacate, or reverse any district-court act, order, or judgment to assure it is just, legal, and free of abuse. It has 14 judges. K.S.A. 60-2101(a); 20-3001; 20-3002(a","#st-ks-appeals"],["Direct appeal to the Supreme Court","Kansas · appeals · Which court hears the appeal","An appeal from a final civil judgment holding a Kansas or federal statute unconstitutional goes directly to the Supreme Court. Appeals of right also go there from a decision holding a statute unconstitutional under article 6 (education/school finance) of the Kansas Constitution, which must be filed within 30 days of the decision, and from final decisions in actions challenging or arising from the Kansas expanded lott","#st-ks-appeals"],["Filed in the wrong court? It is transferred, not dismissed","Kansas · appeals · Which court hears the appeal","No case is dismissed solely because it was docketed in the wrong appellate court; the Supreme Court transfers it to the court with jurisdiction and it is treated as timely filed there. K.S.A. 20-3018(a)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Appeals from non-lawyer magistrate judges go to a district judge","Kansas · appeals · Which court hears the appeal","Orders and final decisions of a district magistrate judge who is not regularly admitted to practice law in Kansas are appealed to a district judge, by notice of appeal filed with the district court clerk within 14 days after entry; the notice must say the appeal is from a magistrate judge’s order. Such orders cannot be appealed directly to the Court of Appeals. K.S.A. 60-2103a(a); 60-2102(a)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Appeals from local-government quasi-judicial orders","Kansas · appeals · Which court hears the appeal","If no other method is provided by law, a party aggrieved by a judicial or quasi-judicial order of a political or taxing subdivision appeals to the district court by filing a notice of appeal with the subdivision or agency within 30 days of entry, then filing true copies of the proceedings with the district court clerk and paying a docket fee. K.S.A. 60-2101(d)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Final decisions, with review of every earlier ruling","Kansas · appeals · What can be appealed","An appeal of right lies from a final decision in any action. In an appeal or cross-appeal from a final decision, any act or ruling from the beginning of the proceedings is reviewable, and a ruling made more than 30 days before the notice of appeal can still be reviewed. K.S.A. 60-2102(a)(4); 60-2103(i)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Interlocutory orders appealable as of right","Kansas · appeals · What can be appealed","Kansas allows appeals of right from orders that discharge, vacate, or modify a provisional remedy; that grant, continue, modify, refuse, or dissolve an injunction; that grant or refuse mandamus, quo warranto, or habeas corpus; that appoint a receiver or refuse to wind up a receivership; and (unusually broad) any order involving the tax or revenue laws, title to real estate, the Kansas Constitution, or the federal con","#st-ks-appeals"],["Certified interlocutory appeals","Kansas · appeals · What can be appealed","For any other civil order, the district judge (or a lawyer-magistrate) may state in writing in the order that it involves a controlling question of law with substantial ground for difference of opinion and that an immediate appeal may materially advance the litigation. The Court of Appeals may then permit the appeal if application is made within 14 days after entry of the order. The application does not stay district","#st-ks-appeals"],["Class certification orders","Kansas · appeals · What can be appealed","The Court of Appeals may permit an appeal from an order granting or denying class certification if application is made within 14 days after entry; no automatic stay. K.S.A. 60-223(f)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Partial judgments","Kansas · appeals · What can be appealed","An order resolving fewer than all claims or parties is final only if the district court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay and directs entry of judgment. K.S.A. 60-254(b)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP denials","Kansas · appeals · What can be appealed","A movant whose motion to strike under the Public Speech Protection Act is denied may take an interlocutory appeal if the notice of appeal is filed within 14 days after entry of the order, or may seek mandamus if the court fails to rule in an expedited fashion. Further trial-court proceedings are stayed pending the appeal. K.S.A. 60-5320(f)","#st-ks-appeals"],["When judgment is entered","Kansas · appeals · Starting the appeal","No judgment is effective until a journal entry or judgment form is signed by the judge and filed with the clerk. The 30-day appeal clock runs from that entry. When judgment is by judgment form, the clerk must serve it on counsel within 3 business days, but failure of service does not affect validity. K.S.A. 60-258; 60-2103(a)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Notice of appeal: 30 days, filed in the district court","Kansas · appeals · Starting the appeal","File the notice of appeal with the clerk of the district court within 30 days from entry of judgment. The notice must name the appealing parties, designate the judgment or part appealed from, and name the appellate court. Serve it on all parties under 60-205; failure to serve does not invalidate the appeal. Later missteps do not void the appeal but can lead to sanctions including dismissal. K.S.A. 60-2103(a)-(b)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Narrow 30-day extension","Kansas · appeals · Starting the appeal","The district court may extend the appeal time by up to 30 days after the original deadline only for excusable neglect based on a party’s failure to learn of the entry of judgment. No other ground is listed in the statute. K.S.A. 60-2103(a)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Docket fee","Kansas · appeals · Starting the appeal","A party filing an appeal with the Court of Appeals or Supreme Court pays a $145 fee to the Clerk of the Supreme Court; a poverty affidavit may be filed instead, and the State and Kansas municipalities are exempt. The statute’s authority for an extra charge of up to $10 ran only through June 30, 2025. K.S.A. 20-3021","#st-ks-appeals"],["Record and docketing","Kansas · appeals · Starting the appeal","The record on appeal is filed and docketed at the time, and in the form, prescribed by Supreme Court rule; security for costs is also set by Supreme Court rule. Those rules were not collected. K.S.A. 60-2103(c), (g); 60-2104","#st-ks-appeals"],["Voluntary dismissal before docketing","Kansas · appeals · Starting the appeal","Before the record is filed in the appellate court, the parties may dismiss by stipulation filed in the district court with its approval, or the district court may dismiss on the appellant’s motion and notice. K.S.A. 60-2103(a)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Which motions restart the clock","Kansas · appeals · Post-trial motions and tolling","A timely renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law (60-250(b)), motion to amend or add findings (60-252(b)), motion to alter or amend (60-259(f)), or motion for new trial (60-259) terminates the running of the appeal time, and the full 30 days runs anew from the order granting or denying it (for a new-trial motion, from the order denying it). A 60-260 motion for relief from judgment is not on the list and does n","#st-ks-appeals"],["28 days, no extensions","Kansas · appeals · Post-trial motions and tolling","Each of those motions must be filed no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, and the court may not extend those deadlines. Opposing affidavits on a new-trial motion are due 14 days after service. K.S.A. 60-250(b); 60-252(b); 60-259(b), (d), (f); 60-206(b)(2)","#st-ks-appeals"],["21 days after the notice of appeal","Kansas · appeals · Cross-appeals","An appellee who wants review of rulings it complains of must give notice of cross-appeal within 21 days after the notice of appeal has been served on it and filed with the trial court clerk. K.S.A. 60-2103(h)","#st-ks-appeals"],["14-day automatic stay; then bond","Kansas · appeals · Stays and supersedeas bonds","No execution may issue until 14 days after entry of judgment. Injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed even on appeal unless the court orders. Otherwise an appellant gets a stay by a supersedeas bond approved by the district court; the stay takes effect on approval. The State and its agencies need not post bond. K.S.A. 60-262(a), (d), (e)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Bond amount and caps","Kansas · appeals · Stays and supersedeas bonds","The bond is normally the full judgment plus costs, interest, and delay damages. On proof of undue hardship or denial of the right to appeal, the court may reduce it to $1,000,000 plus 25% of the excess over $1,000,000 (judgments of $1,000,000 or less stay at full amount). Undue hardship is presumed for a small business (2 to 50 employees, unaffiliated) facing a judgment over $2,500,000 arising from its ordinary busin","#st-ks-appeals"],["Provisional remedies and injunctions","Kansas · appeals · Stays and supersedeas bonds","A party aggrieved by an order discharging, vacating, or modifying a provisional remedy, or modifying or dissolving an injunction, may have it suspended for up to 14 days on condition that it files a notice of appeal and obtains approval of a supersedeas bond within that period. K.S.A. 60-2103(d)(1)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Late or insufficient bond","Kansas · appeals · Stays and supersedeas bonds","Before docketing in the appellate court, the district court may set a time to file a bond; after docketing, leave must be sought from the appellate court. A surety submits to the court’s jurisdiction and may be held liable on motion. K.S.A. 60-2103(e)-(f)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Motion to transfer: 30 days, or the objection is waived","Kansas · appeals · Transfer to the Supreme Court and review","Within 30 days after the notice of appeal is served on the appellee, any party may move in the Court of Appeals to transfer the case to the Supreme Court, alleging that an issue is outside the Court of Appeals’ jurisdiction, the case has significant public interest, it involves legal questions of major public significance, or caseload requires transfer. Failing to file the motion waives any objection to the Court of ","#st-ks-appeals"],["Petition for review: 30 days after the decision","Kansas · appeals · Transfer to the Supreme Court and review","A party aggrieved by a Court of Appeals decision may petition the Supreme Court for review within 30 days after the date of the decision. A rehearing motion in the Court of Appeals is allowed under Supreme Court rules but is not a prerequisite to review. Review is discretionary; factors include general importance, conflict with Supreme Court or other Court of Appeals decisions, the need for supervisory authority, and","#st-ks-appeals"],["Appeal of right from the Court of Appeals","Kansas · appeals · Transfer to the Supreme Court and review","A party may appeal to the Supreme Court as of right when a question under the U.S. or Kansas Constitution arises for the first time as a result of the Court of Appeals’ decision. K.S.A. 60-2101(b)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Opinions and mandate","Kansas · appeals · Transfer to the Supreme Court and review","Appellate judges must file the opinion within 60 days after the decision; a memorandum opinion may be used where no new question of law is decided, while formal opinions carry a syllabus of the points of law. The mandate issues when the decision becomes final under Supreme Court rule and becomes part of the district court judgment. Appellate courts disregard technical errors that did not prejudice substantial rights.","#st-ks-appeals"],["Supreme Court decisions need four justices","Kansas · appeals · Transfer to the Supreme Court and review","The Supreme Court has at least seven justices; every case must be heard by at least four, and a decision requires a majority of those sitting and at least four votes. Kan. Const. art. 3, § 2","#st-ks-appeals"],["Briefs, word limits, rehearing, docketing statements, e-filing","Kansas · appeals · Not collected: verify in the Kansas Supreme Court Rules","Briefing schedules, length and word limits, the time for a Court of Appeals or Supreme Court rehearing motion, docketing requirements, the contents of a petition for review, oral argument, and appellate e-filing are set by Kansas Supreme Court rules. The rule texts could not be collected because kscourts.gov served a Cloudflare bot check to automated requests on November 10, 2026. No deadline, page limit, or word lim","#st-ks-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (civil, district court to appellate court)","Kansas · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of judgment (journal entry or judgment form signed and filed).\nFiled with the clerk of the district court. Extendable by up to 30 days only for excusable neglect based on failure to learn of the entry of judgment. K.S.A. 60-2103(a); 60-258","#st-ks-appeals"],["Appeal after a timely post-trial motion","Kansas · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order granting or denying a 60-250(b), 60-252(b), or 60-259 motion (for a new-trial motion, the order denying it).\nFull 30 days runs anew. K.S.A. 60-2103(a)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law","Kansas · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from entry of judgment (or jury discharge if the issue was not decided by verdict).\nCannot be extended (60-206(b)(2)). K.S.A. 60-250(b)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Motion for new trial; motion to alter or amend","Kansas · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended. The court may order a new trial on its own within the same 28 days. K.S.A. 60-259(b), (f)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Motion to amend or add findings","Kansas · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended. K.S.A. 60-252(b)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Notice of cross-appeal","Kansas · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from service and filing of the notice of appeal. K.S.A. 60-2103(h)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Application for permissive interlocutory appeal","Kansas · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of the order containing the district judge’s written certification.\nFiled with the Court of Appeals; procedure set by Supreme Court rule (not collected). K.S.A. 60-2102(c)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Application to appeal class-certification order","Kansas · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of the order.\nNo automatic stay. K.S.A. 60-223(f)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Notice of appeal from denial of anti-SLAPP motion to strike","Kansas · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of the order denying the motion.\nTrial-court proceedings are stayed pending the appeal. K.S.A. 60-5320(f)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Appeal from a non-lawyer district magistrate judge","Kansas · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of the order or decision.\nHeard by a district judge, not the Court of Appeals. K.S.A. 60-2103a(a)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Motion to transfer from Court of Appeals to Supreme Court","Kansas · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the notice of appeal on the appellee.\nNot filing waives any objection to Court of Appeals jurisdiction. K.S.A. 20-3017","#st-ks-appeals"],["Petition for review in the Supreme Court","Kansas · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from date of the Court of Appeals decision.\nA rehearing motion is not a prerequisite. Whether a rehearing motion affects this time is governed by Supreme Court rule (not collected). K.S.A. 20-3018(b)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Appeal to Supreme Court from decision holding a statute unconstitutional under Kan. Const. art. 6","Kansas · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the preliminary or final decision. K.S.A. 60-2102(b)(1)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Appeal of local-government quasi-judicial order to district court","Kansas · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order.\nOnly if no other appeal method is provided by law. K.S.A. 60-2101(d)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Suspension of order dissolving an injunction or provisional remedy","Kansas · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from order suspending it.\nNotice of appeal and approved supersedeas bond must be filed within the 14 days. K.S.A. 60-2103(d)(1)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Automatic stay of execution","Kansas · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment.\nNot for injunctions or receiverships. K.S.A. 60-262(a)","#st-ks-appeals"],["Supreme Court of Kentucky","Kentucky · courts","Seven justices, one elected from each of seven Supreme Court districts. Hears direct appeals of right in criminal cases with a sentence of death, life, or 20 years or more (RAP 2(A)(1), (D)). Takes civil cases mainly by discretionary review of the Court of Appeals (RAP 44) or by transfer of a pending appeal (RAP 17). Also answers certified questions of Kentucky law (RAP 50) and governs bar admission and lawyer discip","#st-ky"],["Court of Appeals","Kentucky · courts","Headquartered in Frankfort (SCR 1.030(1)) and decides cases in three-judge panels (RAP 2(G)(1)). Hears appeals of right from circuit court, including family court divisions. Hears discretionary review of circuit court decisions in cases that began in district court (RAP 44(B)(1)). Also handles workers’ compensation petitions (RAP 49) and original writ actions against circuit judges (RAP 60).","#st-ky"],["Circuit Court (including Family Court divisions)","Kentucky · courts","The general-jurisdiction trial court, which sits in judicial circuits. The civil filing fee is $188 from July 1, 2026, plus a $20 court technology fee and other fees (CR 3.02(1)). Family courts established under Ky. Const. § 112(6) are divisions of circuit court, and their appeals go to the Court of Appeals (RAP 2(A)(1)). Circuit court also hears appeals from district court (RAP 48).","#st-ky"],["District Court (including Small Claims Division)","Kentucky · courts","The limited-jurisdiction trial court. It handles smaller civil cases, probate, guardianship, forcible detainer, and misdemeanors. The civil filing fee is $94, and $38 in small claims, which covers cases of $2,500 or less (CR 3.03(1)). Appeals go to circuit court within 30 days (RAP 48 and RAP 3), but some statutes set shorter windows, such as 10 days for small claims and 7 days for forcible detainer (RAP 48(B)(2)).","#st-ky"],["Kentucky has an anti-SLAPP statute: KRS 454.460–454.478 (2022)","Kentucky · anti-SLAPP","Kentucky enacted a Uniform Public Expression Protection Act–style statute in 2022 Ky. Acts ch. 233, effective July 14, 2022. It provides a ‘special motion for expedited relief’ to dismiss a cause of action in whole or in part. The statute has no short title, so ‘UPEPA’ is a descriptive label. Cite the sections themselves. KRS 454.460–454.478; 2022 Ky. Acts ch. 233","#st-ky-slapp"],["Scope: government proceedings and speech on matters of public concern","Kentucky · anti-SLAPP","The statute covers claims based on three kinds of activity: (a) communication in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding; (b) communication on an issue under consideration in such a proceeding; and (c) exercise of free speech, press, assembly, petition, or association rights under the U.S. or Kentucky Constitution on a matter of public concern. ‘Matter of public concern’ i","#st-ky-slapp"],["Deadline: 60 days from service of the pleading","Kentucky · anti-SLAPP","File the special motion no later than 60 days after being served with the complaint, crossclaim, counterclaim, third-party claim, or other pleading asserting the claim. A later filing requires a showing of good cause. KRS 454.464","#st-ky-slapp"],["Automatic stay of everything between the movant and respondent","Kentucky · anti-SLAPP","Filing the motion stays all other proceedings between the moving and responding parties, including discovery and pending hearings and motions. On motion, the court may also stay matters involving other parties. The stay lasts until the ruling and the expiration of the time to appeal. If anyone appeals, all proceedings between all parties are stayed until the appeal ends.\nThe court must allow limited discovery if a pa","#st-ky-slapp"],["Hearing within 60 days; ruling within 60 days after the hearing","Kentucky · anti-SLAPP","The court must hear the motion within 60 days after filing, unless it orders a later hearing to allow limited discovery (then within 60 days of that order) or for good cause. It must rule within 60 days after the hearing. The court considers the pleadings, the motion, any response or reply, and any evidence admissible on summary judgment. KRS 454.468, 454.470, 454.474","#st-ky-slapp"],["Three-step test; dismissal is with prejudice","Kentucky · anti-SLAPP","The court must dismiss with prejudice if three things are true. First, the movant shows the statute applies under KRS 454.462(1). Second, the respondent fails to show an exclusion applies under KRS 454.462(2). Third, either the respondent fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows a failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of material fact. A voluntary dismissal without p","#st-ky-slapp"],["Immediate appeal as of right by either side","Kentucky · anti-SLAPP","Any party may appeal as of right from an order granting or denying the motion in whole or in part. The statute says the appeal is filed ‘in accordance with the Kentucky Rules of Civil Procedure’. Since January 1, 2023, appellate procedure has been in the Rules of Appellate Procedure (RAP 1(A); CR 1(2)), so expect a RAP 3 notice of appeal within 30 days. No collected text addresses that wording mismatch. KRS 454.476; ","#st-ky-slapp"],["Fees: mandatory for a winning movant; for a respondent only if the motion lacked good cause","Kentucky · anti-SLAPP","On the motion or an appeal of the order, the court shall award court costs, reasonable attorney’s fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion. A prevailing movant always gets them. A prevailing respondent gets them only if the court finds the motion was brought without good cause. KRS 454.478","#st-ky-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: KBA fee certification first, then a motion to the trial court","Kentucky · out of state","An out-of-state lawyer may practice a case in Kentucky only by doing three things. The lawyer must submit to the jurisdiction and rules of the Supreme Court of Kentucky. The lawyer must pay the Kentucky Bar Association a per-case fee equal to the annual dues of KBA members admitted five years or more. And the lawyer must engage a KBA member as co-counsel, who must be present at all trials and whenever the court requi","#st-ky-oos"],["Annual renewal fee, with automatic suspension 30 days after it is missed","Kentucky · out of state","If the case is still going, the pro hac vice lawyer must pay a renewal fee on each anniversary of the original admission, equal to the same 5-year dues amount. If the fee is not paid within 30 days of the due date, the lawyer is suspended from appearing in every case in which the lawyer is admitted pro hac vice. Kentucky co-counsel must notify the court immediately upon learning of the non-payment. SCR 3.030(3)(a)–(b","#st-ky-oos"],["KBA mechanics: permanent PHV ID, receipt to the court, close-out notice","Kentucky · out of state","Since January 1, 2016, each pro hac vice lawyer has a permanent Pro Hac Vice ID number, which must be given on every later certification request. The KBA emails a letter or receipt certifying payment. Both the lawyer and Kentucky co-counsel are responsible for getting it to the court. When the appearance ends, the lawyer updates the case on the KBA dashboard. KBA Pro Hac Vice Certification page (Nov. 10, 2026)","#st-ky-oos"],["eFiling registration does not list pro hac vice lawyers","Kentucky · out of state","Registration with the KY eCourts eFiling system is limited to four groups: licensed attorneys in good standing with the KBA, non-attorneys delegated access by a registered attorney, self-represented parties, and trusted submitters (court personnel). Out-of-state lawyers admitted pro hac vice are not on the list, so plan for Kentucky co-counsel to eFile. Registering creates consent to electronic service of eFilings (§","#st-ky-oos"],["Kentucky co-counsel must put a 5-digit KBA member number on every filing","Kentucky · out of state","Every Kentucky-licensed attorney must include the 5-digit member identification number on all filings with Kentucky courts. Members must also keep an official Bar Roster address that allows physical service and an official email address, and update each within 10 days of a change. SCR 3.035(1)(a)–(c) (AO 2022-43)","#st-ky-oos"],["In-house counsel: limited certificate (SCR 2.111)","Kentucky · out of state","A lawyer admitted elsewhere who works in Kentucky solely for an employer and its affiliates must apply for a limited certificate. The fee is $1,500 plus KBA dues. The lawyer becomes an active KBA member but may serve only the employer and approved pro bono programs (without fee). To appear as attorney of record for the employer in a Kentucky court, the lawyer must engage fully admitted Kentucky co-counsel. The certif","#st-ky-oos"],["Admission without examination: 5 of the last 7 years, reciprocity required","Kentucky · out of state","A lawyer who has actively practiced 5 of the 7 years before applying, in a jurisdiction with reciprocity or comity with Kentucky, may be admitted without examination. The fee is $1,500, non-refundable, and the applicant must pass character and fitness review. The applicant’s main practice jurisdiction must have reciprocal-admission rules no more restrictive than Kentucky’s. Full-time teaching at an ABA-accredited law","#st-ky-oos"],["Practice pending admission: up to one year, with mandatory disclaimers","Kentucky · out of state","A lawyer in good standing elsewhere who has applied under SCR 2.090 (UBE transfer) or 2.110 may practice for up to one year while the application is pending. Conditions include a continuous Kentucky presence, association with a Kentucky lawyer, notice to the KBA within 30 days of setting up an office, and paying the fee. All written communications must carry the line ‘Practice temporarily authorized pending admission","#st-ky-oos"],["Public-sector lawyers: 18-month limited admission (amended Oct. 14, 2026)","Kentucky · out of state","A lawyer admitted elsewhere who joins an organized public defender program, a legal services program for the indigent, the Attorney General, or a Commonwealth’s or County Attorney may be specially admitted for that office’s matters only. The fee is $100. Admission runs in 18-month terms, renewable until the lawyer is eligible for admission without examination under SCR 2.110. It ends at once if the employment ends. S","#st-ky-oos"],["Mandatory eFiling in probate and estate case types; others by separate order","Kentucky · out of state","Supported case types may be eFiled, and some are made mandatory by separate Supreme Court order. Since January 1, 2024, attorneys must eFile all eligible documents in listed probate/estate case types: guardianship, conservatorship and trusteeship, testate and intestate probate, dispensing with administration, name change, and will contests. From February 1, 2024, circuit clerks reject conventionally filed eligible do","#st-ky-oos"],["Limited-scope representation: not verified","Kentucky · out of state","Kentucky’s versions of Rule 1.2(c) and any civil rule on limited appearances (CR 11 or similar) were not in the collected texts, because the consolidated SCR 3.130 and CR are published officially only on Westlaw, which was excluded. Check SCR 3.130(1.2) and CR 11 before relying on a limited-scope engagement. SCR 3.130(1.2) (not collected)","#st-ky-oos"],["Governing code: SCR 3.130, the Kentucky Rules of Professional Conduct","Kentucky · ethics","Kentucky’s ethics rules are Supreme Court Rule 3.130, cited by rule number in parentheses, for example SCR 3.130(1.1), and each rule carries ‘Supreme Court Commentary’. The amendment orders collected follow the ABA Model Rules numbering through Part 8, but the consolidated text was not collected. This summary therefore lists only differences shown in amendment orders and does not claim to be complete. SCR 3.130 (AO 2","#st-ky-ethics"],["Rule 5.7 is ‘Activities of Suspended Lawyer’, and law-related services moved to 5.8","Kentucky · ethics","Unlike the ABA Model Rules, where 5.7 covers law-related services, Kentucky’s Rule 5.7 regulates work by suspended lawyers. A lawyer may not employ, associate professionally with, or aid a suspended lawyer in doing prohibited acts. A lawyer also may not employ or associate with a suspended member who was with the lawyer or firm when suspended. The commentary lists permitted preparatory and clerical tasks. Kentucky’s ","#st-ky-ethics"],["Prosecutors must report charged lawyers to Bar Counsel","Kentucky · ethics","A lawyer prosecuting any KBA member who has been arrested for, or charged by indictment, information, or complaint with, a felony or Class A misdemeanor must immediately notify Bar Counsel. The ABA Model Rule 8.3 has no counterpart. SCR 3.130(8.3)(f) (AO 2022-43)","#st-ky-ethics"],["Sale of a practice: seller must arrange for trust-account records","Kentucky · ethics","On sale of a law practice, the seller must make reasonable arrangements for maintaining client trust account records. The separate Kentucky Rule 1.20 on the same subject was deleted and folded into this new paragraph (g). SCR 3.130(1.17)(g); former SCR 3.130(1.20) (AO 2022-43)","#st-ky-ethics"],["Malpractice insurance disclosure every year by September 1","Kentucky · ethics","Every active member must certify by September 1 whether they are in private practice and, if so, whether they carry professional liability insurance of at least $100,000 per claim and $300,000 aggregate. The disclosure is made public. Missing the October 1 certification brings a $50 late fee. A lapse in reported coverage must be reported within 30 days. Lawyers working only for a government entity or an organization ","#st-ky-ethics"],["Competence commentary on lawyer well-being (2025)","Kentucky · ethics","New commentary [11] to Rule 1.1 says that a lawyer’s mental, emotional, and physical well-being is an important aspect of competence. It points to the Kentucky Lawyer Assistance Program and to Rules 1.16(a)(2), 5.1, 5.2, 5.3 and 5.8. SCR 3.130(1.1) cmt. [11] (AO 2025-04)","#st-ky-ethics"],["Trial publicity: Kentucky commentary lists presumptively prejudicial subjects","Kentucky · ethics","Rule 3.6(a) is phrased like the ABA rule: it bars extrajudicial statements the lawyer knows or should know will be publicly disseminated and have a substantial likelihood of materially prejudicing the proceeding. Kentucky commentary 5 lists subjects that are more likely than not to be prejudicial. These include a party’s or witness’s character or criminal record, test results, opinions on guilt, likely-inadmissible i","#st-ky-ethics"],["Advertising is overseen by an Attorneys’ Advertising Commission","Kentucky · ethics","Kentucky’s Part 7 includes SCR 3.130(7.02), titled ‘Attorneys’ advertising commission’. The commission’s filing and fee requirements were not in the collected texts. Check the KBA advertising rules page and SCR 3.130(7.01)–(7.50) before advertising in Kentucky. SCR 3.130(7.02) (title only, AO 2022-43)","#st-ky-ethics"],["The appeal clock runs from the clerk’s docket notation of service, not entry","Kentucky · land mine","The 30 days to appeal run from the date of the clerk’s docket notation of service of the judgment under CR 77.04(2). A timely notice of appeal is jurisdictional, and a late one is dismissed. Failing to receive notice does not extend time, except that the trial court may add up to 10 days for excusable neglect in learning of entry. RAP 2(A)(2)–(3), 3(A)(1)–(2), 3(C)–(D); CR 77.04(2), (4)","#st-ky-unique"],["Only CR 50.02, 52.02 and 59 motions toll; CR 60.02 does not","Kentucky · land mine","Time to appeal restarts for all parties only from the order disposing of the last timely CR 50.02, CR 52.02 or CR 59 motion, and not when a new trial is granted. No motion under any other civil rule tolls. To challenge the post-judgment order, you must file a new or amended notice of appeal. RAP 3(E)(2), (E)(4)","#st-ky-unique"],["Cross-appeal: 10 days after the appeal deadline, and no extensions","Kentucky · land mine","A notice of cross-appeal is due no later than 10 days after the last day allowed for the notice of appeal. It does not run from service of the opponent’s notice. Courts may not extend or enlarge time under RAP 3, 4 or 17, even for excusable neglect. Without a cross-appeal, an appellee cannot seek to enlarge its own rights. RAP 4(B)–(C), 6(C)–(D)","#st-ky-unique"],["Notice of appeal is not docketed until the fee is paid","Kentucky · land mine","Pay the fee to the clerk of the court being appealed from when you tender the notice. The notice is not docketed or noted as filed until payment, unless an in forma pauperis motion accompanies it. The fee is $150 for appeals to the Court of Appeals or Supreme Court and $60 for district court appeals, as in the 2022 text. Check for later fee orders. RAP 2(I) (AO 2026-01), 3(A)(3), 13(B), 48(B)(4)","#st-ky-unique"],["Temporary injunction rulings: 20 days to move the Court of Appeals","Kentucky · land mine","There is no appellate review of a CR 65.03 restraining order. A party hurt by an order granting, denying, modifying or dissolving a temporary injunction must move the Court of Appeals within 20 days of entry. Further review in the Supreme Court is due within 10 days and requires extraordinary cause. Rulings under RAP 20 are not reconsidered. The old CR 65.07–65.09 procedures were deleted. RAP 20(A), (B), (E), (F)(1);","#st-ky-unique"],["Anti-SLAPP: 60 days from service, automatic stay, and mandatory fees","Kentucky · land mine","A defendant who waits past 60 days after service needs good cause to file the special motion. Plaintiffs should note that filing the motion stays discovery automatically, and a losing plaintiff pays the movant’s fees and expenses. KRS 454.464, 454.466(1), 454.478(1)","#st-ky-unique"],["Pro hac vice lapses if the annual renewal goes unpaid","Kentucky · land mine","The per-case fee recurs on each anniversary of admission while the case continues. Thirty days after a missed payment, the lawyer is suspended from appearing in every Kentucky pro hac vice case. Kentucky co-counsel must tell the court. SCR 3.030(3)(a)–(b)","#st-ky-unique"],["eFiling deadline is midnight in the receiving court’s time zone; e-service adds 3 days","Kentucky · land mine","An eFiling is filed when the system’s Notice of Electronic Filing (NEF) says it is. It must be completed before midnight in the time zone of the receiving court, and a judge may set an earlier time. Kentucky courts sit in both the Eastern and Central time zones. Emailing a document to the clerk or judge is not filing. Electronic service is treated like mail service under CR 6.05, which adds 3 days. Fix a document the","#st-ky-unique"],["Appellate briefs: paper-style formatting, colored covers, word-count certificate","Kentucky · land mine","Briefs need a 1½-inch left margin and 12-point text and footnotes, and must be bound at the left. Covers are color-coded: red for appellant, blue for appellee, yellow for reply. In the Court of Appeals, initial and response briefs are limited to 8,750 words or 20 computer-generated pages, and replies to 1,750 words or 4 pages. A computer-generated brief over the page limit needs a RAP 15 word-count certificate. Brief","#st-ky-unique"],["If the appellee does not file a brief, the court may treat it as a confession of error","Kentucky · land mine","If the appellee’s brief is late, the court may accept the appellant’s statement of facts and issues, may reverse if the appellant’s brief reasonably appears to support reversal, or may treat the failure as a confession of error and reverse without reaching the merits. RAP 31(H)(3)","#st-ky-unique"],["Citing unpublished Kentucky opinions takes four conditions and the case number","Kentucky · land mine","You may cite a ‘Not To Be Published’ Kentucky opinion only if all four conditions hold: it was rendered after January 1, 2003; it is final; no published opinion adequately addresses the point; and you say it is not binding. Give the style, date and case number. Unpublished out-of-state opinions need a URL or a full copy in the appendix. RAP 41(A), (C)","#st-ky-unique"],["Court of Appeals prehearing conference: 20-day motion window that changes the briefing clock","Kentucky · land mine","Since April 1, 2026, a prehearing conference in civil appeals is by optional motion with a prehearing statement, filed within 20 days of the notice of appeal or cross-appeal. Some case types are excluded. Further steps do not run until the Court of Appeals orders. If no motion is filed, the time runs from the clerk’s notice saying so. Record designation and certification deadlines are keyed to the end of the preheari","#st-ky-unique"],["No peremptory strike; statutory disqualification plus an affidavit route to the Chief Justice","Kentucky · judges","No collected text gives a peremptory change of judge. Under KRS 26A.015(2), a judge must step aside for personal bias, personal knowledge of disputed facts, or having expressed an opinion on the merits. Other grounds include prior work as a lawyer in the matter, a financial interest held by the judge, spouse, or minor child, close relatives involved as parties, lawyers, or likely material witnesses, and any circumsta","#st-ky-unique"],["Appellate settlement judge cannot sit on the panel","Kentucky · judges","A Court of Appeals judge who takes part in a RAP 22 prehearing conference or settlement discussions may not sit on the panel that hears the appeal. RAP 22(D)(5) (as amended by AO 2026-01)","#st-ky-unique"],["Kentucky constitutional speech protections: not established here","Kentucky · speech","No collected text addresses whether the Kentucky Constitution’s free-speech provisions are read more broadly than the First Amendment. The anti-SLAPP statute expressly covers rights ‘guaranteed by the United States Constitution or Kentucky Constitution’ (KRS 454.462(1)(c)), but that does not define their scope. KRS 454.462(1)(c); Ky. Const. (not collected)","#st-ky-unique"],["Mediation rules are part of the Civil Rules (CR 99–100)","Kentucky · unique rules","Since February 1, 2022, court-ordered mediation is governed by CR 99, and mediator conduct by CR 100. Courts may not refer every case, or every case of a type, to mediation, and may not make mediation a condition of getting a trial date. Referral does not stay discovery. Parties have 15 days to agree on a mediator. CR 99.08(4) says an insured party must have a carrier representative present with full authority, who i","#st-ky-unique"],["Rule 1(2). Scope of the rules","Kentucky · civil rules","The Civil Rules govern all civil actions in the Court of Justice, with two exceptions: special statutory proceedings, where an inconsistent statutory procedure controls, and appeals, which are governed by the Rules of Appellate Procedure since January 1, 2023. AOC regulations and manuals issued with Supreme Court authorization on internal court policy have the same effect as the Rules.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 3.02. Circuit Court civil fees and costs","Kentucky · civil rules","From July 1, 2026, the Circuit Court civil filing fee is $188. There is no fee for habeas corpus, RCr 11.42, or KRS 202A/202B/387 mental-health proceedings, and a KRS 311.732 petition costs $10. A $20 court technology fee and KRS 453.060 and 27A.630 fees are added. Other costs: six-person jury $40; larger jury $70; third-party complaint $44; certification $5; copies $0.25 per page; video copy $25; audio copy $15; att","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 3.03. District Court civil fees and costs","Kentucky · civil rules","From July 1, 2026, the District Court civil filing fee is $94. Small claims (cases of $2,500 or less) cost $38. Cases of $500 or less outside small claims, probate, guardianship or conservatorship applications, name changes, paternity, and forcible detainer each cost $50. A petition to marry under KRS 402 costs $25, and a KRS 311.732 petition $13. There is no fee for KRS 202A/202B/387 mental-health cases or KRS 159.0","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 6.05. Additional time after service by mail","Kentucky · civil rules","Service by mail adds 3 days to a prescribed period. Electronic service through the eFiling system is treated the same way. (Rule text not collected. Summary taken from the cross-reference cited.) See eFiling Rules § 13(7).","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 50.02, 52.02, 59. Post-judgment motions (judgment notwithstanding the verdict, amended findings, new trial / alter or amend)","Kentucky · civil rules","A timely motion under any of these rules tolls the time to appeal for all parties until the order disposing of the last such motion. A CR 59 order granting a new trial does not restart the time. Motions under other rules, such as CR 60.02, do not toll. (Rule text not collected. Summary taken from the cross-reference cited.) See RAP 3(E)(2).","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 53.03. Master commissioner: powers","Kentucky · civil rules","An order of reference or local rule may limit the master commissioner to particular issues or acts and may set hearing times and a report deadline. Within those limits, the commissioner regulates hearings, may require production of books and papers, rules on admissibility unless directed otherwise, administers oaths, and examines witnesses and parties. On request, the commissioner makes a record of excluded evidence ","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 53.07. Master commissioner: limit on compensation","Kentucky · civil rules","A master commissioner’s total personal compensation from fees is capped under the Rules of Administrative Procedure, Part IV, unless the Chief Justice approves more. Fees above compensation and office expenses are remitted under CR 53.08, but six months of anticipated expenses may be kept.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 54.02(1). Judgment on fewer than all claims or parties","Kentucky · civil rules","When a judgment is made final and appealable on fewer than all claims or parties, a notice of appeal brings in only the parties against whom that judgment runs. (Rule text not collected. Summary taken from the cross-reference cited.) See RAP 2(A)(2).","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 62.02. Pending appeal of injunction judgments (deleted)","Kentucky · civil rules","Deleted effective January 1, 2023. Injunction relief pending appeal is now under RAP 20(C).","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 62.03. Pending appeal of injunction judgments (deleted)","Kentucky · civil rules","Deleted effective January 1, 2023. See RAP 20 and RAP 63.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 65.06. Enforcement of restraining orders and injunctions","Kentucky · civil rules","Compliance with a restraining order or injunction may be compelled, or disobedience punished as contempt, by the judge where the action is pending. If that judge is disqualified or absent from the district, any circuit judge may act, proceeding by rule and attachment on an affidavit or other evidence of breach. An injunction granted by an appellate court under RAP 20 is enforced by that appellate court.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 65.07. Interlocutory relief in Court of Appeals (deleted)","Kentucky · civil rules","Deleted effective January 1, 2023. Replaced by RAP 20(B): a motion to the Court of Appeals within 20 days of an order on a temporary injunction.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 65.08. Interlocutory relief pending appeal from final judgment (deleted)","Kentucky · civil rules","Deleted effective January 1, 2023. See RAP 20(C) and RAP 21.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 65.09. Interlocutory relief in the Supreme Court (deleted)","Kentucky · civil rules","Deleted effective January 1, 2023. See RAP 20(F): a motion within 10 days, discretionary, requiring extraordinary cause.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 72.01–72.13. Appeals from District Court (deleted)","Kentucky · civil rules","Deleted effective January 1, 2023. Now RAP 48.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 73.01–73.08. All appeals (deleted)","Kentucky · civil rules","Deleted effective January 1, 2023. Now RAP 2–3 and 5–6.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 74.01–74.02. Cross-appeals (deleted)","Kentucky · civil rules","Deleted effective January 1, 2023. Now RAP 4.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 75.01–75.15. Record on appeal (deleted)","Kentucky · civil rules","Deleted effective January 1, 2023. Now RAP 24–28.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 76.01–76.46. Practice in Court of Appeals and Supreme Court (deleted)","Kentucky · civil rules","Deleted effective January 1, 2023. Now RAP 7–63.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 77.04. Notice of entry of judgments and orders","Kentucky · civil rules","Immediately on entry of a judgment, a final order, an order affecting appeal time, or an order required to be served, the clerk serves notice of entry by mail under CR 5 on every party not in default, and notes the service date on the docket. That notation date, or the date of an earlier written waiver, is the date of entry for running appeal time under RAP 3. The court must require notice of entry for orders under C","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 79.05(1). Original record; removal","Kentucky · civil rules","The trial clerk keeps all papers in each action, which together with the docket form the original record. The record may leave the clerk’s office only on court order, on transmittal to an appellate court, or on withdrawal by counsel under RAP 26(D). It may be transferred to another county’s circuit clerk on a signed written request by counsel when a hearing is held there, and must be returned promptly afterward.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 79.06. Clerks of the Court of Appeals and Supreme Court (deleted)","Kentucky · civil rules","Deleted effective January 1, 2023.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 98. Video recorded court proceedings and appeals","Kentucky · civil rules","When a trial judge uses video recording, the official record is two simultaneous recordings, or two copies of the digital recording, labeled by the clerk in a set format. The clerk provides duplicates to counsel for an AOC-set fee. The judge keeps an exhibit list, a trial log, and a witness time log. By pretrial order, photographs may replace physical exhibits. A video deposition or its transcript may be the official","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 99.01. Mediation: authority, preamble and scope","Kentucky · civil rules","Adopted effective February 1, 2022, replacing the Model Mediation Rules. CR 99 governs every court-ordered mediation, and parties are encouraged to follow it otherwise. Mediation may not be ordered where a party may pose a risk of harm, or in conflict with KRS 403.036. A court may enforce its order to mediate. It may not reallocate mediation costs afterward or penalize a party because a case settles after mediation. ","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 99.02. Mediation defined","Kentucky · civil rules","Mediation is an informal process in which a neutral mediator helps the parties reach agreement. Decision-making authority stays with the parties.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 99.03. Referral of cases to mediation","Kentucky · civil rules","The court may refer a case, or part of one, on its own motion or a party’s, after weighing listed factors: stage of the case and discovery, nature of the issues, value of confidentiality and speed, willingness to settle, prior attempts, ability to participate (including virtually), and cost. Blanket referral of all cases or of a case type is barred, and so is requiring mediation before a trial date is set.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 99.04. No stay of proceedings","Kentucky · civil rules","Referral to mediation does not stay discovery or other proceedings unless the court orders a stay or the parties agree in writing.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 99.05. Appointment of mediator","Kentucky · civil rules","Within 15 days of referral, the parties must agree on a mediator or mediation service. If they cannot agree, they notify the court, which selects a mediator recognized as trained or experienced in civil actions. This wording took effect January 1, 2023.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 99.06. Mediator compensation","Kentucky · civil rules","An agreed mediator is paid the agreed rate. A court-appointed mediator may charge no more than the mediator’s standard rate. The parties split fees equally unless they agree or the court orders otherwise.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 99.07. Mediation procedure","Kentucky · civil rules","The conference is held at an agreed time and place, or as the court directs. The mediator may confer with parties beforehand, including ex parte, and may require confidential case statements.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 99.08. Attendance at mediation conference","Kentucky · civil rules","Parties and counsel attend unless the court orders or the parties explicitly agree otherwise. A public entity sends a representative with full authority to negotiate and recommend. Other organizations send a non-counsel representative with full settlement authority. Insured parties’ carriers send a representative, not outside counsel, with full authority. ‘Full authority’ does not require authority for any specific a","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 99.09. Reporting to the court","Kentucky · civil rules","A court-designated mediator gives written notice if the case is not accepted, and may refer it back for good cause. On settlement, counsel promptly notify the court and submit an order. A partial settlement requires a joint statement of resolved and remaining issues within 10 days after mediation ends. Counsel report in writing when the process ends, and they report only the lack of agreement unless all parties conse","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 99.10. Agreement","Kentucky · civil rules","Any agreement reached must be put in writing and signed by the parties. The parties draft it, and the mediator may help with their consent.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 99.11. Confidentiality","Kentucky · civil rules","Conferences are closed. Recording requires everyone’s agreement. Mediation counts as settlement negotiations under KRE 408. All mediation communications, including those before and after the conference, are privileged, confidential, not discoverable, and inadmissible, but otherwise-admissible evidence does not become protected just because it was used in mediation. The mediator may not disclose to non-participants or","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 100.01. Code of Conduct for Mediators: purpose","Kentucky · civil rules","The Code applies to all mediators in mediations governed by CR 99 and CR 100 (as corrected by AO 2022-08). It sets minimum standards. The mediator facilitates and does not decide the dispute.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 100.02. Competency","Kentucky · civil rules","The mediator must obtain appropriate skills and training and keep upgrading them. Process competence matters more than subject-matter expertise.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 100.03. Impartiality","Kentucky · civil rules","The mediator must stay impartial. By the first session, the mediator must disclose relationships with parties or counsel, personal knowledge of the facts, or any economic interest. The mediator must withdraw if a party keeps objecting on impartiality grounds after discussion, or if the mediator cannot be impartial.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 100.04. Confidentiality","Kentucky · civil rules","The mediator must protect confidentiality as required by CR 99.11, subject to its exceptions.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 100.05. Consent","Kentucky · civil rules","The mediator must make reasonable efforts to ensure each party understands the process, the mediator’s role, and their options, and may suggest they get professional advice.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 100.06. Self-determination","Kentucky · civil rules","The mediator respects party self-determination, is not directive or judgmental, and may raise questions and suggest options. The mediator may not impose opinions on the merits, but may give evaluative observations on request.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 100.07. Separation from legal and professional advice","Kentucky · civil rules","A mediator acts only as mediator and gives no legal or other professional advice during the mediation.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 100.08. Conflicts of interest","Kentucky · civil rules","The mediator may not charge contingent or outcome-based fees, use mediation information for personal gain, take on mediations that cannot be delivered timely, or give or receive referral consideration (de minimis refreshments excepted). The mediator should not accept gifts that raise impartiality questions.","#st-ky-rules"],["Rule 100.09. Protecting the integrity of the process","Kentucky · civil rules","The mediator encourages mutual respect and takes reasonable steps, consistent with self-determination, to limit abuse of the process.","#st-ky-rules"],["Appeals in Kentucky: a 30-day jurisdictional notice, counted from the clerk’s docket notation","Kentucky · appeals","Civil appeals from circuit court go to the Court of Appeals as of right. Civil cases reach the Supreme Court mainly by motion for discretionary review (RAP 44) or by a motion to transfer filed within 10 days of the notice of appeal (RAP 17). District court judgments are appealed to circuit court (RAP 48). Since January 1, 2023, all appellate procedure has been in the RAP, and the old CR 72–76 were deleted.\nOnly the t","#st-ky-appeals"],["Notice of appeal in the court below","Kentucky · appeals · Which court and how the appeal is taken","File the notice of appeal in the court from which the appeal is taken. Civil appeals go to the next higher court, and family court appeals go to the Court of Appeals. The notice must name the appellants, identify and date the judgment or order appealed, and certify service on all parties. It should also attach the judgment, name the appellate court and the other parties and counsel, and attach any orders on tolling m","#st-ky-appeals"],["Who becomes a party","Kentucky · appeals · Which court and how the appeal is taken","A timely notice from a final order on all claims brings in every party below, except those dismissed by an earlier final order. A notice from a judgment made final on fewer than all claims or parties under CR 54.02(1) brings in only the parties against whom that judgment runs. RAP 2(A)(2)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Appealing does not waive personal-jurisdiction or service defenses","Kentucky · appeals · Which court and how the appeal is taken","If the trial court denied a CR 12.02 defense of lack of personal jurisdiction, improper venue, insufficient process, or insufficient service, taking an appeal is not an entry of appearance in any court. RAP 2(E)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Transfer to the Supreme Court (bypass)","Kentucky · appeals · Which court and how the appeal is taken","Within 10 days after a notice of appeal to the Court of Appeals is filed, any party may move the Supreme Court to transfer the case, attaching the notice of appeal. Transfer is granted only for great and immediate public importance. The motion suspends further steps until the order on it. The fee is $150, and the 10 days cannot be extended. RAP 17(A), (C)–(D), (H); RAP 6(C)–(D); RAP 13(B)(1)(c)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Appeals from district court","Kentucky · appeals · Which court and how the appeal is taken","Appeals from district court go to circuit court. File the notice in district court within the RAP 3 time or a shorter statutory time, such as small claims (KRS 24A.340) 10 days, paternity (KRS 406.051) 60 days, or forcible detainer (KRS 383.255) 7 days. The fee is $60 plus other fees. Within 30 days after the notice, the appellant must file a statement of appeal (8,750 words or 20 pages). Appellees may file a counter","#st-ky-appeals"],["Joint, heard-together, and consolidated appeals (rewritten 2026)","Kentucky · appeals · Which court and how the appeal is taken","Appeals ‘heard together’ by the same panel keep separate briefs. ‘Consolidated’ appeals are joined for all purposes: all appellants file one joint brief and one joint reply, and each appellee files one response. Consolidation is generally granted only for appeals from the same circuit court action with identical parties. RAP 2(F)–(H) (AO 2026-01)","#st-ky-appeals"],["30 days from the docket notation of service","Kentucky · appeals · Notice of appeal: time and trigger","Unless a statute or rule says otherwise, the notice of appeal is due no later than 30 days from the date of the clerk’s notation of service of the judgment or order. Under CR 77.04(2), that notation date counts as the date of entry for appeal purposes, or the date a waiver of notice was filed if earlier. The clerk must serve notice of entry of every judgment, final order, and order that affects appeal time. RAP 3(A)(","#st-ky-appeals"],["Not getting notice does not help, except for a 10-day extension","Kentucky · appeals · Notice of appeal: time and trigger","Failure to serve or receive notice of entry does not affect appeal time. On a showing of excusable neglect based on failing to learn of entry, the trial court may extend the time by up to 10 days past the original deadline. CR 60.02 and other remedies remain available. RAP 3(C)–(D); CR 77.04(4)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Filing fee at tender; in forma pauperis","Kentucky · appeals · Notice of appeal: time and trigger","Pay the fee to the trial clerk when you tender the notice, or it will not be docketed. A notice accompanied by a timely in forma pauperis motion is treated as timely. If the motion is denied, the party has 30 days to pay or to appeal the denial. RAP 2(I) (AO 2026-01); RAP 3(A)(3); RAP 54–55","#st-ky-appeals"],["Appellate filing fees (2022 text)","Kentucky · appeals · Notice of appeal: time and trigger","The fee is $150 each for an appeal, cross-appeal, certification of law, transfer motion, motion for discretionary review or cross-motion, rehearing petition, amicus motion, RAP 20 motion, original action, or motion to reconsider a final order. Some family-division appeals (paternity, dependency/neglect/abuse, domestic violence, status offense) are $75, and district court appeals are $60. No fee is charged for a motio","#st-ky-appeals"],["Only CR 50.02, 52.02 and 59 toll","Kentucky · appeals · Post-judgment motions that stop the clock","If any party timely files a motion under CR 50.02, CR 52.02 or CR 59, appeal time runs for everyone from entry of the order disposing of the last such motion. A CR 59 order granting a new trial does not restart it. No motion under any other civil rule tolls the time. RAP 3(E)(2)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Premature notice: ask for abeyance","Kentucky · appeals · Post-judgment motions that stop the clock","If you file the notice of appeal while a tolling motion is pending, the trial court keeps jurisdiction to decide it. The appellant must promptly move the appellate court to hold the appeal in abeyance, then file the trial court’s order when entered. To challenge that order or an amended judgment, file a new or amended notice within 30 days of the notation of service of the disposing order. No added fee is charged for","#st-ky-appeals"],["10 days after the last day for the notice of appeal","Kentucky · appeals · Cross-appeals","Any party to the appeal may cross-appeal. The notice is due no later than 10 days from the last day allowed for the notice of appeal. A party first affected by someone else’s cross-appeal has 10 days from that notice to file an additional cross-appeal. Without a cross-appeal, a party cannot seek to enlarge its own rights or reduce an adversary’s. A cross-appeal survives dismissal of the main appeal. RAP 4(A)–(E); RAP","#st-ky-appeals"],["No extensions","Kentucky · appeals · Cross-appeals","Courts may not extend or enlarge time for actions under RAP 3, 4 or 17, even for excusable neglect. Enlargement is also barred for RAP 44 motions for discretionary review. RAP 6(C)–(D)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Prehearing conference by optional motion (since April 1, 2026)","Kentucky · appeals · Prehearing conference and the record","In most civil appeals to the Court of Appeals, any party may move for a prehearing conference with a prehearing statement (court form) within 20 days of the notice of appeal or cross-appeal. Other parties may respond within 10 days. Excluded are criminal cases, CR 23.06 class-certification appeals, prisoner, election, contempt, and writ appeals, family-type orders, and cases transferred to the Supreme Court.\nUntil th","#st-ky-appeals"],["Designating recordings and certifying the record","Kentucky · appeals · Prehearing conference and the record","The appellant must designate the dates of pre- and post-trial recordings needed. The deadline is within 10 days of the notice of appeal, or, where RAP 22 applies, within 10 days of the order ending prehearing procedure. Other parties may counter-designate within 10 days. Trial recordings are included automatically. The clerk certifies the record within 30 days after the first notice of appeal, or 30 days after prehea","#st-ky-appeals"],["Video record","Kentucky · appeals · Prehearing conference and the record","Where proceedings are video-recorded, two recordings form the official record, and duplicates are available for counsel for a fee. Briefs cite recordings as ‘VR mm/dd/yy at hh:mm:ss’ and the written record as ‘TR’ pages. CR 98(2) (AO 2022-49); RAP 31(E)(3)–(4)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Ordinary civil appeal","Kentucky · appeals · Briefing schedule","The appellant’s brief is due 60 days after the docket notation of the clerk’s notice of certification of the record. In the Supreme Court after discretionary review is granted, it is due 60 days from the order granting review. The appellee’s brief is due 60 days after the appellant’s brief. The reply is due 15 days after the last appellee brief, or after it was due where the reply answers several appellees. RAP 30(C)","#st-ky-appeals"],["With a cross-appeal","Kentucky · appeals · Briefing schedule","The appellee/cross-appellant’s combined brief is due 60 days after the appellant’s brief. The appellant’s combined reply/cross-appellee brief is due 60 days after the last appellee brief was filed or due. The cross-appellant’s reply is due 15 days after that. RAP 30(C)(2)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Expedited appeals: 30/30/10","Kentucky · appeals · Briefing schedule","Appeals from orders on adoption, termination of parental rights, paternity, dependency/abuse/neglect, domestic violence, juvenile status offenses, and CR 23.06 class-certification orders are expedited. The appellant has 30 days after the notice of certification, the appellee 30 days, and the reply 10 days, with combined briefs on the same 30-day pattern. Extensions are considered only in extraordinary circumstances. ","#st-ky-appeals"],["Service of briefs, including on the trial judge","Kentucky · appeals · Briefing schedule","Serve each brief on every party and on the judge whose decision is under review. Electronic service is allowed on the trial judge and on consenting parties. RAP 30(B)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Amicus curiae","Kentucky · appeals · Briefing schedule","An amicus brief needs leave. The motion and brief are due within 15 days after the appellant’s brief is filed or due, whichever is later, and the motion carries the $150 fee. The brief has a tan cover and a 5,250-word limit with a certificate, and no appendix except unpublished opinions. Amicus counsel may not argue. RAP 34","#st-ky-appeals"],["Court of Appeals limits","Kentucky · appeals · Length, format and content","Initial and response briefs: 8,750 words or 20 pages if computer-generated (25 pages if handwritten or typed). Reply: 1,750 words or 4 pages, plus 1,750 words or 4 pages for each additional appellee brief answered. Appellee response combined with cross-appeal: 14,000 words or 30 pages. Appellant reply combined with cross-appellee response: 10,500 words or 25 pages. RAP 31(G)(2) (AO 2026-01)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Supreme Court limits","Kentucky · appeals · Length, format and content","Initial and response briefs: 17,500 words or 40 pages. Reply: 3,500 words or 7 pages, plus 1,750 words or 4 pages for each additional appellee brief. Appellee response combined with cross-appeal: 22,750 words or 50 pages. Appellant reply combined with cross-appellee response: 19,250 words or 45 pages, raised in 2026 from 8,750 words or 20 pages. Death-penalty briefs may be extended to 52,500 words on a motion filed a","#st-ky-appeals"],["Word-count certificate and exclusions","Kentucky · appeals · Length, format and content","A computer-generated brief within the page limit needs no certificate. A brief over the page limit but within the word limit needs a certificate stating the word count, in the RAP 15(E) form. The cover, introduction, oral-argument statement, statement of points and authorities, signature block, exhibits, and appendices do not count. RAP 15(B)–(E); RAP 31(G)(1), (5)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Physical format and covers","Kentucky · appeals · Length, format and content","Use 8½ × 11 unglazed white paper, single-sided and double-spaced, with 12-point text and footnotes, a 1½-inch left margin and 1-inch other margins, bound at the left. Covers are red for appellant, blue for appellee and combined appellee/cross-appellant, and yellow for replies. The front cover carries the certificate of service and, in most courts, a certificate that the record was returned. File 1 unbound and 4 bound","#st-ky-appeals"],["Required parts of the appellant’s brief","Kentucky · appeals · Length, format and content","The appellant’s brief has these parts, in order: an introduction and oral-argument statement (one page combined); a statement of points and authorities (required if the brief exceeds 1,750 words since 2026, previously 5 pages); a statement of the case with record cites; argument opening each issue with a statement of whether and how it was preserved; conclusion; signature; and an appendix. The appendix is tabbed and ","#st-ky-appeals"],["Name redaction","Kentucky · appeals · Length, format and content","CR 7.03 applies. Use initials or a descriptive term instead of names in cases involving juveniles, abuse and neglect, termination of parental rights, mental health, and appeals from judgments granting expungement. RAP 5(B)(2), 7(B), 31(B) (AO 2026-01)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Consequences of noncompliance","Kentucky · appeals · Length, format and content","A brief that does not substantially comply may be stricken. A missing appellant’s brief can mean dismissal. A missing appellee’s brief lets the court accept the appellant’s facts, reverse if the appellant’s brief reasonably supports it, or treat the failure as a confession of error. RAP 31(H); RAP 10(B)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Motions practice","Kentucky · appeals · Motions, time computation, extensions","Motions must be in writing with particular grounds. Responses are due within 10 days of filing, and the court may hear oral argument on a motion only by order. File 1 unbound and 4 bound copies in either court unless RAP 43–46 say otherwise. A motion to dismiss an appeal suspends the running of time for other steps until it is denied or passed to the merits. A criminal appellant’s voluntary dismissal needs a sworn af","#st-ky-appeals"],["Counting days and asking for more time","Kentucky · appeals · Motions, time computation, extensions","Do not count the trigger day. A last day falling on a weekend or legal holiday rolls forward. For periods under 7 days, skip intermediate weekends and holidays. Parties cannot extend time by agreement. Before a deadline, the court may extend for cause. After it, the court may enlarge only for excusable neglect. Neither is available for RAP 3, 4 or 17, and enlargement is not available for RAP 44. RAP 6","#st-ky-appeals"],["Deficiency notices: 10 days to fix","Kentucky · appeals · Motions, time computation, extensions","If the Supreme Court or Court of Appeals issues a deficiency notice, file the corrected document within 10 days of the notice. RAP 5(F)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Mailbox rule for appellate clerks only","Kentucky · appeals · Motions, time computation, extensions","A document filed with the Supreme Court or Court of Appeals clerk is timely if sent by U.S. registered mail (not certified) or express mail, or by another recognized carrier, and the carrier’s receipt date on the outside of the container falls within the filing period. Notices of appeal are filed in the trial court and must actually be received there in time. RAP 5(C), (E)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Temporary injunctions: RAP 20 motion within 20 days","Kentucky · appeals · Interlocutory review","There is no appellate review of a CR 65.03 restraining order. A party adversely affected by an interlocutory order granting, denying, modifying or dissolving a temporary injunction may move the Court of Appeals within 20 days of entry. The trial court may suspend a dissolution order for up to 20 days to allow this. Responses are due in 10 days, and the motion is served on the trial judge. Emergency relief is availabl","#st-ky-appeals"],["Permanent injunctions pending appeal","Kentucky · appeals · Interlocutory review","After appealing a final judgment granting or denying an injunction, first ask the circuit court to grant, suspend, or modify relief pending appeal. Then the adversely affected party may move the Court of Appeals. RAP 20(C)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Intermediate relief while an appeal is pending","Kentucky · appeals · Interlocutory review","After a notice of appeal or a motion for discretionary review, a party facing immediate and irreparable injury may move for intermediate relief, attaching the judgment and any stay papers from below. Responses are due in 10 days, and no replies are allowed. Review of a Court of Appeals ruling follows RAP 20(F). RAP 21","#st-ky-appeals"],["Other immediately appealable orders seen in the collected texts","Kentucky · appeals · Interlocutory review","These include a judgment made final on fewer than all claims or parties under CR 54.02(1) (RAP 2(A)(2)); orders granting or denying class certification under CR 23.06, which are expedited (RAP 30(E)); and orders granting or denying an anti-SLAPP special motion, appealable as of right by any party (KRS 454.476). The full text of CR 54.02 and CR 23.06 was not collected. RAP 2(A)(2); RAP 30(E); KRS 454.476","#st-ky-appeals"],["Writs (original actions)","Kentucky · appeals · Interlocutory review","Original actions lie in an appellate court only against a judge or agency whose decisions that court reviews as of right. The petition is limited to 14,000 words or 30 pages, the response is due in 20 days, and no reply is allowed. A final Court of Appeals order in an original action may be appealed to the Supreme Court within 30 days, briefed on the expedited schedule. RAP 60(A)–(G), (I)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing, modification or extension: 20 days","Kentucky · appeals · Rehearing, finality and opinions","A petition for rehearing, modification, extension, or publication is due within 20 days of the opinion, and a response within 20 days of the petition. Rehearing is limited to a material fact, controlling statute, or decision the court overlooked, or a misconception of the issues. The limit is 3,500 words or 8 pages, with a green cover (gray for the response), and the fee is $150. Orders, as opposed to opinions, are c","#st-ky-appeals"],["When opinions become final","Kentucky · appeals · Rehearing, finality and opinions","A Supreme Court opinion is final on the 21st day after rendition. A Court of Appeals opinion is final on the 31st day, unless a timely rehearing petition or motion for discretionary review is filed. A timely motion for discretionary review suspends finality. No mandate issues: the clerk sends the final opinion to the trial court. RAP 40(G)(1)–(6)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Citing unpublished opinions","Kentucky · appeals · Rehearing, finality and opinions","‘Not To Be Published’ Kentucky opinions are not binding and are disfavored. You may cite one only if it was rendered after January 1, 2003, is final, addresses a point no published opinion adequately covers, and you state it is not binding. Give the style, date and case number. A motion to publish is due within 20 days of the opinion. RAP 40(D), 41, 43(H)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Motion for discretionary review: 30 days","Kentucky · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court","File within 30 days after the Court of Appeals decision. If a timely rehearing or reconsideration filing is pending, file within 30 days after its denial or after the final decision on rehearing. The limit is 5,250 words or 13 pages, with 10 copies, and the fee is $150. The response is due in 30 days, and no reply is allowed. Review is granted only for special reasons. Rulings on these motions are not reconsidered, a","#st-ky-appeals"],["Cross-motion for discretionary review: 10 days after a grant","Kentucky · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court","If review is granted, the respondent has 10 days to file a cross-motion designating additional issues. This suspends the briefing time until the order on the cross-motion. RAP 46(A), (D)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Briefing after a grant","Kentucky · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court","The movant becomes the appellant. Briefs run from the order granting review, starting with 60 days for the appellant. RAP 44(J)(1); RAP 30(C)(1)(a)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Certified questions","Kentucky · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court","Federal courts and the highest courts of other states or D.C. may ask the Kentucky Supreme Court to answer a determinative question of Kentucky law when there is no controlling Kentucky precedent. RAP 50(A)–(B)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Supersedeas bond stays a money judgment","Kentucky · appeals · Stays and supersedeas bonds","Except for a judgment granting a permanent injunction, the appellant may stay enforcement by posting a supersedeas bond, effective when approved by the court or clerk. For an unsecured money judgment, the bond covers the unsatisfied judgment, appeal costs, and interest, unless the trial court sets a different amount or other security for good cause after notice and hearing. The trial court keeps jurisdiction over bon","#st-ky-appeals"],["An eFiled appellate document is only ‘tendered’ until the clerk accepts it","Kentucky · appeals · eFiling in the appellate courts","In the appellate courts, eFiling submits a tendered document. The clerk reviews it for conformity before docketing and sends a notice of whether it was accepted. A deficient document must be resent in a new envelope. An untimely document not attached to a motion for enlargement may be rejected. Appellate clerks may post extra technical requirements for briefs. eFiling Rules § 9 (AO 2025-02)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Deadline and service","Kentucky · appeals · eFiling in the appellate courts","eFilings must be completed before midnight in the receiving court’s time zone. Notice of entry sent through the system counts as service of notice under CR 77.04 and RAP 40 for parties who consented to e-service. Parties who have not consented must be served on paper. Electronic service adds 3 days under CR 6.05. eFiling Rules §§ 8(2)(b), 13(3), 13(7), 14(2) (AO 2025-02)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Summary of the 2026 amendments","Kentucky · appeals · What changed on April 1, 2026 (AO 2026-01)","RAP 2 was rewritten: the fee clause moved to 2(I), and heard-together and consolidated appeals were redefined. RAP 7 motions practice was restated, with a 10-day response and rules for voluntary dismissal. The prehearing statement under RAP 22 became an optional motion. New RAP 23 requires notice to the Attorney General under KRS 418.075 before the appellant’s brief when a statute’s constitutionality is challenged; t","#st-ky-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (civil)","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from clerk’s docket notation of service of the judgment or order (CR 77.04(2)).\nJurisdictional; cannot be extended except up to 10 extra days for excusable neglect in learning of entry (RAP 3(D)). RAP 3(A)(1)–(2)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Extension for failure to learn of entry","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from expiration of the original appeal time.\nGranted by the trial court on excusable neglect. RAP 3(D)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Notice of appeal after a tolling motion","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from docket notation of service of the order disposing of the last CR 50.02, 52.02 or 59 motion.\nOther motions, including CR 60.02, do not toll. RAP 3(E)(2), (4)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Notice of cross-appeal","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from last day allowed for filing the notice of appeal.\nNo extension (RAP 6(C)–(D)). RAP 4(B)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Additional cross-appeal","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the notice of cross-appeal that first affects the party. RAP 4(E)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Motion to transfer to Supreme Court","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of notice of appeal to the Court of Appeals.\nNo extension; response 10 days. RAP 17(A)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Pay fee or appeal denial of in forma pauperis","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from denial of the in forma pauperis motion. RAP 3(A)(3)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Motion for prehearing conference (Court of Appeals civil)","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from notice of appeal (or notice of cross-appeal).\nOptional since April 1, 2026; other parties respond within 10 days (RAP 22(C)(3)). RAP 22(A)(1) (AO 2026-01)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Designation of recordings for the record","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from notice of appeal (or order ending prehearing procedure if RAP 22 applies).\nCounter-designation 10 days after (RAP 24(A)(2)). RAP 24(A)(1)(b)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Clerk certifies record","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from first notice of appeal (or order ending prehearing procedure).\nClerk duty; may be extended on motion. RAP 26(B)(4)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from docket notation of clerk’s notice of certification of the record (or order granting discretionary review).\nExpedited appeals: 30 days (RAP 30(E)). RAP 30(C)(1)(a)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from filing of appellant’s brief.\nExpedited: 30 days. RAP 30(C)(1)(b)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Reply brief","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of last appellee’s brief (or when due, if replying to several).\nExpedited: 10 days. RAP 30(C)(1)(c) (AO 2026-01)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Combined appellee/cross-appellant brief","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from filing of appellant’s brief. RAP 30(C)(2)(b)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Combined appellant reply/cross-appellee brief","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from last appellee brief filed or due.\nCross-appellant reply 15 days after (RAP 30(C)(2)(d)). RAP 30(C)(2)(c)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Amicus motion and brief","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from appellant’s brief filed or due, whichever is later. RAP 34(B)(2)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Response to a motion","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the motion. RAP 7(C) (AO 2026-01)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Corrected document after deficiency notice","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from date of the deficiency notice. RAP 5(F)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Motion for relief from temporary-injunction order","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from entry of the circuit court’s interlocutory order.\nResponse 10 days. RAP 20(B)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Supreme Court review of RAP 20/21 ruling","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of the Court of Appeals order.\nDiscretionary; extraordinary cause. RAP 20(F)(1); RAP 21(B)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing, modification, extension or publication","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from date the opinion was issued.\nResponse 20 days after petition. RAP 43(B)(3)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Motion to reconsider an order","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from entry of the order.\nNot available for RAP 17, 20, 21 or 44 orders. RAP 43(D)(1)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Motion for discretionary review (Supreme Court)","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from court of Appeals decision (or denial of, or decision on, rehearing/reconsideration).\nResponse 30 days; no enlargement after the fact (RAP 6(D)). RAP 44(B)(2)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Motion for discretionary review (Court of Appeals, district-court cases)","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the circuit court’s appellate judgment. RAP 44(B)(1)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Cross-motion for discretionary review","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from order granting the motion for discretionary review.\nResponse 10 days. RAP 46(A)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Statement of appeal (district court to circuit court)","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nCounterstatement 30 days after statement (RAP 48(F)(2)). RAP 48(F)(1)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Response to original action (writ) petition","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from filing of the petition. RAP 60(D)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Appeal of Court of Appeals final order in original action","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the judgment or order.\nFiled with the Court of Appeals clerk. RAP 60(I)(2)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP special motion","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from service of the pleading asserting the claim.\nLater only for good cause; order is appealable as of right (KRS 454.476). KRS 454.464","#st-ky-appeals"],["Supreme Court opinion becomes final","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from rendition.\nAbsent timely RAP 43 petition. RAP 40(G)(1)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Court of Appeals opinion becomes final","Kentucky · appeals · Deadlines","31 days from rendition.\nAbsent timely RAP 43 petition or RAP 44 motion. RAP 40(G)(1)","#st-ky-appeals"],["Supreme Court of Louisiana","Louisiana · courts","Court of last resort. Reviews court of appeal judgments mainly by discretionary writ (certiorari) under Supreme Court Rule X, which lists the considerations that ordinarily must be present for a grant (conflicting decisions, significant unresolved issues, erroneous application of law causing material injustice, and so on). Regulates bar admission, pro hac vice (Rule XVII § 13), lawyer discipline (Rule XIX, through th","#st-la"],["Courts of Appeal (First through Fifth Circuits)","Louisiana · courts","Five circuit courts of appeal hear appeals from district, family, juvenile, city, and parish courts within their circuits and exercise supervisory jurisdiction by writ over interlocutory rulings. All five follow the Uniform Rules of Louisiana Courts of Appeal (version with amendments effective July 1, 2026), and each also has local rules (for example, on paper copies and e-filing).","#st-la"],["District Courts (including Civil District Court for the Parish of Orleans)","Louisiana · courts","General-jurisdiction trial courts organized by judicial district (one or more parishes). Civil practice is governed by the Code of Civil Procedure and by the statewide Rules for Louisiana District Courts (Title I general rules, Title II civil rules), plus district-by-district appendices. Cases are randomly allotted to a division (C.C.P. art. 253.1).","#st-la"],["Family and Juvenile Courts","Louisiana · courts","District Court Rules Titles IV and V refer to family courts and juvenile courts as well as district courts; where no separate court exists, district courts hear these matters. Not separately researched.","#st-la"],["City Courts, Parish Courts, and Justice of the Peace Courts","Louisiana · courts","Book VIII of the Code of Civil Procedure (arts. 4831–5003) governs courts of limited jurisdiction. Parish courts share jurisdiction with district courts up to $20,000 in dispute (art. 4842); city courts generally up to $15,000, with higher limits for city courts listed in art. 4843(D) and beyond. Jury trials are prohibited in parish, city, and justice of the peace courts (art. 4871); a defendant entitled to a jury ma","#st-la"],["Louisiana has an anti-SLAPP procedure: the special motion to strike, La. C.C.P. art. 971","Louisiana · anti-SLAPP","Scope: a cause of action against a person arising from any act in furtherance of that person’s right of petition or free speech under the U.S. or Louisiana Constitution in connection with a public issue. Covered acts include statements before, or in connection with an issue under review by, a legislative, executive, or judicial body or other official proceeding; statements in a place open to the public or a public fo","#st-la-slapp"],["Deadline: 90 days from service; hearing within 30 days; discovery stayed","Louisiana · anti-SLAPP","File within 90 days of service of the petition, or later in the court’s discretion on terms it sets. If the plaintiff voluntarily dismisses before the answer delay runs, the defendant keeps the right to file the motion within the same 90 days, and it is heard under art. 971.\nThe motion is to be noticed for hearing not more than 30 days after service unless docket conditions require a later hearing.\nAll discovery is s","#st-la-slapp"],["Fees go to the prevailing party, either side","Louisiana · anti-SLAPP","A prevailing party on a special motion to strike “shall be awarded” reasonable attorney fees and costs. The text is not limited to a prevailing defendant, so a plaintiff who defeats the motion can also recover. La. C.C.P. art. 971(B)","#st-la-slapp"],["Appeal: no special appeal rule in the current article","Louisiana · anti-SLAPP","The current text of art. 971 (last amended by Acts 2012, No. 449) contains no provision of its own on appeal. Whether a ruling is appealable therefore turns on the general rules: a judgment granting the motion and dismissing the claims is a final judgment (or a partial final judgment needing designation under art. 1915(B) if claims remain), while a denial is interlocutory and is reviewed by supervisory writ under art","#st-la-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: apply to the Disciplinary Board first, then move the court","Louisiana · out of state","Step 1: the out-of-state lawyer files the Application for Pro Hac Vice Admission (form in Part C of the Rule XVII Appendix) with the Louisiana Attorney Disciplinary Board, verified by both the out-of-state lawyer and Louisiana counsel, with the fee. Disciplinary Counsel issues a letter approving or disapproving it (with reasons if disapproved).\nStep 2: the Louisiana lawyer files a written ex parte motion in the court","#st-la-oos"],["Fee: $450 to the Disciplinary Board, plus any court fee","Louisiana · out of state","A non-refundable $450.00 fee is paid to the Louisiana Attorney Disciplinary Board, separate from any fee the court or agency charges. No fee if the lawyer will not charge the client and works with a pro bono project or nonprofit legal services organization in a civil case for its clients, or is in a criminal or habeas case for an indigent defendant. La. Sup. Ct. R. XVII, § 13(A)(4)(ii)–(iii)","#st-la-oos"],["Local counsel is mandatory and stays responsible","Louisiana · out of state","Eligibility requires acting in association with a Louisiana-licensed lawyer as required by La. R.S. 37:214. The in-state lawyer remains responsible to the client and for the conduct of the proceeding, and must tell the client when that lawyer’s independent judgment differs from the out-of-state lawyer’s.\nEligible lawyers: members in good standing of the bar of any U.S. District Court or of the highest court of any st","#st-la-oos"],["The 30-day-before-trial cutoff and the ‘regular practice’ ground","Louisiana · out of state","Absent extraordinary circumstances fully explained in the motion, courts and agencies will not entertain a pro hac vice motion filed within 30 days of the scheduled trial or hearing date.\nA motion ordinarily should be granted unless the court finds reason to believe, among other things, that the applicant’s appearances are so frequent as to constitute regular practice in Louisiana, that admission would harm the admin","#st-la-oos"],["Work allowed without pro hac vice; arbitration and mediation","Louisiana · out of state","Without admission, an out-of-state lawyer may consult with Louisiana lawyers and potential clients, prepare for a potential Louisiana proceeding if the lawyer reasonably believes they are eligible for pro hac vice, work on out-of-state proceedings, and prepare for and take part in any ADR proceeding (arbitration, mediation) wherever held. These services are not unauthorized practice even if no case is filed or admiss","#st-la-oos"],["Pro hac vice lawyers are subject to Louisiana discipline and court rules","Louisiana · out of state","From the application on, the out-of-state lawyer submits to the authority of Louisiana courts and the Disciplinary Board for all conduct relating to the proceeding, under Supreme Court Rule XIX, the Rules of Professional Conduct, contempt and sanctions orders, local rules, and court policies, and must become familiar with them. La. Sup. Ct. R. XVII, § 13(A)(5)","#st-la-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances in district court: Notice of Limited Appearance","Louisiana · out of state","A Louisiana lawyer may enroll for a limited purpose by filing a Notice of Limited Appearance (Appendix 9.12A for family law, 9.12B for other cases) stating the limitation by subject matter, proceeding, date, or time period under Rule of Professional Conduct 1.2(c). It is signed by lawyer and client; if the client cannot sign at filing, a client-signed certificate of the scope must be filed within 10 days. Every plead","#st-la-oos"],["E-filing: Supreme Court CDX is for Louisiana-admitted lawyers; lower courts vary","Louisiana · out of state","Supreme Court e-filing through the Court Data/Document Exchange (cdx.lasc.org) is open to attorneys admitted to and in good standing with the Supreme Court of Louisiana who complete mandatory training; filings must be text-searchable PDF/A. A filing completed before midnight Central Time on the due date is timely, though filings after 4:30 p.m. are processed the next business day. A rejected filing may be re-filed wi","#st-la-oos"],["Governing code: Louisiana Rules of Professional Conduct","Louisiana · ethics","Adopted by the Supreme Court and published by the Louisiana Attorney Disciplinary Board. The collected compilation is current “with amendments through May 10, 2023” (the Rule 1.10 order); the Supreme Court’s list of rule-change orders through November 2026 shows no later amendment to these Rules. The Rules track the ABA Model Rules in structure, with the Louisiana differences below. Lawyer discipline is under Supreme","#st-la-ethics"],["Rule 1.8(e): limited financial help to necessitous clients is allowed","Louisiana · ethics","Unlike the ABA rule, Louisiana lets a lawyer give a client in necessitous circumstances minimal financial assistance for documented subsistence needs (food, shelter, utilities, insurance, non-litigation medical care, transportation, education), if without it the client could not start or keep the case going. Limits: no offer before being hired, no use as an inducement, no advertising it, no interest or fees on the la","#st-la-ethics"],["Rule 1.5: written contingency contract and detailed fee-in-advance rules","Louisiana · ethics","A contingent fee agreement must be in a writing signed by the client, with a copy given at signing, stating the percentage on settlement, trial, or appeal, the expenses deducted, and whether before or after the fee. Fee splitting with another firm requires the client’s written agreement, written disclosure of each lawyer’s share, and meaningful legal services by each lawyer (not ABA’s ‘proportion of services or joint","#st-la-ethics"],["Rule 1.10: screening, but not when the switching lawyer was on the other side of a pending case","Louisiana · ethics","Louisiana allows screening of a lateral lawyer to avoid imputed disqualification, with written notice to the former client, but not if the lawyer represented the former client in an adjudicative proceeding directly adverse to a current firm client and that proceeding is still pending when the lawyer changes firms. La. R. Prof. Conduct 1.10(c)–(d)","#st-la-ethics"],["Rule 8.4: no ABA 8.4(g) discrimination rule; bar on threatening charges instead","Louisiana · ethics","Louisiana’s Rule 8.4 does not contain the ABA’s 8.4(g) on harassment or discrimination. Its 8.4(g) instead makes it misconduct to threaten to present criminal or disciplinary charges solely to gain an advantage in a civil matter. La. R. Prof. Conduct 8.4(a)–(g)","#st-la-ethics"],["Advertising: file ads with the LSBA; detailed Rules 7.1–7.10","Louisiana · ethics","Louisiana has its own advertising regime. Subject to Rule 7.8 exemptions, a lawyer who advertises in public media or sends unsolicited written communications must file a copy of each with the LSBA Rules of Professional Conduct Committee before or at first dissemination, with the required information and fee, and gets a filing number; advance review at least 30 days before dissemination is optional. Out-of-state lawye","#st-la-ethics"],["Rule 5.5(e): do not hire disbarred or suspended lawyers","Louisiana · ethics","A Louisiana addition: a lawyer may not employ or engage, in connection with the practice of law, a person the lawyer knows or should know is disbarred or permanently resigned in lieu of discipline, and may employ a suspended or disability-inactive lawyer only under conditions set out in the rule. Rule 5.5(d)(1) in-house practice requires a limited license under Supreme Court Rule XVII § 14. La. R. Prof. Conduct 5.5(d","#st-la-ethics"],["No motion to dismiss: plead the right exception, at the right time, or lose it","Louisiana · land mine","Rule 12-style defenses are raised by exception. Declinatory (insufficient citation or service, including failure to request service within 90 days; lis pendens; improper venue; lack of personal jurisdiction) and dilatory exceptions (prematurity, vagueness, improper cumulation, lack of capacity, and others) must be filed together, before or in the answer and before any other pleading seeking relief, and before a defau","#st-la-unique"],["Abandonment: three years without a step ends the case automatically","Louisiana · land mine","A suit is abandoned, without any order, if no party takes a step in the trial court for three years; formal discovery served on a party, filed or not, counts. A 2026 amendment (Acts 2026, No. 127) requires the mover’s affidavit to list record filings for the prior three years and written communications in the final year, and sets 30 days from sheriff’s service to move to set aside and 60 days to appeal. Abandonment a","#st-la-unique"],["Prescription: tort claims now two years, but check the old one-year rule for older injuries","Louisiana · land mine","Acts 2024, No. 423 repealed Civil Code arts. 3492 and 3493 effective July 1, 2024 and enacted art. 3493.1: delictual actions prescribe in two years from the day injury or damage is sustained (with a special start for damage to immovables in art. 3493.2). Claims arising before July 1, 2024 may still be governed by the former one-year period; the Act’s transition provision was not collected, so confirm it before relyin","#st-la-unique"],["Request service within 90 days, and pay the clerk","Louisiana · land mine","Service of citation must be requested on every named defendant within 90 days of filing (or of an amended petition adding the defendant), and the clerk’s service fees must be paid within that period (or 30 days after a statement of additional fees). A defendant can obtain dismissal without prejudice by declinatory exception or contradictory motion unless good cause is shown, and voluntary or involuntary dismissal can","#st-la-unique"],["Devolutive vs. suspensive appeal: 60 days or 30 days plus bond","Louisiana · land mine","You appeal by obtaining an order of appeal from the trial court, not by filing a notice. A devolutive appeal (no stay, no bond) is due within 60 days, and a suspensive appeal (stays execution) within 30 days, with the security posted within the same 30 days, both counted from the end of the 7-day new-trial/JNOV delay (exclusive of legal holidays) or from mailing of notice denying such a motion. Miss the 30 days and t","#st-la-unique"],["Partial rulings are interlocutory: use a writ, fast","Louisiana · land mine","A judgment, partial summary judgment, or sustained exception disposing of fewer than all claims, issues, or theories by or against a party is interlocutory under art. 1915(C) as amended by Acts 2025, No. 250, with no designation procedure in the current text. Review is by supervisory writ: give notice of intent and get a return date, which may not exceed 30 days from notice of the ruling. Denials of exceptions and of","#st-la-unique"],["Never plead a dollar amount; know the jury threshold and bond","Louisiana · land mine","A petition may not state a specific amount of damages (except in suits on contracts, notes, open accounts, and similar claims); use a general allegation that the claim exceeds or is less than a threshold when it matters, such as for federal diversity or the jury right. A specific amount is struck on motion and can bring fees. No jury where no plaintiff’s claim exceeds $10,000; a jury demand is due within 10 days afte","#st-la-unique"],["Summary judgment has hard, fixed deadlines","Louisiana · land mine","Unless the court extends and all parties agree: the motion and supporting documents must be filed and served at least 65 days before trial, the opposition at least 15 days before the hearing, and the reply at least 5 days before (counting legal holidays). The hearing must be set at least 30 days after filing and 30 days before trial, and judgment rendered at least 20 days before trial. Only listed kinds of documents ","#st-la-unique"],["Answer in 21 days; defaults move fast and need only 7 days’ notice in some cases","Louisiana · land mine","The answer is due 21 days after service of citation (30 if discovery was served with the petition). There is no preliminary default: once the delay runs, the plaintiff may obtain a default judgment on a prima facie case. Notice of intent to take a default is required, at least 7 days before, only if the defendant appeared of record, the defendant’s lawyer wrote to the plaintiff about the case, or it is a tort case. A","#st-la-unique"],["District court filing: paper in person or e-file only since January 1, 2026","Louisiana · land mine","Since January 1, 2026, attorneys’ filings in district court may be made only in person on paper or electronically through the clerk’s system or the Louisiana Clerks’ Remote Access Authority (with listed original documents still allowed by mail or courier), and statutory fax filing ended January 1, 2026. Filings must omit the first five digits of SSNs, tax and account numbers, and other protected information. Do not p","#st-la-unique"],["Pro hac vice starts at the Disciplinary Board and closes 30 days before trial","Louisiana · land mine","Before moving the court, the out-of-state lawyer must file a verified application with the Louisiana Attorney Disciplinary Board and pay $450, then obtain Disciplinary Counsel’s letter, which Louisiana counsel attaches to an ex parte motion served on all parties. Any party or Disciplinary Counsel may object within 20 days. Absent extraordinary circumstances explained in the motion, courts will not consider a motion f","#st-la-unique"],["Local motion practice: 15-day memoranda and judgment circulation","Louisiana · land mine","Under the statewide District Court Rules, the mover’s memorandum must be received by all parties 15 calendar days before the hearing and the opposition 8 calendar days before; replies must arrive by 4:00 p.m. leaving one full working day before the hearing. Proposed judgments not signed at the hearing must be circulated at least 5 working days before presentation, with a Rule 9.5(b) certificate. Late memoranda can co","#st-la-unique"],["No peremptory substitution of judge; recusal for cause only","Louisiana · judges","The Code of Civil Procedure’s recusal chapter (arts. 151–161) has no peremptory challenge or automatic change of judge. A judge must be recused if the judge is a witness; was employed or consulted as a lawyer in the cause and participated; is the spouse of a party or lawyer, or has a parent, child, or immediate family member who is a party or lawyer; or is biased, prejudiced, or interested to the point of being unabl","#st-la-unique"],["Recusal motion: 30 days from discovery and before the case is set for trial","Louisiana · judges","File a written motion stating a ground under art. 151 no later than 30 days after discovering the facts, and in all cases before the matter is scheduled for trial (or immediately, if the facts arise or could not have been discovered until after scheduling). Within 7 days after receiving the motion, the judge must either step aside or ask the Supreme Court to appoint an ad hoc judge to hear it; only that ad hoc judge ","#st-la-unique"],["Judges must disclose certain relationships","Louisiana · judges","Trial and appellate judges must disclose to all lawyers and self-represented parties: a past association with a lawyer during that lawyer’s employment in the cause; continuing to employ the handling lawyer (or that lawyer’s firm) personally at the time of a contested hearing; having performed a judicial act in the cause in another court; kinship with a party or party’s spouse within the fourth degree, or with a lawye","#st-la-unique"],["Appellate judges and Supreme Court justices","Louisiana · judges","Recusal of court of appeal judges and Supreme Court justices has its own procedure (C.C.P. arts. 157–161; Supreme Court Rule XXXVI for justices). Those texts were not collected. La. C.C.P. arts. 157–161 (not collected); La. Sup. Ct. R. XXXVI (not collected)","#st-la-unique"],["La. Const. art. I, § 7 | Breadth relative to the First Amendment not established here","Louisiana · speech","Article I, § 7 of the Louisiana Constitution protects freedom of expression. Neither the constitutional text nor case law comparing it with the First Amendment was collected, so no claim is made that Louisiana protects speech more broadly. The practical speech protection documented here is procedural: the art. 971 special motion to strike. La. Const. art. I, § 7 (not collected); La. C.C.P. art. 971","#st-la-unique"],["Civil-law vocabulary: petition, exceptions, reconvention, prescription","Louisiana · unique rules","Louisiana procedure uses its own terms. A complaint is a petition; Rule 12 defenses are exceptions (declinatory, dilatory, peremptory); a counterclaim is a reconventional demand; joinder of claims is cumulation; a statute of limitations is prescription (and a non-interruptible period is peremption); a notice of appeal is an order of appeal; a cross-appeal is an answer to the appeal. Replicatory pleadings (replies) ar","#st-la-unique"],["Transactional res judicata","Louisiana · unique rules","A party must assert all causes of action arising out of the transaction or occurrence that is the subject of the litigation, with family-law exceptions. Claims left out can be lost. La. C.C.P. art. 425","#st-la-unique"],["Non-unanimous civil juries and a $10,000 jury threshold","Louisiana · unique rules","Civil verdicts need 5 of 6 or 9 of 12 jurors unless the parties stipulate otherwise. No jury is available where no individual plaintiff’s claim exceeds $10,000, and a party demanding a jury must post a bond or, in mid-sized tort cases, a $5,000 cash deposit. La. C.C.P. arts. 1732, 1733, 1797","#st-la-unique"],["Random allotment and statewide district court rules","Louisiana · unique rules","Every case is randomly allotted to a division (C.C.P. art. 253.1). The Rules for Louisiana District Courts apply statewide, with district-by-district appendices: memoranda on exceptions and motions must be received 15 calendar days before the hearing (oppositions 8 days; replies by 4:00 p.m. with one full working day to spare), no hearing is set less than 15 days after filing without good cause, proposed judgments mu","#st-la-unique"],["Rule NOTE. What this file covers (a selection)","Louisiana · civil rules","Louisiana civil procedure is the Code of Civil Procedure (La. C.C.P.), a statute of roughly 1,260 articles in nine Books, not court rules. This file is a SELECTION: it summarizes about 300 key articles, grouped by Book and Title, read from the official text on legis.la.gov on November 10, 2026 (the site reflects acts through the 2026 Regular Session, e.g., Acts 2026, No. 127 amending art. 561). Articles not listed he","#st-la-rules"],["Rule BOOK I. Courts, Actions, and Parties (arts. 1–821)","Louisiana · civil rules","Jurisdiction, venue, recusal, court officers, actions (cumulation, lis pendens, abandonment, class actions), and parties.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1–5. Subject-matter jurisdiction","Louisiana · civil rules","Jurisdiction over the subject matter cannot be conferred by consent; a judgment by a court without it is void (art. 3). When jurisdiction depends on amount, the amount demanded in good faith controls (art. 4). A plaintiff who reduces a claim to fit a court’s jurisdiction remits the excess and cannot sue for it later (art. 5).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 6. Personal jurisdiction","Louisiana · civil rules","Personal jurisdiction rests on service on the defendant or its agent (or an express waiver under art. 1201), service on a court-appointed attorney for an absent or incompetent Louisiana domiciliary, or submission (by suing, or by not timely filing the declinatory exception). Over nonresidents, courts may exercise jurisdiction on any basis consistent with the state and federal constitutions.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 41–45. Venue: general rules","Louisiana · civil rules","Venue is the parish where an action may properly be brought. Individuals are sued in the parish of domicile (or residence); domestic corporations, insurers, and LLCs where the registered office is; licensed foreign corporations and LLCs at the principal business establishment designated with the state; unlicensed foreign entities and nonresidents without an agent in the plaintiff’s parish or where served (art. 42). V","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 71–86. Venue: exceptions","Louisiana · civil rules","Selected: solidary obligors may be sued where venue is proper under art. 42 as to any of them, with a special rule for tort claims (art. 73); tort suits may be filed where the wrongful conduct occurred or where damages were sustained (art. 74); contract suits where the contract was executed or the work was or was to be performed (art. 76.1). Other articles fix venue for immovable property, successions, partition, and","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 121–124. Improper venue; change of venue; forum non conveniens","Louisiana · civil rules","An action in an improper venue may be dismissed or, in the interest of justice, transferred (art. 121). A party may obtain a change of venue to a parish where no party is domiciled on proof that a fair trial is impossible (art. 122). Art. 123 allows forum non conveniens transfer between district courts on contradictory motion, but not of a suit filed in the plaintiff’s home parish in an otherwise proper court; dismis","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 151–161. Recusal of judges","Louisiana · civil rules","Mandatory grounds for recusal of any trial or appellate judge, plus a catch-all where a substantial and objective basis would prevent fair handling (art. 151). Judges must disclose listed relationships (art. 152). A motion to recuse a district judge must be filed within 30 days of discovering the facts and before the case is set for trial; within 7 days the judge must recuse or ask the Supreme Court for an ad hoc jud","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 191–197. Power and authority of courts","Louisiana · civil rules","Courts have inherent power needed to exercise their jurisdiction (art. 191). Courts may adopt local rules not contrary to law (art. 193). In a civil proceeding that needs no witness testimony or evidence, a party may give written notice at least 10 days before the hearing that it will appear remotely, and a court with the technology must allow it unless an exception in art. 195.1 applies.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 221–227. Contempt","Louisiana · civil rules","Contempt is direct (in the court’s presence, or contumacious failure to obey a subpoena or summons) or constructive (for example, willful disobedience of a judgment or order). No one may be held in contempt except for conduct defined as such by law; punishment is in R.S. 13:4611.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 251–259. Clerks of court","Louisiana · civil rules","All pleadings, documents, and exhibits are delivered or transmitted to the clerk, who endorses the filing date (art. 253). All pleadings are randomly allotted to a section or division of court (art. 253.1). (Clerk e-filing statutes, such as R.S. 13:850, were not collected.)","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 371. Attorney as officer of the court","Louisiana · civil rules","An attorney must act with decorum and treat the court, its officers, jurors, witnesses, opposing parties, and opposing counsel with due respect; the article lists further duties.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 421–428. Actions: general","Louisiana · civil rules","A civil action is commenced by filing a pleading presenting the demand to a court of competent jurisdiction; amicable demand is not required unless a law says so (art. 421). An action is premature until the obligation is enforceable (art. 423). Res judicata in Louisiana is transactional: a party must assert all causes of action arising out of the transaction or occurrence that is the subject of the litigation, with f","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 461–465. Cumulation of actions (joinder of claims and parties)","Louisiana · civil rules","A plaintiff may cumulate actions against one defendant if each is within the court’s jurisdiction, in proper venue, mutually consistent, and uses the same form of procedure (art. 462). Joinder of plural parties also requires a community of interest (art. 463). Improper cumulation is raised by dilatory exception (art. 926); if jurisdiction or venue fails as to one action, that action is dismissed (art. 464).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 531–532. Lis pendens","Louisiana · civil rules","When two suits on the same transaction or occurrence between the same parties in the same capacities are pending in Louisiana courts, the defendant may have all but the first dismissed by declinatory exception (art. 531). If the first suit is in another state or federal court, the Louisiana court may stay the second suit instead (art. 532).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 561. Abandonment (3 years without a step)","Louisiana · civil rules","An action is abandoned, without any court order, when the parties take no step in its prosecution or defense in the trial court for three years; formal discovery served on a party, filed or not, is a step. After the period runs the defendant may renounce abandonment. As amended in 2026, a motion to dismiss must attach an affidavit listing record filings during the prior three years and written communications in the f","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 591–597. Class actions","Louisiana · civil rules","Prerequisites track FRCP 23(a) (numerosity, commonality, typicality, adequacy) plus a fifth requirement that the class be objectively definable without inquiring into each member’s merits, and (b) categories modeled on FRCP 23(b) (art. 591). The proponent must move to certify within 90 days after service of the initial class pleading on all adverse parties, extendable by stipulation or for good cause (art. 592). Fili","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 611–617. Derivative actions","Louisiana · civil rules","Shareholder and member derivative suits; not summarized individually.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 641–647. Joinder of needed parties","Louisiana · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 19: a person must be joined when complete relief cannot otherwise be given or the person’s interest would be impaired or others exposed to multiple obligations (art. 641); if joinder is not feasible the court weighs listed factors (art. 642). Nonjoinder is raised by peremptory exception or noticed by the court on its own (arts. 645, 927).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 681–700. Parties plaintiff; procedural capacity","Louisiana · civil rules","Only a person with a real and actual interest may sue (art. 681); lack of interest is the peremptory exception of no right of action (art. 927). Competent majors and emancipated minors have capacity (art. 682); representatives sue for minors, interdicts, successions, and entities (arts. 683–700). A foreign corporation or LLC may sue in its own name, subject to art. 692 (art. 691).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 731–743. Parties defendant","Louisiana · civil rules","Rules on who is sued for minors, successions, partnerships, corporations, insurers in receivership, and trusts; not summarized individually.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 801–807. Substitution of parties","Louisiana · civil rules","When a party dies and the action survives, the legal successor may be substituted on ex parte motion with proof of quality (art. 801), or another party may obtain a summons ordering the successor to appear and substitute (art. 802).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule BOOK II, TITLE I. Ordinary Proceedings: Pleading (arts. 851–1156)","Louisiana · civil rules","Pleadings, petition, exceptions, motions, answer, incidental demands, amendment.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 851–852. Modes of procedure; pleadings allowed","Louisiana · civil rules","Three modes: ordinary, summary, and executory; Book II governs ordinary proceedings in district courts (art. 851). The only pleadings are petitions, exceptions, written motions, and answers; replicatory pleadings are prohibited and new matter in an exception, contradictory motion, or answer is considered denied (art. 852).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 853–865. Form and signing of pleadings","Louisiana · civil rules","Caption, numbered paragraphs, adoption by reference (arts. 853–854). Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity (art. 856). A final judgment grants the relief to which the party is entitled even if not demanded, except in default judgments (art. 862). Art. 863 is Louisiana’s Rule 11: the signer certifies no improper purpose and legal and factual support; the lawyer must state a physical and email address fo","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 891–893. Petition; pleading damages","Louisiana · civil rules","The petition must set out the material facts of the transaction or occurrence and a prayer for relief (art. 891). No specific dollar amount of damages may be pleaded; when an amount matters (jurisdiction, jury right, federal diversity), plead generally that the claim exceeds or is less than the threshold. A specific amount is stricken on motion, with possible fees. Does not apply to suits on conventional obligations,","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 921–934. Exceptions (the Louisiana substitute for Rule 12)","Louisiana · civil rules","Only three exceptions exist: declinatory, dilatory, and peremptory (art. 922). Declinatory: insufficient citation or service (including failure to request service within 90 days under art. 1201(C)), lis pendens, improper venue, lack of personal jurisdiction; waived unless pleaded (art. 925). Dilatory: prematurity, want of amicable demand, unauthorized summary proceeding, nonconformity with art. 891, vagueness, lack o","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 961–964. Motions","Louisiana · civil rules","Applications for orders are by motion, written unless made in open court (art. 961). Ex parte motions are allowed where the mover is clearly entitled without proof; otherwise a contradictory motion or rule to show cause is used (art. 963). The court may strike insufficient demands or defenses or scandalous matter after a hearing (art. 964).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 965. Judgment on the pleadings","Louisiana · civil rules","Any party may move after the answer is filed, but not so as to delay trial; allegations of fact in the mover’s pleadings not denied are taken as admitted.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 966–969. Summary judgment","Louisiana · civil rules","A plaintiff may move after the answer; a defendant any time. Only listed documents may support or oppose the motion (pleadings, memoranda, affidavits, depositions, interrogatory answers, certified medical records, certified public documents and insurance policies, authentic acts, written stipulations, admissions). Unless the court extends and all parties agree: motion filed and served at least 65 days before trial; o","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 970. Offer of judgment","Louisiana · civil rules","Any party may serve a written offer, stating it is under art. 970, at least 20 days before trial; acceptance within 10 days. If the result is at least 25% worse for the offeree than the offer (or a defense verdict on a defendant’s offer), the offeree pays the offeror’s post-offer costs, excluding attorney fees. Unlike FRCP 68, plaintiffs may also make offers.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 971. Special motion to strike (anti-SLAPP)","Louisiana · civil rules","Claims arising from acts in furtherance of petition or free-speech rights in connection with a public issue are struck unless the plaintiff establishes a probability of success. File within 90 days of service of the petition (later in the court’s discretion); hearing within 30 days of service unless docket conditions require later; discovery stayed until notice of the ruling, absent good cause. A prevailing party on ","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1001–1006. Answer","Louisiana · civil rules","Answer due 21 days after service of citation; 30 days if discovery was served with the petition; 15 days after an exception filed before answer is overruled or referred to the merits, or after service of an amended petition ordered on an exception (art. 1001). The defendant may answer any time before a default judgment is signed (art. 1002). Allegations not denied are admitted, except as to damages (art. 1004). Affir","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1031–1041. Incidental demands: general","Louisiana · civil rules","Incidental demands are reconvention, cross-claims, intervention, and third-party demands (art. 1031). They may be filed without leave until the answer to the principal demand is filed, and later with leave if they will not delay the main action (art. 1033). An incidental demand not prescribed when the main demand was filed is timely if filed within 90 days of service of the main demand (or of the third-party demand) ","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1061–1066. Reconvention (counterclaim)","Louisiana · civil rules","A defendant may assert any claim against the plaintiff; claims arising out of the same transaction or occurrence are compulsory and must be asserted, with exceptions for certain divorce and paternity actions and art. 3657 (art. 1061).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1071–1073. Cross-claims","Louisiana · civil rules","A party may assert a cross-claim against a co-party arising out of the same transaction or occurrence (art. 1071). Unlike reconvention, not compulsory under the text.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1091–1094. Intervention","Louisiana · civil rules","A third person with an interest may intervene by joining the plaintiff, uniting with the defendant, or opposing both (art. 1091). The intervener accepts the proceedings as found (art. 1094).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1111–1116. Third-party demands","Louisiana · civil rules","A defendant may bring in any person, including a codefendant, who is a warrantor or may be liable for all or part of the principal demand; the plaintiff may then assert claims against the third-party defendant (art. 1111). Failing to bring in a third party does not forfeit the claim unless the third party proves it had a defense that was lost (art. 1113).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1151–1156. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Louisiana · civil rules","The plaintiff may amend without leave before the answer is served; the defendant may amend the answer once within 10 days after service; otherwise leave or written consent is needed. A response to an amended petition is due within the time left on the original or 10 days after service, whichever is longer (art. 1151). Declinatory and dilatory exceptions may be amended only to amplify objections already pleaded (art. ","#st-la-rules"],["Rule BOOK II, TITLE II. Citation and Service of Process (arts. 1201–1314)","Louisiana · civil rules","","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1201–1203. Citation; 90-day service request","Louisiana · civil rules","Citation and service are essential; without them all proceedings are absolutely null, except in summary and executory proceedings and certain divorce and Children’s Code matters. Service must be requested on all named defendants within 90 days of filing (or of an amended petition adding a defendant), and the clerk’s service fees must be paid within that period or 30 days after a statement of additional fees, whicheve","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1231–1237. Service on individuals","Louisiana · civil rules","Personal or domiciliary service, at any time including Sundays and holidays (art. 1231). Domiciliary service is made by leaving process at the dwelling with a person of suitable age and discretion residing there (art. 1234).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1261–1267. Service on entities","Louisiana · civil rules","Corporations and LLCs are served on a registered agent for service; if there is no agent or the agent cannot be served after due diligence, service may be made on any officer or director (or person so listed with the Secretary of State), on an employee of suitable age and discretion where business is regularly conducted, or under R.S. 13:3204 for out-of-state entities (arts. 1261, 1266); Secretary of State service is","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1291–1293. Who serves","Louisiana · civil rules","The sheriff of the parish where service is made or where the action is pending serves process (art. 1291) and makes a return that is prima facie correct and may be amended unless material prejudice results (art. 1292). If the sheriff has not served within 10 days, or returns that service failed, the court on motion appoints a private process server (art. 1293). Unlike FRCP 4, private service is not available as of ri","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1311–1314. Service of later pleadings","Louisiana · civil rules","Pleadings after the petition are served by mail, delivery, or email to counsel at the address designated for electronic service, with a certificate of service; mail service is complete on mailing (art. 1313(A)–(B)). A pleading or order that sets a court date must be served by registered or certified mail, commercial courier, email to the designated address with electronic delivery confirmation, or by the sheriff (art","#st-la-rules"],["Rule BOOK II, TITLE III. Production of Evidence (arts. 1351–1475)","Louisiana · civil rules","","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1351–1357. Subpoenas","Louisiana · civil rules","The clerk issues subpoenas (art. 1351). Louisiana witnesses may be compelled to attend trial anywhere in the state, subject to R.S. 13:3661 (art. 1352). A person served with a subpoena duces tecum may send written objections within 15 days of service, or before the compliance date if sooner (art. 1354). Noncompliance is contempt (art. 1357).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1420–1428. Discovery: general","Louisiana · civil rules","Signing of discovery requests certifies a reasonable basis (art. 1420). Scope: any non-privileged matter relevant to the subject matter, reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence (art. 1422) – the older, broader federal standard without FRCP 26(b)(1) proportionality. Insurance agreements are discoverable (art. 1423). Work product is protected unless denial would unfairly prejudice (art. 1424). Expert discl","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1429–1456. Depositions","Louisiana · civil rules","Any party may depose any person after suit is filed; the plaintiff needs leave to depose within 15 days after service of citation unless an exception applies (art. 1437). Reasonable written notice is required (art. 1438). Organizational depositions mirror FRCP 30(b)(6) (art. 1442). An expert’s deposition may be used for any purpose on notice, but any counsel may object within 10 days and require live testimony (art. ","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1457–1460. Interrogatories","Louisiana · civil rules","Limit of 35 including subparts; 35 more allowed on ex parte motion; beyond that, only on contradictory motion for good cause (art. 1457). Answers due within 30 days of service (15 days in family law cases unless served with the petition) (art. 1458). Option to produce business records (art. 1460).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1461–1465.1. Production; medical examinations","Louisiana · civil rules","Requests for production and entry on land (art. 1461); written response due within 30 days of service (15 days in family law cases unless served with the petition) (art. 1462). The court may order an additional medical opinion (physical or mental examination) when condition is in controversy (art. 1464).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1466–1468. Requests for admission","Louisiana · civil rules","Requests may be served on the plaintiff after filing and on others with or after service of the petition (art. 1466). Answers must specifically admit, deny, or explain; the requester may test sufficiency, and before a court orders a request deemed admitted (except in a default) a Rule 10.1 conference certificate is required (art. 1467, as amended by Acts 2026, No. 942). The collected text of art. 1467 states no fixed","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1469–1475. Compelling discovery; sanctions","Louisiana · civil rules","Motion to compel and expenses (art. 1469); refusal after an order is contempt (art. 1470); sanctions for disobeying an order include establishing facts, striking pleadings, dismissal, default, and fees (art. 1471); expenses for failing to admit what is later proved (art. 1472); sanctions for failing to attend a deposition or respond (art. 1473). Before moving to compel, District Court Rule 10.1 requires a conference ","#st-la-rules"],["Rule BOOK II, TITLES IV–V. Pretrial, Trial, Dismissal, Default, Jury (arts. 1551–1814)","Louisiana · civil rules","","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1551–1552. Pretrial and scheduling conference","Louisiana · civil rules","The court may, in its discretion, call conferences (in chambers, by phone, or by video) to set deadlines for art. 1425(F), summary judgment, and other motions, set trial and the jury-bond deadline, simplify issues, and rule on exhibits. If a suit has been pending more than one year after service on all defendants with no trial date, on any party’s motion the court must set a conference to schedule discovery and trial","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1561–1563. Consolidation; separate trials","Louisiana · civil rules","Consolidation for trial or limited purposes (art. 1561); separate trial of insurance coverage, liability, or damages issues (art. 1562).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1571–1573. Assignment for trial","Louisiana · civil rules","District courts set trial-assignment procedures by rule (art. 1571). Notice of trial is sent under art. 1313(A)(4) or by certified mail by the clerk at least 10 days before trial (art. 1572). Scheduling preference for a party age 70 or older or with a documented illness making survival beyond six months unlikely (art. 1573).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1601–1605. Continuances","Louisiana · civil rules","Discretionary for good ground (art. 1601); mandatory if, despite due diligence, a party cannot obtain material evidence or a material witness is absent without the party’s contrivance (art. 1602).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1631–1637. Trial procedure","Louisiana · civil rules","Court controls the proceedings and may exclude witnesses (art. 1631); order of trial (art. 1632); a party or person identified with a party may be called and examined as on cross (art. 1634); formal exceptions to rulings are unnecessary (art. 1635); proffer of excluded evidence (art. 1636); art. 1633.1 addresses live trial testimony by video.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1671–1673. Dismissal","Louisiana · civil rules","Voluntary dismissal without prejudice on the plaintiff’s application and payment of costs if made before any appearance by the defendant; afterward, the court may refuse to dismiss except with prejudice (art. 1671). Involuntary dismissal when the plaintiff fails to appear for trial; if all parties fail to appear and a settlement is pending, either may reinstate within 60 days of notice of dismissal (art. 1672(A)). In","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1702–1704. Default judgment","Louisiana · civil rules","There is no preliminary default: if the defendant fails to answer timely, the plaintiff may obtain a default judgment by proving a prima facie case with competent, admissible evidence. Notice of intent to take a default is required at least 7 days before judgment when the defendant has appeared of record (certified mail or delivery to counsel), when the defendant’s lawyer has written to the plaintiff about the case, ","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1731–1736. Jury trial","Louisiana · civil rules","No jury where no individual plaintiff’s claim exceeds $10,000 exclusive of interest and costs, with rules on stipulations made more or less than 60 days before trial (art. 1732(1)); art. 1732 lists other non-jury cases. Demand a jury by pleading within 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to a jury issue, and post the bond set by the court (art. 1733(A)(1), (C)). In tort cases where the plaintiff stipu","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1751–1769. Jury selection","Louisiana · civil rules","The court examines prospective jurors and the parties or lawyers may then examine them individually (art. 1763). Peremptory challenges: 3 per side for a jury of six (court may add up to 2 with multiple parties on a side), 6 per side for a jury of twelve (up to 4 more); each side gets an equal number (art. 1764). Challenges for cause (art. 1765); method and timing (art. 1766).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1791–1797. Jury instructions and verdict","Louisiana · civil rules","The judge may not comment on the facts (art. 1791). Written requests for instructions at the close of evidence or earlier as directed; the court informs the parties of its proposed instructions, and an instruction may be assigned as error only if the party objected before or immediately after the jury retires, stating the matter and grounds (art. 1793). Non-unanimous verdicts: 5 of 6 or 9 of 12 jurors must concur unl","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1810–1814. Directed verdict, JNOV, verdict forms, remittitur","Louisiana · civil rules","A directed-verdict motion does not waive the right to present evidence (art. 1810). A motion for JNOV is due within 7 days, exclusive of legal holidays, after the clerk mails or delivers in open court, or the sheriff serves, the notice of judgment; a new-trial motion after a JNOV ruling is due within 7 days (exclusive of holidays) after notice of the JNOV judgment (art. 1811). Special verdicts and general verdicts wi","#st-la-rules"],["Rule BOOK II, TITLE VI. Judgments (arts. 1841–2031)","Louisiana · civil rules","","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1841. Final and interlocutory judgments","Louisiana · civil rules","As amended in 2026: a judgment that determines the merits in whole is final; one that determines the merits in part is either a partial final judgment under art. 1915(A) or an interlocutory judgment under art. 1915(C); one that decides only preliminary matters is interlocutory.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1871–1883. Declaratory judgments","Louisiana · civil rules","Courts may declare rights, status, and legal relations whether or not further relief is claimed (art. 1871); the court may refuse a declaration in the circumstances stated in art. 1876 (title only collected). Art. 1878 addresses supplemental relief and an expedited hearing for constitutional challenges (per its title; text not collected).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1911–1913. Signing and notice of final judgment","Louisiana · civil rules","No appeal lies from a final judgment until it is signed by the judge (art. 1911(B)). The clerk must mail (or deliver in open court) notice of signing of a final judgment in contested cases to counsel and unrepresented parties and certify the date; a default judgment against a defendant not personally served who never appeared must be served by the sheriff (art. 1913). Appeal and new-trial delays run from this notice.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1914. Interlocutory judgments; notice","Louisiana · civil rules","Rendition of an interlocutory judgment in open court is notice, unless the court orders it written, a party asks within 10 days that it be reduced to writing, the court took it under advisement, or it is a partial judgment under art. 1915(C), in which case the clerk sends notice. Each party then has 10 days to act in the trial court; this does not change writ or appeal deadlines. Notice of denial of a new trial or JN","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1915. Partial final judgments","Louisiana · civil rules","A partial judgment is final (and appealable) only in the listed situations: dismissal of fewer than all parties; judgment on the pleadings; summary judgment (other than one on a particular issue under art. 966(E)); judgment on a separately tried principal or incidental demand; a separately tried liability judgment; and certain sanctions. As amended by Acts 2025, No. 250, a judgment, summary judgment, or sustained exc","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1916–1918. Preparing and form of judgment","Louisiana · civil rules","After a jury verdict, the court signs a judgment within 10 days or orders counsel to submit one (art. 1916). In appealable non-jury contested cases, written findings and reasons must be given if requested within 10 days after mailing of notice of judgment (art. 1917(A)). A final judgment must be identified as final, signed and dated, and name who wins, who loses, and the relief; reasons go in a separate opinion; a ju","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1920–1922. Costs; interest","Louisiana · civil rules","Costs are paid by the party cast unless the judgment says otherwise, and the court may assess them as it considers equitable (art. 1920). Interest is awarded as prayed for or as provided by law (art. 1921).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1951. Amendment of judgment","Louisiana · civil rules","A final judgment may be amended at any time to alter phraseology, fix deficient decretal language, or correct calculation errors, but not to change substance; amendment requires a hearing with notice unless all parties consent or the submitter certifies the amendment was circulated at least 5 days earlier without opposition.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 1971–1979. New trial","Louisiana · civil rules","Peremptory grounds: verdict or judgment clearly contrary to the law and evidence; newly discovered evidence that could not have been found with due diligence; jury bribed or misbehaved (art. 1972). Discretionary grounds: any good ground (art. 1973). Motion due within 7 days, exclusive of legal holidays, after the clerk mails or delivers notice of judgment or the sheriff serves it (art. 1974). Facts for grounds (2) an","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2001–2006. Action of nullity","Louisiana · civil rules","A judgment rendered against an unrepresented incompetent, against a defendant not served and who did not waive, or by a court lacking subject-matter jurisdiction is annulled; the action may be brought any time, unless the defendant acquiesced (arts. 2002–2003). A judgment obtained by fraud or ill practices may be annulled by an action filed within one year of discovery, with possible fees (art. 2004). Nullity actions","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2031. Revival of judgments","Louisiana · civil rules","A money judgment may be revived before it prescribes by ex parte motion in the same suit; filing the motion interrupts prescription. (The 10-year prescription of money judgments is in Civil Code art. 3501, not collected.)","#st-la-rules"],["Rule BOOK III. Proceedings in Appellate Courts (arts. 2081–2201)","Louisiana · civil rules","","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2081–2086. Appealable judgments; who may appeal","Louisiana · civil rules","Final judgments are appealable (art. 2083(A)). A partial final judgment under art. 1915(A) is appealable; other partial judgments only when a law expressly says so; an interlocutory judgment only when a law expressly provides (art. 2083(B)–(C), as amended 2026). A party who confessed judgment or voluntarily and unconditionally acquiesced in it cannot appeal (art. 2085). A person who could have intervened may appeal (","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2087. Devolutive appeal: 60 days","Louisiana · civil rules","A devolutive appeal (one that does not suspend execution) is taken within 60 days of (1) expiry of the delay for a new-trial or JNOV motion if none was timely filed, or (2) mailing of notice of denial of a timely new-trial or JNOV motion. An appellee seeking to change the judgment may take its own devolutive appeal within those 60 days or 10 days after the clerk mails notice of the first appeal, whichever is later. W","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2088. Trial court loses jurisdiction","Louisiana · civil rules","Jurisdiction over matters reviewable on appeal passes to the appellate court when the order of appeal is granted (devolutive) or when the order is granted and the bond timely filed (suspensive). The trial court keeps listed powers, such as extending the return day, correcting the record, testing the bond, granting appeals to others, executing an unsuspended judgment, taxing costs and fees, and amending decretal langu","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2121–2122. How to appeal: order of appeal","Louisiana · civil rules","An appeal is taken by obtaining an order of appeal from the trial court that rendered the judgment, on oral motion in open court, written motion, or petition, within the delay. The order sets the return day and any security. The clerk mails notice of appeal to other parties and the appellate court; the clerk’s failure does not void the appeal (art. 2121). Unlike FRAP 3, there is no simple notice of appeal filed as of","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2123–2124. Suspensive appeal: 30 days plus security","Louisiana · civil rules","A suspensive appeal (one that stops execution) must be taken, and the security furnished, within 30 days of the same triggers as art. 2087 (art. 2123). No security is required for a devolutive appeal. For a money judgment, suspensive-appeal security equals the judgment plus interest to the date furnished, excluding costs; for judgments over $150 million (outside listed exceptions) the court may set a lower amount; fo","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2125–2128.1. Return day, costs, record","Louisiana · civil rules","The return day is 30 days after estimated costs are paid (45 days if testimony must be transcribed), unless the judge sets a shorter time; the trial court may grant one extension of up to 30 days, and further extensions come from the appellate court (art. 2125). The appellant must pay the clerk’s estimated record costs within 20 days after notice (one 20-day extension for good cause); if not paid, the trial judge aft","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2129–2132. Assignments of error; narrative of facts","Louisiana · civil rules","No assignment of errors is needed in the trial court, but if the appellant designates a partial record it must serve a concise statement of points relied on and is limited to them (art. 2129). If testimony was not transcribed, the parties prepare a narrative of facts or the judge makes one (arts. 2130–2131). The trial court may correct the record (art. 2132).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2133. Answer to appeal (cross-appeal substitute)","Louisiana · civil rules","An appellee who wants the judgment changed, or wants damages for frivolous appeal, must file an answer to the appeal stating the relief within 15 days after the return day or the lodging of the record, whichever is later; it operates as an appeal. Other parties to an incidental demand then have 15 days to file similar answers. An appellee not seeking change may defend the judgment on any ground supported by the recor","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2161–2168. Procedure in the appellate court","Louisiana · civil rules","An appeal is not dismissed for record defects; motions to dismiss for irregularities imputable to the appellant must be filed within 3 days (exclusive of holidays) after the return day or lodging, whichever is later (art. 2161). An appeal may be dismissed any time by consent, for lack of jurisdiction or of a right to appeal, or as abandoned; a misdirected appeal may be transferred (art. 2162). A peremptory exception ","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2201. Supervisory writs","Louisiana · civil rules","Supervisory writs are applied for and granted under the constitution and the rules of the Supreme Court and the courts of appeal (see Uniform Rules of the Courts of Appeal, Rule 4: notice of intent and a return date not exceeding 30 days from notice of the ruling under art. 1914).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule BOOK IV. Execution of Judgments (arts. 2251–2789)","Louisiana · civil rules","Selected articles.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2251–2254. Execution generally","Louisiana · civil rules","Execution is only in the trial court (art. 2251). A creditor may execute only after the suspensive-appeal delay has run; recording the judgment earlier is not execution (art. 2252). The clerk issues a writ to the sheriff on the creditor’s request (art. 2253).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2291–2299. Fieri facias","Louisiana · civil rules","A money judgment is executed by writ of fieri facias directing seizure and sale of the debtor’s property (art. 2291).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2411–2417. Garnishment","Louisiana · civil rules","After a writ of fieri facias issues, the creditor may cite a garnishee to declare under oath what property of the debtor it holds (art. 2411). The garnishee must file sworn answers to interrogatories within 30 days of service, and failure may make the garnishee personally liable for the whole debt; the creditor must notify the debtor of the garnishment petition (art. 2412).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2451–2456. Judgment-debtor examination","Louisiana · civil rules","In aid of execution, the creditor may examine the debtor and the debtor’s books and documents about the debtor’s property (art. 2451).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2541–2542. Foreign judgments","Louisiana · civil rules","A judgment of a federal court, another state, or a foreign country may be recognized in an ordinary proceeding against the debtor with an authenticated copy attached; judgments entitled to full faith and credit may also be enforced under the Enforcement of Foreign Judgments Act, R.S. 13:4241 (art. 2541). Art. 2542 lists when a foreign-country defamation judgment is not conclusive (no impartial tribunals or due proces","#st-la-rules"],["Rule BOOK V. Summary and Executory Proceedings (arts. 2591–2814)","Louisiana · civil rules","","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2591–2596. Summary proceedings","Louisiana · civil rules","Conducted with rapidity, without citation and the formalities of ordinary proceedings (art. 2591). Allowed only for listed matters, including incidental questions and attorney-fee awards, new-trial motions, issues raised by exception or rule, habeas, mandamus, quo warranto, custody, support, and injunctive relief (art. 2592). Begun by contradictory motion or rule to show cause; exceptions must be filed before the tri","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2631–2724. Executory proceedings","Louisiana · civil rules","Seizure and sale of mortgaged property without prior citation or judgment, to enforce a mortgage or privilege in an authentic act importing a confession of judgment (arts. 2631–2632). A suspensive appeal from the order of seizure and sale must be taken within 15 days of service of the notice of seizure (art. 2642). The debtor may enjoin the sale on listed grounds such as extinguishment of the debt (art. 2751).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 2781–2783. Making Louisiana judgments executory elsewhere","Louisiana · civil rules","A judgment of one Louisiana court may be made executory in another Louisiana court of competent jurisdiction (art. 2781).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule BOOKS VI–VII. Probate and Other Special Proceedings (arts. 2811–4736)","Louisiana · civil rules","Successions, tutorship, interdiction, partition, concursus, and eviction were not summarized except as noted.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 3601–3613. Injunctions","Louisiana · civil rules","Injunctions issue where irreparable injury may otherwise result or as provided by law (art. 3601). A preliminary injunction is heard 2 to 10 days after service of notice (art. 3602). A TRO without notice requires specific facts in a verified petition, affidavit, or affirmation showing immediate and irreparable injury before the other side can be heard, plus the attorney’s written certification of efforts to give noti","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 3751–3753. Notice of pendency (lis pendens recordation)","Louisiana · civil rules","A suit affecting title to, or asserting a mortgage or privilege on, immovable property is not notice to third persons unless a notice of pendency is made and filed or recorded as art. 3752 requires (art. 3751).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 3781–3866. Extraordinary remedies","Louisiana · civil rules","Habeas corpus, mandamus, and quo warranto are ordered only on petition and may be tried summarily (art. 3781). Mandamus directs a public officer, a corporation or its officer, or an LLC or its member or manager to perform duties listed in arts. 3863–3864, where the law gives no ordinary relief or delay would cause injustice, but not to compel state spending certified as causing a deficit (arts. 3861–3862).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 4701–4736. Eviction","Louisiana · civil rules","Notice to vacate gives the lessee at least 5 days from delivery (art. 4701). After a rule to show cause, a judgment of eviction issues (art. 4732). An appeal does not suspend eviction unless the defendant answered under oath with an affirmative defense and applied for appeal and filed bond within 24 hours of the judgment (art. 4735).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule BOOK VIII. Trial Courts of Limited Jurisdiction (arts. 4831–5003)","Louisiana · civil rules","","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 4831–4852. Jurisdiction and venue","Louisiana · civil rules","Parish courts: concurrent with district courts where the amount in dispute does not exceed $20,000 (art. 4842). City courts: generally $15,000, with higher limits for listed city courts in art. 4843(D) and beyond, and unlimited jurisdiction over certain ordinance-enforcement suits by public bodies in larger cities (art. 4843).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 4871–4875. No jury; transfer to district court","Louisiana · civil rules","No jury trial in parish, city, or justice of the peace courts (art. 4871). A defendant entitled to a jury may transfer the case to district court under art. 4873 (art. 4872).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 4901–4925. Procedure in limited-jurisdiction courts","Louisiana · civil rules","Special pleading, answer, default, and new-trial delays apply (arts. 4901–4908, 4911–4923; headings only, texts not collected). Appeals from justice of the peace courts go to the parish court or, if none, the district court, by suit for trial de novo filed within 15 days of judgment or notice (arts. 4924–4925).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 5001–5003. Appeals from city and parish courts","Louisiana · civil rules","Appeal is to the court of appeal on the record (art. 5001), and must be taken within 10 days of the judgment or of service of notice of judgment when notice is required (art. 5002). Much shorter than the district-court delays.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule BOOK IX. Miscellaneous Provisions and Definitions (arts. 5051–5251)","Louisiana · civil rules","","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 5051–5058. Construction","Louisiana · civil rules","The Code is construed liberally, with regard for the fact that procedure implements substantive law and is not an end in itself (art. 5051). “Shall” is mandatory and “may” permissive (art. 5053).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 5059. Computing time","Louisiana · civil rules","Exclude the day of the triggering event; include the last day unless it is a legal holiday, then run to the next non-holiday. Legal holidays (including half-holidays) count, except when expressly excluded, when the holiday is the last day, or when the period is less than 7 days. Note that several key delays (new trial, JNOV) say “exclusive of legal holidays” expressly.","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 5091–5098. Curator ad hoc for absent defendants","Louisiana · civil rules","On the plaintiff’s petition or ex parte motion, the court appoints an attorney (curator ad hoc) to represent a defendant over whom it has jurisdiction who is a nonresident or absentee not served and not waiving, an unrepresented minor or incompetent, or deceased without a succession representative, and in listed in rem cases (art. 5091). The appointee’s duties are in arts. 5094–5095 (not collected).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 5121–5127. Judicial bonds","Louisiana · civil rules","Rules on bonds payable to the clerk, sureties, and testing sufficiency (headings only; texts not collected).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 5181–5188. Proceeding without prepayment of costs (in forma pauperis)","Louisiana · civil rules","An individual unable to pay court costs because of poverty may sue or defend in any trial or appellate court without paying costs in advance or furnishing security; incarcerated felons must advance costs on a statutory schedule (art. 5181). Affidavits and traverse are in arts. 5183–5184 (not collected).","#st-la-rules"],["Rule 5251. Definitions","Louisiana · civil rules","Defines terms used throughout the Code (not collected).","#st-la-rules"],["Appeals in Louisiana: get an order of appeal, choose devolutive or suspensive, and know when you need a writ instead","Louisiana · appeals","A Louisiana appeal is not started by filing a notice of appeal. You move the trial court for an order of appeal, which sets the return day; the clerk then mails the notice of appeal. The delay depends on the kind of appeal: a devolutive appeal (no stay of execution, no bond) has 60 days, while a suspensive appeal (stays execution) has only 30 days and the security must be posted within those 30 days. Both clocks star","#st-la-appeals"],["Final judgments and listed partial final judgments","Louisiana · appeals · What can be appealed","A final judgment is appealable whether after trial, by default, or reformed by remittitur or additur. A partial final judgment under art. 1915(A) is appealable: dismissal of fewer than all parties; judgment on the pleadings; summary judgment (other than one deciding only a particular issue under art. 966(E)); judgment on a separately tried principal or incidental demand; a separately tried liability judgment; and cer","#st-la-appeals"],["Partial rulings on fewer than all claims are interlocutory","Louisiana · appeals · What can be appealed","When a court grants a judgment or summary judgment, or sustains an exception, as to one or more but fewer than all of the claims, demands, issues, or theories by or against a party, that judgment is interlocutory. The current text of art. 1915 (as amended by Acts 2025, No. 250) contains no mechanism to designate such a ruling final for immediate appeal. Seek a supervisory writ instead. Lawyers used to the old “no jus","#st-la-appeals"],["Interlocutory judgments only when a law allows","Louisiana · appeals · What can be appealed","An interlocutory judgment is appealable only when expressly provided by law. Examples in the collected text: orders relating to a preliminary or final injunction (appeal within 15 days; no appeal from TRO orders), and the order of seizure and sale in executory proceedings (suspensive appeal within 15 days of service of the notice of seizure). A judgment on class certification is appealable as of right, suspensively o","#st-la-appeals"],["Signed judgment with decretal language required","Louisiana · appeals · What can be appealed","No appeal lies until the final judgment is signed. A final judgment must be identified as final, signed and dated, and must name the party who wins, the party who loses, and the relief. If an appealed judgment lacks proper decretal language, the appellate court remands for the trial court to amend it under art. 1951. Reasons for judgment go in a separate opinion; non-jury reasons must be given if requested within 10 ","#st-la-appeals"],["Order of appeal, not a notice","Louisiana · appeals · Starting the appeal","Obtain an order of appeal from the trial court within the delay, on oral motion in open court, written motion, or petition. The order shows the return day and the security when the court must set it. The clerk mails notice of the appeal to the other parties and the appellate court; the clerk’s failure does not invalidate the appeal. Within 7 days of the order of appeal, the clerk of the trial court transmits the noti","#st-la-appeals"],["Devolutive: 60 days; suspensive: 30 days and bond","Louisiana · appeals · Starting the appeal","Devolutive appeal: within 60 days of (1) expiry of the new-trial/JNOV delay if no motion was timely filed, or (2) mailing of notice of denial of a timely motion. Suspensive appeal: within 30 days of the same events, and the security must be furnished within those 30 days. If the suspensive bond is not timely filed, the trial court may convert the appeal to devolutive (not in eviction cases). La. C.C.P. arts. 2087(A),","#st-la-appeals"],["Tolling: new trial and JNOV","Louisiana · appeals · Starting the appeal","A motion for new trial or for JNOV is due within 7 days, exclusive of legal holidays, after the clerk mails or delivers in open court, or the sheriff serves, notice of judgment. If any party files one, the appeal delays for all parties start when they start for the party whose motion is decided last. Notice of denial must be mailed, and the appeal delay runs from that mailing. An order of appeal granted while such a ","#st-la-appeals"],["Cross-appeals: answer the appeal or take your own","Louisiana · appeals · Starting the appeal","An appellee who wants the judgment modified, revised, or reversed, or who wants damages for a frivolous appeal, must file an answer to the appeal stating the relief within 15 days after the return day or the lodging of the record, whichever is later. Alternatively, an appellee may take its own devolutive appeal within the normal 60 days or within 10 days after the clerk mails notice of the first devolutive appeal, wh","#st-la-appeals"],["Removal to federal court interrupts the delays","Louisiana · appeals · Starting the appeal","Filing a notice of removal under 28 U.S.C. § 1446 interrupts the devolutive and suspensive appeal delays for all parties; they start anew on remand. La. C.C.P. arts. 2087(E), 2123(D)","#st-la-appeals"],["Suspensive appeal security","Louisiana · appeals · Security, costs, and the record","No security for a devolutive appeal. For a money judgment, suspensive-appeal security equals the judgment plus interest to the date furnished, excluding costs; for good cause the court may require up to 150% with a surety bond; for judgments over $150 million (outside listed exceptions) the court may set a lower amount after a hearing. A party aggrieved by the amount may seek a priority supervisory writ, which interr","#st-la-appeals"],["Pay estimated costs within 20 days","Louisiana · appeals · Security, costs, and the record","After the order of appeal, the clerk estimates the cost of the record (including transcript and appellate filing fee) and sends notice. The appellant must pay within 20 days after transmission of the notice (or of a revised notice after designation). The trial court may grant one 20-day extension for good cause. If the appellant does not pay in time, the trial judge, after a hearing, must either dismiss the appeal as","#st-la-appeals"],["Return day and lodging","Louisiana · appeals · Security, costs, and the record","Return day: 30 days after estimated costs are paid, or 45 days if testimony must be transcribed, unless the judge sets less. The trial court may grant one extension of up to 30 days; later extensions come from the court of appeal. The trial clerk lodges the certified record on or before the return day, and the court of appeal clerk notifies counsel of the lodging date. La. C.C.P. arts. 2125, 2127; Unif. R. Ct. App. 2","#st-la-appeals"],["Designating a partial record","Louisiana · appeals · Security, costs, and the record","The appellant may designate portions of the record within 10 days after the cost notice; others may counter-designate within 14 days after service. If you designate a partial record, you must serve a concise statement of the points you rely on, and the appeal is limited to them. Otherwise no assignment of errors is required in the trial court. La. C.C.P. arts. 2128, 2129","#st-la-appeals"],["Brief deadlines run from lodging","Louisiana · appeals · Briefing in the court of appeal","Appellant’s brief: 25 calendar days after the record is lodged. Appellee’s brief: 45 calendar days after lodging. Reply: 10 calendar days after the appellee’s brief. When several parties obtain orders of appeal, the earlier order’s party briefs on the appellant schedule and the later on the appellee schedule unless the court directs otherwise. Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-12.7","#st-la-appeals"],["Extensions and abandonment","Louisiana · appeals · Briefing in the court of appeal","Extensions require a written motion filed on or before the due date, for good cause; an appellant’s extension automatically extends the appellee for 20 days from the extended date; no extension that would delay the hearing. A filed motion to suspend briefing does not itself suspend anything. If the appellant misses the brief deadline, the clerk sends a notice; if no brief is filed within 30 days after that notice, th","#st-la-appeals"],["Page limits and format","Louisiana · appeals · Briefing in the court of appeal","Original appellant and appellee briefs: 31 pages on legal-size paper or 41 pages on letter-size; reply briefs 13 (legal) or 18 (letter). Excluded: cover, tables, the judgment and reasons, and certificate of service. Times New Roman 14-point or larger, double-spaced, 1-inch margins. Leave to exceed must be sought before the due date. The appellant’s brief must include a jurisdictional statement and attach the judgment","#st-la-appeals"],["Filing and service","Louisiana · appeals · Briefing in the court of appeal","File with the clerk by delivery, mail, or (if local rule permits) fax or e-filing. Mailing on or before the due date is timely, shown by a USPS postmark or receipt or a bona fide commercial courier record (not a private meter stamp). E-filed or faxed documents are timely if received by the due date. Serve every filing on opposing counsel and unrepresented parties under C.C.P. art. 1313, with a certificate listing all","#st-la-appeals"],["Oral argument","Louisiana · appeals · Briefing in the court of appeal","Up to 40 minutes total, divided equally between sides, unless the court allows more; the appellant opens and closes. Do not read argument from a prepared text. Amicus curiae may not request argument. Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-15, 2-12.11","#st-la-appeals"],["Motions to dismiss for appellant’s irregularities: 3 days","Louisiana · appeals · Briefing in the court of appeal","A motion to dismiss an appeal for an irregularity, error, or defect imputable to the appellant must be filed within 3 days, exclusive of holidays, after the return day or lodging, whichever is later. Dismissal for lack of jurisdiction, no right to appeal, consent, or abandonment may be sought any time. Defects in the record are not grounds for dismissal. La. C.C.P. arts. 2161, 2162","#st-la-appeals"],["Notice of intent and a return date of 30 days or less","Louisiana · appeals · Supervisory writs (interlocutory review)","Give notice of intent to apply for writs to the opposing parties and, at the same time, ask the trial judge to set a return date. In civil cases the return date may not exceed 30 days from the date of notice of the judgment under art. 1914. The judge must set an explicit return date. Extensions require a motion filed within the original or extended period. A late application is not considered unless the delay was not","#st-la-appeals"],["Contents and stays","Louisiana · appeals · Supervisory writs (interlocutory review)","The application must be verified by affidavit certifying service on the respondent judge and all parties, with addresses, phone numbers, and emails. A writ application does not stay trial-court proceedings unless the trial court or court of appeal orders it; ask the trial court first. Requests for expedited consideration need a bold cover statement, a separate “REQUEST FOR EXPEDITED CONSIDERATION” page, and an affida","#st-la-appeals"],["Expedited cases involving minors","Louisiana · appeals · Supervisory writs (interlocutory review)","Child in need of care, delinquency, termination, adoption, custody modifications, and similar cases get preferential handling and initials are used for minors. Unif. R. Ct. App. 5-1, 5-2","#st-la-appeals"],["Court of appeal rehearing: 14 days, no extensions","Louisiana · appeals · Rehearing","In civil cases, apply within 14 days after transmission of notice of the judgment; no extension is allowed. Maximum 10 pages without permission; the supporting brief is filed with the application (more time may be requested in the same filing). Rehearing is considered only when the court granted a writ on the merits, dismissed an appeal, or ruled on the merits of an appeal; no oral argument. La. C.C.P. art. 2166(A); ","#st-la-appeals"],["Supreme Court rehearing: 14 days","Louisiana · appeals · Rehearing","Apply within 14 days after transmission of notice of judgment. Available only when the Court granted a writ, an appeal, or another proceeding; reconsideration of a writ denial is not considered absent extraordinary circumstances. Application and any opposition: 25 pages each; appendix up to 25 pages in civil cases. No rehearing in election cases. La. C.C.P. art. 2167(A); La. Sup. Ct. R. IX","#st-la-appeals"],["Certiorari: 30 days, discretionary","Louisiana · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court of Louisiana","Apply for a writ of certiorari within 30 days after transmission of the court of appeal’s notice of judgment, or within 30 days after transmission of notice denying a timely rehearing application. If one party timely applies, another may apply within the original 30 days or within 10 days after the Supreme Court clerk transmits notice of the first application, whichever is later. “Transmission” means sending by U.S. ","#st-la-appeals"],["Civil writ application requirements","Louisiana · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court of Louisiana","Include the filing sheet, an index, a statement of which Rule X consideration applies, a memorandum of no more than 25 pages (statement of the case, assignments of error, summary of argument, argument), a verification, and the dated trial-court ruling with reasons and the court of appeal’s order and opinion (including rehearing rulings). Appendix of other documents: 25 pages, separately bound, no court of appeal brie","#st-la-appeals"],["Oppositions and replies","Louisiana · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court of Louisiana","Opposition: within 30 days of the Court’s acknowledgment letter, no extension except on clear and convincing proof of circumstances beyond the party’s control (calendaring errors do not count); 25 pages. Reply: within 10 days of the opposition, 7 pages including exhibits, discouraged. If the writ is granted, the record must be lodged within 14 days and the case is briefed under Rule IV. La. Sup. Ct. R. X, §§ 5–7","#st-la-appeals"],["Finality","Louisiana · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court of Louisiana","A court of appeal judgment becomes final if no timely rehearing or certiorari application is filed; if certiorari is timely sought and denied, it becomes final 5 days (exclusive of legal holidays) after the clerk mails the denial. The Supreme Court may stay execution pending certiorari or U.S. Supreme Court review. La. C.C.P. art. 2166(A), (E)","#st-la-appeals"],["Supreme Court: CDX","Louisiana · appeals · E-filing and filing mechanics","Louisiana-admitted lawyers may e-file in the Supreme Court through the Court Data/Document Exchange after mandatory training; filings must be text-searchable PDF/A and obey all page limits. Timely if completed before midnight Central Time on the due date (processing after 4:30 p.m. occurs the next business day). Rejected filings may be re-filed within 7 calendar days and keep the original time. Call the clerk before ","#st-la-appeals"],["Courts of appeal and trial courts","Louisiana · appeals · E-filing and filing mechanics","Courts of appeal accept e-filing and fax only if their local rules permit (local rules not collected). In district courts, since January 1, 2026 attorneys may file only in person on paper or electronically through the clerk’s system or the Louisiana Clerks’ Remote Access Authority, and fax filing under R.S. 13:850 ended January 1, 2026; this matters for filing a motion for appeal or new trial at the last minute. Unif","#st-la-appeals"],["City and parish court appeals: 10 days","Louisiana · appeals · Other courts","Appeals from city and parish courts go to the court of appeal on the record but must be taken within 10 days of the judgment or of service of notice of judgment when notice is required. Appeals from justice of the peace courts are by suit for trial de novo in the parish or district court within 15 days. La. C.C.P. arts. 5001, 5002, 4924, 4925","#st-la-appeals"],["Eviction appeals: 24 hours to suspend","Louisiana · appeals · Other courts","An appeal does not suspend an eviction judgment unless the defendant answered under oath with an affirmative defense and applied for appeal and filed the bond within 24 hours after the judgment. La. C.C.P. art. 4735","#st-la-appeals"],["Frivolous appeals and unpublished opinions","Louisiana · appeals · Other courts","The appellate court may award damages, including attorney fees, for a frivolous appeal or writ application and may tax costs as it finds equitable. Unpublished Supreme Court and court of appeal opinions are posted online and may be cited by case name and the court’s number. La. C.C.P. arts. 2164, 2168; Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-19","#st-la-appeals"],["Motion for new trial","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from clerk’s mailing or delivery in open court, or sheriff’s service, of notice of judgment (art. 1913).\nExclusive of legal holidays. Tolls appeal delays for all parties. La. C.C.P. art. 1974","#st-la-appeals"],["Motion for JNOV","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from notice of judgment (or jury discharge if no verdict).\nExclusive of legal holidays. La. C.C.P. art. 1811(A)","#st-la-appeals"],["Devolutive appeal (order of appeal)","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from expiry of new-trial/JNOV delay, or mailing of notice of denial of a timely motion.\nNo bond. Does not stay execution. La. C.C.P. art. 2087(A)","#st-la-appeals"],["Suspensive appeal (order of appeal and security)","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from expiry of new-trial/JNOV delay, or mailing of notice of denial of a timely motion.\nSecurity must also be furnished within the 30 days; late bond allows conversion to devolutive. La. C.C.P. art. 2123(A)","#st-la-appeals"],["Appellee’s own devolutive appeal","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from clerk’s mailing of notice of the first devolutive appeal.\nOr within the normal 60 days, whichever is later. La. C.C.P. art. 2087(B)","#st-la-appeals"],["Answer to appeal (cross-relief)","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from return day or lodging of the record, whichever is later.\nRequired to obtain any change in the judgment. La. C.C.P. art. 2133(A)","#st-la-appeals"],["Pay estimated appeal costs","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from transmission of the clerk’s notice of estimated costs.\nOne extension of up to 20 days for good cause. La. C.C.P. art. 2126","#st-la-appeals"],["Designate partial record","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from transmission of notice of estimated costs.\nOther parties may counter-designate within 14 days after service. La. C.C.P. art. 2128","#st-la-appeals"],["Return day","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from payment of estimated costs.\n45 days if testimony must be transcribed; one trial-court extension of up to 30 days. La. C.C.P. art. 2125","#st-la-appeals"],["Motion to dismiss appeal for appellant’s irregularity","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","3 days from return day or lodging, whichever is later.\nExclusive of holidays. La. C.C.P. art. 2161","#st-la-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","25 days from lodging of the record.\nCalendar days. Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-12.7","#st-la-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","45 days from lodging of the record.\nExtended 20 days beyond any extension granted to the appellant. Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-12.7","#st-la-appeals"],["Reply brief","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of appellee’s brief.\nConfined to rebuttal. Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-12.7","#st-la-appeals"],["Brief after abandonment notice","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from clerk’s transmission of the abandonment notice.\nOtherwise the appeal is subject to dismissal as abandoned. Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-8.6","#st-la-appeals"],["Supervisory writ application (civil)","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from notice of the interlocutory judgment under art. 1914.\nMaximum; the actual deadline is the return date the judge sets, which may be shorter. Unif. R. Ct. App. 4-3","#st-la-appeals"],["Preliminary-injunction appeal","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from date of the order or judgment.\nBond, if required, also within 15 days. No appeal from TRO orders. La. C.C.P. art. 3612(C)","#st-la-appeals"],["Court of appeal rehearing","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from transmission of notice of judgment.\nNo extensions. La. C.C.P. art. 2166(A); Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-18.2","#st-la-appeals"],["Supreme Court certiorari","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from transmission of the court of appeal’s notice of judgment, or of notice of denial of rehearing.\nSecond applicant: later of 30 days or 10 days after notice of the first application. La. C.C.P. art. 2166(A)–(C)","#st-la-appeals"],["Opposition to Supreme Court writ application","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from date of the Court’s acknowledgment letter.\nNo extensions absent clear and convincing proof of circumstances beyond the party’s control. La. Sup. Ct. R. X, § 5","#st-la-appeals"],["Supreme Court rehearing","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from transmission of notice of judgment.\nOnly after a grant of writ, appeal, or other proceeding. La. C.C.P. art. 2167(A)","#st-la-appeals"],["City or parish court appeal","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from judgment, or service of notice of judgment when required.\nTo the court of appeal. La. C.C.P. art. 5002","#st-la-appeals"],["Suspensive appeal from eviction","Louisiana · appeals · Deadlines","1 days from rendition of judgment of eviction.\n24 hours; also requires a sworn answer pleading an affirmative defense. La. C.C.P. art. 4735","#st-la-appeals"],["Supreme Court of Maryland","Maryland · courts","Seven justices, one from each of seven Appellate Judicial Circuits; the Governor designates one as Chief Justice. Five justices sit in each case unless the Court directs more. A 3–3 or 3–2 division entitles the losing party, on application, to reargument before all seven as of right. Reviews cases mostly by certiorari, answers certified questions of law, and adopts the Maryland Rules. Formerly the Court of Appeals; r","#st-md"],["Appellate Court of Maryland","Maryland · courts","Has exclusive initial appellate jurisdiction over reviewable judgments, orders and other actions of the circuit courts and orphans’ courts, except where the Supreme Court has jurisdiction. Formerly the Court of Special Appeals.","#st-md"],["Circuit Courts (one per county and Baltimore City)","Maryland · courts","General-jurisdiction trial courts. They hear jury trials, equity and declaratory-judgment cases, and appeals from the District Court and agencies. Civil procedure is governed by Title 2 of the Maryland Rules.","#st-md"],["District Court of Maryland","Maryland · courts","Statewide court of limited jurisdiction. It has exclusive civil jurisdiction over contract and tort claims up to $30,000, landlord-tenant and replevin cases regardless of amount, and small claims up to $5,000. It has no general equity jurisdiction and cannot issue declaratory judgments. Above $5,000 the plaintiff may choose the District Court or the circuit court. Civil procedure is governed by Title 3 of the Marylan","#st-md"],["Statute: Courts & Judicial Proceedings § 5-807","Maryland · anti-SLAPP","A suit is a SLAPP suit only if all three elements are met: (1) it is brought in bad faith against a party who communicated with a federal, State or local government body or the public at large on a matter within a government body’s authority or an issue of public concern, exercising rights under the First Amendment or Articles 10, 13 or 40 of the Maryland Declaration of Rights; (2) it is materially related to that co","#st-md-slapp"],["Procedure: motion to dismiss or stay; no stated deadline","Maryland · anti-SLAPP","The defendant may move to (1) dismiss, and the court must hold a hearing ‘as soon as practicable’; or (2) stay all court proceedings until the underlying matter the defendant communicated about is resolved.\nThe statute sets no filing deadline, no discovery stay, no evidentiary burden-shifting, and no time limit for the ruling. It applies notwithstanding any other law or rule, and it preserves every other legal or equ","#st-md-slapp"],["No fee-shifting and no special appeal right","Maryland · anti-SLAPP","Section 5-807 contains no attorney’s fee or cost award. It does not create an immediate appeal from denial of the motion, and denial of a § 5-807 motion is not among the interlocutory orders listed as appealable in CJ § 12-303. Whether any other route to early review exists (for example, the collateral order doctrine) was not checked; the case law was not collected. Cts. & Jud. Proc. §§ 5-807, 12-303","#st-md-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: Rule 19-217 (rule text not collected)","Maryland · out of state","Out-of-state lawyers appear in a particular Maryland case by special admission (pro hac vice) under Maryland Rule 19-217. That rule could not be read because mdcourts.gov blocked automated access, so its requirements are not stated here: the motion contents, the Maryland sponsoring lawyer, any limit on the number of admissions, and any fee. Read the current rule before filing. Md. Rule 19-217 (not verified)","#st-md-oos"],["Statute: admission required to practice, and the special-admission statute for employed lawyers","Maryland · out of state","An individual must be admitted to the Maryland Bar, and meet any requirement the Supreme Court sets by rule, before practicing law in Maryland. Unauthorized practice is prohibited.\nSeparately, a court may grant special admission for one case to a lawyer admitted elsewhere who is employed by a party in a case before a court or a State or local unit. The motion must be filed as the Supreme Court’s rules require. The co","#st-md-oos"],["In-house counsel admitted elsewhere may advise the employer without Maryland admission","Maryland · out of state","A lawyer admitted in another state may give legal advice to the employer and the employer’s affiliates without Maryland admission. That lawyer is subject to Maryland discipline and may not appear before a State or local government unit without a special admission under § 10-215. Any registration requirement under the Maryland Rules was not checked. Bus. Occ. & Prof. § 10-206(d)","#st-md-oos"],["Limited-scope representation and e-filing (MDEC): not verified","Maryland · out of state","The Maryland Rules on limited-scope appearances and on electronic filing through MDEC (Maryland Electronic Courts; e-filing rules are reported to be in Title 20) could not be collected. Coverage, mandatory-filer status, and registration steps for out-of-state counsel are not stated here. Check Title 20 and the MDEC pages on mdcourts.gov. Md. Rules Title 20 (not verified)","#st-md-oos"],["Governing code: Maryland Attorneys’ Rules of Professional Conduct (not collected)","Maryland · ethics","Maryland lawyers are governed by the Maryland Attorneys’ Rules of Professional Conduct, Rules 19-300.1 ff., in Title 19 of the Maryland Rules. They follow the ABA Model Rules numbering after the ‘19-3’ prefix (for example, 19-301.6 is confidentiality). The text could not be collected from mdcourts.gov, so no differences from the ABA Model Rules are stated here. Md. Rules 19-300.1 ff. (not verified)","#st-md-ethics"],["Supreme Court’s rule power and discipline of out-of-state lawyers (statute)","Maryland · ethics","The Supreme Court of Maryland adopts rules on practice, procedure and court administration, and those rules have the force of law. Out-of-state lawyers who advise their employers or practice under special admission are expressly subject to disciplinary proceedings under the Maryland Rules. Md. Const. art. IV, § 18(a); Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 1-201(a); Bus. Occ. & Prof. §§ 10-206(d)(3)(i), 10-215(d)","#st-md-ethics"],["The Maryland Rules, not the Federal Rules","Maryland · land mine","Circuit court civil practice is under Title 2 of the Maryland Rules, District Court practice under Title 3, and appeals under Title 8. Their numbering and deadlines differ from the federal rules. These rules have the force of law. (Rule text was not collected for this page; read the current Rules.) Md. Const. art. IV, § 18(a); Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 1-201","#st-md-unique"],["Two different trial courts, and the $5,000 / $30,000 lines","Maryland · land mine","The District Court has exclusive jurisdiction over contract and tort claims up to $30,000 and over small claims up to $5,000. Above $5,000 (except replevin, landlord-tenant and certain other cases) the plaintiff may choose the District Court or the circuit court. Claims for equity relief (outside the listed exceptions), title to real property, and declaratory judgments must go to the circuit court. The $30,000 cap ch","#st-md-unique"],["District Court appeal: 30 days, filed in the District Court, often de novo","Maryland · land mine","Appeals from District Court civil judgments go to the circuit court for the same county (or Baltimore City), not to the Appellate Court. File the order for appeal with the District Court clerk within 30 days of the final judgment; certain landlord-tenant cases have shorter, section-specific times. If more than $5,000 is in controversy (excluding interest, costs and recoverable fees), or the parties agree, the appeal ","#st-md-unique"],["No further appeal of right after the circuit court reviews the District Court","Maryland · land mine","A circuit court judgment entered in its appellate capacity (reviewing the District Court, an agency or a local legislative body) cannot be appealed under § 12-301 unless a statute expressly grants the right. The only route is a petition for certiorari to the Supreme Court of Maryland, granted for uniformity of decision or special public-interest circumstances. Cts. & Jud. Proc. §§ 12-302(a), 12-305","#st-md-unique"],["Interlocutory appeals are a closed statutory list","Maryland · land mine","Civil interlocutory appeals lie only from the orders listed in § 12-303. Examples: possession of property; injunctions (an appeal from granting an injunction, refusing to dissolve one, or appointing a receiver requires the appellant to have filed an answer first); orders for payment of money or sale of property; certain custody orders; and orders staying arbitration. Denial of an anti-SLAPP motion is not on the list.","#st-md-unique"],["Anti-SLAPP is weak: bad faith required, no fees, no deadline","Maryland · land mine","Unlike many states’ statutes, § 5-807 requires the plaintiff to have sued in bad faith and with intent to inhibit, or with the effect of inhibiting, protected rights. It provides only a hearing ‘as soon as practicable’ or a stay. There is no fee award and no automatic discovery stay. Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 5-807(b), (d)","#st-md-unique"],["Venue: negligence torts may be filed where the claim arose; nonresident defendants anywhere","Maryland · land mine","General venue is where a defendant resides, carries on regular business, is employed or habitually works (and for a corporation, also its principal Maryland office). A negligence tort may also be brought where it arose. A damages action against a nonresident individual may be brought in any county. With several defendants and no common venue, sue where any defendant could be sued or where the claim arose. Cts. & Jud.","#st-md-unique"],["Three-year default limitations period","Maryland · land mine","A civil action at law must be filed within three years from accrual unless another Code provision sets a different period. Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 5-101","#st-md-unique"],["Supreme Court review is by certiorari, and some leave denials are final","Maryland · land mine","Review of Appellate Court cases is by petition for certiorari, which may be filed before or after the Appellate Court decides, within the time set by the Maryland Rules. The writ issues if review is ‘desirable and in the public interest.’ Certiorari is barred where the Appellate Court granted or denied leave to appeal in categories listed in § 12-202, such as guilty-plea judgments and probation revocations. Cts. & Ju","#st-md-unique"],["Pro hac vice and professional conduct rules are not on this page","Maryland · land mine","Rule 19-217 (pro hac vice) and the Maryland Attorneys’ Rules of Professional Conduct could not be collected (mdcourts.gov bot block). Read them directly before appearing; do not rely on another state’s practice for the sponsor, fee, or numerical limits. Md. Rules 19-217, 19-300.1 ff. (not verified)","#st-md-unique"],["Constitutional removal of a case to another county","Maryland · judges","Maryland has no peremptory challenge to a judge in the statute text collected. Instead, the Constitution lets a party remove a case to another court. In a capital case, a sworn written suggestion that the party cannot get a fair and impartial trial is enough. In other criminal cases, in suits at law, and in issues from an orphans’ court, the party must also show the court that the suggestion is true or that there is ","#st-md-unique"],["Recusal: governed by the Code of Judicial Conduct (not collected)","Maryland · judges","Disqualification standards are in the Maryland Code of Judicial Conduct in Title 18 of the Maryland Rules, which could not be collected. One statutory ground is verified: while a judge is receiving buyout payments from a former law firm, the judge may not hear a case in which a partner or employee of that firm, or its successor, is attorney of record. If a justice or judge is disqualified, the Chief Justice may assig","#st-md-unique"],["Declaration of Rights Articles 10, 13 and 40","Maryland · speech","Article 40 protects the liberty of the press and every citizen’s right to speak, write and publish on all subjects, ‘being responsible for the abuse of that privilege.’ Article 13 protects petitioning the Legislature. Article 10 protects legislative speech and debate. The anti-SLAPP statute expressly protects activity under all three. Whether Maryland courts read Article 40 more broadly than the First Amendment was n","#st-md-unique"],["2022 court renaming","Maryland · unique rules","The Court of Appeals is now the Supreme Court of Maryland, and the Court of Special Appeals is now the Appellate Court of Maryland. The current statutes and Constitution use only the new names. Older cases, rules and forms use the old names; cite the current names. Md. Const. art. IV, § 14; Cts. & Jud. Proc. §§ 12-307, 12-308","#st-md-unique"],["District Court jury demand moves the case","Maryland · unique rules","In the District Court no jury trial may be demanded if the amount in controversy is $25,000 or less, excluding attorney’s fees. If a party entitled to a jury files a timely demand, jurisdiction transfers at once to the circuit court (replevin has special handling). Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 4-402(e)","#st-md-unique"],["Appeals in Maryland: three routes, set by which court decided the case","Maryland · appeals","From a circuit court final judgment, appeal goes to the Appellate Court of Maryland (formerly the Court of Special Appeals). From the District Court, a civil appeal goes to the circuit court for the same county, on the record or de novo depending on the amount. Beyond that, review is only by certiorari in the Supreme Court of Maryland (formerly the Court of Appeals).\nThe statutes set who may appeal and from what. The","#st-md-appeals"],["Final judgments of circuit courts","Maryland · appeals · What can be appealed","A party may appeal from a final judgment of a circuit court in a civil or criminal case. This includes judgments in special, limited or statutory jurisdiction unless a law expressly denies the appeal. The Appellate Court has exclusive initial appellate jurisdiction. Cts. & Jud. Proc. §§ 12-301, 12-308","#st-md-appeals"],["No appeal of right from a circuit court acting as an appellate court","Maryland · appeals · What can be appealed","When a circuit court reviews the District Court, an administrative agency or a local legislative body, its judgment is not appealable under § 12-301 unless a statute expressly grants an appeal. Seek certiorari in the Supreme Court instead (see below). Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-302(a)","#st-md-appeals"],["Interlocutory orders: only those listed","Maryland · appeals · What can be appealed","Civil interlocutory orders are appealable only if listed in § 12-303. The list includes: orders about possession of the property in suit or its income; orders granting or denying a motion to quash an attachment; orders granting, dissolving, refusing to dissolve, or refusing an injunction; orders appointing a receiver; orders for sale or delivery of property or payment of money; orders determining a question of right ","#st-md-appeals"],["Contempt","Maryland · appeals · What can be appealed","Any person adjudged in contempt may appeal, including from an interlocutory remedial contempt order and whether or not a party. This does not apply to contempt for violating an interlocutory alimony order. Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-304","#st-md-appeals"],["Cross-appeal after remittitur","Maryland · appeals · What can be appealed","In a civil case, a plaintiff who accepted a remittitur may still cross-appeal from the final judgment. The general timing and procedure for cross-appeals are in Title 8 of the Rules (not verified). Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-301","#st-md-appeals"],["Notice of appeal from circuit court: Rule 8-202 (not verified)","Maryland · appeals · Timing and tolling","The time to note an appeal from a circuit court judgment, and the post-judgment motions that extend it, are set by Maryland Rule 8-202. The rule text was not collected. It is commonly cited as 30 days after entry of judgment, but confirm the period, the tolling motions (for example, motions under Rules 2-532, 2-533 and 2-534) and their own deadlines in the current rule. Md. Rule 8-202 (not verified)","#st-md-appeals"],["District Court civil appeal: 30 days, filed with the District Court clerk","Maryland · appeals · Timing and tolling","File an order for appeal with the District Court clerk within 30 days from the date of the final judgment. Judgments in landlord-tenant and related cases under Real Property §§ 8-332, 8-401, 8-402, 14-109 and 14-120 follow the time set in those sections. Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-401(e)","#st-md-appeals"],["Where and how they are heard","Maryland · appeals · District Court appeals","The appeal goes to the circuit court for the county where judgment was entered, or the Circuit Court for Baltimore City. It is heard on the District Court record if the amount in controversy exceeds $5,000 (excluding interest, costs and recoverable fees), in matters under § 4-401(7)(ii), or when the parties agree. Every other appeal is tried de novo. Cts. & Jud. Proc. §§ 12-401(f), 12-403","#st-md-appeals"],["Briefing schedule and limits (not verified)","Maryland · appeals · Briefs, word limits and e-filing","Briefing deadlines, record extract requirements, and word or page limits for the Appellate Court and the Supreme Court are in Title 8 of the Maryland Rules, which could not be collected. No numbers are given here; read Title 8 (including Rules 8-501 to 8-504 on the record extract and briefs, as commonly cited) before filing. Md. Rules Title 8 (not verified)","#st-md-appeals"],["Electronic filing (MDEC) (not verified)","Maryland · appeals · Briefs, word limits and e-filing","Appellate e-filing through MDEC is governed by Title 20 of the Maryland Rules, which could not be collected. Check Title 20 and the Appellate Court’s current instructions. Md. Rules Title 20 (not verified)","#st-md-appeals"],["Certiorari from the Appellate Court, before or after decision","Maryland · appeals · Supreme Court of Maryland","Any party may petition for certiorari in a case pending in or decided by the Appellate Court on appeal from a circuit court, an orphans’ court or the Tax Court. The petition may be filed before or after the Appellate Court decides, within the time set by the Maryland Rules (not verified). The Supreme Court may also grant certiorari on its own motion. It grants the writ when review is ‘desirable and in the public inte","#st-md-appeals"],["Certiorari barred in some leave-to-appeal cases","Maryland · appeals · Supreme Court of Maryland","No certiorari lies where the Appellate Court granted or denied leave to appeal in: post-conviction cases; habeas corpus on bail; incarcerated individual grievance cases; guilty-plea judgments; and probation revocations. Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-202","#st-md-appeals"],["Certiorari to review a circuit court’s appellate judgment","Maryland · appeals · Supreme Court of Maryland","When a circuit court has decided an appeal from the District Court (or certain Transportation Article agency appeals), the Supreme Court will grant certiorari on petition only if review is needed for uniformity of decision, or other special circumstances make review desirable and in the public interest. Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-305","#st-md-appeals"],["Certified questions","Maryland · appeals · Supreme Court of Maryland","The Supreme Court has exclusive appellate jurisdiction over questions of law certified to it under the Uniform Certification of Questions of Law Act. Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-307(3)","#st-md-appeals"],["Reargument of right after a split decision","Maryland · appeals · Supreme Court of Maryland","Five justices normally sit. If the Court divides equally, or 3–2, the losing party is entitled on application to reargument before all seven justices as of right. An equal division with no application affirms the decision below. Motions for reconsideration in the Appellate Court and their deadlines are in Title 8 (not verified). Md. Const. art. IV, § 14","#st-md-appeals"],["Notice of appeal from a circuit court final judgment","Maryland · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the judgment or order appealed from.\nNOT VERIFIED: rule text not collected (mdcourts.gov blocked). The 30-day period is the commonly cited rule; confirm it and the tolling effect of timely post-judgment motions in the current Rule 8-202. Md. Rule 8-202(a) (not verified)","#st-md-appeals"],["Civil appeal from the District Court to the circuit court","Maryland · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from date of the District Court final judgment.\nFile the order for appeal with the District Court clerk. Certain landlord-tenant judgments (Real Prop. §§ 8-332, 8-401, 8-402, 14-109, 14-120) have their own times. Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-401(e)(1)","#st-md-appeals"],["State’s pretrial appeal from suppression or return-of-property ruling (criminal)","Maryland · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from date the trial court decision is rendered.\nOnly in the case types listed in § 12-302(c)(4)(i), and before jeopardy attaches. The appellate decision is due within 120 days after the record is filed, or the trial ruling becomes final. Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-302(c)(4)(iii)","#st-md-appeals"],["Michigan Supreme Court","Michigan · courts","Court of last resort. Writes the Michigan Court Rules, regulates bar admission (through the Board of Law Examiners) and lawyer discipline (through the Attorney Grievance Commission and Attorney Discipline Board). Hears civil cases almost entirely by discretionary application for leave to appeal, including ‘bypass’ applications before the Court of Appeals decides (MCR 7.303, 7.305; MCR 9.108).","#st-mi"],["Michigan Court of Appeals","Michigan · courts","Statewide intermediate court deciding in three-judge panels. Hears appeals of right from final judgments of the circuit court and the Court of Claims, appeals by leave from interlocutory orders and from circuit-court appellate decisions, and some original actions (MCR 7.203). Published opinions bind later panels; unpublished ones do not (MCR 7.215(C), (J)).","#st-mi"],["Circuit Courts (incl. family division and business court dockets)","Michigan · courts","General-jurisdiction trial courts organized by judicial circuit. Some circuits keep business court dockets for business or commercial disputes defined in MCL 600.8031, and pleadings must state whether the case qualifies (MCR 2.112(O)). Circuit courts also hear appeals from district and probate courts and agencies (MCR 7.101–7.123).","#st-mi"],["Court of Claims","Michigan · courts","Hears claims against the state and its agencies. A defendant may move such a case from circuit court by notice of transfer under MCR 2.228. Its final judgments are appealable of right to the Court of Appeals (MCR 7.203(A)(1)).","#st-mi"],["District Courts, Probate Courts, and Municipal Courts","Michigan · courts","District courts handle civil infraction actions, landlord-tenant and land-contract summary proceedings, small claims, and other civil matters within their statutory limits (Chapter 4 of the MCR); discovery there requires leave or stipulation (MCR 2.301(A)). Probate courts handle estates, trusts, guardianships, and mental-health matters (Chapter 5). Appeals from both go first to the circuit court (MCR 7.103–7.104). Do","#st-mi"],["Statute and scope: Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (eff. March 24, 2026)","Michigan · anti-SLAPP","Michigan now has an anti-SLAPP law. The Uniform Public Expression Protection Act, 2025 PA 52, took effect March 24, 2026, and applies to civil actions filed, or causes of action asserted, on or after that date (Sec. 13).\nIt covers claims based on (i) a communication in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding; (ii) a communication on an issue under consideration or review i","#st-mi-slapp"],["Procedure: special motion within 60 days; automatic stay","Michigan · anti-SLAPP","File a ‘special motion for expedited relief’ to dismiss all or part of the action no later than 60 days after service of the pleading asserting the claim, or later for good cause. Under MCR 2.114 it must meet MCR 2.119 requirements, say it is a special motion under MCL 691.1853 et seq., and state the basis under MCL 691.1857.\nFiling stays all other proceedings between the movant and the responding party, including di","#st-mi-slapp"],["Standard: dismissal with prejudice unless the plaintiff makes a prima facie case","Michigan · anti-SLAPP","The court considers the pleadings, motion papers, affidavits, depositions, admissions, and other documentary evidence. It must dismiss with prejudice if (a) the movant shows the claim is eligible, (b) the responding party fails to show an exclusion applies, and (c) either the responding party fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows failure to state a claim or no genuine is","#st-mi-slapp"],["Fees: mandatory for a winning movant","Michigan · anti-SLAPP","The court must award court costs, reasonable attorney fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion to a prevailing movant. A prevailing respondent gets them only if the motion was frivolous or filed solely to delay. A voluntary dismissal with prejudice counts as the movant prevailing; a dismissal without prejudice does not stop the movant from getting a ruling and fees. MCL 691.1857(2)–(3), 691.1860","#st-mi-slapp"],["Appeal: as of right within 21 days; whole case stayed","Michigan · anti-SLAPP","The statute gives the moving party an appeal as of right from an order denying the motion in whole or in part, filed within 21 days after entry. MCR 2.114(E) goes further, allowing the movant to appeal as of right from an order ‘denying or granting, in whole or in part’, filed under MCR 7.204. Once any party appeals the ruling, all proceedings between all parties are stayed until the appeal ends (stays on appeal are ","#st-mi-slapp"],["Temporary admission (pro hac vice) under MCR 8.126: State Bar first, then the tribunal","Michigan · out of state","A ‘foreign attorney’ (licensed in another U.S. state or territory, D.C., a Tribal court, or a foreign country, not disbarred or suspended anywhere) appears only through a sponsoring Michigan lawyer, who must appear as counsel of record and file a motion with the tribunal. Admission is discretionary.\nBefore filing with the tribunal, file the motion and supporting papers with the State Bar of Michigan with the fee. The","#st-mi-oos"],["Fee and the 5-case limit","Michigan · out of state","The fee equals the discipline and client-protection portions of a State Bar member’s annual dues. The rule does not state a dollar figure; check the State Bar’s current amount.\nA foreign attorney may not appear in more than five cases in any 365-day period. The limit (and, for certain public defender, legal-aid, and clinic lawyers, repeat fees) does not apply while a BLE Rule 5 application for admission without exami","#st-mi-oos"],["Scope: whole case through appeals; separate admission for agencies and arbitration","Michigan · out of state","Temporary admission granted by a court lasts for the entire case, including appeals, remands, and court-ordered facilitation, mediation, or arbitration. An arbitrator or agency may admit a foreign attorney only for that proceeding; if it turns into a court case, apply again.\nThe tribunal may revoke admission for misconduct and must revoke on loss of good standing. If the sponsor withdraws, another Michigan lawyer mus","#st-mi-oos"],["Admission without examination (BLE Rule 5)","Michigan · out of state","A lawyer licensed in a U.S. jurisdiction who is in good standing and intends to practice (or teach full-time) in Michigan may be admitted without exam after practicing law as a principal occupation, teaching full-time at a law school, or serving as an active-duty judge advocate for 3 of the 5 years before applying. Applicants must also complete any Michigan Law Component required by BLE Rule 3a and submit an NCBE cha","#st-mi-oos"],["Temporary and multijurisdictional practice without admission","Michigan · out of state","MRPC 5.5(c)–(d) follows the ABA safe harbors for temporary practice by U.S.-admitted lawyers, such as work with active local co-counsel or work related to a proceeding or ADR. MRPC 5.5(e) also lets a lawyer licensed elsewhere practice the law of that home jurisdiction while physically in Michigan, provided the lawyer does not hold out a Michigan office or offer or provide Michigan legal services unless otherwise perm","#st-mi-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances and ghostwriting","Michigan · out of state","A Michigan-licensed lawyer may limit the scope of representation and file a notice of limited appearance in a civil action that identifies the limits by date, time period, or subject matter. A limited-scope lawyer may later withdraw by notice: it takes effect immediately if the client signs it, or 14 days after filing and service unless the client files a written objection that the agreed services were not completed.","#st-mi-oos"],["E-filing through MiFILE","Michigan · out of state","Attorneys must e-file the required case types in courts where e-filing has been implemented, unless exempt because of a disability. Self-represented filers must e-file only in courts that SCAO has approved to mandate it. An e-filed document is deemed filed when transmission is complete and fees are paid or waived. Anything submitted by 11:59 p.m. on a business day is filed that day; weekend, holiday, and court-closed","#st-mi-oos"],["Governing code: Michigan Rules of Professional Conduct","Michigan · ethics","Adopted by the Michigan Supreme Court and drawn largely from the ABA Model Rules, with Michigan changes. The collected version is updated with orders effective January 1, 2026. Only the rule text is authoritative: the comments do not expand or limit the rules (MRPC 1.0(c)). A violation is a basis for discipline but creates no civil cause of action. Discipline is prosecuted by the Attorney Grievance Commission (MCR 9.","#st-mi-ethics"],["Confidentiality uses the older ‘confidences and secrets’ model","Michigan · ethics","Unlike ABA Model Rule 1.6 (‘information relating to the representation’), MRPC 1.6 protects ‘confidences’ (privileged information) and ‘secrets’ (other information the client asked to keep or whose disclosure would embarrass or harm the client).\nPermissive disclosure covers client consent after full disclosure; disclosure required by law or court order; rectifying the consequences of a client’s illegal or fraudulent ","#st-mi-ethics"],["Fees: contingent-fee cap and looser fee-splitting","Michigan · ethics","In personal injury, wrongful death, and no-fault benefits matters, a contingent fee above one-third of the net recovery is a ‘clearly excessive fee’ under MRPC 1.5(a) (MCR 8.121(A)–(C)).\nA fee may be divided between lawyers in different firms if the client is advised of and does not object to every lawyer’s participation and the total fee is reasonable. The rule does not require the ABA’s proportional-or-joint-respon","#st-mi-ethics"],["Scope of representation: Michigan-specific additions","Michigan · ethics","MRPC 1.2(a) adds that a lawyer does not violate the rule by granting opposing counsel’s reasonable requests that do not prejudice the client, being punctual, or avoiding offensive tactics. The client also decides whether to accept a mediation evaluation. MRPC 1.2(b) adds the limited-appearance and ghostwriting provisions described under out-of-state practice. MRPC 1.2(a)–(b)","#st-mi-ethics"],["Candor: remedial-measures duty tracks the Model Rule, with a Michigan twist","Michigan · ethics","MRPC 3.3(a)–(c) matches the ABA structure: a duty to take reasonable remedial measures, including disclosure to the tribunal if needed, that lasts to the end of the proceeding even if MRPC 1.6 information must be revealed. MRPC 3.3(e) adds black-letter text on false evidence: first try to persuade the client, then take remedial measures, and seek to withdraw if that will fix it. MRPC 3.3(a)–(e)","#st-mi-ethics"],["Trial publicity lists presumptively prejudicial topics in the rule itself","Michigan · ethics","MRPC 3.6(a) says an extrajudicial statement is likely to be materially prejudicial when it concerns a civil matter triable to a jury, a criminal matter, or a proceeding that could result in incarceration, and relates to listed topics such as a party’s or witness’s character, credibility, or criminal record, test results, or likely-inadmissible information. In the ABA Model Rules, comparable topics are listed only in ","#st-mi-ethics"],["21 days to appeal, and it is jurisdictional","Michigan · land mine","A civil appeal of right must be filed within 21 days after entry of the judgment or order, not 30. Only a postjudgment motion for new trial, rehearing, reconsideration, or other relief filed within that initial 21-day period (or extra time the trial court allows within it) restarts the clock. After that, only a delayed application for leave (within 6 months) is available, and it is discretionary. MCR 7.204(A)(1); MCR","#st-mi-unique"],["Summons expires in 91 days and the case dismisses itself","Michigan · land mine","A summons expires 91 days after issuance. Unserved defendants are dismissed without prejudice automatically, with no court order needed. To extend, get a second summons by court order within the 91 days, showing due diligence; the extension may not run beyond 1 year from the original issuance. MCR 2.102(D)–(E)","#st-mi-unique"],["Personal-jurisdiction and service defenses die with your first filing","Michigan · land mine","Lack of personal jurisdiction and insufficient process or service must be raised in your first motion under MCR 2.116 or your responsive pleading, whichever is filed first, or they are waived. Release, statute of limitations, statute of frauds, and arbitration-agreement defenses ((C)(7)) must be in the responsive pleading or an earlier motion. MCR 2.116(D)(1)–(2)","#st-mi-unique"],["Rejecting an offer of judgment can cost you the other side’s attorney fees","Michigan · land mine","Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 68, any party can make an offer, and the sanction includes a reasonable attorney fee from rejection forward. Failing to accept within 21 days is a rejection. Do the adjusted-verdict math before letting an offer lapse. MCR 2.405(B)–(D)","#st-mi-unique"],["Motion papers: 20 pages combined, no reply brief without permission","Michigan · land mine","A motion and its brief together, or a response and its brief, may not exceed 20 double-spaced pages, and reply or supplemental briefs are barred unless the court permits. Default timing: serve a motion at least 9 days before the hearing by mail (7 by delivery) and file at least 7 days before. Serve a response at least 5 days before by mail (3 by delivery) and file at least 3 days before. Check local and judge practic","#st-mi-unique"],["Reconsideration in 21 days, and only for ‘palpable error’","Michigan · land mine","A motion for rehearing or reconsideration is due within 21 days after the order. It gets no response or argument unless ordered, and it must show a palpable error that misled the court and parties and would change the result. Rearguing the same issues fails. MCR 2.119(F)","#st-mi-unique"],["The ‘7-day rule’ for proposed orders","Michigan · land mine","If an order is not entered at the hearing, a party may serve a proposed order with notice that it will be submitted for signature unless written objections are filed within 7 days. Ignore it and the order enters as drafted. The party who obtains a signed judgment or order must serve it on all parties within 7 days. MCR 2.602(B)(3), (E)(1)","#st-mi-unique"],["Interrogatories capped at 20, and initial disclosures come early","Michigan · land mine","Each separately represented party may serve no more than 20 interrogatories on each party, counting discrete subparts. Initial disclosures, including legal theories and expert subject areas, are due without a request: a pleader serves within 14 days after an opposing party answers, and an answering party within the later of 14 days after those are due or 28 days after answering; discovery generally cannot be sought u","#st-mi-unique"],["Setting aside a default needs a verified meritorious defense","Michigan · land mine","Except for lack of jurisdiction, a motion to set aside a default or default judgment requires good cause plus a verified statement of facts showing a meritorious defense. If you were personally served, file before the default judgment or within 21 days after it (MCR 2.612 relief aside). Setting aside is conditioned on paying taxable costs. MCR 2.603(D)","#st-mi-unique"],["Pro hac vice: Bar filing first, 5 cases per 365 days","Michigan · land mine","Filing a pro hac vice motion straight with the court will not work. The papers and fee go to the State Bar first, the Bar reports within 7 days, and the court cannot grant the motion until it gets that report. Out-of-state lawyers are limited to five temporary admissions in any 365-day period unless a BLE Rule 5 application is pending. MCR 8.126(B)(2)–(3), (C)(2)–(3)","#st-mi-unique"],["Unpublished Court of Appeals opinions: not binding, and citing them has rules","Michigan · land mine","Unpublished opinions are not binding precedent and should not be cited where published authority exists. When citing one, explain why and include the docket number and date (attach a copy if issued before 1996). Published opinions issued on or after November 1, 1990 bind later panels until reversed or overruled through the conflict-panel procedure. MCR 7.215(C), (J)(1); MCR 2.119(A)(2)","#st-mi-unique"],["Protected personal identifying information stays out of public filings","Michigan · land mine","Do not put dates of birth, Social Security numbers, driver’s license or state ID numbers, passport numbers, or financial account numbers in public court filings. Use the redaction and SCAO-form procedure in MCR 1.109(D)(9); where an SSN is required, use only the last four digits. MCR 1.109(D)(9)","#st-mi-unique"],["No peremptory challenge of a judge in civil cases","Michigan · judges","The Michigan Court Rules have no peremptory or as-of-right substitution of judge. A judge is removed only by a disqualification motion for cause under MCR 2.003. (The rules’ only ‘peremptory challenges’ are for jurors, MCR 2.511(F).) MCR 2.003; MCR 2.511(F)","#st-mi-unique"],["Grounds for disqualification","Michigan · judges","Grounds include actual bias or prejudice for or against a party or attorney; an objective ‘serious risk of actual bias’ under Caperton v Massey, or failure to meet the appearance-of-impropriety standard in Canon 2 of the Michigan Code of Judicial Conduct; personal knowledge of disputed facts; prior work as a lawyer in the matter; having been a partner of or lawyer for a party, or in a firm representing a party, withi","#st-mi-unique"],["Strict timing: 14 days from discovering the grounds","Michigan · judges","In the trial courts, move within 14 days of discovering the grounds, or forthwith if discovered within 14 days of trial. In the Court of Appeals, move within 14 days of disclosure of the panel assignment or discovery of the grounds. In the Supreme Court, file with the application or within 28 days. Include all known grounds and an affidavit. Late motions may be granted for good cause, but untimeliness counts against ","#st-mi-unique"],["Who decides: the challenged judge, then de novo review on request","Michigan · judges","The challenged judge rules first. If the motion is denied, a party may ask that it be referred to the chief judge (or, in a single-judge court or when the challenged judge is chief judge, to the State Court Administrator for assignment), who decides de novo. In the Supreme Court the challenged justice decides and publishes reasons, and a party may ask the full Court to decide de novo within 14 days. MCR 2.003(D)(1)(c","#st-mi-unique"],["UPEPA: broad construction and creative-work carve-back","Michigan · speech","The anti-SLAPP act directs that it be broadly construed to protect speech, press, assembly, petition, and association rights under both constitutions. Its commercial-speech exclusion does not reach creating, disseminating, exhibiting, or advertising dramatic, literary, musical, political, journalistic, or artistic works. MCL 691.1852(1)(a), (2)(a); MCL 691.1861","#st-mi-unique"],["Michigan Constitution free speech clause: not collected","Michigan · speech","Whether Michigan courts read Const 1963, art 1, § 5 more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked against collected sources. Const 1963, art 1, § 5 (not in collected texts)","#st-mi-unique"],["Summary disposition replaces both Rule 12(b) and Rule 56","Michigan · unique rules","Michigan uses a single motion for summary disposition on ten grounds, MCR 2.116(C)(1)–(10). Jurisdiction, process, and service defenses ((C)(1)–(3)) are waived unless raised in the first motion or the responsive pleading, whichever comes first. MCR 2.116(C), (D)(1)","#st-mi-unique"],["Offers to stipulate to judgment shift attorney fees","Michigan · unique rules","Any party may serve an offer up to 28 days before trial. A party that rejects an offer and does worse than the average of offer and counteroffer pays the offeror’s actual costs, including a reasonable attorney fee. The court may decline to award the fee in the interest of justice. MCR 2.405(A)–(D)","#st-mi-unique"],["Rule 2.001. Applicability","Michigan · civil rules","The Chapter 2 rules govern procedure in all civil proceedings in every Michigan court, except where a court’s limited jurisdiction makes a rule inherently inapplicable or a rule for a specific court or proceeding provides a different procedure.","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.002. Waiver of Fees for Indigent Persons","Michigan · civil rules","Only individuals (not organizations, unless a statute says otherwise) may request a fee waiver, on a nonpublic SCAO “Fee Waiver Request” form filed in each case; prisoners must also file a certified institutional account with a 12-month deposit and withdrawal history. The clerk must waive fees for recipients of means-tested public assistance or clients of qualifying legal services programs or law school clinics, and ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.003. Disqualification of Judge","Michigan · civil rules","Applies to all judges, including Supreme Court justices; grounds include bias, a serious risk of actual bias under Caperton, an appearance of impropriety, personal knowledge of disputed facts, prior involvement as counsel, having been a partner of or lawyer for a party or member of a party’s law firm within the preceding 2 years, more than a de minimis economic interest, or a relative within the third degree involved","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.004. Incarcerated Parties","Michigan · civil rules","In domestic relations and other child custody, guardianship, neglect, foster-care, or termination cases involving a party in Department of Corrections custody, the moving party must confirm the incarceration, serve the prisoner, and state the prison number and location in the filing. The court must then order that the prisoner be allowed to participate by noncollect, unmonitored telephone call or videoconference, ser","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.101. Form and Commencement of Action","Michigan · civil rules","There is one form of action, the “civil action,” and it is commenced by filing a complaint with a court. This mirrors Fed. R. Civ. P. 2 and 3.","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.102. Summons; Expiration; Dismissal for Failure to Serve","Michigan · civil rules","The clerk issues a summons on filing of the complaint, listing 11 required items including its last valid date. A summons expires 91 days after issuance, but within those 91 days the judge may, on a showing of due diligence, order a second summons for a definite period not exceeding 1 year from issuance; the 91 days are tolled while a motion challenging the summons or service is pending. On expiration the action is a","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.103. Process; Who May Serve","Michigan · civil rules","Any legally competent adult who is not a party or an officer of a corporate party may serve process. Writs of restitution and process seizing property may be served only by a sheriff, deputy, bailiff, or appointed court officer (or state or local police where the state or municipality is a party), service in a governmental institution must be made by the person in charge or a designee, and process requiring arrest ma","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.104. Process; Proof of Service","Michigan · civil rules","Proof of service may be made by the recipient’s signed acknowledgment, by a certificate of a sheriff, qualifying deputy or bailiff, court officer, or attorney for service in Michigan, or by a verified statement of the facts of service, stating the manner, time, date, and place. Failure to file proof does not affect the validity of service, and proof of publication, posting, and mailing is governed by MCR 2.106(G).","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.105. Process; Manner of Service","Michigan · civil rules","Individuals may be served by personal delivery or by registered or certified mail, return receipt requested, delivery restricted to the addressee, with service complete when the defendant acknowledges receipt; substituted methods apply to nonresidents, minors, persons with guardians or conservators, and assumed-name businesses. The rule specifies service on partnerships, corporations (including by registered mail to ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.106. Notice by Posting or Publication","Michigan · civil rules","Governs service by publication or posting under a court order obtained by motion under MCR 2.105(J); the order must state the court, parties, nature of the proceeding, where and when to answer, and the effect of failing to answer. Publication runs once a week for 3 consecutive weeks in a qualifying newspaper, and posting runs for 3 continuous weeks in the courthouse and 2 or more other public places, each with a copy","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.107. Service and Filing of Pleadings and Other Documents","Michigan · civil rules","Every party who has filed a pleading, appearance, or motion must be served with all later filed documents, generally through the party’s attorney, except for the original summons and complaint, contempt notices, postjudgment matters after the appeal period, and limited-appearance situations where both the party and the limited scope attorney are served. Service is by delivery, mail (complete on mailing), e-service un","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.108. Time","Michigan · civil rules","A defendant must answer or take other permitted action within 21 days after personal service in Michigan, or 28 days if served outside Michigan or by registered mail, and not less than 28 days after publication or posting is completed. Answers to counterclaims and cross-claims, optional replies, motions directed at a pleading needing no response, medical malpractice answers after notice of the affidavit or security u","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.109. Security for Costs","Michigan · civil rules","On motion, the court may order a party asserting a claim to post a bond with surety, in an amount set in its discretion, covering costs and recoverable expenses in the trial and any appellate court. Security is excused for a party with a legitimate claim who shows by affidavit an inability to pay, and is never required of the United States, Michigan or its governmental units, or exempt officers suing officially; the ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.110. Pleadings","Michigan · civil rules","Pleadings are limited to a complaint, cross-claim, counterclaim, third-party complaint, an answer to any of those, and a reply to an answer, and a responsive pleading is required to each of those claims and to an answer demanding a reply. Counterclaims and cross-claims combined with an answer must be clearly designated, though the court may treat misdesignated claims or defenses as properly designated. Unlike Fed. R.","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.111. General Rules of Pleading","Michigan · civil rules","Allegations must be clear, concise, and direct, and alternative or inconsistent claims and defenses are allowed. A claim must state the facts relied on and a demand for judgment, stating a specific dollar amount only for a sum certain or a claim of $25,000 or less; otherwise no amount may be stated and the pleading must show the claim is within the court’s jurisdiction. Responses must admit, deny (stating the substan","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.112. Pleading Special Matters","Michigan · civil rules","Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity, while capacity, conditions of mind, and conditions precedent may be pleaded generally, though challenges to capacity and denials of conditions precedent must be specific; the rule also covers insurance policies, written instruments (execution admitted unless denied by affidavit), official acts, judgments, statutes, special damages, foreign and tribal law (notice b","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.113. Form, Captioning, Signing, and Verifying of Documents","Michigan · civil rules","Form, captions, signing, and verification are governed by MCR 1.109(D) and (E). Allegations must be in numbered paragraphs limited to a single set of circumstances, separate claims and defenses must be in separate counts, and a written instrument underlying a claim or defense must be attached unless it is of public record in the county, held by the adverse party, inaccessible, or impractical to attach, as stated in t","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.114. Special Motions Under the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act","Michigan · civil rules","Governs special motions to dismiss eligible claims under the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (MCL 691.1853 et seq.), which must be filed within 60 days after service of the pleading asserting the claim (extendable for good cause). Filing stays all proceedings between the movant and responding party, including discovery, until the ruling and the time to appeal expire (with limited discovery allowed on a showi","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.115. Motion to Correct or to Strike Pleadings","Michigan · civil rules","Before responding, a party may move for a more definite statement of a vague or ambiguous pleading, and if the order is not obeyed within 14 days after notice (or another court-set time), the court may strike the pleading or enter another just order. The court may strike redundant, immaterial, impertinent, scandalous, or indecent matter, or a nonconforming pleading, on motion or on its own. This parallels Fed. R. Civ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.116. Summary Disposition","Michigan · civil rules","Parties may submit stipulated facts for judgment, and a party may move for dismissal or judgment on all or part of a claim or defense on 10 grounds: (C)(1) no personal jurisdiction, (2) insufficient process, (3) insufficient service, (4) no subject-matter jurisdiction, (5) lack of capacity, (6) another pending action on the same claim, (7) release, payment, prior judgment, immunity, limitations, statute of frauds, an","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.117. Appearances","Michigan · civil rules","A party appears by filing a notice or appearing in court (then promptly filing a written appearance), and an appearance alone neither confers nor enlarges jurisdiction but entitles the party to service. An attorney’s appearance counts as the party’s, a law firm filing is deemed the appearance of the first attorney filing and of every firm member, and an attorney may file a limited appearance under MRPC 1.2(b) definin","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.118. Amended and Supplemental Pleadings","Michigan · civil rules","A party may amend once as of course within 14 days after being served with a responsive pleading, or within 14 days after serving a pleading that needs no response; otherwise amendment requires leave, freely given when justice requires, or the adverse party’s written consent, and the court may condition leave on reimbursement of expenses caused by inexcusable delay. A party must respond to an amended pleading within ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.119. Motion Practice","Michigan · civil rules","Motions must be in writing, state their grounds, authority, and relief, and be signed; a motion or response raising an issue of law needs a brief, the combined motion and brief (or response and brief) may not exceed 20 double-spaced pages excluding exhibits, quotations and footnotes may be single-spaced, with at least 1-inch margins and type no smaller than 12-point, and reply or supplemental briefs are barred unless","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.201. Parties Plaintiff and Defendant; Capacity","Michigan · civil rules","Actions must be brought by the real party in interest, though representatives, trustees, contracting parties for another’s benefit, and statutorily authorized persons may sue in their own names, and suits to stop illegal state spending may be brought by a civic nonprofit or by at least 5 Michigan residents who own taxable property in their county. The rule sets the capacity of individuals, assumed-name businesses, pa","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.202. Substitution of Parties","Michigan · civil rules","If a party dies and the claim survives, the court may order substitution, but unless a motion is made within 91 days after filing and service of a statement noting the death, the action must be dismissed as to the deceased party unless later substitution would not prejudice anyone. Actions continue on transfer of interest unless the court orders substitution or joinder, actions involving public officers continue agai","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.203. Joinder of Claims, Counterclaims, and Cross-Claims","Michigan · civil rules","A pleader must join every claim it has against an opposing party arising from the same transaction or occurrence, unless adjudication would require third parties beyond the court’s jurisdiction, and may join any other claims. Counterclaims may exceed or differ from the opposing claim, cross-claims against co-parties must arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or property, and both must be filed with the answer ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.204. Third-Party Practice","Michigan · civil rules","Subject to MCL 500.3030, a defending party may bring in a nonparty who may be liable for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim without leave if the third-party complaint is filed within 21 days after its original answer, and otherwise by leave on motion with notice; the third-party summons is valid for 21 days unless the court orders otherwise. The third-party defendant answers within the MCR 2.108(A)(1)-(3) times, ma","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.205. Necessary Joinder of Parties","Michigan · civil rules","Subject to MCR 3.501, persons whose presence is essential for complete relief must be joined and aligned according to their interests, and if subject to jurisdiction the court must order them summoned. If they can be brought in only by consent, the court may proceed after weighing whether a valid judgment can be rendered without them, whether the plaintiff has another remedy, the prejudice to the defendant or absent ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.206. Permissive Joinder of Parties","Michigan · civil rules","Persons may join as plaintiffs or be joined as defendants if the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact, or if their presence will promote the convenient administration of justice; parties need not be interested in all relief, and judgment may be rendered as their rights are determined. The court may order separate trials or other orders to prevent emb","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.207. Misjoinder and Nonjoinder of Parties","Michigan · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped by court order on motion or on the court’s own initiative at any stage on just terms, and claims may be severed. When additional persons are needed for complete relief on a counterclaim or cross-claim, the court must order them brought in as defendants if jurisdiction can be obtained. This parallels Fed. R. Civ. P. 21.","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.209. Intervention","Michigan · civil rules","On timely application a person may intervene as of right when a Michigan statute or rule confers an unconditional right, when all parties stipulate, or when the person has an interest that the action may impair and that existing parties do not adequately represent; permissive intervention is allowed when a statute or rule confers a conditional right or a common question of law or fact exists, considering undue delay ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.221. Motion for Change of Venue","Michigan · civil rules","A motion for change of venue must be filed before or with the defendant’s answer, and an objection not timely raised is waived. A later motion may not be denied as untimely if the facts supporting it were not and could not with reasonable diligence have been known more than 14 days before the motion was filed.","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.222. Change of Venue; Venue Proper","Michigan · civil rules","When venue is proper, the court may change it only on a party’s motion, for the convenience of parties and witnesses or when an impartial trial cannot be had (and, for appellate review of agency proceedings, for attorneys’ convenience), and may separate and transfer improperly joined claims or retain the whole action. The moving party must pay the receiving court’s filing fee (unless waived under MCR 2.002) within 28","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.223. Change of Venue; Venue Improper","Michigan · civil rules","If venue is improper, the court must change it on a defendant’s timely motion, or may do so on its own after notice and an opportunity to be heard, and may transfer only to a county where venue is proper. The plaintiff must pay the filing fee and any compensation and attorney fees ordered for filing in the wrong court within 28 days of the transfer order or the receiving court will dismiss the action, and no proceedi","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.224. Change of Venue in Tort Actions (Repealed)","Michigan · civil rules","Repealed November 6, 1996, effective February 1, 1997.","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.225. Joinder of Party to Control Venue","Michigan · civil rules","On a defendant’s motion, venue must be changed if it is proper only because a codefendant was joined in bad faith to control venue. The plaintiff must pay the receiving court’s filing fee and any ordered expenses or attorney fees within 28 days of the transfer order or the receiving court will dismiss the action.","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.226. Change of Venue; Transfer of Jurisdiction; Orders","Michigan · civil rules","A court changing venue or transferring jurisdiction must enter its orders on an SCAO-approved form. If the order lacks the information needed to identify the rule under which transfer was ordered, the receiving clerk must refuse the transfer and return the case within 7 business days of receipt, and the transferring court must issue a proper order and retransfer within 7 business days.","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.227. Transfer of Actions on Finding of Lack of Jurisdiction","Michigan · civil rules","A court lacking subject-matter jurisdiction may transfer the action to a Michigan court that has jurisdiction where venue is proper, after notice and an opportunity to be heard if the court raises the issue itself; a circuit court may transfer to district court based on amount in controversy only on a good-faith stipulation or a legal certainty that the amount does not exceed the district court limit. The plaintiff m","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.228. Transfer to the Court of Claims","Michigan · civil rules","A notice of transfer to the Court of Claims must be filed before or with the defendant’s answer. Afterward, if the court has concurrent jurisdiction with the Court of Claims, the defendant needs leave, which may be granted if the supporting facts were not and could not with reasonable diligence have been known more than 14 days before the motion; if the Court of Claims has exclusive jurisdiction, MCR 2.227 governs.","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.301. Availability and Timing of Discovery","Michigan · civil rules","Where initial disclosures are required, a party may seek discovery only after serving its own initial disclosures; in district court, discovery before judgment requires leave of court or stipulation of all parties, and none is allowed in small claims or civil infraction actions. In circuit and probate court the discovery cutoff is set by scheduling order, and requests must be served early enough that the response is ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.302. Duty to Disclose; General Rules Governing Discovery","Michigan · civil rules","Without a request, a party must disclose the factual basis and legal theories of its claims and defenses, likely witnesses, documents and ESI, a damages computation, insurance agreements, and the subject areas of expected expert testimony, with extra disclosures in no-fault and personal-injury cases and 10 listed categories of exempt cases. Claimants must serve disclosures within 14 days after an opposing party answe","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.303. Depositions Before Action or Pending Appeal","Michigan · civil rules","A person expecting to be a party to a Michigan action may petition the circuit court of the county where an expected adverse party lives to take depositions to perpetuate testimony, serving notice at least 21 days before the hearing in the manner used for a summons. While an appeal is pending, or before one is taken while appeal time remains, the trial court may allow depositions to perpetuate testimony for possible ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.304. Persons Before Whom Depositions May Be Taken","Michigan · civil rules","In the United States, depositions may be taken before anyone authorized to give oaths, a court-appointed person, or a person the parties stipulate to; abroad, they may be taken on notice before an authorized person, before a court-commissioned person, or under a letter rogatory. Unless the parties agree otherwise, the officer may not be a relative, employee, or attorney of a party, a relative or employee of a party’s","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.305. Discovery Subpoena to a Non-Party","Michigan · civil rules","A represented party may subpoena a non-party for a deposition, documents, things, or entry on land once all parties have had a reasonable time to get counsel (or on court order), and an unrepresented party must ask the court to issue one; the subpoena must allow at least 14 days to comply unless the court shortens it, and a timely motion to quash stays compliance. A subpoena naming an organization as deponent must be","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.306. Depositions on Oral Examination of a Party","Michigan · civil rules","A plaintiff needs leave of court to depose before the defendant has had a reasonable time to get a lawyer, which is deemed to have passed once the defendant answers, appears, seeks discovery, files an MCR 2.116 motion, or 28 days have run after service; depositions of prisoners and state institution patients need leave, and any deposition is limited to one day of seven hours. Notice to an organizational deponent must","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.307. Depositions on Written Questions","Michigan · civil rules","A party may depose any person, including a party or an organization, on written questions served on all parties with a notice. Cross-questions are due within 14 days after service of the notice and questions, redirect questions within 7 days after cross-questions, and recross-questions within 7 days after redirect, unless the parties stipulate in writing or the court changes the times.","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.308. Use of Depositions in Court Proceedings","Michigan · civil rules","Depositions are admissible at trial, at motion hearings, or in interlocutory proceedings only as the Michigan Rules of Evidence allow. Errors in the notice, the officer’s qualifications, and curable defects in the manner of taking are waived unless promptly raised, objections to the form of written questions are waived unless served within the time for the next round of questions and within 7 days after the last ques","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.309. Interrogatories to Parties","Michigan · civil rules","Each separately represented party may serve no more than 20 interrogatories on each party, with each discrete subpart counted separately, and answers must be given separately and fully under oath. Answers and objections are due within 28 days after service, or for a defendant within 42 days after service of the summons and complaint, and a motion to compel must state that the movant conferred or tried to confer in go","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.310. Requests for Production of Documents and Other Things; Entry on Land","Michigan · civil rules","A party may request that another party produce documents, ESI, and tangible things or permit entry on land, and may make similar requests of a nonparty (through a subpoena under MCR 2.305). The written response is due within 28 days after service, or for a defendant within 42 days after service of the summons and complaint; if no ESI form is specified it must be produced as ordinarily kept or in a reasonably usable f","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.311. Physical and Mental Examination of Persons","Michigan · civil rules","When a party’s (or a person in a party’s custody’s) physical or mental condition, including blood group, is in controversy, the court may order an examination on motion for good cause with notice, specifying its time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner. On request the order may let the examinee’s attorney attend or require a mental examination to be video- or audio-recorded, and any recording must be unob","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.312. Request for Admission","Michigan · civil rules","Within the discovery period, a party may serve requests to admit facts, the application of law to fact, or the genuineness of documents, each clearly labeled as a Request for Admission. A matter is deemed admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 28 days after service, or for a defendant within 42 days after service of the summons and complaint (unless the court sets another time), and an admissi","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.313. Failure to Serve Disclosure or to Provide or to Permit Discovery; Sanctions","Michigan · civil rules","A party may move to compel initial disclosures, deposition answers, entity designations, interrogatory answers, inspection, attendance, or non-party subpoena compliance; evasive or incomplete responses count as failures, and the losing side may be ordered to pay reasonable expenses including attorney fees. Disobeying a discovery order can lead to facts being taken as established, exclusion of evidence, striking plead","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.314. Discovery of Medical Information Concerning Party","Michigan · civil rules","When a party’s mental or physical condition is in controversy, medical information about it is discoverable unless the party asserts a valid privilege, which must be raised in the initial disclosures, a written discovery response, at or before a deposition, or by protective-order motion, or it is waived for that action; a party who uses privilege to block discoverable medical information may not later offer evidence ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.315. Video Depositions","Michigan · civil rules","Depositions under MCR 2.303 and 2.306 may be video-recorded without leave or stipulation if the notice and subpoena say so, a digital clock showing hours, minutes, and seconds stays in the picture, and the opening, oath, and closing are made on camera. The recording must be sealed and kept by the operator, may be filed only on a party’s request with an affidavit of elapsed time and no alterations, and cannot be e-fil","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.316. Removal of Disclosure and Discovery Materials From File","Michigan · civil rules","Disclosure and discovery materials in civil court files may be removed and destroyed by stipulation, by court order on a finding they are no longer needed, or by the clerk. The clerk may start removal 18 months after judgment or dismissal if no appeal is taken, or 91 days after appellate proceedings end (unless remanded), giving at least 28 days’ notice during which the filing party may retrieve the materials or any ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.401. Pretrial Procedures; Conferences; Scheduling Orders","Michigan · civil rules","The court may call conferences at any time and may hold an early scheduling conference covering topics such as ADR, ESI, disclosure timing, expert limits, and settlement, then enter a scheduling order setting dates for ADR, amendments, motions, disclosures, discovery limits, discovery completion, witness lists, and pretrial or trial; if the order was entered without meaningful consultation, a party may request amendm","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.402. Use of Communication Equipment","Michigan · civil rules","The court may direct that conference telephones or similar audio equipment (not an audio-only connection to a video platform) be used for motion hearings and pretrial, scheduling, or status conferences, and with all parties’ consent or for good cause may take witness testimony that way, with a verbatim record still required. A party wanting to use such equipment must file a written request at least 7 days in advance,","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.403. Case Evaluation","Michigan · civil rules","The court may send any civil action seeking primarily money damages or property division to case evaluation unless the parties stipulate to another ADR process, which must finish within 60 days after discovery closes and, if there is no discovery plan, be submitted within 120 days of the first responsive pleading; a motion to remove a case must be filed within 14 days after notice of the referral and heard within 14 ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.404. Selection of Case Evaluation Panels","Michigan · civil rules","Each court using case evaluation must adopt a written selection plan by local administrative order and keep a public list of evaluators, who must have practiced law at least 5 years, be State Bar members in good standing, practice locally, and have devoted a substantial part of the last 5 years to civil litigation (at least 3 years in the specialty for specialized sublists). Applications are reviewed at least annuall","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.405. Offers to Stipulate to Entry of Judgment","Michigan · civil rules","Until 28 days before trial, any party may serve a written offer to stipulate to judgment for a sum certain (deemed to include accrued costs and interest); the offeree has 21 days to accept or make a counteroffer, and failure to accept within 21 days is a rejection. If the adjusted verdict (verdict plus interest and costs through the offer date) is more favorable to the offeror than the average of the offer and counte","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.406. Use of Facsimile Communication Equipment","Michigan · civil rules","Courts may allow documents to be filed by fax, but any filing fee must be paid or deposited in advance (except as MCR 2.002 provides), filings must be on 8½ by 11-inch paper in at least 12-point type, and courts may charge SCAO-scheduled fees and cap the number of pages. A fax received during regular business hours is filed that day if transmission begins during business hours and the whole document arrives, while la","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.407. Videoconferencing","Michigan · civil rules","Videoconferenced proceedings, which include audio-only connections to a video platform, follow SCAO standards, and courts may require participants to appear by video, but a participant may ask to appear in person, in which case the judge and that participant’s attorney must appear in person too while others may still appear remotely. The court must weigh 12 listed factors (such as prejudice, cross-examination, fundam","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.408. Use of Videoconferencing Technology in Civil Cases","Michigan · civil rules","A court may allow any participant to appear by video in any civil proceeding, but video may be used for trials or proceedings involving testimony or evidence only in the court’s discretion after notice and an opportunity to be heard. Video is presumed in circuit court for 10 listed proceedings, including civil pretrials, early scheduling conferences, discovery motions, adjournments, scheduling-order changes, motions ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.410. Alternative Dispute Resolution","Michigan · civil rules","All civil cases are subject to ADR (including settlement conferences, case evaluation, mediation, and other stipulated or local processes) unless a statute or rule says otherwise, and each court using ADR must adopt a written ADR plan that designates an ADR clerk and ensures access for indigent parties, who may not be ordered into a paid ADR process if free or low-cost services are unavailable. The court may order AD","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.411. Mediation","Michigan · civil rules","Parties may stipulate to any willing mediator, otherwise the court appoints one by rotation from its list, and the court may not influence the choice except on all parties’ request; mediators are disqualified on the same grounds as judges. The mediator must report completion to the court within 7 days, stating only the date, participants, whether the case settled, and whether further ADR is planned, and if the case s","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.412. Mediation Communications; Confidentiality and Disclosure","Michigan · civil rules","Mediation communications in cases referred under MCR 2.411 or 3.216 are confidential, not discoverable, not admissible, and may not be disclosed to non-participants except under 12 listed exceptions. These include written agreement of all parties, a statute or rule requiring disclosure, the mediator’s report, fee disputes, failure-to-attend issues, open sessions, program administration, threats or crimes, abuse or ne","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.420. Settlements and Judgments for Minors and Legally Incapacitated Individuals","Michigan · civil rules","A consent judgment, settlement, or settlement dismissal in an action brought for a minor or legally incapacitated individual (or involving their share of a wrongful-death recovery) must be approved as fair by the assigned judge, and in injury claims the person must appear unless excused for good cause; a guardian ad litem must be appointed if the next friend, guardian, or conservator will share in the recovery. If a ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.421. Notice of Bankruptcy Proceedings","Michigan · civil rules","A party who is a named debtor in a bankruptcy case must file notice of the bankruptcy in the state court action no later than 3 days after becoming subject to it and serve all other parties, and any other party may file and serve the notice if the debtor has not. The notice must give the debtor’s name, the bankruptcy court and case number, and, if available, the debtor’s bankruptcy attorney’s contact information. The","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.501. Scheduling Trials; Court Calendars","Michigan · civil rules","Unless a scheduling order already governs, the court must schedule a pretrial conference, ADR, or trial, or enter another order to prepare the case, and it may shorten the time to trial; child-custody contests and other cases given statutory or rule priority go first. Parties and attorneys get 28 days’ notice of trial assignments unless a rule or statute provides otherwise, the trial is an adjournment of an earlier d","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.502. Dismissal for Lack of Progress","Michigan · civil rules","The court may give notice that a case with no steps taken within 91 days will be dismissed for lack of progress unless the parties show progress is being made or the delay is not the fault of the party seeking relief; no notice may issue while a scheduling order’s deadlines are running or the case is set for a conference, ADR, hearing, or trial. Such a dismissal is without prejudice unless the court says otherwise, a","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.503. Adjournments","Michigan · civil rules","A request to adjourn a trial, ADR process, pretrial conference, or motion hearing must be made by written or on-the-record motion or stipulation for good cause, stating who asks, why, and how many prior adjournments were granted, and its title must say whether it is the first or a later request. Adjournment for a missing witness or evidence requires materiality and diligent efforts, and the court may impose costs and","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.504. Dismissal of Actions","Michigan · civil rules","A plaintiff may dismiss without a court order, on payment of costs, by notice before the defendant serves an answer or an MCR 2.116 motion, or by stipulation signed by all parties; this is without prejudice unless stated otherwise, but a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits. Otherwise dismissal at the plaintiff’s request needs a court order, and the court may dismiss or ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.505. Consolidation; Separate Trials","Michigan · civil rules","When pending actions share a substantial and controlling common question of law or fact, the court may hold a joint hearing or trial, consolidate them, and enter orders to avoid unnecessary cost or delay. The court may order separate trials of claims or issues for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or for expedition and economy. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 42(a), which requires only a common question, Michigan requires the ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.506. Subpoena; Order to Attend","Michigan · civil rules","The court, or an attorney of record or the clerk, may subpoena a party or witness to testify at trial or a hearing and to produce documents or tangible things (including electronically stored information), and the recipient may file written objections before the appearance time. Unless the court orders otherwise, the subpoena must be served at least 2 days before the appearance, or 14 days before when documents are r","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.507. Conduct of Trials","Michigan · civil rules","The party opening the evidence (usually the plaintiff) makes the first opening statement and the adverse party follows, and the defendant goes first only if the answer admits the plaintiff’s case and the defendant bears the burden on a defense. Only one attorney per party may examine a given witness unless the court allows otherwise, the court sitting without a jury may view the scene, and the party who opened the ev","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.508. Jury Trial of Right","Michigan · civil rules","A party must file a written jury demand, as a separate document with the jury fee paid, within 28 days after the answer or a timely reply is filed; another party may demand jury trial of remaining issues within 14 days after service of a partial demand. A timely demand carries over when a whole case is removed or transferred between district and circuit court (paying any jury-fee difference when going to circuit cour","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.509. Trial by Jury or by Court","Michigan · civil rules","Issues on which a jury was properly demanded must be tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or the court finds no jury right; all other issues are tried by the court, which may in its discretion order a jury anyway. The court may set the order of trial of jury and nonjury issues while preserving the jury right, and in nonjury matters may use an advisory jury or, with all parties’ consent, a jury whose","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.510. Juror Personal History Questionnaire","Michigan · civil rules","Each juror drawn must complete a questionnaire adopted by the state court administrator before being called, and refusing to answer or answering falsely is contempt of court. Only the judges, the clerk’s office, the parties and their attorneys in the juror’s cases, and others authorized by rule or order may see it, attorneys must get a reasonable chance to review questionnaires before challenges for cause, and questi","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.511. Impaneling the Jury","Michigan · civil rules","Prospective jurors are chosen by random blind draw or another fair method, and the court may examine them or let attorneys do so (allowing proper follow-up questions). The court may seat 7 or more jurors, but unless the parties stipulate that all may deliberate, names are drawn to reduce the jury to 6 before deliberations; retained alternates who later replace a juror require deliberations to start over. Challenges f","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.512. Instructions to Jury","Michigan · civil rules","Parties must file written instruction requests when the court directs, or otherwise by the close of evidence, and after the evidence each party must submit a written statement of the issues and may submit its theory of the case; the court must tell attorneys its rulings on requests before closing arguments. A party may claim instructional error only by objecting on the record before the jury retires (or before it res","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.513. Conduct of Jury Trial","Michigan · civil rules","After the jury is sworn the court must give oral and written preliminary instructions, including the elements of the claims and the burdens of proof, and must make all court–jury communications on the record with the parties notified. The court may allow juror notebooks, deposition summaries, sequenced expert testimony, juror note taking (notes destroyed after trial), juror questions to witnesses through the court, j","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.514. Rendering Verdict","Michigan · civil rules","Parties may stipulate to a jury of fewer than 6, a verdict by a stated majority, or deliberation by all jurors when more than 6 were seated; absent a stipulation, and except as MCR 5.740(C) provides, a verdict in a 6-juror civil case is received when 5 jurors agree. The verdict is returned in open court, any party may require a poll, and the jury is sent back if too few agree. The court may discharge a jury for accid","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.515. Special Verdicts","Michigan · civil rules","The court may require a special verdict of written findings on each fact issue, but must tell the attorneys before argument and settle the form on the record or in writing, then enter judgment on the findings. A party waives jury trial on a fact issue omitted from the form unless it demands submission before the jury retires, and the court may make or is deemed to have made a finding on that issue consistent with the","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.516. Motion for Directed Verdict","Michigan · civil rules","A party may move for a directed verdict at the close of the opponent’s evidence, stating specific grounds. If denied, the movant may still present evidence, and a denied motion does not waive jury trial even if all parties moved.","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.517. Findings by Court","Michigan · civil rules","In a bench trial or with an advisory jury, the court must find the facts specially and state conclusions of law separately, briefly and pertinently, on the record or in a written opinion; findings are not needed on motions unless a rule requires them, and no request or exception is needed to preserve review. A party may move within 21 days after entry of judgment to amend or add findings and amend the judgment, and s","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.518. Receipt and Return or Disposal of Exhibits","Michigan · civil rules","Trial evidence is submitted to the judge as exhibits rather than filed with the clerk, and admitted exhibits are not court records. At the end of a trial or hearing parties must retrieve their exhibits (weapons and drugs go back to the confiscating agency), and exhibits not retrieved within 56 days may be disposed of without notice. Confidential materials the court keeps must remain confidential.","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.601. Judgments","Michigan · civil rules","A final judgment may grant whatever relief the winning party is entitled to, even if not demanded in the pleadings. A default judgment may not differ in kind from or exceed the amount demanded unless notice was given under MCR 2.603(B)(1).","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.602. Entry of Judgments and Orders","Michigan · civil rules","Judgments and orders must be written, signed, and dated, the signing date is the entry date, and each judgment (and any order disposing of the last claim) must state just above the judge’s signature whether it resolves the last pending claim and closes the case. An order or judgment may be signed when relief is granted, on approval of form by all parties, or under the 7-day rule: within 7 days after the ruling (or la","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.603. Default and Default Judgment","Michigan · civil rules","The clerk must enter a default against a party who fails to plead or defend when that failure is known or shown by a verified request, notice must go to all appearing parties and the defaulted party, and the defaulted party may not proceed until the default is set aside. Notice of a default-judgment request must be served at least 7 days before entry if the defaulted party appeared, the relief differs from or exceeds","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.604. Judgment in Actions Involving Multiple Claims or Multiple Parties","Michigan · civil rules","An order resolving fewer than all claims or parties does not end the case, remains revisable until final judgment, and is not appealable as of right, though leave to appeal may be sought. Only in receivership and similar actions may the court make such an order final by expressly determining there is no just reason for delay. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 54(b), which allows partial final judgments in any case, Michigan lim","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.605. Declaratory Judgments","Michigan · civil rules","In an actual controversy within its jurisdiction, a Michigan court of record may declare the rights of an interested party whether or not other relief is available, and another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief. Jury trial may be demanded as otherwise allowed, the court may expedite the hearing, declaratory judgments are reviewable as final judgments, and further relief may be granted after reasonable n","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.610. Motion for Judgment Notwithstanding the Verdict","Michigan · civil rules","A party may move for JNOV within 21 days after entry of judgment, or within 21 days after the jury is discharged if no verdict was returned, and may join or alternatively request a new trial; a motion to set aside a verdict or for new trial is deemed to include a JNOV request. If JNOV is granted the court must also conditionally rule on any new-trial motion, and the party whose verdict was set aside must move for a n","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.611. New Trials; Amendment of Judgments","Michigan · civil rules","A new trial may be granted on all or some issues for listed grounds, including irregularity, misconduct, damages influenced by passion or prejudice, a verdict grossly inadequate or excessive or against the great weight of the evidence, newly discovered evidence, errors of law or fact, or an MCR 2.612 ground; in a bench trial the court may instead take testimony or amend findings and judgment. A motion for new trial o","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.612. Relief From Judgment or Order","Michigan · civil rules","Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time (or as MCR 7.208 allows once an appeal is pending), and a defendant subject to personal jurisdiction who did not actually know of the case may appear within 1 year after final judgment and seek relief if innocent third persons are not prejudiced. On motion the court may relieve a party from a final judgment for mistake or excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, fra","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.613. Limitations on Corrections of Error","Michigan · civil rules","An error in evidence rulings or other proceedings is not a ground to grant a new trial or disturb a judgment unless refusing to do so would be inconsistent with substantial justice. Only the judge who entered a judgment or order may set it aside or stay proceedings under it, unless that judge is absent or unable to act, and trial-court fact findings may be set aside only if clearly erroneous, with regard for the tria","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.614. Stay of Proceedings to Enforce Judgment","Michigan · civil rules","No execution or enforcement may occur until 21 days after a final judgment, and if a new-trial, reconsideration, or relief-from-judgment motion is filed and served within 21 days (or extended time allowed within that period), until 21 days after the order deciding it, though the court may enjoin property transfers meanwhile. TROs, preliminary injunctions, injunctive relief in final judgments, interlocutory receiversh","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.615. Enforcement of Tribal Judgments","Michigan · civil rules","Judgments, orders, subpoenas, and other judicial acts of a federally recognized tribe’s court are treated like those of a Michigan court of record, but only if the tribe has adopted a binding measure to enforce Michigan court acts and sent it to the State Court Administrative Office. Such tribal acts are presumed valid unless the objector shows lack of jurisdiction, fraud or coercion, lack of fair notice or hearing, ","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.620. Satisfaction of Judgment","Michigan · civil rules","A judgment may be shown satisfied in whole or part by filing a signed and acknowledged satisfaction from the judgment holder or its attorney, by paying the clerk the judgment, interest, and costs on a money-only judgment, or by motion for an order of satisfaction after the court hears proofs. The clerk must note satisfaction in the court records.","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.621. Proceedings Supplementary to Judgment","Michigan · civil rules","A party with a money judgment may, by motion or separate action, seek creditor’s-bill relief, supplementary relief under MCL 600.6101–600.6143, and other relief in aid of execution, with discovery available; a request to reach a debtor’s equitable interest must be verified, state the net amount due, and show equitable interests exceeding $100. Subpoenas or orders enjoining asset transfers must be served under MCR 2.1","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.622. Receivers","Michigan · civil rules","For good cause the court may appoint a receiver, a fiduciary for all parties, choosing someone with sufficient competence; a nominated receiver is appointed if no objection is filed within 14 days after service of the motion (or the parties stipulate), and parties may object to a court-chosen receiver within 14 days after service of the appointing order, with the court able to shorten these times. Disqualifications i","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.625. Taxation of Costs","Michigan · civil rules","Costs go to the prevailing party unless a statute or rule bars them or the court directs otherwise in writing, frivolous claims or defenses in actions filed on or after October 1, 1986 draw costs under MCL 600.2591, and the rule defines the prevailing party when there are several judgments, counts, or defendants or on circuit court review. A contract or tort plaintiff recovering less than $100 (unless reduced below $","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.626. Attorney Fees","Michigan · civil rules","An attorney fee award may include the time of a legal assistant who provided nonclerical legal support under an attorney’s supervision, if the assistant meets the criteria in Article 1, § 6 of the State Bar of Michigan bylaws.","#st-mi-rules"],["Rule 2.630. Disability of Judge","Michigan · civil rules","If, after a verdict or the filing of findings and conclusions, the trial judge cannot continue because of death, illness, or other disability, another judge of that court may perform the remaining duties. A substitute judge who is not satisfied that the duties can be performed may grant a new trial. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 63, which lets a successor judge proceed at any stage after certifying familiarity with the reco","#st-mi-rules"],["Appeals in Michigan: 21 days, a claim of appeal, and a lot of leave applications","Michigan · appeals","Civil appeals from the circuit court and the Court of Claims go to the Court of Appeals, and from there to the Michigan Supreme Court by discretionary application for leave to appeal. District and probate court decisions generally go first to the circuit court (MCR 7.101–7.123).\nThe defining number is 21. A claim of appeal from a final judgment must be filed within 21 days after entry, and the deadline is jurisdictio","#st-mi-appeals"],["Appeal of right from a final judgment: 21 days, jurisdictional","Michigan · appeals · Starting the appeal","An aggrieved party may appeal as of right from a final judgment or final order of the circuit court or Court of Claims, but not from a circuit-court judgment on appeal from another court. File a claim of appeal and the entry fee with the Court of Appeals within 21 days after entry. ‘Entry’ means the date the judgment is signed, or data entry in the register of actions. The time limit is jurisdictional, and MCR 1.108 ","#st-mi-appeals"],["What counts as ‘final’","Michigan · appeals · Starting the appeal","A final judgment or order is the first one that disposes of all claims and adjudicates the rights and liabilities of all parties. Also final: an order designated final under MCR 2.604(B) (receivership and similar actions), certain postjudgment custody orders, postjudgment orders awarding or denying attorney fees and costs, orders denying governmental immunity (under MCR 2.116(C)(7) or a (C)(10) motion based on immuni","#st-mi-appeals"],["What to file with the claim","Michigan · appeals · Starting the appeal","With the claim of appeal, file the judgment or order appealed from; the court reporter’s certificate (or a statement that the transcript was ordered or that there is none); proof of service; any bond; the lower court register of actions; and the jurisdictional checklist. Within the same 21 days, file a copy of the claim, any fee and bond, and the reporter’s certificate in the trial court, and serve all parties. Cases","#st-mi-appeals"],["Late service of the judgment","Michigan · appeals · Starting the appeal","If the judgment was served later than MCR 2.602 requires (7 days after signing), file an affidavit with the claim. The claim is deemed timely if the court finds service was late and the claim was filed within 14 days after service. The appellee may oppose by affidavit within 14 days. MCR 7.204(A)(3); MCR 2.602(E)(1)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Appellee appearance and docketing statement","Michigan · appeals · Starting the appeal","An appellee may file an appearance within 14 days after service of the claim; without one, the appellee is not entitled to notice. In all civil appeals the appellant must file a docketing statement within 28 days after the claim is filed, and failure may lead to dismissal. MCR 7.204(G)–(H)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Appeal by leave: interlocutory orders and everything else","Michigan · appeals · Starting the appeal","Leave is required for non-final orders of the circuit court and Court of Claims, for circuit-court judgments on appeal from other courts, for agency orders reviewable by the Court of Appeals, and when a timely appeal of right was missed. Applications are due 21 days after entry (or after an order deciding a timely postjudgment motion). The application must show, for an interlocutory order, how waiting for final judgm","#st-mi-appeals"],["Delayed application for leave: 6 months, discretionary","Michigan · appeals · Starting the appeal","If a civil appeal of right or application was not filed in time, a delayed application for leave may be filed within 6 months after entry of the judgment or order. It must explain the reasons for delay, and the court may weigh the length of and reasons for the delay. If the Court of Appeals dismisses a timely claim of appeal for lack of jurisdiction, a delayed application may also be filed within 21 days after the di","#st-mi-appeals"],["Only a motion filed within the first 21 days tolls","Michigan · appeals · Tolling and postjudgment motions","The appeal period runs from the order deciding a postjudgment motion for new trial, rehearing, reconsideration, or other relief only if the motion was filed within the initial 21-day appeal period, or within further time the trial court allowed for good cause during that period. MCR 7.204(A)(1)(d); MCR 7.205(A)(1)(b)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Reconsideration of the postjudgment ruling does not toll again","Michigan · appeals · Tolling and postjudgment motions","A motion for rehearing or reconsideration of an order deciding a tolling postjudgment motion does not extend the appeal time unless it was itself filed within the 21-day period. MCR 7.204(A)(2) (closing paragraph)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Trial-court motion deadlines that match","Michigan · appeals · Tolling and postjudgment motions","Motions for new trial or to alter or amend a judgment are due within 21 days after entry of judgment. Motions for reconsideration of a motion decision are also due within 21 days and must show palpable error. MCR 2.611(B); MCR 2.119(F)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Trial court loses power to amend once the appeal is filed","Michigan · appeals · Tolling and postjudgment motions","After a claim of appeal is filed or leave is granted, the trial court may not set aside or amend the judgment except by Court of Appeals order, by stipulation, after a merits decision in a case with a preliminary injunction, or as otherwise provided by law. MCR 7.208(A)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Cross-appeal within 21 days","Michigan · appeals · Cross-appeals","Any appellee may cross-appeal when an appeal of right is filed or leave is granted. In multi-party cases, any party may cross-appeal against any other party. File the claim of cross-appeal and the entry fee within 21 days after the claim of appeal is filed or served on the cross-appellant, whichever is later. If the case began by application, file within 21 days after the clerk certifies the order granting leave. MCR","#st-mi-appeals"],["Cross-appeal survives dismissal of the main appeal","Michigan · appeals · Cross-appeals","If the appellant abandons the appeal or it is dismissed, the cross-appeal continues. The cross-appellant must file a court reporter’s certificate within 21 days after the dismissal order if there is a record to transcribe. A cross-appellant also files its own docketing statement within 28 days, and a late cross-appeal requires an application under MCR 7.205. MCR 7.207(C)–(E); MCR 7.204(H)(3)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Transcripts: the appellant’s job","Michigan · appeals · Record and transcripts","The appellant must order the transcript and secure its filing. Court reporters have 91 days after ordering to file it in ordinary civil cases (shorter in custody and certain criminal appeals). Parties holding exhibits must file them with the trial court within 21 days after the claim of appeal. Within 21 days after the transcript is filed, the appellant serves the entire record on each appellee. MCR 7.210(B)(1), (B)(","#st-mi-appeals"],["No transcript available: settled record","Michigan · appeals · Record and transcripts","If transcripts are unavailable, the appellant moves in the trial court to settle the record with a proposed statement of facts within 14 days after filing the claim of appeal. Where only some transcripts are available, the deadline is 56 days after filing them (28 in custody and interlocutory criminal appeals). MCR 7.210(B)(2)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief: 56 days, extendable once by stipulation","Michigan · appeals · Briefs","In ordinary civil appeals the appellant’s brief is due 56 days after the latest of: the claim of appeal, certification of the order granting leave, filing of the transcript, or filing of a settled statement of facts. The parties may extend it 28 days by signed stipulation filed with the court, and the court may extend on motion. Custody and guardianship cases use 28 days, extendable only by motion. MCR 7.212(A)(1)(a)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief: 35 days; reply: 21 days","Michigan · appeals · Briefs","The appellee’s brief is due 35 days after service of the appellant’s brief (21 days in custody and similar cases), extendable 28 days by stipulation. A reply brief is due within 21 days after service of the appellee’s brief and is limited to rebuttal. A late brief forfeits oral argument, and filing a motion does not stay brief deadlines. MCR 7.212(A)(2), (A)(4)–(5), (G)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Length and form: 16,000 words; reply 3,200","Michigan · appeals · Briefs","Principal briefs are limited to 16,000 words and reply briefs to 3,200 words. The count includes the statement of facts, arguments, and relief, plus footnotes and text in embedded graphics. State the word count after the signature block. Use 1-inch margins, 12-point font, and 1.5-line spacing. Motions to exceed the limit are due with the brief and are disfavored. Self-represented filers without word processing get 50","#st-mi-appeals"],["Required contents","Michigan · appeals · Briefs","Required contents include a title page stating whether oral argument is requested, a table of contents, an index of authorities, a statement of the basis of jurisdiction with key dates (signing or entry, tolling motions), questions presented with the trial court’s answers, a statement of facts with record page cites, and arguments that each state the standard of review and show preservation. The appellee must say whe","#st-mi-appeals"],["Amicus","Michigan · appeals · Briefs","Amicus briefs on the merits need a motion granted by the court, filed within 21 days after the appellee’s brief, with no fee. Government entities listed in the rule are exempt from the motion requirement. MCR 7.212(H)","#st-mi-appeals"],["No execution for 21 days; appeal alone does not stay","Michigan · appeals · Stays and bonds","Enforcement is automatically stayed for 21 days after a final judgment, and through decision of a timely postjudgment motion plus 21 days. After that, an appeal does not stay enforcement unless a court orders it. Injunctions and receivership orders are enforceable immediately unless the court orders otherwise. MCR 2.614(A); MCR 7.209(A)(1), (E)(1)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Money judgments: 110% bond buys an automatic stay","Michigan · appeals · Stays and bonds","Except in domestic relations matters, filing a bond under MCR 3.604 of at least 110% of the judgment, including costs, interest, attorney fees, and sanctions to date, stays a money judgment automatically. The trial court may also grant a stay with no bond or a reduced bond, or accept other security such as an irrevocable letter of credit. A motion for bond or stay must be decided by the trial court before it can be b","#st-mi-appeals"],["Governmental immunity and anti-SLAPP appeals stay the case","Michigan · appeals · Stays and bonds","An appeal from denial of governmental immunity automatically stays all proceedings. Under the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act, an appeal from an order ruling on a special motion stays all proceedings between all parties until the appeal concludes. MCR 7.209(A)(1); MCR 2.614(D); MCL 691.1854(3); MCR 2.114(C)(4)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Answers to motions","Michigan · appeals · Motions, decisions, and reconsideration","Answers to motions to dismiss, remand, or affirm are due 21 days after service. Answers to motions for peremptory reversal are due in 35 days. Answers to motions for reconsideration, stay, to strike, for amicus filing, or for abeyance are due in 14 days. A motion for immediate consideration can shorten the schedule. MCR 7.211(B)(2), (C)(6)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Applications are decided on the papers","Michigan · appeals · Motions, decisions, and reconsideration","There is no oral argument on an application for leave. The court may grant, deny, enter final relief, or request more record. If leave is granted, the case proceeds as an appeal of right: cross-appeal, docketing statement (28 days), and reporter certificate (14 days) deadlines run from certification of the grant. Unless ordered otherwise, the appeal is limited to the issues in the application. Answers to applications","#st-mi-appeals"],["Reconsideration: 21 days, 3,200 words, no second try","Michigan · appeals · Motions, decisions, and reconsideration","A motion for reconsideration of a Court of Appeals opinion or order may be filed within 21 days after the order or the date stamped on the opinion, in a single document of no more than 3,200 words. Answers are due within 14 days and are limited to 2,500 words. Late motions and motions to reconsider a denial of reconsideration are not accepted. A chief-judge dismissal for lack of jurisdiction has its own 21-day recons","#st-mi-appeals"],["Publication and precedent","Michigan · appeals · Motions, decisions, and reconsideration","Unpublished opinions are not binding. Any party may ask, by letter within 21 days after release, that an opinion be published, and other parties may respond within 14 days. A panel must follow published Court of Appeals decisions issued on or after November 1, 1990; a panel that disagrees must say so, which can trigger a conflict-panel poll. MCR 7.215(C)–(D), (J)","#st-mi-appeals"],["When the judgment takes effect","Michigan · appeals · Motions, decisions, and reconsideration","A Court of Appeals judgment takes effect after the time to apply to the Supreme Court expires, or after Supreme Court disposition if an application is filed, unless the court orders immediate effect. Enforcement proceeds in the trial court after the record is returned. MCR 7.215(E)–(F)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Discretionary application for leave","Michigan · appeals · Review in the Michigan Supreme Court","The Supreme Court reviews Court of Appeals cases by application for leave to appeal. Grounds include a substantial question about the validity of a legislative act, significant public interest in a case involving the state, a legal principle of major significance to state jurisprudence, or a Court of Appeals decision that is clearly erroneous and will cause material injustice or conflicts with Supreme Court or other ","#st-mi-appeals"],["Timing: 42 days civil; 28 days interlocutory; bypass","Michigan · appeals · Review in the Michigan Supreme Court","Applications are due 42 days in civil cases (56 in criminal) after the Court of Appeals decision, its order denying a timely reconsideration motion, or its grant of publication. Applications from non-final Court of Appeals orders, including orders granting leave, are due within 28 days. A bypass application before Court of Appeals decision is due within 42 days after the Court of Appeals filing. The answer is due 28 ","#st-mi-appeals"],["Remand decisions","Michigan · appeals · Review in the Michigan Supreme Court","If the Court of Appeals remands, a party may apply within 42 days (civil) after the remand decision or after the decision following remand, and then may raise all original issues. An application stays remand proceedings only when the remand decision is a judgment under MCR 7.215(E)(1), unless ordered otherwise. MCR 7.305(C)(5)–(6)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Briefing after leave is granted","Michigan · appeals · Review in the Michigan Supreme Court","Briefing follows the order granting leave. Calendar cases: appellant 56 days, appellee 35 days after service, reply 21 days. Cases argued on the application: 42, 21, and 14 days. Form and length follow MCR 7.212, and an argument over 6,500 words on one issue needs a summary of argument. MCR 7.312(A)–(B), (E)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Rehearing","Michigan · appeals · Review in the Michigan Supreme Court","A motion for rehearing of a Supreme Court opinion is due within 21 days after the opinion is filed and is limited to 16,000 words. It is subject to the palpable-error standard of MCR 2.119(F)(3). MCR 7.311(F)(1)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Certified questions and other jurisdiction","Michigan · appeals · Review in the Michigan Supreme Court","The Supreme Court may also answer certified questions, issue advisory opinions on request, and exercise superintending control. MCR 7.303(B)(3)–(5); MCR 7.308","#st-mi-appeals"],["Interlocutory orders: leave required, show substantial harm","Michigan · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","Non-final circuit court orders are reviewable only by leave, applied for within 21 days. The application must show how waiting for final judgment would cause substantial harm. A motion for immediate consideration is available when a decision is needed by a date certain. MCR 7.203(B)(1); MCR 7.205(A)(1), (B)(1), (F)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Partial judgments","Michigan · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","An order adjudicating fewer than all claims or parties is not final, and the jurisdictional statement must show why the court has jurisdiction. MCR 2.604(B) designations of finality are limited to receivership and similar actions. MCR 7.202(6)(a)(i)–(ii); MCR 7.212(C)(4)(b); MCR 2.604","#st-mi-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP special motions: appeal of right","Michigan · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","The Uniform Public Expression Protection Act gives the moving party an appeal of right from an order denying a special motion in whole or in part, due within 21 days. MCR 2.114(E) extends the moving party’s appeal of right to orders ‘denying or granting, in whole or in part,’ filed under MCR 7.204. MCL 691.1859; MCR 2.114(E)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Governmental immunity denials are appealable of right","Michigan · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","An order denying governmental immunity to a governmental party under MCR 2.116(C)(7), or a (C)(10) motion based on immunity, is a ‘final order,’ and the appeal automatically stays the case. MCR 7.202(6)(a)(v); MCR 7.209(A)(1)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Business court assignment orders are not appealable","Michigan · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","An order about assigning a case to the business court may not be appealed to the Court of Appeals. MCR 7.203(D)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Filing date and e-filing","Michigan · appeals · E-filing and computation","In the Court of Appeals, the ‘date of filing’ is the date the clerk receives the document or, if e-filed, the date it is deemed filed under MCR 1.109(G)(5)(b). Submissions by 11:59 p.m. on a business day count that day; weekend, holiday, and court-closed submissions count the next business day. Attorneys must e-file the required case types where e-filing is implemented. MCR 7.202(2), (4); MCR 1.109(G)(3)(f), (G)(5)(b","#st-mi-appeals"],["Computing time","Michigan · appeals · E-filing and computation","Exclude the trigger day and include the last day, unless it falls on a Saturday, Sunday, legal holiday, or court-closed day, in which case the deadline moves to the next such day that is open. MCR 1.108(1)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Claim of appeal (civil appeal of right)","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from entry of the final judgment or order, or entry of an order deciding a timely postjudgment motion.\nJurisdictional. File in the Court of Appeals with the entry fee. MCR 7.204(A)(1)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Application for leave to appeal (Court of Appeals)","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from entry of the order or judgment, or of an order deciding a timely postjudgment motion.\nJurisdictional. MCR 7.205(A)(1)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Delayed application for leave (civil)","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","6 months\nDiscretionary; explain the delay. MCR 7.205(A)(4)(a)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Delayed application after dismissal of a timely claim for lack of jurisdiction","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from entry of the dismissal order or order denying reconsideration. MCR 7.205(A)(4)(b)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Tolling postjudgment motion (new trial, reconsideration, relief)","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from entry of judgment.\nMust be filed within the initial 21-day period to toll. MCR 7.204(A)(1)(d); MCR 2.611(B); MCR 2.119(F)(1)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Late-served judgment: claim of appeal","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the judgment (if served late under MCR 2.602).\nRequires an affidavit showing late service. MCR 7.204(A)(3)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Appellee appearance","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the claim of appeal.\nOptional, but needed to receive notice. MCR 7.204(G)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Docketing statement","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from filing of the claim of appeal (or cross-appeal, or certification of order granting leave).\nCivil appeals only. MCR 7.204(H); MCR 7.205(E)(3)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Claim of cross-appeal","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from later of filing or service of the claim of appeal; or certification of order granting leave. MCR 7.207(B)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Answer to application for leave (Court of Appeals)","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from service of the application. MCR 7.205(C)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief (ordinary civil)","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","56 days from latest of claim of appeal, certified leave order, transcript filing, or settled statement.\nOne 28-day extension by stipulation; more by motion. Custody/guardianship: 28 days. MCR 7.212(A)(1)(a)(iii)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief (ordinary civil)","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","35 days from service of appellant’s brief.\nOne 28-day extension by stipulation. MCR 7.212(A)(2)(a)(ii)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Reply brief","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from service of appellee’s brief.\n3,200 words. MCR 7.212(G)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Motion for reconsideration (Court of Appeals)","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from date of the order or date stamped on the opinion.\n3,200 words; late motions not accepted. MCR 7.215(I)(1)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Request to publish an opinion","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from release of the unpublished opinion (or denial of rehearing). MCR 7.215(D)(1)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Supreme Court application (civil)","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","42 days from court of Appeals decision, order denying timely reconsideration, or grant of publication.\n56 days in criminal cases. MCR 7.305(C)(2)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Supreme Court application from interlocutory Court of Appeals order","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from the Court of Appeals order. MCR 7.305(C)(3)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Supreme Court bypass application","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","42 days from filing of claim, application, or original action in the Court of Appeals. MCR 7.305(C)(1)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Answer to Supreme Court application","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from service of the application.\nReply within 21 days after service of the answer (MCR 7.305(E)). MCR 7.305(D)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Supreme Court motion for rehearing","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from filing of the opinion.\n16,000 words. MCR 7.311(F)(1)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP (UPEPA) appeal by moving party","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from entry of the order on the special motion.\nAppeal of right; all proceedings stayed. MCL 691.1859; MCR 2.114(E)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Appeal of right to circuit court (from district/probate court or agency)","Michigan · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from entry of the judgment, order, or decision, or order deciding a timely postjudgment motion.\nJurisdictional; unless a statute sets another time. MCR 7.104(A)","#st-mi-appeals"],["Minnesota Supreme Court","Minnesota · courts","Reviews Court of Appeals decisions only by discretionary petition for review (Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 117) and may take a case early by accelerated review (Rule 118). Reviews the Tax Court and the Workers’ Compensation Court of Appeals directly by certiorari (Rule 116). Hears argued cases en banc (Rule 135). Controls bar admission, pro hac vice certification through the Board of Law Examiners, and lawyer discipline.","#st-mn"],["Minnesota Court of Appeals","Minnesota · courts","Hears appeals of right from district court judgments and the appealable orders listed in Rule 103.03, discretionary appeals under Rule 105, certiorari review of agency decisions, and declaratory challenges to administrative rules (Rule 114). Sits in panels of at least three judges. Issues precedential, nonprecedential, or order opinions; nonprecedential opinions are persuasive only (Rule 136.01). No petitions for reh","#st-mn"],["District Court","Minnesota · courts","The general trial court. Civil practice is governed by the Rules of Civil Procedure plus the statewide General Rules of Practice (Gen. R. Prac. 101–146 for civil cases), which also set motion deadlines, scheduling, ADR, and e-filing. Districts may have local rules only as approved by the Supreme Court (Minn. R. Civ. P. 83). Attorneys must e-file and e-serve statewide (Gen. R. Prac. 14.01(b)(1)).","#st-mn"],["Tax Court and Workers’ Compensation Court of Appeals","Minnesota · courts","Their decisions go straight to the Supreme Court by writ of certiorari (Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 116), and nonappealable orders may be reviewed by discretionary petition (Rule 105.01). Conciliation court actions and appeals where no jury is demanded, and other listed special proceedings, are carved out of the general civil scheduling rule (Gen. R. Prac. 111.01).","#st-mn"],["Statute: Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (UPEPA), Minn. Stat. §§ 554.07–554.20","Minnesota · anti-SLAPP","Minnesota enacted UPEPA in 2024 (2024 Minn. Laws ch. 123, art. 18) and repealed the old anti-SLAPP law, Minn. Stat. §§ 554.01–554.06. The act took effect the day after final enactment (the codified savings clause puts that date at May 25, 2024) and applies to civil actions pending on or filed after that date, but it does not affect a cause of action asserted before May 25, 2024, or a motion under the old law about th","#st-mn-slapp"],["Scope and the long list of exclusions","Minnesota · anti-SLAPP","Covers a cause of action based on the defendant’s (1) communication in a governmental proceeding, (2) communication on an issue under review in a governmental proceeding, or (3) exercise of speech, press, assembly, petition, or association rights under the U.S. or Minnesota Constitution on a matter of public concern.\nMinnesota added exclusions beyond the uniform act: claims against government units; certain public-he","#st-mn-slapp"],["Procedure: 60 days to move, automatic stay, 60-day hearing and ruling clocks","Minnesota · anti-SLAPP","File the special motion for expedited relief within 60 days after being served with the pleading that asserts the claim, or later for good cause (§ 554.09).\nFiling stays all other proceedings between the movant and the responding party, including discovery, until the ruling and the time to appeal it run out; the court may stay matters involving other parties on motion. Limited discovery is allowed only if specific in","#st-mn-slapp"],["Fees are mandatory for a prevailing movant","Minnesota · anti-SLAPP","The court shall award court costs, reasonable attorney fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion to a prevailing movant. A prevailing responding party gets fees only if the motion was frivolous or filed solely to delay.\nA plaintiff cannot dodge fees by dismissing: a voluntary dismissal without prejudice does not defeat the movant’s right to a ruling and fees, and a dismissal with prejudice counts","#st-mn-slapp"],["Appeal: 30 days, as of right, and the whole case stops","Minnesota · anti-SLAPP","A moving party may appeal as of right from an order denying the motion in whole or in part. The appeal must be filed within 30 days after entry of the order, half the usual 60-day civil appeal period under Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01. While the appeal is pending, all proceedings between all parties are stayed (§ 554.10(c)). The statute grants the special appeal of right only to the moving party; a responding party w","#st-mn-slapp"],["Pro hac vice in district court: Board certification first, then a motion by Minnesota counsel","Minnesota · out of state","A lawyer admitted in another jurisdiction who is retained for a particular case may appear pro hac vice in the court’s discretion on written application if: (1) the Minnesota Board of Law Examiners certifies the lawyer’s good standing and that the lawyer is not suspended or disbarred anywhere for discipline or disability in lieu of discipline; (2) the lawyer pays a non-refundable $450 fee to the Board; (3) a Minnesot","#st-mn-oos"],["Application and motion papers","Minnesota · out of state","Apply electronically to the Board of Law Examiners with the fee and a certificate of good standing from the licensing authority where admitted (Rule 5.03).\nThen an active Minnesota lawyer who is attorney of record moves the admission, served on all parties, with (1) the applicant’s affidavit or declaration listing every Minnesota pro hac vice application in the past two years (caption, venue, file number, outcome) an","#st-mn-oos"],["Fee waivers are narrow","Minnesota · out of state","The $450 fee is waived only for (1) pro bono representation of a person of limited means where the lawyer will not charge or seek fees, and (2) lawyers representing a federal, state, or local government entity. No other waivers may be requested or granted, including for related cases sharing common questions.\nPro hac vice is not needed where another rule exempts it, for example a foreign-case deposition subpoena unde","#st-mn-oos"],["Pro hac vice lawyers must register for e-filing and are subject to Minnesota discipline","Minnesota · out of state","The out-of-state lawyer is bound by all rules for Minnesota lawyers, including e-filing and the Registered User requirements of Gen. R. Prac. 14.02(a); if e-service on that lawyer is unavailable, service on Minnesota co-counsel is service on the party. Applying or appearing submits the lawyer to the Minnesota Rules of Professional Conduct, the Rules on Lawyers Professional Responsibility, and Minnesota court jurisdic","#st-mn-oos"],["Appellate courts: separate pro hac vice motion","Minnesota · out of state","No attorney may sign appellate pleadings or argue in the Court of Appeals or Supreme Court unless licensed in Minnesota or admitted pro hac vice by that court. A Minnesota-licensed lawyer moves the admission, with the applicant’s affidavit of good standing in another state or territory. The appellate rule names no fee. An amicus brief may be filed only by an attorney admitted under this rule (Rule 129.01(a), amended ","#st-mn-oos"],["Temporary practice without admission (MRPC 5.5) and house counsel","Minnesota · out of state","A lawyer admitted elsewhere and not disbarred or suspended may provide temporary services in Minnesota in association with an actively participating Minnesota lawyer; in or related to a pending or potential proceeding where the lawyer is or expects to be authorized; in ADR related to the home practice; or in matters for family members or reasonably related to the home practice.\nMinnesota-specific: an out-of-state law","#st-mn-oos"],["Full admission without exam: 36 of 60 months","Minnesota · out of state","Admission without examination requires active licensed practice in good standing for at least 36 of the 60 months before applying, with at least 1,000 hours a year in listed practice roles. Alternatively, transfer a scaled MBE score of 145+ (from a passed exam, then admitted) or a Legacy UBE score of 260+, within 36 months of the exam. The NextGen UBE transfer score has not yet been set. Fee: $1,250 (Rule 12F).\nLimit","#st-mn-oos"],["Governing code: Minnesota Rules of Professional Conduct","Minnesota · ethics","Effective September 1, 1985, amended through July 1, 2026 in the Revisor’s compilation. The text tracks the ABA Model Rules numbering but has real substantive departures, listed below. Choice of law follows ABA Rule 8.5(b). A lawyer providing or offering legal services in Minnesota is subject to Minnesota discipline even if not admitted here (Rule 8.5(a)). Minn. R. Prof. Conduct 8.5","#st-mn-ethics"],["Fees: no ‘nonrefundable’ fees; advance fees go in trust unless a signed flat-fee agreement says otherwise","Minnesota · ethics","Fee payments received before services are rendered are presumed unearned and must be held in trust. A flat fee may be treated as the lawyer’s property on payment only under a written agreement signed by the client that discloses five listed items, including that the fee will not be held in trust and that the client may be entitled to a refund. Availability (true retainer) fees need a signed writing. Fee agreements ma","#st-mn-ethics"],["Confidentiality: broader permissive disclosure","Minnesota · ethics","Rule 1.6(b) lets a lawyer reveal client information in 11 situations. Unlike the ABA rule, these include: (2) non-privileged information the client has not asked to keep inviolate, if disclosure would not embarrass or likely harm the client; (4) to prevent a fraud using the lawyer’s services that will cause substantial financial injury, or to prevent the commission of a crime; (5) to rectify the consequences of a cli","#st-mn-ethics"],["Conflicts: narrower lateral screening; loan guarantees; sexual relations","Minnesota · ethics","Lateral screening under Rule 1.10(b) cures a former-firm conflict only if confidential information the lawyer received is unlikely to be significant in the new matter, the lawyer is screened, and affected clients get timely notice. The ABA rule allows screening without the ‘unlikely to be significant’ condition.\nRule 1.8(e)(3) lets a lawyer guarantee a loan a client reasonably needs to withstand litigation delay, if ","#st-mn-ethics"],["Advocacy rules that differ: ex parte writings, trial publicity, witness requests","Minnesota · ethics","Rule 3.5(g) allows a written communication on the merits to the judge if a copy is promptly delivered to opposing counsel, or an oral one on adequate notice. Rule 3.6 limits trial-publicity restrictions to extrajudicial statements likely to materially prejudice a jury trial in a pending criminal matter. It does not reach civil cases. Rule 3.4(f) (asking non-clients not to talk to the other side) follows the ABA text.","#st-mn-ethics"],["Misconduct: harassment and discrimination, and fee-arbitration awards","Minnesota · ethics","Rule 8.4(g) bars harassment based on listed characteristics, including status with regard to public assistance and marital status, in connection with professional activities. Rule 8.4(h) bars discriminatory acts prohibited by federal, state, or local statute or ordinance that reflect adversely on fitness, judged by four listed factors. These differ from ABA Model Rule 8.4(g).\nRule 7.2(c) restricts claims of specialis","#st-mn-ethics"],["Hip-pocket service: the action starts at service, and you must file within one year or lose it with prejudice","Minnesota · land mine","A Minnesota civil action is commenced by service of the summons (or a signed waiver, or delivery to the sheriff followed by service within 60 days), not by filing. A case that is not filed with the court within one year after commencement against any party is deemed dismissed with prejudice against all parties, unless all parties signed a stipulation within that year to extend. Family cases are excepted. Calendar the","#st-mn-unique"],["Do not plead a dollar figure over $50,000","Minnesota · land mine","If unliquidated damages over $50,000 are sought, the complaint must state only that reasonable damages greater than $50,000 are sought. Amounts under $50,000 must be stated. Minn. R. Civ. P. 8.01","#st-mn-unique"],["Appeals from orders run from service of notice of filing, not entry","Minnesota · land mine","Appeal a judgment within 60 days after its entry. Appeal an appealable order within 60 days after any party serves written notice of its filing; the court administrator’s own notice does not start the clock (Minn. R. Civ. P. 77.04). If you won, serve notice of filing to start the appeal period. Once the time to appeal the judgment expires, earlier orders can no longer be appealed (Rule 104.02). Minn. R. Civ. App. P. ","#st-mn-unique"],["A Rule 60 motion tolls the appeal time only if filed within new-trial time, and a premature notice of appeal is void","Minnesota · land mine","Timely motions under Rules 50.02, 52.02, 59, and (if filed within the new-trial deadline) 60 restart the appeal period from service of notice of filing of the order deciding the last motion. A notice of appeal filed while such a motion is pending is ‘premature and of no effect’; you must file a new one. A new-trial motion must be served within 30 days after a general verdict or service of notice of filing of the deci","#st-mn-unique"],["Use the notice to remove early or lose it","Minnesota · land mine","The peremptory notice to remove must be served and filed within 10 days after notice of the assigned judge. It cannot be used against a judge who has already ruled on a motion or presided at another proceeding you had notice of. Minn. R. Civ. P. 63.03","#st-mn-unique"],["Anti-SLAPP: 60 days to move, 30 days to appeal","Minnesota · land mine","The UPEPA motion is due within 60 days after service of the pleading (later only for good cause), and an appeal from denial must be filed within 30 days after entry of the order, not the general 60 days. Fees to a prevailing movant are mandatory, and a plaintiff’s voluntary dismissal does not avoid them. Minn. Stat. §§ 554.09, 554.13(b)–(c), 554.15, 554.16","#st-mn-unique"],["No discovery until the Rule 26.06 conference and plan","Minnesota · land mine","Parties may not seek discovery from any source before conferring and preparing a discovery plan, except in exempt proceedings or by stipulation or order. The conference must happen within 30 days from the initial answer due date, and the written plan must be filed within 14 days after the conference. Early Rule 34 requests may be delivered 21 days after service but are deemed served at the conference. Initial disclos","#st-mn-unique"],["Dispositive motions: 28 days’ notice, and summary judgment cutoff","Minnesota · land mine","Dispositive motion papers (notice, motion, proposed order, affidavits, memorandum) must be served and filed at least 28 days before the hearing, or the hearing may be canceled. Unless the court orders otherwise, no summary judgment motion may be filed more than 30 days after the close of all discovery. Summary judgment memoranda need a record-cited statement of undisputed facts. Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 115.03, 115.06; Mi","#st-mn-unique"],["Pro hac vice is a two-step process with a fee and local counsel at the first appearance","Minnesota · land mine","Out-of-state lawyers cannot sign or argue until admitted. First get Board of Law Examiners certification and pay the $450 non-refundable fee; then Minnesota counsel moves admission with a two-year history of Minnesota pro hac vice applications. Minnesota counsel must co-sign everything and attend at least the first appearance. Appellate courts need their own pro hac vice motion. Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 5.01–5.04; Minn. R","#st-mn-unique"],["Restricted identifiers in a filing can get it rejected or struck","Minnesota · land mine","Social security, taxpayer ID, and financial account numbers (other than the last four digits of an account) may not appear in filed documents; use Confidential Information Form 11.1 or a properly designated non-public filing. Non-compliant filings may be rejected; if one slips through, the administrator restricts it and you have 3 days to fix it or it is struck. Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 11.02, 11.05; Minn. R. Civ. P. 5.04","#st-mn-unique"],["No rehearing in the Court of Appeals; petition for review in 30 days","Minnesota · land mine","The Court of Appeals does not entertain rehearing petitions. Seek Supreme Court review by a petition filed within 30 days of the filing of the decision, limited to 2,000 words, with a $550 fee. The appellate courts may not extend this deadline. Rehearing exists only in the Supreme Court (14 days), and not from denial of review. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 117, subds. 1, 3; 126.02; 140.01","#st-mn-unique"],["Motions to reconsider are prohibited without permission","Minnesota · land mine","A district-court motion to reconsider may be brought only with the court’s express permission, granted on compelling circumstances. Ask by letter of no more than two pages, served on all parties. Such a request is not one of the post-decision motions that toll the appeal period under Rule 104.01, subd. 2. Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 115.11; Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subd. 2","#st-mn-unique"],["Notice to remove: one peremptory removal as of right, within 10 days","Minnesota · judges","Any party or attorney may serve and file a notice to remove the assigned judge or judicial officer within 10 days after receiving notice of who will preside at the trial or hearing, and no later than the start of the trial or hearing. No cause is needed.\nThe right is lost against a judge who has already presided at a motion or other proceeding of which the party had notice, and against a judge assigned by the Chief J","#st-mn-unique"],["Removal for actual prejudice or bias","Minnesota · judges","No judge may sit if disqualified under the Code of Judicial Conduct (Minn. R. Civ. P. 63.02). A motion to remove for actual prejudice or bias is heard first by the challenged judge. If denied, a motion to the chief judge (or a designee) for reconsideration must be filed and served within 7 days of the order. Minn. R. Civ. P. 63.02; Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 106","#st-mn-unique"],["Referees and court-selected neutrals","Minnesota · judges","Object to having a referee hear a contested matter within 14 days of notice of the assignment, and before any hearing begins (Gen. R. Prac. 107). A party may remove a court-appointed ADR neutral, without consent, by notice within 7 days of the appointment; a substitute neutral can be removed only on a showing of prejudice (Gen. R. Prac. 114.04(c)). Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 107, 114.04(c)","#st-mn-unique"],["Appellate recusal motions: short fuses and panel review","Minnesota · judges","Supreme Court: move within 14 days after the case-initiating filing or the order granting review; the justice decides within 3 days after the response is due, and a denial can be reviewed within 5 days by a randomly drawn three-member panel of acting justices. Court of Appeals: move within 7 days after the notice of oral argument or nonoral conference (or of a panel change); review of a denial within 5 days goes to t","#st-mn-unique"],["UPEPA must be read broadly, with media and consumer-review carve-backs","Minnesota · speech","Minnesota’s anti-SLAPP act must be broadly construed to protect speech, press, assembly, petition, and association. Its commercial-speech, fraud, and consumer-protection exclusions do not apply to news-gathering, journalism, or creative and audiovisual works, or to consumer opinions, reviews, and ratings of businesses. Minn. Stat. §§ 554.08(a)(1), (d), 554.17","#st-mn-unique"],["Lawyer speech: publicity rule reaches only criminal jury matters","Minnesota · speech","Minnesota’s trial-publicity rule restricts only extrajudicial statements by lawyers in criminal matters that would likely prejudice a jury trial. Civil litigators are not covered by Rule 3.6, though other rules (for example 3.5, 4.4, 8.4(d)) still apply. Minn. R. Prof. Conduct 3.6","#st-mn-unique"],["No recording in courtrooms; remote hearings may not be recorded","Minnesota · speech","No visual or audio recording (including livestreaming) is allowed in courtrooms or courthouse areas during trials and hearings except as the rule permits, and a remote video hearing may not be recorded or broadcast unless the presiding judge specifically authorizes it. Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 4.01","#st-mn-unique"],["Statewide motion practice rules replace most local rules","Minnesota · unique rules","Dispositive motions: serve and file 28 days before the hearing; response 14 days; reply 7 days. Nondispositive: 21/14/7. Memoranda are capped at 35 pages, including any reply. The parties must meet and confer before any motion is heard. Motions to reconsider are prohibited without permission, sought by a letter of no more than 2 pages. Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 115.03–115.05, 115.10, 115.11","#st-mn-unique"],["Personal leave continuance is automatic","Minnesota · unique rules","A timely application by an attorney substantially involved in the case for up to 90 days of leave (own health condition, birth or adoption, caring for a seriously ill family member, or a death in the family) is granted automatically, with client informed consent. An objection must be brought by motion within 14 days. Discovery is suspended during the leave unless ordered otherwise. A parallel appellate rule exists, b","#st-mn-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope of rules; proportionality","Minnesota · civil rules","Govern civil suits in the district courts, except as stated in Rule 81, and are to be construed for the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action. Adds an express duty of the court and parties to keep process and costs proportionate to the amount in controversy and the complexity and importance of the issues.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","Minnesota · civil rules","There is one form of action, the ‘civil action.’","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 3.01. Commencement of the action","Minnesota · civil rules","An action is commenced against each defendant when the summons is served on that defendant, when the defendant signs a Rule 4.05 waiver, or when the summons is delivered to the sheriff of the defendant’s county (by mail, courier, or agreed electronic means) if service or first publication follows within 60 days. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 3, filing does not commence the action; filing within one year is required by Rule ","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 3.02. Service of complaint","Minnesota · civil rules","A copy of the complaint must be served with the summons, except for service by publication under Rule 4.04.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 4.01. Summons; form","Minnesota · civil rules","The summons states the court and parties, is signed by plaintiff or counsel, gives a U.S. address for service on the signer, states the time to answer, and warns of default. A Minnesota address is no longer required (2021).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 4.02. By whom served","Minnesota · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, the sheriff or any nonparty at least 18 years old may serve a summons or other process.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 4.03. Personal service within the state","Minnesota · civil rules","Individuals: personal delivery or abode service on a person of suitable age and discretion residing there; extra steps for persons in state institutions, judicial branch employees (via the court administrator or Clerk of Appellate Courts), and minors under 14 (also serve a parent or custodian). Partnerships and associations: a member or managing agent. Corporations: an officer, managing agent, or authorized or statut","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 4.04. Service by publication; out-of-state and foreign service","Minnesota · civil rules","Publication confers jurisdiction only in listed cases (absconding residents, quasi in rem after attachment or garnishment, dissolution when ordered, property in Minnesota, mortgage or lien foreclosure). It requires filing the complaint and an affidavit, three weeks’ published notice, and is complete 21 days after first publication. Personal service outside the state has the same effect as publication. Service abroad ","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 4.041. Real property description in publication","Minnesota · civil rules","Publication in actions affecting real property must describe the property and state the object of the action.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 4.042. Complaint after publication","Minnesota · civil rules","If a defendant served by publication appears within 14 days after service is complete, the plaintiff must serve the complaint within 7 days, and the defendant then has at least 21 days to answer.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 4.043. Defense after publication; restitution","Minnesota · civil rules","A defendant served by publication without actual notice may be allowed to defend before judgment, and (except in dissolution) within one year after judgment, on just terms, with restitution if the defense succeeds.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 4.044. Nonresident landowner’s agent","Minnesota · civil rules","If a nonresident landowner appoints an agent under Minn. Stat. § 557.01, serve the agent or principal under Rule 4.03; publication against the principal is not allowed.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 4.05. Waiving service of summons","Minnesota · civil rules","Plaintiff may mail a waiver request with the complaint and two copies of Form 22B, giving 30 days (60 if abroad) to return it. A U.S. defendant who refuses without good cause must pay later service costs and fees for the motion to collect them. A defendant who timely waives has 60 days from the request (90 if abroad) to answer. The action is treated as served on the date the waiver is signed. Waiver does not waive ju","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 4.06. Return; proof of service","Minnesota · civil rules","Proof is by the sheriff’s certificate, the server’s affidavit, the written admission of the party served, or for publication the printer’s affidavit, stating time, place, and manner. Failing to file proof does not invalidate service.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 4.07. Amendment of process","Minnesota · civil rules","The court may allow process or proof of service to be amended unless substantial rights of the person served would be prejudiced.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 5.01. Service of later papers; appearance","Minnesota · civil rules","Orders required to be served, later pleadings, written motions (other than ex parte), and notices, demands, offers of judgment, and similar documents must be served on every party. Parties in default for failure to appear need not be served except with new claims, which require Rule 4 service. A party appears when it serves or files any document.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 5.02. How service is made; e-service","Minnesota · civil rules","Serve the attorney of a represented party unless the court orders otherwise. Where Gen. R. Prac. 14 requires or permits e-service, use the E-Filing System. Otherwise deliver (hand, office, or abode) or mail to the last known address. Mail service is complete on mailing; e-service through the court system is complete on transmission. Gen. R. Prac. 14.01(c) gives relief for technical failures.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 5.03. Numerous defendants","Minnesota · civil rules","The court may order that defendants’ pleadings need not be served on one another, with cross-claims and affirmative matter deemed denied.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 5.04. Filing; one-year deadline; certificate of service; rejection","Minnesota · civil rules","(a) An action not filed with the court within one year after commencement against any party is deemed dismissed with prejudice as to all parties, unless the parties sign a stipulation to extend within that year; family cases are excluded. (b) Later documents must be filed within a reasonable time after service with a certificate of service signed under oath or penalty of perjury; discovery requests, responses, disclo","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 5.05. Filing by fax","Minnesota · civil rules","Where e-filing is not required, documents may be faxed to the court on approved equipment; filing is complete on receipt. Within 7 days, send a $25 transmission fee per 50 pages, bulky exhibits, and filing fees. Keep the original.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 5.06. Filing electronically","Minnesota · civil rules","Where required or authorized by Supreme Court order or Gen. R. Prac. 14, an e-filed document is deemed filed when transmitted to the E-Filing System, and is date-stamped if accepted by the administrator.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 5A. Notice of constitutional challenge","Minnesota · civil rules","A party challenging a federal or state statute where the U.S. or the State is not a party must promptly file a notice of constitutional question and serve it with the challenging document by U.S. mail on the U.S. Attorney General or Minnesota Attorney General.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 6.01. Computing time","Minnesota · civil rules","Exclude the trigger day, count all days, include the last day unless it is a weekend or legal holiday. Periods under 7 days exclude weekends and holidays only if another rule expressly says so. Hour periods count every hour. If the administrator’s office is inaccessible, time extends. For e-filing, the last day ends at 11:59 p.m. Minnesota time. Legal holidays include days the U.S. mail does not operate. Add 3 days a","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 6.02. Enlarging time","Minnesota · civil rules","The court may extend time for cause on request made before the period expires, or after it expires on motion showing excusable neglect. It may not extend time under Rules 4.043, 59.03, 59.05, or 60.02 except as those rules allow.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 6.03. Expiration of term","Minnesota · civil rules","A term of court’s expiration does not affect time periods or the court’s power.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 6.04. Motion deadlines","Minnesota · civil rules","Deadlines for serving and filing motions and supporting or opposing papers are set by the General Rules of Practice (see Gen. R. Prac. 115). Rule 6.05 is abrogated.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 7.01. Pleadings allowed","Minnesota · civil rules","Complaint and answer; reply to a counterclaim denominated as such; answer to a cross-claim; third-party complaint and answer. The court may order a reply to an answer. Demurrers and pleas are abolished.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 7.02. Motions and other documents","Minnesota · civil rules","Motions must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state grounds with particularity, and state the relief sought. Rule motions require notice and hearing unless the rule allows ex parte relief. Parties may agree to written submission; the court may hear motions by telephone. Form and Rule 11 signing rules apply.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 8.01. Claims for relief; damages over $50,000","Minnesota · civil rules","A short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment; if money is sought the amount must be stated. For unliquidated damages under $50,000 state the amount; for more than $50,000, state only that reasonable damages greater than $50,000 are sought. The federal rule has no such cap on stating amounts.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 8.02. Defenses; form of denials","Minnesota · civil rules","State defenses in short and plain terms and admit or deny averments; lack of knowledge has the effect of a denial; denials must fairly meet the substance; general denials only if all averments are controverted in good faith.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 8.03. Affirmative defenses","Minnesota · civil rules","Must be pleaded affirmatively, including accord and satisfaction, arbitration and award, assumption of risk, contributory negligence, discharge in bankruptcy, duress, estoppel, failure of consideration, fraud, illegality, injury by fellow servant, laches, license, payment, release, res judicata, statute of frauds, statute of limitations, and waiver. Misdesignated defenses or counterclaims are treated as properly desi","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 8.04. Effect of failure to deny","Minnesota · civil rules","Averments other than the amount of damage are admitted if not denied where a response is required; otherwise deemed denied.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 8.05. Concise pleading; alternative and inconsistent claims","Minnesota · civil rules","Averments must be simple, concise, and direct. Alternative, hypothetical, and inconsistent claims or defenses are allowed, subject to Rule 11.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 8.06. Construction","Minnesota · civil rules","Pleadings are construed to do substantial justice.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","Minnesota · civil rules","Capacity need not be pleaded; challenge it by specific negative averment (9.01). Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity; state of mind generally (9.02). Conditions precedent may be pleaded generally but denied specifically (9.03). Official acts and ordinances by title and date (9.04); judgments without jurisdictional facts (9.05). Time and place are material (9.06). Special damages must be specifically ","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","Minnesota · civil rules","Caption with court, county, title, file number, Rule 7 designation, and, in the upper right corner, the case type from the State Court Administrator’s Case Type Index; name the assigned judge if one is assigned for all purposes (10.01). Pseudonyms or initials only if authorized by law or order. Numbered paragraphs and separate counts (10.02); adoption by reference and exhibits (10.03). A filing without the case type ","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 11.01. Signature","Minnesota · civil rules","Every pleading and motion must be signed by an attorney of record or the self-represented party, with address, phone, e-mail, and attorney registration number. Unsigned documents are stricken unless promptly corrected. Filing through the E-Filing System certifies compliance with signature rules.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 11.02. Representations to court","Minnesota · civil rules","Presenting a document certifies, after reasonable inquiry, that it has no improper purpose, legal contentions are warranted, factual contentions and denials have or will likely have support, and (Minnesota addition) the document contains no restricted identifiers and these have been submitted confidentially under Gen. R. Prac. 11. A motion about restricted identifiers need not wait the 21-day safe harbor.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 11.03. Sanctions","Minnesota · civil rules","Sanctions motions must be separate and served but not filed unless the challenged matter is not withdrawn or corrected within 21 days. The court may act on its own by show-cause order. Sanctions are limited to deterrence; no monetary sanctions against a represented party for legal contentions, or on the court’s initiative after voluntary dismissal or settlement. Tracks Fed. R. Civ. P. 11(c).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 11.04. Discovery excluded","Minnesota · civil rules","Rule 11 does not apply to discovery requests, responses, objections, and motions under Rules 26–37.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 12.01. When to answer","Minnesota · civil rules","Answer within 21 days after service of the summons (unless Rule 4.043 applies); answer a cross-claim and reply to a counterclaim within 21 days. If a Rule 12 motion is denied or postponed, respond within 14 days after notice of the court’s action; after a more definite statement, within 14 days.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 12.02. Defenses by motion","Minnesota · civil rules","Lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction, insufficient process or service, failure to state a claim, and failure to join a Rule 19 party may be raised by motion before pleading. A failure-to-state motion that relies on matters outside the pleadings becomes a Rule 56 motion. Minnesota’s list omits improper venue (the rules do not change venue; Rule 82).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 12.03. Judgment on the pleadings","Minnesota · civil rules","Any party may move after pleadings close, without delaying trial; outside matters convert it to summary judgment.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 12.04. Preliminary hearing","Minnesota · civil rules","Rule 12.02 and 12.03 defenses are decided before trial on application unless deferred.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 12.05. More definite statement","Minnesota · civil rules","Available for vague pleadings or Rule 10.02 violations before responding; if the order is not obeyed within 14 days after notice, the court may strike the pleading.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 12.06. Motion to strike","Minnesota · civil rules","Move before responding (or within 21 days after service if no response is permitted); the court may strike sham pleadings and insufficient, redundant, immaterial, impertinent, or scandalous matter.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 12.07–12.08. Consolidation and waiver of defenses","Minnesota · civil rules","Omitting an available Rule 12 defense from a Rule 12 motion bars a later motion on it. Personal jurisdiction, process, and service defenses are waived if omitted from a Rule 12 motion or from the answer (or a Rule 15.01 amendment as of course). Failure to state a claim and failure to join an indispensable party survive to trial; lack of subject-matter jurisdiction is never waived.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaims and cross-claims","Minnesota · civil rules","Compulsory counterclaims arising out of the same transaction must be pleaded unless they require absent parties or were already pending (13.01). Permissive counterclaims are allowed (13.02) and may exceed the claim (13.03). The rules do not enlarge counterclaims against the State (13.04). Later-acquired and omitted counterclaims need leave (13.05, 13.06). Cross-claims against co-parties arising from the same transact","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","Minnesota · civil rules","A defendant may serve a third-party summons and complaint on one who may be liable to it, but needs consent of all parties or leave of court if it files the third-party complaint more than 90 days after it was served with the summons. Serve the plaintiff’s complaint with it and copies on all parties. 14.02–14.08 track Fed. R. Civ. P. 14 on third-party defendants’ claims and defenses, the plaintiff’s claims, motions t","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 15.01. Amendments","Minnesota · civil rules","Amend once as of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 21 days after service if no response is allowed and the case is not on the trial calendar. Otherwise by leave (freely given) or written consent. Respond to an amended pleading within the time left or 14 days after service, whichever is longer. Differs from the federal 21-days-after-response window.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 15.02. Amendments to conform to the evidence","Minnesota · civil rules","Issues tried by consent are treated as pleaded; amendment may be allowed on objection unless prejudice is shown, with a continuance available.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 15.03. Relation back","Minnesota · civil rules","Amendments arising from the same conduct relate back. A change of party relates back if, within the limitations period, the new party had notice so as not to be prejudiced and knew or should have known that but for a mistake about identity it would have been sued.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 15.04. Supplemental pleadings","Minnesota · civil rules","On motion and reasonable notice, the court may permit supplemental pleadings about later events.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 16. Pretrial conferences; scheduling","Minnesota · civil rules","The court may hold pretrial conferences (16.01). On any party’s written request it must enter a scheduling order limiting time to join parties, amend, hear motions, and complete discovery; a schedule may be modified only for good cause (16.02). Subjects include issue simplification, admissions, Rule 702 limits, summary adjudication, discovery, ADR, and time limits (16.03). Final pretrial conference attended by trial ","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 17. Real party in interest; capacity; guardians ad litem","Minnesota · civil rules","Actions are prosecuted by the real party in interest, with time allowed for ratification or substitution (17.01). Minors and incompetent persons sue through a representative or a court-appointed guardian ad litem, who must be a Minnesota resident, file a consent and oath, and post any required bond; a minor over 14 may apply; applications must be under oath with listed contents; notice is mandatory where the applican","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims and remedies","Minnesota · civil rules","Any number of claims may be joined. Claims normally requiring a prior judgment, such as setting aside a fraudulent conveyance, may be joined with the money claim.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 19. Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","Minnesota · civil rules","Join a person needed for complete relief or whose interest may be impaired or who exposes a party to inconsistent obligations (19.01). If joinder is not feasible, the court weighs prejudice, shaping of relief, adequacy, and the plaintiff’s alternative remedy (19.02). Pleadings must name unjoined Rule 19 persons and reasons (19.03). Subject to Rule 23 (19.04).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder","Minnesota · civil rules","Plaintiffs or defendants may be joined for claims arising from the same transaction or series with a common question; the court may order separate trials or other protective orders.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder","Minnesota · civil rules","Misjoinder is not ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped and claims severed at any stage.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","Minnesota · civil rules","A stakeholder exposed to multiple liability may interplead claimants, including by cross-claim or counterclaim, and a defendant who deposits the fund may be discharged.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 23.01–23.04. Class actions: prerequisites, types, certification, conduct","Minnesota · civil rules","Numerosity, commonality, typicality, and adequacy, plus one of three types (risk of inconsistent adjudications, injunctive or declaratory relief, or predominance and superiority). Certify at an early practicable time, define the class, and appoint class counsel. Best practicable notice with opt-out for 23.02(c) classes; issue classes and subclasses; orders on conduct of the action.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 23.05. Class settlement; residual funds","Minnesota · civil rules","Settlement, voluntary dismissal, or compromise needs court approval after notice and a fairness hearing, disclosure of side agreements, possible second opt-out, and objections withdrawn only with approval. Minnesota-specific: before distributing residual funds, the court must give notice to potential recipients and to the State Board of Civil Legal Aid and set a deadline for statements (amended July 1, 2025).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 23.06. Class-certification appeals","Minnesota · civil rules","The Court of Appeals may permit an appeal from an order granting or denying certification; apply within the Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 105 time. No automatic stay.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 23.07–23.10. Class counsel, fees, derivative and association actions","Minnesota · civil rules","Appointment criteria for class counsel and interim counsel (23.07); attorney fee motions with notice, objections, and findings (23.08); derivative actions require contemporaneous ownership, a particularized demand allegation, adequacy, and court approval of dismissal or compromise (23.09); actions by or against unincorporated associations (23.10).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","Minnesota · civil rules","Intervention of right for an impaired, inadequately represented interest (24.01) or permissive for a common question (24.02). Minnesota procedure: serve and file a notice of intervention with a pleading; if no party objects within 30 days after service, intervention is accomplished. If someone objects, the intervenor must serve a motion within 30 days after service of the objection (24.03). With all parties’ written ","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","Minnesota · civil rules","On death, the court may substitute proper parties on motion served on parties under Rule 5 and on nonparties under Rule 4; no fixed time limit appears in the rule (25.01). Incompetency (25.02), transfer of interest (25.03), and public officers who leave office (25.04, requiring a showing of substantial need).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 26.01. Required disclosures","Minnesota · civil rules","Initial disclosures (witnesses, documents, damages computation, insurance) are due within 60 days after the original answer due date unless otherwise set; parties joined later have 30 days. Many proceedings are exempt (family, Torrens, conciliation appeals, harassment, name changes, defaults, and others). Expert disclosures with signed reports for retained experts, absent order, at least 90 days before trial (rebutta","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 26.02. Scope and limits of discovery","Minnesota · civil rules","Nonprivileged matter relevant to any claim or defense and proportional to the needs of the case. Limits for inaccessible ESI, cumulative or duplicative discovery. Insurance coverage discoverable. Work product and statement rules. Expert discovery by interrogatory, with further discovery on motion; consulting experts only under Rule 35.02 or exceptional circumstances. Privilege logs and clawback of inadvertently produ","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 26.03. Protective orders","Minnesota · civil rules","On motion and good cause, the court may limit or condition discovery, including trade secret protection and sealed filings; expenses under Rule 37.01(d).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 26.04. Timing and sequence of discovery","Minnesota · civil rules","No discovery from any source until the parties confer and prepare the Rule 26.06 discovery plan, except in exempt proceedings or by stipulation or order. Rule 34 requests may be delivered more than 21 days after service but are deemed served at the conference. Methods may be used in any sequence. Expedited-track rules may modify timing.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 26.05. Supplementation","Minnesota · civil rules","Supplement disclosures and responses in a timely manner when materially incomplete or incorrect; expert supplementation by the pretrial-disclosure date.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 26.06. Discovery conference and plan","Minnesota · civil rules","Confer as soon as practicable and within 30 days from the initial answer due date; file a written discovery-plan report within 14 days after the conference or when the action is filed, whichever is later. The plan addresses disclosures, subjects and phasing, ESI, privilege, limits, and orders. The court must hold a discovery conference on a qualifying motion; objections to the motion within 14 days.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 26.07. Signing disclosures and discovery","Minnesota · civil rules","Every disclosure, request, response, and objection must be signed with address, e-mail, and phone; signing certifies completeness and propriety. Improper certification requires sanctions.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions before action or pending appeal","Minnesota · civil rules","A verified petition in the county of an expected adverse party’s residence, with notice served at least 21 days before the hearing, may obtain an order to perpetuate testimony (27.01). The district court may allow depositions pending appeal (27.02). Independent actions remain available (27.03).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions are taken","Minnesota · civil rules","Officers authorized to administer oaths or court appointees in the U.S. (28.01); treaties, letters of request, notice, or commission abroad (28.02); no officer who is a relative, employee, attorney, or financially interested, or who has a contract that tends to affect impartiality (28.03).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 29. Discovery stipulations","Minnesota · civil rules","Parties may stipulate to deposition procedures and modify discovery limits, but extensions of Rules 33, 34, or 36 response times that would interfere with discovery, motion, or trial dates need court approval.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 30.01. When depositions may be taken","Minnesota · civil rules","After service of the summons, any party may depose any person. The plaintiff needs leave to depose within 30 days after service on any defendant, unless a defendant has sought discovery. Note Rule 26.04(a)’s bar on discovery before the discovery conference.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 30.02. Notice; recording; remote depositions; organizations","Minnesota · civil rules","Reasonable written notice stating the deponent, place, and whether in person or by remote technology; objections to the in-person or remote choice require a meet-and-confer, and the objector must show good cause. Audio, video, or stenographic recording at the noticing party’s cost; additional recording methods allowed. Organizational depositions require a duty to confer on topics. A remote deposition is taken where t","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 30.03. Examination; objections noted","Minnesota · civil rules","Examination proceeds as at trial except Evidence Rules 103 and 615; objections are noted and testimony is taken subject to them; written questions may be sent through the noticing party.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 30.04. Objections; 7-hour limit; termination","Minnesota · civil rules","Objections must be concise and non-argumentative; instruct not to answer only to preserve privilege, enforce a court limit, or move to terminate. One day of seven hours unless extended. Sanctions for impeding. Motion to terminate or limit for bad faith or harassment.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 30.05. Review by witness","Minnesota · civil rules","If requested before the deposition ends, the deponent has 30 days after notice that the transcript is available to review and sign a statement of changes.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 30.06–30.07. Certification, custody, and failure to attend","Minnesota · civil rules","The officer certifies and sends the deposition to the attorney who arranged it, who must keep it safe (it is not filed); exhibits may be annexed. Expenses may be awarded if the noticing party fails to attend or fails to subpoena a nonparty who then does not appear.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions on written questions","Minnesota · civil rules","Leave is needed if the witness is in prison or already deposed without stipulation. Cross questions within 14 days after service; redirect within 7 days; recross within 7 days.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 32. Use of depositions","Minnesota · civil rules","Usable as admissible under the Evidence Rules against parties present or noticed: for impeachment; a party’s or its designee’s deposition by an adversary for any purpose; any witness’s if dead, more than 100 miles away or out of state, unable to attend, unsubpoenable, or in exceptional circumstances; and with rule-of-completeness rights (32.01). Objections at trial (32.02); jury trials use nonstenographic form on req","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories","Minnesota · civil rules","No more than 50 interrogatories, counting each subpart, without leave on motion and good cause. Answer or object within 30 days after service, but a defendant has at least 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Objections must be specific; answers are signed under oath or penalty of perjury with the prescribed declaration, date, county, and state, and must restate each interrogatory. Contention interroga","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 34. Production and entry on land","Minnesota · civil rules","Requests for documents, ESI, things, or entry may be served with or after the summons and complaint (but see Rule 26.04(b) on early requests). Respond within 30 days after service (or deemed service). State whether materials are withheld on each objection; produce as kept or by category; ESI in the specified, ordinary, or reasonably usable form. Nonparties by Rule 45 subpoena.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical, mental, and blood examinations","Minnesota · civil rules","On motion and good cause where condition is in controversy (35.01); exchange of reports and waiver of privilege by requesting one (35.02). Placing one’s condition in controversy waives medical privilege (35.03). Minnesota-specific: within 14 days of a written request, the waiving party must provide medical reports and signed authorizations for records; treating or examining experts may be deposed only by court order ","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","Minnesota · civil rules","Matters are admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days after service, but a defendant need not respond before 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Lack of knowledge requires reasonable inquiry. Motions to test sufficiency. Admissions are conclusive unless withdrawn on motion, and only for the pending action.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 37.01. Motions to compel","Minnesota · civil rules","Motions to a party go to the court where the action is pending; to a nonparty, to the court in the county where discovery is taken. A good-faith conference certification is required. Evasive answers are treated as failures. Expenses to the winner unless substantially justified.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 37.02. Failure to comply with an order","Minnesota · civil rules","Contempt by the deposition-county court; issue, preclusion, striking, stay, dismissal, default, and contempt by the trial court, plus mandatory expenses unless substantially justified.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 37.03. Failure to disclose, supplement, or admit","Minnesota · civil rules","Undisclosed information or witnesses are excluded unless substantially justified or harmless, with expenses, jury instruction, or other sanctions. Costs of proving matters wrongly not admitted, subject to listed exceptions.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 37.04. Failure to attend own deposition or respond","Minnesota · civil rules","Sanctions and mandatory expenses; the failure is not excused because the discovery was objectionable unless a protective order was sought.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 37.05. Lost ESI","Minnesota · civil rules","If ESI that should have been preserved is lost through unreasonable steps and cannot be replaced: on prejudice, cures no greater than necessary; only on intent to deprive, adverse presumption or instruction, dismissal, or default. Tracks Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(e).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 37.06. Discovery-plan failure","Minnesota · civil rules","Expenses for failing to participate in good faith in the Rule 26.06 plan.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury trial of right","Minnesota · civil rules","Issues of fact in actions for money only or specific property are tried to a jury unless waived or referred (38.01). In contract actions, and with permission in others, waiver is by failing to appear, written consent filed, or oral consent in open court. Failing to file a jury demand or pay a jury fee is not a waiver (38.02). Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 38, no written demand is required to preserve the right.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by court; advisory jury; preliminary instructions; openings","Minnesota · civil rules","Non-jury issues are tried by the court (39.01); advisory or consensual juries (39.02); preliminary instructions after the jury is sworn (39.03); plaintiff opens first and others may reserve (39.04).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 40. Assignment of cases for trial","Minnesota · civil rules","Judges set trial calendars by order or rule.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 41.01. Voluntary dismissal","Minnesota · civil rules","Without order by notice before service of an answer or summary judgment motion, or by stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared; without prejudice unless stated, but a second notice dismissal of the same claim is an adjudication on the merits. Otherwise by court order; a pending counterclaim must be able to remain.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 41.02. Involuntary dismissal","Minnesota · civil rules","For failure to prosecute or to comply with the rules or orders; motion for dismissal after plaintiff rests in a bench trial (with findings). Unless the order says otherwise, dismissals other than for lack of jurisdiction, forum non conveniens, or failure to join an indispensable party operate on the merits.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 41.03–41.04. Counterclaims; costs of prior action","Minnesota · civil rules","Dismissal rules apply to counterclaims, cross-claims, and third-party claims. A plaintiff who refiles may be ordered to pay costs of the dismissed action, with a stay until paid.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials","Minnesota · civil rules","Consolidation or joint hearings for common questions (42.01); separate trials for convenience, prejudice, or economy (42.02).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 43. Taking of testimony","Minnesota · civil rules","Testimony is oral in open court unless otherwise provided (43.01); affirmation in lieu of oath (43.04); motions may be heard on affidavits or oral testimony (43.05); court-appointed interpreters paid from public funds (43.07). Rules 43.02, 43.03, and 43.06 are abrogated.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 44. Proof of official records","Minnesota · civil rules","Domestic records by official publication or attested copy with custody certificate; foreign records by attested copy with final certification (excusable for good cause) or treaty method; certificate of no record; other lawful methods. Rule 44.04 is abrogated.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 45.01. Subpoena form and issuance","Minnesota · civil rules","Issued in the name of the court where the action is pending, by the administrator (signed in blank) or by an attorney as officer of the court. Must notify the recipient of the right to compensation. Any use other than to compel trial attendance without prior notice to all parties is improper and sanctionable.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 45.02. Service of subpoena","Minnesota · civil rules","Any nonparty 18 or older; personal or abode delivery and tender of one day’s fees and mileage (not required for the State). Document subpoenas require notice to all parties at least 7 days before production. Statewide service. Reasonable compensation arrangements before compliance.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 45.03. Protecting subpoenaed persons","Minnesota · civil rules","Duty to avoid undue burden. Written objections to production within 14 days after service (or before compliance if sooner) stop production absent an order. The court must quash if the subpoena requires a nonparty to travel outside the county where the person resides, works, or does business (except statewide for trial), seeks privileged matter, or imposes undue burden. Nonparty professional witnesses are entitled to ","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 45.04. Duties in responding","Minnesota · civil rules","Produce as kept or by category; ESI form rules; inaccessible ESI showing. The issuing party must share produced materials with all parties and give 7 days’ notice of rescheduled production. Privilege logs and clawback.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 45.05. Contempt","Minnesota · civil rules","Unexcused failure to obey may be contempt; a subpoena exceeding Rule 45.03(c)(1)(B) territorial limits is an adequate excuse.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 45.06. Interstate depositions and discovery","Minnesota · civil rules","Minnesota’s version of the Uniform Interstate Depositions and Discovery Act: submit the foreign subpoena to the administrator in the county where discovery is sought, or a Minnesota attorney may issue it. Submitting is not an appearance but subjects the filer to Minnesota jurisdiction and the Rules of Professional Conduct. Minnesota law governs compliance; motions go to that county’s district court.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 46. Exceptions unnecessary","Minnesota · civil rules","Formal exceptions are not needed; making the objection and grounds known suffices.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 47. Jurors","Minnesota · civil rules","The court or the parties may conduct voir dire, with supplementation allowed; supplemental questionnaires are not public unless admitted (47.01). The jury may separate overnight during deliberations (47.03). Jurors may be excused for good cause (47.04). Rule 47.02 (alternates) is abrogated.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 48. Number of jurors; unanimity","Minnesota · civil rules","Six to twelve jurors, all participating in the verdict unless excused. Unless law or stipulation provides otherwise, the verdict must be unanimous and may not be taken from fewer than six jurors.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 49. Special verdicts and interrogatories","Minnesota · civil rules","Special verdicts; omitted issues are waived unless a jury submission is demanded before retirement. Minnesota-specific: in comparative-fault cases under Minn. Stat. ch. 604, the court informs the jury of the effect of its fault answers and lets counsel comment, unless that would mislead (49.01(b)). General verdicts with interrogatories (49.02).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 50. Judgment as a matter of law; conditional new-trial rulings","Minnesota · civil rules","Move during trial before submission (50.01). Make or renew the motion after trial within the Rule 59 new-trial time; a pre-verdict motion is not a prerequisite (50.02). Conditional rulings on any new-trial motion (50.03). On denial, the winner may assert new-trial grounds on appeal (50.04).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 51. Jury instructions","Minnesota · civil rules","Requests at the close of evidence or earlier as directed (51.01); the court discloses proposed instructions before argument and allows objections on the record (51.02); objections must be distinct and timely (51.03); unpreserved plain error affecting substantial rights may be considered (51.04).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings by the court; amendment","Minnesota · civil rules","Special findings and conclusions in bench trials and on interlocutory injunctions; clearly erroneous review; no findings needed on Rule 12 or 56 motions except as provided (52.01). A motion to amend findings must be served and heard within the Rule 59.03 new-trial times; sufficiency of evidence may be raised on appeal without such a motion (52.02).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters","Minnesota · civil rules","Appointment only by consent, for exceptional conditions or accountings in bench matters, or for matters a judge cannot timely address; disqualification, notice, order contents (including e-filing use), authority, orders, and reports. Object to a master’s order or report within 21 days after service; de novo review of fact and law objections unless stipulated. Compensation allocation. Statutory referees are covered on","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgments; costs","Minnesota · civil rules","Judgment defined (54.01). Final judgment on fewer than all claims or parties only on an express no-just-reason-for-delay determination and direction (54.02). Default judgments may not exceed or differ from the demand (54.03). Costs: serve and file a sworn application within 45 days after entry of final judgment; objections within 7 days; taxation by the administrator or judge; appeal of administrator taxation within ","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 55. Default","Minnesota · civil rules","On affidavit of default: for a sum certain on contract (or taxes), the administrator enters judgment on affidavit of amount due, not exceeding the complaint or a Rule 4 notice of amount. Otherwise apply to the court; a party who has appeared gets 14 days’ notice before the hearing. A bond may be required after publication or out-of-state service, except in title and foreclosure actions. Notes must be filed (55.01). A","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","Minnesota · civil rules","Grant if no genuine issue of material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment; state reasons (56.01). Serve and file under Gen. R. Prac. 115.03 (28 days before hearing) and never less than 14 days before the hearing; unless the court orders otherwise, no motion more than 30 days after the close of all discovery (56.02). Record-cited support, admissibility objections, affidavits on personal knowledge (56.03); Rule","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments","Minnesota · civil rules","Procedure under Minn. Stat. ch. 555 follows these rules; jury rights preserved; another remedy does not bar relief; speedy hearing allowed.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 58. Entry of judgment; stay of entry","Minnesota · civil rules","The administrator enters judgment forthwith on a general verdict or a money-only order unless the court directs otherwise; the court directs entry on special verdicts or other relief. Entry is not delayed for costs, and the judgment is effective only on entry in the judgment roll (58.01). Entry may be stayed pending post-trial motions and then for up to 30 days; security may be required beyond 30 days (58.02).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 59. New trials","Minnesota · civil rules","Grounds: irregularity, misconduct, accident or surprise, newly discovered evidence, passion or prejudice in damages, preserved errors of law, verdict not justified by evidence or contrary to law (59.01). Heard on the files, exhibits, minutes, and affidavits (59.02). Serve the notice within 30 days after a general verdict or service of notice of filing of the decision; hear within 60 days unless extended for good caus","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from judgment or order","Minnesota · civil rules","Clerical errors at any time; during appeal with appellate leave (60.01). Relief for mistake, excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, fraud, void judgment, satisfaction, or any other reason, within a reasonable time and for the first three grounds within one year. Marriage dissolution decrees are excluded. The motion does not affect finality. Independent actions and fraud on the court preserved (60.02).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","Minnesota · civil rules","Errors not affecting substantial rights are disregarded.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 62. Stays of enforcement","Minnesota · civil rules","Discretionary stays pending Rule 50.02, 52.02, 59, or 60 motions (62.01); injunctions pending appeal (62.02); stays on appeal only under Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 107 and 108 (62.03); no bond for the State (62.04); appellate powers preserved (62.05); stays of Rule 54.02 partial judgments (62.06).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 63. Disability, disqualification, and removal of judge","Minnesota · civil rules","Another judge may complete post-verdict duties or grant a new trial (63.01). A judge disqualified under the Code of Judicial Conduct may not sit (63.02). Notice to remove: any party may serve and file a peremptory notice within 10 days after notice of the presiding judge, before the trial or hearing starts, but not against a judge who has presided at a motion or other proceeding or who was assigned by the Chief Justi","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 64. Seizure of person or property","Minnesota · civil rules","Provisional seizure remedies are available as provided by state law.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions","Minnesota · civil rules","TROs without notice need affidavit or verified complaint showing immediate irreparable harm and counsel’s written statement of notice efforts; the affidavit must be served with the TRO; the temporary injunction hearing is set at the earliest practicable time; the adverse party may move to dissolve (65.01). Temporary injunctions need notice; trial may be consolidated (65.02). Security is required for any TRO or tempor","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers","Minnesota · civil rules","Receivership actions are dismissed only by order; foreign receivers may sue but are subordinate to local creditors; administration follows Minn. Stat. ch. 576.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit in court","Minnesota · civil rules","Deposit with leave in an action (67.01); a stakeholder without a pending action may petition to deposit disputed property and be discharged (67.02); the court may order deposit of admitted trust property, enforceable by contempt (67.03); funds deposited in the administrator’s bank account (67.04).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of judgment or settlement","Minnesota · civil rules","Any party may serve, more than 14 days before trial, a written offer of judgment or settlement that expressly refers to Rule 68. Offers are ‘damages-only’ (interest, costs, and applicable fees added) unless expressly ‘total-obligation.’ Acceptance within 14 days; the offer is irrevocable during that time and then deemed withdrawn; a later offer supersedes. If a plaintiff rejects a defendant’s offer and does worse, th","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution","Minnesota · civil rules","Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution under Minn. Stat. ch. 550; judgment creditors may take discovery from any person.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 70. Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","Minnesota · civil rules","If a party fails to perform, the court may appoint another to act, issue a writ of attachment, hold the party in contempt, or vest title by judgment; writs of possession issue on application.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 71. Process for or against nonparties","Minnesota · civil rules","Nonparties may enforce orders in their favor and are subject to process to enforce orders against them.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 72–76. Reserved","Minnesota · civil rules","Reserved for future use.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 77. District courts and court administrators","Minnesota · civil rules","Courts are always open for filing (77.01); trials in open court; chambers proceedings anywhere, but no non-ex parte hearing outside the district without consent (77.02); administrator acts of course, subject to court review (77.03). The administrator must notify parties on filing of an order or entry of judgment by mail, e-mail, or the e-filing system, but that notice does not limit appeal times (77.04).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 78–79. Reserved","Minnesota · civil rules","Reserved for future use.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 80. Transcript as evidence","Minnesota · civil rules","Reported prior testimony admissible at a later trial may be proved by a certified transcript; rebuttable.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 81. Applicability","Minnesota · civil rules","Statutory proceedings listed in Appendix A keep their procedures to the extent inconsistent with the rules; statutes in Appendix B and other conflicting procedural statutes are superseded (81.01). Statutes on appeals to district court are not superseded (81.02). Statutes requiring acts ‘as provided by law’ follow these rules (81.03).","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 82. Jurisdiction and venue","Minnesota · civil rules","The rules do not extend or limit jurisdiction or venue.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 83. District court rules","Minnesota · civil rules","Courts may recommend local rules not in conflict with these rules or the General Rules of Practice, effective only as ordered by the Supreme Court.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 84–85. Forms; title","Minnesota · civil rules","The appendix forms illustrate sufficient pleading. Cited as the Rules of Civil Procedure.","#st-mn-rules"],["Rule 86. Effective date","Minnesota · civil rules","Original rules effective January 1, 1952. Unless the Court specifies otherwise, amendments take effect January 1 or July 1 in the year of or after adoption and apply to pending cases unless infeasible or unjust.","#st-mn-rules"],["Appeals in Minnesota: 60 days, but the trigger depends on whether you appeal a judgment or an order","Minnesota · appeals","Civil appeals of right go to the Minnesota Court of Appeals; the Supreme Court reviews the Court of Appeals only by discretionary petition for review. The general appeal period is 60 days, running from entry of a judgment, but from service by any party of written notice of filing for an appealable order. Statutes can shorten it; for example, the UPEPA anti-SLAPP appeal is 30 days.\nThe appellate courts cannot extend t","#st-mn-appeals"],["What to file, where, and the fee","Minnesota · appeals · Starting the appeal","File a notice of appeal with the clerk of the appellate courts and serve it on adverse parties within the appeal period. The notice identifies the judgment or order and lists opposing counsel. File with it: proof of service, proof of filing a copy with the trial court administrator, a copy of the judgment or order, the statement of the case (Rule 133.03), and the $550 filing fee. Fee exemptions include in forma paupe","#st-mn-appeals"],["Appealable judgments and orders","Minnesota · appeals · Starting the appeal","Appeal lies from a final judgment or a Rule 54.02 partial judgment; orders granting, refusing, or dissolving injunctions; orders vacating or sustaining attachment; orders denying a new trial (or granting one, if expressly based only on errors of law); orders that in effect determine the action; final orders in supplementary proceedings; final orders in special proceedings affecting a substantial right; orders grantin","#st-mn-appeals"],["Time: 60 days, with different triggers","Minnesota · appeals · Starting the appeal","Unless a statute provides otherwise: from a judgment, 60 days after its entry; from an appealable order, 60 days after service by any party of written notice of its filing. A Rule 54.02 partial judgment is appealable within 60 days only if the court expressly finds no just reason for delay and directs entry; otherwise time runs from the final judgment. The court administrator’s notice of orders does not limit the tim","#st-mn-appeals"],["Earlier orders die with the judgment’s appeal period","Minnesota · appeals · Starting the appeal","No order made before entry of judgment is appealable after the time to appeal the judgment expires. Inserting costs and disbursements into the judgment later does not extend the time. On appeal from a judgment, the court may review any order involving the merits or affecting the judgment. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 103.04, 104.02","#st-mn-appeals"],["No extensions of the appeal deadline","Minnesota · appeals · Starting the appeal","The appellate court may extend most deadlines for good cause, but not the time to file a notice of appeal or to seek review, except as specifically authorized by law. A personal leave continuance does not extend it either. Subdivision 2 of Rule 103.01 allows relief only where a good-faith notice was filed and served but later steps were missed. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 103.01, subd. 2; 126.02; 126.03(b)","#st-mn-appeals"],["Which motions restart the clock","Minnesota · appeals · Tolling and post-decision motions","A proper and timely motion for judgment as a matter of law (Rule 50.02), to amend or make findings or alter the judgment (Rule 52.02), for a new trial (Rule 59), or for Rule 60 relief filed within the time for a new-trial motion restarts the appeal period for all parties. The period then runs from service by any party of notice of filing of the order deciding the last such motion. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subd. ","#st-mn-appeals"],["Post-trial motion deadlines","Minnesota · appeals · Tolling and post-decision motions","Serve a new-trial motion within 30 days after a general verdict or service of notice of filing of the decision or order, and have it heard within 60 days (extendable only within that period for good cause). Motions for JMOL and amended findings follow the same timing. A stay of entry of judgment does not extend these times. The court may grant a new trial on its own initiative within 14 days. Minn. R. Civ. P. 50.02, ","#st-mn-appeals"],["Premature notices of appeal are void","Minnesota · appeals · Tolling and post-decision motions","A notice of appeal filed before a tolling motion is decided is premature and of no effect and does not divest the trial court of jurisdiction. File a new notice within the period measured from service of notice of filing of the order deciding the motion; no second filing fee is charged. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subd. 3","#st-mn-appeals"],["Motions to reconsider do not toll","Minnesota · appeals · Tolling and post-decision motions","District-court motions to reconsider are allowed only with the court’s permission, requested by letter of no more than 2 pages. They are not among the motions listed in Rule 104.01, subd. 2. Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 115.11; Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subd. 2","#st-mn-appeals"],["Notice of related appeal: 14 days, $100","Minnesota · appeals · Cross-appeals and related appeals","After a timely notice of appeal, any other party may seek review of a judgment or order in the same action by a notice of related appeal, served and filed within 14 days after service of the first notice or within the ordinary appeal period, whichever ends later. File it with a $100 fee, a statement of the case, and a copy of the order if different. No cost bond unless ordered. This replaced the old notice of review;","#st-mn-appeals"],["Cross-appeal briefing","Minnesota · appeals · Cross-appeals and related appeals","When adverse parties both appeal, the first to file is the appellant (the plaintiff below if same day). Four briefs: appellant’s principal brief; respondent/cross-appellant’s combined brief within 30 days (16,500 words); appellant/cross-respondent’s response and reply within 30 days (10,000 words); cross-appellant’s reply within 14 days (7,000 words). Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 131.01, subd. 4","#st-mn-appeals"],["Order the transcript within 14 days","Minnesota · appeals · Record and transcripts","Within 14 days after filing the notice of appeal, the appellant must order the needed transcript, file a notice to proceed by statement of the proceedings or agreed statement, or notify the respondent that none will be ordered. If ordering only part, serve a description and statement of issues; the respondent then has 14 days to order more. A transcript certificate signed by counsel and the reporter is due within 14 ","#st-mn-appeals"],["Statements of the proceedings","Minnesota · appeals · Record and transcripts","If there is no transcript, the appellant files a proposed statement within 14 days after the notice of appeal; the respondent may object within 14 days; the trial court’s approval must be filed within 60 days of the notice of appeal. An agreed statement also requires trial court approval within 60 days. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 110.03, 110.04","#st-mn-appeals"],["Briefing schedule","Minnesota · appeals · Briefs","Appellant’s brief and addendum: 30 days after delivery of the transcript (or the trial court’s approval of a statement); if no transcript is needed or it was obtained before appeal, 30 days after the notice of appeal or the order granting review. Respondent’s brief: 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief (or of a respondent-ordered transcript, if later). Reply: 14 days after the respondent’s brief or an amicu","#st-mn-appeals"],["Length limits","Minnesota · appeals · Briefs","Principal briefs: 45 pages, or 14,000 words, or 1,300 monospaced lines. Reply and amicus briefs: 20 pages, or 7,000 words, or 650 lines. Word-count briefs need a certificate naming the software. A motion to file an enlarged brief must be filed at least 14 days before the brief is due. Proportional fonts must be at least 13-point. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 132.01, subds. 1, 3","#st-mn-appeals"],["Required contents","Minnesota · appeals · Briefs","The appellant’s statement of issues must show, for each issue, how it was raised below with record cites, the ruling, how it was preserved, and up to four apposite cases. The argument must state the standard of review for each issue. A Court of Appeals brief may say whether the opinion should be precedential. The reply is limited to new matter. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 128.02, subds. 1–4","#st-mn-appeals"],["Addendum, not appendix","Minnesota · appeals · Briefs","Appendices are no longer permitted. The appellant must file a separate addendum with a table of contents and the orders, judgment, findings, and memoranda on appeal, limited to 50 additional pages. Cite the record by Document Index Number where available. Unpublished opinions are generally not included. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 128.03, 130.01, 130.02","#st-mn-appeals"],["Amicus curiae","Minnesota · appeals · Briefs","Request leave within 14 days after the notice of appeal or order granting review (or within 14 days after a petition for review is filed). The request is limited to 1,500 words. Only an attorney admitted in Minnesota or admitted pro hac vice by the appellate court may file an amicus brief. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 129.01","#st-mn-appeals"],["No automatic stay; move first in the trial court","Minnesota · appeals · Stays, bonds, and motions","An appeal does not stay enforcement unless the trial court orders a stay under Rule 108.02. A stay, approval of security, or injunction pending appeal must be sought first in the trial court, and the trial court’s ruling may be reviewed by motion in the Court of Appeals. For money judgments, security normally covers the unpaid judgment, costs, interest during appeal, and delay damages. A timely appeal suspends the tr","#st-mn-appeals"],["No cost bond unless ordered","Minnesota · appeals · Stays, bonds, and motions","No cost bond is required unless the trial court, on motion and a showing of extraordinary circumstances, orders one, capped at $1,000. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 107.01, 107.02","#st-mn-appeals"],["Motion practice","Minnesota · appeals · Stays, bonds, and motions","Responses are due 5 days after service; replies 3 days later, when the motion is submitted. These periods exclude intermediate weekends and holidays. No oral argument unless ordered. Filing a motion does not stay any deadline. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 127","#st-mn-appeals"],["Oral argument","Minnesota · appeals · Argument, decision, costs, and rehearing","Argument is allowed unless no party requested it in the statement of the case, a brief was untimely, it was waived, any party is unrepresented, or the court finds it unnecessary. Court of Appeals: appellant 15 minutes plus 5 for rebuttal, respondent 15. Supreme Court: appellant up to 35 minutes, respondent up to 25. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 134.01, 134.03","#st-mn-appeals"],["Opinions and judgment","Minnesota · appeals · Argument, decision, costs, and rehearing","Court of Appeals opinions may be precedential, nonprecedential, or order opinions; the latter two are not binding but nonprecedential opinions may be cited as persuasive. Judgment is entered no sooner than 30 days after the decision; a petition for review or rehearing stays entry. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 136.01, 136.02","#st-mn-appeals"],["Costs and appellate fees","Minnesota · appeals · Argument, decision, costs, and rehearing","Prevailing party costs: $300 on a merits judgment. File a notice of taxation of costs and disbursements within 14 days of the decision; objections within 7 days. Motions for appellate attorney fees must be made by Rule 127 motion within the time for taxation of costs; response within 14 days. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 139.01, 139.03, 139.05","#st-mn-appeals"],["Rehearing only in the Supreme Court","Minnesota · appeals · Argument, decision, costs, and rehearing","No rehearing in the Court of Appeals. In the Supreme Court, petition within 14 days after the decision, with a $100 fee; the opponent may answer within 7 days. No rehearing of a denial of review or accelerated review. A petition stays entry of judgment. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 140.01–140.03","#st-mn-appeals"],["Default dismissal","Minnesota · appeals · Argument, decision, costs, and rehearing","If the appellant’s brief is 30 days overdue and the respondent has not moved, the court dismisses the appeal without notice, subject to a motion to reinstate showing good cause, merit, and no substantial prejudice. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 142.02","#st-mn-appeals"],["Petition for review","Minnesota · appeals · Review in the Minnesota Supreme Court","File within 30 days of the filing of the Court of Appeals decision, with proof of service and a $550 fee. The petition is limited to 2,000 words. Review is discretionary; listed criteria include an important question, a ruling on a statute’s constitutionality, departure from usual course, and the need to develop, clarify, or harmonize law with statewide impact or likely recurrence. Failing to take other steps does no","#st-mn-appeals"],["Response and conditional cross-review","Minnesota · appeals · Review in the Minnesota Supreme Court","A response is due within 21 days of service. The respondent may conditionally ask for review of additional issues; the petitioner then may not respond unless the court asks. Amicus applicants who want to argue whether to grant review must apply within 14 days after the petition is filed. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 117, subds. 4–5","#st-mn-appeals"],["Accelerated review","Minnesota · appeals · Review in the Minnesota Supreme Court","Any party may petition for accelerated review of a case pending in the Court of Appeals if it is of imperative public importance. Fee $100, 4,000-word limit. Filing does not stay Court of Appeals deadlines. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 118","#st-mn-appeals"],["Discretionary review of nonappealable orders","Minnesota · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","Petition the Court of Appeals within 30 days of the filing of the order (not orders made during trial), with a $550 fee and a copy filed in the trial court. The petition is limited to 4,000 words and must explain why an immediate appeal is necessary. Response 7 days, reply 3 days (2,000 words). If granted, file a statement of the case within 7 days. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 105.01–105.03","#st-mn-appeals"],["Certified questions on denial of dismissal or summary judgment","Minnesota · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","An order denying a failure-to-state motion or summary judgment is appealable as of right only if the trial court certifies the question as important and doubtful. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 103.03(i)","#st-mn-appeals"],["Class certification orders","Minnesota · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","The Court of Appeals may permit an appeal from an order granting or denying class certification; apply within the Rule 105 time. The appeal does not stay district court proceedings unless ordered. Minn. R. Civ. P. 23.06","#st-mn-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP denials: 30 days, as of right","Minnesota · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","A UPEPA movant may appeal as of right from an order denying the special motion in whole or part within 30 days after entry of the order. All proceedings between all parties are stayed during the appeal. Minn. Stat. §§ 554.10(c), 554.15","#st-mn-appeals"],["Extraordinary writs","Minnesota · appeals · Interlocutory and special appeals","Mandamus or prohibition against a trial court is sought by petition to the Court of Appeals ($550 fee); respondents may answer within 7 days. In emergencies, an oral petition may be made through the Chief Staff Attorney (Court of Appeals) or the Supreme Court Commissioner. Denial of a writ by the Court of Appeals is reviewed by petition for review, not a new writ. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 120.01–120.05, 121","#st-mn-appeals"],["Appellate e-filing","Minnesota · appeals · E-filing and time computation","Filing is by the appellate courts’ electronic system where required by Supreme Court order (that order was not collected). Documents e-filed by 11:59 p.m. are filed that day if accepted. Use searchable PDF, Word, or WordPerfect. Documents that must also go to the trial court (such as the notice of appeal) may be served through the district court system, but separate proof of service must then be filed in the appellat","#st-mn-appeals"],["Computing time","Minnesota · appeals · E-filing and time computation","Rule 6.01 of the Civil Rules governs. Add 3 days for service by U.S. mail only, and 1 day for electronic or personal service after 5:00 p.m. E-service is complete on confirmation from the appellate system. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 125.03, 126.01; Minn. R. Civ. P. 6.01","#st-mn-appeals"],["Notice of appeal from a judgment","Minnesota · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from entry of the judgment.\nUnless a statute sets a different time. Not extendable by the appellate court (Rule 126.02). Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subd. 1","#st-mn-appeals"],["Notice of appeal from an appealable order","Minnesota · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from service by any party of written notice of the order’s filing.\nThe court administrator’s notice does not start this period. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subd. 1","#st-mn-appeals"],["Notice of appeal after a tolling motion","Minnesota · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from service by any party of notice of filing of the order deciding the last tolling motion.\nRule 60 motions toll only if filed within the new-trial deadline. Notices filed while the motion is pending are void. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subds. 2–3","#st-mn-appeals"],["Notice of related appeal (cross-appeal)","Minnesota · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the first notice of appeal (or the ordinary appeal period, if later).\n$100 fee and statement of the case. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subd. 4","#st-mn-appeals"],["Motion for new trial (service)","Minnesota · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from general verdict, or service of notice of filing of the decision or order.\nMust be heard within 60 days. JMOL (50.02) and amended findings (52.02) use the same times. Minn. R. Civ. P. 59.03","#st-mn-appeals"],["Petition for discretionary review (Court of Appeals)","Minnesota · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the order.\nResponse 7 days; reply 3 days. Also the deadline for class-certification appeals (Minn. R. Civ. P. 23.06). Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 105.01","#st-mn-appeals"],["Order transcript / file notice re record","Minnesota · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing the notice of appeal.\nTranscript certificate due 14 days after ordering. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 110.02, subd. 1","#st-mn-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief and addendum","Minnesota · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from delivery of the transcript (or approval of a statement); if no transcript, filing of the notice of appeal.\n14,000 words. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 131.01, subd. 1","#st-mn-appeals"],["Respondent’s brief","Minnesota · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the appellant’s brief.\nOr 30 days after delivery of a respondent-ordered transcript, if later. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 131.01, subd. 2","#st-mn-appeals"],["Reply brief","Minnesota · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the respondent’s (or amicus) brief.\n7,000 words; new matter only. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 131.01, subd. 3","#st-mn-appeals"],["Motion to file enlarged brief","Minnesota · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from before the brief’s due date.\nMust be filed at least 14 days before the brief is due. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 132.01, subd. 3","#st-mn-appeals"],["Response to appellate motion","Minnesota · appeals · Deadlines","5 days from service of the motion.\nReply 3 days. Weekends and holidays excluded. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 127","#st-mn-appeals"],["Petition for review (Supreme Court)","Minnesota · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the Court of Appeals decision.\n$550 fee; 2,000 words. Response within 21 days of service. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 117, subd. 1","#st-mn-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing (Supreme Court only)","Minnesota · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the Supreme Court decision or order.\nNone in the Court of Appeals. $100 fee. Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 140.01","#st-mn-appeals"],["Notice of taxation of costs and disbursements","Minnesota · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the appellate decision.\nObjections within 7 days. Fee motions due within the same time (Rule 139.05). Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 139.03","#st-mn-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP (UPEPA) appeal from denial","Minnesota · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order denying the special motion.\nAppeal of right for the moving party only; stays the whole action. Minn. Stat. § 554.15","#st-mn-appeals"],["Recusal motion, Court of Appeals","Minnesota · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from notice of oral argument or nonoral conference (or panel change).\nSupreme Court: 14 days after the case-initiating filing or grant of review (Rule 141.01(b)). Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 141.02(b)","#st-mn-appeals"],["Supreme Court of Missouri","Missouri · courts","Seven judges sitting in Jefferson City; its decisions control all other courts. Exclusive appellate jurisdiction over cases involving the validity of a U.S. treaty or statute or of a Missouri statute or constitutional provision, construction of the state revenue laws, title to a state office, and death sentences. Takes other cases by transfer from the Court of Appeals. Writes binding procedure rules, controls bar adm","#st-mo"],["Missouri Court of Appeals (Eastern, Western, and Southern Districts)","Missouri · courts","One court in three districts: Eastern (14 judges, St. Louis region and eastern counties), Western (11 judges, all counties not assigned elsewhere, including the Kansas City area), and Southern (7 judges, southern counties). General appellate jurisdiction in everything outside the Supreme Court’s exclusive list; sits in divisions of at least three judges. An appeal filed in the wrong district or court is transferred, ","#st-mo"],["Circuit Courts (judicial circuits of contiguous counties)","Missouri · courts","Original jurisdiction over all civil and criminal cases. Each circuit has circuit judges and, in every county, at least one associate circuit judge. Circuits may adopt local rules consistent with Supreme Court rules (Mo. Const. art. V, §§ 14–17).","#st-mo"],["Associate circuit, small claims, and municipal divisions","Missouri · courts","Divisions of the circuit court rather than separate courts. Some judgments of associate circuit judges in non-record cases get a trial de novo (application within 10 days); others are appealed on the record. Municipal judges hear ordinance violations. The Civil Code does not apply in small claims or municipal divisions unless made applicable (Mo. Const. art. V, § 23; RSMo 506.010, 512.180–.190).","#st-mo"],["New Uniform Public Expression Protection Act, effective August 28, 2026","Missouri · anti-SLAPP","Missouri repealed its narrow anti-SLAPP statute (§ 537.528) and enacted the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act, § 537.529 (S.B. 835 & 1111 merged with S.B. 1067, 2026). It covers claims based on communications in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding, communications on an issue under consideration in such a proceeding, or exercise of speech, press, assembly, petiti","#st-mo-slapp"],["Exemptions: government defendants, enforcement actions, and commercial speech","Missouri · anti-SLAPP","The Act does not apply to claims against a governmental unit or its employee acting officially; to claims by a governmental unit enforcing a law against an imminent threat to public health or safety; or to claims against a business arising from communications about its sale or lease of goods or services. Dramatic, literary, musical, political, journalistic, and artistic works are not “goods or services.” RSMo 537.529","#st-mo-slapp"],["Procedure: file within 60 days of service; automatic stay","Missouri · anti-SLAPP","File a special motion to dismiss within 60 days after being served with the pleading asserting the claim (later for good cause). Filing stays all other proceedings between movant and respondent, including discovery and pending motions, until the ruling and the time to appeal expire. The court may also stay related hearings or discovery involving other parties. Limited discovery is allowed only if specific information","#st-mo-slapp"],["Standard: three-step test with summary-judgment evidence","Missouri · anti-SLAPP","The court dismisses with prejudice if (1) the movant shows the Act applies, (2) the respondent fails to show an exemption, and (3) either the respondent fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of material fact. The court considers pleadings, the motion and responses, and any evidence admissible on summary judgment. A voluntary d","#st-mo-slapp"],["Fees: mandatory for a winning movant","Missouri · anti-SLAPP","The court must award costs, reasonable attorney’s fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion to a prevailing movant. A prevailing respondent recovers them only if the motion was frivolous or filed solely to delay. RSMo 537.529.12","#st-mo-slapp"],["Appeal: 21 days, as of right, from a denial","Missouri · anti-SLAPP","A moving party may appeal as of right within 21 days from an order denying the motion in whole or in part. Once any party appeals a ruling on the motion, all proceedings between all parties are stayed until the appeal ends. RSMo 537.529.11, .6(3)","#st-mo-slapp"],["Old § 537.528 (pre-August 28, 2026 cases)","Missouri · anti-SLAPP","The repealed statute applied only to actions over conduct or speech in connection with a public hearing or public meeting in a quasi-judicial proceeding before a state or local body. It required priority consideration of a special motion to dismiss, judgment on the pleadings, or summary judgment; suspended discovery; mandated fees if such a motion filed within 90 days of the answer was granted; and allowed an expedit","#st-mo-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: Supreme Court Rule 9.03 (details not verified)","Missouri · out of state","Out-of-state lawyers appear in Missouri courts under Supreme Court Rule 9.03. Its requirements, including any fee, local counsel duties, and case limits, were not verified. Read the current rule on courts.mo.gov before filing. Mo. Sup. Ct. R. 9.03 (not collected)","#st-mo-oos"],["Unlicensed practice is a crime with a treble-fee remedy","Missouri · out of state","No one may practice law or do law business in Missouri without a Missouri license in full force. A violation is a misdemeanor, and the client may sue for treble the fees paid within two years; if the client does not, the state may. Admission and licensing are exclusively for the Supreme Court’s rules. Make sure any out-of-state appearance or advice is covered by an authorized admission. RSMo 484.020, 484.040","#st-mo-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances and e-filing (not verified)","Missouri · out of state","Missouri’s rules on limited-scope representation and on statewide electronic filing (Case.net / eFiling) are in the Supreme Court Rules, which were not collected. Statute only requires that filings omit listed personal identifiers and put them on a confidential information sheet. RSMo 509.520; Supreme Court Rules (not collected)","#st-mo-oos"],["Governing code: Supreme Court Rule 4 (text not collected)","Missouri · ethics","The Missouri Rules of Professional Conduct are Supreme Court Rule 4, following the ABA Model Rules numbering (Rule 4-1.1 and so on). The rule text could not be collected, so differences from the ABA Model Rules are not stated here. Mo. Sup. Ct. R. 4 (not collected)","#st-mo-ethics"],["Statutory ban on sharing fees with non-lawyers","Missouri · ethics","Beyond the ethics rules, a statute makes it a misdemeanor for a Missouri lawyer to divide fees with anyone who is not a licensed attorney, or with a firm, association, or corporation not wholly made up of licensed attorneys. The payor may sue for treble the amount within two years, and the state may sue if the payor does not. RSMo 484.150","#st-mo-ethics"],["Attorney’s liens arise by statute","Missouri · ethics","A lawyer who appears for a party has a lien on the client’s claim or counterclaim from the start of the action, attaching to any judgment and its proceeds, that settlement cannot defeat. A contingent-fee lawyer who serves written notice of the agreement on the defendant gets a lien on the claim and settlement proceeds from the date of notice, even before suit. RSMo 484.130, 484.140","#st-mo-ethics"],["Tort venue is where the plaintiff was first injured","Missouri · land mine","If any count sounds in tort and the injury occurred in Missouri, venue lies only in the county of first injury (for defamation or privacy, where first published; for exposure, where it happened, not where symptoms appeared). Joinder cannot create venue, adding or dropping parties can force a transfer, an improper-venue motion is deemed granted if not denied within 90 days, and an erroneous denial requires reversal. R","#st-mo-unique"],["Never put a dollar figure in the prayer","Missouri · land mine","A Missouri petition seeking money may state a dollar amount only to establish jurisdiction; otherwise pray for “fair and reasonable” damages. RSMo 509.050.1","#st-mo-unique"],["Punitive damages cannot be pleaded at the start","Missouri · land mine","An initial petition may not claim punitive damages. You need leave on a supported motion filed at least 120 days before the final pretrial conference or trial. RSMo 510.261.5","#st-mo-unique"],["Medical-negligence affidavit within 90 days","Missouri · land mine","In any health care negligence case, file a separate affidavit for each defendant within 90 days after filing the petition (extendable once for up to 90 days for good cause), stating that you have a qualifying provider’s written opinion. Otherwise the case is dismissed on motion. RSMo 538.225.1, .4–.6","#st-mo-unique"],["Two-year limits for defamation, assault, and medical malpractice","Missouri · land mine","Libel, slander, injurious falsehood, assault, battery, false imprisonment, and malicious prosecution must be sued within 2 years, and medical malpractice within 2 years of the act of neglect (with narrow exceptions). Most other torts and contracts have 5 years, and written promises to pay money have 10. RSMo 516.140, 516.105, 516.120, 516.110","#st-mo-unique"],["Notice of appeal: 10 days after the judgment becomes final","Missouri · land mine","No appeal is effective unless the notice of appeal is filed within 10 days after the judgment or order becomes final. When a judgment becomes final is set by Supreme Court Rule (Rules 81–84, not collected), so compute the date from the rule, not from the date of entry. A late appeal requires a special order of the appellate court. RSMo 512.050, 512.060","#st-mo-unique"],["Anti-SLAPP motion: 60 days, and the appeal clock is 21","Missouri · land mine","In actions filed on or after August 28, 2026, a UPEPA special motion to dismiss is due 60 days after service of the pleading, and a denial must be appealed within 21 days, not the ordinary appeal period. RSMo 537.529.5, .11, .15","#st-mo-unique"],["Personal-jurisdiction and venue objections are waived if not raised by motion","Missouri · land mine","Lack of personal jurisdiction, venue, process, and service defects are waived unless asserted by motion within the time to respond. Only failure to state a claim or defense and lack of subject-matter jurisdiction survive. RSMo 509.290, 509.330, 509.340, 509.400","#st-mo-unique"],["Redact identifiers or use the confidential filing sheet","Missouri · land mine","Filings may not contain full Social Security, account, or driver’s license numbers, victim or witness contact information, a minor’s name, address, or birth date, or a party’s full birth date. Put them on the confidential information filing sheet. RSMo 509.520","#st-mo-unique"],["Fee-sharing with non-lawyers is a crime","Missouri · land mine","Dividing fees with a non-lawyer, or a firm not wholly made up of licensed attorneys, is a misdemeanor with treble-damages exposure. This is stricter than an ethics-rule violation alone. RSMo 484.150","#st-mo-unique"],["Peremptory change of judge: Rule 51.05 (not verified)","Missouri · judges","Missouri allows a change of judge without cause by application under Supreme Court Rule 51.05. Its deadlines and limits were not verified, so read the current rule before relying on it. Mo. Sup. Ct. R. 51.05 (not collected)","#st-mo-unique"],["Statutory disqualification for cause","Missouri · judges","A judge may be disqualified in a civil case for interest, prejudice, relationship to a party, prior service as counsel, or the opposing party’s undue influence. Apply by petition stating the cause and when you learned of it, with an affidavit that you cannot get a fair trial, after reasonable notice. Each side gets only one change. A judge who is interested, related, or was counsel must step aside without application","#st-mo-unique"],["Large single-county circuits: one objection moves the case","Missouri · judges","In single-county circuits over 400,000 population with two or more judges, a legal objection to the assigned judge moves the case to another division. Each party may apply once, as to one judge. RSMo 508.110","#st-mo-unique"],["Change of venue for local prejudice","Missouri · judges","Venue may be changed if county residents are prejudiced against the applicant or the opponent unduly influences them. In counties over 65,000, a controverted application is decided on evidence. The applicant pays the costs of the change. RSMo 508.090.2, 508.140, 508.190","#st-mo-unique"],["Missouri Constitution: not established here","Missouri · speech","The UPEPA protects rights “guaranteed by the Constitution of the United States or the Constitution of the state of Missouri.” The collected texts do not include Missouri’s Bill of Rights or case law, so whether Missouri courts read its speech guarantee more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked. RSMo 537.529.3(3), .13; Mo. Const. art. I (not collected)","#st-mo-unique"],["Supreme Court Rules override most procedure statutes","Missouri · unique rules","The Supreme Court’s procedure rules have the force of law, and any rule may be annulled or amended only by a law limited to that purpose. Many old Civil Code timing provisions have been displaced by the Rules. The legislature has overridden Rule 51.03 for venue in tort cases. Mo. Const. art. V, § 5; RSMo 506.030, 508.011","#st-mo-unique"],["Punitive damages need leave of court","Missouri · unique rules","No initial pleading may claim punitive damages. Move for leave, supported by evidence, at least 120 days before the final pretrial conference (or trial), and the court must find a reasonable basis under a clear-and-convincing standard. Damages are capped at the greater of $500,000 or five times net judgment, with exceptions, and either party may require a bifurcated trial. RSMo 510.261.5, 510.263, 510.265","#st-mo-unique"],["Rule RSMo 506.010. Civil Code of Missouri: scope","Missouri · civil rules","Chapters 506–512 are “The Civil Code of Missouri” and govern civil procedure in the Supreme Court, Court of Appeals, and circuit courts unless other law provides otherwise; construed for just, speedy, inexpensive determination. Does not apply to small claims or municipal divisions unless made applicable.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.020. Not applicable to criminal cases","Missouri · civil rules","The Code does not govern criminal practice except where a statute makes it applicable.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.030. Supreme Court may harmonize the Code by rule","Missouri · civil rules","If parts of the Code conflict with each other or with other procedure statutes, the Supreme Court may issue harmonizing rules that do not change substantive rights. Note Mo. Const. art. V, § 5: Supreme Court procedure rules have the force of law and may be annulled or amended only by a law limited to that purpose, so many Code sections below have been displaced by the Rules.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.040. One form of action","Missouri · civil rules","There is one form of action, the “civil action.”","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.050. Orders and motions defined","Missouri · civil rules","A written direction of the court not included in a judgment is an order; an application for an order is a motion.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.060. Computing and extending time; motion notice","Missouri · civil rules","Exclude the trigger day, include the last day unless a weekend or legal holiday; for periods under 7 days, exclude intermediate weekends and holidays. The court may enlarge a period before it expires, or after it expires for excusable neglect, but may not extend time for a new-trial motion, commencing an action, or taking an appeal. Written motions and hearing notice must be served at least 5 days before the hearing;","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.080. Courts always open for filing","Missouri · civil rules","Courts are deemed always open for filing papers, issuing and returning process, and making interlocutory motions and orders.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.090. Clerk’s office hours; clerk may grant routine process","Missouri · civil rules","The clerk’s office is open business hours except Sundays and legal holidays; the clerk may issue process and other relief not requiring a court order, subject to the court’s control.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.100. Service of papers after the petition","Missouri · civil rules","Later pleadings, written motions, notices, offers of judgment, and similar papers must be served on every affected party (not on parties in default for failure to appear, except new claims, which need summons-type service). Serve the attorney of record unless the court orders service on the party, by hand delivery, leaving a copy at the attorney’s office, or leaving it at the party’s home with a family member over 15","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.110. Commencing a suit","Missouri · civil rules","A suit is commenced by filing a petition and either the defendant’s voluntary appearance or suing out a summons or attachment. The official annotation notes that subsection 2 (commencement requires suing out process) has been held overruled by Supreme Court Rule 53.01 (Keys v. Nigro, 913 S.W.2d 947 (Mo. App. W.D. 1996)).","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.120. Issuance of summons","Missouri · civil rules","On filing the petition the clerk issues summons and delivers it for service to the sheriff or a specially appointed server; alias and pluries summons issue on request.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.130. Contents of summons","Missouri · civil rules","Signed and dated by the clerk under seal, naming the court, parties, plaintiff’s attorney, the time and place to appear and defend, and warning that default judgment will be entered for failure to do so.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.140. Who serves process; special process server fees","Missouri · civil rules","Service is by the sheriff (or coroner if the sheriff is disqualified) or a person specially appointed by the court or circuit clerk under local rules, valid only for the process named. A party may apply to have special process server fees awarded in the judgment.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.150. Manner of service","Missouri · civil rules","Summons and petition are served together: on an individual personally, at the dwelling with a family member over 15, or on an authorized agent; on a corporation, partnership, or association by delivery to an officer, partner, managing or general agent, the person in charge of a business office, or a registered agent (plus mailing if the agent statute requires). The section also covers service on conservators and diss","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.160. Service by mail or publication (in rem)","Missouri · civil rules","Allowed in actions affecting property, a fund, trust, res, or status within the court’s jurisdiction, and supports only a judgment as to that property or status, not a general personal judgment. Requires a verified petition or affidavit stating why personal service cannot be had (e.g., nonresident, absconding, concealment); the clerk mails by registered mail, return receipt signed by addressee only, or issues an orde","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.170. Statewide service","Missouri · civil rules","Process may be served anywhere in Missouri and forwarded to any county’s sheriff.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.180. Proof of service","Missouri · civil rules","Officers make a signed written return of time, place, and manner; special servers file an affidavit; mail service is proved by the clerk’s certificate plus the registered-mail receipt; publication by publisher’s affidavit with a copy of the notice and the clerk’s mailing certificate. No civil arrest on mesne process or execution.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.190. Amending process and returns","Missouri · civil rules","The court may allow process, return, or proof of service to be amended unless material prejudice would result.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.500. Long-arm jurisdiction","Missouri · civil rules","A person or firm submits to Missouri jurisdiction, for claims arising from the act, by transacting business, making a contract, committing a tort, owning or using real estate in Missouri, or insuring a Missouri risk (plus paternity and marital-domicile grounds).","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.510. Out-of-state service","Missouri · civil rules","A defendant subject to long-arm jurisdiction may be personally served outside Missouri (a corporation through a managing officer or registered agent in any state) by a person authorized to serve there; the server files an affidavit of time, manner, and place.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 506.520. Personal judgment; 30-day default bar","Missouri · civil rules","Out-of-state service under 506.500–.510 supports a personal judgment. No default judgment may be entered until at least 30 days after service.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 507.010. Real party in interest","Missouri · civil rules","Actions are prosecuted by the real party in interest, but personal representatives, guardians, conservators, trustees of express trusts, contracting parties for another’s benefit, and statutory plaintiffs may sue in their own names.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 507.020. Claims under another state’s law","Missouri · civil rules","A claim under another state’s law may be sued on in Missouri by whoever that state authorizes, or by its authorized representative, who may receive proceeds and give a release.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 507.030. Joinder of persons with a joint interest","Missouri · civil rules","Persons with a joint interest must be joined on the same side; one who will not join as plaintiff may be made a defendant. The court may order other necessary parties brought in.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 507.040. Permissive joinder (2019 limits)","Missouri · civil rules","Parties may join if claims arise from the same transaction or occurrence (or series) and share a common question. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 20, claims arising from separate purchases of, or separate incidents involving, the same product or service do not satisfy this test, and the statute adopts State ex rel. Johnson & Johnson v. Burlison (Mo. 2019): joinder cannot extend venue to a county not proper for each claim.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 507.050. Misjoinder; dropping and adding parties","Missouri · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may drop, add, or sever parties at any stage. A motion to drop or add parties made with 509.290 motions is subject to 509.340’s consolidation and waiver rules.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 507.060. Interpleader","Missouri · civil rules","Persons with claims against the plaintiff or plaintiff’s insured exposing it to multiple liability (including multiple claims on the same insurance coverage) may be joined and required to interplead; a defendant may interplead by cross-claim or counterclaim.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 507.070. Class and derivative actions","Missouri · civil rules","Statutory class action provision (numerous class, adequate representation; joint/common, specific-property, or common-question rights) and shareholder derivative suits; dismissal or compromise is regulated. Class certification orders are appealable only with the Court of Appeals’ permission (RSMo 512.020(3)).","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 507.080. Third-party practice","Missouri · civil rules","Before answering a defendant may move ex parte, and afterward on notice to the plaintiff, for leave to file a third-party petition against a nonparty who is or may be liable for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 14(a)(1), leave is always needed. The third-party defendant may assert the third-party plaintiff’s defenses; a plaintiff facing a counterclaim may also implead.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 507.090. Intervention","Missouri · civil rules","Intervention of right on timely application when a statute grants it, existing representation is or may be inadequate and the applicant may be bound, or a court-held property disposition affects the applicant; permissive intervention when a statute grants a conditional right or there is a common question, considering delay and prejudice.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 507.100. Substitution on death, incapacity, or transfer","Missouri · civil rules","If a party dies and the claim survives, the court on motion orders substitution; the motion and hearing notice are served on parties under 506.100 and on nonparties like a summons. If the right survives only to or against remaining parties, the death is suggested on the record and the case proceeds.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 507.110–.188. Suits by minors; next friends","Missouri · civil rules","A minor sues only through a guardian, conservator, or court-appointed next friend; appointment procedure, bond, cost liability, and court approval of settlements and distribution of proceeds are regulated.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 507.190–.220. Guardian ad litem for minor defendants","Missouri · civil rules","A suit against a minor may not proceed until a guardian ad litem is appointed; the court appoints one if the minor fails to seek one after service.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 508.010. Venue (2005/2019 tort venue)","Missouri · civil rules","Non-tort cases: county of defendant’s residence, or plaintiff’s residence where defendant may be found; any county where a defendant lives; any county if all defendants are nonresidents (with independent personal jurisdiction over each). Any tort count: venue lies only in the county where the plaintiff was first injured in Missouri (defamation and privacy: where first published; trauma or exposure, not symptoms); for","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 508.011. Statute beats Rule 51.03 in tort cases","Missouri · civil rules","To the extent Supreme Court Rule 51.03 contradicts chapter 508, the statute prevails for tort claims.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 508.012. Transfer when parties change","Missouri · civil rules","Before trial, if adding, removing, or severing a party would have changed venue under 508.010, the judge must transfer the case to a proper forum on any party’s application.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 508.013. 2019 venue changes: applicability","Missouri · civil rules","The 2019 joinder and venue amendments apply to actions filed after February 13, 2019, with transition rules for then-pending cases.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 508.020. Attachment and replevin venue","Missouri · civil rules","Attachment and replevin suits for specific property are brought in the county where the property is found.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 508.030. Real estate venue","Missouri · civil rules","Suits for possession of, or affecting title to, real estate are brought in the county where the land (or part of it) lies.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 508.050. Suits against municipalities","Missouri · civil rules","Generally only in the county where the municipality is situated (or its seat of government if in several counties), with listed exceptions.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 508.060. Suits against counties","Missouri · civil rules","Brought in that county’s circuit court unless removed by change of venue.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 508.080. Transfer by written consent","Missouri · civil rules","Parties may agree in a filed writing to move the case to another county or court; see also 508.010.13 (court must transfer on unanimous written agreement).","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 508.090. Statutory grounds to disqualify judge or change venue","Missouri · civil rules","A judge may be disqualified for interest, prejudice, relationship to a party, prior service as counsel, or the opposing party’s undue influence; venue may be changed for prejudice of county inhabitants or the opponent’s undue influence over them. No party gets more than one change. Peremptory change of judge is governed by Supreme Court Rule 51.05, which was not collected.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 508.100. Self-disqualification","Missouri · civil rules","A judge who is interested, related to a party, or was counsel must, without application, call in another judge or request a Supreme Court transfer, unless all parties consent.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 508.110. Large single-county circuits","Missouri · civil rules","In single-county circuits over 400,000 population with two or more judges, a legal objection to the assigned judge sends the case to another division; one application per party, as to one judge.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 508.130. Application for disqualification or change of venue","Missouri · civil rules","Petition stating the cause and when the applicant learned of it, with an affidavit of truth and that the applicant cannot get a fair trial.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 508.140. Hearing on the application","Missouri · civil rules","After reasonable notice, if the application is sufficient the judge is disqualified or venue is changed to a convenient county in the same or adjoining circuit; one change per side. In counties over 65,000, a controverted inhabitants-prejudice application is decided on evidence.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 508.160. Pleadings closed before venue change","Missouri · civil rules","All pleadings must be filed and issues made up before the change is ordered.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 508.190. Costs of venue change","Missouri · civil rules","The applicant pays the costs of the change (loser pays the costs of a contested inhabitants-prejudice hearing); court costs and jury expenses follow rules between the counties.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.010. Pleadings allowed","Missouri · civil rules","Petition and answer; a reply only to a counterclaim so designated; answer to a cross-claim; third-party petition and answer. The court may order a reply.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.020. Caption","Missouri · civil rules","Name of court, title of action, file number, and pleading designation; the petition names all parties.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.030. Attribution and signature","Missouri · civil rules","Pleadings of represented parties are attributed to at least one attorney of record by name with address; self-represented parties sign and give an address. No verification unless a rule or statute requires it; signing follows Supreme Court rule.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.040. Simple, direct pleading","Missouri · civil rules","Averments must be simple, concise, and direct; no technical forms.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.050. Claim for relief; no dollar amount","Missouri · civil rules","Short and plain statement of facts showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment. Unlike federal practice, a money demand may not state a dollar figure except to establish jurisdiction; pray for “fair and reasonable” damages. A party may discover the total damages claimed, but the response cannot be used at trial. Prejudgment interest under 408.040 need not be prayed.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.060. Joinder of claims","Missouri · civil rules","Any number of legal or equitable claims, independent or alternative, may be joined against an opposing party.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.070. Joining contingent claims","Missouri · civil rules","A claim normally cognizable only after another is concluded may be joined with it (e.g., a money claim and a fraudulent-conveyance claim).","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.080. Denials","Missouri · civil rules","State defenses plainly and admit or deny each averment; lack of knowledge has the effect of a denial; denials must fairly meet the substance, and partial denials must specify what is admitted.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.090. Affirmative defenses","Missouri · civil rules","Must be pleaded affirmatively, including accord and satisfaction, arbitration and award, assumption of risk, contributory negligence, bankruptcy discharge, duress, estoppel, failure of consideration, fraud, illegality, fellow-servant injury, laches, license, payment, release, res judicata, statute of frauds, statute of limitations, truth in defamation, waiver, and any other avoidance.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.100. Effect of failure to deny","Missouri · civil rules","Averments other than amount of damage are admitted if not denied. When a reply is filed, affirmative defenses not denied in it are admitted; averments needing no response are deemed denied.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.110. Alternative and hypothetical pleading","Missouri · civil rules","Claims and defenses may be pleaded alternatively or hypothetically; one sufficient alternative saves the pleading.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.120. Numbered paragraphs; separate counts","Missouri · civil rules","Numbered paragraphs limited to a single set of circumstances; separate counts for claims from separate transactions where it aids clarity.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.130. Adoption by reference; exhibits","Missouri · civil rules","Statements may be adopted by reference; an exhibit is part of the pleading for all purposes.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.140. Capacity","Missouri · civil rules","Capacity may be averred generally; challenge it by specific negative averment with particulars.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.150. Partnership existence","Missouri · civil rules","Pleaded partnership with named partners is confessed unless specifically denied.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.160. Fraud and mistake","Missouri · civil rules","Plead circumstances with particularity; state of mind may be averred generally.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.170. Conditions precedent","Missouri · civil rules","May be averred generally; denials must be specific and particular.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.180. Official documents and acts","Missouri · civil rules","Sufficient to aver issuance or performance in compliance with law.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.190. Judgments","Missouri · civil rules","Sufficient to aver the judgment; if controverted, the pleader must prove jurisdictional facts.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.200. Special and punitive damages","Missouri · civil rules","Items of special damage must be specifically stated. The older requirement to state punitive damages separately must now be read with 510.261.5, which bars any punitive damages claim in an initial pleading.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.210. Libel and slander pleading","Missouri · civil rules","No need to plead extrinsic facts showing application to plaintiff; defendant may plead truth and mitigating circumstances.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.220. Pleading private statutes and other states’ law","Missouri · civil rules","Cite a private statute by title and location; when the pleading states that another state’s law is relied on, courts take judicial notice of its statutes and decisions.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.230. Written instruments","Missouri · civil rules","May be pleaded by legal effect, recited at length, or attached as an exhibit.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.240. Execution deemed confessed","Missouri · civil rules","Execution of an instrument set out or attached is confessed unless specifically denied.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.250. Construction","Missouri · civil rules","Pleadings are construed to do substantial justice.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.260. Time to plead","Missouri · civil rules","Answer within 30 days after service of summons and petition (30 days after the return receipt is filed for mail service; 45 days after first publication). Answer a cross-claim within 20 days after it is filed; reply to a counterclaim within 20 days after the answer is filed. After a 509.290–.320 motion is denied or deferred, respond within 10 days after notice (or 10 days after a more definite statement is filed), bu","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.270. Demurrers abolished","Missouri · civil rules","Demurrers and pleas in abatement or to the jurisdiction are not used.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.280. Form of motions","Missouri · civil rules","Written unless made at a hearing or trial, stating grounds with particularity and the relief sought.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.290. Defenses raised by motion","Missouri · civil rules","Lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction, improper venue, insufficient process or service, security for costs, lack of capacity, another action pending in Missouri, improper joinder of claims, and improper counterclaim or cross-claim; grounds may be shown by affidavit.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.300. Failure to state a claim or legal defense","Missouri · civil rules","May be raised by motion when apparent on the face of the pleadings.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.310. More definite statement; bill of particulars","Missouri · civil rules","Available when a pleading is too indefinite to respond or prepare for trial; if an order is not obeyed within 10 days after notice, the court may strike the pleading.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.320. Motion to strike","Missouri · civil rules","Redundant, immaterial, impertinent, or scandalous matter may be struck.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.330. Time for motions","Missouri · civil rules","Within the time to respond to the pleading or, if no response is permitted, within 20 days after service of the last pleading; motions and pleadings may be filed together without waiver.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.340. Consolidation and waiver of motion defenses","Missouri · civil rules","All available motion objections must be joined; objections not raised by motion within 509.330’s time are waived, except failure to state a claim or legal defense and lack of subject-matter jurisdiction.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.350. Motion searches the record","Missouri · civil rules","A motion against an answer or reply lets the court reach earlier pleadings and rule against whoever first failed to state a claim or defense.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.360. Judgment on the pleadings","Missouri · civil rules","After pleadings close, without delaying trial.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.370. Hearing motions before trial","Missouri · civil rules","Motion objections are decided before trial on application unless deferred for good cause.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.380. Hearings in chambers","Missouri · civil rules","Motions may be heard in chambers anywhere, but no non-ex parte hearing outside the county without consent.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.390. Motion days; written submission","Missouri · civil rules","Courts set regular motion times and may decide motions on written submissions without oral hearing.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.400. Defenses in responsive pleading; waiver","Missouri · civil rules","Defenses not raisable by motion go in the responsive pleading and are waived if omitted, except failure to state a claim or legal defense and lack of subject-matter jurisdiction.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.410. Lack of prior demand","Missouri · civil rules","Available only if pleaded with a tender of the amount due; a plaintiff who recovers no more than the tender pays all costs.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.420. Compulsory counterclaims","Missouri · civil rules","Any claim against an opposing party arising from the same transaction or occurrence must be pleaded, unless it is the subject of a pending action or needs parties beyond the court’s jurisdiction.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.430. Counterclaim may exceed claim","Missouri · civil rules","A counterclaim may seek more than, or different relief from, the opposing claim.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.440. After-acquired counterclaims","Missouri · civil rules","May be added by supplemental pleading with court permission.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.450. Omitted counterclaim","Missouri · civil rules","May be added by amendment with leave for oversight, inadvertence, excusable neglect, or when justice requires.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.460. Cross-claims","Missouri · civil rules","Against a coparty for claims from the same transaction or occurrence, including indemnity or contribution.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.470. Adding parties for counterclaim or cross-claim","Missouri · civil rules","The court orders necessary additional parties brought in if jurisdiction can be obtained.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.480. Assigned claims","Missouri · civil rules","Subject to counterclaims the obligor had against the assignor at notice of the assignment (except negotiable instruments law).","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.490. Amendment","Missouri · civil rules","As of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 30 days if no response is required and the case is not on the trial calendar; otherwise by leave (freely given) or written consent. Respond within the remaining original time or 10 days after service of the amendment, whichever is longer. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. ","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.500. Amendment to conform to evidence","Missouri · civil rules","Issues tried by consent are treated as pleaded; amendment may be made any time, even after judgment.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.510. Supplemental pleadings","Missouri · civil rules","On motion, for events after the original pleading.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 509.520. Redaction of personal identifiers","Missouri · civil rules","Since August 28, 2023, filings and court records must omit full Social Security, account, and driver’s license numbers, victim and witness contact information, a minor’s name, address, and birth date, and full birth dates of parties (year allowed except for minors). The information goes on a confidential information filing sheet; family cases need a confidential case filing sheet.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.010. Pretrial conference","Missouri · civil rules","The court may hold a conference on simplifying issues, amendments, admissions, and expert limits; the resulting order controls the case unless modified to prevent manifest injustice.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.020. Interrogatories (statutory)","Missouri · civil rules","Answers under oath within 15 days after delivery; objections within 10 days; only one set per party without leave. The statute is much stricter than practice under the Supreme Court Rules (not collected), which govern discovery timing and limits. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.030. Production and entry (statutory)","Missouri · civil rules","On motion for good cause the court may order production of non-privileged documents and things or entry on land. A party may obtain its own previously made statement without the required showing. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.040. Physical and mental examinations","Missouri · civil rules","On motion for good cause when condition is in controversy, by a physician chosen by the requesting party; the examinee gets the report free.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.050. Requests for admission (statutory)","Missouri · civil rules","After pleadings close, a party may request admission of document genuineness and related facts; matters are admitted unless a sworn specific denial or detailed explanation is served within the time designated (at least 10 days). Admissions bind only the pending action. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.060. Compelling answers; sanctions","Missouri · civil rules","Motion to compel with cost-shifting; for disobedience, orders deeming facts established, precluding evidence, striking pleadings, dismissal, or default. Costs of proving matters improperly denied may be awarded.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.070. Trial docket","Missouri · civil rules","Cases go on the trial docket as soon as possible after issue is joined.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.080. Continuance","Missouri · civil rules","For good cause, at the applicant’s cost if ordered.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.090. Continuance application","Missouri · civil rules","Written motion with affidavit, unless the adversary consents to an oral application.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.130. Voluntary dismissal","Missouri · civil rules","Plaintiff may dismiss without prejudice any time before final submission. After one dismissal and refiling, no dismissal without prejudice once the jury is sworn or evidence begins, absent stipulation or court order on affidavit. Broader than Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(a)(1), which ends unilateral dismissal at the answer or summary judgment motion. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict th","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.140. Involuntary dismissal","Missouri · civil rules","For failure to prosecute or comply with the Code or orders, and at the close of plaintiff’s evidence in a bench trial.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.150. Effect of dismissal","Missouri · civil rules","Without prejudice allows refiling; with prejudice adjudicates the merits. Involuntary dismissals (except jurisdiction or venue) are with prejudice unless the order says otherwise.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.160. Dismissal of counterclaims and cross-claims","Missouri · civil rules","510.130–.150 apply to counterclaims, cross-claims, and third-party claims.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.170. Counterclaims survive dismissal","Missouri · civil rules","Dismissing the plaintiff’s action does not dismiss a pending counterclaim or cross-claim.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.180. Consolidation; separate trials","Missouri · civil rules","Liquidated-demand suits may be consolidated; separate trials of claims or issues may be ordered for convenience or to avoid prejudice.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.190. Jury right and waiver","Missouri · civil rules","The jury right is preserved. Waiver by failing to appear, written or oral consent, or proceeding to bench trial without objection. Note the waiver-by-silence model: unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 38, no written demand is required by this statute.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.200. Open court","Missouri · civil rules","Trials on the merits are in open court; hearings outside the county need consent.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.210. No formal exceptions","Missouri · civil rules","Make the requested action or objection and grounds known when the ruling is made; grounds need not be stated for objections to instructions.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.220–.230. Verdicts","Missouri · civil rules","General or special verdicts; general verdicts in money and specific-property cases.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.261. Punitive damages: standard and pleading","Missouri · civil rules","Clear and convincing proof of intentional harm without just cause or deliberate and flagrant disregard for others’ safety; limits on employer liability for agents. No initial pleading may claim punitive damages: move for leave, with supporting evidence, at least 120 days before the final pretrial conference (or trial); the court rules within 45 days. Applies to actions filed on or after August 28, 2020 (510.262).","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.263. Bifurcated punitive damages trial","Missouri · civil rules","On any party’s request, the same jury decides liability and punitive liability first, then amount (net worth admissible only in stage two). Defendant may seek credit for prior punitive awards for the same conduct within the new-trial-motion time; remittitur and additur apply.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.265. Punitive damages cap","Missouri · civil rules","Greater of $500,000 or five times the net judgment, except when the state is plaintiff or the defendant pleads guilty to or is convicted of a related felony; listed housing-discrimination claims are excluded.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.270. Jury assesses money recovery","Missouri · civil rules","The jury fixes the amount and states punitive damages separately.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.280. Directed verdict","Missouri · civil rules","Replaces demurrer to the evidence; moving does not waive the right to offer evidence or the jury.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.290. Post-verdict motion for judgment","Missouri · civil rules","A party who moved for directed verdict at the close of all evidence may move within 10 days after verdict (or jury discharge) for judgment in accordance with that motion, with or without an alternative new-trial request. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.300. Instructions","Missouri · civil rules","Written instructions on request at the close of evidence; counsel may object outside the jury’s hearing; given and refused instructions are filed.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.310. Bench trials","Missouri · civil rules","On request before submission the court states grounds and, if requested, findings on controverted facts; unaddressed facts are deemed found for the result. Motion to amend the judgment within 10 days after entry. Sufficiency of evidence may be raised on appeal without a trial-court objection. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.330. New trial","Missouri · civil rules","On traditional grounds, for all or some parties and issues; only one new trial on weight of the evidence; the order must state its grounds.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.350. New trial affidavits","Missouri · civil rules","Served with the motion; opposing affidavits within 10 days, extendable up to 20 more days. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.360. New trial motion deemed denied","Missouri · civil rules","If not ruled on within 90 days after filing, the motion is deemed denied for all purposes. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.370. New trial on the court’s own initiative","Missouri · civil rules","Within 30 days after entry of judgment, stating the grounds. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.380. JNOV and arrest of judgment abolished","Missouri · civil rules","Those objections are raised in a new-trial motion or a motion filed at the same time.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 510.390. Substitute judge","Missouri · civil rules","Another judge may complete post-verdict duties or, if unable, grant a new trial.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 511.020. Judgment defined","Missouri · civil rules","The final determination of the parties’ rights in the action.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 511.030. Scope of judgment","Missouri · civil rules","For or against any of several parties, determining their rights among themselves; special protections for minors and disabled persons.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 511.060. Setting aside within 30 days","Missouri · civil rules","For good cause, within 30 days after entry. Check the parallel Supreme Court Rule (not collected); where they conflict the rule usually controls (Mo. Const. art. V, § 5).","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 511.110. Interlocutory default","Missouri · civil rules","Entered against a defendant who fails to answer or plead in time.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 511.120. Setting aside interlocutory default","Missouri · civil rules","For good cause before damages are assessed or final judgment.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 511.140–.150. Damages on default","Missouri · civil rules","The court assesses liquidated damages on written instruments; otherwise plaintiff may demand a jury, or the court assesses.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 511.160. Default relief limited to the petition","Missouri · civil rules","Relief on default may not exceed what the petition as filed and served demanded.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 511.170–.190. Petition for review of default","Missouri · civil rules","A defendant not served or not appearing may petition for review for good cause; the judgment becomes absolute after 3 years.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 511.250. Irregularity motions","Missouri · civil rules","Must be made within 3 years after the term of the judgment.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 511.350–.360. Judgment liens","Missouri · civil rules","Circuit, appellate, Supreme Court, and federal judgments in Missouri are liens on the debtor’s real estate in the county (associate division judgments only after filing with the circuit clerk), for 10 years, reaching after-acquired land.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 511.370. Revival","Missouri · civil rules","Scire facias to revive may issue within 10 years of the judgment, not after.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 511.760. Uniform Enforcement of Foreign Judgments","Missouri · civil rules","Registration of sister-state and federal judgments by verified petition within the time to sue on them.","#st-mo-rules"],["Rule RSMo 511.770–.787. Uniform Foreign-Country Money Judgments Recognition","Missouri · civil rules","Recognition of foreign-country money judgments, grounds for nonrecognition, and stays pending appeal abroad.","#st-mo-rules"],["Appeals in Missouri: the statute gives you 10 days after finality, and the Rules decide when that is","Missouri · appeals","Most civil appeals go to one of the three districts of the Missouri Court of Appeals. The Supreme Court has exclusive appellate jurisdiction only over a short constitutional list (validity of statutes, revenue-law construction, title to state office, death sentences) and otherwise reviews cases by transfer.\nThe notice of appeal is due within 10 days after the judgment becomes final, a much shorter window than the fed","#st-mo-appeals"],["Court of Appeals unless the Supreme Court has exclusive jurisdiction","Missouri · appeals · Where the appeal goes","The Supreme Court has exclusive appellate jurisdiction over cases involving the validity of a U.S. treaty or statute or a Missouri statute or constitutional provision, construction of the revenue laws, title to a state office, and death sentences. The Court of Appeals has general appellate jurisdiction over everything else. Mo. Const. art. V, § 3","#st-mo-appeals"],["Pick the district by county","Missouri · appeals · Where the appeal goes","The Eastern District covers the listed eastern counties and the City of St. Louis, the Southern District covers the listed southern counties, and the Western District covers every other county. RSMo 477.040–.070","#st-mo-appeals"],["Wrong court means transfer, not dismissal","Missouri · appeals · Where the appeal goes","An appeal sent to the wrong district or court is transferred to the right one; want of jurisdiction is not a ground for dismissal. Mo. Const. art. V, § 11; RSMo 477.080","#st-mo-appeals"],["Final judgments and a short list of interlocutory orders","Missouri · appeals · What can be appealed","An aggrieved party may appeal from a final judgment, a special order after final judgment, an order granting a new trial, an order refusing to revoke or modify an interlocutory receivership order or dissolving an injunction, and interlocutory partition judgments determining rights. Orders granting or denying class certification are appealable only if the Court of Appeals permits, and such an appeal does not stay the ","#st-mo-appeals"],["What a judgment is","Missouri · appeals · What can be appealed","A “judgment” is the final determination of the parties’ rights in the action. Requirements that a judgment be denominated as such, and rules for certifying partial judgments, are in the Supreme Court Rules (not collected). RSMo 511.020","#st-mo-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP denials: immediate appeal as of right","Missouri · appeals · What can be appealed","A moving party may appeal within 21 days, as of right, an order denying (in whole or part) a UPEPA special motion to dismiss. Any appeal of a ruling on the motion stays all proceedings between all parties. RSMo 537.529.11, .6(3)","#st-mo-appeals"],["Associate circuit cases: trial de novo or record appeal","Missouri · appeals · What can be appealed","Judgments in small-claims-type and landlord-tenant cases tried before an associate circuit judge (chapters 482 and 535) and in municipal court get a trial de novo; apply with the clerk within 10 days after judgment. Bond is needed to stay execution. Other contested cases before associate circuit judges are appealed on the record like circuit cases. RSMo 512.180, 512.190","#st-mo-appeals"],["File in the trial court within 10 days after the judgment becomes final","Missouri · appeals · Notice of appeal","Appeal by filing a notice of appeal with the trial court clerk. It is not effective unless filed within 10 days after the judgment or order appealed from becomes final. Later steps are not jurisdictional, but failing to take them can lead to dismissal. RSMo 512.050","#st-mo-appeals"],["Contents and clerk’s duties","Missouri · appeals · Notice of appeal","Name the appealing parties, the judgment or order appealed from, and the appellate court. The clerk mails the notice to other parties and sends it with the docket fee to the appellate clerk; clerk errors do not affect validity. RSMo 512.070","#st-mo-appeals"],["Late notice only by special order","Missouri · appeals · Notice of appeal","Under the statute, if the ordinary time has run, the appellate court may allow a notice of appeal within six months of the final judgment by special order. You must show merit and that the delay was not due to culpable negligence. The current Supreme Court Rule may set a different period; not verified. RSMo 512.060","#st-mo-appeals"],["Court reporter must be paid within 10 days","Missouri · appeals · Notice of appeal","Transcript charges are due within 10 days after ordering the transcript, or a deposit of estimated charges within 10 days after the reporter’s written estimate. The reporter must give the estimate within 10 days of the request. RSMo 512.050","#st-mo-appeals"],["New-trial and post-verdict motions (statutory text)","Missouri · appeals · Post-trial motions and finality","Under the statute, a motion for judgment on a directed-verdict motion is due within 10 days after verdict, JNOV and arrest of judgment are abolished (raise those points in the new-trial motion), and in a bench trial a motion to amend the judgment is due within 10 days after entry. The current timing is set by Supreme Court Rule and was not verified. RSMo 510.290, 510.380, 510.310.3","#st-mo-appeals"],["Deemed denial at 90 days; court’s own control for 30 days","Missouri · appeals · Post-trial motions and finality","A new-trial motion not ruled on within 90 days after filing is deemed denied for all purposes. The court may order a new trial on its own within 30 days after entry of judgment, and may set aside a judgment for good cause within 30 days. How these events fix the finality date that starts the 10-day appeal clock is set by Supreme Court Rule (not collected). RSMo 510.360, 510.370, 511.060","#st-mo-appeals"],["Preserve error in the trial court","Missouri · appeals · Post-trial motions and finality","Except for subject-matter jurisdiction and failure to state a claim or defense, no error is considered on appeal unless presented to or decided by the trial court. In bench-tried cases, sufficiency of the evidence may be raised without objection below. RSMo 512.160.1, 510.310.4","#st-mo-appeals"],["Supersedeas bond stays execution","Missouri · appeals · Stays and bonds","An appeal stays execution if the appellant is a personal representative, guardian, conservator, or local government, or posts a court-approved supersedeas bond at or before filing the notice of appeal. The court may set the amount and allow up to 20 days to file it. For money judgments, the bond covers the unpaid judgment, appeal costs, interest, and delay damages unless the court sets a different amount for good cau","#st-mo-appeals"],["Late bonds; $50 million cap in tort cases","Missouri · appeals · Stays and bonds","A late or insufficient bond may be cured in the trial court before transfer, and afterward only in the appellate court. In cases with any tort count, the total bond for all appellants may not exceed $50 million; the cap can be lifted if the appellant dissipates assets. RSMo 512.090, 512.099","#st-mo-appeals"],["Transcript within 90 days of the notice","Missouri · appeals · Record on appeal","The transcript on appeal must be filed and the case transferred to the appellate court within 90 days after the notice of appeal is filed. The trial court may extend this under 506.060. Parties may agree to an abbreviated transcript (with trial court approval) or an agreed statement of the case. RSMo 512.110, 512.120, 512.130, 512.140","#st-mo-appeals"],["Briefing rules come from the appellate courts","Missouri · appeals · Briefs and decision","Brief form, timing, number of copies, service, docketing, and argument are set by appellate court rule (Supreme Court Rule 84 and district local rules). Word limits and brief deadlines were not verified because the rules could not be collected. RSMo 512.150; Mo. Sup. Ct. R. 84 (not collected)","#st-mo-appeals"],["Reversal only for prejudicial error; frivolous-appeal damages","Missouri · appeals · Briefs and decision","Reversal requires error materially affecting the merits. The court should finally dispose of the case where possible. On affirmance or dismissal it may award the respondent damages of up to 10% of the judgment. RSMo 512.160.2–.4","#st-mo-appeals"],["Transfer from the Court of Appeals","Missouri · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court","A case goes to the Supreme Court if a dissenting judge certifies that the majority opinion conflicts with prior precedent. It may also be transferred by the Court of Appeals district after opinion, or by the Supreme Court before or after opinion, for general interest or importance, to reexamine existing law, or under Supreme Court rule. The Supreme Court then decides the case as on original appeal. Transfer applicati","#st-mo-appeals"],["En banc transfer within the Supreme Court","Missouri · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court","A case in a Supreme Court division goes en banc on an even split, by the division’s order, on the losing party’s application when a division member dissents, or under court rule. Mo. Const. art. V, § 9","#st-mo-appeals"],["Notice of appeal","Missouri · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from judgment or order appealed from becomes final.\nFinality timing is set by Supreme Court Rule (Rules 81–84), not collected; verify. RSMo 512.050","#st-mo-appeals"],["Late notice of appeal by special order of appellate court","Missouri · appeals · Deadlines","180 days from date of final judgment.\nThe statute says six months; the current Supreme Court Rule may differ; not verified. RSMo 512.060","#st-mo-appeals"],["Appeal from denial of UPEPA (anti-SLAPP) special motion to dismiss","Missouri · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from order denying the motion in whole or in part.\nActions filed on or after Aug. 28, 2026. RSMo 537.529.11","#st-mo-appeals"],["Transcript filed and case transferred to appellate court","Missouri · appeals · Deadlines","90 days from filing of notice of appeal.\nTrial court may extend under RSMo 506.060.2. RSMo 512.130","#st-mo-appeals"],["Pay court reporter for transcript","Missouri · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from ordering the transcript (or the reporter’s written estimate). RSMo 512.050","#st-mo-appeals"],["File supersedeas bond after court sets amount","Missouri · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from order fixing bond (maximum allowed).\nBond must be presented at or before filing the notice of appeal unless time is allowed. RSMo 512.080.1(2)","#st-mo-appeals"],["New-trial motion deemed denied","Missouri · appeals · Deadlines","90 days from filing of the motion.\nStatutory; confirm against the current Supreme Court Rule. RSMo 510.360","#st-mo-appeals"],["Court’s own order of new trial; setting aside judgment for good cause","Missouri · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of judgment.\nStatutory; confirm against the current Supreme Court Rule. RSMo 510.370, 511.060","#st-mo-appeals"],["Application for trial de novo (associate circuit cases under ch. 482, 535, municipal)","Missouri · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from judgment rendered. RSMo 512.190","#st-mo-appeals"],["NMI Supreme Court (Commonwealth Supreme Court)","Northern Mariana Islands · courts","A chief justice and at least two associate justices; appeals are decided by three justices. Hears appeals from judgments and orders of the Superior Court, issues writs, answers certified questions from federal courts and from Commonwealth officials, has appellate jurisdiction over attorney discipline, and controls bar admission and court rules. It cannot take new evidence and reviews findings of fact only for clear e","#st-mp"],["Commonwealth Superior Court","Northern Mariana Islands · courts","The single general-jurisdiction trial court for all civil (law and equity) and criminal cases. A presiding judge and at least four associate judges; at least one full-time judge must be assigned to Rota and one to Tinian. The Family Court and the Drug Court are divisions within it. There is no intermediate appellate court.","#st-mp"],["Small claims (within the Superior Court)","Northern Mariana Islands · courts","Small claims are heard in the Superior Court. The Judiciary Fee Schedule has small-claims filing tiers up to $5,000, and the civil rules say former Commonwealth Rule 83 governs small claims until separate Small Claims Rules are adopted. The jurisdictional dollar limit was not confirmed from the collected texts.","#st-mp"],["U.S. District Court for the Northern Mariana Islands","Northern Mariana Islands · courts","A federal territorial court, separate from the Commonwealth courts and covered elsewhere in Consi. Its unpublished trial and appellate division opinions may be cited in the Superior Court under NMI R. Civ. P. 82.2(a). This page covers only the Commonwealth courts.","#st-mp"],["No anti-SLAPP statute found in the Commonwealth Code","Northern Mariana Islands · anti-SLAPP","The CNMI has no anti-SLAPP statute that could be found. Searches of the Law Revision Commission’s official Commonwealth Code (by section title, for “SLAPP,” “strategic lawsuit,” “public participation,” and “special motion”) and its site-wide search across public laws, the Code, and the Commonwealth Register found no anti-SLAPP law. The only site-wide “SLAPP” hit was the word “slapping” in a 2025 child-care regulation","#st-mp-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: who is eligible","Northern Mariana Islands · out of state","To appear pro hac vice, the lawyer must:\n1. practice law outside the Commonwealth;\n2. be in good standing everywhere admitted;\n3. associate with a local attorney in good standing;\n4. not be admitted in the Commonwealth;\n5. not keep an office in the Commonwealth;\n6. never have been denied admission to the bar; and\n7. not live in the Commonwealth. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 73-1(a) (Rules of Admission, eff. Aug. 4, 2025)","#st-mp-oos"],["Apply to the Supreme Court, not the trial judge; $5,000 fee","Northern Mariana Islands · out of state","File the application with the Commonwealth Supreme Court. It must show that you meet Rule 73-1, give the name and business address of the local attorney, and explain why the client needs you in particular in the Commonwealth courts.\nPay $5,000 to the Commonwealth Treasury and attach the receipt, or a check or money order payable to the Treasury. A lawyer representing a Commonwealth or U.S. governmental agency or inst","#st-mp-oos"],["One new case per calendar year; admission expires after 12 months","Northern Mariana Islands · out of state","You may be admitted pro hac vice in only one new case per calendar year. You cannot apply in a second case until the first case is closed. Unless the order sets a different date, admission expires 12 months after it is granted. To keep going after that, you must file a new application and pay a new $5,000 fee. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 73-1(f)","#st-mp-oos"],["Local counsel must take part in the case","Northern Mariana Islands · out of state","The civil rules require any off-island counsel to associate with local counsel. Off-island counsel means a lawyer admitted in the Commonwealth who does not regularly keep an office there. Local counsel must keep an office in the Commonwealth and regularly practice in its courts.\nLocal counsel has full authority as attorney of record and must take a meaningful part in preparing and trying the case. Local counsel must ","#st-mp-oos"],["E-filing registration is mandatory, including for pro hac vice counsel","Northern Mariana Islands · out of state","All attorneys must register with the court’s E-system and use it, including lawyers admitted pro hac vice and lawyers representing themselves. A pro hac vice lawyer must register once admitted and must enter an appearance in the case. Every e-filed document must be signed electronically (for example, “/s/”) and show your name, mailing address, email, phone, and bar number. NMI R. Elec. Filing 4(a)–(b), 8(a), 8(c) (ef","#st-mp-oos"],["Pro hac vice lawyers are subject to CNMI discipline","Northern Mariana Islands · out of state","The attorney discipline rules define “attorney” to include anyone admitted pro hac vice. They also cover suspended lawyers, government attorneys with provisional licenses, and legal interns. You are subject to discipline for conduct inside or outside the Commonwealth. NMI R. Att’y Disc. & P. 1(b), 3","#st-mp-oos"],["Full admission: NextGen UBE, or the Attorney’s Exam for experienced lawyers","Northern Mariana Islands · out of state","Regular applicants take the NextGen UBE plus the Local Content Essays (LCE), which start in July 2026. The passing score is 615 on the NextGen UBE and 26 on the LCE.\nA lawyer licensed in any U.S. state, territory, or possession who has actively practiced for at least 5 of the last 10 years may instead take the Attorney’s Exam. That exam is only the LCE, and the passing score is 26. Attorney applicants in good standin","#st-mp-oos"],["Government and legal-services lawyers: limited admission","Northern Mariana Islands · out of state","A salaried lawyer for the Commonwealth Government or Micronesian Legal Services Corporation who passed a bar exam and is admitted in another U.S. jurisdiction may get limited admission without an exam. It covers only work for that employer. The term is 4 years, and it may be extended once for 4 more years if you apply at least 30 days before it expires. You must report employment changes within 7 days. You also need ","#st-mp-oos"],["Guam reciprocity: listed on the Judiciary site, rule not found","Northern Mariana Islands · out of state","The Judiciary’s Bar Admissions page offers a “Guam Attorney Reciprocity Application” with a $100 application fee. The Rules of Admission effective August 4, 2025 that were collected here do not contain a reciprocity rule. Ask the Bar Administrator about eligibility, and find the governing order, before relying on it. nmijudiciary.gov/bar-admissions (form list); no rule located in NMI Sup. Ct. R. 70–75","#st-mp-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances: no rule found","Northern Mariana Islands · out of state","The collected civil rules, Supreme Court rules, and e-filing rules have no limited-scope or unbundled appearance rule. The Superior Court Rules of Practice could not be checked because most of the posted PDF is scanned images with no text layer. Assume an appearance is for the whole case unless the court orders otherwise. None found in NMI R. Civ. P., NMI Sup. Ct. R., or NMI R. Elec. Filing","#st-mp-oos"],["Leaving the islands: file an Off-Island Report 15 days ahead","Northern Mariana Islands · out of state","A lawyer who will be away from the Commonwealth, or unavailable at a regular Commonwealth office, for 3 or more consecutive days must file an Off-Island Report. File it through the CNMI Bar Association website at least 15 days before the absence begins. NMI R. Civ. P. 82.1","#st-mp-oos"],["Governing code: the most recent ABA Model Rules, adopted by reference","Northern Mariana Islands · ethics","The CNMI has no separate code of professional conduct of its own. Lawyers may be disciplined for any act or omission that violates “the most recent version” of the ABA Model Rules of Professional Conduct. Legal interns must swear that they will follow the ABA Model Rules. In practice, the current Model Rules and their comments are the text to read.\nBecause the adoption is dynamic, ABA amendments apply automatically, ","#st-mp-ethics"],["Cannabis carve-out","Northern Mariana Islands · ethics","A lawyer may counsel or assist a client about the CNMI’s marijuana and cannabis laws without violating the Model Rules. If CNMI law differs from federal law, the lawyer must also advise the client about federal law and policy. NMI R. Att’y Disc. & P. 3(1)","#st-mp-ethics"],["Extra grounds for discipline beyond the Model Rules","Northern Mariana Islands · ethics","Beyond the Model Rules, Rule 3 makes these grounds for discipline:\n• any act of moral turpitude, dishonesty, or corruption, even without a conviction;\n• willful violation of a court order;\n• violating the attorney’s oath or duties;\n• appearing for a party without authority;\n• misstatements in an admission or reinstatement application;\n• failing to self-report discipline imposed anywhere else;\n• practicing with a disb","#st-mp-ethics"],["Report discipline elsewhere within 15 days","Northern Mariana Islands · ethics","You must notify the NMI Supreme Court of any pending disciplinary action against you in another jurisdiction within 15 days after you learn of it. Identify the jurisdiction and summarize the matter. Then send a copy of the final decision within 15 days after it issues.\nFor reciprocal discipline, the Court issues an order to show cause, and you have 30 days to respond. To avoid it, you must show by clear and convincin","#st-mp-ethics"],["10-year limitation period; hearings in the Superior Court","Northern Mariana Islands · ethics","Discipline must be sought within 10 years after the act is completed. That limit does not apply to criminal-conviction or reciprocal-discipline proceedings, or to conduct the lawyer intentionally concealed.\nThe Bar’s Disciplinary Committee prosecutes, and the Superior Court holds the hearing. The respondent must answer within 20 days after service of the complaint, or the charges are deemed admitted. The hearing is h","#st-mp-ethics"],["Post-trial motions: 10 days, not 28","Northern Mariana Islands · land mine","A Rule 59(e) motion to alter or amend is due 10 days after entry of judgment. So are a Rule 50(b) renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law and a Rule 52(b) motion to amend findings. A motion for a new trial is due in 20 days, and the court’s own power to order a new trial ends at 20 days. The federal rule allows 28 days for each.\nThe court cannot extend any of these deadlines. Because 10 days is “10 days or les","#st-mp-unique"],["A late post-judgment motion does not toll the appeal","Northern Mariana Islands · land mine","The 30-day appeal clock restarts only for motions filed “within the time allotted” by the NMI civil rules. A Rule 59(e) motion filed on day 20 under the federal habit is untimely and does not toll. A Rule 60 motion tolls only if filed within 28 days after judgment. The Supreme Court cannot extend the notice-of-appeal deadline except under Rule 4(a)(5)–(6). NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(1), 4(a)(4)(A), 26(b)(1)","#st-mp-unique"],["30 days to appeal, even when the government is a party","Northern Mariana Islands · land mine","File the notice of appeal with the Superior Court clerk, not the Supreme Court, within 30 days after entry. There is no 60-day period for government cases as in federal court. Other parties get 14 days after the first notice, or the regular 30 days if that ends later. Pay the $175 fee when you file. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3(a)(1), 3(e), 4(a)(1), 4(a)(3); Fee Schedule item 81","#st-mp-unique"],["No separate judgment document means a 150-day wait","Northern Mariana Islands · land mine","The notice of appeal must attach the judgment or order being appealed. In a civil case that means a separate entry of judgment. Without a separate document, the judgment is treated as entered only after 150 days have run from its docketing. A premature notice is held until the separate judgment is filed. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3(c)(1)(B) & note 1, 4(a)(2), 4(a)(7)(A)(ii)","#st-mp-unique"],["Pro hac vice is $5,000 and one new case a year","Northern Mariana Islands · land mine","The fee is $5,000 per application, and you may apply in only one new case per calendar year. You cannot seek admission in a second case until the first is closed. Admission lapses after 12 months, and renewing requires a new application and a new fee. Budget for it, and plan for long cases. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 73-1(c), (f); Fee Schedule item 116","#st-mp-unique"],["Off-island counsel need active local counsel","Northern Mariana Islands · land mine","Even a CNMI-admitted lawyer without a regular Commonwealth office must associate local counsel. Local counsel must take a meaningful part in preparing and trying the case and attend proceedings. Service on local counsel binds you. NMI R. Civ. P. 5(b)(4)","#st-mp-unique"],["Motions must be filed 30 days before the hearing; opposition is due in 9","Northern Mariana Islands · land mine","Do not choose a hearing date yourself; the clerk sets it. Your opposition is due 9 days after the motion is served, which is 9 days excluding weekends and holidays. It is not keyed to the hearing date as in many state courts. A memorandum over 25 pages filed without permission may be struck. NMI R. Civ. P. 6(c)(1)–(2), 7(b)(5)–(6)","#st-mp-unique"],["Jury demand: 60 days, a $400 fee, and 6 unanimous jurors","Northern Mariana Islands · land mine","Serve the jury demand no later than 60 days after the last pleading is filed. Pay the non-refundable $400 jury fee on or before the day the demand is filed; without proper service, filing, and payment, the jury is waived. A proper demand can be withdrawn only by consent and no later than 45 days before trial. The verdict must be unanimous and returned by 6 jurors. Jury trials also require advance deposits: $1,500 for","#st-mp-unique"],["No mail service in the last 10 days before a hearing or trial","Northern Mariana Islands · land mine","Papers about a motion with a set hearing date, or about a matter set for trial, may not be served by mail within 10 days of the hearing or trial date. Use e-service or hand delivery. NMI R. Civ. P. 5(b)(2)(C)","#st-mp-unique"],["Supreme Court docketing statement within 15 days or risk dismissal","Northern Mariana Islands · land mine","Within 15 days after the Clerk gives notice that the appeal is docketed, file the docketing statement (Form 2). Extensions are disfavored, and failing to file is grounds for dismissal. Also within 15 days after filing the notice of appeal, order transcripts on Form 3 or certify that none will be ordered. Opposing counsel must respond or file an appearance within 10 days after being served with the docketing statement","#st-mp-unique"],["Briefs: 35 pages including everything","Northern Mariana Islands · land mine","A principal brief is limited to 35 pages and a reply to 20, “inclusive of the caption, headings, table of contents, etc.” The limit is pages, not words. Briefs use 11-point Times New Roman with 1.5 spacing. A brief that does not conform may be struck, fined, or lead to dismissal. Only one automatic 30-day extension is available per brief. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 31-1(a)(1), 32(a)(4)–(7), 32(i)","#st-mp-unique"],["Slander has a 2-year limit","Northern Mariana Islands · land mine","Actions for slander, assault and battery, false imprisonment, medical malpractice, and wrongful death or personal injury must be filed within 2 years. Most other actions get 6 years. Do not assume a one-year defamation period as in many states, or a three-year one. 7 CMC §§ 2503, 2505","#st-mp-unique"],["No peremptory challenge; disqualification for cause by statute","Northern Mariana Islands · judges","The collected statutes and rules give no peremptory change of judge. Disqualification is governed by statute. A justice or judge must step aside whenever impartiality might reasonably be questioned. The statute also lists specific grounds:\n• personal bias, or personal knowledge of disputed facts;\n• prior work on the matter as a lawyer, or by a former law partner;\n• government work on the matter;\n• a financial interes","#st-mp-unique"],["Procedure: self-recusal or a motion stating specific grounds","Northern Mariana Islands · judges","A judge who sees grounds for disqualification must recuse on the judge’s own initiative and enter a written recusal order. The clerk sends it to any party not present. A party may move to disqualify, stating the grounds specifically. The statute sets no deadline for the motion, so file promptly; case law on timeliness was not checked. 1 CMC § 3309(a)–(b)","#st-mp-unique"],["Supreme Court: mandatory notice of potential conflicts","Northern Mariana Islands · judges","With its first docketing statement, response, or notice of appearance, each party in a Supreme Court appeal must file a notice listing any potential § 3308 conflicts that might keep a justice from sitting, or state that it knows of none. This notice is not a motion to disqualify, and listing a conflict does not prevent a later motion. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3-1(c) & note 2","#st-mp-unique"],["Pro tem justices and judges may come from outside the CNMI","Northern Mariana Islands · judges","The Chief Justice may designate an active or former justice or judge of the Commonwealth, of any U.S. jurisdiction, or of the Federated States of Micronesia, Palau, or the Marshall Islands to sit pro tem. Expect visiting jurists when local judges are disqualified. NMI Const. art. IV, § 9(d)","#st-mp-unique"],["NMI Const. art. I, § 2 | Text mirrors the First Amendment","Northern Mariana Islands · speech","Article I, § 2 bars laws respecting an establishment of religion or abridging free exercise, speech, press, peaceable assembly, or petitioning the government. Its wording tracks the First Amendment. Whether CNMI courts read it more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked; no case law was collected. NMI Const. art. I, § 2","#st-mp-unique"],["Express constitutional right of privacy, with a compelling-interest test","Northern Mariana Islands · speech","Article I, § 10 states that the right of individual privacy “shall not be infringed except upon a showing of compelling interest.” The Law Revision Commission’s annotation ties it to the invasion-of-privacy action under 7 CMC §§ 2411–2412. Privacy claims therefore sit alongside speech claims. NMI Const. art. I, § 10","#st-mp-unique"],["Single-publication rule and a short slander limitation","Northern Mariana Islands · speech","One edition, broadcast, or exhibition supports only one cause of action for libel, slander, or invasion of privacy, and the recovery covers damages in all jurisdictions. Slander must be sued on within 2 years. Libel is not listed in the 2-year statute, so on the statute’s face it falls under the 6-year catch-all. Confirm with case law before relying on the longer period. 7 CMC §§ 2411, 2503(a), 2505","#st-mp-unique"],["Restatements are the default rules of decision","Northern Mariana Islands · unique rules","When there is no written law or local customary law, CNMI courts apply the common law as expressed in the American Law Institute’s Restatements. Where the Restatements are silent, they apply the common law as generally understood in the United States. 7 CMC § 3401","#st-mp-unique"],["Court rules take effect only after the Legislature has its chance to veto","Northern Mariana Islands · unique rules","Proposed court rules go to the Legislature and take effect 60 days later unless a majority of either house disapproves. The Legislature may also annul or amend any rule by statute. Each adoption order recites this 60-day period, so check the order’s effective date, not the date it was signed. NMI Const. art. IV, § 9(a); 1 CMC § 3403(c)","#st-mp-unique"],["Superior Court motion practice","Northern Mariana Islands · unique rules","The filing clerk, not counsel, sets the hearing date. A pre-judgment motion must be filed and served at least 30 days before the hearing. The opposition is due 9 days after service and the reply 6 days after that; both periods are 10 days or less, so weekends and holidays are skipped. Memoranda are limited to 25 pages, and replies to 15. A memorandum is required when a motion turns on interpreting the law. At the hea","#st-mp-unique"],["Short periods skip weekends in both courts","Northern Mariana Islands · unique rules","In the Superior Court and the Supreme Court, a period of 10 days or less excludes intermediate Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays, unless the rule says “calendar days.” Longer periods count every day. NMI R. Civ. P. 6(a)(1)(D); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 26(a)(2)","#st-mp-unique"],["E-filing deadline is 11:59 p.m. Chamorro Standard Time","Northern Mariana Islands · unique rules","E-filing is available at all hours. A document is timely if submitted by 11:59 p.m. ChST, and the date relates back if the Clerk accepts it later. An outage does not extend deadlines. If the system is down more than 48 hours, file in person or by email to the Clerk.\nThe e-filing rules add 1 day to respond after e-service. The civil rules add 3 days after mail and certain other service, and the Supreme Court rules add","#st-mp-unique"],["Citation rules: local cases, Am. Jur./C.J.S., and unpublished decisions","Northern Mariana Islands · unique rules","Supreme Court briefs must cite relevant local cases, and failing to cite binding local authority can bring sanctions. In the Superior Court, citing Am. Jur. or C.J.S. as primary authority is “strictly prohibited” unless a parenthetical explains why no primary authority could be found. Before citing an unpublished Superior Court or Supreme Court decision, give all parties written notice and copies at least 48 hours be","#st-mp-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope and purpose; definitions","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Governs all civil suits in the Superior Court, at law or in equity, construed for just, speedy, and inexpensive determination. Applies to actions filed after the January 9, 2019 effective date. Defines “address” as physical and mailing address; a Commonwealth physical address must be described well enough to identify the property.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","There is one form of action: the civil action.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencing an action","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","A civil action is commenced by filing a complaint with the Superior Court.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 4. Summons","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 4 with Commonwealth changes. A request to waive service must allow 30 days to return it, or 60 days if the defendant is outside the Commonwealth. A defendant who waives has 60 days to answer, or 90 days if outside the Commonwealth. Serve a corporation through an officer or agent, or the resident agent shown on its last annual report to the Registrar. Serve the Commonwealth by delivery to the Attorney Gen","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 5. Serving and filing pleadings and other papers","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Service on an attorney may be by mail to the attorney’s registered CNMI Bar or other state-bar address. Mail service is not allowed for papers about a motion set for hearing, or a matter set for trial, within 10 days of that date. Off-island counsel must associate local counsel who keeps a Commonwealth office and takes a meaningful part in the case, and service on local counsel is effective (Rule 5(b)(4)). Discovery ","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 6. Time","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Does not track the federal rule. Periods of 10 days or less exclude intermediate weekends and legal holidays unless stated in calendar days. Courts may not extend the times under Rules 50(b), (c)(2), 52(b), 59(b), (d), (e), and 60(b). A pre-judgment motion must be filed and served at least 30 days before the hearing; the opposition is due 9 days after service and the reply 6 days after the opposition. For post-judgme","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings allowed; form of motions; time limits","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Pleadings as in FRCP 7(a). A memorandum is required when a motion turns on interpretation of law. Memoranda are limited to 25 pages and replies to 15, and the court may strike excess pages or the whole memorandum. The clerk, not counsel, sets the hearing date and time on the notice of hearing. The court may rule without a hearing after giving 2 days to object. Papers must be 11-point Times New Roman or another serif ","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 7.1. Disclosure statement","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","A nongovernmental corporate party must file 2 copies of a statement identifying any parent corporation and any public company owning 10% or more of its stock, or stating there is none. File it with the first appearance or filing, and supplement it promptly if the information changes.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 8: a short and plain statement of the claim and relief, admissions and denials, and affirmative defenses (the listed defenses include the statute of frauds).","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 9. Raise capacity by specific denial, plead fraud or mistake with particularity, and specifically plead special damages.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Every pleading needs a caption with the court, title, file number, and the Rule 7(e) designation, and the complaint must name all parties. Use numbered paragraphs, with a separate count for each transaction where that helps clarity. A written-instrument exhibit is part of the pleading. The rule does not track the federal text.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing papers; representations; sanctions","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 11, including the 21-day safe harbor to withdraw or correct a challenged paper. Papers must also list an email address and be filed and signed under the e-filing rules.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections; motions","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","An answer is due 21 days after service, or 60 days after a waiver request is sent (90 days if sent outside any U.S. jurisdiction). The Commonwealth, its agencies, and officers sued officially get 30 days after service on the Attorney General (federal: 60 days). Officers sued individually get 30 days after the later of service on them or on the Attorney General. After a Rule 12 motion is denied, the answer is due in 1","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and crossclaim","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 13 on compulsory and permissive counterclaims and crossclaims.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 14. A defendant needs leave to file a third-party complaint more than 14 days after serving its original answer.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 15. Amendment once as of course within 21 days after serving the pleading, or 21 days after a responsive pleading or Rule 12 motion; otherwise with written consent or leave. A response to an amended pleading is due within the remaining time or 14 days after service, whichever is later. Relation back follows the federal rule.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 16. Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Does not track the federal text. The clerk must set a mandatory scheduling conference within 90 days after the complaint is filed, or within 45 days after the last permitted pleading, whichever is later. The scheduling order limits time to join parties, amend, file motions, and complete discovery, and changes need good cause. The final pretrial order may be modified only to prevent manifest injustice. For noncomplian","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 17. Plaintiff and defendant; capacity; public officers","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 17 on real party in interest, capacity, and minors or incompetent persons.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 18: a party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 19. Required joinder of parties","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 19, subject to Rule 23.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 20.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 21. Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may add, drop, or sever parties and claims.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 22.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 23, including settlement approval and class-counsel fees. The NMI Supreme Court may permit an appeal from an order granting or denying certification if a petition is filed with the Supreme Court clerk within 14 days after the order. The appeal does not stay the case unless ordered.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 23.1. Derivative actions","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 23.1.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 23.2. Actions relating to unincorporated associations","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 23.2.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 24 on intervention of right and permissive intervention.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 25. After death, a motion to substitute must be made within 90 days after service of a statement noting the death, or the action against the decedent must be dismissed.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 26. Duty to disclose; general discovery provisions","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Largely mirrors FRCP 26, including proportionality. Initial disclosures are due within 14 days after the Rule 26(f) conference, or 30 days after a later party is served or joined. Expert disclosures are due at least 90 days before trial, and rebuttal within 30 days after the other side’s. Pretrial disclosures are due at least 30 days before trial, with objections within 14 days. Rule 34 requests may be delivered earl","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions to perpetuate testimony","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 27. Serve the petition and notice at least 21 days before the hearing.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 28.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations about discovery procedure","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 29.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions by oral examination","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 30, with a 10-deposition limit per side and one day of 7 hours. The deponent has 30 days to review the transcript. Commonwealth additions retained from the former rule: a party who lived or had its principal place of business in the Commonwealth when the action began must be deposed in the Commonwealth unless the parties stipulate otherwise, including by videoconference. Other parties and non-parties are","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions by written questions","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 31. Cross-questions are due 14 days after service, redirect 7 days after cross, and recross 7 days after redirect.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 32. Using depositions in court proceedings","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 32. A deposition taken on less than 14 days’ notice may not be used against a party who promptly moved for a protective order. Objections to the form of written questions are waived unless served within 7 days.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Limit of 25, including discrete subparts. Answers and objections are due in 30 days. Grounds for objection must be stated with specificity or are waived, and unobjected parts must be answered. If the court orders answers, they are due within 15 days unless the court directs otherwise (an NMI addition).","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 34. Producing documents, ESI, and things; entry on land","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 34. Respond in writing within 30 days after service, or 30 days after the first Rule 26(f) conference for early requests.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examinations","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 35.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 36: matters are admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days. NMI addition: if an objection is later overruled, the matter is deemed admitted unless the party serves a denial or explanation within 10 days after that ruling, or another period the court sets.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to disclose or cooperate; sanctions","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 37, including the meet-and-confer certification, the expense-shifting rules, and the ESI-loss provision. Measures to cure prejudice are allowed; adverse-inference instructions or dismissal require intent to deprive. There are no sanctions for ESI lost through routine good-faith system operation.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury trial of right","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Serve a written jury demand no later than 60 days after the last pleading is filed, file it, and pay the jury fee on or before the filing date; otherwise the jury is waived. Other parties may demand additional issues within 14 days. A proper demand may be withdrawn only with the parties’ consent and no later than 45 days before trial. Jury fees are non-refundable ($400 under the 2017 fee schedule).","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 39, with Commonwealth law determining the right to a jury. A consent jury is not available against the Commonwealth where a statute provides for nonjury trial.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 40. Scheduling cases for trial","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Does not track the federal rule; parts follow a Kansas statute. Statutory priorities come first, and continuances are for good cause. A case on file 6 months is “at issue,” and a party may file an at-issue memorandum; an opponent who disagrees must respond within 10 days. A continuance for an absent witness or evidence needs an affidavit or declaration with listed contents. Scheduling stipulations must be in writing ","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 41. The two-dismissal rule counts prior Commonwealth, federal, state, and territorial actions. An action may also be dismissed without prejudice for lack of prosecution under Rule 16 of the Commonwealth Rules of Practice.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 42.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 43. Taking testimony","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 43.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 44. Proving an official record","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 44, adapted to records kept in the Commonwealth.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 44.1. Determining foreign law","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Give notice by pleading or other writing. The court may consider any relevant source, and its determination is a ruling on law.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 45 with Commonwealth changes. A subpoena may be served anywhere in the Commonwealth, and outside it only with the court’s authorization on motion. If attendance is required on a different island from where service was made, a round-trip airline ticket must be tendered. Failing to travel to the Commonwealth is excused unless airfare was provided in advance. Objections to production are due before the earl","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 45.1. Notice to appear in lieu of subpoena","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","A written notice served on a party’s attorney at least 10 days before the appearance compels a party, or its officer, director, or managing agent, to attend as if subpoenaed. With at least 20 days’ notice it may require them to bring specified documents. This is an NMI rule with no FRCP counterpart.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 46. Objecting to a ruling or order","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary; state the request or objection and the grounds.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 47. Selection of jurors","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Peremptory challenges are set by 7 CMC § 3102. The court holds a pretrial jury-selection hearing where counsel may stipulate to an English-speaking jury and strike by agreement names that are clearly disqualified, for example by relationship or by 7 CMC §§ 3103, 3104, or 3111. Challenges at trial are unaffected.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 48. Number of jurors; verdict; polling","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","The verdict must be unanimous and returned by a jury of 6. The jury is polled on request.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 49. Special verdict; general verdict and questions","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 49.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 50. Judgment as a matter of law","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 50, but a renewed motion, with or without an alternative new-trial request, is due within 10 days after entry of judgment, or 10 days after the jury is discharged if the issue went undecided (federal: 28). A losing party’s new-trial motion after a JMOL is due within 10 days.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 51. Jury instructions; objections","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 51, including plain-error review of unpreserved instruction errors that affect substantial rights.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings by the court; partial findings","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 52, with clear-error review of findings. A motion to amend or add findings is due within 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28).","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 53, but objections to, or motions to adopt or modify, a master’s order or report are due within 10 days after service unless the court sets another time.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgment; costs","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 54. The clerk taxes costs on 7 days’ notice, and the court reviews on a motion served within the next 7 days. Costs against the Commonwealth are allowed only as permitted by law. An attorney-fee motion is due within 14 days after entry of judgment.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 55. Default; default judgment","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 55. A party who has appeared gets written notice of a default-judgment application at least 7 days before the hearing. A default judgment against the Commonwealth requires proof that satisfies the court.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 56. Unless ordered otherwise, a motion may be filed until 30 days after the close of all discovery. Supporting or opposing evidence over 25 pages must include a table of contents (an NMI addition).","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgment","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 57.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 58. Entering judgments","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Does not track FRCP 58. Within 10 days after a decision that needs a settled judgment or order, the prevailing party serves a draft and delivers it to the clerk. Others have 5 days to approve or object with a substitute draft, and at 15 days the clerk submits everything to the judge. The same procedure applies to findings and conclusions after a bench trial. An initialed approval is enough for entry.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 59. New trial; altering or amending a judgment","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","A new-trial motion is due within 20 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28), and opposing affidavits within 14 days. The court may order a new trial on its own within 20 days. A Rule 59(e) motion is due within 10 days (federal: 28) and is limited to an intervening change in law, new evidence, or clear error or manifest injustice. Rule 11 sanctions expressly apply. None of these times can be extended.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from a judgment or order","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 60, but relief from a void judgment is stated separately and is mandatory. Motions for reasons (1)–(3) must be made within one year, and others within a reasonable time. Clerical corrections need Supreme Court leave once an appeal is docketed.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Errors that do not affect substantial rights are disregarded.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Execution is automatically stayed for 14 days after entry, but injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed. A supersedeas bond for a money judgment is the judgment plus 10% plus $250 for costs unless justice requires otherwise, which retains the former Commonwealth formula. The Commonwealth needs no bond. The Supreme Court’s power to stay is not limited.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 62.1. Indicative ruling while an appeal is pending","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 62.1. The court may defer, deny, or state that it would grant the motion or that it raises a substantial issue, and the movant must then notify the Supreme Court. This is new to the Commonwealth rules.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 63. Inability of a judge to proceed","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","A successor judge may continue after certifying familiarity with the record. In a bench trial, the successor must recall a material, disputed, available witness on request.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 64. Seizure of property","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Prejudgment seizure remedies are available as provided by Commonwealth law.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions and restraining orders","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 65, but a TRO without notice expires within the time the court sets, not to exceed 10 days (federal: 14), unless extended for a like period or by consent. The adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice. The Commonwealth and its instrumentalities need not give security.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 65.1. Proceedings against a surety","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 65.1.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","An action in which the court has appointed a receiver may not be dismissed without court order, and these rules govern receivership actions.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit into court","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 67. Deposits are made by leave on notice and held in interest-bearing accounts or instruments.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of judgment","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Does not track FRCP 68. A defending party may serve an offer more than 10 days before trial, and acceptance must be served within 10 days. An unaccepted offer is deemed withdrawn. After liability is fixed, an offer may be made at least 10 days before the damages hearing. An offeree who does no better than the offer pays the costs incurred after it.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Money judgments are enforced by any means Commonwealth law authorizes, under the law in force when the remedy is sought. The judgment creditor may take discovery from anyone, including the debtor.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 70. Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 70.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 71. Enforcing relief for or against a nonparty","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 71.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 71.1. Condemnation of property","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Does not track FRCP 71.1. Filing follows the e-filing rules, and defendants answer within 20 days after service of the notice.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 71.2. Habeas corpus cases","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Habeas proceedings follow Commonwealth statutes and must be consistent with these rules (formerly Rule 72).","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 72. [Reserved]","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Reserved.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 73. [Reserved]","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Reserved. A former Rule 73 took effect July 6, 2009 and expired July 6, 2014.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 74. [Reserved]","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Reserved.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 75. [Reserved]","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Reserved.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 76. [Reserved]","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Reserved.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 77. Court and clerks","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","The court is always open for e-filing. Hearings other than ex parte ones may be held outside the Commonwealth only if all affected parties consent. The clerk may enter some consent orders, including one 10-day extension to plead or move (not for a new-trial motion) if the time has not run. Lack of notice of entry does not affect the time to appeal except as the Supreme Court Rules allow.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 78. Hearing motions; submission on briefs","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","The court may set regular motion days and may decide motions on the papers.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 79. Records kept by the clerk","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","The clerk keeps the civil docket, judgments, indexes, and calendars. Jury cases are marked “jury.” Entry in the docket under Rule 79(a) triggers appeal time under NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(7).","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 80. Transcript as evidence","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","When former testimony is admissible, it may be proved by a transcript certified by the person who recorded or reported it.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 81. Applicability in general","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","The rules apply to all civil proceedings unless law or another procedural rule provides otherwise. The writs of scire facias and mandamus are abolished in favor of action or motion.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 82. Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","The rules do not extend or limit jurisdiction or venue.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 82.1. Absence of counsel","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","An attorney who will be away from the Commonwealth, or unavailable at a regular Commonwealth office, for 3 or more consecutive days must file an Off-Island Report on the CNMI Bar Association website at least 15 days before the absence. This is an NMI-only rule.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 82.2. Citation of authority","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Commonwealth Reporter and NMI Reporter cases, slip opinions, cases on the Law Revision Commission website, and some unpublished U.S. District Court for the NMI and Ninth Circuit decisions may be cited. To cite an unpublished Superior Court or Supreme Court decision, give written notice and copies at least 48 hours before the hearing. Am. Jur. and C.J.S. may not be cited as primary authority without a parenthetical ex","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 83. [Reserved]","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Reserved. Former Commonwealth Rule 83 on small claims stays in effect until Small Claims Rules are adopted.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 84. Forms","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Court forms are recommended but optional, except that small-claims forms adopted by the court are mandatory.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 85. [Reserved]","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Reserved.","#st-mp-rules"],["Rule 86. [Reserved]","Northern Mariana Islands · civil rules","Reserved.","#st-mp-rules"],["Appeals in the CNMI: one appellate court, federal-style rules, Commonwealth deadlines","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals","The NMI Supreme Court is the only Commonwealth appellate court; there is no intermediate court. Its rules follow the Federal Rules of Appellate Procedure in numbering and much of their text, with sections marked “[Reserved]” where the CNMI departs. They also borrow Ninth Circuit practices such as the docketing statement, automatic brief extensions, and attorney-fee requests.\nThe traps come from the trial-court rules ","#st-mp-appeals"],["File the notice of appeal in the Superior Court","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Starting the appeal","An appeal is taken only by filing a notice of appeal with the Superior Court clerk within the Rule 4 deadline. Missing any other step does not void the appeal, but the Court may dismiss for it. A notice filed by mistake in the Supreme Court is sent down and counts as filed on the date received. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3(a), 4(d)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Contents: name the appellants and attach the judgment","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Starting the appeal","The notice must name each appealing party, designate and attach the judgment or order appealed, and say whether counsel is appointed. Form 1 is a suggested form. In a civil case the attached judgment must be a separate document (Commonwealth v. Kumagai, 2006 MP 20 ¶ 22, cited in the rule’s note). Informality of form is not grounds for dismissal. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3(c)(1), 3(c)(4)–(5) & note 1","#st-mp-appeals"],["Fees","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Starting the appeal","Pay all required fees when you file the notice. The notice-of-appeal fee is $175, paid to the Superior Court, and it also covers interlocutory appeals and civil cross-appeals. Other Supreme Court fees: extraordinary writ $300; petition for rehearing $250; motion to extend brief time $50; motion for late brief $100; motions decided by the full Court $200; emergency motions $200; amicus leave $200. Each e-filing adds $","#st-mp-appeals"],["What the Supreme Court can do","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Starting the appeal","The Court may affirm, modify, vacate, or reverse and remand. It may not take new evidence or decide facts de novo, and it may set aside findings of fact only if clearly erroneous, giving due regard to the trial court’s view of witness credibility. 1 CMC § 3103","#st-mp-appeals"],["30 days after entry, for everyone","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Time to appeal","In a civil case, file the notice within 30 days after entry of the judgment or order. Unlike FRAP 4(a)(1)(B), there is no 60-day period when the Commonwealth is a party. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(1)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Cross-appeals and other parties","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Time to appeal","Once one party files a timely notice, any other party may file within 14 days after the first notice, or within the regular 30 days if that ends later. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(3)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Early notices","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Time to appeal","A notice filed after the decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed on the date of entry. The Superior Court clerk holds it until the separate judgment is filed. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(2)","#st-mp-appeals"],["When a judgment is “entered”","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Time to appeal","Entry occurs when the judgment is docketed under NMI R. Civ. P. 79(a) and either (1) it is set out in a separate document or (2) 150 days have run since docketing, whichever comes first. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(7)(A)(ii)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Extensions and reopening (Superior Court only)","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Time to appeal","The Superior Court may extend the time if a party moves within 30 days after the deadline expires and shows excusable neglect or good cause. The extension may not exceed 30 days past the original deadline or 14 days after the order granting it, whichever is later.\nThe Superior Court may reopen the time for 14 days only if: the movant did not receive notice of entry under Civil Rule 77(d) within 21 days; the motion is","#st-mp-appeals"],["Which motions restart the clock","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Tolling motions: timely under the NMI civil rules","The time to appeal runs from the order disposing of the last of these motions, if the motion was timely under the NMI Rules of Civil Procedure:\n• renewed judgment as a matter of law (Rule 50(b), 10 days);\n• amended findings (Rule 52(b), 10 days);\n• attorney fees (Rule 54(d)(2), 14 days);\n• alter or amend (Rule 59(e), 10 days);\n• new trial (Rule 59(b), 20 days);\n• relief from judgment under Rule 60, if filed within 28","#st-mp-appeals"],["No extensions of the motion deadlines","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Tolling motions: timely under the NMI civil rules","The Superior Court may not extend the time for Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (d), or (e), or 60(b) motions. Periods of 10 days or less skip intermediate weekends and holidays. NMI R. Civ. P. 6(a)(1)(D), 6(b)(2)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Challenging the ruling on the motion","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Tolling motions: timely under the NMI civil rules","A notice of appeal filed while a tolling motion is pending takes effect when the last such motion is decided. To challenge the order on the motion, or a judgment amended because of it, file a new or amended notice within the Rule 4 time measured from that order. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(B)(i)–(ii)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Docketing statement (Form 2): 15 days","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Docketing, transcripts, and the record","Every represented appellant must file a docketing statement within 15 days after the Clerk gives notice that the appeal is docketed. Extensions are disfavored and require substantial need. Failing to file is grounds for dismissal, and an inaccurate statement can be sanctioned. Within 10 days after service, each opposing party must file a response or a notice of appearance. Raise jurisdictional defects by motion to di","#st-mp-appeals"],["Conflicts notice and representation statement","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Docketing, transcripts, and the record","With the first docketing statement, response, or appearance, file a notice of potential conflicts under 1 CMC § 3308 that might affect a justice. Within 15 days after filing the notice of appeal, appellant’s counsel files a representation statement, which may be part of the docketing statement. Any change in representation must be noticed within 30 days. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3-1(c), 12(b)–(c)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Ordering transcripts (Form 3): 15 days","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Docketing, transcripts, and the record","Within 15 days after the notice of appeal, or after the order deciding the last tolling motion if later, the appellant files Form 3 with the Superior Court clerk. Form 3 either orders audio transcript portions or certifies that no transcript will be ordered. The appellee may add portions within 10 days, and the appellant may respond within 5. The clerk prepares audio transcripts within 10 days.\nCertified transcripts ","#st-mp-appeals"],["Certification of the record","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Docketing, transcripts, and the record","The Superior Court clerk keeps the record and certifies it as complete. Certification comes within 20 days after the appellant certifies that no transcript will be ordered, or within 10 days after the certified transcript is filed. The record is filed in the Supreme Court when the certificate is filed, and that date starts the briefing clock. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 10(a), 11(c)(2)–(3)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Appendix","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Docketing, transcripts, and the record","The appellant files one appendix with the opening brief and pays for it, including parts the appellee designates. Absent agreement, the appellant serves designations within 10 days after the record is filed, and the appellee counter-designates within 10 days. Trial-court memoranda generally do not belong in it, and you cannot incorporate trial briefs by reference. Padding the appendix can be sanctioned. NMI Sup. Ct. ","#st-mp-appeals"],["Schedule: 40 / 30 / 14","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Briefs","The appellant’s brief is due 40 days after the record is filed. The appellee’s brief is due 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief. The reply is due 14 days after the appellee’s brief, and at least 3 days before argument. If the appellant misses the deadline, the appellee may move to dismiss. An appellee who files no brief is not heard at argument without permission. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 31(a), 31(c)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Cross-appeals","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Briefs","The first party to file a notice is the appellant; if notices are filed the same day, it is the plaintiff below. There are four briefs, served at 40 days after the record is filed, then 30, 30, and 14 days after each preceding brief. The last brief is due at least 3 days before argument. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 28.1(b), (f)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Extensions: one automatic 30-day extension per brief","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Briefs","A party may get one automatic extension of up to 30 days per brief by a motion citing Rule 31-1(a)(1), filed before the brief is due; the Clerk grants it. Any further extension requires a motion with an affidavit showing diligence and substantial need. Late briefs need leave and are highly disfavored. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 31-1(a)–(b)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Length and form","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Briefs","A principal brief may not exceed 35 pages, and a reply 20 pages, counting everything including the caption, headings, and tables. Format: 11-point Times New Roman, 1.5 spacing, 1-inch margins, a white cover, and a certificate of compliance. Every paper must show the attorney’s CNMI Bar number. An amicus brief may be half the length of a principal brief and is due 7 days after the supported party’s principal brief. A ","#st-mp-appeals"],["Contents","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Briefs","A jurisdictional statement must show the basis for the Superior Court’s and the Supreme Court’s jurisdiction, the filing dates proving timeliness, and that the appeal is from a final judgment entered as a separate document, or another basis for jurisdiction. State the standard of review for each issue. A corporate disclosure statement goes before the table of contents. Supplemental authority letters are limited to 35","#st-mp-appeals"],["Citations","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Briefs","Unpublished dispositions may be cited, though this is highly disfavored, if flagged as unpublished. File copies of any authority not in a public electronic database. A standing order requires citation to relevant local case law, with sanctions possible for omitting binding local authority. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 32.1; Gen. Order 2000-200","#st-mp-appeals"],["Motion practice","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Motions and stays","Responses to motions are due in 8 days and replies in 5; both are counted excluding weekends because they are under 10 days. Motions and responses are limited to 20 pages and replies to 10. A separate supporting brief is not allowed. Each motion needs a proposed order, and a motion for substantive relief must attach the order below. The Clerk may decide routine unopposed motions and brief extensions, and a single jus","#st-mp-appeals"],["Emergency motions","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Motions and stays","If relief is needed within 21 days to avoid irreparable harm, label the motion “Emergency Motion Under Rule 27-1.” Include a Rule 27-1 Certificate with contact details for all counsel, the facts of the emergency, and how and when opponents were notified. Notify the Clerk and opponents in advance. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 27-1","#st-mp-appeals"],["Stays and supersedeas bonds","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Motions and stays","Move first in the Superior Court unless that would be impracticable. Execution is automatically stayed for 14 days after entry, but injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed. A supersedeas bond for a money judgment is the judgment plus 10% plus $250, unless justice requires otherwise. The Commonwealth needs no bond. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 8(a); NMI R. Civ. P. 62(a), (d)–(e)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Oral argument","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Argument, decision, and rehearing","Argument is allowed unless all three panel justices agree it is unnecessary: the appeal is frivolous, the issue has been authoritatively decided, or the briefs are enough. Each side gets 25 minutes. Leave to use physical exhibits must be sought at least one week before argument. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 34(a)–(b), (g)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Rehearing: 14 days, 15 pages, no answer unless requested","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Argument, decision, and rehearing","A petition for rehearing may be filed within 14 days after entry of judgment. It must state with particularity the points of law or fact overlooked or misapprehended. It is limited to 15 pages, and no oral argument is allowed. No answer is permitted unless the Court requests one, and rehearing is ordinarily not granted without such a request. The fee is $250. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 40; Fee Schedule item 87","#st-mp-appeals"],["Mandate, costs, and attorney fees","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Argument, decision, and rehearing","The mandate issues 7 calendar days after the rehearing period ends, or 7 days after denial of a timely rehearing petition, stay motion, or fee petition, whichever is later. File a bill of costs within 14 days after entry of judgment; objections are due within 10 days. File an attorney-fee request no later than 5 calendar days after the rehearing period expires, or 5 days after a rehearing petition is decided. Opposit","#st-mp-appeals"],["No general permissive-appeal rule","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Interlocutory review and writs","Rules 5 and 6 are reserved, so there is no FRAP 5–style petition for permission to appeal an interlocutory order. The Constitution gives the Supreme Court appeals from “final judgments and orders.” The statute speaks of “judgments and orders” of the Superior Court. Whether a particular non-final order is appealable, for example under the collateral-order doctrine, depends on case law not collected here. NMI Const. ar","#st-mp-appeals"],["Built-in interlocutory routes","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Interlocutory review and writs","Class certification: the Supreme Court may permit an appeal if a petition is filed with the Supreme Court clerk within 14 days after the certification order. The appeal does not stay the case unless ordered. Injunctions: the civil rules contemplate appeals from interlocutory orders granting, dissolving, or denying an injunction, and the fee schedule covers interlocutory appeals. NMI R. Civ. P. 23(f), 62(c); Fee Sched","#st-mp-appeals"],["Mandamus and prohibition","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Interlocutory review and writs","Petitions are captioned “In re [petitioner].” All parties below are respondents, but the trial judge is not. The petitioner must declare that the petition was given to all parties and the judge. The petition is limited to 30 pages. The Court may deny without an answer, and writ proceedings take precedence over ordinary civil cases. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 21(a)–(d); 1 CMC § 3102(b)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Further review","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Review beyond the NMI Supreme Court; certified questions","Supreme Court decisions are final and may not be appealed to any other court except as Covenant § 403(a) provides. Section 403(a) applies to Commonwealth courts the federal rules that govern relations between U.S. courts and state courts. Rule 41 provides for staying the mandate while a party seeks certiorari in the U.S. Supreme Court. The stay is limited to 90 days unless the petition is filed and the Clerk notified","#st-mp-appeals"],["Certified questions","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Review beyond the NMI Supreme Court; certified questions","A federal court may certify a question of Commonwealth law that may be determinative where there is no controlling NMI Supreme Court precedent. The Court may accept or refuse it at its sole discretion. Elected or governor-appointed Commonwealth officials in a dispute over their powers may certify legal questions on stipulated facts, and the ruling binds the parties. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 13–14; NMI Const. art. IV, § 11","#st-mp-appeals"],["Everything is e-filed","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · E-filing and computing time","Supreme Court filing and service follow the NMI Rules for Electronic Filing and Service, which apply to all CNMI courts. Attorneys, including pro hac vice counsel, must be registered filers. A filing is timely if submitted by 11:59 p.m. Chamorro Standard Time (ChST). If the system is unavailable for more than 48 hours, file in person or by email to the Clerk. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 25(a), 25(c); NMI R. Elec. Filing 1(b), 4,","#st-mp-appeals"],["Counting days","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · E-filing and computing time","Periods of 10 days or less exclude intermediate weekends and legal holidays. Legal holidays include any day the Court is closed for any reason, including austerity or weather closures. Longer periods are calendar days. Five calendar days are added after service only when service is not made through the e-filing system. Separately, the e-filing rules add 1 day to respond after e-service. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 1-1(c)(6), 26(","#st-mp-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (civil)","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the judgment or order (separate document, or 150 days after docketing).\nFile with the Superior Court clerk. No 60-day period for government cases. Restarts after timely tolling motions. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(1), 4(a)(7)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Notice of appeal by another party (cross-appeal)","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the first notice of appeal.\nOr within the regular 30 days if that ends later. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(3)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Motion to extend time to appeal","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from expiration of the original appeal deadline.\nFiled in the Superior Court; requires excusable neglect or good cause. The extension cannot exceed 30 days past the original deadline or 14 days after the order granting it, whichever is later. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(5)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Motion to reopen time to appeal","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","180 days from entry of the judgment or order.\nOr 14 days after receiving notice of entry, whichever is earlier. Available only if Rule 77(d) notice was not received within 21 days. Reopens for 14 days. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(6)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Renewed JMOL (tolls appeal)","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of judgment (or jury discharge if issue undecided).\nFederal rule allows 28 days. Cannot be extended. Weekends excluded (10 days or less). NMI R. Civ. P. 50(b); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(i)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Motion to amend findings (tolls appeal)","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of judgment.\nFederal rule allows 28 days. Cannot be extended. NMI R. Civ. P. 52(b); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(ii)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Motion to alter or amend judgment (tolls appeal)","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of judgment.\nFederal rule allows 28 days. Cannot be extended. Limited to intervening change in law, new evidence, or clear error or manifest injustice. NMI R. Civ. P. 59(e); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(iv)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Motion for new trial (tolls appeal)","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from entry of judgment.\nFederal rule allows 28 days. Opposing affidavits are due 14 days after service. Cannot be extended. NMI R. Civ. P. 59(b); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(v)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Attorney-fee motion in Superior Court (tolls appeal)","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment.\nUnless a statute or court order provides otherwise. NMI R. Civ. P. 54(d)(2)(B)(i); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(iii)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Rule 60 motion that tolls the appeal","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from entry of judgment.\nA Rule 60(b) motion may be filed later (up to 1 year for reasons 1–3), but then it does not toll. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(vi)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Docketing statement (Form 2)","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from clerk’s notice that the appeal is docketed.\nExtensions disfavored; failure is grounds for dismissal. Not required of pro se appellants. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3-1(a)(4)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Response to docketing statement or notice of appearance","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from service of the docketing statement.\nFile the conflicts notice at the same time (Rule 3-1(c)). Counted excluding weekends (10 days or less). NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3-1(b)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Representation statement","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nMay be included in the docketing statement. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 12(b)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Transcript order or no-transcript certification (Form 3)","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from notice of appeal, or order deciding last tolling motion, if later.\nAppellee may add portions within 10 days; appellant may respond within 5 days. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 11-1(b)(1)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Certified transcript designation (Form 4)","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from service of the Rule 11-1(b)(4) completion notice.\nJoint or separate. Certified transcript is due within 30 days after Form 4 unless more time is granted. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 11-1(c)(1), 11-1(d)(1)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Appellant’s opening brief and appendix","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from filing of the certificate of record.\n35-page limit, inclusive of everything. One automatic extension of up to 30 days is available. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 30(a)(3), 31(a)(1)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the appellant’s brief.\n35-page limit. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 31(a)(1)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Reply brief","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\nMust be filed at least 3 days before argument. 20-page limit. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 31(a)(1)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Amicus brief","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from filing of the supported party’s principal brief.\nHalf the principal-brief length. Leave or consent needed, except for the Commonwealth. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 29(e)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Response to a motion","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","8 days from service of the motion.\nReply is due within 5 days after the response. Both are counted excluding weekends and holidays. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 27(a)(4)(A)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Motion to reconsider an order on a motion","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from service of the order.\nResponse is due within 7 days for orders by the Clerk or a single justice. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 27-2(d)(1)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Petition for permission to appeal class-certification order","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of the order.\nFiled with the Supreme Court clerk; does not stay proceedings unless ordered. NMI R. Civ. P. 23(f)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment.\n15 pages; no answer unless requested; $250 fee. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 40(a)(1)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Bill of costs","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment.\nObjections are due within 10 days after service. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 39(d)(1)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Request for appellate attorney fees","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","5 days from expiration of the rehearing period (or disposition of a timely rehearing petition).\nCalendar days. File separately from the bill of costs. Opposition due in 10 days; reply in 5. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 39-1(a)(1)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Issuance of mandate","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from expiration of rehearing period, or denial of rehearing, stay motion, or fee petition (whichever is later).\nCalendar days. A timely rehearing petition, stay motion, or fee petition stays the mandate. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 41(b)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Stay of mandate pending U.S. Supreme Court certiorari","Northern Mariana Islands · appeals · Deadlines","90 days from grant of the stay.\nContinues until final disposition if the petition is filed and the Clerk notified within the stay period. NMI Sup. Ct. R. 41(d)(2)(B)","#st-mp-appeals"],["Supreme Court of Mississippi","Mississippi · courts","Every appeal from a final trial-court order is filed in the Supreme Court, which keeps some cases and assigns others to the Court of Appeals. It must keep death-penalty, utility-rate, annexation, bond-issue, and election-contest appeals and appeals from rulings that a statute is unconstitutional. It also keeps attorney-discipline, judicial-performance, and federal certified-question matters. It reviews Court of Appea","#st-ms"],["Court of Appeals of the State of Mississippi","Mississippi · courts","Has only the jurisdiction the Supreme Court gives it by assigning cases (Miss. Code Ann. § 9-4-3, as quoted in M.R.A.P. 16(b)). A party has no right to have its case heard by the Supreme Court instead and may not ask for reassignment. Its decision is final unless the Supreme Court grants certiorari, and a motion for rehearing in the Court of Appeals must come first.","#st-ms"],["Chancery Courts","Mississippi · courts","Separate equity courts that hear domestic-relations, estate, guardianship, and similar matters, under the Uniform Chancery Court Rules. Many of these matters use the special Rule 4(i) summons (formerly Rule 81(d)), which sets a hearing date and requires no answer. Juries in chancery are 12, with verdicts by 9 or more.","#st-ms"],["Circuit Courts","Mississippi · courts","General-jurisdiction trial courts at law, under the Uniform Civil Rules of Circuit and County Court Practice (UCRCCC). Circuit court also sits as an appellate court: appeals from justice and municipal court are tried de novo, and appeals from agencies and other lower authorities are decided on the record. Juries are 12, with verdicts by 9 or more.","#st-ms"],["County Courts","Mississippi · courts","Civil juries are 6, with verdicts by 5 or more. When a chancery court assigns a case to county court, the county court uses the chancery rules. Interlocutory appeals from county court go to the Supreme Court under M.R.A.P. 5.","#st-ms"],["Justice Courts and Municipal Courts","Mississippi · courts","Courts of limited jurisdiction with their own rules (Uniform Rules of Procedure for Justice Court). A civil judgment of a justice court is appealed to circuit court for trial de novo. The notice of appeal and costs must be filed within 30 days (UCRCCC 5.01, 5.04), and compulsory counterclaims must be added within 30 days (M.R.C.P. 13(k)).","#st-ms"],["No anti-SLAPP statute; every 2022–2026 bill died in committee","Mississippi · anti-SLAPP","Mississippi has no anti-SLAPP law. The Legislature’s official Bill Status System lists every measure by session. Its lists for the 2018–2026 regular sessions and the 2025 and 2026 extraordinary sessions show anti-SLAPP bills only in 2022, 2025, and 2026, and each one died in committee:\n• 2022: S.B. 2628 (Public Speech Protection Act).\n• 2025: H.B. 1504 (“Freedom of Speech Protection Act”).\n• 2026: H.B. 1268 and S.B. ","#st-ms-slapp"],["What the failed 2026 bills would have done (not law)","Mississippi · anti-SLAPP","H.B. 1268, the Mississippi Uniform Public Expression Protection Act, would have applied to claims based on: communications in government proceedings; communications on issues under consideration in those proceedings; and the exercise of speech, press, assembly, petition, or association rights on a matter of public concern. It excluded claims by and against governmental units, provided an appeal, and would have applie","#st-ms-slapp"],["Related rule that does exist: notice before suing a newspaper or broadcaster for libel","Mississippi · anti-SLAPP","Before suing a newspaper, radio station, or television station for libel, the plaintiff must give the defendant written notice at least 10 days in advance, identifying the article or broadcast and the statements claimed to be false. If the defendant acted in good faith on an honest mistake of fact and published a full and fair correction and retraction within 10 days after the notice, the plaintiff recovers only actu","#st-ms-slapp"],["Pro hac vice is governed by appellate Rule 46(b) for every court and agency","Mississippi · out of state","A foreign attorney (licensed and in good standing in another U.S. jurisdiction but not in Mississippi) may appear only as permitted by M.R.A.P. 46(b). This applies in every Mississippi court and before state and local agencies, not just on appeal. “Appearance” is broad. It covers having your name on any filed or served paper, appearing in person, and taking part in a deposition or other proceeding where testimony is ","#st-ms-oos"],["What to file, and where","Mississippi · out of state","File with the trial court or agency and with the Clerk of the Supreme Court:\n(1) a verified application; and\n(2) a statement from the Clerk of the Supreme Court listing every earlier pro hac vice request you made in Mississippi, with its date and outcome.\nServe both on all parties with a certificate of service. The application must list:\n• every jurisdiction where you are admitted;\n• your good standing, and any disci","#st-ms-oos"],["Fees: $300 to the Mississippi Bar plus the Supreme Court docket fee","Mississippi · out of state","When you file the application, pay $300 to The Mississippi Bar, which funds legal services for the indigent. Certify that payment to the court or agency and to the Supreme Court Clerk. Also pay the Supreme Court Clerk the customary miscellaneous docket fee under Miss. Code Ann. § 25-7-3; the amount is set by statute and is not stated in the rule. The court or agency may waive fees for indigent clients for good cause.","#st-ms-oos"],["Do not appear until the order is lodged with the Supreme Court Clerk","Mississippi · out of state","You may not appear as counsel pro hac vice until you certify to the court or agency that you have given the Supreme Court Clerk a copy of the order authorizing the appearance. Clerks must refuse papers from a foreign attorney who has not complied, and noncompliant papers are struck. A violation is the unlawful practice of law. One exception: requesting a subpoena under M.R.C.P. 45(a)(3) is not subject to the rule. M.","#st-ms-oos"],["Local counsel must sign everything, attend trial, and share liability","Mississippi · out of state","You must associate a Mississippi lawyer in the case, and that lawyer’s name must appear on every notice, order, pleading, and paper. The local lawyer must personally attend every trial. Unless the court or agency excuses it, the local lawyer must also attend every pretrial conference, open-court hearing, deposition, and other proceeding in Mississippi where testimony is taken. Local counsel is jointly and severally r","#st-ms-oos"],["Limit: more than 5 unrelated matters in 12 months is “general practice”","Mississippi · out of state","Appearing in more than 5 separate, unrelated causes or matters in Mississippi courts or agencies in the 12 months before the appearance counts as the general practice of law, which only members of the Mississippi Bar may do. You also cannot appear pro hac vice if you keep an office in Mississippi, unless your firm’s Mississippi office is staffed full time by Mississippi-licensed lawyers. Full-time law teachers at Mis","#st-ms-oos"],["Permission ends automatically on suspension or disbarment anywhere","Mississippi · out of state","Pro hac vice permission ends if you are suspended or disbarred in any jurisdiction where you are admitted. You must promptly report discipline from any other jurisdiction to the Mississippi court or agency. The judge may revoke permission and cite you for contempt for misconduct during the appearance, and must refer the matter to the Bar’s disciplinary counsel. Lawyers for the United States may appear without local c","#st-ms-oos"],["Temporary practice and in-house work without pro hac vice (Rule 5.5)","Mississippi · out of state","A lawyer admitted elsewhere and not suspended or disbarred may provide temporary services in Mississippi if one of these applies:\n• the work is done with a Mississippi lawyer who actively participates;\n• it relates to a pending or expected proceeding where the lawyer is or expects to be authorized to appear;\n• it relates to an arbitration or mediation (unless the forum requires pro hac vice); or\n• it arises from the ","#st-ms-oos"],["Admission without exam: comity after 5 years of practice, only with reciprocal states","Mississippi · out of state","A lawyer from another state may be admitted without the bar exam after at least 5 years of active practice, but only if that state grants similar privileges to Mississippi lawyers and its admission requirements are equivalent. The Board of Bar Admissions decides whether a state is reciprocal. The fee is $1,500 plus NCBE investigation charges, and it is not refundable. If your home state imposes stricter conditions or","#st-ms-oos"],["Limited-scope representation: allowed by Rule 1.2(c), but withdrawal still needs leave","Mississippi · out of state","A lawyer may limit the scope of a representation if the limit is reasonable and the client gives informed consent. The comment encourages limited services such as attending a single deposition or hearing, or handling a specific issue. The collected court rules contain no special limited-appearance or automatic-withdrawal procedure. In circuit and county court, an attorney who has appeared may withdraw only with the c","#st-ms-oos"],["E-filing: MEC in the trial courts, mandatory electronic filing in the appellate courts","Mississippi · out of state","Trial courts that have adopted the Mississippi Electronic Courts system (MEC) require documents to be filed electronically or scanned by the clerk. Attorneys admitted pro hac vice may register as MEC users; pro se non-lawyers may not. Each attorney registers individually and pays a registration fee, and registration waives conventional service.\nIn the Supreme Court and Court of Appeals, briefs, motions, responses, an","#st-ms-oos"],["Governing code: Mississippi Rules of Professional Conduct (effective July 1, 1987, as amended)","Mississippi · ethics","Mississippi follows the ABA Model Rules structure but has kept older wording in many places and has its own provisions. Read the Mississippi text rather than assuming Model Rule language. The examples below come straight from the rule text. Miss. R. Prof’l Conduct (courts.ms.gov)","#st-ms-ethics"],["Living-expense advances to litigation clients: allowed, capped at $1,500, reported within 7 days","Mississippi · ethics","Unlike Model Rule 1.8(e), Mississippi lets a lawyer advance a client’s reasonable and necessary medical and minimal living expenses tied to the litigation, with strict conditions:\n• only in dire and necessitous circumstances, such as preventing foreclosure;\n• only after 60 days have passed since the client signed the fee contract;\n• no promises of future payments, and no mention of such payments in public communicati","#st-ms-ethics"],["Advertisements must be filed with the Bar’s General Counsel before first use","Mississippi · ethics","A copy or recording of any advertisement must be submitted to the Office of the General Counsel of the Mississippi Bar before it is first disseminated. The fee is $25 per submission, or $150 if the submission is late. Exemptions include tombstone-type ads limited to Rule 7.2/7.4 information, telephone directory ads, business cards, letterhead, office signs, websites viewed through a search the user starts, and mail t","#st-ms-ethics"],["Confidentiality: narrower list of exceptions, plus Lawyers and Judges Assistance rules","Mississippi · ethics","Rule 1.6(b) allows disclosure to prevent death or bodily harm; to prevent, mitigate, or rectify a client crime or fraud that used the lawyer’s services; to get ethics advice; for lawyer-client controversies; and to comply with law or a court order. The Mississippi text has no Model Rule 1.6(b)(7) exception for detecting conflicts when changing firms, and no Model Rule 1.6(c) duty to make reasonable efforts against un","#st-ms-ethics"],["Older Model Rule provisions still in force: intermediary rule and no prospective-client rule","Mississippi · ethics","Mississippi keeps Rule 2.2 (lawyer as intermediary between clients), which the ABA deleted in 2002. A lawyer may act as an impartial intermediary for two or more clients working toward a common objective, if the conditions in 2.2(b) are met. The table of rules has no Rule 1.18 (duties to prospective clients). Rule 1.10 is still titled “Imputed Disqualification: General Rule.” When a conflict question turns on these r","#st-ms-ethics"],["Fee rules: written contingent-fee agreements; fee splits without written client consent","Mississippi · ethics","A contingent-fee agreement must be in writing and state:\n• the percentage due on settlement, trial, or appeal;\n• which expenses are deducted; and\n• whether expenses are deducted before or after the fee is calculated.\nAt the end, the lawyer must give the client a written closing statement. Contingent fees are barred in divorce, alimony, and support matters and for criminal defense.\nA fee split between firms must be pr","#st-ms-ethics"],["Choice of law and discipline of non-Mississippi lawyers","Mississippi · ethics","A lawyer not admitted in Mississippi is subject to Mississippi discipline for providing or offering legal services there. For conduct in a matter before a tribunal, the rules of the tribunal’s jurisdiction apply. Pro hac vice counsel must study and comply with the Mississippi rules and are subject to the courts, the Bar’s disciplinary tribunals, and the Board of Bar Admissions. Miss. R. Prof’l Conduct 8.5; M.R.A.P. 4","#st-ms-ethics"],["Post-trial motions: 10 days from entry, and no extensions","Mississippi · land mine","Motions for JNOV, amended findings, a new trial, or to alter or amend the judgment are due 10 days after entry of judgment, not 28 as in federal court. The trial court cannot extend them. A Rule 60 motion tolls the appeal time only if it is filed within 10 days after entry of judgment. M.R.C.P. 6(b), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e); M.R.A.P. 4(d)","#st-ms-unique"],["No appellate extension of the civil appeal deadline","Mississippi · land mine","The notice of appeal is due 30 days after entry of the judgment and is filed with the trial-court clerk. A late notice means mandatory dismissal. Neither appellate court can extend the time to appeal, or to petition for interlocutory appeal, in a civil case. Only the trial court can extend it under Rule 4(g): good cause if you move before the time runs out, excusable neglect if you move within the next 30 days, and t","#st-ms-unique"],["Seven days after the notice of appeal: designate the record and deposit costs","Mississippi · land mine","Within 7 days after filing the notice of appeal, you must:\n• file and serve a designation of the record;\n• deposit the estimated record cost with the trial-court clerk; and\n• file and serve a certificate of compliance.\nIf the court reporter gives no estimate, the deposit is $300 per day of proceedings. Missing these steps does not void the appeal but can lead to sanctions or dismissal. After a deficiency notice, you ","#st-ms-unique"],["Answer extensions by agreement: once only, 10 days, filed stipulation","Mississippi · land mine","The answer is due 30 days after service. The parties may extend it only once, by no more than 10 days, and only by a written stipulation of counsel filed in the record. Open-ended courtesy extensions are not authorized by the rule. M.R.C.P. 12(a)","#st-ms-unique"],["Circuit and county court: discovery closes 90 days after the answer","Mississippi · land mine","Unless the court grants leave on a written motion showing good cause, discovery must be completed within 90 days after the defendant serves its answer. Absent special circumstances, an expert not designated at least 60 days before trial may not testify. Discovery responses must restate each request before the answer. A motion to compel must include a good-faith conference certificate and quote each disputed request v","#st-ms-unique"],["Motion practice: memoranda go to the judge, not the clerk, and unheard motions are abandoned","Mississippi · land mine","In circuit court, file the motion with the clerk and mail a copy to the judge. Memoranda on dismissal and summary-judgment motions are mailed to the judge and not filed with the clerk. The response is due within 10 days and the rebuttal within 5. Memoranda are limited to 25 pages. You must attach copies of any authority that is not a Mississippi or federal statute or rule, a U.S. Supreme Court case, or a case in the ","#st-ms-unique"],["Requests for admission are conclusively admitted after 30 days","Mississippi · land mine","A request for admission is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 30 days. A defendant need not answer before 45 days after service of the summons. Deemed admissions are conclusively established unless the court allows withdrawal. M.R.C.P. 36","#st-ms-unique"],["Serve within 120 days, and know Mississippi’s mail and residence-service rules","Mississippi · land mine","An unserved defendant is dismissed without prejudice after 120 days unless you show good cause. Service by first-class mail works only if the defendant returns the acknowledgment form. Certified mail (return receipt requested) is an added option only for persons outside Mississippi. Residence service is allowed only if personal service cannot be made with reasonable diligence. It requires leaving the papers with the ","#st-ms-unique"],["Recusal: 30-day deadline from notice of the assigned judge","Mississippi · land mine","A recusal motion must be supported by an affidavit and filed within 30 days after the parties are told which judge is assigned. The only exception is facts that could not reasonably have been known then. Review of a denial is by petition to the Supreme Court within 14 days. UCRCCC 1.15; UCCR 1.11; M.R.A.P. 48B","#st-ms-unique"],["Pro hac vice: local counsel must attend trials, hearings, and depositions","Mississippi · land mine","Mississippi local counsel cannot be a name on the pleadings only. Local counsel must personally attend every trial and, unless excused, every hearing, pretrial conference, and deposition in the state. Local counsel is also jointly and severally responsible. Papers you file before the pro hac vice order is lodged with the Supreme Court Clerk are struck. M.R.A.P. 46(b)(4), (b)(7), (b)(11)","#st-ms-unique"],["Interlocutory appeal: 21 days, straight to the Supreme Court, no trial-court certification","Mississippi · land mine","Petition the Supreme Court within 21 days after the order is entered, and serve the trial judge and all parties. Unlike 28 U.S.C. § 1292(b), no trial-court certification is needed. The petition is limited to 15 pages including the brief, and no reply is allowed. The comment condemns seeking belated reconsideration to restart the 21 days. A petition does not stay trial-court proceedings unless ordered. M.R.A.P. 5(a)–(","#st-ms-unique"],["Certiorari from the Court of Appeals requires a rehearing motion first","Mississippi · land mine","To reach the Supreme Court after a Court of Appeals decision, first move for rehearing in the Court of Appeals within 14 days. Then petition for certiorari within 14 days after the ruling on rehearing. The petition is limited to 10 pages, and the opinion and rehearing motion must be attached. Silence for 90 days after the response deadline is a denial. M.R.A.P. 17(b), (e), 40(a)","#st-ms-unique"],["No peremptory substitution of a trial judge; sworn recusal motion within 30 days","Mississippi · judges","The collected Mississippi rules provide no peremptory challenge or automatic substitution of a judge. Recusal is for cause, when a reasonable person who knows all the circumstances might question the judge’s impartiality, or on other grounds in the Code of Judicial Conduct or by law.\n• Form: the motion must come with an affidavit of the party or its attorney stating the facts and swearing good faith.\n• Deadline: file","#st-ms-unique"],["Review of a denied recusal motion: petition the Supreme Court within 14 days","Mississippi · judges","If a circuit, chancery, or county judge denies recusal, or has not ruled within 30 days of the motion, the movant may petition the Supreme Court within 14 days after the ruling, or within 14 days after the 30-day period ends. Attach the recusal order and the hearing transcript. The Supreme Court orders recusal only for an abuse of discretion, and the procedure follows M.R.A.P. 21 (extraordinary writs). M.R.A.P. 48B","#st-ms-unique"],["Appellate justices and judges: motion due 30 days after assignment notice","Mississippi · judges","A motion to recuse a Supreme Court justice or Court of Appeals judge needs the same kind of good-faith affidavit. File it within 30 days after the clerk gives notice of which court the case is assigned to, or within 30 days after the facts could reasonably be discovered. The challenged justice or judge decides first. Reconsideration by the whole court must be sought within 14 days of the denial. Court of Appeals recu","#st-ms-unique"],["State constitutional speech protections: not established here","Mississippi · speech","The text of the Mississippi Constitution’s free-speech provisions, and any case law reading them more broadly than the First Amendment, was not collected. Do not argue an independent state standard without checking the constitution and case law. Miss. Const. (not collected)","#st-ms-unique"],["Separate chancery courts, and a special summons for family and estate matters","Mississippi · unique rules","Mississippi keeps separate courts of equity (chancery) and law (circuit). Since July 1, 2026, M.R.C.P. 4(i), formerly Rule 81(d), governs listed family, estate, and similar matters, such as custody, support, paternity, heirship, will contests, modification, and contempt. These use a Form 1D/1DD summons returnable on a set date, 7 or 30 days after service depending on the matter. No answer is required, and there is no","#st-ms-unique"],["Non-unanimous civil juries","Mississippi · unique rules","Circuit and chancery juries have 12 jurors, and 9 or more decide. County court juries have 6, and 5 or more decide. No jury demand is required: the right is waived only by a specific written stipulation filed with the court, and the court may still order a jury. M.R.C.P. 38, 48","#st-ms-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope of rules","Mississippi · civil rules","Govern procedure in the circuit, chancery, and county courts in all civil suits at law or in equity, subject to the limited-applicability proceedings listed in Rule 81 (which still follow these rules wherever no statute provides otherwise). Construed, administered, and employed by the court and the parties (parties added eff. July 1, 2020) to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","Mississippi · civil rules","There is one form of action, the “civil action.” The note says this unifies procedure only; the traditional separation of law (circuit) and equity (chancery) jurisdiction is unchanged.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencement of action","Mississippi · civil rules","An action is commenced by filing a complaint, with a costs deposit in the amount required by law and court rules. A party may proceed in forma pauperis under Miss. Code §§ 11-53-17 and 11-53-19, and the court (on motion of a party or the clerk, or on its own) may examine the affiant about the claimed poverty. Amended eff. Sept. 10, 2025, deleting former 3(b) (security for costs) and 3(d) (accounting for costs) and re","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 4. Summons","Mississippi · civil rules","The clerk issues a summons on filing; service may be by any nonparty at least 18 (process-server fees taxable only up to the sheriff’s statutory fee), by the sheriff on written request (return within 30 days of receipt), by first-class mail with two notice-and-acknowledgment forms (Form 1B; if not returned within 20 days, use another method and the defendant may be charged the costs), by publication (chancery or wher","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 5. Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","Mississippi · civil rules","Every pleading after the complaint, discovery paper, written motion (other than ex parte), notice, and similar paper must be served on each party, through counsel if represented; parties in default for failure to appear need not be served except with new or additional claims, which require Rule 4 service. Service is by delivery, electronic means (complete when the recipient’s equipment acknowledges receipt, or when t","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 5.1. Privacy protection for filings made with the court","Mississippi · civil rules","Since July 1, 2016, every court and circuit or chancery clerk’s office that stores or files documents electronically (as defined in Miss. Code § 9-1-51) and makes them available online must follow the privacy provisions in Sections 5 and 9 of the Administrative Procedures for Mississippi Electronic Courts.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 6. Time","Mississippi · civil rules","Exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday, statutory legal holiday, or any day the courthouse or clerk’s office is in fact closed; for periods under 7 days, intermediate weekends and holidays are excluded, and a holiday falling on Sunday moves to Monday. The court may enlarge time for cause (before expiration with or without motion; after expiration only on motion for excusable ","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings allowed; form of motions","Mississippi · civil rules","Allowed pleadings are a complaint and answer, an answer to a counterclaim, an answer to a cross-claim, a third-party complaint, and a third-party answer; the court may order a reply to an answer. Motions must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state the grounds with particularity, and state the relief sought; all papers, including depositions, must be on 8½ by 11 inch paper (depositions also follow th","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","Mississippi · civil rules","A claim requires a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment; denials must fairly meet the averments, and averments other than damages are admitted if not denied. Rule 8(c) lists affirmative defenses (accord and satisfaction, arbitration and award, assumption of risk, contributory negligence, bankruptcy discharge, duress, estoppel, failure of consideration, fraud, illegality, i","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","Mississippi · civil rules","Capacity must be stated in the initial pleading; fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity (malice, intent, and knowledge generally); denial of conditions precedent must be specific; and items of special damage must be specifically stated (the note includes consequential damages, lost profits, and punitive damages). A party ignorant of an opponent’s name may sue under a fictitious name and later substitute","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","Mississippi · civil rules","Every pleading needs a caption with the court, title, file number, and Rule 7(a) designation; the complaint names all parties and its first paragraph gives the names and, if known, addresses of all parties. Averments go in numbered paragraphs, with separate counts or defenses where that aids clarity, and an exhibit to a pleading is part of it for all purposes. Former Rule 10(d) (attachment of documents) was deleted e","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing of pleadings and motions","Mississippi · civil rules","Pleadings and motions must be signed by at least one attorney of record (or the unrepresented party) with an address; verification is not required unless a rule or statute says so, and the equity rule that a sworn answer must be overcome by two witnesses is abolished. The signature certifies the signer has read the paper, there is good ground for it, and it is not for delay; an out-of-state lawyer’s signature also ce","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections; motion for judgment on the pleadings","Mississippi · civil rules","An answer is due 30 days after service of the summons and complaint (or as directed under Rule 4), and answers to cross-claims and counterclaims are due 30 days after service; after a Rule 12 motion is denied or deferred, the responsive pleading is due 10 days after notice, or 10 days after service of a more definite statement. These times may be extended once only, for no more than 10 days, by a written stipulation ","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and cross-claim","Mississippi · civil rules","Same-transaction counterclaims are compulsory unless the claim was already pending elsewhere, the opposing claim was brought by attachment without personal jurisdiction, or the opposing party’s claim is one an insurer is defending (though res judicata or collateral estoppel may still bar it later); cross-claims are permissive, and an omitted counterclaim may be added by amendment under Rule 15 (amended eff. June 23, ","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","Mississippi · civil rules","A defending party may implead a nonparty who is or may be liable to it for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim, but needs leave of court on good cause if the third-party complaint is filed more than 14 days after serving its original answer (amended eff. Feb. 26, 2025; the historical note describes the window as ten days, but the rule text says 14). The note stresses that impleader requires derivative or secondary l","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Mississippi · civil rules","A party may amend as of course any time before a responsive pleading is served (or within 30 days after service if no response is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar); otherwise by leave, freely given, or written consent, and the response to an amended pleading is due within the time left on the original or 10 days, whichever is longer. Unlike the federal rule, the Mississippi rule places no limit on ","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 16. Pre-trial procedure","Mississippi · civil rules","The court may on its own or a party’s motion, and must on the motion of all parties, direct counsel to a conference at least 20 days before the trial setting to consider settlement, issue simplification, amendments, itemized special damages, limits on and exchange of expert reports, exchange of medical records (without abridging the physician-patient privilege), references to a master, Rule 37 sanctions, admissions, ","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 16A. Motions for recusal of judges","Mississippi · civil rules","Recusal motions must be timely filed with the trial judge and follow the procedures in the Uniform Rules of Circuit and County Court Practice and the Uniform Rules of Chancery Court Practice. Adopted Apr. 4, 2002.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 17. Parties plaintiff and defendant; capacity","Mississippi · civil rules","Actions are prosecuted by the real party in interest, with reasonable time to ratify, join, or substitute before dismissal. In subrogation cases the subrogee sues alone if the subrogor has no remaining pecuniary interest, otherwise both sue together. A guardian ad litem must be an attorney and a Mississippi resident who files a consent and oath (and any required bond), with a reasonable fee taxed as costs; an unrepre","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims and remedies","Mississippi · civil rules","A claimant may join as many claims, legal or equitable, independent or alternative, as it has against an opposing party; a claim formerly cognizable only after another claim was prosecuted to conclusion may be joined with it, with relief granted according to the parties’ substantive rights.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 19. Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","Mississippi · civil rules","A person subject to jurisdiction must be joined if complete relief cannot be had without the person or the person’s absence would impair the person’s interest or expose parties to inconsistent obligations. If joinder is not feasible, the court decides in equity and good conscience whether to proceed or dismiss, weighing four listed factors, and the pleader must name unjoined required persons and the reasons.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","Mississippi · civil rules","Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, if the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact; the court may order separate trials to prevent embarrassment, delay, or prejudice. The notes (Rule 8 and Rule 20) cite Mississippi Supreme Court decisions requiring a “distinct litigable event” and, in multi-plaintiff cases, pleading of each plaintiff’s cl","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","Mississippi · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be dropped or added by court order at any stage on just terms, and any claim may be severed. The Rule 15 note cites authority that adding a new defendant by amendment requires a Rule 21 court order.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","Mississippi · civil rules","A party exposed to double or multiple liability may require adverse claimants to interplead, even if their claims lack a common origin or the stakeholder denies liability; a defendant may obtain interpleader by cross-claim or counterclaim. The stakeholder may deposit the money or property with the court and be discharged, with the action continuing among the claimants.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","Mississippi · civil rules","[Omitted] in Mississippi; the rules contain no class-action procedure (no explanatory note accompanies the omission).","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 23.1. Derivative actions by shareholders","Mississippi · civil rules","[Omitted] in Mississippi; no rule text or note.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 23.2. Actions relating to unincorporated associations","Mississippi · civil rules","[Omitted] in Mississippi; no rule text or note.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","Mississippi · civil rules","Timely applicants must be allowed to intervene when a statute gives an unconditional right or their interest would be impaired and is not adequately represented, and may be allowed when a statute gives a conditional right or there is a common question of law or fact (also government officers or agencies administering a statute or regulation relied on). The motion must be served under Rule 5 with a pleading setting ou","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","Mississippi · civil rules","If a party dies and the claim survives, a motion to substitute (served on parties under Rule 5 and on nonparties under Rule 4) must be made within 90 days after death is suggested on the record by a served statement, or the action is dismissed without prejudice as to the decedent; the note says interested nonparties such as the estate’s representative must be served for the 90 days to run, and Rule 6(b) extensions ap","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 26. General provisions governing discovery","Mississippi · civil rules","Discovery reaches any nonprivileged matter “relevant to the issues raised by the claims or defenses of any party” (the note says this narrowed the former subject-matter scope), including information “reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence,” insurance agreements, and work product only on substantial need; the rule contains no mandatory initial disclosures. Expert discovery is by interrog","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions before action or pending appeal","Mississippi · civil rules","A person expecting to be a party may file a verified petition in circuit or chancery court in the county of residence of any expected adverse party to perpetuate testimony, serving notice like a summons at least 20 days before the hearing (publication or appointed counsel if service fails). The trial court may also allow perpetuation depositions pending appeal or before the appeal time expires; independent actions to","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Mississippi · civil rules","In the United States, before an officer authorized to administer oaths where the examination is held or a court appointee; abroad, on notice before an authorized person, by commission, or by letter rogatory. No deposition may be taken before a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or counsel, or anyone financially interested in the action.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","Mississippi · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, parties may stipulate in writing to deposition logistics and to modify discovery procedures, including extensions of time, without court approval unless the extension would interfere with the time set for completing discovery, a motion hearing, or trial (amended eff. Oct. 7, 2021).","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions upon oral examination","Mississippi · civil rules","Leave is needed only if the plaintiff seeks to depose before 30 days after service of the summons on any defendant (unless the defendant has sought discovery or the special notice for a deponent about to leave the state is used) and for prisoners; notice may provide for telephone testimony and non-stenographic recording, and an organization must designate witnesses under 30(b)(6). A deposition is deemed taken in the ","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions upon written questions","Mississippi · civil rules","Any party may take a deposition on written questions served with a notice; cross questions are due within 30 days after service of the notice and questions, redirect within 10 days after cross, and recross within 10 days after redirect, and the court may enlarge or shorten these times. The officer takes the answers under Rule 30(c), (e), and (f).","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 32. Use of depositions in court proceedings","Mississippi · civil rules","A deposition may be used to impeach, an adverse party’s or its officer’s or designee’s deposition may be used for any purpose, and any witness’s deposition may be used for any purpose if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles from the place of trial or out of state, unable to attend, unreachable by subpoena, a medical doctor, or exceptional circumstances exist. Objections to admissibility may be made at trial, but ","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories to parties","Mississippi · civil rules","Each party may serve up to 30 interrogatories, each a single question (the note counts every distinct question, including subparts), with more only by leave on a showing of necessity. Answers and objections are due within 30 days after service, except a defendant has 45 days after service of the summons and complaint; objections must be stated with specificity (general objections are improper) or are waived, and a pa","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 34. Production of documents and things and entry upon land","Mississippi · civil rules","Requests for documents, ESI, tangible things, or entry on land may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on other parties with or after service of the summons. Responses are due within 30 days (a defendant has 45 days after service of the summons and complaint); objections must state their grounds with specificity and whether responsive materials are being withheld, production must be completed by the tim","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examination of persons","Mississippi · civil rules","On motion for good cause and notice, the court may order a party (or a person in the party’s custody or control) whose physical or mental condition is in controversy to be examined by a suitably licensed or certified examiner; the requesting party pays the examiner and advances expenses. Reports are exchanged on request, and requesting the report or deposing the examiner waives privilege as to other examiners of the ","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","Mississippi · civil rules","Requests may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on other parties with or after service of the summons; each matter is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 30 days, but a defendant need not respond before 45 days after service of the summons. Lack of knowledge is no excuse without reasonable inquiry, and admitted (including deemed-admitted) matters are conclusively established ","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to make or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","Mississippi · civil rules","A motion to compel must certify a good-faith attempt to confer (eff. Feb. 20, 2023); evasive or incomplete answers count as failures, and expenses including attorney’s fees go to the winner unless the position was substantially justified. Disobeying a discovery order may lead to established facts, preclusion, striking pleadings, dismissal, default, or contempt; failing to produce a person for a Rule 35 exam, unjustif","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury trial of right","Mississippi · civil rules","The jury right declared by the Mississippi Constitution or statute is preserved inviolate; the rule contains no jury-demand requirement. Parties waive a jury only by filing a specific written stipulation of waiver asking for trial by the court, and the court may still require a jury trial.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","Mississippi · civil rules","[Omitted] in Mississippi.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 40. Assignment of cases for trial","Mississippi · civil rules","The court places cases on the trial calendar on its own, on request with notice, or as it deems expedient, after ample opportunity for discovery. The clerk must notify attorneys and unrepresented parties at least 5 days before the trial docket is set, cases must be set at least 20 days before trial unless all parties agree to less or Rule 55 applies (the note says the 20-day wait does not apply to default-judgment he","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","Mississippi · civil rules","On payment of all costs, a plaintiff may dismiss without order by notice before service of an answer or a motion for summary judgment, whichever is first, or by stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared; otherwise by court order on terms (a pending counterclaim survives). Involuntary dismissal for failure to prosecute or obey rules or orders, and a nonjury motion at the close of the plaintiff’s case, operat","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials","Mississippi · civil rules","Actions sharing a common question may be consolidated or jointly heard, and the court may order separate trials of claims or issues for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or for economy, preserving the jury right under Section 31 of the Mississippi Constitution. Actions in different counties of a single circuit or chancery district may be consolidated for pretrial proceedings (all matters before trial except dispositiv","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 43. Taking of testimony","Mississippi · civil rules","Trial testimony is taken orally in open court unless these rules or the Mississippi Rules of Evidence provide otherwise (the note says telephone testimony is within the court’s discretion); an affirmation may replace an oath, and motions on facts outside the record may be heard on affidavits, oral testimony, or depositions. The court may appoint and set compensation for an interpreter, taxable as costs, but interpret","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 44. Proof of documents","Mississippi · civil rules","A domestic official record is proved by official publication or a copy attested by its custodian; a record kept outside Mississippi also needs the custodian’s certificate under oath that the custodian has legal custody and the record is kept under state law. Foreign official records need a final certification (excusable for good cause), a certified no-record statement proves absence of a record, and other lawful meth","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 44.1. Determination of foreign law","Mississippi · civil rules","[Omitted] in Mississippi; the note explains that Miss. Code § 13-1-149 requires courts to take judicial notice of all foreign law.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","Mississippi · civil rules","Subpoenas issue from the court where the action is pending, signed in blank by the clerk or issued by any Mississippi-admitted or pro hac vice attorney of record (eff. Feb. 22, 2023), and a copy must be served on all parties under Rule 5 immediately once filled out, before the subpoena itself is served; foreign (out-of-state) subpoenas are reissued by the clerk of the county where discovery is sought. Service is pers","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 46. Exceptions unnecessary","Mississippi · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary; a party need only state the action it wants or objects to and the grounds, and failing to object does not prejudice a party who had no opportunity to do so.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 47. Jurors","Mississippi · civil rules","Jurors are examined under oath on qualifications, by the court (with counsel allowed to supplement) or by counsel, and are selected as provided by statute. Each side gets 4 peremptory challenges before a 12-person jury and 2 before a 6-person jury; the court may allow multi-party sides to exercise them separately or jointly and allow additional ones, but each side must have the same number. The judge may seat 1 or 2 ","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 48. Juries and jury verdicts","Mississippi · civil rules","Circuit and chancery court juries have 12 members and a verdict of 9 or more is the jury’s verdict; county court juries have 6 members and a verdict of 5 or more controls.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 49. General verdicts and special verdicts","Mississippi · civil rules","Juries return general verdicts unless the court uses a special verdict or a general verdict with written interrogatories; an issue omitted from a special verdict is waived unless a jury submission is demanded before the jury retires. Neither special procedure may be used unless the court gives counsel the written questions a reasonable time before closing arguments; the note says a separation of economic and non-econ","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 50. Motions for a directed verdict and for judgment notwithstanding the verdict","Mississippi · civil rules","A directed-verdict motion must state specific grounds and does not waive a jury; a JNOV motion must be filed within 10 days after entry of judgment (or within 10 days after the jury is discharged if no verdict), and the court cannot extend that time. Unlike the federal rule, a directed-verdict motion is not a prerequisite to a JNOV motion (note). If JNOV is granted the court rules conditionally on any new-trial motio","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 51. Instructions to jury","Mississippi · civil rules","Each party may submit 6 substantive instructions (more with the court’s permission), due at the Rule 16 pretrial hearing or, if none, delivered to the court and all counsel at least 24 hours before trial; instructions are numbered with C, P, or D prefixes (Roman numerals for multiple aligned parties). Objections must be made distinctly on the record before the instructions go to the jury, all instructions except proc","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings by the court","Mississippi · civil rules","In nonjury trials the court may, and must on any party’s request or when a rule requires, find facts specially and state conclusions of law (the note says findings generally should be made in complex cases and are required in marital-asset division). A motion to amend or add findings must be filed within 10 days after entry of judgment or findings, and the court may act on its own in the same period; sufficiency of t","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters, referees, and commissioners","Mississippi · civil rules","Masters (including referees, auditors, examiners, commissioners, and special commissioners) must be attorneys, except non-lawyer experts in extraordinary technical matters and special commissioners for judicial sales and partitions; reference is by written consent of the parties or on an exceptional condition. Unless the order says otherwise, the master holds a first meeting within 10 days of the order of reference; ","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgments; costs","Mississippi · civil rules","“Judgment” includes a final decree and any appealable order; with multiple claims or parties, a judgment on fewer than all is final only on an express determination of no just reason for delay and express direction for entry (the note says certification should be granted cautiously). An order summarily dismissing a post-conviction relief motion under Miss. Code § 99-39-11(2) must identify and file the records relied ","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 55. Default","Mississippi · civil rules","The clerk enters default when a party fails to plead or otherwise defend and that is shown by affidavit; default judgment always requires application to the court, and a party who has appeared (which the note says can include informal written or counsel communications indicating intent to defend) must get written notice at least 3 days before the hearing, except that default judgment may be entered without that notic","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","Mississippi · civil rules","A claimant may move after 30 days from commencement (or after the adverse party moves), and a defending party at any time; the motion must be served at least 10 days before the hearing and opposing affidavits may be served before the day of the hearing. Judgment is rendered if there is no genuine issue of material fact, with Rule 56(d) orders narrowing facts, Rule 56(f) continuances, and expenses and contempt for bad","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments","Mississippi · civil rules","Courts of record may declare rights, status, and legal relations whether or not further relief could be claimed, may decline when a declaration would not end the controversy, and may advance the case for a speedy hearing; another adequate remedy does not bar relief and jury rights are preserved. Covered matters include deeds, wills, contracts, statutes, ordinances, franchises, and fiduciary and estate questions, and ","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 58. Entry of judgment","Mississippi · civil rules","Every judgment must be set out in a separate document titled “Judgment,” but a judgment fully adjudicating all claims as to all parties and entered under Rule 79(a) is final even if mistitled, absent prejudice. A judgment is effective only when entered under Rule 79(a); the note stresses that the 10-day post-trial motion deadlines and the 30-day M.R.A.P. 4 appeal time run from entry.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 59. New trials; amendment of judgments","Mississippi · civil rules","A new trial may be granted on grounds historically recognized at law (jury cases) or for rehearing in equity (nonjury cases); the motion must be filed within 10 days after entry of judgment, and the court may order a new trial on its own within the same 10 days or, after notice and hearing, on a ground not stated in a timely motion. Supporting affidavits are filed with the motion and opposing affidavits are due withi","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from judgment or order","Mississippi · civil rules","Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time until the record is transmitted to the appellate court, then only with its leave. Relief under 60(b) is available for (1) fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct of an adverse party, (2) accident or mistake, (3) newly discovered evidence, (4) a void judgment, (5) satisfaction or prospective inequity, or (6) any other reason, within a reasonable time and not more than 6 m","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","Mississippi · civil rules","No error in evidentiary rulings or other acts or omissions is ground for a new trial or disturbing a judgment unless refusal to act appears inconsistent with substantial justice; the court must disregard errors not affecting substantial rights.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","Mississippi · civil rules","Unless a statute or court order (for good cause) provides otherwise, no execution or enforcement may issue until 30 days after entry of judgment, but injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed. The court may stay enforcement pending a motion under Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59, 59(e), or 60(b), may modify injunctions pending appeal, and stays on appeal are as authorized by statute or otherwise; the State and its of","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 63. Judge’s inability to proceed","Mississippi · civil rules","If the trial judge cannot proceed during trial, another judge sitting in or assigned to the court may finish after certifying familiarity with the record, or may grant a new trial; the same applies to post-verdict or post-hearing duties in nonjury cases.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 64. Seizure of person or property","Mississippi · civil rules","Pre-judgment remedies for seizure of person or property to secure satisfaction of the eventual judgment — attachment, replevin, claim and delivery, sequestration, and equivalents — are available as provided by law, whether ancillary or by independent action. Garnishment was removed from the list eff. Sept. 1, 1987.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions","Mississippi · civil rules","No preliminary injunction issues without notice, and the merits trial may be consolidated with the hearing. A TRO without notice requires specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint showing immediate irreparable injury and counsel’s written certification of notice efforts, and expires within the time fixed, not to exceed 10 days (the 10-day limit does not apply in domestic relations cases), unless extended for ","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 65.1. Security: proceedings against sureties","Mississippi · civil rules","A surety on any bond or undertaking given under these rules submits to the court’s jurisdiction and irrevocably appoints the clerk as agent for service, and its liability may be enforced by motion without an independent action, with the clerk mailing copies to sureties whose addresses are known. (This text appears in the full official file but is missing from the notes-stripped working copy.)","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers","Mississippi · civil rules","An action in which a receiver has been appointed may be dismissed only by court order; otherwise actions seeking a receiver or by or against one are governed by these rules.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit in court","Mississippi · civil rules","By leave of court and on notice to all parties, a party may deposit money or a deliverable thing in dispute with the court; the judge may order money deposited at interest in a federally insured bank or savings and loan authorized to receive public funds, payable only on the clerk’s check with a certified order.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of judgment","Mississippi · civil rules","A party defending a claim may serve an offer of judgment more than 15 days before trial, and the offeree has 10 days after service to accept in writing; an unaccepted offer is deemed withdrawn and inadmissible except on costs. If the offeree’s final judgment is not more favorable, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer; after liability is determined, an offer may be made a reasonable time, not less than 10 d","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution","Mississippi · civil rules","Money judgments are enforced, and supplementary proceedings conducted, as provided by statute. To aid satisfaction of a judgment of more than $100, the judgment creditor may examine the debtor or any other person, and their books and papers, in open court as provided by statute or through the Rule 26–37 discovery devices.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 70. Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","Mississippi · civil rules","If a party fails to perform a judgment directing a conveyance or other specific act within the time specified, the court may have it done by an appointee at the party’s cost, may divest and vest title to property within Mississippi by judgment, may have the sheriff deliver possession on a certified copy of the order, and may hold the party in contempt.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 71. Process in behalf of and against persons not parties","Mississippi · civil rules","A nonparty in whose favor an order is made may enforce it as if a party (except a creditor of a party to a divorce proceeding), and a nonparty against whom obedience may lawfully be enforced is subject to the same process; the note says personal jurisdiction over the nonparty is still required.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 71A. Eminent domain","Mississippi · civil rules","[Omitted] in Mississippi; Rule 81(a)(7) lists eminent domain among proceedings governed by statutory procedure (the note cites Miss. Code § 11-27-1 et seq.).","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 72-76. [Appeals — Chapter IX]","Mississippi · civil rules","[Omitted] in Mississippi (Rules 72 to 76); the Advisory Committee Notes refer to the Mississippi Rules of Appellate Procedure for appeals (e.g., M.R.A.P. 4: notice of appeal within 30 days after entry of judgment, tolled by timely post-trial motions).","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 77. Courts and clerks","Mississippi · civil rules","Courts are deemed always open for filing, process, and interlocutory motions and orders; trials on the merits are in open court, while other proceedings may be held in chambers anywhere in the state, though no hearing may be held outside the district without the consent of all affected parties. The clerk’s office is open business hours except Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays, and the clerk must immediately serv","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 78. Motion practice","Mississippi · civil rules","Each court must set procedures for prompt hearing of motions, the judge may make orders for advancement and conduct of actions on reasonable notice, and courts may by rule or order decide motions without oral hearing on brief written statements.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 79. Books and records kept by the clerk and entries therein","Mississippi · civil rules","The clerk keeps a general docket noting every filing, process, return, appearance, order, verdict, and judgment by file number, with entries showing the actual date of entry (which starts post-judgment and appeal clocks); a Minute Book with a copy of every judgment and order; indexes and trial calendars; and other required records, which may be photocopied. A case file may not leave the clerk’s office without the cou","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 80. Stenographic report or transcript as evidence","Mississippi · civil rules","[Omitted] in Mississippi; the note points to statutes on court reporters (Miss. Code § 9-13-1 et seq.) and M.R.A.P. Appendix III.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 81. Applicability of rules","Mississippi · civil rules","The rules apply to all civil proceedings but only to a limited extent (statutory procedures control where in conflict, these rules fill gaps) in 12 categories: habeas corpus; attorney discipline; Youth Court and Family Court Law proceedings; election contests; bond validations; commitment of addicts and persons needing mental treatment; eminent domain; Title 91 (trusts and estates) and Title 93 (domestic relations) o","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 82. Jurisdiction and venue","Mississippi · civil rules","The rules neither extend nor limit jurisdiction, and venue is as provided by statute except that properly joined claims or parties may be brought in any county where any one claim could be, with Rules 13, 14, 22, and 24 joinders treated as ancillary; the note flags a conflict with Miss. Code § 11-11-3(2), which requires each joined plaintiff to establish venue independently. An action filed in the wrong county is not","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 83. Local court rules","Mississippi · civil rules","The conference of circuit, chancery, and county judges may adopt uniform rules, and any court by majority of its judges (the senior judge has an extra vote if there is no majority) may adopt local rules, consistent with these rules. All local and uniform rules must be filed with and approved by the Supreme Court of Mississippi (with an electronic copy) before taking effect and are published in the Southern Reporter.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 84. Forms","Mississippi · civil rules","The forms in the Appendix of Forms are sufficient under the rules and illustrate the simplicity and brevity of statement the rules contemplate.","#st-ms-rules"],["Rule 85. Title","Mississippi · civil rules","The rules are cited as the Mississippi Rules of Civil Procedure, “M.R.C.P.”; e.g., M.R.C.P. 85.","#st-ms-rules"],["Appeals in Mississippi: file everything in the Supreme Court, then live with the assignment","Mississippi · appeals","Every appeal from a final trial-court judgment starts with a notice of appeal filed with the trial-court clerk within 30 days. The appeal is docketed in the Supreme Court, which either keeps the case or assigns it to the Court of Appeals. A party has no right to a Supreme Court hearing, except in the categories that by statute the Supreme Court must keep, and cannot ask for reassignment. A Court of Appeals decision i","#st-ms-appeals"],["One procedure, one filing court","Mississippi · appeals · Which court, and what is appealable","Appeals of right in civil and criminal cases are perfected only by timely filing a notice of appeal with the trial-court clerk. Every other step is enforced by sanctions, not jurisdiction. All appeals from final trial-court orders are filed in the Supreme Court, which assigns cases to the Court of Appeals. M.R.A.P. 3(a), 16(a)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Cases the Supreme Court keeps","Mississippi · appeals · Which court, and what is appealable","The Supreme Court may not assign appeals involving the death penalty, utility rates, annexations, bond issues, election contests, or a trial-court ruling that a statute is unconstitutional. It also keeps attorney discipline, judicial performance, and federal certified questions. It ordinarily keeps major questions of first impression, urgent issues of broad public importance, substantial constitutional challenges, an","#st-ms-appeals"],["No reassignment requests","Mississippi · appeals · Which court, and what is appealable","Once a case is assigned to the Court of Appeals, neither a party nor that court may ask for reassignment. Only the Supreme Court may move it on its own motion. A party has no right to be heard by the Supreme Court. M.R.A.P. 16(d)–(e)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Partial judgments","Mississippi · appeals · Which court, and what is appealable","Interlocutory review of a judgment on fewer than all claims or parties is available through an M.R.C.P. 54(b) final-judgment certification. This is separate from Rule 5 permissive appeals. M.R.A.P. 5 cmt.; M.R.C.P. 54(b)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Appeals to circuit court from justice court, municipal court, and agencies","Mississippi · appeals · Which court, and what is appealable","Direct appeals from justice and municipal court are tried de novo in circuit court. All other appeals to circuit court, including from agencies and local governing bodies, are on the record. In both, the notice of appeal and the costs must be filed and paid with the circuit clerk within 30 days of entry of the order or judgment, with a certificate of service. In an on-the-record appeal, the record is due within 30 da","#st-ms-appeals"],["30 days from entry, filed with the trial-court clerk","Mississippi · appeals · Notice of appeal: time, tolling, and cross-appeals","File the notice with the trial-court clerk within 30 days after the judgment or order is entered. A notice mistakenly filed in the Supreme Court is sent back down and treated as filed on the date the Supreme Court received it. A notice filed after the decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed on the day of entry. The notice must name the appealing parties and the parties appealed against and designat","#st-ms-appeals"],["Cross-appeals and other parties: 14 days after the first notice","Mississippi · appeals · Notice of appeal: time, tolling, and cross-appeals","Any other party may file a notice of appeal within 14 days after the first notice is filed, or within the normal 30 days if that is later. A cross-appeal requires its own notice of appeal. M.R.A.P. 4(c) & cmt.","#st-ms-appeals"],["Tolling motions, all due 10 days after entry","Mississippi · appeals · Notice of appeal: time, tolling, and cross-appeals","A timely motion under M.R.C.P. 50(b), 52(b), or 59, or a Rule 60 motion filed within 10 days after entry, restarts the appeal time for all parties. The new time runs from the order disposing of the last such motion. Each motion is due 10 days after entry of judgment, and the trial court cannot extend that time. A late “motion to reconsider” therefore does not toll the time. M.R.A.P. 4(d); M.R.C.P. 6(b), 50(b), 52(b),","#st-ms-appeals"],["Premature notices ripen, and no amended notice is needed","Mississippi · appeals · Notice of appeal: time, tolling, and cross-appeals","A notice filed before the tolling motions are decided takes effect once the last motion is decided, and it covers the order deciding the motion too. Unlike the federal rule, no amended notice is required. The comment still advises filing after the motions are decided. M.R.A.P. 4(d) & cmt.","#st-ms-appeals"],["Extension only by the trial court; appellate courts cannot extend","Mississippi · appeals · Notice of appeal: time, tolling, and cross-appeals","The trial court may extend the time on a motion filed no later than 30 days after the appeal time expires. A motion filed before the time expires needs good cause and may be ex parte; one filed after needs notice and excusable neglect. No extension may exceed 30 days past the original deadline or 10 days after the order granting it, whichever is later. The appellate courts may not extend the time to appeal, or to pet","#st-ms-appeals"],["Reopening when notice of the judgment was not received","Mississippi · appeals · Notice of appeal: time, tolling, and cross-appeals","If a party did not receive notice of entry from the clerk or any party within 21 days, and no party would be prejudiced, the trial court may reopen the time for 14 days. The motion must be filed within 180 days of entry or within 7 days of receiving notice, whichever is earlier. Winning parties are encouraged to serve their own notice of entry to cut off this period. M.R.A.P. 4(h) & cmt.","#st-ms-appeals"],["Parties under disability, and taxpayer appeals","Mississippi · appeals · Notice of appeal: time, tolling, and cross-appeals","For minors and persons of unsound mind, the appeal periods do not start until the disability is removed. If such a person was the plaintiff, or was a defendant with a guardian ad litem, the appeal must be taken within 2 years of entry. If a county or municipal board or officer sued in an official capacity does not appeal within 20 days, or within 7 days after another party’s notice, any taxpayer may prosecute the app","#st-ms-appeals"],["Seven-day steps: designation and cost deposit","Mississippi · appeals · Record, costs, and stays","Within 7 days after filing the notice of appeal:\n• Designate the record. File it with the trial-court clerk and serve it on the court reporter and the appellee. If you will argue that a finding is unsupported by or contrary to the evidence, include a transcript of all the evidence relevant to it.\n• Deposit the estimated cost of the record with the trial-court clerk. File a certificate of compliance and serve it on al","#st-ms-appeals"],["Transcript timing and record checks","Mississippi · appeals · Record, costs, and stays","The reporter has 60 days from service of the designation to file the transcript. The trial court may grant one 30-day extension; any later extension comes from the Supreme Court Clerk. Counsel then have 14 days to examine the record and propose corrections. The trial-court clerk assembles the clerk’s papers within 30 days. M.R.A.P. 10(b)(5), 11(c)–(d)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Supersedeas: 125% bond approved by the clerk","Mississippi · appeals · Record, costs, and stays","A bond of 125% of a money judgment, approved by the trial-court clerk, stays execution. Other stays, and contested or reduced bonds, go first to the trial court, which may set a lower bond for good cause. For punitive damages, the bond is the lower of 125% of the punitive award or 10% of the defendant’s net worth, capped at $100,000,000 absent unusual circumstances. The net-worth option requires showing that the full","#st-ms-appeals"],["Schedule: 40 / 30 / 14 days","Mississippi · appeals · Briefs: schedule, contents, and limits","The appellant’s brief is due 40 days after the record is filed, the appellee’s brief 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief, and the reply 14 days after that. In cross-appeals, the combined responsive brief is due 30 days after the appellee/cross-appellant’s brief, and the cross-appellant’s reply 14 days after that. The deadlines run even if the clerk’s notice that the record was filed never arrives. M.R.A.P.","#st-ms-appeals"],["Page limits, not word counts","Mississippi · appeals · Briefs: schedule, contents, and limits","Principal briefs are limited to 50 pages and reply briefs to 25. Pages for the oral-argument statement, certificates, tables, and statutory addendum do not count. Text must be at least 12-point and footnotes at least 11-point, double-spaced, with a 1½-inch left margin. The clerk may raise the limit to 75 pages on an unopposed motion (125 in death-penalty appeals). M.R.A.P. 27(b)(5), 28(h), 32(a)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Required contents unique to Mississippi","Mississippi · appeals · Briefs: schedule, contents, and limits","The appellant’s brief starts with a Certificate of Interested Persons, which is used for recusal review (government parties are exempt). It must also include a Statement of Assignment and numbered issues; issues not distinctly identified are not argued, except plain error. Each brief cover must state whether oral argument is requested. M.R.A.P. 28(a)(1)–(4), 32 cmt.","#st-ms-appeals"],["Record excerpts filed with the appellant’s brief","Mississippi · appeals · Briefs: schedule, contents, and limits","With the brief, the appellant must file separately indexed record excerpts containing the trial-court docket, the judgment or order appealed from, the other rulings under review, and the trial court’s findings or opinion. The appellee may add its own excerpts. M.R.A.P. 30(a)–(b)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Amicus briefs","Mississippi · appeals · Briefs: schedule, contents, and limits","An amicus brief requires leave of court, except one presented by the State through the Attorney General or by a non-party guardian ad litem. The motion is due within 7 days after the initial brief of the party the amicus supports, and the proposed brief is limited to 15 pages. A party may oppose within 7 days. M.R.A.P. 29","#st-ms-appeals"],["Failure to file and deficiency notices","Mississippi · appeals · Briefs: schedule, contents, and limits","An appeal may be dismissed for failure to prosecute or for substantial noncompliance, after the clerk sends a deficiency notice and 14 days pass without a cure. The 2026 amendment deleted the sentence barring motions for more time to file briefs after a deficiency notice. An appellee who files no brief may not argue without permission. M.R.A.P. 2(a)(2) (as amended eff. June 19, 2026), 31(d)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Motions","Mississippi · appeals · Motions, rehearing, and certiorari","A response is due 7 days after service. The clerk may rule on unopposed procedural motions, including extensions totaling up to 60 days, extra brief pages, withdrawal or substitution of counsel, voluntary dismissal, and pro hac vice appearances. M.R.A.P. 27(a)–(b)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Rehearing: 14 days, 25 pages, no oral argument","Mississippi · appeals · Motions, rehearing, and certiorari","A motion for rehearing is due 14 days after the merits decision of either court. It must state with particularity what the court overlooked or misapprehended, is limited to 25 pages, and is decided without oral argument. Any response is due within 7 days. After a denial, no further rehearing motion is allowed; after a grant, a second motion may be filed within 14 days of the judgment on rehearing. M.R.A.P. 40(a)–(b)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Certiorari to the Supreme Court","Mississippi · appeals · Motions, rehearing, and certiorari","A rehearing motion in the Court of Appeals is required first. The petition is due within 14 days after the Court of Appeals rules on rehearing. It is limited to 10 pages, may not incorporate other documents by reference, and must attach the opinion, the judgment, and the rehearing motion. A response (optional, 10 pages) is due within 7 days. No briefs or argument are allowed unless requested. Four justices must vote ","#st-ms-appeals"],["Mandate","Mississippi · appeals · Motions, rehearing, and certiorari","The Supreme Court’s mandate issues 21 days after judgment, or 7 days after rehearing is denied. The Court of Appeals’ mandate issues 21 days after the latest of judgment, decision on rehearing, or denial or dismissal of certiorari. A stay of mandate pending a U.S. Supreme Court petition is limited to 90 days unless extended. M.R.A.P. 41(a)–(c)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Permissive interlocutory appeal: 21-day petition to the Supreme Court","Mississippi · appeals · Interlocutory review and writs","The petition must show a substantial basis for difference of opinion on a question of law. It must also show that review may materially advance the end of the litigation and avoid exceptional expense, protect a party from substantial and irreparable injury, or resolve an issue of general importance. File it with the Supreme Court Clerk within 21 days after the order is entered, and serve the trial judge and all parti","#st-ms-appeals"],["Recusal review and extraordinary writs","Mississippi · appeals · Interlocutory review and writs","Denial of a trial-judge recusal motion, or failure to rule within 30 days, may be reviewed by petition to the Supreme Court within 14 days, under the M.R.A.P. 21 writ procedure. The standard is abuse of discretion. M.R.A.P. 21, 48B","#st-ms-appeals"],["Electronic filing is mandatory for attorneys","Mississippi · appeals · Filing, service, time computation, and e-filing","Since Jan. 1, 2014, briefs, motions, responses, and compliance documents in the Supreme Court and Court of Appeals must be filed electronically by a registered attorney, or are scanned by the Clerk. Pro hac vice attorneys may register. Conventionally filed briefs and record excerpts are timely when mailed, if they include a certificate of mailing; other papers are filed when received. M.R.A.P. 25(a)(1)–(2); Appellate","#st-ms-appeals"],["Computing time","Mississippi · appeals · Filing, service, time computation, and e-filing","Exclude the trigger day. A last day that falls on a weekend, a legal holiday, or a day the clerk’s office is closed rolls to the next business day. For periods under 7 days, intermediate weekends and holidays are excluded. Add 3 days after service by mail. M.R.A.P. 26(a), (c)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Pro hac vice on appeal","Mississippi · appeals · Filing, service, time computation, and e-filing","Lawyers not admitted in Mississippi may file or argue only after pro hac vice admission under Rule 46(b), with local counsel, the $300 Bar payment, and filing with the Supreme Court Clerk. Withdrawal requires court approval. M.R.A.P. 46(a)–(c)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (civil)","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the judgment or order.\nFiled with the trial-court clerk. Mandatory dismissal if late; the appellate courts cannot extend it in civil cases (M.R.A.P. 2(a)(1), 2(c)). The trial court can extend it on a motion filed within 30 days after the time runs: for good cause if filed before the deadline, otherwise only for excusable neglect with notice to the other parties; no extension may run past 30 days","#st-ms-appeals"],["Notice of appeal by another party / cross-appeal","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or the 30-day period, if later). M.R.A.P. 4(c)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Tolling post-trial motions (M.R.C.P. 50(b), 52(b), 59; Rule 60 to toll)","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended (M.R.C.P. 6(b)). Appeal time then runs from the order disposing of the last motion. M.R.C.P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e); M.R.A.P. 4(d)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Motion in trial court to extend appeal time","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from expiration of the original appeal time.\nExcusable neglect after expiration. Extension may not exceed 30 days past the original deadline or 10 days after the order, whichever is later. M.R.A.P. 4(g)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Motion to reopen appeal time (no notice of entry received)","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","180 days from entry of judgment (or 7 days after receiving notice, if earlier).\nIf granted, the time is reopened for 14 days from the order. M.R.A.P. 4(h)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Designation of the record; cost deposit and certificate","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nReporter estimate fallback: $300 per day of proceedings. M.R.A.P. 10(b)(1), 11(b)(1)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Appellee’s additional designation","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the appellant’s designation. M.R.A.P. 10(b)(4)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Court reporter files transcript","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from service of the designation.\nOne 30-day extension from the trial court; later extensions from the Supreme Court Clerk. M.R.A.P. 11(c)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from filing of the record.\n50-page limit (M.R.A.P. 28(h)). Record excerpts filed at the same time (M.R.A.P. 30). M.R.A.P. 31(b)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the appellant’s brief.\n50-page limit. M.R.A.P. 31(b)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Reply brief","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\n25-page limit. M.R.A.P. 31(b)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Cure of a deficiency notice","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from clerk’s notice of deficiency.\nOtherwise the clerk dismisses the appeal. M.R.A.P. 2(a)(2)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Response to a motion","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from service of the motion. M.R.A.P. 27(a)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Petition for interlocutory appeal","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from entry of the trial-court order.\nFiled in the Supreme Court. Not extendable (M.R.A.P. 26(b)). Answer within 14 days after service; 15-page limit. M.R.A.P. 5(a)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Docket fee after interlocutory appeal is granted","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from order granting permission to appeal. M.R.A.P. 5(d)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Motion for rehearing","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from decision on the merits (Supreme Court or Court of Appeals).\n25-page limit. Response within 7 days. M.R.A.P. 40(a)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Petition for certiorari to the Supreme Court","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from court of Appeals’ judgment on the motion for rehearing.\n10-page limit. Rehearing in the Court of Appeals is a prerequisite. Response within 7 days. Deemed denied if not acted on within 90 days after the response deadline. M.R.A.P. 17(b)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Supplemental brief after certiorari is granted","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from notice of grant.\n10-page limit; no extensions. M.R.A.P. 17(h)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Supreme Court mandate","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from entry of judgment.\n7 days after an order denying rehearing. M.R.A.P. 41(a)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Review of a denied trial-judge recusal motion","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from the ruling, or the end of the judge’s 30 days to rule. M.R.A.P. 48B","#st-ms-appeals"],["Appellate recusal motion","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from clerk’s notice of assignment to an appellate court.\nReconsideration by the full court within 14 days of denial. M.R.A.P. 48C(a)(ii)","#st-ms-appeals"],["Appeal from justice or municipal court, or agency, to circuit court","Mississippi · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order or judgment.\nNotice and costs filed together with the circuit clerk. Record due 30 days after the notice in on-the-record appeals (UCRCCC 5.05). UCRCCC 5.04","#st-ms-appeals"],["Montana Supreme Court","Montana · courts","The only appellate court for district court cases: Montana has no intermediate court of appeals, so every civil appeal goes straight to the Supreme Court, which also hears original proceedings and writs of supervisory control and answers certified questions. A chief justice and six associate justices, elected statewide for 8-year terms. The Court adopts the rules of procedure and governs admission, pro hac vice, and ","#st-mt"],["District Courts (22 judicial districts)","Montana · courts","General-jurisdiction trial courts organized into 22 judicial districts, from one judge in many rural districts to 10 judges in the 13th District (Yellowstone County). The Montana Rules of Civil Procedure govern all civil actions and proceedings here, including probate unless the Uniform Probate Code says otherwise.","#st-mt"],["Justices’ Courts, City Courts, and Municipal Courts","Montana · courts","Courts of limited jurisdiction listed in Mont. Code Ann. 3-1-101. They use separate rules (the Justice and City Court Rules of Civil Procedure and Uniform Rules), and appeals from them go first to the district court.","#st-mt"],["Water Court and Workers’ Compensation Court","Montana · courts","Statewide specialized courts. Water Court judges and the workers’ compensation judge are outside the peremptory substitution statute, and all Workers’ Compensation Court appeals go to the Supreme Court and are subject to mandatory appellate mediation.","#st-mt"],["Statute: Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (2025)","Montana · anti-SLAPP","Montana enacted the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act in 2025 as Title 27, chapter 34. It is a true anti-SLAPP statute with a special motion, an automatic stay, mandatory fees for a winning movant, and an immediate appeal. The MCA pages collected do not show the effective date or whether it reaches cases already pending; check Chapter 250, Laws of 2025, before relying on it in a case filed in 2025. Mont. Code ","#st-mt-slapp"],["Scope: three kinds of protected activity","Montana · anti-SLAPP","Covers any claim in a civil action against a person based on (a) communication in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding; (b) communication on an issue under consideration or review in such a proceeding; or (c) exercise of freedom of speech or press, assembly, petition, or association under the U.S. or Montana Constitution on a matter of public concern.\nExcluded: claims a","#st-mt-slapp"],["Procedure: 60 days to file, automatic stay, 60-day hearing and ruling","Montana · anti-SLAPP","File a special motion for expedited relief within 60 days after being served with the complaint, counterclaim, crossclaim, third-party complaint, or other pleading, or later for good cause. Filing stays all other proceedings between the movant and the responding party, including discovery and pending motions, until the ruling and the time to appeal run out; on motion the court may also stay related matters with other","#st-mt-slapp"],["Standard: dismissal with prejudice","Montana · anti-SLAPP","The court looks at the pleadings, the motion papers, and any evidence usable on summary judgment under M. R. Civ. P. 56. It must dismiss with prejudice if the movant shows the Act applies, the responding party fails to show an exclusion, and either the responding party fails to make a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of material fact. A volun","#st-mt-slapp"],["Fees: mandatory for a winning movant","Montana · anti-SLAPP","The court must award costs, reasonable attorney fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion to a movant who prevails. A responding party gets them only if it prevails and the court finds the motion frivolous or filed solely to delay. Mont. Code Ann. 27-34-110","#st-mt-slapp"],["Appeal: of right, on a Montana-specific clock","Montana · anti-SLAPP","A movant may appeal as of right from an order denying the motion in whole or part. The statute sets its own deadline: 30 days after entry of the order between private parties, 60 days if the United States, the State, or a political subdivision is a party. An appeal stays all proceedings between all parties until it ends (subject to the same exceptions). Mont. Code Ann. 27-34-109, -104(3)","#st-mt-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: written application through the Bar, leave of the presiding court","Montana · out of state","A lawyer admitted in the highest court of another state may appear in a Montana action with leave of the presiding court if an active Montana lawyer is associated as attorney of record. Get the application from the Bar Admissions Administrator (State Bar of Montana) and file it there with a fee equal to the annual dues of an active Montana member (no dollar amount is stated in the rule). The Administrator certifies y","#st-mt-oos"],["Two-appearance limit, residency bar, and continuing dues","Montana · out of state","After a lawyer’s or firm’s second pro hac vice appearance (counting appearances after November 17, 1998), no more are allowed except on good cause, which is “not to be routinely granted” (examples: expertise not commonly available in Montana, or multistate class actions). You cannot appear pro hac vice at all if you live in Montana, are regularly employed there, or practice or do substantial business there, unless yo","#st-mt-oos"],["Local counsel must actually work the case","Montana · out of state","The Montana attorney of record must sign all pleadings, motions, and briefs and participate actively in all phases, including attending depositions and hearings and preparing discovery responses and briefs. The court or agency may waive this only on local counsel’s motion showing extraordinary circumstances, and waivers are “not to be routinely granted.” Mont. Bar Admission R. VI.J","#st-mt-oos"],["Sworn certification to the Supreme Court before you practice","Montana · out of state","A lawyer who is not an active Montana Bar member and seeks to practice in any state or federal court in Montana pro hac vice must, before practicing, certify in writing and under oath to the Montana Supreme Court that the lawyer will be bound by the Montana Rules of Professional Conduct (except Rules 6.1–6.4) and subject to Montana discipline, and mail a copy to the State Bar in Helena. This is separate from the pro ","#st-mt-oos"],["Temporary practice without admission","Montana · out of state","Montana adopted the ABA multijurisdictional-practice safe harbors: temporary services with an actively participating Montana lawyer, services related to a proceeding in which you are or expect to be authorized to appear, ADR-related work, and work arising out of your home-state practice. In-house counsel (including qualifying foreign in-house lawyers) may work from a Montana office for their employer, but not in matt","#st-mt-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances are built into the civil rules","Montana · out of state","A lawyer may provide limited representation under Rule 1.2(c). Ghostwriting is allowed: you may draft a filing for a self-represented person without signing it, and may rely on the client’s facts unless you have reason to believe they are false. Appearing in court is a general appearance unless you file and serve a notice of limited appearance before or at the proceeding; service on you is then valid only for that pr","#st-mt-oos"],["E-filing: pro hac vice lawyers may register","Montana · out of state","Montana’s statewide e-filing system is open to licensed Montana attorneys and staff, attorneys appearing pro hac vice, and others the court allows. Registering is consent to electronic notification of service and to e-file everything in the case the system accepts. Updating your e-filing profile does not update the courts’ contact records; notify each clerk separately. Participation is voluntary unless a court design","#st-mt-oos"],["Governing code: Montana Rules of Professional Conduct","Montana · ethics","Adopted by the Montana Supreme Court; the State Law Library compilation shows the last amendment on October 29, 2020 (AF 09-0688). Based on the ABA Model Rules but with Montana text and numbering (no official comments are printed in the compilation). Mont. R. Prof. Conduct (AF 09-0688, through Oct. 29, 2020)","#st-mt-ethics"],["Different numbering after Rule 1.16","Montana · ethics","Rule 1.17 is a Montana rule on full-time government lawyers taking outside work; Rule 1.18 is the mandatory IOLTA program; sale of a practice is Rule 1.19 (entire practice; client consent presumed after 90 days’ notice); duties to prospective clients are Rule 1.20. Rule 7.4 has been eliminated. Cite carefully. Mont. R. Prof. Conduct 1.17–1.20, 7.4","#st-mt-ethics"],["Opponents cannot use the Rules as weapons","Montana · ethics","Montana’s preamble says a violation does not give rise to a cause of action or a presumption of breach, does not necessarily warrant disqualification, and that an opposing party or lawyer has no standing to enforce the Rules in a collateral proceeding outside the disciplinary process. Expect this language in response to motions to disqualify. Mont. R. Prof. Conduct, Preamble ¶ (21)","#st-mt-ethics"],["Fee terms in writing, with a $500 floor","Montana · ethics","Scope of the representation, any change in scope, and the basis or rate of fees and expenses must be communicated in writing, before or within a reasonable time after starting. Exceptions: a regularly represented client billed on the same basis, and matters reasonably expected to cost the client $500 or less. Contingent-fee agreements must be signed and must say whether expenses come off before or after the fee. Mont","#st-mt-ethics"],["Liens are restricted","Montana · ethics","A charging lien may be asserted only against causes of action or counterclaims in litigation, as and to the extent Mont. Code Ann. 37-61-420(2) allows. A retaining lien may reach only the client’s papers and property for the unpaid matter, a written lien agreement must spell out those limits, and on termination you must hand over whatever the client reasonably needs for that matter. You bear copying costs for file co","#st-mt-ethics"],["Living-expense loan guarantees allowed","Montana · ethics","Beyond advancing costs, a lawyer may guarantee a loan from a regulated lender solely for a client’s basic living expenses if needed to withstand litigation delay, the client stays ultimately liable, and the help is not offered or advertised before retention. Mont. R. Prof. Conduct 1.8(e)(3)","#st-mt-ethics"],["Trust records for five years; unclaimed funds to the MJF","Montana · ethics","Trust records must be kept five years after the representation ends. Funds whose owner cannot be found for at least two years may be paid to the Montana Justice Foundation, which refunds them if the owner turns up within two years. IOLTA participation is mandatory. Mont. R. Prof. Conduct 1.15(a), (f), 1.18(b)","#st-mt-ethics"],["No ABA Rule 8.4(g)","Montana · ethics","Montana’s misconduct rule lists only paragraphs (a) through (f); there is no harassment-or-discrimination paragraph like ABA Model Rule 8.4(g). Mont. R. Prof. Conduct 8.4","#st-mt-ethics"],["Cannabis advice expressly permitted","Montana · ethics","The preamble states a lawyer may counsel and assist a client on Montana’s cannabis laws, but where Montana law conflicts with federal or tribal law, the lawyer must also advise on the related federal and tribal law and policy. Mont. R. Prof. Conduct, Preamble ¶ (6)","#st-mt-ethics"],["Substitution of judge: 30 days and the fee, or it is void","Montana · land mine","A peremptory substitution must be filed within 30 calendar days of service (or of the first summons served or an adverse appearance, for the plaintiff), and in a civil case it is not effective until the $100 fee is paid. A late motion is void. Mont. Code Ann. 3-1-804(1)(a), (3), (4); 25-1-201(1)(p)","#st-mt-unique"],["File the notice of appeal in the Supreme Court, with the fee","Montana · land mine","The notice of appeal goes to the Clerk of the Supreme Court (copies to the district court clerk and all parties), within 30 days of entry (60 if the state or a political subdivision or agency is a party). The clerk will not file it without the filing fee or a fee-waiver affidavit, and a timely notice is required to invoke jurisdiction. An out-of-time appeal requires extraordinary circumstances amounting to a gross mi","#st-mt-unique"],["No appeal until fees and costs are fixed","Montana · land mine","A judgment is not final for appeal until any needed determination of the amount of costs and attorney fees or sanctions is made. A notice filed before then may be dismissed sua sponte and must be dismissed on motion. M. R. App. P. 4(1)(a), 4(5)(a)(iii); M. R. Civ. P. 58(e)","#st-mt-unique"],["Appeal time does not start until notice of entry is served","Montana · land mine","Within 14 days after entry, the prevailing party must serve notice of entry of the judgment or order. Where that notice is required, the 30- or 60-day appeal time runs only from its service. Winners who skip it leave the appeal window open. M. R. Civ. P. 77(d); M. R. App. P. 4(5)(a)(i)","#st-mt-unique"],["Post-trial motions are deemed denied at 60 days, and that starts the appeal clock","Montana · land mine","A Rule 59 motion (and Rule 52(b) and 60 motions) not ruled on in a written order within 60 days of filing is deemed denied, unless the court extends the time by order within the 60 days (outer limit 120 days). The appeal time then runs from the deemed denial without any notice. The motion’s heading must list the 60-day and 120-day dates. Unlike the federal rule, a timely Rule 60(b) motion also restarts the appeal tim","#st-mt-unique"],["Pro hac vice: second case is your last, and local counsel cannot be a mailbox","Montana · land mine","After two pro hac vice appearances by you or your firm, more require good cause that is not routinely granted. Montana counsel must sign every paper and attend depositions and hearings. You keep paying annual Montana dues while the case is pending, and you must also file a sworn certification to the Supreme Court that you will be bound by the Montana Rules. Mont. Bar Admission R. VI.C, VI.J, VI.K; Mont. R. Prof. Cond","#st-mt-unique"],["Mandatory appellate mediation in money, domestic, and comp appeals","Montana · land mine","Appeals in actions for money damages, domestic relations cases, and all Workers’ Compensation Court appeals go to mediation. The notice of appeal must certify whether the rule applies. Parties have 15 days to pick a mediator (or the clerk appoints one) and 75 days to finish; briefing deadlines keep running unless the parties file a stipulation holding them in abeyance. M. R. App. P. 4(4)(c), 7(2), 7(3), 7(4)(b)","#st-mt-unique"],["Summary judgment hearing is waived unless you ask","Montana · land mine","The response is due 21 days after service of the motion (or when a responsive pleading is due, if later) and the reply 14 days later. The right to a hearing is waived unless a party requests one within 14 days after the reply period ends. M. R. Civ. P. 56(c)(1)–(2)","#st-mt-unique"],["Anti-SLAPP motion: 60 days from service","Montana · land mine","The special motion under Montana’s UPEPA is due within 60 days after service of the pleading asserting the claim (later only for good cause). Filing stays discovery automatically, and an order denying it must be appealed within 30 days (60 with a government party). Mont. Code Ann. 27-34-103, -104, -109","#st-mt-unique"],["Bias affidavit must beat the trial date by 30 days","Montana · land mine","An affidavit to disqualify a judge for cause must be filed more than 30 days before the date set for hearing or trial, with counsel’s good-faith certificate. Complaints about rulings alone do not count and can draw fees and damages. Mont. Code Ann. 3-1-805(1)(a)–(d)","#st-mt-unique"],["Three extra days for e-mail service in the trial court, none on appeal","Montana · land mine","In district court, 3 days are added to response periods after service by mail, clerk deposit, consented electronic means, or other consented delivery. In the Supreme Court no time is added for mailing or transmitting, weekends count, and filings are due in the clerk’s office between 8:00 a.m. and 5:00 p.m. (e-filing aside). M. R. Civ. P. 6(d); M. R. App. P. 3","#st-mt-unique"],["Service by mail needs a signed acknowledgment, and not for entities","Montana · land mine","Mail service works only if the defendant returns the acknowledgment within 21 days; otherwise serve personally. It cannot be used on minors, incompetent persons, or corporations, partnerships, or associations. Service must be completed within three years of filing or the case is dismissed. M. R. Civ. P. 4(d)(3), 4(t)","#st-mt-unique"],["Peremptory substitution: one per adverse party, 30 days, $100","Montana · judges","Each adverse party gets one substitution of a district judge without cause. In a civil case, the plaintiff must move within 30 calendar days after the first summons is served or an adverse party appears; a served party must move within 30 calendar days after service is complete. Use the one-line form motion and serve all parties. In civil cases the motion is not effective for any purpose until the $100 substitution f","#st-mt-unique"],["Fresh rights on reassignment, new trial, or remand","Montana · judges","If the judge recuses or a new judge assumes jurisdiction, the right revives for any party that has not already used it, with the time running from service of notice naming the new judge. After a district court orders a new trial, or after a remittitur on reversal for a new trial or reversal of a summary judgment or dismissal, each adverse party may move within 20 calendar days (with the fee). Parties joined or interv","#st-mt-unique"],["Disqualification for cause: affidavit 30+ days before trial or hearing","Montana · judges","File an affidavit alleging facts showing the presiding judge’s personal bias or prejudice, with counsel’s certificate of good faith, more than 30 days before the date set for hearing or trial. The judge must proceed no further; for a district judge, the matter goes to the Supreme Court, and the Chief Justice assigns another district judge to hear it. An affidavit based solely on rulings reviewable on appeal is deemed","#st-mt-unique"],["State constitution: truth as a defense and the jury decides law and facts in libel cases","Montana · speech","Montana’s Constitution bars laws impairing freedom of speech or expression, makes every person “responsible for all abuse of that liberty,” lets truth be given in evidence in every libel or slander suit or prosecution, and provides that the jury, under the court’s direction, determines the law and the facts. Mont. Const. art. II, § 7","#st-mt-unique"],["UPEPA protects Montana constitutional rights too","Montana · speech","The anti-SLAPP Act covers speech, press, assembly, petition, and association rights guaranteed by the U.S. Constitution or the Montana Constitution, and directs broad construction to protect them. Mont. Code Ann. 27-34-102(1)(c), -111","#st-mt-unique"],["Jury verdicts by two-thirds; 20-person voir dire panel; four strikes per side","Montana · unique rules","In civil cases at least two-thirds of the jury may return a verdict. Voir dire starts with a 20-person panel, the plaintiff examines first, and each side gets four peremptory challenges, alternating plaintiff first (plus four more panel members for each defendant entitled to separate challenges, and one extra strike per side for alternates). M. R. Civ. P. 47(b), (c)(6), 48","#st-mt-unique"],["Old-style discovery: no initial disclosures, experts by interrogatory","Montana · unique rules","Montana did not adopt federal Rule 26(a) initial disclosures or expert reports. Expert discovery runs through interrogatories identifying each trial expert and the substance of the opinions and grounds, then a deposition; changes must be disclosed by the pretrial order. Scope still includes the “reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence” language. A party may serve up to 50 interrogatories","#st-mt-unique"],["Class action residual funds","Montana · unique rules","At least 50% of residual class funds must go to a Montana Access to Justice Organization. M. R. Civ. P. 23(i)(3)","#st-mt-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope of rules","Montana · civil rules","The rules govern all civil actions and proceedings in Montana district courts, including probate proceedings unless the Uniform Probate Code provides otherwise, subject to Rule 81. They are construed for the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","Montana · civil rules","There is one form of action, the civil action.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencing an action","Montana · civil rules","A civil action is commenced by filing a complaint with the court.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 4. Persons subject to jurisdiction; process; service","Montana · civil rules","Contains Montana’s long-arm rule (Rule 4(b)): anyone found in Montana, and anyone as to claims arising from listed acts such as transacting business, committing an act resulting in accrual of a tort action in Montana, owning property, insuring a Montana risk, or contracting for services or goods in Montana. Service may be by any non-party adult; by mail only with a signed acknowledgment returned within 21 days (not f","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 4.1. Limited representation permitted; process","Montana · civil rules","A lawyer may provide limited-scope representation under Rule 1.2(c) of the Rules of Professional Conduct without entering an appearance for Rule 5(b) service. Appearing before a judge is a general appearance unless a Rule 4.2 notice of limited appearance is filed and served first, and service on a limited-appearance lawyer is valid only for that proceeding. No federal counterpart.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 4.2. Notice of limited appearance and withdrawal","Montana · civil rules","A notice of limited appearance filed before or at the proceeding may confine the lawyer’s role to specific proceedings, and the role ends without leave of court on filing a notice of completion. No federal counterpart.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 5. Serving and filing pleadings and other papers","Montana · civil rules","Papers after the complaint are served on every party (on the lawyer if represented) by hand, office or home delivery, mail (complete on mailing), clerk deposit, electronic means with written consent, or other consented means. Discovery requests, responses, deposition notices, and expert disclosure reports are not filed until used or ordered. Local rules may allow electronic or fax filing, and the clerk may not reject","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 5.1. Constitutional challenge to a statute","Montana · civil rules","A party challenging a state statute must promptly file a notice of constitutional question and serve it on the Montana Attorney General by certified or registered mail or designated e-mail. The Attorney General may intervene within 60 days, and the court may not hold the statute unconstitutional before then; failing to give notice does not forfeit the claim.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 5.2. Privacy protection for filings","Montana · civil rules","Filings may show only the last 4 digits of Social Security, taxpayer ID, and financial account numbers, and only the year of birth. Exemptions, sealed filings, protective orders, and reference lists track the federal rule; access issues follow the Rules for Privacy and Public Access to Court Records in Montana.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 6. Computing and extending time; motion papers","Montana · civil rules","Exclude the trigger day, count every day, and roll a weekend or holiday deadline to the next business day; the last day ends at midnight for e-filing. Legal holidays include Lincoln’s and Washington’s Birthdays, Columbus Day, and state general election day. Time may be extended for good cause (after expiry only for excusable neglect), but never for Rules 50(b) and (d), 52(b), 59(b), (d), (e), and 60(b). Motions and n","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings allowed; motions and other papers","Montana · civil rules","Pleadings are the complaint, answer, answer to a counterclaim or crossclaim, third-party complaint and answer, and a reply only if ordered. Motions must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state the grounds with particularity, and state the relief sought.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 7.1. Disclosure statement","Montana · civil rules","A nongovernmental corporate party must file, with its first appearance or filing, a statement naming any parent corporation and any publicly held corporation owning 10% or more of its stock (or stating there is none), and must supplement it when the information changes.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","Montana · civil rules","Requires a short and plain statement of the claim and a demand for relief, fair admissions and denials (an allegation other than damages is admitted if not denied), and alternative or inconsistent claims as needed. Montana’s affirmative defense list says “comparative negligence” where the federal list says contributory negligence, and adds “action on advice of counsel” and “discharge in bankruptcy.”","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","Montana · civil rules","Capacity is raised by specific denial; fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity, while intent and knowledge may be alleged generally. Denials of conditions precedent must be particular, time and place are material, and special damages must be specifically stated.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","Montana · civil rules","Every pleading needs a caption with the court’s name, title, file number, and Rule 7(a) designation, with claims in numbered paragraphs and separate counts where clearer. Written instruments attached as exhibits are part of the pleading.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing; representations to the court; sanctions","Montana · civil rules","Every paper must be signed by an attorney of record (or the unrepresented party) with address, email, and phone, which certifies proper purpose and legal and factual support after reasonable inquiry. A sanctions motion must be served 21 days before filing to allow withdrawal (safe harbor), and sanctions are limited to deterrence. Montana adds Rule 11(e): a lawyer may ghostwrite a filing for a self-represented person ","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections; motions on the pleadings","Montana · civil rules","Answer within 21 days of service; the State, its agencies, and officers sued officially have 42 days after service on the Attorney General, and state employees sued individually have 42 days after the later service. A Rule 12 motion moves the answer to 14 days after the ruling or after a more definite statement is served. The seven Rule 12(b) defenses, consolidation, and waiver track the federal rule, but an improper","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and crossclaim","Montana · civil rules","Compulsory counterclaims (same transaction or occurrence, no unobtainable parties) must be pleaded unless already pending in another action or the suit was by attachment without personal jurisdiction; permissive counterclaims and crossclaims against coparties are allowed. The rules do not expand counterclaims or credits against the State.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","Montana · civil rules","A defendant may implead a non-party who is or may be liable to it, without leave if the third-party complaint is filed within 14 days after serving its original answer and with leave after that. Plaintiffs facing counterclaims may implead on the same terms.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Montana · civil rules","Amend once as of course within 21 days after serving the pleading, or within 21 days after service of a responsive pleading or a Rule 12(b), (e), or (f) motion, whichever is earlier; otherwise with written consent or leave, freely given. Responses to amendments are due within the remaining time or 14 days, whichever is later. Relation back for a new party requires notice within the Rule 4(t) period for service (3 yea","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 16. Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","Montana · civil rules","On a party’s request (except in exempt categories) the judge must issue a scheduling order within 90 days limiting the time to join parties, amend, complete discovery, and file motions, which may be modified only for good cause. The court may hold pretrial conferences on the listed subjects, including limiting M. R. Evid. 702 testimony, and a final pretrial order may be modified only to prevent manifest injustice. Sa","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 17. Plaintiff and defendant; capacity; public officers","Montana · civil rules","Actions are prosecuted by the real party in interest, with time allowed to ratify or substitute before dismissal. Capacity is governed by statute; minors and incompetents sue through a guardian, conservator, next friend, or guardian ad litem; public officers may be named by title.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims","Montana · civil rules","A party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party, including contingent claims. Montana adds that in tort cases a liability or indemnity insurer may not be joined unless it is directly liable to the injured person by law or contract.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 19. Required joinder of parties","Montana · civil rules","Persons needed for complete relief, or whose interests would be impaired or would expose a party to inconsistent obligations, must be joined if subject to service; if joinder is not feasible, the court weighs equity and good conscience factors before dismissing. The Montana text has no subject-matter-jurisdiction condition on joinder, and a joined party who objects to venue that joinder would make improper must be di","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","Montana · civil rules","Plaintiffs or defendants may be joined if claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact. The court may order separate trials to prevent prejudice.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder","Montana · civil rules","Misjoinder is not grounds for dismissal; the court may add or drop parties on just terms at any time and sever claims.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","Montana · civil rules","A party exposed to double or multiple liability may require claimants to interplead. Montana adds a substitution procedure: before answering, a defendant in a contract or property action may, on affidavit that a non-party claims the same debt or property without collusion, substitute that claimant and be discharged by depositing the money or delivering the property.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","Montana · civil rules","Certification prerequisites, class types, notice, settlement approval, class counsel, and fees follow the federal rule. Orders granting or denying certification, or definitively rejecting a class settlement, are appealable within the regular appellate deadline (rather than by petition), without a stay unless ordered. Montana adds Rule 23(i): at least 50% of residual class funds must go to a Montana Access to Justice ","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 23.1. Derivative actions","Montana · civil rules","The complaint must be verified, allege contemporaneous ownership and no collusion, and state with particularity the demand made on directors or why none was made. Settlement or dismissal needs court approval and notice to shareholders or members.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 23.2. Actions relating to unincorporated associations","Montana · civil rules","Actions by or against members of an unincorporated association as a class may proceed through representatives who fairly and adequately protect the association’s interests, with Rule 23(d) and (e) procedures.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","Montana · civil rules","Intervention of right (unconditional statutory right, or an impaired and inadequately represented interest) and permissive intervention (conditional statutory right, common question, or a state officer or agency administering the governing statute) require a timely motion served under Rule 5 with the proposed pleading.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","Montana · civil rules","After a death, the court may substitute the proper party on motion made within a reasonable time after service of a statement noting the death (rather than the federal 90 days), or the action may be dismissed. It also covers incompetency, transfers of interest, and automatic substitution of public officers.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 26. General provisions governing discovery","Montana · civil rules","Montana has no initial disclosures and no Rule 26(f) conference requirement. Scope is any non-privileged matter relevant to a claim or defense, including information “reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence,” with proportionality limits in Rule 26(b)(2)(C). Trial experts are discovered by interrogatory (identity, subject, substance of facts and opinions, and grounds) and then deposition,","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions to perpetuate testimony","Montana · civil rules","A verified petition in the district where an expected adverse party resides may seek to perpetuate testimony before suit, served at least 21 days before the hearing. Depositions may also be perpetuated pending appeal.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Montana · civil rules","Depositions in Montana are taken before an officer authorized to give oaths or a court appointee, and abroad by treaty, letter of request, notice, or commission. Rule 28(c) is Montana’s interstate deposition procedure: submit a foreign subpoena to the clerk in the county where discovery will occur and the clerk issues a Montana subpoena (not an appearance). Montana adds bars on interested officers and on court-report","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations about discovery procedure","Montana · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, parties may stipulate to deposition logistics and modify other discovery procedures.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions by oral examination","Montana · civil rules","Leave is needed for more than 10 depositions per side, a second deposition of the same person, a deposition of a prisoner, or a plaintiff’s deposition within 30 days after service of the summons (unless the defendant has already served discovery). The notice states the recording method, organizational depositions require designees, objections must be concise and nonsuggestive, and depositions are limited to 1 day of ","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions by written questions","Montana · civil rules","Same 10-per-side and repeat-deposition leave rules. Cross-questions are due 14 days after the direct questions, redirect 7 days after cross, and recross 7 days after redirect.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 32. Using depositions in court proceedings","Montana · civil rules","A deposition may be used against a party who was present or had notice, to the extent admissible, including for any purpose if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or outside the U.S., or otherwise unavailable. It may not be used against a party who got less than 14 days’ notice and had a pending protective-order motion. Waiver of objections follows the federal rule (written-question form objections within t","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories to parties","Montana · civil rules","Up to 50 interrogatories, including discrete subparts, without leave (the federal limit is 25). Answers and objections are due in 30 days, but a defendant has at least 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Unstated objections are waived, and business records may be used in lieu of answers.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 34. Production of documents, ESI, and things; entry on land","Montana · civil rules","Requests must describe items with reasonable particularity and may specify the ESI form. Written responses are due in 30 days, but a defendant has at least 45 days after service of the summons and complaint, and partial objections must specify the part. Unlike the current federal rule, the text does not require stating whether documents are being withheld on an objection.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examinations","Montana · civil rules","The court may order a suitably licensed or certified examiner to examine a party whose condition is in controversy, on motion for good cause. Reports are exchanged on request. Montana provides that putting a condition at issue waives privilege for treatment of the same condition, but not for unrelated conditions, and the court may bar unrelated medical records on timely motion.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","Montana · civil rules","Matters are admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days, but a defendant need not respond before 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Lack of knowledge requires reasonable inquiry, and admissions are conclusive unless withdrawn under the merits-and-prejudice test. Montana states no numerical limit but allows one by order or local rule.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to make discovery; sanctions","Montana · civil rules","Motions to compel require a good-faith conference certification, and expenses follow the federal fee-shifting model. Sanctions for disobeying orders range from established facts to default and contempt. A party that fails to disclose expert opinions under Rule 26(b)(4) or provide requested information cannot use it unless the failure was substantially justified or harmless. Lost ESI is protected only by the older “ro","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 38. Right to a jury trial; demand","Montana · civil rules","The jury right under the Montana Constitution or a Montana statute is preserved. Demand in writing within 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue is served, and file it; other parties have 14 days to demand jury on other issues. Failure to demand waives the jury.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","Montana · civil rules","Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or there is no jury right; the court may still order a jury trial on motion, or use an advisory or consent jury.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 40. Scheduling cases for trial","Montana · civil rules","Each court must provide by rule for scheduling trials, giving priority to cases entitled to it by Montana statute.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","Montana · civil rules","A plaintiff may dismiss by notice before an answer or summary judgment motion is served, or by stipulation, without prejudice unless stated (Montana has no federal-style two-dismissal rule in the text). Otherwise dismissal is by court order. Involuntary dismissal for failure to prosecute or obey rules operates as an adjudication on the merits unless stated otherwise, and a refiled claim may draw costs of the earlier ","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials","Montana · civil rules","The court may consolidate actions with a common question of law or fact and order separate trials of issues or claims, preserving jury rights.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 43. Taking testimony","Montana · civil rules","Testimony at trial is taken in open court, but for good cause in compelling circumstances the court may allow contemporaneous transmission from elsewhere. Motions on facts outside the record may be heard on affidavits or testimony, and the court may appoint interpreters.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 44. Proving an official record","Montana · civil rules","Domestic records are proved by official publication or attested copies with a custody certificate; foreign records by attestation with a final certification or treaty certification, or by other means for good cause.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 44.1. Determining foreign law","Montana · civil rules","A party raising a foreign country’s law must give notice by pleading or other writing; the court may consider any relevant source and decides it as a question of law. (The Montana text refers to the “Federal Rules of Evidence.”)","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","Montana · civil rules","Clerks issue blank subpoenas and attorneys may issue them; any non-party adult may serve with one day’s fees and mileage. Parties must get notice at least 10 days before any commanded production or inspection. Trial subpoenas run statewide; deposition and document subpoenas are limited to places within Montana and within 100 miles of residence, work, or business (or where a non-resident is served). Objections are due","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 46. Objecting to a ruling or order","Montana · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary; stating the requested action or objection and grounds is enough.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 47. Jurors","Montana · civil rules","Counsel conduct voir dire under the court’s supervision. A 20-person initial panel is examined (plaintiff first), and each side gets 4 peremptory challenges exercised alternately starting with the plaintiff, with 4 more panel members added for each defendant entitled to separate challenges. One or two alternates may be seated, with 1 extra peremptory per party for alternates. This differs substantially from the feder","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 48. Juries; verdict","Montana · civil rules","At least two-thirds of the jury may return a verdict, and parties may stipulate to fewer than 12 jurors. Federal civil verdicts must be unanimous unless stipulated.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 49. Special verdicts and interrogatories","Montana · civil rules","The court may require special verdicts or a general verdict with written questions. Issues not submitted are waived unless submission is demanded before the jury retires, and inconsistent answers are handled as in the federal rule.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 50. Judgment as a matter of law; new trial; conditional ruling","Montana · civil rules","A JMOL motion may be made before submission to the jury and renewed within 28 days after entry of judgment (or jury discharge), with an alternative new-trial request. Montana adds that a renewed motion not ruled on within 60 days of filing is deemed denied. A losing party’s new-trial motion after JMOL is due within 28 days.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 51. Instructions to the jury; objections","Montana · civil rules","Requests are filed at the close of evidence or earlier as ordered. The court must disclose proposed instructions and allow objections on the record before argument, objections must state distinct grounds, and unpreserved errors are reviewed only for plain error affecting substantial rights.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings and conclusions by the court","Montana · civil rules","In nonjury trials the court must find facts specially and state conclusions separately. Montana adds that when ruling on Rule 12 or 56 motions the court must state the grounds with enough particularity to show its rationale. Motions to amend findings are due within 28 days after entry of judgment and are deemed denied if not decided within the Rule 59 time limits (60 days, extendable to 120).","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters","Montana · civil rules","Montana’s rule differs from the current federal rule: it provides for standing masters (by a majority of district judges), special masters, and water masters, limits references in jury cases to complicated issues and in nonjury cases to exceptional conditions or accountings, and lets an unpaid master obtain a writ of execution. The first meeting is within 20 days of the order of reference, objections to a report in n","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgments; costs","Montana · civil rules","Rule 54(b) certification of a partial final judgment requires an express no-just-reason-for-delay determination that also meets M. R. App. P. 6(6) (stated factors). Costs other than fees go to the prevailing party, taxed by the clerk on 14 days’ notice with review on motion within 7 days. Attorney fee motions are due within 14 days after entry of judgment unless a statute or order says otherwise; the motion procedure","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 55. Default; default judgment","Montana · civil rules","The clerk enters default on a showing of failure to plead or defend and may enter judgment for a sum certain against a non-appearing, competent defendant. Otherwise the court enters it, with 7 days’ written notice to a party that has appeared, and may hold hearings on damages. Default may be set aside for good cause and a default judgment under Rule 60(b); default against the State requires proof satisfactory to the ","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","Montana · civil rules","Montana keeps the older federal structure. A motion may be filed at any time; the response is due 21 days after service of the motion or when a responsive pleading is due, whichever is later; the reply is due 14 days after the response. The right to a hearing is waived unless a party requests one within 14 days after the reply period ends. Judgment is proper if there is no genuine issue of material fact, an opponent ","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments","Montana · civil rules","Governs procedure under the Uniform Declaratory Judgments Act (Title 27, chapter 8), with jury demands under Rules 38 and 39 subject to Mont. Code Ann. 27-8-302. Another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the court may order a speedy hearing.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 58. Entering judgment","Montana · civil rules","Every judgment must be on a separate document (not orders on Rule 50(b), 52(b), 54, 59, or 60 motions), and if one is not prepared, judgment is treated as entered 150 days after docketing. Montana adds Rule 58(e): a judgment is not final for appeal until any necessary determination of the amount of costs, fees, or sanctions is made, and a premature notice of appeal does not stop the district court from deciding a tim","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 59. New trial; altering or amending a judgment","Montana · civil rules","New-trial and alter-or-amend motions are due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, and the heading must state the dates 60 and 120 days after filing. Opposing affidavits are due 14 days after service. The court may order a new trial on its own within 28 days. Unlike the federal rule, a motion not decided by written order within 60 days is deemed denied, and the court may extend within that period only up to ","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from judgment or order","Montana · civil rules","Clerical errors may be corrected anytime (with Supreme Court leave once an appeal is docketed). The six Rule 60(b) grounds track the federal rule, and motions on grounds (1)–(3) must be made within a reasonable time and no more than a year. Montana adds that Rule 60 motions must use the Rule 59(b) format and are deemed denied if not decided within the Rule 59(f) 60/120-day limits; a timely 60(b) motion also restarts ","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","Montana · civil rules","Errors that do not affect a party’s substantial rights are disregarded.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","Montana · civil rules","Execution is automatically stayed for 14 days after entry, and Montana adds an automatic order during those 14 days barring the judgment debtor from transferring or encumbering property, which the court may modify for good cause. Injunctions and receiverships are not stayed. A stay pending appeal is obtained by supersedeas bond approved by the court, or alternative security if the creditor’s recovery is not in jeopar","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 62.1. Indicative ruling on a motion barred by a pending appeal","Montana · civil rules","While an appeal is docketed, the district court may defer, deny, or indicate it would grant the motion or that it raises a substantial issue. The movant must promptly notify the Supreme Court clerk, and the district court may decide only on remand.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 63. Judge’s inability to proceed","Montana · civil rules","A successor judge may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record, and must recall material disputed witnesses on request in hearings and nonjury trials. Montana adds that the Chief Justice assigns the successor by written order, possibly from another district.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 64. Seizing a person or property","Montana · civil rules","Every remedy for seizing a person or property to secure a potential judgment is available at the start of and during the action.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions","Montana · civil rules","The procedure for restraining orders and temporary and permanent injunctions is governed by statute (Title 27, chapter 19), not by a rule text like federal Rule 65.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 65.1. (No Montana rule)","Montana · civil rules","Montana has no Rule 65.1.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers","Montana · civil rules","The rules govern actions involving receivers, but estate administration follows Montana statutes, historical practice, or local rule, and a receivership action may be dismissed only by court order.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit into court","Montana · civil rules","With notice and leave of court, a party may deposit money or a thing in dispute, and funds are invested and withdrawn under Title 25, chapter 8.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of judgment","Montana · civil rules","A defending party may serve an offer more than 14 days before trial, and the offeree has 14 days to accept in writing. If the final judgment is not more favorable than the unaccepted offer, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer. Offers after liability is determined must be served at least 14 days before the damages hearing.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution","Montana · civil rules","Execution and supplementary proceedings follow Montana statutes, and a judgment creditor may examine anyone, including the debtor, by deposition.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 70. Enforcing a judgment for a specific act","Montana · civil rules","If a party fails to perform a required act, the court may appoint another to do it, vest title by judgment, issue writs of attachment, sequestration, execution, or assistance, or hold the party in contempt.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 71. Enforcing relief for or against a nonparty","Montana · civil rules","Orders for or against non-parties are enforced as for parties.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 71.1. (No Montana rule)","Montana · civil rules","Montana has no Rule 71.1.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 72. Appeal from district court to the Supreme Court","Montana · civil rules","Appeals and original proceedings in the Supreme Court are governed by the Montana Rules of Appellate Procedure and controlling statutes. This replaces the federal magistrate-judge rule with the same number.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 73–76. (No Montana rules)","Montana · civil rules","Montana has no Rules 73 through 76.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 77. Conducting business; clerk’s authority; notice of entry","Montana · civil rules","District courts are always open for filing. Hearings outside the district (other than ex parte) need consent, and the clerk may issue process and enter defaults and sum-certain default judgments. Unlike the federal rule, the prevailing party must serve notice of entry of a judgment or order on all appearing parties within 14 days, and the appeal time runs from that service where it is required. Rule 77(e) covers send","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 78–79. (No Montana rules)","Montana · civil rules","Montana has no Rules 78 or 79.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 80. Stenographic transcript as evidence","Montana · civil rules","Admissible prior testimony may be proved by a transcript certified by the reporter.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 81. Applicability in general","Montana · civil rules","The rules do not supersede statutes on appeals to or review by district courts, but govern practice where not inconsistent. Statutes that refer to civil-action procedure or superseded statutes are applied through these rules.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 82. Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","Montana · civil rules","Except as Rule 4 provides, the rules do not extend or limit district court jurisdiction or venue.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 83. Rules by district courts","Montana · civil rules","Each district court, by agreement of a majority of its judges, may adopt local rules consistent with these and Supreme Court rules, furnishing copies to the Supreme Court.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 84. Forms","Montana · civil rules","The appendix forms suffice under the rules. They include Montana’s Form 18-A notice and acknowledgment for mail service and Form 18-B acknowledgment and waiver.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 85. Title","Montana · civil rules","Cited as the Montana Rules of Civil Procedure, M. R. Civ. P.","#st-mt-rules"],["Rule 86. Effective date; statutes superseded","Montana · civil rules","Rules and amendments take effect when the Supreme Court specifies and apply to pending cases unless unfeasible or unjust. The October 1, 2011 amendments do not change priority in time under Mont. Code Ann. 3-2-706 for conflicts with other law.","#st-mt-rules"],["Appeals in Montana: one appellate court, 30 days, and the notice goes to the Supreme Court","Montana · appeals","Montana has no intermediate court of appeals. Every appeal from a district court goes directly to the Montana Supreme Court, and you start it by filing the notice of appeal with the Clerk of the Supreme Court (not the district court), with the filing fee, within 30 days of entry of the judgment (60 days if the State, a political subdivision, or an officer or agency is a party). If notice of entry must be served under","#st-mt-appeals"],["Supreme Court only","Montana · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","The Montana Rules of Appellate Procedure govern proceedings before the Supreme Court, which hears appeals from the district courts and other courts (including the Water Court and Workers’ Compensation Court) as well as original proceedings and writs. M. R. Civ. P. 72 sends every district court appeal there. M. R. App. P. 1(2); M. R. Civ. P. 72","#st-mt-appeals"],["Final judgment rule","Montana · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","A final judgment conclusively determines the parties’ rights and settles all claims, including any necessary determination of the amount of costs and attorney fees or sanctions. An appeal from a judgment brings up all earlier rulings that were objected to and led to it. M. R. App. P. 4(1)(a), 4(4)(a), 6(1)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Orders appealable now in civil cases","Montana · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","If the order is the court’s final decision on the matter: orders after final judgment (including on motions to vacate a default judgment, for new trial, or to alter or amend); “deemed denied” Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59, or 60(b) motions; denial of a motion to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction; class certification orders and orders definitively rejecting a class settlement; injunctions and attachments (granti","#st-mt-appeals"],["Probate and guardianship orders must be appealed immediately","Montana · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","Listed estate, guardianship, and probate orders (letters, will admission, allowances, partition or sale of real property, distribution, settling accounts, homestead) are final and must be appealed at once; waiting waives the appeal. M. R. App. P. 6(4)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Not appealable","Montana · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","Orders resolving fewer than all claims or parties (absent a proper Rule 54(b) certification); denials of summary judgment or motions to dismiss; grants of partial summary judgment; most contempt orders; sanctions orders; and other interlocutory judgments. M. R. App. P. 6(5)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Rule 54(b) certification is reviewed before the appeal proceeds","Montana · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","The district court must expressly find no just reason for delay and articulate the factors it balanced. Attach the certification order to the notice; the appeal does not proceed until the Supreme Court reviews the certification and allows it. A deficient certification is subject to summary dismissal. M. R. App. P. 4(4)(b), 6(6); M. R. Civ. P. 54(b)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Notice of appeal: Supreme Court clerk, 30 or 60 days, fee required","Montana · appeals · Starting the appeal","File the notice (Form 1) with the Clerk of the Supreme Court within 30 days from entry of the judgment or order, or 60 days in other cases where the United States, Montana, a political subdivision, or an officer or agency is a party. Pay the filing fee or file the Form 3 fee-waiver affidavit at the same time; otherwise the clerk will not file it. Serve copies on the district court clerk and all parties at the same ti","#st-mt-appeals"],["Timeliness is jurisdictional, with a narrow out-of-time escape","Montana · appeals · Starting the appeal","A timely notice is required to invoke the Court’s appellate jurisdiction. Only in an infrequent harsh case with extraordinary circumstances amounting to a gross miscarriage of justice may the Court grant an out-of-time appeal, on a verified petition with affidavits and evidence. Mistake, inadvertence, and excusable neglect do not qualify, and the general suspension-of-rules provision cannot be used for this. M. R. Ap","#st-mt-appeals"],["Premature notices","Montana · appeals · Starting the appeal","A notice filed after the decision is announced but before the written judgment is entered (or notice of entry is served) is treated as filed on entry. But a notice filed before the court fixes the amount of fees, costs, or sanctions may be dismissed sua sponte and must be dismissed on motion. M. R. App. P. 4(5)(a)(ii)–(iii); M. R. Civ. P. 58(e)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Cross-appeals","Montana · appeals · Starting the appeal","Any other party may file a notice of cross-appeal (Form 2) within 15 days after the first notice of appeal was filed, or within the regular appeal time, whichever ends later. A fee or waiver affidavit is required here too. M. R. App. P. 4(2)(b), 4(5)(a)(iv)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Defective notices","Montana · appeals · Starting the appeal","If a notice lacks required content or certifications, the clerk issues a notice to file an amended notice within 11 days; the original filing date holds. Informality alone is not grounds for dismissal. M. R. App. P. 4(4)(e)–(f)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Constitutional challenges when the State is not a party","Montana · appeals · Starting the appeal","Give written notice to the Supreme Court and the Attorney General within 11 days after the notice of appeal or cross-appeal (Form 1A). The Attorney General has 20 days to say whether it will intervene. M. R. App. P. 27","#st-mt-appeals"],["Motions that reset the appeal time","Montana · appeals · Tolling and post-judgment motions","A timely motion under M. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59 (new trial or alter or amend), or 60(b) restarts the appeal time for all parties from entry of the order deciding it or from its deemed denial. No notice of entry is required for these orders. A notice of appeal filed before the motion is decided is treated as filed on the date of decision or deemed denial. M. R. App. P. 4(5)(a)(v)","#st-mt-appeals"],["28-day filing limit, no extensions","Montana · appeals · Tolling and post-judgment motions","Renewed judgment as a matter of law, amended findings, new trial, and alter-or-amend motions must be filed no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, and the court may not extend these times. Rule 60(b)(1)–(3) motions must be made within a reasonable time and no more than a year after entry. M. R. Civ. P. 6(b)(2), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), 60(c)(1)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Deemed denial at 60 days (120 at most)","Montana · appeals · Tolling and post-judgment motions","If the court does not rule in a written order within 60 days of filing, the motion is deemed denied. The court may extend by an order issued within the 60 days, but a motion not decided within 120 days of filing is deemed denied. A new-trial motion (and Rule 60 motions) must state in its heading the dates 60 and 120 days after filing. M. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(d), 59(b), (f), 60(c)(1)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Indicative rulings during an appeal","Montana · appeals · Tolling and post-judgment motions","Once an appeal is docketed, the district court may defer, deny, or state that it would grant (or that the motion raises a substantial issue); the movant must promptly notify the Supreme Court clerk, and the district court may decide only on remand. Clerical errors may be fixed during a pending appeal only with the Supreme Court’s leave. M. R. Civ. P. 60(a), 62.1","#st-mt-appeals"],["Mandatory appellate mediation","Montana · appeals · Mediation, record, and stays","Applies to all Workers’ Compensation Court appeals, domestic relations appeals (with exclusions and an abuse opt-out), appeals in actions for money damages, and any appeal the Court designates. The parties have 15 days after the notice of appeal (or the cross-appeal that triggers the rule) to jointly select a mediator, or the clerk appoints one; 75 days to complete mediation; position statements of up to 10 pages. Th","#st-mt-appeals"],["Transcripts and record","Montana · appeals · Mediation, record, and stays","Order transcripts in writing from the court reporter on the day you file the notice of appeal, and file and serve the request (Form 9); the appellee has 11 days to order more. The record is due in the Supreme Court within 40 days after the notice of appeal. The district court may extend to no more than 90 days from the first notice; later extensions come only from the Supreme Court, on a reporter’s affidavit, with a ","#st-mt-appeals"],["Stay pending appeal: start in the district court","Montana · appeals · Mediation, record, and stays","Move in the district court for a stay, approval of a supersedeas bond, or an injunction pending appeal. For a money judgment, the bond normally covers the full unpaid judgment, costs on appeal, interest, and damages for delay, unless the court sets a different amount or alternative security for good cause; the parties may waive security by written stipulation. Then, within 11 days after the district court’s order, yo","#st-mt-appeals"],["Bond objections","Montana · appeals · Mediation, record, and stays","A party may object to a bond’s sufficiency within 30 days after it is filed; the bonding party or surety must respond within 20 days after service of the objection, or the stay dissolves. M. R. App. P. 24(1)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Briefing schedule","Montana · appeals · Briefing","Appellant’s opening brief: 30 days after the record is filed. Appellee’s answer brief: 30 days after service of the opening brief. Reply: 14 days after service of the answer brief. With a cross-appeal, the cross-appellant combines its answer and cross-appeal brief; the appellant files a combined reply and cross-answer within 30 days; the cross-appellant’s cross-reply is due 14 days after service. M. R. App. P. 12(4),","#st-mt-appeals"],["Length and form","Montana · appeals · Briefing","Principal briefs: 10,000 words (proportional type of at least 14 points) or 30 pages (monospaced). Reply and amicus briefs: 5,000 words or 14 pages, and only one reply is allowed even if it also answers a cross-appeal. A certificate of compliance is required. Over-length motions need an affidavit showing extraordinary justification. The opening brief must include an appendix with the judgment or order appealed, findi","#st-mt-appeals"],["Extensions are limited","Montana · appeals · Briefing","A party may get one 30-day extension per brief on motion (the clerk may grant it if unopposed); the motion must say whether opposing counsel objects. A second extension, and any extension in abuse-and-neglect or parenting-plan appeals, faces a presumption against it and needs a motion at least 7 days before the due date with an affidavit showing diligence and substantial need; “press of business” is not enough. M. R.","#st-mt-appeals"],["Motions practice","Montana · appeals · Briefing","Motions are limited to 1,250 words or 5 pages, must state whether opposing counsel objects, and get a response within 11 days. No reply is allowed. M. R. App. P. 16(1)–(3)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Oral argument is by order only","Montana · appeals · Briefing","Cases are decided on the briefs and record unless the Court orders argument. M. R. App. P. 17(1)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing: 15 days, narrow grounds","Montana · appeals · Decision, rehearing, and further review","File within 15 days after the decision is filed. Grounds are limited to an overlooked material fact, an overlooked decisive question, or a conflict with a statute or controlling decision the Court did not address. Objections are due 15 days after the petition; no replies. Length: 2,500 words or 10 pages. Any extension request must be filed within the 15 days and cannot exceed 15 more days. No rehearing if the Court o","#st-mt-appeals"],["Remittitur","Montana · appeals · Decision, rehearing, and further review","Issues when the rehearing time expires, on denial of rehearing, or immediately if the Court so orders. M. R. App. P. 21(2)–(4)","#st-mt-appeals"],["No further state review","Montana · appeals · Decision, rehearing, and further review","The Supreme Court is Montana’s only appellate court, so there is no further state-level review after its decision and any rehearing. Costs go to the prevailing party, and the Court may sanction frivolous or vexatious appeals with costs and fees. M. R. App. P. 19(3)(a), 19(5), 20, 21","#st-mt-appeals"],["Interlocutory appeals of right","Montana · appeals · Interlocutory review and original proceedings","The Rule 6(3) list (injunctions, class certification, venue, jurisdiction denials, substitution of judge, and so on) operates as Montana’s interlocutory appeal of right, on the regular 30- or 60-day clock. A class certification appeal does not stay the district court unless a judge or the Supreme Court orders it. M. R. App. P. 6(3); M. R. Civ. P. 23(f)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP denial","Montana · appeals · Interlocutory review and original proceedings","A movant may appeal as of right from an order denying a UPEPA special motion in whole or part within 30 days after entry (60 days if the U.S., the State, or a political subdivision is a party). The appeal stays all proceedings between all parties. Mont. Code Ann. 27-34-109, -104(3)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Writ of supervisory control","Montana · appeals · Interlocutory review and original proceedings","The Supreme Court may supervise another court case by case when urgency makes normal appeal inadequate, the case involves purely legal questions, and the lower court is proceeding under a mistake of law causing gross injustice, constitutional issues of statewide importance are involved, or (in criminal cases) a substitution ruling is at issue. Petitions are limited to 4,000 words or 12 pages, with no separate brief, ","#st-mt-appeals"],["Original declaratory actions and certified questions","Montana · appeals · Interlocutory review and original proceedings","An original declaratory action may be filed in the Supreme Court for purely legal statutory or constitutional questions of statewide importance when urgency makes ordinary litigation inadequate. The Court also answers certified questions from federal courts, other states, tribes, Canada, and Mexico. M. R. App. P. 14(4), 15","#st-mt-appeals"],["E-filing in the Supreme Court","Montana · appeals · Filing mechanics and out-of-state counsel","Registered users (Montana lawyers and pro hac vice lawyers) may e-file motions, petitions, and briefs. For motions and petitions, file the original only unless the clerk asks for paper. For briefs, e-file the original (cover colors do not apply); once the clerk approves it, submit seven paper copies within seven days, with the required appendix in the opening brief copies and the colored covers. Temp. E-Filing R. 3(a","#st-mt-appeals"],["Time computation and paper filing","Montana · appeals · Filing mechanics and out-of-state counsel","Weekends and holidays count; no days are added for mailing; a deadline falling on a weekend or holiday moves to the next business day. Paper filings are timely only if actually received, during 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. on business days. Paper filers file an original and 9 copies of briefs (7 for motions and rehearing petitions). M. R. App. P. 3, 10(1)(a), 10(5), 13(2)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Represented parties cannot file on their own","Montana · appeals · Filing mechanics and out-of-state counsel","A party with counsel may file only a motion to substitute or remove counsel. M. R. App. P. 10(1)(c)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Out-of-state counsel","Montana · appeals · Filing mechanics and out-of-state counsel","Pro hac vice admission under Bar Admission Rule VI applies in the Supreme Court as in the trial courts, with an actively participating Montana lawyer who signs all briefs. Mont. Bar Admission R. VI.A, VI.J","#st-mt-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (civil, private parties)","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of judgment or order (or service of notice of entry where M. R. Civ. P. 77(d) requires it).\nFiled with the Clerk of the Supreme Court with the fee; jurisdictional (out-of-time appeal only under Rule 4(6)). M. R. App. P. 4(5)(a)(i)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (government party)","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from entry of judgment or order (or service of notice of entry).\nWhen the U.S., Montana, a political subdivision, or an officer or agency is a party (except abuse-and-neglect cases, which are 30 days). M. R. App. P. 4(5)(a)(i)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Notice of cross-appeal","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or the regular appeal time, if later).\nWhichever period ends last. M. R. App. P. 4(5)(a)(iv)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Notice of entry of judgment (served by prevailing party)","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment or order.\nWhere required, the appeal time does not run until it is served. M. R. Civ. P. 77(d)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59 motions","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended. Restart the appeal time. M. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), 6(b)(2)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Ruling on post-judgment motion, or deemed denied","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from filing of the motion.\nExtendable by order within 60 days, but deemed denied at 120 days. Appeal time runs from the deemed denial. M. R. Civ. P. 59(f); M. R. App. P. 4(5)(a)(v)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Attorney fee motion","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment.\nUnless a statute or order provides otherwise; judgment is not appealable until the amount is fixed. M. R. Civ. P. 54(d)(2)(B)(i)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Amended notice after clerk’s deficiency notice","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","11 days from clerk’s notice. M. R. App. P. 4(4)(e)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Notice of constitutional challenge (State not a party)","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","11 days from filing of the notice of appeal or cross-appeal.\nTo the Supreme Court and the Attorney General. M. R. App. P. 27","#st-mt-appeals"],["Select appellate mediator (Rule 7 cases)","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the notice of appeal or triggering cross-appeal.\nOtherwise the clerk appoints; 75 days to complete mediation. M. R. App. P. 7(4)(b)–(c)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Appellee’s additional transcript order","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","11 days from filing of the notice of appeal or cross-appeal, whichever is later.\nAppellant orders transcripts the same day it files the notice. M. R. App. P. 8(3)(b)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Transmission of the record","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from filing of the notice of appeal (or cross-appeal).\nDistrict court may extend to no more than 90 days from the first notice; further extensions only from the Supreme Court. M. R. App. P. 9(1)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Appellant’s opening brief","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the record.\nOne 30-day extension available; later ones presumptively denied. M. R. App. P. 13(1)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Appellee’s answer brief","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the opening brief. M. R. App. P. 13(1)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Reply brief","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the answer brief.\nCombined reply and cross-answer: 30 days after the cross-appellant’s brief. M. R. App. P. 13(1)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Response to a motion","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","11 days from service of the motion.\nNo reply allowed. M. R. App. P. 16(2)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Motion in Supreme Court for stay relief","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","11 days from entry of the district court’s stay order.\nResponse within 11 days of filing. M. R. App. P. 22(2)(a)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the Supreme Court’s decision.\nObjections 15 days after the petition; extension of at most 15 days. M. R. App. P. 20(2)(a)","#st-mt-appeals"],["Appeal from denial of anti-SLAPP motion","Montana · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order.\n60 days if the U.S., the State, or a political subdivision is a party. Mont. Code Ann. 27-34-109","#st-mt-appeals"],["Supreme Court of North Carolina","North Carolina · courts","A Chief Justice and six associate justices, elected to eight-year terms; four make a quorum. Sits in Raleigh. Hears direct appeals in Business Court cases and class-certification rulings, appeals of right on substantial constitutional questions, and cases it certifies for discretionary review (G.S. 7A-10, 7A-27(a), 7A-30, 7A-31).","#st-nc"],["North Carolina Court of Appeals","North Carolina · courts","Fifteen judges elected to eight-year terms, sitting in panels of three and en banc when a majority votes. Hears most appeals of right from the superior and district courts and many agency appeals (G.S. 7A-16, 7A-27(b)).","#st-nc"],["Superior Court (including the North Carolina Business Court)","North Carolina · courts","General-jurisdiction trial court; the proper division for civil actions over $25,000 (G.S. 7A-243). Special Superior Court Judges for Complex Business Cases hear cases designated as mandatory or discretionary complex business cases (G.S. 7A-45.4). Facial challenges to acts of the General Assembly go to a three-judge panel in Wake County (Rule 42(b)(4); G.S. 1-267.1).","#st-nc"],["District Court and magistrates","North Carolina · courts","District Court is the proper division for civil actions of $25,000 or less and for family matters (G.S. 7A-243). Magistrates hear assigned small claims up to $10,000 (G.S. 7A-210). The clerk of superior court handles estates and special proceedings.","#st-nc"],["No anti-SLAPP statute","North Carolina · anti-SLAPP","North Carolina has no anti-SLAPP law: no special motion to dismiss, no discovery stay, and no speech-based fee-shifting. We searched the General Statutes chapters where such a law would be placed (Chapter 1, Civil Procedure; Chapter 1A, Rules of Civil Procedure; Chapter 1D, Punitive Damages; Chapter 6, Costs; Chapter 7A, Judicial Department; Chapter 99, Libel and Slander) for “public participation” and “SLAPP” and fo","#st-nc-slapp"],["Pro hac vice is statutory: G.S. 84-4.1, $225, resident local counsel, reciprocity","North Carolina · out of state","An attorney domiciled in another state, admitted and in good standing there, and retained by a party, may move the forum (the trial court, Industrial Commission, Utilities Commission, OAH, or an agency) for admission for that proceeding only. The motion, signed by the attorney, must include: name, bar number and status; the mailing address, phone and email to be used for Rule 5 service; a client-signed statement givi","#st-nc-oos"],["Admission can be revoked summarily","North Carolina · out of state","Pro hac vice permission may be summarily revoked by the court or agency on its own motion and in its discretion; no hearing standard appears in the statute. N.C.G.S. § 84-4.2","#st-nc-oos"],["Temporary practice without admission (RPC 5.5)","North Carolina · out of state","A lawyer admitted and not suspended elsewhere in the U.S. may practice temporarily in North Carolina if the lawyer is authorized to appear before a tribunal (or expects to be), the matter is reasonably related to home-state practice, the matter relates to arbitration or mediation, or the lawyer works with an actively participating North Carolina lawyer. Pro hac vice-type services are excluded unless admitted. In-hous","#st-nc-oos"],["Admission by comity: $2,000 and 4 of the last 6 years","North Carolina · out of state","A lawyer licensed in an ‘approved jurisdiction’ (one whose conditions for admitting North Carolina lawyers without exam are not materially greater than North Carolina’s) may be admitted without the written bar exam, other than the MPRE. The lawyer must have actively and substantially practiced for at least four of the six years before applying, be in good standing everywhere licensed, pay a $2,000 fee (largely nonref","#st-nc-oos"],["Limited-scope representation","North Carolina · out of state","The Rules of Professional Conduct allow a lawyer to limit the scope of a representation if the limitation is reasonable. The collected texts do not include a court rule on limited appearances or withdrawal in the trial courts. Those are in the General Rules of Practice, which we could not collect, so check local practice before filing a limited appearance. N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.2(c)","#st-nc-oos"],["E-filing and e-service","North Carolina · out of state","Where e-filing is available in the county, attorneys must file under Rule 5 of the General Rules of Practice (the eCourts File & Serve system). The Supreme Court is authorized to require registration. Service on attorneys through the court’s e-filing system at the email of record is valid service, but it must be sent by 5:00 p.m. Eastern on a business day or it counts as served the next business day. Pro hac vice mot","#st-nc-oos"],["Governing code: North Carolina Rules of Professional Conduct","North Carolina · ethics","The State Bar’s Rules of Professional Conduct (27 NCAC 02) are approved by the Supreme Court; the current set dates from Supreme Court approval on July 24, 1997, with later amendments. They follow the ABA Model Rule numbering but have several real departures, below. N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct (History Notes, e.g., Rule 1.6)","#st-nc-ethics"],["Broader permission to reveal a client’s crime","North Carolina · ethics","Rule 1.6(b)(2) lets a lawyer reveal confidential information to prevent the commission of any crime by the client, not only crimes or frauds causing financial harm through the lawyer’s services as in the Model Rule. Rule 1.6(b)(4) separately covers preventing or rectifying a client’s crime or fraud in which the lawyer’s services were used. N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.6(b)(2), (4)","#st-nc-ethics"],["Fee disputes: mandatory notice of the State Bar program","North Carolina · ethics","Before suing a client over a disputed fee, a lawyer must make reasonable efforts to tell the client about the State Bar’s fee dispute resolution program at least 30 days before filing, and must participate in good faith if the client uses it. Contingent fee agreements must be signed by the client and state how expenses are treated. N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.5(c), (f)","#st-nc-ethics"],["No humanitarian exception for financial assistance","North Carolina · ethics","Rule 1.8(e) allows advancing court costs and litigation expenses and paying them for indigent clients. It contains no exception for modest living-expense gifts to indigent pro bono clients like Model Rule 1.8(e)(3). N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.8(e)","#st-nc-ethics"],["Separate rule banning sexual activity and sexual communications","North Carolina · ethics","Rule 1.19 bars sexual activity and sexual communications with a client (including prospective clients until the lawyer declines and an organizational client’s representatives), and bars demanding them as a condition of representation. Pre-existing consensual relationships are excepted. The Model Rule equivalent, 1.8(j), does not address communications. N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.19","#st-nc-ethics"],["No anti-harassment rule; extra ‘prejudice the client’ rule","North Carolina · ethics","Rule 8.4 has no counterpart to Model Rule 8.4(g) on harassment or discrimination. Instead, North Carolina’s 8.4(g) makes it misconduct to intentionally prejudice or damage a client during the representation (except as Rule 3.3 requires). N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct 8.4","#st-nc-ethics"],["Detailed trust-account rules","North Carolina · ethics","Rules 1.15-1 to 1.15-4 set detailed trust accounting duties. Each general trust account must be reconciled to the bank statement monthly, and a three-way reconciliation (general ledger, client ledgers, adjusted bank balance) must be done at least quarterly. The lawyer must sign and date the reconciliations and keep them for six years. Monthly and quarterly reviews of statements and checks are also required. N.C. R. P","#st-nc-ethics"],["Local customs and courtesy are enforceable","North Carolina · ethics","Rule 3.5(a)(4) treats as conduct intended to disrupt a tribunal failing to follow known local customs of courtesy or practice without giving opposing counsel timely notice, undignified or discourteous conduct degrading to the tribunal, and intentional or habitual violation of procedure or evidence rules. N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct 3.5(a)(4)","#st-nc-ethics"],["Serve within 60 days or keep the summons chain alive","North Carolina · land mine","Personal service must be made within 60 days after the summons issues. To keep the case alive against an unserved defendant, get an endorsement or an alias or pluries summons within 90 days of the last one. If the chain breaks, the action is discontinued as to that defendant and, if revived, counts as commenced only on the new date. That can blow the statute of limitations. N.C. R. Civ. P. 4(c)–(e)","#st-nc-unique"],["Do not plead a dollar figure in negligence or punitive claims","North Carolina · land mine","In negligence actions and punitive damage claims over $25,000, the complaint must say only that damages exceed $25,000. The defendant may demand a written statement of the amount, due within 30 days. N.C. R. Civ. P. 8(a)(2)","#st-nc-unique"],["Jury demand: 10 days after the last pleading","North Carolina · land mine","Serve and file a written jury demand within 10 days after service of the last pleading on the issue (federal: 14). Missing it waives the jury. N.C. R. Civ. P. 38(b), (d)","#st-nc-unique"],["Post-trial motions in 10 days, no extensions","North Carolina · land mine","JNOV, motions to amend findings, and new-trial or alter-or-amend motions are due 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28). Neither the court nor a stipulation can extend these deadlines. The usual 30-day stipulated extension under Rule 6(b) does not apply. N.C. R. Civ. P. 6(b), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)","#st-nc-unique"],["Requests for admission can come with the summons","North Carolina · land mine","A plaintiff may serve requests for admission with the summons and complaint. They are deemed admitted unless answered within 30 days after service, except that a defendant gets at least 60 days from service of the summons. Deemed admissions are conclusive unless withdrawn on motion. N.C. R. Civ. P. 36(a), (b); 4(b)","#st-nc-unique"],["Voluntary dismissal: easy once, fatal twice, one-year refiling","North Carolina · land mine","A plaintiff can dismiss by notice any time before resting. A second notice dismissal of the same claim is an adjudication on the merits. A timely action dismissed without prejudice may be refiled within one year, but the refiled case is stayed until costs from the first case are paid. N.C. R. Civ. P. 41(a)(1), (d)","#st-nc-unique"],["Pro hac vice needs out-of-state domicile, reciprocity and a resident co-counsel","North Carolina · land mine","Only an attorney domiciled in another state qualifies. The motion must state that the home state grants like privileges to North Carolina lawyers and that a North Carolina resident attorney is associated and personally appearing. It must include a client-signed retention statement and the $225 fee. The court keeps discretion and may revoke summarily. N.C.G.S. §§ 84-4.1, 84-4.2","#st-nc-unique"],["Business Court designation: 30 days, and waiver","North Carolina · land mine","A defendant who wants Business Court must file the Notice of Designation within 30 days after receiving service of the pleading. A party opposing designation has 30 days after service of the notice and waives any ground not asserted. N.C.G.S. § 7A-45.4(d)(3), (e)","#st-nc-unique"],["Appeal to the right court","North Carolina · land mine","Final judgments in designated business cases, interlocutory Business Court orders affecting a substantial right, and every Rule 23 class-certification ruling are appealed directly to the Supreme Court, not the Court of Appeals. N.C.G.S. § 7A-27(a)(2)–(4)","#st-nc-unique"],["A Court of Appeals dissent no longer gets you to the Supreme Court","North Carolina · land mine","The appeal of right based on a dissent was repealed effective July 1, 2023. Without a substantial constitutional question, further review requires a petition for discretionary review. N.C.G.S. §§ 7A-30, 7A-31","#st-nc-unique"],["Rule 9(j) certification in medical malpractice","North Carolina · land mine","A malpractice complaint is dismissed unless it asserts that an expert reasonably expected to qualify under Rule 702 reviewed the care and records and will testify that the standard was breached, or it pleads res ipsa loquitur. A 120-day limitations extension is available only by motion before the statute runs. N.C. R. Civ. P. 9(j)","#st-nc-unique"],["Rule 11 sanctions are mandatory, with no safe harbor","North Carolina · land mine","If a paper is signed in violation of Rule 11, the court ‘shall impose’ an appropriate sanction on the signer, the party, or both. The text has no 21-day withdrawal window like the federal rule. N.C. R. Civ. P. 11(a)","#st-nc-unique"],["No peremptory challenge of a judge","North Carolina · judges","No statute or civil rule we collected gives a party a peremptory challenge or automatic substitution of a judge. The Rules of Civil Procedure and General Statutes Chapters 1 and 7A were searched. Disqualification is for cause. N.C.G.S. chs. 1, 1A, 7A (searched)","#st-nc-unique"],["Recusal for cause: Code of Judicial Conduct","North Carolina · judges","Motions to recuse are governed by the North Carolina Code of Judicial Conduct (Canon 3C) and case law. That code was not collected because nccourts.gov blocked downloads, so the grounds, timing, and whether another judge must hear the motion are not stated here. N.C. Code of Jud. Conduct Canon 3C (not collected)","#st-nc-unique"],["When a judge steps aside on a motion","North Carolina · judges","A judge who disqualifies from a motion may refer it to a regular resident superior court judge of the district, or a judge regularly holding court there or in an adjoining district, who decides it as if presiding. If the trial judge becomes unable to act after verdict or hearing, the senior resident superior court judge (or chief district judge) may enter judgment or order a new trial. N.C.G.S. § 7A-49.1; N.C. R. Civ","#st-nc-unique"],["Media libel: pre-suit notice and retraction","North Carolina · speech","Before suing a newspaper or periodical for libel, or a radio or television station for defamation, the plaintiff must serve written notice at least 5 days before filing, identifying the statements claimed to be false. If the publication was in good faith from an honest mistake on reasonable grounds and a full correction, apology and retraction runs within 10 days after the notice, the plaintiff recovers only actual d","#st-nc-unique"],["State constitutional speech protection","North Carolina · speech","Whether the North Carolina Constitution protects speech more broadly than the First Amendment is not addressed here. The constitution and case law were not collected. Not collected","#st-nc-unique"],["Business Court designation","North Carolina · unique rules","Corporate, securities, antitrust (beyond G.S. 75-1.1), trademark, IP, and trade secret disputes may be designated mandatory complex business cases, as may contract cases between entities of $1 million or more if all parties consent. Cases of $5 million or more in those categories must be designated. Defendants designate within 30 days after service of the pleading, and any opposition is due within 30 days after servi","#st-nc-unique"],["Contributory negligence remains a pleading issue","North Carolina · unique rules","Contributory negligence is a listed affirmative defense. When it is pleaded, the plaintiff may serve a reply alleging last clear chance, the one reply allowed without a court order besides replies to counterclaims. N.C. R. Civ. P. 7(a), 8(c)","#st-nc-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope of rules","North Carolina · civil rules","Govern civil actions and proceedings in the superior and district courts, except where a statute prescribes a different procedure, and tort actions before the Industrial Commission unless a statute differs.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","North Carolina · civil rules","There is one form of action, the civil action.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencement of action","North Carolina · civil rules","An action starts by filing a complaint, or by having the clerk issue a summons with an order allowing the complaint to be filed within 20 days; the action abates if the complaint is not filed in the time the order sets. This “summons and order” route has no federal counterpart; it commences the action before the complaint is filed. Where e-filing is available, attorneys must file under Rule 5 of the General Rules of ","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 4. Process","North Carolina · civil rules","The clerk issues the summons on filing (in any event within 5 days); the summons tells the defendant to answer within 30 days. Personal or substituted service must be made within 60 days of issuance (federal: 90 days after filing), but the action can be kept alive by an endorsement or an alias or pluries summons obtained within 90 days of the last summons or endorsement (two years for service abroad and certain tax f","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 5. Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","North Carolina · civil rules","Later papers are served on the attorney of record, through the court’s e-filing system at the email of record, by email, by hand, by fax, or by mail; e-service, email and fax must go out by 5:00 p.m. Eastern on a business day or count as served the next business day, and mail service is complete on deposit. In superior court, briefs on dispositive motions must be served at least 2 days before the hearing. Papers must","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 6. Time","North Carolina · civil rules","Exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a weekend or a legal holiday when the courthouse is closed; for periods under 7 days, weekends and holidays are excluded. Parties may stipulate, without a court order, to extensions totaling up to 30 days, but no one may extend the times under Rules 50(b), 52, 59(b), (d), (e), or 60(b) except as those rules allow. Motions and notices of hearing must be ser","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings allowed; motions","North Carolina · civil rules","Pleadings are the complaint, answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a crossclaim, third-party complaint and answer; a reply alleging last clear chance is allowed when the answer pleads contributory negligence. Motions must be in writing with particular grounds unless made at a hearing or trial. In multicounty districts, a motion may be heard in another county of the district with the senior resident judge’s permi","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleadings","North Carolina · civil rules","Notice pleading, but the claim must be “sufficiently particular” to give notice of the transactions and occurrences to be proved. In negligence actions and punitive-damage claims over $25,000, do not state a dollar demand; plead only that damages exceed $25,000 (any party may then request a written statement of the amount, due within 30 days). In business-type cases listed in G.S. 7A-45.4(a), the pleading must say wh","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","North Carolina · civil rules","Fraud, duress, and mistake must be pleaded with particularity; capacity of a non-natural party must be affirmatively alleged. Rule 9(j): a medical malpractice complaint is dismissed unless it certifies pre-filing review by a reasonably expected Rule 702 expert (or pleads res ipsa loquitur); a resident superior court judge may extend the limitations period up to 120 days to comply. Punitive damages must be specificall","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","North Carolina · civil rules","Caption, numbered paragraphs, and separate counts; exhibits to a pleading are part of it for all purposes.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing and verification of pleadings","North Carolina · civil rules","Every paper must be signed by an attorney of record (with address) or the unrepresented party, certifying reasonable inquiry, factual and legal support, and no improper purpose. Unlike federal Rule 11, there is no 21-day safe harbor and sanctions are mandatory (“shall impose”) once a violation is found. Rules 11(b)–(d) govern verification by a party, agent, attorney, or corporate officer.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections","North Carolina · civil rules","Answer within 30 days after service of summons and complaint (federal: 21 days); replies to counterclaims and answers to crossclaims also 30 days. After a Rule 12 motion is decided, the responsive pleading is due in 20 days. The Rule 12(b) defenses track the federal list except that (7) is failure to join a “necessary” party; personal-jurisdiction, venue, process, and service defenses are waived if omitted from a pre","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and crossclaim","North Carolina · civil rules","Compulsory counterclaims arising from the same transaction or occurrence must be pleaded; permissive counterclaims are allowed. An omitted counterclaim may be added by amendment with leave for oversight, inadvertence, excusable neglect, or when justice requires.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","North Carolina · civil rules","A defendant may bring in a third party without leave if the third-party complaint is filed within 45 days after serving the answer (federal: 14 days); otherwise leave is needed. Special provisions allow the State to be made a third-party defendant in tort cases under Tort Claims Act limits.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","North Carolina · civil rules","A party may amend once as of course before a responsive pleading is served (or within 30 days if no responsive pleading is allowed and the case is not calendared); otherwise by leave, freely given, or written consent. Response to an amended pleading is due within 30 days. Relation back turns on whether the original pleading gave notice of the transactions or occurrences; the text has no separate rule for changing par","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 16. Pre-trial procedure","North Carolina · civil rules","The court may hold a pretrial conference and enter an order that controls the case. In medical malpractice actions, a final conference follows the close of discovery and consent trial-setting orders go to the senior resident or chief district judge for approval.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 17. Parties plaintiff and defendant; capacity","North Carolina · civil rules","Actions must be prosecuted by the real party in interest, with a reasonable time to ratify, join, or substitute before dismissal. Minors and incompetents sue or defend through a general guardian or a guardian ad litem appointed under detailed procedures, including guardians ad litem for unborn persons and entities not yet in existence.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims and remedies","North Carolina · civil rules","Any number of claims may be joined; a money claim may be joined with a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance before judgment.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 19. Necessary joinder of parties","North Carolina · civil rules","Persons “united in interest” must be joined; the court must order joinder of others when complete determination requires them. The pleader must name and explain omitted necessary persons. The Speaker of the House and President Pro Tempore of the Senate must be joined as defendants in any action challenging the validity of a North Carolina statute or constitutional provision.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","North Carolina · civil rules","Parties may join or be joined when claims arise from the same transaction or occurrence and share a common question; the court may order separate trials to prevent prejudice.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder","North Carolina · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped and claims severed at any stage.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","North Carolina · civil rules","Claimants exposing a party to multiple liability may be required to interplead; the court may order disputed funds deposited in an interest-bearing account or with the clerk.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","North Carolina · civil rules","Much shorter than the federal rule: a class suit is allowed when the class is too numerous to bring all before the court and the representatives will fairly insure adequate representation; it has no Rule 23(b) categories in the text. Derivative complaints must be verified; dismissal or compromise needs court approval and notice to the class; tax-refund class actions must follow G.S. 105-241.18. A class-certification ","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","North Carolina · civil rules","Intervention of right (unconditional statutory right or impaired interest not adequately represented) and permissive intervention, on timely motion with a pleading attached. The Speaker and President Pro Tempore may intervene of right by filing a notice at any stage.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","North Carolina · civil rules","On death, substitution may be ordered within the time for presenting estate claims under G.S. 28A-19-3; on adjudicated incompetency, within one year. The court may order abatement unless the action is continued within 6 to 12 months; no abatement after verdict.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 26. General provisions governing discovery","North Carolina · civil rules","Scope is anything relevant to the subject matter that is not privileged and is reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence; the text has no federal-style proportionality test or mandatory initial disclosures. Expert disclosures (by optional written report or by interrogatory) are due at least 90 days before trial, rebuttal within 30 days after the other side’s disclosure; draft reports and most attorney–expe","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions before action or pending appeal","North Carolina · civil rules","A verified petition to perpetuate testimony must be served at least 20 days before the hearing; the trial court may also allow depositions pending appeal.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","North Carolina · civil rules","Lists officers authorized in the U.S. and abroad and disqualifies relatives, employees, attorneys, financially interested persons, and reporters under “blanket contracts” with a party or counsel, unless waived by stipulation disclosed in the notice. Covers commissions for depositions in North Carolina for foreign proceedings.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","North Carolina · civil rules","Parties may stipulate in writing to deposition logistics and modify other discovery procedures unless the court orders otherwise.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions upon oral examination","North Carolina · civil rules","Leave is needed for a plaintiff’s deposition within 30 days of service unless the defendant has sought discovery or special notice is given. Notice must be served at least 10 days ahead (15 days if any party resides out of state), and 15 more days when a Rule 34 request accompanies a party deposition (objections due 7 days before). Residents may be deposed only in their home, work, or business county; nonresidents on","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions upon written questions","North Carolina · civil rules","Cross questions within 30 days after service of the notice and questions; redirect within 10 days; recross within 10 days.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 32. Use of depositions","North Carolina · civil rules","A deposition may be used for any purpose if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles from the place of trial, out of the U.S., unable to attend, unsubpoenable, or is an expert whose testimony was videotaped under Rule 30(b)(4). Rule 32(a)(2) also lets an adverse party use the deposition of a witness who testifies as substantive evidence.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories","North Carolina · civil rules","No more than 50 interrogatories to any party, counting parts and subparts separately (federal: 25), unless leave or agreement. Answers are due in 30 days, but a defendant has 45 days after service of summons and complaint. Responses must follow each interrogatory in the space provided or restate it.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 34. Production of documents, ESI, and things","North Carolina · civil rules","Responses are due in 30 days (defendant: 45 days after service of summons and complaint). Requests may specify the ESI form; objections must state reasons and the form the responder will use. Responses must be in the space provided or restate the request.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examination","North Carolina · civil rules","On motion for good cause, a judge may order an examination “by a physician” when condition is in controversy; requesting the report waives privilege as to others who examined the same condition.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","North Carolina · civil rules","Matters are admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days, but a defendant need not respond before 60 days after service of summons and complaint. Requests may be served with the summons (the summons must say so). Admissions are conclusive unless withdrawn on motion.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to make discovery; sanctions","North Carolina · civil rules","Motions to compel require a good-faith conference certification; expenses follow the loser unless substantially justified. Sanctions for violating orders include deemed facts, preclusion, striking pleadings, dismissal, default, and contempt. ESI lost through routine good-faith operation is protected absent exceptional circumstances (no federal Rule 37(e) framework). Expenses may be shifted for failure to admit and fo","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury trial of right","North Carolina · civil rules","Demand a jury in writing no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue (federal: 14 days); failure to serve and file the demand waives the jury. A demand cannot be withdrawn without consent of the parties who have appeared. Jury trial is guaranteed on just compensation in condemnation.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","North Carolina · civil rules","Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or no right exists; the court may order a jury despite no demand, or use an advisory jury.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 40. Assignment of cases for trial; continuances","North Carolina · civil rules","Senior resident superior court judges set calendaring rules; district court calendaring follows G.S. 7A-146. Continuances only for good cause, and must be granted for General Assembly service and certain appointed state boards.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","North Carolina · civil rules","A plaintiff may dismiss by notice at any time before resting (federal: before an answer or summary judgment motion) or by stipulation; a second notice dismissal of the same claim is an adjudication on the merits. After a timely action is dismissed without prejudice, a new action on the same claim may be filed within one year (a key savings rule). A plaintiff who dismisses pays the costs, and a refiled case is stayed ","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials","North Carolina · civil rules","Courts may consolidate and must, on motion, bifurcate liability and damages in tort cases seeking over $150,000 unless good cause is shown. Facial challenges to an act of the General Assembly must be transferred to a three-judge panel of Wake County Superior Court (G.S. 1-267.1); a defendant may raise such a challenge within 30 days after filing its answer.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 43. Evidence","North Carolina · civil rules","Testimony is taken orally in open court; adverse parties and their agents may be called and led. Motions on facts outside the record may be heard on affidavits.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 44. Proof of official record","North Carolina · civil rules","Official records are proved by official publication or attested copy with a custody certificate.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 44.1. Determination of foreign law","North Carolina · civil rules","A party must give written notice of an issue of foreign-country law; the court may consider any relevant source, and its ruling is one of law.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","North Carolina · civil rules","Any attorney (as officer of the court), judge, magistrate, or the clerk may issue a subpoena; a copy must be served on every party under Rule 5(b). Written objections or a motion to quash are due within 10 days after service (federal: 14 days) or before the compliance date if sooner; an objection stops compliance until a court orders otherwise. The issuing party must notify all parties within 5 business days after re","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 46. Objections","North Carolina · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary. Continuing objections are deemed for a challenged witness or line of questioning, and rulings on non-evidence matters are preserved by making the party’s position and grounds known.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 47. Jurors","North Carolina · civil rules","Juror qualification and challenges follow Chapter 9 of the General Statutes.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 48. Juries of less than twelve","North Carolina · civil rules","Parties may stipulate to fewer than 12 jurors or to a stated majority verdict, except where a statute requires a jury.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 49. Verdicts","North Carolina · civil rules","General and special verdicts; omitted issues are waived unless submission is demanded before the jury retires.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 50. Directed verdict and judgment notwithstanding the verdict","North Carolina · civil rules","North Carolina keeps the older names: “directed verdict” and “JNOV.” A JNOV motion is due within 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28 days), and only a party who moved for directed verdict at the close of all the evidence may make it; the judge may act on its own within the same 10 days. An appellate court cannot direct judgment unless the JNOV motion was made or ruled on.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 51. Instructions to jury","North Carolina · civil rules","The judge may not express an opinion on the facts and need not summarize the evidence; special instruction requests must be written and submitted before the charge. In medical malpractice cases, the instructions must be reduced to writing.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings by the court","North Carolina · civil rules","In bench trials the court must find facts specially and state conclusions separately; findings on motions are required only when requested. A motion to amend findings is due within 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28 days). Sufficiency of evidence for findings may be raised on appeal without a trial-court objection.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 53. Referees","North Carolina · civil rules","Reference by consent waives jury trial on referred issues; a compulsory reference preserves it only if the party objects when ordered, files specific exceptions within 30 days after the report is filed, and tenders issues and a jury demand. Exceptions to a referee’s report are due within 30 days of filing.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgments","North Carolina · civil rules","A partial final judgment requires a determination of “no just reason for delay”; otherwise orders on fewer than all claims or parties are not appealable except as statutes allow (in North Carolina, a “substantial right” order may still be appealable under G.S. 1-277 and 7A-27). Default judgments cannot exceed the demand.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 55. Default","North Carolina · civil rules","The clerk enters default and may enter judgment for a sum certain against a non-appearing, competent defendant. A defendant who has appeared must get written notice of a default-judgment application at least 3 days before the hearing (federal: 7 days). A motion may be decided without a hearing if it says so and no written response is served within 30 days. Entry of default may be set aside for good cause; default jud","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","North Carolina · civil rules","A claimant may move after 30 days from commencement; a defending party at any time. The motion must be served at least 10 days before the hearing and opposing affidavits at least 2 days before. Bad-faith affidavits trigger mandatory expense awards.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments","North Carolina · civil rules","Procedure under Article 26 of Chapter 1 follows these rules, with jury trial available under Rules 38 and 39; another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 58. Entry of judgment","North Carolina · civil rules","A judgment is entered when it is reduced to writing, signed by the judge, and filed with the clerk. The designated party (or the drafter) must serve it on all parties within 3 days; while it is not served, the Rule 50(b), 52(b), and 59 periods are tolled, but not more than 90 days from entry. Add 3 days if served by mail.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 59. New trials; amendment of judgments","North Carolina · civil rules","A new-trial motion and a motion to alter or amend are due within 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28 days); the court may act on its own within the same 10 days. Opposing affidavits are due within 10 days after service, extendable up to 30 more days. The listed grounds include ‘any other reason heretofore recognized.’","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from judgment or order","North Carolina · civil rules","Clerical mistakes may be corrected anytime (with appellate leave after the appeal is docketed). Rule 60(b)(1)–(3) motions must be made within one year; all within a reasonable time. A Rule 60(b) motion does not suspend the judgment.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","North Carolina · civil rules","Errors are disregarded unless refusing relief denies a substantial right.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","North Carolina · civil rules","No execution until the time for noticing an appeal expires, except injunction and receivership judgments, which are not stayed unless ordered. A stay on appeal follows G.S. 1-289 to 1-295 (supersedeas bond, capped at $25 million for judgments of $25 million or more under G.S. 1-289(b)); the State and local governments need no bond. Rule 62(h) gives an immediate appeal of orders granting or denying relief against enfo","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 63. Disability of a judge","North Carolina · civil rules","If the trial judge cannot act after verdict or hearing, the senior resident superior court judge (or chief district judge) may enter judgment or, if unable to perform, order a new trial.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 64. Seizure of person or property","North Carolina · civil rules","Provisional remedies are available as provided by North Carolina law.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions","North Carolina · civil rules","No preliminary injunction without notice. An ex parte TRO expires in no more than 10 days (federal: 14), extendable once for a like period for good cause; the adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice. Security is required except from the State and local governments, and in spousal suits restraining molestation.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 66–67. Omitted","North Carolina · civil rules","Rules 66 and 67 are omitted from the North Carolina rules.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of judgment and disclaimer","North Carolina · civil rules","A defending party may serve an offer more than 10 days before trial; acceptance is due within 10 days (federal: 14). If the offeree does no better, it pays costs incurred after the offer. Rule 68(b) lets a contract defendant offer, with its answer, a stipulated damages figure that the claimant may accept within 20 days.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 68.1. Confession of judgment","North Carolina · civil rules","A prospective defendant may file a verified statement with the clerk authorizing judgment for money due or to become due (including alimony or child support), in the county of the defendant’s residence or real property or the plaintiff’s residence; it is not res judicata on facts except in an action on the judgment.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 69. Omitted","North Carolina · civil rules","Rule 69 is omitted; execution is governed by Chapter 1 of the General Statutes.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 70. Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","North Carolina · civil rules","The court may appoint someone to perform an act a party refuses to do, issue attachment or sequestration, hold the party in contempt, or vest title to in-state property directly.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 71–83. Omitted","North Carolina · civil rules","Rules 71 through 83 are omitted from the North Carolina rules.","#st-nc-rules"],["Rule 84. Forms","North Carolina · civil rules","Sample complaints, motions, and answers illustrate the brevity the rules contemplate.","#st-nc-rules"],["Appeals in North Carolina: statutes decide where and what; the appellate rules decide when and how","North Carolina · appeals","Most civil appeals go from the superior or district court to the 15-judge Court of Appeals, which sits in panels of three. Some go straight to the Supreme Court: final judgments in designated complex business cases, certain interlocutory orders of a Business Court judge, and any trial court ruling on class certification under Rule 23 (G.S. 7A-27(a)).\nSince July 1, 2023, a dissent in the Court of Appeals no longer cre","#st-nc-appeals"],["Default: Court of Appeals","North Carolina · appeals · Which court hears the appeal","Appeal lies of right to the Court of Appeals from any final judgment of a superior court (including review of agency decisions) and any final judgment of a district court in a civil action, unless G.S. 7A-27(a) sends it to the Supreme Court. N.C.G.S. § 7A-27(b)(1)–(2)","#st-nc-appeals"],["Direct to the Supreme Court: Business Court and class certification","North Carolina · appeals · Which court hears the appeal","Appeal goes directly to the Supreme Court from (1) any final judgment in a mandatory complex business case under G.S. 7A-45.4 or a discretionary complex business case under General Rule of Practice 2.1; (2) any interlocutory order of a Business Court judge that affects a substantial right, in effect determines the action, discontinues it, or grants or refuses a new trial; and (3) any trial court decision on class cer","#st-nc-appeals"],["No more dissent-based appeals of right","North Carolina · appeals · Which court hears the appeal","G.S. 7A-30(2), which gave an appeal of right when a Court of Appeals panel had a dissent, was repealed effective July 1, 2023. The remaining appeal of right is from a Court of Appeals decision that directly involves a substantial question under the U.S. or North Carolina Constitution. N.C.G.S. § 7A-30 (as amended by S.L. 2023-134, s. 16.21(d))","#st-nc-appeals"],["Substantial-right interlocutory appeals","North Carolina · appeals · What can be appealed before final judgment","An interlocutory order is immediately appealable to the Court of Appeals if it affects a substantial right, in effect determines the action and prevents a judgment, discontinues the action, or grants or refuses a new trial. The statute also lists orders on claims under G.S. 50-19.1, temporary injunctions against enforcing an act of the General Assembly (when the State or a subdivision is a party), and denial of trans","#st-nc-appeals"],["Personal jurisdiction rulings: appeal now or preserve","North Carolina · appeals · What can be appealed before final judgment","Any interested party may immediately appeal an adverse ruling on jurisdiction over the person or property of the defendant, or may preserve the objection for a later appeal. N.C.G.S. § 1-277(b)","#st-nc-appeals"],["Rule 54(b) certification","North Carolina · appeals · What can be appealed before final judgment","A judgment on fewer than all claims or parties is final and appealable only if the court determines in the judgment that there is no just reason for delay; otherwise it remains revisable and is not appealable except as rules or statutes expressly provide (for example, substantial-right orders). N.C. R. Civ. P. 54(b)","#st-nc-appeals"],["As-applied challenges to acts of the General Assembly","North Carolina · appeals · What can be appealed before final judgment","A party has an immediate right to appeal an order granting or denying interlocutory, temporary, or permanent injunctive or declaratory relief restraining the State or a subdivision from enforcing an act as applied, or denying a motion to stay such an injunction. It does not apply to facial challenges heard by a three-judge panel. N.C. R. Civ. P. 62(h)","#st-nc-appeals"],["Certiorari and other writs","North Carolina · appeals · What can be appealed before final judgment","The Court of Appeals and Supreme Court may issue certiorari, mandamus, prohibition, and supersedeas to supervise trial courts; when used as a substitute for an appeal, certiorari issues on an undertaking for costs only. The petition procedure is in the appellate rules (Rule 21 for certiorari, Rule 23 for supersedeas and temporary stays). Not verified: the Rules of Appellate Procedure could not be downloaded (nccourts","#st-nc-appeals"],["Time and manner come from the appellate rules","North Carolina · appeals · Notice of appeal, tolling, and cross-appeals","A party entitled to appeal in a civil action or special proceeding appeals by giving notice of appeal within the time, in the manner, and with the effect provided in the Rules of Appellate Procedure. Under Appellate Rule 3(c), the notice is due within 30 days after entry of judgment if the judgment is served within the 3 days Rule 58 requires, or within 30 days after service if it is not. Not verified: the Rules of A","#st-nc-appeals"],["Entry and service of the judgment start the clocks","North Carolina · appeals · Notice of appeal, tolling, and cross-appeals","A judgment is entered when it is written, signed by the judge, and filed with the clerk. The designated party (or the drafter) must serve it on all parties within 3 days. Until it is served, the time for Rule 50(b), 52(b), and 59 motions is tolled, but never more than 90 days from entry. Add 3 days if served by mail. N.C. R. Civ. P. 58","#st-nc-appeals"],["Post-trial motions: 10 days, and they suspend the appeal time","North Carolina · appeals · Notice of appeal, tolling, and cross-appeals","JNOV (Rule 50(b)), motions to amend findings (Rule 52(b)), and new-trial or alter-or-amend motions (Rule 59) are all due within 10 days after entry of judgment, and neither parties nor the court may extend these times. Under Appellate Rule 3(c)(3), a timely motion under these rules tolls the time to appeal for all parties until the order disposing of it is entered and served. A Rule 60(b) motion does not affect final","#st-nc-appeals"],["Cross-appeals","North Carolina · appeals · Notice of appeal, tolling, and cross-appeals","Any aggrieved party may appeal or cross-appeal; ‘party aggrieved’ includes a party challenging the grant or denial of a motion under the Rules of Civil Procedure. Under Appellate Rule 3(c), once one party timely files and serves a notice of appeal, any other party may file and serve a notice within 10 days after the first notice was served on it. Not verified: the Rules of Appellate Procedure could not be downloaded ","#st-nc-appeals"],["Stays and bonds","North Carolina · appeals · Notice of appeal, tolling, and cross-appeals","No execution may issue until the time to notice an appeal expires, except injunction and receivership judgments. To stay a money judgment on appeal, the appellant posts an undertaking set by the court after notice and hearing (considering the judgment, insurance limits, and net worth); for judgments of $25 million or more the bond is capped at $25 million unless the appellee proves the appellant is dissipating, hidin","#st-nc-appeals"],["Transcript and record on appeal","North Carolina · appeals · Record, briefs, and limits (appellate rules, not verified)","In North Carolina, the parties assemble and settle a printed record on appeal in the trial court rather than transmitting the trial file. Under Appellate Rules 7, 11 and 12, the appellant must arrange the transcript within 14 days after filing the notice of appeal, serve a proposed record within 35 days after the transcript is delivered (or after notice of appeal if there is no transcript), and file the settled recor","#st-nc-appeals"],["Briefing schedule","North Carolina · appeals · Record, briefs, and limits (appellate rules, not verified)","Under Appellate Rule 13, the appellant’s brief is due 30 days after the clerk mails the printed record, and the appellee’s brief 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief. A reply brief may be filed within 14 days after service of the appellee’s brief (Rule 28(h)). Not verified: the Rules of Appellate Procedure could not be downloaded (nccourts.gov blocked automated requests); check the current codification befo","#st-nc-appeals"],["Word limits and required contents","North Carolina · appeals · Record, briefs, and limits (appellate rules, not verified)","Appellate Rule 28(j) sets word limits for Court of Appeals briefs (8,750 words for principal briefs and 3,750 for replies, with a certificate of compliance). Rule 28(b)(4) requires a statement of the grounds for appellate review, which for an interlocutory appeal must show the substantial right or certification. Issues not presented and argued in the brief are abandoned (Rule 28(b)(6)); preservation of error is gover","#st-nc-appeals"],["Discretionary review (PDR)","North Carolina · appeals · Rehearing and Supreme Court review","The Supreme Court may certify a case for review before or after the Court of Appeals decides it. Before decision: significant public interest, legal principles of major significance, likely delay causing substantial harm, appellate workload, or the court system’s jurisdiction and integrity. After decision: significant public interest, major legal significance, or likely conflict with a Supreme Court decision. Interlo","#st-nc-appeals"],["Timing of PDRs and notices of appeal to the Supreme Court","North Carolina · appeals · Rehearing and Supreme Court review","Under Appellate Rules 14 and 15, a notice of appeal under G.S. 7A-30 and a petition for discretionary review after decision are filed within 15 days after the Court of Appeals’ mandate issues; the mandate ordinarily issues 20 days after the opinion is filed (Rule 32). Rules 14, 15, and 16 were reported amended effective April 1, 2025; the effect of that amendment was not checked. Not verified: the Rules of Appellate ","#st-nc-appeals"],["Rehearing","North Carolina · appeals · Rehearing and Supreme Court review","Appellate Rule 31 governs petitions for rehearing, filed within 15 days after the mandate issues; whether and when rehearing is available in each court should be checked in the current rule. Not verified: the Rules of Appellate Procedure could not be downloaded (nccourts.gov blocked automated requests); check the current codification before relying on this. N.C. R. App. P. 31 (not verified)","#st-nc-appeals"],["Appellate e-filing site","North Carolina · appeals · Electronic filing","Filings in the Supreme Court and Court of Appeals are made through the appellate courts’ electronic filing site, ncappellatecourts.org, which requires registration. Appellate Rule 26 governs filing and service. Trial-court e-filing uses eCourts File & Serve under General Rule of Practice 5, which the Supreme Court adopts under G.S. 7A-49.5. Not verified: the Rules of Appellate Procedure could not be downloaded (nccou","#st-nc-appeals"],["Serve signed judgment on all parties","North Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","3 days from entry of judgment (written, signed, filed).\nFailure tolls Rule 50(b), 52(b) and 59 periods, but not beyond 90 days from entry. N.C. R. Civ. P. 58","#st-nc-appeals"],["JNOV, motion to amend findings, new-trial or alter/amend motion","North Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended by the court or by stipulation (Rule 6(b)). Add 3 days if the judgment was served by mail (Rule 58). N.C. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)","#st-nc-appeals"],["Notice of appeal, civil case","North Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of judgment if served within 3 days under Rule 58; otherwise service of the judgment.\nChecked Nov 11, 2026 against N.C. R. App. P. 3(c) in the Supreme Court’s order amending the Rules (effective Jan 1, 2019) on appellate.nccourts.org. Tolled by a timely Rule 50(b), 52(b) or 59 motion under App. R. 3(c)(3). N.C.G.S. § 1-279.1; N.C. R. App. P. 3(c)","#st-nc-appeals"],["Cross-appeal notice","North Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from service of the first notice of appeal.\nNOT verified against collected text. N.C. R. App. P. 3(c)","#st-nc-appeals"],["Contract for transcript","North Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of notice of appeal.\nNOT verified against collected text. N.C. R. App. P. 7","#st-nc-appeals"],["Serve proposed record on appeal","North Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","35 days from delivery of transcript (or filing of notice of appeal if no transcript).\nNOT verified against collected text. N.C. R. App. P. 11","#st-nc-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief (Court of Appeals)","North Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from clerk’s mailing of the printed record.\nNOT verified against collected text. N.C. R. App. P. 13","#st-nc-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief","North Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of appellant’s brief.\nNOT verified against collected text. N.C. R. App. P. 13","#st-nc-appeals"],["Reply brief","North Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of appellee’s brief.\nNOT verified against collected text. N.C. R. App. P. 28(h)","#st-nc-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (G.S. 7A-30) or petition for discretionary review to the Supreme Court","North Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from issuance of the Court of Appeals’ mandate.\nRules NOT verified; Rules 14–16 reportedly amended effective April 1, 2025. Dissent-based appeals of right were repealed July 1, 2023. N.C.G.S. §§ 7A-30, 7A-31; N.C. R. App. P. 14, 15","#st-nc-appeals"],["Rule 60(b)(1)–(3) motion","North Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","365 days from entry of the judgment or order.\nDoes not toll the time to appeal or suspend the judgment. N.C. R. Civ. P. 60(b)","#st-nc-appeals"],["North Dakota Supreme Court","North Dakota · courts","The state’s appellate court for nearly all purposes: civil appeals go straight from the district court to the Supreme Court by a notice filed with its clerk. It adopts the procedural rules (civil, appellate, Rules of Court, administrative rules), and the power to admit lawyers is vested in it by statute. Review of a decision is by petition for rehearing within 14 days (N.D.R.App.P. 3, 4, 40; N.D.C.C. §§ 27-11-02, 28-","#st-nd"],["Temporary Court of Appeals","North Dakota · courts","Not a standing court. Three-judge panels (retired justices, active or retired district judges, lawyers, or surrogate judges) hear only cases the Supreme Court assigns, usually error-correction categories such as family-law decrees, misdemeanors, small claims, municipal and administrative appeals, and summary-judgment appeals. All filings still go to the clerk of the Supreme Court. Review is by discretionary petition ","#st-nd"],["District Courts","North Dakota · courts","The single trial court of general jurisdiction, organized into eight judicial districts (Northwest, North Central, Northeast, Northeast Central, East Central, Southeast, South Central, Southwest), each with a presiding judge. County courts were abolished effective January 1995 and their work moved to the district courts. District judges serve six-year terms. Civil practice follows the N.D.R.Civ.P., the N.D. Rules of ","#st-nd"],["Small Claims Court; Municipal Courts","North Dakota · courts","Small claims court is the district court sitting under N.D.C.C. ch. 27-08.1 for money claims and certain contract-cancellation claims up to $15,000. Assignees may not file. A plaintiff who chooses small claims cannot appeal, and the defendant gives up appeal unless it elects to remove the case to district court. Municipal courts hear city matters; N.D.R.Civ.P. 60 applies to them for relief from judgments (N.D.C.C. §§","#st-nd"],["No anti-SLAPP statute found in the Century Code","North Dakota · anti-SLAPP","A search of the Legislative Branch’s full Century Code section index (all titles, chapter and section headings) found no anti-SLAPP law, no Uniform Public Expression Protection Act, and no section on strategic lawsuits, public participation in litigation, or public expression. North Dakota therefore has no special motion to dismiss, discovery stay, or interlocutory appeal for speech-based suits. The check was of head","#st-nd-slapp"],["Closest tools: mandatory fees for frivolous claims, and Rule 11","North Dakota · anti-SLAPP","In civil actions the court must award reasonable actual and statutory costs, including attorney’s fees, to the prevailing party on finding a claim for relief frivolous: so completely lacking in facts or law that a reasonable person could not have expected to win. The prevailing party must have alleged the frivolous nature of the claim in its responsive pleading, so a defendant must plead it in the answer. A good-fait","#st-nd-slapp"],["Repeat litigants: statewide vexatious-litigant pre-filing orders","North Dakota · anti-SLAPP","A presiding judge, on request or on the court’s own motion, may designate a vexatious litigant (for example, two adverse litigations involving vexatious conduct in seven years, repeated relitigation, or a prior designation by any court) and enter a pre-filing order requiring leave of court for new filings, possibly with security. The order must award the costs and reasonable attorney’s fees of responding, including t","#st-nd-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: motion, affidavit, Board filing, and fee before you appear","North Dakota · out of state","Every nonresident lawyer licensed elsewhere who appears in person, signs pleadings, or is designated as counsel in a North Dakota state court, agency, or tribunal needs pro hac vice admission. Before appearing, the lawyer must designate a North Dakota-licensed associate lawyer, file a motion and supporting affidavit with the trial court clerk (or hearing or presiding officer), file copies with the State Board of Law ","#st-nd-oos"],["Associate counsel must attend; five appearances a year is presumed excessive","North Dakota · out of state","The North Dakota associate lawyer’s name and address must appear on all papers, and the associate must appear personally and stay in attendance with the nonresident lawyer at all appearances unless excused. Permission is needed separately in each action. More than five pro hac vice appearances in the preceding 12 months is presumed excessive (pro bono work excepted); exceeding it requires good cause tied to the clien","#st-nd-oos"],["Temporary practice and in-house counsel: written disclosure to the client","North Dakota · out of state","Rule 5.5 permits temporary in-state services on the usual ABA-style grounds, but carves out any work that requires pro hac vice admission or registration. A lawyer practicing under Rule 5.5(b) or (c) must disclose in writing to the client that the lawyer is not licensed in North Dakota, a requirement not in the ABA Model Rule. In-house counsel with a systematic and continuous presence must register annually with the ","#st-nd-oos"],["Limited-scope work: ghostwriting allowed; limited appearances need notice and a completion certificate","North Dakota · out of state","A lawyer complying with Rule 1.2 may prepare filings for a self-represented party without appearing; the party signs as “self-represented.” A lawyer making a limited appearance must serve a notice stating its precise scope (amended if the scope grows). Within 14 days after finishing, the lawyer files a Certificate of Completion of Limited Appearance, which operates as withdrawal without court approval. While the limi","#st-nd-oos"],["E-filing: Odyssey in district court, a separate system in the Supreme Court","North Dakota · out of state","All documents in civil cases must be e-filed through Odyssey (self-represented litigants and prisoners exempt). Every attorney must give the State Board of Law Examiners an e-mail address for service, which is posted on the Supreme Court website; service to it is complete on transmission, and an attorney who fails to provide or open e-mail is treated as served. A document submitted by 11:59 p.m. local time is filed t","#st-nd-oos"],["Governing code: North Dakota Rules of Professional Conduct","North Dakota · ethics","North Dakota’s rules follow ABA Model Rule numbering (cited N.D.R. Prof. Conduct), with several substantive departures shown below and a state-specific Rule 1.19 on client files and Rule 4.5 on inadvertently sent documents. N.D.R. Prof. Conduct 1.0-9.1","#st-nd-ethics"],["Confidentiality: disclosure to prevent death or serious harm is mandatory","North Dakota · ethics","A lawyer is required (not merely permitted, as under ABA Model Rule 1.6(b)(1)) to reveal information to the extent reasonably necessary to prevent reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm. The permissive exceptions for client crime or fraud using the lawyer’s services, ethics advice, self-defense, law or court order, and conflict checks track the ABA list. N.D.R. Prof. Conduct 1.6(b)-(d)","#st-nd-ethics"],["Lateral screening is conditional","North Dakota · ethics","A firm can screen a lateral lawyer with a former-client conflict only if the confidential information is unlikely to be significant, there is no reasonably apparent risk its use will materially harm the former client, the lawyer is timely screened and gets no part of the fee, and written notice goes to affected clients. The ABA rule has no “unlikely to be significant” or “material adverse effect” conditions. Imputed ","#st-nd-ethics"],["Fees: nonrefundable fees banned; flat fees may go to operating; contingent-fee closing statement","North Dakota · ethics","Nonrefundable fees and nonrefundable retainers are prohibited, as is any agreement restricting the client’s right to fire the lawyer or unreasonably restricting refunds of unearned fees. A flat, fixed, or minimum fee for future services becomes the lawyer’s property when paid and may be deposited in the operating account; other advance fees go to trust. Contingent-fee agreements must be signed by the client and ident","#st-nd-ethics"],["No retaining lien on client files","North Dakota · ethics","A lawyer may not assert a retaining lien on client files, papers, or property, or condition their return on payment of fees or copying costs. The rule defines what belongs to the client (including filed pleadings, correspondence, and items of evidentiary value even if unpaid) and what is lawyer work product. Copying charges after termination are allowed only if the client agreed in writing beforehand. N.D.R. Prof. Co","#st-nd-ethics"],["Trust accounts: IOLTA, approved banks with overdraft reporting, and malpractice-insurance disclosure","North Dakota · ethics","Nominal or short-term client funds go into a pooled interest-bearing trust account whose interest is paid to the North Dakota Bar Foundation. Trust accounts may be kept only at Disciplinary Board-approved institutions that agree to report overdrafts. Records must be kept six years. With annual license renewal, each lawyer certifies compliance and states whether the lawyer represents private clients and carries profes","#st-nd-ethics"],["Misconduct: bias rule in 8.4(f), no ABA 8.4(g)","North Dakota · ethics","North Dakota has not adopted ABA Model Rule 8.4(g). Instead, conduct prejudicial to the administration of justice includes knowingly manifesting bias or prejudice based on race, sex, religion, national origin, disability, age, or sexual orientation while representing a client, unless it is legitimate advocacy. Rule 8.4(g) makes conduct listed in the Century Code as a ground for revocation or suspension also misconduc","#st-nd-ethics"],["The case starts on service, and the defendant can force you to file in 20 days","North Dakota · land mine","A civil action is commenced by service of the summons, not by filing. A defendant may serve a demand that the plaintiff file; if the complaint is not filed within 20 days after service of the demand, the service of the summons is void (relief only by motion within 60 days on excusable neglect). When you do file, proof of Rule 4 service must accompany the initiating pleading. N.D.R.Civ.P. 3, 5(d)(2)(A)","#st-nd-unique"],["Appeal time runs 60 days from service of notice of entry, and the notice goes to the Supreme Court clerk","North Dakota · land mine","File the notice of appeal with the clerk of the Supreme Court within 60 days from service of notice of entry of judgment, together with the transcript order. The prevailing party must serve notice of entry within 14 days. Do not wait for service if you have acted on the judgment: the record showing your own actual knowledge of entry can start the clock. An extension (up to 30 days) must be sought within 30 days after","#st-nd-unique"],["Ten days to demand a new judge, and one ruling ends the right","North Dakota · land mine","The no-cause demand for change of judge must be filed within 10 days after notice of the judge’s assignment for trial, notice of a trial date, or service of that judge’s ex parte order, whichever comes first. It is barred once the judge rules on any matter in which your client was heard or could have been heard, so even a routine motion hearing can forfeit it. The client must sign, or the attorney signs with permissi","#st-nd-unique"],["Motion practice runs on briefs: 14 days to answer, and silence is an admission","North Dakota · land mine","The movant serves and files a notice and brief; the opponent has 14 days after service to serve and file an answer brief, and the reply is due 7 days later. The motion is then decided on the briefs unless a party requests a hearing within 7 days after the answer-brief deadline, and the requester must secure a hearing time within 14 days or the request is waived. Failing to file an answer brief may be treated as an ad","#st-nd-unique"],["Summary judgment has its own outer deadlines and page limits","North Dakota · land mine","The motion and supporting papers must be filed at least 90 days before trial and 45 days before the hearing unless ordered otherwise. The response is due 30 days after service of the brief, and the reply 14 days later. Principal and answer briefs are limited to 38 pages, replies to 12 (footnotes included); a request to exceed must be filed at least 7 days before the brief is due. N.D.R.Civ.P. 56(c)(1)-(2)","#st-nd-unique"],["Jury demand within 14 days, and you get six jurors unless you ask for nine","North Dakota · land mine","Serve a written jury demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue, and file it; otherwise the jury is waived. A civil jury has six members unless a party specifically demands nine within the same 14 days. Each side gets only four peremptory challenges regardless of the number of parties. N.D.R.Civ.P. 38(b)-(c), (e), 47(c)(1), 48(b)","#st-nd-unique"],["Medical negligence: expert affidavit within three months of commencement","North Dakota · land mine","An action alleging professional negligence by a physician, nurse, hospital, licensed care facility, or other health care organization must be dismissed without prejudice on motion unless the plaintiff serves an affidavit with an admissible expert opinion supporting a prima facie case within three months after commencement. An extension requires good cause and a request made before the three months run. Obvious-occurr","#st-nd-unique"],["Plead frivolousness in the answer if you want mandatory fees","North Dakota · land mine","The court must award the prevailing party costs and attorney’s fees for a frivolous claim, but only if the prevailing party alleged the claim’s frivolous nature in its responsive pleading. Leaving it out of the answer forfeits the mandatory award. N.D.C.C. § 28-26-01(2)","#st-nd-unique"],["Pro hac vice: 45-day filing window and a five-case cap","North Dakota · land mine","The motion and affidavit must be filed no later than 45 days after service of the pleading, motion, or paper, with copies and the fee sent to the State Board of Law Examiners at the same time. More than five pro hac vice appearances within 12 months is presumed excessive. The North Dakota associate must appear with you at every appearance unless excused. Admission to Practice R. 3(A)(1)-(4)","#st-nd-unique"],["Number every paragraph and cite the record by index number","North Dakota · land mine","Every electronically filed trial-court document prepared by the parties must number its paragraphs, and references must be to paragraph numbers, not pages. On appeal, record citations must use the register-of-actions index format, for example (R156:12), and “Id.” may not be used for record citations. Briefs that do not comply will not be filed. N.D.R.Ct. 3.5(b)(2); N.D.R.App.P. 30, 32(a)(7), (c)","#st-nd-unique"],["Post-trial motions run from notice of entry, and some cannot be extended","North Dakota · land mine","Renewed JMOL, amended findings, and motions to alter or amend are due 28 days after notice of entry of judgment, and the court cannot extend those times. A new-trial motion is due within 60 days (6 months for newly discovered evidence). Costs require a verified statement within 30 days after the order for judgment, and an attorney’s-fee motion is due 21 days after notice of entry. N.D.R.Civ.P. 6(b)(2), 50(b), 52(b), ","#st-nd-unique"],["Peremptory demand for change of judge: 10 days, and lost once the judge rules","North Dakota · judges","Any party to a civil or criminal action in district court may remove the assigned judge without cause by filing a written demand with the clerk. The party signs personally, or the attorney signs with permission and certifies that a copy was mailed to the client. The demand must state it is in good faith and not for delay, describe the action, name the judge, and certify that the judge has not ruled on any matter in w","#st-nd-unique"],["Recusal for cause: Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11","North Dakota · judges","A judge must disqualify whenever impartiality might reasonably be questioned, including personal bias, personal knowledge of disputed facts, family relationships to the third degree with a party or lawyer, economic interest, prior public statements committing the judge to a result, and prior work on the matter as lawyer, government official, witness, or judge in another court. Except for bias, the parties and lawyers","#st-nd-unique"],["Post-judgment motions stay with the trial judge","North Dakota · judges","A post-judgment motion or proceeding must be heard by the judge who heard the underlying matter, unless that judge cannot act, a change of judge is demanded under § 29-15-21(3) (for example in support or custody modifications), or another listed exception applies. N.D.R.Ct. 3.3","#st-nd-unique"],["No state-specific speech protection shown in the collected texts","North Dakota · speech","The collected statutes and rules contain no anti-SLAPP law, no statutory actual-malice rule, and no special defamation pleading rule. The North Dakota Constitution and case law were not collected, so whether its speech guarantee is read more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked. N.D. Const. (not collected)","#st-nd-unique"],["Remote (“reliable electronic means”) proceedings need a timely motion","North Dakota · unique rules","In civil actions, a court may hold a hearing, conference, or other proceeding or take testimony by reliable electronic means on its own motion or a party’s. A party must first contact the others about agreement. The motion must be served at least 7 days before the proceeding, and any response at least 4 days before. A trial by remote means requires the parties’ consent and court approval. N.D.R.Ct. 11.10(b)-(c) (effe","#st-nd-unique"],["Continuances","North Dakota · unique rules","A party is entitled to a continuance only for the duration of a trial or hearing in which its attorney is actually engaged. Otherwise a continuance requires good cause shown promptly. A motion filed within 21 days of the trial or hearing must state whether the other side agrees, and stipulated continuances are not recognized without good cause. Requests to continue a trial must be made within 14 days after the notice","#st-nd-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope of rules","North Dakota · civil rules","The rules govern all civil actions and proceedings in district court, except as stated in Rule 81, and are to be construed for the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action. Unlike FRCP 1, they apply in state district court (North Dakota has one level of general trial court).","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","North Dakota · civil rules","There is one form of action, the civil action.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencing an action","North Dakota · civil rules","A civil action is commenced by service of a summons, not by filing. This is the key difference from FRCP 3: North Dakota allows “pocket” service, and the case may exist for some time before anything is filed (see Rule 5(d)(2)).","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 4. Persons subject to jurisdiction; process; service","North Dakota · civil rules","Rule 4(b) is North Dakota’s long-arm provision (jurisdiction over persons found or domiciled in the state, plus contact-based jurisdiction for listed acts such as transacting business or committing a tort causing in-state injury, to the limits of due process), and allows a stay or dismissal for inconvenient forum. The summons must state the time to answer and warn of default, and is served with the complaint (except ","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 5. Service and filing of pleadings and other documents","North Dakota · civil rules","Documents required to be filed must be served electronically under N.D.R.Ct. 3.5 to the attorney’s designated e-service address posted on the Supreme Court website; electronic service is complete on transmission but ineffective if the sender learns it did not arrive. All documents are filed electronically through Odyssey. The initiating pleading must be filed with proof of Rule 4 service, and the filer must serve not","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 6. Computing and extending time; time for motion papers","North Dakota · civil rules","Time is computed as under FRCP 6(a), with an electronic-filing day ending at midnight in the court’s time zone and “legal holiday” defined by N.D.C.C. §§ 1-03-01, 1-03-02, and 1-03-02.1. The court may extend time for good cause, but not the times under Rules 4(e)(7), 50(b) and (d), 52(b), 59(i) and (j), and 60(b). If an evidentiary hearing is requested, the motion and notice must be served at least 21 days before the","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings allowed; form of motions","North Dakota · civil rules","Allowed pleadings are a complaint, answer, answer to a counterclaim or crossclaim, third-party complaint and answer, and a reply only if the court orders one. A motion must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state the grounds with particularity, and state the relief sought.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","North Dakota · civil rules","Notice pleading: a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for relief. Denials, general denials, and lack-of-knowledge responses work as in FRCP 8; an allegation other than damages is admitted if not denied. The list of affirmative defenses that must be pleaded tracks the pre-2010 federal list (it still includes “discharge in bankruptcy”).","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","North Dakota · civil rules","Fraud or mistake must be pleaded with particularity; malice, intent, and knowledge may be alleged generally. Denial of a condition precedent must be particular. Special damages must be specifically stated. Rule 9(h) allows an unknown party to be designated by any name and amended later (a North Dakota addition).","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","North Dakota · civil rules","The caption must give the court’s name and the county, the parties, and the Rule 7(a) designation. Claims go in numbered paragraphs. If the State of North Dakota is a real party in interest but not named, later pleadings must name the State in the title until it files a notice that it is no longer a real party in interest. A document filed with a pleading is part of it for all purposes.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing; representations to court; sanctions","North Dakota · civil rules","Every paper must be signed and must give the signer’s address, e-mail address for electronic service, telephone number, and (for an attorney) State Board of Law Examiners ID number. Notarization is generally not required, and an unsworn declaration may replace an affidavit. The certification and sanctions provisions track FRCP 11, including the 21-day safe harbor; the respondent to a sanctions motion has 10 days afte","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections","North Dakota · civil rules","Answer due 21 days after service of the summons and complaint (21 days for a counterclaim or crossclaim answer; 21 days after an order to reply). A denied or postponed Rule 12 motion resets the answer to 14 days after notice of the court’s action; a more definite statement must be served within 14 days after the order. The seven Rule 12(b) defenses, waiver rules, and the conversion of a 12(b)(6) or 12(c) motion to su","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and crossclaim","North Dakota · civil rules","Compulsory counterclaims (same transaction or occurrence) must be pleaded, as in FRCP 13. In a contract action for money only, if the answer does not deny the claim but asserts a smaller counterclaim, the plaintiff may take default judgment for the excess (Rule 13(c)(2), a North Dakota addition). The rules do not expand counterclaims against the State.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","North Dakota · civil rules","A defending party may implead a nonparty who may be liable to it, without leave if the third-party summons and complaint are served within 14 days after serving its original answer, and otherwise by motion. A plaintiff facing a counterclaim may also bring in a third party. Admiralty provisions of the federal rule are omitted.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","North Dakota · civil rules","A party may amend once as a matter of course before being served with a responsive pleading, or within 21 days after serving a pleading to which no response is allowed if the case is not on the trial calendar. This differs from FRCP 15(a)(1), which allows amendment within 21 days after service of a responsive pleading or Rule 12 motion. Otherwise amendment requires written consent or leave, freely given. Response to ","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 16. Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","North Dakota · civil rules","The court must hold a scheduling conference, within 60 days of the triggering event, if: more than six months pass after filing without final disposition or a dispositive motion; the case was served more than six months before filing and 90 days have passed since filing; a Rule 40(e) notice draws a request to keep the case open; or any party asks in writing. Conferences may be by phone or electronic means. Sanctions ","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 17. Plaintiff and defendant; capacity","North Dakota · civil rules","Actions must be brought by the real party in interest, with time to ratify, join, or substitute before dismissal. Minors and incompetent persons sue through a representative, next friend, or guardian ad litem. Capacity of other persons is set by statute (no FRCP 17(b) choice-of-law rule).","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims and remedies","North Dakota · civil rules","A party may join as many claims as it has. A liability or indemnity insurer may not be joined in a tort case unless directly liable by law or contract to the injured person (a North Dakota addition).","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 19. Required joinder of parties","North Dakota · civil rules","Tracks FRCP 19: persons needed for complete relief or with interests at risk must be joined if feasible; otherwise the court weighs the listed factors in equity and good conscience. A joined party whose joinder makes venue improper must be dismissed if it objects.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","North Dakota · civil rules","Plaintiffs or defendants may be joined if the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact. The court may order separate trials to prevent prejudice.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","North Dakota · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may add or drop parties or sever claims at any time on just terms.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","North Dakota · civil rules","A plaintiff exposed to multiple liability may join claimants and require them to interplead; a defendant may do so by crossclaim or counterclaim.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","North Dakota · civil rules","North Dakota uses the Uniform Class Actions Rule, not FRCP 23. A class action requires numerosity (or impracticable joinder) and a common question; the court then decides whether a class action is fair and efficient under 13 listed factors and whether the representatives (and counsel, and their finances) are adequate. Orders certifying, refusing to certify, or amending certification are appealable, but an appeal does","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","North Dakota · civil rules","Intervention of right and permissive intervention track FRCP 24, on timely motion with a pleading attached. A party challenging the constitutionality of a legislative act affecting the public interest, when the State is not a party, must notify the Attorney General, who must be allowed to intervene.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","North Dakota · civil rules","After a party’s death, a motion to substitute must be made within 90 days after service of a statement noting the death, or the action may be dismissed. The motion is served on parties under Rule 5 and on nonparties under Rule 4. Incompetency, transfer of interest, and public-officer succession track FRCP 25.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 26. General provisions governing discovery","North Dakota · civil rules","North Dakota has no FRCP 26(a) initial, expert-report, or pretrial disclosures. Scope is any nonprivileged matter relevant to any party’s claim or defense (broader subject-matter discovery for good cause), and information need not be admissible if reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence, a standard the federal rule dropped in 2015. Proportionality appears as a mandatory court limit. “Electronically store","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions before action or pending appeal","North Dakota · civil rules","A verified petition to perpetuate testimony is filed in the county where an expected opposing party resides, with at least 21 days’ notice before the hearing. Depositions pending appeal require a motion in the trial court.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","North Dakota · civil rules","Depositions in the United States are taken before an officer authorized by North Dakota, federal, or local law, or a court-appointed person; abroad, under treaty, letter of request, notice, or commission. A party’s relative, employee, or attorney (or someone financially interested) may not serve as officer.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations about discovery procedure","North Dakota · civil rules","Parties may stipulate in writing to deposition logistics and other discovery procedures, but an extension that would interfere with the time set for completing discovery, a motion hearing, or trial needs court approval.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions by oral examination","North Dakota · civil rules","The plaintiff needs leave to take a deposition less than 30 days after serving a defendant, unless the defendant has sought discovery or a special notice says the deponent is about to leave the state. Unlike FRCP 30, there is no 10-deposition limit and no 7-hour limit in the text. Testimony may be recorded by audio, audiovisual, or stenographic means; remote depositions need a stipulation or order. Rule 30(b)(6) orga","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 30.1. Uniform audio-visual deposition rule","North Dakota · civil rules","Repealed.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions by written questions","North Dakota · civil rules","Cross-questions are due 14 days after service of the notice and direct questions; redirect 7 days after cross; recross 7 days after redirect. Leave is needed only if the deponent is in prison.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 32. Using depositions in court proceedings","North Dakota · civil rules","Depositions may be used against a party who was present, represented, or had notice. A witness’s deposition may be used for any purpose if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles from the place of hearing or trial or outside the state, unable to attend, or cannot be subpoenaed, or in exceptional circumstances. A deposition may not be used against a party who had less than 14 days’ notice and promptly moved for a pro","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories to parties","North Dakota · civil rules","Limit of 50 interrogatories per party served on any other party (FRCP allows 25), with logically subsumed subparts not counted separately. Answers and objections are due in 30 days, but a defendant need not respond until 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Each answer must restate the interrogatory. Unstated objections are waived absent good cause. An interrogatory served by one party is considered se","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 34. Producing documents, ESI, and tangible things; entry on land","North Dakota · civil rules","Responses are due within 30 days after service, but a defendant need not respond until 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Objections must state whether responsive materials are being withheld. ESI is produced in the requested form, the form ordinarily maintained, or a reasonably usable form. Nonparties are reached by Rule 45 subpoena.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examination","North Dakota · civil rules","On motion for good cause, the court may order a party whose condition is in controversy to be examined by a suitably licensed or certified examiner. Report exchange and privilege waiver track FRCP 35.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","North Dakota · civil rules","A matter is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 30 days, but a defendant need not respond until 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Admitted matters are conclusively established unless the court allows withdrawal or amendment.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to make or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","North Dakota · civil rules","A motion to compel must certify a good-faith attempt to confer. Expense-shifting and sanction tiers track FRCP 37, but North Dakota has no counterparts to FRCP 37(c)(1) (failure to disclose) because there are no initial disclosures. Expenses and fees may not be awarded against the State except as allowed by statute. Rule 37(f) protects ESI lost through routine, good-faith operation of a system, absent exceptional cir","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 38. Right to a jury trial; demand","North Dakota · civil rules","A jury demand must be served no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue and filed under Rule 5(d). The jury is six unless a party specifically demands nine within the same time (FRCP 38 has no such election). Failure to serve and file a proper demand waives the jury.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","North Dakota · civil rules","A demanded jury trial proceeds unless the parties stipulate otherwise or the court finds no jury right. The court may order a jury on motion even without a demand, use an advisory jury, or, with consent, a binding jury.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 39.1. Change in location; change of venue","North Dakota · civil rules","The court may move a hearing or trial to another county whose seat is less than 10 miles from the venue county’s seat, for impartiality, or for convenience and efficiency; any party may object within 14 days after notice of the new location, and the original venue then controls unless grounds for a venue change exist. Venue may be changed for improper county, impartiality, or convenience of witnesses and the ends of ","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 40. Assignment of cases for trial","North Dakota · civil rules","District courts are in continuous session in each county. A request to continue a trial must be made within 14 days after receiving the notice of trial (waivable for unavoidable circumstances). The court may dismiss without prejudice a filed action with no prosecution for more than one year.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","North Dakota · civil rules","A plaintiff may dismiss without order by notice before an answer or summary-judgment motion is served (not when a provisional remedy has been allowed), or by stipulation; a second notice dismissal of the same claim is an adjudication on the merits. Other voluntary dismissals require a court order. Involuntary dismissals generally operate as adjudications on the merits. The court may order costs of a previously dismis","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials","North Dakota · civil rules","Actions with common questions may be consolidated or jointly heard; separate trials may be ordered for convenience or to avoid prejudice, preserving any jury right.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 43. Evidence","North Dakota · civil rules","Testimony is taken in open court, but for good cause or by agreement the court may allow testimony by contemporaneous transmission from another location; notice is required. Motions may be heard on declarations. The court must provide an interpreter for a party, witness, or other significant participant with limited English proficiency or who is deaf.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 44. Proving an official record","North Dakota · civil rules","Domestic and foreign official records are proved by official publication or attested copy with the required certification; lack of a record by a diligent-search statement.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 44.1. Determining foreign law","North Dakota · civil rules","A party raising foreign law must give notice in a pleading or writing; the court may consider any relevant source, and its determination is a ruling on law.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","North Dakota · civil rules","The clerk issues subpoenas signed but blank, and a North Dakota-licensed attorney may issue one. Subpoenas are served under Rule 4(d) anywhere in the state, with one day’s fees and mileage tendered (or the witness need not obey). Before serving a subpoena for a pretrial deposition or pretrial production, a notice of deposition or notice of demand for production must be served on every party. A records subpoena must c","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 46. Objecting to a ruling or order","North Dakota · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary; a party need only state the action wanted or the objection and grounds.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 47. Jurors","North Dakota · civil rules","The court calls only as many prospective jurors as needed to seat the jury plus the available peremptory challenges, unless otherwise stipulated and approved. The parties have the right to examine jurors themselves. Each side gets four peremptory challenges regardless of jury size or number of parties (more if co-parties are adverse), exercised alternately starting with the plaintiff, plus one more if alternates are ","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 48. Number of jurors; verdict","North Dakota · civil rules","Civil juries have six jurors unless a party demands nine under Rule 38. Parties may stipulate to fewer than nine or to a stated majority verdict. Polling is on request.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 49. Special verdict; general verdict and questions","North Dakota · civil rules","Tracks FRCP 49: special verdicts, general verdicts with written questions, and handling of inconsistencies.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 50. Judgment as a matter of law; new trial; conditional ruling","North Dakota · civil rules","A JMOL motion may be made any time before submission to the jury. A renewed motion must be served and filed no later than 28 days after notice of entry of judgment (FRCP 50(b) runs from entry of judgment), or 28 days after discharge if a jury issue was undecided. A new-trial motion by a party against whom JMOL is entered is due 28 days after notice of entry. These times cannot be extended (Rule 6(b)(2)).","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 51. Instructions to jury","North Dakota · civil rules","Requests are filed at the close of evidence or earlier as ordered, and may cite North Dakota pattern instructions by number. The court must disclose proposed instructions and allow objections on the record outside the jury’s hearing before instructing and before final arguments. Final instructions must be in writing unless the parties agree otherwise. Unpreserved error is reviewable only as plain error. Preliminary i","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings and conclusions in a nonjury proceeding","North Dakota · civil rules","In bench trials the court must find facts specially and state conclusions separately; findings are reviewed for clear error. No findings are required on Rule 12 or 56 motions. A motion to amend or add findings is due no later than 28 days after notice of entry of judgment and cannot be extended. Judgment on partial findings is allowed.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters","North Dakota · civil rules","A master may be appointed by consent, for bench-trial issues on exceptional conditions or complex accounting, or for pretrial and post-trial matters a judge cannot timely address. The disqualification cross-reference is to Canon 3(E) of the Code of Judicial Conduct. Objections to a master’s order, report, or recommendations are due 21 days after service. In eminent-domain cases where a jury is waived, the court may a","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgment; costs","North Dakota · civil rules","Rule 54(b) partial final judgment requires an express determination of no just reason for delay, as in FRCP 54(b). Costs and disbursements are allowed by statute: the party awarded costs must serve and file a detailed, verified statement within 30 days after entry of the order for judgment; objections are due 14 days after service; a hearing may be requested within 7 days of filing objections. A motion for attorney’s","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 55. Default; default judgment","North Dakota · civil rules","There is no separate clerk’s entry of default; the court may direct entry of default judgment. For a sum certain, judgment may be entered on a declaration of the amount due and the written instrument; otherwise the court takes proof. A party who has appeared must be served with the motion for default judgment and notice complying with N.D.R.Ct. 3.2(a). After publication or out-of-state service, the court may require ","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","North Dakota · civil rules","A claimant may move after 21 days from commencement or after the opponent moves; a defending party may move at any time. The motion and supporting documents must be filed at least 90 days before trial and 45 days before the hearing unless otherwise ordered. The opposing party has 30 days after service of the brief to serve and file an answer brief; the reply is due 14 days later. Principal and answer briefs are limit","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments","North Dakota · civil rules","Procedure for declaratory judgments under N.D.C.C. ch. 32-23; jury rights under Rules 38 and 39; another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 58. Entry and notice of entry of judgment","North Dakota · civil rules","After an order for judgment is filed, the prevailing party submits a form of judgment, which becomes effective when the clerk signs, files, and enters it; if the prevailing party does not submit one within 30 days, any party may. The prevailing party must serve notice of entry of judgment (with a copy of the judgment) within 14 days after entry, and file the notice. Service of notice of entry is what starts most post","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 59. New trial; amending a judgment","North Dakota · civil rules","Grounds are listed by statute-style categories (irregularity, juror misconduct, surprise, newly discovered evidence, excessive damages from passion or prejudice with remittitur, insufficient evidence, legal error, inability to obtain a transcript). A new-trial motion must be served and filed within six months after notice of entry of judgment if based on newly discovered evidence, and within 60 days on any other grou","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from a judgment or order","North Dakota · civil rules","Amended effective July 1, 2026. Clerical mistakes and oversights may be corrected at any time, but only with the Supreme Court’s leave while an appeal is docketed and pending. Rule 60(b) grounds track the federal rule; a motion must be made within a reasonable time, and for mistake, newly discovered evidence, or fraud no more than one year after notice of entry of the judgment or order if the opposing party appeared,","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","North Dakota · civil rules","Errors not affecting substantial rights are disregarded.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","North Dakota · civil rules","Execution is automatically stayed for 30 days after filing of notice of entry of judgment or entry of a default judgment, unless ordered otherwise; injunctions, receiverships, and accountings are not stayed. A party may obtain a stay at any time by bond or other security approved by the court. The State and its agencies need no bond. Judgments for delivery of property, conveyances, sale or possession of real property","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 63. Judge’s inability to proceed","North Dakota · civil rules","A successor judge may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record and must, on request in a hearing or bench trial, recall material disputed witnesses who are available.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 64. Seizing property","North Dakota · civil rules","All state-law remedies for seizing property to secure a potential judgment are available at the start of and throughout the action.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions","North Dakota · civil rules","Rewritten North Dakota rule. A TRO motion must be submitted with a proposed complaint and filed by the next court business day or the TRO terminates. The movant must declare its notice efforts. The preliminary-injunction hearing must be set no less than 21 and no more than 28 days after the TRO, and the TRO expires at the end of the 28th day unless extended (up to 14 more days after the hearing if needed to decide). ","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers","North Dakota · civil rules","Administration of receiverships follows statute or local rule. A foreign receiver may sue but is subordinate to local creditors. Dismissal requires a court order.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit into court","North Dakota · civil rules","With leave and notice, a party may deposit money or a thing in dispute; funds are handled under N.D.C.C. ch. 32-11 and may be placed in insured interest-bearing accounts.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of settlement or confession of judgment; tender","North Dakota · civil rules","Either party (not only a defending party, as in FRCP 68) may serve an offer of settlement at least 14 days before trial; acceptance is due within 14 days. If the offeree’s final judgment is not more favorable, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer. After liability is decided, an offer may be served at least 7 days before the damages hearing. In money actions, a party may instead tender the full amount owed ","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution","North Dakota · civil rules","Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution under state statutes; post-judgment discovery is available under the rules.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 70. Enforcing a judgment for a specific act","North Dakota · civil rules","The court may appoint someone to perform the act at the disobedient party’s expense, vest title by judgment, issue writs of attachment, sequestration, execution, or assistance, or hold the party in contempt.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 71. Process for and against nonparties","North Dakota · civil rules","Orders benefiting or binding nonparties are enforced as against parties.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 77. District courts and clerks","North Dakota · civil rules","District courts are always open for filing and process. Trials on the merits are in open court. The clerk may issue process and act on matters not requiring a judge.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 80. Transcript as evidence","North Dakota · civil rules","Admissible prior testimony may be proved by a certified transcript.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 81. Applicability of the rules in general","North Dakota · civil rules","Special statutory proceedings (Table A lists many) are excluded to the extent inconsistent with the rules. The rules do not supersede statutes on appeals to the district court except where not in conflict. When a statute says to act as in a civil action, the rules apply.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 82. [Reserved]","North Dakota · civil rules","No text.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 83. Practice when procedure is not specified","North Dakota · civil rules","Judicial districts may adopt local rules not inconsistent with statewide rules, referred to the Supreme Court for review. Otherwise the district court may regulate its practice consistently with rules and statutes.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 84. Appendix of forms","North Dakota · civil rules","The appendix forms suffice under the rules.","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 85. Title and citation","North Dakota · civil rules","Cited as “N.D.R.Civ.P.”","#st-nd-rules"],["Rule 86. Effective date; statutes superseded","North Dakota · civil rules","Amendments take effect as ordered and apply to pending cases unless infeasible or unjust. Conflicting statutes are superseded and listed in the rule’s explanatory note and in Table B.","#st-nd-rules"],["Appeals in North Dakota: one appellate court in practice, a 60-day clock that runs from service of notice of entry","North Dakota · appeals","Civil appeals go directly from the district court to the North Dakota Supreme Court. The notice of appeal is filed with the clerk of the Supreme Court (not the district court) within 60 days from service of notice of entry of the judgment or order. The prevailing party must serve notice of entry within 14 days after entry, but actual knowledge shown by your own affirmative action can start the clock without it.\nOnly ","#st-nd-appeals"],["Supreme Court is the appellate court","North Dakota · appeals · Which court, and what can be appealed","An appeal allowed by law as of right from a district court to the Supreme Court is taken by filing a notice of appeal with the clerk of the Supreme Court. The clerk of the Supreme Court then files the notice in the district court through Odyssey. A notice mistakenly filed in district court is forwarded and treated as filed on the date the district court clerk received it. N.D.R.App.P. 3(a)(1), (d)(2), 4(f); N.D.C.C. ","#st-nd-appeals"],["Temporary Court of Appeals: assigned cases only","North Dakota · appeals · Which court, and what can be appealed","Panels of three (retired justices, active or retired district judges, lawyers, or surrogate judges) hear only cases the Supreme Court assigns. Everything is still filed with the clerk of the Supreme Court; there are no separate filings with the Court of Appeals. Categories usually assigned include divorce property, support, and parenting appeals; misdemeanor convictions; cases from municipal court, small claims court","#st-nd-appeals"],["Appealable orders","North Dakota · appeals · Which court, and what can be appealed","By statute, a party may appeal a final judgment and these orders: an order affecting a substantial right that in effect determines the action and prevents a judgment; a final order affecting a substantial right in a special proceeding or on summary application after judgment; an order granting, refusing, continuing, or modifying a provisional remedy or injunction; an order granting or refusing a new trial or sustaini","#st-nd-appeals"],["Partial judgments: Rule 54(b) and an extra brief section","North Dakota · appeals · Which court, and what can be appealed","With multiple claims or parties, an order resolving fewer than all is not final unless the district court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay. The appellant’s brief must address whether the Rule 54(b) certification was appropriate, and that argument may run up to 5 pages beyond the normal page limit. N.D.R.Civ.P. 54(b); N.D.R.App.P. 28(b)(7)(C), 32(a)(8)(B)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Class certification orders are appealable","North Dakota · appeals · Which court, and what can be appealed","An order certifying, refusing to certify, or amending a class certification is appealable, but the appeal does not stay district court proceedings unless the district court or Supreme Court orders. N.D.R.Civ.P. 23(d)(3), (e)(4), (t)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Supervisory writs and certified questions","North Dakota · appeals · Which court, and what can be appealed","Other interlocutory review is by petition for a writ (including supervision) filed with the clerk of the Supreme Court and served on all parties, with a copy to the district judge if supervision is sought. No response is allowed unless the court asks. A district court may also certify a determinative question of law with no controlling precedent, but only after ruling on it itself; the Supreme Court may refuse a friv","#st-nd-appeals"],["60 days from service of notice of entry","North Dakota · appeals · Notice of appeal and timing","In a civil case the notice of appeal must be filed with the clerk of the Supreme Court within 60 days from service of notice of entry of the judgment or order. The notice must name the appellant(s), the judgment or order (or part) appealed, and the court. It must be served on all other counsel and self-represented parties, with proof of service filed with the notice. Failure to take any other step does not void the a","#st-nd-appeals"],["Notice of entry triggers the clock, or actual knowledge","North Dakota · appeals · Notice of appeal and timing","The prevailing party must serve notice of entry (with a copy of the judgment) within 14 days after entry and file it. Service of notice of entry is not needed to start the time for an appeal or post-judgment motion if the record clearly shows the appealing party’s actual knowledge of entry through its own affirmative action. N.D.R.Civ.P. 58(b)(2)-(4)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Extensions: up to 30 days, asked for within 30 days","North Dakota · appeals · Notice of appeal and timing","The Supreme Court may extend the time if a party moves no later than 30 days after the deadline and shows excusable neglect or good cause. No extension may exceed 30 days after the prescribed time. Otherwise the court cannot extend the time to appeal. N.D.R.App.P. 4(a)(4), 26(b)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Tolling motions","North Dakota · appeals · Notice of appeal and timing","A timely motion for judgment under Rule 50(b), to amend findings under Rule 52(b), for attorney’s fees under Rule 54, to alter or amend or for new trial under Rule 59, or for Rule 60 relief served and filed within 28 days after notice of entry, restarts the full appeal time for all parties from service of notice of entry of the order deciding the last such motion. If such a motion is filed after the notice of appeal,","#st-nd-appeals"],["Cross-appeals and multiple appeals","North Dakota · appeals · Notice of appeal and timing","If one party timely appeals, any other party may file a notice of appeal within 14 days after the first notice was filed, or within the regular time, whichever is later. The party who files first (or the plaintiff below, if filed the same day) is the appellant. N.D.R.App.P. 1(c), 4(a)(2)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Docket fee","North Dakota · appeals · Notice of appeal and timing","The fee is paid through the electronic filing system with the notice; if payment fails, the appeal may be dismissed. Filers exempt from e-filing must pay by check or money order within 14 days of receipt or the notice is void. Where several parties file separate notices, the first to file pays. The fee amount is set by N.D.C.C. § 27-03-05, which was not collected. N.D.R.App.P. 12(b); N.D.C.C. § 27-02.1-08","#st-nd-appeals"],["Post-judgment mediation (family and probate cases)","North Dakota · appeals · Notice of appeal and timing","In divorce property or support, parenting, child support, grandparent visitation, probate code, and trust code cases, a party may request mediation within 60 days after service of notice of entry or 7 days after service of the notice of appeal. A request does not extend the appeal deadline or automatically toll briefing; transcript ordering is extended to 21 days after the notice of appeal. N.D.R.App.P. 5(d)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Order the transcript with the notice of appeal","North Dakota · appeals · Record and transcript","If there was an evidentiary hearing, the appellant must order a complete transcript (unless all affected parties stipulate to portions) and file the order, and any stipulation, with the clerk of the Supreme Court with the notice of appeal. Voir dire is not transcribed unless requested. Advance payment is due within 14 days of the written estimate, or preparation is suspended. N.D.R.App.P. 10(b)(1), (c)(3)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Transcript and record timing","North Dakota · appeals · Record and transcript","The transcript is due 60 days after the order is transmitted to district court staff; the district court can extend to no more than 90 days, and further extensions come from the Supreme Court. The district court clerk forwards the record (excluding the transcript) 25 to 30 days after the notice of appeal. A statement of the evidence (when no transcript is available) must be settled by the district court and filed wit","#st-nd-appeals"],["Record citation format is mandatory","North Dakota · appeals · Record and transcript","Cite the record by register-of-actions index number and page, for example (R156:12), or (R156:12:¶3) for a paragraph. Recordings are cited by time code (HH:MM:SS). “Id.” may not be used for record citations. Writ-petition exhibits use (E6:12). N.D.R.App.P. 21(a)(3), 30","#st-nd-appeals"],["Briefing schedule","North Dakota · appeals · Briefs","Appellant’s brief: 40 days after the transcript is filed, or 40 days after the notice of appeal if no transcript is ordered. Appellee’s brief: 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief. Reply: 14 days after service of the appellee’s brief (but at least 5 days before argument if argument is less than 14 days away). An appellant who misses the deadline risks dismissal; an appellee who does not file a brief will no","#st-nd-appeals"],["Page limits, not word limits","North Dakota · appeals · Briefs","A principal brief may not exceed 38 pages and a reply 12 pages, excluding any addendum, with footnotes counted. Briefs use 12-point or larger type, no more than 16 characters per inch, double spacing (footnotes too), 1.5-inch left margin, and numbered paragraphs; page numbering starts with 1 on the cover. A certificate of compliance must state the page count. Noncompliant documents will not be filed. N.D.R.App.P. 32(","#st-nd-appeals"],["Required contents: standard of review and preservation for each issue","North Dakota · appeals · Briefs","For each issue, the argument must state the standard of review and cite the record showing the issue was preserved (or the grounds for reviewing an unpreserved issue). Tables must reference paragraph numbers. Incorporation by reference of briefs filed elsewhere is barred. To get oral argument, put “ORAL ARGUMENT REQUESTED” on the cover of the principal or reply brief. N.D.R.App.P. 28(b), (f)(2), (h)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Cross-appeal briefing","North Dakota · appeals · Briefs","The appellee and cross-appellant files one combined brief when the appellee’s brief is due. The appellant answers the cross-appeal in the reply brief. The cross-appellant may file a reply limited to the cross-appeal within 14 days after service of the appellant’s reply. N.D.R.App.P. 28(i)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Amicus briefs","North Dakota · appeals · Briefs","Only with leave or at the court’s request, limited to issues the parties raised, no more than half a principal brief’s length, and due when the supported party’s principal brief is due. Rehearing-stage amicus briefs are limited to 10 pages and due 7 days after the petition. N.D.R.App.P. 29","#st-nd-appeals"],["Motions","North Dakota · appeals · Briefs","Responses are due 14 days after service of a motion; procedural motions may be decided without waiting. A motion to dismiss on the ground the appeal is not authorized by law tolls the briefing time. N.D.R.App.P. 27(b), (c), (f)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Oral argument","North Dakota · appeals · Argument, decision, and further review","Argument is generally scheduled unless no party requested it, a brief was late, the transcript needed for a fact challenge was not provided, the parties waived it, or the court finds it unnecessary. The appellant gets 30 minutes (up to 10 reserved for rebuttal) and the appellee 20. A party that did not request argument must file a notice of intent to participate within 5 days of the argument notice. N.D.R.App.P. 34(a","#st-nd-appeals"],["Summary affirmance","North Dakota · appeals · Argument, decision, and further review","The court may summarily affirm by citing Rule 35.1(a) on listed grounds, including a frivolous appeal or an appellant’s brief that does not meet minimum requirements. N.D.R.App.P. 35.1","#st-nd-appeals"],["Rehearing: 14 days, 10 pages","North Dakota · appeals · Argument, decision, and further review","A petition for rehearing may be filed within 14 days after entry of judgment. It must identify each point overlooked or misapprehended. No answer is allowed unless requested, and rehearing ordinarily is not granted without such a request. Limit 10 pages. The mandate issues 21 days after entry of judgment, or 7 days after denial of a timely rehearing petition. N.D.R.App.P. 40, 41(b)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Review of a Court of Appeals decision","North Dakota · appeals · Argument, decision, and further review","Petition the Supreme Court within 14 days after the Court of Appeals judgment or order is filed, or within 14 days after it disposes of a rehearing petition (rehearing is not required first). A supporting brief of no more than 10 pages must be filed with the petition. Review is discretionary and needs a majority of the justices; listed factors include an undecided question of substance, conflict with Supreme Court or","#st-nd-appeals"],["Frivolous appeals and costs","North Dakota · appeals · Argument, decision, and further review","For a frivolous or dilatory appeal, the court may award just damages and single or double costs, including reasonable attorney’s fees. Costs normally follow the result and include the record, the transcript, supersedeas bond premiums, and the notice-of-appeal fee, taxed in the district court. N.D.R.App.P. 38, 39","#st-nd-appeals"],["30-day automatic stay, then bond","North Dakota · appeals · Stays and bonds","Execution is automatically stayed for 30 days after filing of notice of entry of judgment (not for injunctions, receiverships, or accountings). After that, a stay requires a bond or other security approved by the court. Move first in the district court; a motion in the Supreme Court must show that was impracticable or was denied. The State needs no bond. N.D.R.Civ.P. 62(a)-(c), (e); N.D.R.App.P. 8(a)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Cost bond","North Dakota · appeals · Stays and bonds","The district court may require a bond or other security for costs on appeal in civil cases. N.D.R.App.P. 7(a)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Mandatory electronic filing; 11:59 p.m. Bismarck time","North Dakota · appeals · E-filing and service","All documents are filed through the Supreme Court electronic filing system; self-represented litigants and prisoners are exempt. A compliant document submitted by 11:59 p.m. Bismarck time is filed that day. If a filing is rejected, time is tolled until the rejection notice, and a corrected document is timely if submitted and served within 3 days. Attorneys must accept electronic service; service is complete on transm","#st-nd-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (civil)","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from service of notice of entry of the judgment or order.\nFiled with the clerk of the Supreme Court. Actual knowledge of entry shown by the appellant’s own affirmative action can start the clock without service (N.D.R.Civ.P. 58(b)(4)). N.D.R.App.P. 4(a)(1)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Motion to extend appeal time","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from expiration of the 60-day period.\nExcusable neglect or good cause; extension cannot exceed 30 days past the original deadline. N.D.R.App.P. 4(a)(4)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Cross-appeal or other party’s notice of appeal","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or the regular time, if later). N.D.R.App.P. 4(a)(2)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Notice of entry of judgment by prevailing party","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment.\nMust include a copy of the judgment. N.D.R.Civ.P. 58(b)(2)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Renewed JMOL; motion to amend findings; motion to alter or amend","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from notice of entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended (N.D.R.Civ.P. 6(b)(2)). N.D.R.Civ.P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(j)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Motion for new trial (other than newly discovered evidence)","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from notice of entry of judgment.\nThe court may extend for good cause. Six months for newly discovered evidence. Rule 4(a)(3)(A) counts a new-trial motion filed within the time the civil rules allow, so a motion filed within this period appears to restart the appeal time; no case law on this point was researched. N.D.R.Civ.P. 59(c)(2)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Rule 60 motion that tolls appeal time","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from notice of entry of judgment. N.D.R.App.P. 4(a)(3)(A)(vi)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Attorney’s fees motion","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from notice of entry of judgment.\nA timely fee motion is a tolling motion under N.D.R.App.P. 4(a)(3)(A)(iii). N.D.R.Civ.P. 54(e)(2)(A)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Transcript order","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","0 days from filed with the notice of appeal.\n21 days after the notice of appeal in cases assigned to post-judgment mediation (N.D.R.App.P. 5(d)(6)). N.D.R.App.P. 10(b)(1)(E)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Transcript advance payment","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from written cost estimate.\nPreparation is suspended if not paid. N.D.R.App.P. 10(c)(3)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Transcript filed","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from transmission of the transcript order to district court staff.\nDistrict court may extend to no more than 90 days total. N.D.R.App.P. 10(c)(1), (d)(2)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from filing of the transcript (or of the notice of appeal if no transcript).\n38-page limit. N.D.R.App.P. 31(a)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the appellant’s brief. N.D.R.App.P. 31(a)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Reply brief","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\nMust be filed at least 5 days before argument if argument is less than 14 days away; 12-page limit. N.D.R.App.P. 31(a)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Response to a motion","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the motion. N.D.R.App.P. 27(b)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Notice of intent to participate in oral argument","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","5 days from service of the notice of oral argument.\nOnly for a party that did not request argument. N.D.R.App.P. 34(a)(3)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment.\n10-page limit. N.D.R.App.P. 40(a)(1)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Mandate issues","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from entry of judgment (or 7 days after denial of rehearing). N.D.R.App.P. 41(b)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Petition for Supreme Court review of a Court of Appeals decision","North Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the Court of Appeals judgment or order (or disposition of rehearing).\nSupporting brief of no more than 10 pages filed with the petition. N.D. Sup. Ct. Admin. R. 27, § 13(a)","#st-nd-appeals"],["Nebraska Supreme Court","Nebraska · courts","Court of last resort. It hears direct appeals only in capital cases, life-imprisonment cases, and cases involving the constitutionality of a statute; other appeals go to the Court of Appeals unless the Supreme Court grants a petition to bypass or removes the case. It reviews Court of Appeals decisions by discretionary petition for further review. It writes the court rules (pleading, discovery, appellate practice) and","#st-ne"],["Nebraska Court of Appeals","Nebraska · courts","Six judges, one appointed from each Supreme Court judicial district, sitting in rotating panels of three; a panel majority decides the case. A notice of appeal is treated as going to the Court of Appeals unless it expressly asks for the Supreme Court and cites the statute that sends the case there (Neb. Rev. Stat. § 24-1101; Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-101(B)(1)(a)).","#st-ne"],["District Courts","Nebraska · courts","General original and appellate jurisdiction in civil and criminal matters, organized into 12 district court judicial districts (District 4, Douglas County, has 18 judges; District 3, Lancaster County, has 8). District courts also hear appeals from the county courts (Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 24-301.02, 24-302, 25-2728). Practice is governed by the Uniform District Court Rules, Neb. Ct. R. § 6-1501 ff., plus approved local r","#st-ne"],["County Courts (including Small Claims Court)","Nebraska · courts","Exclusive original jurisdiction over decedents’ estates, guardianships, and conservatorships, and concurrent jurisdiction with the district court over civil actions up to an amount set by the Supreme Court (the current figure is not in the collected texts). If the amount in controversy exceeds the limit, any party may have the case certified to district court. Small Claims Court handles claims up to $7,500 (since Jul","#st-ne"],["Statute: Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-21,241 to 25-21,246 (1994), narrow in scope","Nebraska · anti-SLAPP","Nebraska’s anti-SLAPP law covers only an “action involving public petition and participation”: a claim, cross-claim, or counterclaim for damages brought by a “public applicant or permittee” (someone who applied for or obtained a permit, zoning change, lease, license, certificate, or other permission from a government body, or someone affiliated with that person) that is materially related to the defendant’s efforts t","#st-ne-slapp"],["Procedure: an ordinary motion to dismiss or for summary judgment with a shifted burden; no deadline, no discovery stay","Nebraska · anti-SLAPP","A motion to dismiss for failure to state a cause of action must be granted once the movant shows the claim is an action involving public petition and participation, unless the responding party shows the claim has a substantial basis in law or is supported by a substantial argument to extend, modify, or reverse existing law. A summary-judgment motion works the same way, except the respondent must show a substantial ba","#st-ne-slapp"],["Heightened standard for the plaintiff’s damages","Nebraska · anti-SLAPP","In such an action, the plaintiff may recover damages only by proving by clear and convincing evidence that the communication was made knowing it was false or with reckless disregard of whether it was false, when truth or falsity is material to the claim. Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-21,244(1)","#st-ne-slapp"],["Fees and damages require a separate claim, not a motion","Nebraska · anti-SLAPP","There is no automatic fee award on a successful motion. The defendant must bring its own action, claim, cross-claim, or counterclaim. Costs and attorney’s fees are recoverable on showing the suit was commenced or continued without a substantial basis in fact and law and could not be supported by a substantial argument for changing the law. Other compensatory damages also require proof that the suit was brought to har","#st-ne-slapp"],["Appeal: no special right","Nebraska · anti-SLAPP","The statute gives no immediate appeal from denial of an anti-SLAPP motion. Denial of a motion to dismiss or for summary judgment is not among the listed final orders (the only listed summary-judgment denial is one based on sovereign or official immunity), so review would normally wait for final judgment. No case law on this point was researched. Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-21,245, 25-21,246, 25-1902(1)","#st-ne-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: motion by Nebraska counsel, $250 per case per court, oath after admission","Nebraska · out of state","A lawyer of good moral character admitted and practicing in another state, D.C., or a U.S. territory may be admitted in the court’s (or agency’s) discretion for a specific case. The Nebraska lawyer files the motion (Appendix B for courts) as soon as possible and no later than the date the applicant files any pleading or appears personally. A separate motion is needed in each case, but one motion covers cases consolid","#st-ne-oos"],["Local counsel must be a Nebraska resident who signs everything and attends everything","Nebraska · out of state","Unless exempt under Neb. Rev. Stat. § 7-103, the applicant must associate with a Nebraska-resident lawyer admitted in Nebraska, who accepts service for the case. That lawyer (or a designee) must sign the pro hac vice motion and every pleading, motion, and paper filed in the case, and must personally appear at all proceedings unless the court excuses it.\nReciprocity exemption: under § 7-103, lawyers from states whose ","#st-ne-oos"],["E-filing: pro hac vice counsel cannot file; Nebraska counsel files everything","Nebraska · out of state","E-filing, e-service, and e-notice are mandatory for all Nebraska attorneys in all trial and appellate courts, and every Nebraska attorney who files or appears must register with the court-authorized service provider (annual fee set by the provider). For a lawyer admitted pro hac vice, all electronic filings must be made exclusively by the associated Nebraska counsel. Pro hac vice lawyers do receive notice from the cl","#st-ne-oos"],["Limited-scope work: “Prepared By” ghostwriting and limited appearances","Nebraska · out of state","A Nebraska lawyer may draft pleadings, briefs, or other documents for a self-represented litigant, but the filing must show “Prepared By” with the lawyer’s name, business address, and bar number. The litigant signs as “pro se”; the lawyer does not sign, and this is not an appearance.\nWith the client’s written consent after consultation, a lawyer may enter a “Limited Appearance” that clearly defines the scope. Within ","#st-ne-oos"],["Temporary practice and in-house counsel","Nebraska · out of state","Nebraska’s multijurisdictional-practice rule tracks ABA Model Rule 5.5(c): temporary services in association with an actively participating Nebraska lawyer, services related to a proceeding where the lawyer is or expects to be authorized, related ADR, or work arising out of the home-state practice. Services to an employer are allowed only if the lawyer is registered as in-house counsel under Neb. Ct. R. §§ 3-1201 to ","#st-ne-oos"],["Discovery for an out-of-state case: $75 clerk-issued subpoena, but motions need a Nebraska lawyer","Nebraska · out of state","To take a deposition or get documents in Nebraska for a case pending elsewhere, submit to the clerk of the district court in the county where discovery will happen a request in the form of the rule’s appendix, with the foreign subpoena and a list of all counsel, and pay $75 per subpoena ($75 again for any reissue). Requesting the subpoena is not an appearance or unauthorized practice. A lawyer not admitted in Nebrask","#st-ne-oos"],["Admission without examination","Nebraska · out of state","Admission on motion is available to (A) UBE or NextGen UBE takers with Nebraska’s minimum score plus the MPRE, (B) non-UBE takers with an equivalent score, and (C) lawyers who passed any bar exam where licensed and actively practiced for 3 of the last 5 years (or were registered Nebraska in-house counsel). Under (A) and (B), bar exam and MPRE scores more than 5 years old are not accepted. A reduced-fee track exists f","#st-ne-oos"],["Governing code: Nebraska Rules of Professional Conduct, Neb. Ct. R. of Prof. Cond. §§ 3-501.0 to 3-508.5","Nebraska · ethics","Nebraska’s rules follow the ABA Model Rules numbering, with a “3-50” prefix (Model Rule 1.6 is § 3-501.6). The text internally cites rules by ABA number (for example, “Rule 1.7”). A pro hac vice applicant must affirm being subject to these rules. Neb. Ct. R. of Prof. Cond. § 3-501.0 ff.; Neb. Ct. R. § 3-122(D)","#st-ne-ethics"],["Confidentiality: broader “any crime” exception, but no ABA financial-fraud or conflict-check exceptions","Nebraska · ethics","A lawyer may reveal information to prevent the client from committing a crime (any crime, not limited to crimes causing financial injury) or to prevent reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm. The Nebraska rule has no counterparts to ABA Model Rule 1.6(b)(2)-(3) (fraud using the lawyer’s services, including rectifying loss) or 1.6(b)(7) (conflict checks when changing firms), and no ABA 1.6(c) duty to make","#st-ne-ethics"],["No screening for lateral hires","Nebraska · ethics","Conflicts are imputed across the firm except for personal-interest conflicts that pose no significant risk. Nebraska’s § 3-501.10 has no counterpart to ABA Model Rule 1.10(a)(2), which lets a firm screen a laterally hired lawyer to avoid imputing the lawyer’s former-client conflicts. Without client consent, a lateral’s conflict can disqualify the whole firm. Neb. Ct. R. of Prof. Cond. § 3-501.10(a), (c)","#st-ne-ethics"],["Fees: free itemized accounting on request; no contingent fees in divorce or criminal defense","Nebraska · ethics","On a reasonable and timely request, a lawyer must give the client a free accounting of fees and costs, itemizing hourly charges, costs, interest, and past-due balances, and describing hourly services and who performed them. This duty has no ABA counterpart. A contingent-fee agreement must be in writing signed by the client and must clearly tell the client about any expenses owed whether or not the client prevails. Co","#st-ne-ethics"],["Misconduct: anti-discrimination in § 3-508.4(d) and refusal to pay support","Nebraska · ethics","Nebraska has not adopted ABA Model Rule 8.4(g). Instead, § 3-508.4(d) says a lawyer acting professionally must not engage in adverse discriminatory treatment of litigants, witnesses, lawyers, judges, judicial officers, or court personnel based on race, national origin, gender, religion, disability, age, sexual orientation, or socio-economic status. Legitimate advocacy is excepted. Under § 3-508.4(g), willfully refusi","#st-ne-ethics"],["Solicitation: “This is an advertisement” labels still required","Nebraska · ethics","Every written, recorded, or electronic communication soliciting employment from a prospective client must say “This is an advertisement” on the outside envelope, at the beginning and end of any recorded or electronic communication, and in an email subject line, unless the recipient is a lawyer or has a family, close personal, or prior professional relationship with the lawyer. ABA Model Rule 7.3 no longer requires th","#st-ne-ethics"],["Serve every defendant within 180 days or the case dismisses itself","Nebraska · land mine","An action is commenced when the complaint is filed. Each defendant must be properly served within 180 days, or the action against that defendant is dismissed without prejudice by operation of law the day after the time expires. The Revisor’s annotations describe the statute as self-executing; courts cannot extend it. Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-217 and annotations","#st-ne-unique"],["A notice of appeal filed before a post-trial motion is decided is void","Nebraska · land mine","A timely motion for new trial, to alter or amend, or to set aside the verdict (each due 10 days after entry of judgment) terminates the 30-day appeal time for all parties. A notice of appeal filed before the court announces its decision on such a motion has no effect, whether it was filed before or after the motion. You must file a new notice within 30 days after entry of the order ruling on the motion (no new fee). ","#st-ne-unique"],["Order the bill of exceptions with the notice of appeal, or lose it","Nebraska · land mine","The appellant must file the request for the bill of exceptions (the transcript of the evidence) in the trial court at the same time as the notice of appeal. A late request is a waiver unless the appellate court grants leave for good cause outside the party’s reasonable control. Separately, file the transcript request designating each pleading by name and filing date; generalized “all documents” requests are not allow","#st-ne-unique"],["Errors not separately assigned are not reviewed","Nebraska · land mine","The appellant’s brief must include a separate, concise statement of each claimed error, separately numbered and paragraphed. Review is limited to errors both assigned and discussed in the brief; plain error is reviewed only at the court’s option. Cross-appeals go in a separate section of the appellee’s brief titled “Brief on Cross-Appeal.” Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-109(D)(1)(e), (D)(4); Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1919","#st-ne-unique"],["Excessive or inadequate damages need a new-trial motion","Nebraska · land mine","In a jury case, a motion for new trial is not required to challenge the sufficiency of the evidence, but it is a prerequisite to appellate review of inadequate or excessive damages. The motion is due 10 days after entry of judgment. Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-1912.01(2), 25-1144.01","#st-ne-unique"],["Do not plead a dollar amount for general damages","Nebraska · land mine","A complaint must not state the amount of general damages. It must state each category and the total of special damages, and if you want prejudgment interest, the date from which it runs. Neb. Ct. R. Pldg. § 6-1108(a)(3)(A)-(C)","#st-ne-unique"],["Improper venue is a motion to transfer, not a Rule 12(b)(3) dismissal","Nebraska · land mine","Section 6-1112(b)(3) is “pendency of another action,” not venue. A venue objection is raised by a timely motion to transfer to a proper county. The transferee court may order the plaintiff or its lawyer to pay the defendant’s expenses, including attorney’s fees. Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-403.01; Neb. Ct. R. Pldg. § 6-1112(b)(3) & cmt. [7]","#st-ne-unique"],["Answer in 30 days, and only one pre-answer motion","Nebraska · land mine","The answer is due 30 days after service, not 21. A party that files a § 6-1112 motion cannot file a second pre-answer motion raising a defense that was available but omitted. That applies even to failure to state a claim on a new ground, which must instead be raised in the answer, by motion for judgment on the pleadings, or at trial. Neb. Ct. R. Pldg. § 6-1112(a)(1)(A), (g)(2), (h)(2) & cmts. [11]-[12]","#st-ne-unique"],["Summary judgment: evidence is offered at a hearing, with indexes and annotated statements","Nebraska · land mine","Serve the motion at least 10 days before the hearing; the opponent may serve affidavits before the hearing day. In district court, the movant must file and serve an Evidence Index and an Annotated Statement of Undisputed Facts with pinpoint cites. The opponent files an Evidence Index and an Annotated Statement of Disputed Facts. Leaving out the statement can be grounds to deny (or grant) the motion. Do not put these ","#st-ne-unique"],["Nonparty document subpoenas need 14 days’ notice to the parties first","Nebraska · land mine","Before issuing a subpoena for documents or entry to a nonparty, serve every party with written notice at least 14 days before issuance. Any party may object, and then the subpoena may not command the objected-to items until the objection is withdrawn or overruled. Serve the subpoena at least 14 days before the production date, with copies to the other parties. The nonparty may object in writing within 14 days. Neb. C","#st-ne-unique"],["Expert disclosures are due by default 180 days after the first answer","Nebraska · land mine","Since 2025, Nebraska requires federal-style expert disclosures, with signed reports from retained experts. Absent a court order or stipulation, they are due within 180 days after the first responsive pleading is served, and rebuttal disclosures within 45 days after the other side’s disclosure. A treating physician need not write a report merely because the testimony covers causation or prognosis. Neb. Ct. R. Disc. § ","#st-ne-unique"],["Supersedeas and cost bond within 30 days","Nebraska · land mine","An appeal does not stay a money judgment unless, within 30 days after entry, the appellant posts a supersedeas bond, U.S. Government bonds, or cash. The amount is the lesser of the judgment plus costs and estimated interest, 50% of the appellant’s net worth, or $50 million. If there is no supersedeas bond, a $75 cost bond or cash deposit is due within 30 days. If it is not given, the appeal may be dismissed on motion","#st-ne-unique"],["No peremptory change of judge; disqualification for cause only","Nebraska · judges","None of the collected Nebraska statutes or rules provides a peremptory challenge or automatic substitution of a judge. By statute, a judge at any level is disqualified (unless all parties consent in writing on the record) when the judge is a party or interested; is related to a party within the fourth degree; is related to an attorney in a pending county or district court case as parent, child, sibling, or in-law (or","#st-ne-unique"],["Code of Judicial Conduct: the ABA impartiality standard, with remittal","Nebraska · judges","A judge must step aside whenever impartiality might reasonably be questioned, including for personal bias, personal knowledge of disputed facts, family relationships with a party or lawyer, economic interest, prior work as lawyer, mediator, or government lawyer in the matter, being a material witness, or having previously presided over the matter in another court. Nebraska’s list leaves out the ABA campaign-contribut","#st-ne-unique"],["Statutory actual-malice standard in permit-related suits","Nebraska · speech","When a public applicant or permittee sues over opposition to its permit, zoning change, license, or similar application, it may recover damages only by proving by clear and convincing evidence that the communication was knowingly false or made with reckless disregard for the truth. This is a statutory actual-malice requirement that applies whether or not the plaintiff is a public figure. Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-21,244","#st-ne-unique"],["State constitutional speech protection: not checked","Nebraska · speech","The Nebraska Constitution was not collected, and neither was case law on whether its speech guarantee is read more broadly than the First Amendment. Nothing is claimed here. Neb. Const. (not in collected texts)","#st-ne-unique"],["Civil procedure is a mix of statute and court rule","Nebraska · unique rules","Nebraska has no single code of civil procedure modeled on the Federal Rules. The Legislature directed the Supreme Court to adopt pleading rules for actions filed on or after January 1, 2003, and abolished demurrers, special appearances, pleas in bar or abatement, and cross-petitions. Commencement, service of summons, third-party practice, joinder, venue, summary judgment, new trial, judgments, and appeals are all sta","#st-ne-unique"],["Mandatory fee-shifting for frivolous claims and defenses","Nebraska · unique rules","Instead of a Rule 11 sanctions regime, the court must award reasonable attorney’s fees and costs against any attorney or party who brought or defended a claim the court finds frivolous or made in bad faith, or interposed solely for delay or harassment, or who unnecessarily expanded the proceedings (including discovery abuse). There is no fee award for a good-faith attempt to establish a new theory of law, or if the c","#st-ne-unique"],["Rule 6-1101. Scope and purpose","Nebraska · civil rules","The pleading rules govern civil actions filed on or after January 1, 2003, but only to the extent they are consistent with applicable statutes; a statute with different or additional pleading requirements controls. They are to be construed and used by the court and parties to secure a just and speedy determination without undue cost (Nebraska’s variation on FRCP 1’s “inexpensive”). The 2025 amendments apply to cases ","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-1102. One form of action [Reserved]","Nebraska · civil rules","No text. The single civil action is created by statute (Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-101), not by rule.","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-1103. Commencement of action [Reserved]","Nebraska · civil rules","No text. Commencement is statutory: an action is commenced when the complaint is filed, and each defendant must be served within 180 days or the action against that defendant is dismissed by operation of law (Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-217).","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-1104. Summons [Reserved]","Nebraska · civil rules","No text. Service of process is governed by Neb. Rev. Stat. ch. 25, art. 5 (for example, personal, residence, certified mail, or designated delivery service under § 25-505.01), not by an FRCP 4 counterpart.","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-1105. Serving and filing pleadings and other documents","Nebraska · civil rules","Later pleadings, written motions, required discovery documents, and notices, appearances, demands, and offers of judgment must be served on every party. Unlike FRCP 5, a party in default for not appearing must still be served with a motion for default judgment and the notice of hearing on it, and with any pleading asserting a new claim (served like a summons). Attorneys must e-file and e-serve through the court-autho","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-1106. Time","Nebraska · civil rules","Time is computed under Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-2221 (a period ending on a weekend or court holiday runs to the next open day), and e-filing time under § 2-206. The court may extend a rule-based time for good cause if asked before it expires, or after it expires for excusable neglect, but may not extend a time fixed by statute except as the statute allows. When service is by mail, 3 days are added after the period would ","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-1107. Pleadings allowed; form of motions","Nebraska · civil rules","Allowed pleadings: complaint, answer, counterclaim or cross-claim (which must be stated in the answer, not as a separate pleading), answers to a counterclaim, cross-claim, or third-party complaint, a third-party complaint, and a reply only if ordered. The response to a counterclaim is called an “answer,” not a reply. Written motions need a caption with court, title, file number, and motion title, must use the § 2-103","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-1108. General rules of pleading","Nebraska · civil rules","A claim requires a caption, a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief, and a demand for relief. Unlike federal practice, the amount of general damages must not be stated; each category of special damages and their total must be stated; and if prejudgment interest is sought, the start date must be stated. A general denial is allowed if the party has a good-faith basis to deny at least one material alle","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-1109. Pleading special matters","Nebraska · civil rules","Capacity, authority, and legal existence need not be alleged and are challenged by specific denial. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity; state of mind may be alleged generally. Undue influence was removed from the particularity list in 2025, though its supporting facts must still be pleaded. Conditions precedent may be alleged generally but must be denied with particularity. Official documents, judgm","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-1110. Form of pleadings","Nebraska · civil rules","Every pleading needs a caption with the court, title, file number, and § 6-1107(a) designation; the complaint must name all parties. Claims and defenses go in numbered paragraphs, with separate counts where that promotes clarity. Statements may be adopted by reference, and written-instrument exhibits are part of the pleading. Mirrors FRCP 10.","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-1111. Signing of pleadings; assistance to unrepresented parties","Nebraska · civil rules","Every pleading, motion, and document must be signed by an attorney of record or by a self-represented party (e-signatures under § 2-201(M)), and must state the signer’s address, email, phone, and the attorney’s bar number. An unsigned paper filing is struck unless promptly corrected. Verification is not required unless a statute requires it. Unlike FRCP 11, there are no sanctions provisions; frivolous filings are han","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-1112. Defenses and objections; motion for judgment on the pleadings","Nebraska · civil rules","Answer within 30 days after service of summons and complaint or completion of service by publication (not 21 as in FRCP 12). Answers to counterclaims and cross-claims are due in 30 days. A court-ordered reply is due in 21 days. After denial of a motion, or service of a more definite statement, the answer is due in 21 days. Defenses that may be raised by motion: subject-matter and personal jurisdiction, pendency of an","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-1113. Counterclaim and crossclaim","Nebraska · civil rules","Counterclaims are permissive. There is no compulsory-counterclaim bar like FRCP 13(a); the only rule-based consequence of not pleading a same-transaction counterclaim is losing costs in a later action on it (though claim or issue preclusion may still apply, per the comment). A counterclaim may exceed or differ from the opposing claim. The rules do not expand counterclaims against the State or political subdivisions. ","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-1114. Third-party practice [Reserved]","Nebraska · civil rules","No text. Third-party practice is governed by Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-331.","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-1115. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Nebraska · civil rules","One amendment as of course is allowed until the earlier of 30 days after serving the pleading or, if a response is required, 14 days after service of the first responsive pleading or first § 6-1112(b), (e), or (f) motion (shorter than FRCP 15’s 21 days). Otherwise, amendment requires written consent or leave, which should be freely given. The response to an amended pleading is due within the time remaining or 14 days","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-1116. Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management [Reserved]","Nebraska · civil rules","No text. Pretrial conferences are governed by § 6-1421 (county court) and § 6-1522 (district court). The district court rule allows, but does not require, a pretrial conference after the issues are joined; its order controls unless modified at trial to prevent manifest injustice. There is no FRCP 16(b) mandatory scheduling order.","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-301. Promulgating order","Nebraska · civil rules","The discovery rules were adopted under Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1273.01, effective January 1, 1983. They apply as written in the district courts, and in other Nebraska courts to the extent consistent with statutes. They are cited by the original rule numbers (Rule 26 = § 6-326).","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-302 to 6-325. [Reserved]","Nebraska · civil rules","No text. The numbering starts at § 6-326 so that the last two digits match the corresponding Federal Rule numbers.","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-326. General provisions governing discovery","Nebraska · civil rules","Discovery methods include required (expert) disclosures. Nebraska has no general FRCP 26(a)(1) initial disclosures or 26(f) conference. Scope keeps the pre-2015 federal standard: any nonprivileged matter relevant to the subject matter, discoverable if reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence. Proportionality is not part of the scope test; it is a ground for a protective order. Insurance agreements are dis","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-327. Depositions to perpetuate testimony","Nebraska · civil rules","Before suit, file a verified petition in the district court of the county where an expected adverse party resides, and serve notice at least 21 days before the hearing. The court appoints an attorney for adverse parties served by publication, and a guardian ad litem for minors. The deposition is usable in a later Nebraska action on the same subject. Pending appeal, move in the appellate court, which may decide or rem","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-328. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Nebraska · civil rules","In Nebraska, before an officer authorized to administer oaths under Nebraska law. Elsewhere in the U.S., before an officer authorized by federal or local law, or a court-appointed person. Abroad, under a treaty, a letter of request, on notice before an authorized person, or by commission. Relatives, employees, and attorneys of a party or counsel, and anyone financially interested, are disqualified. Mirrors FRCP 28.","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-329. Stipulations about discovery procedure","Nebraska · civil rules","Parties may stipulate in writing or on the record to deposition logistics and other discovery procedures. A stipulation extending discovery time needs court approval if it would interfere with the discovery deadline, a motion hearing, or trial. Mirrors FRCP 29.","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-330. Depositions by oral examination","Nebraska · civil rules","Leave is needed if the plaintiff deposes within 30 days after service of summons (unless stipulated, the defendant has noticed a deposition, or the deponent is about to leave Nebraska), for a second deposition of the same witness, or for a prisoner. There is no FRCP 30 ten-deposition or one-day/7-hour limit. Depositions may be in person, by video, or by phone, and any party may give notice that it will attend by a di","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-330(A). Interstate deposition and discovery","Nebraska · civil rules","Nebraska’s version of the Uniform Interstate Depositions and Discovery Act. Submit a request in the form in the rule’s appendix, with the foreign subpoena and a counsel list, to the district court clerk of the county where discovery will occur, and pay $75 per subpoena (and per reissue). The clerk issues a Nebraska subpoena. Requesting it is not an appearance or the unauthorized practice of law, but a requesting lawy","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-331. Depositions by written questions","Nebraska · civil rules","Available without leave except in the same situations as oral depositions (a plaintiff within 30 days after service, a repeat deposition, or a prisoner). Serve the questions with a notice naming the deponent and officer (and any interpreter). Cross-questions are due within 14 days, redirect within 7 days, and recross within 7 days. The officer takes and certifies the deposition, and the noticing party notifies all pa","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-332. Using depositions in court proceedings","Nebraska · civil rules","A deposition may be used against a party who was present, represented, or had notice, to the extent the evidence rules would admit it. An adverse party’s deposition (or that of its officer or designee) may be used for any purpose. Any witness’s deposition may be used if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or out of state or beyond subpoena, unable to attend, or not procurable by subpoena, or in exceptional ","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-333. Interrogatories to parties","Nebraska · civil rules","Limit: 50 interrogatories per party served on any other party, including discrete subparts (FRCP 33 allows 25). More may be allowed for good cause. Contention interrogatories are allowed. They may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on others with or after the summons, and an editable electronic copy must be provided on demand. Answers and objections are due in 30 days, except a defendant gets the longe","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-334. Producing documents, ESI, and things; entry onto land","Nebraska · civil rules","Requests to parties within the § 6-326(b) scope. Same service timing and editable-copy rule as interrogatories. Responses are due in 30 days, or for a defendant the longer of 45 days after the summons or 30 days after service. Each request must be reproduced. Objections must be specific, state whether anything is being withheld, and specify any partial objection. ESI form rules track FRCP 34(b)(2)(D)-(E). Production ","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-334(A). Subpoenas to nonparties for documents, ESI, things, or entry","Nebraska · civil rules","The Nebraska substitute for the document-production part of FRCP 45. Serve written notice on all parties at least 14 days before the subpoena is issued; a plaintiff needs leave or a stipulation to serve the notice within 30 days after service of summons unless a defendant has already served one. Any party may object before issuance (privilege, scope, or unreasonable intrusiveness), and the subpoena may not command th","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-335. Physical and mental examinations","Nebraska · civil rules","On motion for good cause and notice, the court may order a party (or a person in its custody or control) whose condition is in controversy to be examined by a suitably licensed or certified examiner. The order specifies the time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner. Report exchange follows FRCP 35(b); an examiner’s testimony may be excluded if the report is not delivered.","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-336. Requests for admission","Nebraska · civil rules","Requests may cover facts, application of law to fact, opinions, and genuineness of documents; they may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on others with or after the summons, with an editable copy on demand. A matter is admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days, or for a defendant the longer of 45 days after the summons or 30 days after service. Lack of knowledge requires a reasonable-inqu","#st-ne-rules"],["Rule 6-337. Failure to make disclosures or cooperate; sanctions","Nebraska · civil rules","Motions to compel require a good-faith conference certification and go to the court where the action is pending. Expenses including fees are mandatory against the losing side unless substantially justified or unjust. Sanctions for violating an order follow FRCP 37(b), and expenses are mandatory. On failure to admit, the requesting party may move within 30 days after proving the matter for its costs of proof, includin","#st-ne-rules"],["Appeals in Nebraska: a statutory 30-day clock, a void-if-early notice, and a record you must order up front","Nebraska · appeals","Civil appeals from the district court are governed mainly by statute (Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1912) and the Supreme Court’s appellate rules. The appeal is perfected, and the appellate court gets jurisdiction, when the notice of appeal is filed and the docket fee deposited (or in forma pauperis status granted) in the trial court within 30 days after entry of the judgment or final order. Entry is the clerk’s file stamp da","#st-ne-appeals"],["Court of Appeals is the default","Nebraska · appeals · Which court, and what can be appealed","A notice of appeal is deemed made to the Court of Appeals unless it specifically asks for the Supreme Court and cites the statute directing the appeal there. By statute, appeals go to the Court of Appeals except capital cases, life-imprisonment cases, and cases involving the constitutionality of a statute. The Court of Appeals sits in rotating three-judge panels. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-101(B)(1)(a); Neb. Rev. Stat. ","#st-ne-appeals"],["Final orders","Nebraska · appeals · Which court, and what can be appealed","Appealable final orders are (a) an order affecting a substantial right that in effect determines the action and prevents a judgment, (b) an order affecting a substantial right made in a special proceeding, (c) an order affecting a substantial right made on summary application after judgment, and (d) denial of summary judgment based on sovereign immunity or a government official’s immunity. A type (d) order may be app","#st-ne-appeals"],["Partial judgments need express findings","Nebraska · appeals · Which court, and what can be appealed","When there are multiple claims or parties, an order resolving fewer than all is final only if the court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay and expressly directs entry of judgment. Otherwise it can be revised at any time before final judgment. The appellant’s jurisdictional statement must quote that language (or cite other authority for the interlocutory appeal). Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1315(1); Neb. ","#st-ne-appeals"],["No general permissive interlocutory appeal found","Nebraska · appeals · Which court, and what can be appealed","The collected statutes and rules contain no procedure like 28 U.S.C. § 1292(b) for discretionary review of interlocutory orders. Outside the final-order categories and § 25-1315 certification, review normally waits for final judgment. Special statutes not collected (for example, for particular proceedings) may provide otherwise. Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-1902, 25-1315","#st-ne-appeals"],["County court cases go first to district court","Nebraska · appeals · Which court, and what can be appealed","Appeals from a final judgment or order of the county court go to the district court of the same county. To perfect the appeal, file a notice of appeal with the county court clerk and deposit the district court docket fee within 30 days after entry, and serve the notice on all parties who appeared. Small-claims appeals also need a $50 cash bond or surety undertaking. In the district court, the appellant must file a st","#st-ne-appeals"],["Notice of appeal and docket fee: 30 days, in the trial court","Nebraska · appeals · Starting the appeal","File in the office of the district court clerk, within 30 days after entry of the judgment, decree, or final order, a notice of intention to prosecute the appeal signed by the appellant or its attorney of record, and deposit the docket fee (Neb. Rev. Stat. § 33-103; amount not collected). A notice or fee filed after the decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed on the date of entry. The trial court c","#st-ne-appeals"],["Transcript request at the same time","Nebraska · appeals · Starting the appeal","With the notice of appeal, file a request directing the clerk to prepare the transcript (the court file). Designate each pleading or document by name and filing date; generalized requests for all documents, or a printed register of actions, are not allowed. The transcript always includes the judgment or order appealed from, the operative pleadings, and the bond record. Do not request exhibits. The clerk transmits the","#st-ne-appeals"],["Bill of exceptions request with the notice, or waived","Nebraska · appeals · Starting the appeal","The request for the bill of exceptions (verbatim record and exhibits) must be filed with the trial court clerk at the same time as the notice of appeal and served on the appellee. It must identify each portion of the evidence and exhibits believed material. If you will argue that a finding is unsupported by the evidence, it must include all evidence relevant to that finding. A later request is a waiver unless the app","#st-ne-appeals"],["Cost deposit and timing of the bill","Nebraska · appeals · Starting the appeal","Court reporting personnel must give the requesting party a cost estimate within 7 days. If the deposit is not timely made, the appeal proceeds as if no bill was requested unless the appellate court extends the time for good cause. In civil cases, the bill is due 7 weeks after the date the deposit was due, subject to extensions approved by the appellate court. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-105(B)(4)(b)-(c), 2-105.01(B)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Cost bond and supersedeas","Nebraska · appeals · Starting the appeal","Within 30 days after entry of the judgment (or of the order overruling a new-trial motion), file a $75 cost bond or make a $75 cash deposit, unless a supersedeas bond is given. The appeal may be dismissed on motion if this is not done. An appeal stays a money judgment only if, within 30 days after entry, the appellant posts a supersedeas bond, U.S. Government bonds, or cash in the lesser of (a) the judgment plus cost","#st-ne-appeals"],["Counsel and e-filing","Nebraska · appeals · Starting the appeal","Trial counsel of record remain counsel on appeal until the appellate court grants withdrawal. Filings in both appellate courts are electronic through the court-authorized service provider, as text-searchable PDFs. Paper filing is limited to self-represented parties who are not registered users. Pro hac vice counsel’s filings must be made by associated Nebraska counsel. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-101(F)(2), (H), 2-103(A","#st-ne-appeals"],["Three tolling motions, each due 10 days after entry of judgment","Nebraska · appeals · Post-trial motions and tolling","A motion for new trial, a motion to alter or amend the judgment, and a motion to set aside the verdict or judgment after a denied directed-verdict motion are each due no later than 10 days after entry of judgment. One filed after the verdict or decision is announced but before entry counts as filed on the day of entry. Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-1144.01, 25-1329, 25-1315.02","#st-ne-appeals"],["Tolling resets the full 30 days, and an early notice is void","Nebraska · appeals · Post-trial motions and tolling","A timely terminating motion by any party stops the appeal clock for all parties, and the full 30 days runs from entry of the order ruling on it. A notice of appeal filed before the court announces its decision on the motion has no effect, whether filed before or after the motion. A new notice must be filed (no additional fee). Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1912(3)","#st-ne-appeals"],["When a new-trial motion is required","Nebraska · appeals · Post-trial motions and tolling","A new-trial motion is not a prerequisite to appellate review of any issue on which the trial court’s ruling appears in the record, including sufficiency of the evidence after a jury trial. It is required to challenge inadequate or excessive damages in a jury case. Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1912.01","#st-ne-appeals"],["No separate notice: assert it in the appellee’s brief","Nebraska · appeals · Cross-appeals","A properly filed appeal gives every appellee the right to cross-appeal against any other party. The cross-appeal is asserted only in the appellee’s brief, noted on the cover, in a separate section headed “Brief on Cross-Appeal” that follows the rules for an appellant’s brief. The appellant answers in a separate “Answer to Brief on Cross-Appeal” in the reply brief. All later attempts by other parties to appeal are fil","#st-ne-appeals"],["Keep the cross-appeal alive if the appellant dismisses","Nebraska · appeals · Cross-appeals","If the appellant moves to dismiss its appeal, a party with a right to cross-appeal may file a notice of intention to cross-appeal within 10 days after the motion is filed. The court then denies the motion and sets a brief date, and the case proceeds as if the cross-appellant had perfected the appeal. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-108(E)","#st-ne-appeals"],["No cross-appeals at later stages","Nebraska · appeals · Cross-appeals","The right of cross-appeal does not apply to petitions for further review, supplemental briefing on further review, or motions for rehearing. A party must file its own petition or motion instead. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-101(E), 2-102(F)(4), (H), 2-113(F)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Briefing schedule","Nebraska · appeals · Briefs: timing, form, and limits","Appellant’s brief: 30 days after the transcript is filed in the appellate court if no bill of exceptions was requested, or 30 days after the bill of exceptions is due if one was. Appellee’s brief: 30 days after the appellant serves and files. Reply: 10 days after the appellee serves and files. Add 3 days if the brief was served by mail. Filing a motion for summary dismissal or affirmance does not extend brief dates. ","#st-ne-appeals"],["Extensions are tiered","Nebraska · appeals · Briefs: timing, form, and limits","A first extension (except in juvenile cases) needs only a general showing of need and may be granted immediately, for up to 30 days. A second request, or a first request in a juvenile case, needs good cause, such as unavailable records, complex issues, specific conflicts with another court, or new counsel. “Press of other business” and stipulations are not good cause. Third and later requests need exceptional cause, ","#st-ne-appeals"],["Word limits are cumulative per party","Nebraska · appeals · Briefs: timing, form, and limits","15,000 words total per party on original submission. For the appellant, that covers the opening brief, reply, and answer to any cross-appeal combined; for the appellee, its brief, the cross-appeal brief, and any cross-reply combined. The limit rises to 18,000 words per party if the appellee cross-appeals. Everything counts, including the cover, tables, and signature blocks. Briefs supporting rehearing motions or othe","#st-ne-appeals"],["Format","Nebraska · appeals · Briefs: timing, form, and limits","8½ × 11 portrait, text-searchable PDF, left-aligned (not justified), 1.5-inch margins, 1.15 or 1.2 line spacing, no footnotes. Century or Century Schoolbook preferred (Times New Roman, Baskerville Old Face, Book Antiqua, or Palatino allowed) at 12-13 point, with no underlining. Hyperlinks only to the record, internal bookmarks, the official Nebraska Reports, the Legislature’s site, and the court rules. The cover is t","#st-ne-appeals"],["Required contents","Nebraska · appeals · Briefs: timing, form, and limits","The appellant’s brief contains, in order: cover; tables; a jurisdictional statement (dates of judgment, any tolling motion and its disposition, notice of appeal and fee, and the basis for any partial-judgment appeal); a statement of the case; separately numbered assignments of error; propositions of law, each followed by authorities; a statement of facts with every fact cited to the record (T26, 156:12, E5, p. 95); a","#st-ne-appeals"],["Challenging a statute’s constitutionality","Nebraska · appeals · Briefs: timing, form, and limits","A party claiming a Nebraska statute is unconstitutional must file a separate notice with the Clerk after docketing and before filing its brief. If the Attorney General is not already in the case, the brief must be served on the Attorney General. Without the notice, the Supreme Court will not consider the constitutional question except by special order. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-109(E)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Briefs are public; amicus needs leave","Nebraska · appeals · Briefs: timing, form, and limits","Briefs are not confidential and may not reproduce confidential material; cite to it instead. An amicus must move for leave at least 30 days before oral argument and file any permitted brief at least 10 days before argument. Leave may be denied if it would cause a recusal. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-109(A)(4), (D)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Motion practice","Nebraska · appeals · Motions, submission, and argument","Responses to motions are due within 10 days of filing, and there are no replies. Motions are submitted on stipulation, when all responses are in, or when the 10-day period ends. There is no oral argument on motions unless ordered (5 minutes per side). A motion to dismiss for lack of jurisdiction may be filed any time after docketing. A motion to affirm as too unsubstantial to need argument may be filed after the appe","#st-ne-appeals"],["Summary disposition on the court’s own motion","Nebraska · appeals · Motions, submission, and argument","The court may dismiss for lack of jurisdiction, or summarily affirm when one of the listed grounds is dispositive and a detailed opinion would have no precedential value. It may summarily reverse only after an order to show cause. Parties cannot request summary affirmance under § 2-107(A). Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-107(A)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Scheduling and oral argument","Nebraska · appeals · Motions, submission, and argument","Cases may be submitted without argument; a party may move for argument within 7 days after the minute entry. Listed categories are advanced automatically (for example, workers’ compensation, child custody, juvenile, and certified questions). Other cases are scheduled in the order appellee briefs are filed. Argument is limited to 10 minutes per side unless ordered otherwise; a motion for more time is due within 10 day","#st-ne-appeals"],["Attorney fees on appeal: motion within 10 days","Nebraska · appeals · Motions, submission, and argument","A party claiming appellate attorney fees in a civil case must file a motion, citing the law or uniform course of practice that allows fees and supported by an affidavit, within 10 days after release of the opinion or the order disposing of the appeal (including an order on rehearing), unless a statute provides otherwise. A motion that cites no authority is summarily overruled. A timely fee motion in the Court of Appe","#st-ne-appeals"],["Citing Court of Appeals opinions","Nebraska · appeals · Motions, submission, and argument","Only opinions designated “For Permanent Publication” may be cited freely, and they bind lower courts until the Supreme Court acts. Other Court of Appeals opinions may be cited only if persuasive on a material issue and no published opinion would serve as well, or if the case is related. Their unpublished status must be noted. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-102(E)(4)-(5)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Motion for rehearing: 10 days, brief required","Nebraska · appeals · Rehearing and review in the Supreme Court","Within 10 days after release of the opinion or the order disposing of the appeal, file both the motion and a supporting brief (up to 3,800 words) containing tables, numbered assignments of error, propositions of law, and argument. Others may respond within 10 days. There is no oral argument. A timely rehearing motion in the Court of Appeals tolls the time to petition for further review. No rehearing is allowed after ","#st-ne-appeals"],["Petition for further review: 30 days, $50, 3,500 words","Nebraska · appeals · Rehearing and review in the Supreme Court","File the petition and a memorandum brief within 30 days after the Court of Appeals’ opinion or its order finally disposing of the appeal, whichever is later. An order on rehearing or on attorney fees counts as a final disposition. The docket fee is $50 per case number and per petitioning party (waived for in forma pauperis). The combined length is limited to 3,500 words, with each Court of Appeals error separately as","#st-ne-appeals"],["If further review is granted","Nebraska · appeals · Rehearing and review in the Supreme Court","The petitioner may file a supplemental brief within 20 days after the order granting review; other parties may file within 20 days after that. Supplemental briefs may not assert cross-appeals. The first petitioner opens and closes any argument. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-102(H)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Petition to bypass the Court of Appeals","Nebraska · appeals · Rehearing and review in the Supreme Court","Any party may petition the Supreme Court to take the case directly. The petition is filed together with that party’s initial brief, limited to 1,800 words, and based on the § 24-1106(2) factors: first impression, constitutional interpretation, statutory validity, conflicting decisions, significant public interest, or qualified immunity in a § 1983 case. Responses are due with the responding party’s brief. Petitions a","#st-ne-appeals"],["Mandate and further review in the U.S. Supreme Court","Nebraska · appeals · Rehearing and review in the Supreme Court","No mandate issues while a rehearing motion or petition for further review may be filed or is pending. To stay the mandate while seeking U.S. Supreme Court review, apply within 7 days after the opinion (with the rehearing motion, if one is filed), showing that a federal question is involved. The court may require a bond. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-114(A)(1)-(2), 2-102(F)(7)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Notice of appeal and docket fee (district court civil judgment)","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the judgment, decree, or final order (clerk’s file stamp date).\nFiled in the district court. The notice and fee are the only jurisdictional steps. No extension provision appears in § 25-1912. Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-1912(1), 25-1301(3)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Request for bill of exceptions; transcript request","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","0 days from same time as the notice of appeal.\nA late bill-of-exceptions request is a waiver absent appellate leave for good cause outside the party’s control. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-105(B)(2)(a), 2-104(A)(1)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Appellee’s supplemental (or substitute) bill of exceptions request","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from service of the appellant’s request (or the notice of appeal, if the appellant requested none). Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-105(B)(2)(e)-(f)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Motion for new trial / to alter or amend / to set aside verdict","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of judgment.\nA timely motion terminates the appeal time for all parties. An earlier notice of appeal is void; refile within 30 days after entry of the ruling. Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-1144.01, 25-1329, 25-1315.02","#st-ne-appeals"],["Notice of appeal after a tolling motion","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order ruling on the motion.\nNo additional fee. Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1912(3)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Cost bond or $75 cash deposit","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of judgment or of the order overruling a new-trial motion.\nNot needed if a supersedeas bond is given. The appeal may be dismissed on motion if missing. Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1914","#st-ne-appeals"],["Supersedeas bond to stay execution","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the judgment, decree, or final order.\nLesser of judgment-based amount, 50% of net worth, or $50 million for money judgments. Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1916","#st-ne-appeals"],["Appeal from denial of summary judgment based on sovereign or official immunity","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order (or entry of judgment). Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1902(1)(d), (2)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the transcript in the appellate court (no bill requested) or the bill of exceptions due date.\nFirst extension up to 30 days on a general showing; later ones need good or exceptional cause. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-109(A)(1)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief (including any cross-appeal)","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from appellant’s service and filing of its brief.\nAdd 3 days if served by mail. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-109(A)(2), 2-101(E)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Reply brief","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from appellee’s service and filing of its brief.\nAdd 3 days if served by mail. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-109(A)(3)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Response to a motion","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the motion.\nNo reply allowed. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-106(B)(2)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Motion for appellate attorney fees","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from release of the opinion or order disposing of the appeal.\nUnless a statute provides otherwise. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-106(G)(2)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Motion for rehearing and supporting brief","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from release of the opinion or order disposing of the appeal.\nTolls the time to petition for further review from the Court of Appeals. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-113(A)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Petition for further review and memorandum brief","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from release of the Court of Appeals opinion or entry of its final order, whichever is later.\n$50 docket fee; 3,500 words; responses due within 10 days. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-102(F)(1); Neb. Rev. Stat. § 24-1107","#st-ne-appeals"],["Supplemental brief after further review is granted","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from entry of the order granting further review (nonpetitioners: 20 days after the petitioner’s brief). Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-102(H)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Application to stay mandate pending U.S. Supreme Court review","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from filing of the opinion or other dispositive entry.\nMust show that a federal question is involved. Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-114(A)(2)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Appeal from county court to district court","Nebraska · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the county court judgment or final order.\nNotice and district court docket fee are filed with the county court clerk. The statement of errors is due 20 days after the bill of exceptions is filed (30 days after the notice if no bill) (§ 6-1518(B)). Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-2729(1)","#st-ne-appeals"],["Supreme Court of New Mexico","New Mexico · courts","Court of last resort. Reviews Court of Appeals decisions by discretionary certiorari (Rule 12-502), and takes direct appeals in cases with a sentence of death or life imprisonment, Public Regulation Commission appeals, appeals from grants of habeas corpus, and other matters reserved to it (Rule 12-102(A)). It also makes the court rules (the NMRA) and controls bar admission, pro hac vice registration, and lawyer disci","#st-nm"],["New Mexico Court of Appeals","New Mexico · courts","Hears all other appeals on the record (Rule 12-102(B)). Sorts each case onto a general, summary, or legal calendar after reading the docketing statement (Rule 12-210). Also reviews district-court decisions in administrative appeals by discretionary certiorari (Rule 12-505).","#st-nm"],["District Courts (13 judicial districts)","New Mexico · courts","General-jurisdiction trial courts. The judiciary describes 34 district courts in 13 judicial districts. Civil practice follows the Rules of Civil Procedure for the District Courts (Rules 1-001 et seq.), plus each district’s local rules (LR1 to LR13). District courts also hear de novo appeals from magistrate and metropolitan courts (Rule 1-072) and statutory administrative appeals (Rule 1-074).","#st-nm"],["Magistrate Courts, Bernalillo County Metropolitan Court, Probate Courts, Municipal Courts","New Mexico · courts","The judiciary lists 43 magistrate courts, the Bernalillo County Metropolitan Court (Albuquerque), 33 county probate courts, and 78 municipal courts. Each has its own rule set (Rule Sets 1B, 2, 3, 6, 7, 8). Civil judgments from magistrate and metropolitan courts are appealed to district court within 15 days (Rule 1-072).","#st-nm"],["Narrow anti-SLAPP statute: only speech at public hearings or meetings in quasi-judicial proceedings","New Mexico · anti-SLAPP","NMSA 1978, § 38-2-9.1 (Laws 2001, ch. 218) covers only actions for money damages based on conduct or speech “in connection with a public hearing or public meeting in a quasi-judicial proceeding” before a tribunal or decision-making body of a political subdivision. A “public meeting in a quasi-judicial proceeding” includes meetings or presentations before state, city, town, or village councils, planning commissions, a","#st-nm-slapp"],["Procedure: a special motion, Rule 12 motion, or summary judgment, heard on a priority basis","New Mexico · anti-SLAPP","A covered action is subject to a special motion to dismiss, a motion for judgment on the pleadings, or a motion for summary judgment, which the court must consider “on a priority or expedited basis.” The statute sets no discovery stay, no burden-shifting test, and no deadline for a hearing. The annotations report that courts apply it only where the speech or conduct was in connection with a qualifying proceeding. Exa","#st-nm-slapp"],["Fees: mandatory if the motion is filed within 90 days of the answer and granted","New Mexico · anti-SLAPP","If the defendant raises the statute’s protection as an affirmative defense, and the court grants a motion to dismiss, for judgment on the pleadings, or for summary judgment that was filed within ninety days after the moving party’s answer, the court shall award reasonable attorney fees and costs. If the court finds the special motion or summary-judgment motion frivolous or solely intended to cause delay, it shall awa","#st-nm-slapp"],["Expedited appeal for any party","New Mexico · anti-SLAPP","Any party has a right to an expedited appeal from an order on the special motions, or from the trial court’s failure to rule on an expedited basis. The annotations report that the appeal covers rulings on whether the statute applies (Ferebee v. Hume, 2021-NMCA-012) and is limited to speech-based defenses (Valenzuela v. My Way Holdings, LLC, 2024-NMCA-009). The statute sets no separate appeal deadline, so the general ","#st-nm-slapp"],["Not available in federal court; no UPEPA found","New Mexico · anti-SLAPP","Per the annotation, the Tenth Circuit held § 38-2-9.1 procedural and inapplicable in federal diversity cases (Los Lobos Renewable Power, LLC v. Americulture, Inc., 885 F.3d 659 (10th Cir. 2018)). The current NMSA Chapter 38 (compiled November 2026) contains no Uniform Public Expression Protection Act, and the 2026 table of newly enacted sections lists none. 2025 session laws were not separately checked, but a 2025 en","#st-nm-slapp"],["Pro hac vice is a State Bar registration, not a court motion","New Mexico · out of state","A lawyer licensed in another U.S. jurisdiction, or before the highest court of record of any country, may perform legal services only in association with an active New Mexico State Bar member in good standing. File a registration certificate with the State Bar for each matter, naming the proceeding and the local counsel. With it, certify good standing (attaching a certificate of good standing from every jurisdiction ","#st-nm-oos"],["Fees: $500 first registration, $375 each later one; 5-per-year cap","New Mexico · out of state","The first registration certificate in a calendar year costs $500 (non-refundable). Each later one that year costs $375. No lawyer may register more than five times in any calendar year. The fee is waived for government lawyers acting for a government, legal-aid lawyers acting for indigent clients, and lawyers representing an indigent client for no fee. Rule 24-106(A), (D) NMRA","#st-nm-oos"],["In district court: file an affidavit, and local counsel must attend everything","New Mexico · out of state","After complying with Rule 24-106, the out-of-state lawyer files, with the first paper filed (or as soon as practicable after being retained), an affidavit of good standing and Rule 24-106 compliance. The lawyer is then deemed admitted, with no separate motion or order. New Mexico counsel must sign the first pleading or motion and appear on every later paper, and is deemed to sign all later pleadings for Rule 1-011 pu","#st-nm-oos"],["In the appellate courts: same association requirement","New Mexico · out of state","Counsel admitted elsewhere may, after complying with Rule 24-106, sign briefs and papers and argue orally only in association with New Mexico counsel. New Mexico counsel signs the first paper filed in the appellate court, and that counsel’s name and address must appear on every later paper. Rule 12-302(E) NMRA","#st-nm-oos"],["Temporary practice and ADR without registration","New Mexico · out of state","Temporary litigation-related work must comply with Rule 24-106 and be done with an actively participating New Mexico lawyer. Some work needs no Rule 24-106 registration: arbitration, mediation, or other ADR reasonably related to the lawyer’s home practice (unless the forum requires pro hac vice), work arising from the home-state practice, in-house work, and federally authorized services. In transactions involving iss","#st-nm-oos"],["Malpractice-insurance notice applies to pro hac vice lawyers too","New Mexico · out of state","A lawyer without professional liability insurance of at least $100,000 per claim and $300,000 aggregate must give the client a prescribed written notice and obtain a signed acknowledgment, and must keep the record for six years. “Lawyer” expressly includes lawyers provisionally admitted under Rule 24-106. Rule 16-104(C) NMRA","#st-nm-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances are allowed but must be labeled","New Mexico · out of state","Limited representation is allowed with informed consent (Rule 16-102(C)). In district court, file a “Limited Entry of Appearance” describing the limit, and put the limit and a service address for the client in every signature block. When the limited work is complete, a notice of withdrawal is enough; otherwise withdrawal needs a court order. The lawyer must disclose the scope to the tribunal (Rule 16-303(E)). In the ","#st-nm-oos"],["E-filing: mandatory for lawyers in the appellate courts; set by local rule in district courts","New Mexico · out of state","In the Supreme Court and Court of Appeals, lawyers must file through the approved EFS, register, and keep a working email address. Self-represented parties may not e-file. The EFS charges no fee, but docket fees must be paid by check within 5 days after the case is accepted, or the case may be dismissed (Rule 12-307.2). In district court, e-filing is mandatory where a Supreme Court-approved local rule requires it. Ea","#st-nm-oos"],["Full admission: UBE transfer, reciprocity, and limited licenses","New Mexico · out of state","Methods include UBE score transfer (a passing score within five years plus the ethics exam), a reciprocal license (active practice in a reciprocal state for 5 of the past 7 years, among other conditions), and limited licenses for in-house counsel, public employees and public defenders, legal-services lawyers, military spouses, law professors, and former federal employees. A “NextGen Bar Exam” amendment to these rules","#st-nm-oos"],["New Mexico Rules of Professional Conduct, Rules 16-100 to 16-805 NMRA","New Mexico · ethics","Based on the ABA Model Rules but renumbered with a 16- prefix: Model Rule 1.6 is Rule 16-106, MR 1.18 is Rule 16-118, MR 8.4 is Rule 16-804. In effect since January 1, 1987, and amended since. Rule Set 16 NMRA (compiler’s note)","#st-nm-ethics"],["Mandatory malpractice-insurance disclosure","New Mexico · ethics","This is not in the Model Rules. A lawyer without coverage of at least $100,000 per claim and $300,000 aggregate (deductibles included) must give the client the exact written notice in the rule, get a signed acknowledgment, renew the notice if coverage lapses during the matter, and keep the records for six years. Full-time judges, single-entity in-house counsel, and government lawyers are exempt. Rule 16-104(C) NMRA","#st-nm-ethics"],["Mandatory succession plan","New Mexico · ethics","This is not in the Model Rules. Every active New Mexico licensee needs a written succession plan naming an “assisting lawyer” who consents in writing. The plan must say where client files, passwords, and trust-account records are kept. Clients must be told the plan exists, and compliance is certified each year on the State Bar registration statement. Rule 16-119 NMRA","#st-nm-ethics"],["Harassment and discrimination is misconduct; one-party recording addressed","New Mexico · ethics","Rule 16-804(G) (similar to MR 8.4(g)) makes knowing or negligent harassment or discrimination in conduct related to the practice of law misconduct. An amendment effective December 31, 2026 adds protected classes, including color, ancestry, pregnancy, and military status. Its new commentary says a lawyer who creates or takes part in a one-party recording does not violate Rule 16-804(C) unless the conduct involves dish","#st-nm-ethics"],["Trust records kept five years; limited-appearance disclosure to the tribunal","New Mexico · ethics","Trust-account records must be kept for five years after the representation ends (Rule 16-115(A)). A lawyer appearing in a limited manner must disclose the scope of representation to the tribunal (Rule 16-303(E)), a New Mexico addition to MR 3.3. Rules 16-115(A), 16-303(E) NMRA","#st-nm-ethics"],["Pro bono: 50-hour aspiration, or 1% of income from December 31, 2026","New Mexico · ethics","Lawyers should aspire to 50 hours of pro bono service a year and must certify their hours or contribution each year. Through December 30, 2026, the financial alternative is $500 a year (or the sliding table in the rule). From December 31, 2026, the alternative is 1% of the lawyer’s annual income from practicing law in New Mexico. Rule 24-108 NMRA (as amended by Order No. S-1-RCR-2025-00152, eff. Dec. 31, 2026); Rule ","#st-nm-ethics"],["Excuse the judge in 10 days, and before asking the judge for anything","New Mexico · land mine","The peremptory-excusal clock is 10 days: from notice of assignment for plaintiffs, and from the first Rule 1-012 filing for defendants. The 120-day outer cap and the bar after any hearing or request to the judge apply on top of that. Co-defendants sharing a firm get only one excusal between them. Rule 1-088.1 NMRA","#st-nm-unique"],["Counting days: 10 or fewer skips weekends; 11 or more counts every day","New Mexico · land mine","Periods of 10 days or less exclude intermediate Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays. Periods of 11 days or more count every day. Three days are added for mail service, but not to appeal, rehearing, or certiorari deadlines. Rules 1-006, 12-308, 12-201(A)(2), 12-404(A), 12-502(B) NMRA","#st-nm-unique"],["Notice of appeal: 30 days from filing in the clerk’s office, with no mail days","New Mexico · land mine","The 30 days run from when the judgment is filed in the district court clerk’s office. Only a timely motion that could affect finality restarts the clock (Rule 1-060(B) motions only if filed within 30 days of judgment). An order granting a new trial is not appealable and makes the earlier judgment non-appealable. Extensions must be sought within 30 days after the deadline, can add at most 30 days, and a notice of appe","#st-nm-unique"],["Trial counsel owes the docketing statement in 30 days","New Mexico · land mine","In the Court of Appeals, trial counsel must file the docketing statement within 30 days after the notice of appeal, with the docket fee. Conclusory issue statements are rejected. Issues and preservation must be stated specifically, because on the summary calendar the court decides on the docketing statement and record alone. Rules 12-208(A), (B), (D), (H), 12-210(D) NMRA","#st-nm-unique"],["Answer a proposed summary disposition within 20 days","New Mexico · land mine","If the case is placed on the summary calendar, the parties have 20 days from service of the notice of proposed summary disposition to file a memorandum in opposition or support. The limit is 35 pages or 11,000 words, and no new issues may be raised outside the docketing statement unless the court allows an amendment. There is no oral argument. Rule 12-210(D) NMRA","#st-nm-unique"],["Anti-SLAPP fees depend on filing within 90 days of the answer","New Mexico · land mine","Mandatory fees follow only if the protection is pleaded as an affirmative defense and the dispositive motion is filed within ninety days after the movant’s answer. The statute reaches only speech at public hearings or meetings in quasi-judicial proceedings before state or local bodies, and it does not apply in federal diversity cases. NMSA 1978, § 38-2-9.1(A), (B), (D)","#st-nm-unique"],["Pro hac vice: 5 registrations a year, and local counsel must physically attend","New Mexico · land mine","Register with the State Bar (not the court) for each matter: $500 for the first registration and $375 for each later one in a calendar year, with a maximum of five a year. Then file the Rule 1-089.1 affidavit with your first paper. New Mexico counsel must be present in person at all proceedings unless excused. Rules 24-106(A), (D), 1-089.1 NMRA","#st-nm-unique"],["Uninsured lawyers, including pro hac vice lawyers, must give written notice","New Mexico · land mine","If your malpractice coverage is below $100,000 per claim and $300,000 aggregate, you must give the client the prescribed notice and get a signed acknowledgment. This applies to lawyers practicing under Rule 24-106. Rule 16-104(C)(1), (3) NMRA","#st-nm-unique"],["Summary-judgment papers: numbered facts, 15-day response","New Mexico · land mine","The movant lists numbered material facts with record citations. The opponent responds within 15 days with numbered disputed facts keyed to the movant’s numbers, and any fact not specifically controverted is deemed admitted. The reply is due within 15 days. Opposed motions generally must recite that concurrence was sought. Rules 1-056, 1-007.1 NMRA","#st-nm-unique"],["Shorter post-trial and responsive-pleading clocks than federal practice","New Mexico · land mine","Motions for new trial or to alter or amend judgment are due within 30 days after entry of judgment. After a Rule 1-012 motion is denied, the answer is due in 10 days. A jury demand is due within 10 days after the last pleading directed to the issue, with a $150 or $300 jury fee, or the jury is waived. Rules 1-059, 1-012(A), 1-038 NMRA","#st-nm-unique"],["Two years of inactivity can mean dismissal with prejudice","New Mexico · land mine","Any party may move to dismiss a claim with prejudice if no significant action toward trial or final disposition has been taken within 2 years after filing. Without a scheduling order, the court may also dismiss without prejudice after 180 days of inactivity, with 30 days to seek reinstatement. Rule 1-041(E) NMRA","#st-nm-unique"],["Appellate amendments take effect December 31, 2026","New Mexico · land mine","Orders approved October 30, 2026 amend Rules 12-202, 12-208, 12-209, 12-211, 12-503, 12-505, 12-601 to 12-603 and add Rules 12-208.1, 12-211.1, and 12-502.1, for cases filed on or after December 31, 2026. Examples: in criminal, delinquency, and Children’s Code cases the notice of appeal is no longer served on the appellate court (other cases still serve it); the district court clerk must file the record proper within","#st-nm-unique"],["One peremptory excusal per party; 10-day windows, 120-day hard stop","New Mexico · judges","Each party may excuse one district judge without cause. A plaintiff must file the excusal within 10 days after service of notice of assignment of the first judge. A defendant must file within 10 days after filing its first Rule 1-012 pleading or motion. After a reassignment, any party has 10 days after the clerk’s notice. No excusal is allowed more than 120 days after the judge was assigned. The right is lost once th","#st-nm-unique"],["Statutory peremptory challenge","New Mexico · judges","By statute, each party to a civil or criminal action may peremptorily challenge one district judge, and the judge “shall proceed no further.” In workers’ compensation cases, the employer and its insurer count as one party. A separate statute sets a 10-day affidavit deadline (after the cause is at issue, after the jury-demand time expires, or after the judge is assigned, whichever is later). The rule sets its own dead","#st-nm-unique"],["Recusal for cause; judges who cannot be peremptorily excused","New Mexico · judges","Recusal for cause is always available, and a judge whose impartiality may reasonably be questioned must file a recusal (Rule 1-088.1). A judge designated by the chief justice, and that judge’s successor, may be excused only under N.M. Const. art. VI, § 18, not peremptorily (Rule 1-088). Water judges and stream-adjudication judges are also excluded (Rules 1-071.5, 1-077.2). The constitutional text was not collected. R","#st-nm-unique"],["No broader speech protection found in collected texts","New Mexico · speech","Beyond § 38-2-9.1, the collected rules and Chapter 38 contain no general speech-protection procedure (no UPEPA). Defamation-specific provisions in Chapter 38 (such as § 38-2-9 on truth and mitigation) were not analyzed. NMSA 1978, Ch. 38 (2026)","#st-nm-unique"],["Docketing statement and calendar system in the Court of Appeals","New Mexico · unique rules","Within 30 days after the notice of appeal, trial counsel (not necessarily appellate counsel) files a docketing statement that sets out the issues, how each was preserved, and the authorities. The court then assigns the case to a summary, general, or legal calendar. On the summary calendar there are no briefs: you get 20 days to answer a notice of proposed summary disposition. Rules 12-208, 12-210 NMRA","#st-nm-unique"],["Either side can make a Rule 1-068 offer, with double costs","New Mexico · unique rules","Unlike FRCP 68, a claimant can serve an offer of settlement, but not until 120 days after the defending party’s responsive pleading. Offers must be served more than 10 days before trial and accepted within 10 days. If the claimant’s rejected offer is beaten, the defending party pays double the post-offer costs (no attorney fees). Rule 1-068 NMRA","#st-nm-unique"],["Rule 1-001. Scope of rules; definitions","New Mexico · civil rules","These rules govern procedure in all civil suits in New Mexico district courts, at law or in equity, except where the Rules of Evidence are inconsistent; unless they say otherwise, they yield to contrary statutes for special statutory or summary proceedings, are subject to Rule 23-114 NMRA (free process), and are to be construed to secure the just, speedy and inexpensive determination of every action. “Defendant” incl","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-002. One form of action","New Mexico · civil rules","There is one form of action, the “civil action,” as under FRCP 2.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-003. Commencement of action","New Mexico · civil rules","A civil action is commenced by filing a complaint, and the clerk endorses on it the time, day, month and year of filing.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-003.1. Commencement of action; domestic relations information sheet","New Mexico · civil rules","A domestic relations information sheet in the Supreme Court-approved form must be submitted with a petition initiating a domestic relations case, a motion to reopen a closed one, and a party’s first responsive pleading, and a blank copy must be served on the respondent with the summons and petition; information drawn from it into the court system is confidential except as disclosed to the parties (unless the court or","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-003.2. Commencement of action; guardianship and conservatorship information sheet","New Mexico · civil rules","A petitioner seeking appointment of a guardian or conservator under NMSA 1978, Chapter 45, Article 5, Parts 3 or 4 must submit, on filing the petition, an information sheet substantially in the Supreme Court-approved form identifying persons entitled to notice and access to court records. Under an amendment effective December 31, 2026, such an action must be initiated by a petition for court appointment that names on","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-003.3. Commencement of residential foreclosure action; certification of pre-filing notice required; definition","New Mexico · civil rules","Every complaint initiating a residential foreclosure action must be submitted with a certification of pre-filing notice substantially in the form of Form 4-227 NMRA, and notwithstanding Rule 1-005(F) the clerk must refuse to file a residential foreclosure complaint without it; the rule does not apply to lien foreclosures. “Residential” means property designed principally for occupancy by one to four families and occu","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-004. Process","New Mexico · civil rules","On filing of the complaint the clerk issues a summons (in the approved form, directing the defendant to serve a responsive pleading or motion within 30 days after service and naming plaintiff’s counsel or the self-represented plaintiff), which any defendant may waive, and service must be made with “reasonable diligence” rather than within a fixed period as under FRCP 4(m), by any nonparty over 18 (writs of attachment","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-004.1. Guardianship and conservatorship proceedings; process","New Mexico · civil rules","In proceedings to appoint a guardian or conservator under NMSA 1978, Chapter 45, Article 5, Parts 3 and 4 (but not temporary appointments under Sections 45-5-310 or 45-5-408), the court must within 5 days of the petition set a hearing no sooner than 60 days after filing and issue a notice of hearing and rights (Form 4-999 NMRA) in lieu of a summons. The notice and petition must be served personally on the alleged inc","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-005. Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","New Mexico · civil rules","Every written order, pleading after the complaint, discovery paper, non-ex parte motion, notice, appearance, demand, offer of settlement and similar paper must be served on every party, except that parties in default for failure to appear need be served only with pleadings asserting new or additional claims, and then in the manner of Rule 1-004; service on a represented party is made on the attorney by delivery (incl","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-005.1. Service and filing of pleadings and other papers by facsimile","New Mexico · civil rules","Pleadings and papers may be filed by fax to a number designated by the clerk (or the judge), with the same effect as any other filing, but direct fax filing is allowed only if no fee is required, only one copy is required, the paper is not more than 10 pages excluding the cover page unless the court approves, and it is preceded by a cover sheet with specified sender, case and page information; these limits do not app","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-005.2. Electronic service and filing of pleadings and other papers","New Mexico · civil rules","A district court may by Supreme Court-approved local rule mandate electronic filing through the approved EFS for represented parties (excluding, unless the local rule provides otherwise, certain child support, domestic violence, sealed, habeas and Children’s Court matters), attorneys must register with a working e-mail address, and self-represented parties may not e-file; documents may be served electronically on reg","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-006. Time","New Mexico · civil rules","The rule governs computing any period in these rules, local rules, court orders or statutes unless another Supreme Court rule expressly supersedes it: for periods of 11 days or more every day is counted, while for periods of 10 days or less intermediate Saturdays, Sundays and legal holidays are excluded (a difference from FRCP 6, though not for statutory pre-suit notices), the trigger day is excluded and a last day f","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-007. Pleadings allowed; form of motions","New Mexico · civil rules","The only pleadings are a complaint and answer, a reply to a counterclaim, an answer to a cross-claim, a third-party complaint and third-party answer, and a reply to an answer or third-party answer only if the court orders one. A request for an order must be by motion, in writing unless made at a hearing or trial, stating the grounds with particularity and the relief sought, and form rules for pleadings apply to motio","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-007.1. Motions; how presented","New Mexico · civil rules","Motions (other than those made at trial or as the court permits) must be in writing with particular grounds; for an unopposed motion an order approved by all parties must accompany it, and an opposed motion must recite that the movant sought all parties’ concurrence or explain why not, except for motions to dismiss, for new trial, for judgment as a matter of law, for summary judgment, or for relief under Rule 1-060(B","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-007.2. Time limit for filing motion to compel arbitration","New Mexico · civil rules","A party seeking to compel arbitration of a claim must file and serve a motion to compel no later than 10 days after service of the answer or of the last pleading directed to that claim, a deadline with no FRCP counterpart.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-008. General rules of pleading","New Mexico · civil rules","A claim for relief must contain proper allegations of venue (the county named in the complaint is taken as the intended venue), a short and plain statement of the claim showing entitlement to relief, and a demand for judgment, which may be in the alternative; unlike FRCP 8, the complaint may not allege damages in any specific monetary amount unless that is a necessary allegation. Responses must admit or deny averment","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-008.1. Pleadings and papers; captions","New Mexico · civil rules","Every pleading or paper filed in district court must have a caption showing “State of New Mexico, County of ___, ___ Judicial District,” the names of the parties, and a title describing the cause of action or relief requested, which has no legal effect in the action.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-009. Pleading special matters","New Mexico · civil rules","Capacity, authority and legal existence need not be averred except to show jurisdiction, and a challenge must be made by specific negative averment; fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity while condition of mind may be averred generally, conditions precedent may be averred generally but denied specifically, and official acts, judgments, special damages (which must be specifically stated), time and place","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-010. Form of pleadings","New Mexico · civil rules","Every pleading needs a caption with the court, title of the action, file number and Rule 1-007(A) designation, with all parties named in the complaint’s title but only the first party on each side in later pleadings. Averments go in numbered paragraphs limited as far as practicable to a single set of circumstances, separate transactions and defenses go in separate counts when that aids clarity, statements may be adop","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-011. Signing of pleadings, motions, and other papers; sanctions; unsworn affirmations under penalty of perjury","New Mexico · civil rules","Every pleading, motion and paper must be signed by at least one attorney of record (or the self-represented party) with address and telephone number, and the signature certifies that the signer has read it, that to the best of the signer’s knowledge, information and belief there is good ground to support it, and that it is not interposed for delay; unlike current FRCP 11, the rule does not set out a safe-harbor proce","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-012. Defenses and objections; when and how presented; by pleading or motion; motion for judgment on the pleadings","New Mexico · civil rules","An answer is due within 30 days after service of the summons and complaint, an answer to a cross-claim within 30 days after its service, and a reply to a counterclaim within 30 days after service of the answer (or of an order requiring a reply); if a Rule 1-012 motion is denied or deferred, or a more definite statement is served, the responsive pleading is due within 10 days, unlike the 14 days under FRCP 12. The def","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-013. Counterclaim and cross-claim","New Mexico · civil rules","A pleading must state as a counterclaim any claim against an opposing party arising out of the same transaction or occurrence that does not require parties beyond the court’s jurisdiction, unless it was already the subject of another pending action or the opposing party sued by attachment or similar process without personal jurisdiction and the pleader asserts no counterclaim; other claims may be asserted as permissi","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-014. Third-party practice","New Mexico · civil rules","A defending party may at any time serve a summons and third-party complaint on a nonparty who is or may be liable to it for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim, without leave if the third-party complaint is filed no later than 10 days after serving the original answer (unlike the 14 days under FRCP 14) and otherwise only on motion with notice to all parties. The third-party defendant answers under Rules 1-012 and 1-","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-015. Amended and supplemental pleadings","New Mexico · civil rules","A party may amend once as of course before a responsive pleading is served or, if no response is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar, within 20 days after serving the pleading; otherwise amendment requires leave, freely given when justice requires, or the adverse party’s written consent, and a response to an amended pleading is due within the time remaining to respond to the original or 10 days after ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-016. Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","New Mexico · civil rules","The court may hold pretrial conferences to expedite disposition, manage the case, discourage waste, improve trial preparation and facilitate settlement, and, except in categories exempted by local rule, may enter after consultation a scheduling order limiting the time to join parties, amend, file and hear motions, and complete discovery, and providing for electronically stored information, privilege clawback agreemen","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-017. Parties plaintiff and defendant; capacity","New Mexico · civil rules","Actions must be prosecuted by the real party in interest, though executors, administrators, guardians, express trustees, contracting parties for another’s benefit and statutorily authorized parties may sue in their own names, and where an honest mistake was made the court must allow a reasonable time for ratification, joinder or substitution with retroactive effect. Unlike FRCP 17, a wrongful death action under Secti","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-018. Joinder of claims and remedies","New Mexico · civil rules","A party asserting a claim or counterclaim may join as many legal or equitable claims as it has against an opposing party, independently or in the alternative, and multiparty, cross-claim and third-party joinder is allowed when Rules 1-019, 1-020, 1-022, 1-013 and 1-014 are satisfied. Claims formerly cognizable only after another claim concluded may be joined, with relief granted according to the parties’ substantive ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-019. Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","New Mexico · civil rules","A person subject to service must be joined if complete relief cannot be accorded without that person or if the person claims an interest whose disposition in the person’s absence may impair its protection or expose existing parties to a substantial risk of double, multiple or inconsistent obligations; the court orders joinder, and one who refuses to join as plaintiff may be made a defendant or involuntary plaintiff. ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-020. Permissive joinder of parties","New Mexico · civil rules","Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, when the rights to relief asserted arise jointly, severally or in the alternative out of the same transaction, occurrence or series and a common question of law or fact will arise, and no party need be interested in all the relief, with judgment given according to each party’s rights or liabilities. The court may order separate trials or other protective orders to pr","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-021. Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","New Mexico · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may drop or add parties on motion or on its own at any stage on just terms, and any claim against a party may be severed and proceeded with separately.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-022. Interpleader","New Mexico · civil rules","Persons with claims against the plaintiff that expose it to double or multiple liability may be joined as defendants and required to interplead, even if their claims lack a common origin or the plaintiff denies liability, and a defendant may obtain interpleader by cross-claim or counterclaim, supplementing Rule 1-020 joinder. On filing, the district court takes full jurisdiction of the thing in dispute and orders all","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-023. Class actions","New Mexico · civil rules","A class action requires numerosity, commonality, typicality and adequate representation, plus one of the three FRCP 23(b)-style grounds (risk of inconsistent or practically dispositive individual adjudications, generally applicable conduct warranting class-wide injunctive or declaratory relief, or predominance and superiority); the court decides certification by a conditional, amendable order as soon as practicable, ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-023.1. Derivative actions by shareholders","New Mexico · civil rules","A shareholder or member suing derivatively to enforce a right the corporation or unincorporated association has failed to enforce must file a verified complaint alleging contemporaneous ownership at the time of the challenged transaction (or devolution by operation of law) and describing with particularity the efforts made to obtain action from the directors and, if necessary, the shareholders or members, or the reas","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-024. Intervention","New Mexico · civil rules","On timely application, a person must be allowed to intervene when a statute gives an unconditional right or when the person claims an interest in the subject property or transaction that the action may practically impair and that existing parties do not adequately represent. The court may allow intervention when a statute gives a conditional right, when the applicant’s claim or defense shares a common question of law","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-025. Substitution of parties","New Mexico · civil rules","If a party dies and the claim survives, any party or the decedent’s successor or representative may move for substitution, served with a notice of hearing on parties under Rule 1-005 and on nonparties as a summons under Rule 1-004, and the action is dismissed as to the decedent unless the motion is made within 90 days after the death is suggested on the record by a statement served the same way; where the right survi","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-026. General provisions governing discovery","New Mexico · civil rules","Parties may obtain discovery of any nonprivileged information relevant to the subject matter of the action, including inadmissible information reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence (a broader standard than current FRCP 26(b)(1)’s proportional “claim or defense” scope), and must provide all nonprivileged responsive information then known, but the court must limit discovery that is unreasonably cumulativ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-027. Depositions before action or pending appeal","New Mexico · civil rules","A person expecting to be a party to an action that cannot yet be brought may file a verified petition in the district court of the county where any expected adverse party resides, describing the expected action, the facts to be established and reasons to perpetuate them, the expected adverse parties, and the witnesses and their expected testimony, and must serve notice and the petition at least 20 days before the hea","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-028. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","New Mexico · civil rules","In the United States, depositions are taken before an officer authorized to administer oaths where the examination is held or a person appointed by the court, and in a foreign country on notice before a person authorized by local or U.S. law, before a court-commissioned person, or under a letter rogatory issued on application and notice (which need not be shown necessary, and whose evidence is not excluded merely for","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-029. Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","New Mexico · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise or earlier orders conflict, the parties may by written stipulation provide for depositions before any person, at any time or place, on any notice and in any manner, usable like other depositions, and may modify the procedures for other discovery methods.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-030. Depositions upon oral examination","New Mexico · civil rules","After commencement any party may depose any person by oral examination, and the parties must confer in good faith on date, time and place; the plaintiff needs leave to depose before 30 days after service of the summons and complaint on any defendant (or service under Rule 1-004(F)) unless a defendant has sought discovery or the notice states with supporting facts that the deponent will be unavailable or leaving the s","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-030.1. Audiotaped and videotaped depositions","New Mexico · civil rules","Defines “stenographic recording” as simultaneous verbatim reporting and, on request, requires the noticing party to let others review and buy at reasonable cost a copy of an audio or video deposition. Unless ordered or stipulated otherwise, recorded depositions must follow standard procedures (identify attendees on the record, video only the deponent and demonstratives, neutral setting, go off the record only by agre","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-031. Depositions on written questions","New Mexico · civil rules","After the action begins, any party may depose any person on written questions, serving the questions on every other party with a notice naming the deponent (or describing the deponent or class) and the officer; organizations may be deposed under Rule 1-030(B)(6), and a prisoner only by leave of court. Cross-questions are due within 30 days after service of the notice and questions, redirect questions within 10 days a","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-032. Use of depositions in court proceedings","New Mexico · civil rules","Depositions may be used at trial or hearings against parties who were present, represented, or had reasonable notice: for impeachment, by an adverse party for any purpose if the deponent is a party or its officer, director, managing agent, or designee, and by any party for any purpose on stipulation or if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or out of state (unless the offering party procured the absence), 1","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-033. Interrogatories to parties","New Mexico · civil rules","Without leave or written stipulation, a party may serve up to 50 interrogatories, including all discrete subparts, on any other party (more than FRCP 33’s 25), and additional ones are allowed consistent with Rule 1-026(B)(2); in multiparty cases the serving party must notify all appearing parties that interrogatories were served and provide copies on request. Answers and objections are due within 30 days after servic","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-034. Production of documents and things and entry on land for inspection and other purposes","New Mexico · civil rules","A party may request another party to produce documents, electronically stored information, or tangible things for inspection, copying, testing, or sampling, or to permit entry on land, describing items with reasonable particularity and optionally specifying the form for electronically stored information; nonparties are reached by subpoena under Rule 1-045. The written response is due within 30 days after service, or ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-035. Physical and mental examination of persons","New Mexico · civil rules","When the mental or physical condition, including blood group, of a party or of a person in a party’s custody or legal control is in controversy, the court may on motion for good cause, with notice to the person and all parties, order an examination by a suitably licensed or certified examiner, specifying time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner. On request the party causing the exam must deliver a detaile","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-036. Requests for admissions","New Mexico · civil rules","A party may serve written requests to admit the truth of matters within the scope of discovery, including facts, opinions, application of law to fact, and the genuineness of documents (copies attached). A matter is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 30 days after service, or other time the court allows, though a defendant need not respond before 45 days after service of the summons and com","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-037. Failure to make discovery; sanctions","New Mexico · civil rules","A party may move, on reasonable notice, to compel deposition answers, organizational designations, interrogatory answers, or inspection (evasive or incomplete answers count as failures), stating a good faith effort to resolve the dispute and setting out or attaching the requests and responses; the losing side generally pays reasonable expenses including attorney’s fees unless substantially justified, with apportionme","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-038. Jury trial in civil actions","New Mexico · civil rules","A party may demand a jury by serving a written demand after the action begins and no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue, and filing it under Rule 1-005(E); a demand is for six jurors unless twelve are specifically demanded, and after a six-person demand any party may demand twelve within 10 days after that demand or the last pleading, whichever is later. A party demanding six ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-039. Trial by jury or by the court","New Mexico · civil rules","Issues not set for jury trial under Rule 1-038 are tried by the court, but the court may on motion order a jury trial despite a party’s failure to make a timely demand. In actions not triable of right by jury, the court may use an advisory jury on motion or its own initiative, or with both parties’ consent order a jury whose verdict has the same effect as a jury of right.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-040. Assignment of cases for trial and order of trial","New Mexico · civil rules","District courts set cases for trial under Rule 1-016, and a case on a trailing calendar counts as set only if its trial is likely to begin within 2 weeks after the first case on that calendar. Absent a scheduling order, any party may submit a request for trial stating the case is ready and the trial time needed, and a party who disagrees must file a response within 10 days after service explaining why and when the ca","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-041. Dismissal of actions","New Mexico · civil rules","A plaintiff may dismiss without court order by notice before the adverse party serves an answer or other responsive pleading, or by stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared generally; such dismissal is without prejudice unless stated otherwise, but a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits, and other voluntary dismissals require a court order and cannot override ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-042. Consolidation; separate trials","New Mexico · civil rules","When actions involving a common question of law or fact are pending within a judicial district, the court may order a joint hearing or trial, consolidate them, or make orders avoiding unnecessary cost or delay. For convenience, to avoid prejudice, or for expedition and economy, the court may order separate trials of any claims or issues, always preserving any constitutional right to jury trial.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-043. Evidence","New Mexico · civil rules","Trial testimony is taken orally in open court unless another rule provides otherwise. Testimony given in any state or federal court in New Mexico may be used at a later trial or hearing of the same issue between the same parties if the witness is dead or insane, is a nonresident, or cannot be located after diligent effort (the extracted text reads “same issued,” apparently a typo for “same issue”), without limiting e","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-044. Judicial notice and determination of foreign law","New Mexico · civil rules","New Mexico courts must take judicial notice of the meaning of English words and legal expressions, whatever is established by law, official acts of the federal government and the laws of the states and territories as interpreted by their highest courts, court and notary seals, the accession and official signatures of government officers, the titles, flags, and seals of recognized sovereigns, and the laws of nature, t","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-045. Subpoena","New Mexico · civil rules","Subpoenas issue from the court for the district where the case is pending, in the Supreme Court-approved form, signed by the clerk in blank or by a New Mexico attorney for a party, and may command testimony, production of documents, electronically stored information, or things, or inspection of premises. They may be served anywhere in the state by a nonparty at least 18 years old, with tender of one full day’s per di","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-045.1. Interstate subpoenas","New Mexico · civil rules","This rule lets a party in a case pending in another state (including D.C., Puerto Rico, the U.S. Virgin Islands, a federally recognized Indian tribe, or a U.S. territory) obtain a New Mexico subpoena for a deposition, documents, electronically stored information, things, or premises inspection by submitting the foreign subpoena to the clerk of the district court where discovery is to occur, which is not an appearance","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-046. Preserving questions for review","New Mexico · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary; a party preserves an issue by telling the court, when the ruling is made or sought, the action desired or the objection and its grounds, and a party with no opportunity to object is not prejudiced by the lack of objection. A motion for new trial is not needed to preserve errors brought to the trial court’s attention, and the rule applies to both jury and bench trials.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-047. Jurors","New Mexico · civil rules","The court may let the parties or their attorneys examine prospective jurors or may conduct the examination itself, allowing supplemental questions as it deems proper. The court may seat up to 6 alternate jurors, who replace regular jurors in order before deliberations and are discharged when the jury retires; each side gets 1 extra peremptory challenge for 1 or 2 alternates, 2 for 3 or 4, and 3 for 5 or 6, usable onl","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-048. Juries of fewer than twelve; stipulation","New Mexico · civil rules","Notwithstanding Rule 1-038, the parties may stipulate to a jury of any number fewer than twelve or that a stated majority of jurors’ verdict or finding will be the jury’s verdict or finding.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-049. Special verdicts and interrogatories","New Mexico · civil rules","The court may require a special verdict of written findings on each fact issue, and any issue omitted without a party’s demand before the jury retires is waived as to jury trial and may be found by the court or deemed found consistently with the judgment. The court may instead submit a general verdict with written interrogatories: consistent answers and verdict yield judgment; answers consistent with each other but i","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-050. Judgment as a matter of law in jury trials; alternative motion for new trial; conditional rulings","New Mexico · civil rules","During a jury trial, after a party has been fully heard on an issue, the court may grant judgment as a matter of law if a reasonable jury would lack a legally sufficient evidentiary basis to find for that party; the motion may be made any time before submission to the jury and must specify the judgment sought and the supporting law and facts. A renewed motion must be filed no later than 30 days after entry of judgmen","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-051. Instructions to juries","New Mexico · civil rules","The judge must instruct the jury in the language of the Uniform Jury Instructions (UJI Civil) unless the parties waive instructions, using UJI Civil wherever applicable unless the court finds and states on the record why it is erroneous or improper, and must follow UJI directions not to instruct on a subject unless it states reasons otherwise; non-UJI instructions must be brief, impartial, and free of hypothesized fa","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-052. Nonjury trials; findings and conclusions","New Mexico · civil rules","In bench trials or trials with an advisory jury, the court enters findings of fact and conclusions of law only on a party’s timely request (unlike FRCP 52, which requires them in every such case), and none are needed on motions under Rules 1-012, 1-050, 1-056, or others except as provided in Rule 1-041(B). Unless the court orders otherwise, a party requests findings by filing proposed findings and conclusions no late","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-053. Masters","New Mexico · civil rules","A court may appoint a special master (including a referee, auditor, or examiner), with compensation fixed by the court and enforceable by writ of execution, but reference is the exception: in jury cases only when issues are complicated, and in nonjury cases only for accounts, difficult damage computations, or an exceptional condition. Unless the order provides otherwise, the master must set a first meeting within 20 ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-053.1. Domestic violence special commissioners; duties","New Mexico · civil rules","Domestic violence special commissioners are at-will employees who must be New Mexico-licensed lawyers with at least 3 years of practice and knowledge of domestic relations and domestic violence, and who review petitions and motions under the Family Violence Protection Act, conduct on-the-record interviews and hearings, and prepare recommendations that take effect only when adopted by the district court; a commissione","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-053.2. Domestic relations hearing officers; duties","New Mexico · civil rules","Domestic relations hearing officers are at-will employees with the qualifications of a child support hearing officer under Section 40-4B-4 NMSA 1978 who may review indigency petitions, hear pre- and post-decree petitions and motions, act as child support hearing officers and domestic violence special commissioners, assist with the Domestic Relations Mediation Act, and prepare recommendations that take effect only whe","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-053.3. Guardians ad litem; domestic relations appointments","New Mexico · civil rules","In contested custody proceedings under Chapter 40 NMSA 1978, the court may appoint a guardian ad litem as a “best interests attorney” and arm of the court, by an order substantially in Form 4-402 specifying the role, duties, reasons, duration, retainer, hourly rate, monthly itemized billing, and fee allocation, and requiring the parties and children over 14 to sign requested releases; the court considers 17 listed fa","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-054. Judgments; costs","New Mexico · civil rules","A judgment includes a decree and any appealable order, and in multi-claim or multi-party cases the court may enter final judgment on fewer than all claims or parties only on an express finding of no just reason for delay; default judgments may not differ in kind from or exceed the demand, while other judgments grant the relief to which the party is entitled even if not demanded. Costs other than attorney fees go to t","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-054.1. Judgments and orders; time limit","New Mexico · civil rules","Notwithstanding Section 39-1-1 NMSA 1978, the court must enter a judgment or order within 60 days after submission, meaning the time the court takes the matter under advisement.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-054.2. Judgments in residential foreclosure actions; certification concerning the absence of loss mitigation negotiations required; definition","New Mexico · civil rules","Before a district court enters judgment in a residential foreclosure, the plaintiff must file a certification, substantially in Form 4-712, concerning the absence of loss mitigation negotiations with the borrower; the rule does not apply to lien foreclosures. “Residential” means property designed principally for occupancy by 1 to 4 families and occupied by the defendant as the defendant’s principal residence.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-055. Default","New Mexico · civil rules","The clerk enters the default of a party who has failed to plead or otherwise defend when shown by affidavit or otherwise, but default judgment must always be sought from the court, cannot be entered against a minor or incompetent person without an appearing representative, and requires written notice at least 3 days before the hearing to a defaulting party who has appeared (an appearance with disclaimer of interest d","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-056. Summary judgment","New Mexico · civil rules","A claimant or defending party may move for summary judgment on all or part of a claim, with or without affidavits, and it is granted if the record shows no genuine issue of material fact and entitlement to judgment as a matter of law (liability alone may be decided), with the order specifying the grounds when alternative grounds are presented; motions must be filed a reasonable time before trial. The movant files a m","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-057. Declaratory judgments","New Mexico · civil rules","Declaratory judgment actions follow these rules, with jury trial available under Rules 1-038 and 1-039, the existence of another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the court may order a speedy hearing. When the State of New Mexico is a party, the summons and complaint must be personally served on both the governor and the attorney general, and the state must answer or plead and serve a copy within 2","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-058. Orders and judgments; preparation and entry","New Mexico · civil rules","When the court announces a decision, it either allows counsel a reasonable time it fixes to submit a proposed order or judgment, designates counsel to prepare it by a set time, or prepares its own; if no satisfactory form is submitted in time, the court takes steps to have one entered promptly. Counsel must have a reasonable opportunity to examine and object before the court signs, and filing in the clerk’s office af","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-059. New trials; motions directed against the judgment","New Mexico · civil rules","A new trial may be granted on all or some issues for any traditionally recognized reason after a jury trial, and after a bench trial the court may reopen the judgment, take more testimony, revise findings and conclusions, and enter a new judgment. A motion for new trial, and a motion to alter, amend, or reconsider a final judgment, must be filed not later than 30 days after entry of judgment (unlike the 28 days under","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-060. Relief from judgment or order","New Mexico · civil rules","Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time on the court’s own initiative or on motion, but once an appeal is docketed only with leave of the appellate court. On motion the court may relieve a party from a final judgment for (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect, (2) newly discovered evidence, (3) fraud or misconduct of an adverse party, (4) a void judgment, (5) satisfaction, reversal of a prio","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-061. Harmless error","New Mexico · civil rules","No error or defect by the court or parties is ground for a new trial or for disturbing a verdict, judgment, or order unless refusing to act appears inconsistent with substantial justice. The court must disregard at every stage any error that does not affect the parties’ substantial rights.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-062. Stay of proceeding to enforce a judgment","New Mexico · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, execution may issue as soon as judgment is entered (unlike FRCP 62(a), the text provides no automatic waiting period), and injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed pending appeal; the court may stay enforcement pending post-trial motions under Rules 1-050, 1-052(D), 1-059, or 1-060 and may suspend, modify, or grant an injunction pending appeal on bond terms. An appellant","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-063. Inability of a judge to proceed","New Mexico · civil rules","If a judge cannot continue a commenced trial or hearing, another judge may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record and determining the case can be completed without prejudice to the parties. The successor judge may recall any witness.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-064. Seizure of person or property","New Mexico · civil rules","From the start of and throughout an action, all state-law remedies for seizing a person or property to secure satisfaction of the eventual judgment are available, under the circumstances and in the manner state law provides.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-065. Writs issued by district courts","New Mexico · civil rules","The clerk may issue writs of execution, writs of possession under Section 42-4-12, and writs of attachment on land without judicial approval; all other writs (apart from those under Rules 1-065.1 and 1-065.2) require the judge’s express written approval endorsed on the writ and are sought by verified petition stating jurisdiction, standing, real parties in interest, grounds, and relief. The writ replaces the summons,","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-065.1. Writs of execution","New Mexico · civil rules","Unless the judgment is stayed, the clerk issues a writ of execution on timely application at any time against a non-natural-person debtor, and against a natural person only on a certificate or affidavit that the exemption notice was served and no claim filed, an order finding the property not exempt, or the debtor’s written waiver describing the property. The creditor must serve a natural-person debtor with a notice ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-065.2. Garnishment","New Mexico · civil rules","After judgment and on timely application the clerk issues a writ of garnishment (medical-debt judgments require an application complying with Form 4-805B), which the creditor serves on the garnishee under Rule 1-004 together with the application, exemption notice and claim form, and answer form, while mailing a copy to the debtor and debtor’s counsel and filing a certificate of service. The garnishee must answer with","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-066. Injunctions and receivers","New Mexico · civil rules","No preliminary injunction or receiver may issue without notice, and the court may consolidate the merits trial with the preliminary-injunction hearing, preserving jury rights. A temporary restraining order without notice requires an affidavit or verified complaint showing immediate irreparable injury and an attorney’s certification of notice efforts; it must state date and hour, define the injury, and expire within a","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-067. Deposit in court","New Mexico · civil rules","When relief sought includes a sum of money or a deliverable thing, a party may, on notice to every other party and with leave of court, deposit all or part of the sum or thing with the court.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-068. Offer of settlement","New Mexico · civil rules","Unlike FRCP 68, any party, including a claimant, may serve an offer to allow judgment on specified terms at any time more than 10 days before trial, but a claimant may not make an offer until 120 days after the defending party files a responsive pleading; the offeree has 10 days after service to accept in writing, after which either party may file the offer and acceptance for entry of judgment, and an unaccepted offe","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-069. Judgment; supplementary proceedings","New Mexico · civil rules","On request of the judgment creditor or a successor, the clerk issues a subpoena requiring any person with knowledge useful for enforcement, including the debtor, to appear before the district court for examination, served at least 3 days before the examination. In lieu of the examination, the creditor may take discovery from any person, including the debtor, by any method in these rules.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-070. Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","New Mexico · civil rules","If a party fails to perform a judgment-directed act (such as conveying land or delivering deeds) within the time specified, the court may have it done by an appointee at the disobedient party’s cost with the same effect, the clerk shall issue a writ of attachment or sequestration on application, and the court may hold the party in contempt. For property within the state the court may instead enter a judgment divestin","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-071. Process in behalf of and against persons not parties","New Mexico · civil rules","A nonparty in whose favor an order is made may enforce it by the same process as a party, and a nonparty against whom an order may lawfully be enforced is subject to the same enforcement process as a party.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-071.1. Statutory stream system adjudication suits; service and joinder of water rights claimants; responses","New Mexico · civil rules","In stream system adjudications under Section 72-4-17, the court may order the plaintiff to join water rights claimants by stream-system subsections, which the plaintiff does by serving a court-approved proposed consent order or other document conspicuously notifying the claimant of the duty to respond. Service follows Rule 1-004 except that the plaintiff issues and signs the summons, and service or a waiver of servic","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-071.2. Statutory stream system adjudication suits; stream system issue and expedited inter se proceedings","New Mexico · civil rules","Before notice of commencement of inter se proceedings, any party (or the court sua sponte) may seek designation of a “stream system issue,” which the court must designate after a hearing if its resolution could directly affect all or a significant number of claimants or if a non-binding resolution would risk inconsistent decisions or practically preclude similarly situated claimants. The plaintiff or any claimant may","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-071.3. Statutory stream system adjudication suits; annual joint working session","New Mexico · civil rules","Thirty (30) days before the end of each fiscal year, the judges, special masters, the state, and other parties in each stream adjudication court must set a joint working session on common issues and resource needs, and state judges must invite federal adjudication judges and special masters. The state must file a report of its suggested priorities and resource analysis for each pending state adjudication 30 days befo","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-071.4. Statutory stream system adjudication suits; ex parte contacts; general problems of administration","New Mexico · civil rules","Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 21-209(A) applies to stream adjudications, except that judges, special masters, and their staff may communicate with the plaintiff on matters not addressing the merits that concern general administration and management of a pending or impending adjudication or accurate reporting of water rights claims in court records.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-071.5. Statutory stream system adjudication suits; excusal or recusal of a water judge","New Mexico · civil rules","Water judges, including pro tem and sitting judges assigned to stream adjudications, are designated by the chief justice; under Rule 1-088(E) they cannot be peremptorily excused. If a water judge is excused for cause or recuses, the chief justice reassigns the matter to another designated water judge.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-072. Appeal from magistrate and metropolitan courts in trial de novo cases","New Mexico · civil rules","An aggrieved party in a magistrate or metropolitan court civil action appeals to the district court of that county by filing a notice of appeal (with the lower-court judgment attached and proof of Rule 1-005 service) within 15 days after the judgment or final order is filed, with any other party having 10 days after service of the first notice or the original period, whichever is later; the 3-day mailing period does ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-073. Withdrawn","New Mexico · civil rules","Withdrawn; no current rule text.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-074. Administrative appeals; statutory review by district court of administrative decisions or orders","New Mexico · civil rules","This rule governs appeals to the district court from state or local agencies where a statute grants review (it creates no right to appeal and excludes non-statutory review under Rule 1-075, Human Rights Act appeals, unemployment compensation appeals, and Article XVI, Section 5 water-rights matters). Unless a statute or local ordinance sets another time (and a conflicting statutory period controls), the notice of appe","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-075. Constitutional review by district court of administrative decisions and orders","New Mexico · civil rules","Where no statutory right of appeal or review exists, an aggrieved party may seek review of a final state or local agency decision by filing a petition for writ of certiorari (stating jurisdiction, the agency proceedings, the parties, entitlement to relief, and relief sought, with the decision attached) within 30 days after the final decision; other parties may petition within 10 days after service of the first petiti","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-076. Appeals from Human Rights Commission","New Mexico · civil rules","De novo appeals from the Human Rights Commission are taken by filing a notice of appeal in the form of a civil complaint, by any person aggrieved by a commission order or, after the director serves a waiver of the right to hearing, by the complainant; the complaint may add new issues and new parties (served under Rule 1-004). The appeal must be filed within 90 days after service of the commission’s order or of the di","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-077. Appeals pursuant to Unemployment Compensation Law","New Mexico · civil rules","Appeals from final decisions of the board of review or the secretary of the Department of Workforce Solutions under Section 51-1-8 are taken by a petition for writ of certiorari, filed in the county where the petitioner resides (another county’s court may hear it if equity and due process require), within 30 days from the final decision, with no 3-day mailing period. The petition, served on the Department’s Office of","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-077.1. Expungement","New Mexico · civil rules","Expungement under the Criminal Record Expungement Act (Sections 29-3A-1 to -9, except automatic cannabis expungement under Section 29-3A-8) is commenced by a civil petition on mandatory forms (Forms 4-951 through 4-960.3), filed in the county where charges originated, the arrest occurred, or the conviction was entered depending on the type (identity theft, no conviction, or conviction); one petition may cover any num","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-077.2. Water court structure and procedure","New Mexico · civil rules","The state is divided into three water regions (WR1: First, Second, Eleventh, and Thirteenth Judicial Districts; WR2: Third, Sixth, and Seventh; WR3: Fourth, Fifth, Eighth, Ninth, Tenth, and Twelfth), and the Supreme Court appoints 5 district judges as water judges (one in WR1, two in WR2, one in WR3, and one alternate), with currently designated judges continuing until replaced. Water judges cannot be excused under R","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-078. Motion day","New Mexico · civil rules","Unless local conditions make it impracticable, each district court must set regular, sufficiently frequent times and places to hear motions requiring notice and hearing, but the judge may at any time, on such notice as the judge considers reasonable, make orders advancing, conducting, and hearing actions.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-079. Public inspection and sealing of court records","New Mexico · civil rules","Court records (filed or lodged material plus the register of actions and docket entries) are presumptively public unless sealed by order or protected by the rule, and no one may knowingly file material taken from a sealed, conditionally sealed, or pending-motion record. All records are automatically sealed without motion in 11 listed proceeding types, including adoption, Section 24-1-15 detention, Section 24-2B-5.1 t","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-079.1. Public inspection and sealing of court records; guardianship and conservatorship proceedings","New Mexico · civil rules","This rule supplements Rule 1-079’s automatic sealing of guardianship (Chapter 45, Article 5, Part 3) and conservatorship (Part 4) proceedings, with all other access matters, including motions to seal or unseal, still governed by Rule 1-079. All records are automatically sealed subject to firearm reporting under Section 34-9-19, except that the register of actions and docket entries remain public without medical or ps","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-080. Stenographer; stenographic report or transcript as evidence","New Mexico · civil rules","A master may have evidence taken stenographically and appoint a stenographer whose fees the court fixes and may tax as costs, and the court may order recording by other means with safeguards, though a party may still arrange a stenographic transcript at its own expense. Stenographically reported testimony admissible at a later trial may be proved by a transcript certified by the reporter.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-081. Remand to district court from federal court","New Mexico · civil rules","After a case removed to federal court is remanded, no default judgment may be entered until 10 days after service on the defendants of notice that the remand order has been filed, and within that time defendants may move or plead as if the case had not been removed.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-082. Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","New Mexico · civil rules","The rules do not extend or limit district court jurisdiction or the venue of actions.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-083. Withdrawn","New Mexico · civil rules","Withdrawn; no current rule text.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-084. Bankruptcy proceedings; stay","New Mexico · civil rules","A party must file written notice of any bankruptcy court stay that may affect the pending action upon becoming aware of it, and likewise must file written notice of the stay’s termination or modification upon becoming aware of it.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-085. Judgments or orders on mandate","New Mexico · civil rules","Within 30 days after the appellate court sends its mandate to the district court, the prevailing party on appeal must present a proposed judgment or order containing the appellate court’s specific directions or, if necessary, request a hearing. The proposed judgment or order must be served on all parties.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-086. Repealing and saving clause","New Mexico · civil rules","Existing Supreme Court rules of pleading, practice, and procedure for courts other than the Supreme Court that are not contained in these rules remain in force unless superseded, modified, or repealed by these rules.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-087. Contest of nomination or election","New Mexico · civil rules","Under Section 1-14-1, any unsuccessful candidate may contest a nomination or election by filing a verified complaint in the district court of the county where a party resides no later than 30 days after issuance of the certificate of nomination or election, with the general civil rules applying where not inconsistent. The contestee must file and serve a verified answer within 15 days after service, and any peremptory","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-088. Designation of judge","New Mexico · civil rules","The trial judge is designated when the complaint is filed under local rule, and when a judge is excused or recused the clerk randomly assigns a judge from another division, or, if all judges in the district are excused or recused, notifies the chief justice, who designates a judge, justice, or judge pro tempore. Civil cases by or against a judge or employee of the district are automatically assigned to a judge from a","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-088.1. Peremptory excusal of a district judge; recusal; procedure for exercising","New Mexico · civil rules","Each party may peremptorily excuse no more than one judge, and not after attending a hearing before or requesting any act of that judge other than free process or an indigency determination; aligned co-plaintiffs or co-defendants count as one party when they share a lawyer or firm, file joint pleadings, are spouses, parents, children, or siblings, are a business or organization with its owners, parents, subsidiaries,","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-089. Entry of appearance; withdrawal or substitution of attorneys","New Mexico · civil rules","An attorney representing a party must file an entry of appearance (unless appointed by court order), and filing a signed pleading counts as one; an attorney with a limited appearance under Rule 16-102(C) must file a “Limited Entry of Appearance” describing the limitation and note the limitation and a service address for the party in every signature block. A court order is needed to withdraw after an unlimited appeara","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-089.1. Nonadmitted and nonresident counsel","New Mexico · civil rules","Lawyers licensed and in good standing in another state or country may, after complying with Rule 24-106, appear only in association with New Mexico counsel, who must attend all proceedings in person unless excused; the out-of-state lawyer files an affidavit of good standing and Rule 24-106 compliance with the first paper filed (or as soon as practicable after being retained) and is then deemed admitted without any se","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-090. Conduct of court proceedings","New Mexico · civil rules","Judicial proceedings exist to find the truth and should be conducted with dignity and decorum. Ceremonial (nonjudicial) proceedings held by judges in open court may be photographed or broadcast from the courtroom with the court’s permission and under its supervision.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-091. Adopting procedural statutes","New Mexico · civil rules","Statutes on pleading, practice and procedure in effect when the 1933 act (L. 1933, ch. 84; Sections 38-1-1 and 38-1-2 NMSA 1978) took effect, and those enacted since, remain in force as rules of court except to the extent the Supreme Court has amended or vacated them or does so in the future.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-092. Nonstenographic recording","New Mexico · civil rules","On its own motion or a party’s motion, the district court may order that all or part of the record be made by nonstenographic means; the order must designate the portions so recorded and the method of recording and preservation, and may include safeguards for accuracy, such as also using a court reporter.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-093. Punitive contempt","New Mexico · civil rules","Punitive contempt arising in civil proceedings is governed by Rule 5-112 NMRA (the criminal-procedure contempt rule), as reinstated and amended for all cases pending or filed on or after October 9, 2025.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-094. Clinical education; university of New Mexico school of law","New Mexico · civil rules","Students admitted to the UNM School of Law clinical program may advise clients, negotiate and appear in civil and criminal matters before state courts and agencies under the active supervision of a State Bar member designated by the dean, who must assign all matters, review all documents, sign all pleadings and be present at court appearances (though not during advising or negotiating). A student may appear in a give","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-094.1. Clinical education; out-of-state law school approved programs","New Mexico · civil rules","Students regularly enrolled at an ABA-accredited law school may practice in New Mexico courts and agencies in civil and criminal matters under the active supervision of a State Bar member admitted for at least 5 years, who must assign matters, review documents, sign pleadings and attend court appearances, and each student needs the presiding judge’s written approval, filed with the dean’s certificate. The dean’s cert","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-095. Informal probate proceedings in probate court","New Mexico · civil rules","For informal probate proceedings filed in probate court, the probate court must notify the district court clerk in writing of the case style, filer and attorney, any personal representative appointed, and, on closing, send a copy of the full docket sheet; after that it must promptly report each later filing (with its type and date, and any change in the personal representative’s name or address). Certified copies of ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-096. Challenge of nominating petition","New Mexico · civil rules","A challenge to a nominating petition under the Primary Election Law must be filed as a complaint with a request for expedited hearing within 10 days after the last day to file the declaration of candidacy, served under Rule 1-004 on the proper filing officer, and immediately delivered both to the assigned judge and to the candidate in a manner reasonably calculated to give actual notice. Each challenged signature mus","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-096.1. Review of election recall petitions","New Mexico · civil rules","Before circulating a petition to recall an elected county official under Article X, Section 9 of the New Mexico Constitution, the proponent must file a district court complaint attaching the proposed petition and stating the alleged malfeasance, misfeasance or oath violation; a challenge to a local school board member recall petition must be filed within 10 days after the county clerk decides whether enough signature","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-097. Eminent domain; notice of presentation of petition; service","New Mexico · civil rules","A copy of an eminent domain petition and notice of when and where it will be presented must be served on each owner at least 10 days before the presentation date, either personally or, for an absent in-state owner, by leaving it at the owner’s usual abode with a resident over 15 years old; service may be made by any disinterested person over 18, proven by affidavit. For unknown, nonresident or unfound owners, notice ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-098. Withdrawn","New Mexico · civil rules","This rule has been withdrawn; no text is in effect.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-099. District court civil filing fees","New Mexico · civil rules","The docket fee prescribed by law is charged for docketing any civil cause, whether original, reopened, or on appeal or transfer from a court of limited jurisdiction, except that no fee is charged for papers filed within 90 days after final disposition, for a motion to correct a mistake in the judgment, order or record, or, if a docket fee was already paid or waived in the case, for a stipulated order or other request","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-100. Form of papers","New Mexico · civil rules","Except exhibits and electronically filed papers under Rule 1-005.2, papers filed in district court must be legible, printed on one side of good-quality white 8½ x 11 inch paper with 1-inch left and right margins and 1½-inch top and bottom margins, consecutively page-numbered at the bottom, stapled at the upper left, and (except a cover page) in pica (10 pitch) type or 12-point typeface, double-spaced except quotation","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-101. Reserved","New Mexico · civil rules","This rule number is reserved; no rule is in effect.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-102. Deposit of litigant funds","New Mexico · civil rules","Litigant funds deposited with the court must be placed within 2 business days in one or more trust checking accounts at an FDIC-member bank, separate from general court funds, and invested under Section 34-6-36 (or, for Section 42A-1-19 deposits, in federal securities or federally insured accounts within the judicial district). Interest goes to the person entitled to the principal only for a single deposit of $25,000","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-103. Court interpreters","New Mexico · civil rules","A court interpreter must be appointed whenever a case participant cannot communicate in English well enough to participate fully, including on request or when the court observes the need; a party must give notice of its own need when filing its complaint, petition, answer or other responsive pleading, and of a witness’s need in writing on service of a notice of hearing (stating whether the proceeding will exceed 2 ho","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-104. Courtroom closure","New Mexico · civil rules","All courtroom proceedings are open unless closed automatically (adoption hearings, communicable-disease detention, testing proceedings under Section 24-2B-5.1, and Uniform Parentage Act pretrial proceedings) or by written order, and the parties’ agreement alone is not enough to close a courtroom. A party or interested person must file and serve a motion to close at least 45 days before the proceeding unless the court","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-105. Notice to statutory beneficiaries in wrongful death cases","New Mexico · civil rules","After a personal representative is appointed under the Wrongful Death Act, the personal representative must give notice under Rule 1-004 to all known or reasonably ascertainable statutory beneficiaries. The notice must give the personal representative’s name and the lawyer’s contact information, acknowledge the duty to act in all beneficiaries’ best interests, instruct beneficiaries to keep their contact information ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-106. Enforcement of mediated settlement agreement","New Mexico · civil rules","When parties settle by a mediated agreement requiring performance over time without a stipulated judgment, they file a stipulation of dismissal and a signed written agreement (unless they agree in writing to waive filing it, in which case they must keep and produce their own copies), and the court closes the case but keeps jurisdiction to reopen and enforce it. Within 5 years of the stipulation of dismissal, a party ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-120. Domestic relations actions; scope; mandatory use of court-approved forms by self-represented litigants","New Mexico · civil rules","Rules 1-120 to 1-128.13 supplement the civil rules for domestic relations actions, and self-represented litigants must use Forms 4A-100 through 4A-315 in dissolution, custody and child support cases (4A-100 to 4A-105 to file and respond, 4A-200 to 4A-215 for temporary relief, 4A-300 to 4A-306 for final orders, and 4A-310 to 4A-315 for default) and Forms 4A-501 through 4A-513 in kinship guardianship cases; courts may ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-121. Temporary domestic orders","New Mexico · civil rules","In original domestic relations actions where a summons issues, the clerk files a temporary domestic order in substantially the Supreme Court-approved form with the summons, and the petitioner must serve an endorsed copy on the respondent; no order is entered in state child support actions or if the court orders otherwise, and added restrictions beyond the form require notice and hearing. The order binds the petitione","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-122. Dissolution of marriage and Section 40-4-3 NMSA 1978 proceedings; interim order allocating income and expenses","New Mexico · civil rules","Absent exceptional circumstances, community income and expenses are divided equally while a dissolution or Section 40-4-3 proceeding is pending, and separate income and expenses may also be divided on motion. Unless the parties stipulate to waive it, a party may move for an interim allocation order, or the court may set a hearing on its own, and the parties must exchange the information in Form 4A-212 at least 5 days","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-123. Mandatory disclosure in domestic relations and paternity actions; preliminary disclosure requirements","New Mexico · civil rules","Unless stipulated and ordered otherwise, in cases involving property or debt division the parties must serve within 45 days after service of the petition a disclosure under Form 4A-212 containing an interim income and expense statement and community and separate property and liability schedules (Forms 4A-212, 4A-214 and 4A-215) with a list of supporting documents. In spousal or child support cases, each side must ser","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-124. Child custody; parenting plans; binding arbitration","New Mexico · civil rules","In domestic relations cases involving custody or visitation, the parties must try to agree on and file a joint parenting plan under Section 40-4-9.1 within 60 days of the filing of the dissolution petition. If no plan is filed, they may agree to binding arbitration under Section 40-4-7.2, and otherwise the court may refer them to family counseling or mediation before a custody or visitation hearing.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-125. Domestic Relations Mediation Act programs","New Mexico · civil rules","In districts with Domestic Relations Mediation Act programs, if no parenting plan is filed and no binding arbitration is pending, the court may order parties to attend an information session, meet a counselor, mediate, or participate in priority or advisory consultation; successful mediation yields a parenting plan submitted for approval. Objections to priority or advisory consultation recommendations must be filed b","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-126. Partial decrees","New Mexico · civil rules","A partial decree dissolving a marriage is granted only on a showing of exceptional circumstances. The motion must address the decree’s effect on the parties and children, including medical coverage, custody, income division, child and spousal support and taxes, and the decree may include provisions needed to protect the parties and minor children.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-127. Attorney fees in domestic relations cases","New Mexico · civil rules","A Rule 1-054 motion for attorney fees in a domestic relations case must itemize time spent and affirm that the fees are correct and necessary. The court considers relevant factors including disparity in resources, prior settlement offers, total fees and costs each party spent (including community funds used, balances due and any interim advances ordered), and success on the merits.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-128. Uniform collaborative law rules; short title; definitions; applicability","New Mexico · civil rules","Rules 1-128 to 1-128.13 are the Uniform Collaborative Law Rules and apply to collaborative law participation agreements meeting Rule 1-128.1. The rule defines key terms, including a “collaborative matter” as a dispute or issue arising under Chapter 40 NMSA 1978 (such as divorce, property division, custody, support, adoption, parentage, and premarital or marital agreements) and a “collaborative law communication” as o","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-128.1. Collaborative law participation agreement; requirements","New Mexico · civil rules","A collaborative law participation agreement must be in a signed record and state the parties’ intent to use the collaborative law process, describe the matter, name each collaborative lawyer, and include each lawyer’s confirmation of the representation. The parties may add other provisions consistent with the rules.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-128.2. Initiation of collaborative law process; voluntary participation; conclusion; termination; notice of discharge or withdrawal of collaborative lawyer; continuation with successor collaborative lawyer","New Mexico · civil rules","The collaborative process begins when the agreement is signed, a tribunal may not order a party into it over objection, and it concludes on full or agreed partial resolution in a signed record, by a method in the agreement, or by termination. Any party may terminate with or without cause, and the process terminates when a party gives notice in a record, starts a related proceeding or seeks tribunal action without eve","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-128.3. Proceedings pending before tribunal; status report; dismissal","New Mexico · civil rules","Parties to a pending proceeding who sign a collaborative agreement must promptly file a certificate or stipulated order of abatement, signed by both counsel and both parties (or it has no effect), stating that they are making significant progress or attempting reconciliation and the case status, which tolls all deadlines. When the process concludes, the parties must promptly file and serve a notice (without stating r","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-128.4. Emergency order","New Mexico · civil rules","Despite a pending collaborative law process, a tribunal may issue any order under the Family Violence Protection Act, Sections 40-13-1 to -12 NMSA 1978.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-128.5. Adoption of agreement by tribunal","New Mexico · civil rules","A tribunal may adopt any agreement resulting from a collaborative law process as its order.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-128.6. Disqualification of collaborative lawyer and lawyers in associated law firm","New Mexico · civil rules","A collaborative lawyer, and any lawyer in the collaborative lawyer’s law firm, is disqualified from representing a party before a tribunal in a proceeding related to the collaborative matter. The only exception is representing a party to seek an order adopting an agreement reached in the collaborative process.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-128.7. Disclosure of information","New Mexico · civil rules","Unless other law provides otherwise, during the collaborative process a party must, on request, make timely, full, candid and informal disclosure of information related to the matter without formal discovery and promptly update information that has materially changed; the parties may define the scope of disclosure.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-128.8. Standards of professional responsibility and mandatory reporting not affected","New Mexico · civil rules","The collaborative law rules do not affect lawyers’ or other licensed professionals’ professional obligations, or anyone’s duty under New Mexico law to report abuse, neglect, abandonment or exploitation of a child or adult.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-128.9. Appropriateness of collaborative law process","New Mexico · civil rules","Before a prospective party signs a collaborative agreement, the prospective collaborative lawyer must assess whether the process is appropriate (including any history of a coercive or violent relationship) and explain its material benefits and risks compared with alternatives such as litigation, mediation or arbitration. The lawyer must also advise that seeking tribunal intervention terminates the process, participat","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-128.10. Coercive or violent relationship","New Mexico · civil rules","Before a prospective party signs a collaborative agreement, the prospective collaborative lawyer must reasonably inquire whether the party has a history of a coercive or violent relationship with another prospective party, and throughout the process the lawyer must continuously and reasonably assess whether such a history exists.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-128.11. Confidentiality of collaborative law communication","New Mexico · civil rules","A collaborative law communication is confidential to the extent the parties agree in a signed record or as provided by other New Mexico law.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-128.12. Privilege against disclosure for collaborative law communication; admissibility; discovery","New Mexico · civil rules","A party (or a guardian, conservator or personal representative of a party, or a nonparty participant as to that participant’s own communications) may refuse to disclose, and prevent others from disclosing, a collaborative law communication, though otherwise admissible or discoverable evidence does not become privileged merely by use in the process. The privilege is waived only expressly by all parties, in a record or","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-128.13. Authority of tribunal in case of noncompliance","New Mexico · civil rules","Even if Rules 1-128.1, 1-128.9 or 1-128.10 were not complied with, a tribunal may enforce an agreement, apply the Rule 1-128.6 disqualification, or apply the Rule 1-128.12 privilege if it finds the parties signed a record showing intent to enter a collaborative agreement, reasonably believed they were in a collaborative process, and the interests of justice require treating them as such.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-129. Proceedings under the Family Violence Protection Act","New Mexico · civil rules","The first Family Violence Protection Act petition between two parties proceeds under the civil rules as the original proceeding. Any later petition between the same parties is filed in that original proceeding under the same case number, treated as a paper in the case if it is still pending, or, if closed, the clerk reactivates the original case number and returns the matter to the original judge or that judge’s succ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-130. Appointment of a treatment guardian","New Mexico · civil rules","When a professional or physician has proposed mental health treatment and someone believes the respondent lacks capacity to make treatment decisions, an authorized interested person may petition under Section 43-1-15 for a treatment guardian; the petition must include detailed information (diagnosis, symptoms, location, treatment provider and proposed treatment, any emergency medications, the proposed guardian and th","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-131. Notice of federal restriction on right to possess or receive a firearm or ammunition","New Mexico · civil rules","Certain orders must include written notice that the person is barred by 18 U.S.C. § 922(g)(4) from receiving or possessing firearms or ammunition, that the Administrative Office of the Courts must report the person to the FBI’s National Instant Criminal Background Check System under Section 34-9-19(B), and that the person may petition under Section 34-9-19 to restore the right and remove the listing. The orders are t","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-140. Guardianship and conservatorship proceedings; mandatory use of forms","New Mexico · civil rules","On appointing a guardian or conservator, the court must file a separate Form 4-993 order identifying who is entitled to notice and access to records, and on appointing a conservator a separate Form 4-994 order requiring or waiving bond under Section 45-5-411. Unless waived, a conservator must file a Form 4-995 notice of bonding, with a Form 4-995.1 corporate surety statement, each time an inventory or report is filed","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-141. Guardianship and conservatorship proceedings; determination of persons entitled to notice of proceedings or access to court records","New Mexico · civil rules","Any court determination of who is entitled to notice of a guardianship or conservatorship proceeding or access to its records must be made by a separate written order that addresses no other matter.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-142. Guardianship and conservatorship proceedings; proof of certification of professional guardians and conservators","New Mexico · civil rules","A professional guardian or conservator is one serving more than two unrelated individuals (not related by marriage, adoption, or third degree of blood or affinity). The order appointing one must require proof that the individual assigned the duties is certified and in good standing with the Center for Guardianship Certification, due by the earlier of 90 days after the appointment order or the filing of the initial re","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-143. Guardianship and conservatorship proceedings; appointment of visitor, qualified health care professional, and guardian ad litem; timing and review of reports","New Mexico · civil rules","On a petition to appoint a guardian or conservator, the court must appoint a qualified health care professional, a visitor and, if necessary, a guardian ad litem, with all time periods counted in calendar days under Rule 1-006(A)(1). Reports are due no later than 14 days before the hearing (health care professional), 11 days before (visitor) and 7 days before (guardian ad litem), and the petitioner must provide copie","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-144. Guardianship and conservatorship proceedings; mandatory viewing of New Mexico Courts’ Guardian and Conservator Orientation Program videos","New Mexico · civil rules","Before appointment, every proposed guardian or conservator, including professional ones (those serving more than two unrelated individuals), must view the listed New Mexico Courts’ orientation videos: 6 for guardians and 8 for conservators. The proposed guardian or conservator must file a certificate of viewing no later than 5 calendar days before the hearing on the petition.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-145. Guardianship and conservatorship proceedings; professional guardians and conservators; procedures and time limits for filing reports and financial statements","New Mexico · civil rules","A professional guardian or conservator must file reports substantially in compliance with Forms 4-996, 4-997 and 4-998, the annual report within 30 days after the appointment anniversary and other reports within 60 days after resignation, removal or termination (or a longer reasonable time set by the court), serving the protected person, any guardian or conservator, and persons listed on Form 4-993. Each professional","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-150. Scope; forms","New Mexico · civil rules","Rules 1-150 to 1-156 govern all kinship guardianship petitions, while a petition concerning a child with at least one living parent is governed by the Domestic Relations Code, and cases involving the Children, Youth and Families Department may be assigned to the family or children’s court division as the chief judge or local rule provides. Self-represented litigants must use Forms 4A-501 through 4A-513, and attorneys","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-151. Petition; parties; intervention; joinder and sealing","New Mexico · civil rules","Only a “caregiver” as defined in Section 40-10B-3 may file a verified kinship guardianship petition, which must allege facts under Section 40-10B-5(B) and attach any guardianship assistance agreement with the Children, Youth and Families Department under seal. When the department is a party it must be served under Rule 1-004; it may intervene if it has court-ordered custody, must answer like other respondents if it h","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-152. Mandatory and discretionary appointment of guardian ad litem; powers and duties of the guardian ad litem; payment","New Mexico · civil rules","The court must appoint a guardian ad litem when a participating parent objects to a kinship guardianship petition or when a parent seeks revocation and the guardian objects, and may appoint one on motion or on its own. Appointed by order substantially in Form 4A-515, the guardian ad litem investigates (including home visits and interviews), reports on the child’s best interests and position, and recommends a transiti","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-153. Advisement of rights","New Mexico · civil rules","At the respondent’s first appearance in a kinship guardianship case, the court must inform the respondent of the right to interpretation, the parties (including whether the Children, Youth and Families Department is one), the petition’s allegations, the right to a trial, and the consequences if the allegations are proven, including possible child support.","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-154. Kinship guardianship hearing; elements of proof; judgment","New Mexico · civil rules","The Rules of Evidence apply, and the petitioner must prove by clear and convincing evidence jurisdiction and venue, service on and notice to the parents, that appointment is in the child’s best interest, for a child aged 14 or older that any nominee of the child is in the child’s best interest and the child has not objected in writing, that the petitioner is qualified under Section 40-10B-5(A), and that no Uniform Pr","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-155. Appointment of guardian","New Mexico · civil rules","On appointing a kinship guardian, the court issues letters of guardianship stating, without unnecessary information, that the guardian has all parental authority except consent to adoption and any rights the court expressly reserves to a parent. If the Children, Youth and Families Department is a party, orders likely to be shown to non-parties use only the caption “In the Matter of [initials of child]” with the case ","#st-nm-rules"],["Rule 1-156. Successor guardian","New Mexico · civil rules","If a kinship guardian dies or becomes incapacitated, the new caregiver may file a new petition. The court is not bound by successor-guardian terms in a guardianship assistance agreement with the Children, Youth and Families Department, but under Section 40-10B-19(B) the department may have to pay a qualified successor guardian’s costs of obtaining a subsidized guardianship, up to the limits in the Kinship Guardianshi","#st-nm-rules"],["Appeals in New Mexico: 30 days, a docketing statement, then a calendar","New Mexico · appeals","Most civil appeals go to the Court of Appeals. The Supreme Court takes direct appeals only in narrow categories (death or life sentences, Public Regulation Commission appeals, grants of habeas corpus, and matters reserved to it) and otherwise reviews the Court of Appeals by discretionary certiorari. You file the notice of appeal in the district court within 30 days after the judgment is filed in the clerk’s office. W","#st-nm-appeals"],["Court of Appeals unless the case is in a Supreme Court category","New Mexico · appeals · Where the appeal goes","Appeals go to the Supreme Court from district courts that imposed a sentence of death or life imprisonment, from the Public Regulation Commission, from grants of habeas corpus, and in matters reserved to the Supreme Court by the Constitution or by Supreme Court order or rule. All other appeals on the record go to the Court of Appeals. Rule 12-102 NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Magistrate and metropolitan court civil judgments go to district court first","New Mexico · appeals · Where the appeal goes","Appeal by filing a notice of appeal in the district court of the county within 15 days after the lower-court judgment is filed. Other parties have 10 days after service of the first notice, or the original period if later. The 3-day mailing period does not apply. A jury demand must be filed with the notice, and no later than 30 days after service of the notice. Rule 1-072 NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Agency decisions: district court first, then certiorari to the Court of Appeals","New Mexico · appeals · Where the appeal goes","Statutory administrative appeals go to the district court within 30 days after the agency’s final decision, unless a statute or ordinance sets another time (Rule 1-074). Review of the district court’s decision is by petition for certiorari to the Court of Appeals, filed within 30 days after the district court’s final action, with no 3 mail days (Rule 12-505). Rules 1-074, 12-505 NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Notice of appeal within 30 days after the judgment is filed","New Mexico · appeals · Starting the appeal","File the notice of appeal with the district court clerk within 30 days after the judgment or order is filed in the district court clerk’s office. The deadline is 10 days for the state’s appeals from suppression orders under § 39-3-3(B)(2). The 3-day mail extension does not apply. A notice filed after the decision is announced but before the judgment is filed counts as filed on the day the judgment is filed. Rules 12-","#st-nm-appeals"],["Contents, attachment, and service","New Mexico · appeals · Starting the appeal","The notice must name each appellant and appellee, give appellate counsel’s name and address if different, and name the court to which the appeal is taken. Attach a copy of the judgment or order showing its date. In civil cases, serve the appellate court, the trial judge, the court monitor or reporter, and trial counsel for every other party. Serve unrepresented parties by mail at their last known address. Disclose an","#st-nm-appeals"],["Mailed or couriered notices","New Mexico · appeals · Starting the appeal","A notice sent by mail or commercial courier is timely on the day it is received if its certificate of service states that it was postmarked or courier-stamped at least one day before the due date. Rule 12-201(F) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Extensions: good cause before the deadline, excusable neglect within 30 days after","New Mexico · appeals · Starting the appeal","Move in the district court before the deadline (good cause) or within 30 days after it (excusable neglect or circumstances beyond the appellant’s control). A motion filed later cannot be granted. An extension may not run more than 30 days past the original due date. The notice of appeal itself must be filed within those 30 days even if the motion is still pending. Rule 12-201(E) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Motions that could affect finality restart the full 30 days","New Mexico · appeals · Tolling and cross-appeals","A timely motion that could affect finality restarts the full appeal period, which then runs from the filing of an order expressly disposing of the last such motion. Qualifying motions include motions under Rules 1-050(B), 1-052(D), and 1-059, and § 39-1-1. A Rule 1-060(B) motion qualifies only if filed within 30 days after the judgment. A motion to reconsider qualifies if filed within the time to appeal. For motions ","#st-nm-appeals"],["Premature notices ripen; new-trial orders are not appealable","New Mexico · appeals · Tolling and cross-appeals","A notice of appeal filed while a tolling motion is pending becomes effective when the motion is decided, denied, or withdrawn. It then brings up the ruling on the motion without a separate notice. An order granting a new trial in a civil case is not appealable and makes the earlier judgment non-appealable. Rule 12-201(D)(4), (5) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Post-trial motion deadlines in district court","New Mexico · appeals · Tolling and cross-appeals","Motions for new trial or to alter, amend, or reconsider a final judgment must be filed within 30 days after entry of judgment. Rule 1-059 NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Cross-appeal: 14 days after the first notice is served","New Mexico · appeals · Tolling and cross-appeals","Any other party may file a notice of appeal within 14 days after the first notice was served, or within the original time, whichever ends later. The first to file is the appellant. An appellee may raise issues to support affirmance, or issues to be decided only if the court reverses, without cross-appealing. Rule 12-201(B), (C) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Docketing statement within 30 days, filed by trial counsel, with the docket fee","New Mexico · appeals · Docketing statement and calendar assignment","Unless the court orders otherwise, trial counsel prepares and files the docketing statement in the Court of Appeals within 30 days after the notice of appeal (or after a premature notice becomes effective). It must cover the nature of the case, timeliness, the material facts, each issue and how it was preserved, the authorities for and against with the standard of review, whether the proceedings were audio recorded, ","#st-nm-appeals"],["Record proper","New Mexico · appeals · Docketing statement and calendar assignment","When the district court clerk receives the docketing statement, the clerk numbers and transmits the record proper (the docketed pleadings, motions, and papers). Under the amendment for cases filed on or after December 31, 2026, the record is due within 5 days (formerly 14), and the appellant pays the costs within 5 days after the clerk files the statement of costs. Rule 12-209 NMRA (as amended eff. Dec. 31, 2026)","#st-nm-appeals"],["Summary calendar: 20 days to oppose a proposed disposition","New Mexico · appeals · Docketing statement and calendar assignment","No briefs or transcripts are filed. The court issues a notice of proposed summary disposition, and parties have 20 days from service to file a memorandum in opposition or support. The limit is 35 pages, or 11,000 words or 1,200 monospaced lines with a compliance statement. Arguments are limited to issues in the docketing statement, but a motion to amend can be combined with the memorandum. No oral argument. Rule 12-2","#st-nm-appeals"],["General calendar: transcript, then 45/45/20-day briefing","New Mexico · appeals · Docketing statement and calendar assignment","For audio-recorded proceedings, the district clerk sends the recording within 15 days after the general calendar assignment, and the appellant proves payment arrangements within 5 days. If proceedings were not recorded, the appellant designates the transcript or files a notice of non-designation within 15 days. The brief in chief is due 45 days after notice that the transcripts are filed (or after the notice of non-d","#st-nm-appeals"],["Legal calendar: 30/30/20-day briefing, no transcript","New Mexico · appeals · Docketing statement and calendar assignment","For cases decided on legal issues only, the brief in chief is due 30 days after service of the calendar notice, the answer brief 30 days after the brief in chief, and the reply 20 days after the answer. No oral argument unless ordered. Rule 12-210(E) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Length: 35 pages or 11,000 words; reply 15 pages or 4,400 words","New Mexico · appeals · Briefs","Briefs in chief, answer briefs, and amicus briefs: 35 pages, or 11,000 words (1,200 monospaced lines) with a statement of compliance giving the word count and the software used. Reply briefs: 15 pages or 4,400 words (480 lines). No attachments to briefs. New issues not in the docketing statement may be raised unless the appellee would be prejudiced. Rule 12-318(A)(1), (F), (G) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Cross-appeal briefing","New Mexico · appeals · Briefs","The appellee files its answer brief and its brief in chief on cross-appeal together, as separate documents: 45 days after the brief in chief on the general calendar, or 20 days on the legal calendar. The appellant’s reply and cross-answer follow on the same 45- or 20-day schedule. The cross-appellant may reply within 20 days. Rule 12-318(I) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Certified interlocutory orders: application within 15 days","New Mexico · appeals · Interlocutory and expedited appeals","If the district court’s order contains the statutory certification (§ 39-3-4(A) in civil cases), file an application for interlocutory appeal with the appellate court clerk within 15 days after entry, with the docket fee and no docketing statement. The application must state the controlling question of law, the substantial ground for difference of opinion, and how an immediate appeal will advance the case. Responses ","#st-nm-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP orders","New Mexico · appeals · Interlocutory and expedited appeals","Any party may take an expedited appeal from an order on an anti-SLAPP special motion, or from the court’s failure to rule on one expeditiously. NMSA 1978, § 38-2-9.1(C)","#st-nm-appeals"],["Stays and supersedeas","New Mexico · appeals · Interlocutory and expedited appeals","Execution may issue when judgment is entered unless the court orders otherwise. A supersedeas bond approved by the district court stays a money judgment, and appeals by the state and its subdivisions stay automatically (Rule 1-062). Apply to the district court first. The appellate court reviews that ruling on motion for abuse of discretion and may allow up to 15 more days to post a bond (Rule 12-207). Rules 1-062, 12","#st-nm-appeals"],["Rehearing within 15 days","New Mexico · appeals · Rehearing and Supreme Court review","A motion for rehearing may be filed within 15 days after the disposition is filed, with no 3 mail days. It must state the overlooked or misapprehended points, without argument. A brief is required if the court relied on a point no party raised. No response unless requested. In the Court of Appeals, any two participating judges can grant rehearing; in the Supreme Court, any three justices. Rule 12-404 NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Certiorari to the Supreme Court within 30 days","New Mexico · appeals · Rehearing and Supreme Court review","File within 30 days after the Court of Appeals’ final action (its decision, or the ruling on a timely rehearing motion), with the docket fee and no mail days. Grounds: conflict with Supreme Court or Court of Appeals precedent, a significant constitutional question, or an issue of substantial public interest. Limit: 10 pages, or 3,150 words or 342 monospaced lines. A conditional cross-petition is due within 15 days af","#st-nm-appeals"],["Expedited certiorari categories (from December 31, 2026)","New Mexico · appeals · Rehearing and Supreme Court review","For certiorari in abuse and neglect, termination of parental rights, delinquency, and interlocutory-application cases, the clerk sets oral argument within 60 days. Simultaneous supplemental briefs are due in 20 days (20 pages or 6,300 words), and replies are due 5 days before argument (10 pages or 3,150 words). Rule 12-502.1 NMRA (new, eff. Dec. 31, 2026)","#st-nm-appeals"],["Mandate","New Mexico · appeals · Rehearing and Supreme Court review","The Court of Appeals’ mandate does not issue until the time for certiorari has run or certiorari is resolved, and it may be recalled within 10 days for good cause. The Supreme Court’s mandate issues 15 days after its disposition or modification. A stay of mandate pending U.S. Supreme Court review is limited to 60 days unless extended. Rule 12-402 NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Mandatory EFS for lawyers; docket fees by check within 5 days","New Mexico · appeals · E-filing","Represented parties, including lawyers representing themselves, must file through the EFS. Self-represented parties may not e-file. The EFS charges no fee, but docket fees for e-filed new cases must be paid by check within 5 days after acceptance. An unpaid fee can lead to dismissal without prejudice, and reinstatement may be sought within 15 days. Electronic service is complete on transmission. If a recipient report","#st-nm-appeals"],["Counting time","New Mexico · appeals · E-filing","Periods of 11 days or more count every day. Periods of 10 days or less skip weekends and holidays. A deadline falling on a weekend or holiday rolls to the next business day. For e-filing, the last day ends at midnight. Rule 12-308(A) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (civil)","New Mexico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the judgment or order in the district court clerk’s office.\nNo 3-day mail extension. Filed in district court. Rule 12-201(A)(1)(b) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Notice of appeal by another party (cross-appeal)","New Mexico · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the first notice of appeal (or the original 30 days, if later). Rule 12-201(B)(1) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Motion to extend time to appeal","New Mexico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from expiration of the original appeal deadline.\nExcusable neglect after the deadline. The extension cannot exceed 30 days past the original deadline. Rule 12-201(E) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Motion for new trial / to alter or amend judgment","New Mexico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of judgment.\nTolls the time to appeal (Rule 12-201(D)). Rule 1-059 NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Docketing statement (Court of Appeals)","New Mexico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the notice of appeal (or the date a premature notice becomes effective).\nFiled by trial counsel, with the docket fee. Rule 12-208(B) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Memorandum opposing a proposed summary disposition","New Mexico · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from service of the notice of proposed summary disposition.\n35 pages or 11,000 words. Rule 12-210(D)(2) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Brief in chief (general calendar)","New Mexico · appeals · Deadlines","45 days from service of notice that transcripts are filed, or the notice of non-designation. Rule 12-210(C)(2)(a) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Answer brief (general calendar)","New Mexico · appeals · Deadlines","45 days from service of the brief in chief. Rule 12-210(C)(2)(b) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Reply brief","New Mexico · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from service of the answer brief.\nOptional. Rule 12-210(C)(2)(c), (E)(2)(c) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Brief in chief (legal calendar)","New Mexico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the calendar assignment notice.\nAnswer brief due 30 days after the brief in chief. Rule 12-210(E)(2)(a) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Application for interlocutory appeal","New Mexico · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from entry of the certified interlocutory order.\nFiled in the appellate court. No mail days. Rule 12-203(A) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Motion for rehearing","New Mexico · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the appellate disposition.\nNo mail days. Rule 12-404(A) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Petition for writ of certiorari to the Supreme Court","New Mexico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from final action by the Court of Appeals (decision, or ruling on a timely rehearing motion).\n10 pages or 3,150 words. No mail days. Rule 12-502(B) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Response to certiorari petition / conditional cross-petition","New Mexico · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from service of the petition (response: or the grant). Rule 12-502(G), (H) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Certiorari to Court of Appeals in administrative cases","New Mexico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from district court’s final action. Rule 12-505(C) NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Appeal from magistrate or metropolitan court to district court","New Mexico · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the lower-court judgment.\nTrial de novo cases. Rule 1-072 NMRA","#st-nm-appeals"],["Supreme Court of Ohio","Ohio · courts","Chief Justice and six justices. Hears appeals of right (for example, cases that began in a court of appeals and death-penalty cases), discretionary jurisdictional appeals (substantial constitutional question, felony, or public or great general interest), certified conflicts between courts of appeals, questions certified by federal courts, agency appeals (such as the Board of Tax Appeals and Public Utilities Commissio","#st-oh"],["Courts of Appeals (twelve appellate districts)","Ohio · courts","Three-judge panels hear appeals of right from final orders of trial courts in the district. Papers are filed with the clerk of the court of appeals in the county where the case began. Each district adopts its own local rules (for example on e-filing, docketing statements, and the accelerated calendar).","#st-oh"],["Courts of Common Pleas","Ohio · courts","One in each county; the general-jurisdiction trial court. Many counties have separate general, domestic relations, probate, and juvenile divisions. Civ.R. 73 (probate) and 75 (domestic relations) add special procedures.","#st-oh"],["Municipal and County Courts; Court of Claims; Mayor’s Courts","Ohio · courts","Municipal and county courts handle smaller civil claims, small claims, evictions, misdemeanors, and traffic. The Court of Claims hears suits against the State. Mayor’s courts hear limited ordinance and traffic matters and are not courts of record.","#st-oh"],["Statute: Ohio’s Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (effective April 9, 2025)","Ohio · anti-SLAPP","Ohio adopted the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (UPEPA) as R.C. Chapter 2747 (S.B. 237, 135th General Assembly), effective April 9, 2025. It applies to civil actions filed, and claims asserted, on or after that date.\nIt covers claims based on a person’s (1) communication in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding; (2) communication on an issue under consideration","#st-oh-slapp"],["What the Act does not cover","Ohio · anti-SLAPP","Exempt: (1) claims against a governmental unit or its employee or agent acting in an official capacity; (2) government enforcement actions to protect against an imminent threat to public health or safety; (3) claims against a person primarily engaged in selling or leasing goods or services when the claim arises from communications about that sale or lease (creative, journalistic, political, and artistic works are not","#st-oh-slapp"],["Procedure: 60-day motion, automatic stay, 60-day hearing, 60-day ruling","Ohio · anti-SLAPP","File a ‘motion for expedited relief’ within 60 days after service of the complaint, counterclaim, cross-claim, third-party claim, or other pleading asserting the claim; the court may extend this for good cause.\nFiling stays all other proceedings between the movant and the responding party, including discovery and pending motions. The stay lasts until 30 days after the ruling or the end of any appeal, whichever is lat","#st-oh-slapp"],["Standard: three steps; Rule 56-type evidence","Ohio · anti-SLAPP","The court considers the pleadings, motion, response, and any evidence usable on summary judgment under Civ.R. 56. It must dismiss with prejudice if (1) the movant shows the claim is based on protected activity; (2) the respondent fails to show an exemption; and (3) either the respondent fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of","#st-oh-slapp"],["Fees: mandatory for a winning movant; only for frivolous motions against one","Ohio · anti-SLAPP","If the motion is granted, the court must award the movant reasonable attorney’s fees, court costs, and other reasonable litigation expenses, and may not reduce them because the representation was pro bono or contingent.\nIf the motion is denied and the court finds it was frivolous conduct under R.C. 2323.51, the respondent gets its fees and expenses, but only after any appeal affirming the denial. R.C. 2747.05(A)-(B)","#st-oh-slapp"],["Appeal: denial is a final order; 30 days","Ohio · anti-SLAPP","A denial is a final, appealable order, giving the movant an interlocutory appeal. The appeal must be filed within 30 days after entry of the order. R.C. 2505.02(B)(9) separately lists an order denying a motion for expedited relief under R.C. 2747.04 as a final order.\nThe statute gives no corresponding immediate appeal from a grant; a grant that disposes of fewer than all claims is subject to the usual final-order and","#st-oh-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: register with the Supreme Court first ($500), then move in the tribunal","Ohio · out of state","Before any Ohio tribunal (court, agency acting adjudicatively, or the Board on the Unauthorized Practice of Law) can grant permission, the lawyer must apply to the Supreme Court Office of Bar Admissions, pay a $500 registration fee, and obtain a certificate of pro hac vice registration. The application lists addresses, every jurisdiction of licensure with dates and registration numbers, an affidavit of no disbarment ","#st-oh-oos"],["Who qualifies, and who is barred","Ohio · out of state","Eligible: lawyers admitted in the highest court of any U.S. state, territory, or D.C., or in a foreign country’s courts, who are in good standing and (a) do not live in or work regularly from an Ohio office; (b) hold Ohio corporate (in-house) registration; (c) live in Ohio but lawfully practice from offices elsewhere, including lawful remote practice; (d) maintain an Ohio presence under Prof.Cond.R. 5.5(d)(2) or (d)(","#st-oh-oos"],["Cap: three new proceedings per calendar year","Ohio · out of state","A lawyer may be admitted pro hac vice in no more than three proceedings in the calendar year of the application. A proceeding carried into later years does not count again, and an appeal, agency appeal, transfer, or consolidation of a case the lawyer was already in does not count. Joining a proceeding for the first time at any stage does count. Gov.Bar R. XII, Section 2(A)(5), (A)(6)(d)","#st-oh-oos"],["After admission: 30-day notice and annual renewal, or automatic exclusion","Ohio · out of state","Within 30 days after any tribunal grants permission, file a Notice of Permission to Appear Pro Hac Vice (with the order) with the Office of Bar Admissions. If the case is still pending on January 1, pay a renewal fee equal to the $500 registration fee and file an updated form within 30 days after the year begins; if the case ended or the lawyer withdrew, tell the Office by that deadline.\nMissing either step means aut","#st-oh-oos"],["Supreme Court of Ohio: separate pro hac vice motion and deadlines","Ohio · out of state","To file anything beyond what is needed to perfect an appeal, or to argue, a lawyer must be registered for active Ohio status or admitted pro hac vice under Gov.Bar R. XII and S.Ct.Prac.R. 2.02. The motion attaches the registration certificate and the XII(2)(A)(3) information.\nIf a non-Ohio lawyer perfects an appeal without moving, the motion is due within 30 days after the appeal is filed; to argue, file it at least ","#st-oh-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances: Notice of Limited Appearance","Ohio · out of state","Ohio allows limited-scope representation if the limitation is reasonable and communicated to the client, preferably in writing; unlike ABA Model Rule 1.2(c), client informed consent is not required.\nIn court, the lawyer files and serves a ‘Notice of Limited Appearance’ describing the scope and stating that the client authorized it, before or at the appearance. Other parties must then serve both the lawyer and the cli","#st-oh-oos"],["Temporary practice without pro hac vice","Ohio · out of state","Lawyers admitted and in good standing elsewhere who regularly practice may provide temporary services in Ohio: with an actively participating Ohio lawyer; for matters related to a pending or expected proceeding where they are or expect to be authorized to appear; for arbitration, mediation, or other ADR related to their home practice (unless the forum requires pro hac vice); or for negotiations and other non-litigati","#st-oh-oos"],["E-filing: no single statewide trial-court system","Ohio · out of state","The Civil Rules let each court adopt electronic filing by court order or local rule, which must cover electronic signatures, the days and hours filings are received and when they count as filed, and fee payment. The courts of appeals follow the same model under App.R. 13(B)(2). Check each county’s and district’s local rules for portal registration and whether pro hac vice counsel can register.\nThe Supreme Court of Oh","#st-oh-oos"],["Governing code: Ohio Rules of Professional Conduct (as amended effective January 1, 2026)","Ohio · ethics","Ohio follows the ABA Model Rules numbering, with official ‘Comparison to ABA Model Rules’ notes after many rules explaining Ohio’s changes. Discipline is governed by Gov.Bar R. V. Ohio Rules of Professional Conduct (eff. Feb. 1, 2007, as amended eff. Jan. 1, 2026)","#st-oh-ethics"],["Malpractice insurance notice signed by the client","Ohio · ethics","A lawyer who lacks professional liability insurance of at least $100,000 per occurrence and $300,000 aggregate, or whose coverage ends, must tell the client at engagement (or when coverage ends) on the separate form that follows the rule, signed by the client. Keep the signed notice for 5 years after the representation ends. A lawyer in a fee division must give the notice before the client is asked to agree to the di","#st-oh-ethics"],["Fees: ‘clearly excessive’ standard; contingent-fee closing statement","Ohio · ethics","Ohio forbids illegal or clearly excessive fees, rather than the Model Rule’s ‘unreasonable’. Contingent-fee agreements must be in a writing signed by both the client and the lawyer, setting out percentages for settlement, trial, or appeal and how expenses are handled. When the lawyer disburses the recovery, the lawyer must give the client a closing statement showing how the fee was computed and what costs were deduct","#st-oh-ethics"],["Broader confidentiality exceptions","Ohio · ethics","Beyond the Model Rule exceptions, a lawyer may reveal information (including privileged information) to the extent reasonably necessary to prevent the commission of any crime by the client or another person, and to mitigate substantial financial injury from a client’s illegal or fraudulent act that used the lawyer’s services. Prof.Cond.R. 1.6(b)(2)-(3)","#st-oh-ethics"],["Duty to report other lawyers and judges","Ohio · ethics","A lawyer with unprivileged knowledge of a violation that raises a question about any lawyer’s honesty, trustworthiness, or fitness must report it. Any ethics violation by a judge must also be reported, broader than the Model Rule’s ‘substantial question’ standard. Prof.Cond.R. 8.3(a)-(b) and Comparison to ABA Model Rules","#st-oh-ethics"],["Other notable departures","Ohio · ethics","Rule 1.2(d) and 4.1(b) use ‘illegal’ rather than ‘criminal’. Rule 1.8(f)(4) adds a Statement of Insured Client’s Rights for insurance defense. Rule 1.10(c)-(d) allows screening of lateral hires except where the lawyer had a substantial role in the same matter. Rule 1.13 omits the Model Rule’s reporting-out provision. Rule 3.5(a)(2) bars giving or lending anything of more than de minimis value to a judicial officer, j","#st-oh-ethics"],["Filing is not commencement unless service is obtained within one year","Ohio · land mine","An Ohio action is ‘commenced’ by filing only if service is obtained on a named defendant within one year of filing. Late service can mean the limitations period was never stopped. Civ.R. 3(A)","#st-oh-unique"],["Do not state a dollar amount above $25,000","Ohio · land mine","A pleading seeking more than $25,000 must say so but may not state the amount. Civ.R. 8(A)","#st-oh-unique"],["Medical-claim complaints need an affidavit of merit","Ohio · land mine","A complaint with a medical, dental, optometric, or chiropractic claim must include an affidavit of merit from a qualified expert for each defendant who needs expert proof. Any motion to extend must be filed with the complaint, and extensions are capped at 90 days except as the rule allows. Civ.R. 10(D)(2)","#st-oh-unique"],["Continuance motions must be signed by the client too","Ohio · land mine","A motion to continue a trial or hearing must be written, state the reason, and be endorsed by the party as well as counsel, unless the judge waives this for good cause. Civ.R. 6(B)(2)(b)","#st-oh-unique"],["Object to the magistrate within 14 days or lose the issue","Ohio · land mine","Failing to file specific objections to a magistrate’s decision within 14 days forfeits appellate review of the adopted findings and conclusions, except for plain error. Civ.R. 53(D)(3)(b)(i), (iv)","#st-oh-unique"],["30 days to appeal, with no extension","Ohio · land mine","The notice of appeal is due in the trial court within 30 days of entry of judgment, and the court of appeals cannot extend it. The clock starts later only if the clerk failed to serve notice of the judgment within 3 days, or after listed post-judgment motions (Civ.R. 50(B), 59, magistrate objections, findings requests, attorney-fee and prejudgment-interest motions) are resolved. Civ.R. 60(B) motions are not on the to","#st-oh-unique"],["Assignments of error must be stated and argued separately","Ohio · land mine","The court of appeals may disregard an assignment of error that does not identify the error in the record or is not argued separately in the brief. App.R. 12(A)(2); App.R. 16(A)(3), (7)","#st-oh-unique"],["Supreme Court deadlines are hard: 45 days, no extensions, memorandum filed with the notice","Ohio · land mine","A discretionary appeal requires both a notice of appeal and a 15-page memorandum in support of jurisdiction within 45 days of the court of appeals’ entry. The Clerk refuses late filings, and the Court will not extend this time. A motion to certify a conflict in the court of appeals does not extend the 45 days. S.Ct.Prac.R. 3.02(B), 3.03(B)(1), 7.01(A)(1), 7.02(B); App.R. 25(A)","#st-oh-unique"],["Pro hac vice is capped and has hidden renewal deadlines","Ohio · land mine","Three new proceedings per calendar year, $500 registration before moving, a 30-day post-grant notice, and a $500 renewal each January for pending cases. Missing a notice or renewal means automatic exclusion. Gov.Bar R. XII, Sections 2(A)(3), 2(A)(5), 4, 5","#st-oh-unique"],["Judge disqualification goes to Columbus, at least 7 days out","Ohio · land mine","The affidavit of disqualification is filed with the Clerk of the Supreme Court, not the trial court, at least 7 calendar days before the next hearing. It must be notarized and served, or the Clerk rejects it. R.C. 2701.03(B), (C)(2)","#st-oh-unique"],["Two notice dismissals operate as an adjudication on the merits","Ohio · land mine","A plaintiff’s Civ.R. 41(A)(1) notice dismissal is without prejudice, but a notice dismissal of a claim the plaintiff has already dismissed once in any court is an adjudication on the merits. Civ.R. 41(A)(1)","#st-oh-unique"],["UPEPA motion due 60 days after service","Ohio · land mine","The anti-SLAPP motion for expedited relief must be filed within 60 days after service of the pleading (extendable for good cause). Filing stays discovery, and a granted motion carries mandatory fees. R.C. 2747.02, 2747.03, 2747.05(A)","#st-oh-unique"],["No peremptory substitution of judge","Ohio · judges","The collected Civil Rules and statutes contain no right to remove a trial judge without cause. Removal is by affidavit of disqualification decided by the Supreme Court’s Chief Justice, or by the judge’s own recusal. Civ.R. 1-86; R.C. 2701.03 (no peremptory procedure found)","#st-oh-unique"],["Affidavit of disqualification goes to the Supreme Court Clerk, at least 7 days before the next hearing","Ohio · judges","A party or counsel claiming a common pleas judge is interested, related, biased, or otherwise disqualified files a sworn affidavit with the Clerk of the Supreme Court of Ohio, not with the trial court. File it at least 7 calendar days before the next scheduled hearing. It must include specific allegations and supporting facts, a notary’s jurat, a certificate of service on the judge and all parties, and the next heari","#st-oh-unique"],["Recusal standard for all judges","Ohio · judges","Judges must disqualify themselves whenever impartiality might reasonably be questioned. Examples: personal bias or knowledge of disputed facts; a spouse, domestic partner, or relative within the third degree who is a party, lawyer, interested person, or likely material witness; family economic interests; public statements committing to a result; prior work on the matter as a lawyer or government official; or having p","#st-oh-unique"],["Recusal of a Supreme Court justice","Ohio · judges","Request it by a letter to the Clerk of the Supreme Court, with an affidavit stating the specific basis and supporting facts, served on all parties. File promptly, and in argued cases at least 15 days before oral argument absent leave. The named justice responds in writing. Amici may not file recusal requests. S.Ct.Prac.R. 4.04","#st-oh-unique"],["Ohio Constitution free-speech clause: not established here","Ohio · speech","The Ohio Constitution has its own speech, press, assembly, and petition guarantees, but the collected texts include neither the Constitution nor case law. Whether Ohio courts read them more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked. Ohio Const. art. I, §§ 3, 11 (not in collected texts)","#st-oh-unique"],["UPEPA protects speech and press on matters of public concern","Ohio · speech","R.C. Chapter 2747 expressly protects the exercise of freedom of speech and of the press, assembly, petition, and association under the U.S. and Ohio Constitutions on matters of public concern, and must be construed broadly to protect those rights. R.C. 2747.01(B)(3); R.C. 2747.06(B)","#st-oh-unique"],["Final, appealable orders are defined by statute","Ohio · unique rules","Appellate jurisdiction depends on R.C. 2505.02(B)’s list of final orders, such as orders affecting a substantial right that determine the action, provisional-remedy orders meeting the statute’s tests, and denials of UPEPA motions. In multi-claim or multi-party cases, a ruling on fewer than all claims also needs a Civ.R. 54(B) finding of ‘no just reason for delay’. R.C. 2505.02(B); Civ.R. 54(B)","#st-oh-unique"],["Magistrates do much of the trial-court work","Ohio · unique rules","Courts may refer motions and non-jury trials to magistrates. Objections to a magistrate’s decision are due within 14 days, and failing to object to a finding or conclusion forfeits all but plain-error review on appeal. Civ.R. 53(D)(3)(b)(i), (iv)","#st-oh-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope of rules; construction; exceptions","Ohio · civil rules","The Civil Rules govern civil cases in all Ohio courts and are construed to secure just results without delay or unnecessary expense, except under Rule 73 (probate) and Rule 75 (domestic relations) and except where clearly inapplicable to appeals, appropriation of property, forcible entry and detainer, small claims under R.C. Chapter 1925, uniform reciprocal support actions, commitment of the mentally ill, adoptions u","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 1.1. Definitions","Ohio · civil rules","Defines “appear,” “appearance,” “in person,” and “attendance” to include physical or remote presence; “open court” includes proceedings the public can attend in person or by remote access; “personally” includes remote presence except for service of process under Civ.R. 4.1–4.5 and subpoenas under Civ.R. 45; and “remote presence” means participating by live two-way video and audio.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","Ohio · civil rules","There is only one form of action, the civil action.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencement of action; venue","Ohio · civil rules","An action is commenced by filing a complaint only if service is obtained on a named (or later correctly named or fictitiously named) defendant within one year of filing; unlike FRCP 3, filing alone does not commence the action. An attorney may make a limited appearance by filing a notice, and it ends on filing a notice of completion if no one objects within 10 days. The rule itself sets venue (unlike federal practice","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 4. Summons","Ohio · civil rules","On filing, the clerk issues a summons, with the complaint attached, for each defendant, stating the time to appear and warning of default. A person at least 18 and not under disability, or the party’s attorney, may waive service in writing (an individual’s own waiver must give a mailing or e-mail address). If service is not made within six months after filing and there is no good cause, the action is dismissed withou","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 4.1. Methods of service","Ohio · civil rules","In-state service is made by the clerk by certified or express mail, or by a commercial carrier requiring a signed receipt, with return receipt docketed and the serving party notified of any failure. On written request, the plaintiff may instead use personal service (handing the papers to the person) or residence service (leaving them at the person’s usual residence with someone of suitable age and discretion living t","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 4.2. Who may be served","Ohio · civil rules","Specifies whom to serve: individuals directly; persons under 16 through a guardian or the parent or caretaker they live with; incompetent persons through a guardian or institution head; prisoners directly; corporations, LLCs, partnerships, unincorporated and professional associations through a registered agent, an officer, manager, member, partner, or shareholder, or by clerk mail service to a usual place of business","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 4.3. Out-of-state service","Ohio · civil rules","Allows service outside Ohio on nonresidents (or absent residents) whose claim-related conduct falls within the long-arm list, including transacting business or contracting to supply goods or services in Ohio, causing tortious injury in or affecting Ohio, owning or using Ohio real property, insuring Ohio risks, living in a marriage in Ohio as to support, custody, and property obligations, and criminal acts with an ele","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 4.4. Service by publication","Ohio · civil rules","When a party’s residence is unknown and publication is authorized by law, the requesting party files an affidavit describing diligent efforts to find it, and notice is published in a newspaper of general circulation once a week for six successive weeks (unless law allows fewer); service is complete on the last publication, and the defendant must answer within 28 days after it. In divorce, annulment, legal separation,","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 4.5. Service in a foreign country","Ohio · civil rules","Service in a Hague Service Convention country must use a method the Convention allows, including Article 8 or 10 methods the country has not objected to. Elsewhere, service may be by clerk mail or carrier, publication if applicable, the foreign country’s own methods, letter rogatory, personal delivery, delivery to an entity’s officer or managing agent, signed-receipt delivery addressed by the clerk, or as the court o","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 4.6. Service limits; amendment; refused or unclaimed service","Ohio · civil rules","Process may be served anywhere in Ohio and outside Ohio when authorized, and the court may allow amendment of process or proof of service absent material prejudice. If certified, express, or carrier service is refused (or personal or residence service is refused) or the mail is returned unclaimed, the clerk notifies the serving party, who may request ordinary mail service; service is complete when the mailing is ente","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 4.7. Waiving service","Ohio · civil rules","A plaintiff may mail (first-class or other reliable means) a written notice and request to waive service, with the complaint, two copies of the official waiver form, and prepaid return, giving the defendant at least 28 days to return it (60 days if outside the United States); unlike FRCP 4(d), which allows at least 30 days, and unlike federal practice, this procedure applies only in common pleas courts and not to civ","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 5. Service and filing of later pleadings and papers","Ohio · civil rules","Every later pleading, written motion (other than ex parte), discovery paper, notice, and similar document must be served on every party (on the attorney if represented, and on both the attorney and the party for a limited appearance), but parties in default for failure to appear need be served only with new or additional claims, by summons. Service may be by hand, leaving at the office or home, mail (complete on mail","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 6. Time","Ohio · civil rules","Exclude the triggering day and include the last day unless it is a weekend, legal holiday, or the office is closed; for periods under seven days, intermediate weekends and holidays are excluded (unlike FRCP 6(a), which counts every day). The court may extend time for good cause before it expires, or after on motion for excusable neglect, but not the deadlines under Civ.R. 50(B), 59(B), 59(D), and 60(B); a continuance","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings and motions","Ohio · civil rules","The only pleadings are a complaint and answer, a reply to a counterclaim, an answer to a cross-claim, and a third-party complaint and answer; the court may order a reply to an answer. Requests for orders are by motion, in writing unless made at a hearing or trial, stating the grounds with particularity and the relief sought, served under Civ.R. 5 and signed under Civ.R. 11; courts may decide motions on written submis","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","Ohio · civil rules","A claim needs a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment, but a party seeking more than $25,000 must say so without stating the amount sought (unless the claim is on an attached instrument), and an opposing party may demand a written statement of the amount; unlike FRCP 8(a), the amount is not pleaded. Denials must fairly meet the averments, a lack of knowledge counts as a den","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","Ohio · civil rules","Capacity to sue need not be alleged, but a challenge to it must be made by specific negative averment. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity while intent and knowledge may be alleged generally; conditions precedent may be alleged generally but must be denied specifically; official documents and judgments may be pleaded simply; time and place are material; and special damages must be specifically stated","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","Ohio · civil rules","Pleadings need a caption with the court, title, case number, and designation, and the complaint must list all parties’ names and addresses; claims and defenses go in numbered paragraphs, separate counts where helpful. A claim or defense founded on an account or written instrument must attach a copy or explain why not. A medical, dental, optometric, or chiropractic claim must include an expert affidavit of merit for e","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing of pleadings, motions, and other documents","Ohio · civil rules","Every document must be signed (by hand or electronically) by an attorney of record, with address, registration number, phone, and any fax and e-mail, or by an unrepresented party with address, phone, and any fax and e-mail for electronic service. The signature certifies the signer has read it, believes there is good ground to support it, and is not filing it for delay; an unsigned or sham document may be stricken, an","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections","Ohio · civil rules","Answer within 28 days after service of summons and complaint (or after publication is complete); answers to cross-claims and replies to counterclaims are also due in 28 days (unlike the federal 21 days). Defenses of lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction, improper venue, insufficient process or service, failure to state a claim, and failure to join a party under Civ.R. 19 or 19.1 may be raised by pre-answer ","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and cross-claim","Ohio · civil rules","A counterclaim arising from the same transaction or occurrence as the opposing claim is compulsory unless it is already pending elsewhere or the suit was by attachment without personal jurisdiction; other counterclaims are permissive and may exceed or differ from the opposing claim. Later-maturing counterclaims may be added by supplemental pleading with leave, and omitted ones by amendment with leave; cross-claims ag","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","Ohio · civil rules","A defending party may bring in a nonparty who may be liable for all or part of the claim against it, without leave if the third-party complaint is filed within 14 days after serving the original answer and otherwise with leave on notice. The third-party defendant answers under Civ.R. 12 and 13, may raise the defendant’s defenses against the plaintiff, and claims between plaintiff and third-party defendant may be asse","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Ohio · civil rules","A party may amend once as of right within 28 days after serving the pleading or, if a response is required, within 28 days after service of the responsive pleading or a Civ.R. 12(B), (E), or (F) motion, whichever is earlier (FRCP 15 uses 21 days); otherwise amendment needs written consent or leave, which is freely given, and responses to amended pleadings are due within the time remaining or 14 days after service, wh","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 16. Pretrial procedure","Ohio · civil rules","The court may order pretrial conferences to expedite the case, control management, discourage waste, improve trial preparation, and encourage settlement. Except in Civ.R. 1(C) matters, the court must issue a scheduling order as soon as practicable and, absent good cause, within the earlier of 90 days after any defendant is served or 60 days after any defendant responds; it may set deadlines for joinder, amendment, di","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 17. Parties; capacity","Ohio · civil rules","Actions must be prosecuted by the real party in interest, though representatives such as executors, guardians, and trustees may sue in their own names, and no dismissal for that defect is allowed until there is a reasonable time to ratify, join, or substitute the real party. A minor or incompetent person may sue or defend through a guardian or similar representative, or otherwise through a next friend or guardian ad ","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims and remedies","Ohio · civil rules","A party with a claim may join as many claims, legal or equitable, as it has against an opposing party. Claims that would ordinarily wait for another to be resolved may be joined, such as a money claim together with a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance, with relief granted according to the parties’ substantive rights.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 19. Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","Ohio · civil rules","A person subject to service must be joined if complete relief is impossible without them, if their absence would impair their interest or expose parties to inconsistent obligations, or (unlike FRCP 19) if they are an assignor, assignee, subrogor, or subrogee; the defense must be timely raised under Civ.R. 12(B)(7) or is waived. If joinder is not feasible, the court decides in equity and good conscience whether to pro","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 19.1. Compulsory joinder","Ohio · civil rules","An Ohio-specific rule with no federal counterpart: unless good cause is shown, persons with claims arising from the same wrongful act must be joined, including a decedent’s survival claim and the wrongful-death claim; a spouse’s injury and the other spouse’s consortium or expense claim; a minor’s injury and the parent’s or guardian’s claim; an employee’s or agent’s injury and the employer’s or principal’s property-da","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","Ohio · civil rules","Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, when the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact; not every party need be interested in all relief, and judgment may be given according to each party’s rights or liabilities. The court may order separate trials or other protective orders to prevent prejudice, delay, or expense.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","Ohio · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may add or drop parties on motion or on its own at any stage on just terms and may sever any claim to proceed separately.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","Ohio · civil rules","A plaintiff exposed to double or multiple liability may join the competing claimants as defendants and require them to interplead, even if their claims have different origins or the plaintiff denies liability, and a defendant may interplead by cross-claim or counterclaim. With notice and leave of court, a party may deposit the disputed money or property with the court for safekeeping or disposition.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","Ohio · civil rules","A class action requires numerosity, common questions, typical claims, and adequate representation, plus one of three types: risk of inconsistent or impairing individual adjudications, generally applicable conduct warranting class-wide injunctive or declaratory relief, or predominance of common questions and superiority (requiring the best practicable notice, including individual notice, with a right to opt out). The ","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 23.1. Derivative actions by shareholders","Ohio · civil rules","A shareholder suing to enforce a corporate right must file a verified complaint alleging ownership at the time of the challenged transaction (or by later operation of law) and describing with particularity the demand made on directors and, if necessary, shareholders, or why no demand was made. The plaintiff must fairly and adequately represent similarly situated shareholders, and the case cannot be dismissed or settl","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","Ohio · civil rules","On timely application, intervention is allowed as of right when an Ohio statute gives an unconditional right or the applicant has an interest that may be impaired and is not adequately represented, and permissively when a statute gives a conditional right, the applicant shares a common question, or a government agency administers a statute or order at issue, considering undue delay or prejudice. The motion must state","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","Ohio · civil rules","If a party dies and the claim survives, a motion to substitute must be made within 90 days after the death is suggested on the record, or the action is dismissed as to that party; the motion is served on parties under Civ.R. 5 and on nonparties like a summons. Unlike FRCP 25, the deceased or incompetent party’s attorney must suggest the death or incompetency on the record within 14 days after learning of it. Actions ","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 26. General provisions governing discovery","Ohio · civil rules","Unlike FRCP 26, the rule opens with a policy protecting attorneys’ trial preparation and preventing one side from exploiting the other’s work; discovery covers any nonprivileged matter relevant to any claim or defense and proportional to the needs of the case, with no limit on frequency unless the court orders one. Initial disclosures (witnesses, documents, damages computation, insurance) are due by the first pretria","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions before action or pending appeal","Ohio · civil rules","A person expecting to be a party but unable yet to bring or defend the action may file a verified petition in the common pleas court of the county where any expected adverse party resides to perpetuate testimony, serving notice like a summons at least 28 days before the hearing (unlike FRCP 27’s 21 days) unless the court shortens it for extraordinary circumstances; the court appoints an attorney to cross-examine for ","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Ohio · civil rules","In Ohio, depositions may be taken before anyone authorized to give oaths under Ohio law, a court appointee, or a person stipulated by all parties; outside Ohio, before a local oath officer, an appointee, a stipulated person, or a U.S. consular officer abroad, and out-of-state witnesses may be deposed remotely before an Ohio oath officer. Unless the parties agree, the officer may not be a relative, employee, or attorn","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","Ohio · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, the parties may agree in writing that depositions be taken before any person, at any time or place, on any notice, and in any manner, and may modify the procedures for other discovery methods.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions upon oral examination","Ohio · civil rules","After the action begins, any party may depose any person on reasonable written notice to all parties (a prisoner only with leave), compelling a nonparty by subpoena; unlike FRCP 30, there is no 10-deposition cap or leave requirement for repeat depositions in the rule text. A deposition is limited to one day of seven hours unless stipulated or ordered; it may be recorded non-stenographically if the notice says so, tak","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions upon written questions","Ohio · civil rules","Any party may depose any person on written questions served with a notice naming the witness and officer, and organizations may be deposed as under Civ.R. 30(B)(5). Cross-questions are due within 21 days after the notice and questions are served, redirect questions within 14 days after cross-questions, and recross questions within 14 days after redirect (unlike FRCP 31’s 14, 7, and 7 days), and the court may change t","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 32. Use of depositions in court proceedings","Ohio · civil rules","A deposition to be used as evidence must be filed at least one day before trial or hearing unless the court allows later filing. Any deposition may be used to impeach, an adverse party’s or its officer’s or designee’s deposition may be used for any purpose, and any witness’s deposition may be used if the witness is dead, outside the court’s subpoena power or resides outside the county, unable to attend from age, illn","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories to parties","Ohio · civil rules","Without leave, a party may serve up to 40 interrogatories on any other party, counting each subpart separately (unlike FRCP 33’s 25 including discrete subparts), preferably electronically in an editable format (plus a paper copy to an unrepresented party), and the responder need answer only the first 40 if more are served. Answers are made separately under oath, quoting each interrogatory before the answer or objecti","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 34. Producing documents, ESI, and things; entry onto land","Ohio · civil rules","A party may request to inspect, copy, test, or sample documents, ESI, and tangible things in another party’s possession, custody, or control, or to enter land, describing items with reasonable particularity and specifying a time, place, manner, and ESI form, and must serve an electronic, editable copy of the request by e-mail unless excused. The written response is due within the period stated in the request, not les","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examinations","Ohio · civil rules","When a party’s (or a person in a party’s custody or control) physical or mental condition, including blood group, is in controversy, the court may order an examination on motion for good cause with notice, specifying time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner. On request, the examined party gets the examiner’s detailed written report, and the requesting party is then entitled to reports of other examination","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","Ohio · civil rules","A party may serve requests to admit facts, application of law to fact, or the genuineness of documents, preferably electronically in an editable format (plus a paper copy to an unrepresented party); each matter is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within the period stated in the request, not less than 28 days after service (FRCP 36 gives 30 days), unless the court changes it. Answers must quote ","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to make discovery; sanctions","Ohio · civil rules","After certifying a good-faith effort to confer, a party may move to compel answers, designations, interrogatory responses, or inspection, with evasive or incomplete answers treated as failures, and the losing side on the motion generally pays reasonable expenses and attorney fees unless substantially justified. Disobeying a discovery order can lead to facts being taken as established, preclusion, striking pleadings, ","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury trial of right","Ohio · civil rules","The right to a jury trial is preserved, but a party must serve a written demand (it may be endorsed on a pleading, captioned “jury demand endorsed hereon”) no later than 14 days after service of the last pleading on the issue; if a demand covers only some issues, another party has 14 days (or less if the court orders) to demand a jury on other issues. A civil jury has 8 members (12 in corporate right-of-way appropria","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","Ohio · civil rules","Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate to a bench trial or the court finds no jury right exists; failing to appear for trial waives the jury. Undemanded issues are tried by the court, though it may order a jury on motion, and in non-jury cases it may use an advisory jury or, with both parties’ consent, a binding jury. A party may ask for a bench trial by live two-way video by the scheduling-","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 40. Pre-recorded testimony","Ohio · civil rules","All testimony and other appropriate evidence may be presented at trial by video recording, subject to the Rules of Superintendence. Unlike FRCP 40, which deals with scheduling cases for trial, Ohio’s Rule 40 covers prerecorded video trials.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","Ohio · civil rules","A plaintiff may dismiss without a court order by filing a notice any time before trial begins (unless a counterclaim that can’t stand independently has been served) or by a stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared; such dismissals are without prejudice, but a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as a judgment on the merits (the “two-dismissal rule”). Otherwise dismissal requires a court order","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials","Ohio · civil rules","Actions sharing a common question of law or fact may be joined for hearing or trial, consolidated, or otherwise managed to avoid cost and delay, and the court may order separate trials of issues or claims while preserving any jury right. In asbestos, silicosis, and mixed-dust-disease tort cases, actions may be consolidated for case management, but for trial only with all parties’ consent or, absent consent, only acti","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 43. Taking testimony","Ohio · civil rules","Witnesses testify in open court unless a statute or rule provides otherwise, but for good cause and with safeguards the court may allow remote testimony; a request for remote testimony must be discussed with counsel and unrepresented parties and filed by the scheduling-order deadline or 30 days before the trial or hearing, unless the court allows later notice for good cause. Motions may be heard on affidavits, deposi","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 44. Proof of official record","Ohio · civil rules","A domestic official record may be proved by an official publication or a copy attested by its custodian with a certificate of custody; a foreign record needs an attested copy plus a final certification of genuineness by a U.S. or foreign diplomatic or consular official, though the court may excuse certification for good cause. A certified statement that a diligent search found no record is admissible to prove absence","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 44.1. Judicial notice of law; foreign law","Ohio · civil rules","Courts must judicially notice Ohio Supreme Court rules and Ohio case, constitutional, and statutory law. A party relying on an Ohio municipal ordinance, local rule, or administrative regulation, or on the law of another U.S. state or jurisdiction, must give notice in a pleading or other reasonable notice, though a court may notice its own rules and local ordinances without notice. A party relying on foreign-country l","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","Ohio · civil rules","A subpoena, issued blank by the clerk or signed by an attorney of record, may command attendance at a trial or hearing anywhere in Ohio, a deposition in the county where the person lives or works (or as ordered), or production or inspection of documents, ESI, things, or premises, but it can’t be used to get a party’s deposition or documents in discovery (use Civ.R. 30 and 34). It may be served by sheriff, bailiff, at","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 46. Exceptions unnecessary","Ohio · civil rules","A formal exception is not needed to preserve an issue for review once the matter has been raised by objection, motion, or otherwise and the court has ruled.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 47. Jurors","Ohio · civil rules","Prospective jurors are examined under oath by the court or the parties, and the court may give a brief introduction to the case. Each party (or side, if interests are essentially the same) gets 3 peremptory challenges, exercised alternately starting with the plaintiff; passing waives that challenge only, but if all sides pass in sequence, all remaining peremptories are waived. The court may seat up to 4 alternates, w","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 48. Majority verdict; number of jurors","Ohio · civil rules","A civil verdict requires agreement of three-fourths or more of the jurors, signed in writing by those concurring; on request the jury is polled, and if more than one-fourth disagree the jury deliberates further. Parties may stipulate to fewer jurors than the Civ.R. 38(B) maximum, three-fourths is rounded up to the next whole juror, and juries of fewer than 4 must be unanimous. Unlike FRCP 48, which requires a unanimo","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 49. Verdicts; interrogatories","Ohio · civil rules","General verdicts are used, and on any party’s request before argument the court must submit written interrogatories along with the general verdict form. If answers and verdict conflict, the court may enter judgment on the answers, send the jury back, or order a new trial. Unlike FRCP 49, Ohio abolishes special verdicts.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 50. Directed verdict; JNOV","Ohio · civil rules","A party may move for a directed verdict, stating specific grounds, after the opponent’s opening statement, at the close of the opponent’s evidence, or at the close of all evidence; it is granted when, construing the evidence most strongly for the opponent, reasonable minds could reach only a conclusion adverse to that party. A motion for judgment notwithstanding the verdict (or for judgment if no verdict) must be ser","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 51. Jury instructions; objections","Ohio · civil rules","At the close of evidence or earlier as directed, parties may file written requested instructions (copies to all parties), and the court must rule on them before closing arguments, give complete instructions after argument (it may also instruct before), and give the jury at least one written copy or recording of the final instructions. To raise instruction error on appeal, a party must object before the jury retires, ","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings by the court","Ohio · civil rules","In a bench trial, judgment may be general unless a party requests in writing, before entry of judgment or within 7 days after notice of the court’s announced decision (whichever is later), separate findings of fact and conclusions of law. The court may require proposed findings, and findings are not needed on motions such as those under Civ.R. 12, 55, and 56. Unlike FRCP 52, which requires findings in every bench tri","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 53. Magistrates","Ohio · civil rules","A court may appoint magistrates (lawyers with at least 4 years of practice in good standing) to decide motions, try non-jury cases, preside over jury trials on the parties’ unanimous written consent, handle temporary protection orders, and regulate proceedings, including contempt in their presence by written order subject to immediate judicial review. A party may move to set aside a non-dispositive magistrate’s order","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgments; costs","Ohio · civil rules","A “judgment” is a written, judge-signed entry granting or denying relief and journalized on the docket. In multi-claim or multi-party cases, a final judgment on fewer than all claims or parties requires an express finding of “no just reason for delay”; otherwise the order stays revisable. A default judgment can’t exceed or differ in kind from what was demanded, other judgments grant all relief the winner is entitled ","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 55. Default","Ohio · civil rules","When a party fails to plead or defend, the opposing party applies to the court for default judgment; no default may be entered against a minor or incompetent person without an appearing guardian or representative, and a defaulting party who has appeared must get written notice at least 7 days before the hearing. The court may hold hearings or a jury trial to fix damages, may set aside a default judgment under Civ.R. ","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","Ohio · civil rules","A claimant may move for summary judgment once the adverse party’s time to respond to the pleading has expired or after the adverse party moves, and a defending party may move at any time, but if the case is set for pretrial or trial, leave of court is required. Responses and replies follow Civ.R. 6(C), and the court may consider only listed materials (pleadings, depositions, interrogatory answers, admissions, affidav","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments","Ohio · civil rules","Declaratory judgment actions under R.C. Chapter 2721 follow these rules; another adequate remedy doesn’t bar declaratory relief, and the court may advance the hearing on the trial list.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 58. Entry of judgment","Ohio · civil rules","After a general verdict, an announced decision, or a grant or denial of relief, the court must promptly prepare (or have counsel prepare) a judgment entry and file and journalize it within 30 days absent good cause; a judgment is effective only when the clerk enters it on the journal, and approving an entry doesn’t waive appeal objections. Within 3 days of journalizing, the clerk must serve notice on all parties not ","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 59. New trials","Ohio · civil rules","A new trial may be granted for irregularity, misconduct, accident or surprise, excessive or inadequate damages from passion or prejudice, error in amount of recovery, weight of the evidence (only once per case), judgment contrary to law, newly discovered evidence, error of law at trial, or other good cause, with grounds stated in writing. Motions for new trial, remittitur, additur, prejudgment interest, or attorney f","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from judgment","Ohio · civil rules","Clerical mistakes may be corrected any time (with appellate leave once an appeal is docketed). On motion, a court may relieve a party from a final judgment for (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect; (2) newly discovered evidence; (3) fraud or misconduct; (4) satisfaction, reversal of an underlying judgment, or inequity of prospective application; or (5) any other justifying reason; the motion must","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","Ohio · civil rules","No error in admitting or excluding evidence or in any ruling justifies a new trial or disturbing a judgment unless refusing relief would be inconsistent with substantial justice; errors not affecting substantial rights are disregarded.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of judgment","Ohio · civil rules","On motion any time after judgment, the court may stay execution, on security conditions, until the time to move under Civ.R. 50, 59, or 60 or to appeal runs, and while such motions are pending. An appellant obtains a stay pending appeal by posting an adequate supersedeas bond, effective when the court approves it; the state and its subdivisions, agencies, and officers need no bond. Appellate courts keep their own sta","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 63. Disability of a judge","Ohio · civil rules","If the trial judge can’t continue a jury trial, or can’t perform post-verdict or post-findings duties, another judge designated by the administrative judge (or by the Chief Justice for a single-judge division) may take over after certifying familiarity with the record, or may grant a new trial if unable to do so.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 64. Seizure of person or property","Ohio · civil rules","All remedies provided by law for seizing a person or property to secure a future judgment, such as arrest, attachment, garnishment, replevin, and sequestration, are available at the start of and during an action.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions","Ohio · civil rules","A temporary restraining order may issue without notice only on specific sworn facts showing immediate irreparable harm and the attorney’s written certification of notice efforts; it expires in no more than 14 days unless extended once for a like period for good cause or longer by consent, the preliminary-injunction hearing must be set at the earliest possible time, and the adverse party may move to dissolve it on 2 d","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 65.1. Civil protection orders","Ohio · civil rules","Governs domestic violence, dating violence, stalking, and sexually oriented offense civil protection order proceedings under R.C. 3113.31, 2151.34, and 2903.214, overriding inconsistent civil rules. The clerk serves the petition and any ex parte order by personal service (later papers under Civ.R. 5(B)), and service may be modified to protect the petitioner’s address; discovery is allowed only by court order addressi","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers","Ohio · civil rules","An action in which a receiver has been appointed can be dismissed only by court order, and receiverships are administered as provided by law and court rules.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 67. Reserved","Ohio · civil rules","Reserved.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of judgment","Ohio · civil rules","A refused offer of judgment can’t be filed with the court for purposes of determining costs, though voluntary settlement offers remain allowed. Unlike FRCP 68, Ohio’s rule has no cost-shifting penalty for rejecting an offer.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution","Ohio · civil rules","A money judgment is enforced by writ of execution unless the court directs otherwise, with procedures as provided by law, and the judgment creditor may take discovery from anyone, including the debtor, under these rules in aid of the judgment.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 70. Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","Ohio · civil rules","If a party fails to perform a specific act ordered by judgment (such as conveying land or delivering documents) within the time set, the court may have another person do it at that party’s cost, issue a writ of attachment, hold the party in contempt, or for property in Ohio enter a judgment transferring title directly; a party entitled to possession may obtain a writ of execution from the clerk.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 71. Process for and against nonparties","Ohio · civil rules","A nonparty in whose favor an order is made may enforce it as if a party, and a nonparty against whom an order is lawfully enforceable is subject to the same enforcement process as a party.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 72. Reserved","Ohio · civil rules","Reserved.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73. Probate division","Ohio · civil rules","Rules 73 to 73.29 apply only in the probate division and control over conflicting civil rules; venue follows law rather than Civ.R. 3(C), and improperly venued cases are transferred with possible costs and attorney fees against the filer. Summons service follows Civ.R. 4 to 4.6; other required notice may be given by personal or residence delivery, certified or express mail or signed-receipt commercial carrier, ordina","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.1. Definitions","Ohio · civil rules","Defines a probate “case” to include civil complaints and administrative appeals, estates, trusts, guardianships, conservatorships, emergency orders, adoptions, name changes, and any other matter given a case number (such as will filings, minor settlements, birth corrections, commitments, and powers of attorney); each wrongful-death trust beneficiary, ward, adoptee, or name-change applicant counts as a separate case.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.2. Standard probate forms","Ohio · civil rules","Prescribes the Supreme Court’s Standard Probate Forms for designated probate filings; where none exists, Civil Rule or local forms are used. Forms may be neatly modified by deletion or interlineation (courts can’t require routine modification), and original instruments may be prepared on 8½ by 11 inch paper following the standard caption and format.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.3. Printing specifications for probate forms","Ohio · civil rules","Sets printing specifications for blank probate forms (estates, guardianships, adoptions, and related matters): pages no larger than 8½ by 11 inches, side margins of about ½ to ¾ inch and top margin of about ⅞ to 1⅛ inches, sans-serif type without italics, main headings in 16-point or larger, captions and subheadings in 12 to 16 points, body in 8 to 12 points, and under-line instructions no larger than 8 points. It al","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.4. Hours of the court","Ohio · civil rules","Each probate court sets its own business hours.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.5. Conduct in the court","Ohio · civil rules","Conduct interfering with court business is prohibited, and no recording, broadcasting, or photography (other than by the court reporter) is allowed without the court’s advance consent under Sup.R. 11.00.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.6. Examination of probate records","Ohio · civil rules","Records can’t be removed from the court without the judge’s approval, copies cost a judge-set per-page fee, and adoption, mental illness, minor settlement, and similar confidential records are accessible only as the judge authorizes, with contempt for unauthorized removal or disclosure.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.7. Continuances","Ohio · civil rules","Continuance motions must be in writing with caption and case number and a proposed entry leaving the new date blank, and are granted only on proof of reasonable notice to or consent of the adverse party; failing to object within a reasonable time counts as consent.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.8. Filings and judgment entries","Ohio · civil rules","Filings (except wills) must be on 8½ by 11 inch microfilmable paper, typed or printed and captioned, with the case number on each page and counsel’s (or the fiduciary’s) contact information and registration number. A fiduciary who fails to keep an address current may be removed after at least 10 days’ written notice of the hearing; the prevailing party’s counsel must submit a proposed judgment entry within 7 days aft","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.9. Deposit for court costs","Ohio · civil rules","A deposit in the amount set by local rule is required when any action or proceeding is filed, additional deposits may be required, and the deposit is applied as filings occur.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.10. Wills","Ohio · civil rules","Before applying to probate a will, appoint a fiduciary, or relieve an estate from administration, the applicant or attorney must check the index of deposited wills, and earlier deposited wills are filed for record only. The fiduciary of a testate estate must file a Certificate of Service of Notice of Probate of Will (Form 2.4) within 2 months of appointment or face removal; a minor can’t sign a waiver of notice, nor ","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.11. Letters of authority and notice of appointment","Ohio · civil rules","Notice of an application to appoint an administrator must be served at least 7 days before the hearing (on court-designated persons if there is no known Ohio spouse or next of kin). The administrator must notify all heirs and support-allowance claimants within 7 days after appointment unless they had notice of the hearing or waived it, and the court must serve the surviving spouse with the spousal citation and summar","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.12. Appraisers","Ohio · civil rules","A fiduciary may pay an appraiser a reasonable agreed fee up to the local-rule limit without applying to the court (court approval is required if there is no local rule), and a specially qualified appraiser may be appointed for unusual property.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.13. Claims against estate","Ohio · civil rules","The fiduciary must file a copy of any rejection of a claim filed with the court, and if a claims or insolvency hearing is held, must file a schedule of all claims with the hearing application or within 10 days after the court notifies the fiduciary of a court-initiated hearing.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.14. Sale of personal property","Ohio · civil rules","An application to sell personal property must adequately describe it, and except for good cause no order of sale issues before the inventory is filed.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.15. Accounts","Ohio · civil rules","Vouchers and receipts must be keyed to the account by number, letter, or date; land sales must show gross proceeds with the closing statement; receipts signed under a power of attorney require the recorded power to be attached. The court may require assets to be exhibited with a partial account (by bank statement, certified inventory, or examination by a deputy clerk or commissioner), and a final or distributive acco","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.16. Land sales","Ohio · civil rules","In R.C. Chapter 2127 land sales, the plaintiff must file evidence of title dated after the complaint before the court finds the sale necessary, and must mail notice of a public sale to all defendants at least 3 weeks beforehand and certify notice and advertising before the sale. Private-sale confirmation entries must show gross proceeds and the proposed closing statement, and the court may appoint a disinterested inv","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.17. Estates of minors up to $25,000","Ohio · civil rules","Applications for minors’ estates of not more than $25,000 are brought by the parent or custodian in the minor’s name and must state the amount and payee. If no guardian is appointed, the minor’s attorney prepares an entry depositing the funds in the minor’s name, impounding principal and interest until court order or majority, deposits the funds, and files a Verification of Receipt and Deposit (Form 22.3) within 7 da","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.18. Settlement of minors’ injury claims","Ohio · civil rules","The guardian of the estate, or if none the parents or custodian, applies in the minor’s name to settle a minor’s injury claim; a noncustodial parent is entitled to 7 days’ notice, which may be waived. The application needs a current physician’s statement on the injuries, disclosure of payments to others and of the fee arrangement (fees need court approval), and the minor and applicant must attend the hearing.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.19. Settlement of adult wards’ claims","Ohio · civil rules","The guardian of the estate applies to settle claims for or against an adult ward, with notice to interested parties as the court determines (the court may dispense with notice), and the court may authorize settlement in the ward’s best interest. Injury settlements need a current physician’s statement and disclosure of payments to others and of counsel fees, which require court approval.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.20. Wrongful death and survival settlements","Ohio · civil rules","An application to approve settlement and distribution (Form 14.0) must state the facts, the amounts allocated to the wrongful-death and survival claims, and the proposed distribution of wrongful-death proceeds. The fiduciary must give written notice and a copy to interested persons who haven’t waived, the court keeps jurisdiction despite waivers, and counsel fees need court approval.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.21. Counsel fees","Ohio · civil rules","Probate attorney fees are governed by Prof.Cond.R. 1.5, aren’t paid in estates until the final account is prepared unless the court approves earlier, and require a written application and hearing unless local rule provides otherwise, with notice to affected parties. No fees are allowed to counsel for fiduciaries delinquent in filing accounts absent good cause, there are no automatically approved minimum or maximum fe","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.22. Executor and administrator commissions","Ohio · civil rules","Extra compensation for extraordinary services requires an itemized application, possibly with a hearing. The court may deny or reduce commissions for late inventories or accounts or unfaithful service; co-fiduciaries together get no more than one would (unless the will provides otherwise), and commissions are reduced by counsel fees paid for the fiduciary’s own work absent good cause.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.23. Guardian’s compensation","Ohio · civil rules","Guardian compensation is set by local rule, expenses must be itemized and not exceed court-approved amounts, and extra compensation or person-only guardian fees require an itemized application, possibly with a hearing. Co-guardians together get no more than one guardian would, and compensation may be denied or reduced for delinquent filings or unfaithful service.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.24. Trustee’s compensation","Ohio · civil rules","Trustee compensation is set by local rule, with extraordinary-service fees on itemized application; co-trustees together get no more than one would unless the instrument provides otherwise. Absent good cause, no trustee compensation or counsel fees are allowed while an account is delinquent, and compensation may be reduced for delinquency or unfaithful service.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.25. Local rules","Ohio · civil rules","Local probate rules must be numbered to match the rule they supplement (for example, Local Rule 73.12).","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.26. Exception to the rules","Ohio · civil rules","For good cause on application, the probate division may grant exceptions to Rules 73 to 73.29.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.27. Compliance","Ohio · civil rules","Failure to follow the probate rules may result in sanctions as the court directs.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.28. Probate case management","Ohio · civil rules","Fiduciaries must meet statutory or court-ordered deadlines for inventories, accounts, and guardian’s reports, enforced by citation against fiduciary and attorney and by reduced commissions or fees. An estate open more than 6 months under R.C. 2109.301(B)(1) needs an application to extend administration (Form 13.8), extension applications must be signed by the fiduciary, and a status report is due in estates open 13 m","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 73.29. Dispute resolution","Ohio · civil rules","Probate courts may encourage alternative dispute resolution, including suggesting settlement talks, referring parties to eldercaring coordination or mediation, referring cases to early neutral evaluation with a stay of up to 60 days, or, on all parties’ written agreement, any other ADR method.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 74. Reserved","Ohio · civil rules","Reserved.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 75. Divorce, annulment, and legal separation","Ohio · civil rules","The civil rules apply with modifications: Civ.R. 14, 19, 19.1, and 24 don’t apply, but property holders, a child (with a guardian ad litem), persons claiming custody rights, and a support obligor’s employer may be joined. There is no jury; issues are heard by the judge or a magistrate; Civ.R. 55 default doesn’t apply; and judgment isn’t final under Civ.R. 54(B) unless property, spousal support, and parental rights ar","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 76. Reserved","Ohio · civil rules","Reserved (Rules 77 to 80 are also reserved).","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 81. References to Ohio Revised Code","Ohio · civil rules","A reference to a Revised Code section means that section as amended over time, including later-enacted sections numbered after it.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 82. Jurisdiction unaffected","Ohio · civil rules","These rules neither extend nor limit the jurisdiction of Ohio courts.","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 83. Local rules","Ohio · civil rules","Courts may adopt local practice rules consistent with Supreme Court rules, must file them with the Clerk of the Supreme Court, and must give notice and an opportunity for comment before adoption (or promptly afterward if an immediate need required adopting without it).","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 84. Forms","Ohio · civil rules","Forms in the Appendix of Forms approved by the Supreme Court are sufficient and must be accepted for filing, and substantially similar local forms may also be accepted. Unlike the FRCP, which abrogated its official forms and Rule 84 in 2015, Ohio keeps an appendix of forms (general civil, domestic relations, civil protection order, and probate).","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 85. Title","Ohio · civil rules","These rules are the Ohio Rules of Civil Procedure and may be cited as “Civil Rules” or “Civ.R.”","#st-oh-rules"],["Rule 86. Effective date","Ohio · civil rules","The rules took effect July 1, 1970, and amendments filed with the General Assembly take effect the following July 1 unless disapproved, applying to new and pending cases unless that would be infeasible or unjust. The rule itself lists no amendment dates; the most recent amendments in this compilation took effect July 1, 2026.","#st-oh-rules"],["Appeals in Ohio: 30 days to the district court of appeals, then 45 days to the Supreme Court","Ohio · appeals","Civil appeals of right go from the trial court (usually the court of common pleas) to one of Ohio’s district courts of appeals, which sit in three-judge panels. The appeal must be from a final order under R.C. 2505.02, with a Civ.R. 54(B) ‘no just reason for delay’ finding when fewer than all claims or parties are resolved. The notice of appeal is filed in the trial court within 30 days, and that deadline cannot be e","#st-oh-appeals"],["Notice of appeal: in the trial court, with the judgment attached","Ohio · appeals · Getting into the court of appeals","File the notice of appeal with the clerk of the trial court, attaching a time-stamped copy of the judgment or order appealed. The notice names each appellant, designates the judgment or order (or part) appealed, and names the court of appeals. Only a late notice is fatal; other defects allow the court to act as it considers appropriate, including dismissal. Courts may require a docketing statement by local rule. App.","#st-oh-appeals"],["Time: 30 days from entry, or from late clerk service","Ohio · appeals · Getting into the court of appeals","Appeal within 30 days of entry of a final order, or within 30 days after a non-final order becomes final. If the clerk did not serve notice of the judgment within the 3 days required by Civ.R. 58(B), the 30 days run from when service is actually completed. ‘Entry’ means entry under Civ.R. 58(A). A notice filed after the decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed immediately after entry. App.R. 4(A), (","#st-oh-appeals"],["Tolling post-judgment motions","Ohio · appeals · Getting into the court of appeals","The appeal clock starts for all parties when the trial court resolves the last of these timely filings: a Civ.R. 50(B) motion, a Civ.R. 59 new-trial motion, objections to a magistrate’s decision, a request for findings of fact and conclusions of law, a motion for attorney fees, or a motion for prejudgment interest. If a notice of appeal is filed while one is pending, the court of appeals remands and stays the appeal.","#st-oh-appeals"],["Cross-appeals and second appeals","Ohio · appeals · Getting into the court of appeals","An appellee who wants to change the judgment, or an interlocutory ruling that would merge into it if reversed, must file a notice of cross-appeal. An appellee who only defends the judgment on other grounds needs no cross-appeal or cross-assignment of error. Any other party may appeal within the original period or within 10 days after the first notice, whichever is later. Cross-appeals receive their own case number. A","#st-oh-appeals"],["Final orders and partial judgments","Ohio · appeals · Getting into the court of appeals","Whether an order is appealable is governed by R.C. 2505.02(B). A ruling on fewer than all claims or parties is final only with a Civ.R. 54(B) express determination that there is no just reason for delay. If an appeal is allowed from a partial order not entered under Civ.R. 54(B), a party may appeal within 30 days of that order or of the order disposing of the remaining claims. R.C. 2505.02(B); Civ.R. 54(B); App.R. 4(","#st-oh-appeals"],["Interlocutory appeals in civil cases","Ohio · appeals · Getting into the court of appeals","There is no general discretionary interlocutory appeal in civil cases; immediate review depends on the order fitting R.C. 2505.02(B), such as certain provisional-remedy orders. One statutory example: denial of a UPEPA (anti-SLAPP) motion for expedited relief is a final order, appealable within 30 days of entry. During that appeal, all proceedings among all parties are stayed. R.C. 2505.02(B)(4), (B)(9); R.C. 2747.05(","#st-oh-appeals"],["Stays and supersedeas bonds","Ohio · appeals · Getting into the court of appeals","Seek a stay or bond approval in the trial court first. A motion to the court of appeals must show that trial-court relief was impracticable or was denied by journal entry, and it is ordinarily decided by at least two judges. An appellant gets a stay as of right by posting an adequate supersedeas bond approved by the trial court; the State and its subdivisions need no bond. Ohio has no automatic post-judgment stay. Ap","#st-oh-appeals"],["Transcript: order it and file the App.R. 9(B)(5) statement with the notice","Ohio · appeals · Record and briefing in the court of appeals","The appellant must order the transcript in writing and file the transcript order with the trial clerk. Unless the whole transcript will be included, the appellant files with the notice of appeal a statement listing the assignments of error and the parts to be transcribed. The appellee may designate more parts within 10 days. Without a usable recording, use a statement of the evidence (App.R. 9(C)) or an agreed statem","#st-oh-appeals"],["Record due in 40 days (20 on the accelerated calendar)","Ohio · appeals · Record and briefing in the court of appeals","The record is transmitted when complete, or 40 days after the last notice of appeal (20 days in accelerated cases), unless extended. Extensions must be sought within the original or an extended period. If the appellant fails to arrange timely transmission, the appeal may be dismissed after notice, with 10 days to respond. App.R. 10(A)-(C); App.R. 11(C)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Brief schedule: 20/20/10 days","Ohio · appeals · Record and briefing in the court of appeals","The appellant’s brief is due 20 days after the clerk mails notice that the record was filed; the appellee’s brief 20 days after service of that brief; and the reply 10 days after service of the appellee’s brief. Three days are added after service by mail or commercial carrier, but not to court-ordered dates. Extensions require a motion showing good cause. An appellant who misses the deadline risks dismissal; an appel","#st-oh-appeals"],["Accelerated calendar: 15/15, no reply","Ohio · appeals · Record and briefing in the court of appeals","In districts with an accelerated calendar, cases needing little or no transcript (for example, summary judgment), agreed-statement cases, and cases designated by local rule go on it. Briefs are due 15 and 15 days, and replies only if ordered. A motion to move the case to the regular calendar is due within 7 days of assignment; a unique issue of substantial precedential value ordinarily counts as good cause. App.R. 3(","#st-oh-appeals"],["Brief contents and word limits","Ohio · appeals · Record and briefing in the court of appeals","Required parts: tables of contents and authorities, assignments of error with record references, issues presented, statement of the case, statement of facts with record citations, argument by assignment of error, and conclusion. An assignment not identified in the record or not separately argued may be disregarded.\nLimits: 9,000 words for initial and answer briefs and 4,500 for replies, excluding covers, tables, cert","#st-oh-appeals"],["Motions and oral argument","Ohio · appeals · Record and briefing in the court of appeals","Responses to motions are due within 10 days after service, and replies within 7. Procedural motions may be decided at once. Oral argument is scheduled in all cases unless a local rule requires a request, made by printing ‘ORAL ARGUMENT REQUESTED’ on the brief cover. Each side gets 15 minutes. The panel’s names are disclosed at least 14 days before argument or submission. Additional authorities must be given in writin","#st-oh-appeals"],["Filing and e-filing","Ohio · appeals · Record and briefing in the court of appeals","File with the clerk of the court of appeals in the county where the case originated; papers sent anywhere else are not filed. Paper filings are timely only if received, except that briefs count as filed when mailed by first-class mail or given to a commercial carrier for delivery within 3 days. Electronic filing is available only where adopted by local rule. Service may be by e-mail to an address listed on a prior fi","#st-oh-appeals"],["Frivolous appeals and costs","Ohio · appeals · Record and briefing in the court of appeals","The court may order an appellant in a frivolous appeal to pay the appellee’s reasonable expenses, including attorney fees. Costs (record, transcript, filing fee, not brief printing) generally fall on the appellant if the judgment is affirmed or the appeal dismissed, and on the appellee if it is reversed. App.R. 23; App.R. 24","#st-oh-appeals"],["Reconsideration and en banc: 15 days, 15 pages","Ohio · appeals · After the court of appeals decides","An application for reconsideration and/or en banc consideration is due 15 days after the judgment is filed; seeking both requires one document unless a local rule says otherwise. Limit: 15 pages; responses are due in 15 days. It must explain in detail each point of law or fact the court overlooked or misconstrued; it is not a vehicle to reargue. En banc review is granted only for conflicting decisions within the dist","#st-oh-appeals"],["Motion to certify a conflict: 10 days","Ohio · appeals · After the court of appeals decides","Move in the court of appeals to certify a conflict with another district within 10 days after the clerk mails the judgment and notes the mailing on the docket. Oppositions are due in 10 days and replies in 7. The court rules within 60 days. The motion does not extend the time to appeal to the Supreme Court, and the time to file it cannot be extended. App.R. 25; App.R. 14(B)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Judgment entry","Ohio · appeals · After the court of appeals decides","A court of appeals judgment takes effect only when the clerk journalizes it. For a Supreme Court appeal, the date the judgment entry is filed with the clerk is the date of entry. App.R. 22; S.Ct.Prac.R. 7.01(A)(1)(a)(i)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Jurisdictional (discretionary) appeal: 45 days, memorandum filed with the notice","Ohio · appeals · Supreme Court of Ohio","File the notice of appeal and a memorandum in support of jurisdiction in the Supreme Court within 45 days of the court of appeals’ entry. The deadline is mandatory and the Clerk refuses late filings. Only if a stay of the court of appeals’ judgment is sought may the notice and stay motion come first, with the memorandum still due by day 45.\nA timely App.R. 26(A) reconsideration or en banc application restarts the 45 ","#st-oh-appeals"],["Jurisdictional memoranda: 15 pages, no reply","Ohio · appeals · Supreme Court of Ohio","The memorandum in support may not exceed 15 numbered pages. It must explain why the case involves a substantial constitutional question or is of public or great general interest, and it attaches the date-stamped court of appeals opinion and judgment entry. The appellee’s response is due 30 days later (20 in parental-rights, adoption, and bail cases), also capped at 15 pages. Supplemental and reply memoranda are prohi","#st-oh-appeals"],["Appeals of right and certified conflicts","Ohio · appeals · Supreme Court of Ohio","Appeals of right (for example, in cases that began in the court of appeals) need a notice within 45 days of entry. In a certified-conflict case, the court of appeals’ certification order must be filed in the Supreme Court within 45 days after that order. A party pursuing both a certified conflict and a jurisdictional appeal must tell the Supreme Court about the pending conflict motion. S.Ct.Prac.R. 6.01(A); 8.01(D); ","#st-oh-appeals"],["Merit briefs: 40/30/20 days; 50-page limit","Ohio · appeals · Supreme Court of Ohio","If jurisdiction is accepted, the appellant’s merit brief is due 40 days after the Clerk files the record (20 days in parental-rights, adoption, and bail cases); the appellee’s brief 30 days after the appellant’s brief (20); and the reply 20 days after the appellee’s brief (15 in parental-rights and adoption cases). Limits: 50 numbered pages for principal briefs and 20 for the reply, excluding tables and appendix; dea","#st-oh-appeals"],["Extensions: one stipulated 20-day extension, or one 10-day court extension","Ohio · appeals · Supreme Court of Ohio","The Supreme Court generally does not extend deadlines, and the Clerk refuses extension requests. Exception: for merit briefs (and responses to original-action complaints), each party may get either one stipulated extension of up to 20 days, filed before the original deadline, or, where no stipulation can be obtained, one court-granted extension of up to 10 days for good cause. An extension for one party covers its wh","#st-oh-appeals"],["Reconsideration in the Supreme Court: 10 days","Ohio · appeals · Supreme Court of Ohio","A motion for reconsideration is due 10 days after the judgment entry or order is filed. It is allowed only for refusal to accept jurisdiction, sua sponte dismissal, grant of a motion to dismiss, or a merits decision, and it may not reargue the case. Oppositions are due in 10 days. Amici may not move but may file supporting memoranda. S.Ct.Prac.R. 18.02","#st-oh-appeals"],["E-filing and timing in the Supreme Court","Ohio · appeals · Supreme Court of Ohio","The E-Filing Portal is optional; filings are PDFs, and anything received after 11:59:59 p.m. Columbus time is filed the next business day. Paper received after 5:00 p.m. is reviewed the next business day but is timely if received by the due date. Deadlines falling on a weekend or holiday roll to 11:59:59 p.m. the next business day. For periods under 7 days, weekends and holidays are counted (unlike Civ.R. 6(A)). Out-","#st-oh-appeals"],["Notice of appeal to court of appeals (civil)","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the final judgment or order (or clerk’s actual service if not made within Civ.R. 58(B)’s 3 days).\nFiled in the trial court. The court of appeals cannot extend it (App.R. 14(B)). App.R. 4(A)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Notice of appeal by another party / cross-appeal","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the first timely notice of appeal (or the original 30 days, if later).\nNeeded only if the appellee seeks to change the judgment. App.R. 4(B)(1); App.R. 3(C)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Restarted appeal period after tolling motions","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order resolving the last timely Civ.R. 50(B), 59, magistrate-objection, findings, attorney-fee, or prejudgment-interest filing.\nCiv.R. 60(B) motions do not toll. App.R. 4(B)(2)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Civ.R. 50(B) JNOV / Civ.R. 59 new-trial motion","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from entry of judgment (or the clerk’s actual service if late).\nCannot be extended (Civ.R. 6(B)(2)(e)). Civ.R. 50(B); Civ.R. 59(B)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Objections to magistrate’s decision","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the magistrate’s decision.\nFailure to object forfeits all but plain-error review. Civ.R. 53(D)(3)(b)(i)","#st-oh-appeals"],["App.R. 9(B)(5) transcript statement / docketing statement","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","0 days from filed with the notice of appeal.\nThe record is deemed complete if not filed within 10 days of the notice (App.R. 10(B)(7)). App.R. 9(B)(5); App.R. 3(H)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Appellee’s designation of additional transcript","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from service of the appellant’s App.R. 9(B)(5) statement. App.R. 9(B)(5)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Transmission of record","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from filing of the last notice of appeal.\n20 days on the accelerated calendar. Extensions must be requested before the period expires. App.R. 10(A)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from clerk’s mailing of notice that the record was filed.\n15 days on the accelerated calendar (App.R. 11.1(C)). App.R. 18(A)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from service of the appellant’s brief.\n15 days on the accelerated calendar. App.R. 18(A)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Reply brief","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\nNot allowed on the accelerated calendar unless ordered. App.R. 18(A)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Response to motion in court of appeals","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from service of the motion.\nReply in 7 days. App.R. 15(A)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Application for reconsideration / en banc","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the court of appeals’ judgment.\n15-page limit; response in 15 days. App.R. 26(A)(1)(a), (A)(2)(c)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Motion to certify a conflict","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from clerk’s mailing of the judgment and docket notation.\nDoes not extend the 45-day Supreme Court deadline and cannot itself be extended. App.R. 25(A)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Notice of appeal + memorandum in support of jurisdiction to Supreme Court","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","45 days from filing of the court of appeals’ judgment entry.\nMandatory; runs from the ruling on a timely App.R. 26(A) application. 15-page memorandum. S.Ct.Prac.R. 7.01(A)(1)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Memorandum in response to jurisdiction","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the memorandum in support.\n20 days in parental-rights, adoption, and bail cases. S.Ct.Prac.R. 7.03(A)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Certified-conflict filing in Supreme Court","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","45 days from date of the court of appeals’ order certifying the conflict. S.Ct.Prac.R. 8.01(D)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Supreme Court merit brief (appellant)","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from clerk’s filing of the record.\nAppellee 30 days later; reply 20 days later. 50/50/20-page limits. S.Ct.Prac.R. 16.02(A)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Supreme Court motion for reconsideration","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the judgment entry or order. S.Ct.Prac.R. 18.02(A)(1)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Pro hac vice motion in Supreme Court","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the appeal (or at least 30 days before oral argument). S.Ct.Prac.R. 2.02(B)(3)-(4)","#st-oh-appeals"],["UPEPA (anti-SLAPP) denial appeal","Ohio · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order denying the motion for expedited relief.\nInterlocutory appeal of right; all proceedings stayed during the appeal. R.C. 2747.05(C)","#st-oh-appeals"],["Supreme Court of Oklahoma","Oklahoma · courts","Court of last resort in civil cases. Every civil appeal is filed there by petition in error; it keeps some cases and assigns others to the Court of Civil Appeals, and it reviews Court of Civil Appeals decisions only on certiorari granted by a majority of the Justices. It has exclusive authority over bar admission and lawyer discipline (5 O.S. §§ 12-13).","#st-ok"],["Court of Criminal Appeals","Oklahoma · courts","Separate appellate court for criminal cases; civil appeals do not go there. Its recusal substitution procedure appears in 20 O.S. § 1402(C). Its own rules were not collected.","#st-ok"],["Court of Civil Appeals","Oklahoma · courts","Hears only civil cases assigned by the Supreme Court. At least four permanent three-judge divisions, two in Tulsa County and two in Oklahoma County, plus divisions the Supreme Court convenes, including a Court of Existing Claims Division for pre-2014 workers’ compensation appeals. Opinions are not precedent unless the Supreme Court approves them for publication in the official reporter (20 O.S. §§ 30.1-30.5, 30.14).","#st-ok"],["District Courts","Oklahoma · courts","General-jurisdiction trial courts organized in 26 judicial districts (20 O.S. § 92.1). Civil procedure is set by statute: the Oklahoma Pleading Code (12 O.S. §§ 2001-2027) and Discovery Code (12 O.S. §§ 3224-3237), supplemented by the Supreme Court’s Rules for District Courts.","#st-ok"],["Business Court Divisions I (Oklahoma County) and II (Tulsa County)","Oklahoma · courts","Created within the district courts by 2025 legislation, effective September 1, 2025, for qualifying business and complex cases filed on or after January 1, 2026; damages claims generally need at least $500,000 in controversy, and listed consumer, personal-injury, foreclosure, probate, and similar matters are excluded. Judges are appointed by the Governor with Senate consent; the filing or removal fee is $1,500 (20 O.","#st-ok"],["Statute: Oklahoma Citizens Participation Act","Oklahoma · anti-SLAPP","12 O.S. §§ 1430-1440, effective November 1, 2014, with exemptions amended in 2022 and 2025. It protects the rights to free speech (communication on a matter of public concern: health or safety, environmental, economic or community well-being, government, public officials or figures, or marketplace goods and services), to petition (broadly defined to include communications in or about judicial, official, legislative, ","#st-ok-slapp"],["Motion to dismiss: 60 days; discovery stops","Oklahoma · anti-SLAPP","A party may move to dismiss a legal action (including a counterclaim or cross-claim) that is based on, relates to, or responds to its protected activity. File within 60 days after service of the legal action; the court may extend for good cause. Filing suspends all discovery until the ruling, except specified and limited discovery the court allows for good cause. 12 O.S. §§ 1431(6), 1432, 1435(B)","#st-ok-slapp"],["Hearing within 60 days, ruling within 30","Oklahoma · anti-SLAPP","The hearing must be set within 60 days after service of the motion, never later than 90 days (120 days if limited discovery is allowed). The court must rule within 30 days after the hearing, considering the pleadings and affidavits. On request of the movant, it must also issue findings within 30 days on whether the suit was brought to deter protected activity or for an improper purpose. 12 O.S. §§ 1433, 1434(A), 1435","#st-ok-slapp"],["Burden shifting","Oklahoma · anti-SLAPP","The movant must show by a preponderance that the action is based on, relates to, or responds to protected activity. The claimant then avoids dismissal only by establishing by clear and specific evidence a prima facie case for each essential element. Even so, the court must dismiss if the movant proves each element of a valid defense by a preponderance. 12 O.S. § 1434(B)-(D)","#st-ok-slapp"],["Mandatory fees and sanctions","Oklahoma · anti-SLAPP","On dismissal, the court must award the movant costs, reasonable attorney fees, and other expenses as justice and equity require, and must sanction the claimant enough to deter similar suits. If the motion is frivolous or solely for delay, the court may award the respondent costs and fees. 12 O.S. § 1438","#st-ok-slapp"],["Appeal: denial by inaction is appealable and expedited","Oklahoma · anti-SLAPP","If the court does not rule within the 30 days, the motion is denied by operation of law and the movant may appeal. Appellate courts must expedite appeals and writs from orders on the motion or failure to rule. 12 O.S. § 1437","#st-ok-slapp"],["Exemptions","Oklahoma · anti-SLAPP","Does not apply to enforcement actions by the Attorney General or a district attorney; claims against sellers or lessors over statements arising from sales or leases of goods, services, or insurance aimed at actual or potential customers; bodily injury, wrongful death, or survival claims (and statements about them); Insurance Code or insurance-contract claims; and claims from officer-director, employment, or independe","#st-ok-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: motion with affidavit and Oklahoma associate counsel","Oklahoma · out of state","Out-of-state counsel’s motion to appear in a district court case must comply with Article II, § 5 of the Rules Creating and Controlling the Oklahoma Bar Association (5 O.S. ch. 1, app. 1). The statement that rule requires must be an affidavit attached to the motion, the motion must show the rule’s requirements are met, and the Oklahoma associate attorney’s entry of appearance must be filed with the motion. 12 O.S. § ","#st-ok-oos"],["Article II, § 5 terms (fees, limits, local counsel duties)","Oklahoma · out of state","The OSCN index lists Art. II § 5 as “Out-of-State Attorneys and Attorneys Granted a Special Temporary Permit To Practice,” but its text — including any application fee, any annual limit, and the associate counsel’s duties — was not collected. Read it on OSCN before filing; no fee or limit is stated here. Rules Creating and Controlling the Oklahoma Bar Ass’n, art. II, § 5 (5 O.S. ch. 1, app. 1). Not collected: OSCN be","#st-ok-oos"],["Entry of appearance with OBA number is required","Oklahoma · out of state","Every party must file an entry of appearance (counsel or pro se) no later than its first filing, including the OBA number, mailing address, phone and fax, and firm. Every signed paper must show the signing attorney’s OBA number. Filing the entry of appearance does not waive § 2012(B) defenses. 12 O.S. §§ 2005.2(A), 2011(A)","#st-ok-oos"],["Electronic service only by written consent","Oklahoma · out of state","E-mail or fax service on a party or counsel is valid only if that person consented in writing in the particular case and gave instructions, which can be done in the entry of appearance. Otherwise serve by hand, mail, or third-party commercial carrier. 12 O.S. §§ 2005(B), 2005.2(A)","#st-ok-oos"],["E-filing is by Supreme Court-approved rule","Oklahoma · out of state","Fax or electronic filing in the district courts is permitted only as allowed by rules of the Administrative Office of the Courts approved by the Supreme Court, and the Supreme Court is authorized to provide e-filing in the Supreme Court and district courts and must do so in the business courts. The current e-filing rules (District Court Rule 2.1, Supreme Court Rule 1.4) and which counties are live were not collected.","#st-ok-oos"],["Limited-scope representation","Oklahoma · out of state","The OSCN index shows District Court Rule 33, “Limited Scope Representation,” and Rule of Professional Conduct 1.2 covers scope of representation, but neither text was collected. Withdrawal under the statute requires leave of court, a proposed order, and either the client’s signature or a certificate of notice. Rule 33, Rules for District Courts (not collected); 12 O.S. § 2005.2(C)","#st-ok-oos"],["Governing code: Oklahoma Rules of Professional Conduct","Oklahoma · ethics","The Oklahoma Rules of Professional Conduct are at 5 O.S. ch. 1, app. 3-A (cited that way in 12 O.S. § 3226.2), and the Supreme Court has exclusive power to discipline lawyers. The OSCN index shows they follow the ABA Model Rules numbering (Rules 1.0 through 8.5). The rule text was not collected, so no textual differences from the Model Rules are stated here. 5 O.S. § 13; 5 O.S. ch. 1, app. 3-A. Not collected: OSCN be","#st-ok-ethics"],["Statutory duties of attorneys still on the books","Oklahoma · ethics","A 1910 statute lists duties of every attorney, including to maintain respect for the courts, to counsel only actions that appear legal and just (except criminal defense), never to mislead judges by artifice or false statements, to preserve the client’s secrets “at any peril to himself,” to abstain from offensive personalities, and never to reject the cause of the defenseless or oppressed for personal reasons. The sta","#st-ok-ethics"],["Fee-sharing with litigation funders points to Rules 1.5 and 5.4","Oklahoma · ethics","The 2025 litigation-funding amendments say fee sharing by lawyers or firms must follow the Rules of Professional Conduct, naming Rules 1.5 and 5.4, and lawyer-funder agreements are outside the discoverable “commercial litigation funding agreement” definition. Funding agreements between a funder and a party must be produced on request with a sworn certification about foreign-state funding sources. 12 O.S. §§ 3226(B)(1","#st-ok-ethics"],["180-day service deadline with automatic dismissal","Oklahoma · land mine","If a defendant is not served within 180 days after the petition is filed and good cause is not shown, the action is deemed dismissed without prejudice as to that defendant, and the court must enter a dismissal order within 200 days if no defendant was served. Refiling depends on the savings statute, 12 O.S. § 100. Relation back of a new defendant also turns on notice within the 180 days. 12 O.S. §§ 2004(I), 2015(C)(3","#st-ok-unique"],["Don’t plead a dollar figure above the diversity amount","Oklahoma · land mine","In non-contract cases seeking more than the 28 U.S.C. § 1332 amount, the petition must say only that damages exceed that amount; below it, state the exact amount. Punitive-damage petitions may not state a figure. Defendants can file a Motion to Clarify Damages before the pretrial order to test a below-threshold pleading for removal purposes. 12 O.S. §§ 2008(A), 2009(G)-(H)","#st-ok-unique"],["A “reservation of time” waives your Rule 12-type defenses","Oklahoma · land mine","The answer is due 20 days after service. Filing a reservation of time buys 20 more days but waives personal jurisdiction, venue, process, service, failure to state a claim, and capacity-to-be-sued defenses. A motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim that does not separately list each defect is denied without a hearing. 12 O.S. § 2012(A)(1), (B)","#st-ok-unique"],["There is no notice of appeal","Oklahoma · land mine","An appeal is commenced only by a petition in error filed with the Clerk of the Supreme Court within 30 days after the written, signed judgment is filed. Filing something in the trial court does not commence an appeal, and a minute entry, verdict, or letter ruling never starts or satisfies the clock. 12 O.S. §§ 990A(A), 696.2(D)","#st-ok-unique"],["Post-trial motions toll only if filed within 10 days","Oklahoma · land mine","A motion for new trial, JNOV, or to vacate or reconsider suspends the appeal time only if filed within 10 days after the judgment is filed; a motion to vacate filed on day 11 through 30 is allowed but does not extend the 30-day appeal clock. Fee and cost motions never do. 12 O.S. §§ 990.2(A)-(B), (D), 1031.1(B)","#st-ok-unique"],["Interlocutory appeals by right need the record within 30 days too","Oklahoma · land mine","For orders on injunctions, receivers, class certification, attachment, or granting a new trial, the petition in error and the record must both be filed with the Supreme Court within 30 days after the order is filed. 12 O.S. § 993(A)","#st-ok-unique"],["Discovery response clocks wait for the answer","Oklahoma · land mine","Interrogatories, document requests, and requests for admission may be served any time after the petition is filed, but the 30-day response period does not start until an answer to the petition is filed, unless the court orders otherwise or the parties agree in writing. 12 O.S. §§ 3233(A), 3234(B)(2)(a), 3236(A)","#st-ok-unique"],["30-question caps and a 6-hour, business-hours deposition","Oklahoma · land mine","Interrogatories and requests for admission are each capped at 30 per party (subparts count separately for interrogatories). Depositions are limited to 6 hours between 8 a.m. and 5 p.m. on non-holiday weekdays unless agreed or ordered, and a nonparty need only attend in the county of residence, an adjoining county, or where served. 12 O.S. §§ 3230(A)(3), (B), 3233(A), 3236(A)","#st-ok-unique"],["Expert depositions are usable at trial for any purpose","Oklahoma · land mine","Any expert witness’s deposition may be used at trial for any purpose without showing the expert is unavailable, and a witness who simply lives outside the county qualifies as unavailable — far looser than FRCP 32(a)(4). 12 O.S. § 3232(A)(3)(b), (e)","#st-ok-unique"],["Rule 11 motions here can bring mandatory fees","Oklahoma · land mine","Monetary sanctions, including the movant’s attorney fees, are mandatory for a paper presented for an improper purpose, and in non-contract cases the court must order fees against a party whose claim or defense it finds frivolous or knowingly false at the dismissal, summary-judgment, or merits stage. 12 O.S. §§ 2011(C)(2)(c), 2011.1","#st-ok-unique"],["Anti-SLAPP motion: 60 days from service, and it freezes discovery","Oklahoma · land mine","Defendants sued over speech, petitioning, or association must move under the Citizens Participation Act within 60 days after service; filing suspends discovery, and a win carries mandatory fees and sanctions. Plaintiffs need clear and specific evidence of every element. 12 O.S. §§ 1432, 1434, 1438","#st-ok-unique"],["No peremptory challenge in the statutes; disqualification for cause","Oklahoma · judges","Title 20 provides no peremptory change of judge. A judge may not sit, without the parties’ consent of record, where the judge is interested, is related to a party within the fourth degree, was counsel, or the validity of the judge’s own prior work as counsel is at issue. In contested civil cases a judge may not sit if related within the third degree to any attorney of record, which includes that attorney’s associates","#st-ok-unique"],["Procedure: written application, then mandamus","Oklahoma · judges","A party files a written application with the court clerk stating the grounds and asks the judge to certify the disqualification; if the judge does not do so at least 3 days before the trial setting, the party may seek mandamus from the proper tribunal. District Court Rule 15 (“Disqualification or Recusal of Judges in Civil and Criminal Cases”) supplies the detailed steps and time limits, but its text was not collecte","#st-ok-unique"],["Appellate judges","Oklahoma · judges","A Justice who presided at trial may not participate; if fewer than seven qualified Justices remain, the Governor assigns a retired Justice or a qualified member of the Bar. A Court of Civil Appeals judge who presided at trial or was counsel may not participate. 20 O.S. §§ 1402(A)-(B), 30.3","#st-ok-unique"],["Not established from collected text","Oklahoma · speech","No Oklahoma constitutional text or case law on free speech was collected, so no claim is made that Oklahoma protects speech more broadly than the First Amendment. The Citizens Participation Act (12 O.S. §§ 1430-1440) is the main procedural protection. Okla. Const. art. 2, § 22 (not collected)","#st-ok-unique"],["Civil procedure is statutory","Oklahoma · unique rules","The Pleading Code and Discovery Code are statutes enacted by the Legislature, with section captions part of the act. Expect statutory amendments every session (for example, the 2025 litigation-funding discovery changes effective November 1, 2025), not court rule packages. 12 O.S. §§ 2001, 3224","#st-ok-unique"],["Assignment of tort claims prohibited","Oklahoma · unique rules","Claims not arising out of contract cannot be assigned, though subrogation is unaffected. 12 O.S. § 2017(D)","#st-ok-unique"],["Rule 2001. Scope of the Oklahoma Pleading Code","Oklahoma · civil rules","Governs procedure in the district courts in all civil suits, at law or in equity, unless a statute specifies a different procedure, and is construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action. 12 O.S. §§ 1 through 2027 may be cited as the “Oklahoma Pleading Code,” and section captions are part of the act. Unlike the federal rules, the Code is a statute enacted by the Legislature, not a ","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2002. One form of action","Oklahoma · civil rules","There is one form of action, the “civil action.”","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2003. Commencement of action","Oklahoma · civil rules","A civil action is commenced by filing a petition (Oklahoma’s name for the complaint) with the court.","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2003.1. Commencement of actions by inmates","Oklahoma · civil rules","Pro se inmate filings must be on court-approved forms supplied free by the clerk and must include 11 listed items, including a sworn list of every suit the inmate filed as an inmate in the previous 10 years and its disposition. A noncomplying filing is returned with the reasons and may not proceed; a knowing and willful failure to comply may lead to dismissal with prejudice. If a defendant pleads qualified or absolut","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2003.2. Construction-related accessibility claims: pre-suit notice","Oklahoma · civil rules","Before suing over a facility’s noncompliance with accessibility construction standards, the plaintiff must send the defendant written notice of the specific violations by certified mail, return receipt requested, at least 120 days before filing, and attach the notice and signed receipt to the petition. Missing documentation or a filing less than 120 days after notice requires dismissal without prejudice on the defend","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2003.3. Website accessibility claims: pre-suit notice","Oklahoma · civil rules","Same scheme for claims that an organization’s website is not accessible to visually or hearing impaired users: certified-mail notice of the specific violations at least 120 days before filing, attached to the petition with the signed return receipt. Noncompliance means dismissal without prejudice on motion; a pre-suit fix means dismissal plus costs and attorney fees to the defendant, and sanctions if the action is fr","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2004. Process","Oklahoma · civil rules","The clerk issues the summons on filing. Service by sheriff, licensed process server, or specially appointed person; individuals may be served personally, by abode service on a resident 15 or older, or on an authorized agent. Plaintiff may elect certified mail, return receipt requested, delivery restricted to the addressee (effective on receipt or refusal), but mail service supports a default only if the record shows ","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2004.1. Subpoena","Oklahoma · civil rules","Subpoenas issue from the court where the action is pending and may be served anywhere in Oklahoma; the clerk issues blank signed subpoenas, and an Oklahoma-licensed attorney may also issue one. For out-of-state actions, the district court of the county where the deposition or production occurs issues the subpoena on proof of service of the deposition notice or document request. A plaintiff needs leave to serve a nonp","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2004.2. Notice of pendency of action (lis pendens)","Oklahoma · civil rules","Filing the petition charges third persons with notice, but for real property notice is effective only after a notice of pendency identifying the case and legal description is filed with the county clerk. Notice of pendency has no effect unless service of process is made, or publication commenced, within 120 days after the petition is filed. Interests acquired or perfected after the notice is filed are void as against","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2004.3. Courier and overnight delivery in place of mail","Oklahoma · civil rules","Where Title 12 requires or allows certified mail with restricted delivery, a party may instead use a commercial courier or overnight service with a signed written or electronic receipt; acceptance by a resident 15 or older at the abode counts. Courier may also replace ordinary mail. Deadlines are computed as if service were by mail. This section does not apply to filing with the court clerk.","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2005. Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","Oklahoma · civil rules","Every later pleading, discovery paper, written motion (except ex parte), notice, appearance, demand, and offer of judgment must be served on each party; defaulted parties get new claims served like a summons and judgments served under § 696.2(B). Service on a represented party goes to the attorney, by hand, mail, third-party commercial carrier for delivery within 3 calendar days, or electronic means (fax or e-mail) o","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2005.1. Service of post-judgment motions in divorce actions","Oklahoma · civil rules","All post-judgment motions in divorce proceedings must be served like a summons under § 2004(C).","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2005.2. Entry of appearance; out-of-state counsel; withdrawal; address of record","Oklahoma · civil rules","Every party must file an entry of appearance (by counsel or pro se) no later than its first filing; it must list name, signature, party represented, mailing address, phone and fax, OBA number, and firm, and may contain the written consent to electronic service. Filing it does not waive the § 2012(B) defenses. Out-of-state counsel’s motion must comply with Art. II § 5 of the Rules Creating and Controlling the Oklahoma","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2006. Time","Oklahoma · civil rules","Exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a legal holiday or a day the court clerk’s office is not open until its regular closing time. For periods under 11 days, intermediate holidays and closed days are excluded (except for times in §§ 765, 990.3, 1148.4, 1148.5, 1148.5A, and 1756). The court may enlarge a period on request before it expires, or after it expires for excusable neglect, but may no","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2007. Pleadings allowed; form of motions","Oklahoma · civil rules","Allowed pleadings: petition and answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a cross-claim, third-party petition and third-party answer; the court may order a reply to an answer. Motions must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state the grounds with particularity, and state the relief sought. Demurrers, pleas, and exceptions for insufficiency of a pleading are abolished.","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2008. General rules of pleading","Oklahoma · civil rules","A claim needs a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment. Key difference: in non-contract cases seeking money damages above the federal diversity amount (28 U.S.C. § 1332), the pleading must not demand a specific sum and must state only that the damages exceed that amount; a claim at or below that amount must state the specific amount sought. Denials, the 20 listed affirmative","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2009. Pleading special matters","Oklahoma · civil rules","Capacity, fraud or mistake with particularity, conditions precedent, official documents, judgments, time and place, and special damage track FRCP 9. Oklahoma additions: a petition seeking punitive damages may not state a dollar amount but must say whether damages sought exceed the federal diversity amount; and when the plaintiff pleads less than that amount, the defendant may file a Motion to Clarify Damages before t","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2010. Form of pleadings","Oklahoma · civil rules","Caption with court, title, file number, and designation; the petition names all parties, later pleadings may use the first party on each side. Numbered paragraphs, separate counts where helpful, adoption by reference, and written-instrument exhibits are part of the pleading. Tracks FRCP 10.","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2011. Signing of pleadings; sanctions","Oklahoma · civil rules","Every paper must be signed by at least one attorney of record with the attorney’s Oklahoma Bar Association number (or by the unrepresented party), with address and phone. Signing certifies the same four points as FRCP 11(b). A sanctions motion must be separate and served, and it may not be filed unless the paper is not withdrawn or corrected within 21 days after service. Sanctions are limited to deterrence; no moneta","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2011.1. Frivolous or knowingly false claims in non-contract actions: mandatory fees","Oklahoma · civil rules","In any action not arising out of contract, when ruling on a motion to dismiss or summary judgment, or after adjudication on the merits, the court may determine whether a nonprevailing party’s claim or defense was frivolous or proven knowingly false (intent to deceive, by clear and convincing evidence). On that finding the court must order the nonprevailing party to reimburse the prevailing party’s reasonable costs an","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2012. Defenses and objections: when and how presented","Oklahoma · civil rules","Answer within 20 days after service of summons and petition; a defendant may file a reservation of time extending the deadline 20 more days, but doing so waives personal jurisdiction, venue, process, service, failure-to-state-a-claim, and capacity-to-be-sued defenses (B(2)-(6), (9)). A cross-claim answer and a reply to a counterclaim are due in 20 days; the party requesting the summons or filing a counterclaim or cro","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2013. Counterclaim and cross-claim","Oklahoma · civil rules","Compulsory and permissive counterclaims and cross-claims track FRCP 13, with contingent counterclaims allowed. Oklahoma limitations rules: a same-transaction counterclaim is not time-barred even if barred when the petition was filed and may support affirmative judgment; an unrelated money counterclaim that accrued before either claim was barred but is now barred may be used only to reduce the opposing claim; a counte","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2014. Third-party practice","Oklahoma · civil rules","A defending party may serve a third-party petition on a nonparty who is or may be liable for all or part of the claim, or who is liable on a claim from the same transaction or occurrence; no leave is needed if filed within 10 days after serving the original answer (FRCP 14 allows 14 days). Third-party defendants’ defenses, claims against the plaintiff, and further impleader track FRCP 14; a plaintiff facing a counter","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2015. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Oklahoma · civil rules","Amend once as a matter of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 20 days after service if no response is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar; omitted counterclaims and adding or dropping parties may be done as of course in the same window. Otherwise leave (with the proposed amendment attached to the motion) or written consent, freely given. Response due within the remaining time or 2","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2016. Pretrial procedure","Oklahoma · civil rules","Absent superseding legislation, pretrial conferences are governed by rules of the Oklahoma Supreme Court (see District Court Rule 5). Unlike FRCP 16, the statute itself contains no scheduling-order provisions.","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2017. Parties; capacity; ban on assignment of tort claims","Oklahoma · civil rules","Real party in interest, capacity of any person or entity to sue and be sued, and representatives, next friends, and guardians ad litem for minors and incompetents track FRCP 17. Oklahoma addition: assignment of claims not arising out of contract is prohibited, though subrogation law is unaffected.","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2018. Joinder of claims and remedies; consolidation; separate trials","Oklahoma · civil rules","Unlimited joinder of claims against an opposing party; a money claim may be joined with a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance without first getting judgment. Consolidation of actions with common questions and separate trials of any claim or issue (preserving jury trial) are in this section, corresponding to FRCP 18, 42(a) and 42(b).","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2019. Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","Oklahoma · civil rules","Required-party joinder and the four equity-and-good-conscience factors for proceeding or dismissing when joinder is not feasible track FRCP 19. A pleading must name known required persons not joined and say why. Subject to the class-action section.","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2020. Permissive joinder of parties","Oklahoma · civil rules","Plaintiffs or defendants may be joined if claims arise from the same transaction or occurrence, from a series of transactions with a common question, or — broader than FRCP 20 — if the claims are connected with the subject matter of the action. In quiet title and lien-foreclosure cases anyone claiming an interest in the property may be joined. The court may order separate trials and must consider whether joinder prov","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2021. Misjoinder and nonjoinder","Oklahoma · civil rules","Misjoinder is not grounds for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped and claims severed at any stage on just terms, considering whether the result is a fair and convenient forum for all parties.","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2022. Interpleader","Oklahoma · civil rules","A stakeholder exposed to double or multiple liability may join and require claimants to interplead, even if claims are not of common origin, and a defendant may interplead by cross-claim or counterclaim. Applies to suits against a sheriff over property taken on execution. The court may order the fund deposited and a bond; a disinterested stakeholder who deposits the property should be discharged with costs and, in th","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2023. Class actions","Oklahoma · civil rules","Prerequisites and the three maintainable types track FRCP 23(a)-(b), but the petition must also contain factual allegations showing a plausible claim for relief. Certification is decided as soon as practicable, by an order defining the class and claims and appointing class counsel. Key differences: the certification order is reviewed de novo on appeal, and while that appeal is pending class discovery is stayed (the t","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2023.1. Derivative actions by shareholders","Oklahoma · civil rules","The petition must be verified, allege contemporaneous ownership (or devolution by operation of law), and state with particularity the demand efforts or reasons for not making them. The plaintiff must fairly and adequately represent similarly situated holders, and dismissal or compromise requires court approval and notice. Tracks FRCP 23.1.","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2023.2. Actions relating to unincorporated associations","Oklahoma · civil rules","Members of an unincorporated association may sue or be sued as a class through representative members only if they will fairly and adequately protect the association and its members; § 2023(D) and (E) orders and settlement procedures apply. Tracks FRCP 23.2.","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2024. Intervention","Oklahoma · civil rules","Intervention of right and permissive intervention track FRCP 24, with a rebuttable presumption that a guardianship petition impairs the interests of persons entitled to notice under 30 O.S. § 3-110. The motion must be served with the intervenor’s pleading; after it is granted, existing parties have 20 days to respond unless the court sets a shorter time. When a state statute’s constitutionality is challenged in a cas","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2025. Substitution of parties","Oklahoma · civil rules","On a party’s death, any party or the decedent’s former attorney of record may file and serve a statement of death (Form 22) with proof of death. A motion for substitution must be made within 90 days after service of the statement, or the action is dismissed without prejudice as to the deceased party; nonparties are served with the motion like a summons. Incompetency, transfer of interest, and automatic substitution o","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2025.1. Parent’s assignment to minor child of claim for child’s injury","Oklahoma · civil rules","Parents entitled to recover damages for injury to a minor child may assign that claim to the child, and are deemed to have done so when they sue as guardian, guardian ad litem, or next friend and seek those damages for the child. Recoveries are handled under 12 O.S. § 83.","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2026. Forms","Oklahoma · civil rules","The forms in § 2027 are sufficient under the Code and illustrate the simplicity and brevity it contemplates.","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 2027. Appendix of forms","Oklahoma · civil rules","Twenty-two forms, including the summons (Form 1), petitions on a note, account, goods sold, money lent or paid by mistake, negligence, conversion, specific performance, fraudulent conveyance, and FELA (Forms 2-13), a motion to dismiss and answers presenting § 2012(B) defenses (Forms 14-16), third-party summons and motion (Forms 17-18), motion to intervene (Form 19), allegation of reason for omitting a party (Form 21)","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3224. Oklahoma Discovery Code: short title and scope","Oklahoma · civil rules","§§ 3224 through 3237 are the Oklahoma Discovery Code, which governs discovery in all civil suits in all courts of the state.","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3225. Construction","Oklahoma · civil rules","The Discovery Code is construed, administered, and employed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3225.1. Discovery master","Oklahoma · civil rules","On motion or its own initiative, after a hearing unless waived, the court may appoint a discovery master for consented duties or, with findings of necessity, cost-benefit, and no undue burden on court access, to resolve pretrial and post-trial discovery matters. The master must disclose conflicts within 14 days of appointment, and a motion to disqualify is due within 14 days of the disclosure, with review by the assi","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3226. General provisions governing discovery","Oklahoma · civil rules","Methods include the usual devices plus authorizations for release of records. Initial disclosures are narrower than FRCP 26(a)(1): only a damages computation with supporting documents and, in injury cases, medical (and when relevant employment and school) record authorizations, due within 60 days after service unless stipulated, ordered, or objected to by motion; the same eight categories of cases are exempt. Scope: ","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3226.1. Abusive discovery; protective orders and expenses","Oklahoma · civil rules","In addition to § 3226(C) orders, the court may issue a protective order authorizing or denying discovery on motion with a good-faith in-person or telephone conferral certification, on a finding that justice requires protection from annoyance, harassment, embarrassment, oppression, or undue delay, burden, or expense. Expenses, including attorney fees, may be shifted to the losing side after an opportunity for hearing ","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3226.2. Commercial litigation funding definitions","Oklahoma · civil rules","Defines “commercial litigation funder” (anyone other than a contingent-fee attorney with a right to compensation contingent on and sourced from the proceeds of a civil action) and “commercial litigation funding agreement,” excluding attorney contingency fees and cost advances, agreements between lawyers or firms and funders (fee sharing remains governed by Rules of Professional Conduct 1.5 and 5.4), and consumer liti","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3226A. Withdrawal of discovery materials after judgment","Oklahoma · civil rules","No less than 30 nor more than 60 days after a judgment or final order is filed when no appeal is taken, or within 30 days after the appellate mandate issues, the party or counsel must withdraw, on receipt to the clerk, filed discovery items not introduced into evidence, not in the appellate record, or not needed on remand.","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3227. Depositions before action or pending appeal","Oklahoma · civil rules","A verified petition to perpetuate testimony is filed in the district court of the county where any expected adverse party resides, with notice served like a summons at least 20 days before the hearing (publication and appointed counsel for those not personally served; guardians ad litem for minors and incompetents). The court may order depositions if perpetuation may prevent a failure or delay of justice. Depositions","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3228. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Oklahoma · civil rules","Within Oklahoma, depositions must be taken before a certified or licensed shorthand reporter (CSR or LSR), subject to a grandfather clause for pre-1990 steno-mask reporters certified to the Board of Examiners of Official Shorthand Reporters. Outside Oklahoma: before a person authorized by local or Oklahoma law, a court-commissioned person, or by letter rogatory. No deposition before a relative, employee, or attorney ","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3229. Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","Oklahoma · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, parties may by written stipulation change who takes depositions and when, where, and how, and modify other discovery procedures, but a stipulation extending response times under §§ 3226, 3233, 3234, and 3236 needs court approval if it would interfere with a time set for completing discovery. Tracks FRCP 29.","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3230. Depositions upon oral examination","Oklahoma · civil rules","No leave needed except for a prisoner, a person already deposed, or a deposition within 30 days after service of summons on any defendant (unless the notice certifies the witness is leaving the state); a request for leave must certify an in-person or phone attempt to get a stipulation. Unless agreed or ordered, a deposition may not exceed 6 hours (FRCP 30 allows 7) and must be taken between 8:00 a.m. and 5:00 p.m. on","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3231. Depositions upon written questions","Oklahoma · civil rules","Any party may depose any person on written questions served with a notice naming the witness and officer; prisoners only by leave. Cross questions within 30 days after service, redirect within 10 days after cross, recross within 10 days after redirect (FRCP 31 uses 14, 7, and 7 days). The officer takes the answers and handles the record under § 3230, and the taking party gives notice of filing. Costs taxed under § 32","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3232. Use of depositions in court proceedings","Oklahoma · civil rules","Deposition testimony is usable against parties present, represented, or noticed, to the extent the Oklahoma Evidence Code would admit it. Party and organizational-designee depositions may be used for any purpose. A witness’s deposition may be used for any purpose if the witness is dead; does not reside in the county where the case is pending or is absent from it (FRCP 32 uses 100 miles); is unable to testify; cannot ","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3233. Interrogatories to parties","Oklahoma · civil rules","Limit of 30 interrogatories per party; an interrogatory asking for witness names and locations, or the existence, location, and custodian of documents, counts as one, but subparts count separately (FRCP 33 allows 25 including discrete subparts). More require a written stipulation or a motion showing good-faith conferral, good cause, and the proposed questions. Answer each separately under oath, restating the interrog","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3234. Production of documents and things; entry on land","Oklahoma · civil rules","Requests for documents, ESI, tangible things, and entry onto land track FRCP 34. Requests may be served without leave after the petition is filed; the written response is due within 30 days after service, but that period does not start until an answer to the petition is filed (unless ordered or agreed in writing). Objections must be specific, say whether materials are being withheld, and the response must state ESI f","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3235. Physical and mental examinations","Oklahoma · civil rules","Two tracks. When a party relies on its own physical (including blood group) or mental condition as an element of a claim or defense, an adverse party may obtain an examination by serving a request (no earlier than 30 days after service of summons, setting the exam at least 5 days after service) without a court order; the person examined must move to object, and bears the burden. Otherwise an examination by a suitably","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3236. Requests for admission","Oklahoma · civil rules","Limit of 30 requests per party (FRCP 36 has no numeric limit), with more allowed only by written stipulation or a motion showing conferral and good cause. May be served without leave after the petition is filed; matters are admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days after service, but the 30 days do not start until an answer to the petition is filed (unless ordered or agreed in writing). Answer content, r","#st-ok-rules"],["Rule 3237. Failure to make or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","Oklahoma · civil rules","Motions to compel go to the court where the action is pending or, for depositions, the court in the county where taken; the motion must certify an in-person or telephone good-faith conference. The court may order a privilege log listing author, recipients, date, length, nature or purpose, and basis, and may review documents in camera. Evasive or incomplete answers are failures to answer. Expense-shifting is mandatory","#st-ok-rules"],["Appeals in Oklahoma: one jurisdictional step, and the clock runs from a filed written judgment","Oklahoma · appeals","There is no notice of appeal. A civil appeal is commenced by filing a petition in error with the Clerk of the Supreme Court within 30 days after a written judgment, decree, or appealable order that meets 12 O.S. § 696.3 is filed with the trial court clerk. That filing is the only jurisdictional step; every other step in perfecting the appeal is not jurisdictional. All appeals go to the Supreme Court, which keeps some","#st-ok-appeals"],["Only a filed, signed, written judgment counts","Oklahoma · appeals · What starts the clock","The filing of a written judgment, decree, or appealable order that conforms to § 696.3 and is signed by the court is a jurisdictional prerequisite to an appeal. A minute entry, a verdict, or an informal statement such as a letter announcing the ruling is not a judgment. Even where the ruling is enforceable when pronounced (for example, injunctions, divorce, probate, quiet title), the appeal time runs only from the fi","#st-ok-appeals"],["Required contents of the judgment","Oklahoma · appeals · What starts the clock","Caption, a statement of the disposition including the relief awarded, the liabilities imposed, and the amount of any prejudgment interest, and the court’s signature and title. It may include costs, fees, and post-judgment interest if already decided. The clerk endorses the filing date. 12 O.S. § 696.3","#st-ok-appeals"],["Service within 3 days, and the late-mailing extension","Oklahoma · appeals · What starts the clock","The party who prepared the judgment (or the court’s designee) must serve a file-stamped copy on all parties, including defaulted parties, within 3 days after filing and file a certificate of service. If the appellant did not prepare the judgment and the court record does not show mailing or service on the appellant within 3 days (excluding weekends and holidays), the 30 days run from the earliest date the record show","#st-ok-appeals"],["Petition in error in the Supreme Court within 30 days","Oklahoma · appeals · Commencing the appeal","File the petition in error with the Clerk of the Supreme Court, not the trial court, within 30 days after the judgment is filed. Filing may be by delivery, or by certified or first-class mail, in which case the postmark (or other Postal Service proof of mailing) is the filing date; without that proof, the date the Clerk receives it controls. 12 O.S. § 990A(A)-(B)","#st-ok-appeals"],["Only the petition in error is jurisdictional","Oklahoma · appeals · Commencing the appeal","All other steps in perfecting the appeal are not jurisdictional, and parties may waive defects in perfecting an appeal except the timely petition in error (and a timely petition to review a certified interlocutory order). Defects that could have been raised in the trial court must be raised there promptly. 12 O.S. §§ 990A(E), 992","#st-ok-appeals"],["Premature petitions can be saved","Oklahoma · appeals · Commencing the appeal","A petition filed before the appeal time begins is dismissed as premature, but if the time begins before dismissal the appellant may file a supplemental petition in error without new costs. If the appeal is dismissed as premature, a new petition may be filed within the regular time or within 30 days after the dismissal notice is mailed, whichever is later. 12 O.S. § 990A(F)","#st-ok-appeals"],["Designation of record needs the court reporter’s acknowledgment","Oklahoma · appeals · Commencing the appeal","The district court clerk will not accept a designation of record unless it includes the court reporter’s signed acknowledgment of the transcript request (with date and deposit) or the attorney’s signed statement that no transcript was ordered and why. The same applies to counter-designations. The record must be complete within the time set by Supreme Court rule (not collected). 12 O.S. § 990A(D), (G)","#st-ok-appeals"],["Whole case is before the Court; cross-appeals by rule","Oklahoma · appeals · Commencing the appeal","A timely petition in error gives the Supreme Court jurisdiction of the entire action, and no further jurisdictional step is needed for any party to assert errors involving another party to the appeal. Time limits for counter-appeals and cross-appeals are set by Supreme Court rule; that rule was not collected, so check Okla. Sup. Ct. R. 1.25-1.27 on OSCN. 12 O.S. §§ 990.1, 990A(C)","#st-ok-appeals"],["Motions filed within 10 days suspend the appeal","Oklahoma · appeals · Post-trial motions","A motion for new trial, for judgment notwithstanding the verdict, or to correct, open, modify, vacate, or reconsider, filed within 10 days after the judgment is filed, means no appeal may be commenced until an order disposing of it is filed. Then the appeal is due within 30 days after that order is filed, and the movant who lost may appeal the judgment and the ruling together in one appeal; successive appeals are not","#st-ok-appeals"],["Motions filed after 10 days do not extend anything","Oklahoma · appeals · Post-trial motions","A motion to correct, open, modify, vacate, or reconsider filed more than 10 days after the judgment does not extend the appeal time, and an appeal filed before it is not premature; the parties must tell the Supreme Court about the pending motion and its disposition. A motion for costs or attorney fees never extends the time to appeal. 12 O.S. § 990.2(B), (D)","#st-ok-appeals"],["New trial: 10 days; vacatur by motion: 30 days","Oklahoma · appeals · Post-trial motions","A motion for new trial must be filed within 10 days after the conforming judgment is filed (later only by petition under § 655). The court may correct, open, modify, or vacate on motion filed within 30 days, or on its own within 30 days; after 30 days relief is by petition under § 1033 unless all appearing parties approve. Motions filed after the decision is announced but before the judgment is filed are deemed filed","#st-ok-appeals"],["A new-trial motion narrows the appeal","Oklahoma · appeals · Post-trial motions","A motion for new trial is not required to appeal, but if one is filed, no appeal may be taken until it is decided, and after denial the movant may not raise on appeal errors that were available but not included in the motion. 12 O.S. § 991","#st-ok-appeals"],["Interlocutory orders appealable by right","Oklahoma · appeals · Interlocutory and partial-judgment appeals","Orders on attachments; granting (except ex parte), denying, or modifying injunctions; provisional remedies affecting substantial rights; appointing or refusing receivers; pendente lite payments; certifying or refusing to certify a class (with certain jurisdiction and exhaustion rulings reviewable only with a certification appeal); and orders granting a new trial or opening or vacating a judgment. The petition in erro","#st-ok-appeals"],["Certified interlocutory orders","Oklahoma · appeals · Interlocutory and partial-judgment appeals","Any other order affecting a substantial part of the merits may be reviewed if the trial judge certifies that an immediate appeal may materially advance the end of the litigation; the Supreme Court may decline, and if it accepts, states whether the trial court proceedings are stayed. The petition deadline is set by Supreme Court Rules 1.50-1.56 (not collected). Failing to take an available interlocutory appeal does no","#st-ok-appeals"],["Judgment on fewer than all claims or parties","Oklahoma · appeals · Interlocutory and partial-judgment appeals","Requires an express determination that there is no just reason for delay and an express direction to file a final judgment; otherwise the ruling is not final and remains revisable. The court may stay enforcement until later judgments are filed. Tracks FRCP 54(b). 12 O.S. § 994","#st-ok-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP rulings are expedited","Oklahoma · appeals · Interlocutory and partial-judgment appeals","If the trial court does not rule on an Oklahoma Citizens Participation Act motion within 30 days after the hearing, it is denied by operation of law and the movant may appeal. Appellate courts must expedite any appeal or writ, interlocutory or not, from an order on the motion or the failure to rule. 12 O.S. §§ 1434(A), 1437","#st-ok-appeals"],["Assignment, panels, and precedent","Oklahoma · appeals · Court of Civil Appeals and certiorari","The Court of Civil Appeals decides only cases the Supreme Court assigns to it. It sits in at least four permanent three-judge divisions (two in Tulsa County, two in Oklahoma County), plus additional divisions the Supreme Court convenes; two judges must concur. Its opinions are not binding or citable as precedent unless approved for publication in the official reporter by a majority of the Supreme Court. 20 O.S. §§ 30","#st-ok-appeals"],["Certiorari to the Supreme Court","Oklahoma · appeals · Court of Civil Appeals and certiorari","A Court of Civil Appeals decision is reviewed only if a majority of the Justices grant certiorari, and the Supreme Court may recall a case. The petition for certiorari requires a $100 nonrefundable cost deposit (indigents may proceed under court rule). The deadline and form are in Supreme Court Rules 1.177-1.182, which were not collected. 20 O.S. §§ 30.1, 30.4(B)","#st-ok-appeals"],["Frivolous appeals","Oklahoma · appeals · Court of Civil Appeals and certiorari","The Supreme Court or Court of Civil Appeals must dismiss a frivolous appeal, cross-appeal, or original proceeding and may sanction the appellant, counsel, or both, including reasonable expenses and attorney fees. 12 O.S. § 995","#st-ok-appeals"],["Briefing schedule, brief length, rehearing, record deadlines, e-filing","Oklahoma · appeals · Not collected: verify on OSCN","These are governed by the Oklahoma Supreme Court Rules (12 O.S. ch. 15, app. 1), including Rules 1.10-1.11 (briefs), 1.13 (rehearing), 1.34 (time to complete the record), 1.36 (accelerated procedure for summary judgments and certain dismissals), and 1.4 (filings). The rule titles were seen on the OSCN index, but their text was not collected because OSCN began serving a bot check. No deadline or page or word limit is ","#st-ok-appeals"],["Petition in error (appeal from final judgment)","Oklahoma · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the written, signed judgment conforming to § 696.3 with the trial court clerk (or, if service on the appellant under § 696.2 is not shown within 3 days, excluding weekends and holidays, the earliest date the court records show service).\nJurisdictional. Filed with the Clerk of the Supreme Court; mailing date by postmark counts. 12 O.S. § 990A(A)","#st-ok-appeals"],["Appeal after timely post-trial motion","Oklahoma · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the order disposing of a new-trial, JNOV, or correct/open/modify/vacate/reconsider motion filed within 10 days of judgment.\nNo appeal may be commenced while such a motion is pending. Fee and cost motions do not count. 12 O.S. § 990.2(A)","#st-ok-appeals"],["Motion for new trial","Oklahoma · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the conforming judgment.\nLater relief only by petition under § 655. Extended from the record mailing date if the judgment was not mailed within 3 days. 12 O.S. § 653(A)","#st-ok-appeals"],["Motion to correct, open, modify, or vacate","Oklahoma · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the conforming judgment.\nOnly a motion filed within 10 days suspends appeal time (§ 990.2). After 30 days, use a petition under § 1033. 12 O.S. § 1031.1(B)","#st-ok-appeals"],["Interlocutory appeal by right (injunction, receiver, class certification, new trial granted, etc.)","Oklahoma · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the conforming order.\nPetition in error AND record due within the 30 days; the Supreme Court may extend time for the record for good cause. 12 O.S. § 993(A)","#st-ok-appeals"],["New petition after dismissal as premature","Oklahoma · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from mailing of notice that the appeal was dismissed as premature (or the regular period, whichever is later). 12 O.S. § 990A(F)(2)","#st-ok-appeals"],["Service of filed judgment on all parties","Oklahoma · appeals · Deadlines","3 days from filing of the judgment.\nDuty of the preparing party; a certificate of service must be filed. 12 O.S. § 696.2(B)","#st-ok-appeals"],["OCPA ruling (else denied by operation of law, appealable)","Oklahoma · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from hearing on the anti-SLAPP motion.\nAppeal is expedited. 12 O.S. §§ 1434(A), 1437(A)","#st-ok-appeals"],["Supreme Court of Pennsylvania","Pennsylvania · courts","The court of last resort, with administrative authority over the whole Unified Judicial System. It reviews the intermediate courts mostly by discretionary petition for allowance of appeal (allocatur). It also hears some direct appeals, including appeals as of right from matters that began in the Commonwealth Court. It makes the procedural rules and regulates admission, pro hac vice and lawyer discipline.","#st-pa"],["Superior Court of Pennsylvania","Pennsylvania · courts","A statewide intermediate appellate court that hears most civil and criminal appeals from the courts of common pleas.","#st-pa"],["Commonwealth Court of Pennsylvania","Pennsylvania · courts","A statewide intermediate appellate court for cases involving the Commonwealth, its agencies and local governments. It hears appeals from the trial courts and from state agencies (by petition for review), and it also has original jurisdiction over some actions brought against the Commonwealth.","#st-pa"],["Courts of Common Pleas","Pennsylvania · courts","General-jurisdiction trial courts organized into judicial districts that mostly follow county lines. Civil filings go to the county prothonotary (the civil clerk). Each county adopts its own local rules, including the dollar limit for compulsory arbitration.","#st-pa"],["Magisterial district courts; Philadelphia Municipal Court; Pittsburgh Municipal Court","Pennsylvania · courts","The entry-level courts, with more than 500 magisterial districts statewide plus municipal courts in Philadelphia and Pittsburgh. Civil cases before magisterial district judges follow separate rules (Pa.R.Civ.P.M.D.J.), not the Pa.R.Civ.P.","#st-pa"],["Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (2024): immunity and fees","Pennsylvania · anti-SLAPP","Act 72 of 2024 (signed July 17, 2024) added 42 Pa.C.S. §§ 8340.11-8340.18. A person is immune from civil liability for a cause of action based on protected public expression if the claimant fails to establish a prima facie case on each element, or fails to state a claim, or if there is no genuine issue of material fact and the defendant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law.\n‘Protected public expression’ means c","#st-pa-slapp"],["What UPEPA does not cover","Pennsylvania · anti-SLAPP","The Act excludes claims against, or enforcement claims by, government units and officials acting officially. It also excludes claims against a business arising from communications about its own goods or services (creative and journalistic works are not ‘goods or services’), and bodily injury and death claims unless they sound in defamation, privacy, misappropriation of likeness or emotional distress, or arise solely ","#st-pa-slapp"],["Special motion: 60 days, automatic stay, quick hearing","Pennsylvania · anti-SLAPP","A party may file a special motion to dismiss, or for judgment on, all or part of a claim based on protected public expression immunity. It is due no later than 60 days after service of the pleading asserting the claim, extendable for good cause. The court hears argument within 60 days after filing and rules, with a written opinion, within 60 days after argument. It decides on the summary-judgment record (Pa.R.Civ.P. ","#st-pa-slapp"],["Fees and immediate appeal","Pennsylvania · anti-SLAPP","If the defendant is immune, the court must award attorney fees, court costs and litigation expenses, jointly and severally against each adverse party that asserted the claim. It must do the same if the claimant voluntarily discontinues (with or without prejudice) after immunity is asserted. If the immunity assertion is frivolous or solely for delay, the court must award the opposing party its fees incurred in opposin","#st-pa-slapp"],["Older environmental anti-SLAPP law still applies","Pennsylvania · anti-SLAPP","A person who files an action to enforce an environmental law, or communicates with a government agency about enforcing or implementing one, is immune from civil liability in a resulting damages action when the action or communication aims to procure favorable government action. The immunity is lost if the allegation or communication is not relevant or material to environmental enforcement or implementation and also i","#st-pa-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: pay the IOLTA Board first, then a sponsor’s motion","Pennsylvania · out of state","An out-of-state or foreign lawyer may be specially admitted for one case. First, give the Pennsylvania IOLTA Board the required information and pay the $375 fee for each case; it is never refunded. The fee is waived if the client has in forma pauperis status, and for special courts (the Traffic Division of Philadelphia Municipal Court and the Pittsburgh Municipal Court). Pay by check drawn on a U.S. bank, money order","#st-pa-oos"],["What the pro hac vice motion must contain","Pennsylvania · out of state","The motion attaches verified statements from both lawyers. The candidate states every jurisdiction of admission and license number, any discipline or pending disciplinary proceedings, the number of pending Pennsylvania pro hac vice applications and any denials, and agrees to be bound by Pennsylvania law and the Rules of Professional Conduct. The candidate also submits to Pennsylvania courts and the Disciplinary Board","#st-pa-oos"],["The sponsor stays attorney of record and attends","Pennsylvania · out of state","A lawyer admitted pro hac vice is not authorized to act as attorney of record. The Pennsylvania sponsor enters an appearance and stays attorney of record, signs and receives papers, and attends all proceedings unless excused. The sponsor need not attend depositions unless ordered. Pa.B.A.R. 301(a); 204 Pa. Code § 81.502(b); Pa.R.Civ.P. 1012.1","#st-pa-oos"],["Temporary practice without admission","Pennsylvania · out of state","A lawyer admitted elsewhere and not disbarred or suspended may provide temporary services in Pennsylvania: in association with an actively participating Pennsylvania lawyer; in connection with a pending or potential proceeding where the lawyer is or expects to be authorized to appear; in arbitration or mediation reasonably related to the lawyer’s home practice (unless the forum requires pro hac vice); or in other wor","#st-pa-oos"],["In-house counsel need a limited license","Pennsylvania · out of state","A lawyer not admitted in Pennsylvania who works in Pennsylvania for a non-governmental business employer on more than a temporary basis, or with an office or continuous presence there, must obtain a Limited In-House Corporate Counsel License. The license is limited to advising and representing that employer and does not cover court appearances that require pro hac vice. It does not authorize lawyers employed by the C","#st-pa-oos"],["Admission by reciprocity or UBE score transfer","Pennsylvania · out of state","Pennsylvania admits on motion (reciprocity) lawyers on active status in a reciprocal state who meet the education and good-standing requirements. They must have practiced law for a majority of their time in 5 of the last 7 years after passing a reciprocal state’s bar exam or gaining admission there, or meet a UBE-based alternative. A UBE score from another jurisdiction transfers if it meets the Pennsylvania passing s","#st-pa-oos"],["Limited-scope representation","Pennsylvania · out of state","A lawyer may limit the scope of a representation if the limit is reasonable and the client gives informed consent. The collected civil rules contain no special limited-entry-of-appearance procedure: an attorney who has appeared needs leave of court to withdraw unless substitute counsel enters at the same time and there is no delay. Check county local rules and practice before planning an unbundled court appearance. P","#st-pa-oos"],["E-filing is county by county in trial courts; PACFile on appeal","Pennsylvania · out of state","Each court of common pleas decides by local rule whether to permit or require e-filing. The e-filed document is the original, and the filer must keep the signed paper original for 2 years after the case ends and produce it within 14 days of a request. Electronic service of papers other than original process needs the parties’ agreement or an e-mail address on an appearance or prior filing; an e-mail address on letter","#st-pa-oos"],["Governing code: Pennsylvania Rules of Professional Conduct","Pennsylvania · ethics","The Pennsylvania Rules of Professional Conduct (204 Pa. Code § 81.4) follow the ABA Model Rules’ numbering and structure, with Pennsylvania-specific changes. They are enforced through the Disciplinary Board of the Supreme Court under the Pennsylvania Rules of Disciplinary Enforcement. A Code of Civility also appears at 204 Pa. Code ch. 99 (not collected here). 204 Pa. Code §§ 81.1-81.4","#st-pa-ethics"],["Fees: writing required for new clients, but no client signature","Pennsylvania · ethics","When the lawyer has not regularly represented the client, the basis or rate of the fee must be communicated in writing before or within a reasonable time after the representation starts. The ABA rule only prefers writing.\nContingent fee agreements must be in writing and must state the method, the percentages for settlement, trial or appeal, and how expenses are handled. Unlike ABA Model Rule 1.5(c), the Pennsylvania ","#st-pa-ethics"],["Confidentiality: broader permitted disclosures","Pennsylvania · ethics","Disclosure is mandatory where needed to comply with the candor duties of Rule 3.3. Disclosure is permitted, among other grounds, to prevent a client’s criminal act likely to cause substantial financial injury, and to prevent, mitigate or rectify a client’s crime or fraud in which the lawyer’s services were used. Unlike the ABA version, the Pennsylvania text reaches criminal acts without requiring that the lawyer’s se","#st-pa-ethics"],["Lateral screening without ABA-style certifications","Pennsylvania · ethics","A firm may avoid imputation of a newly associated lawyer’s former-client conflict by screening that lawyer, giving no part of the fee, and giving prompt written notice to the affected client. The Pennsylvania text does not include the ABA rule’s additional certification and response requirements. Pa.R.P.C. 1.10(b)","#st-pa-ethics"],["Pennsylvania-only rules: lobbying and investment products","Pennsylvania · ethics","Rule 1.19 requires lawyer-lobbyists to comply with lobbying disclosure laws, treats those disclosures as authorized, and bars former public officers or employees whose service ended on or after June 1, 2023 from lobbying their former body for one year.\nRule 5.8 bars lawyers from selling or placing investment products unless separately licensed, and from recommending them to clients or fiduciary beneficiaries when the","#st-pa-ethics"],["Other text differences","Pennsylvania · ethics","Rule 1.2(e) lets a lawyer counsel or assist a client about conduct expressly permitted by Pennsylvania law, with advice on consequences under other law. Rule 8.4(c) expressly permits supervising lawful investigative activities. Rule 8.4(g) prohibits knowingly engaging, in the practice of law, in harassment or discrimination on listed grounds including gender identity or expression and socioeconomic status. Pa.R.P.C. ","#st-pa-ethics"],["Confidential Information Form and certification on every filing","Pennsylvania · land mine","Every filing in the trial and appellate courts must certify compliance with the Case Records Public Access Policy. Social Security numbers, financial account numbers (other than the last four digits where needed), driver license and SID numbers, minors’ names and birth dates, and abuse victims’ contact information in family cases go only on a contemporaneously filed Confidential Information Form. The court is not req","#st-pa-unique"],["Preliminary objections, not motions to dismiss; some defenses only as new matter","Pennsylvania · land mine","Rule 12-type challenges are raised by preliminary objections, and all of them must be raised at once. The statute of limitations and statute of frauds cannot be raised by preliminary objection and must be pleaded as new matter. Improper venue is waived if not raised by preliminary objection. A complaint can be amended as of course within 20 days after service of the objections. Pa.R.Civ.P. 1028, 1030, 1006","#st-pa-unique"],["A general denial is an admission","Pennsylvania · land mine","Every averment that requires a response is admitted unless specifically denied. A general denial or demand for proof counts as an admission. Lack of knowledge works as a denial only after a stated reasonable investigation. New matter endorsed with a notice to plead must be answered within 20 days or it is admitted. Pa.R.Civ.P. 1026, 1029","#st-pa-unique"],["Serve within 30 days in state (90 out of state), or reissue","Pennsylvania · land mine","Original process must be served within 30 days in Pennsylvania and 90 days outside it. If not served in time, it must be reissued or reinstated by praecipe, and the plaintiff must make a good-faith effort to serve, or the filing may not stop the statute of limitations (see the Rule 405 citation to Lamp v. Heyman). Pa.R.Civ.P. 401, 404","#st-pa-unique"],["Ten-day notice before default or non pros, every time","Pennsylvania · land mine","The prothonotary cannot enter a default judgment or judgment of non pros by praecipe unless the praecipe certifies that written notice of intent was mailed or delivered at least 10 days earlier, with a copy attached. The notice cannot be waived. For default, it must go to the defaulting party and its counsel. Pa.R.Civ.P. 237.1, 237.5","#st-pa-unique"],["Certificate of merit within 60 days in professional liability cases","Pennsylvania · land mine","Any claim that a licensed professional deviated from professional standards needs a certificate of merit for each professional defendant, filed with the complaint or within 60 days. After a defendant’s notice of intent (no earlier than day 31), a missing certificate leads to judgment of non pros 30 days later. Pa.R.Civ.P. 1042.3, 1042.6, 1042.7","#st-pa-unique"],["Pro hac vice motion 3 days before appearing, after paying IOLTA","Pennsylvania · land mine","The $375 IOLTA fee per case and the information filing must come first. The sponsor’s Rule 1012.1 motion, with both verified statements and the IOLTA fee certification or averment, must be filed at least 3 days before the out-of-state lawyer appears. The sponsor remains attorney of record. Pa.B.A.R. 301(b); 204 Pa. Code §§ 81.503(d), 81.505; Pa.R.Civ.P. 1012.1","#st-pa-unique"],["Post-trial motion within 10 days, then enter judgment","Pennsylvania · land mine","After any civil trial, grounds not raised at trial and specifically stated in a Rule 227.1 motion filed within 10 days are waived on appeal. The appeal runs from the judgment entered on praecipe, not from the order denying post-trial relief. If the motions are undecided after 120 days, a party may have judgment entered. Pa.R.Civ.P. 227.1, 227.4","#st-pa-unique"],["Rule 1925(b) Statement: omit an issue and it is gone","Pennsylvania · land mine","If the trial judge orders a concise Statement of errors, file it of record and serve it on the judge within the time ordered (at least 21 days). Every issue not included is waived. The appeal clock is 30 days, is not tolled by a reconsideration motion unless reconsideration is expressly granted within that time, and cannot be extended. Pa.R.A.P. 1925(b), 1701(b)(3), 903(a), 105(b)","#st-pa-unique"],["Document subpoenas to non-parties need 20 days’ notice first","Pennsylvania · land mine","Before serving a subpoena for documents on a non-party, give every party 20 days’ written notice with the proposed subpoena. An objection blocks service until the court rules. Federal-style same-day service of notice and subpoena will not work. Pa.R.Civ.P. 4009.21-4009.24","#st-pa-unique"],["Check county local rules before filing anything","Pennsylvania · land mine","Motion procedure, briefing, argument lists, preliminary objection and summary judgment practice, e-filing availability, cover sheets and the compulsory arbitration limit all vary by county. Each county also designates the office listed in the notice to defend. Read the county’s published local rules (Pa.R.J.A. 103(d)) before the first filing. Pa.R.Civ.P. 239-239.9, 1018.1(c), 1301; Pa.R.J.A. 103(d)","#st-pa-unique"],["Anti-SLAPP special motion: confirm it is in force, and file within 60 days","Pennsylvania · land mine","UPEPA immunity and mandatory fee awards apply to actions filed after July 17, 2024. The special-motion procedure in § 8340.16 (60-day deadline, automatic stay) depends on a Pennsylvania Bulletin notice that this research could not confirm. Plead immunity in preliminary objections, new matter or summary judgment as well, and file any special motion within 60 days of service. 42 Pa.C.S. §§ 8340.15-8340.18; Act 72 of 20","#st-pa-unique"],["No peremptory challenge of a judge","Pennsylvania · judges","The collected Pennsylvania civil and appellate rules provide no peremptory challenge or automatic substitution of a judge (Rule 221’s four peremptory challenges are for jurors). Disqualification follows the Code of Judicial Conduct. Pa.R.Civ.P. 221; Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11 (207 Pa. Code ch. 33)","#st-pa-unique"],["Disqualification standard","Pennsylvania · judges","A judge must step aside when the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned. Listed examples: personal bias or knowledge of disputed facts; close family relationships to a party, lawyer or material witness; economic interests; public statements committing to a result; prior involvement as a lawyer, government participant or witness; and campaign contributions by a party, lawyer or firm large enough to raise ","#st-pa-unique"],["Pa. Const. art. I, §§ 7 and 20","Pennsylvania · speech","Pennsylvania’s Constitution separately protects free communication, speech and press (art. I, § 7) and assembly and petition (art. I, § 20). The anti-SLAPP statute expressly protects expression guaranteed by these sections as well as by the First Amendment. Whether the state provisions are broader than the First Amendment depends on case law that was not collected or checked here. Pa. Const. art. I, §§ 7, 20 (not col","#st-pa-unique"],["County local rules govern a great deal","Pennsylvania · unique rules","Each judicial district adopts local rules on motions practice, briefs, preliminary objections, summary judgment procedure, e-filing, cover sheets and the compulsory arbitration limit. The statewide rules require local rules on several of these, numbered to match the statewide rule (Local Rule 208.3(b), 1028(c), 1035.2(a) and so on). A prothonotary may not refuse a filing that complies with the statewide rules only be","#st-pa-unique"],["Fact pleading and verified pleadings","Pennsylvania · unique rules","Pennsylvania requires fact pleading: the material facts on which a claim or defense is based must be stated, and claims on a writing must attach it. Every pleading with factual averments must be verified by a party, not counsel, except in narrow cases. Pa.R.Civ.P. 1019, 1024","#st-pa-unique"],["Compulsory arbitration below the local limit","Pennsylvania · unique rules","Each county sets by local rule which cases go to compulsory arbitration and the dollar limit, within the 42 Pa.C.S. § 7361(b) maximum. An appeal for trial de novo is due within 30 days after the prothonotary notes on the docket that notice of the award was sent. The appellant must pay the arbitrators’ compensation, capped at 50% of the amount in controversy and not recoverable. Fee-and-cost waiver on petition is avai","#st-pa-unique"],["Discovery differs from federal practice","Pennsylvania · unique rules","There are no automatic initial disclosures. Expert discovery is by interrogatory, with no automatic reports or depositions. A party must give every other party 20 days’ notice before serving a document subpoena on a non-party, and an objection blocks service until the court rules. Pa.R.Civ.P. 4003.5, 4009.21","#st-pa-unique"],["Rule 51. Title and citation","Pennsylvania · civil rules","These rules are the Pennsylvania Rules of Civil Procedure, cited as “Pa.R.Civ.P.”; older citations to “Pa.R.C.P. No.” remain valid. They do not apply in magisterial district courts, which have their own rules (Pa.R.Civ.P.M.D.J.).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 52. Effective date; application to pending actions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A rule or amendment takes effect on the date the Supreme Court specifies; if none is specified, it takes effect on the first day of July or January following the 30th day after adoption, whichever is earlier. Unless the Supreme Court says otherwise, it applies to actions already pending on the effective date.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 76. Definitions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Defines terms used throughout the rules, including: adult and majority (age 18 or over), minor (under 18), competent adult (18 or older and not a party, nor a party’s employee or relative), and prothonotary (the clerk of the civil court, or the equivalent officer). An affidavit or verified statement may be either sworn before an authorized officer or unsworn and made subject to the penalties of 18 Pa.C.S. § 4904 (uns","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 101-104. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 101, 102, 103 and 104 are rescinded (effective January 1, 2024). Their construction principles now appear in Pa.R.J.A. 104-106; former Rule 104 (numerals) has no replacement.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 105. Bonds","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Wherever a rule requires a bond or undertaking with sureties, a bond from an indemnity or surety company authorized to do business in Pennsylvania and approved by the proper authority may be given instead.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 106-108. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 106, 107 and 108 (computation of time, including weeks and months) are rescinded (effective January 1, 2024). Time computation is now governed by Pa.R.J.A. 107.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 126. Application and construction of rules","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The rules are to be liberally applied to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action, and at any stage the court may disregard a procedural error or defect that does not affect the parties’ substantive rights. The rules are construed under the principles in Pa.R.J.A. 104-115, which replaced former Rules 101-104, 106-108 and 127-153.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 127-153. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 127, 128, 129, 130, 131, 132, 133, 151, 152 and 153 are rescinded. Rule 151 was rescinded in 1982; the others were rescinded effective January 1, 2024, and their principles now appear in Pa.R.J.A. 108-115.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 201. Agreements of attorneys","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Attorneys’ agreements about the business of the court must be in writing, unless they are made at the bar and noted by the prothonotary in the minutes or by the stenographer in the stenographer’s notes.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 202-204. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 202, 203 and 204 are rescinded. Contingent-fee agreements, payments by attorneys, and payments to persons in connection with litigation are now governed by the Rules of Professional Conduct.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 204.1. Format of pleadings and other legal papers","Pennsylvania · civil rules","All pleadings, motions and other legal papers must be on good-quality white 8 1/2 by 11 inch paper (dividers excepted), with a 3-inch blank space at the top of the first page for court stamps. Text must be double-spaced (quotations over two lines may be indented and single-spaced), margins at least one inch on all four sides, type no smaller than 12 point, printed on one side only (exhibits may be two-sided), and doc","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 205. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 205, which adopted the ABA Code of Professional Responsibility as the standard of attorney conduct, is rescinded; attorney conduct is governed by the disciplinary rules.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 205.1. Filing legal papers by delivery or mail","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A legal paper that needs no judge’s signature or action before filing may be delivered or mailed to the prothonotary, sheriff or other officer with any filing fee; no personal appearance is needed. The attorney’s signature certifies authority to file, the endorsed service address registers the address, and the attorney’s Supreme Court identification number proves the right to practice. Trap: a mailed paper is not fil","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 205.2. Filing with the prothonotary; local rules cannot block filing","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The prothonotary may not refuse to file a pleading or legal paper that complies with the statewide Rules of Civil Procedure because it fails a local rule requirement, including local format rules (Local Rule 205.2(a)) and local cover-sheet rules (Local Rule 205.2(b)).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 205.3. Filing originals and copies","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A party may file either an original or a copy (including a facsimile copy) of a pleading or legal paper, as long as the copy shows the original was properly signed and, where applicable, verified; the copy is treated as the original. Any other party may demand the original by filing and serving a notice to file it within 14 days of the filing of the notice. The rule does not authorize filing by fax transmission.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 205.4. Electronic filing and service of legal papers","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A court may permit or require e-filing by local rule (Local Rule 205.4); papers are filed in PDF or another locally designated format, the e-filed version is the original, and e-filing certifies that a signed (and, where needed, verified) hard copy exists and is subject to Rule 1023.1 sanctions. The filer must keep the signed hard copy for two years after the latest of case disposition, the order resolving the issue,","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 205.5. Cover sheet","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Whoever starts an action must complete and file the statewide cover sheet designed by the Court Administrator, and the prothonotary may not accept a filing commencing an action without it; the prothonotary must help pro se parties complete it. Family-law actions (protection from abuse, support, custody, divorce, other domestic relations, custody mediation) are excluded, and judicial districts with a qualifying e-fili","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 205.6. Confidential information and documents; certification","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Anyone filing a document with the prothonotary (the attorney, or the party if unrepresented) must comply with Sections 7.0 and 8.0 of the Case Records Public Access Policy of the Unified Judicial System, unless other authority restricts access. Trap: every filing needs a certification of compliance with the Policy, and, where the Policy requires, confidential information or documents must be submitted on a Confidenti","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 206. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 206 is rescinded (effective January 1, 1996).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 206.1. Petition: definition, content, form","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A “petition” is an application to strike and/or open a default judgment or judgment of non pros (a judgment against a plaintiff for failure to prosecute), plus any other application a court designates by Local Rule 206.1(a); confessed-judgment relief goes under Rule 2959 and other applications are motions. The petition must specify the relief sought and state the material facts supporting it in consecutively numbered","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 206.2. Answer to petition","Pennsylvania · civil rules","An answer must state the material facts constituting the defense, in consecutively numbered paragraphs that correspond to the petition’s paragraphs.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 206.3. Verification of petition or answer","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A petition or answer that alleges facts not already of record must be verified (sworn or made subject to the penalties for unsworn falsification).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 206.4. Rule to show cause","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A petition proceeds on a rule to show cause (a court order directing the respondent to answer why relief should not be granted), issued in the court’s discretion under Rule 206.5 unless the court by local rule adopts issuance as of course under Rule 206.6; later procedure follows Rule 206.7. A judgment is stricken without any rule to show cause when a defect on the face of the record is a ground for striking a defaul","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 206.5. Rule to show cause: discretionary issuance","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A petitioner seeking a rule to show cause must attach a proposed order in the prescribed form and notify all other parties that the rule will be requested. If the petition is within Rule 206.1(a), properly pleaded and states prima facie grounds, the court must issue the rule and may stay the proceedings; the order sets the days for an answer, depositions, and argument (or an evidentiary hearing). A default judgment w","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 206.6. Rule to show cause: issuance as of course","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Where a court has adopted this procedure, a rule to show cause issues automatically when the petition is filed, directing an answer within 20 days after service of the petition on the respondent. The court may stay proceedings, and the petitioner must attach a proposed order in the prescribed form.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 206.7. Procedure after rule to show cause issues","Pennsylvania · civil rules","If no answer is filed, the petition’s factual averments may be deemed admitted and the court enters an appropriate order; if the answer raises no disputed material facts, the court decides on petition and answer at the petitioner’s request. If the answer raises disputed material facts, the petitioner may take depositions (or other discovery the court allows) within the time the order sets. Trap: if the petitioner doe","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 207. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 207 is rescinded (effective January 1, 1996).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 207.1. Motion to exclude expert testimony relying on novel scientific evidence","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A motion to exclude expert testimony based on novel science as inadmissible under Pa.R.E. 702 or 703 must state the expert’s name and credentials, summarize the expected testimony and pinpoint the part to be excluded, give the specific basis and supporting evidence, and attach all relevant CVs and expert reports. No response is needed unless ordered; the court first decides whether to address the issue before trial o","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 208. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 208 is rescinded (effective January 1, 1996).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 208.1. Motion: definition and scope","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A “motion” is any application to the court for an order, but the motion rules (208.1-208.4) do not cover preliminary objections, judgment on the pleadings, summary judgment, requests for special relief (including preliminary injunctions), trial-related motions (nonsuit, jury selection, Rule 207.1 expert motions, motions in limine), post-trial motions, delay-damages motions, petitions, or petitions to open or strike c","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 208.2. Motion: form and content","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A motion must have a caption (court, case number, motion name, moving party), consecutively numbered paragraphs, the material facts and relief sought, a proposed order, and a certificate of service listing each represented party’s attorney, each pro se party, and the service address; it must be signed and endorsed. Verification is not required unless a rule or court order requires it. Local rules may add a statement ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 208.3. Motion procedures","Pennsylvania · civil rules","By default the court first considers a motion without written responses or briefs, and may not grant relief unless the motion is uncontested or the other parties have had an opportunity for argument (it may deny a defective or untimely motion without argument). A court may instead require responses and/or briefs for motions listed in Local Rule 208.3(b); then an opposing party must respond within 20 days after servic","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 208.4. Initial consideration of motion; disputed facts","Pennsylvania · civil rules","At first consideration the court may decide the motion or set procedures such as responses, briefs, affidavits, depositions, an evidentiary hearing, or a rule to show cause. When relief depends on disputed facts requiring a record, the court on its own or on request may issue a rule to show cause in the prescribed form, setting days for an answer, depositions, and argument, and Rule 206.7 then governs.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 209. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 209 is rescinded (effective January 1, 1996).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 210. Form of briefs","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Briefs must be typewritten, printed or otherwise duplicated and endorsed with the case name, court and number, and the name, address and telephone number of the attorney (or of the party if unrepresented). Courts may add requirements only by Local Rule 210.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 211. Oral argument","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Any interested party may request oral argument on a motion, and the court may require it whether or not requested. The court may also decide any motion without oral argument.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 212. Pre-trial conference (renumbered)","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Former Rule 212 was renumbered Rule 212.3, effective December 1, 1997; see Rule 212.3.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 212.1. Jury cases: earliest trial date, discovery, pre-trial statement deadlines","Pennsylvania · civil rules","In jury cases seeking damages above the compulsory arbitration limit, the court gives notice of the earliest trial date, which should come at least 30 days before the plaintiff’s pre-trial statement is due and may set a discovery deadline. Pre-trial statements are due from the plaintiff at least 60 days, the defendant at least 30 days, and an additional defendant at least 15 days before the earliest trial date. These","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 212.2. Jury cases: pre-trial statement content and sanctions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A pre-trial statement must include a brief narrative, the types and amounts of all damages, the names and addresses of all witnesses classified as liability or damages witnesses (“anyone named in discovery” is not enough), all trial exhibits, the expert reports (or Rule 4003.5 interrogatory answers) of every expert, any stipulations, and anything else required locally; listed exhibits must be made available. Trap: if","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 212.3. Pre-trial conference","Pennsylvania · civil rules","At any time the court, on its own or a party’s motion, may call a conference on simplifying issues, a scheduling order, admissions, limiting experts, settlement or mediation, and other matters, and may order an insurance representative with settlement authority to attend. Absent a court order, for a post-pre-trial-statement conference involving settlement, counsel must try in good faith to settle beforehand, a knowle","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 212.4. Eminent domain valuation experts","Pennsylvania · civil rules","In eminent domain cases, the name and statement of valuation of a valuation expert required by Section 703(2) of the Eminent Domain Code must be served within the time for filing a pre-trial statement under Rule 212.1, subject to Rule 212.2(c) sanctions. The statute’s deadline of at least 10 days before trial is suspended to that extent.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 212.5. Settlement conference","Pennsylvania · civil rules","At any time the court may, on its own or on motion, schedule a settlement conference (using the Rule 212.6 form), and the attorneys or unrepresented parties must make good-faith efforts to settle beforehand. A knowledgeable attorney with authority must attend, an insurance representative with settlement authority must be present unless the court permits telephone availability, and the court may order others to attend","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 212.6. Settlement conference order form","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Provides the form of order scheduling a Rule 212.5 settlement conference, stating the judge, courthouse and time and directing all parties to comply with Rule 212.5(b).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 213. Consolidation, severance and transfer within a county; wrongful death and survival actions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The court may order joint trials or consolidation of actions in the same county that share a common question of law or fact or arise from the same transaction, and may order separate trials of any claim or issue for convenience or to avoid prejudice (subdivisions (c) and (d) are rescinded). Wrongful death and survival claims may be brought in one action, but if brought separately they must be consolidated for trial (","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 213.1. Coordination of actions in different counties","Pennsylvania · civil rules","When actions pending in different counties share a common question of law or fact or arise from the same transaction, any party may, with notice to all parties, move the court where a complaint was first filed to coordinate them; that court may stay the actions and hold a hearing. The court weighs factors such as whether the common question predominates, convenience, delay or prejudice, judicial efficiency, the risk ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 214. Preferences on trial lists","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Trial lists give preference to cases in which a new trial has been granted and to cases the court designates on application and cause shown.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 215-215.1. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 215 and 215.1 are rescinded; the subject of former Rule 215.1 (Commonwealth Court jury trial cases) is now governed by Pa.R.A.P. 3735.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 216. Grounds for continuance","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Grounds for a continuance are: agreement of all parties approved by the court; illness of counsel of record, a material witness or a party (with a physician’s certificate if requested); inability to subpoena or depose a material witness, shown by an affidavit stating the expected testimony, why it is expected, the efforts made, and why the witness will be available later; a special ground allowed by the court; and co","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 217. Costs on continuance","Pennsylvania · civil rules","If a continuance is requested after the preliminary call of the trial list, the court may make the requesting party pay the opponent’s reasonable costs that an earlier request would have avoided. Trap: while those costs remain unpaid, the party may take no further step in the case without leave of court; a party at fault for the delay cannot recover them even if it wins, and otherwise the costs follow the judgment.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 218. Party not ready when case is called for trial","Pennsylvania · civil rules","If the plaintiff is not ready without satisfactory excuse when the case is called, the court may enter a nonsuit on the defendant’s motion or a non pros on its own motion; if the defendant is not ready, the plaintiff may proceed to trial or, on an appeal from compulsory arbitration, ask the court to dismiss the appeal and reinstate the award. Trap: failing to appear for trial is automatically treated as not ready wit","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 219. View of premises","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A party may ask for a jury view of premises involved in the case before the case is called or during trial; whether to allow it is in the court’s discretion. The court may impose the reasonable costs on the applicant or have them follow the judgment.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 220. Challenge to the array","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A challenge to the whole panel (array) of jurors returned for trial must be made in writing and filed on or before the first day of the trial period for which the issues are listed.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 220.1. Preliminary instructions to jurors; electronic devices","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Everyone reporting for jury service must be instructed on arrival that, until their service ends, they may not discuss the case (except as the court authorizes), read or listen to news about it, use computers, phones or other communicating devices during trial or deliberations (use during breaks is allowed), or use any device or method to research or disclose information about the parties, witnesses, lawyers, judge, ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 220.2. Sanctions for violating Rule 220.1","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Anyone who violates Rule 220.1’s limits on juror use of electronic devices, or any local or judge-imposed device restriction during court proceedings, may be held in contempt under 42 Pa.C.S. § 4132 et seq. and may face other sanctions, including confiscation of the device.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 220.3. Voir dire of jurors","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Voir dire (jury questioning) must be conducted in a judge’s presence unless all parties waive it with the court’s consent, and after the oath the judge instructs prospective jurors on their duties and the Rule 220.1-220.2 sanctions. Voir dire must allow parties to obtain at least 17 categories of information about jurors and their households, including name, year and place of birth, neighborhood and zip code (not str","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 221. Peremptory challenges","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Each party gets four peremptory challenges, exercised in turn starting with the plaintiff and then in the order parties were named or joined. To distribute challenges fairly, the court may allow additional challenges and allocate them, or treat multiple plaintiffs, defendants or additional defendants as a single party.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 222. Attorney as witness","Pennsylvania · civil rules","If trial counsel is called as a witness for the party the attorney represents, the court may decide whether the attorney may continue as trial counsel for the rest of the trial.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 223. Conduct of trial generally","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Subject to due process and the parties’ constitutional rights, the court may make and enforce rules and orders limiting cumulative witnesses, limiting how many attorneys for the same party may actively try the case or examine witnesses, regulating the number and length of addresses, and excluding the public or uninterested persons for good order or morals. Photography and broadcasting are governed by Pa.R.J.A. 1910.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 223.1. Conduct of jury trial","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Before evidence is taken, the trial judge must give the Rule 220.1 instructions. The court may also allow jury views, have testimony read back on the jury’s request, charge the jury at any time during trial, give exhibits to the jury for deliberations, and provide a written copy of all or part of the charge for deliberations.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 223.2. Juror note taking","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Jurors must be allowed to take notes during evidence, openings and closings (but not during the judge’s legal instructions) and may use them in deliberations, and the court must give a cautionary instruction before openings covering eight specified points (notes are optional, are memory aids not evidence, are confidential, may not be shared until deliberations, stay in the courtroom, and will be destroyed). The court","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 223.3. Jury instructions on noneconomic loss in bodily injury or death cases","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Where a viable claim for noneconomic loss is made in a bodily injury or death action, the court must give the prescribed instruction covering four items of past and future noneconomic loss: pain and suffering, embarrassment and humiliation, loss of enjoyment of life, and disfigurement. The jury considers eight listed factors, including the plaintiff’s age, injury severity and permanence, effect on daily activities, m","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 224. Order of proof","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The court may require the plaintiff to present all liability evidence before calling any witness solely on the extent of injury or damages, after which the defendant may move for a nonsuit; if refused, trial continues. The court may allow witnesses out of order.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 225. Opening and closing addresses","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Attorneys for each party or group of parties may make an opening address to the jury and an address after the close of testimony; the court may regulate their number, length and order.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 226. Points for charge; directed verdict","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Requested jury instructions (points for charge) must be framed so each can be answered by a simple yes or no, and copies must be given to the judge and opposing counsel before closing addresses begin. Trap: a point becomes part of the record only when read into the record or filed with the prothonotary before the post-trial motion; otherwise an appellate court will not review the ruling on it. At the close of all evi","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 227. Exceptions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Formal exceptions to trial rulings are unnecessary; an exception is deemed taken by the party against whom a ruling is made. Unless the court specially allows otherwise, exceptions to the jury charge must be taken before the jury retires, and on request they are argued outside the jury’s hearing.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 227.1. Post-trial relief","Pennsylvania · civil rules","After a jury or non-jury trial, a party seeks a new trial, judgment (including judgment notwithstanding the verdict), removal of a nonsuit, or modification of the decision through a single written Motion for Post-Trial Relief, which replaces the old separate motions and exceptions. It must be filed within 10 days after the verdict, discharge of a deadlocked jury, or nonsuit (jury trial), or after notice of nonsuit or","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 227.2. Court en banc","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The trial judge hears and decides all post-trial motions unless the judge orders a court en banc (a multi-judge panel), which must include the trial judge and may have no more than three judges. If the trial judge cannot act, another judge is designated.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 227.3. Transcript of testimony for post-trial motions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Every post-trial motion must designate the portion of the record to be transcribed; any other party may object within 10 days after the motion is filed and ask for a different, larger or smaller portion. If no portion is designated, transcription is deemed unnecessary, and the trial judge promptly decides any objection.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 227.4. Entry of judgment on praecipe","Pennsylvania · civil rules","On a party’s praecipe (a written request to the prothonotary), the prothonotary must enter judgment on a nonsuit, verdict or non-jury decision if no timely post-trial motion is filed, or if timely motions are filed but not all decided within 120 days after the first was filed. Trap: a judgment entered after that 120 days is final as to all parties and issues and cannot be reconsidered, which starts the appeal clock. ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 228. Testimony on juror misconduct","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Testimony from a juror or anyone else during trial about alleged juror misconduct or jury tampering becomes part of the record, must be taken outside the jury’s hearing, and jurors may be questioned about it.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 229. Discontinuance","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A discontinuance is the only way a plaintiff may voluntarily end an action, in whole or in part, before trial begins. Trap: discontinuing as to fewer than all defendants requires the written consent of all parties or leave of court, except that in a professional liability case a plaintiff may discontinue as to a defendant for whom no Rule 1042.3 certificate of merit was filed. The court, on petition and notice, may s","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 229.1. Late delivery of settlement funds; sanctions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Unless the parties agree otherwise in writing, a settling defendant must deliver settlement funds (not including structured-settlement annuity or future installments) to plaintiff’s counsel, or to an unrepresented plaintiff, within 20 calendar days after receiving the executed release; if court approval is needed, the clock starts upon approval. If payment is late, the plaintiff may seek to invalidate the settlement ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 229.2. Petition to transfer structured settlement payment rights","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A petition to transfer structured settlement payment rights is filed in the county where the payee is domiciled, with the payee and transferee as parties; terms defined in the Structured Settlement Protection Act carry their statutory meaning. The petition, verified by the transferee, must describe the payment provisions and rights being transferred and state in bold the net amount payable to the payee after all fees","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 230. Voluntary nonsuit","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A voluntary nonsuit is the only way for a plaintiff to voluntarily end an action, in whole or in part, during trial. It requires leave of court on good cause shown and is not available after the close of all the evidence.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 230.1. Compulsory nonsuit at trial","Pennsylvania · civil rules","In a one-plaintiff, one-defendant case, the court on the defendant’s oral motion may enter a nonsuit at the close of the plaintiff’s liability case if the plaintiff has not established a right to relief, considering only the plaintiff’s evidence and any evidence favorable to the plaintiff the defendant introduced before then. With multiple plaintiffs, no nonsuit may be entered until all plaintiffs close; with multipl","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 230.2. Termination of inactive cases","Pennsylvania · civil rules","At least once a year the court must start proceedings to terminate cases with no activity of record for two years or more, serving a notice of proposed termination (electronically under Rule 205.4(g)(1) or under Rule 440) at least 30 days before the proposed termination date. Trap: unless a statement of intention to proceed is filed by that date, the prothonotary enters an order terminating the case for failure to pr","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 231. Second action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","After a discontinuance or voluntary nonsuit, the plaintiff may bring a second action on the same cause of action upon paying the costs of the first action. Trap: after a compulsory nonsuit, no second action on the same cause of action is allowed; the remedy is a motion to remove the nonsuit under Rule 227.1.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 232. Counterclaim survives termination of plaintiff’s action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A discontinuance or nonsuit of the plaintiff’s action does not affect the defendant’s right to pursue an already-filed counterclaim. A defendant may end a counterclaim only by discontinuance or voluntary nonsuit, on conditions like those that apply to plaintiffs.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 233. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 233 is rescinded (reserved); service of legal papers other than original process is governed by Rules 440 and 441.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 233.1. Frivolous litigation by pro se plaintiff","Pennsylvania · civil rules","When a pro se plaintiff sues, a defendant may move to dismiss on the ground that the plaintiff is raising the same or related claims against the same or related defendants that were already resolved by a written settlement or a court proceeding, and the court may stay the action meanwhile. If it dismisses, the court may bar the plaintiff from further pro se suits raising the same or related claims against those defen","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 234. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 234 is rescinded; subpoenas are governed by Rules 234.1 et seq.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 234.1. Subpoena to attend and testify","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A subpoena is a court order commanding a person to attend and testify at a stated time and place, and it may also require production of documents or things in that person’s possession, custody or control. It may be used only for a trial, hearing or deposition in a pending action, never to compel an ex parte appearance or production before an attorney, party or party representative, and must be served reasonably in ad","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 234.2. Subpoena: issuance, service, fees, prisoners, minors","Pennsylvania · civil rules","On a party’s request the prothonotary issues a signed, sealed subpoena otherwise in blank (Rule 234.6 form). Any adult may serve it on an adult in Pennsylvania by personal service under Rule 402(a), by return-receipt restricted-delivery mail, or by ordinary mail with two copies of the Rule 234.9 acknowledgment and a stamped return envelope. One day’s attendance fee plus round-trip mileage must be tendered on demand a","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 234.3. Notice to attend; notice to produce","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A party may compel another party (or its officer or managing agent) to attend a trial or hearing, and to bring documents or things, by serving a notice to attend in the Rule 234.7 form reasonably in advance; if attendance is not needed, a notice to produce in the Rule 234.8 form compels production. These notices go only to parties, may be served inside or outside Pennsylvania, and are served under Rule 440.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 234.4. Excusing compliance; motion to quash","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The party who served a subpoena, notice to attend or notice to produce may excuse compliance. A party, the person served, or anyone with sufficient interest may move to quash, and after a hearing the court may enter an order protecting any party, witness or other person from unreasonable annoyance, embarrassment, oppression, burden or expense.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 234.5. Failure to comply with subpoena or notice","Pennsylvania · civil rules","If a witness disobeys a subpoena, the court may issue a bench warrant and, for willful failure, hold the witness in contempt. Trap: a witness served by ordinary mail cannot be subject to a bench warrant or contempt unless the witness returned the signed Rule 234.9 acknowledgment. A party who fails to comply may face Rule 4019(c) sanctions, the opponent’s reasonable expenses including attorney’s fees if the failure wa","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 234.6-234.9. Subpoena and notice forms","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 234.6 through 234.9 prescribe the forms for a subpoena to attend and testify (with an optional paragraph for documents and a verified return of service subject to 18 Pa.C.S. § 4904), a notice to attend (naming the officer or managing agent if the party is an entity), a notice to produce, and the notice and acknowledgment of receipt for a subpoena served by ordinary mail. Each form warns of Rule 234.5 sanctions;","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 235. Notice to Attorney General: constitutionality; charitable bequests or trusts","Pennsylvania · civil rules","When an Act of Assembly is challenged as unconstitutional, or a charitable bequest or trust is involved, and the Commonwealth is not a party, the party raising the constitutional question (or the plaintiff in the charity case) must promptly notify the Pennsylvania Attorney General by registered or certified mail, enclose the pleading raising the issue, and file proof of notice. The Attorney General may intervene or b","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 236. Prothonotary’s notice of orders and judgments","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The prothonotary must immediately give written notice of the entry of any order or judgment, with a copy, to each party’s attorney of record (or the party if unrepresented), and for a confessed judgment must mail the defendant notice and copies of the supporting documents, which the plaintiff supplies with a stamped, addressed envelope. The prothonotary notes in the docket that notice was given; the failure to give n","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 237. Notice of praecipe for final judgment","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The prothonotary may not accept a praecipe to enter judgment on a nonsuit, jury verdict or non-jury decision unless it certifies that a copy was mailed to every other party who has appeared, or to that party’s attorney of record.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 237.1. 10-day notice before judgment of non pros or default judgment","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The prothonotary may not enter a judgment of non pros for failure to file a complaint, or a default judgment for failure to plead, by praecipe unless the praecipe certifies that written notice of intent to file it was mailed or delivered after the failure and at least 10 days before the praecipe was filed: for non pros, to the party’s attorney of record or the unrepresented party; for default, to the party against wh","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 237.2. Agreement to extend time after 10-day notice","Pennsylvania · civil rules","After a Rule 237.1 notice is given, the parties may agree in writing, using the Rule 237.6 form signed for both sides, to extend the time to file a complaint, answer or preliminary objections. Trap: if the action is not taken by the agreed date, the prothonotary may enter judgment of non pros or by default without any further notice.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 237.3. Relief from judgment of non pros or default judgment","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A petition to open a judgment of non pros or default judgment entered under Rule 237.1 must attach the complaint, preliminary objections and/or answer the petitioner wants to file, and all grounds must be raised in a single petition. If filed within 10 days after the judgment is entered on the docket, the court must open a non pros judgment if the proposed complaint states a meritorious cause of action, and must open","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 237.4-237.6. Forms: 10-day notices and agreement to extend time","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 237.4 and 237.5 prescribe the “Important Notice” forms for the Rule 237.1 10-day notice of intent to seek a judgment of non pros (served on the plaintiff or plaintiff’s attorney) or a default judgment (served on the defendant), each warning that judgment may be entered without a hearing unless the recipient acts within ten days from the date of the notice, and listing the lawyer-referral office designated under","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 238. Delay damages in bodily injury, death or property damage cases","Pennsylvania · civil rules","On the plaintiff’s request, damages for delay are added to compensatory damages awarded by a jury verdict, non-jury decision or compulsory arbitration award in actions for bodily injury, death or property damage, running from one year after original process was first served until the verdict, decision or award, at the Wall Street Journal prime rate (first edition of each year) plus one percent, simple interest. The p","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 239. Local rules","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Requirements for adopting and amending local rules of civil procedure are now set out in Pa.R.J.A. 103(d); local rules adopted under former Rule 239 remain effective once compiled and published under Pa.R.J.A. 103(d)(7).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 239.1-239.7. Required numbering and content of local rules","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A court that imposes local requirements must put them in specially numbered local rules: format of papers (Local Rule 205.2(a)), local cover sheets (205.2(b)), applications designated as petitions (206.1(a)), statements of authority, uncontested-motion certifications and discovery meet-and-confer certifications (208.2(c)-(e)), motion responses and briefs (208.3(b), which may treat an unanswered motion as uncontested)","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 239.8. Local rules: promulgation, publication, effective date","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The requirements for promulgating, publishing and making effective local rules of civil procedure are now in Pa.R.J.A. 103(d), which replaced this rule’s former requirements effective August 1, 2016. Local rules adopted under the former rule remain effective once compiled and published under Pa.R.J.A. 103(d)(7). Rule 239 says the same.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 239.9. Local e-filing rules (Local Rule 205.4)","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A court that permits or requires e-filing must adopt a detailed Local Rule 205.4 stating whether e-filing is permissive or mandatory and which actions and papers it covers, the accepted formats (which must include PDF), how non-attorneys access the e-filing website, approved credit and debit cards and whether advance deposits are allowed, payment to any third-party system operator, and the Rule 205.4(f) procedures (f","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 240. Application to waive fees and costs","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Requests to waive fees and costs (in forma pauperis status) in civil actions follow Pa.R.J.A. 1990. If the action is begun by writ of summons, the court will not act on the application until a complaint is filed and may deny it if no complaint is filed within 90 days of the application’s filing date. The court may on its own dismiss as frivolous an action filed together with a fee-waiver application, and the filings ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 242. Citation of authorities","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Authorities are cited as provided in Pa.R.A.P. 126, including its rules on citing non-precedential Superior Court and Commonwealth Court decisions.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 247-247.1. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 247 and 247.1 are rescinded; appeals from public-employment arbitration awards are now governed by 42 Pa.C.S. § 933(b).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 248. Modification of time","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Any time period set by a rule of civil procedure may be extended or shortened by written agreement of the parties or by court order.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 249. Authority of individual judge","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Except where the court must act en banc, a single judge may perform any function of the court, including interlocutory and ex parte orders, and may do so (other than trying a case) at any time and place within the judicial district. Each court may regulate how business is assigned among its judges.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 250. Scope of chapter","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The Chapter 200 rules (Rules 201-250) apply to all civil actions and proceedings.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 400. Who serves original process","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Within Pennsylvania, original process (the writ of summons or complaint that starts the case) must be served by the sheriff, except as provided in Rules 400.1 and 1930.4. A competent adult may also serve it in actions seeking an injunction, perpetuation of testimony or a receiver (Rules 1531-1533), partition, declaratory judgment actions seeking only declaratory relief, and actions with complete diversity of citizens","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 400.1. Service in the First Judicial District (Philadelphia)","Pennsylvania · civil rules","In actions begun in Philadelphia, original process may be served in Philadelphia by the sheriff or a competent adult, and in other counties by deputized sheriff service or by a competent adult forwarding the process to that county’s sheriff. In actions begun elsewhere, process may be served in Philadelphia by deputized service or by a competent adult.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 401. Time for service; reissuance, reinstatement, substitution","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Original process must be served within Pennsylvania within 30 days after the writ is issued or the complaint is filed (Rule 404 governs out-of-state service). If not served in time, the prothonotary on praecipe reissues the writ or reinstates the complaint, which may be done any number of times, and the new process must be served within 30 days (or the Rule 404 period) after reissuance or reinstatement; a lost writ o","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 402. Manner of service; acceptance of service","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Original process may be served by handing a copy to the defendant, or by handing it to an adult family member at the defendant’s residence (or, if none, the adult in charge there), to the clerk or manager of the hotel, apartment house or other lodging where the defendant lives, or to the defendant’s agent or person in charge at any office or usual place of business. Instead, the defendant or an authorized agent may a","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 403. Service by mail","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Where a rule authorizes mail service, process is mailed by any form of mail requiring a receipt signed by the defendant or authorized agent, and service is complete on delivery. If the mail comes back marked refused, the plaintiff may re-mail by ordinary mail with a return address to the same address, and service is complete if it is not returned within 15 days after mailing. Trap: if it comes back marked unclaimed, ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 404. Service outside Pennsylvania","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Original process must be served outside Pennsylvania within 90 days of the issuance of the writ, filing of the complaint, or its reissuance or reinstatement. Service may be made by a competent adult as in Rule 402(a), by mail under Rule 403, as provided by the law of the place of service for its courts of general jurisdiction, as provided by treaty, or as directed by a foreign authority in response to a letter rogato","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 405. Return of service","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The person making service must file a return of service with the prothonotary forthwith, and if service is not made and the process is not reissued or reinstated, a return of no service when the service period expires; the return must state the date, time, place and manner of service, who was served, and other facts showing proper service. Proof of mail service requires the signed return receipt or, after a refusal, ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 410. Service in real property actions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","In actions involving title, interest, possession, or liens on real property, the defendant is served under the general rules. In possession or mortgage foreclosure actions, process must also be served on any non-party found in possession; in a possession action that person becomes a defendant (indexed on the plaintiff’s praecipe), but in foreclosure does not become a party. If service is by special order under Rule 4","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 411. Service in support actions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","When a domestic relations section official serves original process in a support action within Pennsylvania, the official sends the complaint and order either by ordinary mail, or with one copy by receipt-required mail and one by ordinary mail (complete on return of a receipt personally signed by the defendant or other satisfactory evidence). A defendant who attends the hearing in person is deemed served. The rule app","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 412. Service by mail in custody, divorce and annulment actions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","In custody, partial custody or visitation actions and in divorce or annulment actions, original process may be served within Pennsylvania by mail under Rule 403 as well as by the Rule 402(a) methods.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 420. Service on minors","Pennsylvania · civil rules","When the defendant is a minor (under 18), original process is served on the minor as on an adult defendant or by handing a copy to the minor’s guardian. A valid judgment against a minor cannot be obtained until a guardian is appointed.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 421. Service on incapacitated persons","Pennsylvania · civil rules","When the defendant is an incapacitated person, original process is served on that person as on a competent defendant, or by handing a copy to the person’s guardian.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 422. Service on the Commonwealth and political subdivisions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Process against the Commonwealth or a Commonwealth officer, department, board, commission or instrumentality must be served by handing a copy to the person in charge at both the defendant’s office and the office of the Attorney General. A political subdivision is served by handing a copy to its authorized agent, the person in charge at its office, or the mayor or the president, chair, secretary or clerk of its tax-le","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 423. Service on partnerships and unincorporated associations","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A partnership (and partners named in the action) or an unincorporated association is served on any partner, officer or registered agent, an agent authorized in writing to receive process, or the manager, clerk or person in charge of any regular place of business or activity, provided the person served is not a plaintiff.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 424. Service on corporations and similar entities","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A corporation or similar entity is served by handing a copy to an executive officer, partner or trustee, the manager, clerk or person in charge of any regular place of business or activity, or an agent authorized in writing to receive process, provided the person served is not a plaintiff. Trap: residence-type substituted service under Rule 402(a)(2) is not permitted on a corporation.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 425. Service on additional defendants","Pennsylvania · civil rules","An additional defendant not already a party is served with original process as if an original defendant, and on request the joining party must furnish copies of all or specified pleadings. A defendant or additional defendant must serve its joinder complaint on every prior party.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 430. Service by special order of court; publication","Pennsylvania · civil rules","If service cannot be made under the applicable rule, the plaintiff may move for a special order directing the method of service, with an affidavit describing the investigation into the defendant’s whereabouts and why service cannot be made (a sheriff’s “not found” return alone is insufficient; a good-faith search includes postal inquiries, relatives, neighbors and employers, public records and a reasonable internet s","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 440. Service of legal papers other than original process","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Every paper filed or served in an action (other than original process) must be served on every other party, by handing, mailing or leaving a copy at the attorney of record’s address endorsed on an appearance or prior pleading (or another agreed address), or by fax; if a party has no attorney, service is on the party at the endorsed address, residence or business, or else the last known address. Service by mail is com","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 441. Service of other papers on minors or incapacitated persons","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Papers other than original process are served, in the Rule 440 manner, on a minor party’s attorney of record, or if none the minor’s guardian, or if neither the minor; and on an incapacitated party’s guardian, guardian ad litem, or attorney of record.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1001. Definition; scope","Pennsylvania · civil rules","One “civil action” replaces the old forms of action in assumpsit, trespass and equity, and the procedural distinctions among them are abolished. In Chapter 1000 and Rules 1506 and 1531–1535, “action” means a civil action brought in or appealed to a court subject to these rules. Other forms of action that borrow these rules are styled “civil action—[type],” e.g. “civil action—mandamus.”","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1002. Authority of attorney","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Anything this chapter requires or allows a party to do may be done by the party’s attorney, except verification, which must come from the party (see Rule 1024).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1003. Waiver of rules; extension of time","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The parties may waive by agreement the rules on how an action is commenced and the time for serving process or filing and serving pleadings. On cause shown the court may extend or shorten the time to file pleadings or serve process.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1006. Venue; change of venue","Pennsylvania · civil rules","An action against an individual may be brought only in a county where the individual may be served, the cause of action arose, a transaction or occurrence giving rise to it took place, venue is authorized by law, or (for equitable relief about property) the property is located; entity defendants follow Rules 2103 (political subdivisions), 2130 (partnerships), 2156 (unincorporated associations) and 2179 (corporations)","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1007. Commencement of action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","An action may be commenced by filing with the prothonotary (the clerk of the civil court) either a praecipe (a written request to the prothonotary) for a writ of summons, or a complaint. A writ of summons is the court’s formal notice to the defendant that suit has begun, letting the plaintiff file the complaint later; the note warns that a praecipe for a writ may not be used when an unknown defendant is to be identif","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1007.1. Jury trial; demand; waiver","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Where a right to jury trial exists, it is waived unless a party files and serves a written jury demand no later than 20 days after service of the last permissible pleading, by endorsement on a pleading or a separate writing. On an appeal from compulsory arbitration, the right is waived unless the appellant endorses a demand on the appeal or the appellee files and serves a demand within 10 days after being served with","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1008-1011. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 1008, 1009 (service), 1010 (reissuance, reinstatement and substitution of writ or complaint) and 1011 (acceptance of service) are rescinded. Service of original process is now governed by Rule 400 et seq., reissuance and reinstatement by Rule 401(b), and acceptance of service by Rule 402(b).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1012. Entry of appearance; withdrawal of appearance; notice","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A party may (but need not) enter a written appearance stating an address for service under Rule 440(a)(1) and a telephone number, and optionally a fax number (listing a fax number is an agreement to accept service by fax); entering an appearance does not waive any defense, including jurisdiction or venue, and all parties must be notified promptly. An attorney may not withdraw without leave of court unless another att","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1012.1. Admission pro hac vice; motion; content","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A Pennsylvania-admitted “sponsor” must file a written motion in the action to admit an out-of-state or foreign “candidate” pro hac vice, averring that the information required by Section 81.504 of the IOLTA regulations has been given to the IOLTA Board and either that the fee required by Section 81.505(a) has been paid, attaching the IOLTA Board’s fee payment certification, or averring that the fee is not required un","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1013. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 1013 (sheriff’s return) is rescinded; return of service is governed by Rule 405.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1017. Pleadings allowed","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Except in asbestos cases under Rule 1041.1, the only pleadings are a complaint (including a complaint joining an additional defendant) and answer; a reply if the answer contains new matter, a counterclaim or a cross-claim; a counter-reply if a reply to a counterclaim or cross-claim contains new matter; and preliminary objections and a response. No formal joinder of issue is required. Unlike federal practice, prelimin","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1018. Caption","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Every pleading must carry a caption with the court’s name, the action number and the name of the pleading. The complaint’s caption must state the form of action and the names of all parties, including a Doe designation for an unknown defendant under Rule 2005; later pleadings may name just the first party on each side with an indication of others. Notes: medical professional liability papers must say “Civil Action—Me","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1018.1. Notice to defend; form","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Every complaint, including a complaint against an additional defendant, must begin with a notice to defend substantially in the prescribed form, and no other notice to plead to a complaint is needed. The notice tells the defendant “You have been sued in court” and must act within twenty (20) days after service by entering a written appearance and filing written defenses or objections, warns that judgment may be enter","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1019. Contents of pleadings; general and specific averments","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Pennsylvania is a fact-pleading state: the material facts on which a cause of action or defense is based must be stated in concise and summary form, not just notice of the claim. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity (conditions of mind may be averred generally); conditions precedent may be averred generally but must be denied specifically; time, place and items of special damage must be stated specifi","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1020. Pleading more than one cause of action; alternative pleading; failure to join; bar","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A plaintiff may plead multiple causes of action against the same defendant, each in a separate count with its own demand for relief, and joined plaintiffs must each have separate, headed counts; causes of action and defenses may be pleaded in the alternative. Trap: if one transaction or occurrence gives rise to more than one cause of action formerly in assumpsit and trespass against the same person, they must all be ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1021. Claim for relief; determination of amount in controversy","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A pleading must specify the relief sought, which may be alternative or of several types, including an accounting, but a demand for unliquidated damages may not claim a specific sum. In counties with compulsory arbitration, the plaintiff must state whether the amount claimed does or does not exceed the local arbitration limit, and the court may determine the true amount in controversy and refer the case to arbitration","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1022. Paragraphing","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Every pleading must be divided into consecutively numbered paragraphs, each containing, as far as practicable, only one material allegation.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1023. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 1023 (former signing and sanctions rule) was rescinded effective July 1, 2002 and replaced by Rules 1023.1–1023.4.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1023.1. Scope; signing of documents; representations to the court; violation","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Every pleading, written motion and other paper directed to the court must be signed by at least one attorney of record in the attorney’s own name, or by an unrepresented party; Rules 1023.1–1023.4 do not apply to discovery matters. Signing, filing or later advocating a paper certifies that, after reasonable inquiry, it is not for an improper purpose, its legal contentions are warranted by existing law or a nonfrivolo","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1023.2. Motion for sanctions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A sanctions motion must be separate from other requests and describe the specific conduct claimed to violate Rule 1023.1(c). Trap (safe harbor): it may not be filed unless it certifies, and attaches, a written notice and demand served on the signer that identifies each offending portion with specificity, explains why, and demands withdrawal or correction, and the paper was not withdrawn or corrected within 28 days af","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1023.3. Sanctions upon rule to show cause","Pennsylvania · civil rules","On its own initiative, the court may enter an order describing conduct that appears to violate Rule 1023.1(c) and directing an attorney, law firm or party to show cause why it has not violated the rule.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1023.4. Sanctions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A sanction must be limited to what suffices to deter repetition by the offender or others similarly situated, and may include nonmonetary directives (such as striking the offending document), a penalty paid into court, or, if imposed on motion and warranted for deterrence, payment of some or all of the movant’s reasonable attorneys’ fees and expenses directly caused by the violation; except in exceptional circumstanc","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1024. Verification","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Every pleading containing an averment of fact not of record or a denial of fact must state that it is true on the signer’s personal knowledge or information and belief and must be verified; inconsistent factual averments require a statement that the signer cannot determine after reasonable investigation which is true but believes one is. Unlike the federal rules, verification must be made by one or more of the partie","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1025. Endorsement","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Every pleading or legal paper must be endorsed with the name of the party’s attorney (or of the party, if unrepresented), an address where papers may be served under Rule 440(a), and a telephone number; a fax number may be added, and listing one is an agreement to accept service by fax.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1026. Time for filing; notice to plead","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Every pleading after the complaint must be filed within 20 days after service of the preceding pleading, but no responsive pleading is required unless the preceding pleading contains a notice to defend or is endorsed with a notice to plead. A defendant served outside the United States has 60 days from service of the complaint to plead, and professional liability defendants follow Rule 1042.4; extra time may be sought","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1027. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 1027 (service of copies) is rescinded; service of pleadings and other legal papers is governed by Rule 440.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1028. Preliminary objections","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Instead of federal-style Rule 12 motions, any party may file preliminary objections to any pleading, limited to: (1) lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction, improper venue, or improper form or service of a writ or complaint; (2) failure to conform to law or rule of court, or scandalous or impertinent matter; (3) insufficient specificity; (4) legal insufficiency (demurrer); (5) lack of capacity to sue, nonjoi","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1029. Denials; effect of failure to deny","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A responsive pleading must admit or deny each averment of fact, paragraph by paragraph, specifying what part of a partly denied averment is admitted. Trap: averments requiring a response are admitted unless denied specifically or by necessary implication, and a general denial or demand for proof counts as an admission; a statement that after reasonable investigation the party lacks knowledge sufficient to form a beli","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1030. New matter","Pennsylvania · civil rules","All affirmative defenses, including accord and satisfaction, arbitration and award, consent, discharge in bankruptcy, duress, estoppel, failure of consideration, fair comment, fraud, illegality, immunity from suit, impossibility, justification, laches, license, payment, privilege, release, res judicata, statute of frauds, statute of limitations, truth and waiver, must be pleaded in the responsive pleading under the h","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1031. Counterclaim","Pennsylvania · civil rules","In the answer, under the heading “Counterclaim,” the defendant may assert any cause of action cognizable in a civil action that the defendant has against the plaintiff when the answer is filed. A counterclaim need not diminish or defeat the plaintiff’s claim and may seek relief greater in amount or different in kind.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1031.1. Cross-claim","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Any party may plead, in an answer or reply under the heading “Cross-claim,” a cause of action against any other party asserting that the other party is solely liable on the underlying cause of action, or is liable to or with the cross-claimant on any cause of action arising out of the same transaction or occurrence (or series) as the underlying claim. The note says this covers liability-over and joint-and-several cla","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1032. Waiver of defenses; exceptions; suggestion of lack of subject-matter jurisdiction or failure to join indispensable party","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A party waives every defense and objection not raised by preliminary objection, answer or reply, except defenses that need not be pleaded under Rule 1030(b), failure to state a claim, failure to join an indispensable party, failure to state a legal defense, failure to exercise or exhaust a statutory remedy, an adequate remedy at law, and other nonwaivable defenses. Whenever it appears the court lacks subject-matter j","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1033. Amendment","Pennsylvania · civil rules","With the adverse party’s filed consent or leave of court, a party may at any time change the form of action, add a party, correct a party’s name, or otherwise amend, including to plead new events or new causes of action or to conform to the evidence. A name correction relates back if, within 90 days after the limitations period ran, the correct party received notice so as not to be prejudiced and knew or should have ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1034. Motion for judgment on the pleadings","Pennsylvania · civil rules","After the relevant pleadings are closed (only those between the parties to the motion), but early enough not to unreasonably delay trial, any party may move for judgment on the pleadings, and the court enters whatever judgment or order the pleadings warrant. Each court must have a local rule (Local Rule 1034(a)) on the procedure.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1035. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Former Rule 1035 (summary judgment) was rescinded effective July 1, 1996 and replaced by Rules 1035.1–1035.5. Its note says that in asbestos litigation a summary judgment motion by one defendant on a ground common to others is deemed filed for all of them.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1035.1. Motion for summary judgment; definition","Pennsylvania · civil rules","For summary judgment purposes, the “record” includes the pleadings; depositions, answers to interrogatories, admissions and affidavits; and expert reports signed by the expert that would comply with Rule 4003.5(a)(1) if filed, whether or not they were produced in discovery.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1035.2. Motion","Pennsylvania · civil rules","After the relevant pleadings close, but without unreasonably delaying trial, any party may move for summary judgment in whole or in part when (1) there is no genuine issue of material fact as to a necessary element of the claim or defense that could be established by more discovery or an expert report, or (2) after completion of relevant discovery, including expert reports, the adverse party who bears the burden of p","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1035.3. Response; judgment for failure to respond","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Trap: the adverse party may not rest on the pleadings and must file a response within 30 days after service of the motion, identifying either fact issues arising from record evidence that contradicts the movant’s evidence or from a challenge to the credibility of the movant’s witnesses, or record evidence establishing the essential facts the motion says are missing. The respondent may supplement the record or explain","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1035.4. Affidavits","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Supporting and opposing affidavits must be made on personal knowledge, set out facts that would be admissible in evidence, and show that the signer is competent to testify to them. Verified or certified copies of papers referred to must be attached or served with the affidavit, and the court may allow affidavits to be supplemented or opposed by depositions, interrogatory answers or further affidavits.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1035.5. Procedure when judgment is denied or not rendered on the whole case","Pennsylvania · civil rules","If summary judgment is denied or granted only in part and trial is needed, the court may, if practicable, determine which material facts are undisputed and which are actually controverted, and enter an order specifying the undisputed facts, including any amount of damages not in controversy. Those facts are deemed established at trial.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1036. Dismissal upon affidavit of noninvolvement","Pennsylvania · civil rules","In actions where a statute allows dismissal on an affidavit of noninvolvement (the note cites claims against construction design professionals under 42 Pa.C.S. § 7502 and health care providers under Section 506 of the MCARE Act), a party seeking dismissal files a motion with the affidavit attached, and any opponent may respond. If the court finds a prima facie case for dismissal, it must allow the opponent limited di","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1036.1. Reinstatement of claim dismissed upon affidavit of noninvolvement","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Any party may move to reinstate a party dismissed under Rule 1036 by setting forth facts showing the affidavit of noninvolvement was false or inaccurate, and any party may respond. If the court finds a prima facie case of involvement, it must allow limited discovery solely on involvement and the filing of evidence, then hold argument limited to whether the movant’s evidence, viewed most favorably to the movant, would","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1037. Judgment upon default or admission; assessment of damages","Pennsylvania · civil rules","If an action began by writ of summons, the defendant may have the prothonotary enter a rule to file a complaint, and if none is filed within 20 days after service of the rule, a judgment of non pros (dismissal for the plaintiff’s failure to prosecute) on praecipe; on the plaintiff’s praecipe, the prothonotary enters default judgment for failure to plead in time to a complaint containing a notice to defend, or for rel","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1038. Trial without jury","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A nonjury trial is conducted as nearly as possible like a jury trial, with the same rights, including the right to move for nonsuit. The judge’s decision may consist of general findings but must dispose of all claims, may include specific findings and conclusions, and may be given orally (then transcribed and filed) or in writing; the prothonotary notifies all parties of the filing date. The decision must be rendered","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1038.1. Case submitted on stipulated facts","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A case may be submitted to a judge without a jury on stipulated facts, following the nonjury trial rules as far as practicable.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1038.2. Abolition of case stated","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The common law “case stated” procedure is abolished; parties use submission on stipulated facts under Rule 1038.1 instead.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1038.3. Equitable relief; advisory verdict by jury","Pennsylvania · civil rules","In any case with a claim for equitable relief, the court on its own motion or a party’s petition may submit any or all fact issues on that claim to a jury. The jury’s verdict is advisory only, in the form of answers to specific questions, and does not bind the court or create a jury right that did not previously exist.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1039. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 1039 (entry of judgment on praecipe) is rescinded; see Rule 227.4.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1040. Suspension of Acts of Assembly","Pennsylvania · civil rules","All statutes inconsistent with these rules are suspended to the extent of the inconsistency.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1041. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 1041 (former action in trespass) is rescinded.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1041.1. Asbestos litigation; special provisions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","In any action alleging injury or death from asbestos exposure (including added silica claims), the general civil rules apply with special changes: papers must be captioned “Civil Action—Asbestos,” and within 20 days after service of the complaint the defendant must enter an appearance, which itself operates as a denial of all factual averments, an assertion of all affirmative defenses, and a claim for indemnity and c","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1041.2. Civil action; alternative procedure","Pennsylvania · civil rules","In multi-party actions such as mass torts, product liability or toxic substance cases, the court on motion or its own initiative may order the parties to follow the asbestos procedure of Rule 1041.1(a) and (c)–(f). If many cases are affected, the court may require a special caption designation and a separate docket or docketing code.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 1042 is rescinded.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.1. Professional liability actions; scope; definition","Pennsylvania · civil rules","These rules govern civil actions in which a patient or client asserts a professional liability claim, including lack of informed consent, against a licensed professional or an entity responsible for one. “Licensed professional” means a health care provider as defined in Section 503 of the MCARE Act, an accountant, architect, chiropractor, dentist, engineer or land surveyor, nurse, optometrist, pharmacist, physical th","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.2. Complaint","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A complaint must identify each defendant against whom a professional liability claim is asserted (the note suggests stating that the defendant is a licensed professional with offices in a named county and that a professional liability claim is asserted). A defendant may raise noncompliance by preliminary objection, but doing so is not a prerequisite to a Rule 1042.6 notice of intent to enter non pros.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.3. Certificate of merit","Pennsylvania · civil rules","In any claim that a licensed professional deviated from an acceptable professional standard (including lack of informed consent), the plaintiff’s attorney, or an unrepresented plaintiff, must file with the complaint or within 60 days after filing it a signed certificate of merit stating that (1) an appropriate licensed professional has given a written statement that there is a reasonable probability the defendant’s c","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.4. Responsive pleading","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A professional liability defendant must file a responsive pleading within the time set by Rule 1026 or within 20 days after service of the certificate of merit on that defendant, whichever is later.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.5. Discovery","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Before filing a certificate of merit, a professional liability plaintiff may seek only production of documents and things and entry on property, and needs leave of court for any other discovery on that claim. The note says the court must allow discovery needed for a licensed professional to determine whether there was a deviation, and defendants may still seek a protective order under Rule 4012.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.6. Notice of intent to enter judgment of non pros for failure to file certificate of merit; motion to determine necessity; form of notice","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Trap: a defendant wanting a non pros under Rule 1042.7 must first file and serve a written notice of intention on the plaintiff’s attorney (or the unrepresented plaintiff) no sooner than the 31st day after the complaint was filed, warning that judgment will be entered after 30 days if no certificate is filed; no notice is needed if the court already extended the deadline and it passed, or denied an extension. After a","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.7. Entry of judgment of non pros for failure to file certification; form of praecipe","Pennsylvania · civil rules","On the defendant’s praecipe, the prothonotary must enter judgment of non pros for failure to file a timely certificate of merit only if no motion to determine necessity or timely extension motion is pending, no certificate has been filed, and (unless notice was excused under Rule 1042.6(b)) a certificate of service of the notice of intention is attached and the praecipe is filed at least 30 days after the notice was ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.8. Motion to strike; defect of certificate of merit","Pennsylvania · civil rules","If the court strikes a claim because the certificates of merit did not comply with Rule 1042.3(b) (for example, no separate certificate per defendant), it must give the plaintiff 20 days to file a certificate that cures the defect.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.9. Sanctions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","If a defendant covered by a certificate of merit is dismissed by voluntary dismissal, verdict or court order, the plaintiff must give that defendant the supporting licensed professional’s written statement within 30 days of the defendant’s written request, or within 30 days after resolution of all claims against other licensed professionals if those remain pending. The court may impose sanctions, including those in R","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.10. Certificate of merit; form","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The Rule 1042.3(a) certificate must be substantially in the prescribed form, captioned as to a named defendant and signed and dated by the attorney or party, checking one or more options: an appropriate licensed professional has supplied a written statement that the defendant’s care fell outside acceptable standards and caused the harm; and/or the claim rests solely on others for whom the defendant is responsible and","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.11. Notice of intent to enter non pros for failure to file a written statement from an appropriate licensed professional; form","Pennsylvania · civil rules","When a certificate of merit signed by a party rather than an attorney lacks the licensed professional’s written statement required by Rule 1042.3(e), a defendant seeking non pros under Rule 1042.12 must first file a notice of intent in the prescribed form, warning that judgment of non pros will be entered after 30 days from the filing of the notice if the written statement is not filed, and identifying the claims aff","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.12. Entry of judgment of non pros for failure to file a written statement; form of praecipe","Pennsylvania · civil rules","On the defendant’s praecipe, the prothonotary must enter judgment of non pros for failure to file the Rule 1042.3(e) written statement if none has been filed, a certificate of service of the Rule 1042.11 notice is attached, and the praecipe is filed at least 30 days after the notice was filed. Judgment may not be entered if the statement was filed before the praecipe, Rule 237.1 does not apply, and the rule supplies ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.16. Medical professional liability actions; legal papers; caption","Pennsylvania · civil rules","In any action with a medical professional liability claim, the caption of all legal papers (or the cover sheet, where a county requires one) must state “Civil Action—Medical Professional Liability Action.”","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.21. Medical professional liability actions; motion for settlement conference or mediation","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Before expert reports are exchanged, a health care provider may move for a settlement conference or court-ordered mediation; if not all parties consent, the movant must certify a belief that settlement is realistically possible, and a movant seeking mediation must describe it and pay for it. The court must consider any objection before ruling.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.26. Medical professional liability actions; expert reports","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 1042.26–1042.38 govern pretrial production of expert reports in medical professional liability actions against health care providers. They apply only where the court has not set case management deadlines for expert reports by order or otherwise.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.27. Requests for production of expert reports; responses; general provisions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Parties may request expert reports under Rules 1042.28–1042.30, naming the requesting party and the party to whom the request is directed. Reports must cover all liability issues, including negligence and causation, on which the responding party will offer expert testimony against the requesting party, reflect the best information then available, be signed by the expert, and comply with Rule 4003.5; requests may be f","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.28. Defendant’s request to plaintiff for production of expert reports; response","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A defendant facing a professional negligence claim may serve a request on the plaintiff (in the Rule 1042.36 form) no earlier than 90 days after filing its original answer. The plaintiff must furnish expert reports supporting the claims against that defendant within 180 days after service of the request.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.29. Plaintiff’s request to defendant or additional defendant for production of expert reports; response","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A plaintiff who has furnished expert reports to a defendant may serve a request (in the Rule 1042.37 form) for that defendant’s defense expert reports: if the plaintiff’s reports answered a Rule 1042.28 request, the defendant must respond within 60 days after service; if the plaintiff produced reports unrequested, the request may be served only after 90 days following the filing of the certificate of merit as to that","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.30. Defendant’s or additional defendant’s request to another defendant for production of expert reports; response","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A defendant or additional defendant that has furnished expert reports supporting its claims against other parties may serve a request (in the Rule 1042.38 form) on another defendant, but not earlier than 90 days after a certificate of merit was served on that party. The recipient must furnish expert reports supporting its defenses and its claims against the requester and other joined parties within 60 days after serv","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.31. Failure to produce report; sanctions; summary judgment","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A party not receiving a required expert report may move to compel; the court considers case complexity, diligence and other factors and must give a diligent party reasonable time to complete discovery and produce a report (the note says incomplete discovery or unidentified experts alone is no excuse). Violating an order compelling production may be sanctioned, including by barring expert testimony, and the court must","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.32. Additional and supplemental reports","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Until a court-set expert deadline passes, and unless the court has precluded it, a party may serve additional or supplemental expert reports without leave of court, which may raise new liability or causation theories or new defenses and may come from different experts.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.36-1042.38. Expert report request forms","Pennsylvania · civil rules","These rules set the required forms for expert report requests in medical professional liability actions: Rule 1042.36 (defendant to plaintiff, requesting reports within one hundred eighty (180) days of service), Rule 1042.37 (plaintiff to defendant or additional defendant, requesting reports within sixty (60) days under Rule 1042.29(a)(2) or one hundred twenty (120) days under Rule 1042.29(a)(3), deleting the inappli","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.41. Medical professional liability actions; scheduling order","Pennsylvania · civil rules","One year after the first answer is filed in a medical professional liability action, any party may move for a scheduling order, and the court must within 30 days issue one, covering completion of discovery and production of expert reports, or schedule a case management conference. The rule does not apply where the court has already set such schedules.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.51. Medical professional liability actions; motion for pre-trial conference; mediation; report of cases not tried","Pennsylvania · civil rules","After liability expert reports have been produced (or when the motion states all parties have exchanged them), any party may move for a pretrial conference, which must be scheduled within 60 days of the motion under Rule 212.3; at it the court sets another conference or a trial or tentative trial date and asks whether the parties will mediate. On February 1 and September 1 each year, each court administrator must rep","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.71. Medical professional liability actions; damages; findings","Pennsylvania · civil rules","At any party’s request, the trier of fact (jury or judge) must make separate findings for each plaintiff of past damages for medical and related expenses, lost earnings and noneconomic loss, each in a lump sum (subject to Section 508 of the MCARE Act on collateral sources), and of future damages for medical and related expenses by year, and for loss of earnings or earning capacity and noneconomic loss each in a lump ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1042.72, 1043-1048. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 1042.72, 1043, 1044, 1045, 1046, 1047 and 1048 are rescinded.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1051. Ejectment; conformity to civil action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Except as this subchapter provides otherwise, an action in ejectment (to recover possession of land) follows the civil action rules.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1052. Ejectment; venue","Pennsylvania · civil rules","An ejectment action may be brought only in a county where the land, or part of it, is located.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1053. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 1053 (service in ejectment) is rescinded; service in actions involving real property is governed by Rule 410.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1054. Ejectment; specific averments; abstract of title","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The plaintiff must describe the land in the complaint, and each party must set out in the complaint or answer an abstract of the title it relies on, going back at least to the common source of the parties’ competing titles.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1055. Ejectment; pleading more than one cause of action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The ejectment plaintiff may join claims for rents, profits or any other damages arising from the defendant’s possession of the land, such as delinquent rent or installment-contract payments.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1056. Ejectment; counterclaim; conditional verdict","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The defendant may counterclaim on any claim arising from the same transaction or occurrence (or series) as the plaintiff’s cause of action, whether or not the plaintiff seeks damages, and a conditional verdict may be entered in an appropriate case.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1057. Ejectment; judgment; execution","Pennsylvania · civil rules","An ejectment judgment is enforced under Rules 3160 through 3165.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1058. Ejectment; trial without jury","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A nonjury ejectment trial follows Rule 1038.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1061. Quiet title; conformity to civil action; scope","Pennsylvania · civil rules","An action to quiet title follows the civil action rules through judgment, but the rule confers no right to a jury trial. It may be brought to compel an adverse party to bring ejectment; where ejectment will not lie, to determine any right, lien, title or interest in land or the validity or discharge of a document affecting it; to compel an adverse party to record, cancel, surrender, satisfy or admit the validity, inv","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1062. Quiet title; venue","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A quiet title action may be brought only in a county where the land, or part of it, is located.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1063. Quiet title; commencement of action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A quiet title action must be commenced by filing a complaint with the prothonotary, not by writ of summons.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1064. Quiet title; service","Pennsylvania · civil rules","In quiet title actions involving subsurface mineral, oil or natural gas rights, a plaintiff serving by publication under Rule 430 who learns of a last known address of the defendant outside the county where the property lies must describe the search for the defendant in that locale in the Rule 430(a) affidavit. Service otherwise follows Rule 410 for actions involving real property.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1065. Quiet title; specific averments","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The plaintiff must describe the land in the complaint. For subsurface mineral, oil or natural gas rights, the complaint must attach a summary of the abstract of that title (or the full abstract if the title documents are not in the courthouse records) and a statement of acreage with a metes and bounds description if available, or another description sufficient to identify the land.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1065.1. Quiet title; notice; claim for adverse possession","Pennsylvania · civil rules","In a quiet title action under 42 Pa.C.S. § 5527.1 by a possessor claiming title by adverse possession of not less than ten years, the plaintiff must give the record owner (and heirs, successors and assigns) the prescribed notice identifying the property by address, deed reference, parcel number and metes and bounds description. The notice tells the owner that to challenge the claim, the owner must respond within one ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1066. Quiet title; form of judgment or order","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The court grants relief on an affidavit that a complaint with notice to defend was served and no answer was filed, or after a hearing or trial. Relief may include an order forever barring the defendant from asserting any inconsistent interest unless the defendant takes the action the order directs within thirty days, after which the prothonotary enters final judgment on the plaintiff’s praecipe; a final judgment decl","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1067. Quiet title; trial without jury","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A nonjury quiet title trial follows Rule 1038.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1068. Quiet title; Acts of Assembly","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The rules do not suspend the 1895 Act (as amended in 1899) on recording subdivision plans, 21 P.S. §§ 399, 497–499, or Section 506 of the Mechanics’ Lien Law, 49 P.S. § 1506 (rule to file a mechanics’ lien or be barred). The 1881 Act (as amended in 1927), 21 P.S. § 688, authorizing a rule to foreclose a mortgage or be barred, is suspended absolutely.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1071. Replevin; conformity to civil action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Except as this subchapter provides otherwise, an action of replevin (to recover specific personal property) follows the civil action rules from commencement to judgment.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1072. Replevin; venue","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A replevin action may be brought in any county where a civil action could be brought or in the county where the property is found.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1073. Replevin; commencement of action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A replevin action must be commenced by filing a complaint with the prothonotary.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1073.1. Replevin; complaint; specific averments; verification","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The complaint must describe the property, state its value and its location if known, and set out the material facts of the claim. It must be verified by the plaintiff, or by another person with sufficient knowledge who states the source of the information and why the plaintiff did not verify.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1074. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 1074 (service of the replevin complaint) is rescinded; service of original process is governed by Rule 400 et seq.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1075. Replevin; seizure of property before judgment","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The sheriff may seize the property before judgment under a writ of seizure, which may issue only on a court order entered after notice and hearing under Rule 1075.1 or ex parte under Rule 1075.2.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1075.1. Writ of seizure upon notice and hearing","Pennsylvania · civil rules","After filing the complaint (served or not), the plaintiff may move for a writ of seizure with the complaint attached (verified if adding new facts); the hearing must be set at least 48 hours after the motion is filed, and notice in the Rule 1353 form must be served at least 24 hours before the hearing, though the court may shorten these times for perishable property or other cause. The hearing proceeds even if the de","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1075.2. Ex parte issuance of writ of seizure","Pennsylvania · civil rules","After the complaint is filed, the court may issue a writ ex parte, on a bond under Rule 1075.3, if the plaintiff shows the probable validity of its claim and probable cause that before notice and hearing the property’s value will be harmed by the defendant’s continued use or the property will be concealed, disposed of, encumbered, wasted or removed from the county. The complaint and motion are served with the writ, a","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1075.3. Writ of seizure; bond","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The writ follows the Rule 1354 form. The plaintiff’s bond must be double the value of the property averred in the complaint, with security approved by the prothonotary and the Commonwealth as obligee, conditioned on paying the value of the property and all costs, fees and damages caused by the writ if the plaintiff fails to maintain its right to possession.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1075.4. Service of the writ of seizure","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The sheriff serves the writ (with the complaint and ex parte motion, if applicable) on the defendant and any nonparty found in possession, in the manner of Rule 402(a), and takes possession of the property. A nonparty in possession who is served becomes a defendant, the sheriff must tell that person so and note it in the return, and the plaintiff must promptly serve that person with all prior pleadings and motions un","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1075.5. Duration of validity of writ of seizure","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A writ of seizure remains valid until served and need not be reissued.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1076. Replevin; counterbond","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A defendant or intervenor claiming the right to possession (but not one claiming only a lien) may file a counterbond with the prothonotary within 72 hours after seizure, or within 72 hours after service where the plaintiff waived the sheriff’s taking possession, or within any extension the court grants for cause. The counterbond must equal the original bond, with prothonotary-approved security and the Commonwealth as","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1077. Replevin; disposition of seized property; sheriff’s return","Pennsylvania · civil rules","If the plaintiff authorizes it in writing, the sheriff may leave the property with the person in possession during the counterbond period; otherwise the sheriff holds it until that period ends, then delivers it to the plaintiff if it is not impounded, no counterbond is filed and no vacatur proceedings are pending. If a counterbond is filed, the property goes to the person in possession at seizure if that person filed","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1078. Replevin; exemption of property; preliminary objection","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A claim that property is immune or exempt from replevin must be raised by preliminary objection.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1079. Replevin; impounding property","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Before the sheriff delivers the property to any party, any party may petition to have it impounded with the sheriff or another custodian. The court must order impoundment if money would not adequately compensate the petitioner for its loss and the petitioner posts security for storage and related expenses, which are taxed as costs; at the end of the case the property goes to the winning party.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1079.1. Replevin; special equitable relief","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A pending replevin action does not impair any party’s right to separate and independent equitable relief.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1080. Replevin; objections to bond","Pennsylvania · civil rules","On any party’s petition, after notice and hearing, the court may review the prothonotary’s approval or rejection of security, raise or lower any bond or require more security for cause, strike an improperly filed bond, or permit substitution of a bond and exonerate the prior one.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1081. Replevin; concealment of property; examination of defendant","Pennsylvania · civil rules","On the plaintiff’s petition showing that the plaintiff cannot locate the property despite reasonable diligence, the sheriff cannot find it, or the defendant has concealed, removed or transferred it, the court may at any time order the defendant to be examined orally under oath about its whereabouts and enforce the order by attachment. If a writ of seizure has issued, the court may order the defendant to deliver the p","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1082. Replevin; counterclaim; lien; conditional verdict","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The only counterclaim allowed in replevin is a claim secured by a lien on the property. If any party has a lien, the court may enter a conditional verdict to protect everyone’s rights.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1083. Replevin; judgment in rem when defendant is not served and does not appear","Pennsylvania · civil rules","If the sheriff has seized the property, the court on the plaintiff’s motion after forty-five days from seizure may enter judgment in rem for the property against any defendant who has neither been served nor appeared. A defendant who was served or appeared is instead subject to default judgment for failure to answer.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1084. Replevin; judgment before trial when defendant is served or appears","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A pretrial judgment for the party in possession determines its right to keep the property and any special damages; one for a party not in possession determines its right to recover the property, the property’s money value based on the value stated in the complaint, and any special damages. Special damages are assessed at a trial limited to their amount.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1085. Replevin; judgment after trial","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A judgment after trial for the party in possession determines its right to keep the property and any special damages; one for a party not in possession determines its right to recover possession, the money value of the property, and any special damages.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1086. Replevin; judgment; enforcement","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A replevin judgment is enforced under Rules 3170 through 3173.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1087. Replevin; trial without jury","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A nonjury replevin trial follows Rule 1038.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1088. Replevin; Acts of Assembly not suspended","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The rules do not suspend Section 1 of the Act of May 7, 1925, 6 P.S. § 11 (determining a common law lien in replevin), or Section 7 of the Act of June 10, 1931, 3 P.S. § 635 (stray cattle).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1091. Mandamus; conformity to civil action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Except as this subchapter provides otherwise, an action of mandamus (to compel performance of a public act or duty) follows the civil action rules.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1092. Mandamus; venue","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A mandamus action by the Commonwealth on relation of the Attorney General may be brought only where the defendant political subdivision is located or where a defendant corporation has its registered office or principal place of business; one on relation of the District Attorney against a political subdivision only in its county. An action by a party with a beneficial interest distinct from the public’s may be brought","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1093. Mandamus; commencement of action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A mandamus action may be commenced by filing a complaint with the prothonotary.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1094. Mandamus; parties defendant","Pennsylvania · civil rules","To compel a political subdivision, it suffices to name the officers concerned in their official capacities; a corporation must be joined with the persons or body responsible for the duty. A Commonwealth department, board or commission, or a board of a political subdivision, may be named by itself without joining its head or members.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1095. Mandamus; the complaint","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The complaint must state the names and descriptions of the parties, the facts relied on, the act or duty the defendant must perform and its refusal, the plaintiff’s interest in the result, any damages, the lack of any other adequate remedy at law, and a prayer for judgment commanding performance plus any damages and costs.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1096. Mandamus; counterclaim","Pennsylvania · civil rules","No counterclaim may be asserted in mandamus.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1097. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 1097 (service in mandamus) is rescinded; see Rules 422 and 424 for service on the Commonwealth, political subdivisions and corporations.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1098. Mandamus; peremptory judgment","Pennsylvania · civil rules","At any time after the complaint is filed, the court may enter judgment if the plaintiff’s right to it is clear, with prior notice to all parties unless the emergency requires acting first, in which case notice follows as soon as possible. The note says a peremptory mandamus judgment is appealable as of right without first petitioning to open it.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1099. Mandamus; trial without jury","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A nonjury mandamus trial follows Rule 1038.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1100. Mandamus; suspension of Acts of Assembly","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Section 1 of the Act of May 9, 1889, 52 P.S. § 1, is suspended insofar as it concerns mandamus actions to recover the bodies of entombed coal miners.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1111. Quo warranto; conformity to civil action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Except as this subchapter provides otherwise, an action of quo warranto (challenging the right to hold an office or exercise a franchise) follows the civil action rules.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1112. Quo warranto; venue","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A quo warranto action on relation of the Attorney General may be brought only where the political subdivision is located (against its officer), where a municipal corporation is located (challenging its charter), or where a corporation has its registered office or principal place of business (challenging its charter, a franchise or privilege, or an officer); on relation of the District Attorney, only in the political ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1113. Quo warranto; commencement of action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A quo warranto action must be commenced by filing a complaint with the prothonotary.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1114. Quo warranto; trial without jury","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A nonjury quo warranto trial follows Rule 1038.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1121-1137. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 1121 through 1137 (Subchapter H, action of divorce) were all rescinded effective July 1, 1980.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1141. Mortgage foreclosure; definition; conformity to civil action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","“Action” here means an action to foreclose a mortgage on an estate, leasehold or interest in land, or on both personal property and real property where the secured party elects under Section 9604(a) of the Uniform Commercial Code, but not an action to enforce personal liability. Otherwise the civil action rules apply.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1142. Mortgage foreclosure; venue","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A foreclosure action may be brought only in a county where the land, or part of it, is located.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1143. Mortgage foreclosure; commencement of action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A foreclosure action must be commenced by filing a complaint with the prothonotary.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1144. Mortgage foreclosure; parties; release of liability","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The plaintiff must name the mortgagor, the personal representative, heir or devisee of a deceased mortgagor if known, and the real owner (or, if unknown, the grantee in the last recorded deed). The mortgagor or the mortgagor’s representative, heir or devisee need not be joined, unless named as real owner, if the complaint releases that person from liability for the mortgage debt.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1145. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 1145 (service in foreclosure) is rescinded; service in actions involving real property is governed by Rule 410.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1146. Mortgage foreclosure; pleading more than one cause of action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The plaintiff may plead two or more grounds for foreclosure but only one cause of action.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1147. Mortgage foreclosure; the complaint","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The complaint must state the parties to and date of the mortgage and any assignments and where they are recorded, describe the land, give the names, addresses and interests of the defendants (and that the real owner is unknown if not joined), specifically aver the default, give an itemized statement of the amount due, and demand judgment for it. If proceeding against both real and personal property under UCC Section ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1148. Mortgage foreclosure; counterclaim","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A defendant may counterclaim only on a claim arising from the same transaction or occurrence (or series) as the plaintiff’s cause of action.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1149. Mortgage foreclosure; judgment; execution","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A foreclosure judgment is enforced under Rules 3180 through 3183.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1150. Mortgage foreclosure; trial without jury","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A nonjury foreclosure trial follows Rule 1038.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1161. Ground rent; enforcement of personal liability; conformity to civil action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","An in personam action to recover amounts due on a ground rent follows the civil action rules, and execution on the judgment follows the rules for enforcing money judgments.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1162. Ground rent; enforcement in rem; conformity to mortgage foreclosure","Pennsylvania · civil rules","An in rem action to enforce a ground rent follows the mortgage foreclosure rules, substituting “ground rent,” “covenantor” and “covenantee” for “mortgage,” “mortgagor” and “mortgagee,” and the judgment is enforced like a foreclosure judgment under Rule 3180 et seq.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1163. Ground rent; other remedies","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The procedures in Rules 1161 and 1162 are in addition to distraint for rent due, the right of entry, and any other remedy provided by law.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1164. Ground rent; trial without jury","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A nonjury ground rent trial follows Rule 1038.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1251-1279. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 1251 through 1279 (Subchapter K, foreign attachment) were all rescinded effective October 1, 1989.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1285-1292. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 1285 through 1292 (Subchapter L, fraudulent debtor’s attachment) are all rescinded.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1301. Scope (compulsory arbitration)","Pennsylvania · civil rules","These rules govern cases sent to compulsory arbitration under a local court rule adopted under 42 Pa.C.S. § 7361. Each court of common pleas decides by local rule whether to use arbitration, which kinds of cases go to it, and the dollar limit for arbitration, within the statutory maximum in § 7361(b).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1302. List of arbitrators; appointment to board; oath","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A list of available arbitrators, drawn from lawyers who practice mainly in the judicial district, is prepared as local rule directs. Each board has three lawyers appointed from the list, chaired by a lawyer admitted at least three years, with no more than one member from the same firm. An arbitrator who would be disqualified under the Code of Judicial Conduct must withdraw, and each arbitrator takes the oath required","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1303. Hearing; notice","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Local rule sets how the hearing date, time and place are fixed, but the parties must get at least 30 days’ written notice. The notice may warn that if a party is absent, the case may be heard the same day by a judge, and there is no right to a trial de novo (a completely new trial) after a judge’s decision. If a party is not ready when the board convenes, the case proceeds to an award unless the court grants a contin","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1304. Conduct of hearing generally","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The board conducts the hearing like a non-jury trial under Rule 1038(a); the plaintiff may take a voluntary nonsuit, and the board must find for the defendant if the plaintiff fails to appear or its evidence is insufficient at the close of its case. The board may administer oaths and adjourn an unfinished hearing from day to day. No transcript or recording is made unless a party pays for one, and that party must give","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1305. Conduct of hearing; evidence","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The rules of evidence apply, and the board rules on objections by majority vote. Certain documents (bills, business and government records, hospital and health care provider records, expert reports and qualifications, written property estimates, and employer wage-loss reports) come in without a live witness if at least 20 days’ notice and copies were given to every other party; if copies were provided at least 20 day","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1306. Award","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The board must make an award promptly after the hearing, disposing of all claims and substantially in the Rule 1312 form, with any Rule 238 delay damages stated separately. A majority must sign it (a dissent may be noted), and it is filed immediately with the prothonotary (the clerk of the civil court).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1307. Award; docketing; notice; judgment; molding the award","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The prothonotary dockets the award, immediately mails a copy to each party or lawyer with the date and time of entry and the arbitrators’ compensation payable on appeal, and notes the mailing date on the docket. If no appeal is filed within 30 days after entry, the prothonotary enters judgment on the award upon a praecipe (a party’s written request to the clerk). Within the 30-day appeal period a party may ask the co","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1308. Appeal; arbitrators’ compensation; notice","Pennsylvania · civil rules","To appeal for a trial de novo, a party must file a Rule 1313 notice of appeal no later than 30 days after the day the prothonotary notes on the docket that notice of the award was sent, and must pay the arbitrators’ compensation, capped at 50% of the amount in controversy, which is not taxable as costs or recoverable. The court may, on petition, allow an appellant to waive fees and costs (wording effective November 1","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1309. Parties to appeal","Pennsylvania · civil rules","An appeal by any party is treated as an appeal by all parties on all issues unless all parties stipulate otherwise in writing.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1310. Discontinuance","Pennsylvania · civil rules","An appeal may be discontinued only with leave of court after notice to all parties or on filing the written consent of all parties.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1311. Procedure on appeal","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The appeal is tried de novo, and Rule 1305’s evidence shortcuts do not apply except as Rule 1311.1 allows. An arbitrator may not be called to testify about what happened before the board.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1311.1. Procedure on appeal; admission of documentary evidence","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A plaintiff (including a counterclaiming defendant) may elect to cap its recoverable damages on appeal at the district’s compulsory-arbitration limit by filing and serving the election at least 30 days from the date the appeal is first listed for trial; the election may be withdrawn by agreement or, on the plaintiff’s motion, for good cause. If the election is made, any party may offer the Rule 1305(b)(1) documents a","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1312. Form of oath, award and notice of entry of award","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Sets out the form of the arbitrators’ oath, the award (with delay damages stated separately and room to note a dissent), and the prothonotary’s notice of entry of the award stating the arbitrators’ compensation payable on appeal.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1313. Form of notice of appeal","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Sets out the notice-of-appeal form, which includes a box to demand a jury trial (otherwise waived) and a certification that the arbitrators’ compensation was paid or that permission to waive fees and costs has been sought (form amended effective November 1, 2026). No affidavit or verification is required.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1314. Suspension of Acts of Assembly; abolition of practice under repealed statutes","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Statutes inconsistent with these rules are suspended to that extent, and compulsory-arbitration procedures from statutes repealed by the Judiciary Act Repealer Act of April 28, 1978 (JARA) do not survive as common law.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1326. Definitions; scope (consumer credit arbitration)","Pennsylvania · civil rules","“Arbitration” means statutory arbitration under the Uniform Arbitration Act (42 Pa.C.S. § 7301 et seq.) or common law arbitration (42 Pa.C.S. § 7341 et seq.), and a “consumer credit transaction” is credit extended to an individual primarily for personal, family or household purposes. Rules 1326-1331 govern proceedings to compel arbitration and confirm awards on claims arising from consumer credit transactions.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1327. Confirming arbitration award","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A party may move to confirm a consumer-credit arbitration award only if the party against whom it is sought attended the arbitration hearing or, after the claim was filed with the arbitrator, signed a writing agreeing to submit it, or if the award followed a court order or docket entry staying the case pending arbitration under Rule 1329.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1328. Motion to confirm award as an original proceeding","Pennsylvania · civil rules","When the award qualifies under Rule 1327(1), the motion is filed as a new proceeding in the county where the defendant lives or does business (or else where the hearing was held), begins with the Rule 1331 notice, is served like original process, and must allege facts showing Rule 1327(1) is met. An opposing party must answer within 30 days after service; if no answer is filed, the prothonotary enters judgment on the","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1329. Civil action to compel arbitration; motion to confirm as ancillary to the action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A plaintiff seeking to compel arbitration files a civil action whose complaint alleges an agreement to arbitrate; a default judgment is available if the defendant does not respond. If the answer admits arbitrability, either party may within 20 days file a praecipe for a docketed stay pending arbitration; if the defendant objects or denies, the plaintiff may within 20 days move for a rule to show cause (with the Rule ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1330-1331. Forms: notices to answer","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 1330 gives the notice for a motion to compel arbitration, warning the defendant to answer within 20 days or the case will go to arbitration. Rule 1331 gives the notice for a motion to confirm an award, warning of a 30-day answer period and possible money judgment and listing an office for help finding a lawyer; courts may require translations by local rule.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1501-1505. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 1501, 1502, 1503, 1504 and 1505 are rescinded. The separate action in equity has been abolished; equitable relief is sought in an ordinary civil action under Rule 1001 et seq., with Rules 1506 and 1531-1535 as special rules.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1506. Stockholder’s derivative action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A complaint by stockholders or members suing to enforce a right the corporation or entity has failed to enforce must allege the plaintiff’s ownership, the efforts made to get the entity to act (or why none were made), and either ownership at the time of the challenged transaction (or by operation of law from such an owner) or a strong prima facie case plus serious injustice without suit. A plaintiff relying on the se","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1507-1530. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 1507 through 1530 are all rescinded. Their subjects are now covered by the general civil-action rules (for example Rules 1020, 1028, 1031, 1037, 1038, 227.1 and 3021).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1531. Special relief; injunctions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A preliminary or special injunction requires written notice and a hearing unless immediate and irreparable injury would occur first, and the court may rely on pleadings, affidavits or other proof. Except when the plaintiff is the Commonwealth, a political subdivision, or one of their agencies or officers, the plaintiff must file a court-approved bond naming the Commonwealth as obligee (or deposit cash with the protho","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1532. Special relief; perpetuation of testimony","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A complaint to preserve testimony for a contemplated action must name the prospective parties, describe the action and the need to preserve the testimony, and identify each witness and the expected substance. Testimony may be taken before the court or by deposition, and the final order says whether it is preserved. Preserved testimony may be used at trial like a Rule 4020 deposition, including by or against successor","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1533. Special relief; receivers","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A temporary receiver may be appointed without notice if the situation requires, but (unless a statute provides otherwise) only after the plaintiff posts a court-approved bond or cash deposit; a prompt hearing on continuing the appointment must follow, with notice by the receiver to interested persons including known creditors and stockholders. A corporate officer or partner cannot be sole temporary receiver; every re","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1534. Accounting by fiduciaries","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Unless a statute provides otherwise, a receiver, assignee or other fiduciary filing an account must give notice of the date set for confirmation, stating that the account may be confirmed and distribution ordered unless exceptions are filed first. Notice is given by personal service, mail or publication as local rule or order directs, and the court may order partial distributions and require refunding bonds.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1535. Objections to security","Pennsylvania · civil rules","On petition, notice and hearing, the court may for cause increase or decrease required security, strike an improperly filed bond, or allow substitute security and release the original.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1536. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 1536 is rescinded.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1549. Acts of Assembly not suspended","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Lists statutes that the civil-action rules do not suspend, including laws on timber cutting by co-owners, insolvency, insurance and bank receiverships, Labor Relations Board enforcement, labor-dispute injunctions, Clean Streams Law enforcement, corporate-dissolution receivers (15 Pa.C.S. §§ 1984-1986), and Public Utility Code injunctions (66 Pa.C.S. § 502).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1550. Acts of Assembly suspended","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Suspends the venue provisions of three statutes on injunctions: the seed-labeling law (3 P.S. § 297) and two mining laws (52 P.S. § 701-121 and 52 P.S. § 70-1201).","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1551. Partition: form of action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Except as this subchapter provides, an action to partition (divide) real estate follows the ordinary civil-action rules.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1552. Partition: venue","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A partition action, including one involving the Commonwealth, may be brought only in a county where all or part of the property is located.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1553. Partition: parties","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Any one or more co-tenants (co-owners) may sue for partition, and all other co-tenants must be joined as defendants.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1554. Partition: specific averments","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The complaint must describe the property and state the nature and extent of each party’s interest.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1555. Partition: pleading more than one cause of action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The plaintiff may seek partition of any properties co-owned with the defendants anywhere in Pennsylvania, whatever the plaintiff’s share in each. If all parties’ rights come from a common source of title, claims may be joined even if some defendants co-own fewer than all the properties.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1556. Partition: counterclaim","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A defendant may counterclaim for partition of any property the plaintiff could have included in the complaint.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1557. Order directing partition; post-trial relief","Pennsylvania · civil rules","If the court decides partition is warranted (by default, admission, hearing or trial), it enters an order directing partition that names all co-tenants and their interests. No exceptions may be filed to that order; under the official note it is appealable as of right, though post-trial motions are still required for orders that do not direct partition or that resolve other claims.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1558. Preliminary conference; appointment of hearing officer","Pennsylvania · civil rules","After the order directing partition, the court holds a preliminary conference on whether the parties can agree on a plan of partition or sale, simplifying issues, referring matters to a hearing officer, and other matters. Afterward the court may appoint a hearing officer to hear the whole matter, conduct a sale, or handle specified issues.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1559. Hearing officer; hearing","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A hearing officer conducts the needed examinations and hearings on reasonable notice and may hire appraisers and, with court approval, other experts.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1560. Property capable of division without prejudice","Pennsylvania · civil rules","If the property can be divided without spoiling the whole, it is divided first into as many purparts (shares of land) as there are parties, proportionate in value to their interests; if that is impossible, into that many purparts regardless of value; and if that too is impossible, into whatever number is most advantageous.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1561. Property capable of proportionate division; award","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Purparts proportionate in value under Rule 1560(a) are awarded to the parties according to their interests.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1562. Property not capable of proportionate division; award","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Property divisible only under Rule 1560(b) or (c) is awarded equitably among the parties with owelty (cash payments to equalize unequal shares), subject to acceptance, rejection and private sale among the parties; the court sets how owelty is secured and paid.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1563. Property not capable of division without prejudice; sale; objections","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Property that cannot be divided without spoiling the whole is offered for private sale limited to the parties. However, defendants owning a majority in value may object in writing and have the whole property awarded to them as tenants in common at the court’s valuation, with the amounts owed to the parties seeking partition becoming liens payable as the court directs.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1564. Life estates","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Property subject to a life estate may be partitioned if security is posted to ensure the life tenant receives the annual rental value, interest or income. If a life tenant has exclusive possession of part of the property, the court may refuse partition of that part or partition it subject to that possession.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1565. Retention of undivided interests; election; parties not appearing","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The court must let two or more co-tenants keep their shares together if they so elect in a writing filed within the time set by the court or hearing officer. The court may also keep together the shares of co-tenants who do not appear.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1566. Preliminary determination; notice to accept or reject; private sale confined to parties","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Parties receive preliminary notice, as local rule or order directs, of a proposed division under Rule 1560(b) or (c) or of a finding that the property cannot be divided. For a proposed division, parties have 20 days after service to accept or reject; for indivisible property, the notice warns of sale unless majority owners object under Rule 1563(b). The notice must describe the property, plan, valuations and encumbra","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1567. Private sale confined to parties; conduct; confirmation","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Divided property is offered both by purparts and as a whole to see which brings more. No sale is confirmed unless the bid at least equals the court’s valuation of the whole or of the purpart, and awards are subject to owelty where required.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1568. Public sale","Pennsylvania · civil rules","If a private sale among the parties is not confirmed, the property or purpart is sold at public sale or private sale open to outsiders, as the court directs.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1569. Hearing officer’s report; exceptions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The hearing officer files a report following the Rule 1570 decision format, after giving all interested persons written notice of the intended filing date and where the report and proposed order may be examined (with written notice of any later changes). Any party may file exceptions within 10 days after notice of filing, and the court may remand or enter a decision adopting the report in whole or part.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1570. Decision and order","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The decision must find, among other things, whether and how the property can be divided, the values of the whole and the purparts, encumbrances, credits and charges for use, taxes, rents and services, whether interests remain undivided, the parties’ acceptance or bids, and whether an outside sale is needed and which kind will bring a better price. The order must award the property or purparts (with owelty amounts, ti","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1571. Trustees to satisfy liens and charges","Pennsylvania · civil rules","On motion or the hearing officer’s recommendation, the court may appoint a trustee to receive sums owed to unknown or missing lienholders, principal needed to secure charges on the property, or sale proceeds of property subject to a life estate and remainder. After posting security and receiving payment, the trustee may satisfy the lien of record, freeing the property.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1572. Sale not confined to parties","Pennsylvania · civil rules","An outside sale is conducted as local rule or the sale order directs, subject to resale for inadequate price, and a public sale must be advertised in each county where any part of the property lies. A purchasing party or discharged lienholder gets credit for its distributive share, paying the excess in cash, and a hearing officer conducting the sale must post bond in double the payment amount, or a lower amount set b","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1573. Return of sale and schedule of distribution","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A hearing officer who conducts a sale promptly files a return of sale and proposed order confirming the sale, authorizing deeds, protecting life tenants and other absent interests, directing distribution, and providing for costs, after written notice to all interested persons. If the court approves, any part of its order not challenged by a post-trial motion within 10 days becomes final.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1574. Costs and counsel fees","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Costs, including appraisers’, hearing officer’s and court-authorized experts’ fees, are paid by the parties in proportion to their interests. Reasonable counsel fees may be charged against the property or fund and apportioned as the court finds equitable.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1576-1577. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 1576 and 1577 (actions to prevent waste) are rescinded; a claim to prevent waste is brought as a civil action seeking equitable relief.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1580-1581. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rules 1580 and 1581 are rescinded; Rule 1580’s content moved to Rule 1549(1), and Rule 1581 suspended statutes that were later repealed.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1590. Partition; Acts of Assembly not suspended","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The partition rules do not affect the 1895 statute (68 P.S. § 101) requiring co-tenants in possession to account for rental value owed to co-tenants out of possession, or the 1943 statute (16 P.S. §§ 9905-9906) on registering partition titles with county Registry Survey Bureaus.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1591. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 1591 is rescinded.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1601. Action for declaratory relief alone; jury trial; waiver","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A plaintiff seeking only declaratory relief files a complaint captioned “Action for Declaratory Judgment,” and the case follows the civil-action rules as nearly as possible. Any right to a jury on disputed facts is waived unless demanded in the time and manner Rule 1007.1 requires; the rule itself does not decide whether such a right exists.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1602. Declaratory judgment as ancillary relief","Pennsylvania · civil rules","In any civil action, a party may include a request for declaratory relief, and the procedure follows the rules for that action.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1603. Effective date; pending action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","These rules applied to actions pending when they took effect; the court could let cases proceed on pleadings filed under the old practice or allow or require amended or supplemental pleadings.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1604. Suspension of Acts of Assembly; abolition of practice under repealed statutes","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Inconsistent statutes are suspended to that extent, and declaratory-judgment procedures from statutes repealed by JARA (repeals effective June 27, 1978, June 27, 1979 and June 27, 1980) do not continue as common law.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1701. Definitions; conformity","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A “class action” is any action by or against parties as representatives of a class until the court refuses or revokes certification, and “residual funds” are what remains after paying approved class claims, expenses, costs, attorney’s fees and other approved disbursements. Otherwise, a class action follows the rules for the underlying form of action.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1702. Prerequisites to a class action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Class members may sue or be sued as representatives only if the class is so numerous that joinder is impracticable, there are common questions of law or fact, the representatives’ claims or defenses are typical, the representatives will fairly and adequately protect the class under Rule 1709, and a class action is a fair and efficient method of adjudication under Rule 1708.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1703. Commencement of action; assignment to a judge","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A class action may be started only by filing a complaint with the prothonotary, not by writ of summons. On filing, it is immediately assigned to one judge for all purposes.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1704. Form of the complaint","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The caption must say “Class Action,” and a separate section titled “Class Action Allegations” must plead facts supporting Rule 1702 and the Rule 1708 and 1709 criteria. The complaint may join equitable, declaratory and money claims arising from the same transaction or series of transactions.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1705. Preliminary objections","Pennsylvania · civil rules","All Rule 1028(a) preliminary objections must be raised at once. Factual disputes about the Class Action Allegations cannot be raised by preliminary objection and must be raised in the answer.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1706. Form of the answer","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Factual averments under “Class Action Allegations” are deemed admitted unless denied as Rule 1029 requires.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1706.1. Joinder of additional defendants; cross-claims","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A defendant may join a new additional defendant, or cross-claim against another party, only if that person may be solely liable, liable over to the joining party, or jointly or severally liable on the plaintiff’s cause of action. This is narrower than general joinder under Rule 2252(a) and cross-claims under Rule 1031.1.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1707. Motion for certification; time for filing; hearing","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The plaintiff must move for class certification within 30 days after the pleadings close or within 30 days after the last required pleading was due; the court may extend the time for cause, and if no motion is filed the court, once notified, promptly sets a certification hearing. The hearing may be postponed for other motions or class discovery, is limited to the Class Action Allegations, and the court considers all ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1708. Criteria: class action as fair and efficient method","Pennsylvania · civil rules","For money claims, the court weighs whether common questions predominate, class size and manageability, risks of inconsistent or practically dispositive separate rulings, other pending litigation, the suitability of the forum, whether individual claims are too small to sue on separately, and whether recoveries would be too small to justify the expense of a class action. For equitable or declaratory relief alone, it we","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1709. Criteria: fair and adequate representation","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The court considers whether class counsel will adequately represent the class, whether the representatives have a conflict of interest, and whether they have or can get enough financial resources to protect the class.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1710. Order certifying or refusing to certify; revocation; amendment; findings","Pennsylvania · civil rules","An order certifying, refusing or revoking certification must be accompanied by an opinion with findings of fact and conclusions of law on Rules 1702, 1708 and 1709, and a certification order must describe the class. The court may limit the class action to particular issues or forms of relief or create subclasses, and may make the order conditional and change it before a merits decision, with a memorandum of reasons. ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1711. The plaintiff class; exclusion; inclusion","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Ordinarily every member of a certified plaintiff class is included unless the member files a written election to opt out by a date the court sets. The court may instead require members to opt in by a specified date if individual claims are substantial and members have the resources and sophistication to sue on their own, or if other special circumstances described in the order exist.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1712. Order; notice of action","Pennsylvania · civil rules","After certification and a hearing on notice, the court enters an order setting the type and content of notice and which members get it, weighing class size, relief sought, cost and possible prejudice. The court may require individual notice by personal service or mail to members identifiable with reasonable effort, and otherwise notice by methods such as newspaper, television, radio, or trade, union or public-interes","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1713. Conduct of actions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The court may issue orders to manage the case, require additional notice to members of any step or proposed judgment, allow intervention under Rule 2326 et seq., impose conditions on representatives or intervenors, ensure adequate representation, and handle other procedural matters; these orders may be changed from time to time.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1714. Compromise; settlement; discontinuance","Pennsylvania · civil rules","No class action may be settled, compromised or discontinued without court approval after a hearing. Before certification, the representative may discontinue without notice to the class if the court finds no prejudice to other members; after certification, notice of the proposed settlement or discontinuance goes to all members as the court directs. A settlement need not create residual funds.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1715. Judgment","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Except by special order, no default, judgment on the pleadings, or summary judgment may be entered for or against the class until the court rules on certification. A judgment on preliminary objections before certification binds only the named parties, while a judgment in a certified class action binds all members unless the court directs otherwise; the court frames every judgment and identifies who is bound.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1716. Residual funds","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Any judgment or settlement order that sets up a process to identify and pay class members must provide for disbursing residual funds. At least 50% of residual funds must go to the Pennsylvania Interest on Lawyers Trust Account Board to support civil legal aid for the poor through nonprofit 501(c)(3) organizations; the rest may go to that Board or to another entity whose purposes relate to the objectives of the case o","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 1717. Counsel fees","Pennsylvania · civil rules","When the court is authorized by law to set counsel fees, it considers, among other things, the time and effort reasonably spent, the quality of services, the results and benefits to the class or public, the size, complexity and uniqueness of the case, and whether the fee was contingent on success. The rule does not decide when fees may be awarded, which is a matter of substantive law, and the factors are not listed i","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4001. Scope; definitions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The discovery rules apply to any civil action or proceeding in a court subject to these rules, including eminent domain and Municipal Claims Act cases and cases referred to compulsory arbitration; “court” means the court where the case is pending, and “trial” includes a hearing before arbitrators or viewers. Any party may depose any person, including a party, orally or by written interrogatories for discovery, pleadi","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4002. Agreement regarding discovery or deposition procedure","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The parties may agree that depositions be taken before any person, at any time or place, on any notice and in any manner (and then used like other depositions), and may agree to modify the procedures for any discovery method.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4002.1. Filing discovery material","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Discovery materials are not filed with the court unless relevant to a motion or other pretrial proceeding, ordered by the court, or required by statute.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4003. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 4003 (right to take depositions; notice) is rescinded; its content moved to Rules 4001(c), 4007.1 and 4007.2.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4003.1. Scope of discovery generally; opinions and contentions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Subject to Rules 4003.2-4003.5 and 4011, a party may discover any non-privileged matter relevant to the subject matter of the case, whether it relates to its own or another party’s claim or defense, including documents and the identity and location of people with knowledge. Inadmissibility at trial is no objection if the information appears reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence, and requests calling fo","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4003.2. Scope of discovery; insurance","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A party may discover whether any insurance agreement exists that may cover all or part of a judgment, and its terms. Disclosure does not make the information admissible at trial, and an insurance application is not part of the agreement.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4003.3. Scope of discovery; trial preparation material generally","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Material prepared in anticipation of litigation by or for another party or its representative (lawyer, consultant, insurer, agent and the like) is discoverable if otherwise within Rule 4003.1, with no requirement to show substantial need. Protected are the attorney’s mental impressions, conclusions, opinions, memoranda, notes, summaries, legal research and legal theories, and, for non-attorney representatives, their ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4003.4. Scope of discovery; trial preparation material; statements","Pennsylvania · civil rules","On written request, a party is entitled to immediate receipt of a copy of any prior statement about the case made by that party, any other party, or a witness, and a non-party may obtain a copy of their own statement; if refused, they may move for a court order. A “statement” is a signed or adopted written statement, or a substantially verbatim recording or transcript made at the time.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4003.5. Discovery of expert testimony; trial preparation material","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Expert discovery proceeds by interrogatory: a party may require others to identify each expected trial expert and the subject matter, and to have the expert state the substance of the facts and opinions and a summary of the grounds, answered by a signed expert report or the expert’s signed answers; further discovery (such as an expert deposition) requires court order on cause shown, so there are no automatic federal-","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4003.6. Discovery of treating physician","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Information may be obtained from a party’s treating physician only with that party’s written consent or through a discovery method authorized by these rules. This does not stop a lawyer from getting information from the lawyer’s own client or the client’s actual or ostensible employee.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4003.7. Punitive damages","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Discovery of a defendant’s wealth on a punitive-damages claim requires a court order setting limits on the timing, scope and dissemination of the discovery, though the parties may agree otherwise under Rule 4002.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4003.8. Pre-complaint discovery","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A plaintiff may take discovery before filing a complaint if the information is material and necessary to file it and the discovery will not cause unreasonable annoyance, embarrassment, oppression, burden or expense. On a protective-order motion or objection, the court may require the plaintiff to explain with particularity how the discovery will materially advance the complaint and must weigh its importance against t","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4004. Procedure on depositions by written interrogatories","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The party taking a deposition on written questions serves them on every party, with notice of the officer, time, place and deponents (an organization may be named under Rule 4007.1(e)); cross-interrogatories may be served within 30 days, and later rounds within 10 days. Objections to form are waived unless served within the time for the next round or within 10 days after the last interrogatories. The officer asks the","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4005. Written interrogatories to a party","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Subject to Rule 4011, any party may serve interrogatories on any other party (an organization answers through an officer or agent with available information), starting at service of original process; interrogatories served before the complaint must be limited to preparing it and briefly state the cause of action, and space must be left after each question for the answer. Interrogatories may cover anything within Rule","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4006. Answers to written interrogatories by a party","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Answers must be written, verified, inserted in the spaces provided, and complete unless objected to with stated reasons; answers are signed by the person answering and objections by the attorney, and an objection does not excuse answering the rest. Answers and objections must be served within 30 days after service of the interrogatories, and the asking party may move to dismiss an objection. If the answer can be deri","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4007. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 4007 is rescinded; its content moved to Rules 4001(c), 4003.1, 4007.1 and 4007.2.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4007.1. Procedure in deposition by oral examination","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A party must give every other party reasonable written notice (except a defendant served by publication who has not appeared) stating the time, place and deponent, and a party noticed for deposition must appear without a subpoena. In pre-complaint depositions after a writ of summons, the notice must briefly state the cause of action and topics; a party deponent may be asked to bring documents under Rule 4009.1 et seq","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4007.2. When leave of court required","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Depositions generally need no court permission, except under Rule 1042.5 (professional liability before a certificate of merit), Rule 4003.5(a)(2) (further expert discovery), and this rule. A plaintiff needs leave to depose someone within 30 days after service of original process unless the defendant has already sought discovery or the deponent is aged or infirm or about to leave for a place outside Pennsylvania or m","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4007.3. Sequence and timing of discovery","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, discovery methods may be used in any order, and one party’s discovery does not delay another’s.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4007.4. Supplementing responses","Pennsylvania · civil rules","There is generally no duty to supplement a complete response, except that a party must seasonably supplement answers about people with knowledge and about expected trial experts, their subject matter and the substance of their testimony. A party or expert must seasonably amend a response learned to be incorrect when made or no longer true, and further duties may arise by court order, agreement, or new requests before","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4008. Oral examination; limitation","Pennsylvania · civil rules","If an oral deposition is to be taken more than 100 miles from the courthouse, the court may on motion order payment of reasonable expenses, including attorney’s fees.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4009. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 4009 (production of documents and entry for inspection) is rescinded; the subject is now covered by Rules 4009.1-4009.33.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4009.1. Production of documents and things; general provisions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A party may request documents, tangible things and electronically stored information (ESI) from a party under Rules 4009.11-4009.12, or subpoena them from a non-party under Rules 4009.21-4009.27, to inspect, copy, test or sample items within Rules 4003.1-4003.6 that are in the recipient’s possession, custody or control, one or more times. The requester may specify an ESI format, subject to objection; otherwise ESI ma","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4009.11. Request upon a party for production","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A document request may be served without leave of court on the plaintiff after the action begins and on any other party with or after service of original process. It must list items in numbered paragraphs, each seeking a single item or category described with reasonable particularity, with space for the answer after each.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4009.12. Answer to request upon a party for production","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Within 30 days after service, the responding party must answer each paragraph (including objections) and produce the unobjected-to items, or, where finding them requires reviewing a larger group with equal burden either way, let the requester review that group. The answer must identify what is produced, identify with reasonable particularity anything withheld as outside the scope of discovery and the basis, object on","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4009.21. Subpoena upon a non-party for production; prior notice; objections","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A party seeking documents from a non-party must give every other party written notice, with a copy of the proposed subpoena, at least 20 days before serving it (the non-party is not notified). Any party may file and serve written objections; if objections arrive before service, the subpoena may not be served until the court rules on a motion, and if none arrive, it may be served subject to any party’s or interested p","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4009.22. Service of subpoena","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The subpoena may be served only if it is identical to the one attached to the notice and the party has filed a certificate that the notice was mailed or delivered to each party at least 20 days before service, that a copy is attached, that no objection was received, and that the subpoena is identical. The subpoena is issued by the prothonotary and served under Rule 234.2; the official note says the 20-day period may ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4009.23. Certificate of compliance by a non-party; notice of documents received","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A subpoenaed non-party must deliver the documents or things with a signed certificate of compliance to the serving party within 20 days of service, and a blank certificate must be served with the subpoena. The receiving party must notify all other parties and, on payment of reasonable cost, provide legible copies or reasonable access to things on request.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4009.24-4009.27. Forms for non-party document subpoenas","Pennsylvania · civil rules","These rules give the forms for the notice of intent to serve a subpoena (stating the 20-day objection period), objections to a subpoena, the certificate prerequisite to service, the subpoena itself (ordering production within 20 days after service and noting the right to seek reasonable copying costs in advance), and the non-party’s certificate of compliance.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4009.31. Entry upon property for inspection; general provisions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A party may request entry onto a party’s property under Rule 4009.32, or move for entry onto a non-party’s property under Rule 4009.33, to inspect, measure, survey, photograph, test or sample the property or objects or operations on it, within Rules 4003.1-4003.6.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4009.32. Request for entry upon property of a party","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The request may be served without leave on the plaintiff after the case begins and on other parties with or after original process, and must describe the property and activities with reasonable particularity. The responding party must allow entry unless it objects with reasons within 30 days after service; the requester may seek a Rule 4019(a) order on objections or noncompliance, and entry may occur more than once t","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4009.33. Motion for entry upon property of a non-party","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Entry onto a non-party’s land requires a motion beginning with the prescribed notice, describing the property and activities, personally served by an adult like original process and served on all parties. If the non-party does not affirmatively consent, the motion may be presented to the court on at least 15 days’ notice to the non-party, and the court may grant or deny entry or set a hearing, specifying reasonable t","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4010. Physical and mental examination of persons","Pennsylvania · civil rules","When a party’s (or a person in a party’s custody or legal control’s) mental or physical condition is in controversy, the court may, on motion for good cause and notice, order an examination by a licensed physician, dentist or psychologist, specifying time, place, manner, conditions, scope and examiner. The person examined may have counsel or a representative present, questioning is limited to the scope of the exam, a","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4010.1. Evaluation of earning capacity","Pennsylvania · civil rules","When a party’s earning capacity is in controversy, the court may order an evaluation by a suitably licensed or certified evaluator, subject to the procedures of Rule 4010(a)(3)-(b)(3). The evaluator may testify only on damages, not liability.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4011. Limitation of scope of discovery","Pennsylvania · civil rules","No discovery, including ESI discovery, is allowed if it is sought in bad faith, would cause unreasonable annoyance, embarrassment, oppression, burden or expense to anyone, is beyond the scope of Rules 4003.1-4003.6, is barred by law protecting mediation communications and documents (42 Pa.C.S. § 5949), or would require an unreasonable investigation by the deponent or any party or witness.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4012. Protective orders","Pennsylvania · civil rules","On motion and good cause, the court may protect a party or person from unreasonable annoyance, embarrassment, oppression, burden or expense, for example by barring discovery, setting terms, time and place, requiring a different method, excluding topics, limiting scope or attendance, sealing depositions, or protecting trade secrets and confidential commercial information; if the motion is denied, the court may order d","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4013. Stay of proceedings","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Filing a protective-order motion does not stay the challenged discovery unless the court orders, but the court may for good cause stay any or all proceedings until the motion is decided.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4014. Request for admission","Pennsylvania · civil rules","A party may serve written requests to admit the truth of matters within Rules 4003.1-4003.5, including facts, opinions of fact, application of law to fact, and the genuineness of documents (with copies served unless already available), on the plaintiff after the action begins and on others with or after original process. Each matter is admitted unless a verified answer or signed objection is served within 30 days aft","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4015. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Pennsylvania · civil rules","In the United States and its territories, depositions are taken before an officer authorized to administer oaths under federal, Pennsylvania or local law, or a court-appointed person. Abroad, they may be taken on notice before a person authorized locally, before a court-commissioned person, or by letter rogatory, and letter-rogatory evidence is not excluded merely for being non-verbatim or unsworn. No deposition may ","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4016. Taking of depositions; objections","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Objections to the officer’s qualification are waived unless made at the start or as soon as the problem is or should have been discovered. Objections to competency, relevancy or materiality are preserved for trial unless the ground could have been cured if raised at the deposition, while errors in manner, form, oath or conduct that could have been cured are waived without timely objection, and notice defects are waiv","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4017. Transcript of testimony; objections","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The officer swears the witness and records the testimony, which is transcribed; objections to the transcript are waived unless filed promptly once known. The witness reads and signs (noting changes and reasons) unless waived or the witness is ill, missing or refuses; if not signed within 30 days of submission, the officer signs, states why, and the deposition may be used as if signed unless the court rules otherwise.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4017.1. Video depositions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Any oral deposition may be videotaped as of course, with optional simultaneous stenographic recording, but it may be used in court only with a transcript. The notice or subpoena must state it is a video deposition and list the deponent, officer, whether there will be a stenographic record, and the operator and employer (who may be the deposing attorney’s employee); the operator makes on-camera announcements, a digita","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4018. Rescinded","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Rule 4018 is rescinded; see Rule 234.1 et seq. for subpoenas.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4019. Sanctions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","On motion, the court may act when a party fails to answer interrogatories, an organization fails to designate a deponent, a deponent fails to appear or a witness is induced not to, a court order is disobeyed, document inspection is refused, or discovery is otherwise not made; failure is not excused because the discovery was objectionable unless an objection or protective-order motion was filed. Sanctions include deem","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4020. Use of depositions at trial","Pennsylvania · civil rules","At trial, an admissible deposition may be used against any party present, represented or properly noticed: by any party to contradict or impeach, or as the Rules of Evidence allow; by an adverse party for any purpose if the deponent was a party or its officer, director, managing agent or designee; and by any party for any purpose if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or outside Pennsylvania (unless procure","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4023. Acts of Assembly not suspended","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The rules do not affect 42 Pa.C.S. § 5325 on depositions outside Pennsylvania, or statutes on shareholder inspection of corporate records (15 Pa.C.S. § 1508), examinations in insolvency proceedings, election contests, and appeals from registration commissions.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4024. Effective date; pending actions","Pennsylvania · civil rules","The November 20, 1978 amendments, effective April 15, 1979, applied to all actions pending on April 15, 1979.","#st-pa-rules"],["Rule 4025. Abolition of practice under repealed statutes","Pennsylvania · civil rules","Deposition and discovery procedures from statutes repealed by the Judiciary Act Repealer Act (Act of April 28, 1978, No. 53) are abolished and do not continue as common law.","#st-pa-rules"],["Appeals in Pennsylvania: three appellate courts, 30-day notices filed in the trial court, and waiver everywhere","Pennsylvania · appeals","Pennsylvania has two statewide intermediate appellate courts. The Superior Court hears most civil and criminal appeals from the courts of common pleas. The Commonwealth Court hears appeals involving government parties and agencies and some original actions against the Commonwealth. The Supreme Court mostly reviews their decisions by discretionary petition for allowance of appeal (allocatur). Which intermediate court ","#st-pa-appeals"],["Final orders","Pennsylvania · appeals · What can be appealed","An appeal lies as of right from a final order. A final order disposes of all claims and all parties, or is certified final under Rule 341(c). There is no federal-style collateral finality for an order resolving only some parties: an order adjudicating fewer than all claims or parties is not final without a Rule 341(c) determination. Pa.R.A.P. 341(a)-(b)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Partial final orders: Rule 341(c) determination","Pennsylvania · appeals · What can be appealed","With multiple claims or parties, the trial court may enter a final order as to fewer than all of them only on an express determination that an immediate appeal would facilitate resolution of the entire case. An application for that determination must be filed within 30 days of entry of the order, the action is stayed while it is pending, and it is deemed denied if the court does not act within 30 days after filing. A","#st-pa-appeals"],["Interlocutory appeals as of right","Pennsylvania · appeals · What can be appealed","Rule 311 lists orders appealable immediately without a Rule 341(c) determination, including: refusing to open, vacate or strike off a judgment; orders on attachments, receiverships and similar matters affecting possession of property; injunction orders (with exceptions for some orders after trial but before the final order); peremptory judgment in mandamus; an order granting a new trial in a civil case; an order dire","#st-pa-appeals"],["Orders sustaining venue or personal jurisdiction","Pennsylvania · appeals · What can be appealed","An order sustaining venue or personal or in rem jurisdiction is appealable as of right only if (1) the party benefiting from the order files an election within 10 days after its entry that the order be deemed final, or (2) the court states in the order that a substantial issue of venue or jurisdiction is presented. After an election, the notice of appeal is due within 30 days after the election is filed. Pa.R.A.P. 31","#st-pa-appeals"],["Collateral orders","Pennsylvania · appeals · What can be appealed","An order separable from and collateral to the main cause of action is appealable as of right if the right involved is too important to be denied review and the claim would be irreparably lost if review waited for final judgment. Appeal it by notice of appeal; the comment says the rule codifies existing case law. Pa.R.A.P. 313","#st-pa-appeals"],["Interlocutory appeals by permission","Pennsylvania · appeals · What can be appealed","For other interlocutory orders, ask the trial court to amend the order to include the statement in 42 Pa.C.S. § 702(b) (a controlling question of law with substantial ground for difference of opinion, where an immediate appeal may materially advance termination). The application is due in the trial court within 30 days after entry of the order and is deemed denied if not acted on within 30 days after filing. Then fil","#st-pa-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP immunity orders","Pennsylvania · appeals · What can be appealed","By statute, an order granting, denying or otherwise determining protected public expression immunity under Pennsylvania’s Uniform Public Expression Protection Act is immediately appealable under 42 Pa.C.S. § 702. The collected appellate rules contain no rule implementing this. Treat it as an appeal to file within the ordinary 30 days and confirm the procedure with the appellate prothonotary. 42 Pa.C.S. § 8340.17","#st-pa-appeals"],["One post-trial motion within 10 days, or the issues are waived","Pennsylvania · appeals · Preserving issues in the trial court (civil)","After a civil trial (jury or non-jury), all requests for a new trial, judgment notwithstanding the verdict, removal of a nonsuit, or other changes go in a single Motion for Post-Trial Relief. It is due within 10 days after the verdict, discharge of a deadlocked jury, or nonsuit in a jury trial, or after notice of nonsuit or filing of the decision in a non-jury trial. Another party may file its own motion within 10 da","#st-pa-appeals"],["Appeal from the judgment, not from the order denying post-trial motions","Pennsylvania · appeals · Preserving issues in the trial court (civil)","The prothonotary enters judgment on praecipe when no timely post-trial motion is filed, or when timely motions are filed and the court does not dispose of all of them within 120 days after the first motion is filed. A judgment entered after the 120 days is final as to all parties and issues and is not subject to reconsideration. Enter judgment by praecipe so there is an appealable final order. Pa.R.Civ.P. 227.4(1)-(2","#st-pa-appeals"],["Date of entry means the Rule 236 notice date","Pennsylvania · appeals · Preserving issues in the trial court (civil)","For civil orders, the date of entry that starts the appeal clock is the day the clerk notes on the docket that written notice of entry was given under Pa.R.Civ.P. 236(b). An order must be entered on the docket, and on a separate document, before it is appealable. A notice of appeal filed after a decision is announced but before an appealable order is entered is treated as filed on the day of entry. Pa.R.A.P. 108(b), ","#st-pa-appeals"],["A reconsideration motion does not stop the appeal clock","Pennsylvania · appeals · Preserving issues in the trial court (civil)","Filing a motion for reconsideration does not toll the 30 days. The trial court may grant reconsideration after an appeal is filed only if the motion is timely and an order expressly granting reconsideration is filed within the time to appeal. Only then is the notice of appeal inoperative and the 30 days start over after the decision on reconsideration. If the court does not expressly grant reconsideration within 30 d","#st-pa-appeals"],["Where and when to file","Pennsylvania · appeals · Notice of appeal","File the notice of appeal with the clerk of the trial court (not the appellate court) within 30 days after entry of the order. Missing the deadline makes the appeal invalid. Other defects do not invalidate the appeal but may lead to remand or other action. A notice mistakenly filed in an appellate court or another wrong office is transferred and treated as filed on the original date once an additional filing fee is p","#st-pa-appeals"],["One notice per docket","Pennsylvania · appeals · Notice of appeal","A notice of appeal must be filed in each docket in which the order was entered. A single notice is allowed for completely consolidated civil cases if the consolidation order is attached. Pa.R.A.P. 902(a), 904(h)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Contents and attachments","Pennsylvania · appeals · Notice of appeal","Use the form in Rule 904. The caption lists all parties of record in the trial court on the date of appeal, and the notice must state that the order was entered on the docket and attach a copy of the docket entry. Attach the transcript request or counsel’s statement that there is no verbatim record or that the transcript is already of record. File two copies with proof of service and pay the fees, including the appel","#st-pa-appeals"],["Cross-appeals","Pennsylvania · appeals · Notice of appeal","Once a timely notice of appeal is filed, any other party may file a notice of appeal within 14 days after the first notice was served, or within the original 30 days, whichever is later. Identify it as a cross-appeal. Pa.R.A.P. 903(b) & cmt.","#st-pa-appeals"],["Shorter periods","Pennsylvania · appeals · Notice of appeal","Appeals from orders in Election Code matters, Local Government Unit Debt Act matters, and orders changing criminal venue or venire are due within 10 days. The Supreme Court has also temporarily shortened Election Code appeal periods by order for particular elections (for example, to 3 days for the November 5, 2024 general election). Pa.R.A.P. 903(c)(1); Order No. 622 Judicial Administration Docket (Aug. 27, 2024), 54","#st-pa-appeals"],["Direct appeals to the Supreme Court: jurisdictional statement","Pennsylvania · appeals · Notice of appeal","When the appeal goes directly to the Supreme Court, the appellant also files a jurisdictional statement (original and 8 copies) with the trial court clerk. An adverse party may answer within 14 days after service. Pa.R.A.P. 909(a)-(b)","#st-pa-appeals"],["The trial judge writes an opinion after the appeal","Pennsylvania · appeals · Rule 1925: the concise statement","After a notice of appeal, the trial judge files an opinion giving the reasons for the order, unless the reasons already appear of record. Pa.R.A.P. 1925(a)(1)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Statement of errors complained of on appeal","Pennsylvania · appeals · Rule 1925: the concise statement","The judge may order the appellant to file of record and serve on the judge a concise Statement of errors. The order must allow at least 21 days from its entry on the docket. Extensions are available for good cause, including a transcript delay not caused by the appellant. A motion to extend because an ordered transcript has not arrived is deemed granted if filed at least 5 days before the due date and not ruled on by","#st-pa-appeals"],["Issues not in the Statement are waived","Pennsylvania · appeals · Rule 1925: the concise statement","The Statement lists only errors the appellant intends to raise, each with enough detail to identify the issue. Each error includes every subsidiary issue raised in the trial court. Issues not included, or not raised in the required way, are waived. If the basis for a civil ruling cannot be discerned, explain that at the start of the Statement so its generality is not held against you. Both filing of record and servic","#st-pa-appeals"],["Limited rescue by remand","Pennsylvania · appeals · Rule 1925: the concise statement","In a civil case, an appellate court may remand for a Statement nunc pro tunc, or for amendment, only on application and good cause shown. Do not count on it. Pa.R.A.P. 1925(c)(2)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Transcript and record","Pennsylvania · appeals · Record and briefing","The appellant must request and pay for the transcript under Pa.R.J.A. 4001 et seq. The record is due in the appellate court within 60 days after the notice of appeal, unless that time is extended. Pa.R.A.P. 1911(a), 1931(a)(1)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Briefing schedule","Pennsylvania · appeals · Record and briefing","Appellant’s brief: 40 days after the record is filed, unless the prothonotary sets another date. Appellee’s brief: 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief and reproduced record. Reply brief: 14 days after service of the preceding brief, and received at least 3 days before argument. In cross-appeals, the designated appellant’s second brief is due 30 days after service of the designated appellee’s brief. Briefs ","#st-pa-appeals"],["Reproduced record","Pennsylvania · appeals · Record and briefing","Unless proceeding on a deferred basis, the appellant serves and files a designation of the parts of the record to reproduce, with a short statement of issues, at least 30 days before the brief is due. The appellee may counter-designate within 10 days. Pa.R.A.P. 2154(a)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Word limits","Pennsylvania · appeals · Record and briefing","Principal brief: 14,000 words. Reply brief: 7,000 words. In a cross-appeal, the designated appellee’s brief and the designated appellant’s second brief: 16,500 words each. A certificate of compliance is required if a principal brief exceeds 30 pages or a reply exceeds 15 pages (35 pages for the cross-appeal briefs). Covers, tables, proof of service and addenda do not count. The court may reduce word counts by rule or","#st-pa-appeals"],["Statement of questions involved","Pennsylvania · appeals · Record and briefing","No question will be considered unless it is stated in the statement of questions involved or fairly suggested by it. After each question, state whether the lower court agreed, disagreed, or did not answer it. Pa.R.A.P. 2116(a)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Late briefs","Pennsylvania · appeals · Record and briefing","If the appellant misses the deadline for the brief, reproduced record or designation, the appellee may move to dismiss. A late appellee may not be heard at argument without permission. Pa.R.A.P. 2188","#st-pa-appeals"],["Extensions","Pennsylvania · appeals · Record and briefing","An appellate court may extend most deadlines for good cause. It may not extend the time to file a notice of appeal, petition for allowance of appeal, petition for permission to appeal, petition for review, or petition for specialized review. Pa.R.A.P. 105(b)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Effect of appeal","Pennsylvania · appeals · Stays and supersedeas","After an appeal, the trial court generally may not proceed further. It may still preserve the status quo, correct formal errors, handle the record, grant supersedeas, enforce orders not superseded, and grant reconsideration under Rule 1701(b)(3). Pa.R.A.P. 1701(a)-(b)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Automatic supersedeas for money judgments: 120%","Pennsylvania · appeals · Stays and supersedeas","An appeal from an order solely for the payment of money operates as a supersedeas once appropriate security of 120% of the unpaid amount is filed with the clerk of the lower court. For installment awards, the amount counted is what is payable within 18 months after entry. Domestic relations money orders need an order and security set by the trial court. Pa.R.A.P. 1731(a)-(b)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Reargument","Pennsylvania · appeals · After decision: reargument and Supreme Court review","An application for reargument is due within 14 days after entry of the judgment or order (7 days in children’s fast track appeals) and is limited to 3,000 words. Reargument is discretionary, allowed only for compelling reasons, and not permitted from intermediate appellate final orders under the Election Code or the Local Government Unit Debt Act. Pa.R.A.P. 2542(a), 2543, 2544(c)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Petition for allowance of appeal (allocatur)","Pennsylvania · appeals · After decision: reargument and Supreme Court review","Supreme Court review of the Superior or Commonwealth Court is discretionary. Petition within 30 days after entry of the order, with a limit of 9,000 words (20 pages is deemed compliant). A timely reargument application by any party restarts the 30 days from the order disposing of it. The intermediate court has 60 days to act on reargument (45 days in children’s fast track appeals), after which it is deemed denied. A ","#st-pa-appeals"],["Grounds for allowance","Pennsylvania · appeals · After decision: reargument and Supreme Court review","Allowance is not a matter of right. Listed grounds include: a conflict between intermediate appellate decisions or with the Pennsylvania or U.S. Supreme Court; a question of first impression; substantial public importance; the constitutionality of a Pennsylvania statute; a departure from accepted judicial practice that calls for supervisory review; and an erroneous order quashing or dismissing an appeal. Pa.R.A.P. 11","#st-pa-appeals"],["Appeals as of right from the Commonwealth Court","Pennsylvania · appeals · After decision: reargument and Supreme Court review","Rule 1101 provides appeals as of right to the Supreme Court from certain Commonwealth Court final orders, mainly in matters that began in the Commonwealth Court. Otherwise, Superior and Commonwealth Court final orders are reviewable only by allowance. Pa.R.A.P. 341(d), 1101","#st-pa-appeals"],["Petition for review: 30 days","Pennsylvania · appeals · Agency review","Review of a quasi-judicial (agency) order, an arbitration award appealable under 42 Pa.C.S. § 763(b), or other orders reviewable by law is by petition for review filed with the appellate prothonotary within 30 days after entry of the order. Cross-petitions are due within 14 days of service of the first petition. Some statutes set 15-day periods (for example, certain Department of Community and Economic Development an","#st-pa-appeals"],["PACFile","Pennsylvania · appeals · Filing, e-filing and confidential information","Appellate e-filing is through PACFile, governed by Supreme Court administrative orders. In expedited election matters, the Court has required filings through PACFile whenever counsel has an account. The rule text itself is still labeled interim, so check the current administrative orders for when e-filing is mandatory. Pa.R.A.P. 125 & cmt.; Order No. 622 J.A. Docket (2024)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Public Access Policy certification","Pennsylvania · appeals · Filing, e-filing and confidential information","Every appellate filing by an attorney or unrepresented party must include a certification of compliance with the Case Records Public Access Policy, plus a Confidential Information Form or Confidential Document Form where needed. Information such as Social Security and financial account numbers, driver license numbers, and minors’ names and birth dates goes only on the Confidential Information Form. Records sealed bel","#st-pa-appeals"],["Mailbox rule for some filings","Pennsylvania · appeals · Filing, e-filing and confidential information","Petitions for permission to appeal, applications for reargument and briefs sent by first class, express or priority U.S. mail are treated as filed on the mailing date. For petitions and reargument, that date must be shown on a USPS Form 3817 or similar USPS receipt. The notice of appeal is filed in the trial court and is dated when the clerk receives it. Pa.R.A.P. 1311(b), 2542(b), 2185(a)(1), 905(a)(3)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Notice of appeal from a final civil order (filed in the trial court)","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order (civil: the date the clerk notes Rule 236 notice on the docket).\nCannot be extended (Pa.R.A.P. 105(b)); late filing invalidates the appeal (Pa.R.A.P. 902(b)(2)). Pa.R.A.P. 903(a), 108(b)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Cross-appeal","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the first notice of appeal (or the original 30 days, whichever is later).\nIdentify it as a cross-appeal. Pa.R.A.P. 903(b)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Appeal in Election Code, Local Government Unit Debt Act, or criminal venue/venire matters","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of the order.\nThe Supreme Court has shortened Election Code periods by order for specific elections. Pa.R.A.P. 903(c)(1)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Election that an order sustaining venue or jurisdiction is final","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of the order.\nFiled by the party benefiting from the order. The notice of appeal is then due 30 days after the election (Pa.R.A.P. 903(c)(2)). Pa.R.A.P. 311(b)(1)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Motion for post-trial relief (civil)","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from verdict, discharge of jury, or nonsuit (jury); notice of nonsuit or filing of decision (non-jury).\nOther parties: 10 days after the first motion is filed. Issues not raised are waived. Pa.R.Civ.P. 227.1(c)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Judgment on praecipe if post-trial motions are undecided","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","120 days from filing of the first post-trial motion.\nA judgment entered this way is final and not subject to reconsideration. Pa.R.Civ.P. 227.4(1)(b)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Application for a Rule 341(c) determination of finality","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order.\nDeemed denied if the trial court does not act within 30 days of filing. Notice of appeal is due within 30 days of the amended order. Pa.R.A.P. 341(c)(1)-(2)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Application to amend an interlocutory order to add § 702(b) certification","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the interlocutory order.\nDeemed denied after 30 days without action. Pa.R.A.P. 1311(b)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Petition for permission to appeal (interlocutory)","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the amended (certified) order, or the denial or deemed denial of certification.\nFiled with the appellate prothonotary. Cannot be extended (Pa.R.A.P. 105(b)). Pa.R.A.P. 1311(b)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Rule 1925(b) Statement of errors","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from entry on the docket of the trial judge’s order (minimum; the order sets the actual number of days).\nIssues omitted are waived. Must be filed of record and served on the judge. Pa.R.A.P. 1925(b)(2)(i), (b)(3)(i)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Transmission of record to appellate court","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nExtended if a Rule 1925(b)(2) extension is granted. Pa.R.A.P. 1931(a)(1)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Designation of reproduced record","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from before the appellant’s brief is due.\nAppellee may counter-designate within 10 days after receiving it. Pa.R.A.P. 2154(a)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from filing of the record (unless the prothonotary fixes another date).\nChildren’s fast track appeals: 30 days. Pa.R.A.P. 2185(a)(1)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the appellant’s brief and reproduced record.\nChildren’s fast track appeals: 21 days. Pa.R.A.P. 2185(a)(1)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Reply brief","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the preceding brief.\nMust also be received at least 3 days before argument. Pa.R.A.P. 2185(a)(1)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Application for reargument","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of the appellate judgment or order.\nChildren’s fast track: 7 days. Limit 3,000 words. Pa.R.A.P. 2542(a)(1)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Petition for allowance of appeal to the Supreme Court","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the Superior or Commonwealth Court order (or of the order disposing of a timely reargument application).\nCannot be extended (Pa.R.A.P. 105(b)). Limit 9,000 words. Pa.R.A.P. 1113(a)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Cross-petition for allowance of appeal","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the first petition.\nOr within the original 30 days, whichever is later. Pa.R.A.P. 1113(b)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Answer to petition for allowance of appeal","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the petition.\nLimit 9,000 words. Pa.R.A.P. 1116(a)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Answer to jurisdictional statement (direct Supreme Court appeal)","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the jurisdictional statement. Pa.R.A.P. 909(b)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Petition for review of agency (quasi-judicial) order","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order.\nSome statutes set 15 days (Pa.R.A.P. 1512(b)). Cannot be extended. Pa.R.A.P. 1512(a)(1)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Cross-petition for review","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the first petition for review.\nOr within the original 30 days, whichever is later. Pa.R.A.P. 1512(a)(2)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP special motion (UPEPA)","Pennsylvania · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from service of the pleading asserting the claim.\nExtendable for good cause. Effective date of § 8340.16 depends on a Pa. Bulletin notice under Act 72 of 2024 § 3(4); confirm before relying on it. 42 Pa.C.S. § 8340.16(b)","#st-pa-appeals"],["Tribunal Supremo de Puerto Rico (Supreme Court)","Puerto Rico · courts","Court of last resort. Also controls admission to the bar, courtesy (pro hac vice) admission, notaries, and lawyer discipline, and answers certified questions of Puerto Rico law from federal and state courts.","#st-pr"],["Tribunal de Apelaciones (Court of Appeals)","Puerto Rico · courts","Sits in San Juan in panels. Hears appeals of right from final trial judgments, discretionary certiorari from trial-court orders, and petitions to review final agency decisions.","#st-pr"],["Tribunal de Primera Instancia, Superior Division (Sección Superior)","Puerto Rico · courts","The single, island-wide general-jurisdiction trial court for civil and criminal cases, organized by judicial region.","#st-pr"],["Tribunal de Primera Instancia, Municipal Division (Sección Municipal)","Puerto Rico · courts","Part of the same trial court. Municipal judges handle limited matters assigned by law, such as smaller civil claims, warrants, and initial probable-cause determinations.","#st-pr"],["Status not confirmed from the collected texts","Puerto Rico · anti-SLAPP","The collected Puerto Rico court texts (civil and criminal procedure, evidence, court rules, ethics rules, e-filing guidelines) contain no anti-SLAPP procedure: no special motion to dismiss, no discovery stay, and no fee-shifting rule tied to speech or petitioning. An anti-SLAPP law, if one exists, would be a statute outside these rules. Before telling a client anything, search the Leyes de Puerto Rico Anotadas (LPRA)","#st-pr-slapp"],["Pro hac vice is called ‘admisión por cortesía’ and goes to the Supreme Court","Puerto Rico · out of state","A lawyer admitted in any U.S. jurisdiction may ask the Tribunal Supremo (not the trial judge) for courtesy admission to appear in a specific case before the courts, administrative agencies, or ADR proceedings, including arbitration. File the official Solicitud de Admisión por Cortesía form with the Supreme Court Clerk (Secretaría).\nThe Clerk grants a properly filed application unless the volume suggests regular pract","#st-pr-oos"],["What the application must include","Puerto Rico · out of state","Certificate of good standing from the highest court of every U.S. jurisdiction where the applicant is admitted, issued within 90 days before filing. The applicant must not be disbarred or suspended anywhere.\nDisclosures: any disciplinary proceeding opened in the last 5 years; any discipline or suspension ever; any courtesy admission denied by a U.S. jurisdiction in the last 5 years (with reasons); and every Puerto Ri","#st-pr-oos"],["Local counsel: the endorsing Puerto Rico lawyer does real work","Puerto Rico · out of state","A lawyer admitted by the Puerto Rico Supreme Court, in good standing there, must endorse and co-sign the application. That lawyer must:\n(A) appear as counsel of record with the applicant;\n(B) sign every pleading, motion, and document in the case;\n(C) receive service of everything served on the applicant; and\n(D) accompany the applicant at every appearance, deposition, or other proceeding unless the forum excuses it.\n","#st-pr-oos"],["Courtesy admittees cannot e-file","Puerto Rico · out of state","Lawyers admitted by courtesy cannot file through SUMAC (the mandatory e-filing system) until the judiciary implements the technology. They must file on paper at the Clerk’s office of the court with the case or in the courthouse drop box (buzón de presentaciones), during regular hours (8:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m.). In practice the endorsing Puerto Rico lawyer, who must sign everything anyway, is the one with SUMAC access. ","#st-pr-oos"],["Work that does not need courtesy admission","Puerto Rico · out of state","No courtesy admission is needed only to: assist proceedings in a jurisdiction where the lawyer is admitted; consult with a Puerto Rico lawyer about that lawyer’s client’s case; consult, at the person’s request, with someone in Puerto Rico about hiring the lawyer; prepare a Puerto Rico case while reasonably believing courtesy admission will be granted; prepare a case to be filed elsewhere; or advise a Puerto Rico clie","#st-pr-oos"],["Language of filings and proceedings","Puerto Rico · out of state","Civil pleadings, motions, and applications may be filed in Spanish or English. Translating English filings is not required, but the court must order a translation when justice requires it, when it is indispensable to decide the case fairly, or when any party asks.\nThe rules otherwise assume Spanish as the working language: a witness who does not know Spanish testifies through a sworn interpreter, and the Supreme Cour","#st-pr-oos"],["Bar admission: no admission on motion","Puerto Rico · out of state","Every applicant must pass Puerto Rico’s own bar exam (Reválida General); there is no reciprocity route in the collected rules. Requirements: at least 21 years old; a law degree from a school approved by the ABA or the Puerto Rico Supreme Court; a bachelor’s degree before law school (for U.S. and Puerto Rico graduates); good reputation; and passing the exam.\nThe exam is written in Spanish and may be answered in Spanis","#st-pr-oos"],["Continuing legal education","Puerto Rico · out of state","Active Puerto Rico lawyers need 24 CLE hours every 3 years, at least 4 in professional ethics. Active notaries must take at least 9 of the 24 in notarial law. Lawyers admitted only by courtesy are outside the CLE program’s definition of covered professionals.\nSeparately, a one-time 6-hour course on the new Rules of Professional Conduct is due by December 31, 2026 (see Ethics).\nCurrency note: the CLE Reglamento text i","#st-pr-oos"],["Notary practice is separate","Puerto Rico · out of state","Puerto Rico notaries are lawyers with a separate license. A notary must be admitted to the Puerto Rico bar and also pass a separate notarial exam (Reválida Notarial), then apply to the Supreme Court Clerk with the bond required by law. No one may act as a notary in Puerto Rico without Supreme Court authorization, and courtesy admission authorizes appearing as a lawyer in one case only. Reglamento TS R. 12(f)(1), 13(a","#st-pr-oos"],["Domesticating an out-of-state judgment (exequátur)","Puerto Rico · out of state","There is no simple registration procedure in the civil rules. Recognition goes through exequátur in the Tribunal de Primera Instancia, either by complaint against everyone affected by the judgment or by a sworn ex parte petition signed by all of them.\nAttach a certified, complete copy of the judgment. A Spanish translation is required only if the judgment is in neither Spanish nor English.\nFor a judgment from a U.S. ","#st-pr-oos"],["Governing code: Reglas de Conducta Profesional (effective January 1, 2026)","Puerto Rico · ethics","The Supreme Court adopted the Rules of Professional Conduct of Puerto Rico by Resolution ER-2025-02 (June 17, 2025), effective January 1, 2026. They use the ABA Model Rules as the starting point, with exceptions, and for the first time govern lawyers and notaries in one body of rules. Each rule has a comment that explains it but adds no obligations.\nThe Spanish text is the adopted version. The Supreme Court’s Bureau ","#st-pr-ethics"],["What it replaced, and which code governs older conduct","Puerto Rico · ethics","The new rules replace the Código de Ética Profesional of 1970, which was based mainly on the ABA’s 1908 Canons. The Cánones were repealed as of January 1, 2026, but disciplinary proceedings for violations before that date are still governed by the 1970 Cánones. For conduct spanning both periods, check which code applies to each act.\nNot yet in force: Rule 7.2(b)(2) on lawyer referral services takes effect only after ","#st-pr-ethics"],["Mandatory 6-hour CLE on the new rules, due December 31, 2026","Puerto Rico · ethics","Every lawyer and notary authorized by the Supreme Court, whether active or temporarily suspended, must complete at least 6 CLE hours mainly on the new rules by December 31, 2026, in courses approved by the CLE Program (PEJC). It is a one-time requirement. The hours count toward the 24-hour triennial minimum and the 4 ethics hours, but must be completed even if the ethics hours are already done. Report compliance to t","#st-pr-ethics"],["Difference from ABA Model Rules: nonlawyer ownership up to 49%","Puerto Rico · ethics","Unlike ABA Model Rule 5.4, Puerto Rico lets a lawyer practice in a firm partly owned by nonlawyers, if all conditions are met: nonlawyer ownership does not exceed 49%; the nonlawyer contributes only money and provides no services (not even marketing); a Puerto Rico lawyer runs the firm, represents the nonlawyer owner on votes, notifies the Supreme Court when the arrangement begins, and files a sworn annual statement ","#st-pr-ethics"],["Difference from ABA Model Rules: standalone technology and AI rule","Puerto Rico · ethics","Rule 1.19 is a separate rule on technological competence and diligence, covering both lawyers and notaries. The adopting resolution says the Court asked for it to cover technology, including artificial intelligence, and lists it among the rules that depart from the ABA Model Rules’ structure. The comment says diligence can be breached by failing to check an electronic notice, filing documents without validating conte","#st-pr-ethics"],["Other notable changes for practitioners","Puerto Rico · ethics","Contingent fee agreements must be in writing and signed by the client, with a written closing statement at the end. The adopting resolution calls this a substantial change from prior Puerto Rico law. Contingent fees remain barred in divorce, support, and community-property division matters and in criminal defense.\nThe new rules drop the 1970 Code’s general ‘appearance of impropriety’ standard (Canon 38) as a basis fo","#st-pr-ethics"],["Reconsideration: 15 days, two different clocks, and simultaneous service","Puerto Rico · land mine","From an interlocutory order or resolution: 15 days from notice, a strict-compliance term. From a judgment: 15 days from archivo en autos of the notice, a jurisdictional term that cannot be extended. The motion must be served on all parties within the same 15 days, and it must state with particularity the facts and law to reconsider. A motion that lacks that specificity is denied and does not toll the time to appeal. ","#st-pr-unique"],["Appeal and certiorari: 30 days, and some cannot be saved","Puerto Rico · land mine","Civil appeal to the Court of Appeals: 30 days from archivo en autos of the notice of judgment, jurisdictional. Certiorari from interlocutory trial orders: 30 days from notice, strict compliance, extendable only for special circumstances explained in the petition itself. Certiorari to the Supreme Court from Court of Appeals judgments in appeals: 30 days, jurisdictional. The trial court cannot extend the terms in Rules","#st-pr-unique"],["Court of Appeals: the fee payment date is the filing date","Puerto Rico · land mine","Uploading the appeal to SUMAC is not filing. The filing receipt warns that the document is not officially filed until the filing fees are paid, and the filing date is the fee payment date. It does not relate back to the upload. The 11:59 p.m. e-filing rule also does not apply to filings that require a fee. Pay on the platform the same day. If the trial case has no electronic record, also send the trial court clerk a ","#st-pr-unique"],["Serve the appellate brief on the other side within the filing period","Puerto Rico · land mine","The appellant must serve the appeal and appendices on the other parties within the time to file the appeal (strict compliance) and certify the date of service. E-filing serves lawyers, but pro se parties and others the law requires must be served separately. Reglamento TA R. 13(B)","#st-pr-unique"],["Summons must be served within 120 days, or the case is dismissed","Puerto Rico · land mine","Serve the summons within 120 days of filing the complaint (or of issuance of a summons by publication). If service is not completed in time, the court must dismiss without prejudice. A second dismissal for the same failure operates as an adjudication on the merits. Service cannot be done through SUMAC. Out-of-island defendants may be served personally, under the law of the place of service, by letters rogatory, by pu","#st-pr-unique"],["Nonresident plaintiffs must post a cost bond","Puerto Rico · land mine","When the claimant lives outside Puerto Rico or is a foreign corporation, the court must require a bond of at least $1,000 for costs, expenses, and attorney’s fees, and all proceedings are stayed until it is posted. If it is not posted within 60 days of the order, the court dismisses the case. Narrow exceptions apply: indigent litigants exempt from filing fees, co-owners suing over Puerto Rico property alongside a res","#st-pr-unique"],["Court of Appeals reconsideration: 15 days, no extensions","Puerto Rico · land mine","A motion to reconsider a Court of Appeals decision, civil or criminal, is due within 15 days of archivo en autos of the notice, and the term cannot be extended. A timely motion stops the clock to go to the Supreme Court, which restarts when notice of the ruling on the motion is filed. Reglamento TA R. 84(A); R. Proc. Civ. 52.2(f)","#st-pr-unique"],["Criminal appeals run from the date of sentencing","Puerto Rico · land mine","A criminal appeal is due within 30 days after the sentence is pronounced, not after notice. The term is jurisdictional. A timely new-trial or reconsideration motion restarts the 30 days from notice of the order resolving it. Certiorari from a conviction on a guilty plea is also 30 days from the sentence, jurisdictional. Reglamento TA R. 23(A), 32(A)","#st-pr-unique"],["Agency review: 30 days to the Court of Appeals","Puerto Rico · land mine","Judicial review of a final agency order or decision goes directly to the Court of Appeals within 30 days (jurisdictional) of archivo en autos of the notice, or of the restarted date after a timely agency reconsideration motion, unless a special law sets a different term. Reglamento TA R. 57","#st-pr-unique"],["Pro hac vice counsel cannot e-file and must bring local counsel everywhere","Puerto Rico · land mine","A courtesy-admitted lawyer has no SUMAC filing access and must file on paper. The endorsing Puerto Rico lawyer must sign every filing and attend every hearing and deposition unless excused. Budget for full local-counsel participation, not a name on the signature block. Reglamento TS R. 12(f)(2); Directrices SUMAC § V(3)","#st-pr-unique"],["Withdrawal needs leave of court","Puerto Rico · land mine","Counsel who has appeared must move in writing to withdraw, give reasons, provide the client’s phone and mailing address, and certify the client was notified. The court may refuse in exceptional cases if a party’s rights would be seriously harmed or the case unduly delayed. R. Proc. Civ. 9.2","#st-pr-unique"],["No peremptory challenge; sworn recusal motion within 20 days","Puerto Rico · judges","Puerto Rico has no peremptory change of judge. Recusal is for cause under Civil Procedure Rule 63.1, which lists grounds from bias and financial interest to a catch-all for anything that could reasonably cast doubt on impartiality.\nProcedure: a sworn motion filed with the challenged judge within 20 days after the party learns of the ground, stating specific facts with supporting documents and affidavits. If these for","#st-pr-unique"],["Recusal in the Court of Appeals","Puerto Rico · judges","A recusal motion must be written and sworn, state its facts, and be filed as soon as the grounds are known. If the challenged appellate judge declines to step aside, the administrative judge assigns the motion to a different panel for final decision. Reglamento TA R. 9(B)-(D)","#st-pr-unique"],["P.R. Const. art. II, § 4 | Broader than federal: not established here","Puerto Rico · speech","Article II, § 4 of the Puerto Rico Constitution bars laws abridging freedom of speech or press, peaceable assembly, or petitioning the government. The collected texts do not include the Constitution or case law, so whether the Puerto Rico Supreme Court reads it more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked. P.R. Const. art. II, § 4 (not in collected texts)","#st-pr-unique"],["Practical effect: reputation and privacy are also constitutional","Puerto Rico · speech","Article II, § 8 gives every person a constitutional right to legal protection against abusive attacks on honor, reputation, and private or family life. Defamation and privacy claims in Puerto Rico are therefore framed against a constitutional interest on the plaintiff’s side as well. Not checked against the collected texts. P.R. Const. art. II, § 8 (not in collected texts)","#st-pr-unique"],["In short","Puerto Rico · language","Spanish is the working language of Puerto Rico courts, but civil filings may be in English without translation unless the court orders one. ","#st-pr-unique"],["Civil filings: Spanish or English","Puerto Rico · language","Pleadings, motions, and applications may be filed in Spanish or English. A party who knows neither may file in their own language with copies in Spanish or English. Translating English filings is not required, but the court must order translation when justice requires it, when it is indispensable to a fair decision, or when any party asks. A party opposing an English-speaking litigant can therefore force translations","#st-pr-unique"],["Testimony and decisions in Spanish","Puerto Rico · language","A witness who does not know Spanish testifies through an interpreter the judge finds qualified, sworn to translate faithfully. The Supreme Court distributes its opinions and judgments in Spanish. No collected rule requires certified English translations of exhibits generally, but interpreter and translation needs should be planned early. R. Evid. 614; Reglamento TS R. 9(l)","#st-pr-unique"],["Supreme Court certified questions","Puerto Rico · language","Briefs in certified-question proceedings may be in English or Spanish without translation, but must be signed by a lawyer admitted by the Puerto Rico Supreme Court. The Court’s answering opinion is translated into English by its Bureau of Translations, with costs split equally among the parties unless the certifying court orders otherwise. Reglamento TS R. 25(h), (l)","#st-pr-unique"],["Foreign judgments","Puerto Rico · language","For exequátur, a faithful Spanish translation is required only when the judgment is in neither Spanish nor English. R. Proc. Civ. 55.3(b)","#st-pr-unique"],["Bar exam","Puerto Rico · language","The bar exam is written in Spanish and may be answered in Spanish or English. Reglamento para la Admisión de Aspirantes R. 5.2.1","#st-pr-unique"],["SUMAC e-filing is mandatory for Puerto Rico lawyers","Puerto Rico · unique rules","Lawyers admitted in Puerto Rico and registered in the Registro Único de Abogados (RUA) must file through SUMAC (Sistema Unificado de Manejo y Administración de Casos), using their RUA credentials. Filing in SUMAC is filing with the court, and it also serves the other lawyers and self-represented parties. An electronic signature counts as a handwritten one.\nSummonses and subpoenas cannot be served electronically; serv","#st-pr-unique"],["Interlocutory review is narrow","Puerto Rico · unique rules","The Court of Appeals may grant certiorari from an interlocutory trial order only for orders under Rules 56 (provisional remedies) and 57 (injunctions), or the denial of a dispositive motion. Exceptions: rulings on admitting essential fact or expert witnesses, evidentiary privileges, entries of default, family-relations cases, cases of public interest, or where waiting for appeal would be an irremediable failure of ju","#st-pr-unique"],["Government parties double the clock","Puerto Rico · unique rules","When the Commonwealth, its municipalities, their officials, or instrumentalities (but not public corporations) are parties, the answer is due in 60 days instead of 30, and that period cannot be extended. The appeal to the Court of Appeals, and certiorari to the Supreme Court from its appellate judgments, are due in 60 days (jurisdictional) instead of 30. This applies to every party in the case, not only the governmen","#st-pr-unique"],["Deadlines run from ‘archivo en autos’ of the notice","Puerto Rico · unique rules","Many post-judgment deadlines run from the date a copy of the notice of judgment is filed in the record (archivo en autos), not from receipt. For electronic notices, the date the platform sends the notice is the archivo date. If notice was mailed and the mailing date differs from the archivo date, the term runs from the mailing date. The extra 3 days for mail service do not apply to terms counted from archivo en autos","#st-pr-unique"],["Criminal procedure text is out of date","Puerto Rico · unique rules","The official compilation of the 1963 Rules of Criminal Procedure includes amendments only through February 2020. It still shows the pre-2022 text of Rule 10 (‘delitos graves de cuarto grado’), which Act 22-2022 amended to conform to the 2012 Penal Code. Verify any criminal rule against laws enacted after February 2020 before relying on it. R. Proc. Crim. 10 (34 LPRA Ap. II); Ley 22-2022","#st-pr-unique"],["Complex civil cases have their own rules","Puerto Rico · unique rules","Puerto Rico has separate Rules for Complex Civil Litigation Cases, which apply once a case is designated complex. The collected copy is certified current only to May 2016, so confirm the current text. Reglas para Casos Civiles de Litigación Compleja (ER-99-7, as amended by ER-2006-2)","#st-pr-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope of the rules","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Govern all civil proceedings before the General Court of Justice (Tribunal General de Justicia), and are to be construed to ease access to the courts and case management so as to secure a just, speedy, and economical resolution of every proceeding.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 2. Commencing an action","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A civil action begins by filing a complaint (demanda) in court.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 3. Jurisdiction, venue, and transfer","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 3.1–3.6, which cover the court’s jurisdiction, the proper courtroom (sala) for filing, and transfer between salas.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 3.1. Jurisdiction","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The General Court of Justice has jurisdiction over every matter arising within Puerto Rico and over domiciliaries and non-domiciliaries with contacts that make jurisdiction constitutionally permissible. It also hears voluntary-jurisdiction (ex parte) proceedings to record and perpetuate a fact that is not then in judicial controversy and cannot prejudice a specific person.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 3.2. Venue (competencia)","Puerto Rico · civil rules","File in the sala designated by law and these rules, but no case is dismissed for being filed in the wrong sala. A case stays in the filing sala only by agreement of the parties plus the presiding judge’s reasoned consent; otherwise the judge transfers it to the proper sala.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 3.3. Actions affecting real property","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Actions over title to or any right or interest in real property must be filed in the sala where the property, or part of it, is located.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 3.4. Venue where the claim arose","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Actions against the Commonwealth, against insurance or surety companies, and for damages are filed in the sala where the insured or bonded object is located or where the cause of action, or part of it, arose.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 3.5. Venue by residence of the parties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Otherwise, file where a defendant resides; wage claims go to the plaintiff’s residence and child-support (alimentos) cases to the minors’ residence. If no defendant resides in Puerto Rico or the residence is unknown, any sala of the Court of First Instance may be used; businesses and entities with offices or agents in several places may be sued where they have their principal office, operations center, or agent, or w","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 3.6. Transfer of actions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A defendant challenging venue must move to transfer within 30 days after notice of the complaint and summons, stating the supporting facts in detail unless they appear on the face of the complaint or record; if no opposition is filed within 10 days after notice of the motion, the case is transferred. Filing other motions or a responsive pleading within those 30 days does not waive the right to seek transfer, and the ","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 4. Summons (emplazamiento)","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 4.1–4.8 on issuing, serving, waiving, and proving service of the summons; Puerto Rico’s service regime (120 days under Rule 4.3(c), waiver under Rule 4.5, edicts under Rule 4.6) differs in important ways from federal Rule 4.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 4.1. Issuance","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The plaintiff files the summons form with the complaint for immediate issuance by the Clerk (Secretaría), who will issue individual or additional summonses against any defendants on request.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 4.2. Form of summons","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Signed by the Clerk, bearing the court’s name and seal, the sala, and the parties’ names; addressed to the defendant; and stating the name, postal address, telephone, fax, email, and Supreme Court attorney number of plaintiff’s counsel (or the plaintiff if pro se). It must state the time to appear and warn that a default judgment may be entered granting the relief requested or any other relief the court deems proper.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 4.3. Who may serve; 120-day service deadline","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Service may be made by a marshal (alguacil) or any literate person at least 18 years old who is not a party, a party’s attorney, a relative within the fourth degree of consanguinity or second of affinity, or interested in the case; defendants outside Puerto Rico may be served personally, as the law of the place of service allows, by letters rogatory, by edict under Rule 4.6, or as the court directs. Under Rule 4.3(c)","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 4.4. Personal service","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The summons and complaint are served together, and the server notes on the back of the summons copy their signature, date, place, manner, and recipient. The rule sets the method for adults (personally or to an authorized or statutory agent), minors under 14 (parent with patria potestad or guardian), minors 14 and over (the minor and a parent or guardian), adjudged incapacitated persons (the person and guardian), peop","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 4.5. Waiver of personal service","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Adults and entities notified of a suit have a duty to avoid service costs, and waiving service does not waive jurisdictional defenses or a venue transfer. The plaintiff’s written request must go by certified mail with return receipt and restricted delivery, include a filed-and-stamped copy of the complaint, explain the consequences, and allow 20 days from mailing to return the signed waiver (30 days if the defendant ","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 4.6. Service by edict (publication)","Puerto Rico · civil rules","On a sworn statement of the diligent efforts made, the court may order service by edict when the defendant is outside Puerto Rico, cannot be found in Puerto Rico, is hiding, or is a foreign corporation without a resident agent, and the affidavit or complaint shows a claim justifying relief; a failed-service return is not required. The edict is published once in a newspaper of general circulation in Puerto Rico, and w","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 4.7. Proof of service","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The server files proof within the time the defendant has to appear: a marshal’s certification or a private server’s sworn statement. Edict service is proved by the newspaper’s sworn statement, a copy of the published edict, and counsel’s certification of mailing (plus the defendant’s return receipt); service under Rule 4.3(b)(2) and (5) is proved by sworn statement or the judge’s order. Failure to file proof does not","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 4.8. Amendment of summons","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The court may at any time allow amendment of a summons or the proof of service on just terms, unless doing so would clearly and substantially prejudice the served party’s essential rights.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 5. Pleadings allowed","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 5.1–5.2.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 5.1. Pleadings","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The only pleadings are the complaint, counterclaim, crossclaim (demanda contra coparte), third-party complaint, and their answers; the court may order a reply to an answer or third-party answer.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 5.2. Action on stipulated facts","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Parties to an actual controversy may skip pleadings and submit a stipulation of facts with a sworn statement that a real controversy exists and the stipulation is made in good faith; if the court agrees a controversy exists, the rules then govern.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 6. General rules of pleading","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 6.1–6.6 on claims, denials, affirmative defenses, and extensions.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 6.1. Claims for relief","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A pleading seeking relief must contain a short and plain statement of the facts showing the pleader is entitled to relief and a demand for the relief sought; alternative relief or relief of different kinds may be requested.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 6.2. Defenses; form of denials","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The responding party must admit or deny each allegation and state its defenses with the supporting facts, and the court may order compliance. A party lacking knowledge must say so, investigate, and amend by the deadline set at the initial conference or by the pretrial conference date; allegations not later admitted or denied are deemed admitted. Denials must fairly meet the substance and affirmatively state the denyi","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 6.3. Affirmative defenses","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Listed defenses (settlement, accord and satisfaction, arbitration award, assumption of risk, negligence, discharge in bankruptcy, duress, estoppel, failure of consideration, fraud, illegality, laches, authorization, payment, release, res judicata, acquisitive or extinctive prescription, waiver, and any other excuse or affirmative defense) must be pleaded clearly, expressly, and specifically or are waived, unless the ","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 6.4. Effect of failure to deny","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Allegations, other than the amount of damages, in a pleading that requires a response are deemed admitted if not denied; allegations in a pleading that requires no response are deemed denied.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 6.5. Concise and direct pleading; inconsistency","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Each allegation must be simple, concise, and direct, with no technical forms required, and pleadings are construed to do justice; subject to Rule 9, a party may plead alternative or inconsistent claims or defenses.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 6.6. Extensions of time","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Every extension request must show good cause (justa causa) with concrete, well-supported explanations and must be filed before the deadline expires and in conformity with Rule 68.2. The extension runs from the day after the original deadline expires.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleading special matters","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 7.1–7.5.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 7.1. Capacity","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Capacity, representative authority, and legal existence need not be alleged, but a party contesting them must do so specifically, with the particulars within its knowledge.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 7.2. Fraud, mistake, or state of mind","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The circumstances of fraud or mistake must be pleaded in detail; malice, intent, knowledge, and other states of mind may be alleged generally.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 7.3. Time and place","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Allegations of time and place are material for testing the sufficiency of a pleading.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 7.4. Special damages","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Special damages must be itemized by category.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 7.5. Description of real property","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A pleading claiming a right in real property must describe it precisely enough to identify it.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 8. Form of pleadings and motions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 8.1–8.9 on captions, motions, filings to the record, language, and corporate disclosure.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 8.1. Caption","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Every pleading has a caption with the court and sala, the parties’ names, the filing number, and the nature or subject of the action; the complaint names all parties, later filings may name the first party on each side with a reference to others, and voluntary-jurisdiction filings put the petitioner’s full name above “Ex parte.”","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 8.2. Paragraphs; separate statements","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Allegations go in numbered paragraphs, each limited as far as practicable to one set of circumstances; claims founded on separate occurrences and defenses other than denials are stated separately when that promotes clarity.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 8.3. Adoption by reference; exhibits","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Statements may be adopted by reference within the same or another pleading or motion, and an exhibit attached to a pleading is part of it for all purposes.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 8.4. Motions; 20-day opposition","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A request for an order is made by motion, in writing unless made at a hearing, stating with particularity the legal grounds and arguments and the relief sought, with any needed documents or affidavits. Any opposition, with its supporting documents, is due within 20 days after notice of the motion; if none is filed in that time, the motion is deemed submitted. Motions are decided without a hearing unless the court set","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 8.5. Motions to continue or reschedule a hearing","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Pretrial motions to continue or reschedule a hearing must be written and state grounds; an oral request on the hearing day is allowed only for extraordinary, unforeseeable circumstances beyond the control of the parties and counsel. The movant must propose at least three new dates after confirming the other side has no conflict, and any stipulation to continue needs the presiding judge’s approval.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 8.6. Filings “to the judicial record”","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Parties may place on record matters that need no judicial attention at that stage by a filing titled “AL EXPEDIENTE JUDICIAL”; it is treated like a motion but is not referred to the judge, though the court may later take notice of its contents and filing date.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 8.7. Language","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Pleadings and motions must be in Spanish or English; filings that must be signed by someone who knows neither may be in that person’s language with Spanish or English copies. Translation of English documents is not required, but the court must order translation when justice requires, when indispensable to fair adjudication, or when a party requests it.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 8.8. Form of other papers","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Rules on captions, signatures, and form for pleadings apply to motions and other papers.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 8.9. Corporate disclosure statement","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Every nongovernmental corporation must file, at its first appearance, a certification identifying every parent, subsidiary, and affiliate corporation owning 10% or more of its stock, or stating there is none, and must promptly disclose any later change in writing.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 9. Legal representation","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 9.1–9.4 on signatures, appearance and withdrawal of counsel, attorney conduct, and self-representation.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 9.1. Signing and contents of filings; sanctions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Every filing by a represented party must be signed by at least one attorney of record with name, Puerto Rico Supreme Court attorney number, telephone, fax, postal address, and email as registered with the Supreme Court; counsel’s first filing must also give the client’s physical and postal address and telephone, and pro se parties sign and give their own contact information. Changes in contact information must be rep","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 9.2. Appearance and withdrawal of counsel","Puerto Rico · civil rules","An attorney taking on a pending case must file a notice motion with their Supreme Court number and contact information. To withdraw, counsel must file a written motion giving reasons, the client’s telephone and postal address, and a statement that the client was notified and the Code of Professional Ethics canons were met; the court may deny withdrawal in exceptional cases where a party’s rights would be seriously ha","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 9.3. Attorney conduct","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Appearing at a hearing, conference, or proceeding unprepared may be treated as conduct obstructing the administration of justice. Under its inherent power, the court may on its own or on motion impose monetary or other sanctions or disqualify an attorney for such conduct or for breaching duties to the court, clients, or colleagues.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 9.4. Self-representation","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Natural persons may represent themselves in ordinary civil cases if they are unrepresented, choose to do so voluntarily and knowingly (understanding they will be treated like represented parties), can adequately handle the case given its complexity, have the minimum knowledge to follow the rules and argue the law, and will not cause undue delay or disruption. The court must monitor these requirements throughout and m","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 10. Defenses and objections","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 10.1–10.8 on answer deadlines, pre-answer motions, judgment on the pleadings, more definite statement, striking, and waiver of defenses.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 10.1. Time to answer","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A defendant inside or outside Puerto Rico must serve the answer within 30 days after delivery of the summons and complaint, or after publication of the edict if served under Rule 4.6 (federal Rule 12 allows 21 days). Answers to crossclaims and replies to counterclaims are due within 10 days after notice. When the Commonwealth or municipalities, their officers, or instrumentalities (excluding public corporations) are ","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 10.2. How defenses are presented; motion to dismiss","Puerto Rico · civil rules","All defenses go in the responsive pleading, except that the pleader may raise by well-founded motion (1) lack of subject-matter jurisdiction, (2) lack of personal jurisdiction, (3) insufficient summons, (4) insufficient service of summons, (5) failure to state a claim that justifies relief, and (6) failure to join an indispensable party. Joining defenses does not waive any of them. If matters outside the pleading are","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 10.3. Judgment on the pleadings","Puerto Rico · civil rules","After the pleadings close, any party may move for partial or total judgment on the pleadings, subject to Rule 42.3; if matters outside the pleadings are presented and not excluded, the motion becomes a Rule 36 summary judgment motion.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 10.4. More definite statement","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A party may seek a more definite statement before responding to a pleading so vague or ambiguous that a response cannot reasonably be framed, pointing out the defects and the details wanted. If granted and not obeyed within 10 days after notice (or another period the court sets), the court may strike the pleading or enter another just order.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 10.5. Motion to strike","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The court may strike insufficient defenses or redundant, immaterial, impertinent, or scandalous matter, and supporting documents, on its own at any time or on motion filed before responding, or within 10 days after service of the pleading if no response is allowed.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 10.6. Preliminary determinations","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Rule 10.2 defenses (1)–(6), judgment-on-the-pleadings motions, and motions to strike are to be decided on the merits at or before the initial conference.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 10.7. Consolidation of defenses","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A Rule 10 motion may be joined with other Rule 10 motions then available; a party who files a Rule 10 motion but omits an available defense that could be raised by motion cannot later move on the omitted defense, except lack of subject-matter jurisdiction (Rule 10.2(1)) and the Rule 10.8(b) defenses.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 10.8. Waiver of defenses","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Lack of personal jurisdiction, insufficient summons, and insufficient service are waived if omitted from a consolidated Rule 10.7 motion, or if neither raised by Rule 10 motion nor included in a responsive pleading or an amendment allowed without leave under Rule 13.1. Failure to state a claim, failure to join an indispensable party under Rule 16, and failure to state a legal defense may be raised in any permitted pl","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 11. Counterclaims and crossclaims","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 11.1–11.7.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 11.1. Compulsory counterclaims","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A pleading must state as a counterclaim any claim against an opposing party that arises from the act, omission, or event underlying the opposing party’s claim and does not require third parties over whom the court cannot obtain jurisdiction; it need not be asserted if it was already the subject of another pending action when the suit began.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 11.2. Permissive counterclaims","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A pleading may assert as a counterclaim any claim against an opposing party not arising from the same act, omission, or event.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 11.3. Scope of counterclaim","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A counterclaim may reduce or defeat the opposing claim and may seek greater or different relief.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 11.4. Counterclaim maturing after pleading","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A counterclaim that matures after the party served its pleading may be asserted with the court’s permission.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 11.5. Omitted counterclaim","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A counterclaim omitted through oversight, inadvertence, or excusable neglect, or when justice requires, may be added by amendment with leave of court.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 11.6. Crossclaims (demanda contra coparte)","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A crossclaim may assert any claim arising from the act, omission, or event of the original complaint or a counterclaim, or relating to property that is the subject of the original complaint, including a claim that the coparty is or may be liable to the crossclaimant. It may be filed without leave within 30 days after the filing of all defendants’ answers; afterward, leave is required on a showing of good cause.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 11.7. Additional parties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Persons other than existing parties may be added to a counterclaim or crossclaim under Rules 16 and 17.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 12. Third-party practice","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 12.1–12.2.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 12.1. Third-party complaint by defendant","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A defendant may serve a summons and third-party complaint on a nonparty who is or may be liable to the defendant for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim, or who is or may be liable to any party. It may be filed without leave within 30 days after the filing of the answer to the complaint or the reply to a counterclaim; after that, leave is required on a showing of good cause. The third-party defendant answers under R","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 12.2. Third-party claim by plaintiff","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A plaintiff facing a claim may bring in a third party the same way a defendant may under Rule 12.1.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 13. Amended pleadings","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 13.1–13.3.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 13.1. Amendments","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A party may amend at any time before being served with a responsive pleading, or, for a pleading that permits no response and has not been set for trial, within 20 days after serving it. Otherwise amendment requires leave of court (freely given when justice so requires) or the adverse party’s written consent, and the motion for leave must attach the full amended pleading. The response to an amended pleading is due wi","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 13.2. Amendments to conform to the evidence","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Issues tried by express or implied consent are treated as if pleaded, and a conforming amendment may be sought at any time, even after judgment, though failure to amend does not affect the result. If evidence is objected to as outside the pleadings, the court may allow amendment if it aids presentation of the case, the movant shows good cause for not amending earlier, and the evidence will not prejudice the other sid","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 13.3. Relation back","Puerto Rico · civil rules","An amendment relates back when the claim or defense arises from the conduct, act, omission, or event in the original pleading. An amendment changing the defendant relates back if, in addition and within the prescriptive (limitations) period, the new party knew of the pending action so as not to be prejudiced in defending on the merits and, but for a mistake about identity, would have been sued originally. Adding a pl","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 14. Joinder of claims","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 14.1–14.2.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 14.1. Joinder of claims","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A party may join as many independent or alternative claims as it has against an opposing party.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 14.2. Contingent claims","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A claim that depends on another claim being pursued to conclusion may be joined in the same action, but the court will not decide the contingent claim until the principal claim is resolved.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 15. Standing and capacity","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 15.1–15.4 on real party in interest, minors and incapacitated persons, common names, and unknown defendants.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 15.1. Real party in interest (legitimación activa)","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Actions are prosecuted in the name of the person holding the right under law, though a person authorized by law may sue without that person, and the Commonwealth may sue for another’s benefit where the law allows. No dismissal for that defect until, after objection, a reasonable time is allowed for the real party to ratify, join, or be substituted, which has the same effect as if that party had originally sued.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 15.2. Minors and incapacitated persons","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A minor appears through a parent with patria potestad or, failing that, a general guardian; an adult or emancipated person adjudged incapacitated appears through a general guardian; the court may appoint a guardian ad litem (defensor judicial) when convenient or required by law. In the situations in Rule 4.4(c) and Rule 22.2, the court decides the party’s mental state and whether a guardian ad litem is needed.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 15.3. Defendants under a common name","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Two or more persons doing business under a common name may be sued under that name, and serving one of them suffices.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 15.4. Defendant of unknown name","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A plaintiff who does not know a defendant’s true name must say so and state the specific claim against that defendant, may use a fictitious name, and must promptly amend once the true name is discovered.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 16. Indispensable joinder of parties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 16.1–16.2.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 16.1. Indispensable parties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Persons with a common interest without whom the controversy cannot be adjudicated must be joined as plaintiffs or defendants; one who refuses to join as plaintiff may be made a defendant.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 16.2. Necessary but not indispensable parties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The court may order persons subject to its jurisdiction to appear if their joinder is needed to give complete relief to existing parties.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 17. Permissive joinder of parties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 17.1–17.2.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 17.1. Permissive joinder","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Any number of persons may join as plaintiffs or be joined as defendants if the claims arise from the same act, omission, event, or series and share a common question of fact or law; each party need not be interested in all the relief, and judgment may be entered for or against parties according to their respective rights and liabilities.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 17.2. Orders to prevent prejudice","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The court may issue orders to prevent embarrassment, delay, or expense to a party joined with another who has no claims against it, including separate trials and judgment on fewer than all claims or parties under Rule 42.3.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 18. Misjoinder of parties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped by the court at any stage on just terms, and any claim may be severed and proceed separately.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 19. Interpleader","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Persons with justiciable claims against the plaintiff may be joined as defendants and required to litigate among themselves when the plaintiff is or may be exposed to double or multiple liability, even if their claims lack a common origin or the plaintiff denies liability in whole or part. A defendant facing similar exposure may get the same relief by crossclaim, third-party claim, or counterclaim, and this rule supp","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 20. Class actions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 20.1–20.7 on class actions, residual class funds, and accommodations for parties who cannot communicate effectively.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 20.1. Prerequisites for a class action","Puerto Rico · civil rules","One or more members may sue or be sued as class representatives only if the class is so numerous that joinder is impracticable, there are common questions of law or fact, the representatives’ claims or defenses are typical, and they will fairly and adequately protect the class.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 20.2. Types of class actions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Beyond Rule 20.1, the action must fit one category: separate suits would risk inconsistent adjudications or would as a practical matter dispose of absent members’ interests; the opposing party acted or refused to act on grounds applicable to the whole class so that injunctive or declaratory relief is appropriate; or common questions predominate and a class action is superior, considering members’ interest in individu","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 20.3. Certification order; notice; judgment","Puerto Rico · civil rules","As soon as practicable, and after a hearing, the court decides whether the case proceeds as a class action; the order may be conditional and changed before a merits decision. In Rule 20.2(c) classes the court must direct the best practicable notice, including individual notice to identifiable members unless unduly burdensome, telling members they may opt out by a set date, that the judgment binds those who do not, an","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 20.4. Orders in class actions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The court may issue orders setting the course of proceedings, requiring notice to members, imposing conditions on representatives or intervenors, striking representative allegations, setting special discovery rules and deadlines, and dealing with similar procedural matters; these may be combined with a Rule 37 order and modified over time.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 20.5. Dismissal or settlement","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A class action may not be dismissed or settled without court approval, and all members must be notified of the proposed dismissal or settlement as the court directs.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 20.6. Residual (cy pres) class funds","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Every judgment or settlement order in a class action, or in a suit for unidentified beneficiaries, that sets up a claims process must provide for disbursing leftover funds. After the parties report that approved class members and court-authorized costs, fees, and expenses have been paid, the court orders all residual funds paid to the Fund for Access to Justice (Fondo para el Acceso a la Justicia, Law 165-2013) for c","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 20.7. Parties who cannot communicate effectively","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A party who is deaf or hard of hearing, or has another condition preventing effective communication, must be assigned a sign-language or lip-reading interpreter or other reasonable accommodation consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act. The court must arrange it as soon as it learns of the need, reschedule promptly if a hearing must be suspended, and presume the need if it is disputed.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 21. Intervention","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 21.1–21.7, including third-party claims to attached property.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 21.1. Intervention as of right","Puerto Rico · civil rules","On timely application, anyone may intervene when a law or these rules give an unconditional right, or when the applicant claims a right or interest in the property or subject matter that may in fact be affected by the final disposition (the text does not add the federal “adequately represented” limitation).","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 21.2. Permissive intervention","Puerto Rico · civil rules","On timely application, the court may allow intervention when a law gives a conditional right or the applicant’s claim or defense shares a question of law or fact with the main action. When a party relies on a law, executive order, or regulation administered by a government officer or agency, the court orders that party to notify the officer or agency, which may seek to intervene; the court weighs undue delay and prej","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 21.3. Constitutional challenges","Puerto Rico · civil rules","When the constitutionality of a Commonwealth law, executive order, franchise, or administrative regulation is challenged in a suit where the Commonwealth or its officer or agency is not a party, the court must notify the Secretary of Justice and allow the Commonwealth to intervene, and may order it to appear.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 21.4. Intervention procedure","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The would-be intervenor serves the motion on all parties under Rule 67, stating its grounds and attaching a pleading setting out the claim or defense.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 21.5. Third-party claims to attached property","Puerto Rico · civil rules","When a marshal executes an order of execution, attachment, or similar order against personal or real property and a third party claims the property or an interest in it, that third party may file a complaint in intervention governed by these rules.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 21.6. Motion for delivery of property; bond","Puerto Rico · civil rules","An intervenor seeking possession of attached property must file a motion, and the parties get an opportunity for a hearing. If granted, the intervenor must post a bond for the amount of the attachment plus any further sum the court sets to protect the affected party.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 21.7. Conditions of the bond","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The bond is conditioned on returning the property and answering for any deterioration or total loss, plus any other just compensation, if the claimant fails to establish the right; if the claimant prevails, the bond is canceled.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 22. Substitution of parties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 22.1–22.4.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 22.1. Death of a party","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If the claim is extinguished by death, judgment of dismissal is entered. If not, the parties or their counsel must notify the court and other parties of the death within 30 days after learning of it, and the court orders substitution on a motion filed within 90 days after that notice; the motion may be filed by successors or representatives and is served on parties under Rule 67 and on nonparties under Rule 4, and th","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 22.2. Incapacity","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If a party becomes incapacitated, the court may, on motion served as in Rule 22.1, allow the action to continue by or against the party’s guardian or guardian ad litem.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 22.3. Transfer of interest","Puerto Rico · civil rules","After a transfer of interest the action may continue by or against the original party unless the court, on motion served as in Rule 22.1, orders the transferee substituted or joined.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 22.4. Public officers","Puerto Rico · civil rules","When a federal, Commonwealth, municipal, or agency officer sued in an official capacity dies, resigns, or otherwise leaves office during the case, the action is not dismissed and the successor is automatically substituted.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 23. General provisions on discovery","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 23.1–23.4. Instead of federal Rule 26(a) initial disclosures, Rule 37.1 requires counsel to exchange documents, witness information, and expert information at a case-management meeting; the request-based methods carry their own response times (30 days for interrogatories, 15 days for document requests, 20 days for admissions).","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 23.1. Scope of discovery; experts; preservation; supplementation","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Unless limited by the court, parties may discover any nonprivileged matter relevant to the subject matter of the action, including ESI, documents, and witnesses’ identities; inadmissibility is no objection if the request is reasonably likely to lead to admissible evidence (a broader “subject matter” standard than federal proportionality). Trial-preparation materials are discoverable, but not counsel’s or a representa","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 23.2. Limits and protective orders","Puerto Rico · civil rules","On its own or on motion, the court may limit discovery that is duplicative or cumulative, obtainable more conveniently or cheaply, already obtainable by the requester, or costlier than its benefit. On motion with a certification of a good-faith attempt to resolve the dispute under Rule 34.1 and on good cause, the court may protect a party or person from harassment, oppression, or undue burden or expense, including by","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 23.3. Privilege claims; inadvertent production","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A party withholding information as privileged or trial-preparation material must make the claim expressly and describe what is withheld so others can assess it without disclosure. If privileged information is produced inadvertently, the producing party notifies the receiver, who must promptly return, sequester, or destroy it and all copies, may not use or disclose it until the claim is resolved, may submit it to the ","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 23.4. Sequence of discovery","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Discovery methods may be used in any order, and one party’s discovery does not delay another’s unless the court orders otherwise on motion.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 24. Depositions before action or pending appeal","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 24.1–24.2.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 24.1. Before an action is filed","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A person expecting to be a party to an action who cannot yet bring it may file a sworn petition to perpetuate testimony, stating the expected action, the facts to be established and reasons, the expected adverse parties, the deponents and substance of testimony, and whether the deposition will be oral or written. The petition is served on expected adverse parties as a summons is served under Rule 4.4, giving at least","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 24.2. Pending appeal","Puerto Rico · civil rules","While an appeal is pending or the time to appeal has not expired, the trial court may allow depositions to perpetuate testimony for further proceedings, on a motion naming the deponents, the substance of expected testimony, and the reasons; it may grant leave if needed to prevent a failure or delay of justice.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 25. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 25.1–25.3.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 25.1. Within Puerto Rico","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Depositions in Puerto Rico are taken before a person authorized to administer oaths under Puerto Rico law or a person the court designates, who may administer oaths, take testimony, and direct the deposition; the oath-giver need not remain after swearing the deponent. For a litigant recognized as indigent, the court may order the deposition under conditions it deems convenient.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 25.2. Outside Puerto Rico","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Depositions outside Puerto Rico, on notice, are taken before a person authorized to administer oaths in Puerto Rico or the place of the deposition, before a person appointed by commission, or by letter rogatory (addressed “To the Appropriate Judicial Authority in” the place). Evidence from a letter rogatory should not be excluded merely because it is not verbatim, not under oath, or otherwise not in Puerto Rico form.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 25.3. Disqualification for interest","Puerto Rico · civil rules","No deposition may be taken before a relative within the fourth degree of consanguinity or second of affinity, employee, or attorney of a party, a relative or employee of such attorney, or a person financially interested in the action, except as Rule 26 allows.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 26. Stipulations about depositions and discovery","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If not contrary to the Rule 37.3 scheduling order, parties may stipulate that depositions be taken before any person, at any time or place, on any notice, and in any manner and used like other depositions; modify procedures for other discovery methods; and let any attorney present swear the deponent, reporter, and translator.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions by oral examination","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 27.1–27.9 on timing, notice, recording, remote depositions, entity depositions, objections, review and signature, and certification.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 27.1. When depositions may be taken","Puerto Rico · civil rules","After the action begins any party may depose any person, including a party, without leave, except that the plaintiff needs leave to take a deposition within 30 days after service on the defendant, unless the defendant has begun discovery or the notice certifies the deponent is about to leave Puerto Rico and will be unavailable (counsel’s signature certifies those facts and is subject to Rule 9). Witnesses are compell","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 27.2. Notice; time, place, and method","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A party must give at least 20 days’ written notice to all other parties, stating date, time, place, method, and each deponent’s name and address (or a description). A notice to a party may include a document request under Rule 31; documents subpoenaed from a nonparty must be listed in the notice, and Rule 40.4 governs the place of examination and subpoenas.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 27.3. Recording method","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Depositions may be recorded stenographically or by any audio-video or digital method that preserves the integrity of the proceeding and allows reproduction. If a party or deponent is deaf or cannot otherwise communicate effectively, the deposition must be video- or digitally recorded to preserve sign-language or lip-reading testimony, at the noticing party’s expense.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 27.4. Remote depositions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","By written stipulation, or on motion and court order, an oral deposition may be taken by telephone, videoconference, or other remote means, on at least 20 days’ written notice to the deponent and all parties stating date, time, place, and method. Requested documents must be provided reasonably in advance and shared with the reporter and other parties; if the noticing party appears remotely it must arrange for others ","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 27.5. Court regulation","Puerto Rico · civil rules","On motion of a noticed party and for good cause, the court may lengthen or shorten the time for the deposition and regulate its date, place, order, and other Rule 27.2–27.3 matters.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 27.6. Depositions of organizations","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A notice or subpoena may name a private corporation, partnership, or association and describe the examination topics with reasonable particularity; absent a designation by the requesting party, the organization designates the persons with the most knowledge to testify on its behalf and may specify their topics. A nonparty organization’s subpoena must advise it of this duty, and the rule also applies to the Government","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 27.7. Examination, oath, objections, and conduct","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Examination and cross-examination proceed under oath before the Rule 25 officer, and every deposition must be transcribed unless all parties stipulate otherwise; the noticing party pays the reporter, transcript, and the deponent’s copy. Only objections based on privilege or that would be waived if not made may be stated and argued; others are noted and the answer given subject to them (but in depositions to perpetuat","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 27.8. Review, correction, and signature","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The reporter delivers the transcript to the noticing party within 45 days after the deposition ends; the deponent then gets it for review within 10 days unless review is waived on the record, and any changes in form or substance must be submitted within 30 days after notice of the transcript. For untranscribed recorded depositions, the deponent reviews within 30 days after delivery and submits changes in that time. C","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 27.9. Certification and custody","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The officer (or recorder) certifies the oath and accuracy, seals and labels the original, and delivers it to the noticing party within the Rule 27.8 time; that party must notify all others, keep the original, and produce it at trial. Exhibits are marked and attached (copies may be substituted after verification), copies are available to any party or the deponent for a fee, and where transcription is waived the notici","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 28. Depositions on written questions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 28.1–28.3.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 28.1. Notice and service of questions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The noticing party serves its questions with a notice naming the deponent, the officer, and the recorder. Cross-questions are due within 10 days after the notice, redirect questions within 5 days after cross-questions, and recross questions within 3 days after redirect questions.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 28.2. Taking responses; duties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The noticing party gives the notice and questions to the officer, who promptly takes the testimony and prepares, certifies, and handles the deposition under Rules 27.7–27.9; the noticing party must notify others when it receives the deposition, keep the original, and produce it at trial.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 28.3. Protective orders","Puerto Rico · civil rules","After questions are served and before testimony is taken, on prompt motion, notice, and good cause, the court may enter any Rule 23.2 order, change the officer, or require that the deposition be taken only by oral examination.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 29. Use of depositions in court","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 29.1–29.3.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 29.1. Use of depositions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","At trial or a hearing, a deposition may be used against any party present, represented, or duly notified, to the extent admissible under the Rules of Evidence as if the deponent were testifying: by any party to contradict or impeach; by an adverse party for any purpose if the deponent is a party or a party organization’s officer, director, managing agent, or Rule 27.6 designee; and by any party for any purpose if the","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 29.2. Objections to admissibility","Puerto Rico · civil rules","At trial or a hearing, a party may object to any deposition testimony on any ground that would exclude it if the witness were testifying live.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 29.3. Effect of errors and irregularities","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Notice defects are waived unless written objection is promptly served; objections to the officer’s qualification are waived unless made before the deposition starts or as soon as discoverable. Objections to competence or admissibility survive unless the ground could have been cured at the time; curable errors in the manner of taking, form of questions or answers, oath, or conduct are waived unless timely objected to;","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 30. Interrogatories to parties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 30.1–30.3. The text sets no numerical cap on interrogatories, unlike federal Rule 33’s 25-question limit.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 30.1. Procedure","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Written interrogatories may be served on the plaintiff without leave after the action begins and on any other party after 30 days from that party’s service of summons (the limit does not apply if the defendant starts discovery first); entity parties answer through an officer or agent with available information. Each is answered separately, fully, in writing, and under oath unless objected to; objections are stated by","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 30.2. Scope; use at trial","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Interrogatories may cover any matter within Rule 23 and answers may be used as the Rules of Evidence allow; an interrogatory is not objectionable merely because it calls for an opinion or contention about facts or the application of law, though the court may defer or excuse the answer for good cause.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 30.3. Option to produce business records or ESI","Puerto Rico · civil rules","When the answer can be found in the answering party’s books, documents, records, or ESI and the burden is substantially the same for both sides, it is enough to specify the records and give a reasonable opportunity to examine, audit, and copy them.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 31. Production of documents and things; entry on land","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 31.1–31.2.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 31.1. Scope","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Subject to Rule 23.2, a party may request another party to produce for inspection, copying, or photographing nonprivileged documents, ESI (translated into usable form if necessary), books, photographs, and tangible things within Rule 23.1 in its possession, custody, or control, or to permit entry onto land or property to inspect, measure, survey, or photograph. The request specifies the date, time, place, and manner ","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 31.2. Procedure; 15-day response","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Requests may be served on the plaintiff without leave after commencement and on any other party after 30 days from its service of summons (unless the defendant started discovery first), describing items with reasonable particularity. The responding party must respond within 15 days, stating for each item that inspection will be permitted unless objected to, with reasons; the requester may seek a Rule 34.2 order. Docu","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 32. Physical and mental examinations","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 32.1–32.2.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 32.1. Order for examination","Puerto Rico · civil rules","When the physical or mental condition (including blood group or genetic makeup) of a party or person in its custody or care is in controversy, the court may on motion and notice order an examination by a licensed professional, specifying the date, time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner. A party who makes allegations about its own physical or mental condition waives privacy in the related medical or psyc","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 32.2. Examiner’s report","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The party that obtained the examination must give the examined party a detailed written report within 45 days after the exam, and is then entitled to reports of other exams of the same condition; the court may compel delivery, and may exclude the examiner’s testimony if the examiner fails or refuses to report. By obtaining the report or deposing the examiner, the examined party waives privilege as to anyone else who ","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 33. Requests for admission","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A party may serve written requests to admit the truth of matters within Rule 23.1 (facts, opinions of fact, application of law to fact, and genuineness of documents), on the plaintiff without leave after commencement and on others after 30 days from their service (unless the defendant started discovery first). Each matter is deemed admitted unless within 20 days after service (or as the court allows) the party serves","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 34. Discovery disputes and sanctions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 34.1–34.6 on meet-and-confer, motions to compel, sanctions, and expenses.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 34.1. Meet-and-confer certification","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The court will consider a discovery motion only if it contains the movant’s particularized certification that it made prompt, reasonable, good-faith efforts to reach agreement with opposing counsel on the issues in the motion and those efforts failed.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 34.2. Motion to compel","Puerto Rico · civil rules","After good-faith efforts fail, a party may move to compel answers when a deponent refuses to answer under Rules 27 or 28, an organization fails to designate under Rule 27.6, a party fails to answer Rule 30 interrogatories, or a party fails to respond to or allow a Rule 31 inspection; an oral deposition may be completed or adjourned before moving. Evasive or incomplete answers count as failures to answer. After a hear","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 34.3. Failure to obey an order; ESI loss","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A deponent’s refusal to be sworn or to answer after a court order may be treated as contempt. When a party, officer, managing agent, or designee disobeys a discovery order (including under Rules 32 and 34.2), the court may deem facts established, bar claims, defenses, or evidence, strike pleadings, stay proceedings, dismiss, enter default judgment, hold the party in contempt (except for an order to submit to a physic","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 34.4. Expenses for failure to admit","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If a party fails to admit a document’s genuineness or a matter’s truth under Rule 33 and the requester later proves it, the court awards the reasonable cost of that proof, including attorney’s fees, unless the request was objectionable, the admission had no substantial importance, the party reasonably believed it would prevail, or there was another good reason.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 34.5. Failure to attend or respond","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If a party, officer, managing agent, or designee fails to appear for a properly noticed deposition, to serve answers or objections to interrogatories, or to respond in writing to an inspection request, the court may enter any Rule 34.3(b)(1)–(3) order and must award reasonable expenses including attorney’s fees unless justified or unjust. That the discovery was objectionable is no excuse unless a Rule 23.2 protective","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 34.6. Expenses against the Commonwealth","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Expenses and attorney’s fees under Rule 34 may be imposed on the Commonwealth only after a prior hearing.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 35. Offers of judgment and payment","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 35.1–35.4.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 35.1. Offer of judgment","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A party defending a claim may, at any time more than 20 days before trial begins, serve an offer to allow judgment for a specified amount, property, or result plus costs accrued to date; the offer must be in writing, sent by certified mail, identify offeror and offeree, state any damages amount, the total amount or property and conditions, and the accrued costs. If accepted in writing within 10 days after service, ei","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 35.2. Offer of payment","Puerto Rico · civil rules","In a suit solely to collect money, if the defendant alleges in its answer that before suit it offered the plaintiff the full amount owed and immediately deposits it in court, and that proves true, the plaintiff recovers no costs and must pay the defendant’s costs, expenses, and attorney’s fees.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 35.3. Deposit in court","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Where relief sought includes payment of money or delivery of a thing, a party may, on notice and with leave of court, deposit all or part of it with the Clerk, to be withdrawn by court order.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 35.4. Judgment by consent (confession of judgment)","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Judgment may be entered without trial or even without a filed action on the consent of a person with legal capacity, for money owed or to become due or to secure another against contingent liability; once the court approves it, the Clerk records and notifies it and it is final and firm from recording. The consent must be in a sworn writing by the defendant authorizing judgment for a specific sum and concisely stating","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 36. Summary judgment","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 36.1–36.7. Puerto Rico’s summary judgment rule is far more prescriptive than federal Rule 56: it sets filing windows tied to service and the discovery cutoff, requires numbered statements of uncontested and contested facts with pinpoint citations, a 20-day opposition, and mandatory findings when the motion does not end the case.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 36.1. By a claimant","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A party seeking relief may move at any time after 20 days from service on the defendant (or after the opponent serves a summary judgment motion) but no later than 30 days after the court’s discovery cutoff date, on affidavits or other evidence showing there is no substantial controversy of essential and pertinent facts, for judgment on all or part of the claim.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 36.2. By a defending party","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A party against whom a claim is made may move from the date it was served but no later than 30 days after the court’s discovery cutoff, on affidavits or other evidence showing no substantial controversy of essential and pertinent facts.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 36.3. Motion, opposition, and decision","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The motion must contain a brief statement of the pleadings, the issues, the claim or part at issue, a concise, organized list in numbered paragraphs of all essential and pertinent undisputed facts with pinpoint citations to affidavits or other admissible evidence in the record, legal argument, and the relief sought. The opposition is due within 20 days after service and must address items (1)–(3), respond by referenc","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 36.4. Case not fully decided on motion","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If the motion does not dispose of the whole case, grants less than all relief, or is denied and trial is needed, the court must resolve it by findings specifying the essential facts without substantial controversy, the facts genuinely in dispute, and the extent to which damages are undisputed, and must order further proceedings, including an evidentiary hearing limited to the disputed issues; facts so specified are d","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 36.5. Form of affidavits","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Supporting and opposing affidavits must be made on personal knowledge, set out admissible facts, and affirmatively show the affiant’s competence, with sworn or certified copies of referenced documents attached or served; the court may allow supplementation or rebuttal by depositions or further affidavits.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 36.6. When affidavits are unavailable","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If the opponent shows by affidavit reasons why it cannot present facts essential to its opposition, the court may deny or defer the motion and allow a reasonable time to obtain affidavits, take depositions, or obtain evidence, or make another just order.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 36.7. Affidavits in bad faith","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If an affidavit was presented in bad faith or solely for delay, the court must order the responsible party to pay the other side’s reasonable expenses, including reasonable attorney’s fees, and the party or attorney may also be held in contempt.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 37. Case management","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 37.1–37.7 on the attorneys’ case-management meeting and report, initial conference, scheduling order, pretrial report and conference, settlement conference, and sanctions. Unlike the federal model, the deadlines in Rules 37.1 and 37.3 are expressly “de estricto cumplimiento” (strict compliance), enforced by mandatory monetary sanctions under Rule 37.7.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 37.1. Attorneys’ case-management meeting and report","Puerto Rico · civil rules","In all contested cases except those under Rules 45 and 60, family-relations cases, and cases governed by special laws, counsel must meet no later than 40 days after the last answer of a defendant, last served codefendant, or third-party defendant, or expiration of the time to answer; plaintiff’s counsel coordinates the date. At the meeting counsel exchange legible copies of all documents, audiovisual material, and ES","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 37.2. Initial conference","Puerto Rico · civil rules","In all contested cases except Rule 60 cases and those under special laws, the court sets an initial conference no later than 60 days after the Case Management Report is filed; if all parties agree and the judge allows, it may be held by telephone, videoconference, or other simultaneous remote means. A party that fails without good cause to join a remote conference may be sanctioned under Rule 37.7, and if the confere","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 37.3. Scheduling order","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The court issues a scheduling order capturing the initial conference’s rulings and agreements, or, if no initial conference is held, adopting those in the Case Management Report. Deadlines and settings in the scheduling order are of strict compliance (estricto cumplimiento), subject to Rule 37.7 sanctions.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 37.4. Attorneys’ pretrial meeting and report","Puerto Rico · civil rules","In cases set for a pretrial conference, counsel must meet informally at least 15 days before the conference and prepare a Preliminary Report Between Attorneys covering trial counsel’s contact information, a brief statement of facts and itemized damages, stipulations, each side’s position on disputed facts with legal basis, applicable law and anticipated legal questions with case law, uncontested and contested documen","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 37.5. Pretrial conference","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Held at least 30 days before trial, with parties’ attendance if the court requires; it covers the items in the attorneys’ report, settlement, rulings on pending controversies including admissibility, and a trial plan. The resulting pretrial order records agreements, allowed amendments, and stipulations, limits trial to unresolved issues, and governs the case unless modified at trial to prevent manifest injustice.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 37.6. Settlement conference","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The court may at any time order a settlement conference attended by the parties and their counsel.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 37.7. Sanctions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If a party or counsel fails without good cause to comply with the deadlines and settings of Rule 37 or any case-management order, the court must impose an appropriate monetary sanction on the party or attorney; the money goes to the Fund for Access to Justice under Law 165-2013.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 38. Consolidation; separate trials","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 38.1–38.2.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 38.1. Consolidation","Puerto Rico · civil rules","When pending actions involve common questions of law or fact, the court may order a joint hearing or trial of any or all issues, consolidate the actions, and make orders to avoid unnecessary cost or delay.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 38.2. Separate trials","Puerto Rico · civil rules","For convenience, to avoid prejudice or unnecessary expense, or to expedite the case, the court may order separate trials of any claim, crossclaim, counterclaim, third-party claim, or separate issue, and enter judgment under Rule 42.3.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 39. Voluntary and involuntary dismissal","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 39.1–39.4.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 39.1. Voluntary dismissal (desistimiento)","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Subject to Rule 20.5, the plaintiff may dismiss without a court order by filing a notice before the adverse party serves an answer or a summary judgment motion, whichever comes first, or by a stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared. Unless stated otherwise the dismissal is without prejudice, but a notice of dismissal operates as an adjudication on the merits if the plaintiff previously dismissed an action","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 39.2. Involuntary dismissal; inactivity; dismissal after plaintiff’s case","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If the plaintiff fails to comply with the rules or a court order, the court may dismiss or strike pleadings, but for a first failure it must first warn counsel and allow a response, then sanction counsel and notify the party directly, and only after the party is warned of the consequences may it dismiss, giving a reasonable time to cure of no less than 30 days unless circumstances justify less. The court must dismiss","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 39.3. Dismissal of counterclaims, crossclaims, and third-party claims","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Rule 39 applies to counterclaims, crossclaims, and third-party claims; a voluntary dismissal by notice under Rule 39.1(a) must be made before a responsive pleading is served, or if none, before evidence is presented at trial.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 39.4. Costs of a previously dismissed action","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If a plaintiff who once dismissed an action sues the same defendant again on the same claim, the court may order payment of the costs or attorney’s fees of the earlier action and stay the new action until the plaintiff complies.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 40. Subpoenas (citación)","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 40.1–40.10 on the form, issuance, service, objection to, and enforcement of subpoenas.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 40.1. Form","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A subpoena states the issuing court, sala, case title, and civil number, and commands the person or entity to appear and testify at a hearing, trial, or deposition, to produce or permit inspection of books, documents, ESI, or tangible things, or to permit inspection of premises, at a specified date, time, and place. A production command may be combined with or separate from a command to appear, and an ESI subpoena mu","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 40.2. Issuance","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The Clerk issues subpoenas on request, or an admitted attorney who has appeared for the party may issue them: to attend trial or a hearing in the sala where it is set; to attend a deposition only where the person resides, is employed, or personally does business; to produce materials in the judicial region of the sala where the case is pending (subject to Rule 31); or to inspect premises (subject to Rule 31).","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 40.3. Service","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Service may be made by a marshal or any literate person at least 18 who is not a party, a party’s attorney, a relative within the fourth degree of consanguinity or second of affinity, or interested, by personal delivery or as in Rule 4.4; a subpoena to appear must include a check or money order for the statutory per diem and mileage, except when issued for the Commonwealth, its agencies, municipalities, or officers. ","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 40.4. Protection of persons subject to subpoenas","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The requesting party or issuing attorney must take reasonable steps to avoid undue burden or expense, and the court may sanction violations, including lost income and attorney’s fees. A person commanded only to produce need not appear in person unless also commanded to testify. Within 15 days after service the person may serve a written objection to inspection, copying, the form of ESI, or inspection of premises, aft","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 40.5. Duties in responding","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Documents must be produced as kept in the usual course of business or organized and labeled by request category; a privilege or trial-preparation claim must be express and describe the withheld material enough for the requester to contest it, and does not excuse producing the rest.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 40.6. Deposition subpoenas for witnesses outside Puerto Rico","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Witnesses outside the jurisdiction are summoned for deposition by commission or letter rogatory to the competent judicial authority where they are, testimony is taken under Rule 25.2, and the transcript may be used in place of live testimony.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 40.7. Subpoena unnecessary","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A person present in court or before a judicial officer may be called to testify as if subpoenaed.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 40.8. Concealed witnesses","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If a witness hides to avoid service, the court may, on a sworn statement showing the concealment and the relevance of the testimony, order the marshal to serve the subpoena, and the marshal may enter any building or property where the witness is hiding.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 40.9. Imprisoned witnesses","Puerto Rico · civil rules","On a sworn motion showing relevance, the court may order an imprisoned person subpoenaed and brought to testify at a trial, hearing, or deposition.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 40.10. Contempt","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Failure without good cause to obey a properly served subpoena may be punished as contempt of court.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 41. Special commissioners (masters)","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 41.1–41.5 on appointment, powers, procedure, and reports of commissioners, a term that includes arbitrators, auditors, and examiners.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 41.1. Appointment and compensation","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The court may appoint a special commissioner (including an arbitrator, auditor, or examiner), set fees chargeable to the parties as ordered or payable from funds or property in the court’s custody. The commissioner may not withhold the report to secure payment, but may obtain a writ of execution against a party that fails to pay within the time ordered, and an unjustified refusal to pay may be sanctioned under Rule 3","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 41.2. Reference","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The Supreme Court and Court of Appeals may refer matters to a commissioner in original-jurisdiction cases. In the Court of First Instance reference is the exception, limited to difficult accounting and damages computations or highly technical or specialized expert matters, and no commissioner may be appointed if a party shows it would cause unnecessary delay or unreasonable cost.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 41.3. Powers","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The order of reference specifies the commissioner’s powers, the issues to report on or acts to perform, and a reasonable deadline for the report. Within those limits the commissioner regulates hearings, may require production of evidence, rules on admissibility unless the order says otherwise, administers oaths, examines witnesses and parties under oath, and on request makes a record of excluded evidence under the Ru","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 41.4. Proceedings","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The Clerk gives the commissioner the order at once, and unless otherwise ordered the commissioner notifies the parties of a first meeting to be held within 20 days after the order and must proceed with reasonable diligence; any party may ask the court to order expedition, and the commissioner may proceed in a party’s absence or postpone. Witnesses are subpoenaed under Rule 40 and face contempt and Rules 34 and 40 con","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 41.5. Report","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The commissioner files a report on all referred matters, with findings and conclusions if required, by the date in the order, accompanied (unless otherwise ordered) by a transcript of proceedings, summary of evidence, and original exhibits, and may first circulate a draft to counsel; the Clerk notifies all parties on filing. The court accepts the commissioner’s findings of fact unless clearly erroneous; objections ar","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 42. Judgments and resolutions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 42.1–42.4.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 42.1. Definitions: judgment and resolution","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A “sentencia” (judgment) is any Court of First Instance determination that finally resolves the controversy and is appealable; a “resolución” is a ruling that ends an incident within the case. In an appellate court, a judgment is its final determination on an appeal or on a discretionary writ it has granted, or its dismissal of an appeal for any reason; a discretionary denial of the writ is a “resolución.”","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 42.2. Findings of fact and conclusions of law","Puerto Rico · civil rules","In all actions the court states the facts proved and its conclusions of law separately and orders entry of judgment, and does the same when granting or denying preliminary injunctions; findings based on oral testimony are set aside only if clearly erroneous, with due regard for the trial court’s opportunity to judge credibility, and adopted commissioner findings count as the court’s. Findings are not required on moti","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 42.3. Judgment on multiple claims or parties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","With multiple claims or parties, the court may enter final judgment on fewer than all only by expressly concluding there is no reason to delay entry and expressly directing entry of judgment. That partial judgment is then final for all purposes as to what it adjudicates, and once entered and a copy of its notice is filed in the record, the deadlines of Rules 43.1, 47, 48, and 52.2 start to run as to it.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 42.4. Relief granted","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Every judgment grants the relief to which the prevailing party is entitled even if not requested in the pleadings, but a default judgment may not differ in kind from or exceed in amount what was demanded.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 43. Amended or additional findings","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 43.1–43.2.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 43.1. Motion for initial or additional findings","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Findings are not required to preserve an appeal, but on a motion filed no later than 15 days after a copy of the notice of judgment is filed in the record, the court may make initial findings and conclusions where Rule 42.2 excused them, amend or add findings, and amend the judgment. A party seeking findings, reconsideration, or a new trial must combine them in one filing, which the court decides together, and suffic","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 43.2. Tolling of post-judgment deadlines","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The motion must state with sufficient particularity the facts the movant considers proved and rest on substantial issues about pertinent findings or material conclusions of law. A motion for amended, initial, or additional findings by any party interrupts the time to appeal for all parties, which starts again once a copy of the notice of the order granting or denying it, or of the amended judgment, is filed in the re","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 44. Costs, attorney’s fees, and legal interest","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 44.1–44.3. Unlike federal practice, attorney’s fees are mandatory against a party that acted with temerity or frivolousness, and prejudgment interest is imposed for temerity.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 44.1. Costs and attorney’s fees","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Costs go to the prevailing party at trial or on appeal or review unless a law or these rules provide otherwise, covering expenses necessarily incurred that the law requires or the court in its discretion finds should be reimbursed. The claimant files and serves a sworn (or attorney-certified) memorandum of costs within 10 days after a copy of the notice of judgment is filed in the record; any objection is due within ","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 44.2. Interlocutory costs and sanctions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","At any stage the court may impose interlocutory costs and monetary sanctions on a party or counsel for delay, inaction, abandonment, obstruction, or lack of diligence harmful to the administration of justice, payable electronically or as the Chief Justice provides; sanctions go to the Judicial Branch Special Fund (Law 235 of 1998), except that sanctions against the Commonwealth or its agencies go to the opposing part","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 44.3. Legal interest","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Every money judgment bears post-judgment interest, including on costs and fees, at the rate set by regulation of the Financial Board of the Office of the Commissioner of Financial Institutions in effect when judgment is entered, stated in the judgment. The court must also impose prejudgment interest at that rate on a party that acted with temerity: from accrual of the cause of action in collection cases and from the ","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 45. Default (rebeldía)","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 45.1–45.5. A Puerto Rico default deems the well-pleaded allegations admitted, a Clerk’s default judgment is limited to liquidated sums, and judgments against the government in collection cases require proof of the claim.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 45.1. Entry of default","Puerto Rico · civil rules","When a party against whom affirmative relief is sought fails to plead or otherwise defend, and this is shown by affidavit or otherwise, the Clerk enters its default; the court may also enter default as a discovery sanction under Rule 34.3(b)(3). Default means the allegations of the affirmative pleadings are deemed admitted, subject to Rule 45.2(b), and failure to enter default does not invalidate a default judgment.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 45.2. Default judgment","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The Clerk may enter judgment for a sum certain or computable sum plus costs, on the plaintiff’s request and affidavit of the amount owed, against a defaulted defendant who is not a minor or incapacitated. In all other cases the party applies to the court; no default judgment may be entered against a minor or incapacitated person unless represented by a parent, guardian, guardian ad litem, or other appearing represent","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 45.3. Setting aside default","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The court may set aside an entry of default for good cause, and a default judgment under Rule 49.2.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 45.4. Who may seek default","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Rule 45 applies to any party entitled to default, including plaintiffs, third-party plaintiffs, crossclaimants, and counterclaimants, and every default judgment is subject to the Rule 42.4 limits.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 45.5. Default against the Commonwealth","Puerto Rico · civil rules","In any money-collection claim against the Commonwealth, its municipalities, agencies, or officials in their official capacity, no default judgment may be entered unless the claimant proves its claim or right to relief to the court’s satisfaction.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 46. Notice and entry of judgments","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The Clerk must notify judgments as soon as possible under Supreme Court standards, filing in the record a copy of the judgment and proof of notice and entering it; entry in the Register of Actions constitutes entry of judgment. A judgment has no effect until a copy of its notice to all parties is filed in the record (archivo en autos), and the time to appeal runs from that filing date.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 47. Reconsideration","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A party adversely affected by an order or resolution of the Court of First Instance may move for reconsideration within 15 days after notice of it, a strict-compliance (cumplimiento estricto) term; a motion to reconsider a judgment must be filed within 15 days after a copy of the notice of judgment is filed in the record, a jurisdictional (jurisdiccional) term. The motion must state with sufficient particularity the ","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 48. New trial","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 48.1–48.4.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 48.1. Grounds","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A new trial may be ordered for all or some parties and issues when essential evidence is discovered that could not with reasonable diligence have been found and presented at trial, when a narrative statement of the evidence or a transcript cannot be prepared, or when substantial justice requires.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 48.2. Time for motion","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A motion for new trial must be filed within 15 days after a copy of the notice of judgment is filed in the record, except that one based on newly discovered evidence may be filed before the time to appeal expires, with notice, a hearing, and a showing of due diligence, and one based on the impossibility of preparing a transcript or narrative statement may be filed within 30 days after that impossibility becomes known","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 48.3. Affidavits","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Affidavits supporting a new-trial motion are served with it; the opponent has 15 days after service to serve opposing affidavits, extendable once for up to 15 more days on good cause, and the court may allow reply affidavits.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 48.4. On the court’s own initiative","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Within 15 days after entry of judgment, the court may on its own order a new trial for any reason that would justify one on motion, stating its grounds.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 49. Relief from judgments or orders","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 49.1–49.2.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 49.1. Clerical errors","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Clerical errors and errors from oversight or omission in judgments, orders, or the record may be corrected at any time, on the court’s initiative or motion; during an appeal or certiorari they may be corrected before the record is sent up and afterward only with the appellate court’s leave.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 49.2. Mistake, neglect, new evidence, fraud, and other grounds","Puerto Rico · civil rules","On motion and just terms, the court may relieve a party or counsel from a judgment, order, or proceeding for mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect; newly discovered essential evidence not discoverable in time for a Rule 48 motion; fraud (whether intrinsic or extrinsic), misrepresentation, or misconduct of an adverse party; voidness; satisfaction, release, reversal of an underlying judgment, or inequit","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 50. Harmless error","Puerto Rico · civil rules","No error in admitting or excluding evidence, or other error or defect by the court or a party, justifies a new trial or setting aside or altering a judgment or order unless refusing to act would be inconsistent with substantial justice; errors that do not affect substantial rights are disregarded.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 50.1. Void judgment against a party unable to communicate effectively","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A judgment against a person who is deaf or hard of hearing, or has another condition preventing effective communication, may be declared void if the person requested and was not provided a sign-language or lip-reading interpreter or other reasonable accommodation under the Americans with Disabilities Act.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 51. Execution of judgments","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 51.1–51.10 on writs of execution, specific-act and foreclosure judgments, supplementary proceedings, judicial sales, and execution expenses.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 51.1. When execution is available","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A prevailing party may execute the judgment under Rule 51 at any time within five years after it becomes final and firm (firme); after that, execution requires leave of court on motion with notice to all parties. Any period during which execution was stayed by court order or by law is excluded from the five years.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 51.2. Money judgments","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Money judgments and awarded costs are enforced by a writ of execution signed by the Clerk under the court’s seal, stating the judgment terms and the unpaid amount, directed to the marshal and delivered to the interested party; one writ may cover several judgments or orders in the case. The marshal must return the executed writ and any surplus to the Clerk within 15 days after the execution, including judicial sales; ","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 51.3. Judgments for specific acts; foreclosure","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If a party fails to convey land, execute deeds, or perform another specific act within the time ordered, the court may have a designee do it at that party’s expense with the same effect, issue a writ of attachment on the party’s property, punish contempt, or enter a judgment divesting title and vesting it in another; a party awarded possession gets a writ of execution on request to the Clerk. An order for a judicial ","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 51.4. Supplementary proceedings","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A judgment creditor or assignee may examine any person, including the judgment debtor, in aid of the judgment using the deposition rules; for written-question depositions the subpoena may excuse personal attendance if sworn answers are delivered beforehand. The court may issue any just order needed for execution and to protect creditor, debtor, and third parties.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 51.5. Levy on property","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A writ against the debtor’s property directs the marshal or court designee to satisfy the judgment, interest, and costs from it; if the debtor’s property exceeds the judgment, only the part the debtor designates is attached, provided it is amply sufficient. A writ on a Rule 60 summary-procedure judgment may not be executed on Saturdays, Sundays, holidays, or outside business hours absent a showing of compelling need.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 51.6. Enforcement by and against nonparties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A nonparty in whose favor an order is made may enforce it, and a nonparty bound by an order may be compelled to comply, by the same process as a party.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 51.7. Judicial sales","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Notice of sale must be posted for two weeks in three public places in the municipality of the sale (such as city hall, the courthouse, and the tax collection office) and published twice in a newspaper of general circulation in Puerto Rico over two consecutive weeks, at least seven days apart; a copy goes by certified mail with return receipt to the debtor and counsel within 5 days after the first publication if the d","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 51.8. Buyer’s remedies if title fails; renewal of judgment","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If a buyer at judicial auction does not obtain title or possession after paying because of an irregularity voiding the sale or because the property was not subject to execution, the court, on notice and hearing, must either order the judgment creditors to repay what each received plus legal interest, or revive the judgment in the buyer’s favor up to the amount paid with interest at the judgment rate, with priority ov","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 51.9. Contribution among joint debtors","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A jointly and severally liable judgment debtor who pays more than its share may use the judgment to recover the excess from the codebtors by filing proof of payment and a request with the Clerk within 30 days after paying; the Clerk notes it in the judgment register and issues a writ against the other debtors on request.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 51.10. Execution expenses","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Reasonable necessary expenses of enforcing a judgment are recoverable by a sworn or attorney-certified request filed within 10 days after the judgment is fully satisfied; objections are due within 10 days after service of the request, and the ruling is reviewable by certiorari in the Court of Appeals. Without objection, the court approves the request but may strike improper items after letting the requester justify t","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 52. Appeal, certiorari, certification, and other review","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 52.1–52.3. Key terms: appeal of a judgment, 30 days (jurisdictional) or 60 days when the government is a party (jurisdictional); certiorari against interlocutory orders, 30 days (strict compliance); all measured from the filing in the record of a copy of the notice (or mailing, if later).","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 52.1. Review procedures; limits on interlocutory certiorari","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Appeal, certiorari, certification, and other review follow the applicable statute, these rules, and the Supreme Court’s rules. The Court of Appeals may issue certiorari to review an interlocutory order of the Court of First Instance only for orders under Rules 56 (provisional remedies) and 57 (injunctions) or the denial of a dispositive motion, and, by exception, for rulings on the admissibility of essential fact or ","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 52.2. Deadlines and effects of appeal, certiorari, and certification","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Appeals to the Court of Appeals or Supreme Court from judgments must be filed within a jurisdictional (jurisdiccional) 30 days after a copy of the notice of judgment is filed in the record; certiorari to the Court of Appeals from final resolutions in voluntary-jurisdiction proceedings, and to the Supreme Court from Court of Appeals decisions in appeals or in voluntary-jurisdiction certiorari, is also jurisdictional a","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 52.2(d). Certification to the Supreme Court","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The Supreme Court may, in its discretion and on its own or on request, bring up any matter pending in the Court of First Instance or Court of Appeals when there are conflicting Court of Appeals decisions, novel questions of law, or high-public-interest questions including substantial constitutional questions under the Puerto Rico or U.S. Constitution. It also accepts certified questions of Puerto Rico law that may be","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 52.2(e)–(h). Interruption of the time to seek review","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The time to appeal is interrupted by a timely motion under Rule 43.1 (findings), Rule 47 (reconsideration), or Rule 48 (new trial denied), and in the Supreme Court by a reconsideration motion in the Court of Appeals, restarting when a copy of the notice of the ruling on the motion is filed in the record. A timely reconsideration motion likewise interrupts the time to seek Supreme Court certiorari (computed from maili","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 52.3. Stay of proceedings","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Filing a notice of appeal stays lower-court proceedings on the judgment or the part appealed unless the appellate court orders otherwise, though the trial court may proceed on matters not appealed and may order perishable property sold and the proceeds deposited. Filing a certiorari petition does not stay proceedings unless the appellate court so orders, but issuance of the writ does stay them unless the Court of App","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 53. Special proceedings and extraordinary remedies","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Special legal proceedings and extraordinary remedies not covered by Rules 54–60 follow their governing statutes, with these rules applying where not inconsistent. A preliminary injunction where the main relief is a permanent injunction is governed exclusively by Rule 57 and applicable special laws, while an order to do or refrain as a provisional remedy securing a judgment in an action not mainly for injunction is go","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 54. Mandamus","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A writ of mandamus, peremptory or alternative, is sought by sworn petition. If the right to immediate performance is clear and no excuse for nonperformance could be given, the court may order the relief peremptorily; otherwise it orders an answer, holds a hearing as soon as practicable taking evidence if needed, and decides promptly, enforcing its orders like any others.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 55. Exequatur (recognition of foreign judgments)","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 55.1–55.6.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 55.1. Definition","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Exequatur is the judicial validation and recognition of another jurisdiction’s judgment by the courts of the forum where it is to be enforced, and may proceed ex parte or as an ordinary action.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 55.2. Initial pleading","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The applicant files in the proper sala of the Court of First Instance either a complaint against all others affected by the judgment or an ex parte petition sworn by all affected persons; where minors’ or incapacitated persons’ interests may be affected, parents with patria potestad or guardians must be included.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 55.3. Required documents","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The filing must attach a certified, legible, complete copy of the judgment that satisfies the Rules of Evidence, and a faithful Spanish translation if the judgment is not in Spanish or English.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 55.4. Notice","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Besides serving affected persons under Rule 4, the applicant must serve the Family Affairs Prosecutor (Procurador de Asuntos de Familia) when minors’ or incapacitated persons’ interests may be affected, the Public Prosecutor (Ministerio Fiscal) when validation is sought for recording in the Property Registry under Article 45 of the Mortgage and Property Registry Law (30 LPRA sec. 2208), and the Secretary of Justice w","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 55.5. Standards for recognition","Puerto Rico · civil rules","After resolving procedural issues, the court recognizes a judgment of a U.S. state or territory if rendered by a court with personal and subject-matter jurisdiction, with due process, and not procured by fraud. A judgment from any other jurisdiction additionally must come from a competent court, observe basic due process, arise from an impartial system without bias against foreigners, and not be contrary to public or","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 55.6. Execution","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A recognized foreign judgment is executed under the procedures that apply to Puerto Rico judgments.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 56. Provisional remedies","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 56.1–56.8 on remedies to secure the effectiveness of a judgment (attachment, garnishment, prohibition against alienation, replevin, receivership, and orders to do or refrain).","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 56.1. General principles","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Before or after judgment, on a claimant’s motion, the court may issue any provisional order needed to secure the effectiveness of the judgment, including attachment (embargo), garnishment of funds held by a third party, prohibition against alienation (prohibición de enajenar), recovery and delivery of personal property, receivership, an order to do or refrain from specific acts, or any other appropriate measure, weig","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 56.2. Notice and hearing","Puerto Rico · civil rules","No provisional remedy may be granted, modified, or vacated without notice to the adverse party and a hearing, except as Rules 56.4 and 56.5 allow. If sought before the defendant is served, the movant must serve the order setting the hearing along with the pleadings, the motion, and supporting documents.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 56.3. Bond","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A provisional remedy may be granted without bond if public or private documents signed before a person authorized to administer oaths show the obligation is legally enforceable; if the movant is an indigent litigant expressly exempt by law from filing fees, the complaint shows an evident or demonstrable likelihood of success, and after a hearing there is well-founded fear that without the remedy the judgment would be","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 56.4. Attachment or prohibition against alienation","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If Rule 56.3 is satisfied, the court must issue an attachment or prohibition against alienation on motion, but not without prior notice and hearing unless the claimant shows a prior property interest in the thing, extraordinary circumstances, or likely success through reliable documentary evidence that the debt is liquid, due, and payable. An affected party may move at any time to modify or vacate an ex parte order, ","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 56.5. Order to do or refrain","Puerto Rico · civil rules","No order to do or refrain from a specific act may issue without notice to the adverse party unless sworn specific facts clearly show irreparable harm before a hearing can be held, or extraordinary circumstances, or likely success through reliable documentary evidence. An ex parte order is effective on notification, and any affected party may move at any time to modify or vacate it, to be heard at the earliest date an","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 56.6. Receivers","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A receiver may be appointed only if no other provisional remedy would be effective, acts under the rules for judicial administration of estates unless otherwise ordered, may not be a party, a party’s attorney, or an interested person without the affected parties’ written consent on file, and may be required to post a bond before acting.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 56.7. Cancellation of a recorded attachment","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The court where the action is pending may order cancellation of a preventive annotation of attachment after a hearing and on posting of a bond in a reasonable amount, considering the plaintiff’s likelihood of success, the property’s value, and other circumstances.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 56.8. Enforcement","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Orders under Rule 56 may be enforced through the court’s civil contempt power.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 57. Injunctions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 57.1–57.7 on temporary restraining orders (entredicho provisional), preliminary and permanent injunctions, bonds, labor disputes, and injunctions pending review.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 57.1. Temporary restraining order","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A TRO may issue without notice only if a sworn affidavit or verified complaint clearly shows immediate and irreparable injury before the adverse party can be heard, and the applicant or counsel certifies in writing any efforts made to give notice and why notice should not be required. An ex parte TRO must state its date and hour of issuance, be filed and entered at once, define the injury and why it is irreparable an","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 57.2. Preliminary injunction","Puerto Rico · civil rules","No preliminary injunction may issue without prior notice, served like a summons under Rule 4.4 by delivering the order with a copy of the petition, which has the same effect as service of summons, with proof and amendment under Rules 4.7 and 4.8. Before or after the hearing begins, the court may consolidate the trial on the merits with it; even without consolidation, evidence admitted at the hearing that would be adm","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 57.3. Factors for a TRO or preliminary injunction","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The court must consider, among others, the nature of the harm to the petitioner, irreparability or lack of an adequate remedy at law, the movant’s likelihood of prevailing, the likelihood the case will become moot, the impact of the relief on the public interest, and the petitioner’s diligence and good faith.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 57.4. Bond","Puerto Rico · civil rules","No TRO or preliminary injunction issues without a bond in the amount the court deems just for costs and damages of a party wrongfully restrained; no bond is required of the Commonwealth, its municipalities, agencies, or officials in their official capacity. The surety submits to the court’s jurisdiction, appoints the Clerk as agent for service, and may be held liable on motion without a separate suit.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 57.5. Form and scope of injunctive orders","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Every TRO or preliminary or permanent injunction must state the reasons for issuance, be specific in terms, and describe the restrained acts in reasonable detail without referring to the complaint or other documents. It binds only the parties, their officers, agents, servants, employees, and attorneys, and persons in active concert or participation with them who receive notice of the order by any means.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 57.6. Labor disputes","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Rule 57 does not modify Law No. 50 of August 4, 1947 (29 LPRA secs. 101–107) on restraining orders and injunctions in cases arising from labor disputes, or any other Puerto Rico law on injunctions in suits affecting employers and employees.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 57.7. Injunction pending appeal or certiorari","Puerto Rico · civil rules","When an order or judgment granting, dissolving, or denying an injunction is appealed or reviewed, the trial court may in its discretion suspend, modify, restore, or grant an injunction during review on bond and other terms protecting the opposing party. The appellate court or a judge of it may also stay proceedings, act on the injunction, or preserve the status quo, but when review is sought of an order dissolving or","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 58. Eminent domain (expropiación forzosa)","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 58.1–58.9 on condemnation of real and personal property.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 58.1. Applicability of other rules","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The Rules of Civil Procedure govern condemnation of personal and real property except where they conflict with Rule 58 or a special law; when the amount involved does not exceed the Rule 60 limit, the court may simplify the proceedings substantially along Rule 60 lines.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 58.2. Joinder of properties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The plaintiff may join one or more properties in one action, whether of the same or different owners and whether or not for the same use.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 58.3. Complaint and condemnation file","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The complaint is captioned under Rule 8.1 but names the property as “defendant,” described generally by kind, quantity, and location, plus at least one owner of a part or interest. It must state the authority and statute, the public use, a description sufficient to identify the property, the rights and duration sought, the defendants for each property, the time requested for taking possession, and a request for an or","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 58.4. Summons in condemnation","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The plaintiff delivers summonses to the Clerk at filing for immediate issuance, with the same effect as Rule 4 service; each must state the court, title, defendant, that the action is for condemnation, a description of that defendant’s property, the right sought, the authority, the use, and that the defendant may serve an answer on plaintiff’s counsel within 20 days after service (30 days if served by edict), with co","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 58.5. Appearance or answer","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A defendant with no objection may simply file an appearance identifying the property in which it claims an interest and then receives notice of all proceedings. A defendant with defenses or objections must serve an answer within 20 days after notice of the condemnation (30 days if served by edict) identifying the property, the nature and extent of its interest, and all defenses and objections, which are otherwise wai","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 58.6. Amendment of pleadings","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The plaintiff may amend without leave until a defendant first appears; afterward leave is required, at any time before the compensation hearing, only for good cause and not if it would amount to a dismissal barred by Rule 58.8. The plaintiff need not serve a copy of the amendment but must give notice of its filing under Rule 67 to appearing parties affected and under Rule 58.4 to nonappearing parties affected, and de","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 58.7. Substitution of parties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If a defendant dies, becomes incapacitated, or transfers its interest after joinder, the court may order substitution on motion and notice of hearing, served on nonparties as in Rule 58.4(c).","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 58.8. Dismissal","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The plaintiff may dismiss as to a property by notice if no compensation hearing has begun and it has not acquired title or another interest or taken possession; by stipulation with the interested defendant before entry of judgment vesting title or possession (and the court may vacate a judgment if so stipulated); or by court order on motion and hearing any time before compensation is determined and paid, but not as t","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 58.9. Deposit and distribution","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The plaintiff deposits any money the law requires as a condition of condemnation, and may deposit where a statute permits; court and counsel must expedite distribution and the determination and payment of just compensation. If the final award exceeds what a defendant received from the deposit, judgment enters for the deficiency against the plaintiff; if less, judgment enters against the defendant for the excess. Ever","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 59. Declaratory judgments","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 59.1–59.5.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 59.1. When available","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The Court of First Instance may declare rights, status, and other legal relations whether or not other relief is or could be sought, and a request for declaratory relief is not a ground to attack an action; the declaration may be affirmative or negative and has the effect of a final judgment. Regardless of Rule 37, the court may order a speedy hearing and give the case calendar priority.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 59.2. Who may seek it; interpretation","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Anyone interested in a deed, will, written contract, or other contract document, or whose rights or status are affected by a statute, municipal ordinance, contract, or franchise, may seek a determination of any question of construction or validity and a declaration of resulting rights; a contract may be construed before or after breach. Executors, administrators, trustees, fiduciaries, guardians, creditors, legatees,","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 59.3. Court’s discretion","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The court may decline to render or enter a declaratory judgment that would not end the uncertainty or controversy giving rise to the proceeding.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 59.4. Further relief","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Further necessary or proper relief based on a declaratory judgment may be sought by application to a court with jurisdiction to grant it; if sufficient, the court orders any adverse party whose rights were adjudicated to show cause within a reasonable time why the relief should not be granted at once.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 59.5. Parties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","All persons who have or claim an interest that would be affected by the declaration must be made parties, and no declaration prejudices nonparties; when the validity of a municipal ordinance or franchise is at issue, the municipality must be made a party and the Secretary of Justice notified under Rule 21.3.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 60. Claims of $15,000 or less (summary procedure)","Puerto Rico · civil rules","For a collection suit for no more than $15,000, excluding interest, unless the complaint requests ordinary procedure, the plaintiff files a proposed notice-summons (notificación-citación) that the Clerk issues at once and must serve it with a copy of the complaint within 10 days after filing, by personal delivery under Rule 4 or by certified mail. The notice sets the trial date, which must be no later than 3 months a","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 61. The court always open","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The court is always open, within hours the Supreme Court sets, for filing papers, issuing and returning writs, and making orders.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 62. Hearings and records","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 62.1–62.3.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 62.1. Hearings, chambers orders, and access to records","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Trials on the merits are held in open court unless the nature of the proceeding, a law, or the court provides otherwise; other acts may be done by the judge in chambers or elsewhere without the Clerk. Records made confidential by law or court order may be shown or copied only to persons with a legitimate interest or by court order for good cause, and to court officials or accredited researchers only on a showing of n","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 62.2. Lost papers","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If a paper in the record is lost, the court may allow a copy to be filed and used in its place.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 62.3. Visual record for parties unable to communicate effectively","Puerto Rico · civil rules","When a party who is deaf or hard of hearing or otherwise cannot communicate effectively is before the Court of First Instance, the court, in its discretion and on request, must arrange for hearings and other in-person proceedings, including preliminary ones, to be video- or digitally recorded to preserve sign-language, lip-reading, or other accommodated testimony and argument; the recording becomes part of the record","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 63. Disqualification and recusal of the judge","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 63.1–63.2.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 63.1. Grounds","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A judge must withdraw, on the judge’s own initiative or on a recusal motion, for bias or prejudice toward any person or attorney in the case or having prejudged it; a personal or financial interest in the outcome; kinship within the fourth degree of consanguinity or affinity with the prosecutor, family-affairs or minors’ prosecutor, guardian ad litem, any party, or any party’s counsel; a friendship with a party, atto","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 63.2. Recusal motion and procedure","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A recusal motion must be sworn and filed before the challenged judge within 20 days after the movant learns of the ground, stating specific facts with supporting documents and affidavits; if these formalities are not met, the judge may continue with the case. If the judge agrees, a written resolution states the applicable ground under Rule 63.1(a)–(i) or the specific reason under (j), notifies the parties, and the ca","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 64. Substitution of judge","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If a judge cannot continue because of death, illness, retirement, or another reason, another judge may act; if trial has begun or ended and the successor cannot perform the duties without a new trial on all or part of the facts or rehearing a witness, the successor may take the steps needed to decide the case.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 65. The Clerk’s office (Secretaría)","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 65.1–65.4.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 65.1. Office hours","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The Clerk’s office is open every business day during working hours except Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays, but special rule or administrative order may extend hours or open it on holidays.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 65.2. Clerk’s actions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The Clerk handles motions and applications for writs, entries of default, default judgments, and other matters not requiring a judge’s order, subject to the court’s power to suspend, alter, or vacate for good cause. Where a writ must issue on a judicial order, a certified copy of the order suffices with full legal effect, and the Clerk may not reject any document for failure to meet formal requirements of these rules","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 65.3. Notice of orders, resolutions, and judgments","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Immediately after a copy of the notice of entry of an order, resolution, or judgment is filed in the record, the Clerk must notify it the same day to all appearing parties under Rule 67, and mailing is sufficient notice; notice goes to a pro se party’s last address of record or to counsel’s address in the Supreme Court registry. Defaulted parties who appeared are notified the same way; defaulted parties served person","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 65.4. Court seal","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The court’s seal is affixed to writs, summonses, subpoenas, arrest orders, and copies of record documents certified by the Clerk or another officer.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 66. Register of actions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The Clerk keeps a “Register of Actions, Proceedings, and Interlocutory Orders” recording every action, proceeding, and interlocutory order under the applicable regulation, with the date each document or judgment is registered.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 67. Service and filing of papers","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 67.1–67.6.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 67.1. When service is required","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Every court order and every paper filed by a party must be served on all parties on the same day the order issues or the paper is filed. Parties in default for failure to appear need not be served, except that pleadings seeking new or additional relief against them must be served as a summons is under Rule 4.4 or, failing that, Rule 4.6.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 67.2. How service is made","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Service on a represented party is made on the attorney unless the court orders service on the party, by delivery or by mail, fax, or electronic means to a pro se party’s last address of record or counsel’s address in the Supreme Court registry; if the address is unknown, the court is so informed with a copy of the paper. Delivery means handing it to the attorney or party, leaving it with a clerk or person in charge a","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 67.3. Certificate of service","Puerto Rico · civil rules","When an order must by its nature be served by a party or counsel on another person, proof of service must be filed within the time the court sets by a certification of compliance with the order’s requirements, subject to Rule 9.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 67.4. Filing papers","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Every paper after the complaint must be filed with the court, but depositions, interrogatories, requests for admission, answers to them, and offers of judgment are not filed until needed in the proceedings or ordered filed.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 67.5. How papers are filed","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Pleadings and papers are filed with the Clerk, though a judge may accept papers, note the date received, and immediately send them to the Clerk. After filing, counsel may also send the judge a courtesy copy of a paper needing immediate attention, by delivery to authorized chambers staff, fax, or electronic means, stating that the other side was previously advised, and must then serve the other side on the same date a","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 67.6. Electronic filing and service","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Once the necessary administrative measures and technology are in place, pleadings, motions, and other papers are filed electronically, and orders, resolutions, interlocutory rulings, judgments, and Clerk notices are served electronically. Electronic transmission to the address or portal the Chief Justice designates constitutes filing and also service among counsel and pro se parties, and an electronic signature satis","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 68. Time","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 68.1–68.3.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 68.1. Computing time","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Exclude the day of the triggering act, event, or default, and include the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday, or legal holiday, in which case the period runs to the end of the next day that is not. The Supreme Court may suspend or extend any term by resolution for good cause; when a period is less than 7 days, intermediate Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays are excluded, and a half-day holiday counts as a f","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 68.2. Extending or shortening time","Puerto Rico · civil rules","For good cause the court may at any time, with or without motion or notice, extend or shorten a period if requested before it expires (as originally set or previously extended), or on motion after it expires permit the act if the failure was due to good cause. But it may not extend or shorten the periods under Rules 43.1, 44.1, 47, 48.2, 48.4, 49.2, and 52.2 except as those rules themselves provide.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 68.3. Additional time after service by mail","Puerto Rico · civil rules","When a party must act within a period after service of a notice or paper and it is served by mail, 3 days are added, except for periods counted from the filing in the record of a copy of the notice of judgment. Periods counted from the filing of a copy of the notice of a judgment, resolution, or order run from the date the notice is mailed when that differs from the filing date.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 69. Bonds","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Chapter heading for Rules 69.1–69.9, including the nonresident cost bond.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 69.1. Personal sureties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A personal bond must be accompanied by the surety’s sworn written statement that the surety resides in Puerto Rico, owns the real property offered, and has assets, excluding exempt property, worth twice the bond amount after all debts; multiple sureties must state that their combined liability equals that of one good surety. The statement must give the surety’s residence, describe and estimate the value of the qualif","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 69.2. Corporate sureties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A corporation organized under Puerto Rico or any state’s law to issue bonds or guarantee legally required obligations may act as surety, alone or with others, with the same rights and liabilities as a natural-person surety, if it complies with all Puerto Rico legal requirements for such businesses.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 69.3. Cash deposit in lieu of bond","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Whenever a bond is required, the court may order and accept a deposit of the full bond amount instead.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 69.4. Mortgage bond","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A mortgage bond must be secured by real property whose unencumbered value exceeds the required bond by one-third, proved by a certified market appraisal.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 69.5. Nonresident cost bond","Puerto Rico · civil rules","When the claimant resides outside Puerto Rico or is a foreign corporation, the court must require a bond of at least $1,000 to secure costs, expenses, and attorney’s fees that may be imposed, and all proceedings are stayed until it is posted; the court may require an additional bond if the first proves insufficient, with a further stay. If the bond or additional bond is not posted within 60 days after notice of the o","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 69.6. When no bond is required","Puerto Rico · civil rules","No bond is required of the Commonwealth, its officers in their official capacity, public corporations, or municipalities; of any party in divorce, family-relations, or community-property cases unless the court orders otherwise in meritorious cases; in support claims when the court so orders; or of an indigent litigant expressly exempt by law from filing fees.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 69.7. Approval of bonds","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Clerks, marshals, and other court officers may not accept a bond unless approved by the judge of the sala where the case is pending, except a cash deposit under Rule 69.3.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 69.8. Who may not be sureties","Puerto Rico · civil rules","No court officer, including attorneys, may act as surety in any action or proceeding.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 69.9. Cancellation of bond","Puerto Rico · civil rules","If a party who posted a bond prevails, the court must order the bond canceled in its final judgment.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 70. Time to decide cases and motions","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Contested cases heard on the merits and summary judgment motions must be decided within 90 days after they are submitted; all other motions, default cases, and other matters within 30 days after submission, though either period may be reasonably extended when the nature of the matter or an extraordinary cause requires. A case is submitted when the presentation of evidence ends, extended by any period allowed for lega","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 71. Mislabeled action or prayer","Puerto Rico · civil rules","A defect in naming the action or in the prayer for relief does not prevent the court from granting the relief warranted by the pleadings and evidence.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 72. Cases not provided for","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Where these rules provide no specific procedure, the court may regulate its practice in any manner consistent with the rules and applicable law.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 73. Repeal and savings clause","Puerto Rico · civil rules","Repeals the 1979 Rules of Civil Procedure, as amended, but keeps in force the articles of the 1933 Code of Civil Procedure that survived under former Rule 72. Articles 39–42, 44–49, 249, and 252 of the 1933 Code, Section 2 of Law No. 39 of April 20, 1942, and Articles 1 and 2 of Law No. 433 of May 14, 1951 remain provisionally in effect until repealed or modified by special law.","#st-pr-rules"],["Rule 74. Effective date","Puerto Rico · civil rules","The rules took effect July 1, 2010.","#st-pr-rules"],["Appeals in Puerto Rico: pick the right recurso, then watch whether the term is jurisdictional","Puerto Rico · appeals","Puerto Rico has two appellate courts. The Tribunal de Apelaciones (TA) hears appeals (apelación) from final civil and criminal judgments of the Tribunal de Primera Instancia (TPI), discretionary certiorari against interlocutory orders and a few other rulings, judicial review (revisión) of final agency decisions, and some original writs. The Tribunal Supremo (TS) reviews the TA almost entirely by discretionary certior","#st-pr-appeals"],["Apelación to the TA: final civil judgments","Puerto Rico · appeals · Which recurso, which court","A final judgment (sentencia) of the TPI in a civil case is reviewed by apelación. The escrito de apelación is the appellant’s brief; no separate brief or memorandum of authorities is allowed. Interlocutory rulings not reviewable by certiorari can be raised in the appeal from the judgment, subject to the harmless-error rule (Regla 50 R. Proc. Civ.). Regla 13 y 16(C)(2) Reglamento TA; Reglas 52.1 y 52.2(a) R. Proc. Civ","#st-pr-appeals"],["Certiorari to the TA: civil interlocutory orders, only on listed subjects","Puerto Rico · appeals · Which recurso, which court","The TA may issue certiorari against a TPI interlocutory order or resolution only when it involves Rule 56 (provisional remedies) or Rule 57 (injunction), or the denial of a dispositive motion. By exception, it may also review rulings on the admissibility of essential fact or expert witnesses, evidentiary privileges, entries of default (anotaciones de rebeldía), family-relations cases, cases of public interest, or any","#st-pr-appeals"],["Certiorari to the TA: other rulings that go by certiorari","Puerto Rico · appeals · Which recurso, which court","Also by certiorari: (1) a criminal conviction on a guilty plea, which cannot be appealed (Regla 193 R. Proc. Crim.; Regla 32(A) Reglamento TA); (2) final resolutions in non-contentious proceedings (jurisdicción voluntaria) (Regla 32(B)); (3) any other order reviewable by certiorari under law, which the 2025 text says expressly includes protection orders and a TPI judgment on a petition to review an arbitration award ","#st-pr-appeals"],["Criminal apelación to the TA","Puerto Rico · appeals · Which recurso, which court","The accused may appeal a final judgment after trial. The escrito de apelación in a criminal case is a short notice (see page limits); the full brief (alegato) comes later, 30 days after the record (expediente) is sent up, unless the TA orders otherwise. A guilty-plea conviction goes by certiorari, not appeal. A self-represented appellant in prison files by handing the notice to the custodian within the term. Reglas 1","#st-pr-appeals"],["Revisión judicial to the TA: final agency decisions","Puerto Rico · appeals · Which recurso, which court","Final orders, resolutions and regulations of administrative agencies (adjudicative or quasi-legislative) are reviewed by recurso de revisión under Part VII of the Reglamento TA and the LPAU (Ley 38-2017). A simplified recurso de revisión especial (a court form, which may be handwritten) is available to a person acting pro se against a final decision denying welfare (beneficencia social) services or benefits. Reglas 5","#st-pr-appeals"],["Original writs at the TA: habeas corpus and mandamus","Puerto Rico · appeals · Which recurso, which court","File the petition with the TA Secretaría, not directly with an individual judge (allowed only in extreme urgency when the court and Secretaría are unavailable). Serve all parties under the Rules of Civil Procedure. For mandamus against a judge to perform a ministerial duty in a pending case, the judge is not served with summons; notify the judge, the other parties and the court where the case is pending under Regla 1","#st-pr-appeals"],["Electoral cases: a separate fast track","Puerto Rico · appeals · Which recurso, which court","TPI judgments reviewing State Elections Commission (CEE) decisions under Art. 13.3 of the Código Electoral de 2020 (Ley 58-2020) go to the TA by certiorari under Part V, with terms of 10 days, 48 hours or 24 hours depending on how close the election is. Review in the TS goes by certiorari under the TS electoral rule. Reglas 41, 42 y 53 Reglamento TA; Regla 22 Reglamento TS","#st-pr-appeals"],["To the Tribunal Supremo: certiorari is the normal route","Puerto Rico · appeals · Which recurso, which court","The TS reviews TA judgments and resolutions by certiorari, which it issues at its discretion. A direct apelación to the TS is available only where the TA judgment declares a law, joint or concurrent resolution, rule, agency regulation or municipal ordinance unconstitutional, or where there is a substantial conflict among prior TA decisions; if the TS finds neither, it may treat the appeal as a certiorari, which must ","#st-pr-appeals"],["Orders in aid of jurisdiction (auxilio de jurisdicción)","Puerto Rico · appeals · Which recurso, which court","To stop something from happening while a recurso is pending, ask the TA for an order in aid of jurisdiction. File it in the TA Secretaría with the case caption, follow the form rules for motions, and notify the other parties and anyone the order would bind at the same moment you file (personally, by phone or by email). If you file it with the recurso, that simultaneous notice must include the recurso and its appendix","#st-pr-appeals"],["Effect of filing: what is and is not stayed","Puerto Rico · appeals · Which recurso, which court","Filing a civil appeal stays the TPI proceedings on what is appealed (the TPI may go on with other matters). It does not stay an injunction, mandamus or order to do or desist, a support (alimentos) order, or a custody or visitation order, unless the TA orders it. Filing a certiorari stays nothing unless the TA orders it; issuing the writ stays the TPI proceedings unless the TA says otherwise. Reglas 18 y 35 Reglamento","#st-pr-appeals"],["Jurisdictional terms cannot be extended or excused","Puerto Rico · appeals · Jurisdictional vs. strict-compliance terms (the classic trap)","The TA will not grant an extension of a jurisdictional term, and a recurso filed late must be dismissed for lack of jurisdiction. The TPI cannot extend the terms in Civil Rules 47 (reconsideration), 48.2 (new trial) or 52.2 (appeal and certiorari) either. Jurisdictional: civil appeals (30 or 60 days); criminal appeals (30); certiorari against a guilty-plea conviction (30); certiorari in jurisdicción voluntaria (30); ","#st-pr-appeals"],["Strict-compliance terms: excusable only for good cause, shown up front","Puerto Rico · appeals · Jurisdictional vs. strict-compliance terms (the classic trap)","A strict-compliance term may be exceeded only when special circumstances are duly supported in the filing itself; the court may dismiss a recurso filed outside such a term without good cause. Put the explanation in the petition, not in a later motion. Strict-compliance: certiorari to the TA against interlocutory orders and the other Regla 32(C) rulings (30 days); certiorari to the TS against TA rulings in discretiona","#st-pr-appeals"],["Defects of form and service: the court should let you fix them","Puerto Rico · appeals · Jurisdictional vs. strict-compliance terms (the classic trap)","The TA must read its notice and form requirements to minimize dismissals and, for good cause, give a reasonable chance to cure form or service defects that do not affect the parties’ rights. The 2025 text of Regla 83(C) adds that the TA loses jurisdiction only for failure to meet a jurisdictional or strict-compliance requirement. This forgiveness does not reach a late jurisdictional filing. Reglas 12.1 y 83(C) Reglam","#st-pr-appeals"],["Premature recursos","Puerto Rico · appeals · Jurisdictional vs. strict-compliance terms (the classic trap)","A recurso filed while a timely reconsideration motion is still pending in the TPI is premature: the appeal term has not started running again. The TA may take steps to make the later appeal cheaper, but expect to refile after the reconsideration is decided and notified, within the new term. Regla 83(E) Reglamento TA; Regla 47 R. Proc. Civ.","#st-pr-appeals"],["The trigger is the archivo en autos of the copy of the notice","Puerto Rico · appeals · When the clock starts and how to count","Civil appeal and certiorari terms run from the filing in the record (archivo en autos) of a copy of the notice of the judgment or order. In SUMAC cases, that date is the date the platform sent the notice. If notice went by post and the mailing date differs from the archivo en autos date, count from the mailing date. Civil Rule 47 and 52.2(b) speak of the “fecha de notificación” for interlocutory orders; Regla 32(C) o","#st-pr-appeals"],["Criminal judgments: from the day sentence is pronounced","Puerto Rico · appeals · When the clock starts and how to count","Criminal appeal and guilty-plea certiorari terms run from the date the sentence was dictated if the defendant was present in the courtroom. If not present, count from the mailing of the notice when that date differs from the archivo en autos. Reglas 193 y 194 R. Proc. Crim.; Reglas 23(A) y 32(A) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Counting days","Puerto Rico · appeals · When the clock starts and how to count","Do not count the day of the triggering event. If the last day is a Saturday, Sunday or legal holiday, the term runs to the end of the next business day. For terms under 7 days, intermediate weekends and holidays are excluded. A half-day holiday counts as a full holiday. The 3 extra days for service by mail do not apply to terms that run from the archivo en autos of a judgment. Reglas 68.1 y 68.3 R. Proc. Civ.","#st-pr-appeals"],["Deadline hour","Puerto Rico · appeals · When the clock starts and how to count","TA: an electronic filing is due by 11:59 p.m. on the last day, and SUMAC accepts filings every day, including weekends and holidays. A paper filing must be made at the Secretaría from 8:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m., or in the after-hours drop box (buzón) from 5:00 p.m. to 11:59 p.m. on business days. The SUMAC hours do not change how terms are computed. TS: its Reglamento still says 5:00 p.m. unless the court adopts extended","#st-pr-appeals"],["Extensions: ask at least 3 business days ahead","Puerto Rico · appeals · When the clock starts and how to count","An extension request must reach the Secretaría at least 3 business days before the term expires. Any extension runs from the original due date unless the panel says otherwise. Late filings are not accepted unless the panel allows them, and jurisdictional terms are never extended. In the TS, extension motions need concrete facts; an attorney’s heavy workload is generally not good cause. Regla 72(B)–(C) Reglamento TA; ","#st-pr-appeals"],["In the TPI (civil): Rule 47 reconsideration","Puerto Rico · appeals · Reconsideration and other motions that stop the clock","Against a judgment: 15 days from the archivo en autos of the notice of judgment (jurisdictional). Against an interlocutory order or resolution: 15 days from its notice (strict compliance). The motion must state the facts and law with particularity; one that does not is denied and does not interrupt the term. Serve it on the other parties within the same 15 days (strict compliance). A proper, timely motion interrupts ","#st-pr-appeals"],["Other civil motions that interrupt the appeal term","Puerto Rico · appeals · Reconsideration and other motions that stop the clock","A timely Rule 43.1 motion for initial or additional findings and a timely Rule 48 new-trial motion (generally 15 days from the archivo en autos of the notice of judgment) also interrupt the appeal term, which restarts from the archivo en autos of the notice of the order deciding them. Reglas 48.2 y 52.2(e)(1), (3) R. Proc. Civ.","#st-pr-appeals"],["In the TPI (criminal)","Puerto Rico · appeals · Reconsideration and other motions that stop the clock","A motion to reconsider the sentence or conviction filed within 15 days (not extendable) after sentence interrupts the appeal or certiorari term, which restarts from notice of the ruling on it. A new-trial motion under Rules 188(e) and 192 filed within the 30-day appeal term lets the appeal be filed within 30 days after notice of the order denying it. The criminal rules compilation in Consi’s sources is current only t","#st-pr-appeals"],["In the TA: 15 days, one motion","Puerto Rico · appeals · Reconsideration and other motions that stop the clock","A motion to reconsider any TA decision, civil or criminal, must be filed within 15 days (not extendable) from the archivo en autos of the notice of the decision. A timely motion suspends the term to go to the TS, which starts again from the archivo en autos of the notice of the resolution that finally decides it. Argue only the specific errors; the other side may oppose within 10 days. A party whose first motion was ","#st-pr-appeals"],["In the TS: 10 business days, jurisdictional","Puerto Rico · appeals · Reconsideration and other motions that stop the clock","A motion to reconsider a TS decision is due within 10 business days (jurisdictional), may not exceed 10 pages, and cannot be extended or supported by a separate memorandum. A second motion by the same party is allowed only within 3 business days after notice of the denial of the first; no further motions. In electoral certiorari the term is 3 days. Regla 45 Reglamento TS; Regla 22(f) Reglamento TS","#st-pr-appeals"],["Agency reconsideration","Puerto Rico · appeals · Reconsideration and other motions that stop the clock","The 30-day revisión term runs from the archivo en autos of the notice of the agency’s final order, or from the applicable date when a timely reconsideration motion interrupted it. The agency-level reconsideration terms are in the LPAU (Ley 38-2017), which is not among Consi’s sources for this page; check the statute. Regla 57 Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["60 days to appeal when the government is a party: for every party","Puerto Rico · appeals · Government parties get 60 days (sometimes)","When the Commonwealth (ELA), its officials, one of its instrumentalities other than a public corporation, or a municipality or its officials is a party, any aggrieved party in that suit, not only the government, has a 60-day jurisdictional term to appeal a TPI judgment to the TA. Regla 13(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 52.2(c) R. Proc. Civ.","#st-pr-appeals"],["60 days in the TS too, but only for appeal-track cases","Puerto Rico · appeals · Government parties get 60 days (sometimes)","The 60-day term also applies to certiorari to the TS against a TA judgment in an appeal and to a direct civil appeal to the TS in such cases. Regla 52.2(c) R. Proc. Civ.; Reglas 17(a) y 20(a)(1) Reglamento TS","#st-pr-appeals"],["Where the 60 days does not apply","Puerto Rico · appeals · Government parties get 60 days (sometimes)","Public corporations are excluded. Civil Rule 52.2(c) covers only appeals and TS certiorari against TA judgments in appeals; certiorari to the TA against interlocutory orders stays at 30 days. Criminal terms and the 30-day administrative revisión term have no government extension in these sources (a special law may set a different term). Regla 52.2(b)–(c) R. Proc. Civ.; Reglas 23, 32 y 57 Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Electronic filing is mandatory at the TA","Puerto Rico · appeals · Filing through SUMAC","All recursos and other filings go through SUMAC (the Sistema Unificado de Manejo y Administración de Casos), and filing in SUMAC equals filing in the Secretaría. Exceptions: self-represented litigants (until the technology is available), items that by nature, law or court order cannot be e-filed, and technical problems with the platform. Cases filed on paper before SUMAC stay on paper. Sign as “f/full name of the att","#st-pr-appeals"],["Not filed until the fee is paid","Puerto Rico · appeals · Filing through SUMAC","The date the filing fee (arancel) is paid is the filing date. The SUMAC receipt (comprobante) says the document is not officially filed until fees are paid, and the filing date does not relate back to the upload. Paying at the deadline is risky: pay electronically in SUMAC the same day. Note that the SUMAC directives treat fee-bearing filings as an exception to the rule that anything filed by 11:59 p.m. counts as fil","#st-pr-appeals"],["File formats","Puerto Rico · appeals · Filing through SUMAC","Each main document and each attachment must be a legible PDF of no more than 4 MB. Scans should be black and white, both sides, portrait orientation. Electronic filings still must meet the form rules of the Reglamento TA. Directrices SUMAC, Sec. VIII","#st-pr-appeals"],["When SUMAC fails","Puerto Rico · appeals · Filing through SUMAC","Problems with your own equipment never excuse any term. If the platform is down or in maintenance, file in person at the Secretaría or in the drop box before the term expires (8:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m.). The Courts Administration Office may announce that filings can be emailed as PDFs, and the TA has its own SUMAC email address for that. If the problem is not the platform’s, a filing by email or in person is allowed onl","#st-pr-appeals"],["Telling the lower court","Puerto Rico · appeals · Filing through SUMAC","If the TPI case has an electronic record, SUMAC automatically posts an entry in it, which satisfies the duty to notify the lower court. If not, file the stamped cover or first page with the TPI Secretaría within 72 hours (civil appeal) or 48 hours (criminal appeal, certiorari). On paper: a civil appeal filed at the TA requires notice to the TPI within 72 hours; a recurso filed at the TPI must be sent in original to t","#st-pr-appeals"],["The Tribunal Supremo still describes paper filing","Puerto Rico · appeals · Filing through SUMAC","The Reglamento TS (through ER-2023-0001) requires an original and 10 copies, with an option to file appendix copies on CD in PDF (plus the brief in PDF and Word). It has not been updated for SUMAC, and Consi’s sources do not say whether the TS now accepts SUMAC filings. Check with the TS Secretaría before filing. Regla 40(d) Reglamento TS","#st-pr-appeals"],["Keep your RUA email current","Puerto Rico · appeals · Filing through SUMAC","TA decisions are notified to attorneys at the email on file in the Registro Único de Abogados y Abogadas (RUA), and all electronic communications between counsel and with the court go to that address. Reglas 14(D), 15 y 71(B) Reglamento TA; Regla 9(j) Reglamento TS","#st-pr-appeals"],["In the TA, e-filing serves the lawyers, but not everyone","Puerto Rico · appeals · Serving (notifying) the other parties","Filing a recurso in SUMAC is service on counsel (and, in criminal cases and criminal certiorari, on the Procurador General and the District Attorney). The platform then lists who was notified; check that list. You must yourself serve self-represented parties and anyone else the law requires. Serve the recurso with its appendix within the filing term (strict compliance). Reglas 13(B), 23(B) y 33(B) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["When electronic service is not possible","Puerto Rico · appeals · Serving (notifying) the other parties","Serve a copy stamped with the filing date and time by certified mail or private courier with return receipt; ordinary mail, personal delivery or email (to an address in the TPI record) also work if the copy is exact and there is no dispute about the date or receipt. The service date is the SUMAC receipt date, the mailing date, or the courier’s intake date. For certiorari served by personal delivery, certify the manne","#st-pr-appeals"],["Certify how you served","Puerto Rico · appeals · Serving (notifying) the other parties","State in the recurso how and when you served; your signature certifies it. Report any change in a supplementary motion within 48 hours after filing (3 business days in administrative revisión). Both are strict-compliance terms. Even with e-filing, lawyers must still state the method of service. Reglas 13(B)(1), 15, 58(B)(4) y 69(B) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Administrative revisión: serve the agency too","Puerto Rico · appeals · Serving (notifying) the other parties","Serve the stamped recurso on counsel of record in the agency proceeding (or the parties) and on the agency or official whose decision is challenged, within the filing term (strict compliance). Regla 58(B) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Later filings","Puerto Rico · appeals · Serving (notifying) the other parties","Every later filing is served at the same time it is filed, through the platform or, if that is not possible, by personal delivery, mail, courier or email, and the filing states how. Motions are served on the day they are filed. In criminal cases, later motions need not go to the District Attorney unless a rule or the court requires it. Reglas 24(C), 36, 68(A) y 71(A) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["In the TS","Puerto Rico · appeals · Serving (notifying) the other parties","Serve every party’s counsel (or unrepresented parties) within the term to file, by certified mail or courier with return receipt or by email; mailed service goes to the attorney’s RUA address and is dated by the mailing. Certify personal delivery within 72 hours. Also give the TA Secretaría a stamped copy of the recurso without the appendix within 72 hours (strict compliance). Reglas 17(b), 20(d)–(e) y 39 Reglamento ","#st-pr-appeals"],["What goes in a TA appendix","Puerto Rico · appeals · The appendix (apéndice)","For an apelación or certiorari: the pleadings (complaint, cross-claims, third-party complaints, counterclaims and answers; in criminal certiorari, the complaint and accusation); the decision under review with its findings and the notice of its archivo en autos; every motion (showing the TPI filing date), resolution and order needed to prove that the term was interrupted and restarted, with their notices; every filing","#st-pr-appeals"],["A missing document is not automatically fatal","Puerto Rico · appeals · The appendix (apéndice)","The TA may allow late filing of omitted appendix documents, on request in the recurso, by motion or on its own, within 15 days after notice of the order allowing it. If you do not cure within that time, the recurso may be dismissed. Reglas 16(E)(2) y 34(E)(2) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["How to build it in SUMAC","Puerto Rico · appeals · The appendix (apéndice)","Arrange documents chronologically. In an electronic case, the index points to the SUMAC entry of each document; SUMAC displays the entries you select, and that screen is the index, so you need not upload a separate one. Cite record documents by entry number and page (for example, “Ent. #123”). On paper, number pages consecutively and add an index of pages. Include only documents in the TPI or agency record, and do no","#st-pr-appeals"],["What goes in a TS appendix","Puerto Rico · appeals · The appendix (apéndice)","An exact and complete copy of: the recurso filed in the TA, the opposition and all parties’ briefs, with their appendices; the judgment or resolution under review with the notice (volante) of its archivo en autos, plus the envelope if the mailing date differs; any reconsideration ruling with its notice; any document needed to show jurisdiction, with a legible filing stamp; the transcript or narrative statement if the","#st-pr-appeals"],["TA page limits","Puerto Rico · appeals · Page limits and format","Apelación, certiorari, revisión, habeas corpus and mandamus petitions, certiorari oppositions, show-cause responses, and the briefs of appellees, respondents and intervenors: 25 pages in a paper case, 35 pages in an electronic case, not counting the certificate of service, index and appendix. Criminal escrito de apelación (the notice): 3 pages on paper, 6 electronic, not counting cover and certificate. Criminal brief","#st-pr-appeals"],["TA format","Puerto Rico · appeals · Page limits and format","Electronic filings: letter-size pages (8.5 × 11 in.). Paper cases: legal size (8.5 × 14 in.), printed on one side. Both: double-spaced, pica or larger type (except block quotes and footnotes), Courier or a similar 12-point font, left margin at least 1.5 in., right margin at least 0.5 in. No separate memorandum of authorities: argue the law in the body. The index is a table of contents that sets out each issue in full","#st-pr-appeals"],["Required contents of a TA recurso","Puerto Rico · appeals · Page limits and format","Cover (with the Judicial Region; in electronic cases leave off the TA case number, which SUMAC assigns), index, then in order: who appears, the provisions giving jurisdiction, the decision under review with its date and the archivo en autos date and any motions that interrupted the term, the facts, the errors assigned, the discussion, and the relief requested. Since 2025 you must also disclose any related recurso in ","#st-pr-appeals"],["TS page limits and format","Puerto Rico · appeals · Page limits and format","Certiorari petition or appeal: 25 pages, not counting index and appendix. Opposition to certiorari: 25 pages. Briefs (alegatos) after the writ issues or the appeal is accepted: 50 pages. Show-cause responses: 25 pages. Reconsideration: 10 pages. Times New Roman, Courier New or Arial 12-point, double-spaced, legal-size paper on one side, margins of at least 0.5 in.; original and 10 copies. The TS still requires an ind","#st-pr-appeals"],["No general language rule in the appellate rules Consi has","Puerto Rico · appeals · Language and translation","Neither the Reglamento TA nor the Reglamento TS, as provided to Consi, contains a general rule on the language of briefs; both courts’ rules and forms are in Spanish. In the TPI, pleadings, applications and motions may be in Spanish or English, documents in English need not be translated unless the court orders it (when justice requires, when translation is indispensable to a fair decision, or when a party asks), and","#st-pr-appeals"],["TS: certified questions and U.S. Supreme Court review","Puerto Rico · appeals · Language and translation","In certification from another jurisdiction, briefs may be in English or Spanish without translation, and the TS opinion is translated into English with the cost split among the parties unless otherwise ordered. An indigent litigant seeking U.S. Supreme Court review of a TS judgment may ask the TS for free certified English translations of the designated record. Reglas 25(h), 25(l) y 47 Reglamento TS","#st-pr-appeals"],["Say within 10 days whether you will reproduce the testimony","Puerto Rico · appeals · Oral evidence (transcripts) in the TA","Within 10 days after filing the recurso, tell the TA whether you will reproduce the oral evidence and by what method (transcript, stipulated narrative statement or other), identifying the portions, dates and witnesses. If an error depends on the evidence and you say nothing, the TA must order you to state your intent and warn that errors depending on the testimony may be deemed waived. The TA may instead let the part","#st-pr-appeals"],["Transcript and narrative timetable","Puerto Rico · appeals · Oral evidence (transcripts) in the TA","Once authorized, ask the lower court for the re-recording within 10 days after notice of the TA order (with fees). Prepare the transcript at your own cost within 30 days after receiving the re-recording, certified by the transcriber, with a witness index, and serve copies within the same time. For a stipulated narrative: draft to the other parties within 30 days of authorization, then 20 days to agree; failing agreem","#st-pr-appeals"],["The rewrite in one paragraph","Puerto Rico · appeals · What changed on June 16, 2025 (Resolución ER-2025-0001)","Resolución ER-2025-0001 (approved April 24, 2025; in force June 16, 2025; applies to pending cases) amended 56 rules, added Reglas 2.1 and 80.1, and folded Regla 76.1 into Regla 76 to fit the TA’s move to SUMAC and to speed up appellate practice. The points below come from the “Comentario – Enmiendas del 2025” notes in the official text. Resolución ER-2025-0003 (December 19, 2025) also amended the Reglamento; Consi h","#st-pr-appeals"],["E-filing, service and timing","Puerto Rico · appeals · What changed on June 16, 2025 (Resolución ER-2025-0001)","New Regla 2.1 makes SUMAC filing the rule and equal to filing in the Secretaría. The SUMAC send date is the archivo en autos. The fee-payment date is the filing date. E-filing a recurso serves opposing counsel, and SUMAC notifies the TPI automatically when the TPI case is electronic. Electronic filings are due at 11:59 p.m. (formerly 5:00 p.m.) on any day. The requirement to file 3 paper copies is gone. Electronic si","#st-pr-appeals"],["Briefs and appendices","Puerto Rico · appeals · What changed on June 16, 2025 (Resolución ER-2025-0001)","Page limits rose from 25 to 35 for electronic cases because pages dropped from legal to letter size. The index of authorities was eliminated. Record documents are cited by SUMAC entry (“Ent. #”), and SUMAC generates the appendix index. A voluminous appendix may go on CD or USB with leave. Recursos must disclose related cases, and the duty continues after filing. The criminal notice of appeal may now run 6 pages when ","#st-pr-appeals"],["Procedure","Puerto Rico · appeals · What changed on June 16, 2025 (Resolución ER-2025-0001)","Rules 76 and 76.1 on oral evidence were merged; the court must now ask about reproducing the evidence and warn of waiver, and may accept the re-recording instead of a transcript. New Regla 80.1 requires consolidation of recursos against the same ruling or arising from the same case. Regla 83(C) now says jurisdiction depends only on jurisdictional or strict-compliance requirements. Regla 79(C) lets the TA issue aid-of","#st-pr-appeals"],["Civil appeal (apelación) to the TA","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of judgment (SUMAC send date; mailing date if later).\nJurisdictional. Restarts after a timely Rule 43.1, 47 or 48 motion is decided and notified. Regla 13(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 52.2(a) R. Proc. Civ.","#st-pr-appeals"],["Civil appeal to the TA when the government is a party","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of judgment.\nJurisdictional. Applies to any aggrieved party when the ELA, its officials, a non-public-corporation instrumentality, or a municipality or its officials is a party. Regla 13(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 52.2(c) R. Proc. Civ.","#st-pr-appeals"],["Certiorari to the TA against interlocutory orders (civil or criminal), protection orders, and judgments on arbitration-award review","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the order (Civ. Rule 52.2(b) says “fecha de notificación”).\nStrict compliance: excusable only for special circumstances explained in the petition. No 60-day government extension. Regla 32(C) Reglamento TA; Regla 52.2(b) R. Proc. Civ.","#st-pr-appeals"],["Certiorari to the TA against final resolutions in jurisdicción voluntaria","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the resolution.\nJurisdictional, unless a special law sets another term. Regla 32(B) Reglamento TA; Regla 52.2(b) R. Proc. Civ.","#st-pr-appeals"],["Certiorari to the TA against a guilty-plea conviction","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from date the sentence was dictated (mailing date of notice if the defendant was not present).\nJurisdictional. Criminal rules compilation current only to Feb. 2020. Regla 32(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 193 R. Proc. Crim.","#st-pr-appeals"],["Criminal appeal to the TA","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from date the sentence was dictated; or notice of the order denying a timely new-trial motion (Rules 188(e), 192) or deciding a timely reconsideration.\nJurisdictional. Regla 23 counts from notice to the accused of the ruling; Rule 194 counts a reconsideration ruling from its archivo en autos. Criminal rules compilation current only to Feb. 2020. Regla 23(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 194 R. Proc. Crim.","#st-pr-appeals"],["Recurso de revisión (final agency decision) to the TA","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the final order (mailing date if later), or the applicable date after a timely reconsideration.\nJurisdictional unless a special law provides otherwise. Agency reconsideration terms are in the LPAU, not reviewed here. Regla 57 Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Recurso de revisión especial (welfare benefits, pro se)","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from receipt of the agency’s final order.\nOnly for self-represented persons challenging welfare-benefit decisions. Regla 67(C) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Electoral certiorari to the TA (CEE cases)","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the TPI judgment (or of the ruling on reconsideration).\nJurisdictional. Within 30 days before an election: 48 hours. Within 5 days before: 24 hours, with same-day notice. No reconsideration in those windows. Regla 42 Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Serve the recurso and appendix on the other parties (TA)","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","Within the filing term\nStrict compliance. E-filing serves counsel; you must serve self-represented and other required parties. Reglas 13(B)(1), 23(B), 33(B) y 58(B)(1) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Notify the TPI when its case has no electronic record","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","72 hours (civil appeal); 48 hours (criminal appeal, certiorari)\nStrict compliance. Not needed when SUMAC posts the entry automatically in an electronic TPI record. Reglas 14(A), 24(A) y 33(A) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Send the original to the TA after filing on paper at the TPI","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","48 hours\nStrict compliance. Only when paper filing is allowed under Regla 2.1. Reglas 14(C), 24(B) y 33(A) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Supplementary certification of service","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","48 hours (3 business days in revisión)\nStrict compliance. Reglas 15 y 58(B)(4) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Late appendix documents, with leave","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from notice of the TA resolution allowing late filing.\nFailure to cure may lead to dismissal. Reglas 16(E)(2) y 34(E)(2) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Notice of intent to reproduce oral evidence","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the recurso.\nIdentify portions, dates and witnesses, and the method proposed. Reglas 19(B), 29(B) y 76(A) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Motion in the TPI or agency for the re-recording (regrabación)","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from notice of the TA order authorizing reproduction.\nPay the fees with the motion. Regla 76(B) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Transcript of oral evidence","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from delivery of the re-recording.\nOr the term the TA orders; extendable only for good cause by a reasoned motion. Serve copies within the same term. Regla 76(C) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Stipulated narrative statement: draft, then negotiation","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30, then 20\nIf no agreement, the other side files specific objections. Regla 76(D) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Supplementary brief after transcript or narrative (civil appeal)","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the transcript or narrative statement.\nAnnounce it when filing the transcript. The appellee may reply within 30 days. Regla 21 Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief, civil appeal (TA)","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the escrito de apelación; or filing of the reproduced oral evidence if any is brought up.\nMay include its own appendix. Regla 22 Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Opposition to certiorari (TA)","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from notification of the petition.\n25 pages paper / 35 electronic. Regla 37(A) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Respondent’s brief after the TA issues certiorari","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from notice of issuance of the writ.\nExtendable. The petition is the petitioner’s brief. Regla 39(A) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Opposition brief in administrative revisión","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from notification of the recurso.\nDue from the agency, the official and other parties. Regla 63(A) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Criminal appellant’s brief; then El Pueblo’s brief","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30, then 30\nUnless the TA orders otherwise. Regla 28(A), (E) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Response to a motion (TA)","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from notification of the motion.\nThe TA may rule sooner in extraordinary circumstances. Regla 68(B) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Request for an extension (TA or TS)","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","At least 3 business days before the term expires\nNever available for jurisdictional terms. Regla 72(B) Reglamento TA; Regla 48(b) Reglamento TS","#st-pr-appeals"],["Panel review of a single judge’s habeas corpus or mandamus resolution","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from archivo en autos of the notice of the resolution. Regla 55(H) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Reconsideration of a TPI civil judgment","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of judgment.\nJurisdictional. Serve the other parties within the same 15 days (strict compliance). Must be specific or it does not interrupt. Regla 47 R. Proc. Civ.","#st-pr-appeals"],["Reconsideration of a TPI interlocutory order","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from notice of the order.\nStrict compliance. Regla 47 R. Proc. Civ.","#st-pr-appeals"],["Reconsideration of a criminal sentence or conviction (TPI)","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from date the sentence was dictated.\nNot extendable. Criminal rules compilation current only to Feb. 2020. Regla 194 R. Proc. Crim.","#st-pr-appeals"],["Reconsideration in the TA","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the TA decision.\nNot extendable. Suspends the term to go to the TS. Opposition within 10 days. Electoral cases: 10 days under Regla 42. Regla 84(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 216 R. Proc. Crim.","#st-pr-appeals"],["Certiorari to the TS against a TA judgment in an appeal","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice (or of the ruling on reconsideration; mailing date if later).\nJurisdictional. 60 days in civil cases where the government is a party. Regla 20(a)(1), (c) Reglamento TS; Regla 52.2(b) R. Proc. Civ.","#st-pr-appeals"],["Certiorari to the TS against TA rulings on guilty-plea certiorari, jurisdicción voluntaria, or administrative revisión","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice (or of the ruling on reconsideration).\nJurisdictional. Regla 20(a)(2), (3), (5) Reglamento TS","#st-pr-appeals"],["Certiorari to the TS against other TA rulings in discretionary recursos, or TA interlocutory resolutions","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice (Civ. Rule 52.2(b) says “fecha de notificación”).\nStrict compliance. In criminal cases, Rule 217 R. Proc. Crim. calls the 30-day term for certiorari against a TA judgment or final resolution denying certiorari jurisdictional; treat it as jurisdictional. Regla 20(b)(1) Reglamento TS; Regla 52.2(b) R. Proc. Civ.","#st-pr-appeals"],["Direct appeal to the TS (unconstitutionality or conflicting TA decisions)","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the TA judgment (or of the ruling on reconsideration).\nJurisdictional. 60 days in civil cases where the government is a party. Regla 17(a) Reglamento TS","#st-pr-appeals"],["Notify the TA of a recurso filed in the TS","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","72 hours\nStrict compliance. Stamped copy without the appendix. Reglas 17(b) y 20(d) Reglamento TS","#st-pr-appeals"],["Opposition to certiorari (TS)","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from notification of the petition.\n25 pages. Regla 20(l) Reglamento TS","#st-pr-appeals"],["Briefs in the TS after the writ issues or the appeal is accepted","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","30, then 30\nRegla 33(i) counts the second 30 days from filing of the other side’s brief; Reglas 19 and 21 from receipt. Use the earlier date. Reglas 19, 21 y 33(i) Reglamento TS","#st-pr-appeals"],["Reconsideration in the TS","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","10 business days\nJurisdictional; 10 pages; no extensions. A second motion only within 3 business days after notice of the denial. Regla 45(a)–(b) Reglamento TS","#st-pr-appeals"],["Electoral certiorari to the TS","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the TA judgment (or of the ruling on reconsideration).\nJurisdictional. Reconsideration within 3 days. The TS rule still cites the 2011 Electoral Code; the Reglamento TA (Regla 41) refers to it as “Regla 21” of the Reglamento TS. Regla 22(a) Reglamento TS","#st-pr-appeals"],["TA mandate to the TPI or agency","Puerto Rico · appeals · Deadlines","10 business days\nSince 2025 the TA may send it earlier for cause. Regla 84(E) Reglamento TA","#st-pr-appeals"],["Rhode Island Supreme Court","Rhode Island · courts","The state’s only appellate court; there is no intermediate court of appeals. Hears appeals from the Superior and Family Courts, petitions for certiorari and other extraordinary writs, and certified questions from federal courts and Rhode Island trial courts. Also controls bar admission, pro hac vice policy, lawyer discipline, and the statewide e-filing rules (Article X).","#st-ri"],["Superior Court","Rhode Island · courts","General-jurisdiction trial court sitting in four venues (Providence/Bristol, Kent, Newport, Washington). Exclusive original jurisdiction of actions at law over $10,000, concurrent with the District Court from $5,000 to $10,000, plus equity, title to real estate, and review of many agency decisions. Has a business calendar and mandatory court-annexed arbitration for money claims of $100,000 or less. Hears District Cou","#st-ri"],["District Court","Rhode Island · courts","Exclusive original jurisdiction of civil actions at law up to $5,000, all landlord-tenant possession actions, small replevin, housing-code and ordinance cases; concurrent jurisdiction with the Superior Court from $5,000 to $10,000 (a defendant may demand removal to Superior Court in the answer). A losing party may claim a de novo appeal to the Superior Court within 2 business days after judgment.","#st-ri"],["Family Court, Workers’ Compensation Court, and Rhode Island Traffic Tribunal","Rhode Island · courts","Statewide courts with subject-matter jurisdiction set by statute. Family Court appeals go directly to the Supreme Court under Article I. Each of these courts may admit out-of-state lawyers pro hac vice under the delegation in Sup. Ct. Art. II, Rule 9(a).","#st-ri"],["Statute: R.I. Gen. Laws ch. 9-33 (conditional immunity, not a dismissal framework)","Rhode Island · anti-SLAPP","Rhode Island’s 1993 anti-SLAPP law makes a party’s exercise of the right of petition or free speech, under the U.S. or Rhode Island constitutions, in connection with a matter of public concern, conditionally immune from civil claims, counterclaims, and cross-claims directed at that activity.\nCovered activity is defined as any written or oral statement (1) made before or submitted to a legislative, executive, or judic","#st-ri-slapp"],["The only exception: ‘sham’ petitioning or speech, tested two ways","Rhode Island · anti-SLAPP","Immunity is lost only if the speech or petition is a sham, meaning it is not genuinely aimed at procuring a favorable government action, result, or outcome. It is a sham only if it is BOTH objectively baseless (no reasonable person could realistically expect success in procuring the government action) AND subjectively baseless (an attempt to use the governmental process itself, rather than its outcome, for its own di","#st-ri-slapp"],["Procedure: no special motion form, no deadline, automatic discovery stay","Rhode Island · anti-SLAPP","Immunity may be raised by an appropriate motion or by other appropriate means under the civil rules. The statute sets no filing deadline and no hearing deadline.\nFiling a motion asserting the immunity automatically stays all discovery until notice of entry of the order ruling on it. The court may allow specified discovery only on motion, after a hearing, for good cause. R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-33-2(b)-(c)","#st-ri-slapp"],["Fees are mandatory for the winner; damages if the suit was frivolous or meant to harass","Rhode Island · anti-SLAPP","If the court grants the immunity motion, or the speaker or petitioner ultimately prevails at trial, the court must award costs and reasonable attorney’s fees, including those for the motion and related discovery.\nThe court must also award compensatory damages, and may award punitive damages, if the prevailing party shows the claims were frivolous or brought to harass or otherwise inhibit petitioning or speech. Other ","#st-ri-slapp"],["Intervention by government and the Attorney General; other protections preserved","Rhode Island · anti-SLAPP","The government agency or subdivision to which the petition or speech was directed, or the Attorney General, may intervene to defend or support the speaker. The chapter does not limit other constitutional, statutory, or common-law protections. R.I. Gen. Laws §§ 9-33-3, 9-33-4","#st-ri-slapp"],["Appeal of a denial: the statute is silent","Rhode Island · anti-SLAPP","Chapter 9-33 has no appeal provision, and a denial of an immunity motion is not among the interlocutory orders made appealable by R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-24-7 (injunctions, receivers, ordered sales, and new-trial orders after a jury trial). Review before final judgment would have to come by petition for certiorari to the Supreme Court under Article I, Rule 13. Whether the Supreme Court routinely grants such review is a ca","#st-ri-slapp"],["Currency check","Rhode Island · anti-SLAPP","The legislature’s site shows § 9-33-2 last amended by P.L. 1995, ch. 386, and § 9-33-4 by P.L. 1997, ch. 326. No later amendment (for example, adoption of the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act) appears on the official code site as fetched November 10, 2026. R.I. Gen. Laws §§ 9-33-1 to 9-33-4 (History of Section notes)","#st-ri-slapp"],["Pro hac vice is ‘special and infrequent’ and only for nonresidents","Rhode Island · out of state","An attorney in good standing in another state, who does not reside in Rhode Island, may be permitted, ‘upon special and infrequent occasion and for good cause shown,’ to take part in a case to the extent the court prescribes. Admission is by written miscellaneous petition presented by a Rhode Island lawyer.\nThe Supreme Court has delegated authority to the Superior, Family, District, and Workers’ Compensation Courts a","#st-ri-oos"],["Hard cap: 3 cases in 5 years","Rhode Island · out of state","Unless the Supreme Court waives it for good cause on a separate miscellaneous petition, a nonresident attorney may not appear pro hac vice in more than 3 cases or proceedings in Rhode Island within the 5 years before the petition. The applicant’s certification must state the number of prior Rhode Island pro hac vice appearances in that period and identify each one. Sup. Ct. Art. II, R. 9(a)(1), (a)(3)","#st-ri-oos"],["Good cause is about the client, not the lawyer","Rhode Island · out of state","Good cause is limited to facts affecting the client’s personal or financial welfare. Listed examples: a complex specialty field, a long-standing attorney-client relationship, lack of local counsel with expertise, questions of foreign law, and extensive discovery in another jurisdiction. Sup. Ct. Art. II, R. 9(a)(i)-(v)","#st-ri-oos"],["Local counsel signs everything and must sit in the courtroom","Rhode Island · out of state","A Rhode Island lawyer actively practicing from an office in Rhode Island must sign all pleadings, briefs, and other papers and assumes full responsibility for them, for the conduct of the case, and for the visiting lawyer.\nUnless the court permits otherwise, the visiting lawyer may not take part in any proceeding, hearing, or trial unless a Rhode Island lawyer prepared to continue in the visiting lawyer’s absence is ","#st-ri-oos"],["Paperwork: three signatures, one petition per lawyer, filed electronically","Rhode Island · out of state","The sponsoring Rhode Island attorney signs the miscellaneous petition; the applicant and the client (the named party) each sign certifications on the Clerk’s forms. Each attorney needs a separate petition and filing fee; in an already pending case a motion may be filed in lieu of a petition, with the fee. Petitions are filed through the Judiciary’s Electronic Filing System. In the Supreme Court, an attorney may not a","#st-ri-oos"],["In-house counsel registration (no courtroom work)","Rhode Island · out of state","A lawyer admitted elsewhere and employed by an entity at an office in Rhode Island may register as in-house counsel through the Supreme Court Attorney Portal. Registration covers work only for the employer and its affiliates, directors, officers, and employees, and supervised Bar Association pro bono. It does not permit appearing in court or in an agency or municipal proceeding the lawyer expects to be contested with","#st-ri-oos"],["Full admission without the full exam","Rhode Island · out of state","Lawyers admitted 5 or more years who practiced full time (or taught at an ABA law school) for 5 of the last 10 years take only the essay portion of the Rhode Island exam and need a scaled score of 135. Alternatively, a UBE score of 270 or higher from an exam within 2 years of filing may be transferred. Application fee: $975. Temporary admission is available for lawyers with 1 year of practice working with an approved","#st-ri-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances are formal and event-specific","Rhode Island · out of state","A limited-scope engagement requires a written agreement with informed consent. Ghost-writing is not anonymous: a lawyer who only drafts a filing must sign it and disclose the nature and extent of the help, stating that the signature is not an appearance.\nFor court work, the lawyer files an Entry of Limited Appearance only after the client files a pro se appearance, naming the precise court event. More than one event ","#st-ri-oos"],["Statewide mandatory e-filing (Article X)","Rhode Island · out of state","E-filing through the Judiciary’s Electronic Filing System is mandatory for all parties in the Supreme and Superior Courts, except incarcerated people and attorneys who obtain a Supreme Court waiver; self-represented litigants may opt in. Registering (signing the Usage Agreement) is consent to electronic service.\nFiling is timely if submitted by 11:59 p.m. on the deadline day. A rejected filing that is promptly correc","#st-ri-oos"],["Temporary multijurisdictional practice follows the ABA model","Rhode Island · out of state","Rule 5.5(c)-(d) tracks the ABA Model Rule safe harbors: work with an actively participating Rhode Island lawyer, matters related to a pending or expected proceeding where the lawyer is or expects to be authorized, related ADR, other work arising from the lawyer’s home practice, and in-house or federally authorized work. A systematic and continuous presence in Rhode Island still requires admission. R.I. Sup. Ct. R. Pr","#st-ri-oos"],["Governing code: Supreme Court Rules Article V (revised October 2023)","Rhode Island · ethics","Rhode Island’s Rules of Professional Conduct are Article V of the Supreme Court Rules, based on the ABA Model Rules with commentary. Rule 9.1 adds a strong safe harbor: a lawyer who acts in accordance with an opinion of the Supreme Court’s five-member Ethics Advisory Panel, requested by that lawyer about a proposed action, is conclusively presumed to have complied with the Rules. Appendix I adds Standards for Profess","#st-ri-ethics"],["Fees: writing required, plus quarterly bills","Rhode Island · ethics","Unlike ABA Rule 1.5(b) (‘preferably in writing’), Rhode Island requires that the scope of representation and the basis or rate of fees and expenses be communicated in writing, before or within a reasonable time after starting, except for a regularly represented client on the same basis. Where the fee is neither fixed nor contingent, bills must go to the client quarterly or as the agreement provides. R.I. R. Prof. Con","#st-ri-ethics"],["Confidentiality: much narrower exceptions than the ABA rule","Rhode Island · ethics","Rhode Island permits disclosure only to prevent the client from committing a criminal act likely to result in imminent death or substantial bodily harm, for lawyer-client disputes and defense of claims against the lawyer, to get ethics advice, and to comply with law or court order.\nThere is no counterpart to the ABA exceptions for client crimes or frauds causing substantial financial injury using the lawyer’s service","#st-ri-ethics"],["Rule 1.8: no client-sex rule and no humanitarian-gift exception","Rhode Island · ethics","Rhode Island’s Rule 1.8 has no counterpart to ABA Rule 1.8(j) (sexual relations with a client); its paragraph (j) is the imputation rule. Its financial-assistance rule allows advancing costs and paying costs for indigent clients but lacks the ABA’s 2020 exception for modest gifts of living expenses to indigent pro bono clients. Other conduct rules may still reach such behavior. R.I. R. Prof. Conduct 1.8(e), (j)","#st-ri-ethics"],["Advertising: file ads with Disciplinary Counsel within 48 hours","Rhode Island · ethics","Rhode Island keeps filing rules the ABA dropped. Copies of print ads (except yellow pages), radio recordings, and TV videotapes must go to Supreme Court Disciplinary Counsel before or within 48 hours of first dissemination, and the lawyer must keep a copy with a record of when and where it ran for 3 years. Targeted written or electronic solicitations must say ‘Advertising Material’ on the envelope and at the start an","#st-ri-ethics"],["Discrimination is folded into 8.4(d); recordkeeping rule 1.19","Rhode Island · ethics","Rhode Island did not adopt ABA Rule 8.4(g). Instead, 8.4(d) defines conduct prejudicial to the administration of justice to include harmful or discriminatory treatment of litigants, jurors, witnesses, lawyers, and others based on listed characteristics.\nRule 1.19 (no ABA counterpart in the Model Rules) requires keeping trust and operating account records, retainer agreements, client statements, and bills for 7 years.","#st-ri-ethics"],["20 days to appeal, and the Supreme Court cannot extend it","Rhode Island · land mine","A civil notice of appeal, with the $150 filing fee, must be filed in the trial court within 20 days of entry of the judgment, order, or decree (not 30). Only the trial court may extend, for excusable neglect, by no more than 30 days past the original deadline; the Supreme Court has no power to extend the time to appeal. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a), 20(b)","#st-ri-unique"],["Post-trial motions are due in 10 days, and only some stop the appeal clock","Rhode Island · land mine","Renewed motions for judgment as a matter of law, motions to amend findings, new-trial motions, and motions to alter or amend must be served within 10 days after entry of judgment, and the time cannot be extended. Only those four motions restart the 20-day appeal period. A Rule 60(b) motion does not. Super.R.Civ.P. 6(b), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e); Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a)","#st-ri-unique"],["District Court civil appeals: 2 business days","Rhode Island · land mine","To get a de novo trial in Superior Court after a District Court civil judgment, a party must file a written claim of appeal with the District Court clerk within 2 days after entry of judgment, excluding weekends and legal holidays, and pay the costs, including a $50 attorney’s fee for the other side, at that time. R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-12-10","#st-ri-unique"],["12% interest from accrual changes settlement math","Rhode Island · land mine","Prejudgment interest at 12% a year runs from the date the cause of action accrued and is added by the clerk; post-judgment interest is also 12%, accruing on the prejudgment interest as well. Each year between accrual and judgment adds about 12% of the damages award. R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-21-10(a)","#st-ri-unique"],["Arbitration certificate within 3 days and a 30-day rejection window","Rhode Island · land mine","Money cases of $100,000 or less are diverted to court-annexed arbitration. Counsel must file the arbitration certificate within 3 days after the last responsive pleading. Missing the 30-day deadline to file a written rejection (with $300) after the award is filed means judgment enters on the award. Super. Ct. R. Governing Arbitration of Civil Actions 1(a), (e), 5","#st-ri-unique"],["Pro hac vice: 3 cases per 5 years, and local counsel in the room","Rhode Island · land mine","Nonresident lawyers are limited to 3 Rhode Island pro hac vice cases in 5 years unless the Supreme Court waives it, and admission is for ‘special and infrequent’ occasions based on client need. Rhode Island counsel must sign every paper and attend every hearing and trial day, ready to proceed alone, unless excused. Sup. Ct. Art. II, R. 9(a); Super.R.Civ.P. 85","#st-ri-unique"],["Slander has a 1-year statute of limitations","Rhode Island · land mine","Claims for words spoken must be filed within 1 year after the words were spoken. Personal-injury claims get 3 years. R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-1-14(a)-(b)","#st-ri-unique"],["Uncontested-motion trap: object 3 days before the hearing","Rhode Island · land mine","Motions to amend, compel, for protective orders, to consolidate, to add third parties, for Rule 35 exams, to enlarge time after a deadline, and to assign are granted as a matter of course and never reach the calendar unless a written objection stating particular grounds is served and filed at least 3 days before the hearing. The extra day for electronic service does not apply. Super.R.Civ.P. 7(b)(3)","#st-ri-unique"],["Discovery runs on Rhode Island numbers, not federal ones","Rhode Island · land mine","No initial disclosures. Interrogatory answers and document responses are due in 40 days (a defendant gets 60 days after service of process for document requests), with a 30-interrogatory cap. A plaintiff needs leave to serve interrogatories within 60 days after service of process. Untimely interrogatory objections are waived. Supplemental interrogatory answers are due no later than 30 days before trial. An order comp","#st-ri-unique"],["Do not plead a dollar amount in injury cases","Rhode Island · land mine","In actions for personal injury, property damage, or wrongful death, the complaint must not state the amount claimed, only that it is enough for the court’s jurisdiction. Super.R.Civ.P. 8(a)","#st-ri-unique"],["Miss the Rule 12A statement and the appeal is conditionally dismissed","Rhode Island · land mine","The Rule 12A statement is due 20 days after the appeal is docketed. If it is late, the Clerk enters a conditional dismissal that becomes final unless the statement or an extension motion is filed within 10 days. A late appellee counter-statement or brief leads to a conditional default barring further filings and argument. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12A(1)-(2), 18A","#st-ri-unique"],["Supreme Court briefs need 9 paper copies within 5 days","Rhode Island · land mine","Even with mandatory e-filing, parties must deliver 9 paper copies of Rule 12A statements and briefs (6 of appendices, writ petitions, reargument petitions, and supporting memoranda) within 5 days after the Clerk accepts the electronic filing. Briefs must be in 14-point Times New Roman, double-spaced, with a signed word-count certification and the Clerk’s ‘Checklist for Filing Briefs’; a brief without the checklist is","#st-ri-unique"],["No peremptory substitution of judge; recusal under Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11","Rhode Island · judges","None of the collected rules provides a peremptory challenge or automatic substitution of a judge. Disqualification is for cause under Rule 2.11 of the Rhode Island Code of Judicial Conduct (Article VI, revised May 2024): whenever the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned, including listed grounds. Rhode Island’s version adds a judge who ‘has demonstrated an actual bias towards a party’s lawyer.’ The dut","#st-ri-unique"],["No judge-shopping on ex parte TROs and receivers","Rhode Island · judges","If one judicial officer denies an ex parte TRO or an ex parte temporary receiver, the application may not be made to another judicial officer without a material change in circumstances, and the first judge notes the action in the file. Super.R.Civ.P. 65(b), 66(b)","#st-ri-unique"],["Successor judge mid-trial","Rhode Island · judges","If a judicial officer cannot continue, another may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record; in a non-jury matter the successor must recall, on request, any available witness whose material testimony is disputed. Super.R.Civ.P. 63","#st-ri-unique"],["R.I. Const. art. I, § 21 | Broader than federal: not established here","Rhode Island · speech","The Rhode Island Constitution protects freedom of speech, press, assembly, and petition. The collected texts do not include the Constitution or case law, so whether the Rhode Island Supreme Court reads these clauses more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked. The anti-SLAPP statute expressly protects speech and petitioning under both the U.S. and Rhode Island constitutions. R.I. Const. art. I, § 21 (not in","#st-ri-unique"],["One-year limitations period for slander","Rhode Island · speech","Actions for words spoken must be brought within 1 year after the words were spoken. Written defamation is not addressed by this section; check which limitations period applies to libel. R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-1-14(a)","#st-ri-unique"],["Mandatory court-annexed arbitration for claims of $100,000 or less","Rhode Island · unique rules","Superior Court civil actions seeking money of $100,000 or less in total (excluding interest, costs, and fees), and District Court appeals, go to court-annexed arbitration, with listed exceptions (class actions, substantial injunctive or declaratory claims, family, real-estate title, estates, landlord-tenant, book accounts, formal and special cause matters). Within 3 days after the last responsive pleading, counsel mu","#st-ri-unique"],["12% prejudgment and post-judgment interest","Rhode Island · unique rules","In any civil action with a verdict or decision for money damages, the clerk adds interest at 12% a year from the date the cause of action accrued, and post-judgment interest also runs at 12% on both principal and prejudgment interest. It does not apply to contracts that already set interest. In medical-malpractice cases against listed providers, interest runs from written notice of the claim or filing, whichever is f","#st-ri-unique"],["Supreme Court ‘show cause’ calendar and prebriefing statements","Rhode Island · unique rules","Most appeals do not get full briefing first. Within 20 days after docketing, the appellant files a Rule 12A statement of the case (3,000 words maximum, with the judgment and the trial decision attached); the appellee’s counter-statement follows within 15 days. A single justice may then order the case decided on the show cause calendar (10 minutes of argument a side before a panel of at least 3 justices) or assign it ","#st-ri-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope of rules and mandatory electronic filing","Rhode Island · civil rules","Govern all civil suits in the Superior Court, at law or in equity (exceptions in Rule 81); cited as Super.R.Civ.P. Unlike the federal rule, Rule 1(b) makes e-filing through the Judiciary’s Electronic Filing System (EFS) mandatory for all parties except incarcerated people and attorneys granted a waiver under Art. X, Rule 3(c); self-represented litigants may opt in. Case-initiating papers must include the Language Ass","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","Rhode Island · civil rules","There is one form of action, the civil action.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencement of action","Rhode Island · civil rules","An action is commenced by filing the complaint, all other required documents, and the fees. Incarcerated people, waived attorneys, and non-e-filing self-represented litigants may mail the papers with the fee to the clerk or file at the clerk’s office.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 4. Process, attachment, trustee process, arrest","Rhode Island · civil rules","The court generates the summons in the EFS after accepting the complaint. Service by an authorized officer under R.I. Gen. Laws Title 9, ch. 5, or any non-party age 18 or older; the summons, complaint, and Language Assistance Notice are served together. Waiver of service: at least 30 days to return (60 if abroad), answer due 60 days after the request was sent (90 if abroad), and unjustified refusal shifts service cos","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 5. Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","Rhode Island · civil rules","Later papers are served electronically on Registered Users through the EFS with the Rule 1(b)(1)(B) certificate of service; others by hand delivery, mail (complete on mailing), or court-ordered means. Interrogatories, document requests, requests for admission, responses, deposition notices, and transcripts are not filed until used or ordered, but relevant parts must be filed with Rule 26(c), 37, and 56 motions. If a ","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 6. Time","Rhode Island · civil rules","Exclude the trigger day; include the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday, or legal holiday. Extensions before expiry for cause; after expiry only for excusable neglect; no extension of the Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (d), (e), or 60(b) times. Written motions and notice of hearing must be served at least 10 days before the hearing; opposing affidavits by 1 day before. Add 1 day (not 3 as in the federal rule) after ","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings allowed; form of motions","Rhode Island · civil rules","Complaint, answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a cross-claim, third-party complaint and answer; a jury demand is a separate pleading. Certain motions (to assign, consolidate, enlarge time after expiry, third-party complaint, amend, Rule 35 exam, protective order or compel, wage or non-wage attachment) must be served and filed 10 days before hearing and are granted as a matter of course unless a written objecti","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","Rhode Island · civil rules","Short and plain statement plus a demand for judgment. In personal-injury, property-damage, and wrongful-death actions the complaint must not state an amount, only that it is enough for jurisdiction. Affirmative defenses listed as in the federal rule (including injury by fellow servant); unanswered averments other than damages are admitted.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","Rhode Island · civil rules","Fraud and mistake with particularity; capacity challenged by specific negative averment; conditions precedent pleaded generally but denied with particularity; special damages stated specifically.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","Rhode Island · civil rules","Caption must show the court, the county, the title, the file number, and the Rule 7(a) designation; numbered paragraphs; exhibits are part of the pleading.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing of pleadings, motions, and other papers; sanctions","Rhode Island · civil rules","Signed by at least one attorney of record with address, email, bar number, and phone (/s/ signature under Art. X, Rule 7). The signature certifies reasonable inquiry and proper purpose. Unlike the current federal rule, there is no 21-day safe harbor in the text; sanctions may be imposed on motion or on the court’s own initiative, including reasonable expenses and attorney’s fees.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections; motion for judgment on the pleadings","Rhode Island · civil rules","Answer within 20 days after service (or 20 days after last publication); 60 days after a waiver request was sent (90 if abroad). Answer to cross-claim and reply to counterclaim: 20 days. After denial of a Rule 12 motion, respond within 10 days of notice. Seven listed defenses may be raised by motion, including failure to join an indispensable party; a Rule 12(b)(6) motion or defense must include a short, concise stat","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and cross-claim","Rhode Island · civil rules","Compulsory counterclaim rule as in the federal rule, with a Rhode Island exception: a claim need not be pleaded as a counterclaim if the opposing party’s claim is for damage arising from the pleader’s ownership, maintenance, operation, use, or control of a motor vehicle. In an action entered in Superior Court on appeal from the District Court, counterclaims are not compulsory. Omitted counterclaims may be added by le","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","Rhode Island · civil rules","A defendant may serve a third-party complaint without leave if it is filed no later than 10 days after serving the original answer (the federal rule allows 14 days); otherwise leave on motion. The third-party plaintiff must tell the third-party defendant whether and when the case is assigned for trial.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Rhode Island · civil rules","One amendment as of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 20 days after service if no responsive pleading is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar (narrower than the federal 21-day window after a Rule 12 motion). Otherwise by leave, freely given, or written consent; in the EFS the amended pleading is attached as an exhibit to the motion. Respond within the time left or 10 days after s","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 16. Pre-trial procedure; formulating issues","Rhode Island · civil rules","Discretionary pretrial conference on issues, amendments, admissions, number of expert witnesses, and electronically stored information; the resulting order controls unless modified to prevent manifest injustice. There is no federal-style mandatory scheduling order in the text. A trial judge may hold a conference on the day trial starts.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 17. Parties plaintiff and defendant; capacity","Rhode Island · civil rules","Real party in interest, with time allowed for ratification or substitution; a subrogated insurer may sue in the insured’s name. Capacity of individuals and associations follows Rhode Island law; corporations, the law of incorporation. Guardian ad litem or next friend for minors and incompetent persons.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims and remedies","Rhode Island · civil rules","Unlimited joinder of claims against an opponent; a money claim and a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance may be joined. A liability insurer may not be joined in a personal-injury or property-damage action unless it is directly liable to and directly suable by the injured person.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 19. Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","Rhode Island · civil rules","Federal-style required-party joinder and the four-factor ‘equity and good conscience’ test when joinder is not feasible; the pleading must name absent required persons and explain why they are not joined.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","Rhode Island · civil rules","Plaintiffs or defendants may be joined for claims from the same transaction or series with a common question; the court may order separate trials.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","Rhode Island · civil rules","Not grounds for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped and claims severed at any stage.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","Rhode Island · civil rules","Claimants exposing the plaintiff to double or multiple liability may be required to interplead; a defendant may interplead by cross-claim or counterclaim.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","Rhode Island · civil rules","Rule 23(a) prerequisites and (b)(1)-(3) types track the older federal rule; certification order as soon as practicable; best practicable notice with opt-out in (b)(3) classes. Dismissal or compromise needs court approval and notice to the class. The text lacks the later federal additions (class counsel appointment, attorney-fee procedure, interlocutory appeal of certification).","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 23.1. Derivative actions by shareholders","Rhode Island · civil rules","Complaint must be verified, plead contemporaneous ownership, and plead with particularity demand efforts or why none was made; dismissal or compromise needs court approval and notice.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 23.2. Derivative action; unincorporated associations","Rhode Island · civil rules","Actions by or against members as a class through representatives, if they fairly and adequately protect the association’s interests; Rule 23(d)-(e) procedures apply.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","Rhode Island · civil rules","Intervention of right (statute or impaired interest not adequately represented) and permissive intervention, on timely motion with an accompanying pleading. A party challenging the constitutionality of a state statute in a case where the state is not a party must serve the Attorney General in time to allow intervention.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","Rhode Island · civil rules","On death, any party or the successor may move to substitute, serving non-parties as for a summons. Unlike the federal rule there is no 90-day deadline after a statement of death; if no motion is made, the action is subject to dismissal under Rule 41(b). Incompetency, transfer of interest, and automatic substitution of public officers as in the federal rule.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 26. General provisions governing discovery","Rhode Island · civil rules","Unlike the federal rule, there are no mandatory initial disclosures and no required discovery conference (an ESI conference happens only by agreement or court order). Scope is the older federal standard: any non-privileged matter relevant to the subject matter, including information reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence, limited for cumulative, duplicative, or unduly burdensome discovery. Expert discov","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions before action or pending appeal","Rhode Island · civil rules","Perpetuation of testimony before suit follows state statute; pending appeal, the Superior Court may allow depositions on motion showing who will be examined, the expected testimony, and why.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Rhode Island · civil rules","In state, before an officer authorized to administer oaths or a court appointee (no officer needed for non-stenographic depositions); out of state, before an authorized person, a commissioned person, or by letter of request. Officers related to or employed by a party or counsel, or financially interested, are disqualified.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations regarding depositions","Rhode Island · civil rules","Written stipulations may change who takes, when, where, and how depositions are taken.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions upon oral examination","Rhode Island · civil rules","No leave needed except for prisoners, repeat depositions, or a plaintiff’s deposition within 30 days after service (unless the defendant has started discovery or the witness is leaving Rhode Island). Notice must state the recording method; organizational (30(b)(6)) designations are served and filed before the deposition; remote depositions by stipulation or order. No federal-style 1-day/7-hour limit or 10-deposition ","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions upon written questions","Rhode Island · civil rules","Leave needed only for prisoners or repeat depositions. Cross questions within 14 days of service of the notice; redirect within 7 days after cross; recross within 7 days after redirect.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 32. Use of depositions in court proceedings","Rhode Island · civil rules","Federal-style uses, with Rhode Island’s ‘out of the state’ unavailability ground (rather than the federal 100-mile rule). A videotaped deposition of a medical witness or other non-party expert, taken by stipulation or order, may be used at trial for any purpose whether or not the witness is available. Objections to written-question form within the time for the next questions and within 5 days after the last questions","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories to parties","Rhode Island · civil rules","Plaintiff needs leave to serve interrogatories within 60 days after service of process on the defendant. Answers and objections due within 40 days after service (the federal rule allows 30). Limit: 30 interrogatories in total (federal: 25), more only by court order for good cause. Objections not timely served are waived. Amended answers must be served within a reasonable time and no later than 30 days before the tria","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 34. Production of documents, ESI, and things; entry on land","Rhode Island · civil rules","May be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on any other party with or after service of process. Written response due within 40 days after service (federal: 30), but a defendant gets 60 days after service of the summons and complaint. Must state for each item whether production will be permitted or the objection and reasons; ESI form rules as in the federal rule. Non-parties are reached by Rule 45 subpoena.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examination of persons","Rhode Island · civil rules","On motion for good cause when a party’s condition (including blood group) is in controversy, by a suitably licensed or certified examiner. On request, the examining party must provide the detailed report and is then entitled to reciprocal reports; requesting the report waives privilege as to other examiners of the same condition.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","Rhode Island · civil rules","Matters are deemed admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 30 days after service, but a defendant need not respond until 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Lack-of-knowledge answers require a statement of reasonable inquiry. Admissions bind only in the pending action (and a later action between the same parties on the same claim). There is no numerical limit in the text.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to make or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","Rhode Island · civil rules","Motions to compel need a good-faith conference certification; expenses usually follow. A Rhode Island feature: an order compelling discovery may expressly provide for a final judgment of dismissal or default if the order is not obeyed within 30 days (or other set time), the so-called conditional order. No sanctions, absent exceptional circumstances, for ESI lost by routine good-faith operation of a system. Expenses f","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury trial of right","Rhode Island · civil rules","Right preserved under R.I. Const. art. I, § 15 or statute. A written jury demand must be served no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue (federal: 14) and filed; others may demand additional issues within 10 days. Failure to serve and file a demand waives the jury.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","Rhode Island · civil rules","Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or no right exists; the court may in its discretion order a jury despite a missed demand; advisory or consent juries allowed.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 40. Assignment of cases for trial; continuances","Rhode Island · civil rules","Cases are assigned by court order or rules of practice, or on motion with notice, but no motion to assign for trial may be made within 40 days after service on all necessary parties. Continuances only on motion for good cause; a motion based on an absent witness requires a detailed affidavit, and one based on illness requires a physician’s certificate.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","Rhode Island · civil rules","Voluntary dismissal by notice before service of an answer or summary-judgment motion, or by stipulation signed by all appearing parties; a second notice dismissal operates as an adjudication on the merits. The court may dismiss for lack of prosecution an action pending more than 5 years, or at any time for failure to comply with the rules or to proceed when reached for trial, after notice. Involuntary dismissals (oth","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials","Rhode Island · civil rules","Actions with a common question may be consolidated even if pending in different counties; separate trials of claims or issues may be held in the county of filing or a different county.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 43. Evidence","Rhode Island · civil rules","Testimony is taken orally in open court; admissibility follows state statutes and the Rhode Island Rules of Evidence. Rhode Island additions: only one attorney per side may examine a given witness, and the attorney must stand unless permitted otherwise; examination is limited to direct, cross, and redirect without leave. The party with the burden of proof opens and closes; on motions, the movant. Exhibits not withdra","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 44. Proof of official record","Rhode Island · civil rules","Official records proved by official publication or attested copy, with certification for out-of-state or foreign records; a no-record statement is admissible.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 44.1. Determination of foreign law","Rhode Island · civil rules","Notice by pleading or other reasonable written notice; the court may consider any relevant source, and its determination is a ruling of law.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","Rhode Island · civil rules","Issued by the clerk, a notary public, or another authorized officer; served by an authorized officer or any non-party age 18 or older, with one day’s fees and mileage tendered (not required for the state); may be served anywhere in Rhode Island. Prior notice of a document subpoena must be served on all parties. Written objections to production within 14 days after service (or before the compliance date if sooner) sto","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 46. Exceptions unnecessary","Rhode Island · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary; making the objection and grounds known when the ruling is made preserves it, and the court may allow a standing objection to a line of testimony.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 47. Jurors","Rhode Island · civil rules","The court or counsel may conduct voir dire, under oath on request; 1 or 2 alternates may be seated.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 48. Number of jurors","Rhode Island · civil rules","Six jurors and a unanimous verdict unless the parties stipulate otherwise with court approval.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 49. Special verdicts and interrogatories","Rhode Island · civil rules","Special verdicts and general verdicts with interrogatories, with federal-style rules for inconsistent answers; an omitted issue is waived unless jury submission is demanded before the jury retires. Separate verdicts on multiple counts may be required.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 50. Judgment as a matter of law in jury trials; new trial; conditional rulings","Rhode Island · civil rules","Motion any time before submission, stating the judgment sought and the law and facts. At the close of the opponent’s evidence the court may instead order an involuntary dismissal without prejudice. Renewed motion within 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28 days), with an optional joined or alternative new-trial motion; conditional new-trial rulings as in the federal rule. A timely Rule 50(b) motion stops the ","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 51. Argument of counsel; instructions to jury","Rhode Island · civil rules","Each party gets 1 hour for closing argument (40 minutes in cases commenced in the District Court) unless more is allowed. Written requests for instructions at close of evidence or earlier; an instruction error is preserved only by objecting before the jury retires, stating the matter and grounds distinctly.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings by the court; judgment on partial findings","Rhode Island · civil rules","In non-jury trials the court finds facts specially and states conclusions of law, orally on the record or in a decision; not needed on Rule 12 or 56 motions. Motion to amend findings within 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28). Judgment on partial findings, or dismissal without prejudice, after a party is fully heard.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters","Rhode Island · civil rules","Masters (including referees, auditors, examiners) appointed by agreement or, without agreement, only as an exception: in jury cases for accounts or complicated issues, in non-jury cases for account and damage computation or an exceptional condition. First meeting within 20 days of the order of reference. In non-jury cases findings are accepted unless clearly erroneous, and objections to the report are due within 10 d","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgment; costs","Rhode Island · civil rules","‘Judgment’ includes any appealable order. Rule 54(b) partial final judgment requires an express determination of no just reason for delay and an express direction. Default judgments cannot exceed or differ from the demand. Costs go to the prevailing party as provided by statute and the rules, taxed by the clerk on 10 days’ notice with an itemized bill; objections are heard by the court. Deposition costs, if objected ","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 55. Default","Rhode Island · civil rules","Clerk enters default on affidavit. Clerk enters judgment for a sum certain against a non-appearing, competent defendant; otherwise application to the court, with 10 days’ written notice to a party who has appeared (federal: 7). A Servicemembers Civil Relief Act affidavit is required before any default judgment. Default set aside for good cause; default judgment under Rule 60(b).","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","Rhode Island · civil rules","A claimant may move after 20 days from commencement; a defending party at any time. The motion must be served at least 10 days before the hearing, and opposing affidavits may be served any time before the hearing day. This tracks the pre-2010 federal text (‘no genuine issue as to any material fact’), with no statement-of-undisputed-facts requirement in the rule. An opponent must set out specific facts by affidavit or","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments","Rhode Island · civil rules","Declaratory relief under the statute follows these rules; jury demands per Rules 38-39; another adequate remedy does not bar relief; the court may advance the hearing.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 58. Entry of judgment","Rhode Island · civil rules","The clerk enters judgment on a general verdict or a decision for a sum certain, costs, or denial of all relief; otherwise the court approves the form first. Every judgment must be on a separate document and is entered when set forth and signed by the clerk; entry is not delayed for costs. The clerk may enter agreed judgments for a sum certain or denying relief, except where a party is a minor or incompetent. Entry st","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 59. New trials; amendment of judgments","Rhode Island · civil rules","New trial for error of law at trial or any ground historically recognized in Rhode Island courts. Motion for new trial, and motion to alter or amend, must be served no later than 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28 days). Opposing affidavits within 10 days of service, extendable by up to 20 more days. The court may order a new trial on its own within 10 days of entry. Rule 6(b) bars extending these times.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from judgment or order","Rhode Island · civil rules","Clerical errors corrected any time (with Supreme Court leave once the appeal is docketed). Rule 60(b) grounds as in the federal rule; motions within a reasonable time and, for mistake, newly discovered evidence, or fraud, not more than 1 year after entry. The motion does not suspend the judgment; independent actions and fraud-on-the-court relief preserved. A Rule 60(b) motion does not stop the appeal clock (it is not","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","Rhode Island · civil rules","Errors not affecting substantial rights are disregarded.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","Rhode Island · civil rules","No execution or enforcement until 20 days after entry or until the appeal time expires, but injunctions and receivership judgments are not stayed unless ordered. Stay pending appeal by supersedeas bond, effective when the court approves it; the State need not post one. Stays pending Rule 60 motions and Rule 54(b) judgments in the court’s discretion.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 63. Inability of a judicial officer to proceed","Rhode Island · civil rules","A successor judicial officer may continue after certifying familiarity with the record; in non-jury matters, must recall on request a material, disputed, available witness.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 64. Replevin","Rhode Island · civil rules","Unlike the federal rule (which defers to state seizure law), Rhode Island’s Rule 64 sets the procedure: a writ of replevin on motion, never ex parte, on a showing of probable success and substantial need for possession pending suit; the court may instead order the defendant to give security.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions","Rhode Island · civil rules","Preliminary injunction only with notice; hearing may be consolidated with trial. A TRO without notice requires specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint and an attorney’s written certification of notice efforts; it expires within no more than 10 days (federal: 14) unless extended for a like period or by consent. The adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice. A TRO denied by one judge may not be re-","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers","Rhode Island · civil rules","A Rhode Island-specific rule: ordinarily one receiver, a Rhode Island resident; ex parte temporary receivers only on a sworn written showing plus attorney certificate; no forum-shopping a denied ex parte application. The decree fixes deadlines for inventory, reports, and claims; a temporary receivership over 30 days requires such a decree. Receivers report every 30 days. Receiver-lawyers generally may not hire separa","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit in court; registry","Rhode Island · civil rules","Deposits by leave and on notice; registry funds by certified or cashier’s check to the Registry of the Superior Court for the county, withdrawn only on written court order; no clerk fees on disbursement.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of judgment; payment into court","Rhode Island · civil rules","A defending party may serve an offer of judgment more than 10 days before trial; the offeree has 10 days to accept in writing (federal: 14 days for both). If the final judgment is not more favorable, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer. Rhode Island adds payment into court: the defendant may deposit money and plead it is the most owed; the claimant may accept with costs, reject, or accept as part payment ","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution; supplementary proceedings; post-judgment attachment and trustee process","Rhode Island · civil rules","Execution procedure follows statute; discovery in aid of judgment allowed. A Rhode Island-specific citation in supplementary proceedings orders a debtor whose execution was returned unsatisfied to appear for examination; served at least 6 days before the appearance; failure to appear can lead to a civil body attachment. The court may enter installment-payment decrees enforceable by contempt. Post-judgment non-wage tr","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 70. Judgment for specific acts","Rhode Island · civil rules","If a party fails to perform a directed act, the court may appoint someone to do it, issue attachment or sequestration, hold the party in contempt, or vest title to in-state property directly.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 71. Process in behalf of and against persons not parties","Rhode Island · civil rules","Non-parties may enforce, and be bound by, orders as if parties.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 72. Certification","Rhode Island · civil rules","Where a statute allows certification of an action or question to the Supreme Court, a party’s motion must be served at least 10 days before the hearing and specify the matter; on the court’s own initiative the parties must be heard first. (Unlike federal Rule 72, this is not about magistrate judges.)","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 73-76. [Repealed]","Rhode Island · civil rules","Repealed; no current text.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 77. Superior Court and clerks","Rhode Island · civil rules","Court always open for filing; trials in open court; clerk’s office open business days. Docket notation of an order or judgment is sufficient notice for all purposes, though parties may also serve notice. A proposed written order filed with a certificate of service is entered by the clerk if no objection or competing order is filed within 4 days.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 78. Stenographic reports; transcripts","Rhode Island · civil rules","Transcript rates capped at $3.00 per page for originals and $1.50 per page for copies, with a $5.00 minimum; ordering and payment follow the Supreme Court Finance Office policy.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 79. Books and records kept by the clerk","Rhode Island · civil rules","The clerk keeps the civil docket, with consecutive file numbers, chronological entries, entry dates, and jury demands, plus required indices.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 80. Review of administrative action","Rhode Island · civil rules","Where a statute provides Superior Court review of agency action, review begins by complaint stating the grounds and relief; no responsive pleading unless required. Time limits come from the governing statute; serve the agency and all parties under Rule 5. Fact trials are non-jury unless a right exists.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 81. Applicability of rules","Rhode Island · civil rules","The rules do not apply at the process and pleading stages of probate appeals, condemnation, mechanics’ lien enforcement, and statutory receivership or dissolution petitions, and do not apply at all to assignments for creditors, tax-sale foreclosure of redemption, naturalization, most prerogative writs, and habeas corpus. On a District Court appeal for trial de novo, the plaintiff may replead within 10 days after cert","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 82. Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","Rhode Island · civil rules","The rules do not extend or limit Superior Court jurisdiction or venue.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 83. Rules of practice and orders","Rhode Island · civil rules","Rules of practice adopted by a majority of Superior Court judicial officers and administrative orders of the presiding justice have the force of these rules.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 84. Forms","Rhode Island · civil rules","Forms on the Judiciary’s website are sufficient under the rules.","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 85. Out-of-state counsel","Rhode Island · civil rules","No one who is not a Rhode Island attorney may act for a party in Superior Court without leave of the Superior or Supreme Court. Leave is by miscellaneous petition in the Supreme Court’s form, with certifications from the applicant and Rhode Island associate counsel and a client certification, subject to Sup. Ct. Art. II, Rule 9. Unless excused, a Rhode Island lawyer prepared to continue must be present in the courtro","#st-ri-rules"],["Rule 86. Effective date","Rhode Island · civil rules","These amended rules took effect November 5, 2014, and apply to pending actions unless application would be infeasible or unjust.","#st-ri-rules"],["Appeals in Rhode Island: one appellate court, a 20-day clock, and a prebriefing screen","Rhode Island · appeals","Rhode Island has no intermediate appellate court. Appeals from final judgments of the Superior Court and Family Court go straight to the Supreme Court by a notice of appeal filed in the trial court within 20 days of entry of judgment. Everything else (most interlocutory orders, Workers’ Compensation and District Court rulings, agency matters) reaches the Supreme Court, if at all, by petition for certiorari or another","#st-ri-appeals"],["Final judgments: notice of appeal in the trial court","Rhode Island · appeals · What can be appealed, and how","A party aggrieved by a final judgment, decree, or order of the Superior Court may appeal to the Supreme Court within the time set by the rules. The notice of appeal is filed with the trial-court clerk, names the appealing parties and the judgment or part appealed from (use the Judiciary’s current form), and is served on every party’s attorney or an unrepresented party at the last known address. Only a timely notice a","#st-ri-appeals"],["Interlocutory orders appealable by statute","Rhode Island · appeals · What can be appealed, and how","An appeal lies, as from a final judgment and with precedence in the Supreme Court, from Superior Court interlocutory orders or judgments that grant or continue an injunction, appoint a receiver, order a sale of real or personal property, or order or deny a new trial after a jury trial. R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-24-7","#st-ri-appeals"],["Partial final judgment under Rule 54(b)","Rhode Island · appeals · What can be appealed, and how","In multi-claim or multi-party cases, an order resolving fewer than all claims is appealable only if the trial court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay and expressly directs entry of judgment. Otherwise it can be revised until final judgment. Cases sent to appellate mediation with incomplete judgments must obtain a Rule 54(b) judgment. Super.R.Civ.P. 54(b); Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 35(d)(2)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Other interlocutory review: petition for certiorari or another writ","Rhode Island · appeals · What can be appealed, and how","Review of other orders is sought by petition to the Supreme Court for an extraordinary writ. The petition must state the facts, explain with particularity why relief is unavailable in any other court or through other appellate processes (including whether a notice of appeal was filed and why it is not enough), and attach the order and essential record parts, with a supporting memorandum and a $150 fee per petitioner.","#st-ri-appeals"],["Post-judgment orders","Rhode Island · appeals · What can be appealed, and how","Orders entered after judgment may be appealed in the same manner, as near as may be, as judgments. An appeal from the judgment also preserves claims of error in rulings on reserved directed-verdict motions, motions to amend findings or the judgment, new-trial motions, and motions in arrest of judgment. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 30","#st-ri-appeals"],["Certified questions","Rhode Island · appeals · What can be appealed, and how","The Supreme Court may answer questions of Rhode Island law certified by U.S. courts or the Superior, Family, District, or Workers’ Compensation Courts if the question may be determinative and there is no controlling precedent. Unless ordered otherwise, the plaintiff files the opening brief. In the Superior Court, a party’s motion to certify under a statute must be served at least 10 days before the hearing. Sup. Ct. ","#st-ri-appeals"],["Below the Superior Court: District Court and magistrates","Rhode Island · appeals · What can be appealed, and how","District Court civil judgments are not appealed to the Supreme Court; a party claims an appeal for a de novo trial in the Superior Court within 2 days after entry of judgment, excluding weekends and legal holidays, paying costs including a $50 attorney’s fee for the other side. A Superior Court magistrate’s decision is appealed to a Superior Court justice by notice filed within 20 days of entry (no fee); the appellan","#st-ri-appeals"],["20 days from entry, with a $150 fee","Rhode Island · appeals · Timing: the notice of appeal and what stops the clock","In a civil case the notice of appeal, with a $150 filing fee, must be filed with the trial-court clerk within 20 days of the date of entry of the judgment, order, or decree. A judgment is entered when it is set forth and signed by the clerk. A notice filed after the decision but before entry is treated as filed on the day of entry. Joint appellants each pay $150. The State is exempt from the fee. An indigent appellan","#st-ri-appeals"],["Tolling motions: only four, and each is due in 10 days","Rhode Island · appeals · Timing: the notice of appeal and what stops the clock","The appeal time is terminated for all parties by a timely Superior Court motion under Rule 50(b) (renewed judgment as a matter of law), 52(b) (amend findings), 59 (alter or amend judgment), or 59 (new trial), or comparable Family Court motions. The full 20 days runs again from entry of the order deciding the motion. Each of these motions must be served within 10 days after entry of judgment, and that time cannot be e","#st-ri-appeals"],["Extension only from the trial court, only for excusable neglect, at most 30 days","Rhode Island · appeals · Timing: the notice of appeal and what stops the clock","On a showing of excusable neglect, the trial court may extend the appeal time for any party by up to 30 days from the end of the original period, before or after it expires (after expiry, by motion with notice). The Supreme Court may not extend the time to file a notice of appeal. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a), 20(b)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Cross-appeals","Rhode Island · appeals · Timing: the notice of appeal and what stops the clock","After a timely notice of appeal, any other party may file a notice within 20 days after the first notice was filed (or deemed filed), or within the ordinary appeal time, whichever ends later. In consolidated cases or cross-appeals, the first to file is the appellant and files the opening brief; the second files one brief that responds and presents its cross-appeal; the appellant replies; the cross-appellant may reply","#st-ri-appeals"],["Order the transcript within 20 days","Rhode Island · appeals · Transcript, record, and docketing","Within 20 days after filing the notice of appeal the appellant orders the needed transcript. If less than all of it is ordered, the appellant must file and serve a description of the parts and a statement of the points to be raised; the appellee then orders any additional parts. If no transcript exists, the appellant may prepare a statement of the evidence, with objections due 10 days after service, settled by the tr","#st-ri-appeals"],["Record transmitted within 60 days; extensions capped at 90","Rhode Island · appeals · Transcript, record, and docketing","The record, including the transcript, must be transmitted to the Supreme Court within 60 days after the notice of appeal (for mediation cases, 60 days after the transcript is ordered). The trial court may extend, on a request made before the deadline and showing causes beyond the appellant’s control or excusable neglect, but not beyond 90 days from the first notice of appeal; beyond that, only the Supreme Court can g","#st-ri-appeals"],["After docketing","Rhode Island · appeals · Transcript, record, and docketing","Report errors in the case title within 7 days after notice of docketing. Motions to correct the record are due within 20 days after docketing. Each attorney must file a written appearance (name, address, email, bar number, phone) by the Rule 12A deadline or, where Rule 12A does not apply, the brief deadline; no one may argue without one. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12(b), (d), 19","#st-ri-appeals"],["Most counseled civil appeals are screened for mediation","Rhode Island · appeals · Appellate mediation","All civil appeals are eligible except post-conviction and habeas cases, prisoner cases, cases with an unrepresented party (unless included by order), Family Court appeals, juvenile cases, and petitions for extraordinary relief. If a case is eligible, the Mediation Statement (Part I case information, served on opponents; Part II confidential, filed only with the program) is due 20 days from the Notice of Eligibility, ","#st-ri-appeals"],["Statement of the case within 20 days of docketing","Rhode Island · appeals · Prebriefing: Rule 12A statements and the show cause calendar","Within 20 days after the appeal is docketed (or after the record arrives on a granted certiorari petition), the appellant files a statement of the case and summary of issues, no more than 3,000 words, with copies of the judgment or order and the trial court’s bench or written decision; the Clerk rejects a statement missing them. The counter-statement (3,000 words) is due 15 days after the statement is filed. Replies ","#st-ri-appeals"],["Single-justice conference and routing","Rhode Island · appeals · Prebriefing: Rule 12A statements and the show cause calendar","A single justice may hold a conference (counsel must come with settlement authority in civil cases) and then order the parties to show cause why the appeal should not be decided summarily, refer it to the full Court, place it on the regular calendar for full briefing, consolidate, or remand. Show cause supplemental statements (3,000 words) are due 20 days after the show cause order, with the response 10 days later. S","#st-ri-appeals"],["Briefing schedule: 40 / 40 / 20","Rhode Island · appeals · Full briefing, length, and form","The appellant’s brief is due 40 days after the Clerk gives notice that the case is assigned to the regular calendar for full briefing; the appellee’s brief 40 days after the appellant’s is filed; any reply 20 days after the appellee’s brief. No further briefs or post-argument memoranda without leave. Supplemental authorities may be cited by a short ‘Citation of Supplemental Authority’ without argument; a response is ","#st-ri-appeals"],["Contents, and what is waived","Rhode Island · appeals · Full briefing, length, and form","The brief must include facts and prior proceedings with record and appendix cites, a specification of errors with page cites, numbered questions each with a standard of review, argument with authority, a specific conclusion, and an alphabetical index of authorities. Errors not claimed, questions not raised, and points not made are ordinarily treated as waived. Use parties’ names or descriptive terms rather than ‘appe","#st-ri-appeals"],["Word limits and format","Rhode Island · appeals · Full briefing, length, and form","Briefs: 15,000 words; reply briefs: 7,500 words (typed or handwritten briefs: 50 and 25 pages). Rule 12A statements and show cause statements: 3,000 words. Headings, footnotes, and quotations count; cover, tables, statement of issues, signature blocks, and certifications do not. All papers: 8.5 by 11 inches, double-spaced, Times New Roman at least 14-point (footnotes too, single-spaced), 1-inch margins, page numbers ","#st-ri-appeals"],["Amicus briefs","Rhode Island · appeals · Full briefing, length, and form","Allowed with all parties’ written consent or by leave, except the United States and the State of Rhode Island need neither. A motion must state the amicus’s interest and why the brief is desirable; the brief is due with the brief of the party it supports unless leave is granted. Amicus argument is allowed only for extraordinary reasons. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 16(h)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Citing cases","Rhode Island · appeals · Full briefing, length, and form","Unpublished orders have no precedential effect and may not be cited. Cite an electronic database only when the case is not yet in the bound national reporter, and a Lexis cite must be accompanied by the Westlaw cite. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 18(l)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Two 30-day extensions from the Clerk","Rhode Island · appeals · Extensions, defaults, and dismissal","The Clerk’s office may grant one 30-day extension on motion, then one more 30-day extension (two more in criminal cases) unless an objection is filed within 7 days, in which case the Court decides. Further extensions require a Supreme Court order for good cause. These limits do not apply to reargument petitions or cures of conditional dismissal. In an expedited appeal, no extensions without the Court’s express permis","#st-ri-appeals"],["Conditional dismissals and defaults","Rhode Island · appeals · Extensions, defaults, and dismissal","A late Rule 12A statement or appellant’s brief leads to a conditional dismissal; a late counter-statement or appellee’s brief to a conditional default barring further filings and argument. Each is cured only by filing the paper (or, for 12A papers, an extension motion) within 10 days. The Clerk also conditionally dismisses any case with no party action for at least 90 days while no Court action is pending; it is rein","#st-ri-appeals"],["Voluntary dismissal","Rhode Island · appeals · Extensions, defaults, and dismissal","Before oral argument is scheduled the appellant may withdraw the appeal by written notice; afterward, dismissal is by stipulation signed by the parties. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 18B(1)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Automatic 20-day stay, then a supersedeas bond","Rhode Island · appeals · Stays pending appeal","No execution issues until 20 days after entry of judgment or until the appeal time expires, but injunctions and receivership judgments are not stayed unless ordered. A stay pending appeal is obtained with a supersedeas bond, effective when the court approves it (the State is exempt). Seek a stay or injunction pending appeal in the trial court first; a motion to the Supreme Court must show that was impracticable or wa","#st-ri-appeals"],["Oral argument","Rhode Island · appeals · Argument, decision, and reargument","Full-calendar cases get 30 minutes a side, with 10 minutes of rebuttal for the appellant; only one attorney argues per party without leave. The Court hears arguments September through May. Reading argument from a prepared text is disfavored. If counsel does not appear, the Court may decide on the papers. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 22(b), (f), 24","#st-ri-appeals"],["Reargument: 10 days, no extensions from the Clerk","Rhode Island · appeals · Argument, decision, and reargument","A petition for reargument, with a memorandum of grounds, must be filed within 10 days after the decision is filed, including a request for reargument before the full court after an evenly divided decision. Opposition is due within 10 days after receipt. The Clerk’s extension authority does not apply to reargument petitions. There is no further state appellate review: the Supreme Court is the court of last resort. Sup","#st-ri-appeals"],["Mandatory e-filing, plus paper copies","Rhode Island · appeals · Filing and service in the Supreme Court","All parties must e-file through the Judiciary’s Electronic Filing System, except incarcerated people and attorneys with a waiver; self-represented litigants may opt in. Filing is timely only if received by the deadline; the e-filing system accepts filings until 11:59 p.m. on the due date, and the Clerk’s file mark is the only proof of filing time. Each document goes as a separate file. The Clerk may reject for listed","#st-ri-appeals"],["Service, motions, and emergencies","Rhode Island · appeals · Filing and service in the Supreme Court","Serve every filing on all parties (on counsel if represented) at or before filing, with proof of service. Motions need a supporting memorandum; opposition within 10 days of service and reply within 10 days after that. Sealed filings require a motion when filed. Emergency motions go through the Office of the Administrative Assistant to the Chief Justice, with a certificate of efforts to notify others, and ordinarily o","#st-ri-appeals"],["Notice of appeal, civil (with $150 fee)","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from entry of the judgment, order, or decree (set forth and signed by the clerk).\nFiled in the trial court. Restarts from entry of an order deciding a timely Rule 50(b), 52(b), or 59 motion. The Supreme Court cannot extend it. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a), 5(a)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Cross-appeal / other party’s notice of appeal","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from filing (or deemed filing) of the first notice of appeal, or the ordinary appeal period if longer.\nWhichever period ends later. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Extension of appeal time for excusable neglect","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from expiration of the original appeal period.\nGranted only by the trial court; maximum 30 days. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Rule 50(b), 52(b), or 59 post-trial motion (tolls the appeal period)","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended (Super.R.Civ.P. 6(b)). Rule 60(b) motions do not toll. Super.R.Civ.P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e); Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Order transcript (and file statement of parts and points if partial)","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\n60 days from the notice of appeal for mediation-eligible civil appeals (R. 35(e)). Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 10(b)(1)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Transmit record to the Supreme Court","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nTrial court may extend to no more than 90 days from the first notice of appeal. Mediation cases: 60 days from ordering the transcript (R. 35(e)). Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 11(a), (c)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Mediation Statement (Parts I and II)","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from date of the Notice of Eligibility.\nPart II is confidential and goes only to the mediation program. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 35(d)(1)-(2)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Report errors in docketed case title","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from notice of docketing. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12(b)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Motion to correct the record","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from docketing.\nOr further time the Court allows. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12(d)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Rule 12A statement of the case (3,000 words)","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from docketing of the appeal (or receipt of the record on granted certiorari).\nMust attach the judgment and the trial court’s decision. Late filing leads to conditional dismissal (R. 18A(1)). Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12A(1)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Rule 12A counter-statement (3,000 words)","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the appellant’s statement.\nLate filing leads to conditional default (R. 18A(2)). Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12A(2)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Show cause supplemental statement (3,000 words)","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from issuance of the show cause order.\nResponding party’s counter-statement 10 days after that statement is filed or was due. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12A(4)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief (15,000 words)","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from clerk’s notice of assignment to the regular calendar for full briefing. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 16(a), (f)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief (15,000 words)","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from filing of the appellant’s brief. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 16(b), (f)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Reply brief (7,500 words)","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from filing of the appellee’s brief. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 16(c), (f)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Paper copies of Rule 12A statements and briefs (9) or appendices, petitions, memoranda (6)","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","5 days from clerk’s acceptance of the electronic filing. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 18(h)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Correct and resubmit a rejected filing","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from service of the rejection notice. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 18(g), (i)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Cure a conditional dismissal or default for a late statement or brief","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of the conditional order. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 18A","#st-ri-appeals"],["Show cause after conditional dismissal for 90 days’ inactivity","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from entry of the conditional order of dismissal. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 18B(2)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Objection to a second Clerk extension","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from filing of the motion to extend.\nEach Clerk extension is 30 days. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 20(b)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Opposition to a motion","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from service of the motion and memorandum.\nReply 10 days after service of the opposition (R. 28(c)). Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 28(b)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Memorandum opposing a petition for extraordinary writ / certiorari","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from service of the petition and memorandum.\nReply 10 days after service of the opposition (R. 13(c)). No rule deadline to file the petition itself. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 13(b)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Response to Citation of Supplemental Authority","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the citation.\nNo reply allowed. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 16(e)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Petition for reargument","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the decision.\nOpposition within 10 days after receipt (R. 25(b)). Not extendable by the Clerk. Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 25(a)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Automatic stay of execution","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from entry of judgment.\nOr until the appeal time expires. Injunctions and receivership judgments are not stayed unless ordered; afterward a supersedeas bond is needed (R. 62(d)). Super.R.Civ.P. 62(a)","#st-ri-appeals"],["District Court civil appeal for de novo trial in Superior Court","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","2 days from entry of District Court judgment.\nExcludes Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays; costs including a $50 attorney’s fee paid when the appeal is claimed. R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-12-10","#st-ri-appeals"],["Appeal from a Superior Court magistrate to a Superior Court justice","Rhode Island · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from entry of the magistrate’s judgment, order, or decree.\nTranscript must be filed within 60 days of entry or the appeal is dismissed absent good cause (R. Prac. 2.9(g)). Super. Ct. R. Prac. 2.9(e)","#st-ri-appeals"],["Supreme Court of South Carolina","South Carolina · courts","A Chief Justice and four associate justices, elected by the General Assembly for ten-year terms (S.C. Code § 14-3-10). Hears appeals of right directly in a short list of case types (death sentences, cases whose principal issue is the constitutionality of a state law or ordinance, public bond cases, election cases, orders limiting a State Grand Jury, minors’ abortion orders, and Public Service Commission rate cases), ","#st-sc"],["South Carolina Court of Appeals","South Carolina · courts","A Chief Judge and eight associate judges, sitting in three panels of three (S.C. Code §§ 14-8-10, 14-8-80). Hears every appeal from the circuit court, family court, administrative agencies, the Administrative Law Court, and the Workers’ Compensation Commission that is not reserved to the Supreme Court (S.C. Code § 14-8-200). En banc hearing takes the vote of six judges (Rule 219, SCACR).","#st-sc"],["Circuit Court: Court of Common Pleas (civil) and Court of General Sessions (criminal)","South Carolina · courts","The general-jurisdiction trial court, organized in judicial circuits. Civil cases are ’common pleas’ cases governed by the SCRCP; masters-in-equity and special referees hear references from it, and their orders are appealed the same way as common pleas orders (Rule 203(b)(4), SCACR).","#st-sc"],["Family Court, Probate Court, and the Administrative Law Court","South Carolina · courts","Family court hears domestic relations and juvenile matters; probate court hears estates, guardianships and similar matters (direct appeal only where S.C. Code § 62-1-308(g) allows it); the Administrative Law Court hears contested agency cases, with appeals to the Court of Appeals unless a statute says otherwise (Rules 202(b), 203(b)(5)-(6), 203(d)(2), SCACR).","#st-sc"],["Magistrates Courts and Municipal Courts","South Carolina · courts","Summary courts for small civil claims, minor criminal offenses, and preliminary matters. The SCRCP applies there only where not inconsistent with those courts’ own rules (Rule 81, SCRCP).","#st-sc"],["No anti-SLAPP statute in the South Carolina Code","South Carolina · anti-SLAPP","South Carolina has no anti-SLAPP law: no special motion to dismiss, no discovery stay, and no fee-shifting tied to speech or petitioning. The suggested cite ’S.C. Code § 15-26-10 et seq.’ does not exist. Title 15 has no Chapter 26 (its chapter list jumps from Chapter 19 to Chapter 27), and the old chapter URL returns 404. Searches of the Code of Laws on scstatehouse.gov (current through the 2026 session) for ’SLAPP’,","#st-sc-slapp"],["Pending bill only: S. 43 / H. 3305, ’South Carolina Public Expression Protection Act’","South Carolina · anti-SLAPP","S. 43 (2025-2026 session, sponsored by Sen. Campsen, similar to H. 3305) would add Article 7 to Chapter 3 of Title 15. It would create a procedure for civil claims based on a person’s communication in certain circumstances. It was prefiled December 11, 2024 and referred to the Senate Judiciary Committee on January 14, 2025. The bill status page shows no later action. It is not law. If it is ever enacted, its scope, d","#st-sc-slapp"],["What a speech defendant can use instead","South Carolina · anti-SLAPP","Without an anti-SLAPP statute, the tools are an ordinary Rule 12(b)(6) motion (the SC wording is failure ’to state facts sufficient to constitute a cause of action’), summary judgment under Rule 56, Rule 11 sanctions, and the Frivolous Civil Proceedings Sanctions Act. Under the Act, after a verdict or a dismissal by directed verdict, summary judgment, or JNOV, the prevailing party may move for sanctions against a fri","#st-sc-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: the SC lawyer files the motion; the application goes to Bar Admissions first","South Carolina · out of state","A lawyer admitted in the highest court of another state or D.C. may be admitted pro hac vice before any South Carolina ’tribunal’: any court, the Administrative Law Court, or an agency hearing contested cases. The order of steps matters:\n1. Before any motion, submit the Application for Admission Pro Hac Vice (on the Supreme Court’s form, with a certificate of good standing) to the Supreme Court Office of Bar Admissio","#st-sc-oos"],["Local counsel is mandatory and must sign everything and attend everything","South Carolina · out of state","No one is admitted pro hac vice unless a regular member of the South Carolina Bar in good standing is associated as attorney of record (except under Rule 244(d), which covers certified questions). The SC lawyer must at all times be prepared to go forward with the case, must sign all papers filed after admission, and must attend all later proceedings unless the tribunal specifically excuses attendance. Separately, SCR","#st-sc-oos"],["Six-application ceiling and the ’regularly engaged’ bar","South Carolina · out of state","A lawyer who is regularly employed in South Carolina, or regularly engaged there in law practice or substantial business or professional activity, may not appear pro hac vice without applying for full admission under Rule 402. Filing more than six pro hac vice applications in a calendar year (counting Rule 404(k) arbitration and mediation statements) is treated as regularly practicing in South Carolina. Rule 404(f), ","#st-sc-oos"],["Admission lasts through appeal; disclosures continue","South Carolina · out of state","An admission continues through the rest of the proceeding, including appeals, unless it is withdrawn. When the case moves to another tribunal, the lawyer must immediately give the new tribunal a copy of the admission order and the name and SC Bar number of local counsel. The application discloses every SC pro hac vice application and pending appearance, and the lawyer must keep it current for as long as the appearanc","#st-sc-oos"],["Arbitration and mediation in SC: a verified statement and $250 per matter","South Carolina · out of state","Under Rule 5.5(c)(3), a lawyer licensed elsewhere may temporarily handle an arbitration, mediation, or other ADR matter in South Carolina that arises out of a pre-existing representation. For each matter, the lawyer must file a verified statement with the Office of Bar Admissions, with a $250 fee, stating that the lawyer has not filed more than three such statements in a 365-day period. The statement must be served o","#st-sc-oos"],["E-filing: pro hac vice lawyers cannot e-file in the trial courts","South Carolina · out of state","Common pleas e-filing is mandatory for represented parties in e-filing counties. Only SC-licensed lawyers in good standing may register through the Attorney Information System (AIS). The policies say lawyers admitted pro hac vice ’are not eligible’ and must file through local counsel of record. In the appellate courts, the OneDrive e-filing method is for SC-licensed lawyers, but e-mail and fax filing are open to all ","#st-sc-oos"],["Limited-scope representation: allowed by the ethics rule; no court rule on limited appearances found","South Carolina · out of state","Rule 1.2(c) lets a lawyer limit the scope of a representation if the limit is reasonable and the client gives informed consent. The collected SCRCP, SCACR, and e-filing texts contain no rule on ’limited scope’ or ’limited appearances’ in litigation. Once a lawyer appears of record, assume the lawyer is counsel until the court allows withdrawal. In the appellate courts, trial counsel is deemed appellate counsel until ","#st-sc-oos"],["Full admission: UBE score transfer, but no admission on motion in the text","South Carolina · out of state","Rule 402 requires a UBE score of 266 or higher (from SC or any UBE jurisdiction, no more than 3 years old), an MPRE scaled score of 77 within 3 years of applying, a Course of Study on South Carolina Law, and character and fitness approval. The application fee is $1,000, plus $750 more for anyone admitted elsewhere for over a year. For SC exam-takers the fee is $1,500 if filed late in the application window. The colle","#st-sc-oos"],["Newly admitted lawyers must clear Rule 403 ’trial experiences’ before appearing alone","South Carolina · out of state","Even after admission, a South Carolina lawyer may not appear as counsel at any hearing, trial, or deposition until the Supreme Court approves the Rule 403 trial experiences. Until then, a qualified lawyer must be present throughout. A lawyer admitted in another jurisdiction for 2 years can satisfy the rule by an affidavit of equivalent experience, or a letter from a judge attesting to trial competence, filed with a $","#st-sc-oos"],["Governing code: SC Rules of Professional Conduct, Rule 407, SCACR","South Carolina · ethics","Based on the ABA Model Rules, with official comments. A pro hac vice lawyer must comply with these rules and is subject to the Supreme Court’s disciplinary jurisdiction (Rule 404(g)). The differences below are the ones the SC text itself flags or that appear on the face of the rule. Rule 407, SCACR; Rule 404(g), SCACR","#st-sc-ethics"],["Rule 4.5: no threats of criminal or disciplinary charges to gain civil advantage","South Carolina · ethics","A lawyer may not present, help present, or threaten to present criminal or professional disciplinary charges solely to gain an advantage in a civil matter. The comment says this rule is not in the Model Rules; it comes from former DR 7-105. Demand letters that mention prosecution or a bar complaint are a common trap for out-of-state counsel. Rule 4.5 & cmt., RPC, Rule 407, SCACR","#st-sc-ethics"],["Rule 8.4: ’moral turpitude’ crimes added; no anti-harassment paragraph","South Carolina · ethics","SC Rule 8.4(c) separately makes any criminal act involving moral turpitude professional misconduct; comment [2] notes this is a South Carolina addition. The rule’s paragraphs run (a) through (g), and (g) covers knowingly assisting a judge in misconduct. There is no separate harassment or discrimination paragraph. Biased conduct is addressed through 8.4(e) (prejudicial to the administration of justice) in comment [3].","#st-sc-ethics"],["Fees: objective reasonableness test and ’earned on receipt’ advance fees","South Carolina · ethics","Comment [1] says SC’s Rule 1.5(a)(2) uses an objective test rather than the Model Rule’s subjective one. Rule 1.5(f) allows an advance fee to be treated as earned immediately, and kept out of trust, only under a written fee agreement. The agreement must tell the client the nature and scope of the fee, the total amount and payment terms, that the fee will not be held in trust until earned, that the client can discharg","#st-sc-ethics"],["Advertising and solicitation are stricter than the Model Rules","South Carolina · ethics","Advertisements must be ’predominately informational.’ Every ad must name a responsible lawyer and give the office address and the city or town where the lawyers principally practice. Copies must be kept for 2 years. An advertised fee must be honored for 90 days, or 1 year in an annual publication. Targeted written or electronic solicitations must say ’ADVERTISING MATERIAL’ (in the e-mail subject line, too) and includ","#st-sc-ethics"],["Smaller SC-specific provisions","South Carolina · ethics","Rule 1.19 (not a Model Rule number) encourages written succession plans and a successor-lawyer registry kept by the SC Bar. Rule 3.5 covers ’members of the jury venire’ and allows judicial portrait funds only under listed conditions. The comment to Rule 1.16(b)(5) says the SC version expressly treats nonpayment as a ground for withdrawal. Rules 1.19, 1.16 cmt. [8], 3.5(a), (e) & cmt. [1], RPC, Rule 407, SCACR","#st-sc-ethics"],["Filing alone does not commence the action","South Carolina · land mine","An action is commenced on filing only if the summons and complaint are served within the statute of limitations or, failing that, actually served within 120 days after filing. Serve early when limitations is close. Rule 3(a), SCRCP","#st-sc-unique"],["Answers in 30 days, motions on 10 days’ notice, 5 extra days after mail service","South Carolina · land mine","The answer is due in 30 days. A written motion and its hearing notice must be served at least 10 days before the hearing. Service by mail adds 5 days in the trial courts, but no days are added for mail in the appellate courts (Rule 263(a), SCACR). Rules 6(d), 6(e), 12(a), SCRCP; Rule 263(a), SCACR","#st-sc-unique"],["Every motion needs a good-faith consultation affirmation","South Carolina · land mine","All motions must affirm that counsel communicated with opposing counsel and tried in good faith to resolve the issue, or certify why consultation would be useless or untimely. The listed exceptions include dispositive and post-trial motions and motions against pro se parties. Rule 11(a), SCRCP","#st-sc-unique"],["Discovery caps you will not see in federal court","South Carolina · land mine","General interrogatories beyond the standard set require at least $25,000 in controversy (or declaratory, injunctive, or family court relief) and are capped at 50 including subparts. Requests for admission are capped at 20 per party. Rule 35 exams are available only when more than $100,000 in actual damages is in controversy. Rules 33(b), 35(a), 36(c), SCRCP","#st-sc-unique"],["Notice of appeal: serve in 30 days from receipt of written notice of entry, then file within 10 days","South Carolina · land mine","In civil cases, the notice of appeal must be served on all respondents within 30 days after receipt of written notice of entry of the order or judgment. Within 10 days after service, file it with the lower court clerk and the appellate clerk, with proof of service, the order, and the $250 fee. A late notice or an unpaid fee means dismissal. No court can extend the notice-of-appeal deadline. Rules 203(b)(1), 203(d)(1)","#st-sc-unique"],["Post-trial motion deadlines changed in 2026: now 20 days","South Carolina · land mine","An April 30, 2026 order extended the time for JNOV, Rule 52(b), and Rule 59 motions (including 59(e)) from 10 to 20 days. A timely motion stays the appeal time for all parties until receipt of written notice of entry of the order deciding it. Older SC sources and practice guides still say 10 days. The amendment’s effective date is not shown in the collected text, so check it. Rules 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), SCRCP (as","#st-sc-unique"],["Rule 60(b) has no catch-all ground","South Carolina · land mine","South Carolina’s Rule 60(b) omits the federal ’any other reason that justifies relief’ ground. Grounds (1)-(3) (mistake, new evidence, fraud) carry a hard 1-year limit. Rule 60(b), SCRCP","#st-sc-unique"],["Settlements between counsel bind only if in the record or signed","South Carolina · land mine","An agreement between counsel is enforceable only if it is a consent order or signed stipulation entered in the record, made in open court and noted on the record, or reduced to writing and signed by the parties and their counsel. A handshake or an e-mail exchange is not enough. Rule 43(k), SCRCP","#st-sc-unique"],["Appeal stays civil judgments automatically (with exceptions)","South Carolina · land mine","Unlike federal practice, serving a notice of appeal in a civil matter automatically stays the order appealed from. Money judgments, injunctions, family court orders regarding a child or support, ejectment orders, and other listed exceptions are not stayed without supersedeas. Apply to the lower court first. Rule 241(a), (b), (d)(1), SCACR","#st-sc-unique"],["Rehearing must be actually received in 15 days, and it frames certiorari","South Carolina · land mine","A petition for rehearing (limit 15 pages) must be actually received by the appellate court within 15 days after the opinion is filed; mailing it is not enough. A Court of Appeals decision cannot be reviewed by the Supreme Court until rehearing has been decided. The certiorari petition may raise only questions raised in the Court of Appeals and in the rehearing petition. Rules 221(a), 242(c), 242(d)(1), SCACR","#st-sc-unique"],["Pro hac vice is not a shortcut: local counsel signs and attends, and you cannot e-file","South Carolina · land mine","Your SC co-counsel must sign every paper and attend every proceeding unless excused. You cannot register for trial-court e-filing. More than six applications in a calendar year bars further pro hac vice appearances. Rule 404(f), (i), SCACR; SC Electronic Filing Policies and Guidelines § 3(a)(1)","#st-sc-unique"],["Demand letters: no threats of prosecution or bar complaints","South Carolina · land mine","South Carolina keeps the old Code rule: a lawyer may not threaten criminal or disciplinary charges solely to gain an advantage in a civil matter. Rule 4.5, RPC, Rule 407, SCACR","#st-sc-unique"],["No peremptory change of judge","South Carolina · judges","None of the collected texts (SCRCP, SCACR, the e-filing policies, and the court statutes in S.C. Code Title 14, Chapters 3 and 8) provides for a peremptory challenge, notice of substitution, or automatic change of judge. The only peremptory challenges in the SCRCP are challenges to jurors (Rule 47). Disqualification must be sought for cause. SCRCP (whole text searched); Rule 47(b), SCRCP","#st-sc-unique"],["Disqualification for cause under the Code of Judicial Conduct","South Carolina · judges","South Carolina’s Code of Judicial Conduct is Rule 501, SCACR. It still uses Canon and Section numbering, so cite Canon 3E, not Model Code Rule 2.11. A judge must step aside whenever the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned, including in the listed situations. The commentary says the judge should disclose on the record anything the parties might consider relevant, should tell the parties that a motion i","#st-sc-unique"],["Supreme Court justices: statutory disqualification","South Carolina · judges","No justice may preside in a case in which the justice is interested, was counsel, or presided in a lower court. If justices are disqualified, the Governor commissions special justices. S.C. Code §§ 14-3-50, 14-3-60","#st-sc-unique"],["Cameras in court: banned by default, allowed for media on notice","South Carolina · speech","Broadcasting, recording, and photographing in and next to courtrooms is prohibited except as Rule 605 allows. News media may cover proceedings with video, still cameras, or recorders if they give the presiding judge reasonable notice (Form 1 if written notice is required). Without reasonable notice, the judge may refuse coverage. Rule 605(b), (f)(1), SCACR","#st-sc-unique"],["State constitutional speech protection: not checked","South Carolina · speech","The South Carolina Constitution’s free-speech and petition guarantees were not among the collected texts. Whether South Carolina courts read them more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked. S.C. Const. art. I (not in collected texts)","#st-sc-unique"],["Expert affidavit must be filed with professional-negligence complaints","South Carolina · unique rules","In a negligence suit against a listed SC-licensed professional, including lawyers, doctors, nurses, CPAs, architects, and surveyors, the complaint must be filed with an expert’s affidavit naming at least one negligent act or omission and its factual basis. There are two exceptions: if the limitations period expires within 10 days of filing, the affidavit may follow within 45 days; and no affidavit is needed for matte","#st-sc-unique"],["Secure leave (new, adopted September 23, 2026)","South Carolina · unique rules","Any lawyer admitted to practice in South Carolina may designate up to four full calendar weeks (Monday to Friday) a year as secure leave in AIS. The designation must be made at least 60 days ahead and before anything is scheduled in that week. The lawyer then cannot be required to appear at a trial, hearing, deposition, or court-annexed ADR proceeding that week. Secure leave does not extend any filing or service dead","#st-sc-unique"],["Statewide priority list for conflicting hearing dates","South Carolina · unique rules","When a lawyer is called to two SC tribunals at once, Rule 601 sets the priority. The order starts with the Supreme Court, Court of Appeals, and disciplinary bodies, then certain family court matters, General Sessions, Common Pleas jury terms, other family court matters, Common Pleas non-jury, the Administrative Law Court, court-annexed ADR, probate, and magistrate and municipal courts. A lawyer already in trial canno","#st-sc-unique"],["E-filing timing and e-service","South Carolina · unique rules","A document received by the trial-court E-Filing System by 11:59:59 p.m. Eastern Standard Time is filed that day, if the clerk later accepts it. Service by Notice of Electronic Filing is complete when the document is submitted, and the policies treat it as the equivalent of mailing under Rule 5(b)(1). Whether that triggers Rule 6(e)’s added 5 days is not stated in the collected text. The appellate courts use the same ","#st-sc-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope of Rules","South Carolina · civil rules","The rules govern procedure in all South Carolina courts in every civil suit, whether at law or in equity, subject to the exceptions in Rule 81, and are construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 2. One Form of Action","South Carolina · civil rules","There is one form of action, the “civil action”; the Note says this abolishes the largely cosmetic distinction between actions and special proceedings (such as mandamus or habeas corpus).","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencement of Action","South Carolina · civil rules","An action is commenced when the summons and complaint are filed with the clerk, but only if they are served within the statute of limitations or, if not, actual service is made no later than 120 days after filing (unlike the federal rule, commencement turns on service as well as filing). A plaintiff may move to proceed in forma pauperis with the proposed complaint and an affidavit of inability to pay; inability is pr","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 4. Process","South Carolina · civil rules","The plaintiff or plaintiff’s attorney (not the clerk, unlike the federal rule) issues the summons; service may be made by the sheriff, a deputy, or any non-party, non-attorney at least 18 years old, and summons and complaint must be served together. Methods include personal or abode service, service on agents, officers of entities, the Attorney General (with registered or certified mail) for the State, statutory serv","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 4.1. Service of Process in Foreign Countries","South Carolina · civil rules","Adopted in 2022 from the federal rule: individuals abroad may be served by internationally agreed means such as the Hague Service Convention, by methods allowed by the foreign country’s law or a letter rogatory, by personal delivery or clerk-sent signed-receipt mail unless foreign law prohibits it, or by other court-ordered means; entities may be served the same ways except personal delivery. Proof follows the treaty","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 5. Service and Filing of Pleadings and Other Papers","South Carolina · civil rules","Orders, later pleadings, written motions, discovery requests and responses, notices, offers of judgment, and similar papers must be served on every party of record (parties in default need service only of new claims, served as under Rule 4, and notice of any hearing on unliquidated damages); service on a represented party is made on the attorney by delivery or mail, and mail service is complete on mailing. Civil proc","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 6. Time","South Carolina · civil rules","Exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday, or State or Federal holiday, and exclude intermediate weekends and holidays when the period is under 7 days. Counsel may extend a deadline once by written agreement for up to the original period, and the court may enlarge time before expiration or for good cause after it, but not for Rules 50(b), 52(b), 59, and 60(b), and the time for n","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings Allowed; Form of Motions","South Carolina · civil rules","The pleadings are the complaint, answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a cross-claim, and third-party complaint and answer, plus a court-ordered reply and an optional reply to affirmative defenses under Rule 8(c). Motions must be in writing, state the grounds with particularity, and state the relief sought unless made in open court with a court reporter present; demurrers, pleas, and exceptions for insufficiency","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 8. General Rules of Pleading","South Carolina · civil rules","Unlike the federal rule, South Carolina keeps fact pleading: a claim must state the jurisdictional grounds, the facts showing entitlement to relief, and a demand for judgment, and punitive damages may be demanded only in general terms, not a stated sum, though a party may cap the total amount in controversy. Denials must fairly meet each averment (a general denial only if the pleader in good faith contests everything","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading Special Matters","South Carolina · civil rules","Capacity need not be pleaded but must be challenged by specific negative averment; fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity, while conditions of mind and performance of conditions precedent may be averred generally. Special damages must be specifically stated, libel or slander may be pleaded generally as published concerning the pleader with truth and mitigation allowed in the answer, and an action on an ","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of Pleadings","South Carolina · civil rules","Pleadings need a caption with State, county, court, title, file number, and Rule 7(a) designation; unknown adverse parties may be named with “whose true name is unknown”; averments go in numbered paragraphs with each cause of action and defense stated separately, and exhibits are part of a pleading only if a copy is attached. Papers must be 8½ by 11 inches with at least 1½ line spacing, 1-inch margins, and 12-point t","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing of Pleadings; Attorneys","South Carolina · civil rules","Every paper must be signed by at least one attorney of record admitted in South Carolina, with address and telephone number (or by the unrepresented party), and the signature certifies the paper was read, has good ground, and is not interposed for delay; the Note says SC kept a standard closer to the pre-1983 federal rule. Every motion must affirm that counsel consulted opposing counsel in good faith first, except mo","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and Objections","South Carolina · civil rules","An answer is due within 30 days after service of the complaint (the Note says the federal rule allows 20), as are answers to cross-claims and replies to counterclaims; the State answers post-conviction relief applications within 60 days for guilty pleas and 90 days for trials. After a Rule 12 motion is denied or postponed, or after a more definite statement or ruling on a motion to strike, the responsive pleading is ","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and Cross-Claim","South Carolina · civil rules","A pleading must state any claim against an opposing party arising from the same transaction or occurrence (compulsory, barred if omitted), may state any other claim (permissive), and may state cross-claims against co-parties arising from the same transaction, occurrence, or property; later-maturing claims come in by supplemental pleading and omitted ones by leave of court. Unique to South Carolina, Rule 13(j) transfe","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-Party Practice","South Carolina · civil rules","A defending party may serve a third-party complaint on a nonparty who is or may be liable for all or part of the claim, without leave if filed no later than 10 days after serving the original answer and otherwise on motion; a plaintiff facing a counterclaim may do the same. Unlike the federal rule, Rule 14(c) lets the court order a third-party defendant joined as a regular plaintiff or defendant under Rules 19 or 20,","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and Supplemental Pleadings","South Carolina · civil rules","A party may amend once as a matter of course before or within 30 days after a responsive pleading is served, or within 30 days after service if no response is required and the case is not on the trial roster (the Note says the federal period was 20); otherwise amendment requires leave, freely given when justice requires and no party is prejudiced, or written consent. A response to an amended pleading is due within th","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 16. Pre-Trial Procedure; Formulating Issues","South Carolina · civil rules","After issues are joined the court may hold a pre-trial hearing on matters such as simplifying issues, limiting expert witnesses and discovery time, and referring issues to a master, and counsel must come prepared to state provable facts and law, itemize damages, provide medical records, make good-faith settlement offers, and exchange witness and exhibit lists. A written pre-trial order controls the case and may exclu","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 17. Parties Plaintiff and Defendant; Capacity","South Carolina · civil rules","Actions are brought by the real party in interest, with a reasonable time after objection to ratify, join, or substitute before dismissal, and capacity is governed by South Carolina law. Representatives sue for minors and incompetents, otherwise a next friend or guardian ad litem, and persons imprisoned outside South Carolina appear by guardian ad litem; Rule 17(d), which is not in the federal rule, sets who may appo","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of Claims and Remedies","South Carolina · civil rules","A party may join as many legal or equitable claims as it has against an opposing party, and claims that formerly required a prior judgment (such as a money claim and setting aside a fraudulent conveyance) may be joined. Unlike federal Rule 18, Rule 18(c) expressly lets the court order separate trials or other orders to prevent embarrassment, delay, or unnecessary expense.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 19. Joinder of Persons Needed for Just Adjudication","South Carolina · civil rules","Same as the federal rule: a person must be joined if feasible when complete relief requires it or the person’s absence risks impairing that person’s interest or exposing parties to inconsistent obligations, and if joinder is not feasible the court weighs prejudice, ability to shape relief, adequacy of judgment, and the plaintiff’s alternative remedy in deciding whether to proceed or dismiss. A pleader must name nonjo","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive Joinder of Parties","South Carolina · civil rules","Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, when the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact; parties need not be interested in all the relief. The court may order separate trials or other orders to prevent embarrassment, delay, or expense.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and Non-Joinder of Parties","South Carolina · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may add or drop parties at any stage on motion or its own initiative on just terms, and may sever any claim.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","South Carolina · civil rules","Claimants against a plaintiff may be joined and required to interplead when the plaintiff faces double or multiple liability, even if their claims lack a common origin or the plaintiff denies liability, and a defendant may seek interpleader by cross-claim or counterclaim; the federal statutory-interpleader provision is omitted. Unlike the federal rule, Rule 22(b) lets the party seeking interpleader deposit the money ","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 23. Class Actions","South Carolina · civil rules","A class action requires numerosity, commonality, typicality, adequate representation, and, unless the primary relief is injunctive or declaratory for the class, more than $100 in controversy for each class member (a requirement the Note says is not in the federal rule). The rule also covers shareholder derivative actions (verified complaint, demand allegations) and unincorporated-association actions, requires court a","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","South Carolina · civil rules","Same as the federal rule: intervention of right on timely application where a statute grants an unconditional right or the applicant’s interest may be impaired and is not adequately represented, and permissive intervention for a conditional statutory right, a common question, or a government officer or agency administering a relevant statute or regulation. The motion must be served under Rule 5 with a pleading attach","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of Parties","South Carolina · civil rules","If a party dies and the claim survives, any party or the decedent’s successor may move for substitution, served under Rule 5 on parties and under Rule 4 on nonparties; the action may be dismissed if substitution is not made within a reasonable time, which the Note says replaces the federal 90-day limit, and the decedent’s counsel must promptly notify other parties of the death and the proper substitutes. Actions do n","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 26. General Provisions Governing Discovery","South Carolina · civil rules","Discovery extends to any nonprivileged matter relevant to the subject matter, including insurance agreements, with work-product and expert protections; the court must limit discovery that is cumulative, already available, or unduly burdensome or disproportionate, including electronically stored information that is not reasonably accessible. Unlike the federal rule, a testifying expert may be discovered by any method ","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions Before Action or Pending Appeal","South Carolina · civil rules","A person expecting to be a party but presently unable to sue may file a verified petition in the circuit where an expected adverse party resides to perpetuate testimony, serving notice at least 20 days before the hearing as Rule 4(d) provides for summons (or as the court orders, with an appointed attorney for those not served); the court may order the depositions if perpetuation may prevent a failure or delay of just","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons Before Whom Depositions May Be Taken; Depositions in Out-of-State Actions","South Carolina · civil rules","Depositions in the United States are taken before an officer authorized to administer oaths or a court appointee, and abroad on notice, by commission, or by letter rogatory; no deposition may be taken before a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or counsel, or anyone financially interested. Unique to South Carolina, Rule 28(d) lets a South Carolina-licensed attorney or the clerk issue a subpoena for a depositi","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations Regarding Discovery Procedure","South Carolina · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, parties may stipulate in writing that depositions be taken before any person, at any time or place, on any notice, and in any manner, and may modify other discovery procedures only to the extent Rule 6(b) allows; the 2001 Note says written agreement allows only one extension, for no more than the original time, without court approval.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions Upon Oral Examination","South Carolina · civil rules","Leave is needed for a plaintiff’s deposition within 30 days after service on any defendant (unless the defendant has sought discovery or the deponent is about to travel more than 100 miles away, leave the State, or go to sea) and for prisoners; written notice must be given 10 days in advance (the Note says the federal rule requires only reasonable notice). Unlike the federal rule, a person may be deposed only once ab","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions Upon Written Questions","South Carolina · civil rules","Any party may depose any person by written questions after the action begins, serving the questions with a notice naming the deponent and officer (prisoners only by leave); cross questions are due within 30 days after service, redirect within 10 days after cross, and recross within 10 days after redirect, and the officer takes and certifies the answers as under Rule 30. The Note says this device had no counterpart in","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 32. Use of Depositions in Court Proceedings","South Carolina · civil rules","Depositions may be used to impeach, an adverse party’s or its officer’s or designee’s deposition may be used for any purpose, and any witness’s deposition may be used for any purpose if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles from the place of trial or out of the State (unless procured by the offering party), unable to attend, not obtainable by subpoena, or in exceptional circumstances; depositions from a prior acti","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories to Parties","South Carolina · civil rules","Interrogatories may be served on any party (the Note says not just an adverse party), and answers or objections are due within 30 days after service, but a defendant has 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Unique to South Carolina, standard interrogatories (witnesses and statements, documents and photographs, treating physicians and medical costs in injury cases, liability insurance, itemized damages ","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 34. Production of Documents and Things and Entry Upon Land","South Carolina · civil rules","A party may request inspection, copying, testing, or sampling of documents, electronically stored information, and tangible things in another party’s possession, custody, or control, or entry onto land, describing items with reasonable particularity and optionally specifying the form for ESI. The written response is due within 30 days after service, but a defendant has 45 days after service of the summons and complai","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and Mental Examinations","South Carolina · civil rules","Unlike the federal rule, an examination may be ordered only where more than $100,000 in actual damages is in controversy, on motion for good cause, when a party’s mental or physical condition (including blood group) is in controversy. The examined party’s own physician may attend, and unless agreed or ordered for good cause the exam is held in the county where the examinee or the examinee’s physician resides; the cou","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 36. Request for Admission","South Carolina · civil rules","A party may request admission of facts, opinions of fact, application of law to fact, and genuineness of documents; a matter is admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days after service (or as the court allows or the parties stipulate under Rules 29 and 6(b)), but a defendant need not respond before 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Admissions are conclusive unless withdrawn on motion, an","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to Make or Cooperate in Discovery; Sanctions","South Carolina · civil rules","A party may move to compel answers, designations, or inspection, an evasive or incomplete answer counts as a failure, and the losing side ordinarily pays reasonable expenses and attorney’s fees unless substantially justified. Disobeying a discovery order allows established facts, preclusion, striking pleadings, dismissal, default, contempt (except for refusing an exam), and expenses, and the same sanctions (except co","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury Trial of Right","South Carolina · civil rules","The constitutional and statutory right to a jury is preserved, and fact issues in an action for money only or for specific real or personal property must be tried by a jury unless waived. A written jury demand must be served no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading on the issue, another party may demand other issues within 10 days after a partial demand, failure to serve and file the demand waives a j","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by Jury or by the Court","South Carolina · civil rules","Demanded issues are tried by jury unless the parties stipulate in writing or on the record to a bench trial or the court finds no jury right exists; other issues are tried by the court or referred to a master under Rule 53, though the court may order a jury despite a missing demand. In non-jury actions the court may use an advisory jury, or with both parties’ consent a jury whose verdict has the same effect as one of","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 40. General Docket, Trial Rosters, and Call of Cases for Trial","South Carolina · civil rules","Unique to South Carolina (the federal rule leaves calendaring to local rules): the clerk keeps a General Docket, a Jury Trial Roster, and a Nonjury Docket; filings must state jury or nonjury (undesignated cases go nonjury), and discovery, scheduling, and emergency motions are captioned “Priority Matter” and heard first. A case is tried no earlier than 30 days after it reaches the Jury Trial Roster and is called in ro","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of Actions; Non-Suit","South Carolina · civil rules","A plaintiff may dismiss without a court order by filing and serving notice before the adverse party serves an answer or summary judgment motion (the Note says SC requires service as well as filing) or by stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared; dismissal is without prejudice unless stated, except a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits. Otherwise dismissal req","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 41.1. Sealing Documents and Settlement Agreements","South Carolina · civil rules","Unique to South Carolina and grounded in the State constitution’s open-courts guarantee: absent another governing rule, a party must file and serve a Motion to Seal identifying the documents, with a non-confidential description and a sealed in camera attachment, explaining why less drastic alternatives fail and addressing listed factors (fair trial, witness cooperation, reliance on confidentiality, public significanc","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 41.2. Privacy Protection for Filings","South Carolina · civil rules","Filers must omit or redact Social Security numbers (no portion may appear), taxpayer, driver’s license, passport, and similar identifying numbers; fully redact names of minors who are sexual assault or abuse or neglect victims and otherwise use first name and last initial or initials; use only the last 4 digits of financial account numbers; give only city and state for home addresses of minors, victims, and nonpartie","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; Separate Trials","South Carolina · civil rules","When actions share a common question of law or fact the court may order a joint hearing or trial or consolidate them, and for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or for economy it may order separate trials of any claim or issue, always preserving the right to jury trial; the Note says this is substantially identical to the federal rule and that consolidated actions keep their separate identities.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 43. Conduct of Trial","South Carolina · civil rules","Testimony is taken orally in open court, and the rule keeps SC-specific trial conduct provisions not in the federal rule: one counsel per party examines each witness (standing), opening statements may not argue and pleadings are not given to the jury, no argument on evidentiary objections unless the court asks, final argument is limited to 2 hours per side without permission, the plaintiff (or movant) opens and close","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 44. Proof of Official Record; Foreign Law","South Carolina · civil rules","Domestic official records are proved by official publication or a custodian-attested copy with a certificate of custody, and foreign records by attested copy with a final certification (which the court may excuse for good cause); a certified statement of diligent search is evidence that no record exists, and other lawful methods remain available. A party raising foreign law must give notice in the pleadings or by oth","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","South Carolina · civil rules","A subpoena must state the court, title, and civil action number and set out the text of subdivisions (c) and (d); attorneys may issue and sign subpoenas as officers of the court and the clerk issues blank signed forms, a nonparty deposition or production subpoena must issue from and be served in the county where the nonparty resides, works, or regularly does business, and a copy of any pretrial document or inspection","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 46. Exceptions Unnecessary","South Carolina · civil rules","Formal exceptions to rulings are unnecessary; it is enough to make known to the court, when the ruling is made or sought, the action desired or the objection and its grounds, and a party with no opportunity to object is not prejudiced. The Note says this does not apply to exceptions to masters’ reports under Rule 53 or to appellate exceptions.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 47. Jurors","South Carolina · civil rules","The court may let counsel conduct voir dire or conduct it itself with supplemental questions, and may seat up to 6 alternate jurors, with each side getting 1 extra peremptory challenge for 1 or 2 alternates, 2 for 3 or 4, and 3 for 5 or 6, usable only against alternates. Unlike the federal rule, Rule 47(c) lets the court lodge a deliberating jury overnight, kept together under bailiff supervision and shielded from ou","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 48. Juries of Less Than Twelve; Majority Verdict","South Carolina · civil rules","The jury is 12 persons with a unanimous verdict unless the parties stipulate to fewer than 12 jurors (or fewer than 6 in magistrate’s court civil actions) or to a stated majority verdict.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 49. Special Verdicts and Interrogatories","South Carolina · civil rules","The court may require only a special verdict with written findings on each fact issue (an omitted issue is waived unless a party demands its submission before the jury retires) or a general verdict with written interrogatories, with rules for entering judgment, resubmitting, or ordering a new trial when answers conflict. Unlike the federal rule, Rule 49(c) requires the jury in an action to recover specific personal p","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 50. Motion for a Directed Verdict and for Judgment Notwithstanding the Verdict","South Carolina · civil rules","The judge may direct a verdict when only questions of law are presented; the motion must state specific grounds and may be made at the close of an opponent’s evidence without waiving the right to present evidence. A JNOV motion, which may be joined with or alternative to a new-trial motion, must be made promptly after the jury is discharged or, in the court’s discretion, no later than 20 days after (extended from 10 ","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 51. Instructions to Jury; Objection","South Carolina · civil rules","Any party may file written requests to charge at the close of evidence or earlier as directed; the court must tell counsel its proposed action on them before closing arguments but instructs the jury after arguments. Error in an instruction is preserved only by objecting before the jury retires, stating the matter and grounds distinctly, with an opportunity to object outside the jury’s hearing.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings by the Court","South Carolina · civil rules","In bench trials and with advisory juries the court must find facts specially and state conclusions of law separately (also for interlocutory injunctions), but findings are not needed on Rule 12, Rule 56, or other motions except Rule 41(b). A motion to amend or add findings is due within 20 days after receipt of written notice of entry of judgment (extended from 10 days by the 2026 amendment), stays the time to appeal","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters and Special Referees","South Carolina · civil rules","Unique to South Carolina, the “master” is the county master-in-equity and a “special referee” is a South Carolina Bar member appointed under S.C. Code § 14-11-60; with consent, in default cases, or in foreclosures a circuit judge or the clerk may refer causes of action, and otherwise the circuit court may refer on motion or its own initiative, but any party may demand a jury under Rule 38, which returns the case to c","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgments; Costs","South Carolina · civil rules","A judgment includes any decree or order dismissing the action as to a party or finally determining a party’s rights; partial final judgments in multi-claim or multi-party cases require an express finding of no just reason for delay, and default judgments may not exceed or differ in kind from the demand. Costs go to the prevailing party as of course (against the State only as law allows) on a motion with a supporting ","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 55. Default","South Carolina · civil rules","The clerk enters default upon affidavit or other showing that a party failed to plead or defend, but unlike the federal rule only a judge may enter a default judgment: on affidavit or verified pleading for a liquidated or sum-certain claim against a non-appearing, competent party, and otherwise on application, with at least 3 days’ written notice to a party who has appeared, a hearing as needed, any jury right preser","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary Judgment","South Carolina · civil rules","A claimant may move after 30 days from commencement (or after the adverse party moves) and a defending party at any time, with or without affidavits; the motion must be served at least 10 days before the hearing and opposing affidavits at least 2 days before. Judgment is granted when there is no genuine issue of material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law, including on liability alone; the","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory Judgments","South Carolina · civil rules","Declaratory judgment actions under S.C. Code §§ 15-53-10 through 15-53-140 follow these rules, with jury trial available under Rules 38 and 39; another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the court may order a speedy hearing. The Note says it matches the federal rule except for the State statutory references.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 58. Entry of Judgment","South Carolina · civil rules","On a general jury verdict or a decision for a sum certain, costs only, or denial of all relief, the clerk promptly prepares, signs, and enters judgment unless the court orders otherwise; for other relief or special verdicts the court prepares or directs counsel to prepare the judgment for approval. Every judgment must be on a separate document and is effective only when entered, entry is not delayed for costs, and un","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 59. New Trials; Amendment of Judgments","South Carolina · civil rules","A new trial may be granted for any reason previously recognized in South Carolina law actions (jury) or rehearings (non-jury). In jury cases the motion is made promptly after discharge or, in the court’s discretion, no later than 20 days after; in non-jury cases within 20 days after receipt of written notice of entry of judgment (or filing of an order disposing of the action); a motion to alter or amend under Rule 59","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief From Judgment or Order","South Carolina · civil rules","Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time (with appellate leave while an appeal is pending, and a copy of any written motion to the judge within 10 days after filing). Relief from a final judgment is available for mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect; newly discovered evidence; fraud or misconduct; a void judgment; or a satisfied, reversed, or no-longer-equitable judgment, on motion within a rea","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless Error","South Carolina · civil rules","No error in admitting or excluding evidence or in any ruling or act is grounds for a new trial or for disturbing a verdict, judgment, or order unless refusing relief appears inconsistent with substantial justice, and the court must disregard errors that do not affect substantial rights.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of Proceedings to Enforce a Judgment","South Carolina · civil rules","No execution or enforcement may occur until 10 days after entry of judgment, except that injunction, receivership, and accounting judgments are not stayed unless the court orders; the court may stay enforcement pending post-trial motions under Rules 50, 52(b), 59, or 60 on security conditions. On appeal, a supersedeas bond obtains a stay (subject to the exceptions and the South Carolina Appellate Court Rules), effect","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 63. Disability of a Judge","South Carolina · civil rules","If a judge cannot proceed after a trial or hearing has begun but before final judgment, a successor judge is assigned and may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record and finding no prejudice; in non-jury proceedings the successor must, on request, recall any available witness whose material testimony is disputed. The 2004 Note says the language is similar to federal Rule 63.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 64. Remedies for Seizure; Remedies Not Provided","South Carolina · civil rules","Remedies for seizure of person or property to secure satisfaction of a judgment are available at the start of and during an action as provided by law, and where no statute or rule applies, procedures and remedies follow prior South Carolina court practice.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions; Mandamus, Habeas Corpus, and Other Remedial Writs","South Carolina · civil rules","No temporary injunction issues without notice; a temporary restraining order without notice requires specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint showing immediate irreparable injury, must be endorsed with date and hour, served with the summons and complaint if not yet served, and expires within the time fixed, not exceeding 10 days, unless extended once for good cause or by consent; the adverse party may move t","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers","South Carolina · civil rules","An action in which a receiver has been appointed may be dismissed only by court order, and administration by receivers follows State law. Unlike the federal rule, Rule 66(b) gives every receiver of a debtor’s property, unless restricted, general power to sue for and collect the debtor’s debts and rents, compromise doubtful claims, and sue or defend in the debtor’s name.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit in Court","South Carolina · civil rules","In an action seeking money or a deliverable thing, any party may, on notice to all parties and with leave of court, deposit all or part of the money or thing with the court; money is placed in a bank authorized to receive public funds and withdrawn only by the clerk’s check as the court directs.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of Judgment","South Carolina · civil rules","The Note says this rule follows the State statutes and is not identical to the federal rule: any party except in domestic relations actions may file a written offer of judgment no later than 20 days before trial, and the offeree may file a written acceptance within 20 days after service or at least 10 days before trial, whichever is earlier, after which the court issues judgment. An offer not accepted by then is reje","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution","South Carolina · civil rules","Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution unless the court directs otherwise, with execution and supplementary proceedings as provided by State law, and the judgment creditor may examine any person, including the debtor, using the discovery rules.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 70. Judgment for Specific Acts; Vesting Title","South Carolina · civil rules","If a party fails to perform a judgment directing a conveyance, delivery of documents, or another specific act, the court may have it done by an appointee at that party’s cost, issue attachment or sequestration, hold the party in contempt, or for property in the State enter a judgment divesting and vesting title; a party awarded possession may get a writ of execution or assistance from the clerk.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 71. Foreclosure and Partition","South Carolina · civil rules","Unique to South Carolina, foreclosure and partition actions are tried by the court and ordinarily referred to a master, who computes each claimant’s total debt (principal, interest to the hearing and the pre- and post-judgment rates, escrow items, costs, and attorney’s fees through conclusion), may set lien priorities, confirms lis pendens compliance, and keeps a record of hearings. The judgment orders a sale by the ","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 71.1. Post-Conviction Relief Actions","South Carolina · civil rules","Unique to South Carolina (the Note says there is no federal counterpart), post-conviction relief follows the Uniform Post-Conviction Procedure Act, S.C. Code §§ 17-27-10 to -120, with these rules applying where not inconsistent. An application may not be filed while a direct appeal is pending or the time to perfect one has not run, must use the prescribed form, and is a separate civil action captioned applicant v. St","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 72. Judgments and Orders Subject to Appeal","South Carolina · civil rules","An appeal may be taken, as provided by law, from any final judgment or appealable order; the 1989 Note says the rule does not change existing appellate jurisdiction law, such as S.C. Code § 14-3-330.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 73. Procedure on Appeal to Supreme Court or Court of Appeals","South Carolina · civil rules","Appeals to the South Carolina Supreme Court or Court of Appeals follow the South Carolina Appellate Court Rules (SCACR), not these rules.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 74. Procedure on Appeal to the Circuit Court","South Carolina · civil rules","Unique to South Carolina, appeals to circuit court from inferior courts and administrative agencies follow the governing statutes except that the notice of appeal must be served on all parties within 30 days after receipt of written notice of the judgment, order, or decision; the notice of intention to appeal is filed with the circuit clerk and the lower tribunal within the statutory time or, if none, this rule’s tim","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 75. Record on Appeal to the Circuit Court; Transmittal","South Carolina · civil rules","Appeals to circuit court are heard on the original record, which the lower court’s or agency’s clerk (or its judge or chief official if there is no clerk) must certify and transmit within 30 days after the notice of appeal is filed, extendable for good cause; the circuit clerk then notifies the parties in writing that the record has been filed.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 76. Arbitration; Automobile Reparation","South Carolina · civil rules","Unique to South Carolina, resident circuit judges designate panels of arbitrators for actions under the Automobile Reparation Reform Act of 1974, made up of all attorneys in good standing actively practicing with principal offices in the judicial circuit. Depositions follow Rules 26 through 32 but the Rule 30(a)(2) monetary limit does not apply, and the clerk keeps an arbitration docket in the form specified by the D","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 77. Courts and Clerks","South Carolina · civil rules","Courts are always open for filing papers, issuing process, and making interlocutory motions and orders; trials on the merits are held in open court, other matters may be handled in chambers anywhere, but no hearing other than ex parte or post-trial motions may be held outside the circuit without all affected parties’ consent. The clerk’s office is open business hours except Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays, and","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 78. Clerks of Court; Records, Abstracts, Books","South Carolina · civil rules","Subject to Supreme Court approval, the Office of Court Administration sets procedures for creating, maintaining, and destroying records of the clerks and other courts and for court statistics, with noncompliance punishable as contempt. The clerk enters in the Abstracts of Judgments book every final judgment or order affecting title to or liens on property, keeps indices and other required books, and produces the Code","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 79. Calendar of Civil Actions; File Book","South Carolina · civil rules","Unique to South Carolina, each county clerk keeps one cross-indexed File Book of all circuit civil actions and appeals from inferior courts and agencies, assigns civil action numbers under the Supreme Court’s approved system, and keeps a motion calendar; the plaintiff must state at filing whether the case is jury or non-jury. No action may be heard until entered in the file book with all pleadings filed, only a judge","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 80. Stenographic Report of Transcript as Evidence","South Carolina · civil rules","When reported testimony from a prior trial or hearing is admissible at a later trial, it may be proved by a copy of the transcript certified by the reporter or by the clerk who has custody of the original.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 81. Applicability","South Carolina · civil rules","These rules apply to every trial court of civil jurisdiction in South Carolina within its legal powers, and as far as practicable in magistrate’s, probate, and family courts where not inconsistent with their statutes and rules; where neither statute nor these rules provide, prior State practice governs.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 82. Jurisdiction and Venue","South Carolina · civil rules","The rules do not extend or limit any court’s jurisdiction or venue, except that an action filed in the wrong county or court must be transferred to a proper one rather than dismissed. No order accepting or directing removal is required when a petition to remove a case to federal court is filed. (The text contains no Rule 83.)","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 84. Forms","South Carolina · civil rules","The Supreme Court prescribes the content and format of forms required by these rules, and use of the prescribed forms is mandatory.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 85. Title; Criminal Procedure; Rules Repealed","South Carolina · civil rules","The rules are cited as “Rule __, SCRCP.” Former Circuit Court criminal practice rules 4, 25, 35, 61, 72, 95, 98, 103, 104, and 105 were renumbered Criminal Practice Rules 1 through 10 (an Editor’s Note says that appendix was later repealed by Rule 39, SCRCrimP), and all other prior Circuit Court practice rules were repealed.","#st-sc-rules"],["Rule 86. Effective Date","South Carolina · civil rules","The rules took effect July 1, 1985, governing new actions and further proceedings in pending ones unless applying them would be infeasible or unjust in a particular pending case, and the 1986 amendments took effect May 1, 1986.","#st-sc-rules"],["Appeals in South Carolina: serve the notice within 30 days of receiving written notice of entry, and nothing extends it","South Carolina · appeals","Most civil appeals go to the Court of Appeals. A short list goes directly to the Supreme Court, including constitutional challenges where constitutionality is the principal issue, bond cases, election cases, and death sentences. The Supreme Court then reviews Court of Appeals decisions only by discretionary certiorari. The notice of appeal is served, not just filed, and the clock runs from receipt of written notice o","#st-sc-appeals"],["Court of Appeals by default","South Carolina · appeals · Which court","Every appeal from the circuit court, family court, probate court (where direct appeal is authorized), masters and special referees, and administrative tribunals goes to the Court of Appeals unless it falls in the Supreme Court’s list. A notice filed in the wrong appellate court is transferred, not dismissed. Rules 203(d)(1)(A), 203(d)(2)(A), 204(a), SCACR; S.C. Code § 14-8-200","#st-sc-appeals"],["Direct to the Supreme Court","South Carolina · appeals · Which court","File with the Clerk of the Supreme Court for:\n- a final judgment with a death sentence;\n- a final judgment where the principal issue is the constitutionality of a state law or local ordinance (the Supreme Court may transfer the case if the issue is not significant);\n- public bond and debt cases;\n- election cases;\n- orders limiting a State Grand Jury investigation;\n- family court orders on a minor’s abortion;\n- Public","#st-sc-appeals"],["Certification to the Supreme Court before decision","South Carolina · appeals · Which court","On a party’s motion, the Court of Appeals’ request, or its own motion, the Supreme Court may take a case pending in the Court of Appeals. This is normally for issues of significant public interest or major legal principles. Rule 204(b), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Statutory list of appealable orders","South Carolina · appeals · What can be appealed now (interlocutory orders)","Appealable orders include final judgments and intermediate orders ’involving the merits’. They also include orders affecting a substantial right that in effect determine the action and prevent a judgment, that grant or refuse a new trial, or that strike out an answer or pleading. Final orders affecting a substantial right in special proceedings or after judgment are appealable, as are interlocutory orders granting, c","#st-sc-appeals"],["Only an aggrieved party may appeal","South Carolina · appeals · What can be appealed now (interlocutory orders)","A party must be aggrieved by the order, judgment, sentence, or decision to appeal. Rule 201(b), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Civil (common pleas): serve within 30 days after receipt of written notice of entry","South Carolina · appeals · Notice of appeal","The notice must be served on all respondents within 30 days after receipt of written notice of entry of the order or judgment. If a short or form order says a fuller order will follow, a party may wait for written notice of entry of the fuller order. The same rule governs domestic relations appeals from family court, appeals from masters and special referees, and authorized direct probate appeals. Rule 203(b)(1), (3)","#st-sc-appeals"],["Post-trial motions stay the time for all parties","South Carolina · appeals · Notice of appeal","A timely motion for JNOV (Rule 50), to alter or amend (Rules 52 and 59), or for a new trial (Rule 59) stays the appeal time for all parties. The time runs from receipt of written notice of entry of the order granting or denying the motion. As of the April 30, 2026 SCRCP amendments, those motions are due within 20 days (formerly 10). Rule 203(b)(1), SCACR; Rules 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), SCRCP","#st-sc-appeals"],["Then file within 10 days after service, with the fee","South Carolina · appeals · Notice of appeal","File the notice with the lower court clerk and the appellate clerk within 10 days after service. Attach proof of service, a copy of the written order(s) appealed, and the $250 filing fee (set by order of October 9, 2018; not charged in criminal appeals or to the State). If the notice is not filed on time or the fee is not paid in full, the appeal is dismissed and can be reinstated only under Rule 260. Rule 203(d)(1)(","#st-sc-appeals"],["Administrative tribunal appeals: 30 days from receipt of the decision","South Carolina · appeals · Notice of appeal","Serve the agency, the Administrative Law Court (if involved), and all parties of record within 30 days after receipt of the decision. File with the appellate clerk within the same 30 days. A timely petition for rehearing to the tribunal stays the time. Rule 203(b)(6), (d)(2)(B), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Cross-appeals","South Carolina · appeals · Notice of appeal","A respondent cross-appeals by serving a notice of appeal within 5 days after receiving the appellant’s notice, or within the regular Rule 203(b) time, whichever expires later. Rule 203(c), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Contents and form","South Carolina · appeals · Notice of appeal","The notice names the court, judge, and county, the lower court docket number and any related appeals, the date of the order and (where timeliness depends on it) when notice of it was received, the appealing party, and every attorney of record with contact details. Use the Appendix form. Appeals from guilty pleas need a written explanation of the reviewable issue. Rule 203(d)(1)(B)(iv), (e), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["The appellate court takes exclusive jurisdiction on service","South Carolina · appeals · Notice of appeal","Once the notice is served, the appellate court has exclusive jurisdiction over the appeal. The lower court keeps power over supersedeas petitions and matters not affected by the appeal. Rule 205, SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Automatic stay in civil matters","South Carolina · appeals · Stays and supersedeas","Serving a notice of appeal in a civil matter automatically stays the matters decided and the relief ordered, for the whole appeal, unless the stay is lifted. Rule 241(a), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Exceptions (no automatic stay)","South Carolina · appeals · Stays and supersedeas","The exceptions include money judgments (S.C. Code § 18-9-130), judgments directing delivery of documents or property or the execution of conveyances, sales or delivery of real property, perishable property, family court orders regarding a child or support, workers’ compensation awards, injunctions and TROs, family court temporary fee awards, ejectment orders, and certain administrative appeals. The list is not comple","#st-sc-appeals"],["Procedure","South Carolina · appeals · Stays and supersedeas","Apply first to the lower court or tribunal unless extraordinary circumstances make that impracticable. Then petition the appellate court with a petition verified by the client. Attach a certified copy of the order and the notice of appeal with proof of service. Relief may be conditioned on a bond. Rule 241(c)(3), (d), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Order the transcript in 10 days (civil)","South Carolina · appeals · Transcript, briefs, and the Record on Appeal","In civil and domestic appeals, arrange the transcript in writing with the court reporter within 10 days after serving the notice of appeal (30 days in criminal and juvenile cases). Copy all parties, Court Administration, and the appellate clerk. The reporter has 60 days to deliver, and Court Administration may grant up to three extensions totaling 90 days. The whole transcript must be ordered unless the parties agree","#st-sc-appeals"],["Initial briefs: 30 / 30 / 10 days","South Carolina · appeals · Transcript, briefs, and the Record on Appeal","The appellant’s initial brief is due 30 days after receiving the transcript (or 30 days after serving the notice of appeal if no transcript). The respondent’s brief is due 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief, and an optional reply 10 days after service of the respondent’s brief. If the appellant misses the deadline, the clerk signs a dismissal order. Rule 208(a), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Designation of the record served with each brief","South Carolina · appeals · Transcript, briefs, and the Record on Appeal","Each party serves, with its initial brief (including a reply), a Designation of Matter to be Included in the Record on Appeal. Signing it certifies that nothing irrelevant is included. Rule 209, SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Record on Appeal: 30 days after the last brief","South Carolina · appeals · Transcript, briefs, and the Record on Appeal","The appellant serves the Record on Appeal within 30 days after service of the last brief. It must be filed no later than the final briefs. The court will not consider facts outside the Record. Rule 210(a), (b), (h), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Final briefs: 20 days after service of the Record","South Carolina · appeals · Transcript, briefs, and the Record on Appeal","Final briefs must be identical to the initial briefs except for record citations, changed to ’(R. p. _, line _)’ form, and correction of obvious typographical errors. Rule 211, SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Brief contents","South Carolina · appeals · Transcript, briefs, and the Record on Appeal","The brief must contain:\n- tables of contents and authorities;\n- a statement of issues on appeal (points not stated ordinarily will not be considered);\n- a statement of the case, which binds the appellant and must not contain contested matters;\n- a ’Standard of Review’ section;\n- argument by issue;\n- a short conclusion.\nThe respondent may argue for affirmance on any ground in the record. Rules 208(b)(1)-(2), 220(c), S","#st-sc-appeals"],["Page limits (not word counts)","South Carolina · appeals · Length and format limits","Principal briefs are limited to 50 pages and reply briefs to 25 (no limit in death-penalty cases); exceeding them requires a motion. Other limits: a petition for rehearing, 15 pages; a petition for certiorari to the Court of Appeals, 25 pages; the return, 25; the reply, 15. Rules 208(b)(5), 221(a), 242(d)(3), (f), (g), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Typeface and paper","South Carolina · appeals · Length and format limits","Use 12-point or larger type, double spaced, with one-inch margins. Initial briefs are stapled at the top left. Electronic filings must be PDF. Rules 208(b)(8), 267, SCACR; Order re Rule 262 (Apr. 24, 2024) ¶ (b)","#st-sc-appeals"],["Motions practice","South Carolina · appeals · Motions, time, and dismissal","Every motion needs a certificate of service, a supporting memorandum, and supporting affidavits where facts are outside the record. Returns are due in 10 days and replies in 5. The motion fee is $50, with listed exceptions. Filing a motion does not stay briefing deadlines, except a motion to dismiss or to be relieved as counsel. A single judge or justice may rule, and review is by petition for rehearing. Rule 240(b)-","#st-sc-appeals"],["Counting time: no extra days for mail","South Carolina · appeals · Motions, time, and dismissal","Exclude the trigger day. If the last day falls on a weekend or holiday, the period runs to the next business day. Periods under 7 days exclude weekends and holidays. Unlike the trial courts, no days are added for service by mail; SCRCP Rule 6(e) does not apply. The appellate court may extend any deadline except the time to serve a notice of appeal. Parties cannot extend deadlines by agreement. Rule 263, SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Dismissal and reinstatement","South Carolina · appeals · Motions, time, and dismissal","If the appellant misses a rule requirement, the clerk issues a dismissal order. To reinstate, a motion showing good cause must be actually received within 15 days after the dismissal order is filed, or the case is remitted. Rule 260(a), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Frivolous appeals","South Carolina · appeals · Motions, time, and dismissal","The court may sanction attorneys or parties for frivolous appeals, petitions, motions, or returns, or for rule violations, after 10 days’ notice. Rule 269, SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Rehearing: actually received within 15 days","South Carolina · appeals · Rehearing, remittitur, and costs","A petition for rehearing must be actually received within 15 days after the opinion is filed. It must state with particularity what was overlooked or misapprehended, and is limited to 15 pages. No return is allowed unless the court requests one, and rehearing is ordinarily not granted without a request. No rehearing is allowed from denial of certiorari, refusal of original jurisdiction, or denial of reinstatement. Ru","#st-sc-appeals"],["En banc in the Court of Appeals","South Carolina · appeals · Rehearing, remittitur, and costs","A suggestion for en banc rehearing goes inside the petition for rehearing. Six judges must vote for it. Rule 219, SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Remittitur","South Carolina · appeals · Rehearing, remittitur, and costs","The remittitur is sent no sooner than 15 days after the decision. It is held while a rehearing petition is pending and, in the Court of Appeals, until the time for certiorari runs out. Rule 221(b), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Costs and the fixed attorney’s fee","South Carolina · appeals · Rehearing, remittitur, and costs","In civil appeals the prevailing party may tax:\n- the filing fee;\n- transcript costs;\n- bond premiums;\n- printing costs;\n- an attorney’s fee set by the Supreme Court at $2,500 (order of January 17, 2018).\nMove for costs, with a sworn itemized statement, within 15 days after the remittitur issues. Rule 222(b), (d) & n.1, SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Rehearing in the Court of Appeals comes first","South Carolina · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court","A Court of Appeals decision is not final for Supreme Court review until the Court of Appeals rules on a petition for rehearing or reinstatement. Only questions raised in the Court of Appeals and in the rehearing petition may be presented. Rule 242(c), (d)(1), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Certiorari: 30 days after rehearing is decided","South Carolina · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court","Serve the petition and file it with both clerks within 30 days after the Court of Appeals finally decides rehearing, with a $250 fee. Returns are due in 30 days and replies in 10. Review is discretionary; listed factors include novel questions, a dissent below, conflict with Supreme Court precedent, substantial constitutional issues, and conflict with the U.S. Supreme Court on a federal question. Since April 30, 2024","#st-sc-appeals"],["If certiorari is granted","South Carolina · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court","Briefing is 30 / 30 / 10 days from the grant, and there is no oral argument unless ordered. Rule 242(i), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Certified questions and original jurisdiction","South Carolina · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court","The Supreme Court may answer questions of SC law certified by a federal court or another state’s appellate court. Out-of-state counsel must move for pro hac vice within 15 days after the order accepting the question. Original-jurisdiction petitions must show why the matter cannot first be decided in a lower court. Rules 244(a), (d), 245(a), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Filing methods and the mailbox rule","South Carolina · appeals · Filing and service (including electronic)","Documents may be filed by hand delivery, by first-class mail (filed on the mailing date), or electronically as the Supreme Court orders. The mailbox rule does not apply to petitions for rehearing or motions to reinstate, which must be actually received. Every filing needs proof of service. Rule 262(a), (b), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Electronic filing","South Carolina · appeals · Filing and service (including electronic)","SC-licensed lawyers may file through OneDrive for Business (registration through AIS, ’Appellate Filings’). Anyone may file by e-mail to supctfilings@sccourts.org or ctappfilings@sccourts.org (PDF only, no file-sharing links) or by fax. A document received by 11:59:59 p.m. Eastern Standard Time is filed that day. Any fee must be mailed or delivered within five days. Order re Rule 262 (Apr. 24, 2024) ¶¶ (b), (c)","#st-sc-appeals"],["Electronic service","South Carolina · appeals · Filing and service (including electronic)","SC lawyers may serve each other at the primary e-mail address in AIS (PDF attachment), and must include a copy of the sent e-mail with the proof of service. Service on the associated SC lawyer counts as service on a pro hac vice lawyer. A notice of appeal may be served through trial-court e-filing, with the Notice of Electronic Filing as proof. Rule 262(c)(3), SCACR; Order re Rule 262 (Apr. 24, 2024) ¶ (d)","#st-sc-appeals"],["Counsel continue on appeal","South Carolina · appeals · Filing and service (including electronic)","Trial counsel and guardians ad litem remain counsel on appeal until the appellate court approves withdrawal by written order. Rule 264, SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Serve notice of appeal (civil, common pleas, domestic family court, masters, probate)","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from receipt of written notice of entry of the order or judgment.\nCannot be extended by any court (Rule 263(b)). Restarts from receipt of written notice of entry of the order deciding a timely Rule 50, 52 or 59 motion. Rule 203(b)(1), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["File the served notice of appeal with lower court and appellate clerks","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from service of the notice of appeal.\nWith proof of service, copy of order, and $250 fee; failure means dismissal (Rule 203(d)(3)). Rule 203(d)(1)(B), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Serve and file notice of appeal from an administrative tribunal","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from receipt of the decision.\nA timely petition for rehearing to the tribunal stays the time. Rule 203(b)(6), (d)(2)(B), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Cross-appeal","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","5 days from receipt of appellant’s notice of appeal.\nOr the regular Rule 203(b) time, whichever expires later. Rule 203(c), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Post-trial motions that stay appeal time (JNOV, 52(b), 59 new trial, 59(e))","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from jury discharge (jury cases) or receipt of written notice of entry (non-jury and 59(e)).\nExtended from 10 days by the April 30, 2026 amendments; effective date not shown in collected text. Rules 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), SCRCP","#st-sc-appeals"],["Order transcript (civil)","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from service of notice of appeal.\n30 days in General Sessions and juvenile appeals. Rule 207(a)(1), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Appellant’s initial brief","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from receipt of transcript (or service of notice of appeal if none).\nMissing it produces a clerk’s dismissal order. Rule 208(a)(1), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Respondent’s initial brief","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of appellant’s brief. Rule 208(a)(2), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Reply brief","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from service of respondent’s brief.\nOptional. Rule 208(a)(3), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Serve Record on Appeal","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the last brief.\nFile no later than final briefs. Rule 210(a), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Final briefs","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from service of the Record on Appeal. Rule 211(a), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Return to motion / reply","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from service of motion (reply: 5 days from service of return). Rule 240(e), (f), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the opinion, order, judgment, or decree.\nMust be actually received; 15-page limit. Rule 221(a), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Motion to reinstate after clerk’s dismissal","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the dismissal order.\nMust be actually received. Rule 260(a), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Petition for certiorari to review Court of Appeals","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from final decision on petition for rehearing or reinstatement in the Court of Appeals.\n$250 fee; 25-page limit. Rule 242(c), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Return to certiorari petition","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the petition.\nReply due 10 days after service of return. Rule 242(f), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Motion for costs","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from issuance of the remittitur.\nSworn itemized statement required. Rules 222(d), 242(j)(4), SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["Motion to argue against precedent orally","South Carolina · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from before oral argument.\nCounted backward; no leave needed to argue against precedent in the brief. Rule 217, SCACR","#st-sc-appeals"],["South Dakota Supreme Court","South Dakota · courts","The state’s highest court and its only appellate court. Hears appeals from the circuit courts directly (there is no intermediate court of appeals), has original jurisdiction over certain proceedings, and administers the Unified Judicial System, including bar admission and lawyer discipline. Civil appellate procedure is codified at SDCL ch. 15-26A.","#st-sd"],["Circuit Courts (seven judicial circuits)","South Dakota · courts","The general-jurisdiction trial courts for civil and criminal cases, organized into seven judicial circuits, each with a presiding judge. Civil procedure is codified at SDCL ch. 15-6. On October 2, 2026, the State Court Administrator’s Office proposed a rule merging the Fourth and Seventh Judicial Circuits into one administrative circuit; the Supreme Court’s rules hearing was set for November 9, 2026. Check whether th","#st-sd"],["Magistrate Courts","South Dakota · courts","Operate under the supervision of the circuit courts. Handle misdemeanor criminal cases, minor civil actions, small claims, and preliminary hearings in criminal cases. Magistrate judges and nonlaw-trained magistrates are subject to the same affidavit-for-change procedure as circuit judges.","#st-sd"],["Tribal courts","South Dakota · courts","Tribal courts of the Native American nations located in South Dakota are separate sovereign court systems, not part of the Unified Judicial System. The UJS website keeps a tribal-courts page. The state bar examination includes an Indian law question.","#st-sd"],["New anti-SLAPP law: SDCL ch. 15-40 (2026)","South Dakota · anti-SLAPP","South Dakota enacted its first anti-SLAPP statute in 2026 (SL 2026, ch 91), codified as SDCL ch. 15-40, “Expedited Procedure for SLAPPs.” The text follows the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act model. The codified text does not state an effective date. Before relying on it for conduct or suits that predate the Act, confirm when it took effect and whether it applies to pending cases. SDCL 15-40-1 to 15-40-13; SL","#st-sd-slapp"],["Scope: protected public expression","South Dakota · anti-SLAPP","The chapter covers a cause of action based on a person’s: (1) communication in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative or other governmental proceeding; (2) communication on an issue under consideration or review in such a proceeding; or (3) exercise of free speech, press, assembly, petition or association rights under the U.S. or South Dakota Constitution on a matter of public concern. It does not apply t","#st-sd-slapp"],["Procedure: 60-day motion, automatic stay, 60-day hearing and ruling","South Dakota · anti-SLAPP","File a special motion for expedited relief within 60 days after service of the pleading asserting the claim, or later for good cause. Filing stays all other proceedings between the movant and the respondent, including discovery, until the motion is decided and the time to appeal expires. The court may allow limited discovery if specific information is needed and not otherwise available. The court must hear the motion","#st-sd-slapp"],["Standard: dismissal with prejudice","South Dakota · anti-SLAPP","The court considers the pleadings, motion papers and any evidence admissible on summary judgment. It must dismiss with prejudice if: the movant shows the chapter applies; the respondent fails to show an exclusion; and either the respondent fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of material fact. A voluntary dismissal without pr","#st-sd-slapp"],["Mandatory fees and immediate appeal","South Dakota · anti-SLAPP","A prevailing movant must be awarded court costs, reasonable attorney fees and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion. A prevailing respondent gets them only if the court finds the motion frivolous or filed solely to delay. A movant may appeal an order denying the motion in whole or in part as of right, within 30 days after entry of the order, and an appeal stays all proceedings between all parties until","#st-sd-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: sworn motion, $200, and local counsel who personally participates","South Dakota · out of state","A nonresident lawyer licensed in another U.S. jurisdiction may take part in the trial or hearing of a particular case only if a resident South Dakota lawyer, a State Bar member, is actually employed and associated and personally participates in the trial or hearing.\nFile a written sworn motion with the court where admission is sought. It must state: your post-office address; the office address of the associated South","#st-sd-oos"],["Sales and use tax license for out-of-state lawyers","South Dakota · out of state","The pro hac vice motion must state either that you have applied for, or hold, a South Dakota Sales and Use Tax License from the Department of Revenue, or that you are not in private practice and are appearing for your full-time employer without extra pay. The multijurisdictional-practice rule likewise requires a lawyer providing temporary services in South Dakota to obtain a South Dakota sales tax license and pay the","#st-sd-oos"],["Consequences of pro hac vice admission","South Dakota · out of state","Filing the motion submits you to the State Bar Disciplinary Board’s jurisdiction, with venue in the county where the court sits. The admitting judge may revoke admission for professional misconduct, cite you for contempt, and refer you for discipline. Appearing in a contested administrative hearing under SDCL ch. 1-26 follows the same procedure, subject to approval by the circuit court for the county of the hearing o","#st-sd-oos"],["Pro hac vice counsel cannot sign attorney subpoenas","South Dakota · out of state","Only an attorney of record who is admitted in South Dakota and on the State Bar’s active list may issue a subpoena. The subpoena must be filed with the clerk at the same time it is issued and must carry the required warning text above the signature. In practice the associated South Dakota lawyer must issue subpoenas. SDCL 15-6-45","#st-sd-oos"],["Interstate deposition subpoenas: no pro hac vice needed","South Dakota · out of state","To take discovery in South Dakota for an out-of-state case, submit the foreign subpoena to the clerk in the county where discovery will happen; the clerk issues a South Dakota subpoena that must conform to SDCL 15-6-45. The request is not an appearance. A lawyer not licensed in South Dakota may litigate enforcement, quash and protective-order disputes over that subpoena without pro hac vice admission. SDCL 15-6-28.1 ","#st-sd-oos"],["Temporary practice without admission (Rule 5.5)","South Dakota · out of state","A lawyer admitted and in good standing in another U.S. jurisdiction may provide temporary services in South Dakota that are: done in association with an actively participating South Dakota lawyer; related to a pending or expected proceeding in which the lawyer is or expects to be authorized to appear; related to arbitration, mediation or other ADR arising from the home practice, where pro hac vice is not required; or","#st-sd-oos"],["Permanent admission: exam (not the UBE) or admission without exam","South Dakota · out of state","Admission on examination costs $450. The South Dakota exam combines the MEE, MPT, MBE and an Indian law question, with its own passing scores: a 75% average on the written portion, a 133 scaled MBE, and an 85 MPRE. It is not described as a Uniform Bar Examination.\nAdmission without examination costs $650 and requires an ABA-accredited law degree and 3 of the last 5 years in active, lawful practice as a principal occu","#st-sd-oos"],["Limited-scope representation","South Dakota · out of state","Rule 1.2(c) allows a lawyer to limit the scope of representation if the limitation is reasonable and the client gives informed consent. The collected civil rules and e-filing rules contain no specific limited-appearance or ghostwriting procedure, so check local practice and the court’s expectations before entering a limited appearance. S.D. R. Prof. Conduct 1.2(c)","#st-sd-oos"],["E-filing: Odyssey registration required","South Dakota · out of state","Lawyers must e-file in all civil cases in circuit and magistrate courts (small claims excepted) and in the Supreme Court through the UJS Odyssey system. Each user must register and designate an e-mail address first, and registration is consent to electronic service. A pro hac vice lawyer should plan for registration, or have local counsel file. Fees are paid at filing, and if a fee waiver is denied the fee is due wit","#st-sd-oos"],["Governing code: South Dakota Rules of Professional Conduct","South Dakota · ethics","The Rules are codified as the Appendix to SDCL ch. 16-18, adopted by Supreme Court Rule 03-26 effective January 1, 2004 and amended since (most recently noted: Supreme Court Rule 25-06, effective March 5, 2025, amending Rule 1.8). They follow the ABA Model Rules numbering with the South Dakota differences below. Chapter 16-18 also contains statutory attorney duties (for example, §§ 16-18-13 to 16-18-19) and the attor","#st-sd-ethics"],["Malpractice-insurance disclosure on letterhead","South Dakota · ethics","A lawyer without professional liability insurance of at least $100,000, or whose policy lapses during the representation, must say so on the letterhead in prescribed words (“This lawyer is not covered by professional liability insurance” or the firm equivalent). The disclosure must appear in every written communication with a client. Some trust-account exempt classes, full-time in-house counsel and government lawyers","#st-sd-ethics"],["Contingent fees: written notice of expenses","South Dakota · ethics","Beyond the ABA requirements, a contingent-fee agreement must clearly tell the client about potential expenses the client will owe whether or not the client prevails. S.D. R. Prof. Conduct 1.5(c)","#st-sd-ethics"],["Confidentiality exceptions differ from the ABA","South Dakota · ethics","South Dakota permits disclosure to prevent the client from committing a criminal act likely to result in imminent death or substantial bodily harm. The ABA standard is reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm, from any cause. South Dakota also permits disclosure to rectify the consequences of a client’s criminal or fraudulent act in which the lawyer’s services were used. It does not have the ABA’s separate","#st-sd-ethics"],["No anti-discrimination/harassment misconduct rule","South Dakota · ethics","South Dakota Rule 8.4 has paragraphs (a) through (f) only. It does not include ABA Model Rule 8.4(g) on harassment or discrimination. Separately, active State Bar members must complete sexual harassment prevention training within two years of admission and every three years after, or be placed on inactive status. S.D. R. Prof. Conduct 8.4; SDCL 16-18-1.1","#st-sd-ethics"],["Stricter advertising rules","South Dakota · ethics","All lawyer communications must be “predominantly informational.” Rule 7.1(c) lists specific forms of misleading communication beyond the ABA rule. Examples: mentioning past success without a disclaimer that it cannot assure future results; unverifiable self-praise; claiming experience that cannot be substantiated; omitting the lawyer’s name and city; and testimonials without a prescribed disclaimer.\nRule 7.2 requires","#st-sd-ethics"],["Cannabis advice","South Dakota · ethics","A lawyer may counsel or assist a client on conduct expressly permitted by South Dakota cannabis laws even if it violates federal law. The lawyer must tell the client that the conduct violates federal law and advise on the federal consequences. S.D. R. Prof. Conduct 1.2(e)","#st-sd-ethics"],["Your action is not commenced until the summons is served","South Dakota · land mine","Filing a complaint does not commence an action or stop the statute of limitations. An action is commenced as to each defendant when that defendant (or a codefendant united in interest) is served with the summons. Delivering the summons to the sheriff of the proper county counts only if service or first publication follows within 60 days. SDCL 15-6-3, 15-2-30, 15-2-31","#st-sd-unique"],["No notice of entry, no appeal clock, and the notice of appeal can’t be extended","South Dakota · land mine","The 30-day appeal time runs only after the judgment is signed, attested, and filed and written notice of entry is given to the adverse party. A notice of entry not filed within 10 days after service is deemed served on its filing date. The Supreme Court cannot enlarge the time to appeal. The notice of appeal is filed in the circuit court, and the clerk must reject it without the docketing statement, proof of service ","#st-sd-unique"],["Post-trial motions: 10 days, and a 20-day deemed denial","South Dakota · land mine","Renewed JMOL and new-trial motions are due 10 days after notice of entry of judgment. If the court does not rule, or extend its time by order, within 20 days after the new-trial motion is served and filed, the motion is deemed denied. The appeal clock then starts on that date with no further notice. SDCL 15-6-50(b), 15-6-59(b), 15-26A-6","#st-sd-unique"],["Cross-appeal is a ‘notice of review’ filed in the Supreme Court within 20 days","South Dakota · land mine","An appellee seeking relief must file a notice of review with section B of the docketing statement and proof of service with the Supreme Court clerk within 20 days after service of the notice of appeal. Defending the judgment in the brief is not enough. SDCL 15-26A-22","#st-sd-unique"],["Order the transcript within 10 days or lose it","South Dakota · land mine","The appellant must order the transcript on the Supreme Court form within 10 days after filing the notice of appeal, serving the order and filing a copy. Late ordering waives the transcript. SDCL 15-26A-48, 15-26A-49","#st-sd-unique"],["Interlocutory review: 10 days from notice of entry","South Dakota · land mine","Most pretrial orders are reviewable only by a petition for permission to appeal. File it with the Supreme Court within 10 days after notice of entry of the order, with a 10-page limit and required attachments. The petition does not stay the case. SDCL 15-26A-3(6), 15-26A-13 to 15-26A-15, 15-26A-19","#st-sd-unique"],["Short-time periods skip weekends; e-service after midnight slips a day","South Dakota · land mine","For periods under 11 days, intermediate Saturdays, Sundays and legal holidays are excluded. Three days are added only for mail service, not e-mail or fax. Service by e-mail, fax or Odyssey must be completed by 11:59 p.m. Central time on a non-holiday weekday, or it is deemed made the next weekday. Motions must be served at least 10 days before the hearing, responses 5 days before, and replies 2 days before. SDCL 15-6","#st-sd-unique"],["Summary judgment runs on a 28/14/7-day clock with numbered fact statements","South Dakota · land mine","The motion, brief, statement of undisputed material facts and affidavits must be served at least 28 calendar days before the hearing, the response at least 14, and the reply at least 7. The opponent must answer each numbered fact with record citations. Facts not controverted are admitted. SDCL 15-6-56(c)","#st-sd-unique"],["Answer in 30 days, but jury demand in 10","South Dakota · land mine","The answer is due 30 days after service of the complaint, not 21 as in federal court. A jury demand must be served no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue, or the jury is waived. SDCL 15-6-12(a), 15-6-38(b), (d)","#st-sd-unique"],["Pro hac vice requires a sales tax license and local counsel at trial","South Dakota · land mine","The out-of-state lawyer’s sworn motion must confirm a South Dakota Sales and Use Tax License (or an application for one), unless the lawyer appears for a full-time employer without extra pay. The resident lawyer must personally participate in the trial or hearing, and only South Dakota-admitted active lawyers can issue subpoenas. SDCL 16-18-2, 15-6-45; S.D. R. Prof. Conduct 5.5(c)(5)","#st-sd-unique"],["Change of judge: ask informally first, and don’t argue anything","South Dakota · land mine","The affidavit for change of judge must be preceded by an informal request to the judge. Submitting argument or proof to that judge on any motion waives the right for the rest of the case. The deadline before a jury trial is 15 days. SDCL 15-12-21.1, 15-12-24, 15-12-27","#st-sd-unique"],["Costs waived after 30 days; fee motions due in 14","South Dakota · land mine","Costs are waived unless a verified application is filed within 30 days after entry of judgment, and objections to costs are due within 10 days after service. A motion for attorney fees is due 14 days after entry of judgment. Appellate attorney fees must be sought by motion with a verified itemized statement before the appeal is submitted. SDCL 15-6-54(d), 15-26A-87.3","#st-sd-unique"],["Peremptory change of judge by affidavit: informal request first","South Dakota · judges","South Dakota allows one change of judge per side, without stating a reason, by affidavit. Before filing, the party or lawyer must informally ask the assigned judge or magistrate to step aside, by letter, orally, or on the record. Opposing parties should be told of the request but cannot contest it. If the judge declines, the judge notifies the parties in writing, and the affidavit may then be filed. SDCL 15-12-21, 15","#st-sd-unique"],["Affidavit contents, filing and service","South Dakota · judges","The affidavit must state the case title and that it is made in good faith and not for delay. It must also state that the case is expected to come on for trial before the named judge, and that the party believes it cannot get a fair and impartial trial before that judge. No grounds need be stated. Name only one judge. Parties united in interest must join, and one party’s filing counts for all. File with the clerk of t","#st-sd-unique"],["Deadlines and waiver","South Dakota · judges","Against the judge who would preside in the ordinary course, file:\n• for a noticed motion, at least 2 days before the hearing;\n• for a nonjury trial, at least 5 days before trial;\n• for a jury trial, at least 15 days before trial;\n• against a replacement judge, within 2 days after notice of the replacement’s name.\nLate-retained counsel must file within 5 days after appointment or retention.\nArguing or presenting proof","#st-sd-unique"],["Who decides and what happens","South Dakota · judges","The presiding judge of the circuit (or the senior judge if the presiding judge is the target) reviews timeliness and waiver and assigns another judge. If every judge in the circuit is disqualified, the Chief Justice assigns one. Orders the disqualified judge already signed stay in effect. Judges must recuse on their own motion when a Canon 3E ground exists, unless the parties waive under Canon 3F. (The Code of Judici","#st-sd-unique"],["South Dakota Constitution | Broader than federal: not established here","South Dakota · speech","The South Dakota Constitution has its own free-speech guarantee, and the new anti-SLAPP chapter protects rights “guaranteed by the United States Constitution or South Dakota Constitution.” The constitutional text and the case law were not collected, so no section number is given here. Nothing here establishes that South Dakota protects speech more broadly than the First Amendment. SDCL 15-40-1(3), 15-40-13; S.D. Cons","#st-sd-unique"],["Expedited civil actions for claims of $75,000 or less","South Dakota · unique rules","A plaintiff whose only relief sought is a money judgment, with all claims by or against any one party totaling $75,000 or less, may elect an expedited civil action by certification; the judgment is then capped at $75,000 (excluding interest and costs), and parties may also stipulate into the track. Each side gets 10 interrogatories, 10 production requests and 10 requests for admission (discrete subparts counted), one","#st-sd-unique"],["Court reporters tied to insurers are disqualified","South Dakota · unique rules","No deposition may be taken before a reporter who has a contractual relationship with a person or entity interested in the outcome, including anyone who may ultimately pay, such as an insurer. Reporters may not enter into prohibited reporting agreements. SDCL 15-6-28(c)","#st-sd-unique"],["Rule 15-6-1. Scope of chapter","South Dakota · civil rules","Chapter 15-6 governs procedure in the circuit courts in all civil suits, subject to the exceptions in § 15-6-81, and is to be construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-2. One form of action","South Dakota · civil rules","There is one form of action, the “civil action”; the distinctions between actions at law and suits in equity, and their old forms, are abolished.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-3. Commencement of action","South Dakota · civil rules","A civil action is commenced as provided in SDCL 15-2-30 and 15-2-31, not by filing a complaint as under FRCP 3. Under 15-2-30 the action is commenced as to each defendant when the summons is served on that defendant (or on a codefendant united in interest); under 15-2-31, delivering the summons to the sheriff of the proper county with intent that it be served counts as an attempted commencement only if service or fir","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-4. Summons and service of process","South Dakota · civil rules","The summons must require the defendant to answer and serve the answer within 30 days after service (excluding the day of service), and the complaint need not be served with it; if it is not, a defendant who appears and demands the complaint in writing within 30 days must be served with it within 20 days, and then has 30 days to answer. Service may be made by a sheriff, constable, or any non-party who is an elector of","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-5. Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","South Dakota · civil rules","Later pleadings, written motions, briefs, notices, demands, offers of judgment, and similar papers must be served on every party (parties in default need not be served, except with new or added claims, which must be served like a summons). Service on a represented party goes to the attorney; documents filed or served through the Odyssey e-filing system are presumed served on all attorneys of record; other papers may ","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-6. Time","South Dakota · civil rules","Exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday, legal holiday (§ 1-5-1), or (for filing) a day the clerk’s office is inaccessible; for periods under 11 days, intermediate weekends and holidays are excluded (the pre-2009 federal method, not FRCP 6’s count-every-day rule). Fax, email, and Odyssey service must be completed by 11:59 p.m. Central on a non-holiday weekday or is deemed made","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-7. Pleadings allowed; form of motions","South Dakota · civil rules","The pleadings are a complaint, answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a cross-claim, third-party complaint, and third-party answer; the court may order a reply to an answer. Motions must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state the grounds with particularity, and set out the relief sought; exhibits to motions are subject to Rule 11 and the protective provisions of § 15-6-26(c). Demurrers, pleas, a","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-8. General rules of pleading","South Dakota · civil rules","A claim needs a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment, and relief may be demanded in the alternative. Answers must admit or deny (lack of knowledge operates as a denial) and must plead listed affirmative defenses, including contributory negligence, assumption of risk, statute of limitations, statute of frauds, release, and res judicata; averments other than the amount of da","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-9. Pleading special matters","South Dakota · civil rules","Capacity need not be pleaded, but a challenge to capacity or legal existence requires a specific negative averment; fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity, while intent and other states of mind may be pleaded generally. Conditions precedent may be pleaded generally but denied specifically; official documents, judgments, statutes (by number), and ordinances may be pleaded in short form; time and place ar","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-10. Form of pleadings","South Dakota · civil rules","Each pleading needs a caption with the court, county, title, and § 15-6-7(a) designation (all parties in the complaint), with averments in numbered paragraphs and separate counts where that aids clarity; statements may be adopted by reference, and exhibits are part of the pleading. A South Dakota addition: Roman numerals may not be used to number paragraphs, and all filed papers (except exhibits) must be typed or han","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-11. Signing of pleadings; sanctions","South Dakota · civil rules","Every paper must be signed by an attorney of record (or an unrepresented party) with address and phone number; pleadings need not be verified unless a rule or statute says so, and unsigned papers are stricken unless promptly corrected. Signing certifies proper purpose, legal warrant, and evidentiary support as in FRCP 11(b); a sanctions motion must be separate and may not be filed until 21 days after service if the c","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-12. Defenses and objections","South Dakota · civil rules","The answer is due 30 days after service of the complaint (not 21 as under FRCP 12); an answer to a cross-claim and a reply to a counterclaim are due in 20 days; after denial or postponement of a Rule 12 motion, the responsive pleading is due in 10 days after notice, in 10 days after service of a more definite statement, and in 20 days after a reversal of a dismissal order is filed in the trial court. The defenses of ","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-13. Counterclaim and cross-claim","South Dakota · civil rules","Counterclaims arising from the same transaction or occurrence are compulsory, unless already pending in another action, the opposing claim was by attachment without personal jurisdiction, or the court would lack jurisdiction over the claim as an original action; other counterclaims are permissive and may exceed or differ from the opposing claim. The chapter does not enlarge counterclaims against the State of South Da","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-14. Third-party practice","South Dakota · civil rules","A defending party may serve a third-party summons and complaint on a non-party who is or may be liable to it, without leave if served within 10 days after serving its original answer (FRCP 14 now allows 14 days), and otherwise only with leave on motion. The third-party defendant may raise the third-party plaintiff’s defenses against the plaintiff and assert related claims against the plaintiff, and vice versa; any pa","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","South Dakota · civil rules","A party may amend once as a matter of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 20 days after serving a pleading that needs no response if the case is not on the trial calendar or set for trial; otherwise amendment requires leave (freely given) or written consent. The response to an amended pleading is due within the remaining original response time or 10 days after service, whichever is longer. Issues","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-16. Pretrial procedure: scheduling order","South Dakota · civil rules","Before trial the court, on its own or a party’s motion and after consulting counsel and unrepresented parties, must enter a scheduling order limiting time to join parties and amend, file and hear motions, and complete discovery, and setting conference, final pretrial conference, and trial dates. The schedule may be modified only by leave of the judge for good cause. The South Dakota rule is a short version: it does n","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-17. Parties; capacity","South Dakota · civil rules","Actions must be brought by the real party in interest, though representatives such as personal representatives, guardians, conservators, bailees, and trustees may sue in their own names, and dismissal for that defect must await a reasonable time for ratification, joinder, or substitution. South Dakota capacity rule: persons doing business under a common name may sue or be sued by that name with service on one or more","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-18. Joinder of claims and remedies","South Dakota · civil rules","A party may join as many claims, legal or equitable, as it has against an opposing party. Claims formerly allowed only after another claim concluded may be joined, so a creditor may sue for money and to set aside a fraudulent conveyance without first obtaining a money judgment.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-19. Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","South Dakota · civil rules","A person subject to service must be joined if complete relief cannot be given without the person, or if the person’s absence would impair the person’s interest or expose parties to inconsistent obligations; a joined party whose joinder makes venue improper and who objects must be dismissed. If joinder is not feasible, the court decides in equity and good conscience whether to proceed or dismiss, using four listed fac","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-20. Permissive joinder of parties","South Dakota · civil rules","Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, when claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact; judgment may be given for or against any of them according to their rights and liabilities. The court may order separate trials or other orders to prevent embarrassment, delay, expense, or prejudice.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","South Dakota · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal. Parties may be dropped or added by court order at any stage on just terms, and any claim against a party may be severed.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-22. Interpleader","South Dakota · civil rules","Claimants against a plaintiff who is exposed to double or multiple liability may be joined as defendants and required to interplead, even if their claims lack a common origin or the plaintiff denies liability; a defendant facing similar exposure may interplead by cross-claim or counterclaim. The rule supplements § 15-6-20; the text contains no counterpart to FRCP 22(b)’s reference to statutory interpleader.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-23. Class actions","South Dakota · civil rules","A class action requires numerosity, commonality, typicality, and adequacy, plus one of the three FRCP 23(b) types; South Dakota adds a fifth prerequisite barring class suits against the state to recover taxes imposed by chapters 10-39, 10-39A, 10-43, 10-44, 10-45, 10-46, 10-46A, 10-46B, or 10-52. The court decides certification as soon as practicable, may make it conditional, must give (b)(3) members the best practic","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-23.1. Derivative actions by unincorporated associations","South Dakota · civil rules","A member’s derivative action to enforce a right of an unincorporated association needs a verified complaint alleging membership at the time of the transaction (or by operation of law), that the suit is not collusive to create jurisdiction, and the demand efforts made (or why none was made). The suit fails if the plaintiff does not fairly represent similarly situated members, and dismissal or compromise needs court ap","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-23.2. Actions relating to unincorporated associations","South Dakota · civil rules","An action by or against association members as a class through named representatives may proceed only if the representatives will fairly and adequately protect the association and its members. The court may make orders like those under § 15-6-23(d), and dismissal or compromise follows § 15-6-23(e).","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-24. Intervention","South Dakota · civil rules","Intervention of right is allowed on timely application when a state statute confers an unconditional right or when the applicant’s interest may be impaired and is not adequately represented; permissive intervention is allowed for a common question of law or fact, including by government officers or agencies when a party relies on a statute or regulation they administer. The intervenor must serve a motion stating the ","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-25. Substitution of parties","South Dakota · civil rules","If a party dies and the claim survives, a motion to substitute must be made within 90 days after the death is suggested on the record, or the action is dismissed as to the decedent; the motion is served on parties under § 15-6-5 and on non-parties like a summons. The court may continue the action against an incompetent party’s guardian, conservator, or guardian ad litem, and after a transfer of interest the action co","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-25.1. Dismissal of parties: later pleadings","South Dakota · civil rules","Once a party is dismissed or dropped from a multiparty action by court order, that party’s name may not appear on later pleadings. This has no FRCP counterpart.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-26. General provisions governing discovery","South Dakota · civil rules","Discovery methods are depositions, interrogatories, production and entry requests, examinations, and requests for admission, with frequency not limited unless the court orders otherwise; the scope remains the older standard of any non-privileged matter “relevant to the subject matter” and “reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence,” not the federal proportionality standard, though the cour","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-27. Depositions before action or pending appeal","South Dakota · civil rules","A person expecting to be a party to an action who cannot yet bring it may file a verified petition in the circuit court of the proper county to perpetuate testimony, serving notice on expected adverse parties at least 20 days before the hearing in the manner of a summons (with publication and appointed counsel for those not served); the court orders depositions if perpetuation may prevent a failure or delay of justic","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","South Dakota · civil rules","In the United States, depositions are taken before an officer authorized to administer oaths or a court-appointed person; abroad, by treaty, letter of request, on notice before an authorized person, or before a commissioned person. South Dakota adds a detailed disqualification rule: no deposition may be taken before a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or of counsel, or anyone financially interested, and “emp","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-28.1. Interstate depositions and discovery: scope","South Dakota · civil rules","Sections 15-6-28.1 to 15-6-28.6 (South Dakota’s version of the Uniform Interstate Depositions and Discovery Act) govern depositions and discovery in South Dakota for civil suits pending in another state.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-28.2. Interstate discovery: definitions","South Dakota · civil rules","Defines “foreign jurisdiction” (another state), “foreign subpoena,” “person,” “state” (including D.C., Puerto Rico, the U.S. Virgin Islands, federally recognized Indian tribes, and U.S. territories), and “subpoena” (to testify, produce documents or electronically stored information, or permit inspection of premises).","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-28.3. Interstate discovery: issuing the subpoena","South Dakota · civil rules","A party submits the foreign subpoena to the clerk of court in the county where discovery is sought, and the clerk promptly issues a South Dakota subpoena that conforms to § 15-6-45, advises the recipient of the right to move to quash or modify, and lists all counsel of record and unrepresented parties with contact information. Submitting the request is not an appearance, but it gives South Dakota courts jurisdiction ","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-28.4. Interstate discovery: service","South Dakota · civil rules","A subpoena issued by the clerk under §§ 15-6-28.1 to 15-6-28.6 must be served as required by § 15-6-45(c).","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-28.5. Interstate discovery: other subpoena provisions","South Dakota · civil rules","All other provisions of § 15-6-45 (on depositions, production, inspection, witness fees, expenses, place of examination, and required attendance) apply to subpoenas issued under §§ 15-6-28.1 to 15-6-28.6.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-28.6. Interstate discovery: applications to court","South Dakota · civil rules","Motions for protective orders or to enforce, quash, or modify a clerk-issued interstate subpoena must follow South Dakota law and be filed in the court of the county where the discovery is to be conducted.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-29. Stipulations about depositions","South Dakota · civil rules","If the parties stipulate in writing, depositions may be taken before any person, at any time or place, on any notice, and in any manner, and used like other depositions. Unlike FRCP 29, the text does not address stipulations modifying other discovery procedures.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-30. Depositions by oral examination","South Dakota · civil rules","Any party may depose any person after commencement, but the plaintiff needs leave to notice a deposition within 30 days after service of the summons and complaint on any defendant unless a defendant has begun discovery or a special notice certifies that the witness is about to leave the state or country; deposing a prisoner, or anyone already deposed in the case, requires leave or (for a repeat deposition) consent of","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-31. Depositions by written questions","South Dakota · civil rules","Any party may take a deposition on written questions after commencement, with leave needed for prisoners and consent or leave needed for persons already deposed; the notice must identify the deponent and the officer, and organizations may be deposed under § 15-6-30(b)(6). Cross-questions are due within 14 days after the notice and questions are served, redirect questions within 7 days after cross-questions, and recro","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-32. Use of depositions; errors and irregularities","South Dakota · civil rules","Depositions may be used against parties who were present, represented, or had notice: for impeachment or any purpose the rules of evidence allow; an adverse party’s, officer’s, or designee’s deposition for any purpose; and any witness’s deposition if the witness is dead, out of the state (rather than FRCP 32’s more than 100 miles away), unable to attend because of age, illness, infirmity, imprisonment, or occupationa","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-33. Interrogatories to parties","South Dakota · civil rules","Interrogatories may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on any other party with or after service of the summons and complaint; the text sets no numerical limit (FRCP 33 caps them at 25), and § 15-6-26(a) says frequency is not limited unless the court orders otherwise. Answers under oath and objections are due within 30 days after service, but a defendant has at least 45 days after service of the summons","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-34. Production of documents and things; entry on land","South Dakota · civil rules","A party may request inspection, copying, testing, or sampling of documents and tangible things in another party’s possession, custody, or control, or entry on land, with items described with reasonable particularity and a reasonable time, place, and manner specified. Requests may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on other parties with or after the summons and complaint, and the written response is due","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-35. Physical and mental examinations and blood tests","South Dakota · civil rules","When a party’s mental or physical condition, or a party’s blood relationship (consanguinity) with another, is in controversy, the court may order an examination or blood test by a physician on motion for good cause and notice, specifying time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner. The examined party may request the physician’s detailed written report, after which the requesting side is entitled to like repo","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-36. Requests for admission","South Dakota · civil rules","A party may serve requests to admit facts, the application of law to fact, opinions of fact, or the genuineness of documents, on the plaintiff after commencement and on other parties with or after the summons and complaint. Each matter is admitted unless a written, signed answer or objection is served within 30 days after service (a defendant need not respond before 45 days after service of the summons and complaint)","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-37. Failure to make discovery; sanctions","South Dakota · civil rules","A motion to compel goes to the court where the action is pending (or, for a non-party, the circuit where discovery is taken) and must certify a good-faith attempt to confer; evasive or incomplete answers count as failures, and the court must award expenses and fees to the winner unless substantially justified, with apportionment on mixed results. Disobeying a discovery order may lead to facts deemed established, prec","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-38. Jury trial of right","South Dakota · civil rules","The jury right under S.D. Const. art. VI, § 6 or a South Dakota statute is preserved. A party demands a jury by serving a written demand (it may be endorsed on a pleading) no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue; a demand naming only some issues lets any other party demand a jury on other issues within 10 days after service of that demand. Failing to serve a demand waives the ju","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-39. Trial by jury or by the court","South Dakota · civil rules","Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties consent to a bench trial by written stipulation filed or oral stipulation in open court, a party waives by not appearing or by going to trial to the court without objecting, or the court finds no jury right exists. The court may still order a jury on a late motion, may use an advisory jury in nonjury actions, and with both parties’ consent may use a jury whose ve","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-40. Assignment of cases for trial","South Dakota · civil rules","The judges set the trial calendar by order or rule, giving precedence to cases entitled to it. Subdivision (b) was repealed (SL 1993, ch 386). Once a case is reached on the calendar, a party may proceed in the adverse party’s absence and take a dismissal, verdict, or judgment unless the court for good cause directs otherwise.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-41. Dismissal of actions","South Dakota · civil rules","A plaintiff may dismiss without a court order by notice before the adverse party serves an answer or a summary-judgment motion (whichever comes first), or by stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared; dismissal is without prejudice except that a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits. Otherwise dismissal at the plaintiff’s request requires a court order, and not ","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-42. Consolidation; separate trials","South Dakota · civil rules","The court may order joint hearings or trials, or consolidate actions that share a common question of law or fact, to avoid unnecessary cost or delay. It may order separate trials of any claim or issue for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or for expedition and economy, preserving the right to a jury under the state or federal constitution or a statute.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-43. Evidence","South Dakota · civil rules","Trial testimony is taken orally in open court unless this chapter or the South Dakota Rules of Evidence provide otherwise; subdivisions (b) and (c) are superseded. Oaths may follow §§ 19-19-603.1 and 19-19-603.2, motions on facts outside the record may be heard on affidavits, oral testimony, or depositions, and on a party’s motion a hearing under § 21-1-4.1 must be decided before trial. Interpreters are appointed and","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-44. Proof of official record","South Dakota · civil rules","A domestic official record may be proved by an official publication or a copy certified by its legal custodian or deputy; a foreign record by official publication or an attested copy with a final certification by a U.S. or foreign diplomatic or consular official, which the court may excuse for good cause when parties had a reasonable chance to investigate. A written statement of diligent search, authenticated the sam","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-44.1. Determination of foreign law","South Dakota · civil rules","A party raising an issue of a foreign country’s law must give notice in the pleadings or other reasonable written notice. The court may consider any relevant source, admissible or not, and its ruling is treated as a ruling on a question of law.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-45. Subpoena","South Dakota · civil rules","Subpoenas may be issued by clerks, judges, magistrates, notaries, referees, and authorized agencies, and by any active, in-good-standing South Dakota attorney of record, who must contemporaneously send a copy to the clerk (or tribunal’s filing officer) for filing; every subpoena must carry a required bold, capitalized warning above the signature. Before a document subpoena is served, notice and a copy must be served ","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-46. Exceptions unnecessary","South Dakota · civil rules","Formal exceptions are not needed; it is enough to make known the desired action or objection and the grounds when the ruling is made or sought. A party with no opportunity to object is not prejudiced by the lack of an objection.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-47. Jurors","South Dakota · civil rules","The court must permit the parties or their attorneys to conduct voir dire. The court may seat up to 6 alternates, who replace jurors in order before deliberations and are discharged when the jury retires; each side gets 1 extra peremptory challenge for 1–2 alternates, 2 for 3–4, and 3 for 5–6, usable only against alternates. The court may excuse a juror for good cause during trial or deliberation.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-48. Juries of fewer than twelve; majority verdict","South Dakota · civil rules","The parties may stipulate to a jury of fewer than 12 or to accept a verdict or finding of a stated majority of jurors.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-49. Special verdicts and interrogatories","South Dakota · civil rules","The court may require a special verdict with written findings on each fact issue; an omitted fact issue is waived for jury trial unless a party demands its submission before the jury retires, and the court may find it or is deemed to have found it consistent with the judgment. With a general verdict plus interrogatories, judgment enters under 15-6-58 when they agree; if answers are consistent with each other but conf","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-50. Judgment as a matter of law; renewed motion","South Dakota · civil rules","A motion for judgment as a matter of law (still titled ‘directed verdict and judgment notwithstanding the verdict’) may be made any time before submission and must state the judgment sought and the supporting law and facts. A renewed motion must be filed no later than 10 days after notice of entry of judgment, and may join or alternatively request a new trial under 15-6-59; the court may let the judgment stand, order","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-51. Jury instructions; objections","South Dakota · civil rules","Parties file written instruction requests at the close of evidence or an earlier time set by the court, with later requests allowed for unanticipated issues or by permission. The court must tell the parties its proposed instructions and rulings on requests before instructing and before final arguments, and give an on-the-record chance to object outside the jury’s hearing. Objections must distinctly state the matter a","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-52. Findings by the court","South Dakota · civil rules","In bench and advisory-jury trials the court must find facts specially and state conclusions of law separately (also when granting or refusing a TRO or preliminary injunction, except in chapters 25-3 and 25-4 actions); findings are set aside only if clearly erroneous. Findings may be oral on the record or in a memorandum, or the court may direct prevailing counsel to serve proposed findings, conclusions, and judgment ","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-53. Referees","South Dakota · civil rules","A court may appoint a referee who is not disqualified as a judge would be (unless parties consent after disclosure); the court fixes and allocates pay, and an unpaid referee may get a writ of execution; in chapter 25-7A support cases the referee may recommend fees and costs and is paid by the court. References are the exception: in jury cases only for complicated issues, and in nonjury cases only for accounts, diffic","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-54. Judgments; costs; attorney fees","South Dakota · civil rules","A judgment is the final determination of the parties’ rights (other written directions are orders), and in multi-claim or multi-party cases a partial final judgment requires an express determination of no just reason for delay and an express direction for entry. Default judgments are limited in kind and amount to the demand; other judgments grant the relief earned. Costs (not fees) go to the prevailing party on a ver","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-55. Default","South Dakota · civil rules","A party’s failure to plead or otherwise defend must be shown by affidavit, and every default judgment is entered by application to the court (there is no clerk’s default judgment); no default may enter against a minor or incompetent person without an appeared guardian, conservator, or guardian ad litem. A party who has appeared must be served with written notice of the application at least 3 days before the hearing, ","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-56. Summary judgment","South Dakota · civil rules","A claimant may move any time after 30 days from commencement (or after the adverse party moves), and a defending party may move at any time. Unless the court orders otherwise, the motion, brief, statement of undisputed material facts, and affidavits must be served at least 28 calendar days before the hearing, responses (including a numbered response to each fact with record cites) at least 14 calendar days before, an","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-57. Declaratory judgments","South Dakota · civil rules","Declaratory judgments under chapter 21-24 follow this chapter, with a jury available under 15-6-38 and 15-6-39. Another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the court may order a speedy hearing and advance the case.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-58. Entry of judgment and orders","South Dakota · civil rules","Judgment is promptly rendered, every judgment must be on a separate document, and a judgment or order becomes effective only when written, signed, attested by the clerk, and filed; the clerk dockets it at once (divorce and foreclosure judgments are docketed ‘see file’), and entry is not delayed for costs. Under subdivision (b) (added by Supreme Court Rule 25-07), a party directed to prepare an order or judgment witho","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-59. New trials; amendment of judgments","South Dakota · civil rules","Grounds are irregularity or abuse of discretion preventing a fair trial, jury misconduct (including chance verdicts, provable by a juror’s affidavit), accident or surprise, newly discovered evidence, damages from passion or prejudice, insufficient evidence or a decision against law, and preserved errors of law; grounds (1)–(4) require affidavits unless (1) appears of record, and insufficiency claims must state partic","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-60. Relief from judgment or order","South Dakota · civil rules","Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time; during an appeal, before the settled record goes to the Supreme Court clerk, and afterward only with the Supreme Court’s leave. Relief from a final judgment is available for mistake or excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, fraud or misconduct, a void judgment, satisfaction or changed circumstances, or any other justifying reason; the motion must be made within a","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-61. Harmless error","South Dakota · civil rules","No error in evidence rulings or other acts justifies a new trial or disturbing a judgment unless refusing relief would be inconsistent with substantial justice. Errors not affecting substantial rights are disregarded.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","South Dakota · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise for good cause, or on a default judgment, no execution or enforcement may proceed until 30 days after entry; injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed unless the court orders. The court may stay enforcement pending motions under 15-6-50, 15-6-59, or 15-6-60 on security conditions, and may stay a 15-6-54(b) partial judgment until later judgments. Injunctions pending appeal,","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-63. Disability of a judge","South Dakota · civil rules","If the trial judge dies, is sick or disabled, or leaves office after a verdict or after findings and conclusions are filed, another judge sitting in or assigned to the court may finish the post-trial duties. A successor who cannot perform them, because of not presiding at trial or otherwise, may grant a new trial.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-64. Seizure of person or property","South Dakota · civil rules","All state-law remedies for seizing a person or property to secure a future judgment are available at the start of and during an action, under the circumstances and in the manner state law provides.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-65. Injunctions and restraining orders","South Dakota · civil rules","No preliminary injunction issues without notice, and the court may consolidate the merits trial with the hearing (preserving jury rights). A TRO without notice requires specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint showing immediate irreparable harm and the attorney’s written certification of notice efforts; it must expire within the time the court sets, not over 10 days, unless extended for good cause for a like","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-66. Receivers","South Dakota · civil rules","An action in which a receiver has been appointed may be dismissed only by court order. Estate administration by receivers follows statute and prior state practice; otherwise this chapter governs.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-67. Deposit in court","South Dakota · civil rules","By leave of court and on notice, a party may deposit money or a deliverable thing in dispute with the court. A defendant sued on a contract or for specific property may, before answering and on affidavit of an adverse nonparty claim, seek substitution of the claimant and discharge by depositing the debt or property; a bailee facing adverse claims with no action pending may apply to the circuit judge to designate a de","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-68. Offer of judgment","South Dakota · civil rules","Any party (not only a defending party) may serve an offer of judgment more than 10 days before trial; the offeree has 10 days after service to accept in writing, after which either party files the offer and acceptance and the clerk enters judgment. An unaccepted offer is deemed withdrawn and is inadmissible except on costs; if the offeree’s final judgment is not more favorable, the offeree pays costs incurred after t","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-69. Execution","South Dakota · civil rules","Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution unless the court directs otherwise, under the applicable statutes and rules. The judgment creditor (or a successor of record) may take discovery from any person, including the debtor, in aid of the judgment.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-70. Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","South Dakota · civil rules","If a party fails to convey land, deliver documents, or perform a specific act within the time set, the court may appoint someone to do it at the party’s cost, the clerk issues a writ of attachment or sequestration on application, and contempt is available. For property in the state the court may simply divest and vest title by judgment, and a party entitled to possession gets a writ of execution or assistance from th","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-71. Process for and against nonparties","South Dakota · civil rules","A nonparty in whose favor an order is made may enforce it as if a party, and a nonparty against whom an order may lawfully be enforced is subject to the same process as a party.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-72. Expedited civil actions: general provisions","South Dakota · civil rules","Article IX expedited civil actions are money-only cases in which all claims by or against any one party total $75,000 or less, including damages, penalties, and attorney fees but excluding prejudgment interest, post-judgment interest, and costs; small claims and domestic relations cases are excluded. A plaintiff elects the track by a Supreme Court form certification (Form 27) signed by all plaintiffs and their attorn","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-72.1. Local intergovernmental disputes","South Dakota · civil rules","When two or more local government bodies are in a legal dispute, the court on motion or on its own may order them into the expedited civil action process of 15-6-73 to 15-6-76.1 regardless of amount, or into alternative dispute resolution other than binding arbitration.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-72.2. Local government body defined","South Dakota · civil rules","For 15-6-72.1, local government bodies include counties, municipalities, townships, and unorganized territory; school districts; public-service organizations they organize or authorize (law enforcement, fire, emergency medical); and listed special districts (for example ambulance, conservation, consumer power, irrigation, sanitary, water, and watershed districts).","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-73. Discovery in expedited civil actions","South Dakota · civil rules","Unless the parties agree or the court grants leave for good cause on a written motion stating the proposed discovery, all discovery must be finished no later than 60 days before trial. Each side may serve no more than 10 interrogatories, 10 requests for production, and 10 requests for admission (each counting discrete subparts; document-genuineness requests excepted) on any other side, may take one deposition of each","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-74. Motions in expedited civil actions","South Dakota · civil rules","Any 15-6-12(b) motion may be filed, but unless the court stays the case it does not postpone pleading or disclosure duties. Each party may file only one summary-judgment motion (which may raise several grounds), no later than 90 days before trial.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-75. Procedure for expedited trials","South Dakota · civil rules","An expedited action is tried to the court unless a party serves a jury demand under 15-6-38(b); trial is set for a firm date and, absent good cause, must be held within 1 year of filing, and the parties must jointly file one set of proposed jury instructions and verdict forms (noting objections and alternatives) in word-processing format. A jury is 12 persons chosen from a panel of 18 with 3 strikes per side unless o","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-76. Settlement conference and ADR in expedited actions","South Dakota · civil rules","Unless the parties agree or a contract or statute requires it, the court may not by order or local rule require a settlement conference or other ADR in an expedited civil action.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-76.1. Preclusive effect of expedited judgments","South Dakota · civil rules","A judgment or order in an expedited civil action supports claim or issue preclusion only for a party, or one in privity with a party, to that action.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-77. Courts of record and clerks","South Dakota · civil rules","Trial courts of record are always open for filing papers, issuing and returning process, and interlocutory motions and orders. Merits trials must be in open court; other proceedings may be held in chambers anywhere, but no non-ex parte hearing may be held outside the circuit without all affected parties’ consent. The clerk may grant routine process applications as of course, subject to the court’s review. Sections 15","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-80. Stenographic transcript as evidence","South Dakota · civil rules","When stenographically reported testimony from an earlier trial or hearing is admissible, it may be proved by a transcript certified by the reporter.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-81. Applicability","South Dakota · civil rules","The chapter does not govern the statutory and other proceedings listed in Appendix A (for example plat vacation, election contests, forcible entry and detainer, habeas corpus, mandamus, prohibition, certiorari, contempt, eminent domain, partition, mortgage foreclosure by action, divorce, mechanics liens, and certain corporate dissolutions) to the extent their procedure is inconsistent with this chapter. Subdivision (","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-82. Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","South Dakota · civil rules","The chapter does not extend or limit circuit court jurisdiction or venue.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-83. Circuit court rules","South Dakota · civil rules","A majority of a circuit’s judges may adopt practice rules consistent with this chapter after notice filed with each county clerk in the circuit and a public hearing at least 30 days after notice (notice may be posted on the UJS or State Bar website, with email to State Bar members). Rules take effect only when filed with and approved by the Supreme Court and are published as an appendix to Title 15; Appendix B includ","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-84. Forms","South Dakota · civil rules","The Appendix of Forms (Appendix C) is sufficient under the chapter and illustrates the intended brevity; the sample summons gives 30 days after service to answer.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-85. Title","South Dakota · civil rules","The chapter is known as the Rules of Civil Procedure and cited as RCP.","#st-sd-rules"],["Rule 15-6-86. Effective date","South Dakota · civil rules","The chapter governs actions brought after July 1, 1966, and further proceedings in actions then pending unless applying it would be infeasible or unjust, in which case the prior procedure applies.","#st-sd-rules"],["Appeals in South Dakota: one appellate court, and the clock starts with notice of entry","South Dakota · appeals","South Dakota has no intermediate appellate court. Civil appeals from the circuit courts go directly to the South Dakota Supreme Court, which is both the first and the last stop for state-law review.\nThe 30-day appeal clock does not start when the judgment is signed. It starts only after the judgment or order is signed, attested, and filed and written notice of entry has been given to the adverse party. The winning si","#st-sd-appeals"],["Appeals of right","South Dakota · appeals · What can be appealed","An appeal lies from: (1) a judgment; (2) an order affecting a substantial right that in effect determines the action and prevents a judgment; (3) an order granting a new trial; (4) a final order affecting a substantial right in special proceedings or on a summary application after judgment; (5) an order granting, refusing, continuing, dissolving, or modifying arrest and bail, claim and delivery, injunction, attachmen","#st-sd-appeals"],["Partial judgments need Rule 54(b) language","South Dakota · appeals · What can be appealed","In a case with multiple claims or parties, an order deciding fewer than all of them is not final unless the circuit court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay and expressly directs entry of judgment. Without both, the order can be revised and is not appealable as a final judgment. SDCL 15-6-54(b)","#st-sd-appeals"],["Discretionary appeal of intermediate orders","South Dakota · appeals · What can be appealed","Any other intermediate order made before trial may be reviewed only by permission of the Supreme Court, granted when the ends of justice are served by deciding the question now. File a petition for permission to appeal with the Supreme Court clerk, with proof of service on all parties and the filing fee, within 10 days after notice of entry of the order. The petition (and any response) is limited to 10 pages, exclusi","#st-sd-appeals"],["Petition procedure and stays","South Dakota · appeals · What can be appealed","Any party may respond within 7 days after service of the petition; the petition is decided without argument unless ordered. Filing the petition does not stay the circuit court case; the petitioner must apply for a stay, which may require security. Willful noncompliance or an inaccurate statement in the petition is ground for denial, and a vexatious petition can draw terms. If permission is granted, the order sets the","#st-sd-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP denials are appealable of right","South Dakota · appeals · What can be appealed","A party whose special motion for expedited relief under the 2026 anti-SLAPP chapter is denied in whole or in part may appeal as a matter of right, no later than 30 days after entry of the order. An appeal from an order ruling on the motion stays all proceedings between all parties until the appeal ends, with limited exceptions. SDCL 15-40-11, 15-40-4","#st-sd-appeals"],["30 days after written notice of entry","South Dakota · appeals · Notice of appeal: timing and trigger","The appeal must be taken within 30 days after the judgment or order is signed, attested, and filed and written notice of entry has been given to the adverse party. A notice of appeal filed before the signed judgment is attested and filed is treated as filed on the date of attestation and filing. SDCL 15-26A-6","#st-sd-appeals"],["File the notice of entry promptly","South Dakota · appeals · Notice of appeal: timing and trigger","A notice of entry of judgment, with proof of service, must be filed forthwith; if it is not filed within 10 days after service, the time of service is deemed to be the date it is filed. A late-filed notice of entry can therefore move the appeal clock. SDCL 15-6-5(d)","#st-sd-appeals"],["No extensions of the notice of appeal","South Dakota · appeals · Notice of appeal: timing and trigger","The Supreme Court may enlarge most times in chapter 15-26A for good cause, but it may not enlarge the time for filing or serving a notice of appeal. SDCL 15-26A-92","#st-sd-appeals"],["What the notice must contain and where it is filed","South Dakota · appeals · Notice of appeal: timing and trigger","The notice names the appealing parties, designates the judgment or order (or part) appealed from, and is signed by the appellant or counsel. Serve the notice and a docketing statement (on the Supreme Court’s form, with the findings, conclusions and any memorandum decision attached) on counsel for every other party, then file both with the clerk of the trial court before the appeal time expires, with proof of service ","#st-sd-appeals"],["Only a timely notice is jurisdictional","South Dakota · appeals · Notice of appeal: timing and trigger","Failing to take any step other than timely service and filing of the notice of appeal does not void the appeal, but the Supreme Court may act as it deems appropriate, including dismissal. SDCL 15-26A-4","#st-sd-appeals"],["Amended notices fix clerical errors only","South Dakota · appeals · Notice of appeal: timing and trigger","An amended notice may correct clerical errors or omissions. It cannot be used to appeal a later order or judgment unless that later ruling amends the one originally appealed. It does not extend any appellate deadline; times still run from the original notice. SDCL 15-26A-4.1","#st-sd-appeals"],["Joint and combined appeals","South Dakota · appeals · Notice of appeal: timing and trigger","Parties may file a joint notice or join after filing separate timely notices. One notice and one undertaking may cover appeals from several judgments and orders in the same action, subject to each appeal’s time limit. The Chief Justice may consolidate appeals on motion. SDCL 15-26A-4(6), 15-26A-11","#st-sd-appeals"],["Rule 50(b) and Rule 59 motions terminate the appeal time","South Dakota · appeals · Post-trial motions that stop the clock","A timely motion by any party under SDCL 15-6-59 (new trial) or 15-6-50(b) (renewed judgment as a matter of law) terminates the running of the appeal time for all parties. The full 30 days starts again after the order deciding the motion is signed, attested, and filed and written notice of entry is given. SDCL 15-26A-6","#st-sd-appeals"],["10-day motion deadlines","South Dakota · appeals · Post-trial motions that stop the clock","A renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law and a motion for new trial must each be served and filed no later than 10 days after notice of entry of judgment. SDCL 15-6-50(b), 15-6-59(b)","#st-sd-appeals"],["Deemed denial after 20 days","South Dakota · appeals · Post-trial motions that stop the clock","The court must file its order on a new-trial motion within 20 days after service and filing of the motion, unless it files an order within those 20 days extending the time. Otherwise the motion is deemed denied, and the appeal time runs from the date the court’s time to act expired, with no notice of entry needed. SDCL 15-6-59(b), 15-26A-6","#st-sd-appeals"],["Other motions do not toll","South Dakota · appeals · Post-trial motions that stop the clock","Chapter 15-26A names only motions under 15-6-59 and 15-6-50(b) as terminating the appeal time. A motion under 15-6-60(b) or any other post-judgment motion is not listed; do not rely on it to extend the appeal deadline. SDCL 15-26A-6","#st-sd-appeals"],["20 days after service of the notice of appeal","South Dakota · appeals · Cross-appeals: the notice of review","An appellee who wants review of a judgment or order that adversely affects it files a notice of review, with section B of the docketing statement and proof of service on all parties, with the clerk of the Supreme Court (not the trial court) within 20 days after service of the notice of appeal. The clerk will not accept it without the docketing statement and proof of service. SDCL 15-26A-22","#st-sd-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief carries the cross-issues","South Dakota · appeals · Cross-appeals: the notice of review","If a notice of review is filed, the appellee’s brief must include the judgment or order under review and any related findings, conclusions and memorandum decision not already in the appellant’s appendix. SDCL 15-26A-61","#st-sd-appeals"],["$500 cost bond","South Dakota · appeals · Bonds and stays","Unless exempt, or covered by a supersedeas bond that secures costs, the appellant files a $500 bond for costs (or equivalent security) with the circuit court clerk within the 30-day appeal time. It is waived by filing, within the same time, the written consent of each appellee or an affidavit of indigency. A cash deposit or a written waiver by the protected party may replace an undertaking. SDCL 15-26A-23, 15-26A-24,","#st-sd-appeals"],["Automatic 30-day stay, then supersedeas","South Dakota · appeals · Bonds and stays","Except on default judgments or as ordered, no execution issues for 30 days after entry of judgment; injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed. After that, an appeal stays enforcement only with a supersedeas bond in the amount and form the circuit court approves. Give at least 24 hours’ notice of an application to fix or approve a bond. SDCL 15-6-62(a), 15-26A-25, 15-26A-34","#st-sd-appeals"],["Money judgments: bond capped at $25 million","South Dakota · appeals · Bonds and stays","For a money judgment, the bond secures payment of the affirmed amount plus interest from the judgment date. The total bond for all appellants may not exceed $25 million, but the court may require up to the full judgment if the appellee proves by a preponderance that an appellant is dissipating assets outside the ordinary course of business to avoid payment. SDCL 15-26A-26","#st-sd-appeals"],["Personal sureties must justify","South Dakota · appeals · Bonds and stays","Unless the surety is corporate, sureties must give affidavits of worth totaling double the undertaking. The appellee may except within 10 days after the bond is filed; the appellant then has 10 days, on at least 4 days’ notice, to produce the sureties for examination. If they do not justify in time, the appeal is treated as if no undertaking had been given. SDCL 15-26A-35","#st-sd-appeals"],["Government appellants","South Dakota · appeals · Bonds and stays","When the state, a county, a municipality, a school district or another listed public body or officer appeals in an official capacity, serving and filing the notice of appeal perfects the appeal and stays the judgment without a bond, though the Supreme Court may require security on motion. SDCL 15-26A-38","#st-sd-appeals"],["Relief from the Supreme Court","South Dakota · appeals · Bonds and stays","A motion for stay or bond relief may go to the Supreme Court only after showing that asking the circuit court is impracticable or that it denied or failed to give the relief, with its reasons. SDCL 15-26A-39","#st-sd-appeals"],["Order the transcript within 10 days or waive it","South Dakota · appeals · The record and transcript","Within 10 days after filing the notice of appeal, the appellant must order the transcript in writing on the Supreme Court’s form, serve the order on all parties, and file a copy with the circuit court clerk. Failing to order within the time waives the right to a transcript. SDCL 15-26A-48, 15-26A-49","#st-sd-appeals"],["Partial transcripts and designations","South Dakota · appeals · The record and transcript","If ordering less than the whole transcript, the appellant files a statement of issues within the same 10 days. The appellee may designate more within 10 days after service; if the appellant does not order those parts within 10 days, the appellee may order them or move the circuit court within the following 10 days. SDCL 15-26A-50","#st-sd-appeals"],["Reporter’s 45 days","South Dakota · appeals · The record and transcript","Make payment arrangements when ordering; if the reporter requires prepayment, pay within 10 days. The transcript is due within 45 days after the reporter receives the order (or the prepayment); extensions come from the Supreme Court clerk. SDCL 15-26A-51","#st-sd-appeals"],["Clerk’s record","South Dakota · appeals · The record and transcript","Within 5 days after the notice of appeal is filed, the trial clerk assembles and paginates the record, prepares an alphabetical index, and serves it on counsel and the Supreme Court clerk. Cite the record by these page numbers. SDCL 15-26A-53, 15-26A-64","#st-sd-appeals"],["No transcript: statement of proceedings or agreed statement","South Dakota · appeals · The record and transcript","If no report was made or the transcript is unavailable, the appellant may prepare a statement of the proceedings within 15 days after service of the notice of appeal; the appellee has 15 days to object, and the trial court settles it. Alternatively, the parties may sign an agreed statement of the case within 15 days of service of the notice of appeal. SDCL 15-26A-54, 15-26A-55","#st-sd-appeals"],["45 / 45 / 30 days","South Dakota · appeals · Briefing schedule","The appellant’s brief is due 45 days after service of the notice of appeal if the transcript was obtained before the appeal or none was ordered; otherwise 45 days after service of the transcript or filing of the statement under 15-26A-54 or -55. The appellee’s brief is due 45 days after service of the (last) appellant’s brief, and the reply 30 days after service of the (last) appellee’s brief. In adoption and abuse-a","#st-sd-appeals"],["One 15-day stipulated extension","South Dakota · appeals · Briefing schedule","The parties may stipulate to one extension of up to 15 days for each initial brief, presented to the Supreme Court clerk before the brief is due. Anything more requires an application to the Chief Justice on notice and good cause. SDCL 15-26A-76","#st-sd-appeals"],["Missed brief deadlines","South Dakota · appeals · Briefing schedule","If the appellant’s brief is late, the appellee may move to dismiss; a late appellee is not heard at oral argument without permission. The clerk may not accept an untimely brief. Defaults can be excused by stipulation with an affidavit of reasonable excuse, or by the court on application (opposition due in 7 days), only if no dismissal order has been entered. SDCL 15-26A-77, 15-26A-78, 15-26A-80","#st-sd-appeals"],["Reinstatement after dismissal","South Dakota · appeals · Briefing schedule","A petition to reinstate a dismissed appeal may be filed within 20 days after entry of the dismissal order; answers are due within 10 days after service. SDCL 15-26A-91","#st-sd-appeals"],["Required sections","South Dakota · appeals · Brief contents, length and format","Table of contents; table of authorities; jurisdictional statement showing the judgment date and form and the notice-of-appeal date; statement of issues, each followed by how the trial court ruled and no more than 4 most relevant cases; statement of the case and facts with record cites; argument; short conclusion; and an appendix with the judgment or order, findings and conclusions, any summary-judgment fact statement","#st-sd-appeals"],["Length: 32 pages or 10,000 words","South Dakota · appeals · Brief contents, length and format","In proportional type (serif, 12-point or larger, including footnotes), principal briefs are limited to 32 pages, replies and amicus briefs to 16, and supplemental briefs to 5. A brief may exceed the page limit if it stays within the greater of 10,000 words or 50,000 characters (half that for reply and amicus briefs), with a certificate of compliance stating the count. Monospaced briefs: 40 / 20 / 10 pages. Ask leave ","#st-sd-appeals"],["Format details","South Dakota · appeals · Brief contents, length and format","Double-spaced, left margin 1½ inches and others at least 1 inch, not right-justified, pages numbered at bottom center; boldface only for captions, section names and argument headings. The cover must give the trial judge’s name and the date the notice of appeal was filed. The clerk may refuse a nonconforming brief, and the party then has 10 days to file a compliant one. SDCL 15-26A-66(b)(1), 15-26A-69, 15-26A-70","#st-sd-appeals"],["Public-domain citations","South Dakota · appeals · Brief contents, length and format","Cite South Dakota Supreme Court opinions released on or after January 1, 1996 by year, “S.D.,” and sequential number, with pinpoints to the clerk-assigned paragraph numbers, adding the N.W.2d cite when available. SDCL 15-26A-69.1","#st-sd-appeals"],["Amicus and supplemental briefs","South Dakota · appeals · Brief contents, length and format","Amicus briefs need the Court’s request or leave on motion and are due with the brief of the supported party. Supplemental briefs on late authorities, restricted to the new matter, may be filed until the case is called for hearing. Length: 15-26A-73 says 10 pages, but 15-26A-66 allows 10 pages monospaced and only 5 pages proportional, so use the lower figure. SDCL 15-26A-73, 15-26A-74","#st-sd-appeals"],["Motions","South Dakota · appeals · Motions, argument and decision","Serve motions on all parties and file with proof of service; responses are due within 10 days after service. SDCL 15-26A-87.2","#st-sd-appeals"],["Appellate attorney fees","South Dakota · appeals · Motions, argument and decision","Where fees are allowable, a motion with a verified, itemized statement of services must be served and filed before the case is submitted on the merits. SDCL 15-26A-87.3","#st-sd-appeals"],["Oral argument","South Dakota · appeals · Motions, argument and decision","Argument is not guaranteed; the Court may decide on the briefs. When argued: appellant 20 minutes, appellee 20, rebuttal 10, and no more than two lawyers per side. SDCL 15-26A-82, 15-26A-83, 15-26A-85","#st-sd-appeals"],["Summary affirmance or reversal","South Dakota · appeals · Motions, argument and decision","After briefing, the Court may summarily affirm or reverse by order or memorandum opinion on listed grounds. These dispositions may not be cited as authority except for law of the case, res judicata, collateral estoppel and similar uses. SDCL 15-26A-87.1","#st-sd-appeals"],["Rehearing: 20 days","South Dakota · appeals · Motions, argument and decision","A petition for rehearing may be served and filed within 20 days after the formal opinion or summary-disposition order is filed; answers within 10 days after service. State the points overlooked or misapprehended, with record and authority references. Rehearing is the last step in state court; there is no further state review. SDCL 15-30-4, 15-26A-87.1","#st-sd-appeals"],["Costs and delay damages","South Dakota · appeals · Motions, argument and decision","The prevailing party recovers listed costs; brief-reproduction and transcript costs only if proof is filed before the decision. On affirmance the Court may add damages for delay of up to 10% of the judgment. Objections to the clerk’s taxation are due within 10 days after mailing of the notice; costs are payable 20 days after notice if no rehearing petition is filed. SDCL 15-30-6, 15-30-8.1, 15-30-9","#st-sd-appeals"],["Odyssey filing is mandatory for lawyers","South Dakota · appeals · E-filing in the Supreme Court","Attorneys must e-file all documents with the Supreme Court, including petitions, notices of review, motions, briefs and appendices, through Odyssey unless the Court allows otherwise in advance. Registration is consent to electronic service, and filed documents must be served through Odyssey. SDCL 15-26C-1(1)-(2), 15-26C-4","#st-sd-appeals"],["Formats and the paper original","South Dakota · appeals · E-filing in the Supreme Court","Documents go in as PDF. A brief is one document, filed in PDF and an approved word-processing format, with the appendix in the same PDF behind a table of contents; electronically filed transcripts are not repeated in the appendix except short excerpts. Lawyers must still submit an original paper brief to the clerk. SDCL 15-26C-2, 15-26C-3(3), 15-26A-79","#st-sd-appeals"],["11:59 p.m. Central and clerk review","South Dakota · appeals · E-filing in the Supreme Court","A compliant document submitted by 11:59 p.m. Central time is filed that day. The clerk then accepts or rejects it by Odyssey e-mail; rejection grounds include wrong court, fees not paid or waived, incomplete documents, and rule noncompliance. SDCL 15-26C-3(1)-(2)","#st-sd-appeals"],["Notice of appeal goes in through the circuit court","South Dakota · appeals · E-filing in the Supreme Court","The notice of appeal is filed in the circuit court where the judgment was entered (through the circuit-court Odyssey system), and the trial clerk transmits it to the Supreme Court. SDCL 15-26A-4(4)-(5), 16-21A-2","#st-sd-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (with docketing statement, proof of service, fee) in the circuit court","South Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from judgment or order signed, attested, filed, and written notice of entry given to the adverse party.\nCannot be extended (15-26A-92). Restarts after a timely 15-6-50(b) or 15-6-59 motion is decided and notice of entry given, or after the court’s time to rule expires. SDCL 15-26A-6, 15-26A-4(4)","#st-sd-appeals"],["Renewed JMOL or new-trial motion","South Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from notice of entry of judgment.\nNew-trial motion is deemed denied if not decided (or time extended by order) within 20 days of service and filing. SDCL 15-6-50(b), 15-6-59(b)","#st-sd-appeals"],["Cost bond ($500) or consent/indigency affidavit","South Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from same trigger as the notice of appeal.\nFiled with the circuit court clerk within the appeal time. SDCL 15-26A-23, 15-26A-24","#st-sd-appeals"],["Petition for permission to appeal an intermediate order","South Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from notice of entry of the order.\nFiled with the Supreme Court clerk; 10-page limit. Responses due 7 days after service (15-26A-16). SDCL 15-26A-13","#st-sd-appeals"],["Notice of review (cross-appeal)","South Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from service of the notice of appeal.\nFiled with the Supreme Court clerk with docketing statement section B and proof of service. SDCL 15-26A-22","#st-sd-appeals"],["Order transcript","South Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nLate ordering waives the transcript. SDCL 15-26A-48, 15-26A-49","#st-sd-appeals"],["Statement of proceedings or agreed statement","South Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from service of the notice of appeal.\nOnly when no transcript is available or the parties agree. SDCL 15-26A-54, 15-26A-55","#st-sd-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief","South Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","45 days from service of the notice of appeal, or service of the transcript if ordered but not received before appeal.\n25 days in adoption and abuse-and-neglect appeals. One stipulated 15-day extension (15-26A-76). SDCL 15-26A-75(1)","#st-sd-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief","South Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","45 days from service of the (last) appellant’s brief.\n25 days in adoption and abuse-and-neglect appeals. SDCL 15-26A-75(2)","#st-sd-appeals"],["Reply brief","South Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the (last) appellee’s brief.\n15 days in adoption and abuse-and-neglect appeals; confined to new matter. SDCL 15-26A-75(3)","#st-sd-appeals"],["Request to exceed brief length","South Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from before the brief’s due date.\nCounted backward from the filing date. SDCL 15-26A-66(c)","#st-sd-appeals"],["Response to a motion","South Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from service of the motion.\nUnless the court sets another time. SDCL 15-26A-87.2","#st-sd-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing","South Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from filing of the opinion or summary-disposition order.\nAnswer due 10 days after service. SDCL 15-30-4","#st-sd-appeals"],["Petition to reinstate a dismissed appeal","South Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from entry of the dismissal order.\nAnswer due 10 days after service. SDCL 15-26A-91","#st-sd-appeals"],["Objections to taxation of appellate costs","South Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from mailing of the clerk’s notice of taxation.\nReplies within 5 days after service of objections. SDCL 15-30-9","#st-sd-appeals"],["Appeal from denial of anti-SLAPP motion","South Dakota · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order.\nRuns from entry, not notice of entry. Chapter 15-40 enacted by SL 2026, ch 91. SDCL 15-40-11","#st-sd-appeals"],["Utah Supreme Court","Utah · courts","Court of last resort. Exclusive appellate jurisdiction over capital cases, lawyer discipline, election and redistricting cases, removal of public officers, and (since 2026) judgments of three-judge district court panels; original appellate jurisdiction over most other civil appeals from district court, which it may “pour over” to the Court of Appeals. Reviews the Court of Appeals by discretionary certiorari, answers ","#st-ut"],["Utah Court of Appeals","Utah · courts","Hears appeals from juvenile courts, domestic relations appeals, most agency reviews, criminal appeals other than first-degree and capital felonies, and cases transferred by the Supreme Court. May certify a case to the Supreme Court by a vote of four judges.","#st-ut"],["District Courts (with court commissioners)","Utah · courts","General-jurisdiction trial courts organized in judicial districts. Court commissioners hear many domestic-relations motions under Civil Rule 101, and their recommendations are orders unless a party objects within 14 days. Since 2026 a party in a constitutional challenge against the State seeking declaratory or injunctive relief may require a randomly drawn three-judge district court panel (Utah Code § 78A-5-102.7).","#st-ut"],["Business and Chancery Court","Utah · courts","Court with jurisdiction concurrent with the district court over business, contract, fiduciary, trade-secret, securities, and similar actions seeking at least $300,000 or solely equitable relief (its procedure rules took effect September 1, 2024). It cannot hear actions in which a governmental entity is a party, and generally may not take supplemental consumer-contract, personal-injury, or domestic claims. The judge i","#st-ut"],["Juvenile Courts and Justice Courts","Utah · courts","Juvenile courts hear child welfare and delinquency matters (appeals go to the Court of Appeals). Justice courts are local courts of limited jurisdiction; their structure was not part of the collected texts.","#st-ut"],["Statute: Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (2023)","Utah · anti-SLAPP","Utah adopted the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (UPEPA), effective May 3, 2023. It applies to civil actions filed, and causes of action asserted, on or after May 3, 2023; earlier claims are saved. The former narrow anti-SLAPP part of the Judicial Code (78B-6-1401 et seq.) shows no current version on the Legislature’s site, consistent with repeal, but that was not separately confirmed. Utah Code §§ 78B-25-10","#st-ut-slapp"],["Scope: three kinds of protected activity","Utah · anti-SLAPP","Covers any cause of action (including counterclaims, crossclaims, and third-party claims) based on: (a) communication in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding; (b) communication on an issue under consideration in such a proceeding; or (c) exercise of freedom of speech or press, assembly, petition, or association under the U.S. or Utah Constitution on a matter of public c","#st-ut-slapp"],["Procedure: 60 days to file, automatic stay, 60-day hearing and ruling","Utah · anti-SLAPP","File a special motion for expedited relief within 60 days after service of the pleading asserting the claim, or later for good cause. Filing automatically stays all other proceedings between the movant and the responding party, including discovery and pending motions, until the ruling and the time to appeal pass; the court may stay related proceedings with other parties. Limited discovery is allowed only if specific ","#st-ut-slapp"],["Standard: dismissal with prejudice","Utah · anti-SLAPP","The court considers the pleadings, motion papers, and evidence admissible on summary judgment. It must dismiss with prejudice if the movant shows the Act applies, the responding party fails to show an exclusion, and either the responding party fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element or the movant shows failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of material fact. A voluntary dismissal without","#st-ut-slapp"],["Fees: mandatory for a prevailing movant","Utah · anti-SLAPP","The court must award court costs, reasonable attorney fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion to a prevailing movant, and to a prevailing responding party only if the motion was frivolous or filed solely to delay. Utah Code § 78B-25-110","#st-ut-slapp"],["Appeal: as of right, 21 days, with a full stay","Utah · anti-SLAPP","A moving party may appeal as of right from an order denying the motion in whole or in part. The notice of appeal is due 21 days after entry of the order (not the usual 30). While the appeal is pending, all proceedings between all parties in the action are stayed until it concludes, subject to the statutory exceptions. Utah Code §§ 78B-25-104(3), -109; Utah R. App. P. 4(a)(2)","#st-ut-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: Bar application first, then a motion in each case","Utah · out of state","A lawyer not admitted in Utah must be admitted pro hac vice before appearing in any Utah court or state or local administrative body, and for court-annexed, court-ordered, or statutorily mandated arbitration or ADR, including discovery and settlement work incident to those proceedings. Private, voluntary arbitration is not covered.\nEligibility: not a Utah Bar member, not a Utah resident, not regularly employed in Uta","#st-ut-oos"],["What the application needs and what it costs","Utah · out of state","Submit a sworn application to the Utah State Bar for each matter, listing the tribunal and case, the client, other Utah pro hac vice matters in the past 5 years, any current suspension, discipline in the past 5 years, or pending proceedings, consent to Utah Office of Professional Conduct jurisdiction, and local counsel’s details. Attach a certificate of good standing dated no more than 60 days before the application.","#st-ut-oos"],["Local counsel’s role and the bar on early appearances","Utah · out of state","Local counsel’s name, bar number, and address must appear on every filing; local counsel must personally appear at conferences and hearings when the tribunal deems it appropriate, shares joint responsibility to the client, the court, and opponents, and stays of record until another Utah lawyer is substituted.\nThe applicant may not appear or have its name on any pleading until the order granting admission is entered. ","#st-ut-oos"],["Annual renewal and appeals","Utah · out of state","On or before each anniversary of the Bar application, the lawyer must certify the case is still pending (or concluded) and, within 28 days of the anniversary, pay an annual fee equal to active Bar dues. Failure suspends the lawyer from appearing after 28 days; reinstatement requires the fees plus a $50 late penalty. On appeal, the admitted lawyer files only a notice of appearance in the appellate court. Pro hac vice ","#st-ut-oos"],["Temporary practice and remote work under Rule 5.5","Utah · out of state","A lawyer admitted elsewhere may provide temporary services in Utah in association with an actively participating Utah lawyer, or related to pending or expected proceedings or ADR, or arising out of the lawyer’s home practice. Utah also expressly lets a lawyer physically located in Utah serve clients of the jurisdiction where the lawyer is admitted, remotely, as long as the lawyer opens no public-facing Utah office an","#st-ut-oos"],["Moving to Utah: admission by motion, UBE transfer, practice pending admission","Utah · out of state","Admission by motion requires admission by exam elsewhere, an approved law-school degree, the MPRE, a reciprocal jurisdiction, and full-time practice for 36 of the past 60 months in that jurisdiction (proved by clear and convincing evidence). A transferred UBE score must be at least 270 (pre-July 2023 Legacy UBE) or 260 (July 2023 or later) and is transferable for 36 months; processing takes at least 4 months.\nA lawye","#st-ut-oos"],["Pro bono authorization for out-of-state lawyers","Utah · out of state","A lawyer in good standing elsewhere may provide uncompensated pro bono services in Utah through an approved sponsoring organization after filing a statement with the Bar, renewed annually; the lawyer must tell the court at the first appearance that the lawyer appears under this rule. It does not authorize other practice without pro hac vice admission. Utah Sup. Ct. R. Prof. Prac. 14-803","#st-ut-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances","Utah · out of state","An attorney may appear for limited purposes (a filing, a motion, a discovery event, a hearing) by filing a Notice of Limited Appearance signed by attorney and client; documents within that scope are served on both. Opposing lawyers may contact a limited-scope client directly about matters outside the scope unless told otherwise, because the limited-scope lawyer must disclose the limits. The limited-scope lawyer withd","#st-ut-oos"],["E-filing access","Utah · out of state","In district court civil and probate cases, pleadings must be e-filed by an attorney (or licensed paralegal practitioner) of record using the electronic filer’s interface and a unique identifier from the filer’s service provider; hardship exemptions require Judicial Council approval. E-filing serves anyone with an e-filing account. Service on an out-of-state attorney without an e-filing account may be made by email to","#st-ut-oos"],["Licensed paralegal practitioners on the other side","Utah · out of state","Utah licenses paralegal practitioners (LPPs) to practice in limited areas defined in Rule 14-802; the Civil Rules treat them as attorneys within that scope, and the Rules of Professional Conduct apply to them. Expect LPPs in family, eviction, and debt-collection matters. Utah R. Civ. P. 86; Utah R. Prof. Conduct Preliminary Note","#st-ut-oos"],["Governing code: Utah Rules of Professional Conduct (Chapter 13)","Utah · ethics","Utah’s Rules of Professional Conduct follow the ABA Model Rules with Utah changes, each flagged in a comment saying the rule “differs from the ABA Model Rule.” They also govern licensed paralegal practitioners, so “lawyer” includes LPPs unless a rule says otherwise. Comments explain and do not add duties. Several rules (1.0, 1.4, 1.5, 5.4, 5.8, 8.4) were amended effective November 1, 2026. Utah R. Prof. Conduct Preli","#st-ut-ethics"],["Referral fees banned; fee sharing between lawyers under new Rule 5.8","Utah · ethics","As of November 1, 2026, referral fees (compensation paid solely for referring a matter to someone outside the firm) are prohibited; lead-generation and advertising payments are not referral fees. Fee sharing with lawyers in other firms is allowed only if no one is paid before the fee is payable, the total fee does not increase, and the client agrees in a confirmed writing, including each lawyer’s share, and each shar","#st-ut-ethics"],["Nonlawyer ownership and fee sharing through the Supreme Court’s authorization","Utah · ethics","Unlike ABA Model Rule 5.4, a Utah lawyer may share fees with nonlawyers, or practice in an entity with nonlawyer owners or managers, if the arrangement or entity has been authorized under Utah Supreme Court Standing Order No. 15 and the lawyer gives clients the required written notice (before taking the representation) of the fee-sharing or ownership structure. Standing Order No. 15 itself was not collected. Utah R. ","#st-ut-ethics"],["Candor: recklessness is enough","Utah · ethics","Utah’s Rule 3.3 was amended after In re Larsen, 2016 UT 26, so that a lawyer violates the rule by recklessly (not only knowingly) making a false statement of law or fact to a tribunal or failing to disclose controlling adverse authority. Utah R. Prof. Conduct 3.3 cmt. [3]","#st-ut-ethics"],["Conflicts and files: lateral screening, indigent expenses, client files","Utah · ethics","Utah permits screening to remove imputation when a lateral lawyer is disqualified under Rule 1.9, without the client’s consent, though courts may demand more on disqualification motions. Rule 1.8(e) lets lawyers pay minor litigation-related expenses (transportation, lodging) for indigent clients. On termination, the lawyer must give the client the file on request notwithstanding attorney lien laws or unpaid fees; wor","#st-ut-ethics"],["Represented persons and limited-scope clients","Utah · ethics","Rule 4.2 adds paragraphs (b)-(e): a lawyer may deal directly with a person represented only on a limited basis about matters outside that scope (the limited-scope lawyer must disclose the limits), and government lawyers in criminal and civil enforcement have specific authority to contact represented persons. Rule 4.2(a) also recognizes “judicially created rules” as authority for contact. Utah R. Prof. Conduct 4.2 & c","#st-ut-ethics"],["Misconduct: no ABA 8.4(g), but civility counts","Utah · ethics","Utah’s Rule 8.4 has no counterpart to ABA Model Rule 8.4(g) on harassment and discrimination; bias manifested in representation is addressed under 8.4(d) when prejudicial to the administration of justice. An egregious violation or a pattern of violations of the Standards of Professionalism and Civility may support an 8.4(d) violation, and conditioning a settlement on not filing a bar complaint is prejudicial to the a","#st-ut-ethics"],["Standards of Professionalism and Civility","Utah · ethics","The Supreme Court’s civility standards (Rule 14-301) apply in all Utah proceedings and set expectations for courtesy, no unfounded attacks on opposing counsel’s motives, and decorum in depositions, email, and other exchanges. Lawyers must tell clients that civility is expected; Practice Pending Admission supervisors must ensure compliance. Utah Sup. Ct. R. Prof. Prac. 14-301; 14-809(a)(1)(B)","#st-ut-ethics"],["Advertising and solicitation: Utah’s single Rule 7.1","Utah · ethics","Utah folds its advertising rules into one Rule 7.1: no false or misleading communications (including unjustified-expectation and testimonial rules), no coercive or harassing contacts, and no direct communication with a prospective client about a disaster, personal injury, or wrongful death within 30 days of the event unless there is a family, close personal, or prior professional relationship or the prospect initiate","#st-ut-ethics"],["Your tier pleading caps your damages","Utah · land mine","A damages claimant must plead an amount or a discovery tier, and the tier must appear in the caption. Pleading Tier 1 or Tier 2 waives damages above that tier’s limit ($50,000, or under $300,000) unless the pleading is amended under Rule 15. Utah R. Civ. P. 8(a), 10(a)(1), 26(c)(3)","#st-ut-unique"],["No discovery until you serve your own initial disclosures, and the clock is already running","Utah · land mine","A party may not seek discovery from any source until its own Rule 26(a)(1) disclosures are served (plaintiff: 14 days after the first answer; defendant: 42 days after its answer). Standard fact discovery runs only 120, 180, or 210 days by tier from the first defendant’s first disclosure deadline, and a statement of discovery issues does not toll it. Undisclosed witnesses and documents are excluded unless harmless or ","#st-ut-unique"],["Expert discovery is lost if you do not elect within 14 days","Utah · land mine","After an expert disclosure, the opposing party has 14 days to elect a 4-hour deposition or a written report; with no election, no further discovery of that expert is allowed. An expert who gives a report cannot be deposed. Utah R. Civ. P. 26(a)(4)(B)-(C), 30(a)","#st-ut-unique"],["Fully briefed motions sit until someone files a Request to Submit","Utah · land mine","A motion is not submitted for decision until a party files a “Request to Submit for Decision” after briefing closes. Oppositions are due 14 days after the motion and replies 7 days after the opposition. Evidence objections go in the next memorandum, not a separate motion to strike. Utah R. Civ. P. 7(d)-(e), (g), (n)","#st-ut-unique"],["Seven days to use a peremptory change of judge","Utah · land mine","Where Rule 63A applies (counties with seven or more district judges), the defendant side’s notice is due within 7 days after service or at its first filing, whichever is first; missing it forfeits the right. Confirm the rule’s effective status first. Utah R. Civ. P. 63A(a)(2)","#st-ut-unique"],["Out-of-state counsel cannot sign anything until the order is entered","Utah · land mine","A pro hac vice applicant may not appear or have its name on any pleading until the court grants the motion, and the motion can be filed only after the Bar issues its Acknowledgment. Build in time for the Bar step, the 60-day good-standing certificate, and annual renewal within 28 days of each anniversary. Utah Sup. Ct. R. Prof. Prac. 14-806(g), (i)-(l), (n)-(o)","#st-ut-unique"],["Anti-SLAPP: 60 days to move, 21 days to appeal","Utah · land mine","The UPEPA special motion is due 60 days after service of the pleading, and an order denying it must be appealed within 21 days, not 30. The motion triggers an automatic stay of discovery between the parties and mandatory fees for a prevailing movant. Utah Code §§ 78B-25-103, -104, -110; Utah R. App. P. 4(a)(2)","#st-ut-unique"],["Rule 60(b) motions for mistake or fraud: 90 days, not one year","Utah · land mine","Relief for mistake, excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, or fraud must be sought within 90 days after entry of the judgment or order. Separately, a Rule 60(b) motion tolls the appeal time only if filed within 28 days after judgment. Utah R. Civ. P. 60(c); Utah R. App. P. 4(b)(1)(E)","#st-ut-unique"],["Summons, service, and commencement work differently","Utah · land mine","The plaintiff’s attorney signs and issues the summons. An action can start by serving the summons and complaint before filing, but then the complaint, summons, and proof of service must be filed within 10 days or the action is deemed dismissed. Serve within 120 days after filing. Answers are due in 21 days, or 30 days if served outside Utah. Utah R. Civ. P. 3(a), 4(a)-(b), 12(a)","#st-ut-unique"],["Different mail-service add-ons at trial and on appeal","Utah · land mine","In the trial court, 7 days are added only when service is exclusively by mail; on appeal, 3 days are added for mail service, and appellate periods under 11 days exclude weekends and holidays. Utah R. Civ. P. 6(c); Utah R. App. P. 22(a), (d)","#st-ut-unique"],["Jury waived if the jury fee is not paid","Utah · land mine","A jury demand must be served within 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue and the statutory jury fee paid; failing to pay the fee or to serve and file the demand waives the jury. Utah R. Civ. P. 38(b), (d)","#st-ut-unique"],["Appeal extras: $300 bond, docketing statement, paper copies","Utah · land mine","File a $300 cost bond with the notice of appeal (unless an exception applies), a docketing statement within 21 days, and transcript orders within 21 days after that. Even after e-filing a brief, deliver 10 (Supreme Court) or 6 (Court of Appeals) paper copies, binder-clipped with the right cover color, within 7 days. Utah R. App. P. 6, 9(b), 11(c), 26(d), 27(e)","#st-ut-unique"],["New: peremptory change of judge in larger counties (Rule 63A)","Utah · judges","Effective February 13, 2026, in a civil action in a county with seven or more district court judges, each side gets one change of judge as of right by filing a notice with the clerk, without stating reasons but attesting it is not for delay or based on race, gender, or religious affiliation. Deadlines are short: plaintiff side within 7 days after a judge is first assigned; defendant side within 7 days after service o","#st-ut-unique"],["Change of judge by unanimous agreement","Utah · judges","All parties may jointly change the judge once, without cause, by a notice signed by all parties filed within 90 days after commencement or before the trial-setting notice, whichever comes first. No one may tell the court who sought consent. Utah R. Civ. P. 63A(b)","#st-ut-unique"],["Disqualification for cause: 21 days, one motion","Utah · judges","A motion to disqualify for bias, prejudice, or conflict needs a good-faith certificate and an affidavit or unsworn declaration of facts, and must be filed within 21 days after the latest of assignment, appearance, or when the grounds were or should have been known. The challenged judge must grant it or certify it to a reviewing judge, without a response, and take no further action meanwhile. Only one motion per party","#st-ut-unique"],["Three-judge district court panels","Utah · judges","In a civil action against the State or a state official challenging the constitutionality of a statute, constitutional provision, legislative action, executive order, or rule and seeking declaratory or injunctive relief, a party may file a notice within 45 days after commencement (or an amended complaint) to require a panel of three randomly selected judges from different judicial districts. The assigned judge must t","#st-ut-unique"],["UPEPA: broad construction for speech, press, petition, and association rights","Utah · speech","Utah’s anti-SLAPP statute expressly protects rights “guaranteed by the United States Constitution or Utah Constitution” and directs courts to construe it broadly to protect those rights, with uniformity among enacting states considered. Utah Code §§ 78B-25-102(2)(c), -111, -112","#st-ut-unique"],["Utah Constitution free-speech provisions not collected","Utah · speech","Whether the Utah Constitution’s speech and press guarantees are read more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked; the constitutional text and case law were not collected. Utah Const. art. I (not collected)","#st-ut-unique"],["Tiered, proportional discovery with the burden on the requester","Utah · unique rules","Discovery limits depend on the damages pleaded: Tier 1 ($50,000 or less), Tier 2 (more than $50,000 and under $300,000, or non-monetary relief), Tier 3 ($300,000 or more), Tier 4 (domestic). The party seeking discovery always bears the burden of showing proportionality, and going past standard limits requires a stipulation (with a discovery budget since November 1, 2026) or a statement of discovery issues. Utah R. Ci","#st-ut-unique"],["Business and Chancery Court","Utah · unique rules","A plaintiff that files there waives a jury on its own claims; a defendant must demand a jury with its first responsive pleading or waive it, and a jury demand with a right to jury sends the claim to district court. Presumptive discovery there is 20 interrogatories, 25 document requests, 30 admission requests, and 10 depositions per side, completed within 210 days. A party may also ask the district court to transfer a","#st-ut-unique"],["Non-unanimous civil juries","Utah · unique rules","A civil verdict needs agreement of three-fourths of the jurors unless the parties stipulate otherwise, and parties may stipulate to fewer than eight jurors. Utah R. Civ. P. 47(r), 48","#st-ut-unique"],["Two-way offers of settlement","Utah · unique rules","Either side may make a Rule 68 offer (more than 14 days before trial, open at least 14 days). An offeree who does not beat it loses post-offer costs, prejudgment interest, and attorney fees and pays the offeror’s post-offer costs. Utah R. Civ. P. 68","#st-ut-unique"],["Rule 1. General provisions","Utah · civil rules","Govern all civil actions and statutory proceedings in Utah courts except where other Supreme Court rules or statutes govern, to be construed for the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action. The 2026 amendment adds a definition of “district court panel” (a three-judge district court panel convened to hear and decide an action).","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","Utah · civil rules","There is one form of action, the civil action.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencement of action","Utah · civil rules","An action starts either by filing a complaint or by serving a summons with a copy of the complaint under Rule 4 (unlike the federal rule). If started by service, the complaint, summons, and proof of service must be filed within 10 days of service or the action is deemed dismissed and the court loses jurisdiction. A dishonored filing-fee payment must be replaced by cash or cashier’s check within 10 days after notice.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 4. Process","Utah · civil rules","The summons is signed and issued by the plaintiff or plaintiff’s attorney, not the clerk. Serve within 120 days after filing the complaint (the federal rule is 90), or the unserved defendant may be dismissed without prejudice. Methods: personal service by a non-party 18 or older; mail or commercial courier with a signed receipt; acceptance of service (including electronic acceptance, which must not be deceptive); Hag","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 5. Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","Utah · civil rules","Every document filed after the complaint must be served on every party, on or before the day it is filed (by the fastest method if a hearing is 7 days away or less). Service is by e-filing if the recipient has an e-filing account, otherwise by email, then mail or hand delivery; mail and electronic service are complete on sending. Serve both the attorney and the party when the attorney entered a Rule 75 limited appear","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 6. Time","Utah · civil rules","Count days by excluding the trigger day and including the last day, rolling past weekends and legal holidays (including Utah’s Pioneer Day and Washington and Lincoln Day). Electronic filing is timely before midnight on the last day. The court may extend time for good cause before expiry or for excusable neglect after, but never the times under Rules 50(b) and (d), 52(b), 59(b), (d), and (e), and 60(c). Add 7 days (th","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings allowed; motions, memoranda, hearings, orders","Utah · civil rules","Utah’s detailed motion-practice rule (no federal counterpart of this detail). The motion itself contains the memorandum; opposition is due 14 days after the motion is filed and the reply 7 days after the opposition; evidence objections go in the next memorandum, never in a motion to strike. Nothing is decided until a party files a “Request to Submit for Decision.” Dispositive motions need bold caution language and th","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 7A. Motion to enforce order and for sanctions","Utah · civil rules","Replaces orders to show cause: file an ex parte verified motion with a proposed “order to attend hearing” (stating contempt penalties of up to a $1,000 fine and 30 days in jail if contempt is sought). Serve the order and papers at least 28 days before the hearing (Rule 4 service if the other side has had no counsel active in the last 120 days). Any opposition is due within 14 days of service of the order; reply withi","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 7B. Motion to enforce order and for sanctions in domestic law matters","Utah · civil rules","Domestic-relations version of Rule 7A: serve the order to attend hearing at least 28 days before the hearing; any opposition is due at least 14 days before the hearing and any reply at least 7 days before. Counter motions must be filed with the opposition, and a reply on a counter motion is due at least 3 business days before the hearing by a method ensuring actual receipt.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleadings","Utah · civil rules","Notice-pleading claims with a demand for specified relief. A damages claimant who pleads no amount must plead the Rule 26(c)(3) discovery tier, and pleading tier 1 or 2 waives damages above that tier’s cap unless amended. A pleading seeking relief must carry bold caution language at the top right of page one warning that judgment could be entered; omitting it can support Rule 60(b) relief. Utah’s list of affirmative ","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","Utah · civil rules","Mirrors federal Rule 9 (particularity for fraud and mistake; general allegations of conditions precedent) and adds Utah provisions: “unknown” defendants in quiet-title actions; general allegations of publication in defamation cases; and allocation of fault to a non-party under Utah Code Title 78B, Chapter 5, Part 8, which requires a filed description of the basis and the non-party’s identifying information, no later ","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings and other papers","Utah · civil rules","Caption must show the court, title, case number, document name, and assigned judge (and commissioner), and any claim for relief must state the Rule 26 discovery tier in the caption and come with the Judicial Council cover sheet. Filer’s name, address, email, phone, and bar number go in the top left corner of page one. Papers are 8½ × 11, 1-inch margins, double-spaced, at least 12-point type. Domestic-relations captio","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing of pleadings, motions, affidavits, and other papers; representations to court; sanctions","Utah · civil rules","Tracks federal Rule 11, including the 21-day safe harbor before a sanctions motion may be filed. An unsworn declaration under the Uniform Unsworn Declarations Act (Utah Code Title 78B, Chapter 18a) may replace an affidavit or notarized signature.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections","Utah · civil rules","Answer within 21 days after service in Utah, or 30 days if served outside Utah (no federal-style 60-day waiver period); crossclaim and counterclaim answers are due in 21 days. After a Rule 12 motion is denied or deferred, answer within 14 days. Defenses (1)-(7) mirror federal Rule 12(b), with “failure to join an indispensable party” as (7); consolidation and waiver rules track federal law. Utah-only: a defendant may ","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and crossclaim","Utah · civil rules","Mirrors federal Rule 13: compulsory counterclaims from the same transaction or occurrence, permissive counterclaims, after-acquired counterclaims by supplemental pleading, and crossclaims against coparties.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","Utah · civil rules","A defendant may serve a third-party complaint without leave if it is filed within 14 days after serving the original answer; later requires leave on motion. A plaintiff facing a counterclaim may implead likewise.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Utah · civil rules","Amend once as of course within 21 days after serving the pleading, or 21 days after service of a responsive pleading or a Rule 12(b), (e), or (f) motion, whichever is earlier; otherwise by leave (attach the proposed amended pleading) or written consent. Respond to an amended pleading within the time left on the original or 14 days after service, whichever is later. Relation back tracks federal Rule 15(c), keyed to th","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 16. Pretrial conferences","Utah · civil rules","The court may hold scheduling and management conferences and set deadlines for joinder, amendment, motions, and discovery. Unless an order sets the trial date, the plaintiff must certify at the close of discovery that discovery and any required ADR are complete and the case is ready for trial (a certificate of readiness). Failure to attend, prepare, or participate in good faith allows Rule 37(b) sanctions. There is n","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 17. Parties plaintiff and defendant","Utah · civil rules","Real party in interest, with time to ratify or substitute before dismissal. Minors and incompetent persons appear by a general guardian or a guardian ad litem; a minor defendant 14 or older may apply for a guardian ad litem within 21 days after service, and a guardian ad litem for a nonresident minor defendant has 21 days after appointment to plead. Associations may sue or be sued in a common name; nonresidents doing","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims and remedies","Utah · civil rules","Mirrors federal Rule 18: unlimited joinder of claims against an opposing party, and a money claim may be joined with a claim to set aside a voidable transaction without first obtaining judgment.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 19. Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","Utah · civil rules","Older federal Rule 19 language: required parties must be joined if feasible; if not, the court decides in equity and good conscience whether to proceed, treating the absent person as indispensable if the action is dismissed. A claimant must plead the names of required persons not joined and why.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","Utah · civil rules","Mirrors federal Rule 20: same transaction or series plus a common question; the court may order separate trials to prevent prejudice.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and non-joinder of parties","Utah · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped and claims severed at any stage on just terms.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","Utah · civil rules","Rule interpleader when the plaintiff faces double or multiple liability, available by crossclaim or counterclaim; claims need not share a common origin.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","Utah · civil rules","Utah’s text follows the pre-2003 federal Rule 23: the (a) prerequisites, (b)(1)-(3) categories, certification “as soon as practicable,” best-practicable notice with opt-out in (b)(3) classes, and court approval with notice for any dismissal or compromise. The collected text has no counterpart to federal Rule 23(e)-(h) settlement-review standards, 23(f) interlocutory appeal of certification, or class-counsel appointme","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 23A. Derivative actions by shareholders","Utah · civil rules","Verified complaint alleging contemporaneous ownership, non-collusion, and with particularity the demand made or why none was made. Dismissal or compromise requires court approval and notice to shareholders or members.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","Utah · civil rules","Intervention of right and permissive intervention track the federal rule. A party challenging the constitutionality of a Utah statute where the Attorney General has not appeared must serve a “Notice of Constitutional Challenge Under URCP 24(d)” on the Attorney General (by email to notices@agutah.gov) on or before filing the challenge, and file proof of service; similar notice goes to a governmental entity whose ordin","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","Utah · civil rules","Move to substitute within 90 days after a death is suggested on the record, or the action is dismissed as to the deceased party. Covers incompetency, transfer of interest, and public officers (substitution of a successor officer within 6 months after the successor takes office on a showing of substantial need).","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 26. General provisions governing disclosure and discovery","Utah · civil rules","Utah’s proportionality and tiered-discovery system, the biggest difference from federal practice. Initial disclosures (witnesses with summaries of expected testimony for those a party may call, copies of documents it may offer, damages computation with supporting documents, insurance, and documents referred to in pleadings) are due from a plaintiff 14 days after the first answer and from a defendant 42 days after its","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 26.1. Disclosure and discovery in domestic relations actions","Utah · civil rules","In divorce, separation, parentage, custody, support, and modification cases, disclosures are due 14 days after the first answer, including a court-form Financial Declaration with attachments: 2 years of tax returns, 12 months of income proof and loan applications, real-estate valuations, and 3 months of statements for every financial account. Where assets are not at issue, a short list suffices (3 paystubs, last tax ","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 26.2. Disclosures in personal injury actions","Utah · civil rules","Adds to Rule 26(a) in physical-injury cases: the plaintiff lists treating providers for the injury and all providers for the prior 5 years, gives SSN or Medicare number, benefits and 5 years of employers if wage loss is claimed, bills, official reports, and witness statements. The defendant discloses insurance limits, excess coverage, deductibles, and reservations of rights (declarations page suffices unless the full","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 26.3. Disclosure in unlawful detainer actions","Utah · civil rules","In evictions the plaintiff serves the rental agreement, eviction notice, itemized amounts, and factual basis with the summons and complaint. Disclosures for an evidentiary hearing under Utah Code § 78B-6-810 go with the request or at least 2 days before the hearing; pretrial disclosures are due 14 days before trial and objections 7 days before.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 26.4. Provisions governing disclosure and discovery in contested proceedings under Title 75 of the Utah Code","Utah · civil rules","In contested probate, guardianship, and trust matters, the petitioner is presumed the plaintiff and objectors defendants. An oral objection at a hearing must be put in writing and filed within 7 days. Nomination documents (wills, trusts, powers of attorney) and, in guardianships, less-restrictive alternatives must be served within 14 days after a written objection; originals whose validity is disputed must be made av","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions before action or pending appeal","Utah · civil rules","Verified petition to perpetuate testimony in the county where an expected adverse party resides, with notice served at least 21 days before the hearing in the Rule 4(d) manner. The trial court may also allow depositions pending appeal.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","Utah · civil rules","Before an officer authorized to administer oaths, a court appointee, or in a foreign country on notice, by commission, or by letter rogatory. No deposition before a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or anyone financially interested.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations regarding disclosure and discovery procedure","Utah · civil rules","Amended effective November 1, 2026: parties may expand discovery by filing, before standard discovery closes and after reaching its limits, a stipulated statement that the extraordinary discovery is necessary and proportional and that each party has reviewed and approved a discovery budget. Stipulations only extending time need no proportionality or budget statement and need court approval only if they would interfer","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions upon oral questions","Utah · civil rules","A witness may be deposed only once in standard discovery, and an expert who gave a Rule 26(a)(4)(B) report may not be deposed. During standard discovery, questioning of a nonparty is capped at 4 hours and of a party at 7 hours, all within the tier’s total deposition hours. An organization served with a Rule 30(b)(6)-style notice may object in writing within 14 days, and the parties must confer before the deposition o","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions upon written questions","Utah · civil rules","Cross questions within 14 days after service of the questions, redirect within 7 days, re-cross within 7 days. In standard discovery, written-question depositions are capped at 15 questions (including discrete subparts) per side cumulatively.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 32. Use of depositions in court proceedings","Utah · civil rules","Mirrors federal Rule 32: impeachment use, adverse use of a party or organizational designee’s deposition, and use of an unavailable witness’s deposition (dead, more than 100 miles away, ill, or not subpoenable, or exceptional circumstances). Objections to the form of written questions are waived unless served within the time for the next round and within 5 days after the last questions.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories to parties","Utah · civil rules","Allowed only within Rule 26(c)(5) tier limits (none in Tier 1; 10 in Tiers 2 and 4; 20 in Tier 3, including discrete subparts). Answers or objections within 28 days (the federal rule gives 30), restating each interrogatory; unstated objections are waived. Business-records option as in federal Rule 33(d).","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 34. Production of documents and things and entry upon land for inspection and other purposes","Utah · civil rules","Respond in writing within 28 days, restating each request; objections must be specific and say whether anything is withheld, and stating the search terms used counts as notice that items outside them may be withheld. Tier limits on the number of requests apply. ESI is produced in the form requested or as ordinarily maintained or reasonably usable, and only in one form.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examination of persons","Utah · civil rules","Court-ordered on motion for good cause; the examinee may audio- or video-record the exam unless the requesting party shows it would unduly interfere (unlike federal practice). The requesting party must disclose the examiner’s detailed report within the shorter of 60 days after the exam or 7 days before fact discovery closes. Noncompliance allows Rule 37(b) sanctions but not contempt.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 36. Request for admission","Utah · civil rules","Requests must carry bold caution language on page one warning that matters are admitted if not answered within 28 days; omitting it may support Rule 60(b) relief. Matters are admitted unless a written response is served within 28 days (restating each request). Tier limits apply (5 in Tier 1; 10 in Tiers 2 and 4; 20 in Tier 3). Admissions bind only in the pending action and may be withdrawn if it promotes the merits w","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 37. Statement of discovery issues; Sanctions; Failure to admit, to attend deposition or to preserve evidence","Utah · civil rules","Discovery disputes (compel, protective orders, extraordinary discovery, subpoenas) go by a “statement of discovery issues” of no more than 4 pages or 1,500 words, with a meet-and-confer certification (in person or by phone), a proportionality statement, and, for extraordinary discovery, certification of a reviewed and approved discovery budget (budget certification added November 1, 2026). Objections are due within 7","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury trial of right","Utah · civil rules","Demand a jury in writing and pay the statutory jury fee no later than 14 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue; others may demand on other issues within 14 days of service of the demand. Failure to pay the fee or to serve and file the demand waives the jury (fee payment is a Utah-specific condition).","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","Utah · civil rules","Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise, no jury right exists, or a party fails to appear at trial. The court may allow a late jury on motion and may use an advisory jury.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 40. Scheduling and postponing a trial","Utah · civil rules","The court need not set trial until the case is certified ready under Rule 16; trial may be postponed for good cause on just terms, and witness testimony may be preserved at a hearing for use like a deposition.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","Utah · civil rules","Voluntary dismissal by notice before an answer or summary judgment motion is served, or by stipulation of all appearing parties; a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits. Involuntary dismissal for failure to prosecute or comply is on the merits unless the order says otherwise. A counterclaim, crossclaim, or third-party claimant may voluntarily dismiss only before a respons","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials; venue transfer","Utah · civil rules","Consolidation may reach actions in other judicial districts; the motion is filed in and heard by the judge with the first-filed action, with notice filed in each case. In medical malpractice cases, past medical expenses are kept from the factfinder until liability and noneconomic damages are resolved. Improper venue must be transferred on timely motion, with substantial deference to a plaintiff’s proper venue choice.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 43. Evidence","Utah · civil rules","Testimony is taken in open court, but in civil cases the court may allow remote testimony for good cause with required safeguards (notice, verbatim record, confidential attorney-client channel, document sharing); a special remote oath bars communicating with anyone during testimony. Motions on facts outside the record may be heard on affidavits, declarations, testimony, or depositions.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 44. Proof of official record","Utah · civil rules","Official records are proved by official publication or an attested copy with a custodian certificate, including foreign-record certifications by U.S. consular officers. Certified copies of Utah or U.S. records may be read in evidence; foreign and sister-state law may be proved by official publications, and the court determines it.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","Utah · civil rules","Utah-admitted attorneys may issue subpoenas as officers of the court; the clerk issues blank signed subpoenas to others. Serve as in Rule 4(d) by a non-party adult, tendering one day’s fees and mileage for appearances. A document or inspection subpoena must first be served on every party by a method giving actual notice, must allow at least 14 days to comply, and may require a Utah resident to appear for deposition o","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 46. Exceptions unnecessary","Utah · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary; a party preserves an issue by stating the desired action or objection and grounds when the ruling is made.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 47. Jurors","Utah · civil rules","The court or counsel may conduct voir dire. Each side gets 3 peremptory challenges (federal practice also uses 3), plus 1 more if one or two alternates are called; multiple parties on a side are one party unless their interests substantially conflict. Lists statutory and impartiality grounds for cause; the judge picks strike-and-replace or struck method. Jurors may submit written questions if the judge allows, may ta","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 48. Juries of less than eight - Majority verdict","Utah · civil rules","The parties may stipulate to a jury of fewer than eight or to a verdict by a stated majority.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 49. Special verdicts and interrogatories","Utah · civil rules","Mirrors federal Rule 49: special verdicts (omitted issues are waived unless submission is demanded before the jury retires) and general verdicts with interrogatories, with rules for inconsistent answers.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 50. Judgment as a matter of law in a jury trial; related motion for a new trial; conditional ruling","Utah · civil rules","JMOL may be sought any time before submission. A renewed motion is due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment (or after the jury is discharged on an undecided issue), optionally joined with a new-trial request; a losing party’s new-trial motion after JMOL is due 28 days after that judgment. These times cannot be extended (Rule 6(b)(2)).","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 51. Instructions to jury; objections","Utah · civil rules","Requested instructions are filed at the final pretrial conference or when the court directs. Object to written instructions before they are given (oral ones before the jury retires), stating the matter and grounds; unpreserved errors are reviewable only to avoid manifest injustice. Closing arguments come after final instructions, and the court may not comment on the evidence.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings and conclusions by the court; amended findings; waiver of findings and conclusions; correction of the record; judgment on partial findings","Utah · civil rules","Bench trials require separate findings and conclusions, reviewed for clear error; findings are not required on Rule 12(b), 50, 56, or 59 motions, but a brief statement of grounds is required when a motion rests on more than one ground. Motion to amend findings within 28 days after judgment (not extendable). Findings may be waived except in divorce actions. A motion to correct the transcript is due within 14 days afte","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters","Utah · civil rules","Reference to a master is the exception; the first meeting is set within 21 days after the order of reference. In non-jury cases the court accepts the master’s findings unless clearly erroneous, and objections to the report are due within 14 days after notice of its filing. Parties may object to a master’s appointment on juror-challenge grounds.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 53A. Special masters for parenting disputes in domestic relations actions","Utah · civil rules","Only on the parties’ stipulation and after a parenting plan or order is entered, the court may appoint a special master to issue directives on parenting disputes, effective as orders. A party may move to modify or set aside a directive within 14 days; the court reviews de novo. Special masters cannot find contempt or issue sanctions unless authorized, and neither party may end the appointment unilaterally.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgments; costs","Utah · civil rules","Rule 54(b) certification requires an express finding of no just reason for delay; otherwise partial rulings remain revisable. File and serve a verified memorandum of costs within 14 days after entry of judgment; objections are due 7 days after service. When costs or fees are awarded after judgment, the prevailing party files an amended judgment, which is entered unless someone objects within 7 days.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 55. Default","Utah · civil rules","The clerk enters default for failure to plead or defend. The clerk may enter a sum-certain default judgment only if the defendant failed to appear, is not a minor or incompetent, was personally served under Rule 4(d)(1), and the amount is verified by verified complaint, affidavit, or unsworn declaration; otherwise apply to the court. Defaults are set aside for good cause, default judgments under Rule 60(b); no defaul","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","Utah · civil rules","The motion must contain a statement of material facts in separately numbered paragraphs with record citations; the opposition must restate verbatim each disputed fact and explain the dispute with citations, and undisputed facts are deemed admitted. A claimant may move after 21 days from commencement (or after the adverse party moves); a defending party any time; absent an order, no later than 28 days after the close ","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments","Utah · civil rules","Declaratory actions under Utah Code Title 78B, Chapter 6, Part 4 follow these rules; jury trial may be demanded under Rules 38-39; another adequate remedy does not bar relief, and the court may expedite.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 58A. Entry of judgment; abstract of judgment","Utah · civil rules","Every judgment needs a separate document (not required for orders on Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59, 60, or 73 motions). The prevailing party serves a proposed judgment within 14 days after verdict or decision; objections to form are due within 7 days. A judgment requiring a separate document is entered when signed and docketed or 150 days after the decision is docketed, whichever is earlier (federal law also uses 150 days). ","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 58B. Satisfaction of judgment","Utah · civil rules","The judgment creditor must file an acknowledgment of satisfaction within 28 days after full satisfaction (in both district and juvenile court for juvenile restitution judgments); the court may also declare satisfaction on motion.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 58C. Motion to renew judgment","Utah · civil rules","Renew a judgment by Rule 7 motion in the original action before the limitations period on the judgment runs, with an affidavit accounting for payments and confirming notice to the debtor’s last known address. The renewed judgment runs from the order date or the original expiration date, whichever is first.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 59. New trial; altering or amending a judgment","Utah · civil rules","Lists 7 grounds, including insufficiency of the evidence and error of law. Motions for new trial and to alter or amend are due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, with supporting affidavits for grounds (a)(1)-(4); the court may order a new trial on its own within 28 days. These times cannot be extended.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from judgment or order","Utah · civil rules","Clerical errors may be fixed any time (with appellate leave once an appeal is pending). Grounds track federal Rule 60(b), but motions for mistake, excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, or fraud must be filed within 90 days after entry of the judgment or order (the federal limit is one year); other grounds within a reasonable time. The motion does not suspend finality.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","Utah · civil rules","Errors not affecting substantial rights are disregarded.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","Utah · civil rules","No execution for 28 days after entry of a judgment or a Rule 7(j)(8) order to pay money. A stay is available by bond or other security at any time after judgment; the presumptive amount for compensatory damages is the damages plus costs and fees plus 3 years of interest, capped at $25 million in class or aggregate multi-plaintiff actions, with no bond for punitive damages. Personal sureties must be Utah residents wit","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 63. Disability or disqualification of a judge","Utah · civil rules","A motion to disqualify for bias, prejudice, or conflict needs a good-faith certificate, a supporting affidavit or unsworn declaration, and a request to submit, and must be filed no later than 21 days after the latest of assignment, the party’s appearance, or when the party knew or should have known the grounds (as soon as practicable if that falls within 21 days of a hearing). Only one motion per party absent newly d","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 63A. Change of judge as a matter of right","Utah · civil rules","New February 13, 2026: in a civil action in a county with seven or more district court judges, each side (only two sides, regardless of adverse interests) gets one peremptory change of judge by filing a notice with the clerk, giving no reason but attesting it is not for delay or because of race, gender, or religious affiliation. Deadlines: plaintiff side within 7 days after a judge is first assigned; defendant side w","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 64. Writs in general","Utah · civil rules","Common definitions and procedures for writs of replevin, attachment, garnishment, and execution. The court sets security (none for governmental entities); objections to security are due within 5 days after service of the writ. The clerk issues writs, the officer returns the writ within 14 days after service, and only one earnings garnishment may be in effect at a time. Third-party claimants have the defendant’s right","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 64A. Prejudgment writs in general","Utah · civil rules","Prejudgment replevin, attachment, or garnishment only by court order on motion, security, and a non-conclusory affidavit showing non-exempt, non-earnings property, no intent to defraud, a substantial likelihood of success, and at least one listed ground (such as concealment, flight, fraud, or decline in value). The defendant may reply at least 24 hours before the hearing. An ex parte writ requires irreparable-injury ","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 64B. Writ of replevin","Utah · civil rules","Compels delivery of specific personal property; requires that the plaintiff is entitled to possession and the defendant wrongfully detains it, plus the Rule 64A grounds before judgment.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 64C. Writ of attachment","Utah · civil rules","Seizes property held by the defendant; before judgment also requires that the defendant is indebted to the plaintiff, the action is on a contract or against a nonresident or unqualified foreign corporation (or a statute authorizes it), and the claim is not secured by a Utah lien.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 64D. Writ of garnishment","Utah · civil rules","Reaches the defendant’s property held by third parties, before or after judgment. Earnings garnishment is capped at the lesser of 25% of disposable earnings (50% for child-support judgments) or the excess over 30 times the federal minimum hourly wage per week. The garnishee answers interrogatories and serves the defendant within 7 business days; replies and hearing requests are due within 14 days after service of the","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 64E. Writ of execution","Utah · civil rules","After final judgment, on an application stating the amount due and the property. The defendant may reply and request a hearing within 14 days after service, and the hearing must be held within 14 days; no sale until the issues are decided. Mortgage foreclosure is governed by Utah Code Title 78B, Chapter 6, Part 9.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 65A. Injunctions","Utah · civil rules","Preliminary injunctions require notice. An ex parte TRO requires specific facts of immediate irreparable injury and a written certification of notice efforts, and expires within no more than 14 days unless extended for a like period; the restrained party may move to dissolve on 48 hours’ notice. Grounds: substantial likelihood of success (Utah’s rule no longer allows “serious issues” as a substitute), irreparable har","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 65B. Extraordinary relief","Utah · civil rules","Replaces the common-law writs (habeas corpus outside Rule 65C, quo warranto, mandamus, certiorari, prohibition) with a single petition when no plain, speedy, and adequate remedy exists. Covers wrongful restraint of liberty, wrongful use of public or corporate authority (generally brought by the Attorney General; a private petitioner must post an undertaking), and wrongful use of judicial authority, failure to perform","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 65C. Post-conviction relief","Utah · civil rules","Governs petitions under the Post-Conviction Remedies Act, Utah Code Title 78B, Chapter 9, filed in the county of conviction and assigned to the sentencing judge. The court must decide procedural bars under § 78B-9-106 before commenting on the merits; facially frivolous or previously adjudicated claims are summarily dismissed (not an initial petition in a death case), and deficient claims get 21 days to amend (one mor","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers","Utah · civil rules","Grounds for appointing a receiver include property at risk, enforcing a judgment, an unsatisfied execution, and corporate insolvency or dissolution. A party, attorney, or non-disinterested person may serve only with all interested parties’ written consent. Taxes must be paid before personal property is sold, with receipts filed within 14 days after payment; the appointment order must be recorded before the receiver t","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit in court","Utah · civil rules","The court may order money or things held for or due to another party deposited in court or delivered, with deposits handled under Utah Code § 78B-5-804.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 68. Settlement offers","Utah · civil rules","Unlike federal Rule 68, either party may make an offer. The offer must be in writing, cite the rule, be made more than 14 days before trial, stay open at least 14 days, and be served under Rule 5. If the offeree’s “adjusted award” (verdict plus pre-offer costs, interest, and fees unless excluded) is not more favorable, the offeror owes no post-offer costs, prejudgment interest, or fees, and the offeree pays the offer","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 69A. Seizure of property","Utah · civil rules","The officer seizes the property the debtor designates, otherwise personal property before real property. Real property is seized by recording the writ with the county recorder and leaving it with an occupant or posting it; farm products, securities, and bulky property have special methods.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 69B. Sale of property; delivery of property","Utah · civil rules","Execution sales are auctions Monday through Saturday (not legal holidays) between 9:00 a.m. and 8:00 p.m. Personal property: post notice at least 7 days and publish once at least a day before the sale; real property: post at least 21 days and publish weekly for 3 successive weeks, with the sale at the county’s district courthouse. Postponements longer than 72 hours require new notice. A bid is an irrevocable offer.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 69C. Redemption of real property after sale","Utah · civil rules","Real property sold on execution may be redeemed within 180 days after the sale by the debtor or a junior lienholder, at the sale price plus 6% (later redemptions plus 3%), plus recorded taxes and other outlays. A price dispute requires paying the undisputed amount into court and petitioning within 21 days; an accounting request extends redemption to 7 days after the accounting.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 70. Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","Utah · civil rules","If a party fails to perform a judgment-ordered act, the court may appoint someone to do it at the party’s cost, issue attachment or sequestration, hold the party in contempt, or vest title directly by judgment.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 71. Process in behalf of and against persons not parties","Utah · civil rules","Non-parties may enforce orders in their favor, and be subject to enforcement, like parties.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 72. Property bonds","Utah · civil rules","A real-property bond must be signed and notarized by all record owners, with legal description, title and tax documents, a title or foreclosure report, and statements from each lienholder; it is effective only when recorded with the county recorder.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 73. Attorney fees","Utah · civil rules","Claim fees by motion no later than 14 days after judgment is entered, specifying the basis, amount, and reasonableness, with an affidavit itemizing time by person, position, and rate (and disclosure and a Rule 5.4 statement for fees for assignees or debt collectors). If liability is decided, a proposed order on an affidavit is entered unless someone objects within 7 days. Scheduled fees without affidavit: up to $475 ","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 74. Withdrawal of counsel","Utah · civil rules","An attorney may withdraw by notice only if no motion is pending, no hearing or trial is set, and no court-ordered obligation is outstanding; otherwise by motion and order. The order tells the client to appoint counsel or appear personally within 21 days, during which proceedings are stayed, and that entities must appear through a Utah-admitted attorney. When counsel otherwise leaves, the opposing party must serve a n","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 75. Limited appearance","Utah · civil rules","An attorney may appear for limited purposes (a filing, a motion, a discovery event, a hearing, or another purpose with leave) by filing a Notice of Limited Appearance signed by attorney and client describing the scope; the party remains responsible for everything else. Both the attorney and party must be served on matters within the scope (Rule 5).","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 76. Notice of contact information change","Utah · civil rules","Attorneys and self-represented parties must promptly notify the court and other parties in writing of any change in address, email, or phone, unless disclosure is barred by order or law.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 77. District courts and clerks","Utah · civil rules","District courts are always open for filing; merits trials are in open court, but other proceedings may be held in chambers anywhere in Utah, except non-ex parte hearings outside the county of the case require all affected parties’ consent. Clerks may grant process, defaults, and default judgments of course.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 81. Applicability of rules in general","Utah · civil rules","The rules apply to special statutory proceedings unless clearly inapplicable; not to uncontested probate and guardianship (they apply once issues are joined) or to small claims unless incorporated. In the Business and Chancery Court they apply except where the Business and Chancery rules have a same-numbered rule or exclude one by number. They govern review of agency action unless the statute conflicts, and gaps in c","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 82. Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","Utah · civil rules","The rules do not extend or limit jurisdiction or venue.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 83. Vexatious litigants","Utah · civil rules","A person (including an attorney acting pro se) may be declared vexatious for: at least 5 claims finally lost in the past 7 years without 2 wins (small claims excluded); 2 or more attempts to relitigate a decided claim or issue; 3 or more unmeritorious, scandalous, disproportionate, or harassing filings or tactics in an action; or invoking non-recognized “courts.” On clear and convincing findings, the court may requir","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 85. Title","Utah · civil rules","Cited as the Utah Rules of Civil Procedure (U.R.C.P.).","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 86. Licensed paralegal practitioners","Utah · civil rules","Licensed paralegal practitioners (LPPs) are treated as attorneys within their limited license and the practice areas in Rule 14-802; they may sign and file only on court-approved forms or consistent with Judicial Council forms, and may serve disclosures. They recover fees only with a supporting affidavit (no Rule 73(f) schedule). An LPP who only prepares a paper need not appear. Amended November 1, 2026.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 87. In-person, remote, and hybrid hearings; request for different format","Utah · civil rules","The court sets each hearing as in-person, remote, or hybrid after weighing listed factors. A participant (through counsel if represented) may request a different format by motion, email, or letter (with a prescribed subject line, copied to all parties) at least 7 days before the hearing, and the court must accommodate a timely request unless it finds good cause on the record.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 100. Coordination of cases pending in district court and juvenile court","Utah · civil rules","In custody, support, or parent-time cases, parties must notify the court of related custody, support, criminal, delinquency, or protective-order cases (caption, number, judge) with the initial pleading or as soon as practicable; judges and commissioners must confer about consolidation or coordination.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 100A. Case Management of Domestic Relations Actions","Utah · civil rules","After an answer, domestic cases (other than Office of Recovery Services cases) get a case management conference and are placed in Track 1 (standard, certified for trial after mediation), Track 2 (complex discovery, such as business valuation, with a set discovery schedule), or Track 3 (significant custody disputes, addressing custody evaluations and private guardians ad litem).","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 101. Motion practice before court commissioners","Utah · civil rules","Motions heard by court commissioners (mainly domestic cases) must carry bold caution language and the bilingual notice, with evidence by affidavit or declaration. Serve the motion and notice of hearing at least 28 days before the hearing (Rule 4 service if the other party has filed or served nothing in 120 days); response at least 14 days before; reply at least 7 days before; replies on countermotions at least 3 busi","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 102. Motion and order for payment of costs and fees","Utah · civil rules","In actions under Utah Code § 81-1-203(1), either party may move, with an affidavit, for the other to pay costs, attorney fees, and witness and expert fees needed to prosecute or defend; the court must find need, ability to pay, necessity, and reasonableness. The award is due in a lump sum within 30 days unless ordered otherwise.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 103. Child protective orders","Utah · civil rules","When juvenile court transfers a child protective order petition to district court, a hearing must be held within 21 days of any ex parte order. A child may be compelled to testify only for extenuating circumstances, with procedures for recorded statements, recorded testimony, and testimony outside the courtroom. Amended November 1, 2026.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 104. Divorce decree upon affidavit","Utah · civil rules","A divorce decree may be entered without a hearing on an affidavit with sufficient evidence when the other party defaults, waives notice, or stipulates.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 105. Shortening 30 day waiting period in divorce actions","Utah · civil rules","A request to enter a divorce decree less than 30 days after filing requires an affidavit stating the filing date and the extraordinary circumstances.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 106. Modification of final domestic relations order","Utah · civil rules","Modification starts with a petition served with a summons under Rule 4 (except as Utah Code § 81-9-209 provides); answer within Rule 12 time. The existing order stays in effect, a modification may be retroactive to service of the petition, and temporary changes to custody, parent-time, and related support are allowed in limited situations.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 107. Decree of adoption; Petition to open adoption records","Utah · civil rules","A petition to open adoption records must state good cause and why the Office of Vital Records histories or voluntary registry is insufficient; the court balances disclosure against confidentiality, limits inspection to what serves the purpose, and the clerk reseals the records.","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 108. Objection to court commissioner’s recommendation","Utah · civil rules","A commissioner’s recommendation is the court’s order until modified. Object in writing within 14 days after the recommendation is made in open court (or after service of the minute entry if taken under advisement), with Rule 7 briefing. New evidence is barred unless circumstances have substantially changed; in domestic matters a party may request an evidentiary hearing on custody facts. The judge makes independent fi","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 109. Injunction in certain domestic relations cases","Utah · civil rules","On filing a divorce, custody, support, parentage, or similar petition, the court enters a standard mutual injunction: no transfer of property outside the ordinary course, harassment, use of the other’s identity for credit, cancellation of insurance, or non-routine travel with a child without an itinerary, and no disparaging the other parent in the child’s presence. It binds the petitioner on filing and the respondent","#st-ut-rules"],["Rule 110. Judicial interview of a minor child","Utah · civil rules","New November 1, 2026: a judge or commissioner may interview a child only if extenuating circumstances require it, no other reasonable method exists, and it is in the child’s best interest, after Judicial Council training. Parties may propose questions; the interview is recorded; parties and their attorneys are excluded except in extraordinary circumstances; disputed potentially dispositive factual allegations must be","#st-ut-rules"],["Appeals in Utah: one notice, two courts, and a Supreme Court that pours cases over","Utah · appeals","Utah has two appellate courts. A notice of appeal is filed in the trial court within 30 days after entry of the judgment, and the appeal is docketed in whichever appellate court has statutory jurisdiction. The Supreme Court has original appellate jurisdiction over most civil appeals from district court but may transfer (“pour over”) them to the Court of Appeals; the Court of Appeals hears domestic relations appeals, ","#st-ut-appeals"],["Supreme Court: exclusive jurisdiction it cannot transfer","Utah · appeals · Which court","The Supreme Court alone hears, and may not transfer: Court of Appeals judgments (by certiorari) and cases certified by the Court of Appeals; lawyer discipline; Judicial Conduct Commission orders; capital cases; legislative-subpoena rulings; election, voting-contest, and redistricting judgments and interlocutory appeals; retention or removal of public officers; and, new in 2026, judgments and interlocutory appeals fro","#st-ut-appeals"],["Supreme Court: transferable (“pour-over”) jurisdiction","Utah · appeals · Which court","The Supreme Court also has original appellate jurisdiction over first-degree felony appeals, final orders in formal adjudications of listed agencies (Public Service Commission, Tax Commission, and others), facial-unconstitutionality judgments, and any court-of-record judgment the Court of Appeals cannot hear, which in practice includes ordinary civil appeals from district court. It may transfer any of these to the Co","#st-ut-appeals"],["Court of Appeals: direct jurisdiction","Utah · appeals · Which court","Appeals from juvenile courts; domestic relations appeals (divorce, custody, support, parent-time, adoption, paternity); most agency adjudications and district-court review of local-agency decisions; criminal appeals and interlocutory appeals other than first-degree and capital felonies; certain extraordinary-writ appeals by prisoners and Board of Pardons challenges; Utah Military Court appeals; and cases poured over ","#st-ut-appeals"],["Filed in the wrong appellate court","Utah · appeals · Which court","A timely notice, interlocutory petition, or petition for review filed in the wrong appellate court is transferred to the right one, and deadlines then follow the receiving court’s schedule. A notice of appeal sent to an appellate court instead of the trial court is forwarded and treated as filed on the date the appellate court received it. Utah R. App. P. 44","#st-ut-appeals"],["Notice of appeal: 30 days from entry, filed in the trial court","Utah · appeals · Starting the appeal","File the notice with the trial court clerk within 30 days after entry of the judgment or order (10 days in forcible entry and unlawful detainer cases; 21 days from an order denying, in whole or part, an anti-SLAPP motion under § 78B-25-103). If the judgment is entered on a weekend or holiday, entry is deemed the next business day. The notice must name the appealing parties, the courts, and the judgment, and say wheth","#st-ut-appeals"],["When is a judgment “entered”?","Utah · appeals · Starting the appeal","A judgment must be in a separate document, and it is entered when the judge signs it and it is recorded in the docket, or 150 days after the decision is docketed if no separate document is entered. A pending fee motion does not affect finality, but the appeal clock runs from its disposition (see tolling). Utah R. Civ. P. 58A(a), (e), (f)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Filing fee and $300 cost bond","Utah · appeals · Starting the appeal","Pay the appellate filing fee to the trial court clerk with the notice (the clerk must accept an unpaid notice, but an unpaid fee can lead to dismissal). File a cost bond of at least $300 with the notice unless the adverse party waives it in writing, fees are waived, the appeal is criminal, or the appellant is a government entity or officer; a supersedeas bond replaces it. Utah R. App. P. 3(f), 6, 21(b)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Docketing statement within 21 days","Utah · appeals · Starting the appeal","Within 21 days after the notice of appeal (or cross-appeal or petition for review), file and serve a docketing statement showing jurisdiction (key dates, any tolling motions, any Rule 54(b) certification and what remains below) and at least one substantial issue, without argument. Failure can lead to dismissal of a civil appeal. None is filed after an interlocutory petition is granted unless ordered. Utah R. App. P. ","#st-ut-appeals"],["Transcripts and the record","Utah · appeals · Starting the appeal","Within 21 days after filing the docketing statement, order transcripts online at utcourts.gov and serve a designation, or file a certificate that none are needed. The appellee may cross-designate within 14 days. If you challenge a finding as unsupported, you must include all evidence relevant to it. Transcripts are due 30 days after payment arrangements; the trial court sends the Bates-paginated record within 20 days","#st-ut-appeals"],["Stays pending appeal","Utah · appeals · Starting the appeal","No execution for 28 days after judgment. A supersedeas bond in the presumptive amount (compensatory damages plus costs, fees, and 3 years of interest; capped at $25 million in class or aggregate cases; none for punitive damages) stays a money judgment. Ask the trial court first; an appellate motion must explain why relief was denied below or what extraordinary circumstances excuse asking. The appellate court ordinari","#st-ut-appeals"],["Motions that restart the clock","Utah · appeals · Tolling and extensions","A timely motion under Civil Rule 50(b), 52(b), or 59 (new trial or alter or amend), a Rule 60(b) motion filed within 28 days after judgment, or a Rule 73 attorney-fee motion or claim restarts the appeal time for all parties from entry of the order disposing of the last such motion. These post-judgment motions are due within 28 days of judgment (fees within 14 days) and the 28-day limits cannot be extended. A notice f","#st-ut-appeals"],["Extending the notice deadline","Utah · appeals · Tolling and extensions","The trial court may extend the time on a motion filed before the deadline for good cause, or within 30 days after it for good cause or excusable neglect. No extension may exceed 30 days past the original deadline or 14 days after the order granting it, whichever is later. Utah R. App. P. 4(e)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Reinstating the time when notice of judgment never arrived","Utah · appeals · Tolling and extensions","In a civil case, the trial court must reinstate the 30-day period if the party lacked actual notice of the judgment in time to seek an extension, monitored the case diligently, and the party responsible for serving the signed judgment under Civil Rule 58A did not promptly serve it. Move within one year after entry of judgment; the notice is then due 30 days after the reinstatement order. Utah R. App. P. 4(g)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Cross-appeals","Utah · appeals · Tolling and extensions","Any other party may file a notice of appeal within 14 days after the first notice is filed, or within the regular time, whichever ends later. No cross-appeal may be taken in a granted interlocutory appeal, and cross-petitions for permission to appeal are not allowed. Utah R. App. P. 4(d), 5(g), (i)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Computing time","Utah · appeals · Tolling and extensions","Exclude the trigger day and include the last day, rolling past weekends and Utah legal holidays. When a period is shorter than 11 days, intermediate Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays are excluded (unlike the Civil Rules). Add 3 days when a document is served by mail (the Civil Rules add 7). Jurisdictional deadlines listed in Rule 2 cannot be extended. Utah R. App. P. 2, 22(a), (d); compare Utah R. Civ. P. 6(c)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Briefing schedule","Utah · appeals · Briefing","Appellant’s principal brief is due 40 days after the clerk’s Rule 13 notice (sent when the record index is filed); appellee’s brief 30 days after service of appellant’s; reply 30 days after appellee’s (at least 5 days before argument if argument is set within 35 days). With a cross-appeal, the cross-appellant’s combined brief and each later brief follow in 30-day steps. If a summary-disposition or Rule 23B motion is ","#st-ut-appeals"],["Extensions by stipulation, then by motion","Utah · appeals · Briefing","Parties may extend any brief deadline by filing a notice of stipulation before the deadline, effective without an order, up to a total of 60 days per brief. Further extensions require a motion before the deadline showing specific good cause (other litigation identified by caption, complexity, or detailed hardship) and are disfavored. Utah R. App. P. 22(b)-(c)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Length and content","Utah · appeals · Briefing","Principal briefs: 14,000 words or 30 pages; reply briefs 7,000 words or 15 pages (death-penalty cases 28,000/14,000). In cross-appeals the cross-appellant’s combined brief may run 21,000 words or 45 pages. Each issue needs the standard of review and a record cite showing preservation; include an introduction, statement of the case with record cites, explicit attorney-fee requests, a certificate of compliance, and an ","#st-ut-appeals"],["Format, covers, and paper copies","Utah · appeals · Briefing","Text and footnotes in 13-point or larger type, double-spaced, 1-inch margins. Brief covers are color-coded (appellant blue, appellee red, intervenor or amicus green, reply gray). Besides e-filing, deliver 10 paper copies to the Supreme Court or 6 to the Court of Appeals within 7 days, printed double-sided on 20 lb. paper and held with a binder clip, not stapled. Utah R. App. P. 26(d), 27(a), (e)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Constitutional challenges: serve the Attorney General","Utah · appeals · Briefing","When a party challenges the constitutionality of a statute and the Attorney General has not appeared, every party must serve its briefs on the Attorney General (notices@agutah.gov) on or before filing and file proof of service; similar service goes to a governmental entity whose ordinance or rule is challenged. The Attorney General then says within 14 days whether it will file an amicus brief. Utah R. App. P. 25A","#st-ut-appeals"],["Amicus briefs","Utah · appeals · Briefing","Only a guardian ad litem, the State, another state’s Attorney General, or the United States may file without consent or leave. Others need all parties’ consent or leave on motion, and must give the parties at least 7 days’ notice of intent to file. Utah R. App. P. 25(a)-(c)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Motions practice","Utah · appeals · Briefing","Responses to motions are due within 14 days after service and replies within 5 days after the response; procedural motions may be decided at once. A motion for expedited review is limited to 5 pages, with any response due within 3 days. Utah R. App. P. 23, 23C","#st-ut-appeals"],["Summary disposition and simplified appeals","Utah · appeals · Decision, rehearing, and Supreme Court review","A party may move at any time to dismiss for lack of jurisdiction (response within 14 days). After the docketing statement, the court may summarily affirm when no substantial question is presented or reverse for manifest error; other deadlines are suspended while such a motion is pending. In the Court of Appeals, a party may ask within 10 days after the docketing statement (or transfer) for the simplified process for ","#st-ut-appeals"],["Oral argument and mediation","Utah · appeals · Decision, rehearing, and Supreme Court review","The Supreme Court holds argument unless it would not aid decision; the Court of Appeals allows argument only when it would significantly aid decision. The clerk gives at least 28 days’ notice; Court of Appeals parties may waive within 14 days of the notice. The court may order appellate mediation, which stays all appellate deadlines while pending. Utah R. App. P. 28A(d), 29","#st-ut-appeals"],["Rehearing: 14 days, response only on request","Utah · appeals · Decision, rehearing, and Supreme Court review","A petition for rehearing (15 pages, certifying good faith) may be filed within 14 days after an opinion or merits order, stating the points of law or fact overlooked or misapprehended. No response is allowed unless the court requests one, and the court will not grant rehearing without requesting one. Consecutive or late petitions are rejected; clerical errors are raised by letter. Utah R. App. P. 35","#st-ut-appeals"],["Certiorari to the Supreme Court","Utah · appeals · Decision, rehearing, and Supreme Court review","Review of a Court of Appeals decision is discretionary, granted mainly for questions with significant precedential value. File the petition within 30 days after the Court of Appeals’ decision (not the remittitur), or after denial of a timely rehearing petition; the limit is 4,000 words or 15 pages. As amended November 1, 2026, no response may be filed unless the Court requests one (then due in 30 days; 4,000 words), ","#st-ut-appeals"],["Remittitur and costs","Utah · appeals · Decision, rehearing, and Supreme Court review","The Supreme Court issues its remittitur 15 days after judgment (5 days after a rehearing ruling); the Court of Appeals issues it when the certiorari period expires, or 5 days after certiorari is denied. Costs (record, transcripts, printing, bond premiums, filing fees) go to the prevailing party in civil appeals; file a verified bill of costs in the trial court within 14 days after the remittitur is filed there, and o","#st-ut-appeals"],["Citing unpublished decisions","Utah · appeals · Decision, rehearing, and Supreme Court review","Published opinions of either court, and Court of Appeals unpublished decisions issued from October 1, 1998 through December 31, 2010, are precedent. Other unpublished decisions may be cited only if accurate copies are given to the court and all parties when first cited. Decisions by order are not precedent. Utah R. App. P. 30(e), (g)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Petition for permission to appeal: 21 days","Utah · appeals · Interlocutory and extraordinary review","File a petition with the appellate court within 21 days after entry of the trial court’s signed order (since May 1, 2026 the order must be signed, and if a party was told to prepare an order, the clock starts only when that signed order is entered). Limit 20 pages; show preservation, the standard of review, why immediate review is warranted, and how it may materially advance termination. No response unless the court ","#st-ut-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP denials are appealable as of right","Utah · appeals · Interlocutory and extraordinary review","An order denying an anti-SLAPP special motion in whole or in part may be appealed as of right by the moving party, within 21 days after entry. A stay of proceedings between the parties lasts until the order and the time to appeal pass, and during an appeal all proceedings between all parties are stayed until the appeal concludes, except fee motions and listed matters. Utah Code §§ 78B-25-104, -109; Utah R. App. P. 4(","#st-ut-appeals"],["Extraordinary relief in the appellate courts","Utah · appeals · Interlocutory and extraordinary review","When no plain, speedy, and adequate remedy exists (including interlocutory review), a petition for extraordinary relief may be filed in an appellate court, explaining why it is not filed in the trial court. Limit 20 pages or 7,000 words (reply 10 pages or 3,500 words). No response unless requested (then 30 days); a reply is due in 14 days. A single justice or judge may deny a facially frivolous petition, reviewable o","#st-ut-appeals"],["Certified questions from federal courts","Utah · appeals · Interlocutory and extraordinary review","The Utah Supreme Court may answer questions of Utah law certified by any federal court when the question is controlling and no controlling Utah law appears. Utah R. App. P. 41; Utah Code § 78A-3-102(1)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Appellate e-filing is mandatory for attorneys","Utah · appeals · Filing mechanics","All licensed attorneys must file through the appellate e-filing system; unrepresented parties may file by email (supremecourt@utcourts.gov or courtofappeals@utcourts.gov), mail, or in person. Files must be searchable PDFs of no more than 7 MB each. E-filed documents are timely if received by 11:59 p.m. on the due date; briefs are also timely if postmarked by the due date. E-filers pay fees at filing. A redacted publi","#st-ut-appeals"],["Pro hac vice on appeal","Utah · appeals · Filing mechanics","An out-of-state lawyer admitted pro hac vice in the trial court files a notice of appearance in the appellate court; no new Bar application is needed. Utah Sup. Ct. R. Prof. Prac. 14-806(p)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (civil, as of right)","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the judgment or order (signed and docketed).\nJurisdictional; filed with the trial court clerk. Restarts after a timely Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59, 73, or 28-day Rule 60(b) motion is decided. Utah R. App. P. 4(a)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Notice of appeal, forcible entry or unlawful detainer","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of the judgment or order.\nJurisdictional. Utah R. App. P. 4(a)(1)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Notice of appeal, order denying anti-SLAPP motion","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from entry of the order denying the § 78B-25-103 motion in whole or part.\nAppeal of right for the moving party. Utah R. App. P. 4(a)(2); Utah Code § 78B-25-109","#st-ut-appeals"],["Cross-appeal or additional notice","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or the regular period, if later). Utah R. App. P. 4(d)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Motion to extend notice deadline (excusable neglect)","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from expiration of the original appeal period.\nExtension capped at 30 days past the deadline or 14 days after the order, whichever is later. Utah R. App. P. 4(e)(2)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Motion to reinstate appeal time (no notice of judgment)","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","365 days from entry of judgment.\nStated as “within one year”; notice of appeal then due 30 days after the reinstatement order. Utah R. App. P. 4(g)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Post-judgment motions that toll (Rules 50(b), 52(b), 59)","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from entry of judgment.\nNot extendable. Rule 60(b) tolls only if filed within 28 days. Utah R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e); 6(b)(2)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Attorney-fee motion","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment.\nAppeal time runs from disposition of the fee motion or claim (Utah R. App. P. 4(b)(1)(F)). Utah R. Civ. P. 73(a)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Petition for permission to appeal (interlocutory)","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from entry of the trial court’s signed order resolving the motion or issue.\nJurisdictional; 20-page limit; filed in the appellate court. Utah R. App. P. 5(a)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Docketing statement","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nFailure may result in dismissal of a civil appeal. Utah R. App. P. 9(b)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Order transcripts (or certify none)","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from filing of the docketing statement.\nAppellee cross-designation within 14 days after service. Utah R. App. P. 11(c)(1)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Appellant’s principal brief","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from clerk’s Rule 13 briefing notice.\nUp to 60 days total by notice of stipulation (Rule 22(b)). Utah R. App. P. 26(a)(1)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Appellee’s principal brief","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of appellant’s principal brief. Utah R. App. P. 26(a)(2)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Reply brief","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing and service of appellee’s principal brief.\nAt least 5 days before argument if argument is set within 35 days. Utah R. App. P. 26(a)(3)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Paper copies of e-filed briefs","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from filing.\n10 copies (Supreme Court) or 6 (Court of Appeals). Utah R. App. P. 26(d)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Response to a motion","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the motion.\nReply within 5 days after the response. Utah R. App. P. 23(b)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Retention request after notice of intent to transfer","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from issuance of the Supreme Court’s notice of intent to transfer.\nResponse within 5 days. Utah R. App. P. 42(c)(3)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from issuance of the opinion or merits decision.\n15 pages; no response unless requested. Utah R. App. P. 35(a)(2)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Petition for writ of certiorari","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from court of Appeals’ final decision (or denial of timely rehearing).\nJurisdictional; one extension of up to 30 days available under Rule 48(e). Utah R. App. P. 48(a), (c)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Response to certiorari petition (only if requested)","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from order requesting a response.\nReply within 7 days after the response. Utah R. App. P. 50(a)(1)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Bill of costs","Utah · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the remittitur with the trial court clerk.\nObjections within 7 days after service. Utah R. App. P. 34(d)","#st-ut-appeals"],["Supreme Court of Virginia","Virginia · courts","Court of last resort. Since January 1, 2022 it hears civil appeals mainly by discretionary petition for appeal from the Court of Appeals (Code §§ 8.01-670, 17.1-411); appeals still lie directly to it from habeas corpus decisions, collateral attacks on criminal convictions, the State Corporation Commission, and attorney-discipline proceedings under §§ 54.1-3935 and 54.1-3937 (Code § 17.1-406(B)). It also decides 15-da","#st-va"],["Court of Appeals of Virginia","Virginia · courts","Hears appeals of right from any final judgment of a circuit court in a civil matter (other than the § 17.1-406(B) categories), from administrative-agency appeals decided by a circuit court, from the Virginia Workers’ Compensation Commission, and from criminal convictions, plus listed interlocutory orders (equitable orders requiring payment or adjudicating the principles of a cause, and § 8.01-675.5 certified question","#st-va"],["Circuit Courts","Virginia · courts","General-jurisdiction courts of record. Civil practice is governed by Parts One, Three and Four of the Rules of Supreme Court of Virginia and Title 8.01 of the Code. Electronic filing is available only in courts that have set up a system, and a civil case becomes an Electronically Filed Case only with all parties’ consent (Rules 1:17, 3:3). Circuit courts may adopt local rules, which must be entered in the order book,","#st-va"],["General District Courts","Virginia · courts","Courts not of record for smaller civil claims, traffic and misdemeanor cases, governed by Parts Seven A–C of the Rules. A party who timely perfects an appeal to circuit court may take a nonsuit there, which annuls the district court judgment (Code § 8.01-380(F)). Jurisdictional amount limits are statutory and were not collected.","#st-va"],["Juvenile and Domestic Relations District Courts","Virginia · courts","Courts not of record for juvenile, family, custody, support and protective-order matters, governed by Part Eight of the Rules. Rule 1:5(f)(5) allows a qualified legal services lawyer a one-hearing limited appearance in these courts and in general district court.","#st-va"],["Virginia’s anti-SLAPP law is an immunity statute, not a special motion to strike: Code § 8.01-223.2","Virginia · anti-SLAPP","A person is immune from tort liability if the claim is based solely on statements (i) on matters of public concern that would be protected by the First Amendment and are communicated to a third party; (ii) made at a public hearing before, or otherwise communicated to, a local governing body or its boards, commissions, agencies and authorities on matters properly before it; (iii) made at a Title IX hearing at a colleg","#st-va-slapp"],["No special motion, no discovery stay, no statutory deadline","Virginia · anti-SLAPP","The statute sets out no special motion to strike, no filing deadline, no automatic stay of discovery, and no expedited hearing. The immunity has to be raised through ordinary Virginia procedure, for example a demurrer, plea in bar, or summary-judgment motion (Rules 3:8, 3:20), or a motion to quash a subpoena. Keep in mind that Rule 3:20 bars summary judgment based on discovery depositions unless an exception applies.","#st-va-slapp"],["Fees: discretionary award to the prevailing defendant or witness","Virginia · anti-SLAPP","A person who has a suit dismissed, has a witness subpoena or subpoena duces tecum quashed, or otherwise prevails under the immunity “may be awarded” reasonable attorney fees and costs. The award is discretionary, not mandatory. Under Rule 3:25, a fee claim generally has to be demanded in a pleading; whether that rule reaches a defendant’s § 8.01-223.2 fee request was not resolved from the collected text, so plead it.","#st-va-slapp"],["Appeal: no interlocutory appeal specific to anti-SLAPP rulings","Virginia · anti-SLAPP","The collected statutes give no interlocutory appeal for the denial of § 8.01-223.2 immunity. The 15-day petition for review under § 8.01-670.2 covers only pleas of sovereign, absolute, or qualified immunity that would spare the movant from participating in the case. Whether § 8.01-223.2 immunity counts as one of those was not determined from the collected text; otherwise review comes after final judgment, or by certi","#st-va-slapp"],["Pro hac vice under Rule 1A:4: Virginia local counsel must sign everything and attend everything","Virginia · out of state","An out-of-state lawyer currently licensed in another U.S. state, territory or possession may appear in a particular case before any Virginia court, board or agency only after associating with an active Virginia State Bar member as local counsel. Local counsel’s name must appear on all filed documents; local counsel must personally appear and take part in every pretrial conference, hearing and trial; and local counsel","#st-va-oos"],["Application: notarized form, $250 per case, motion by local counsel","Virginia · out of state","The out-of-state lawyer gives local counsel an original notarized application on the Supreme Court’s form and a non-refundable $250 fee, payable to the Clerk of the Supreme Court of Virginia, for each separate case. Local counsel files a motion to associate with proof of service, the application, a proposed order and the fee. The out-of-state lawyer may not appear until the order is entered, although the tribunal may","#st-va-oos"],["Twelve-case limit in 12 months","Virginia · out of state","The tribunal should deny the motion if the lawyer has been admitted pro hac vice in 12 cases anywhere in Virginia in the 12 months before the application. It may take related or consolidated matters into account, and it checks the count with the Supreme Court before ruling. Admitted lawyers must report disciplinary developments promptly and are subject to Virginia discipline as if they were bar members. Rule 1A:4(5),","#st-va-oos"],["Appellate argument also needs Rule 1A:4 admission","Virginia · out of state","A lawyer not licensed in Virginia who wants to present oral argument in the Court of Appeals must comply with Rule 1A:4. Rule 5A:28(c)","#st-va-oos"],["Work allowed without pro hac vice admission","Virginia · out of state","Subject to Rule 5.5 of the Virginia Rules of Professional Conduct, an out-of-state lawyer may, without a pro hac vice order: work in Virginia on a proceeding pending elsewhere in which the lawyer is admitted; if the lawyer reasonably expects to qualify for pro hac vice, consult with Virginia counsel, talk with a prospective client about retention, and prepare a potential Virginia case; and prepare for and take part i","#st-va-oos"],["Other routes: admission without exam (amended effective January 8, 2027), corporate counsel, military spouses","Virginia · out of state","Part One A also covers admission without examination for experienced lawyers from reciprocal jurisdictions (Rule 1A:1), Virginia corporate counsel and corporate counsel registrants (Rule 1A:5), military legal assistance attorneys (1A:6), foreign legal consultants (1A:7), military servicemembers and spouses (1A:8), and legal aid counsel (1A:9). An Order of November 9, 2026 amends Rule 1A:1 effective January 8, 2027. T","#st-va-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances","Virginia · out of state","A lawyer employed by a qualified legal services provider, or acting pro bono on a direct referral from one, may file and serve a notice of limited scope appearance in any civil proceeding. Any other lawyer needs leave of court. During the limited appearance, papers must be served on both the lawyer and the party. The lawyer leaves by filing a notice of completion endorsed by the client, or by a motion to terminate wi","#st-va-oos"],["E-filing access: circuit-court portal remote access is limited to Virginia State Bar members","Virginia · out of state","Remote electronic access to documents in an Electronically Filed Case is limited to judges and court staff, active members of the Virginia State Bar and their authorized agents, and registered pro se parties. Pro hac vice counsel should plan to work through local counsel’s account. Appellate filings go through VACES, where lawyers must e-file in PDF and electronically serve opposing counsel (Rules 5:1B, 5A:1(c)). The","#st-va-oos"],["Governing code: Virginia Rules of Professional Conduct (Rules of Court, Part Six, § II)","Virginia · ethics","The Rules of Professional Conduct are Part Six, § II of the Rules of Supreme Court of Virginia, but the Supreme Court’s compilation points to the Virginia State Bar’s website for the text. The collected Bar compilation is dated July 27, 2026 (reflecting the Rule 1.9 amendment effective that day). The rules follow ABA Model Rules numbering, but many ABA comments are marked “not adopted”, and each rule carries a “Virgi","#st-va-ethics"],["Confidentiality: narrower definition, and mandatory disclosure of some client crimes","Virginia · ethics","Rule 1.6(a) protects privileged information and other information gained in the representation that the client asked to keep confidential or whose disclosure would embarrass or likely harm the client. That is narrower than the ABA’s “information relating to the representation”. Rule 1.6(c) requires the lawyer to promptly reveal a client’s stated intention to commit a crime reasonably certain to cause death, substanti","#st-va-ethics"],["Fees: nonrefundable advance fees are banned; contingent-fee writing need not be client-signed","Virginia · ethics","Rule 1.5(g) prohibits nonrefundable advanced legal fees. A contingent-fee agreement must “state in writing” the method, percentages for settlement, trial or appeal, and how expenses are handled. The text does not require the client’s signature, unlike ABA Model Rule 1.5(c). Fee division between lawyers in different firms requires client consent to the participation of all lawyers and to the terms of the division, obt","#st-va-ethics"],["No screening to cure lateral-hire conflicts","Virginia · ethics","Virginia Rule 1.10(a) imputes a lawyer’s Rule 1.6, 1.7, 1.9 or 2.10(e) conflict to everyone in the firm. Unlike ABA Model Rule 1.10(a)(2), it has no exception for a personally disqualified lateral lawyer who is timely screened; the remedy is waiver by the affected client under Rule 1.7 (1.10(c)). Screening appears only for former government lawyers (1.10(e), 1.11). Va. RPC 1.10(a), (c), (e)","#st-va-ethics"],["Former clients: 2026 amendment on criminal-history information","Virginia · ethics","Rule 1.9(c)(1) bars using a former client’s information to the former client’s disadvantage. The exceptions are what Rules 1.6 or 3.3 allow, information that has become generally known, and information that consists solely of criminal history obtainable from law-enforcement databases or court records. The compilation is marked effective July 27, 2026 for Rule 1.9. That the criminal-history clause is the July 2026 cha","#st-va-ethics"],["Threatening criminal or disciplinary charges to gain a civil advantage is prohibited","Virginia · ethics","Rule 3.4(i) forbids presenting or threatening to present criminal or disciplinary charges solely to obtain an advantage in a civil matter. The ABA Model Rules have no comparable black-letter rule. Rule 3.4(j) separately bars filing suit or taking other action that the lawyer knows, or that is obvious, would serve merely to harass or maliciously injure. Va. RPC 3.4(i)–(j)","#st-va-ethics"],["Misconduct: no ABA 8.4(g); bar on agreements limiting bar complaints","Virginia · ethics","Virginia Rule 8.4 has no counterpart to ABA Model Rule 8.4(g) on harassment or discrimination. Rule 8.4(b) reaches criminal or “deliberately wrongful” acts reflecting on honesty or fitness, and 8.4(c) reaches dishonesty, fraud, deceit or misrepresentation that reflects adversely on fitness to practice. Rule 8.4(f), effective March 4, 2024 per the amendment list, forbids agreements limiting a client’s or former client","#st-va-ethics"],["Trial publicity rule covers only criminal jury matters","Virginia · ethics","Rule 3.6 restricts extrajudicial statements only by lawyers involved in the investigation, prosecution or defense of a criminal matter that may be tried by a jury. The standard is a statement the lawyer knows or should know will have a substantial likelihood of interfering with the fairness of the jury trial. Unlike ABA Model Rule 3.6, it does not reach civil cases. Va. RPC 3.6","#st-va-ethics"],["Mediator and third-party neutral rules not in the ABA set","Virginia · ethics","Virginia keeps Rules 2.10 (third-party neutral) and 2.11 (mediator), which the Committee adopted outside the ABA Model Rules, alongside Rule 2.4. Rule 2.2 (intermediary) is also retained. Va. RPC 2.2, 2.4, 2.10, 2.11","#st-va-ethics"],["Post-trial motions do not extend the 21-day finality window or the 30-day appeal clock","Virginia · land mine","A final judgment leaves the trial court’s control 21 days after the judge signs it. Filing a motion for a new trial or reconsideration does not extend that time or the time to note an appeal. Only an order that actually modifies, vacates or suspends the judgment within the 21 days does, and appeal time then runs from the new final judgment. Get a suspending order entered, not just a motion filed. Rule 1:1(a); Rule 5A","#st-va-unique"],["Notice of appeal goes to the trial court clerk within 30 days, with the $50 fee and an appeal bond","Virginia · land mine","The notice of appeal to the Court of Appeals must be filed with the clerk of the circuit court within 30 days after entry of the final judgment. A copy goes to the Court of Appeals clerk with the $50 fee (the appeal is dismissed if the fee does not arrive within 10 days). In an appeal of right in a civil case, a $500 appeal bond (or the amount the trial court sets) must be filed simultaneously. The bond is not jurisd","#st-va-unique"],["Transcripts are due in the trial court within 60 days of judgment","Virginia · land mine","A transcript becomes part of the record only if filed in the circuit court clerk’s office within 60 days after entry of final judgment. The Court of Appeals can extend that only on a written motion filed within 90 days after entry. Notice of filing the transcript is due to all counsel within 10 days. Without needed transcripts, the assignments of error may not be reachable. Rule 5A:8(a)–(b)","#st-va-unique"],["Preliminary assignments of error are due 15 days after the record is filed","Virginia · land mine","In an appeal of right, the appellant must file a preliminary statement of assignments of error within 15 days after the record is filed in the Court of Appeals, and the appellee files any preliminary assignments of cross-error within 10 days after that. The opening brief is due 40 days after the record is filed. Assignments of error must point to exact record pages where each error was preserved; a bare “contrary to ","#st-va-unique"],["Preserve every objection with reasonable certainty at the time of the ruling","Virginia · land mine","No ruling will be considered as a basis for reversal unless an objection was stated with reasonable certainty at the time, except for good cause or to attain the ends of justice. A general statement that the judgment is contrary to the law and the evidence preserves nothing. Rule 5A:18; Rule 5:25","#st-va-unique"],["Pro hac vice papers are invalid without local counsel’s signature","Virginia · land mine","Any pleading or paper that must be served, including discovery, is invalid unless signed by Virginia local counsel. Local counsel must personally attend every proceeding. The first pleading in a court of record must give a Virginia State Bar number, and a signature by anyone other than an active VSB member or pro se party is not valid. A signature defect must be cured within 21 days after it is raised, or the paper i","#st-va-unique"],["Jury demand within 10 days after the last pleading","Virginia · land mine","A party who wants a jury must demand it in the complaint or serve and file a written demand within 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue. Amended pleadings do not revive the right unless the order says so. A defaulting defendant waives jury trial. Rules 3:21(b), (d); 3:19(a)","#st-va-unique"],["No summary judgment on discovery depositions","Virginia · land mine","A motion for summary judgment or to strike the evidence cannot be sustained if based in whole or in part on discovery depositions under Rule 4:5. The exceptions are all parties agreeing, or the motion coming within § 8.01-420(B)–(C), which allows depositions and affidavits when the only parties are business entities and $50,000 or more is at issue. Summary judgment is decided on the pleadings, pretrial orders and adm","#st-va-unique"],["Answer in 21 days, no general denial, no serial motions","Virginia · land mine","Responsive pleadings are due 21 days after service (60 days after a timely waiver request, 90 if sent outside Virginia). Answers must respond paragraph by paragraph. After the first response, further demurrers, pleas or motions need leave of court. The time to move for a change of venue cannot be extended except as § 8.01-264 allows. Rules 3:8(a)–(b), 1:9","#st-va-unique"],["Leave to amend from the bench starts a 21-day clock that can end the case","Virginia · land mine","There is no amendment as of right. When leave is granted other than on a written motion (for example, when a demurrer is sustained with leave to amend), the amended pleading is due within 21 days unless the court sets another time. If it is not filed in time, the demurrer order becomes a final disposition of the claim and the 30-day appeal clock can start running. Rules 1:8, 1:1(c)","#st-va-unique"],["Fee claims must be pleaded","Virginia · land mine","Except for § 8.01-271.1 sanctions and certain domestic-relations fees, a party seeking attorney fees must demand them in a complaint, counterclaim, cross-claim, third-party claim or responsive pleading, stating the basis. Otherwise the claim is waived unless leave to amend is granted. Rule 3:25","#st-va-unique"],["Nonsuit is a right, but only once and only before submission","Virginia · land mine","A plaintiff may take one nonsuit as of right before a motion to strike is sustained, the jury retires, or the case is submitted to the court. The plaintiff may then refile within six months or the remaining limitations period, whichever is longer, generally in the same court. Defendants should expect it: expert fees can be shifted only if notice comes within 7 days of trial, and a nonsuit cannot defeat a related coun","#st-va-unique"],["No peremptory substitution of judge; recusal under Canon 1D","Virginia · judges","The collected Rules of Court provide no peremptory challenge or automatic substitution of a trial judge. A judge must recuse in any proceeding in which the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned. Listed examples include personal bias or personal knowledge of disputed facts, prior service as a lawyer in the matter, having presided over it in another court, an economic interest of the judge or listed famil","#st-va-unique"],["Remittal of disqualification","Virginia · judges","For any ground other than personal bias or prejudice concerning a party, the judge may have the parties and lawyers consider waiver outside the judge’s presence. If all agree and the judge is willing, the judge may sit, and the written agreement goes into the record. The identity of anyone who declines to waive is not to be told to the judge. Disclosure of past professional associations ended more than one year earli","#st-va-unique"],["Canon 1 amendment pending","Virginia · judges","The Supreme Court’s amendment list shows an order amending Canon 1 effective December 7, 2026. That order was not collected; check it before relying on Canon 1D. Supreme Court of Virginia, Amendment Orders page (Canon 1, eff. Dec. 7, 2026)","#st-va-unique"],["Immediate Supreme Court review of court-closure orders (2026)","Virginia · speech","New Code § 8.01-670.3 (2026, c. 366) makes immediately reviewable any circuit court order that grants or denies a motion to close a proceeding not otherwise authorized to be closed, closes one on its own motion, or affirms or reverses a district court’s closure order. Any aggrieved person may petition the Supreme Court within 15 days under § 8.01-626. Rule 1:1C, amended effective September 15, 2026, gives the appella","#st-va-unique"],["Anti-SLAPP immunity extends to Title IX hearings and protected employee statements","Virginia · speech","Beyond public-concern speech and statements to local governing bodies, § 8.01-223.2 immunity covers statements made at a Title IX hearing at an institution of higher education and statements by an employee against an employer where retaliation is barred by § 40.1-27.3. Statements made with knowledge of falsity or reckless disregard are not covered. Va. Code § 8.01-223.2(A)(iii)–(iv), (B)","#st-va-unique"],["Lawyer speech: trial publicity limits apply only in criminal jury matters","Virginia · speech","Virginia Rule 3.6 does not restrict lawyers’ public statements about civil cases; it reaches only criminal matters that may be tried by a jury. Va. RPC 3.6","#st-va-unique"],["Civil procedure is split between the Rules of Court and Title 8.01","Virginia · unique rules","Pleading, discovery and motion practice are in Parts One, Three and Four of the Rules of Supreme Court of Virginia. Nonsuit, limitations and tolling, service timing, signature requirements, sanctions, appeal bonds and appellate jurisdiction are statutory (Title 8.01 and Title 17.1). Virginia uses demurrers, pleas in bar and motions craving oyer rather than Federal Rule 12 motions. Rules 3:8, 1:4; Va. Code §§ 8.01-271","#st-va-unique"],["Rules in flux: orders effective January 8, 2027","Virginia · unique rules","An Order of November 9, 2026 amends Rules 4:12 (new sanctions for failing to supplement discovery unless substantially justified or harmless), 4:15 (closure-motion notice), 5:17A and 5A:12 (a petition under § 8.01-675.5 must address each certification factor or may be denied). A separate order amends Rule 1A:1. A pending call for comment proposes requiring email service of pleadings in courts of record (new Rule 1:12","#st-va-unique"],["Rule NOTE. What this file covers","Virginia · civil rules","Virginia civil procedure in the circuit courts lives in Parts One (rules for all proceedings), Three (civil actions) and Four (discovery and pretrial) of the Rules of Supreme Court of Virginia, plus Title 8.01 of the Code of Virginia (Civil Remedies and Procedure), which is statutory and is not summarized rule by rule here. Key statutes to read alongside the rules: Code § 8.01-271.1 (signing and sanctions; amended 20","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:1. Finality of Judgments, Orders and Decrees","Virginia · civil rules","A final judgment stays under the trial court’s control, and can be modified, vacated or suspended, for 21 days after entry and no longer; the entry date is the date the judge signs it (on paper or electronically). A judgment is final if it disposes of all claims against all parties and leaves only ministerial execution; an order sustaining a demurrer or plea in bar, or granting summary judgment, disposes of the claim","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:1A. Recovery of Appellate Attorney Fees in Circuit Court","Virginia · civil rules","A prevailing appellee who recovered attorney fees or costs in circuit court under a contract, statute or other law may apply to that circuit court for fees and costs incurred on appeal by filing the application and a copy of the final appellate judgment within 30 days after the final appellate judgment (the mandate, or the final appellate order if no mandate issues; for a petition under Rule 5:17, the later of the de","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:1B. Jurisdictional Transfer During Appeal of Final or Partial Final Judgment in Circuit Court","Virginia · civil rules","Filing a notice of appeal gives the appellate court jurisdiction, but if it is filed within Rule 1:1’s 21 days the circuit court keeps full concurrent jurisdiction until day 21 (a vacatur in that window moots the notice, and a new notice is needed from any later final judgment). After day 21 the circuit court keeps only limited jurisdiction, for example over stays, appeal and suspending bonds under Code § 8.01-676.1,","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:1C. Jurisdictional Transfer During Appeal of Interlocutory Orders","Virginia · civil rules","When an interlocutory order is appealed under Code §§ 8.01-626, 8.01-670.2, 8.01-670.3, 17.1-405(A)(4) or 17.1-405(A)(5), the appellate court has exclusive jurisdiction over that order while the circuit court keeps the rest of the case unless either court stays proceedings. On motion for good cause, the appellate court may let the circuit court withdraw or modify the order (last amended effective September 15, 2026).","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:2. Appeal From Partial Final Judgment in Multi-Party Cases","Virginia · civil rules","In a multi-party civil action the court may enter final judgment as to fewer than all parties only by an order expressly labeled “Partial Final Judgment” with findings that the claims against those parties are separate and distinct and that the appeal and the remaining claims cannot affect each other. Entry of that order starts the time to note an appeal under Rule 5A:6; refusal to enter one is not appealable, and an","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:3. Reporters and Transcripts of Proceedings in Courts","Virginia · civil rules","Court reporters must be sworn and are subject to the judge’s control. Any interested person may obtain a transcript on terms the judge sets, and proceedings may be recorded by any device the judge approves.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:4. General Provisions as to Pleadings","Virginia · civil rules","Counsel’s tender of a pleading certifies good faith and no purpose of delay; pleadings must be signed with an address, state facts in numbered paragraphs, and need only clearly inform the other side of the true nature of the claim or defense (brevity is “enjoined”). Allegations not denied when a responsive pleading is required are deemed admitted, denials must fairly meet the substance, and pleading lack of knowledge","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:5. Counsel and Parties Appearing Without Counsel","Virginia · civil rules","A firm signature must include at least one individual lawyer’s signature (electronic or digital-image signatures count for Code § 8.01-271.1), service on one firm member is service on the firm, and “counsel of record” includes anyone who signed a pleading, gave written notice of appearance, or endorsed a draft order; self-represented parties must sign every paper, state an address, and file and update a residence and","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:5A. Signature Defects","Virginia · civil rules","A paper not signed as Code § 8.01-271.1(A) requires is defective and voidable; the defect must be raised in the trial court before its jurisdiction ends under Rules 1:1 and 1:1B (or in the appellate court for appellate filings) or it is waived. The defect must be cured within 21 days after it is brought to the filer’s attention, in which case the paper relates back; otherwise it is stricken, and the court may award f","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:6. Res Judicata Claim Preclusion","Virginia · civil rules","A final judgment on the merits bars any later civil action against the same parties (including those in privity) on any claim arising from the same conduct, transaction or occurrence, whatever the legal theory, elements, evidence or remedy, and this covers claims in complaints, counterclaims, cross-claims and third-party pleadings. It applies to judgments in civil actions commenced after July 1, 2006, and does not ba","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:7. Computation of Response Dates","Virginia · civil rules","When a deadline runs from service of a paper on counsel, no days are added for hand delivery, same-day courier, fax or email completed by 5:00 p.m.; one day is added for next-day commercial delivery or for hand delivery, fax or email completed after 5:00 p.m. but before midnight; and three days are added for service by mail. In Parts Five and Five A this applies only to the time to file a brief in opposition.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:8. Amendments","Virginia · civil rules","No pleading may be amended after filing except by leave of court, which should be liberally granted; a written motion for leave must attach the proposed amended pleading, which is deemed filed on the date of the order granting leave. Where leave is granted other than on a written motion (for example, on sustaining a demurrer), the amended pleading must be filed within 21 days after leave is granted unless the court s","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:9. Discretion of Court","Virginia · civil rules","The court may review and correct all clerk’s-office steps in maturing a case, and may extend the time to file pleadings even after it has expired. The time to file a motion objecting to venue may never be extended except as Code § 8.01-264 permits.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:10. Verification","Virginia · civil rules","If a statute requires a pleading to be sworn or supported by affidavit and it is not, but it contains the required allegations, an objection must be made by motion to strike within seven days after the pleading is filed or it is waived. The pleading may be sworn or the affidavit filed before the court rules on the motion or within any time the court allows.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:11. Motion to Strike the Evidence","Virginia · civil rules","If the court sustains a motion to strike the evidence in a jury trial, it should enter summary judgment or partial summary judgment consistent with that ruling. If the motion was overruled and the jury hangs, the movant may renew it immediately after the jury is discharged, and the court should enter judgment if it concludes it erred.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:12. Service of Papers after the Initial Process","Virginia · civil rules","Papers after initial process (and requests for subpoenas duces tecum) must be served on each counsel of record on or before the day of filing by hand delivery, same-day or next-day commercial delivery, fax, mail, or email when Rule 1:17 applies or the person served has consented in writing; service is effective on delivery, dispatch, transmission or mailing, but email service fails if the sender learns it did not arr","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:13. Endorsements","Virginia · civil rules","Draft orders and decrees must be endorsed by counsel of record, or reasonable notice of the time and place of presentation, with copies, must be served under Rule 1:12 on counsel who have not endorsed them. The court may modify or dispense with this, and in electronically filed cases endorsements and objections may be handled under Rule 1:17.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:14. Preservation of the Record","Virginia · civil rules","A court may authorize electronic or photographic means to preserve the record or parts of it.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:15. Local Rules of Court","Virginia · civil rules","A circuit court’s local rules must be entered in the order book, posted in the clerk’s office, filed with the Executive Secretary and furnished to regular practitioners; a lawyer appearing in an unfamiliar circuit is responsible for learning and following its local rules. Local praecipe and pre-trial jury-instruction rules must substantially follow the Part One appendix forms (the instruction form requires proposed i","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:16. Filing Format and Procedure","Virginia · civil rules","Except in electronically filed cases, filed papers must be on 8 1/2 by 11 inch pages and typed material double spaced except quotations (oversized tables, charts, plats and photos excepted), and transcripts must be filed one transcript page per page, never condensed. The clerk may not refuse a noncompliant paper but may require a corrected substitute certified as identical in content.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:17. Electronic Filing and Service","Virginia · civil rules","Governs courts with electronic filing systems: filings in an Electronically Filed Case must be electronic PDF documents unless otherwise provided, are deemed filed when received by the e-filing portal at any hour (but receipt on a weekend, holiday or closed day counts as filed the next open day), and portal outages during the last filing hours of a business day are treated as a clerk’s-office closing for computing de","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:18. Pretrial Scheduling Order","Virginia · civil rules","Parties may submit an agreed pretrial scheduling order, and if none is otherwise entered the court may (on request or on its own) enter the Uniform Pretrial Scheduling Order in the Part One appendix; except in domestic relations and eminent domain cases, the court may deviate from it only by agreement of all counsel or after a hearing and a finding that it does not fit the case. The uniform order requires discovery t","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:19. Pretrial Conferences","Virginia · civil rules","In addition to Rule 4:13 conferences, the court may schedule a final pretrial conference on request or on its own, and must do so in cases set for five or more trial days if any counsel asks at least 45 days before trial. The conference may be held in person, by phone or video and may address settlement, issues for trial, stipulations, limits on witnesses, pending motions in limine and jury instructions.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:20. Scheduling Civil Cases for Trial","Virginia · civil rules","Each circuit court must use one or more listed methods to set civil trials: counsel’s agreed date approved by phone or electronic communication, a Rule 1:18 written plan, a scheduling conference, or a docket call under Code § 17.1-517 (with or without a praecipe). The procedures must be available online through the Executive Secretary and in each clerk’s office.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:21. Preliminary Voir Dire Information","Virginia · civil rules","At the start of jury selection in any civil or criminal case the court must explain the purpose of voir dire and the difference between peremptory and cause challenges, summarize the case, estimate trial length, and say whether sequestration is expected.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:22. Exercise of Challenges to Prospective Jurors","Virginia · civil rules","Counsel must be given the opportunity to challenge jurors for cause outside the presence of the panel.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:23. Note Taking by Jurors","Virginia · civil rules","The court may let jurors take notes; if so, it must collect and secure them at the end of each trial day and collect and destroy them when the trial ends.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:24. Requirements for Court Payment Agreements for the Collection of Fines and Costs","Virginia · civil rules","Criminal and traffic-side rule: any defendant may enter a deferred, modified deferred or installment agreement to pay fines and costs, with installments of $25 a month or more based on ability to pay (or less if indigent), no required down payment except on a re-entry after default (capped at 10 percent if $500 or less owed, otherwise the greater of 5 percent or $50). Payments within 10 days of the due date are timel","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:25. Specialty Dockets","Virginia · civil rules","Criminal-side rule: specialty dockets are judicially monitored treatment dockets, and only three types are recognized (recovery court, veterans and behavioral health dockets); grouping cases by area of law is not a specialty docket. A circuit or district court must get Supreme Court authorization to start or continue one, new types need Supreme Court recognition, and advisory committees set operating standards while ","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:26. File Format Requirements for Pleadings, Motions, Notices, Briefs and Exhibits Sought to be Filed or Admitted in Electronic or Digital Form","Virginia · civil rules","Electronic documents made part of a court record are public unless sealed or otherwise provided by law. The filer or offeror must supply them in an unencrypted, non-proprietary, open-source format or provide the software needed to view them.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 1:27. Testimony by Audiovisual Means in Circuit Court Civil Cases","Virginia · civil rules","The court may allow live testimony by audiovisual means in any civil trial or hearing on a motion made at least 15 days before the trial or hearing (unless the court allows shorter notice), with written objections due 5 days after service of a written motion unless the court sets otherwise. The offering party must supply and pay for equipment and arrangements unless the courtroom meets Code § 19.2-3.1 standards, and ","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:1. Scope","Virginia · civil rules","There is one form of action, the civil action, covering legal and equitable claims in circuit court, including cases appealed or removed from lower courts where applicable. The rules do not apply to habeas corpus petitions, and established practice continues for matters the rules do not cover.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:2. Commencement of Civil Actions","Virginia · civil rules","A civil action is commenced by filing a complaint (or a “Petition” where statute or practice requires) in the clerk’s office, and the writ tax and clerk’s fees must be paid before a summons issues. The caption must name the court and all parties, the complaint need only ask for the specific relief sought, and every complaint seeking money damages must include an ad damnum clause stating the amount sought (amendable u","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:3. Filing of Pleadings; Return of Certain Writs","Virginia · civil rules","The clerk must file all pleadings when tendered and note the filing date; disputes over a right to file go to the court. A case may be designated an Electronically Filed Case only with consent of all parties, and certain originals (sworn pleadings, wills, contracts and deeds, settlement agreements, negotiable instruments, and others) must be flagged for retention. No writ may be returnable more than 90 days after its","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:4. Copies of Complaint","Virginia · civil rules","Unless service is waived under Code § 8.01-286.1, the plaintiff must give the clerk one paper copy of the complaint for each defendant to be served (also in electronically filed cases); physical copies of exhibits need not be served, but an electronic copy of exhibits incorporated by reference must be filed with the complaint. A shortage of copies does not affect the action’s pendency, but if copies are not furnished","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:5. The Summons","Virginia · civil rules","The summons tells the defendant that unless a written responsive pleading is filed within 21 days after service, the allegations may be taken as admitted and judgment entered by default or after evidence. Defendants may appear voluntarily or waive process (in divorce or annulment only as statutes allow), and summonses need not issue for persons under a disability except in divorce, annulment or in personam cases. No ","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:6. Proof of Service","Virginia · civil rules","Returns are made on a “Proof of Service” form, and the summons and attached complaint are served as one paper. Process servers must serve within five days after receipt and make return within 72 hours after the earliest service shown on each Proof of Service, but late service or return does not prejudice any party except as Rule 3:5 provides.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:7. Bills of Particulars","Virginia · civil rules","On a prompt motion the court may order a bill of particulars to amplify a pleading that does not give fair notice, and may strike an inadequate bill (and ultimately the underlying pleading) on a prompt motion; the order must fix the filing time. A defendant must respond to a complaint amplified by a bill of particulars within 21 days after it is filed unless relying on pleadings already filed, and responses to other ","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:8. Answers, Pleas, Demurrers and Motions","Virginia · civil rules","A defendant must file responsive pleadings within 21 days after service of the summons and complaint, or, if service was timely waived under Code § 8.01-286.1, within 60 days after the waiver request was sent (90 days if addressed outside Virginia); the only permitted non-answer responses are a demurrer (Virginia’s counterpart to a Rule 12(b)(6) motion), plea, motion to dismiss, motion for a bill of particulars, moti","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:9. Counterclaims","Virginia · civil rules","A defendant may (but need not) plead any cause of action it has against the plaintiff as a counterclaim, related or not and in any amount. It may be filed within 21 days after service (60 or 90 days after a timely waiver request, as in Rule 3:8), or, if a demurrer, plea, motion to dismiss or motion for bill of particulars was timely filed, up to 21 days after the order ruling on all of them; the plaintiff must respon","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:10. Cross-Claims","Virginia · civil rules","A defendant may plead against a co-defendant any claim growing out of matters pled in the complaint, including a claim for contribution or indemnity. It may be filed within 21 days after service on the cross-claimant (60 or 90 days after a timely waiver request), the cross-claim defendant must respond within 21 days after service, and the court may order a separate trial.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:11. Reply","Virginia · civil rules","If a pleading, motion or affirmative defense sets up new matter and expressly requests a reply, the adverse party must file a reply admitting or denying it within 21 days; without that express request, the new matter is taken as denied. Allegations in a reply are taken as denied.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:12. Joinder of Additional Parties","Virginia · civil rules","A person subject to service may be joined if complete relief cannot be given without them or their absence would impair their interest or expose parties to inconsistent obligations; a motion to join may be filed within 21 days after service of the complaint (subject to Rule 1:9). If joinder is not feasible, the court weighs four listed factors to decide whether to proceed or dismiss, and a claimant must plead the nam","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:13. Third-Party Practice","Virginia · civil rules","A defending party may file a third-party complaint against a non-party who may be liable to it for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim without leave if filed within 21 days after serving its original responsive pleading, and otherwise with leave on notice. The plaintiff may assert claims against the third-party defendant from the same transaction or occurrence within 21 days after the third-party complaint is served","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:14. Intervention","Virginia · civil rules","A new party may intervene as plaintiff or defendant only by leave of court, to assert a claim or defense germane to the subject matter. All civil rules apply to the intervention pleading except writ tax and clerk’s fees.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:14A. Intervention by the Commonwealth where constitutionality of law challenged","Virginia · civil rules","When a party challenges the constitutionality of a Virginia statute, regulation or constitutional provision and no party is represented by the Attorney General, the challenger must promptly file a notice and serve it and the filing on the Attorney General by certified or registered mail or by email to the address in the rule, and the Attorney General may intervene by motion within 30 days after service (extendable). ","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:15. Statutory Interpleader","Virginia · civil rules","Statutory interpleader proceedings follow the Part Three rules to the extent consistent with the governing statutes.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:16. New Parties","Virginia · civil rules","On the plaintiff’s motion, served under Rule 1:12 with a proposed amended complaint, the court may add a new party at any stage as justice requires. The amended complaint is deemed filed on the date of the order, Rule 3:4 copy requirements apply, no writ tax, clerk’s fee or cost deposit is required, and defendants must respond as the rules require unless the court orders otherwise.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:17. Substitution of Parties","Virginia · civil rules","If a party can no longer sue or defend because of death, disability, felony conviction, removal from office or other cause, a successor in interest may be substituted on motion; if the successor does not consent, the motion and a proposed amended pleading must be served on the successor as original process, and the successor must respond in writing within 21 days after service unless otherwise agreed or ordered. A pu","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:18. General Provisions as to Pleadings","Virginia · civil rules","All written motions, including motions for a bill of particulars and to dismiss, are pleadings. Negligence or contributory negligence may be alleged generally, contributory negligence is not a defense unless pleaded or shown by the plaintiff’s evidence, a limitations defense need not cite the specific statute, and answers, counterclaims, cross-claims, pleas, demurrers, defenses and motions may be combined in one fili","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:19. Default","Virginia · civil rules","A defendant who does not timely file a responsive pleading under Rule 3:8 is in default, is not entitled to notice of further proceedings except notice of the default-judgment motion (counsel of record still gets written notice), and waives jury trial; before judgment the court may allow a late pleading for good cause, conditioned on paying the plaintiff’s extra costs and fees. Except in divorce and annulment, the co","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:20. Motion for Summary Judgment","Virginia · civil rules","Any party may move for summary judgment once the parties are at issue (not in divorce or annulment); it is granted on the pleadings, pretrial-conference orders and admissions if the movant is entitled to judgment, but not if any material fact is genuinely disputed, and may be partial (for example, liability only). Unlike Federal Rule 56, summary judgment or a motion to strike may not rest in whole or part on discover","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:21. Jury Trial of Right","Virginia · civil rules","The constitutional and statutory right to jury trial is unchanged, and equitable claims continue to be tried by the judge. A party demands a jury in the complaint or by serving a written demand no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue and filing it with the court; other parties have 10 days after a partial demand to demand other issues, amended pleadings do not reopen the time un","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:22. Trial by Jury or by the Court","Virginia · civil rules","Issues properly demanded are tried by jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise in writing or on the record, or the court finds no jury right exists; all other issues are tried by the court. The court may seat an advisory jury on equitable claims under Code § 8.01-336(E), must use a binding jury on a plea in an equitable claim under § 8.01-336(D), may use advisory or binding juries by consent, and in mixed cases mu","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:22A. Examination of Prospective Trial Jurors (Voir Dire)","Virginia · civil rules","After the venire is sworn, the court must ask whether any juror is related to a party, works for a party, has an interest in the outcome, has outside information, has formed an opinion, is biased, or otherwise could not be impartial; counsel then may examine jurors as of right. The court may excuse an unqualified juror on its own motion or on a challenge for cause and call a replacement.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:23. Use of and Proceedings Before a Commissioner in Chancery","Virginia · civil rules","A commissioner in chancery may be appointed (including in uncontested divorces) only by agreement of the parties with the court’s concurrence or on a finding of good cause in the individual case. The commissioner sets the first meeting, may compel evidence and examine witnesses under oath, rules on admissibility while recording excluded evidence on request, and files a report of findings and conclusions with exhibits","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:24. Appeal of Orders of Quarantine or Isolation Regarding Communicable Diseases of Public Health Threat","Virginia · civil rules","Appeals of quarantine orders and isolation orders for communicable diseases of public health threat are governed by Code §§ 32.1-48.010 and 32.1-48.013 and related sections. The circuit court must hold such hearings in a way that protects health and safety, for example by phone or video or with protective equipment.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:25. Claims for Attorney Fees","Virginia · civil rules","Except for sanctions under Code § 8.01-271.1 and domestic relations fees, a party seeking attorney fees must demand them, and state the basis, in its complaint, counterclaim, cross-claim, third-party pleading or responsive pleading. Failure to do so waives the claim unless leave to amend is granted under Rule 1:8, and on a party’s motion the court must set a pretrial procedure for deciding fee claims.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 3:26. Temporary Restraining Orders and Preliminary Injunctions","Virginia · civil rules","Where no statute sets different criteria, a court may issue a brief TRO to preserve the status quo pending a preliminary injunction hearing after adequate notice, or without notice only on sworn specific facts showing immediate irreparable harm plus the movant’s written certification of notice efforts. A preliminary injunction first requires a finding that irreparable harm is more likely than not, then that the claim","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:0. Application of Part Four","Virginia · civil rules","Part Four applies to civil cases in circuit court, including separate maintenance, divorce, annulment, eminent domain, and habeas corpus or coram nobis proceedings (with limits under Rule 4:1(b)(5)). It does not change how evidence is taken at trial, including the practice of taking evidence ore tenus on legal claims and by deposition on equitable claims.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:1. General Provisions Governing Discovery","Virginia · civil rules","Discovery reaches any non-privileged matter relevant to the subject matter of the action, including inadmissible information reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence, subject to court limits for cumulative, available-elsewhere or unduly burdensome requests; the text has no general initial-disclosure requirement, and expert discovery is by interrogatory and deposition (draft reports and most attorney-exper","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:2. Depositions Before Action or Pending Appeal","Virginia · civil rules","This is the exclusive procedure to perpetuate testimony: a person expecting to be a party may file a verified petition in the circuit court where an expected adverse party resides, serving notice at least 21 days before the hearing (as for a complaint in Virginia or under Code § 8.01-320 outside it), and the court may order depositions if perpetuation may prevent a failure or delay of justice. The petitioner pays the","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:3. Persons Before Whom Depositions May Be Taken","Virginia · civil rules","In Virginia, depositions may be taken before anyone authorized to administer oaths; elsewhere in the United States, before any officer authorized locally or a commissioner appointed by the Governor; and abroad, before U.S. diplomatic or consular officers, commissioned U.S. military officers, or local magistrates or notaries. No Governor’s commission is needed, and officers outside Virginia must certify under seal or ","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:4. Stipulations Regarding Discovery","Virginia · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, parties may stipulate in writing to take depositions before any person, at any time or place and on any notice, and to modify other discovery procedures including ESI, and may include agreements with non-party witnesses under Code § 8.01-420.4. Stipulations must be filed with the discovery taken under them.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:5. Depositions Upon Oral Examination","Virginia · civil rules","Any party may depose any person after the action starts, but the plaintiff needs leave to depose before the defendant’s Rule 3:8 response time expires unless a defendant has noticed a deposition or a certified special notice shows the witness is about to leave; party and party-designee depositions must be taken in the forum county or city, an adjacent one, an agreed place or a court-designated place, and non-parties ","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:6. Depositions Upon Written Questions","Virginia · civil rules","Any party may take a deposition on written questions by serving the questions with a notice naming the deponent and officer (entities designate witnesses under Rule 4:5(b)(6)). Cross questions are due within 21 days after service of the notice and questions, redirect questions within 10 days after cross questions, and recross questions within 10 days after redirect, subject to court change; the officer takes and cert","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:6A. Number of Depositions","Virginia · civil rules","There is no limit on the number of witnesses a party may depose unless the court orders one for good cause (unlike the federal 10-deposition presumptive limit, the text sets no cap).","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:7. Use of Depositions in Court Proceedings","Virginia · civil rules","Depositions may be used for any purpose on equitable claims, for impeachment by any party, and by an adverse party for a party or its officer, director, managing agent or designee; any witness’s deposition may be used on legal claims if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or out of state (unless procured by the offering party), unable to attend, unable to be subpoenaed, a judge, a listed treating health pro","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:7A. Audio-Visual Depositions","Virginia · civil rules","Any deposition may be taken by audio-visual means (including video or teleconference) and recorded without a stenographer; the recording is an official record, and any party may make its own stenographic or audio record and get a copy at its own expense. The rule prescribes on-camera opening statements, oaths and identifications, bars editing except by stipulation or order (on a copy only), lets the recording be used","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:8. Interrogatories to Parties","Virginia · civil rules","Interrogatories may be served on the plaintiff after the action starts and on any other party with or after service of the complaint; answers must restate each question, be under oath, state objections with specificity, and be served within 21 days after service (a defendant gets 28 days after service of the complaint), and are not filed unless the court directs. Contention interrogatories are allowed, business recor","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:9. Production by Parties of Documents, Electronically Stored Information, and Things; Entry on Land for Inspection and Other Purposes; Production at Trial","Virginia · civil rules","A party may request production, inspection, testing or sampling of documents, ESI and things in another party’s control, production of documents to the court at trial, or entry on land, serving the request on the plaintiff after commencement and on others with or after the complaint. The written response is due within 21 days after service (a defendant gets 28 days after service of the complaint), must state objectio","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:9A. Production from Non-Parties of Documents, Electronically Stored Information, and Things and Entry on Land for Inspection and Other Purposes; Production at Trial","Virginia · civil rules","A subpoena duces tecum to a non-party may be issued by the clerk on a written request served under Rule 1:12, or by an active Virginia State Bar member in a pending civil case (except those excluded by Code § 8.01-407), who must sign it like a pleading and send a copy to the clerk on the day of issuance; if the return time is under 14 days after service, the recipient may serve a written objection that halts complian","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:10. Physical and Mental Examination of Persons","Virginia · civil rules","When a party’s (or a person in its custody’s) physical or mental condition is in controversy, the court may on motion for good cause order an examination by health care providers employed by the movant, specifying time, place, manner, scope and examiner and fixing when the report is due. Examiners must be licensed in and reside or have an office in Virginia unless the court allows an out-of-state licensed examiner; t","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:11. Requests for Admission","Virginia · civil rules","Requests may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on others with or after the complaint; each matter is deemed admitted unless a signed answer or objection is served within 21 days after service (a defendant need not respond before 28 days after service of the complaint), and lack of knowledge is no excuse without reasonable inquiry. Admissions are conclusive for the pending action only unless the court ","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:12. Failure to Make Discovery; Sanctions","Virginia · civil rules","A party may move (with a meet-and-confer certification) to compel answers, designations or inspection, evasive or incomplete answers count as failures, and the court must award expenses including attorney fees to the winner of the motion unless the losing position was substantially justified; failure to obey a discovery order can bring orders establishing facts, precluding claims or evidence, striking pleadings, dism","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:13. Pretrial Procedure; Formulating Issues","Virginia · civil rules","The court may call counsel to a pretrial conference to consider issues, a discovery plan and limits, amendments, admissions, limits on expert witnesses, reference to a master, preservation and production of ESI, privilege claw-back agreements and use of electronic exhibits. The resulting order controls the course of the action unless modified at trial to prevent manifest injustice.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:14. Disposition of Discovery Material","Virginia · civil rules","The clerk may destroy filed discovery material not admitted in evidence one year after the final judgment, or, if appealed, one year after receipt of the mandate or entry of any later final judgment.","#st-va-rules"],["Rule 4:15. Motions Practice","Virginia · civil rules","Civil motions are set on the court’s motions day or through the clerk or chambers; absent leave, written notice must be served at least seven days before the hearing, after a reasonable effort to confer on the motion and a hearing date, with a meet-and-confer certification. If a supporting brief is five pages or less, the notice and brief must be filed and served at least 14 days before the hearing and any opposition","#st-va-rules"],["Appeals in Virginia: notice of appeal to the Court of Appeals, then a petition to the Supreme Court","Virginia · appeals","Since January 1, 2022, a party aggrieved by any final circuit court judgment in a civil matter has an appeal of right to the Court of Appeals of Virginia, except in the categories that go directly to the Supreme Court (habeas corpus, collateral attacks on convictions, State Corporation Commission, and attorney-discipline proceedings). Review in the Supreme Court of Virginia is then by discretionary petition for appea","#st-va-appeals"],["Court of Appeals: appeals of right in civil cases","Virginia · appeals · Which court","Any aggrieved party may appeal to the Court of Appeals from any final judgment, order or decree of a circuit court in a civil matter; final circuit court decisions on appeal from an administrative agency or a § 2.2-3005 grievance decision; final Workers’ Compensation Commission decisions; and certain concealed-weapons-permit, prisoner-treatment and § 57-2.02 judgments. Va. Code § 17.1-405(A)(1)–(3), (6)","#st-va-appeals"],["Direct to the Supreme Court","Virginia · appeals · Which court","Appeals go straight to the Supreme Court, and the Court of Appeals has no jurisdiction, in habeas corpus cases, actions collaterally attacking a criminal conviction (including § 8.01-428 motions), final State Corporation Commission orders, and proceedings under §§ 54.1-3935 and 54.1-3937. In an appeal direct from a trial court, the petition for appeal is due within 90 days after entry of the order. Va. Code §§ 17.1-4","#st-va-appeals"],["Partial final judgments in multi-party cases","Virginia · appeals · Which court","An order resolving claims as to fewer than all parties is appealable only if entered as a “Partial Final Judgment” with the findings Rule 1:2 requires; the time to appeal runs from that entry. Refusal to enter one is not appealable. Rule 1:2","#st-va-appeals"],["Notice of appeal: 30 days, filed in the circuit court","Virginia · appeals · Starting an appeal of right","Within 30 days after entry of the final judgment or appealable order (the date the judge signs it), file a notice of appeal with the clerk of the trial court and send a copy to all opposing counsel. A copy also goes to the Court of Appeals clerk with the $50 filing fee; if the fee is not received within 10 days, the appeal is dismissed. The notice must say whether a transcript or written statement will be filed and m","#st-va-appeals"],["The deadline can be extended for good cause","Virginia · appeals · Starting an appeal of right","The notice-of-appeal deadline is mandatory, but the Court of Appeals may extend it on motion for good cause shown. Such a motion is timely if filed within 30 days after the deadline it seeks to extend. Filing the motion does not toll the deadline. Va. Code § 8.01-675.3; Rule 5A:3(a)–(c)","#st-va-appeals"],["Appeal bond filed with the notice","Virginia · appeals · Starting an appeal of right","In an appeal of right in a civil case, the appellant must simultaneously file an appeal bond or irrevocable letter of credit of $500 (or the sum the trial court requires) for costs. To suspend execution, a suspending bond is required, capped at $50 million for an appellant and its affiliates combined (CPI adjustment from April 1, 2031) and including one year’s interest. The cost bond is not jurisdictional, and its de","#st-va-appeals"],["Post-trial motions do not toll","Virginia · appeals · Starting an appeal of right","The time for the notice of appeal is not extended by a motion for new trial, petition for rehearing or similar pleading. It restarts only if the trial court modifies, vacates or suspends the judgment within Rule 1:1’s 21 days, in which case it runs from the later final judgment. If the court vacates the judgment, an earlier notice of appeal becomes moot and a new one is required; if the judgment is only suspended or ","#st-va-appeals"],["Trial court jurisdiction after the notice","Virginia · appeals · Starting an appeal of right","The appellate court acquires jurisdiction immediately when the notice is filed. If the notice was filed within the 21-day window, the circuit court keeps plenary, concurrent jurisdiction until the window closes. After that, it keeps only limited jurisdiction, for matters such as stays, bond amount and form, and the other items listed in Rule 1:1B(a)(3). Rule 1:1B(a)(1)–(3)","#st-va-appeals"],["Transcript within 60 days; notice of filing within 10 days","Virginia · appeals · Record and transcripts","A transcript is part of the record only if filed in the trial court clerk’s office within 60 days after entry of final judgment. A judge of the Court of Appeals may extend that only on a written motion filed within 90 days after entry, for good cause. Within 10 days after the transcript is filed (or after the notice of appeal, if the transcript came first), appellant’s counsel must give all counsel written notice of ","#st-va-appeals"],["Electronic record is the norm","Virginia · appeals · Record and transcripts","Most circuit courts transmit an electronic record, and briefs cite record pages (“R. 1”). A joint appendix (“J.A.”) is used only where a paper record is filed. The appellant designates appendix contents within 15 days after the record is filed, and the appellee may add designations within 10 days. Rules 5A:10, 5A:10A, 5A:19(f), 5A:25","#st-va-appeals"],["Appeals of right","Virginia · appeals · Briefing schedule (Court of Appeals)","Preliminary statement of assignments of error: 15 days after the record is filed in the Court of Appeals. Appellee’s preliminary assignments of cross-error: 10 days after that. Opening brief: 40 days after the record is filed. Appellee or guardian ad litem brief: 30 days after the opening brief, or 30 days after the record is filed, whichever is later. Reply: 14 days after the appellee’s brief. Reply in support of cr","#st-va-appeals"],["Discretionary appeals after a petition is granted","Virginia · appeals · Briefing schedule (Court of Appeals)","Opening brief: 40 days after the clerk’s certificate of appeal. Appellee: 30 days after the opening brief. Reply: 14 days. Extension motions: within 10 days after the deadline. Rule 5A:19(c)","#st-va-appeals"],["Cross-error","Virginia · appeals · Briefing schedule (Court of Appeals)","In the Court of Appeals, an appellee raises its own claims of error as assignments of cross-error: preliminary cross-error within 10 days after the appellant’s preliminary statement, then in the appellee’s brief with record references showing preservation. The collected rules do not separately say whether an appellee seeking affirmative relief must also file its own notice of appeal; if in doubt, file one within 30 d","#st-va-appeals"],["Word and page limits (Court of Appeals)","Virginia · appeals · Length, format, and content","Opening and appellee briefs: the longer of 50 pages or 12,300 words. Reply: the longer of 20 pages or 3,500 words. Petition for appeal and brief in opposition: the longer of 35 pages or 7,500 words. Petition for rehearing (panel or en banc): the longer of 25 pages or 5,300 words. Headings, footnotes and quotations count. The cover, tables, signature blocks and certificate do not. The certificate of service must state","#st-va-appeals"],["Word and page limits (Supreme Court)","Virginia · appeals · Length, format, and content","Petition for appeal: the longer of 35 pages or 6,125 words. Brief in opposition: the longer of 25 pages or 4,375 words. Merits briefs: the longer of 50 pages or 8,750 words. Reply: the longer of 15 pages or 2,625 words. Petition for review under § 8.01-626: the longer of 20 pages or 3,500 words. Petition for rehearing: the longer of 10 pages or 1,750 words. Rules 5:17(f), 5:18(b), 5:26(b), 5:17A(c)(i), 5:37(d)","#st-va-appeals"],["Assignments of error are jurisdiction-like","Virginia · appeals · Length, format, and content","Each assignment of error must identify the specific ruling (or failure to rule) and give an exact reference to where it was preserved. An assignment that says only that the judgment is contrary to the law and the evidence is insufficient. A petition with no assignments of error is dismissed. Arguments may not be incorporated by reference to filings below. Unpublished dispositions may be cited only as informative. Rul","#st-va-appeals"],["Preservation","Virginia · appeals · Length, format, and content","A ruling is a basis for reversal only if an objection was stated with reasonable certainty at the time of the ruling, unless good cause is shown or review is needed to attain the ends of justice. Rules 5A:18, 5:25","#st-va-appeals"],["Appeals of right from certain interlocutory orders","Virginia · appeals · Interlocutory appeals","An interlocutory decree or order involving an equitable claim that requires money to be paid, requires possession or title of property to change, or adjudicates the principles of a cause may be appealed to the Court of Appeals. The notice of appeal is due within 30 days of the order. This does not apply to listed domestic-relations and non-final protective orders. Va. Code §§ 17.1-405(A)(5), (B), 8.01-675.3","#st-va-appeals"],["Certified questions by permission (§ 8.01-675.5)","Virginia · appeals · Interlocutory appeals","Before trial, a party may ask the circuit court to certify an otherwise non-appealable order. The court must find a question of law with substantial ground for difference of opinion, no clear controlling Virginia appellate precedent, a determination that will dispose of a material aspect of the case, and that appeal is in the parties’ best interest. The petition for appeal is due in the Court of Appeals within 15 day","#st-va-appeals"],["15-day petitions for review in the Supreme Court","Virginia · appeals · Interlocutory appeals","A petition for review may be filed with the Supreme Court within 15 days after a circuit court order that grants, refuses, dissolves or refuses to enlarge a preliminary injunction; grants or denies a pretrial plea of sovereign, absolute or qualified immunity; or (since 2026) grants, denies or reviews an order closing a proceeding. A three-Justice panel decides. The response is due 15 days after service. The petition ","#st-va-appeals"],["Mass-tort (MCLA) orders","Virginia · appeals · Interlocutory appeals","Petitions under § 8.01-267.8 are due 10 days after the trial court’s order, and the brief in opposition 7 days after service. Rules 5A:12(a)(1), 5A:13(a)(1)","#st-va-appeals"],["Argument and summary disposition","Virginia · appeals · Oral argument and decision","In appeals of right and granted appeals, argument is allowed unless the parties agree it is unnecessary or the panel unanimously finds the appeal wholly without merit, controlled by settled authority, or adequately presented on the papers. The clerk gives at least 15 days’ notice, and each side gets 15 minutes. Amici argue only with leave. Rules 5A:27, 5A:28","#st-va-appeals"],["Amicus briefs","Virginia · appeals · Oral argument and decision","The United States and the Commonwealth may file without leave; anyone else needs leave by motion. A brief is timely if filed no later than 7 days after the principal brief of the party supported. Rule 5A:23","#st-va-appeals"],["Court of Appeals: panel rehearing and rehearing en banc","Virginia · appeals · Rehearing","A petition for panel rehearing, or for rehearing en banc, of a decision finally disposing of a case is due within 14 days. No response is received unless requested, and there is no oral argument on the petition. The court may order en banc rehearing on its own motion within 20 days. If en banc rehearing is granted, the mandate is stayed as to the panel’s issues. Since July 1, 2026, review is limited to the issues gra","#st-va-appeals"],["Denial of a petition for appeal","Virginia · appeals · Rehearing","In the Court of Appeals, a petition for rehearing of a denied petition for appeal is due within 14 days. In the Supreme Court, rehearing of a refused or dismissed petition for appeal is due within 14 days. Rules 5A:15, 5:20(b)(1)","#st-va-appeals"],["Supreme Court rehearing after a merits decision","Virginia · appeals · Rehearing","File a written notice of intent within 10 days after the decision (this holds the mandate), then the petition within 30 days. The limit is the longer of 10 pages or 1,750 words. Rule 5:37(b)–(d)","#st-va-appeals"],["Notice of appeal plus petition for appeal, both within 30 days","Virginia · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court of Virginia","To seek review of a Court of Appeals decision, file a notice of appeal with the clerk of the Court of Appeals within 30 days after entry of the final judgment or of the order denying a timely rehearing petition. Separately file the petition for appeal in the Supreme Court within 30 days after the judgment or rehearing denial. The petition deadline may be extended for good cause. Only assignments of error relating to ","#st-va-appeals"],["Brief in opposition and merits briefing","Virginia · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court of Virginia","The brief in opposition is due 21 days after service of the petition. If the petition is granted, the opening brief and appendix are due 40 days after the certificate of appeal, the appellee’s brief 25 days after the opening brief, and the reply 14 days after that. Granting is discretionary. Rules 5:18(a), 5:26(c), 5:23; Va. Code § 17.1-411","#st-va-appeals"],["Deadlines are strict","Virginia · appeals · Review in the Supreme Court of Virginia","The times for a notice of appeal, a § 8.01-626 petition for review, and rehearing petitions in the Supreme Court are mandatory. A single extension of up to 30 days may be granted only if at least two Justices concur on a good-cause motion. Rule 5:5(a) (as amended eff. Sept. 15, 2026)","#st-va-appeals"],["Mandatory e-filing through VACES","Virginia · appeals · Filing, fees, and service","In both appellate courts, everything other than the record must be filed electronically in PDF through the Virginia Appellate Courts Electronic System and served electronically. Pro se litigants (Court of Appeals) and pro se prisoners or others with leave may file on paper, and paper filers may use priority, express, registered or certified mail or next-day commercial carrier to be timely. Each filing needs a certifi","#st-va-appeals"],["Fees and out-of-state counsel","Virginia · appeals · Filing, fees, and service","The Court of Appeals filing fee is $50, due with the notice of appeal or petition for appeal. Out-of-state lawyers must be admitted under Rule 1A:4 ($250 per case, with Virginia local counsel) to argue. Rules 5A:6(c), 5A:12(e), 5A:28(c), 1A:4","#st-va-appeals"],["Notice of appeal to the Court of Appeals (final judgment or appealable interlocutory order)","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry (judge’s signature) of the judgment or order.\nFiled with the circuit court clerk. Extendable by the Court of Appeals for good cause; the motion must be filed within 30 days after the deadline (Rule 5A:3(a), (c)). Va. Code § 8.01-675.3; Rule 5A:6(a)","#st-va-appeals"],["Trial court’s control over final judgment","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from entry of final judgment.\nPost-trial motions do not extend the appeal time unless the judgment is modified, vacated or suspended within this period (Rule 5A:3(a)). Rule 1:1(a)","#st-va-appeals"],["Appeal bond (appeal of right, civil)","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","0 days from filed simultaneously with the notice of appeal.\n$500 or the sum the trial court sets. Not jurisdictional; extendable. Va. Code § 8.01-676.1(A), (P); Rule 5A:6(a)","#st-va-appeals"],["Court of Appeals filing fee","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the notice of appeal (grace period).\n$50; the appeal is dismissed if the fee is not received within 10 days. Rule 5A:6(c)","#st-va-appeals"],["Transcript filed in circuit court","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from entry of final judgment.\nExtension only on a written motion filed within 90 days after entry. Rule 5A:8(a)","#st-va-appeals"],["Notice of filing transcript","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the transcript (or of the notice of appeal, if later). Rule 5A:8(b)","#st-va-appeals"],["Preliminary statement of assignments of error","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the record in the Court of Appeals.\nAppellee’s preliminary cross-error is due 10 days after the appellant’s statement. Rule 5A:19(b)(1)","#st-va-appeals"],["Opening brief (appeal of right)","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from filing of the record in the Court of Appeals.\nLonger of 50 pages or 12,300 words. Rule 5A:19(b)(2)","#st-va-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief (Court of Appeals)","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the opening brief (or of the record, if later). Rule 5A:19(b)(3)","#st-va-appeals"],["Reply brief (Court of Appeals)","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the appellee’s brief.\nLonger of 20 pages or 3,500 words. Rule 5A:19(b)(4)","#st-va-appeals"],["Motion to extend a Court of Appeals briefing deadline","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from expiration of the deadline. Rule 5A:19(b)(6), (c)(4)","#st-va-appeals"],["Petition for appeal of a certified interlocutory order (§ 8.01-675.5)","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from entry of the circuit court’s certification.\nBrief in opposition due 7 days after service (Rule 5A:13(a)(2)). Va. Code § 8.01-675.5(A); Rule 5A:12(a)(2)","#st-va-appeals"],["Petition for review in the Supreme Court (injunction, immunity, or closure order)","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from entry of the circuit court order.\nResponse due 15 days after service. One extension of up to 30 days if two Justices concur (Rule 5:5(a)). Va. Code §§ 8.01-626, 8.01-670.2, 8.01-670.3; Rule 5:17A(a)","#st-va-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing or rehearing en banc (Court of Appeals)","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from the decision finally disposing of the case.\nExtendable for good cause (Rule 5A:3(a)). The court may order en banc rehearing on its own motion within 20 days. Rules 5A:33(a), 5A:34(a)","#st-va-appeals"],["Notice of appeal to the Supreme Court (from the Court of Appeals)","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the Court of Appeals judgment or of the order denying timely rehearing.\nFiled with the clerk of the Court of Appeals. Mandatory (Rule 5:5(a)). Rule 5:14(a)","#st-va-appeals"],["Petition for appeal to the Supreme Court (from the Court of Appeals)","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the judgment or of the denial of timely rehearing.\nExtension possible for good cause. Va. Code § 8.01-671(B); Rule 5:17(a)(2)","#st-va-appeals"],["Petition for appeal direct from a trial court to the Supreme Court","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","90 days from entry of the order appealed from.\nOnly in categories that go directly to the Supreme Court (e.g., habeas, § 8.01-428 collateral attacks). Va. Code § 8.01-671(A); Rule 5:17(a)(1)","#st-va-appeals"],["Brief in opposition to a petition for appeal (Supreme Court)","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from service of the petition. Rule 5:18(a)","#st-va-appeals"],["Opening brief after a granted petition (Supreme Court)","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from date of the certificate of appeal.\nAppellee’s brief 25 days later; reply 14 days after that (Rule 5:26(c)(2)–(3)). Rule 5:26(c)(1)","#st-va-appeals"],["Notice of intent to seek rehearing (Supreme Court)","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from the Supreme Court’s decision.\nThe petition itself is due within 30 days after the decision (Rule 5:37(d)). Rule 5:37(b)","#st-va-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing after refusal of a petition for appeal (Supreme Court)","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from date of the order refusing or dismissing the petition. Rule 5:20(b)(1)","#st-va-appeals"],["Increase in appeal or suspending bond ordered by a court","Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from the order requiring the increase.\nIf not effected, the appeal is dismissed (cost bond) or suspension ends (suspending bond). Va. Code § 8.01-676.1(E)(3)–(4)","#st-va-appeals"],["Supreme Court of the Virgin Islands","U.S. Virgin Islands · courts","The territory’s court of last resort, sitting in regular sessions on St. Croix and elsewhere as ordered. Hears appeals of right from final Superior Court judgments and listed interlocutory orders, permissive interlocutory appeals, original writs (mandamus, prohibition, habeas in exceptional cases), and certified questions from federal and state courts. It also controls bar admission (including pro hac vice), discipli","#st-vi"],["Superior Court of the Virgin Islands","U.S. Virgin Islands · courts","The single general-jurisdiction trial court, with two judicial districts (St. Thomas/St. John and St. Croix). Divisions include Civil, Criminal, Family, Probate, Small Claims, Traffic, and a Complex Litigation Division (V.I. R. Civ. P. 92–96) for mass-tort, disaster, class, and similar cases.","#st-vi"],["Magistrate Division of the Superior Court","U.S. Virgin Islands · courts","Magistrate judges hear the matters assigned to them by statute (original jurisdiction) and pretrial matters a judge designates, and may try any civil case, jury or non-jury, if all parties consent within 15 days after the pleadings close. A final decision in an original-jurisdiction case is appealed within 14 days to a Superior Court judge (the Appellate Division); a judgment in a consent case goes straight to the Su","#st-vi"],["District Court of the Virgin Islands","U.S. Virgin Islands · courts","An Article IV federal court, not part of the territorial Judicial Branch and not covered by this package. It is already covered separately in Consi. Cases removed to it and remanded can bring a District Court pro hac vice admission back into Superior Court (V.I. S. Ct. R. 201(d)(3)).","#st-vi"],["No anti-SLAPP statute found, but the Code was not checked directly","U.S. Virgin Islands · anti-SLAPP","No anti-SLAPP law was found. The Legislature’s official bill tracker (billtracking.legvi.org) was searched across every legislature it holds (roughly the 28th through 36th) for “SLAPP,” “strategic lawsuit,” “public participation,” and “defamation.” None of those searches returned a record. A search for “free speech” turned up only a 2013 request for a legal opinion.\nThe collected court rules contain no special motion","#st-vi-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: Supreme Court Rule 201, on a local lawyer’s motion","U.S. Virgin Islands · out of state","A lawyer not regularly or specially admitted in the Virgin Islands may be admitted pro hac vice, in the Supreme Court’s discretion, for one matter only. The motion must be made by a regularly admitted Virgin Islands lawyer. The applicant must:\n• be an active member in good standing of the bar of any state, territory, or foreign country;\n• never have been disbarred or suspended anywhere;\n• have been retained or asked ","#st-vi-oos"],["What to file and what it costs","U.S. Virgin Islands · out of state","File the motion with the Court’s Pro Hac Vice Questionnaire, served on counsel for all parties with certificates of service. The application fee is $350: a $100 Clerk’s filing fee plus a $250 Committee of Bar Examiners fee. Both can be paid through VIJEFS or by certified check or money order.\nThe checklist also requires:\n• a supporting affidavit from the moving lawyer;\n• a proposed order;\n• certificates of good stand","#st-vi-oos"],["Local counsel must sign every pleading and answers for the case","U.S. Virgin Islands · out of state","Every pleading filed by a pro hac vice lawyer must be signed by local counsel. If it is not, the pleading will be rejected. When filing electronically, this is satisfied by local counsel’s electronic signature or by a statement that local counsel reviewed and consented to the filing.\nThe regularly admitted lawyer of record is accountable to the Supreme Court for prosecuting the matter on time and following the rules.","#st-vi-oos"],["One admission per client per matter; appeals and remands are covered","U.S. Virgin Islands · out of state","An existing admission carries over when the same matter is appealed to the Supreme Court or remanded. It also carries over when the matter is remanded from the District Court, assigned to a Rule 93 master case, consolidated (if the lawyer does no work on the other matters), or certified as a class the client belongs to. Collateral writ proceedings are not covered.\nA new admission, with new fees for each matter, is re","#st-vi-oos"],["Remand from the District Court: 15-day notice","U.S. Virgin Islands · out of state","A lawyer admitted pro hac vice by the District Court of the Virgin Islands may keep representing the same client after a remand to Superior Court without a new application. To do so, the sponsoring lawyer must file a notice with the Supreme Court Clerk within 15 days after the remand order. The notice must include the District Court admission and remand orders, the Superior Court docket number, proof that pro hac vic","#st-vi-oos"],["New annual registration and $300 fee for pro hac vice lawyers from 2027","U.S. Virgin Islands · out of state","Supreme Court Rule 214 takes effect January 1, 2027. It requires every active member of the Virgin Islands Bar, expressly including lawyers admitted only pro hac vice, to e-file an annual attorney registration statement and pay $300 between January 1 and January 31. Late filing is allowed through February 28 with a $200 late fee. The statement includes certifications on Virgin Islands taxes, child support, trust-acco","#st-vi-oos"],["E-filing is mandatory, and pro hac vice admission comes first","U.S. Virgin Islands · out of state","All documents in Superior Court and Supreme Court cases must be filed through the Virgin Islands Judiciary Electronic Filing System (VIJEFS). Every admitted lawyer must register, including pro hac vice, special, and limited-license admittees and their staff. An out-of-territory lawyer must be admitted pro hac vice before registering.\nA filing is timely if submitted by 11:59 p.m. Atlantic Standard Time on the due date","#st-vi-oos"],["Limited-scope representation is allowed, but withdrawal needs leave of court","U.S. Virgin Islands · out of state","A lawyer may limit the scope of a representation if the limit is reasonable and the client gives informed consent. Short-term limited services through nonprofit or court-annexed programs get relaxed conflict rules.\nThe collected civil rules have no limited-appearance procedure. Once counsel appears, withdrawal requires leave of court after notice to the client. The motion must include a verified statement about conta","#st-vi-oos"],["No out-of-territory practice without admission","U.S. Virgin Islands · out of state","The Virgin Islands version of Rule 5.5 has only paragraphs (a) and (b). It omits the ABA Model Rule’s safe harbors for temporary practice. A lawyer not admitted in the Virgin Islands may not set up an office or a systematic and continuous presence there except as allowed by 4 V.I.C. § 443 or other law, and may not hold out as admitted there.\nBecause Rule 201 treats transactions, arbitrations, and mediations as “matte","#st-vi-oos"],["Governing code: Supreme Court Rule 211 (V.I. Rules of Professional Conduct)","U.S. Virgin Islands · ethics","The Virgin Islands Rules of Professional Conduct are Supreme Court Rule 211, numbered 211.1.0 through 211.8.5 to track the ABA Model Rules (for example, Model Rule 1.6 is Rule 211.1.6). The collected version (posted April 2021) has no official comments. Pro hac vice lawyers are bound by these rules, and the attorney oath requires following them. V.I. S. Ct. R. 211; V.I. S. Ct. R. 201(a); V.I. S. Ct. R. 204(i)(3)","#st-vi-ethics"],["Rule 5.5 has no temporary-practice safe harbors","U.S. Virgin Islands · ethics","Rule 211.5.5 contains only (a), which bars unauthorized practice, and (b), which bars an office or continuous presence and holding out. It has none of Model Rule 5.5(c)–(e): no temporary practice tied to a pending or expected proceeding, no ADR or transactional safe harbor, and no in-house or federal-law exceptions. Statutory exceptions are left to 4 V.I.C. § 443, which was not collected. V.I. S. Ct. R. 211.5.5","#st-vi-ethics"],["Confidentiality: no conflict-check exception and no Rule 1.6(c)","U.S. Virgin Islands · ethics","Rule 211.1.6(b) lists six permitted disclosures (harm, crime or fraud, rectification, ethics advice, the lawyer’s own claims or defenses, and court order or other law). It omits Model Rule 1.6(b)(7), which allows disclosure to detect conflicts when a lawyer changes firms. It also has no counterpart to Model Rule 1.6(c), the duty to make reasonable efforts to prevent unauthorized disclosure. V.I. S. Ct. R. 211.1.6","#st-vi-ethics"],["No screening of lateral hires; no humanitarian gifts; no Rule 8.4(g)","U.S. Virgin Islands · ethics","• Rule 211.1.10(a) imputes a conflict to the whole firm unless it rests on a personal interest. It does not include the Model Rule’s screening exception for lateral lawyers. Screening appears only for prospective clients (211.1.18(d)) and government lawyers (211.1.11).\n• Rule 211.1.8(e) lets a lawyer advance litigation costs, or pay them for an indigent client. It does not include the Model Rule’s 2020 exception for ","#st-vi-ethics"],["Client files: 10-year retention and a defined ‘file’","U.S. Virgin Islands · ethics","Rule 211.1.15-2 is a Virgin Islands addition. The client’s file does not include the lawyer’s notes, research, and internal memos, or unfiled drafts unless the client paid for them. The lawyer must turn the file over on request and may charge only limited copying costs.\nThe file must be kept for 10 years after the representation ends, unless delivered to the client or the client authorizes destruction in a signed wri","#st-vi-ethics"],["Detailed trust-account and IOLTA rules","U.S. Virgin Islands · ethics","Beyond Rule 211.1.15, which requires 5-year retention of trust records, the Virgin Islands adds rules on:\n• specific trust-account records (211.1.15-1);\n• account requirements (211.1.15-3);\n• use of trust accounts (211.1.15-4);\n• approved institutions (211.1.15-5); and\n• the IOLTA program (211.1.15-6).\nReview them before handling client money in the territory. From 2027, each active lawyer must certify annually that ","#st-vi-ethics"],["Candor about Virgin Islands law is a filing duty","U.S. Virgin Islands · ethics","In the Superior Court, signing a paper certifies that the applicable Virgin Islands law has been cited, including authority for and against the party’s position. This duty is in V.I. R. Civ. P. 11(b)(5) and goes beyond federal Rule 11.\nIn the Supreme Court, a lawyer who appears to mislead by citing other jurisdictions’ law when Virgin Islands law points the other way, or who fails to present controlling contrary law,","#st-vi-ethics"],["Short deadlines skip weekends and holidays in Superior Court","U.S. Virgin Islands · land mine","In Superior Court, any period of 14 days or less excludes intermediate Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays. Periods of 15 days or more count every day. This is the pre-2009 federal method, not today’s federal rule. The Supreme Court’s Rule 16(b) has no such exclusion, so the same “14 days” can end on different dates in the two courts. Use the Virgin Islands holiday list. V.I. R. Civ. P. 6(a)(1)(B); V.I. R. App. P.","#st-vi-unique"],["Cite Virgin Islands law, including adverse authority, in every paper","U.S. Virgin Islands · land mine","Rule 11(b)(5) makes citing the applicable Virgin Islands law, both for and against your position, part of the Rule 11 certification. The Supreme Court can reject arguments that lack adequate citation and sanctions lawyers who rely on other jurisdictions’ law when Virgin Islands law points the other way. Supreme Court briefs must also cite authorities in a set order of precedence, with parallel citations to the V.I. R","#st-vi-unique"],["Fee shifting: the loser may pay your opponent’s lawyer","U.S. Virgin Islands · land mine","The prevailing party may recover attorney’s fees, except in personal-injury cases without frivolousness. Serve and file the verified, itemized bill of costs with invoices within 30 days after judgment, or costs may be waived. On appeal, a separate verified bill of costs is due within 14 days after the Supreme Court’s judgment. Any attorney’s fees component is decided by the Superior Court on remand. V.I. R. Civ. P. 5","#st-vi-unique"],["No waiver of service; 120 days to serve; out-of-territory defendants get 30 days","U.S. Virgin Islands · land mine","Rule 4(d) is reserved, so there is no federal-style waiver-of-service procedure. Serve within 120 days after filing, not 90. Out-of-territory service follows 5 V.I.C. § 4911, and a defendant served that way has 30 days to answer, not 21. The Government of the Virgin Islands has 30 days after service on the Governor and Attorney General. Its employees sued individually have 45 days. V.I. R. Civ. P. 4(d), 4(n), 12(a)(1","#st-vi-unique"],["Motion format traps","U.S. Virgin Islands · land mine","Every motion, response, and reply:\n• is limited to the greater of 20 pages or 6,000 words;\n• must carry a compliance statement in the certificate of service; and\n• if it seeks affirmative relief, must come with a proposed order filed in Word format (not locked PDF) through VIJEFS.\nResponses are due in 14 days, 20 days for Rule 12 motions. Further filings after the reply need leave. Extension motions must state whethe","#st-vi-unique"],["Discovery motions need a letter and a live conference","U.S. Virgin Islands · land mine","Before any discovery motion other than one about depositions, the moving party must serve a letter specifying each disputed request and the order sought. The lawyers must then confer in person if practicable, otherwise by phone or video, within 15 days. Exchanging letters or emails does not satisfy the rule. V.I. R. Civ. P. 37-1","#st-vi-unique"],["Summary judgment: numbered fact statements and a 30-day response","U.S. Virgin Islands · land mine","The motion must contain a separately numbered statement of undisputed facts with record citations. The opposition, due 30 days after filing, must respond to each numbered fact. The reply is due 14 days later and must answer any additional facts. Unless the court sets a different time, a motion is untimely if filed more than 30 days after all discovery closes. V.I. R. Civ. P. 56(b), 56(c)(1)–(3)","#st-vi-unique"],["Continuances need the client’s signature and 7 days’ notice","U.S. Virgin Islands · land mine","Since July 1, 2026, a continuance request must be in writing, filed at least 7 days before the event, and signed by both the attorney and the party. Agreement of counsel, incomplete discovery, new counsel, an unsubpoenaed witness, and likely settlement are listed as generally not good cause. Any trial continuance beyond the second trial setting is also disfavored. V.I. S. Ct. R. 111(b)–(c); V.I. R. Civ. P. 6-3(d)","#st-vi-unique"],["File the notice of appeal in the Supreme Court and watch the 120-day rule","U.S. Virgin Islands · land mine","The notice of appeal is filed with the Supreme Court Clerk, served conventionally, and mailed to the trial judge. The deadline is 30 days after entry, or 60 if the Virgin Islands or U.S. government is a party.\nTime runs from docket entry even if no separate judgment document is entered. A tolling motion that the Superior Court has not decided within 120 days is deemed denied, which starts a new 30 days. A fee motion ","#st-vi-unique"],["Appellate extension motions are due 5 working days early","U.S. Virgin Islands · land mine","Supreme Court deadlines are strictly construed. A motion to extend any deadline other than for a notice of appeal must be filed at least 5 working days before the deadline and served at least 3 working days before. Missed deadlines can lead to dismissal without further notice. V.I. R. App. P. 17","#st-vi-unique"],["Pro hac vice is per client, per matter, and local counsel signs everything","U.S. Virgin Islands · land mine","Each additional client, even in the same case, and each new matter needs a new admission with new fees ($100 + $250) and per-case V.I. Bar dues. Local counsel must sign every pleading. From January 1, 2027, pro hac vice lawyers also owe the annual Rule 214 registration and $300 fee. Rule 5.5 offers no temporary-practice safe harbor, so get admitted before doing transactional or ADR work. V.I. S. Ct. R. 201(b)–(d); 21","#st-vi-unique"],["No peremptory substitution of judge in the collected rules","U.S. Virgin Islands · judges","None of the collected civil, appellate, or Superior Court rules gives a party a peremptory right to substitute or challenge a judge. Disqualification is for cause under 4 V.I.C. § 284, which the rules cross-reference (for example, for masters and mediators) but which was not collected.\nThe Superior Court can reassign a case to a judge from the other district when a judge or court employee from one district is a party","#st-vi-unique"],["Magistrate judges: listed grounds and on-the-record disclosure","U.S. Virgin Islands · judges","A magistrate judge may not sit if the magistrate judge:\n• is a party or primarily interested;\n• is related to a party or a corporate party’s officer within the third degree;\n• was counsel in the same or a related matter; or\n• cannot give a fair hearing because of probable bias.\nA magistrate judge who knows of a ground must declare it in open court and state the reason, with the memorandum sent to parties not present,","#st-vi-unique"],["Supreme Court: appellant must flag possible disqualification","U.S. Virgin Islands · judges","If any justice, or a designated justice on the panel, took part in the case or a related proceeding in the Superior Court, the appellant must promptly notify the Clerk in writing after filing the notice of appeal and copy the appellee. The appellee must do so if the appellant does not. En banc votes count only justices who are not disqualified, and an en banc decision needs at least three justices to agree. V.I. R. A","#st-vi-unique"],["Revised Organic Act bill of rights: broader than federal not established here","U.S. Virgin Islands · speech","The Virgin Islands has no constitution of its own. Section 3 of the Revised Organic Act of 1954 (48 U.S.C. § 1561) carries a bill of rights that extends the First Amendment to the territory. Neither that text nor any case law reading it was collected, so whether Virgin Islands courts give speech broader protection than the First Amendment was not checked. Revised Organic Act § 3 (not in collected texts)","#st-vi-unique"],["Its own holiday calendar affects every deadline","U.S. Virgin Islands · unique rules","Legal holidays in the Virgin Islands include several that mainland lawyers will not expect:\n• Three Kings Day (Jan. 6)\n• Transfer Day (Mar. 31)\n• Holy Thursday, Good Friday, and Easter Monday\n• Emancipation Day (July 3)\n• Columbus Day and Puerto Rico Friendship Day\n• D. Hamilton Jackson Day (Nov. 1)\n• Election Day\n• Christmas Second Day (Dec. 26)\nJuneteenth was added in 2023. V.I. R. Civ. P. 6(a); V.I. R. App. P. 16(","#st-vi-unique"],["Notice pleading and no dollar demand","U.S. Virgin Islands · unique rules","V.I. R. Civ. P. 8(a)(2) expressly says the Virgin Islands is a notice-pleading jurisdiction. The Reporter’s Note explains that this rejects the federal plausibility standard. Under 5 V.I.C. § 5, the prayer must say damages are within the court’s jurisdictional limit and must not state a dollar amount. V.I. R. Civ. P. 8(a)(2)–(3) & Reporter’s Note","#st-vi-unique"],["Prevailing-party attorney’s fees","U.S. Virgin Islands · unique rules","Unlike the American rule, 5 V.I.C. § 541, as restated in Rule 54(d)(2), lets the court award the prevailing party a discretionary amount for attorney’s fees, as indemnity. Fees are barred in personal-injury cases unless the complaint or defense was frivolous. Costs and fees go in a verified bill of costs with invoices, due within 30 days after judgment. If the prevailing party does not comply, costs may be waived. V.","#st-vi-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope and purpose","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The rules govern all civil actions in the Superior Court, except where other Supreme Court rules or the law provide otherwise. They are to be construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action. Specialized rule sets (small claims, family, probate, habeas) govern their own case types.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 1-1. Title, citation and application","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Cite the rules as “V.I. R. Civ. P.” They apply to actions filed after their effective date and to pending actions, unless the Supreme Court orders otherwise or the Superior Court expressly finds that applying them would be infeasible or unjust.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 1-2. Definitions; magistrate judges","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Defines “judge” (appointed under 4 V.I.C. § 72(a) or designated by statute) and “magistrate judge” (appointed under 4 V.I.C. § 122(a)). Wherever the rules say “judge,” a magistrate judge may exercise that authority unless it conflicts with the rules, precedent, or a statute.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 1-3. Procedure where no rule or Virgin Islands law controls","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Where no rule, Supreme Court precedent, or Code provision prescribes a procedure, the judge may regulate practice consistent with Virgin Islands law. No sanction may be imposed for breaking an unwritten requirement unless the court first gave the parties actual notice of it by order.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","There is one form of action: the civil action.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencing an action","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A civil action begins by filing a complaint with the clerk. Under the Reporter’s Note, filing fees must be paid in advance under 4 V.I.C. §§ 511–512, and in forma pauperis status is governed by 4 V.I.C. § 513, not by the rules.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 3-1. Contact information; case information form","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Every attorney and self-represented party must give a phone number, mailing address, and e-mail under the signature on every filing, and must immediately file and serve a notice of any change. In e-filed cases, VIJEFS registration and updates satisfy this (amended eff. Jan. 1, 2021). The plaintiff files a Case Information and Litigant Data Form with the complaint, and each other party files one with its first appeara","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 4. Summons and service of process","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Any non-party adult (18+) or the V.I. Marshal may serve. Service methods:\n• Individuals in the territory: personal delivery, abode service, an agent, or a court-ordered alternative after diligent attempts.\n• Individuals outside the territory: 5 V.I.C. ch. 503 and § 4911.\n• LLCs: may be served through a member.\n• Government of the Virgin Islands: special rules, including personal service on the Executive Director of t","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 4-1. Service by publication","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Publication is available only when Rule 4 service cannot be made and the affidavit requirements of 5 V.I.C. § 112(a) are met. The published summons must contain what § 112(b) requires, and the court’s order controls which newspapers are used under § 112(c).","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 4.1. Serving other process","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Process other than a summons, such as contempt-type and some in rem process, is served by the Marshal or a person specially appointed. Ordinary civil service follows Rule 4, and later papers follow Rule 5.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 5. Serving and filing pleadings and other papers","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Every paper after the complaint must be served on every party. In cases governed by the Electronic Filing Rules, filing through VIJEFS and the electronic notice accomplish service, and proof of service follows E-Filing Rule 7(c) (amended eff. Jan. 1, 2021).\nOtherwise, permitted methods are hand delivery, leaving at the office or abode, mail (complete on mailing), leaving with the V.I. Marshal if the person has no kno","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 5.1. Constitutional challenge to a statute","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A party challenging a Virgin Islands statute must file a notice and serve the Attorney General, and the court must certify the challenge. The Government may intervene within 60 days after the notice or certification, whichever is earlier. A failure to give notice does not forfeit the claim.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 5.2. Privacy protection for filings","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Filers must redact Social Security and taxpayer ID numbers and financial-account numbers (last four digits only), birth dates (year only), and minors’ names (initials only), unless the court orders otherwise. The exemptions and the sealed or reference-list options track the federal rule.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 6. Computing and extending time","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Differences from the federal rule:\n• For periods of 14 days or less, intermediate Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays are excluded. Periods of 15 days or more count every day.\n• Legal holidays are the Virgin Islands list (including Three Kings Day, Transfer Day, Holy Thursday, Easter Monday, Juneteenth (added 2023), Emancipation Day, D. Hamilton Jackson Day, Election Day, and Christmas Second Day).\n• 3 days are ad","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 6-1. Motion requirements, length, and timing","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Motions must be in writing, state their grounds with authority, and attach a proposed order if they seek affirmative relief. Only a motion, response, and reply are allowed without leave. Supplemental affidavits are due at least 10 days before the hearing, and responsive ones at least 5 days before.\nUse at least 12-point font. Each paper is limited to the greater of 20 pages or 6,000 words, and the certificate of serv","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 6-2. Motions for extension of time","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","An extension motion must state that the movant conferred with opposing parties, and their position, or that their position could not be learned despite diligence. It must also report how many extensions the movant has already sought for the same deadline.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 6-3. Continuances","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Continuances of trials, conferences, and hearings require good cause, by written motion or stipulation filed at the earliest practical date and not less than 7 days before the event, except in exceptional circumstances. Requests based on a missing witness or a counsel conflict must make specific showings, including alternative dates. Supreme Court Rule 111 (eff. July 1, 2026) adds that the request must be signed by b","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 6-4. Motions for reconsideration","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A motion to reconsider a non-final order is due within 14 days after entry, extendable only for good cause. Final judgments are governed by Rules 59 and 60. Grounds are limited to an intervening change in law, new evidence, clear error of law, or the court’s failure to address an issue actually raised, and in that last case the movant must cite where it was raised.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 6-5. Withdrawal of counsel","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Counsel may withdraw only with leave of court after notice to the client. The motion must include a verified statement about contacting the client, proof or attempts of service on the client, and updated litigant data forms with the client’s last known address and phone number.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 6-6. Stay for National Guard members","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Added effective Jan. 1, 2021, under 15 V.I.C. § 1519(c). A Guard member called up by the Governor may move to stay any civil case, with copies of the orders and an Adjutant General statement. The court must stay the case for 30 consecutive days unless the member’s ability to proceed is not materially affected, and may require recertification every 30 days.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings allowed; motions","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Lists the permitted pleadings, with a reply to an answer only if ordered. Requests for orders are made by motion under Rules 6-1 to 6-5.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 7.1. Disclosure statement","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A nongovernmental corporate party, or a corporation seeking to intervene, must file two copies of a statement identifying any parent and any public company owning 10% or more of its stock, or stating there is none. It must be filed with the first appearance or filing and supplemented promptly (as amended eff. Sept. 1, 2023).","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Rule 8(a)(2) says expressly that the Virgin Islands is a notice-pleading jurisdiction. The Reporter’s Note says this departs from the federal plausibility standard. Claims go in numbered paragraphs with separate counts. Under 5 V.I.C. § 5, the prayer must say damages are within the court’s jurisdictional limit and may not state a dollar amount. Rules for denials and affirmative defenses track the federal rule, and Ru","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 8-1. Fictitious-name (‘John Doe’) defendants","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Process may issue against a defendant described by a fictitious name when the true name is unknown. Before judgment, the pleadings must be amended on motion and notice, with an affidavit showing how the true name was learned. No affidavit is needed if the defendant acknowledged the name.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Tracks the federal rule. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity. Capacity is raised by specific denial. Conditions precedent, official documents, judgments, time and place, and special damages are handled as in federal practice.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Requires a caption, numbered paragraphs, and incorporation by reference, with exhibits forming part of the pleading. A jury demand endorsed on a pleading goes on the front page under the title (“Demand For Jury Trial”), but failing to use that form is not a waiver. Rule 10(e), added 2018, lets any party designate or counter-designate a case as “Complex” in the caption of its initial pleading.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing papers; representations; sanctions","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Tracks federal Rule 11, including the 21-day safe harbor for sanctions motions. Virgin Islands addition, Rule 11(b)(5): signing also certifies that the applicable Virgin Islands law has been cited, including authority for and against the party’s position.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Answer deadlines:\n• 21 days if served in the territory.\n• 30 days if served outside it under 5 V.I.C. § 4911.\n• 30 days after publication ends, or after mail receipt, for service under 5 V.I.C. § 112(c).\n• 30 days for the Government of the Virgin Islands and its agencies and official-capacity officers, after service on the Governor and Attorney General.\n• 45 days for government employees sued individually.\n• 21 days ","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and crossclaim","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Tracks the federal rule. Counterclaims arising from the same transaction or occurrence are compulsory, others are permissive, and crossclaims must relate to the original transaction.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A defending party may implead a non-party who may be liable to it without leave if it files within 14 days after serving its original answer. After that, leave is required. In complex cases, fourth- and further-party complaints need an express finding (Rule 94).","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A party may amend once as of course no later than 21 days after serving the pleading, or 21 days after service of a responsive pleading or Rule 12(b), (e), or (f) motion, whichever is earlier (“no later than” added 2023). Otherwise, amendment needs consent or leave, freely given. A response to an amended pleading is due within the time left to answer the original or 14 days after service, whichever is later. Relation","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 15-1. Form, filing and effect of amendments","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A motion to amend must attach a complete, signed copy of the proposed pleading. Every amendment must restate the whole pleading, show the changes, and be labeled (for example, FIRST AMENDED COMPLAINT), without incorporating earlier pleadings by reference. A pleading filed with leave is deemed filed on the date the motion was filed.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 15-2. Amendment by the court","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The court may amend any process or pleading for an omission, defect, or variance from the evidence. If a party is surprised, the court may adjourn the hearing for an appropriate period.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 16. Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","In Track 1–3 cases under the Superior Court’s differentiated case-management program, the judge must issue a scheduling order after the Rule 26(f) report or a conference. Absent good cause, the order is due within the earlier of 90 days after any defendant is served or 60 days after any defendant appears. Final pretrial conference and sanctions provisions track the federal rule. Supreme Court Rule 110 (eff. July 1, 2","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 16-1. Joint final pretrial order","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The parties prepare a single joint final pretrial order. The plaintiff convenes a conference to reconcile disagreements and files the signed order. Sanctions may enforce it. Sample Form 16-A (joint discovery and scheduling plan) uses defaults of 25 interrogatories, 25 document requests, 25 requests for admission, 10 depositions, and 7-hour single-day depositions, and calls for Rule 90 mediation.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 17. Real party in interest; capacity; public officers","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Tracks the federal rule. Capacity is determined by Virgin Islands law. A minor or incompetent person without a representative may sue by next friend without a court appointment.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party, including alternative and contingent claims.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 19. Required joinder of parties","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Tracks federal Rule 19. A person needed for complete relief or to protect an interest must be joined if feasible. If not feasible, the court decides in equity and good conscience whether to proceed.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Plaintiffs or defendants may be joined if the claims arise from the same transaction or occurrence and share a common question. The court may order separate trials to prevent prejudice. The word “admiralty” was struck from 20(a)(2) in 2018.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal. The court may add or drop parties, or sever claims, on just terms.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A plaintiff or defendant exposed to double or multiple liability may require claimants to interplead, even if their claims lack a common origin or the stakeholder denies liability.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Tracks federal Rule 23 for prerequisites, types, notice, and class counsel. Settlement approval in 23(e), as amended eff. Jan. 1, 2021, requires reasonable notice, a hearing, a finding that the deal is fair, reasonable, and adequate, and disclosure of side agreements. It also allows a second opt-out. Rule 23(f) (interlocutory appeal of certification) is reserved, and every putative class action is automatically desig","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 23.1. Derivative actions","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A shareholder or member may sue on the entity’s behalf. The complaint must be verified, allege ownership at the time of the transaction, and state with particularity the demand made or why none was made. Settlement or dismissal requires court approval and notice.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 23.2. Actions relating to unincorporated associations","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Members may sue or be sued as representatives of an unincorporated association only if they will fairly and adequately protect its interests. Rule 23(d) and (e) procedures apply.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Intervention of right and permissive intervention track the federal rule, with express provision for the Government of the Virgin Islands and its agencies, boards, and officers. The motion must state its grounds and attach a pleading.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Difference from the federal rule: after a party dies and the claim survives, a motion to substitute may be granted any time within 2 years after the death, not 90 days after a statement noting the death. Substitution for incompetency, transfer of interest, and successors in public office otherwise track the federal rule.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 26. Duty to disclose; general discovery provisions","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Disclosure deadlines:\n• Initial disclosures are due within 14 days after the 26(f) conference, or 30 days after joinder for later parties.\n• Expert disclosures with written reports (publications from the past 10 years, testimony from the past 4) are due at least 90 days before trial. Rebuttal experts are due within 30 days after the other side’s disclosure.\n• Pretrial disclosures are due 30 days before trial, with ob","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 26-1. Expert witness fees and testimony","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Expert deposition fees are paid under Rule 26(b)(4)(E) unless agreed otherwise. A proposed bill must be given a reasonable time in advance, and disputes go to the court before the deposition. The deposing party must advance any fee demanded in advance. An expert’s trial testimony is limited to opinions in the report or supplement and those elicited at deposition.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions to perpetuate testimony","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A verified petition may be filed before suit, or a motion made pending appeal, to preserve testimony. The petition and notice of hearing must be served at least 21 days before the hearing.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Within the territory, a deposition is taken before an officer authorized to give oaths by Virgin Islands law or the law of the place. Outside the territory, depositions must comply with 5 V.I.C. § 4921. Related or interested persons are disqualified.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations about discovery procedure","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Parties may stipulate to deposition and discovery procedures. Court approval is needed only if a stipulation would interfere with court-ordered deadlines.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 29-1. Stipulations expanding disclosures","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The parties may stipulate to expand the scope of Rule 26(a) disclosures.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions by oral examination","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Leave is needed for more than 10 depositions per side or a repeat deposition. A deposition is limited to 1 day of 7 hours unless extended. Objections must be concise and non-suggestive, and only one attorney per party may question unless stipulated or ordered. The deponent has 30 days to review. The officer seals the transcript and sends it to the attorney who arranged it.\nThe organizational-deposition rule (30(b)(6)","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions by written questions","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Cross-questions are due within 14 days after service of the notice and direct questions, redirect within 7 days, and recross within 7 days. Leave is needed beyond 10 depositions or for a repeat deposition.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 32. Using depositions in court","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Tracks the federal rule. A witness is unavailable if, among other grounds, more than 100 miles away. A deposition may not be used against a party who got less than 14 days’ notice and promptly sought a protective order that was still pending. Objections to the form of written questions must be served within the time for responsive questions, or 7 days for recross.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A party may serve no more than 25 interrogatories, including discrete subparts, absent stipulation or leave. Answers are due in 30 days, under oath. The business-records option is available only if the party represents in good faith that it cannot otherwise answer with reasonable effort, and it must produce copies of the records with the answer unless duplication is unduly burdensome.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 34. Producing documents, ESI, and things; entry on land","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Tracks the federal rule. Responses are due in 30 days, or 30 days after the first 26(f) conference for early requests. Objections must state whether anything is being withheld, and ESI form objections follow the federal rule.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examinations","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","An examination requires a court order on good cause, or the parties’ agreement. The order sets time, place, manner, scope, examiner, and who may attend or record. Examinations expressly include DNA testing where it is in controversy. Report exchange tracks the federal rule.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A matter is admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days. Denials must fairly meet the substance, and lack of knowledge requires a reasonable inquiry. Admissions may be withdrawn or amended on motion.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to disclose or cooperate; sanctions","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Tracks federal Rule 37. Motions to compel need a good-faith conferral certification, and evasive answers count as failures. Rule 37(e) on lost ESI allows an adverse inference or dispositive sanctions only on a finding of intent to deprive.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 37-1. Pre-motion discovery conference","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Before any discovery motion other than one about depositions, the movant must serve a letter listing each disputed request, its position, and the order sought. Counsel must then confer in person if practicable, otherwise by phone or video (mail or e-mail is not enough), within 15 days after the letter.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 38. Right to jury trial; demand","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The jury right is preserved under the Seventh Amendment and the Revised Organic Act. A written demand is due no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue, and other parties may demand on other issues within 14 days after service. A party that does not properly serve and file a demand waives the right.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or there is no jury right. Without a demand, the court may still order a jury on motion. Advisory juries are allowed.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 40. [Reserved]","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","No rule.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Voluntary dismissal without order is allowed before an answer or summary judgment motion, or by stipulation. A second dismissal of the same claim, after an earlier federal or Virgin Islands dismissal, operates as an adjudication on the merits. Involuntary dismissal for failure to prosecute or comply operates on the merits unless the order says otherwise. The court may award the costs of a previously dismissed action.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The court may consolidate actions sharing a common question, or order separate trials of issues or claims, preserving the jury right.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 43. Taking testimony","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Testimony is taken orally in open court unless a statute or rule provides otherwise. Motions may be heard on affidavits. Parties needing an interpreter must submit qualified interpreters’ names for approval at least 6 days before the services are needed. Interpreter pay is subject to 4 V.I.C. § 323.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 44. Proving an official record","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Proof of official records follows 5 V.I.C. ch. 509 and Articles VIII–IX of the Virgin Islands Rules of Evidence.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 44.1. Determining foreign law","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Proof of other jurisdictions’ law follows 5 V.I.C. ch. 507.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Tracks the federal rule, with these differences:\n• Notice and a copy of a pretrial document subpoena must be served on each party at least 5 days before the subpoena is served.\n• Any non-party adult may serve.\n• Attendance is limited to within 100 miles.\n• Objections are due before the earlier of the compliance date or 14 days after service.\nOut-of-territory subpoenas use 5 V.I.C. § 4922 et seq. (amended 2018) and le","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 46. Objecting to a ruling","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary. It is enough to state the action wanted or the objection and its grounds.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 47. Selecting jurors","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The court or the attorneys may examine prospective jurors, and if the court examines, it must allow further inquiry. Peremptory challenges are governed by 5 V.I.C. § 323. Jurors may be excused for good cause.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 47-1. Juror contact","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","No attorney, party, or witness may communicate, directly or indirectly, with prospective or sitting jurors before or during trial. After trial, no contact is allowed until the jury is discharged without the court’s permission (as amended 2023).","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 48. Number of jurors; verdict; polling","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The number of jurors, verdicts, and polling are governed by 4 V.I.C. § 80 and 5 V.I.C. chapters 31 and 33, not set in the rule.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 49. Special verdicts; general verdict with questions","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Tracks the federal rule, including how inconsistencies between answers and a general verdict are handled. Issues not submitted are deemed waived or found by the court.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 50. Judgment as a matter of law; new trial","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","JMOL may be made after a party is fully heard. A renewed motion is due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, or after the jury is discharged if an issue was not decided by verdict, and may be joined with a Rule 59 request. A new-trial motion by a party against whom JMOL is entered is due within 28 days.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 51. Jury instructions; objections","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Requests are filed at the close of evidence or earlier as ordered. They must be marked with the case name and number, cite authority, and be numbered in sequence. The court must disclose proposed instructions before argument, and objections must be made on the record to preserve error.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings and conclusions by the court","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","In a non-jury case, the court finds facts specially. The parties must file proposed findings and conclusions within 21 days after trial ends. A motion to amend findings is due no later than 28 days after judgment. Judgment on partial findings is available.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Masters may be appointed by consent or for exceptional needs, after notice. They must be free of 4 V.I.C. § 284 disqualifying ties unless the parties consent, and may impose Rule 37 and 45 sanctions. Objections or motions to adopt or modify a master’s order or report are due no later than 21 days after service. The Complex Litigation Division may appoint masters under Rule 95.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgments; costs; attorney’s fees","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Rule 54(b) partial final judgment requires an express no-just-reason-for-delay finding.\nThe Virgin Islands differences are in costs and fees:\n• The prevailing party must serve and file a bill of costs within 30 days after judgment. It must be itemized, verified by affidavit under 5 V.I.C. § 543(b), and supported by invoices. Noncompliance may waive costs.\n• Under 5 V.I.C. § 541, the court may award the prevailing par","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 55. Default; default judgment","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The clerk or court enters default on a showing of failure to plead or defend. Judgment for a sum certain may be entered on affidavit. Otherwise, apply to the court, with 7 days’ written notice of the hearing to a party that has appeared. Defaults are set aside for good cause or under Rule 60(b). A default judgment against the Government of the Virgin Islands, its officers, or agencies requires the claimant to establi","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 55-1. Default judgment applications","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The court may require notice to the defaulting party when justice requires. In deficiency suits or claims tied to a repossessed chattel, the plaintiff must prove a description of the property, the sale amount or credit, and the cost of sale.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Unless the court sets another time, a motion may be filed until 30 days after all discovery closes.\nThe motion must include a separately numbered statement of undisputed facts with record citations, which does not count toward the 20-page or 6,000-word limit. The opposition is due within 30 days of filing. It must respond to each numbered fact and may add numbered disputed facts. The reply is due within 14 days and m","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgment","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Procedure for declaratory judgments under 5 V.I.C. ch. 89. A jury may be demanded, other adequate remedies do not bar relief, and a speedy hearing may be ordered.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 58. Entering judgment","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Every judgment needs a separate document, except orders on Rule 50(b), 52(b), 54 fees, 59, and 60 motions. The judge signs promptly and the clerk enters it, and the judgment takes effect for appeal on entry. Under V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(9), appeal time runs from docket entry even without a separate document.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 59. New trial; altering or amending a judgment","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Motions for a new trial and to alter or amend are due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, and opposing affidavits are due 20 days after service. The court may order a new trial on its own within 28 days. The Reporter’s Note says remittitur is not valid in the Virgin Islands.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from judgment or order","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time, but only with the Supreme Court’s leave while an appeal is docketed. Rule 60(b) grounds track the federal rule. A motion must be made within a reasonable time, and within one year for mistake, newly discovered evidence, and fraud. Under V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(4), only a Rule 60 motion filed within 28 days tolls appeal time.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Errors that do not affect a party’s substantial rights are not grounds for a new trial or for disturbing a judgment.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A judgment is automatically stayed for 30 days after entry, as amended in 2019 to match the federal rule. Injunctions and receiverships are not stayed. The court may stay pending post-trial motions on security, and a party may obtain a stay at any time by bond. The Government of the Virgin Islands is exempt from bonds to the extent of 5 V.I.C. § 1141. The Supreme Court’s powers under V.I. R. App. P. 8 are preserved.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 63. Judge’s inability to proceed","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A successor judge may continue after certifying familiarity with the record. In non-jury matters, the judge must recall material disputed witnesses on request if they are available.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 64. Seizing a person or property","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Every Virgin Islands provisional remedy for seizing a person or property to secure a potential judgment is available from the start of the action.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions and restraining orders","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A preliminary injunction requires notice, and the hearing may be consolidated with trial. A TRO without notice expires within the time set, not more than 14 days, and may be extended once for good cause. The adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice. The movant must give security in an amount the court considers proper, but the Government of the Virgin Islands, its officers, and agencies need not give secu","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 65.1. Proceedings against a security provider","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A security provider (surety) submits to the court’s jurisdiction and appoints the clerk as agent, and liability may be enforced by motion without an independent action (terminology amended 2019).","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The rules govern receivership actions. An action in which a receiver has been appointed may be dismissed only by court order (added 2018).","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit into court","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","A party may deposit money or a thing on notice and with leave. Deposits and withdrawals follow 5 V.I.C. § 1391.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of judgment","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Unlike the federal rule, either side may make the offer, at least 14 days before trial. Acceptance must come within 14 days. An unaccepted offer is withdrawn and inadmissible except on costs. After a liability finding, an offer may be made at least 14 days before the damages hearing. Cost-shifting applies only as allowed by 5 V.I.C. § 541 and other statutes.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution on judgments","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution under Virgin Islands law. Examination of the judgment debtor follows 5 V.I.C. §§ 501–502, and certified orders for asset discovery must be served personally at least 7 days before the appearance date.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 70. Enforcing a judgment for a specific act","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","If a party fails to perform a specific act, the court may appoint another person to do it at that party’s expense, enter a judgment vesting title to property located in the territory, issue a writ of execution or assistance on a judgment for possession, and hold the party in contempt.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 71. Enforcing relief for or against a nonparty","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","An order for or against a nonparty is enforced by the same procedure as for a party.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 71.1. Condemning real or personal property","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Eminent-domain procedure adapted from the federal rule. The notice tells defendants they may answer within 21 days after service, and objections or defenses must be raised by answer within 21 days. Issues are tried before commissioners.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 72–76. [Reserved]","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","No rules.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 77. Conducting judicial business","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The court is always open for emergency filings. Regular sessions are held at Charlotte Amalie (St. Thomas/St. John) and Kingshill (St. Croix). The clerk serves notice of entry of orders and judgments, but lack of notice does not extend appeal time except as V.I. R. App. P. 5(a) allows.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 77-1. Advancing a case on the calendar","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","For good cause, the court may advance the trial of any contested case.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 77-2. Cases not reached","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Contested matters not reached on the trial date carry over until a new date is set.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 78. Hearing motions","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The court may set regular times and places for motion hearings.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 79. Records kept by the clerk","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The clerk keeps calendars, the civil docket, and records of judgments and orders.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 79-1. Sealing of records","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The court may seal the entire record of a case it deems sensitive. A case-number cross-reference stays public.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 79-2. Exemplification of court records","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Parties may obtain attested copies with an exemplification (the clerk’s exemplification, the judge’s certificate, and the clerk’s certificate) for a fee the court sets. The fixed $50 fee was removed in 2018.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 79-3. Exhibits","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The clerk keeps exhibits through any appeal, while proponents keep sensitive exhibits such as narcotics, weapons, and currency. Offering parties must claim exhibits within 30 days after the clerk’s written request. Exhibits unclaimed 120 days after trial and any appeal may be destroyed.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 80. Record of proceedings","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The reporter-secretary prepares certified transcripts on request at court-set rates, and the clerk provides electronic transcripts of digital recordings on request.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 81. [Reserved]","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","No rule.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 82. Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The rules do not extend or limit the Superior Court’s jurisdiction or venue.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 83. [Reserved]","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","No rule.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 84. Unsworn declarations under penalty of perjury","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","An unsworn declaration under penalty of perjury, in the prescribed form, may substitute for an affidavit or verification, except for depositions and oaths of office.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 85. Court orders","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Orders and judgments must be written and signed by the judge, magistrate judge, or clerk. Oral orders are enforceable until reduced to writing.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 86–89. [Reserved]","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","No rules.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 90. Civil mediation","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Any civil matter may be referred except criminal cases, agency appeals, forfeitures, and habeas and extraordinary writs.\n• Within 14 days after referral, the parties notify the court of the mediator and date, or the plaintiff reports there is no agreement and the court appoints one.\n• The first session is held within 60 days after referral.\n• Each party must tell the others at least 5 days before who will attend.\nPar","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 91. Writs of review","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Petitions to review decisions of statutory officers, boards, commissions, or tribunals are filed in the Appellate Division within the statutory time, or 30 days after the decision if none is set. Counsel must certify the decision is erroneous and the petition is not for delay, and a bond may be required.\nThe respondent returns the writ and certified record within 20 days. The petitioner’s brief is due 20 days later a","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 92. Complex cases","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Adopted eff. Oct. 1, 2018; not in the 2019 compilation. Presumptively complex cases include multi-party environmental, mass, or toxic torts, construction or product defect claims, natural-disaster claims, securities claims, class actions, and related insurance-coverage claims. Cases designated in a pleading, or referred by the assigned judge, go to the Complex Litigation Division judge, who decides whether to keep th","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 93. Centralized management in the Complex Litigation Division","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","The division judge may designate related cases, open master cases with their own dockets, and require filings in the master or individual case. Grouping does not consolidate cases without an express order. The first case-management conference is held within 60 days after the master case opens, and a joint pre-conference statement is due 21 days before it. A case-management order issues within 28 days after each confe","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 94. Crossclaims, counterclaims, third-party actions in complex cases","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Rules 7–15-2 apply, but fourth- and further-party complaints are not of right and need an express finding that they will not delay the case. They are filed in the individual case, and in the master case only if directed.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 95. Masters in the Complex Litigation Division","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","Because complex cases are inherently exceptional, the division judge may appoint masters for individual, related, or master cases, with powers set by the appointment order consistent with Rule 53.","#st-vi-rules"],["Rule 96. Ending centralized management","U.S. Virgin Islands · civil rules","If centralized management is no longer warranted for a category of cases, the division judge must issue a written explanation and a report on transfers, followed by a notice-and-comment process. A master’s authority ends on that determination unless extended.","#st-vi-rules"],["Appeals in the Virgin Islands: one appellate court, a 30-day clock, and a 120-day trap","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals","The Supreme Court of the Virgin Islands hears appeals from final judgments of the Superior Court, from interlocutory orders that 4 V.I.C. § 33(b)–(c) makes appealable, and from orders a Superior Court judge certifies for permissive appeal. There is no intermediate appellate court. Decisions of the Magistrate Division in its original-jurisdiction cases go first to a Superior Court judge, sitting as the Appellate Divis","#st-vi-appeals"],["Appeal of right to the Supreme Court","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Which route","An appeal permitted by law goes from the Superior Court to the Supreme Court. To be appealable as of right, an order must be final or fall within the interlocutory categories in 4 V.I.C. § 33(b) and (c). That statute was not collected. V.I. R. App. P. 4(a), 5(a)(2)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Magistrate Division decisions go to a Superior Court judge first","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Which route","Final orders of the Magistrate Division in its original-jurisdiction cases, and interlocutory orders appealable by law, are appealed to a Superior Court judge. File the notice with the Superior Court Clerk within 14 days after entry. It must include a position statement of no more than 780 words (3 pages if self-represented). The filing fee, or an IFP motion, is due within 14 days, and any response is due within 14 d","#st-vi-appeals"],["Agency decisions: writ of review in the Superior Court","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Which route","Review of a decision by a statutory officer, board, commission, or tribunal is by petition for writ of review, filed with the Superior Court’s Appellate Division within the statutory time. If no time is set by statute, file within 30 days after the decision.\nThe agency returns the writ and record within 20 days. The petitioner’s brief is due 20 days after the record or briefing schedule, and the respondent’s brief 20","#st-vi-appeals"],["Certified questions and transfers","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Which route","The Supreme Court may answer questions of Virgin Islands law certified by any U.S. court or a state or territorial court of last resort. It accepts or rejects within 60 days, and a request not granted within 60 days is deemed denied. Briefs follow at 40 days and then 30 days.\nApplications under 4 V.I.C. § 32(d) to transfer a case to the Supreme Court must show issues of public importance urgent enough that normal app","#st-vi-appeals"],["File in the Supreme Court, not the trial court","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Filing and serving the notice of appeal","The notice of appeal is filed with the Clerk of the Supreme Court, through VIJEFS. A notice filed by mistake in the Superior Court is forwarded and treated as filed on the date the Superior Court received it. A notice filed after the decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed on the date of entry. V.I. R. App. P. 4(a), 5(a)(1), 5(a)(11); V.I. E-Filing R. 13(a)(1)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Contents and appendix-to-notice duties","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Filing and serving the notice of appeal","The notice must:\n• name the appealing parties, with their physical addresses and telephone numbers;\n• designate the judgment or order appealed; and\n• state the reasons or issues to be presented.\nDefects of form do not defeat the appeal, but omissions of substance can draw sanctions. In a civil case, failing to take any step other than the timely notice is not jurisdictional, but can lead to dismissal. V.I. R. App. P.","#st-vi-appeals"],["Serve conventionally and mail a copy to the trial judge","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Filing and serving the notice of appeal","Serve the notice conventionally on every other party, even if it was e-filed, and file a certificate of service. Notices of appeal are “initiating documents,” which the E-Filing Rules require to be served by conventional methods unless the other side consented to e-service.\nMail a copy to the trial judge by ordinary mail. Within 15 days, the judge may file a written opinion or amplify an earlier ruling. V.I. R. App. ","#st-vi-appeals"],["Docket fee","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Filing and serving the notice of appeal","Each notice must be accompanied by the docket fee, which the Supreme Court Clerk lists as $105 for filing and docketing an appeal, unless IFP is granted. If an appeal is docketed without the fee, it must be paid within 14 days after docketing. Otherwise the Clerk may dismiss without further notice unless a supported IFP motion is pending. Parties filing separate notices each pay a fee. A joint notice means one fee, o","#st-vi-appeals"],["Tell the Court about possible disqualification","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Filing and serving the notice of appeal","If any justice took part in the case or a related proceeding below, the appellant must promptly notify the Clerk in writing after filing the notice, with a copy to the appellee. V.I. R. App. P. 19","#st-vi-appeals"],["30 days (60 with a government party) from docket entry","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · When the clock starts and what stops it","In a civil case, the deadline is 30 days after the date of entry. It is 60 days for any party if the Government of the Virgin Islands, the United States, or an officer or agency of either is a party. A judgment is entered when it is entered on the docket under Superior Court practice. Failure to enter a separate document does not toll the time. V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(1), 5(a)(9)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Tolling motions and the 120-day deemed denial","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · When the clock starts and what stops it","A timely motion for judgment as a matter of law, to amend findings, for a new trial, to alter or amend, or for relief from judgment (if filed within 28 days) extends the appeal time for all parties to 30 days after entry of the order disposing of the last such motion.\nIf the Superior Court does not rule within 120 days after the motion was filed, the motion is deemed denied for appeal purposes. A motion for attorney’","#st-vi-appeals"],["Premature notices and amending the notice","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · When the clock starts and what stops it","A notice filed while a tolling motion is pending becomes effective only when the last motion is decided, or 120 days after it was filed. To challenge the ruling on the motion, or an amended judgment, file a notice or amended notice within the Rule 5 time measured from that ruling. No extra fee is charged. V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(4)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Keep the Supreme Court informed","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · When the clock starts and what stops it","The appellant must promptly notify the Supreme Court of any pending trial-court motion and file the ruling within 14 days after it is made. If 120 days pass without a ruling, say so in the notice of appeal or within 14 days after the 120th day. Sanctions are possible for failure. V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(6)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Late motions after 28 days","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · When the clock starts and what stops it","A Rule 60 motion filed more than 28 days after judgment does not toll the time. While an appeal is pending, the Superior Court may deny it but cannot grant it. It must instead ask the Supreme Court to remand. The same applies to untimely or deemed-denied tolling motions. V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(5)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Interlocutory reconsideration is a different clock","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · When the clock starts and what stops it","Motions to reconsider non-final rulings are due within 14 days after entry under Civil Rule 6-4. They are not among the Rule 5(a)(4) tolling motions. V.I. R. Civ. P. 6-4(a); V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(4)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Extension for excusable neglect or good cause","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Extensions and reopening","The Superior Court may extend the time to appeal on a motion filed no later than 30 days after the original deadline expires. The extension may not exceed 30 days past the original deadline or 14 days after the order granting it, whichever is later. If the Superior Court does not act within 120 days, the Supreme Court may decide the motion itself. Rule 2 cannot be used to enlarge appeal time. V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(8);","#st-vi-appeals"],["Reopening when notice of entry was not received","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Extensions and reopening","If a party did not receive notice of entry and no party would be substantially prejudiced, the Superior Court may reopen the time for 14 days. The motion must be filed within 90 days after entry or within 14 days after receiving notice, whichever is earlier. Parties still must monitor their cases. V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(10)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Statutory deadlines cannot be extended","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Extensions and reopening","Where a statute sets the time to appeal or seek relief, the rules’ extension provisions do not apply. V.I. R. App. P. 1(i)","#st-vi-appeals"],["14 days after the first notice","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Cross-appeals and multiple parties","Once one party files a timely notice, any other party may file a notice within 14 days after the first notice was filed, or within the otherwise applicable time, whichever ends later. V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(3)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Who is the appellant","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Cross-appeals and multiple parties","In a cross-appeal, the first party to file a notice is the appellant for briefing and argument. If the notices were filed the same day, the plaintiff below is the appellant. The appellee’s brief covers both its response and its cross-appeal, and the appellee may file a reply on the cross-appeal issues. Cross and separate appeals are argued together. V.I. R. App. P. 22(c), (g); 27(d)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Permissive appeal of a certified order: 10 days","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Interlocutory and expedited appeals","If a Superior Court judge certifies that an order involves a controlling question of law with substantial ground for difference of opinion, and that immediate appeal may materially advance the case, petition the Supreme Court within 10 days after entry. If the order is amended to add the certification, petition within 14 days after the amendment.\nThe petition may not exceed 2,600 words. An answer of up to 1,300 words","#st-vi-appeals"],["Every interlocutory appeal must ask for expedition","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Interlocutory and expedited appeals","In all interlocutory appeals the appellant must move for expedited review under Rule 5(e), or face sanctions. That motion is due within 14 days after the notice of appeal and must state the exceptional reason. Any opposition is due within 10 days. Juvenile-transfer appeals must also seek expedition. V.I. R. App. P. 6(b), 5(e), 5(d)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Stays pending appeal: ask the Superior Court first","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Interlocutory and expedited appeals","A request for a stay, supersedeas bond, or injunction pending appeal ordinarily goes first to the Superior Court. A motion to the Supreme Court must show that asking below was impracticable or was denied, and must attach the relevant record. It is normally decided by a three-justice panel, or by the Chief Justice in emergencies.\nIn Superior Court, a judgment is automatically stayed for 30 days after entry, except inj","#st-vi-appeals"],["Writs of mandamus and prohibition","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Interlocutory and expedited appeals","A petition directed to a Superior Court judge is filed in the Supreme Court and served on the judge, all parties, and the Superior Court Clerk. It is titled “In re [petitioner]” and may not exceed 5,200 words. If an answer is ordered, it is limited to 5,200 words. Writ proceedings get preference over ordinary civil cases. V.I. R. App. P. 13(a)–(b)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Order transcripts within 14 days on the TPO form","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Record and transcripts","Within 14 days after the notice of appeal (or after the order resolving a tolling motion), order the needed transcripts on the Transcript Purchase Order (TPO) form. If you will argue that a finding is unsupported, you must include all relevant evidence. File a statement of issues unless the entire transcript is ordered.\nThe appellee may designate more within 14 days. If the appellant does not order those parts within","#st-vi-appeals"],["No transcript: statement of the evidence","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Record and transcripts","Serve a statement of the evidence within 14 days after the notice of appeal. Objections are due 14 days after service. Disputes are referred to the Superior Court to settle. V.I. R. App. P. 10(c)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Electronic record","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Record and transcripts","The Superior Court Clerk files the electronic record or a hyperlink to it, with a certification that it is accurate and complete. Court reporters file transcripts with the Superior Court Clerk for transmission. Under the older Rule 40.3(j), the e-record was due 10 days after the last transcript, or 28 days after the notice if there was no transcript. V.I. E-Filing R. 13(a)(1)–(2); V.I. R. App. P. 40.3(j) (2011)","#st-vi-appeals"],["40 / 30 / 14 days","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Briefs: schedule, length, and form","The appellant’s brief is due 40 days after the record is complete or the briefing schedule issues, whichever is earlier. The appellee’s brief is due 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief, and the reply 14 days after service of the appellee’s brief.\nIf the appellant misses its deadline, the appellee may move to dismiss. An appellee who misses its deadline is not heard at argument without permission. Amicus br","#st-vi-appeals"],["Word limits and certificate","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Briefs: schedule, length, and form","Principal briefs are limited to 7,800 words and replies to 3,900, excluding the tables. A word-count certificate is required. Unrepresented parties who are not registered filers may use pages instead, at 260 words per page (for example, 30 pages for a principal brief). Other limits: a motion for permission to appeal is 2,600 words, a writ petition 5,200, and rehearing and en banc petitions 3,900. V.I. R. App. P. 22(f","#st-vi-appeals"],["Required contents","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Briefs: schedule, length, and form","The appellant’s brief must include a jurisdictional statement with the filing dates that show timeliness. For each issue it must give the record pages where the issue was raised and ruled on, and the standard of review. It must also include a statement of related cases, a statement of the case with record citations, the argument, and the precise relief sought.\nOnly issues fairly presented below are reviewed unless th","#st-vi-appeals"],["Appendix","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Briefs: schedule, length, and form","The appellant files a joint appendix with the brief. It must include the notice of appeal, the order appealed, the certified docket entries, and the relevant record. If sufficiency of the evidence is challenged, it must contain all evidence supporting the challenged finding. The parties should exchange designations within 14 days of each other. V.I. R. App. P. 24(a)–(b)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Format","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Briefs: schedule, length, and form","Use at least 14-point type, including footnotes, with double spacing, 1-inch side margins, and three-quarter-inch top and bottom margins. On paper copies, covers are blue for the appellant, red for the appellee, green for an intervenor or amicus, gray for a reply, and white for the appendix. Cover colors do not apply to the e-filed version. V.I. R. App. P. 15(a); V.I. E-Filing R. 13(a)(4)","#st-vi-appeals"],["14-day responses; proposed order required","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Motions and extensions in the Supreme Court","A response to a motion is due 14 days after service. Every motion must be accompanied by a proposed order with a distribution list. Procedural motions may be decided without waiting for a response, and a single justice may decide non-dispositive motions. No oral argument on motions unless ordered. V.I. R. App. P. 21(a)–(c), (f)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Extensions: 5 working days before the deadline","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Motions and extensions in the Supreme Court","Deadlines are strictly construed, and a missed one can lead to dismissal without notice. A motion to extend a deadline, other than for a notice of appeal, must be filed at least 5 working days before it and received by the other parties at least 3 working days before. Any opposition is due at least 1 working day before the original deadline. V.I. R. App. P. 17","#st-vi-appeals"],["Computing time","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Motions and extensions in the Supreme Court","In the Supreme Court, exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a weekend, a legal holiday, or a day the Clerk’s office is inaccessible. Intermediate weekends are counted. Add 3 days after service by mail, but nothing after electronic service. Holidays follow 1 V.I.C. § 171, including Three Kings Day, Transfer Day, Holy Thursday, Easter Monday, Juneteenth, D. Hamilton Jackson Day, and Christmas Se","#st-vi-appeals"],["Panel rehearing: 14 days, 3,900 words","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Rehearing and en banc review (effective Dec. 1, 2024)","A petition for panel rehearing may be filed within 14 days after entry of judgment. It must state with particularity each point of law or fact the panel overlooked or misapprehended. No oral argument is allowed, and no response may be filed unless requested. In consolidated cases, one party’s petition does not count for another. V.I. R. App. P. 31(d)(1)–(4), (6) (Prom. 2024-0001)","#st-vi-appeals"],["En banc: disfavored, with a strict opening statement","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Rehearing and en banc review (effective Dec. 1, 2024)","En banc review is not favored. It is ordered only to keep decisions uniform or for a question of exceptional importance. The petition must open by stating either the conflict with controlling authority (with citations) or each exceptional question.\nA petition for initial hearing en banc is due by the date the appellee’s brief is due. A petition for rehearing en banc follows the Rule 31 time. Combined panel and en ban","#st-vi-appeals"],["Panel decisions bind everyone","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Rehearing and en banc review (effective Dec. 1, 2024)","A panel decision is binding precedent for all lower courts and for later panels. Only the en banc Court can overrule or modify a prior decision. V.I. R. App. P. 31(c)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Mandate in 21 days","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Mandate, costs, and further review","The mandate issues 21 days after entry of judgment. A timely rehearing petition stays it. If the petition is denied, the mandate issues 10 days after the denial. Seeking further review does not stay the mandate, so move for a stay, which requires a substantial question and good cause and may require a bond. V.I. R. App. P. 32(a)–(b)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Appellate costs and fees: 14 days","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Mandate, costs, and further review","File an itemized, verified bill of costs, including any attorney’s fees, within 14 days after entry of judgment. Untimely bills are denied unless a motion shows extraordinary circumstances. Objections are due 14 days after service, and an answer 14 days after that. The amount of attorney’s fees is fixed by the Superior Court on remand. Frivolous appeals can draw damages and single or double costs. V.I. R. App. P. 30(","#st-vi-appeals"],["Review beyond the Supreme Court of the Virgin Islands","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Mandate, costs, and further review","Rule 32 still refers to certiorari to the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit as well as the U.S. Supreme Court, and says such review follows those courts’ rules. Federal legislation in 2012 (Pub. L. 112-226, amending 48 U.S.C. § 1613) ended Third Circuit certiorari review and sends review to the U.S. Supreme Court. The federal statute was not among the collected texts, so confirm the route and the 90-day cer","#st-vi-appeals"],["VIJEFS for everything; paper copies of briefs","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · E-filing in the Supreme Court","Notices of appeal, writ petitions, and other initiating documents are filed through VIJEFS. Motions and petitions need only the electronic original unless the Clerk asks for more.\nBriefs are e-filed, and seven paper copies following the cover-color rules must be submitted within seven days or as directed. Four paper copies of the appendix go with the briefs. A filing is timely if submitted by 11:59 p.m. Atlantic Stan","#st-vi-appeals"],["Older Rule 40 (VISCEFS) is superseded","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · E-filing in the Supreme Court","The 2017 appellate compilation still contains a Rule 40 on the former VISCEFS system. It required paper copies within three days and allowed one business day to cure a rejected notice of appeal. The 2020 Interim E-Filing Rules state that they prevail over conflicting procedural rules, and Prom. 2024-0001 reassigned Rule 40 to en banc procedure. Follow the E-Filing Rules, and check with the Clerk on any cure period af","#st-vi-appeals"],["Technical failure","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · E-filing in the Supreme Court","If a filing is late because of a failure of the court’s system, the court shall grant appropriate relief on satisfactory proof. For other technical problems, relief is discretionary. The rules do not extend any deadline. V.I. E-Filing R. 14(a)–(b)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Civil notice of appeal to the Supreme Court","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the judgment or order on the Superior Court docket.\nFiled with the Supreme Court Clerk. A missing separate document does not toll. V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(1), (9)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Civil notice of appeal when the V.I. or U.S. government is a party","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from entry of the judgment or order.\nAvailable to any party when the Government of the Virgin Islands, the United States, or an officer or agency of either is a party. V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(1)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Cross-appeal or other party’s notice","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or the regular deadline, if later). V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(3)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Notice of appeal after a tolling motion is decided","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order disposing of the last Rule 50/52/59 motion (or Rule 60 motion filed within 28 days).\nA motion not decided within 120 days of filing is deemed denied. Fee motions do not toll. V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(4)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Post-judgment motions in Superior Court (JMOL, amend findings, new trial, alter or amend)","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended (Rule 6(b)(2)). An opposition to new-trial affidavits is due 20 days after service (Rule 59(c)). V.I. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Motion to extend appeal time (excusable neglect or good cause)","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from expiration of the original appeal deadline.\nThe extension may run up to 30 days past the deadline or 14 days after the order granting it, whichever is later. V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(8)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Motion to reopen appeal time (no notice of entry)","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","90 days from entry of judgment (or 14 days after receiving notice, if earlier).\nIf granted, the time is reopened for 14 days. V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(10)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Petition for permission to appeal a certified interlocutory order","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from entry of the order in Superior Court (14 days after an amended order adding the certification).\n2,600 words. The answer is due in 10 days. V.I. R. App. P. 6(a)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Motion for expedited appeal (required in all interlocutory appeals)","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nOpposition due within 10 days. V.I. R. App. P. 5(e), 6(b)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Docket fee if not paid at filing","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from docketing of the appeal.\nFee is $105 per the Clerk’s schedule. The Clerk may dismiss without notice. V.I. R. App. P. 3(a)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Order transcripts (TPO) and file a statement of issues","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the notice of appeal (or order resolving a tolling motion).\nThe appellee may designate more within 14 days after service of the TPO. V.I. R. App. P. 10(b)(1)–(2)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief and appendix","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from completion of the record or issuance of the briefing schedule, whichever is earlier.\n7,800 words. V.I. R. App. P. 25(a), 24(a)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the appellant’s brief.\n7,800 words. V.I. R. App. P. 25(a)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Reply brief","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\n3,900 words. V.I. R. App. P. 25(a)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Response to a motion in the Supreme Court","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the motion. V.I. R. App. P. 21(a)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Motion to extend a Supreme Court deadline","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","5 days from working days before the deadline to be extended (file no later than this).\nServe at least 3 working days before. Opposition at least 1 working day before. V.I. R. App. P. 17","#st-vi-appeals"],["Petition for panel rehearing or rehearing en banc","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment.\n3,900 words. No response unless requested. V.I. R. App. P. 31(d)(1); 40(c)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Bill of costs in the Supreme Court","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment.\nObjections due 14 days after service. V.I. R. App. P. 30(b)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Mandate issues","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from entry of judgment (10 days after denial of a rehearing petition).\nSeeking further review does not stay the mandate. V.I. R. App. P. 32(a)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Appeal from Magistrate Division to a Superior Court judge","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of the magistrate judge’s judgment or order.\n780-word position statement. Fee or IFP motion due within 14 days. Super. Ct. R. 322(b)(1)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Petition for writ of review of an agency decision","U.S. Virgin Islands · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from date of the decision (unless a statute sets another time).\nFiled in the Superior Court’s Appellate Division. V.I. R. Civ. P. 91(a)","#st-vi-appeals"],["Vermont Supreme Court","Vermont · courts","Vermont’s only appellate court; there is no intermediate court of appeals. Five justices sit in Montpelier (109 State Street). It hears appeals from the Superior Court and from state agencies and boards, decides some matters in its original jurisdiction, adopts the rules of procedure, and controls admission and discipline of lawyers and judges. Some appeals go to three-justice panels, which usually rule within days; ","#st-vt"],["Vermont Superior Court (14 county units)","Vermont · courts","One unified trial court with a unit in each of Vermont’s 14 counties. Divisions: Civil, Criminal, Family, Probate, and the statewide Environmental Division. Civil cases go to the Civil Division of the county unit.","#st-vt"],["Superior Court, Civil Division (including Small Claims)","Vermont · courts","General civil trial court: contract, tort, eviction, foreclosure, land disputes, malpractice, and appeals from the Probate Division. Small claims go up to $10,000, but consumer-credit and medical-debt claims are capped at $5,000. Post-conviction relief petitions are also filed here (V.R.C.P. 80.12, effective January 1, 2027).","#st-vt"],["Environmental Division; Family Division; Probate Division","Vermont · courts","The Environmental Division is statewide and hears environmental and land-use cases, including appeals from municipal panels. The Family Division handles divorce, parentage, support, juvenile, and abuse-prevention cases, with no jury trials. The Probate Division handles estates, trusts, guardianships, and adoptions; its 14 judges are elected to four-year terms.","#st-vt"],["Judicial Bureau","Vermont · courts","Statewide tribunal for civil violations such as traffic tickets, municipal ordinance violations, and fish-and-game violations.","#st-vt"],["12 V.S.A. § 1041: special motion to strike","Vermont · anti-SLAPP","Vermont’s anti-SLAPP statute lets a defendant file a special motion to strike a claim arising from the defendant’s exercise, in connection with a public issue, of the right of free speech or the right to petition the government under the U.S. or Vermont Constitution.\nProtected activity includes: statements made before a legislative, executive, or judicial proceeding or other official proceeding; statements made in co","#st-vt-slapp"],["Deadline: 60 days from filing of the complaint; hearing within 30 days","Vermont · anti-SLAPP","File and serve the motion within 60 days after the complaint is filed. The clock runs from filing, not from service. The plaintiff may respond within 15 days after service of the motion. The court may extend these times for good cause.\nThe court must hold a hearing within 30 days after the motion is served, unless good cause supports an extension. 12 V.S.A. § 1041(b), (d)","#st-vt-slapp"],["Discovery stay","Vermont · anti-SLAPP","Filing the motion stays all discovery in the action until the court rules. On motion and for good cause, the court may allow limited discovery to help it decide the motion. 12 V.S.A. § 1041(c)","#st-vt-slapp"],["Standard: plaintiff must show no reasonable basis AND actual injury","Vermont · anti-SLAPP","The court must grant the motion unless the plaintiff shows both that (A) the defendant’s exercise of speech or petition rights was devoid of any reasonable factual support and any arguable basis in law, and (B) the defendant’s acts caused actual injury to the plaintiff. The court decides on the pleadings and on supporting and opposing affidavits.\nThe ruling, and the fact that the court made it, are not admissible lat","#st-vt-slapp"],["Fees: mandatory for a winning defendant; two-way for frivolous motions","Vermont · anti-SLAPP","If the motion is granted, the court must award costs and reasonable attorney’s fees to the defendant. If the motion is denied and the court finds it frivolous or meant only to cause delay, the court must award costs and fees to the plaintiff.\nSeparately, 12 V.S.A. § 5771 lets the court award costs and fees to a defendant who wins a defamation action that it finds frivolous and without merit. 12 V.S.A. § 1041(f)(1); 1","#st-vt-slapp"],["Appeal; exclusions","Vermont · anti-SLAPP","An order granting or denying the motion is appealable in the same manner as an interlocutory order under V.R.A.P. 5. The statute does not say more. Check whether the appellate courts treat this as an appeal as of right or one that still requires the V.R.A.P. 5 permission procedure; case law was not collected.\nThe statute does not apply to (1) enforcement actions or criminal proceedings brought by the State or a polit","#st-vt-slapp"],["Pro hac vice is a two-step process: license first, then motion (not Admission Rule 18)","Vermont · out of state","Pro hac vice is governed by Administrative Order No. 41, § 16 and V.R.C.P. 79.1(e), not by the Rules of Admission. Admission Rule 18 deals with a character-and-fitness panel’s decision after a hearing.\nStep 1: A lawyer admitted in another state or D.C., and not suspended or disbarred anywhere, must file a pro hac vice licensing statement under oath with the State Court Administrator before moving for admission. Attac","#st-vt-oos"],["Local counsel must stay in the case and sign everything","Vermont · out of state","The motion is made by a Vermont bar member who is actively associated with the out-of-state lawyer, and it must say who will be lead counsel. Admission is in the court’s discretion and may be revoked at any time for good cause.\nThroughout the case, the Vermont lawyer must sign every document filed, receives service of all process, notices, and documents, and may be required to attend.\nThe admitted lawyer is bound by ","#st-vt-oos"],["Limited appearances are expressly allowed","Vermont · out of state","Under a limited-representation agreement that complies with the Rules of Professional Conduct, a lawyer may appear for a self-represented client for listed purposes only. The purposes are: filing a pleading; a specific motion; specific discovery; a pretrial conference or ADR session; a particular hearing or trial; taking and perfecting an appeal; or, with leave, a specific issue or part of a trial.\nFile and serve a n","#st-vt-oos"],["E-filing is mandatory for lawyers (eFile & Serve)","Vermont · out of state","All documents in the Supreme Court, Superior Court, Judicial Bureau, and Professional Responsibility Board hearing panels must be e-filed, subject to listed exceptions. The Judiciary says attorneys must use eFile & Serve (EFS) for all filings in all divisions. EFS is the Tyler platform that replaced Odyssey File & Serve. The page says OFS stays available until end of day September 25 but gives no year.\nUser fee: $14 ","#st-vt-oos"],["Admission without exam: 5 of the last 10 years (3 for Maine/New Hampshire lawyers)","Vermont · out of state","An attorney-applicant must have been actively engaged in practice for 5 of the preceding 10 years in U.S. jurisdictions. Part of that may be waived down to 3 of the last 10 years if a jurisdiction where the applicant is licensed, and has practiced at least 6 months, requires fewer than 5 years for its own admission on motion.\nLawyers licensed in Maine or New Hampshire who have practiced there for the 3 years right be","#st-vt-oos"],["UBE transfer: 270 (legacy) or 620 (NextGen); MPRE 80","Vermont · out of state","A transferred UBE score must be 270 or higher on the legacy UBE, or 620 or higher on the NextGen UBE. It must be earned within 5 years before the application, or at the next administration after filing. The MPRE score must be 80 or higher, earned from 5 years before to 1 year after filing.\nThe rule that a score must come within 4 sittings is deleted effective January 1, 2027 (order of October 27, 2026). Vt. R. Admiss","#st-vt-oos"],["New: provisional licensure while character and fitness review is pending","Vermont · out of state","Since July 1, 2026, an applicant for admission who is licensed and in good standing in another U.S. jurisdiction, and who has been found eligible by the Board of Bar Examiners, may practice law in Vermont on a provisional basis while waiting for the Character and Fitness report or admission.\nThe applicant must first file a form with the Office of Attorney Licensing agreeing to be bound by Vermont rules, including the","#st-vt-oos"],["Governing code: Vermont Rules of Professional Conduct (text not collected)","Vermont · ethics","Vermont lawyers are governed by the Vermont Rules of Professional Conduct (V.R.Pr.C.), which other collected rules cite by Model Rule-style numbers (Rules 1.16, 5.5, and 6.1 cmt. [4]).\nThe Judiciary publishes the full current text only through LexisNexis, which this project’s sourcing rules exclude, so the rules were not collected. No differences from the ABA Model Rules are stated here. Check the Lexis-hosted offici","#st-vt-ethics"],["Unauthorized practice is contempt; Rule 5.5 sets the exceptions","Vermont · ethics","Practicing law in Vermont without a license is prohibited and may be punished as contempt, unless Rule 5.5 of the Vermont Rules of Professional Conduct authorizes it. The text of Vermont’s Rule 5.5 was not collected, so compare it with your home state’s temporary-practice rule before doing any Vermont work. A.O. 41, § 1","#st-vt-ethics"],["Discipline: Professional Responsibility Program (A.O. 9), recently restructured","Vermont · ethics","Lawyer discipline runs through the Professional Responsibility Program under Administrative Order No. 9.\nSince January 1, 2026, Bar Counsel screens all disciplinary complaints, replacing Screening Counsel. A complaint that does not justify investigation or prosecution, but should not simply be dismissed, may be referred to the Bar Assistance Program for nondisciplinary resolution.\nSince July 1, 2025, a complainant mu","#st-vt-ethics"],["Anti-SLAPP clock runs from the complaint’s filing, not service","Vermont · land mine","The special motion to strike is due 60 days after the complaint is filed. If service was late, much of that time may be gone before you are retained. Calendar it from the docket date. 12 V.S.A. § 1041(b)","#st-vt-unique"],["Pro hac vice needs a license from the Court Administrator before the motion","Vermont · land mine","You cannot just have local counsel move for admission. First file the sworn pro hac vice licensing statement, with a good-standing certificate and fee, with the State Court Administrator, separately for each case. Then file the licensing card with local counsel’s motion. Local counsel must sign every filing for the life of the case. A.O. 41, § 16; V.R.C.P. 79.1(e)","#st-vt-unique"],["No three extra days for mailed service","Vermont · land mine","Vermont abolished its ‘three-day rule’ across all courts effective September 6, 2022. Response periods triggered by service are not extended because service was by mail. To compensate, appellate briefing periods were lengthened (see appeals). Vermont Judiciary news release on Sept. 6, 2022 amendments (former V.R.C.P. 6(e), V.R.A.P. 26(c), V.R.Cr.P. 45(e))","#st-vt-unique"],["Notice of appeal goes to the trial court, and the clock is jurisdictional","Vermont · land mine","File the notice of appeal with the Superior Court clerk (or the agency) within 30 days of entry, not with the Supreme Court. The Supreme Court treats timely filing as jurisdictional.\nThen, within 14 days of filing the notice, file the docketing statement in the Supreme Court and order transcripts. V.R.A.P. 3(e), 4(a)(1), 10(b)(1); 12 V.S.A. § 2383; In re Petition of VT Real Estate Holdings 1 LLC, 2026 VT 13, ¶ 4","#st-vt-unique"],["Printed case is back: file it with the opening brief","Vermont · land mine","Since January 1, 2024, the appellant must file a printed case for most appeals, even when there is an appeal volume. It is due at the same time as the appellant’s principal brief, and briefs must cite to it. No printed case is required in appeals from small claims, the Human Services Board, or the Employment Security Board, and the Court may waive it. V.R.A.P. 28(c), 30(a)(1)(B), 30(e) (eff. July 1, 2025)","#st-vt-unique"],["Preliminary injunction needs a separate motion","Vermont · land mine","Since January 1, 2026, you can no longer apply for a preliminary injunction just by including it in the complaint. A motion is required, and the movant must serve the notice of hearing with the motion on all parties. V.R.C.P. 65(b)(1) (eff. Jan. 1, 2026)","#st-vt-unique"],["Default judgment on out-of-state service: cite the foreign law","Vermont · land mine","From January 1, 2027, a motion for default judgment that relies on service outside Vermont under the law of the place of service must name the statute or rule of that place that authorizes the method used. V.R.C.P. 55(a) (eff. Jan. 1, 2027)","#st-vt-unique"],["Summary judgment: response must reproduce each numbered fact","Vermont · land mine","The movant files a separate statement of undisputed material facts in numbered paragraphs with record cites. The opponent must respond paragraph by paragraph, reproducing each numbered paragraph before its response, and may add a separate statement of additional facts. On request, each side must provide an editable copy. V.R.C.P. 56(c)(1)–(2)","#st-vt-unique"],["E-filing rejections: 7 days to fix and keep your filing date","Vermont · land mine","If court staff reject an e-filing or the submission fails, you may file a corrected version within 7 days of the notice and keep the original submission date. It is your burden to prove the rejection date. You may appeal a rejection to the Court Administrator within 7 days, which tolls the correction period.\nYour own computer or internet failure does not excuse a missed deadline. 2020 V.R.E.F. 5(c)(2), 5(d)(4), (5), ","#st-vt-unique"],["One motion per document","Vermont · land mine","Every motion must be filed as a separate lead document. You may not respond to a motion and make a new motion in the same document. Affidavits and exhibits must be filed separately from the motion; a compiled exhibit file needs page numbers that match the PDF counter and a table of contents. 2020 V.R.E.F. 5(g)(1)–(2)","#st-vt-unique"],["No live external links in filed documents or briefs","Vermont · land mine","E-filed documents and briefs may not contain live external hyperlinks. Internal links within the document are allowed from January 1, 2027. Briefs must use 13-point serif font, 1.2 line spacing, left alignment, one-inch margins, and page numbers centered at the bottom. 2020 V.R.E.F. 7(a)(7); V.R.A.P. 32(a)(1)(D)–(E) (amended eff. Jan. 1, 2027)","#st-vt-unique"],["Reconsideration may or may not toll the appeal clock, depending on the forum","Vermont · land mine","A timely motion to alter or amend filed with the Public Utility Commission tolls the time to appeal to the Supreme Court under V.R.A.P. 4(b)(5). An informal request to a town zoning board to reconsider did not toll the time to appeal to the Environmental Division, because the Environmental Court rules do not incorporate Appellate Rule 4(b) tolling. Check the forum’s rules before relying on tolling. In re Petition of ","#st-vt-unique"],["No peremptory substitution found; disqualification for interest or kinship","Vermont · judges","Statute bars a justice, judge, or juror from acting in a case where they were counsel, have an interest in the outcome, or are related to a party within the fourth degree of consanguinity or affinity. For a corporate party, the bar covers relatives of its officers, directors, trustees, or agents. Being a taxpayer or resident of a municipality that is a party is not disqualifying.\nA judge may not sit en banc on a case","#st-vt-unique"],["Post-conviction relief: never the sentencing judge","Vermont · judges","Under new Civil Rule 80.12, the judge who reviews a post-conviction relief petition may not be the judge who imposed the sentence or entered the challenged judgment. V.R.C.P. 80.12(e)(1) (eff. Jan. 1, 2027)","#st-vt-unique"],["Vermont Constitution speech protections: not established here","Vermont · speech","The anti-SLAPP statute protects petition and speech rights under both the U.S. and the Vermont Constitutions. The Vermont Constitution’s text and case law on whether it protects speech more broadly than the First Amendment were not collected. 12 V.S.A. § 1041(a) (refers to the Vermont Constitution); Vt. Const. not collected","#st-vt-unique"],["Court rules are subject to a legislative check","Vermont · unique rules","The Supreme Court makes procedural rules under 12 V.S.A. § 1. They take effect on the date in the promulgation order unless the Legislative Committee on Judicial Rules objects. The General Assembly may repeal or revise any rule, and later rules cannot override its action. Promulgation orders usually take effect January 1 or July 1. 12 V.S.A. §§ 1, 3, 4; see Order making V.R.C.P. 9.2 permanent (June 2, 2025) (referrin","#st-vt-unique"],["Declarations replace affidavits in civil cases","Vermont · unique rules","Since July 1, 2025, whenever a civil rule or court order calls for an affidavit or sworn statement, a party may file a declaration signed under penalty of perjury using the language in Rule 43(h). Summary-judgment declarations must be on personal knowledge. Rule 43(h) does not cover acknowledgments and other notarizations. V.R.C.P. 43(h), 56(c)(6) (eff. July 1, 2025)","#st-vt-unique"],["Certificate of merit in medical-malpractice cases","Vermont · unique rules","A medical-negligence complaint must be filed together with a certificate of merit stating that a qualified health care provider was consulted and found a reasonable likelihood of breach and causation, for each defendant. The clerk grants an automatic 90-day extension of the limitations period on petition so the consultation can be done. Without a certificate, the case is dismissed without prejudice, except in the rar","#st-vt-unique"],["Rule 4(c). Process: by whom served","Vermont · civil rules","Process is served by a sheriff, deputy sheriff, constable, or other person authorized by law, or by an indifferent person specially appointed by a superior judge. Mail service under (d)(2) or (f), and waiver requests under (l), may be mailed by the plaintiff or counsel; subpoenas are served under Rule 45. Since January 1, 2026, special appointments must be made freely and promptly on motion without notice if service ","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 4(e). Service outside the state","Vermont · civil rules","Cross-referenced in the Rule 55(a) order: service outside Vermont may be made in the manner required in Vermont or in any manner allowed by the law of the state where the person is served. Full text not collected.","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 5. Service and filing of pleadings and other documents","Vermont · civil rules","Service between e-filers with service contacts must go through the e-filing system, unless the parties agree otherwise under V.R.E.F. 11(d)(2). Non-e-filers may be served by delivery or mail, or by email if they consented in their notice of appearance, agreed in a filed writing, or have no known physical or postal address but listed an email in a filing. E-filers must serve discovery through the system unless otherwi","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 6(a)(4). Time: ‘last day’ defined","Vermont · civil rules","Unless a statute or court order sets a different time, the last day ends at midnight in the court’s time zone for email and e-filing, and when the clerk’s office is scheduled to close for other filing methods. (Eff. Sept. 6, 2022.)","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 6(e). Additional time after mail service (abolished)","Vermont · civil rules","The rule adding 3 days when a period runs from service by mail was eliminated across all divisions and the Supreme Court effective September 6, 2022, according to the Judiciary’s announcement. Unlike FRCP 6(d), Vermont adds no extra days. The current text of Rule 6(e) was not collected.","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 9.2. CARES Act notice requirement (eviction)","Vermont · civil rules","Adopted effective October 1, 2022 and amended effective July 1, 2024, to enforce the notice requirement of 15 U.S.C. § 9058(c)(1) (CARES Act). Made permanent effective October 1, 2025. The rule text itself was not collected.","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 11(e). Use of declaration in place of notarization (deleted)","Vermont · civil rules","Deleted effective July 1, 2025. It is replaced by Rule 43(h), which allows declarations without exception wherever a rule or order requires an affidavit or sworn statement.","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 16.2. Scheduling orders","Vermont · civil rules","The court may enter a scheduling order on motion or stipulation, or after a status conference, discovery conference, or hearing. A conference is no longer required. The order may set deadlines for pretrial motions (except those based on later events or lack of subject-matter jurisdiction), third-party practice under Rule 14, e-discovery, privilege clawback agreements, and the close of all discovery, plus ‘other appro","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 16.3. Mediation","Vermont · civil rules","Cross-referenced in Rule 26(f): if Rule 16.3 requires mediation, the discovery-conference order must set a date for completing it. Full text not collected.","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 26(f). Discovery conference","Vermont · civil rules","The court may call a discovery conference at any time, and must do so on a motion that includes the issues, a proposed discovery plan (including ESI), proposed limits, other proposed orders, and a statement of good-faith efforts to agree. Objections or additions are due 14 days after service of the motion. The resulting order is a Rule 16.2 scheduling order and must set a date to complete any Rule 16.3 mediation. It ","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","Vermont · civil rules","Unless a judge orders otherwise, parties may by written stipulation set deposition logistics and modify discovery procedures. All discovery must be served as provided in Rule 5(b)(2)(D). (Eff. Sept. 6, 2022.)","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 43(d). Affirmation in place of oath","Vermont · civil rules","Whenever a rule or court order requires a person to take an oath, the court may accept a solemn affirmation instead. (Reworded eff. July 1, 2025.)","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 43(h). Declaration in place of affidavit or oath","Vermont · civil rules","Wherever a rule or court order requires an affidavit or a statement under oath, a party may file or serve a document signed under penalty of perjury with the prescribed declaration language above the signature. It has no exceptions, but it does not cover acknowledgments or other notarizations. Effective July 1, 2025; replaces former Rule 43(h) (order of evidence, abrogated).","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 55(a). Default: motion for default judgment","Vermont · civil rules","When a party has failed to plead or otherwise defend, the party seeking affirmative relief may move for default judgment. From January 1, 2027, a motion that relies on service outside Vermont made under the law of the place of service must cite the statute or rule of that place that authorizes the method used.","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","Vermont · civil rules","The movant must file a separate, concise statement of undisputed material facts in numbered paragraphs with specific record citations. The opponent must respond paragraph by paragraph, reproducing each numbered paragraph, and may file a separate statement of additional facts. Each side must give the other an editable copy on request. Affidavits or declarations must be on personal knowledge, set out admissible facts, ","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 65(b)(1). Preliminary injunction: notice","Vermont · civil rules","The court may issue a preliminary injunction only on motion, after notice and a hearing at a time and place set by the clerk. The notice may be signed by the clerk or the court. The movant must serve the notice of hearing together with the motion on all parties. Effective January 1, 2026, the former option to request a preliminary injunction in the complaint without a motion is eliminated.","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 79(a). Civil docket","Vermont · civil rules","The clerk keeps the civil docket, grouping civil and small claims cases with consecutive file numbers and entering papers, process, appearances, orders, verdicts, and judgments chronologically. Each entry of an order or judgment shows the date of entry. From January 1, 2027, a temporary after-hours order is entered as of the date the judge signed it. This matters for appeal timing under V.R.A.P. 4(e).","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 79.1. Appearance and withdrawal of attorneys and self-represented parties","Vermont · civil rules","A signature on a pleading is an appearance. Appearances made in open court must be confirmed in writing and served within 7 days, and must give a postal and email address. Self-represented parties file a notice-of-appearance form, give an email if they have one, and say whether they consent to email service. (e) Pro hac vice: a lawyer in good standing elsewhere who has filed an A.O. 41, § 16 licensing statement and p","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 80.10(c). Orders against sexual assault: after-hours relief","Vermont · civil rules","Cross-referenced in the Rule 79(a) Reporter’s Notes as the source of ex parte temporary orders available outside regular court hours (see 12 V.S.A. § 5134(a)). Full text not collected.","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 80.12. Procedures for post-conviction relief","Vermont · civil rules","New effective January 1, 2027. Governs Civil Division petitions under 13 V.S.A. §§ 7131–7137 by persons in custody under a Vermont sentence. The petition may be informal but must give the case number, sentencing date, and the alleged defect. File in the county where the sentence was imposed, with a separate petition for each county, and list any prior challenge. The reviewing judge may not be the sentencing judge. Th","#st-vt-rules"],["Rule 81(a). Applicability: limited applicability","Vermont · civil rules","The civil rules do not alter statutory practice for commencing and conducting habeas corpus proceedings. Where the statute is silent, the rules apply, but discovery requires a court order on motion for good cause. From January 1, 2027, the former reference to post-conviction relief proceedings is removed because Rule 80.12 now governs them.","#st-vt-rules"],["Appeals in Vermont: one appellate court, a 30-day jurisdictional clock, and filing in the trial court","Vermont · appeals","Vermont has no intermediate appellate court. Appeals from the Superior Court’s divisions and from state agencies go straight to the Vermont Supreme Court, which decides some cases in three-justice panels and others as a full court. Its state-law rulings are final.\nThe notice of appeal is filed with the trial court or agency within 30 days after entry of the judgment. Timely filing is jurisdictional. Within 14 days of","#st-vt-appeals"],["Notice of appeal: 30 days, filed in the trial court","Vermont · appeals · Starting the appeal","The notice required by Rule 3 must be filed with the superior court clerk within 30 days after entry of the judgment or order appealed from. Statute sets the same 30-day period for appeals from courts, commissions, boards, and agencies unless another law provides otherwise.\nIf the notice is mistakenly filed in the Supreme Court, the Supreme Court clerk notes the receipt date and forwards it; it counts as filed in the","#st-vt-appeals"],["Shorter periods for the State in criminal cases and some civil matters","Vermont · appeals · Starting the appeal","In a criminal case, the State must appeal within 7 business days after entry. If the sentence is life imprisonment and the defendant has not waived appeal, the State has 30 days.\nThe Court’s guidance says some case types, including foreclosure judgments, small claims, and traffic court (Judicial Bureau) appeals, require a request to appeal within 14 days. The underlying rules for those case types were not collected, ","#st-vt-appeals"],["Docketing statement and transcripts within 14 days","Vermont · appeals · Starting the appeal","The appellant files a docketing statement in the Supreme Court within 14 days after the notice of appeal is filed. The appellee may file one within 14 days after the appellant’s.\nAlso within 14 days, the appellant orders the transcripts needed to review the issues, or says on the docketing statement that none are needed. In bail appeals and juvenile abuse-and-neglect cases, transcripts must be ordered when the notice","#st-vt-appeals"],["Record: appeal volume, then ‘record complete’ notice","Vermont · appeals · Starting the appeal","The trial court sends the appeal to the Supreme Court within 14 days after the notice is filed. The Supreme Court opens the case and, where applicable, prepares an appeal volume (a PDF of the electronic trial-court record) within 14 days.\nWhen transcripts are done, or none are needed, the Court sends notice that the record is complete. That notice starts the briefing clock.\nIn qualifying fee-waiver cases with 4 hours","#st-vt-appeals"],["Post-judgment motions that restart the clock","Vermont · appeals · Tolling, extensions, and cross-appeals","If a party timely files one of the listed motions, the full appeal period runs for all parties from entry of the order disposing of the last remaining motion. Listed civil motions: V.R.C.P. 50(b) judgment as a matter of law; denial of a Rule 50(c)(2) new-trial motion; a Rule 52(b) motion to amend findings; a Rule 54 fee motion if the court so orders under Rule 58; a Rule 59 motion to alter or amend; denial of a Rule ","#st-vt-appeals"],["Extending or reopening the appeal time","Vermont · appeals · Tolling, extensions, and cross-appeals","Extension: the superior court may extend the time on a motion filed no later than 30 days after the original deadline, for excusable neglect or good cause. An extension may not run past the later of 30 days after the original deadline or 14 days after the order granting it. A motion filed after the deadline must be noticed to the other parties.\nReopening: in a civil action, if a party did not get notice of entry with","#st-vt-appeals"],["Separate-document rule: 150-day backstop","Vermont · appeals · Tolling, extensions, and cross-appeals","In civil cases, a judgment is entered for appeal purposes when it is docketed under V.R.C.P. 79(a). If Rule 58(a) requires a separate document, entry happens at the earlier of the separate document or 150 days after docketing. Failing to use a separate document does not invalidate an appeal.\nFrom January 1, 2027, temporary after-hours orders are docketed as of the date the judge signed them. V.R.A.P. 4(e)(1)–(2); V.R","#st-vt-appeals"],["Cross-appeals: 14 days after the first notice","Vermont · appeals · Tolling, extensions, and cross-appeals","If one party timely appeals, any other party may file a notice of appeal in the superior court within 14 days after the first notice was filed, or within the normal appeal time, whichever ends later. If the first notice comes from an inmate under Rule 4(f), the 14 days run from when the superior court dockets it.\nAn appellee who wants any part of the judgment changed must cross-appeal. V.R.A.P. 4(a)(6), 4(f)(2); Supr","#st-vt-appeals"],["Inmate filings","Vermont · appeals · Tolling, extensions, and cross-appeals","An inmate’s notice of appeal is timely if deposited in the institution’s internal mail system, using the legal-mail system if there is one, by the last day. A notarized statement of the deposit date creates a rebuttable presumption. V.R.A.P. 4(f)(1), (3)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Brief deadlines: 40 / 30 / 21 days","Vermont · appeals · Briefing","Appellant’s principal brief (and printed case): 40 days after the record is complete. Appellee’s brief: 30 days after the appellant’s brief. Reply brief: 21 days after the appellee’s brief.\nThese periods replaced the old 30/21/14 schedule on September 6, 2022, when the three-day mail rule ended.\nAn appellee who misses the deadline will not be heard at oral argument without permission. If the appellant misses it, the ","#st-vt-appeals"],["Stipulated extensions: once per brief","Vermont · appeals · Briefing","The parties may stipulate once to extend the time for each type of brief. The stipulation must be signed by all counsel and state the period, the new date, and the reason. No stipulation may extend the time for a notice of appeal, a request for permission to appeal, or briefs in juvenile proceedings under Title 33, Chapters 51 and 53.\nThe 2021 text capped stipulated extensions at 30 days for the appellant’s brief, 21","#st-vt-appeals"],["Word limits and certificate of compliance","Vermont · appeals · Briefing","Principal brief: 9,000 words (appellee’s principal brief in a cross-appeal: 12,000). Reply: 4,500 (appellant’s reply in a cross-appeal: 6,000; appellee’s reply in a cross-appeal: 3,000).\nCounts include headings, footnotes, and quotations. Excluded: statement of issues, tables, signature blocks, the certificate of compliance, any addendum of statutes or rules, and the certificate of service. The certificate must give ","#st-vt-appeals"],["Brief contents and format","Vermont · appeals · Briefing","The appellant’s brief must contain, in order: statement of issues (with page references), table of contents and table of authorities, statement of the case with record references and specific claims of error, argument, and conclusion. The appellee need not include a statement of the case.\nCite the printed case if the document is in it; otherwise cite the appeal volume.\nE-filed briefs: text-searchable PDF, 1-inch marg","#st-vt-appeals"],["Printed case required again (since 2024)","Vermont · appeals · Briefing","Most appellants must file a printed case at the same time as the principal brief, even where there is an appeal volume. It is a separately filed compilation with sequential page numbers and an index cross-referencing the appeal volume. At a minimum it contains the record of actions, the decision appealed, and the relevant parts of the record.\nNo printed case is required for appeals from small claims, the Human Servic","#st-vt-appeals"],["Motions practice","Vermont · appeals · Motions, argument, and decision","Motions must be in writing and state with particularity the grounds and the relief sought. Supporting documents are filed with the motion. Any party may respond within 14 days after service; the Court’s guidance says the movant may reply to a response to a substantive motion within 7 days.\nThe Court may act on procedural motions at any time without waiting for a response. A single justice may decide any motion except","#st-vt-appeals"],["Oral argument and decision timing","Vermont · appeals · Motions, argument, and decision","The case is ready for the calendar after the appellee’s brief is filed, and parties may request argument. Cases are usually heard or conferenced within about 60 days of being ready.\nThree-justice panels usually decide within two business days of argument or conference. Full-Court cases can take months. V.R.A.P. 34(a); Supreme Court guidance","#st-vt-appeals"],["Reargument: 14 days","Vermont · appeals · Motions, argument, and decision","A motion for reargument must be filed within 14 days after entry of judgment. The text of Rule 40 was not collected; this comes from the Court’s published timeline. V.R.A.P. 40 (per Supreme Court timeline; rule text not collected)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Further review","Vermont · appeals · Motions, argument, and decision","The Vermont Supreme Court is the last word on Vermont law. Review beyond it is available only in the U.S. Supreme Court, and only on federal questions. Supreme Court guidance","#st-vt-appeals"],["Permissive interlocutory appeal (civil): 14 days","Vermont · appeals · Interlocutory and discretionary appeals","On motion, the superior court must permit an appeal from an interlocutory order if it finds (A) a controlling question of law with substantial ground for difference of opinion and (B) that an immediate appeal may materially advance the end of the litigation. The motion is due within 14 days after entry of the order. The State’s motion in a criminal case is due within 7 business days.\nIf the trial court denies permiss","#st-vt-appeals"],["Report by agreement","Vermont · appeals · Interlocutory and discretionary appeals","Before final judgment, if all appearing parties agree, the superior court may report a question of law of sufficient importance or doubt to the Supreme Court. In a civil case, the answer must finally dispose of the action in at least one alternative. The order of report states the question and the parties’ agreement on the entry fee. The fee is due within 14 days after the Court accepts the report. V.R.A.P. 5(a)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Collateral final orders","Vermont · appeals · Interlocutory and discretionary appeals","The superior court may permit an appeal from an order that conclusively decides a disputed question, resolves an important issue separate from the merits, and would be effectively unreviewable after final judgment. Request permission within 14 days after entry.\nIf the trial court denies permission or a stay, it must give the movant a chance to contact a single justice for a stay. A motion for permission may be filed ","#st-vt-appeals"],["Appeals needing trial-court permission from a final judgment","Vermont · appeals · Interlocutory and discretionary appeals","Where a final judgment is appealable only with the superior court’s permission, move within 14 days of entry. Rule 4(b) tolling applies. If permission is denied, move in the Supreme Court within 14 days. V.R.A.P. 6(a)(1), (4)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Anti-SLAPP rulings","Vermont · appeals · Interlocutory and discretionary appeals","An order granting or denying a special motion to strike is appealable in the same manner as an interlocutory order under V.R.A.P. 5. Whether this makes the appeal one of right was not determined from the collected texts. 12 V.S.A. § 1041(g)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Lawyers must e-file; self-represented parties may","Vermont · appeals · E-filing and fees in the Supreme Court","Attorneys must file through the Judiciary’s e-filing system (eFile & Serve). Self-represented parties may e-file, mail, deliver, or email filings to supremecourt@vtcourts.gov. A self-represented party who e-filed in the trial court is presumed to keep e-filing on appeal unless the party gives e-filed notice.\nAn appeal opens a new case, which triggers the $14 per-case e-filing fee unless a waiver applies. Failure to p","#st-vt-appeals"],["Filing time and rejections","Vermont · appeals · E-filing and fees in the Supreme Court","E-filings may be made at any hour and count for a day if submitted before midnight. Supreme Court staff review filings for compliance with the e-filing rules, the appellate rules, and the public-access rules.\nA rejected filing may be corrected within 7 days and keep its original date. A rejection may be appealed to the Court Administrator within 7 days. Your own technology failure is no excuse. 2020 V.R.E.F. 5(c), 5(","#st-vt-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (civil, and most agency appeals)","Vermont · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the judgment or order.\nFile with the superior court clerk or agency. Jurisdictional (2026 VT 13, ¶ 4). V.R.A.P. 4(a)(1); 12 V.S.A. § 2383","#st-vt-appeals"],["State’s notice of appeal in a criminal case","Vermont · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from entry of the judgment or order (business days).\nCounted in business days. 30 days if the sentence is life imprisonment and the defendant has not waived appeal. V.R.A.P. 4(a)(2)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Cross-appeal / other party’s notice","Vermont · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or the normal period, whichever ends later).\nFile in the superior court. V.R.A.P. 4(a)(6)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Motion to extend time to appeal","Vermont · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from expiration of the original appeal period.\nExcusable neglect or good cause. Extension capped at the later of 30 days past the original deadline or 14 days after the order. V.R.A.P. 4(d)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Motion to reopen time to appeal (no notice of entry)","Vermont · appeals · Deadlines","90 days from entry of judgment (or 14 days after receiving notice, if earlier).\nCivil only. Notice not received within 21 days. Reopened period is 14 days. V.R.A.P. 4(c)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Rule 60 motion that tolls the appeal period","Vermont · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from entry of judgment.\nA Rule 60(b) motion attacking a default judgment need only be timely under that rule. V.R.A.P. 4(b)(7)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Docketing statement (appellant)","Vermont · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nFiled in the Supreme Court. The appellee may file one within 14 days after the appellant’s. V.R.A.P. 3(e)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Order transcripts","Vermont · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nAt filing of the notice in bail appeals and juvenile abuse/neglect cases (10(b)(4)). V.R.A.P. 10(b)(1)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Appellant’s principal brief and printed case","Vermont · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from notice that the record is complete.\n40 days since Sept. 6, 2022 (formerly 30). V.R.A.P. 31(a)(1); 30(e)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief (and any supplemental printed case)","Vermont · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the appellant’s brief.\nFormerly 21 days. The Court’s timeline says ‘after appellant’s brief is filed.’ V.R.A.P. 31(a)(2); 30(e)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Reply brief","Vermont · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\nFormerly 14 days. V.R.A.P. 31(a)(3)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Response to a motion","Vermont · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the motion.\nReply to a response on a substantive motion: 7 days (Court guidance). V.R.A.P. 27(a)(3)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Motion for permission to appeal an interlocutory order (trial court)","Vermont · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of the order or ruling.\nState in criminal cases: 7 business days. V.R.A.P. 5(b)(5)(A); 5.1(a)(2)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Motion for permission to appeal in the Supreme Court after denial","Vermont · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of the trial court’s denial.\nResponses due 14 days after service. V.R.A.P. 5(b)(7)(A); 5.1(b)(2); 6(a)(4)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Entry fee after an interlocutory appeal is accepted","Vermont · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of the order accepting the appeal. V.R.A.P. 5(a)(3), 5(b)(6)(B), 5.1(b)(5)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Motion for reargument","Vermont · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of judgment.\nFrom the Court’s timeline; rule text not collected. V.R.A.P. 40","#st-vt-appeals"],["Correct a rejected e-filing (keep original filing date)","Vermont · appeals · Deadlines","7 days from notice of rejection or failed submission.\nAppeal of a rejection to the Court Administrator: 7 days; tolls the correction period. 2020 V.R.E.F. 5(d)(4)–(5), 5(e)(4)","#st-vt-appeals"],["Wisconsin Supreme Court","Wisconsin · courts","Has appellate jurisdiction only, except as the constitution or law provides (it also hears original actions and supervisory writs under ss. 809.70–809.71). Reviews Court of Appeals decisions only by discretionary petition for review (s. 808.10; s. 809.62), and can take a case directly by bypass petition, certification from the Court of Appeals, or on its own motion (s. 808.05). Writes the procedure statutes by rule (","#st-wi"],["Wisconsin Court of Appeals","Wisconsin · courts","16 judges in four districts: District I (Milwaukee), II (Waukesha), III (Wausau), IV (Madison) (ss. 752.03, 752.11–752.19). Hears appeals of right from final circuit court judgments and orders, appeals by permission from nonfinal orders, and supervisory and prerogative writs (ss. 752.01–752.02, 808.03). Sits in 3-judge panels, but small claims (ch. 799), ordinance and traffic, ch. 48/938 juvenile, ch. 51/55, misdemea","#st-wi"],["Circuit Courts","Wisconsin · courts","The single general-jurisdiction trial court, organized by county judicial circuits with numbered branches and grouped into judicial administrative districts (ss. 753.03, 753.06, 753.061). Civil procedure is statutory: Wis. Stat. chs. 801–847, written largely by Supreme Court rule (s. 801.01(2)). Circuits may adopt local rules approved by the chief judge of the administrative district (s. 753.35). Small claims are han","#st-wi"],["Municipal courts","Wisconsin · courts","Local courts for ordinance violations; their judgments can be transcribed to circuit court (s. 806.12). Not otherwise covered here.","#st-wi"],["No anti-SLAPP statute in force","Wisconsin · anti-SLAPP","Wisconsin has no anti-SLAPP law. The statutes certified November 4, 2026 contain no special motion to dismiss for speech or petitioning (ch. 802 has no s. 802.065, and s. 895.69 is the structured-settlement transfer procedure, not an anti-SLAPP provision).\nThe 2025–26 Legislature considered the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (2025 A.B. 701 / S.B. 666), which would have created s. 802.065 and added it to the","#st-wi-slapp"],["Frivolous-claim fees (s. 895.044) are the closest fee tool","Wisconsin · anti-SLAPP","A party or the party’s lawyer can be liable for actual costs and reasonable attorney fees for commencing, using, or continuing a claim, defense, or appeal that was brought in bad faith solely to harass or maliciously injure, or that the party or lawyer knew or should have known had no reasonable basis in law or equity. The court must find this by clear and convincing evidence. There is a 21-day safe harbor: if the ch","#st-wi-slapp"],["Pro hac vice: court motion with a Wisconsin sponsor, after applying to the State Bar","Wisconsin · out of state","A circuit court or judge may let nonresident counsel appear in a particular action in association with an active member of the State Bar of Wisconsin who also appears and participates (SCR 10.03(4)(b)). The applicant must give the information in Appendix A to SCR 10 (sworn statement: admissions, no pending discipline, no suspension or disbarment, name and bar number of the Wisconsin sponsor, does not practice in Wisc","#st-wi-oos"],["Fee: $250 per application, nonrefundable, paid to the State Bar","Wisconsin · out of state","$250 per application, per case (consolidated or jointly litigated cases can share one application). Not refunded if the case is dismissed or the application is withdrawn or denied. Waived for lawyers representing an indigent client through an indigent-services agency or without charge. Nonresident military counsel representing military personnel and counsel representing a tribe in an ICWA custody case need no Wiscons","#st-wi-oos"],["One admission follows the case up and down","Wisconsin · out of state","An order granting PHV continues through later appellate or circuit court proceedings in the same matter, provided counsel files notice of the order with the court handling the later proceeding. Make sure the order is in the record on appeal. A court may rescind PHV after a hearing for incompetence or unwillingness to follow the ethics rules or court decorum. SCR 10.03(4)(b), (e)","#st-wi-oos"],["Agency hearings, depositions, and temporary practice","Wisconsin · out of state","An administrative law judge may let nonresident counsel appear in a particular agency proceeding without local counsel, using the same standards as a court (SCR 10.03(4)(d)); the court says no State Bar application or fee is needed for agency matters. For depositions and other work with no court appearance, look to SCR 20:5.5(c), which allows occasional services by a lawyer admitted and in good standing elsewhere (wi","#st-wi-oos"],["In-house counsel must register within 60 days","Wisconsin · out of state","A lawyer admitted elsewhere who works in Wisconsin on a continuing basis exclusively for a non-law-firm employer must register as in-house counsel with the Board of Bar Examiners within 60 days after starting work, with the Appendix B application, a $250 fee, proof of admission, and an employer affidavit. Registration allows advice to the employer and its affiliates and pro bono work, but not court appearances; appea","#st-wi-oos"],["Full admission without the bar exam","Wisconsin · out of state","Wisconsin admits on proof of practice: 3 years of substantial practice within the last 5 years, with admission by another state’s court of last resort ($850 application fee). An applicant who failed the Wisconsin bar exam cannot use this route. Wisconsin also keeps the diploma privilege for graduates of ABA-approved Wisconsin law schools. From July 2026, Wisconsin gives the UBE and accepts a transferred UBE score of ","#st-wi-oos"],["E-filing: PHV lawyers must register for eFiling after the PHV order","Wisconsin · out of state","Licensed Wisconsin lawyers and lawyers appearing under SCR 10.03(4) are mandatory e-filing users in circuit court and the appellate courts. A PHV lawyer registers after the court approves the PHV motion, then must ‘opt in’ on each case; a mandatory user who does not opt in gets no notices or electronic service. The court’s FAQ says all PHV attorneys must create an eCourts account. Wis. Stat. §§ 801.18(3)(a), (f)-(g),","#st-wi-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances are allowed by statute","Wisconsin · out of state","A lawyer may appear for only specific proceedings or issues by filing and serving a notice of limited appearance before or at the proceeding. The notice must name the client, list the covered proceedings or issues, state that the lawyer will file a notice of termination, and give the client’s contact information. Both the client and the lawyer must be served while it lasts. The role ends, without a court order, when ","#st-wi-oos"],["Governing code: SCR chapter 20 (Wisconsin Rules of Professional Conduct)","Wisconsin · ethics","Based on the ABA Model Rules, with Wisconsin Committee Comments that flag every departure. Adopted June 10, 1987 (effective January 1, 1988), most recently amended March 30, 2023 per the chapter history. ABA comments are printed but the numbering often differs, so check the Wisconsin text first. SCR ch. 20 (history note)","#st-wi-ethics"],["Mandatory disclosure to prevent client crime or fraud (20:1.6(b))","Wisconsin · ethics","Unlike Model Rule 1.6, Wisconsin requires (‘shall reveal’) disclosure to the extent reasonably necessary to prevent a client’s criminal or fraudulent act likely to cause death, substantial bodily harm, or substantial injury to another’s financial interests or property. Permissive disclosure in 20:1.6(c)(1) uses ‘reasonably likely’ rather than ‘reasonably certain.’ The conflict-check exception in (c)(6) is broader tha","#st-wi-ethics"],["Fee terms in writing when the total will exceed $1,000","Wisconsin · ethics","Scope and fee basis must be communicated in writing unless total cost is foreseeably $1,000 or less, and any change in rate must be in writing. Above $1,000, the purpose and effect of any retainer or advance fee must be in writing. Contingent fees need a writing signed by the client; none in family matters (except collecting past-due support, maintenance, or property division) or criminal defense. Fee division by ref","#st-wi-ethics"],["Conflict waivers must be signed by the client","Wisconsin · ethics","Informed consent to a current-client conflict must be confirmed in a writing signed by the client, not just ‘confirmed in writing’ as in the Model Rule. SCR 20:1.7(b) Wis. Comm. Comment","#st-wi-ethics"],["Lateral screening is narrow","Wisconsin · ethics","A former-client conflict of a lateral lawyer is not imputed only if the lawyer did no more than minor and isolated work on the matter at the old firm, is timely screened and gets no part of the fee, and the former client gets prompt written notice (plus a public-defender variant). This is narrower than Model Rule 1.10(a)(2), which allows screening regardless of how much the lateral worked on the matter. SCR 20:1.10(a","#st-wi-ethics"],["Other Wisconsin-specific rules","Wisconsin · ethics","Insurance defense: the insurer may limit the scope of representation of the insured (20:1.2(e)); 20:1.8(f) keeps an insurance-defense exception. Lawyers must tell unrepresented persons their role (20:4.3). Inadvertently sent privileged material: stop reviewing, notify, and follow instructions pending a court ruling (20:4.4(c)). A lawyer may advise or supervise lawful investigations involving deception (20:4.1(b)). Ex","#st-wi-ethics"],["Filing commences the case, but you have 90 days to serve, and that cannot be extended","Wisconsin · land mine","An action is commenced when the summons and complaint are filed, but only if an authenticated copy is served within 90 days after filing. The court cannot enlarge the 90 days. Service must be made with reasonable diligence; substitute service at the home and publication are allowed only after reasonable diligence fails at personal service. Wis. Stat. §§ 801.02(1), 801.15(2)(a), 801.11(1)","#st-wi-unique"],["Answer deadline is 20 days, or 45 days in tort, insurer, and state cases","Wisconsin · land mine","An answer is due 20 days after service of the complaint, but 45 days if any claim sounds in tort, if an insurance company is a defendant, or if the defendant is the state. The summons must state the correct period (20 or 45 days; 40 days after first publication if served by publication). The same 45-day rule applies to replies, motions to strike, and responses to amended pleadings. Wis. Stat. §§ 801.09(2), 802.06(1),","#st-wi-unique"],["Do not plead a dollar amount in a tort complaint","Wisconsin · land mine","In a tort claim for money, the demand for judgment may not state the amount sought. You must disclose the amount to the court and other parties before a hearing on summary judgment or judgment on the pleadings, and before default or final judgment. Wis. Stat. §§ 802.02(1m), 802.06(3), 802.08(2), 806.01(1)(c), 806.02(2)","#st-wi-unique"],["Time computation: periods under 11 days skip weekends and holidays","Wisconsin · land mine","Exclude Saturdays, Sundays, and holidays (including a full day on Good Friday) when a period is less than 11 days. Add 3 days for mail service. Add 1 day for fax, email, or e-filing service only if completed between 5 p.m. and midnight. Late motions to enlarge need excusable neglect, and the court cannot extend the time to appeal, to decide post-verdict motions, or to move under s. 805.17(3) or s. 806.07. Wis. Stat. ","#st-wi-unique"],["60-day window to substitute the judge","Wisconsin · land mine","The peremptory substitution request must be filed within 60 days (plaintiff: from filing; others: from service) and before any preliminary contested matter is heard. Arguing a contested motion first forfeits it. Wis. Stat. § 801.58(1)","#st-wi-unique"],["Summary judgment within 8 months, and amend once within 6 months","Wisconsin · land mine","Absent a scheduling order, a summary judgment motion must be made within 8 months after the summons and complaint are filed, served at least 20 days before the hearing, with opposing affidavits at least 5 days before. A pleading may be amended once as of course only within 6 months after filing, and a third-party complaint may be served without leave only within 6 months. Wis. Stat. §§ 802.08(1)-(2), 802.09(1), 803.0","#st-wi-unique"],["Discovery has hard caps and no initial disclosures","Wisconsin · land mine","Limits absent stipulation or order: 25 interrogatories including subparts; 10 depositions of no more than 7 hours each; document requests reaching back no more than 5 years before the claim accrued. There is no general initial-disclosure rule, but litigation-funding agreements must be produced unprompted. A motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim, for judgment on the pleadings, or for a more definite statement","#st-wi-unique"],["Jury: demand early, 5/6 verdict, special verdicts","Wisconsin · land mine","Demand a jury at or before the scheduling or pretrial conference, whichever is first, and pay the fee on the scheduling order’s timetable, or the jury is waived. Five-sixths of the jurors decide, and the same five-sixths must agree on every question needed for a verdict on a claim. Special verdicts are the default. Failing to object at the instruction and verdict conference waives instruction and verdict errors. Wis.","#st-wi-unique"],["Post-verdict motions: 20 days, then deemed denied at 90","Wisconsin · land mine","Motions after verdict must be filed and served within 20 days after the verdict unless the court sets a longer time within those 20 days. If not decided within 90 days after the verdict, they are deemed denied and judgment is entered. In bench trials, a motion to amend findings or the judgment is due 20 days after entry of judgment; undecided at 90 days, it is deemed denied and the appeal clock starts 90 days after e","#st-wi-unique"],["Appeal deadline depends on notice of entry: 45 or 90 days, unextendable","Wisconsin · land mine","Appeal within 45 days of entry of the final judgment or order if written notice of entry is given within 21 days of entry; otherwise within 90 days. No court can enlarge it. Serving notice of entry within 21 days is how the winner shortens the loser’s time. Nonfinal orders require a petition for leave within 14 days. Wis. Stat. §§ 808.04(1), 806.06(5), 809.82(2)(b), 809.50(1)","#st-wi-unique"],["Email service needs written consent unless both sides e-file","Wisconsin · land mine","Service of papers by email is allowed only if the recipient consented in writing; between registered e-filing users, the system’s notice of activity is service. Self-represented ‘paper parties’ must be served by traditional methods. Documents needing personal service still need it. Wis. Stat. §§ 801.14(2), 801.18(6)(a)-(c)","#st-wi-unique"],["Rule 11 safe harbor and a separate frivolous-claims statute","Wisconsin · land mine","Sanctions under s. 802.05 have a 21-day safe harbor before filing the motion. Separately, s. 895.044 allows actual fees, on clear and convincing proof, for claims or defenses brought in bad faith or without reasonable basis, also with a 21-day withdrawal window. Wis. Stat. §§ 802.05(3)(a)1., 895.044(2)","#st-wi-unique"],["One peremptory substitution of judge per side, with no reason required","Wisconsin · judges","Any party may file a written request to substitute a new judge. Deadlines: before the hearing of any preliminary contested matter, and no later than 60 days after the summons and complaint are filed (plaintiff) or 60 days after that party was served (other parties). If a new judge is assigned for trial: 10 days after notice of assignment; 24 hours if notice comes less than 10 days before trial. After a reversal or ne","#st-wi-unique"],["Mandatory disqualification and recusal","Wisconsin · judges","A judge must disqualify for kinship to a party or counsel within the 3rd degree, being a party or material witness, prior work as counsel in the same case or on a disputed instrument, a significant financial or personal interest, or when the judge determines that the judge cannot, or it appears the judge cannot, act impartially. All parties and the judge may waive after full disclosure on the record. Abuse is referre","#st-wi-unique"],["Judge disqualified by interest: change of venue","Wisconsin · judges","When the judge is a party, interested, related to a party, or was counsel for a party, the court must change the place of trial or call in another judge on any party’s application. Wis. Stat. § 801.56","#st-wi-unique"],["Libel in a periodical: written retraction demand before suit","Wisconsin · speech","Before suing for libel published in a newspaper, magazine, or periodical, the plaintiff must give written notice identifying the article, the statements claimed to be false and defamatory, and the true facts, and allow a reasonable chance to correct. A timely correction published without comment and as prominently as the original limits recovery to actual damages. Also: a newspaper is not liable for a true and fair r","#st-wi-unique"],["Defamation pleading: quote the words","Wisconsin · speech","A libel or slander complaint must set out the particular words complained of; publication and application to the plaintiff may be pleaded generally. Wis. Stat. § 802.03(6)","#st-wi-unique"],["Constitutional speech protections broader than federal: not established here","Wisconsin · speech","Wisconsin Constitution art. I, §§ 3–4 protect speech, press, assembly, and petition. Whether Wisconsin courts read them more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked; the constitution and case law were not collected. Wis. Const. art. I, §§ 3–4 (not in collected texts)","#st-wi-unique"],["Procedure is statutory, not a separate rule book","Wisconsin · unique rules","Wisconsin’s rules of civil and appellate procedure are statutes (chs. 801–847 and ch. 809), most created or amended by Supreme Court order under s. 751.12. Cite them as ‘Wis. Stat. § 802.06,’ not as rules. Appellate rules carry the label ‘Rule’ in their titles (e.g., s. 809.19 Rule (Briefs and appendix)). Wis. Stat. §§ 751.12, 801.01(2)","#st-wi-unique"],["Unpublished Court of Appeals opinions","Wisconsin · unique rules","An unpublished opinion is never precedent. It may be cited for persuasive value only if issued on or after July 1, 2009 and authored by a judge (not per curiam, memorandum, or summary orders), and a copy must be filed with the brief. Otherwise it may be cited only for claim or issue preclusion or law of the case. Wis. Stat. § 809.23(3)","#st-wi-unique"],["Litigation-funding agreements must be disclosed without a request","Wisconsin · unique rules","A party must, without awaiting a discovery request, give the other parties any agreement under which someone other than the party’s contingent-fee lawyer has a right to compensation contingent on and sourced from the proceeds of the case. Wis. Stat. § 804.01(2)(bg)","#st-wi-unique"],["Rule 801.01. Kinds of proceedings; scope of chs. 801 to 847","Wisconsin · civil rules","Proceedings are actions or special proceedings. Chapters 801–847 govern all civil actions and special proceedings in circuit court unless a statute or rule prescribes a different procedure, and are construed to secure the just, speedy and inexpensive determination of every action (like FRCP 1).","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.02. Commencement of action","Wisconsin · civil rules","An action is commenced as to a defendant when the summons and complaint are filed, provided an authenticated copy is served on that defendant within 90 days after filing (unlike FRCP 3–4(m), the 90 days cannot be extended: s. 801.15(2)(a)). No service before filing. Fees are paid at filing. Prisoner suits about prison conditions require exhaustion of administrative remedies, and a prisoner with 3 prior dismissals for","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.03–801.04. Jurisdiction definitions; jurisdictional requirements","Wisconsin · civil rules","Defines defendant, person and plaintiff. Subject-matter jurisdiction comes from the constitution and statutes and cannot be conferred by consent. A personal judgment requires a jurisdictional ground under s. 801.05 or 801.06 plus service under s. 801.11 (or waiver); in rem or quasi in rem judgments proceed under ss. 801.07 and 801.12.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.05. Personal jurisdiction, grounds for generally (long-arm statute)","Wisconsin · civil rules","Lists the grounds for personal jurisdiction over a served defendant: presence, domicile, domestic entity, or substantial and not isolated activities in Wisconsin; local acts or omissions; local injury from a foreign act with solicitation or local use of products; local services, goods or contracts; local property; deficiency judgments; officers of Wisconsin corporations; taxes; insurance; certain family and restraini","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.06–801.08. Jurisdiction without summons; in rem grounds; objection to jurisdiction","Wisconsin · civil rules","A plaintiff is subject to jurisdiction on counterclaims, and a defendant who appears without raising the defense waives it (s. 802.06(8)). In rem or quasi in rem jurisdiction exists over property in the state, mortgage foreclosures, attached property, public nuisances, and marital status. Personal jurisdiction objections are tried by the court without a jury before the merits, and those findings do not bind the merit","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.09. Summons, contents of","Wisconsin · civil rules","The summons must give the court, county, case classification code, and party names and addresses, and direct the defendant to answer (or demand the complaint) within 20 days of personal or substituted service, 45 days if the state is the defendant or if an insurer is a defendant or any claim sounds in tort, or 40 days after first publication. It must be signed by hand by the plaintiff or attorney, with the attorney’s","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.095. Summons form","Wisconsin · civil rules","Prescribes four statutory summons forms: personal service with or without the complaint attached, and service without personal service (publication) with or without the complaint. Each warns that judgment may be entered and may become a lien on real estate or be enforced by garnishment.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.10. Summons, by whom served; proof","Wisconsin · civil rules","Any adult nonparty resident of the state where service is made may serve, and adult nonparty residents of Illinois, Iowa, Michigan or Minnesota may also serve in Wisconsin. The server endorses the summons and makes proof of service; if service is challenged, proof is by the server’s affidavit (showing reasonable diligence for substituted service), the sheriff’s certificate, a publisher’s affidavit plus mailing affida","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.11. Personal jurisdiction, manner of serving summons","Wisconsin · civil rules","Individuals: personal service in or out of state; only if that fails with reasonable diligence, leaving a copy at the usual abode with a competent family member at least 14 or a competent adult resident; only if both fail, publication as a class 3 notice plus mailing. Separate rules for minors under 14 and persons under guardianship, the state (Attorney General), counties, towns, cities, villages and school districts","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.12–801.13. In rem service; notice of object of action; when service is deemed made","Wisconsin · civil rules","In rem defendants may be served under s. 801.11 or, if unknown, by publication, with the complaint or a notice of object of action describing the property. Personal or substituted service is effective the day it is made; publication is effective on the first day of required publication.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.14. Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","Wisconsin · civil rules","Every pleading, discovery paper, written motion, notice and similar paper must be served on each party (parties in default need new-claim pleadings served as a summons). Serve the attorney of a represented party, by hand, mail, fax, or leaving at the office or abode; email service is allowed only with written consent (e-filing service is governed by s. 801.18). Mail service is complete on mailing; fax and email on tr","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.145. Form of papers","Wisconsin · civil rules","Papers other than exhibits and wills may be no larger than 8.5 by 11 inches; the clerk returns nonconforming papers.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.15. Time","Wisconsin · civil rules","Exclude the trigger day; include the last day unless the clerk’s office is closed. For periods under 11 days, exclude Saturdays, Sundays and holidays (including all of Good Friday). The court may enlarge time on motion for cause; after expiration only for excusable neglect. Not enlargeable: the 90-day service period, the time to appeal, the time to decide post-verdict motions, and the times for s. 805.17(3) and s. 80","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.16. Filing; facsimile filing","Wisconsin · civil rules","Papers are filed with the clerk of circuit court, and the judge may require a copy. Fax filing of no-fee papers is allowed under a chief-judge-approved local rule (15-page limit unless the judge approves) or with the assigned judge’s permission.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.18. Electronic filing (circuit court)","Wisconsin · civil rules","Licensed Wisconsin lawyers, lawyers admitted pro hac vice (after the PHV order), and high-volume filers must register and e-file all new filings; self-represented parties may opt in or use paper. Users must opt in on each case or receive no notices. A document is filed the day it is submitted by 11:59 p.m. Central time if later accepted by the clerk, but this does not change time computations. The notice of activity ","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.19–801.21. Protected information; confidential filings; motions to seal","Wisconsin · civil rules","Parties must omit or redact Social Security, taxpayer ID, financial account, driver license and passport numbers from filings and submit them on the protected information form when required; the court does not check. Documents on the Director of State Courts’ confidential list are automatically confidential if properly identified at filing. Otherwise, sealing or redaction requires a served motion stating the authorit","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.50. Venue","Wisconsin · civil rules","Venue lies where the claim arose, where the property at issue is, or where a defendant resides or does substantial business; otherwise in any county the plaintiff designates. Suits against the state are venued where the plaintiff designates (Dane County for prisoners); special rules for rule challenges, habeas corpus, redistricting and other matters. A venue defect does not invalidate any order or judgment.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.51–801.53. Challenges to venue; discretionary change","Wisconsin · civil rules","Improper venue is raised by a motion for change of venue made at or before the first motion or responsive pleading, or later if grounds could not be found with diligence. The court may change venue at any time in the interest of justice or for convenience (election and ethics forfeiture cases follow criminal venue rules). Venue motions are decided on submissions, with any argument by telephone unless ordered otherwis","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.54. Discretionary transfer to tribal court","Wisconsin · civil rules","Where a circuit court and a Wisconsin tribal court have concurrent jurisdiction, the circuit court may, after notice and hearing, transfer the action to the tribal court after weighing listed factors (including whether tribal law must be interpreted), unless all parties stipulate.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.56. Change of venue if judge disqualified by interest","Wisconsin · civil rules","If the judge is a party, interested, related to a party, or was counsel for a party, the court must change the place of trial or call in another judge on any party’s application, and may do so on its own.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.58. Substitution of judge","Wisconsin · civil rules","Each party (parties united in interest count as one) gets one peremptory request to substitute the assigned judge, filed before any preliminary contested matter is heard and within 60 days after filing (plaintiff) or service (others). A newly assigned trial judge: 10 days after notice, or 24 hours if notice comes within 10 days of trial. If timely and proper, the judge loses jurisdiction; a ruling of untimeliness is ","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.59. Assigned judge","Wisconsin · civil rules","When a judge is assigned under s. 751.03, the clerk notifies the parties, and a substitution request against that judge must be filed within 7 days after notice of the assignment.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.61–801.62. Proceedings after change of venue; conclusiveness","Wisconsin · civil rules","The clerk transmits the file to the new county; if transmission and filing are not made within 20 days after the order, the moving party loses the change. After trial in the new court, the change is conclusive unless a remand motion was made before trial; a reversed change may be renewed within 20 days after the papers return.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.63. Stay to permit trial in a foreign forum","Wisconsin · civil rules","On motion, the court may stay an action that in substantial justice should be tried outside Wisconsin (forum non conveniens), on conditions such as consent to jurisdiction and waiver of limitations. The court keeps jurisdiction for 5 years after the last order affecting the stay; then the clerk enters dismissal.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 801.64. Legislative findings; 2007 Wisconsin Act 1","Wisconsin · civil rules","Findings supporting a place of trial in the county where an offender resides under 2007 Wisconsin Act 1 (ethics and election enforcement); not a general procedure rule.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 802.01. Pleadings allowed; form of motions","Wisconsin · civil rules","Pleadings are the complaint, answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a cross claim, and third-party complaint and answer; no others unless ordered. Motions must be in writing with particular grounds and relief sought, and supporting papers must be served with the notice of motion. Orders to show cause may not be used unless a statute authorizes them.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 802.02. General rules of pleading","Wisconsin · civil rules","A claim requires a short and plain statement identifying the transaction or occurrence and showing entitlement to relief, plus a demand for judgment. Unlike FRCP 8, a tort claim for money may not state the amount sought (s. 802.02(1m)). Lists required affirmative defenses (including contributory negligence, superseding cause, and the statute of limitations). Averments not denied are admitted, except those about the f","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 802.025. Asbestos trust claims in personal injury actions","Wisconsin · civil rules","In asbestos-related personal injury actions, the plaintiff must give all parties a sworn statement of asbestos-trust claims within 45 days after joinder of issues and trust claim materials within 60 days, and supplement within 30 days of new claims; the court enters a trust claims document at least 30 days before trial. Collection of damages is conditioned on assignment of trust claims.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 802.03. Pleading special matters","Wisconsin · civil rules","Capacity is denied by specific negative averment or motion. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity. Conditions precedent may be pleaded generally but denied with particularity. In libel or slander, the complaint must set out the particular words. In goods or services claims, the plaintiff may itemize; otherwise must give an itemized statement within 10 days of a written demand.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 802.04. Form of pleadings","Wisconsin · civil rules","Every pleading needs a caption with court, venue, title, file number and designation; the complaint caption includes the case classification code. Claims are stated in numbered paragraphs, and exhibits are part of the pleading.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 802.045. Limited scope representation","Wisconsin · civil rules","A lawyer may appear for limited proceedings or issues by filing and serving a notice of limited appearance before or at the proceeding; it is not a general appearance. Both client and lawyer are served. The role ends without court order when the lawyer files and serves a notice of termination certifying the work and court-ordered acts are done. No FRCP counterpart.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 802.05. Signing; representations to court; sanctions","Wisconsin · civil rules","Mirrors FRCP 11: every paper is signed with the signer’s address, phone, email and bar number, and a lawyer must give the court a primary email address. A lawyer drafting for a self-represented person need not sign, but the paper must say ‘This document was prepared with the assistance of a lawyer.’ Sanctions motions have a 21-day safe harbor. Monetary sanctions are not available against a represented party for legal","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 802.06. Defenses and objections; motions","Wisconsin · civil rules","Answer within 20 days after service of the complaint; 45 days if an insurer is a defendant, any claim sounds in tort, or the state is a defendant (same for replies and cross-claim answers). Ten listed defenses may be raised by pre-answer motion, including res judicata, statute of limitations, and another action pending, beyond the FRCP 12(b) list; venue is raised under s. 801.51. A motion to dismiss for failure to st","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 802.07. Counterclaim and cross claim","Wisconsin · civil rules","A defendant may counterclaim any claim against the plaintiff; unlike FRCP 13(a), the statute does not label any counterclaim compulsory. Cross claims must arise from the same transaction or concern the same property. Additional parties may be joined, and separate judgments may be entered.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 802.08. Summary judgment","Wisconsin · civil rules","A party may move within 8 months after the summons and complaint are filed, or within the time in a scheduling order. Serve the motion at least 20 days before the hearing; opposing affidavits at least 5 days before, unless the scheduling order sets earlier times. The standard matches FRCP 56 (no genuine issue of material fact). Affidavits must be on personal knowledge with admissible facts; the opponent cannot rest o","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 802.09. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Wisconsin · civil rules","A party may amend once as of course within 6 months after the summons and complaint are filed (or as the scheduling order sets); after that, only with leave (freely given) or written consent. Respond within 20 days (45 days in tort, insurer or state cases). Amendments to conform to the evidence and relation back follow FRCP 15, including relation back for a changed party who had notice within the limitations period.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 802.10. Calendar practice; scheduling and pretrial","Wisconsin · civil rules","Except in exempt case types (e.g., small claims, family, certiorari, probate), the court may enter a scheduling order after consulting counsel, covering joinder, amendment, motions, discovery, experts, ESI, ADR, and, within 30 days after entry of the order, the mode of trial including the jury demand and fee. Pretrial conferences address issues, evidence and settlement; attorneys must have authority to stipulate. Vio","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 802.12. Alternative dispute resolution","Wisconsin · civil rules","Defines settlement alternatives (binding and nonbinding arbitration, mediation, early neutral evaluation, mini-trial, summary jury trial, etc.). The judge may order parties to select one and may require personal participation, but without consent may not order arbitration or summary jury trial, and the order may not delay the schedule. Aggrieved parties get a hearing.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 803.01. Parties; real party in interest; capacity; guardians ad litem","Wisconsin · civil rules","No dismissal for lack of the real party in interest until a reasonable time is allowed for ratification, joinder or substitution. Minors and incompetent persons appear by guardian ad litem; a defendant minor 14 or older may apply within 20 days after service, otherwise the court appoints.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 803.02. Joinder of claims and remedies","Wisconsin · civil rules","A party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party, and claims normally cognizable only after another (e.g., a creditor’s claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance) may be joined.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 803.03. Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","Wisconsin · civil rules","Follows FRCP 19: a person must be joined if feasible when complete relief requires it or the person’s interest may be impaired or others exposed to inconsistent obligations; if joinder is not feasible, the court decides whether to proceed. Unlike FRCP 19, a claimant must also join everyone holding subrogated, derivative (including loss of consortium) or partially assigned claims; special rules apply to public assista","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 803.04. Permissive joinder of parties","Wisconsin · civil rules","Plaintiffs and defendants may be joined when claims arise from the same transaction or series and share a common question (like FRCP 20). In negligence actions, an insurer with an interest adverse to the plaintiff or that controls the defense may be joined directly as a defendant (s. 803.04(2)), a significant difference from federal practice. The court may order separate trials.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 803.045. Actions to satisfy spousal obligations","Wisconsin · civil rules","A creditor suing on a family-purpose or marital-property obligation under s. 766.55(2) may proceed against the obligated or incurring spouse or both, and against the other spouse in limited circumstances.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 803.05. Third-party practice","Wisconsin · civil rules","A defending party may implead a person who is or may be liable to it for the plaintiff’s claim, or a necessary party under s. 803.03, without leave if served within 6 months after the summons and complaint are filed (or by the scheduling order deadline); after that, by motion. The third-party defendant may assert defenses and claims as in FRCP 14.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 803.06–803.07. Misjoinder and nonjoinder; interpleader","Wisconsin · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped and claims severed. Claimants against the plaintiff may be joined and required to interplead when the plaintiff faces double or multiple liability; a defendant may obtain interpleader by counterclaim or cross claim.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 803.08. Class actions","Wisconsin · civil rules","Rewritten to track FRCP 23: numerosity, commonality, typicality, adequacy, and the three types of class actions; certification order, notice, settlement approval, objectors, and class counsel. At least 50 percent of residual funds go to the Wisconsin Trust Account Foundation. An order granting or denying certification or denying decertification is appealable as of right if the notice of appeal is filed within 14 days","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 803.09. Intervention","Wisconsin · civil rules","Intervention of right and permissive intervention follow FRCP 24. The Assembly, Senate and Legislature may intervene as of right at any time when a party challenges the constitutionality, validity or construction of a statute (s. 803.09(2m), with s. 13.365). A motion to intervene must be served with a pleading stating the claim or defense.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 803.10. Substitution of parties","Wisconsin · civil rules","On death, a motion to substitute must be made within 90 days after death is suggested on the record or the action is dismissed as to the decedent. Also covers incompetency, transfer of interest, and public officers who leave office.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 804.01. General provisions governing discovery","Wisconsin · civil rules","Scope: nonprivileged matter relevant to a claim or defense and proportional to the needs of the case (as in FRCP 26(b)(1)). There are no FRCP 26(a)-style initial disclosures, but litigation-funding agreements must be disclosed without a request (s. 804.01(2)(bg)). Testifying experts are discovered by interrogatory and deposition; consulting experts only on exceptional circumstances. Certain inaccessible ESI need not ","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 804.015. Limits on discovery by prisoners","Wisconsin · civil rules","A prisoner may not obtain discovery until the court receives the answer, and none while a motion to dismiss or for summary judgment is pending, unless the court orders otherwise.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 804.02. Perpetuation of testimony","Wisconsin · civil rules","Before an action, a verified petition may be filed to depose a witness to perpetuate testimony, with notice served at least 20 days before the hearing. Pending appeal, the trial court may allow depositions to perpetuate testimony.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 804.03–804.04. Persons before whom depositions are taken; stipulations","Wisconsin · civil rules","Depositions are taken before an officer authorized to administer oaths where taken, or a court-appointed person; rules for foreign depositions and disqualification for interest. Parties may stipulate in writing to modify deposition and discovery procedures unless the court orders otherwise.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 804.045. Limits on number of depositions","Wisconsin · civil rules","Absent stipulation or order, each party is limited to 10 depositions, none longer than 7 hours (FRCP 30 sets 10 depositions per side and one 7-hour day).","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 804.05. Depositions upon oral examination","Wisconsin · civil rules","Any party may depose any person after commencement (prisoners only with leave). Reasonable written notice to all parties; party deponents may be compelled by notice; nonparties by subpoena. Provides for organizational (designee) depositions, remote and non-stenographic recording, objections, and termination motions. The deponent has 30 days to read and sign the transcript.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 804.06. Depositions upon written questions","Wisconsin · civil rules","After notice and service of questions, cross questions are due within 30 days, redirect within 10 days after cross, and recross within 10 days after redirect.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 804.07. Use of depositions in court proceedings","Wisconsin · civil rules","Depositions are usable against parties present or noticed: to impeach, an adverse party’s or its designee’s for any purpose, and a nonparty’s if unavailable (dead, more than 30 miles away or out of state, ill, or not subpoenable). Unlike the federal rule, any party may use a medical expert’s deposition for any purpose. Objections to the form of written questions must be served within the time for the next questions a","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 804.08. Interrogatories to parties","Wisconsin · civil rules","Limited to 25 including subparts absent stipulation or order. Answer and object within 30 days after service, except a defendant has 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Business records may be produced instead when the burden is equal.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 804.09. Production of documents and inspection","Wisconsin · civil rules","Requests to produce documents, ESI and things or to enter land; absent stipulation or order, requests are limited to a reasonable time period not exceeding 5 years before the claim accrued (no federal counterpart). Respond in writing within 30 days, or 45 days after service of the summons and complaint for a defendant. Covers form of ESI production. Nonparties are reached by subpoena (s. 805.07).","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 804.10. Physical and mental examinations; medical records","Wisconsin · civil rules","The court may order a physical, mental or vocational examination for cause when a party’s condition is in issue. In personal injury actions the claimant must give access to X-rays and medical records. Reports must be exchanged, and a report is inadmissible unless copies of all reports received are delivered within 10 days of receipt.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 804.11. Requests for admission","Wisconsin · civil rules","Matters are admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days after service, but a defendant need not respond until 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Denials must fairly meet the request; lack of knowledge requires reasonable inquiry. Admissions may be withdrawn or amended on motion; unreasonable failure to admit leads to expenses.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 804.12. Failure to make discovery; sanctions","Wisconsin · civil rules","Motions to compel, with mandatory expenses to the winner unless substantially justified. For violation of an order: deemed facts, preclusion, striking pleadings, dismissal, default, contempt, and expenses. A party’s failure to appear for its own noticed deposition, or to serve any answers or response to interrogatories or inspection requests, or to supplement, allows sanctions without a prior order. Includes ESI safe","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.01. Jury trial of right","Wisconsin · civil rules","The constitutional and statutory jury right is preserved. A party must demand a jury (in writing or orally on the record) at or before the scheduling or pretrial conference, whichever comes first, or the right is waived; payment of the jury fee is set in the scheduling order (s. 802.10(3)(e)). A jury demand may not be withdrawn without the parties’ consent.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.02. Advisory jury and trial by consent","Wisconsin · civil rules","In nonjury actions the court may use an advisory jury, or with both parties’ consent a jury with the effect of a jury of right.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.03. Failure to prosecute or comply with procedure","Wisconsin · civil rules","The court may make just orders, including s. 804.12 sanctions and dismissal, for failure to prosecute or obey statutes or orders. A dismissal is on the merits unless the order states otherwise for good cause. A dismissal on the merits may be set aside under s. 806.07; one not on the merits for good cause within a reasonable time.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.04. Voluntary dismissal","Wisconsin · civil rules","The plaintiff may dismiss without order before service of a responsive pleading or motion, or by stipulation of all appearing parties; it is without prejudice unless the plaintiff has previously dismissed an action including the same claim (two-dismissal rule). Otherwise dismissal requires a court order on terms. Costs of a previously dismissed action may be ordered.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.05. Consolidation; separate trials","Wisconsin · civil rules","Actions that could have been joined may be consolidated or tried jointly, including transfer between counties by joint order of both courts. The court may order separate trials of claims or issues for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or for economy.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.06. Referees","Wisconsin · civil rules","The court may appoint a referee and fix fees. Hearings begin within 20 days after the order of reference unless it provides otherwise. Objections to the referee’s report are due within 10 days after notice of its filing.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.07. Subpoena","Wisconsin · civil rules","Subpoenas issue under ch. 885, and any attorney of record may issue one for deposition, hearing or trial. Documents and ESI may be commanded. Notice of a third-party discovery subpoena must go to all parties at least 10 days before the scheduled deposition, and the materials may not be produced before the subpoena date unless all parties agree. The court may quash or modify oppressive subpoenas and condition them on ","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.08. Jurors","Wisconsin · civil rules","The court examines jurors under oath for relationship, interest, opinion and bias; parties may supplement voir dire. Each side gets 3 peremptory challenges, exercised alternately starting with the plaintiff; adverse co-defendants may get up to 3 more, and each side gets one more when extra jurors are selected. Extra jurors are excused by lot before deliberation.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.09. Jury size; five-sixths verdict","Wisconsin · civil rules","The jury’s size is set by s. 756.06(2)(b) (not collected). A verdict agreed to by five-sixths of the jurors is the verdict, and the same five-sixths must agree on all questions needed for a verdict on a claim. No unanimity requirement in civil cases.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.10. Examination of witnesses; arguments","Wisconsin · civil rules","Unless the judge orders otherwise, one attorney per side examines a witness and no more than 2 per side argue. The plaintiff opens and closes; rebuttal is limited to matters raised by the opponent. The court may limit argument time.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.11. Objections; exceptions","Wisconsin · civil rules","A party with fair opportunity to object before a ruling must do so with specific grounds or the error is waived; formal exceptions are abolished. Evidentiary objections follow s. 901.03.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.12. Special verdicts","Wisconsin · civil rules","Unless it orders otherwise, the court directs a special verdict of written questions on ultimate facts (Wisconsin’s default, unlike FRCP 49’s option). In negligence cases each respect of negligence need not be submitted separately. An omitted issue not raised by any party is deemed found by the court consistent with the judgment.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.13. Jury instructions; note-taking; form of verdict","Wisconsin · civil rules","Statements by the judge to the jury must be on the record. The court decides whether jurors may take notes and may give preliminary instructions. At the instruction and verdict conference, counsel must object with particularity on the record, or errors in the proposed instructions or verdict are waived. The jury gets one complete set of written instructions.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.14. Sufficiency of evidence; motions after verdict","Wisconsin · civil rules","A challenge to sufficiency succeeds only if there is no credible evidence to sustain a finding for the nonmovant. Involuntary nonsuit is abolished. Motions to dismiss at the close of plaintiff’s case, for directed verdict at the close of all evidence, and after verdict for JNOV, to change answers, and for judgment on the verdict (not required).","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.15. New trials","Wisconsin · civil rules","Grounds: trial errors, verdict contrary to law or the weight of evidence, excessive or inadequate damages, newly discovered evidence, or the interest of justice; orders must state the reasons. Newly discovered evidence requires diligence, materiality and a probable different result. For excessive or inadequate damages, the court sets a reasonable amount and gives the party 10 days to accept remittitur or additur or f","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.16. Time for motions after verdict","Wisconsin · civil rules","Motions after verdict must be filed and served within 20 days after the verdict unless the court sets a longer schedule within those 20 days; hearing between 10 and 60 days after verdict. If not decided within 90 days after the verdict, the motion is deemed denied and judgment is entered (not extendable). A newly-discovered-evidence motion may be made within one year after verdict and is deemed denied if not decided ","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.17. Trial to the court","Wisconsin · civil rules","After the plaintiff rests, the defendant may move to dismiss. The court finds ultimate facts and states conclusions of law, which are reviewed for clear error, within 60 days after submission. A motion to amend findings or the judgment, or for a new trial, is due within 20 days after entry of judgment; if not decided within 90 days after entry, it is deemed denied and the appeal time runs from day 90.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 805.18. Harmless error","Wisconsin · civil rules","Courts disregard errors that do not affect substantial rights, and no judgment is reversed or new trial granted for jury, evidence, pleading or procedure errors unless substantial rights were affected.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.01. Judgment","Wisconsin · civil rules","A judgment determines the action and may be final or interlocutory. It grants all relief to which the party is entitled even if not demanded, but on default may not exceed the complaint’s demand; in tort money claims the plaintiff must specify the amount before judgment. Partial and interlocutory judgments are allowed.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.02. Default judgment","Wisconsin · civil rules","When no issue has been joined and the time has expired, a party may move for default judgment on proof of service and an affidavit of default; appearing defendants get notice. If the defendant never appeared, the court requires proof of service and of facts showing personal jurisdiction. The clerk may enter judgment on an express contract for a liquidated sum after personal service (not abode service). Default is als","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.025. Payment of judgments to prisoners","Wisconsin · civil rules","A money judgment for a prisoner is applied first to unpaid restitution and crime-victim judgments, then to other listed obligations.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.03. Judgment on admitted claim","Wisconsin · civil rules","On an express contract for a liquidated sum, the clerk enters judgment for any amount the answer admits, and the court may order payment of the admitted part.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.04. Uniform Declaratory Judgments Act","Wisconsin · civil rules","Courts may declare rights, status and legal relations under instruments, statutes, ordinances and contracts, before or after breach, with the effect of a final judgment, but appeal finality is governed by s. 808.03(1). All persons with an interest affected must be made parties, and the Attorney General must be served when a statute’s validity is challenged.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.05. Declaratory judgments against obscene matter","Wisconsin · civil rules","A special in rem proceeding in which a district attorney seeks a declaration that specific matter is obscene, with notice, hearing and jury rights.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.06. Rendition, perfection and entry of judgment","Wisconsin · civil rules","A judgment is rendered when signed, entered when filed with the clerk, perfected by taxing costs, and granted when given orally on the record. The prevailing party must perfect within 30 days of entry or lose costs. Any party may serve notice of entry; notice given within 21 days of entry shortens the appeal time to 45 days (s. 808.04(1)).","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.07. Relief from judgment or order","Wisconsin · civil rules","Grounds mirror FRCP 60(b): mistake, inadvertence, surprise or excusable neglect; newly discovered evidence; fraud or misconduct; void judgment; satisfaction; reversal of a prior judgment; prospective inequity; or any other reason. Motions within a reasonable time, and no more than one year for mistake or fraud grounds; newly-discovered-evidence motions follow s. 805.16. The motion does not affect finality.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.08. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","Wisconsin · civil rules","Injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed unless ordered. The court may stay enforcement pending post-trial or relief motions on security, and may suspend or modify an injunction pending appeal. Stays pending appeal are governed by s. 808.07 (bond capped at $100 million).","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.09. Restitution after reversal","Wisconsin · civil rules","If a collected judgment is reversed, the trial court orders restitution with interest; if a new trial is ordered, the collecting party may keep the money on bond.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.10–806.14. Judgment and lien docket; transcripts to other counties","Wisconsin · civil rules","Money judgments are entered in the judgment and lien docket on payment of the fee. Covers tax warrants, transcripts of municipal court judgments, and docketing a transcript of a judgment in other counties to create a lien there.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.15. Lien of judgment","Wisconsin · civil rules","A docketed judgment is a lien for 10 years from entry on the debtor’s nonexempt real property in the county (not the exempt homestead), including after-acquired property. The period is suspended while enforcement is delayed by law, and the lien may be released when the judgment is secured on appeal.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.16–806.17. Appellate and federal judgments","Wisconsin · civil rules","Appellate judgments (e.g., for costs) are entered in the circuit court on remittitur. Federal court judgments may be docketed in the county to create a lien.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.18–806.22. Assignment and satisfaction of judgments","Wisconsin · civil rules","Assignments are filed and noted on the docket. Judgments are satisfied by an acknowledged instrument or docket entry; the court may order satisfaction. A creditor who fails to satisfy within 7 days after full payment and request owes $50 plus actual damages. Satisfied judgments are not liens; partial satisfactions are recorded.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.23. Action on judgment","Wisconsin · civil rules","No new action may be brought on a Wisconsin judgment between the same parties without leave of court for good cause on notice.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.24. Uniform Enforcement of Foreign Judgments Act","Wisconsin · civil rules","An authenticated sister-state or federal judgment filed with the clerk is treated as a Wisconsin judgment. The creditor files an affidavit with names and addresses, the clerk mails notice, and no execution may issue until 15 days after filing. Enforcement is stayed on proof of a pending appeal or stay with the required security.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.245. Tribal court documents: full faith and credit","Wisconsin · civil rules","Judgments, records and acts of Wisconsin tribal courts and legislative bodies get full faith and credit if the tribe is federally organized, documents are authenticated, the tribal court is a court of record, the judgment is valid, and the tribe reciprocates.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.25. No judgment without action","Wisconsin · civil rules","A cognovit authorization in a note to confess judgment without an action is void.","#st-wi-rules"],["Rule 806.30–806.44. Uniform Foreign-Money Claims Act","Wisconsin · civil rules","For claims payable in foreign currency: the parties may agree on the money of the claim; otherwise the court determines it. Judgment is stated in the foreign money, payable in that money or, at the debtor’s option, in U.S. dollars at a bank-offered spot rate on the conversion date. Covers interest, enforcement of foreign-money judgments from other states, distribution proceedings, and currency revalorizations.","#st-wi-rules"],["Appeals in Wisconsin: the clock runs from entry of the final judgment, and nothing extends it","Wisconsin · appeals","Civil appeals go from the circuit court to the Court of Appeals as of right from a final judgment or final order (one that disposes of the entire matter as to one or more parties), or by permission from a nonfinal order. The Supreme Court reviews the Court of Appeals only by discretionary petition for review, or takes a case early by bypass or certification.\nThe notice of appeal is filed in the circuit court within 4","#st-wi-appeals"],["Appeal as of right: final judgments and final orders","Wisconsin · appeals · What can be appealed","A final judgment or order is one that disposes of the entire matter in litigation as to one or more parties and is entered under s. 806.06(1)(b) (filed with the clerk) or s. 807.11(2). Unless the law says otherwise, it is appealable as of right to the Court of Appeals. A declaratory judgment is final for appeal only if it meets this test. Wis. Stat. §§ 808.03(1), 806.04(1)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Appeal by permission: nonfinal orders","Wisconsin · appeals · What can be appealed","A nonfinal judgment or order may be appealed only with leave of the Court of Appeals, granted if an immediate appeal will materially advance the termination of the litigation or clarify further proceedings, protect the petitioner from substantial or irreparable injury, or clarify an issue of general importance in the administration of justice. Wis. Stat. § 808.03(2); § 809.50","#st-wi-appeals"],["Class certification orders: appeal of right within 14 days","Wisconsin · appeals · What can be appealed","An appellate court must hear an appeal of an order granting or denying class certification, or denying decertification, if the notice of appeal is filed within 14 days after entry. Discovery and other proceedings are stayed during that appeal, except the trial court may approve a settlement. Wis. Stat. § 803.08(11)(b)","#st-wi-appeals"],["What an appeal from the final judgment brings up","Wisconsin · appeals · What can be appealed","An appeal from a final judgment or order brings before the court all prior nonfinal rulings adverse to the appellant and favorable to the respondent that were not previously appealed and decided. Wis. Stat. § 809.10(4)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Notice of appeal: 45 days with notice of entry, 90 days without","Wisconsin · appeals · Starting the appeal","File the notice of appeal with the clerk of the circuit court within 45 days of entry of the final judgment or order if written notice of entry was given within 21 days of entry; otherwise within 90 days. A notice filed before entry is treated as filed on the day of entry. If a party dies during the appeal period, the time is the normal period or 120 days after death, whichever is later. Shorter periods apply to some","#st-wi-appeals"],["Contents, docketing statement, and fee","Wisconsin · appeals · Starting the appeal","The notice must give the circuit court case name and number, identify the judgment or order and its entry date, say whether the case is one decided by a single judge under s. 752.31(2), and whether it has statutory preference. A docketing statement on the Court of Appeals form must accompany it (not required for self-represented parties and some case types). The $195 filing fee is paid to the clerk of the Court of Ap","#st-wi-appeals"],["Ask for a 3-judge panel at the start in one-judge case types","Wisconsin · appeals · Starting the appeal","In case types decided by a single judge (small claims, ordinance and traffic, ch. 48/938, ch. 51/55, misdemeanors, forfeitures, contempt), an appellant who wants a 3-judge panel must file the motion with the notice of appeal; any other party within 14 days after service of the notice. Failing to file waives the request. Wis. Stat. §§ 752.31(2)-(3), 809.41(1)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Transcript: order it and file a statement on transcript in 14 days","Wisconsin · appeals · Starting the appeal","Within 14 days after filing the notice of appeal, the appellant must request and arrange payment for the transcript and file a statement on transcript in the circuit court (or state that none is needed). Other parties may designate more within 14 days after that. The reporter has 60 days to file and serve the transcript. The circuit clerk transmits the record within 20 days after the transcript is filed. Wis. Stat. §","#st-wi-appeals"],["No tolling: motions after verdict are decided before judgment","Wisconsin · appeals · Post-judgment motions and the appeal clock","Motions after verdict (new trial, JNOV, change of answer) must be filed and served within 20 days after the verdict unless the court, within those 20 days, sets a longer schedule. If the court does not decide them within 90 days after the verdict, they are deemed denied and judgment is entered on the verdict. The 90-day decision period cannot be enlarged. The appeal then runs from entry of judgment. Wis. Stat. §§ 805","#st-wi-appeals"],["Bench trials: motion to amend within 20 days after judgment","Wisconsin · appeals · Post-judgment motions and the appeal clock","In a trial to the court, a motion to amend findings, conclusions, or the judgment must be made within 20 days after entry of judgment. If not decided within 90 days after entry, it is deemed denied and the appeal time from the judgment starts 90 days after entry. This time cannot be enlarged. Wis. Stat. §§ 805.17(3), 801.15(2)(c)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Relief from judgment does not affect finality","Wisconsin · appeals · Post-judgment motions and the appeal clock","A s. 806.07 motion (mistake, new evidence, fraud, void judgment, etc.) must be made within a reasonable time, and within one year for mistake or fraud grounds. It does not affect finality or suspend the judgment, so it does not extend the appeal time. The circuit court may hear it while an appeal is pending. Wis. Stat. §§ 806.07(2), 808.07(2)(am), 808.075(1)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Cross-appeal needed to change the judgment","Wisconsin · appeals · Cross-appeals and multiple appellants","A respondent who seeks to modify the judgment or order appealed from, or another judgment or order in the same action, must file a notice of cross-appeal within the time for a notice of appeal or 30 days after the notice of appeal is filed, whichever is later. A cross-appellant has an appellant’s rights and duties. Wis. Stat. § 809.10(2)(b)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Joint and co-appeals; consolidation","Wisconsin · appeals · Cross-appeals and multiple appellants","Parties entitled to appeal the same judgment may file a joint notice or proceed as a single appellant; otherwise they proceed as appellant and co-appellant with equal rights. The court may consolidate appeals from separate cases. Wis. Stat. § 809.10(2)(a), (3)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Schedule: 40 / 30 / 15 days","Wisconsin · appeals · Briefs","Appellant’s brief: 40 days after the record is filed in the Court of Appeals. Respondent’s brief: 30 days after the later of service of the appellant’s brief (plus 3 days if served by mail), its filing, or filing of the record. Reply (or a statement that none will be filed): 15 days after the later of service (plus 3 days for mail) or filing of the respondent’s brief. Wis. Stat. § 809.19(1), (3)(a), (4)(a)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Length: 11,000 words main briefs, 3,000 words reply","Wisconsin · appeals · Briefs","Main briefs: 11,000 words in a proportional serif font, or 50 pages monospaced, counting the statement of the case, argument, and conclusion. Reply briefs and nonparty briefs: 3,000 words or 13 pages. Proportional font at least 13-point body text; certify form and length with the brief. Wis. Stat. § 809.19(8)(b)-(c), (8g)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Appendix and unpublished opinions","Wisconsin · appeals · Briefs","The appellant files a short appendix with the circuit court’s findings or opinion, essential parts of the record showing the court’s reasoning, and a copy of any unpublished opinion cited, with a certification. Unpublished opinions issued on or after July 1, 2009 and authored by a judge may be cited only as persuasive authority. Wis. Stat. §§ 809.19(2)(a), (8g)(b), 809.23(3)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Amicus (nonparty) briefs need leave","Wisconsin · appeals · Briefs","A nonparty must move for permission, stating its interest and why the brief is desirable. A brief supporting or opposing a petition for review or original action accompanies the motion and is due within the time for the opposing party’s response. Wis. Stat. § 809.19(7)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Appeal does not stay the judgment; move first in the trial court","Wisconsin · appeals · Stays, bonds, and motions","An appeal does not stay enforcement unless relief is granted. The trial or appellate court may stay enforcement, modify an injunction, or preserve the status quo, usually conditioned on an undertaking. Seek relief in the trial court first unless impractical. Injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed unless ordered. Wis. Stat. §§ 808.07(1)-(2), 809.12, 806.08(1), (3)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Supersedeas cap of $100 million","Wisconsin · appeals · Stays, bonds, and motions","In a civil action, the court sets the undertaking to stay execution for all appellants collectively, but it may not exceed $100,000,000, unless the appellee proves by a preponderance that the appellant is dissipating assets to avoid payment. Objections to a surety must be made within 14 days after service of the undertaking. Wis. Stat. § 808.07(2m), (6)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Motions practice","Wisconsin · appeals · Stays, bonds, and motions","Responses to motions are due 11 days after service. Motions that may affect the disposition or a brief, consolidation motions, transcript-related motions, and motions to supplement or correct the record automatically toll appellate deadlines while pending. Wis. Stat. § 809.14(1), (3)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Oral argument and publication","Wisconsin · appeals · Decision, costs, and reconsideration","The court decides whether to hear argument or decide on briefs. Publication is decided by a committee of Court of Appeals judges; one-judge decisions and most per curiam opinions are not published. Wis. Stat. §§ 809.22, 809.23(1)-(2)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Reconsideration in the Court of Appeals: 20 days, 1,100 words","Wisconsin · appeals · Decision, costs, and reconsideration","A motion for reconsideration may be filed within 20 days after the decision; it must state the points with particularity and may not exceed 1,100 words (5 pages monospaced). No response unless ordered. This time cannot be enlarged. A timely motion postpones the 30-day petition-for-review period until the court rules. Wis. Stat. §§ 809.24(1), 809.82(2)(e), 808.10(2)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Costs and frivolous-appeal sanctions","Wisconsin · appeals · Decision, costs, and reconsideration","Costs generally go against the losing side. File a statement of costs within 14 days of the decision; objections within 11 days. For an appeal taken for delay, the court may award double costs, a penalty up to 10% of the judgment, damages, and attorney fees; the motion must be filed by the time the respondent’s brief is filed. Failure to follow other rules is not jurisdictional but can lead to dismissal or summary re","#st-wi-appeals"],["Remittitur","Wisconsin · appeals · Decision, costs, and reconsideration","The record and decision go back to the circuit court 31 days after the decision, stayed while a petition for review or reconsideration is pending. After remittitur, a party has one year to move for further proceedings when the mandate does not order specific action or a new trial. A party may file a new judge-substitution request within 20 days after the remittitur is filed when further trial-court proceedings are ne","#st-wi-appeals"],["Petition for review: 30 days, 8,000 words","Wisconsin · appeals · Supreme Court review","File within 30 days of the Court of Appeals decision (or of the order on a timely reconsideration motion). Review is discretionary and granted only for special and important reasons (constitutional questions, policy, law development, conflicts, or precedent ripe for reexamination). The petition is limited to 8,000 words (35 pages monospaced) plus a separate appendix; no supporting memorandum is allowed. Response due ","#st-wi-appeals"],["Petition for cross-review","Wisconsin · appeals · Supreme Court review","A party seeking to reverse or modify the Court of Appeals decision must file a petition for cross-review within the time for a petition for review or 30 days after another party’s petition is filed, whichever is later. Not needed merely to defend the result on other grounds. Wis. Stat. § 809.62(3m)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Bypass and certification","Wisconsin · appeals · Supreme Court review","A party may petition to bypass the Court of Appeals no later than 14 days after the respondent’s brief is filed; responses are due 14 days after service, and the petition stops the Court of Appeals from taking the case under submission. The Court of Appeals may also certify a case, or the Supreme Court may take it on its own motion. Wis. Stat. §§ 808.05, 809.60, 809.61","#st-wi-appeals"],["After review is granted; reconsideration","Wisconsin · appeals · Supreme Court review","Court of Appeals procedure applies in the Supreme Court unless the court orders otherwise. A motion for reconsideration of a Supreme Court decision is due within 20 days. Wis. Stat. §§ 809.63, 809.64","#st-wi-appeals"],["Original actions and supervisory writs","Wisconsin · appeals · Supreme Court review","The Supreme Court may be asked to take an original action or issue a supervisory writ by petition (ss. 809.70–809.71). The Court of Appeals has its own supervisory and prerogative writ jurisdiction; those petitions are limited to 8,000 words and responses are due 14 days after the court orders one. Wis. Stat. §§ 752.01(2), 752.02, 809.51, 809.70, 809.71","#st-wi-appeals"],["Petition for leave to appeal: 14 days","Wisconsin · appeals · Interlocutory appeals","File a petition (and any memorandum) in the Court of Appeals within 14 days after entry of the nonfinal order, limited to 8,000 words, with a copy of the order and a showing under the s. 808.03(2) criteria. Responses are due 14 days after service. If leave is granted, the order counts as the notice of appeal and a docketing statement is due within 11 days. Wis. Stat. § 809.50(1)-(3)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Appellate e-filing is mandatory for lawyers","Wisconsin · appeals · E-filing, time computation, and format","Wisconsin lawyers and PHV lawyers must register and e-file in the Supreme Court and Court of Appeals. A document is filed on a day if submission is completed by 11:59 p.m. Central time and the clerk later accepts it. Self-represented parties may opt in or file on paper. Wis. Stat. § 809.801(2)-(4)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Computing time; extensions","Wisconsin · appeals · E-filing, time computation, and format","Use s. 801.15(1) and (5): exclude weekends and holidays for periods under 11 days; add 3 days for mail service. The court may enlarge most deadlines for good cause, but not the time for a notice of appeal or cross-appeal from a final judgment (outside certain criminal and TPR appeals) or for reconsideration under s. 809.24. Wis. Stat. §§ 809.82(1)-(2), 801.15(1), (5)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Format","Wisconsin · appeals · E-filing, time computation, and format","Fit 8.5 x 11 paper, monospaced or proportional serif font, double-spaced, 1.25-inch side margins and 1-inch top and bottom, paginated at the bottom center. Captions use full party names with circuit and appellate designations; confidential matters use initials. Wis. Stat. § 809.81","#st-wi-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (final judgment or order), notice of entry given","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","45 days from entry of the final judgment or order, if written notice of entry was given within 21 days of entry.\nJurisdictional; cannot be enlarged (s. 809.82(2)(b)). Wis. Stat. §§ 808.04(1), 806.06(5)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (final judgment or order), no notice of entry","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","90 days from entry of the final judgment or order.\nJurisdictional; cannot be enlarged. Wis. Stat. § 808.04(1)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Notice of entry of judgment or order (to start the 45-day period)","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","21 days from entry of the judgment or order.\nLate notice leaves the 90-day period in place. Wis. Stat. § 806.06(5)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Notice of cross-appeal","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the notice of appeal (or the full appeal period, if later).\nWhichever is later of the appeal period or 30 days after the notice of appeal. Wis. Stat. § 809.10(2)(b)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Appeal of class certification order","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of the order.\nProceedings stayed during the appeal. Wis. Stat. § 803.08(11)(b)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Petition for leave to appeal nonfinal order","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from entry of the judgment or order.\n8,000-word limit; response due 14 days after service. Wis. Stat. § 809.50(1)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Motions after verdict","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from verdict.\nUnless the court sets a longer time by order within the 20 days. Wis. Stat. § 805.16(1)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Decision on motions after verdict (else deemed denied)","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","90 days from verdict.\nCannot be enlarged (s. 801.15(2)(c)); judgment then entered on the verdict. Wis. Stat. § 805.16(3)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Motion to amend findings or judgment (bench trial)","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from entry of judgment.\nDeemed denied if undecided 90 days after entry; appeal time then runs from day 90. Wis. Stat. § 805.17(3)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Motion for new trial on newly discovered evidence","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","365 days from verdict.\nDeemed denied unless decided within 90 days after the motion. Wis. Stat. § 805.16(4)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Relief from judgment for mistake, excusable neglect, or fraud","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","365 days from entry of judgment or making of the order or stipulation.\n‘Not more than one year’; other grounds within a reasonable time. Wis. Stat. § 806.07(2)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Statement on transcript; request and pay for transcript","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nFiled in the circuit court. Wis. Stat. § 809.11(4)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Motion for 3-judge panel by a party other than appellant","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the notice of appeal.\nAppellant must file with the notice of appeal; failure waives. Wis. Stat. § 809.41(1)(d)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from filing of the record in the Court of Appeals.\n11,000 words. Wis. Stat. § 809.19(1)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Respondent’s brief","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from later of service (+3 days if mailed) or filing of appellant’s brief, or filing of the record.\n11,000 words. Wis. Stat. § 809.19(3)(a)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Reply brief or statement that none will be filed","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from later of service (+3 days if mailed) or filing of respondent’s brief.\n3,000 words. Wis. Stat. § 809.19(4)(a)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Response to a motion in the appellate court","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","11 days from service of the motion.\n5 days in TPR appeals. Wis. Stat. § 809.14(1)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Petition to bypass the Court of Appeals","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the respondent’s brief or response.\nResponse due 14 days after service. Wis. Stat. § 809.60(1)(a)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Motion for reconsideration in the Court of Appeals","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from date of the decision.\nCannot be enlarged (s. 809.82(2)(e)); 1,100 words. Wis. Stat. § 809.24(1)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Statement of costs in the Court of Appeals","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the decision.\nObjection by motion within 11 days after service. Wis. Stat. § 809.25(1)(c)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Petition for review in the Supreme Court","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from date of the Court of Appeals decision (or order on timely reconsideration).\n8,000 words; review discretionary. Wis. Stat. §§ 808.10, 809.62(1m)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Response to petition for review","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the petition. Wis. Stat. § 809.62(3)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Petition for cross-review","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of another party’s petition for review (or the review period, if later).\nWhichever is later. Wis. Stat. § 809.62(3m)(a)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Motion for reconsideration in the Supreme Court","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from date of the Supreme Court decision. Wis. Stat. § 809.64","#st-wi-appeals"],["Substitution of judge after remand","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from filing of the remittitur in the trial court.\nOnly when further trial-court proceedings are needed after reversal, modification, or new trial. Wis. Stat. § 801.58(7)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Motion for further proceedings after remittitur","Wisconsin · appeals · Deadlines","365 days from receipt of the remitted record by the trial court clerk.\nApplies when the mandate orders neither specific action nor a new trial; otherwise dismissal. Wis. Stat. § 808.08(3)","#st-wi-appeals"],["Supreme Court of Appeals of West Virginia","West Virginia · courts","Court of last resort and the State’s rulemaking court. It reviews Intermediate Court of Appeals decisions by discretionary petition (W. Va. Code § 51-11-10; RAP 5), and may take any civil case filed in the Intermediate Court on its own motion or grant a party’s motion for direct review, filed within 20 days of the notice of appeal, when the appeal involves a question of fundamental public importance and time is of th","#st-wv"],["Intermediate Court of Appeals of West Virginia","West Virginia · courts","Created by the Legislature in 2021 and hearing appeals since July 1, 2022 (W. Va. Code § 51-11-1 et seq.). It has no original jurisdiction. It hears appeals of right from final circuit court judgments in civil cases entered after June 30, 2022, final family court orders (except domestic violence), guardianship and conservatorship orders, agency and ALJ decisions, and Workers’ Compensation Board of Review decisions (§","#st-wv"],["Circuit Courts","West Virginia · courts","General-jurisdiction courts of record, organized in judicial circuits whose terms of court are fixed by Trial Court Rule 2. Civil practice is governed by the revised Rules of Civil Procedure (effective January 1, 2025) and the statewide Trial Court Rules, which preempt differing local rules (TCR 1.01–1.03). Specialized assignment bodies include the Business Court Division (seven regional assignment areas; referral by","#st-wv"],["Family Courts","West Virginia · courts","Hear divorce, custody, support and related domestic matters under the Rules of Practice and Procedure for Family Court; the Rules of Civil Procedure apply there only as those rules allow (RCP 81(b)(1)). Final orders are appealed to the Intermediate Court within 30 days and perfected within 60 days, unless both parties file a notice of intent within 14 days to appeal directly to the Supreme Court (RAP 13(b)–(c)). Dome","#st-wv"],["Magistrate Courts","West Virginia · courts","Limited-jurisdiction courts governed by the Rules of Civil Procedure for the Magistrate Courts and W. Va. Code ch. 50 (neither collected). On appeal to circuit court the Rules of Civil Procedure apply, but Rules 26–37 discovery is unavailable absent an order (RCP 81(a)(1)). The jurisdictional amount was not collected.","#st-wv"],["No anti-SLAPP statute; the 2025 UPEPA bill died in committee","West Virginia · anti-SLAPP","West Virginia has not enacted an anti-SLAPP law. House Bill 3205 of the 2025 Regular Session (“Uniform Public Expression Protection”) was introduced and referred to House Judiciary on March 6, 2025, and that is its last recorded action. The 2026 Regular Session bill list shows no public-expression or anti-SLAPP bill. The only related item found was SB 668 (“Providing compensation to victims of abusive lawsuits”), ref","#st-wv-slapp"],["What a defendant uses instead","West Virginia · anti-SLAPP","There is no special motion, discovery stay or fee-shifting for speech-based suits. Defenses come through ordinary procedure: a Rule 12(b)(6) motion (on which the court must state findings and conclusions when it grants the motion, RCP 52(a)(3)); summary judgment, which may be filed until 30 days after discovery closes (RCP 56(b)); Rule 11 sanctions after the 21-day safe harbor; and protective orders under RCP 26(c). ","#st-wv-slapp"],["Pro hac vice under Admission Rule 8.0: a West Virginia local counsel with a West Virginia office, who must attend everything","West Virginia · out of state","A lawyer licensed in another U.S. state, territory or possession may apply to appear in a particular case before any West Virginia court, board or agency, if that jurisdiction extends like courtesy to West Virginia lawyers. The lawyer must first associate with local counsel: an active West Virginia State Bar member who maintains an actual physical office in West Virginia that is the primary place from which local cou","#st-wv-oos"],["Application: verified statement, $350 per lawyer per case, $350 annual fee","West Virginia · out of state","The out-of-state lawyer gives local counsel a verified application to file with the tribunal and the West Virginia State Bar. It must list:\n- all West Virginia matters, including temporary Rule 5.5 practice, by the applicant and by the applicant’s firm in the preceding 24 months;\n- good standing everywhere admitted, and no discipline in the preceding 24 months;\n- an agreement to comply with West Virginia law, includi","#st-wv-oos"],["Admission does not follow the case to the appellate courts","West Virginia · out of state","Pro hac vice admission carries over if venue is transferred, but it does not carry into the Supreme Court of Appeals on appeal or in an extraordinary proceeding. A new application and fee are required there. RAP 3(c) applies the same rule to each appellate tribunal, including the Intermediate Court. A filing may be lodged before admission only if a complete pro hac vice motion is filed at the same time. W. Va. R. Adm","#st-wv-oos"],["Non-compliant filings are rejected or stricken","West Virginia · out of state","Circuit clerks must reject any filing not signed as Rule 8.0 requires. A non-compliant document may be expunged as a fugitive document, but a party has 30 days after notice to cure by filing a certification signed by local counsel. Separately, under TCR 4.02 papers filed by a visiting attorney who has not complied may be stricken after 15 days’ written notice. W. Va. R. Admission 8.0(j); TCR 4.02","#st-wv-oos"],["Work allowed without a pro hac vice order, and the Rule 5.5(f) disclosure","West Virginia · out of state","Subject to RPC 5.5, an out-of-state lawyer may do the following without admission:\n- work in West Virginia on a proceeding pending elsewhere in which the lawyer is admitted;\n- if the lawyer reasonably expects to qualify for pro hac vice, consult with West Virginia counsel or a prospective client and prepare a potential West Virginia case;\n- prepare for and take part in arbitration, mediation or other ADR anywhere.\nWe","#st-wv-oos"],["Limited-scope representation: permitted by the ethics rules, but no court procedure for limited appearances","West Virginia · out of state","RPC 1.2(c) lets a lawyer limit the scope of a representation with informed consent, and RPC 6.5 covers short-term programs. The collected Rules of Civil Procedure and Trial Court Rules contain no limited-appearance or notice-of-limited-scope procedure. Once a lawyer appears in a civil action, withdrawal requires a court order after notice to the client covering eight specified points and a written notification certif","#st-wv-oos"],["E-filing access: training and registration; bar number or pro hac vice notation on every filing","West Virginia · out of state","E-filing is mandatory in all actions in circuit and family courts of “Active Counties”, and those courts may not offer lawyers fax or other alternatives except in an emergency. Users must complete Supreme Court-required training and register before getting credentials. Every e-filed document must carry an /s/ signature, address and phone number, and either the lawyer’s West Virginia State Bar ID or a notation that th","#st-wv-oos"],["Governing code: West Virginia Rules of Professional Conduct (effective January 1, 2015)","West Virginia · ethics","The Supreme Court of Appeals approved comprehensive amendments on September 29, 2014, effective January 1, 2015; the rules follow the ABA Model Rules’ numbering and much of their text. The Court’s rules page shows no later amendment history. In February 2026 the Court entered an order refusing to adopt proposed changes to the Rules; that order was not reviewed. Several later ABA changes are absent, including the 2018","#st-wv-ethics"],["Fees: written scope-and-fee statement required; no fee-division rule in the text","West Virginia · ethics","Rule 1.5(b) requires that the scope of the representation and the basis or rate of the fee and expenses be communicated in writing, and that changes also be in writing. The ABA rule says only “preferably in writing”. Contingent-fee agreements must be signed by the client and must clearly notify the client of expenses owed whether or not the client prevails.\nThe West Virginia rule has no counterpart to ABA Model Rule ","#st-wv-ethics"],["No screening to cure a lateral lawyer’s conflict","West Virginia · ethics","Rule 1.10(a) imputes a lawyer’s Rule 1.7 or 1.9 conflict to the whole firm unless the conflict rests on a personal interest that poses no significant risk to the firm’s representation. It omits the ABA Model Rule 1.10(a)(2) exception that lets a firm screen a laterally hired lawyer, so a lateral hire’s former-client conflict disqualifies the firm unless the client waives under Rule 1.7. W. Va. RPC 1.10(a), (c)","#st-wv-ethics"],["Rule 1.2(e): advising on West Virginia law that conflicts with federal law","West Virginia · ethics","A lawyer may counsel and assist a client on conduct the lawyer reasonably believes West Virginia law authorizes. Where West Virginia law conflicts with federal law, the lawyer must also advise on the related federal law and its potential consequences. The ABA Model Rules have no such paragraph. W. Va. RPC 1.2(e)","#st-wv-ethics"],["Confidentiality: ethics advice from Disciplinary Counsel is a permitted disclosure","West Virginia · ethics","Rule 1.6(b)(4) allows disclosure to obtain legal advice, or informal ethics advice from the Office of Disciplinary Counsel, about the lawyer’s own compliance. Otherwise the exceptions track the ABA rule, including the crime-fraud exceptions in (b)(2)–(3) and the conflict-check exception in (b)(7). There is no mandatory disclosure duty in Rule 1.6. W. Va. RPC 1.6(b)","#st-wv-ethics"],["Advertising and specialization: pre-2018 rules remain","West Virginia · ethics","Targeted written, recorded or electronic solicitations must be labeled “Advertising Material” on the envelope and at the beginning and end of any recorded or electronic message. Every advertisement must include the name and office address of at least one responsible lawyer or firm. Rule 7.4(d) states that West Virginia does not recognize specialization, so a lawyer may not state or imply certification as a specialist","#st-wv-ethics"],["Other West Virginia variations","West Virginia · ethics","- Rule 5.8 lets lawyers practice through LLCs or registered LLPs only if the firm registers with the West Virginia State Bar, and it keeps each lawyer personally liable for the lawyer’s own professional acts.\n- Rule 8.4 has no ABA 8.4(g) harassment and discrimination provision. TCR 4.06 separately bars biased conduct and statements toward others in court proceedings when unrelated to good-faith advocacy.\n- Rule 6.1 s","#st-wv-ethics"],["The notice of appeal is filed with the appellate Clerk, not the circuit clerk, within 30 days","West Virginia · land mine","Appeals from final civil judgments entered after June 30, 2022 go to the Intermediate Court of Appeals. File the Appendix A notice of appeal, with all required attachments including the order appealed, in the Office of the Clerk of the Supreme Court/Intermediate Court within 30 days of entry. Serve it on all parties, on the circuit clerk, and on each court reporter from whom a transcript is ordered. The appellate cou","#st-wv-unique"],["The appeal is “perfected” by the opening brief and appendix within four months, with at most two months’ extension","West Virginia · land mine","Unless law provides otherwise, the petitioner must file the brief and the appendix record within four months of entry of the judgment. Total extensions may not exceed two months, and failure to perfect results in dismissal. There is no automatic transmission of the record; the petitioner builds and files the appendix. RAP 5(f)–(g), 5(i)(2), 6(c), 7, 10(b)","#st-wv-unique"],["No interlocutory appeals to the Intermediate Court","West Virginia · land mine","The Intermediate Court has no jurisdiction over interlocutory appeals, certified questions or extraordinary writs. A non-final order is reviewed by petition for a writ of prohibition in the Supreme Court, which is discretionary and “sparingly exercised”, or by a certified question under § 58-5-2. A partial judgment is appealable only with an express Rule 54(b) no-just-reason-for-delay determination. The notice of app","#st-wv-unique"],["After a jury trial, skipping a new-trial motion waives trial errors","West Virginia · land mine","A party that does not file a timely Rule 59 new-trial motion after a jury verdict is deemed to have waived all trial errors it could have raised in that motion. A denied Rule 50(b) motion is the only exception. The motion is due 28 days after entry of judgment and cannot be extended. W. Va. R. Civ. P. 59(b), 59(f), 6(b)(2)","#st-wv-unique"],["Local counsel must have a West Virginia office and must show up, including at depositions","West Virginia · land mine","Pro hac vice requires association with a West Virginia lawyer whose primary daily office is physically in West Virginia. That lawyer must sign every served paper (unsigned papers are invalid) and attend every hearing, trial and deposition unless the judge permits remote attendance. Budget $350 per lawyer per case plus a $350 annual fee, and a fresh application for any appeal. W. Va. R. Admission 8.0(b), (c), (e), (f)","#st-wv-unique"],["Judge-disqualification motions have short windows and a verified certificate","West Virginia · land mine","There is no peremptory strike of a judge. A disqualification motion must be filed within 30 days of learning the ground, at least 7 days before a hearing or 21 days before trial, cite the specific Rule 2.11 ground, and carry a verified certificate of counsel. A late motion can be decided by the challenged judge, and rulings are not immediately appealable. TCR 17.01, 17.05, 17.07","#st-wv-unique"],["Answers are due in 30 days, but motion responses in 21 days and hearing notices 14 days ahead","West Virginia · land mine","Defendants answer in 30 days after service (and must attach a civil case information statement). By contrast:\n- motion response memoranda are due 21 days after service of the motion;\n- replies are due 7 days after the response;\n- a motion and notice of hearing must be served at least 14 days before the hearing;\n- supporting memoranda are capped at 20 pages without leave;\n- no pleading may be filed less than 48 hours ","#st-wv-unique"],["Do not count on three extra days after e-service","West Virginia · land mine","RCP 6(e) adds three days only after service by mail, by leaving the paper with the clerk, or by other consented means; e-service under the Trial Court Rules (RCP 5(b)(2)(E)) is not listed. TCR 15A.18 says e-service is “treated the same as service by mail” for Rules of Civil Procedure purposes. Until that conflict is resolved, calendar from the e-service date. On appeal, the 3-day mail addition never applies to docket","#st-wv-unique"],["Six-person civil juries, two peremptories per side, and a 14-day jury demand","West Virginia · land mine","A civil jury has six members unless the court orders more. Each side gets two peremptory challenges, exercised alternately starting with the plaintiff, and multiple parties on a side may be treated as one. A jury demand must be served within 14 days after the last pleading on the issue and filed, or the jury is waived. W. Va. R. Civ. P. 38(b), (d), 47(b), 48","#st-wv-unique"],["Summary judgment motions are cut off 30 days after discovery closes","West Virginia · land mine","Unless the court sets a different time, a summary-judgment motion may be filed only until 30 days after the close of all discovery. The court must state findings of fact and conclusions of law when it grants the motion, a common basis for reversal. W. Va. R. Civ. P. 56(b), 52(a)(3)","#st-wv-unique"],["Election days are legal holidays, and the trial and appellate holiday lists differ","West Virginia · land mine","Under RCP 6, any day on which a general, special or primary election is held statewide or in the county is a legal holiday, as are Lincoln’s Birthday and West Virginia Day. The appellate holiday list in RAP 39 adds Juneteenth, which RCP 6 does not list unless declared by the Governor or President. RAP 39 also excludes weekends and holidays from periods under seven days, while RCP 6 counts every day. W. Va. R. Civ. P.","#st-wv-unique"],["Cases idle for a year can be dismissed","West Virginia · land mine","A circuit court may dismiss any action pending more than one year in which the plaintiff has taken no action, after notice and an opportunity to be heard. Reinstatement requires good cause on a motion filed within three terms of court after the dismissal order. Actions may also be dismissed for unpaid accrued court costs. W. Va. R. Civ. P. 41(b)(2)–(4); TCR 2","#st-wv-unique"],["No peremptory substitution of a circuit judge; disqualification motions go to the Chief Justice","West Virginia · judges","No rule or statute collected gives a peremptory challenge to a judge. Under TCR 17.01 a party may move to disqualify only where the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned under Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11. Timing: the motion must be filed within 30 days after discovering the ground, and at least 7 days before any hearing or 21 days before any trial, final hearing or disposition date.\nThe motion mu","#st-wv-unique"],["Voluntary recusal, single-judge circuits, and review","West Virginia · judges","A judge who recuses on the judge’s own motion enters an order. In a multi-judge circuit the case is reassigned by local practice. Under the February 17, 2026 amendments, in a single-judge circuit the order asks the Chief Judge of the Raleigh County Circuit Court to assign one of its judges. Venue does not change and filings stay in the original county. A Chief Justice-appointed special judge who later cannot serve mu","#st-wv-unique"],["Statutory disqualification by relationship or interest","West Virginia · judges","W. Va. Code § 51-2-8 bars a circuit or intermediate judge from hearing a case without all parties’ written consent if the judge:\n- is a party or interested in the result;\n- is related to a party in listed degrees, up to first cousin;\n- or a listed relative is a stockholder or officer of a corporate party;\n- is a material witness.\nA judgment is not invalidated for the relationship unless it appears of record. A disqua","#st-wv-unique"],["Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11 amended August 25, 2026","West Virginia · judges","The Court approved amendments to the Code of Judicial Conduct on August 25, 2026 (No. 25-882). The order’s Rule 2.11 lists the familiar Model Code grounds and adds “judge-designate” to the public-statement ground. It omits the Model Code’s campaign-contribution ground (A)(4), moves the duty to disclose possibly disqualifying information into the rule as 2.11(D), and allows waiver of non-bias grounds through agreement","#st-wv-unique"],["Appellate judges: Justices decide their own disqualification motions","West Virginia · judges","In the Supreme Court a motion to disqualify a Justice must be filed within 30 days after discovering the ground and at least 7 days before any scheduled proceeding, with a verified certificate. The challenged Justice decides it, and the Chief Justice may appoint a senior justice, senior judge or circuit judge as a substitute. Counsel must also tell the Clerk by letter, on appearing, of any circumstance that may creat","#st-wv-unique"],["Cameras in court are at the presiding judge’s discretion","West Virginia · speech","Cameras and audio equipment in and around courtrooms are allowed only at the discretion of the presiding circuit judge or magistrate. Requests must be made at least one day in advance, and any party, witness or counsel may object. The judge must end coverage that would impede justice or deny a fair trial. Coverage is limited to proceedings open to the public, and there may be no audio of attorney–client or bench conf","#st-wv-unique"],["Sealing and limiting access to court files","West Virginia · speech","On motion by either party, a circuit court may limit access to a civil file by an order stating the nature, duration and reason for the limitation. The limit may be granted ex parte if the motion is filed with the complaint and supported by affidavit, and any person may later seek review (TCR 10.03).\nUnder RCP 5.2, confidential filings must be filed conventionally and conspicuously identified. A motion to seal must s","#st-wv-unique"],["Business Court Division and Mass Litigation Panel","West Virginia · unique rules","Business litigation goes to the Business Court Division through a Motion to Refer filed with the Clerk of the Supreme Court of Appeals, not the circuit clerk. Referred cases go to the Division, whose Chair assigns a presiding judge and a resolution judge. Qualifying cases involve commercial or technology disputes between business entities, and consumer, tort, employment, insurance bad-faith and similar cases are excl","#st-wv-unique"],["Separate case numbers and fees for each unrelated plaintiff","West Virginia · unique rules","A complaint joining individual plaintiffs who are not related by marriage or a derivative or fiduciary relationship must be docketed as a separate civil action for each plaintiff, with a separate fee. Each such action also needs its own pro hac vice admission and $350 fee. W. Va. R. Civ. P. 3(a); W. Va. R. Admission 8.0(c)","#st-wv-unique"],["Class-action residual funds: at least 25% to Legal Aid of West Virginia","West Virginia · unique rules","When class claims are exhausted and residual funds remain, at least 25% must go to Legal Aid of West Virginia. The court may distribute the remaining 75%, after notice and hearing, to West Virginia nonprofits, schools or foundations whose programs benefit the class, or to Legal Aid. W. Va. R. Civ. P. 23(h)","#st-wv-unique"],["Judgment interest rate for 2026: 6.25%","West Virginia · unique rules","The Supreme Court’s rules page states that, under W. Va. Code § 56-6-31 and an administrative order, the interest rate for judgments and decrees entered in 2026, including prejudgment interest, is 6.25%. The statute and the order were not collected. W. Va. Code § 56-6-31; Supreme Court of Appeals 2026 interest-rate order (as stated on courtswv.gov court-rules page)","#st-wv-unique"],["Rule NOTE. What this file covers","West Virginia · civil rules","These are the revised West Virginia Rules of Civil Procedure, which took effect January 1, 2025 and govern all civil actions in West Virginia trial courts of record except as stated in Rule 81 (the former rules remain posted for cases governed by them under Rule 86). The revision follows the restyled Federal Rules closely but keeps many West Virginia departures: a 30-day answer period, a broad subject-matter discover","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 1. Scope and purpose","West Virginia · civil rules","The rules govern all civil actions and proceedings in West Virginia trial courts of record, except as stated in Rule 81, and are to be construed, administered and employed by the court and the parties to secure the just, speedy and inexpensive determination of every action.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","West Virginia · civil rules","There is one form of action, the civil action.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencing an action","West Virginia · civil rules","An action is commenced by filing a complaint. Unlike federal practice, a complaint naming more than one individual plaintiff who are not related by marriage or a derivative or fiduciary relationship must be split: each plaintiff gets a separate civil action number, a separate docket and a separate filing fee. Every complaint must be accompanied by a completed civil case information statement (or its electronic equiva","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 4. Summons","West Virginia · civil rules","The clerk signs, seals and issues a summons for each defendant. Service may be made by any non-party aged 18 or older, by the sheriff, through the Secretary of State as statutory attorney-in-fact, or by the clerk by certified mail (restricted delivery, $20 per complaint) or first-class mail with a Form 11 notice and acknowledgment ($5 per complaint). Mail service cannot support a default unless the record shows a sig","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 4.1. Service of other process","West Virginia · civil rules","A rule, or an order in lieu of summons or rule, is served in the manner provided in Rule 4(d) unless the order says otherwise; Rule 45 governs subpoenas. All writs, process, returns, orders served or published and proofs of service are part of the record.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 5. Serving and filing pleadings and other documents","West Virginia · civil rules","Pleadings after the complaint, discovery papers, written motions and notices must be served on every party; service on a represented party is made on the attorney. Service may be by hand, by leaving the paper at the office or dwelling, by mail (complete on mailing), by leaving it with the clerk if there is no known address, by e-service in e-filing counties under the Trial Court Rules or by fax under TCR 12, or by ot","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 5.1. [Reserved]","West Virginia · civil rules","Reserved; West Virginia has no counterpart to Federal Rule 5.1’s constitutional-challenge notice in this slot, but Rule 24(d) requires the court to notify the Attorney General when a state statute’s constitutionality is drawn in question in a case to which the State is not a party.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 5.2. Privacy protection for filings","West Virginia · civil rules","Confidential filings must be filed conventionally (not electronically) and conspicuously identified; motions to seal or unseal must state the legal authority, the material stays confidential while a motion to seal is pending, and an opposing party has 10 days to respond. Filings must use initials in juvenile, sexual-offense-victim, abuse and neglect, mental hygiene and expungement cases; Social Security numbers may n","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 6. Computing and extending time","West Virginia · civil rules","Day-based periods exclude the trigger day, count every day, and roll forward from a Saturday, Sunday or legal holiday; the last day ends at midnight for electronic or fax filing and at the clerk’s closing time otherwise. West Virginia legal holidays include Lincoln’s Birthday, West Virginia Day (June 20), Columbus Day, and any day on which a general, special or primary election is held statewide or in the county. All","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings allowed; form of motions","West Virginia · civil rules","Only a complaint, answer, answer to a counterclaim, answer to a crossclaim, third-party complaint, third-party answer and, if ordered, a reply are allowed. A motion must be in writing unless made at a hearing or trial, state its grounds with particularity and state the relief sought. TCR 22.01 adds a 20-page limit on supporting memoranda absent leave and requires delivery of copies to the assigned judge.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","West Virginia · civil rules","A claim requires a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for relief, and (unlike the federal rule) must be accompanied by a completed civil case information statement. Allegations other than the amount of damages are admitted if not denied in a required responsive pleading. The list of affirmative defenses still includes “discharge in bankruptcy”, which the federal list dropped in 2010,","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","West Virginia · civil rules","Capacity need not be pleaded but must be challenged by specific denial; fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity while malice, intent and knowledge may be alleged generally; conditions precedent may be pleaded generally but must be denied with particularity; special damages must be specifically stated. A West Virginia addition requires an eminent-domain pleading to describe the property and its intended u","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","West Virginia · civil rules","Every pleading needs a caption with the court’s name, a title, the civil action number and a Rule 7(a) designation; claims and defenses go in numbered paragraphs; exhibits attached to a pleading are part of it for all purposes. TCR 6.01 sets statewide paper format: 8½ × 11, double-spaced, at least 12-point proportional type, one-inch margins, with the assigned judge’s name on the line below the case number and the fi","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing; representations to court; sanctions","West Virginia · civil rules","Every paper must be signed by an attorney of record (or an unrepresented party) and state the signer’s address, e-mail address, phone number and West Virginia State Bar ID number. Presenting a paper certifies the standard federal representations (proper purpose, warranted legal contentions, evidentiary support). A sanctions motion must be made separately and may not be filed if the challenged matter is withdrawn or c","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections; when and how presented","West Virginia · civil rules","A defendant must answer within 30 days after service of the summons and complaint (federal: 21), and the answer must be accompanied by a civil case information statement; answers to counterclaims and crossclaims are also due in 30 days, and a court-ordered reply within 30 days. A Rule 12 motion suspends the deadline until 14 days after the court’s ruling or service of a more definite statement. The seven Rule 12(b) d","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and crossclaim","West Virginia · civil rules","Counterclaims arising from the same transaction or occurrence are compulsory unless they were already the subject of another pending action or the opposing party sued by attachment without obtaining personal jurisdiction; others are permissive. The rules do not expand counterclaims or credits against the State. Crossclaims against co-parties must arise from the same transaction or occurrence or relate to property in ","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","West Virginia · civil rules","A defending party may implead a nonparty who is or may be liable to it without leave if the third-party complaint is filed within 14 days after serving its original answer (or as a Rule 16(b) scheduling order provides); otherwise leave by motion is required. The third-party defendant asserts its Rule 12 defenses and Rule 13 claims, may raise the third-party plaintiff’s defenses against the plaintiff, and the plaintif","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","West Virginia · civil rules","A party may amend once as a matter of course within 21 days after serving the pleading or, if a response is required, within 21 days after service of the responsive pleading or a Rule 12(b), (e) or (f) motion, whichever is earlier; otherwise written consent or leave (freely given) is needed. A response to an amended pleading is due within the time left on the original or 14 days, whichever is later. Amendments relate","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 16. Pretrial conference; scheduling; management","West Virginia · civil rules","Except in exempted case categories, the court must issue a scheduling order after the Rule 26(f) report or a scheduling conference, within the earlier of 120 days after any defendant is served or 90 days after any defendant appears, unless it finds good cause for delay. The order must limit the time to join parties, amend pleadings, complete discovery and file motions, and may address disclosures, expert reports, ESI","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 17. Plaintiff and defendant; capacity; public officers","West Virginia · civil rules","Actions are prosecuted in the name of the real party in interest; executors, administrators, guardians, bailees, express trustees and contracting parties may sue in their own names, and statutes may require suit in the name of the State or a subdivision for another’s use. Dismissal for a real-party defect is barred until a reasonable time for ratification, joinder or substitution. Capacity is determined by domicile f","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims","West Virginia · civil rules","A party may join as many claims, legal or equitable, as it has against an opposing party, including contingent claims such as a money claim joined with a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 19. Required joinder of parties","West Virginia · civil rules","A person subject to service must be joined if complete relief is impossible without the person or the person’s interest would be impaired or would expose a party to inconsistent obligations; if joinder would make venue improper and the joined party objects, that party is dismissed. When joinder is not feasible, the court decides in equity and good conscience whether to proceed, using the four federal factors. A claim","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","West Virginia · civil rules","Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, when the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence or series and share a common question of law or fact; the court may order separate trials to prevent prejudice. Rule 3(a) still requires separate civil action numbers and fees for unrelated individual co-plaintiffs.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","West Virginia · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may add or drop a party on just terms at any time and may sever any claim.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","West Virginia · civil rules","A plaintiff exposed to double or multiple liability may join the claimants as defendants and require them to interplead even if their claims lack a common origin or the plaintiff denies liability; a defendant may interplead by crossclaim or counterclaim. There is no statutory-interpleader counterpart.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","West Virginia · civil rules","Certification requires the four Rule 23(a) prerequisites and one Rule 23(b) category, with the federal settlement-approval factors, objector rules and class-counsel standards. Three numbering points: the class-counsel provision is lettered (f) and the fee provision (g), although the text cross-refers to them as (g) and (h). The posted text contains no Rule 23(f)-style interlocutory appeal from certification orders. A","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 23.1. Derivative actions","West Virginia · civil rules","A shareholder or member derivative complaint must be verified, allege contemporaneous ownership (or devolution by operation of law), and state with particularity the demand made on directors (and, if necessary, shareholders) or why none was made; settlement, dismissal or compromise needs court approval with notice ordered by the court. There is no federal-jurisdiction collusion allegation.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 23.2. Actions relating to unincorporated associations","West Virginia · civil rules","Actions by or against association members as a class through named representatives may proceed only if the representatives will fairly and adequately protect the association’s and members’ interests; Rule 23(d) and (e) procedures apply.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","West Virginia · civil rules","Intervention of right and permissive intervention follow the federal standards; the motion must be served under Rule 5, state the grounds and attach the proposed pleading. Under Rule 24(d), when the constitutionality of a West Virginia statute affecting the public interest is drawn in question in an action to which the State, its officers, agencies or employees are not parties, the court must notify the Attorney Gene","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","West Virginia · civil rules","If a party dies and the claim survives, a motion to substitute must be made within 90 days after service of a statement noting the death or the action by or against the decedent is dismissed; the motion and statement must be served on nonparties under Rule 4, in any judicial circuit. The court may continue an action by or against the representative of a party who becomes incompetent or incarcerated, and may substitut","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 26. Duty to disclose; general provisions governing discovery","West Virginia · civil rules","Initial disclosures (witnesses, documents, damages computation, insurance) are due within 30 days after the Rule 26(f) written report is filed; parties joined later have 30 days. Exempt proceedings include eminent domain, Board of Equalization and Review appeals, administrative summons enforcement, ancillary proceedings, arbitration-award enforcement, administrative-record review, appellate review of inferior bodies,","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions to perpetuate testimony","West Virginia · civil rules","Before suit, a person expecting to be a party may file a verified petition in the circuit where any expected adverse party resides and must serve each expected adverse party under Rule 4 at least 21 days before the hearing; the court appoints counsel for those not personally served. Pending appeal, the trial court may permit perpetuation depositions on motion. A perpetuation deposition may be used in any later action","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken","West Virginia · civil rules","In the United States, before an officer authorized by federal law, West Virginia law, or the law of the place of examination, or a court-appointed person; abroad, under a treaty, letter of request, notice before an authorized officer, or commission. A deposition may not be taken before a party’s relative, employee or attorney, a person related to or employed by a party’s attorney, or anyone financially interested in ","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations about discovery procedure","West Virginia · civil rules","Parties may stipulate to deposition logistics and to modify discovery procedures, but a stipulation extending discovery time needs court approval if it would interfere with the deadline for completing discovery, a motion hearing, or trial.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions by oral examination","West Virginia · civil rules","Leave is required to depose before the Rule 26(d) time or to depose an incarcerated person, except for a plaintiff’s notice certifying that the witness is about to leave the State (signed by the attorney as a certification). The text has no 10-deposition limit or federal leave requirement for a second deposition. The noticing party states the recording method and bears its cost; any deposition may be taken remotely, ","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions by written questions","West Virginia · civil rules","Leave is required only for a deponent confined in prison. Cross-questions are due within 14 days after service of the notice and direct questions, redirect within 7 days after cross, and recross within 7 days after redirect; the court may change these times.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 32. Using depositions in court proceedings","West Virginia · civil rules","A deposition may be used against a party who was present, represented or had reasonable notice, to the extent admissible. An adverse party may use a party’s or its officer’s or designee’s deposition for any purpose. Any party may use a witness’s deposition for any purpose if the witness is dead, “out of the state” (rather than the federal 100-mile test) unless the offering party procured the absence, unable to attend","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories to parties","West Virginia · civil rules","Each party may serve up to 25 interrogatories including discrete subparts, unless stipulated or ordered. Answers and objections are due within 30 days after service, each answer under oath; unstated objections are waived absent good cause. Contention interrogatories are allowed but may be deferred. A party may answer by specifying business records when the burden of deriving the answer is the same for both sides.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 34. Producing documents, ESI and things; entry on land","West Virginia · civil rules","A request must describe items with reasonable particularity and may specify the ESI form. The written response is due within 30 days after service, or within 30 days after the first Rule 26(f) conference for an early request delivered under Rule 26(d)(2). Objections must be stated with specificity and say whether responsive materials are being withheld; production must be completed by the time stated in the request o","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examinations","West Virginia · civil rules","On motion for good cause and notice, the court may order a party whose physical or mental condition (including blood group) is in controversy to be examined by a suitably licensed or certified examiner, specifying time, place, manner, conditions, scope and examiner. The examined party may obtain the detailed report and thereby waives privilege as to other examinations of the same condition; the moving party may then ","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","West Virginia · civil rules","Requests may cover facts, application of law to fact, opinions about either, and the genuineness of documents. A matter is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 30 days after service. Lack of knowledge is a permissible answer only after reasonable inquiry; a request may not be objected to solely because it presents a genuine issue for trial. Admissions are conclusive unless withdrawn on motio","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to make disclosures or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","West Virginia · civil rules","Motions to compel need a good-faith conferral certification; a motion directed to a nonparty is filed in the circuit court where the discovery is taken. Expense-shifting follows the federal model, but the posted text of Rule 37(a)(5)(A)(i) excuses payment if “the movant filed the motion good faith to obtain the disclosure” (the federal rule excuses payment when the movant filed before attempting in good faith to obta","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 38. Jury trial of right","West Virginia · civil rules","A jury demand must be served no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue and filed under Rule 5(d); another party may demand jury on other issues within 14 days after service of a partial demand. A party waives a jury unless its demand is properly served and filed, and a proper demand may be withdrawn only with the parties’ consent.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","West Virginia · civil rules","Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or the court finds no jury right exists; the court may order a jury on any issue even without a proper demand, and may use an advisory jury or, with consent, a binding jury in non-jury actions.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 40. Scheduling cases for trial","West Virginia · civil rules","Trials may be set by the court on its own, by Rule 16 scheduling order, or on motion, with priority to actions entitled to it by constitution, statute or rule.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions or claims","West Virginia · civil rules","A plaintiff may dismiss without order by notice before an answer or summary-judgment motion is served, or by stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared; a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits. Otherwise dismissal requires a court order. Involuntary dismissals operate on the merits unless the order says otherwise or the dismissal is for lack of jurisdiction, venu","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials","West Virginia · civil rules","The court may join for hearing or trial, or consolidate, actions sharing a common question, including appeals from magistrate court. When actions arising from the same transaction or occurrence are pending before different circuit judges, or a circuit judge and a magistrate court, the judge before whom the first action was commenced must order them transferred to one court. A magistrate-court judgment of $15.00 or le","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 43. Taking testimony","West Virginia · civil rules","Trial testimony is taken in open court unless a statute or rule provides otherwise; affirmation may replace an oath; motions may be heard on affidavits, oral testimony or depositions; the court may appoint and set compensation for an interpreter and tax it as costs. The text has no federal-style provision for remote trial testimony (see TCR 14 on videoconferencing).","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 44. Proving an official record","West Virginia · civil rules","A domestic official record is proved by official publication or an attested copy with a custody certificate under seal from a judge of the circuit or subdivision or a public officer with a seal; foreign records by publication or an attested copy with final certification or treaty certification, or for good cause without it. A certified no-record statement proves absence of a record.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 44.1. Determination of foreign law","West Virginia · civil rules","A party intending to raise foreign-country law must give notice by pleading or other writing; the court may consider any relevant source, and its determination is a ruling on a question of law.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","West Virginia · civil rules","Subpoenas issue from the court where the action is pending, either from the clerk signed in blank or signed by an attorney authorized to practice in that court (so an out-of-state lawyer needs pro hac vice admission to sign one). Before serving a pre-trial document or inspection subpoena, notice and a copy must be served on each party. Service is made as for personal service of process under Rule 4(d)(1)(A), with one","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 46. Objecting to a ruling or order","West Virginia · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary; a party need only state the action it wants or objects to and the grounds, and a party with no opportunity to object is not prejudiced.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 47. Selecting jurors","West Virginia · civil rules","The court may let counsel conduct voir dire or conduct it itself while allowing supplemental questions. A civil jury consists of six persons unless the court directs a larger number, and the plaintiff and defendant each get two peremptory challenges, exercised one at a time alternately beginning with the plaintiff; multiple parties on a side may be treated as one or given additional challenges. Up to six alternates m","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 48. Juries; verdict; polling","West Virginia · civil rules","The parties may stipulate to fewer than six jurors. The verdict must be unanimous unless the parties stipulate otherwise, and the court must poll the jury on request.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 49. Special verdicts; general verdict and questions","West Virginia · civil rules","Tracks the federal rule: the court may require special verdicts or a general verdict with written questions, and a party waives jury trial on an issue not submitted unless it demands submission before the jury retires. Inconsistent answers may lead to judgment on the answers, further deliberation, or a new trial.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 50. Judgment as a matter of law in jury trials","West Virginia · civil rules","A JMOL motion may be made any time before submission to the jury and must specify the judgment sought and the law and facts supporting it. A renewed motion, with an optional alternative new-trial request, must be filed no later than 28 days after entry of judgment (or after discharge of the jury if no verdict decided the issue). If the renewed motion is granted, the court must rule conditionally on the new-trial moti","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 51. Instructions to the jury; objections; preserving error","West Virginia · civil rules","Requested instructions are filed at the close of evidence or earlier when the court orders. The court must inform the parties in writing of its proposed instructions before instructing, give an opportunity to object on the record outside the jury’s hearing, and may send written instructions to the jury room. Error may be assigned only to properly objected-to instructions or properly requested and objected-to refusals","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings and conclusions by the court","West Virginia · civil rules","In bench trials the court must find facts specially and state conclusions separately; findings and conclusions are also required for preliminary injunctions and when the court grants a motion under Rule 12, 23(c)(1) or 56 (a requirement the federal rule does not impose). Findings are set aside only if clearly erroneous. A motion to amend findings is due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment and cannot be exte","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 53. Commissioners; discovery commissioners","West Virginia · civil rules","Commissioners may be appointed under statute or rule. Discovery commissioners should be used only in complex cases; objections to an appointment order are due within 14 days of its entry, and compensation may be assessed to the parties. A discovery commissioner issues a non-binding written report and recommendation; objections are due within 7 days of service, responses within 5 days after service of objections, and ","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgments; costs","West Virginia · civil rules","A judgment includes any order from which an appeal lies. With multiple claims or parties, partial final judgment requires an express determination that there is no just reason for delay; otherwise the order is revisable (W. Va. Code § 58-5-1(a) ties appealability to this determination). Costs other than fees go to the prevailing party as of course; the clerk must tax costs within 14 days after judgment, and a motion ","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 55. Default","West Virginia · civil rules","The clerk enters default on a showing by affidavit or otherwise that a party has failed to plead or defend. The clerk may enter a default judgment for a sum certain on affidavit against a defaulted defendant who is not a minor or incompetent person; otherwise application is made to the court, and a party that has appeared must get written notice at least 7 days before the hearing. Rule 4 bars a default based on clerk","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","West Virginia · civil rules","Summary judgment is granted if there is “no genuine issue as to any material fact” and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. Unless the court sets a different time, a motion may be filed at any time until 30 days after the close of all discovery. Factual positions must cite particular record materials; the nonmovant may obtain deferral by affidavit showing it cannot yet present essential facts. When ","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgments","West Virginia · civil rules","Declaratory judgment actions under the Uniform Declaratory Judgments Act, W. Va. Code § 55-13-1 et seq., proceed under these rules with jury rights under Rules 38 and 39. Another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief; declaratory and coercive relief may be sought together; further relief may be granted in the action or by petition; and the court may expedite.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 58. Entry of judgments","West Virginia · civil rules","The court settles or approves the form of the judgment and signs it; the clerk’s notation in the civil docket is the entry, and the judgment is not effective before entry. Entry is not delayed for costs or post-trial motions. There is no separate-document requirement or 150-day deemed-entry rule as in the federal rule. In e-filing counties the date of the official e-filing court stamp is the date of entry (TCR 15A.25","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 59. New trials; amendment of judgments","West Virginia · civil rules","A new trial may be granted after a jury trial for any reason previously allowed at law, or after a bench trial for any reason previously allowed in equity. Motions for a new trial and to alter or amend are due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, and cannot be extended; opposing affidavits are due 14 days after service. The court may order a new trial on its own within 28 days. A West Virginia addition, Rul","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from judgment or order","West Virginia · civil rules","Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time, but only with the appellate court’s leave once an appeal is docketed. Rule 60(b) grounds include “unavoidable cause” alongside mistake, inadvertence, surprise and excusable neglect. Motions must be made within a reasonable time, and for grounds (1)–(3) no more than one year after entry; the time cannot be extended. The motion does not suspend finality. Independent actio","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","West Virginia · civil rules","Errors and defects that do not affect any party’s substantial rights are disregarded and are not grounds for a new trial or for disturbing a judgment.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","West Virginia · civil rules","Execution is automatically stayed for 30 days after entry of judgment unless the court orders otherwise. A party may obtain a stay at any time by bond or other security, effective when the court approves it. The court may stay execution pending Rule 52(b), 59(e) or 60 motions, may stay a Rule 54(b) judgment, and on motion may stay execution for a reasonable time specified in the order to allow the moving party to pre","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 63. Judge’s inability to proceed","West Virginia · civil rules","A successor judge may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record and determining the case can be completed without prejudice; in a hearing or bench trial the successor must, on request, recall any available witness whose material testimony is disputed.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 64. Seizure of person or property","West Virginia · civil rules","State-law prejudgment remedies (arrest, attachment, garnishment, seizure of specific property, sequestration) are available. Orders for seizure of specific personal property must be returned within 21 days after issuance; orders of civil arrest or attachment within 30 days; and a garnishee must answer within 90 days after service of the attachment order unless the answer is waived.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions and restraining orders","West Virginia · civil rules","Preliminary injunctions require notice and may be consolidated with trial. A TRO without notice requires specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint and counsel’s certification of notice efforts, expires within the time set (not over 14 days) unless extended for good cause for a like period or by consent, and the adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice. Security is required in an amount the court c","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 65.1. Security; proceedings against sureties","West Virginia · civil rules","A surety submits to the court’s jurisdiction and appoints the clerk as agent for service; liability may be enforced by motion without an independent action.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers","West Virginia · civil rules","An action in which a receiver has been appointed may not be dismissed without court order; appointment and administration follow West Virginia practice, and the rules otherwise govern.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit in court","West Virginia · civil rules","With notice and leave of court, a party may deposit money or a deliverable thing in dispute and must serve the order on the clerk; funds go into a federally insured interest-bearing account or a court-approved interest-bearing instrument.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of judgment; payment into court","West Virginia · civil rules","A defending party may serve an offer of judgment more than 14 days before trial; the offeree has 14 days to accept in writing. If the judgment obtained is not more favorable than an unaccepted offer, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer. West Virginia keeps a payment-into-court procedure: a defending party may deposit a sum with the clerk and plead that no more is owed, and the claimant may accept with cos","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 69. Executions and other final process","West Virginia · civil rules","Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution, suggestee execution and other statutory writs under state procedure. A writ of execution is returnable not less than 30 nor more than 90 days after issuance; an answer to a suggestion summons is due within 21 days of service; a suggestee execution is returned after one year. A writ of possession must be returned within 21 days. Post-judgment discovery is available un","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 70. Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","West Virginia · civil rules","If a party fails to perform a judgment for a specific act, the court may have it done by another at the party’s expense, divest and vest title to property within the State, issue writs of attachment, sequestration, execution or assistance, and hold the party in contempt.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 71. Enforcing relief for or against a nonparty","West Virginia · civil rules","An order for or enforceable against a nonparty is enforced as if the nonparty were a party.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 71.1. Eminent domain","West Virginia · civil rules","The rules govern circuit court eminent domain proceedings to the extent consistent with the constitution and statutes. The jury consists of twelve freeholders meeting W. Va. Code § 54-2-10. Rule 9(h) imposes special pleading requirements.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 71.2. Extraordinary writs in circuit court","West Virginia · civil rules","The rules govern extraordinary writs in circuit court except as this rule provides. A plaintiff may join demands for different writs and other relief, names as defendants the state agencies, entities, subdivisions or individuals to whom relief is directed, and pleads the authority for the writ. A defendant may concede and the court then enters an appropriate writ; a contesting defendant answers. On default the court ","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 72. Appeals","West Virginia · civil rules","Appeals are governed by the West Virginia Rules of Appellate Procedure.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 73. [Abrogated]","West Virginia · civil rules","Abrogated; see the Rules of Appellate Procedure.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 74. [Reserved]","West Virginia · civil rules","Reserved.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 75. [Reserved]","West Virginia · civil rules","Reserved.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 76. [Reserved]","West Virginia · civil rules","Reserved.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 77. Court and proceedings; clerk’s authority; notice of orders; indigents","West Virginia · civil rules","Courts are always open for filing; trials on the merits are in open court, and no non-ex parte hearing may be held outside the circuit without all affected parties’ consent. The clerk may issue process, enter defaults and clerk default judgments. The clerk must serve notice of every order or judgment immediately after entry, but lack of notice does not extend the time to appeal except as Rule 5 of the Rules of Appell","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 78. Motion day; hearings; submission on briefs","West Virginia · civil rules","A court may set regular motion days and provide for deciding motions on briefs without a hearing. TCR 22.03 allows telephone hearings with the judge’s permission and requires notice to all parties when a motion is decided without a hearing.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 79. Records kept by the clerk","West Virginia · civil rules","The clerk keeps the civil docket, judgment and order records, indexes and trial calendars in the form the Supreme Court prescribes, notes “jury” for demanded cases, and may keep records digitally. In e-filing counties the electronic register of actions is the docket (TCR 15A.25).","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 80. Transcript or statement of evidence made part of record","West Virginia · civil rules","A certified transcript becomes part of the record when filed, and the filing party must notify all other parties; any party may move to correct errors. When no transcript is available, a party may prepare a statement of the proceedings, which must be served within a reasonable time; other parties have 14 days to object or propose amendments, and the statement becomes part of the record when the judge approves, signs ","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 81. Applicability of the rules in general","West Virginia · civil rules","In circuit court appeals from magistrate court the rules apply, but Rules 26–37 may not be used and only magistrate-court pleadings are allowed absent an order. The rules apply in circuit court review of agency decisions when testimony is taken. In family court divorce and related cases they apply only as the Family Court Rules authorize; in circuit court divorce cases pleadings must be verified, no divorce is grante","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 82. Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","West Virginia · civil rules","The rules neither extend nor limit circuit court jurisdiction or venue.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 83. Local rules","West Virginia · civil rules","Circuit courts may adopt local rules consistent with these rules and the Trial Court Rules, effective only after filing with and approval by the Supreme Court of Appeals. TCR 1.01–1.03 add that the Rules of Civil Procedure and Trial Court Rules preempt any differing local rule and no local procedure is effective unless adopted and approved as a local rule.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 84. Forms","West Virginia · civil rules","The Appendix of Forms is sufficient under the rules (for example Form 11, notice and acknowledgment of mail service; Forms 23 and 24, medical and general protective orders). The forms were not separately collected.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 85. Title","West Virginia · civil rules","These rules are the West Virginia Rules of Civil Procedure.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 86. Effective date","West Virginia · civil rules","Rules and amendments take effect on the date set in the Supreme Court’s adopting order and govern later-filed cases and, as far as just and practicable, pending cases. The Court’s rules page states the revised rules govern on and after January 1, 2025.","#st-wv-rules"],["Rule 87. [Abrogated]","West Virginia · civil rules","Abrogated.","#st-wv-rules"],["Appeals in West Virginia: a two-tier system since July 1, 2022","West Virginia · appeals","Final circuit court judgments in civil cases entered after June 30, 2022 are appealed as of right to the Intermediate Court of Appeals. Review in the Supreme Court of Appeals then comes by a discretionary petition (styled a notice of appeal) filed within 30 days of the Intermediate Court’s mandate. The Supreme Court can pull a case up directly, either on its own motion or by granting a party’s motion for direct revie","#st-wv-appeals"],["Intermediate Court of Appeals: civil final judgments, family court, agencies, workers’ compensation","West Virginia · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","The Intermediate Court hears final judgments or orders of circuit courts in all civil cases entered after June 30, 2022, as well as family court final orders (except domestic violence, which go first to circuit court), guardianship and conservatorship orders, agency and ALJ decisions, and Workers’ Compensation Board of Review decisions. It has no original jurisdiction. It cannot hear:\n- criminal, juvenile or abuse-an","#st-wv-appeals"],["Final judgments and Rule 54(b) partial judgments","West Virginia · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","A party may appeal a final judgment, or a judgment on fewer than all claims or parties if the circuit court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay and directs entry. Without that determination, an order resolving fewer than all claims remains revisable and is not final. The notice-of-appeal form asks whether the order is final as to all issues and parties or entered under Rule 54(b), and otherwise req","#st-wv-appeals"],["Direct review and Supreme Court takeover","West Virginia · appeals · Which court and what is appealable","Within 20 days after a notice of appeal is filed in the Intermediate Court, a party may move in the Supreme Court for direct review. A response is due in 10 days, and replies need leave. The motion may be granted only if the appeal involves fundamental public importance and exigencies make time of the essence. If the Supreme Court grants it within 20 days after filing, jurisdiction transfers to the Supreme Court. The","#st-wv-appeals"],["Notice of appeal: 30 days from entry, filed with the appellate Clerk","West Virginia · appeals · Starting the appeal","Within 30 days of entry of the final order (or the Intermediate Court’s mandate), the party appealing must file the Appendix A notice of appeal with all required attachments in the Office of the Clerk. Attachments include copies of all orders appealed and, for a Supreme Court appeal from the Intermediate Court, that court’s mandate. Serve the notice on all parties, on the circuit clerk (where it becomes part of the c","#st-wv-appeals"],["Parties, transcripts and the scheduling order","West Virginia · appeals · Starting the appeal","All parties below are parties on appeal unless the notice of appeal designates them as having no interest. A party mistakenly so designated has 20 days from the notice to say otherwise.\nThe petitioner requests transcripts in the notice of appeal after getting estimates and making payment arrangements. A respondent wanting more transcripts must request them within 14 days after service of the notice. Court reporters h","#st-wv-appeals"],["Perfecting the appeal: four months from entry of judgment","West Virginia · appeals · Starting the appeal","Unless law provides otherwise, an appeal must be perfected within four months of entry of the judgment by filing the petitioner’s brief (Rule 10) and the appendix record (Rule 7). Failure to perfect results in dismissal. The circuit court or the appellate court may extend the time for good cause, but by no more than two months in total, and only if a complete notice of appeal was timely filed. Leave to perfect withou","#st-wv-appeals"],["Rule 50, 52 and 59 motions: 28 days, not extendable","West Virginia · appeals · Post-judgment motions and timing","A renewed JMOL motion, a motion to amend findings, a new-trial motion and a motion to alter or amend are each due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, and the court may not extend those times. After a jury trial, failing to move for a new trial waives trial errors that could have been raised, except that a denied Rule 50(b) motion is preserved. Rule 60(b) motions must be made within a reasonable time, and w","#st-wv-appeals"],["Tolling: not addressed in the collected rules","West Virginia · appeals · Post-judgment motions and timing","Neither the Rules of Appellate Procedure nor the Rules of Civil Procedure as collected say whether a timely Rule 50(b), 52(b) or 59 motion postpones the 30-day notice-of-appeal period. No counterpart to Federal Appellate Rule 4(a)(4) was found. Any tolling rule would come from case law, which was not collected.\nConservative practice is to treat the order disposing of the motion as the operative final order but to fil","#st-wv-appeals"],["Lack of notice of entry does not extend the time","West Virginia · appeals · Post-judgment motions and timing","The clerk must serve notice of entry of every order, but lack of notice does not affect the time to appeal except as RAP 5 allows. In e-filing counties the date of the e-filing court stamp is the date of entry. W. Va. R. Civ. P. 77(d); TCR 15A.25","#st-wv-appeals"],["Cross-assignments of error in the response brief; no separate notice needed","West Virginia · appeals · Cross-appeals","A respondent who believes the record shows error to its prejudice may raise cross-assignments of error in a separate part of its brief, even without perfecting a separate appeal within the statutory period. The cover page must say so. The petitioner may respond in the reply brief, which then gets a 40-page limit and an automatic deadline of 30 days after the response. The respondent may file a non-duplicative supplem","#st-wv-appeals"],["The petitioner builds the appendix","West Virginia · appeals · Record, briefs and limits","The record is not transmitted automatically. The petitioner must prepare an appendix containing:\n- the order appealed and related orders;\n- material pleadings and transcript excerpts;\n- critical exhibits;\n- the complete docket sheet.\nIt needs a cover page, a certification of accuracy and good-faith conferral, a table of contents, and sequential page numbers. Absent agreement, the petitioner serves a list of intended ","#st-wv-appeals"],["Briefing schedule","West Virginia · appeals · Record, briefs and limits","Briefing follows the scheduling order. Typically the petitioner’s brief is due four months from entry of the final order, the respondent’s brief 45 days later, and the reply 20 days after that. Briefs are filed when received, not when mailed.\nInstead of a brief, a respondent may file a summary response of up to 15 pages, which waives oral argument. A respondent that fails to answer an assignment of error is deemed to","#st-wv-appeals"],["Page limits and format","West Virginia · appeals · Record, briefs and limits","Limits under Rule 38(c): 40 pages for petitioner’s and respondent’s briefs, 20 for a reply (30 with multiple response briefs, 40 with cross-assignments), 15 for a summary response, 15 for motions and responses, 15 for a rehearing petition, and 25 for an amicus brief. Covers, tables and certificates of service do not count.\nText must be double-spaced, at least 12-point proportional (or 11-point monospaced), with one-i","#st-wv-appeals"],["Oral argument and decision","West Virginia · appeals · Record, briefs and limits","Oral argument is unnecessary when waived, frivolous, authoritatively decided, or adequately briefed. Rule 19 arguments (settled law, discretion, sufficiency) get 10 minutes per side, and Rule 20 arguments (first impression, public importance, constitutional questions, conflicts) get 20 minutes. Notice issues at least 30 days ahead, and continuance motions are due within 10 days of the notice.\nCases may be decided by ","#st-wv-appeals"],["Rehearing within 30 days; mandate after 30 days","West Virginia · appeals · Rehearing, mandate and Supreme Court review","A rehearing petition may be filed within 30 days of release of a merits opinion or memorandum decision. It is limited to 15 pages, must state points overlooked or misapprehended, and is granted only in exceptional cases. An optional response is due within 14 days. A timely petition stays the mandate. Otherwise the mandate issues soon after 30 days from release, or within 7 days after rehearing is denied. Decisions ar","#st-wv-appeals"],["Supreme Court review of the Intermediate Court: notice within 30 days of the mandate","West Virginia · appeals · Rehearing, mandate and Supreme Court review","A party in interest may petition the Supreme Court for review of an Intermediate Court final order or judgment, and review is discretionary. The procedure is Rule 5: a notice of appeal filed within 30 days of the Intermediate Court’s mandate with the mandate attached, then perfection and briefing under the scheduling order. Filing the notice may support a stay of the Intermediate Court’s judgment under court rules. N","#st-wv-appeals"],["Stay of mandate pending U.S. Supreme Court certiorari","West Virginia · appeals · Rehearing, mandate and Supreme Court review","On motion, the Supreme Court may stay its mandate for up to 90 days pending a certiorari petition. The stay continues if the petition is filed and may be conditioned on security. RAP 26(c)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Writ of prohibition (and mandamus) in the Supreme Court","West Virginia · appeals · Interlocutory review, writs and certified questions","Original-jurisdiction writs are discretionary and “sparingly exercised”. The petition, captioned “State ex rel.”, names the presiding judge and the real parties in interest as respondents and must be verified under W. Va. Code § 53-1-3. The argument must explain why relief is unavailable elsewhere. A rule to show cause in prohibition stays the underlying case. Denial of a rule to show cause is without prejudice unles","#st-wv-appeals"],["Certified questions from circuit courts","West Virginia · appeals · Interlocutory review, writs and certified questions","A circuit court may certify any question of law to the Supreme Court and stay the case. Examples include the sufficiency of service or a pleading, venue, personal or subject-matter jurisdiction, a denied summary-judgment motion, judgment on the pleadings, and joinder of an indispensable party. The certification order must state each question, the circuit court’s answer, the extent of the stay, and a direction to prep","#st-wv-appeals"],["Recusal rulings are interlocutory","West Virginia · appeals · Interlocutory review, writs and certified questions","Trial-court recusal and disqualification rulings are not directly or immediately appealable; extraordinary writs remain available. The same rule applies to Intermediate Court judges. TCR 17.05; RAP 33B","#st-wv-appeals"],["Stay pending appeal starts in the circuit court","West Virginia · appeals · Stays and bonds","Execution is automatically stayed for 30 days after entry of judgment, and a supersedeas stay may be obtained by court-approved bond. An application for a stay pending appeal goes first to the circuit court on written notice. If refused or inadequate, a motion may be made to the appellate court showing the lower court’s reasons, with a response due in 10 days.\nThe bond may not exceed the total judgment (including cos","#st-wv-appeals"],["Mandatory e-filing in both appellate courts","West Virginia · appeals · E-filing and time computation","E-filing is mandatory for all actions in the Intermediate Court and the Supreme Court. A lawyer unable to e-file must move on paper for leave, showing good cause. A filing is complete only when the e-filing receipt is generated. E-service through the system is complete on the Notice of Electronic Filing. If the system is unavailable, a missed filing may be submitted with a sworn declaration by noon on the clerk’s nex","#st-wv-appeals"],["Computing appellate time","West Virginia · appeals · E-filing and time computation","Exclude the trigger day and include the last day, rolling past weekends and holidays. Periods under seven days exclude intermediate weekends and holidays. Three days are added after service by mail, but not to docketing or perfection deadlines or court-set dates. Courts may enlarge time for good cause, and a motion to hold a case in abeyance does not toll any deadline until granted. RAP 39(a)–(c), 28(f)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (circuit court final order to Intermediate Court)","West Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the final order.\nFiled with the appellate Clerk; extendable for good cause (RAP 5(i)(1)); no 3-day mail extension (RAP 39(c)). RAP 5(b)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Notice of appeal to the Supreme Court from the Intermediate Court","West Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the Intermediate Court’s mandate.\nAttach the mandate; review is discretionary. RAP 5(b); W. Va. Code § 51-11-10","#st-wv-appeals"],["Motion for direct review in the Supreme Court","West Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from filing of the notice of appeal in the Intermediate Court.\nResponse due 10 days after filing. RAP 29(f); W. Va. Code § 51-11-5(a)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Perfect the appeal (petitioner’s brief and appendix)","West Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","120 days from entry of the judgment appealed.\nRule states four months, not 120 days; total extensions capped at two months (RAP 5(i)(2)). RAP 5(f), 10(b)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Respondent’s brief","West Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","45 days from filing of the petitioner’s brief.\nTypical schedule; the scheduling order controls. RAP 10(b)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Reply brief","West Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from filing of the respondent’s brief.\n30 days if the response contains cross-assignments of error. RAP 10(b), 10(g)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Respondent’s additional transcript request","West Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from service of the notice of appeal. RAP 9(d)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Party designated as uninterested asks to remain a party","West Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from filing of the notice of appeal. RAP 5(c)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Respondent’s additions to appendix list","West Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from receipt of the petitioner’s list. RAP 7(e)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Motion for disposition by memorandum decision","West Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from perfection of the appeal.\nResponse due 10 days after filing. RAP 21(b)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Response to a motion","West Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from filing of the motion.\nReplies need leave; expedited-relief responses are due in 2 days (RAP 29(c)). RAP 29(a)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Continuance or waiver of oral argument","West Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","10 days from date of the notice of argument. RAP 19(c), (f); 20(c), (f)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing","West Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from release of the opinion or memorandum decision.\nResponse optional, due 14 days after the petition. RAP 25(a)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Mandate issues","West Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from release of the decision (if no rehearing petition).\nWithin 7 days after denial of rehearing. RAP 26(b)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Renewed JMOL / new trial / alter or amend / amend findings","West Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from entry of judgment.\nNot extendable (Rule 6(b)(2)); effect on appeal time not stated in the collected rules. W. Va. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Family court appeal to the Intermediate Court: notice / perfection","West Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the family court order.\nPerfect within 60 days; respondent 15 days and reply 10 days after; direct Supreme Court appeal requires both parties’ notice within 14 days (RAP 13(b)). RAP 13(c)(1), (3)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Motion to disqualify a circuit judge","West Virginia · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from discovery of the ground.\nAnd at least 7 days before a hearing or 21 days before trial. TCR 17.01(a)","#st-wv-appeals"],["Wyoming Supreme Court","Wyoming · courts","Five justices in Cheyenne, each serving an 8-year term subject to retention elections. Wyoming has no intermediate appellate court, so the Supreme Court hears appeals directly from the district courts and the chancery court. It also decides petitions for a writ of review (interlocutory and extraordinary relief), answers certified questions, admits lawyers, and supervises the Wyoming State Bar.","#st-wy"],["District Courts","Wyoming · courts","General-jurisdiction trial courts in all 23 counties, grouped into nine judicial districts. They hear everything except the civil cases of $50,000 or less, small claims, and misdemeanors that go to the circuit courts. They also sit as appellate courts for circuit courts, municipal courts, and administrative agencies.","#st-wy"],["Chancery Court","Wyoming · courts","A single statewide court based in Casper for commercial, business, and trust disputes. It hears equitable and declaratory actions and money claims over $50,000 (not counting punitive damages, interest, costs, and fees) arising from listed causes of action, such as breach of contract, breach of fiduciary duty, fraud, trade secrets, UCC transactions, and the internal affairs of business organizations. It holds nonjury ","#st-wy"],["Circuit Courts","Wyoming · courts","Limited-jurisdiction courts in all 23 counties, with the same nine-district boundaries as the district courts. Exclusive original civil jurisdiction covers claims of $50,000 or less (exclusive of costs), small claims, and forcible entry and detainer. They also hear protection-order cases and all misdemeanors. They have their own civil rules (the W.R.C.P. for Circuit Courts).","#st-wy"],["Municipal Courts","Wyoming · courts","Hear violations of city and town ordinances. Their judgments are appealed to the district court.","#st-wy"],["No anti-SLAPP statute in force","Wyoming · anti-SLAPP","Wyoming has no anti-SLAPP law: no special motion to dismiss, no discovery stay, and no fee-shifting tied to speech or petitioning. Title 1 of the Wyoming Statutes (civil procedure), in the legislature’s compilation dated May 2026, ends at chapter 43 (Mediation). It has no chapter on lawsuits against public participation, and neither ‘SLAPP’ nor ‘public participation’ appears anywhere in it.\nThe Legislative Service Of","#st-wy-slapp"],["Watch: anti-SLAPP bill and 2026 interim study","Wyoming · anti-SLAPP","House Bill 103 of the 2026 session (engrossed version), the ‘Wyoming First Amendment Protection Act,’ would have created W.S. 1-44-101 through 1-44-107 (‘Lawsuits Against Public Participation’). The bill provided immunity from suits based on petition, assembly, religious freedom, and speech, and set procedures for such suits. As drafted, it was to take effect July 1, 2026.\nThe 2026 Title 1 compilation contains no cha","#st-wy-slapp"],["Pro hac vice is a two-step process: State Bar certificate first, then a motion by local counsel","Wyoming · out of state","Admission pro hac vice is discretionary, and a court is not bound by an earlier admission. For each case, the applicant first applies to the Wyoming State Bar, not the court. The application must include:\n• every jurisdiction where the applicant is licensed;\n• every Wyoming matter in the past 5 years where the applicant sought pro hac vice admission, with the result;\n• all discipline (public or private), disability s","#st-wy-oos"],["Local counsel must sign, attend, and answer for the out-of-state lawyer","Wyoming · out of state","Local counsel must be an active member of the Wyoming State Bar. Before anything else is filed, local counsel files an entry of appearance and a certificate of compliance with Rule 8.\nLocal counsel must:\n• move the applicant’s admission at the first hearing;\n• sign the first pleading and stay in the case;\n• attend every proceeding unless excused, with full authority to act for the client.\nService on local counsel alo","#st-wy-oos"],["Trial-court admission does not carry over to the Supreme Court","Wyoming · out of state","Admission pro hac vice in a trial court does not admit the lawyer before an appellate court. A new motion by local counsel, with Rule 8 compliance, is needed in the Supreme Court.\nUnless excused, appellate local counsel must sign all papers and attend all proceedings. A brief or motion may not carry the name or signature of a lawyer the appellate court has not admitted pro hac vice. W.R.A.P. 19.01(b)-(e)","#st-wy-oos"],["E-filing: mandatory for Wyoming lawyers, closed to pro hac vice counsel","Wyoming · out of state","Statewide trial-court e-filing rules took effect April 5, 2025 and override any local rules. Wyoming-licensed lawyers (and their designated staff) must e-file in courts where e-filing is in place. To register, a filer must complete training and pass an e-filing proficiency exam.\nPro hac vice counsel may not e-file; local counsel e-files for them.\nA document is filed when submitted, and a filing day ends at 11:59:59 p","#st-wy-oos"],["Limited-scope appearances","Wyoming · out of state","Except in criminal cases, a written entry of appearance may be limited to a particular proceeding or matter. An attorney named on a pleading only as having helped prepare it has not appeared.\nSince August 17, 2026, a limited-scope lawyer is deemed withdrawn only after fulfilling the limited duties and filing a notice of conclusion of the limited entry of appearance.\nRPC 1.2(c) treats a client who receives limited rep","#st-wy-oos"],["Permanent admission: on motion or by UBE transfer","Wyoming · out of state","Admission on motion (rules amended effective August 12, 2026) requires:\n• a J.D. from an ABA-accredited law school;\n• admission elsewhere by written exam;\n• at least 300 hours a year of active, authorized practice in 5 of the 7 years before applying;\n• active good standing where the applicant now practices;\n• an NCBE character report.\nUBE transfer is available if the UBE was taken less than 3 years before applying. I","#st-wy-oos"],["Temporary and in-house practice without pro hac vice","Wyoming · out of state","Wyoming’s Rule 5.5(c) follows the ABA temporary-practice safe harbors: work with an actively participating Wyoming lawyer; work related to a proceeding where the lawyer is (or expects to be) authorized; ADR work; and work arising out of the lawyer’s home-state practice.\nUnder Rule 5.5(d), in-house lawyers, including qualifying foreign in-house counsel, may serve their employer. Foreign lawyers must base advice on U.S","#st-wy-oos"],["Governing code: Wyoming Rules of Professional Conduct for Attorneys at Law","Wyoming · ethics","These rules follow the ABA Model Rules’ numbering and structure, with Wyoming changes. The judiciary’s current compilation is effective September 21, 2026. It includes the July 21, 2026 amendment to Rule 1.15 (safekeeping property and IOLTA, effective September 21, 2026) and the June 17, 2026 amendment to Rule 6.1(b) (effective August 17, 2026).\nRelated rule sets: the Rules Governing Contingent Fees, the Wyoming Rule","#st-wy-ethics"],["Confidentiality: broader permission to disclose than the ABA rule","Wyoming · ethics","Wyoming Rule 1.6(b)(1) lets a lawyer reveal information to the extent reasonably necessary ‘to prevent the client from committing a criminal act.’ That covers any crime, not just the ABA’s reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm.\nWyoming also adds (b)(8): disclosure to protect the best interests of an individual when the lawyer is appointed guardian ad litem. The rule protects ‘confidential information,’ ","#st-wy-ethics"],["Contingent fees: separate rules, a mandatory form, and presumptive percentages","Wyoming · ethics","Rule 1.5(b) requires contingent fee agreements to be in writing and to comply with the separate Rules Governing Contingent Fees. Those rules require:\n• the agreement in duplicate, signed by the attorney and each client;\n• a signed copy delivered to the client, and the lawyer’s copy kept for 3 years;\n• substantially the prescribed form (Attachment I); changes that affect the client’s rights in paragraphs I-XI are void","#st-wy-ethics"],["Rule 8.4(g) is different: no anti-harassment rule; a ban on using disbarred lawyers","Wyoming · ethics","Wyoming’s Rule 8.4 does not include the ABA Model Rule 8.4(g) ban on harassment and discrimination. Wyoming’s 8.4(g) instead makes it misconduct to knowingly employ or contract with anyone disbarred, suspended, or on incapacity or disability-inactive status in any jurisdiction, in any law-related role (employee, contractor, paralegal, secretary, investigator, or consultant), paid or unpaid. Wyo. R. Prof. Conduct 8.4(","#st-wy-ethics"],["Rule 1.15A: client-file rule with a 5-year retention period","Wyoming · ethics","Wyoming adds Rule 1.15A, which has no ABA counterpart. It defines the client file and requires the lawyer to provide it to the client or former client on request, in commonly available electronic or paper form. The lawyer may withhold documents the client has not paid for under the fee agreement.\nUnless the file is transferred or the client agrees in writing to another arrangement, the lawyer must keep it at least 5 ","#st-wy-ethics"],["Pro bono: 50-hour goal, or a $500 contribution","Wyoming · ethics","Like the ABA rule, each lawyer should aspire to 50 hours of pro bono service a year, with a substantial majority in Wyoming and for persons of limited means. Wyoming’s Rule 6.1(b), amended effective August 17, 2026, offers an alternative: a voluntary $500 yearly contribution to a nonprofit that provides direct legal help to persons of limited means. Examples given are the Wyoming Foundation for Civil Justice, Legal A","#st-wy-ethics"],["Guardian-ad-litem role written into the rules","Wyoming · ethics","Rule 1.2(e): a lawyer appointed guardian ad litem represents what the lawyer reasonably believes is in the individual’s best interests and is not bound by the individual’s objectives, but must consult with the individual in an age- and ability-appropriate way. Wyo. R. Prof. Conduct 1.2(e), 1.6(b)(8)","#st-wy-ethics"],["Peremptory strike of the judge: plaintiff gets 14 days; defendant must use it by the first response","Wyoming · land mine","Out-of-state lawyers often expect no peremptory challenge, or a longer window. In Wyoming a plaintiff must move within 14 days after the clerk’s notice of assignment (or of a reassignment). A defendant must move by its first responsive pleading or Rule 12 motion, or 14 days after a reassignment, whichever is later. A party gets one strike per case, and parties added later get none. W.R.C.P. 40.1(b)(1)","#st-wy-unique"],["Post-judgment motions are deemed denied at 90 days, and the appeal clock starts then","Wyoming · land mine","A Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59, or 60(b) motion not decided within 90 days after filing is deemed denied, unless the court enters an order continuing it, for no more than 60 days. The 30 days to appeal then run from the deemed denial, not from any later ruling. A judge who rules after the deemed-denial date has lost jurisdiction to decide the motion. W.R.C.P. 6(c)(4); W.R.A.P. 2.01(a), 2.02(b)","#st-wy-unique"],["Notice of appeal: file in the trial court, serve the Supreme Court clerk, and attach the appendix list","Wyoming · land mine","The notice of appeal is filed with the trial-court clerk within 30 days of entry of the appealable order, and that deadline is jurisdictional. A copy must also go to the appellate clerk at the same time.\nIn civil cases the notice needs an appendix listing (not attaching) every pleading asserting a claim, every order disposing of claims or parties, and the judgment and any decision letter. The notice must also carry a","#st-wy-unique"],["Pro hac vice does not travel, and you cannot e-file","Wyoming · land mine","Trial-court pro hac vice admission does not admit you in the Supreme Court; a new motion is required. Every admission requires a separate Wyoming State Bar application and Rule 8 certificate for each case. Pro hac vice counsel cannot e-file in the trial courts, so local counsel files everything. W.R.A.P. 19.01(c); Rules Governing the Wyo. State Bar R. 8(c)-(d); Wyo. R. Elec. Filing & Serv. 4(b)(1)","#st-wy-unique"],["A Wyoming lawyer must attend every hearing","Wyoming · land mine","An active Wyoming Bar member must attend all hearings of any represented party unless the court excuses it after notice to other counsel. For pro hac vice matters, local counsel must be present at all proceedings, with full authority. Budget for local counsel at every setting, including pretrial conferences. U.R.D.C. 101(c), 104(f)(3)","#st-wy-unique"],["Counsel cannot stipulate to extensions or continuances","Wyoming · land mine","Unlike common practice elsewhere, Wyoming counsel cannot extend rule- or order-based deadlines by agreement. Only a court order does it, except where the W.R.C.P. allow otherwise. One exception: a timely, one-time clerk’s extension of up to 15 days to answer, or to respond to interrogatories, document requests, or requests for admission. U.R.D.C. 201, 202; W.R.C.P. 6(b)(3)","#st-wy-unique"],["No discovery until initial disclosures are done, and there is no automatic Rule 26(f) conference","Wyoming · land mine","Initial disclosures are due within 30 days after the answer is due. No party may seek discovery from any source until that period has run and the party has made its own disclosures. Wyoming has no mandatory early meet-and-confer; a discovery conference happens only if the court orders it or a party moves for one. W.R.C.P. 26(a)(1.3), 26(d)(1), 26(f)","#st-wy-unique"],["Voluntary dismissal no longer gets the savings statute (since September 21, 2026)","Wyoming · land mine","Under amended Rule 41, a voluntary dismissal is not a ‘failure otherwise than upon the merits’ under W.S. 1-3-118. Do not dismiss voluntarily and plan to refile under the savings statute after limitations has run. W.R.C.P. 41(a)(3) (eff. Sept. 21, 2026)","#st-wy-unique"],["Contingent fee agreements must use Wyoming’s form","Wyoming · land mine","A standard engagement letter from another state will not comply. Wyoming requires an agreement in duplicate, signed by the lawyer and each client, substantially in the prescribed form, with a copy of the contingent fee rules given to and explained to the client. Changes that affect the client’s rights need district-court approval. Each fee-sharing lawyer is liable for the others’ malpractice as if partners. Rules Gov","#st-wy-unique"],["Costs and fees: two short clocks after judgment","Wyoming · land mine","Under W.R.C.P. 54(d), costs other than attorney’s fees and claims for attorney’s fees both require a motion within 21 days after entry of judgment. U.R.D.C. 501(a) also requires an itemized certificate of costs within 20 days after entry of a final judgment allowing costs, and objections are due within 10 days. File by the earlier date and satisfy both.\nU.R.D.C. 501(a)(3)(B) limits taxable witness fees to $30 a day, ","#st-wy-unique"],["Answer in 20 days and serve within 90","Wyoming · land mine","A defendant served in Wyoming answers within 20 days, or 30 days if served outside Wyoming or by publication. A plaintiff who has not served a defendant within 90 days after filing faces dismissal without prejudice under W.R.C.P. 4(w) and U.R.D.C. 203(b). W.R.C.P. 4(w), 12(a)(1)(A); U.R.D.C. 203(b)","#st-wy-unique"],["Chancery court means no jury","Wyoming · land mine","Removing a business case to chancery court (or filing there) waives a jury, because the court uses only nonjury trials. Removal from district court requires every party’s written consent within 60 days after service on the last defendant. W.S. 5-13-115(a); W.R.C.P. 3.2(a)","#st-wy-unique"],["One peremptory disqualification per party, on a short clock","Wyoming · judges","In district court, a party may disqualify the assigned judge without cause by motion, once per case and against one judge only. The clerk must enter a notice of assignment within 5 days after the complaint is filed.\nDeadlines:\n• Plaintiff: 14 days after that notice, or 14 days after an order reassigning the case, whichever is later.\n• Defendant: by the time of its first responsive pleading or Rule 12 motion, or 14 da","#st-wy-unique"],["Disqualification for cause","Wyoming · judges","A party may move for a change of judge, supported by affidavits, whenever the grounds become known. The grounds are that the judge:\n• was counsel in the action before becoming a judge;\n• is interested in the action;\n• is related by blood to a party;\n• is a material witness; or\n• is biased or prejudiced against the party or counsel.\nOther parties may file counter-affidavits. The presiding judge hears the motion or may","#st-wy-unique"],["Transfer of trial to another county","Wyoming · judges","Separate from change of judge: any party may move to transfer trial within 15 days after the last pleading, for local prejudice or witness convenience. The court sends the case to the most convenient county to which no party’s objection applies. The moving party pays the transmission costs and the new county’s docket fee within 14 days. W.R.C.P. 40.1(a)","#st-wy-unique"],["Statutory fair-report privileges","Wyoming · speech","A fair and impartial report of proceedings before state or municipal legislative or executive bodies, or of documents presented in them, is privileged unless published maliciously.\nA fair report of an indictment, warrant, arrest, or any civil or criminal court filing is privileged unless it was published maliciously, or the publisher refused to publish the plaintiff’s reasonable explanation or contradiction, or refus","#st-wy-unique"],["State constitutional speech protection: not checked","Wyoming · speech","The Wyoming Constitution’s speech and press guarantees and any case law reading them more broadly than the First Amendment are not in the collected texts and were not checked. Wyo. Const. art. 1 (not collected)","#st-wy-unique"],["Chancery court: Wyoming’s business court, with no jury","Wyoming · unique rules","The chancery court is a statewide court of limited jurisdiction for commercial, business, and trust disputes. It uses nonjury trials, ADR, and limited motion practice.\nA district-court case can move there only with every party’s written consent:\n• within 60 days after service on the last defendant; or\n• within 60 days after a paper first shows the case is removable.\nThe case must also meet the W.S. 5-13-115 eligibili","#st-wy-unique"],["Uniform Rules for District Courts: no stipulated continuances or extensions","Wyoming · unique rules","Cases are not continued on counsel’s stipulation; a continuance requires written good cause. Time limits set by rule or order cannot be extended by agreement of counsel, only by court order (except as the W.R.C.P. allows).\nThe court will dismiss a case on file 90 days without service on the defendant unless extended for good cause before then. Cases with no substantial action of record for 90 days are subject to dism","#st-wy-unique"],["Rule 1. Scope and purpose","Wyoming · civil rules","Govern procedure in all civil actions in Wyoming state courts, except actions in the chancery court (which has its own W.R.C.P.Ch.C.) and the exceptions in Rule 81. Construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 2. One form of action","Wyoming · civil rules","There is one form of action: the civil action.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 3. Commencing an action","Wyoming · civil rules","A civil action is commenced by filing a complaint. Wyoming departure from FRCP 3: when an earlier action was dismissed under Rule 4(w) for failure to serve and a new action is filed, it is commenced by filing only if service is obtained within 90 days of the applicable statute of limitations; the comment ties this to Wyoming’s savings statute, W.S. 1-3-118.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 3.1. Civil cover sheet [Repealed]","Wyoming · civil rules","Repealed (order of April 30, 2024, effective July 1, 2024). No civil cover sheet rule remains in the W.R.C.P.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 3.2. Removal from district court to chancery court","Wyoming · civil rules","Wyoming-only rule. A district court case may be removed to the chancery court if all parties consent in writing within 60 days after service on the last defendant, or, if the case first becomes removable through an amended pleading, motion, order or other paper, by a notice of removal filed in chancery court within 60 days after the defendant receives that paper (again with all parties’ written consent). The case mus","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 4. Summons and service of process [version effective September 21, 2026]","Wyoming · civil rules","Summons issued by the clerk for each defendant; served by any nonparty at least 18 or by a sheriff or U.S. marshal. Individuals are served personally, by leaving copies at the dwelling with a resident over 14, at the usual place of business with the employee in charge, or on an authorized agent; corporations through an officer, manager, general agent or agent for process (or, if none is found in the county, any agent","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 5. Serving and filing pleadings and other papers","Wyoming · civil rules","Papers after the complaint are served on every party (on the attorney if represented) by hand, office or abode delivery, leaving with the clerk if no known address, mail (complete on mailing, sent simultaneously by electronic means unless the court orders otherwise), or any other means consented to in writing. Rule 26(a)(1)-(2) disclosures and discovery requests and responses are not filed until used, but a notice of","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 5.1. Constitutional challenge to a statute","Wyoming · civil rules","When a party challenges the constitutionality of a Wyoming statute in a case where the state (or its officer, agency or employee) is not a party, the party must serve the attorney general with the pleading or motion raising the issue. Narrower than FRCP 5.1: no separate notice, certification or intervention deadline is stated.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 5.2. Privacy protection for filings","Wyoming · civil rules","Unless the court orders otherwise, all filings must comply with the Wyoming Rules Governing Access to Case Records (instead of the federal redaction list).","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 6. Time","Wyoming · civil rules","Exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a weekend, legal holiday, or (for filing) a day the clerk’s office is inaccessible. The court may extend time before expiry for good cause or after expiry for excusable neglect, but not for Rules 50(b), (c)(2), 52(b), 59(b), (d), (e) and 60(b); the clerk may grant once, ex parte, an extension of up to 15 days to answer or move against a complaint or to res","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 7. Pleadings allowed; form of motions","Wyoming · civil rules","Lists the allowed pleadings (complaint, answer, answer to counterclaim, answer to crossclaim, third-party complaint and answer, and a reply only if ordered). Motions must be in writing with particular grounds and relief sought; Wyoming requires that every Rule 12 and Rule 56 motion contain or be accompanied by a memorandum of points and authorities. Demurrers, pleas and exceptions are abolished.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 8. General rules of pleading","Wyoming · civil rules","Short and plain statement of jurisdiction, the claim, and the relief demanded; admissions, denials, and lack-of-knowledge responses as in FRCP 8. Unlike current FRCP 8(c), the affirmative-defense list still includes contributory negligence, discharge in bankruptcy, failure of consideration, and injury by fellow servant. Allegations other than damages are admitted if not denied in a required responsive pleading.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 9. Pleading special matters","Wyoming · civil rules","Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity; conditions of mind may be alleged generally; denial of conditions precedent must be particular; special damages must be specifically stated. Wyoming adds 9(h): a municipal ordinance may be pleaded by its title or designation and the name of the municipality; there is no admiralty provision.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 10. Form of pleadings","Wyoming · civil rules","Caption with court name, title, file number and Rule 7(a) designation; numbered paragraphs; exhibits are part of the pleading. Wyoming adds 10(d): every filed document must be on 8½ by 11 inch white paper, single-sided, unless the law requires filing an original of another size (such as a will).","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 11. Signing; representations to the court; sanctions","Wyoming · civil rules","Every paper is signed by an attorney of record (or unrepresented party) with address, email, telephone and attorney number. Signing certifies proper purpose and legal and factual support, as in FRCP 11. A sanctions motion must be made separately and served but not filed if the paper is withdrawn or corrected within 21 days (the safe harbor); no monetary sanction against a represented party for legal contentions; does","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 12. Defenses and objections; time to answer","Wyoming · civil rules","Answer within 20 days after service, 30 days if served outside Wyoming, 30 days after the last day of publication, or 60 days after a waiver request was sent (90 if sent outside the United States). Answer to a counterclaim or crossclaim within 20 days; reply within 20 days after an order to reply. A Rule 12 motion moves the answer deadline to 14 days after notice of denial or after service of a more definite statemen","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 12.1. Change of venue (improper venue)","Wyoming · civil rules","Adopted effective October 6, 2025. A party’s motion to change venue must be made within 30 days after service of the complaint on the last party served; responses are due within 20 days after the motion is filed. The court may also act on its own after notice, with objections due within 20 days of the notice. If venue is improper the court must transfer to a district court where venue is proper and order the plaintif","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 13. Counterclaim and crossclaim","Wyoming · civil rules","Compulsory and permissive counterclaims and crossclaims as in FRCP 13. The rules do not expand counterclaims or credits against the state, counties, municipalities or other political subdivisions or their officers and agencies. An omitted counterclaim may be added by amendment with leave.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 14. Third-party practice","Wyoming · civil rules","A defending party may serve a third-party complaint on a nonparty who may be liable to it; leave of court is needed if filed more than 14 days after serving the original answer. Mirrors FRCP 14 without the admiralty provisions.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 15. Amended and supplemental pleadings","Wyoming · civil rules","One amendment as of course within 21 days after serving the pleading, or 21 days after service of a responsive pleading or a Rule 12(b), (e) or (f) motion, whichever is earlier; otherwise by written consent or leave, freely given. Response to an amended pleading is due within the time remaining or 14 days after service, whichever is later. Relation back for a changed party must occur within the Rule 4(w) 90-day servi","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 16. Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","Wyoming · civil rules","The court may (not must) enter a scheduling order after consulting the parties; if entered it must limit time to join parties, amend, complete discovery and file motions, and it may be modified only for good cause with the judge’s consent. Wyoming adds exploring removal to chancery court as a conference purpose. Sanctions, including mandatory expenses, for failing to appear, being unprepared, or disobeying a pretrial","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 17. Parties; capacity; public officers","Wyoming · civil rules","Real party in interest; capacity of individuals is governed by Wyoming law and of corporations by the law of organization; partnerships and unincorporated associations may sue or be sued in their common name. Wyoming 17(d) (no federal equivalent): an unknown defendant may be sued under a fictitious name if the complaint states the true name could not be discovered, the summons says ‘real name unknown,’ and a copy is ","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 18. Joinder of claims","Wyoming · civil rules","A party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party, including contingent claims (such as a money claim plus a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance).","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 19. Required joinder of parties","Wyoming · civil rules","Persons needed for complete relief or with an interest that would be impaired must be joined if feasible; if not feasible, the court decides in equity and good conscience whether to proceed, using the listed factors. A claimant must plead the names of required persons not joined and the reasons.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 20. Permissive joinder of parties","Wyoming · civil rules","Plaintiffs and defendants may be joined if claims arise from the same transaction or series and share a common question of law or fact; the court may order separate trials to prevent prejudice.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 21. Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","Wyoming · civil rules","Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may add or drop parties or sever claims at any time on just terms.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 22. Interpleader","Wyoming · civil rules","A plaintiff exposed to double or multiple liability may require claimants to interplead, and a defendant may seek interpleader by crossclaim or counterclaim.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 23. Class actions","Wyoming · civil rules","Prerequisites and class types follow FRCP 23(a)-(b); certification, notice, judgment, settlement approval and class counsel follow the federal structure. Differences in the text: class counsel is Rule 23(f) and attorney’s fees are Rule 23(g), and there is no provision for a discretionary interlocutory appeal of a class-certification order (federal Rule 23(f)).","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 23.1. Derivative actions","Wyoming · civil rules","A shareholder or member derivative complaint must be verified, allege contemporaneous ownership and no collusion, and state with particularity the demand made on directors (or why none was made). Settlement or dismissal requires court approval and notice as the court orders.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 23.2. Actions relating to unincorporated associations","Wyoming · civil rules","Members may sue or be sued as representatives of an unincorporated association if they will fairly and adequately protect its interests; Rules 23(d) and (e) procedures apply.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 24. Intervention","Wyoming · civil rules","Intervention of right (unconditional statutory right or impaired interest not adequately represented) and permissive intervention on timely motion, served under Rule 5 and accompanied by a pleading. The court must weigh undue delay or prejudice.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 25. Substitution of parties","Wyoming · civil rules","After a party’s death, a motion to substitute must be made within 90 days after service of a statement noting the death, or the action by or against the decedent must be dismissed. Also covers incompetency, transfer of interest, and automatic substitution of public officers; substitution may occur before or after judgment.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 26. Duty to disclose; general discovery provisions","Wyoming · civil rules","Initial disclosures (witnesses, documents, damages computation, insurance) are due within 30 days after the answer is due (as extended by a Rule 12(b) dispositive motion), or within 30 days after a later-joined party is served; the list of exempt proceedings includes Title 14 (juvenile) cases, probate, administrative-record review, and others, and divorce and unmarried custody/support cases have their own detailed di","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 27. Depositions to perpetuate testimony","Wyoming · civil rules","Before suit, a verified petition is filed in the district court where an expected adverse party resides, with notice served at least 20 days before the hearing; the court may appoint an attorney for unserved parties. Depositions may also be perpetuated pending appeal on motion.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 28. Persons before whom depositions may be taken; interstate subpoenas","Wyoming · civil rules","Depositions in the United States are taken before an officer authorized by Wyoming, federal or local law, or a court-appointed person; foreign depositions by treaty, letter of request, notice or commission. Wyoming 28(c) (interstate discovery): to subpoena a witness in Wyoming for an out-of-state case, submit the foreign subpoena to the clerk of court in the county where discovery will occur; the clerk issues a Wyomi","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 29. Stipulations about discovery procedure","Wyoming · civil rules","Parties may stipulate to deposition logistics and other discovery procedures, but a stipulation extending discovery time needs court approval if it would interfere with the discovery cutoff, a motion hearing, or trial.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 30. Depositions by oral examination","Wyoming · civil rules","Leave is required for more than 10 depositions per side, a second deposition of the same person, a deposition before the Rule 26(d) time (unless the notice certifies the deponent will leave Wyoming), or a prisoner. One day of 7 hours unless stipulated or ordered; objections concise and nonsuggestive; instructions not to answer only for privilege, court limits, or a 30(d)(3) motion. The deponent has 30 days after noti","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 30.1. Remote depositions","Wyoming · civil rules","Wyoming-only protocol (effective October 1, 2024) for depositions where participants are not in the same place, unless stipulated or ordered otherwise. The noticing party must identify the remote vendor and give access instructions at least 7 days before; the parties must meet and confer on logistics and run a technology test at least 24 hours before; an attorney who will sit in the same room as the witness must give","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 31. Depositions by written questions","Wyoming · civil rules","Same 10-deposition and leave limits as Rule 30. Cross-questions are served within 14 days after the notice and direct questions, redirect within 7 days after cross, and recross within 7 days after redirect; the court may change these times.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 32. Using depositions in court","Wyoming · civil rules","Deposition use at trial or hearing follows FRCP 32, with a Wyoming difference in the unavailability test: a witness’s deposition may be used if the witness is ‘absent from the state’ (not the federal 100-mile test), dead, unable to attend, or could not be subpoenaed, or in exceptional circumstances. A deposition taken on less than 14 days’ notice cannot be used against a party with a pending protective-order motion; ","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 33. Interrogatories to parties","Wyoming · civil rules","No more than 25 interrogatories, including discrete subparts, without stipulation or leave. Answers and objections are due within 30 days after service; objections must be specific or are waived; answers are under oath, and business records may be produced instead when the burden is equal.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 34. Producing documents, ESI, and tangible things; entry onto land","Wyoming · civil rules","Requests must describe items with reasonable particularity and may specify ESI form. Written response due within 30 days after service; objections must state reasons and say whether responsive material is being withheld. Nonparties are reached by Rule 45 subpoena.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 35. Physical and mental examinations","Wyoming · civil rules","On motion for good cause, the court may order a party (or a person in its custody or legal control) whose condition is in controversy to be examined by a suitably licensed or certified examiner; the order sets time, place, manner and scope. Report exchange and privilege waiver as in FRCP 35(b).","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 36. Requests for admission","Wyoming · civil rules","A matter is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 30 days after service. Denials must be specific; lack of knowledge requires reasonable inquiry. No numerical limit in the rule itself, but Rule 26(b)(2)(A) lets the court limit the number. Admissions are conclusive unless withdrawn on motion.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 37. Failure to disclose or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","Wyoming · civil rules","Motions to compel require a good-faith conferral certification; expenses follow the result. Sanctions for disobeying discovery orders (including Rule 26(f) discovery-conference orders), automatic exclusion of undisclosed information or witnesses unless substantially justified or harmless, expenses for failing to admit, sanctions for failing to attend one’s own deposition or respond to discovery, and the federal-style","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 38. Right to jury trial; demand","Wyoming · civil rules","Issues of fact in actions for money only or specific real or personal property are tried to a jury unless waived; other fact issues go to the court. Serve a written jury demand no later than 14 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue, and file it. Wyoming requires a jury fee deposit with the demand: in district court $50 for a 6-person jury or $150 for a 12-person jury; in circuit court $50. The","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 39. Trial by jury or by the court","Wyoming · civil rules","Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise, the court finds no jury right, or a party fails to appear and those present consent. The court may use an advisory jury, or with consent a binding jury, except where a statute requires a nonjury trial against the State.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 39.1. Juror note taking; juror notebooks","Wyoming · civil rules","At the start of civil trials the court must tell jurors they may take notes and supply materials; notes stay in the courthouse, are collected daily, and are collected and promptly destroyed after deliberations end. The court may provide identical juror notebooks.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 39.2. Juror questionnaires","Wyoming · civil rules","In appropriate cases the court may use case-specific questionnaires; counsel should confer on the questions, and the court rules on disputed questions and states its reasons on the record.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 39.3. Copies of instructions for jurors","Wyoming · civil rules","Each juror receives a personal copy of all written instructions read to the jury, which may be kept during recesses and deliberations but not taken out of the courthouse.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 39.4. Juror questions for witnesses","Wyoming · civil rules","The court must tell jurors at the start of civil trials that they may submit written questions for witnesses after counsel finish. The court reviews questions with counsel out of the jury’s hearing, rules on objections on the record, and allows rebuttal evidence if needed to prevent unfair prejudice.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 40. Assignment for trial; ADR","Wyoming · civil rules","The court sets cases for trial on its own, on request, or as it finds expedient, with statutory priorities. For nonbinding ADR, the court may (and on any party’s request must) assign a settlement conference or mediation to another active judge, a retired judge or justice, or another qualified person, or the parties may choose one; recommended mediation procedures are listed, the neutral’s bill is due within 30 days o","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 40.1. Transfer of trial and change of judge","Wyoming · civil rules","Transfer of trial to another county for local prejudice or witness convenience: move within 15 days after the last pleading is filed; the movant pays transfer costs within 14 days. Peremptory disqualification (Wyoming-specific; no federal equivalent): a party may disqualify the assigned district judge without cause, once per case and against only one judge. The plaintiff must move within 14 days after the clerk’s not","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 41. Dismissal of actions [version effective September 21, 2026]","Wyoming · civil rules","Plaintiff may dismiss without order by notice before an answer or summary-judgment motion is served, or by stipulation of all parties who have appeared; a second notice dismissal of the same claim is an adjudication on the merits. Otherwise dismissal is by court order, and a counterclaim pleaded before service of the plaintiff’s motion stays pending for independent adjudication. New 41(a)(3) (September 21, 2026): a v","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 42. Consolidation; separate trials","Wyoming · civil rules","Actions with a common question of law or fact may be joined for hearing or trial or consolidated; separate trials may be ordered for convenience, prejudice or economy, preserving any jury right.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 43. Taking testimony","Wyoming · civil rules","Trial testimony is taken in open court unless a rule or statute provides otherwise; remote contemporaneous testimony is allowed for good cause in compelling circumstances with safeguards. Affirmation may replace oath; motions may be heard on affidavits or testimony; the court may appoint and tax the cost of an interpreter.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 44. Determining foreign law","Wyoming · civil rules","A party intending to raise foreign-country law must give written notice; the court may consider any relevant source, and its determination is a ruling on law.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 45. Subpoena","Wyoming · civil rules","The clerk issues signed blank subpoenas, and attorneys may issue them as officers of the court; notice to other parties is required before serving a document subpoena. Served anywhere in Wyoming by a sheriff or any nonparty adult, with one day’s fee and mileage tendered; a witness from another county also gets the state-employee per diem afterward. Place limits differ from federal: trial subpoenas can require attenda","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 46. Objecting to a ruling or order","Wyoming · civil rules","Formal exceptions are unnecessary; a party need only state the action it wants or objects to and the grounds. No prejudice if there was no opportunity to object.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 47. Selecting jurors","Wyoming · civil rules","Jurors answer qualification questions in writing under penalty of perjury. Attorneys (or pro se parties) conduct voir dire under the judge’s control, with listed prohibitions (no preconditioning, no questions on the law or the pleadings, no individual questions that could be asked of the group, no hypothetical-verdict questions) but may preview evidence in a nonargumentative way. Each party has 3 peremptory challenge","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 48. Number of jurors; verdict; polling","Wyoming · civil rules","A jury of at least 6 and no more than 12; the verdict must be unanimous and returned by at least 6 jurors unless the parties stipulate otherwise; polling on request.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 49. Special verdict; general verdict with questions","Wyoming · civil rules","Special verdicts and general verdicts with written questions as in FRCP 49; issues not submitted are waived unless submission is demanded before the jury retires.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 50. Judgment as a matter of law; renewed motion","Wyoming · civil rules","JMOL may be moved for any time before submission. A renewed motion (with or without an alternative new-trial request) must be filed no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, or 28 days after discharge if the issue was not decided by verdict; a losing party’s new-trial motion after JMOL is due within 28 days after entry. Undecided post-trial motions are deemed denied under Rule 6(c)(4) after 90 days.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 51. Jury instructions; objections","Wyoming · civil rules","Requests at the close of evidence or earlier as ordered; the court must disclose proposed instructions and rulings before instructing and arguments, and objections must be made on the record, stating distinctly the matter and grounds. Instructions are reduced to writing, numbered, delivered to the jury, and made part of the record.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 52. Findings by the court; reserved questions","Wyoming · civil rules","Different from FRCP 52(a): in a bench trial the court need not state findings except generally, unless a party requests written special findings before evidence is introduced; findings are unnecessary on Rule 12 or 56 motions. A motion to amend or add findings is due within 28 days after entry of judgment. Judgment on partial findings as in federal practice. Wyoming 52(d): a court may reserve an important and difficu","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 53. Masters","Wyoming · civil rules","Wyoming’s master rule differs from current FRCP 53: reference is the exception (complicated issues in jury cases; exceptional conditions in nonjury cases except accounts and difficult damages). The master sets the first meeting within 20 days after the order of reference; in nonjury actions the court accepts findings unless clearly erroneous, and objections to the report are due within 14 days after notice of its fil","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 54. Judgment; costs; attorney’s fees","Wyoming · civil rules","A decision or opinion letter is not a judgment. Rule 54(b) certification requires an express finding of no just reason for delay. Costs other than fees go to the prevailing party on a motion filed no later than 21 days after entry of judgment (Wyoming requires a motion and has a deadline for costs, unlike the federal clerk-taxation process). Attorney’s fee motions are also due within 21 days after entry unless a stat","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 55. Default; default judgment","Wyoming · civil rules","The clerk enters default on a showing of failure to plead or defend, and may enter judgment for a sum certain against a nonappearing, competent adult. Otherwise the court enters judgment; a party who has appeared must get written notice at least 7 days before the hearing. Defaults are set aside for good cause; default judgments under Rule 60(b); judgment against the state requires evidence satisfying the court.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 56. Summary judgment","Wyoming · civil rules","A party may move at any time unless an order sets a different time; the standard (no genuine dispute of material fact) and procedures follow FRCP 56, and the court should state on the record its reasons for granting or denying. Deadlines to respond are set by Rule 6(c) (response 20 days after service or 3 days before the hearing, whichever is earlier), and Rule 7(b)(2) requires a memorandum of points and authorities.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 56.1. Summary judgment: statement of material facts","Wyoming · civil rules","Wyoming-only rule. The movant must attach a separate, short, concise statement of material facts claimed to be undisputed, and the opponent must attach a separate statement of facts claimed to be genuinely disputed; both must include pinpoint citations to the record.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 57. Declaratory judgment","Wyoming · civil rules","These rules govern statutory declaratory-judgment actions; Rules 38-39 govern jury demands; another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief; the court may order a speedy hearing.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 58. Entering judgment","Wyoming · civil rules","If parties cannot agree on the form, the proposed judgment or order is presented to the court and served within 14 days after the decision is made known; objections, with an alternate form, are due within 5 days after service. Every judgment must be on a separate document naming all parties in the caption, and a judgment is entered when the signed form is filed with the clerk. A timely Rule 54(d)(2) fee motion can be","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 59. New trial; altering or amending a judgment","Wyoming · civil rules","Wyoming lists 8 specific grounds for a new trial (irregularity, misconduct, accident or surprise, excessive damages from passion or prejudice, error in the amount, insufficient evidence or contrary to law, newly discovered evidence, and error of law at trial). Motions for new trial and to alter or amend are due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment; opposing affidavits within 14 days (extendable up to 21 days","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 60. Relief from a judgment or order","Wyoming · civil rules","Clerical mistakes may be corrected anytime, but only with Supreme Court leave once an appeal is docketed and pending. Rule 60(b) grounds mirror the federal rule; motions must be made within a reasonable time, and for mistake, newly discovered evidence, or fraud no more than 1 year after entry. The motion does not affect finality; independent actions and fraud-on-the-court relief are preserved.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 61. Harmless error","Wyoming · civil rules","Errors that do not affect substantial rights are disregarded and are not grounds for a new trial or for disturbing a judgment.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 62. Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","Wyoming · civil rules","No execution until 14 days after entry of judgment (the federal automatic stay is 30 days), except injunctions and receiverships, which are not stayed unless ordered. The court may stay execution pending Rule 50, 52(b), 59 or 60 motions on terms. On appeal, a stay is obtained by a supersedeas bond, effective when the court approves it, subject to the Wyoming Rules of Appellate Procedure; no bond is required of the St","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 62.1. Indicative ruling while an appeal is pending","Wyoming · civil rules","If an appeal is docketed and pending, the trial court may defer, deny, or state it would grant the motion (or that it raises a substantial issue); the movant must promptly notify the appellate clerk, and the trial court may decide the motion on remand.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 63. Judge’s inability to proceed","Wyoming · civil rules","A successor judge may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record; at a party’s request the successor must recall material, disputed witnesses in a hearing or bench trial. After verdict or findings, another judge in the district or a judge or justice designated by the Supreme Court may perform the remaining duties, or grant a new trial if unable to do so.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 64. Seizing a person or property","Wyoming · civil rules","All statutory remedies for seizing a person or property to secure satisfaction of the eventual judgment are available at the start of and during the action.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 65. Injunctions and restraining orders","Wyoming · civil rules","Preliminary injunctions require notice; a TRO without notice requires specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint and an attorney certification, expires in no more than 14 days unless extended for a like period, and may be challenged on 2 days’ notice. Security in an amount the court considers proper is required before any preliminary injunction or TRO. The rule does not apply to divorce, alimony, separate main","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 65.1. Proceedings against a surety","Wyoming · civil rules","A surety on a bond or undertaking submits to the court’s jurisdiction and appoints the clerk as agent for service; liability may be enforced by motion without a separate action.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 66. Receivers","Wyoming · civil rules","An action in which a receiver has been appointed may be dismissed only by court order; administration of estates by receivers follows prior Wyoming practice, and otherwise these rules apply.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 67. Deposit into court","Wyoming · civil rules","With leave and on notice, a party may deposit money or a thing in dispute; funds go into an interest-bearing account or court-approved instrument and are withdrawn by court order or written stipulation. Before disbursement the requesting party must supply payee information in a form that complies with the redaction rules.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 68. Offer of settlement or judgment","Wyoming · civil rules","Broader than FRCP 68: any party (not only a defending party) may serve an offer of settlement or judgment, at any time more than 60 days after service of the complaint and at least 28 days before trial. Acceptance within 14 days; an unaccepted offer is withdrawn and inadmissible except on costs. If the offeree’s final judgment is not more favorable, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer; ‘costs’ do not incl","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 69. Execution","Wyoming · civil rules","Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution unless the court directs otherwise; the judgment creditor (or a successor of record) may take discovery in aid of execution under these rules.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 70. Enforcing a judgment for a specific act","Wyoming · civil rules","If a party fails to perform a required act, the court may appoint another to do it at that party’s expense, divest and vest title to property within the district, issue writs of attachment, sequestration, execution or assistance, or hold the party in contempt.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 71. Enforcing relief for or against a nonparty","Wyoming · civil rules","Orders granting relief to, or enforceable against, a nonparty are enforced by the same procedure as for a party.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 71.1. Condemnation of property","Wyoming · civil rules","Wyoming eminent-domain procedure, keyed to W.S. 1-26-501 et seq. The complaint must describe the authority, use, necessity and property and the pre-suit negotiation efforts required by statute; an answer is needed only to contest the right to take or necessity and is due 5 days before the hearing. Show-cause hearings on immediate entry and hearings on the condemnation complaint are held no sooner than 15 days after s","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 77. District courts and clerks; notice of orders and judgments","Wyoming · civil rules","District courts are always open for filing; trials are in open court, and nonex parte hearings outside the county need the consent of all nondefaulted parties. The clerk must serve every nondefaulted party with each order or judgment immediately on entry (by mail, hand, clerk’s box, or electronically), but lack of notice does not extend the time to appeal except as the appellate rules allow. (There are no W.R.C.P. Ru","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 78. Hearing motions; decision on briefs","Wyoming · civil rules","A court may set regular times for motion hearings and may decide motions on briefs without oral hearing.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 79. Books and records kept by the clerk","Wyoming · civil rules","The clerk keeps the books and records required by statute and any others required by the Supreme Court or the district judge.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 80. Stenographic transcript as evidence","Wyoming · civil rules","When previously reported testimony is admissible at a later trial, it may be proved by a transcript certified by the reporter.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 81. Applicability in general","Wyoming · civil rules","Statutes inconsistent with these rules do not apply, except that in special statutory proceedings a rule does not apply where clearly inapplicable, and where a statute creating a special proceeding sets form, content, or timing of papers, either the statute or these rules may be followed.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 82. Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","Wyoming · civil rules","The rules do not extend or limit district court jurisdiction or venue.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 83. Uniform rules; judge’s directives","Wyoming · civil rules","A court conference may adopt uniform rules with Supreme Court approval, and no court may adopt procedural rules applicable only in that court (no individual local rules). A judge may regulate practice consistently with law, but no sanction may be imposed for violating a requirement not in statute, state rules, or the uniform rules unless the party had actual notice of it in the case.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 84. Forms","Wyoming · civil rules","No forms are provided with these rules (an Appendix of Forms is published separately on the court website).","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 85. Title","Wyoming · civil rules","Cited as the Wyoming Rules of Civil Procedure, W.R.C.P.","#st-wy-rules"],["Rule 86. Effective dates","Wyoming · civil rules","The current rules took effect March 1, 2017, for new and pending cases unless infeasible or unjust. Amendments take effect on dates the Supreme Court sets, or, if none is set, 60 days after publication in the Pacific Reporter advance sheets.","#st-wy-rules"],["Appeals in Wyoming: one appellate court, a jurisdictional 30-day notice, and deemed-denied post-trial motions","Wyoming · appeals","Wyoming has no intermediate appellate court. Final judgments and appealable orders of the district courts and the chancery court go straight to the Wyoming Supreme Court. District courts sit as appellate courts for circuit courts, municipal courts, and agencies; their final judgments can then be appealed to the Supreme Court.\nOnly one thing is jurisdictional: filing a compliant notice of appeal in the trial court wit","#st-wy-appeals"],["Final judgments and ‘appealable orders’","Wyoming · appeals · What can be appealed","Appealable orders are:\n• an order affecting a substantial right that in effect determines the action and prevents a judgment;\n• an order affecting a substantial right made in a special proceeding;\n• an order on a summary application after judgment;\n• an order (including a conditional one) granting a new trial under W.R.C.P. 59(a)(4) or (5); and\n• interlocutory orders granting, continuing, modifying, dissolving, or re","#st-wy-appeals"],["Partial judgments need Rule 54(b) certification","Wyoming · appeals · What can be appealed","When multiple claims or parties are involved, an order resolving fewer than all of them is final only if the court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay (W.R.C.P. 54(b)). Without that, the order can be reviewed only by writ of review or after final judgment. A decision letter is not a judgment (W.R.C.P. 54(a)). W.R.C.P. 54(a)-(b); W.R.A.P. 13.02","#st-wy-appeals"],["Change-of-judge rulings wait for the final appeal","Wyoming · appeals · What can be appealed","A ruling on a motion to change the district judge, whether peremptory or for cause, is not an appealable order. It is entered on the docket and may be raised as error on appeal from the final judgment. W.R.C.P. 40.1(b)(3)","#st-wy-appeals"],["30 days, filed in the trial court, copy to the appellate clerk","Wyoming · appeals · Notice of appeal","File the notice with the trial-court clerk within 30 days from entry of the appealable order, and serve it under W.R.C.P. 5. At the same time, serve a copy on the clerk of the appellate court. In criminal cases, also serve the public defender and the attorney general, and serve any guardian ad litem.\nTimely filing of a notice that complies with Rule 2.07(a) is jurisdictional. W.R.A.P. 2.01(a), 1.03(a)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Contents and civil appendix","Wyoming · appeals · Notice of appeal","The notice must:\n• name the appealing parties;\n• identify the judgment or order (or the portion) appealed;\n• name the court appealed to;\n• carry the transcript certificate required by Rule 2.05(a); and\n• show which counsel represents which parties when there are several.\nIn civil cases, add an appendix that lists (does not attach):\n• all pleadings asserting claims or adding or removing parties;\n• all orders disposing","#st-wy-appeals"],["Fees at filing","Wyoming · appeals · Notice of appeal","When filing the notice, deliver to the trial-court clerk the appellate docket fee and the trial clerk’s fee for preparing the record, or file a motion to proceed in forma pauperis. The Supreme Court’s docket fee for an appeal or any original proceeding, including writs of review and certifications, is $140 (Rules of the Supreme Court of Wyoming, May 2022 compilation).\nIn civil cases, IFP status on appeal is available","#st-wy-appeals"],["Extension for excusable neglect: apply before day 45","Wyoming · appeals · Notice of appeal","The trial court may extend the time to appeal for excusable neglect, but the application must be filed before 45 days from entry of the order have passed. File with it a proposed notice of appeal and the docket fee (or an IFP motion). If the court does not grant the extension within 14 days after the application is filed, it is deemed denied. W.R.A.P. 2.01(a)(1)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Cross-appeals","Wyoming · appeals · Notice of appeal","Once a timely notice is filed, any other party may file its own notice within the original 30 days or within 15 days after the first notice was filed. A separate notice is needed for each order you challenge. Amended notices may correct only clerical errors, or bring up a later order amending the one appealed or an order on a pending post-trial motion. W.R.A.P. 2.01(a)(2), (c)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Premature notices","Wyoming · appeals · Notice of appeal","A notice filed after the court announces a decision but before the judgment or order is entered is treated as filed on, and after, the date of entry. W.R.A.P. 2.04","#st-wy-appeals"],["Which motions toll","Wyoming · appeals · Post-trial motions that stop the clock","The time to appeal stops running for all parties when a party timely files one of these:\n• a Rule 50(b) motion for judgment;\n• a Rule 52(b) motion to amend or make additional findings;\n• a Rule 59 motion to alter or amend; or\n• a Rule 59 motion for a new trial.\nThe chancery-court equivalents also toll. Each motion is due 28 days after entry of judgment, and the court cannot extend that time.\nA bare ‘motion to reconsi","#st-wy-appeals"],["Deemed denial at 90 days","Wyoming · appeals · Post-trial motions that stop the clock","A motion under Rule 50(b), (c)(2), 52(b), 59, or 60(b) that is not decided within 90 days after filing is deemed denied. The only exception is an order, entered within that period, continuing the determination for no more than 60 days.\nThe full 30-day appeal time runs from the order granting or denying the motion or, if no order is entered, from the deemed denial. W.R.C.P. 6(c)(4); W.R.A.P. 2.02(b)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Notice filed while a motion is pending","Wyoming · appeals · Post-trial motions that stop the clock","A notice of appeal filed after judgment but before the last tolling motion is resolved takes effect when the final order disposing of that motion is entered, or when the motion is deemed denied. To challenge the ruling on the motion itself, file an amended notice within the Rule 2.01 time; no extra fee is required. W.R.A.P. 2.02(c)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Order the transcript with the notice","Wyoming · appeals · Record and transcript","When filing the notice, order the transcript and arrange payment with the court reporter, or file an IFP motion. Certify this on the notice. If no transcript will be ordered, say so and say whether you will seek a statement of evidence (Rule 3.03) or an agreed statement (Rule 3.08).\nIn civil cases, also file and serve a description of the transcript parts you are ordering and, unless ordering the whole transcript, a ","#st-wy-appeals"],["Reporter’s deadline","Wyoming · appeals · Record and transcript","The court reporter files the transcript in the trial court within 60 days after the notice of appeal. The trial court may extend that for good cause on a specific motion. The reporter then notifies the appellate court and parties. Condensed transcripts are not allowed in the Supreme Court. W.R.A.P. 2.06, 3.02(d)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Designate the record with your brief","Wyoming · appeals · Record and transcript","The appellant files a designation of the record parts it relies on in the trial court at the same time as filing its brief. The appellee designates any additions with its brief, and the appellant may add more by the reply deadline.\nIn civil appeals, do not designate the entire record without an appellate court order, and leave out discovery papers, subpoenas, and setting notices unless they are relevant. Failure to d","#st-wy-appeals"],["Docketing and jurisdiction","Wyoming · appeals · Record and transcript","The appeal is docketed when the trial clerk certifies that the record is complete and forwards the docket fee or IFP order. The appellate clerk then serves a docketing notice that sets the briefing schedule. The appellate court acquires jurisdiction on docketing. The trial court keeps jurisdiction over matters not on appeal, including bonds and stays. W.R.A.P. 6.01(a)-(b)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Supersedeas bond set by the trial court","Wyoming · appeals · Stays and bonds","To stay execution, present a supersedeas bond to the trial court. For an unsecured money judgment, the bond covers the unsatisfied judgment, costs on appeal, and interest, unless the court, after notice and hearing and for good cause, sets another amount or other security. Executors, administrators, and guardians must give a bond.\nUnder W.R.C.P. 62(a), the automatic stay of execution lasts only 14 days after judgment","#st-wy-appeals"],["Schedule: 45 / 45 / 15","Wyoming · appeals · Briefs","The appellant’s brief is due 45 days after service of the docketing notice. The appellee’s brief is due 45 days after service of the appellant’s brief. An optional reply is due 15 days after service of the appellee’s brief.\nThe court may shorten the schedule. In termination of parental rights, adoption, abuse and neglect, delinquency, and CHINS cases, the court will not entertain extension motions. W.R.A.P. 7.06","#st-wy-appeals"],["Extensions are hard to get","Wyoming · appeals · Briefs","Only the appellate court can extend briefing time, on a motion certifying good cause filed before the deadline and at least 3 working days before the due date. A later motion must explain why it could not be filed earlier.\nGood cause means things like a death in counsel’s immediate family or serious illness. ‘Counsel is too busy’ is not enough. Absent extraordinary circumstances, the court will not consider extension","#st-wy-appeals"],["Page limits and format","Wyoming · appeals · Briefs","Principal briefs may not exceed 60 pages and reply briefs 18 pages, excluding the title page, tables, certificate of service, and appendices. Amicus briefs are limited to 35 pages.\nFormat: 8½ x 11 pages, 1-inch margins, double spacing (except quotations over 50 words), and 13-point minimum type, with footnotes in the same size. Bind only at the upper left corner. W.R.A.P. 7.05, 7.12(d)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Required contents","Wyoming · appeals · Briefs","The appellant’s brief contains, in order:\n• a title page (with the Wyoming Bar number);\n• a table of contents and a table of authorities;\n• a jurisdictional statement covering finality and timeliness;\n• the issues and the statement of the case, with record cites;\n• the argument, with a standard of review for each issue;\n• a conclusion stating the precise relief sought;\n• signature and a certificate of service; and\n• ","#st-wy-appeals"],["Amicus curiae","Wyoming · appeals · Briefs","Amicus briefs are allowed only by leave of court. The motion, with the proposed brief attached, is due within 11 days after the principal brief of the party supported is filed, or within 11 days after the first party brief for an amicus supporting neither side. Amici may not file reply briefs, and they may argue only with the court’s permission and the supported party’s consent. W.R.A.P. 7.12","#st-wy-appeals"],["Brief-only docket or oral argument","Wyoming · appeals · Argument, decision, rehearing, mandate","The Supreme Court assigns each case to either the brief-only docket (submitted without argument) or the oral argument docket. A party may move, with good cause, within 15 days after a brief-only assignment to have the case reassigned for argument.\nEach side gets 30 minutes unless the court orders otherwise. A motion for more time is due at least 15 days before argument. Argument by videoconference is allowed only in ","#st-wy-appeals"],["Rehearing: 15 days","Wyoming · appeals · Argument, decision, rehearing, mandate","A petition for rehearing, with a supporting brief (they may be combined), is due no later than 15 days after the decision and must be served within that time. There is no oral argument unless the court requests it. A timely petition suspends proceedings under the decision. If rehearing is granted, the other party answers within 15 days. W.R.A.P. 9.08-9.10","#st-wy-appeals"],["Mandate and costs","Wyoming · appeals · Argument, decision, rehearing, mandate","The mandate issues when rehearing is denied, or if no petition or other motion is filed within 15 days after the decision. Motions for costs or fees are due within 15 days after the final opinion or order is filed. The court cannot hear a costs motion after the mandate issues.\nIf the court certifies there was no reasonable cause for the appeal, it may award the appellee attorney’s fees of $100 to $10,000 plus up to $","#st-wy-appeals"],["Further review","Wyoming · appeals · Argument, decision, rehearing, mandate","The Supreme Court’s decision is final on questions of state law. Only federal questions can go further, by certiorari to the U.S. Supreme Court. Wyoming Judicial Branch, Supreme Court page (wyocourts.gov)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Petition for writ of review: 15 days","Wyoming · appeals · Interlocutory review and original writs","Interlocutory or extraordinary relief from a district court or chancery court order is sought in the Supreme Court by a petition for a writ of review. Habeas corpus, mandamus, prohibition, and quo warranto are treated as writs of review.\nThe petition is due within 15 days after entry of the order, with the docket fee. Responses are due within 15 days after filing. The court may grant the petition after day 30 from th","#st-wy-appeals"],["Standard for interlocutory review","Wyoming · appeals · Interlocutory review and original writs","A writ of review may issue for an interlocutory order that is not otherwise appealable but involves a controlling question of law with substantial grounds for difference of opinion, where an immediate appeal may materially advance the litigation. Granting it is discretionary. W.R.A.P. 13.02, 13.01(a)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Certified questions","Wyoming · appeals · Interlocutory review and original writs","The Supreme Court may answer questions of law certified by a federal court, the chancery court, or a district court. The question must be potentially determinative, with no controlling Wyoming Supreme Court precedent. Any party may move for certification. No mandate issues after the answer. W.R.A.P. 11.01, 11.02, 9.07","#st-wy-appeals"],["Petition for review in district court: 30 days","Wyoming · appeals · Review of agency action","Judicial review of agency action starts with a petition for review in the district court with venue. The petition is due within 30 days after service of the final agency decision, or of the denial of rehearing or the decision after rehearing, even if a statute allows a different time. No summons is needed.\nFor excusable neglect, the district court may extend the time by up to 30 days. Cross-petitions are due within 1","#st-wy-appeals"],["Certification straight to the Supreme Court","Wyoming · appeals · Review of agency action","Within 30 days after the petition is filed, any party may move to have the district court certify the case to the Supreme Court (responses are due in 15 days). Grounds include a novel or constitutional question, statewide impact, or a likely appeal anyway.\nThe Supreme Court accepts or rejects a certified case within 30 days after receiving the order. The district court’s final judgment on review can be appealed to th","#st-wy-appeals"],["E-filing in the Supreme Court","Wyoming · appeals · Filing, service, and time","Briefs, motions, and other papers are e-filed through the C-Track Electronic Filing System (CTEF), and the electronic version is the official filing. Until otherwise ordered, also file paper copies: the original and 6 copies in the Supreme Court, or the original and 2 in the district court. District-court filings need a proposed order; Supreme Court filings must not include one.\nEmail filing is prohibited. Nonconform","#st-wy-appeals"],["Computing time","Wyoming · appeals · Filing, service, and time","Exclude the day of the event and include the last day. If the last day is a weekend, a legal holiday, or a day the clerk’s office is closed by weather, the deadline moves to the next business day.\nAdd 3 days when a paper is served by mail. Nothing is added for service through the court’s e-filing system, or when the period runs from a filing or a fixed date. W.R.A.P. 14.02, 14.03","#st-wy-appeals"],["Motions in the appellate court","Wyoming · appeals · Filing, service, and time","A motion that may substantially affect the disposition must be supported by a memorandum of points and authorities. The opposing party may respond within 15 days after service. The court may decide motions without argument. W.R.A.P. 16(b)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Notice of appeal (civil or criminal)","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the appealable order or judgment.\nJurisdictional. File with the trial-court clerk, and serve a copy on the appellate clerk at the same time. On a showing of excusable neglect the trial court may extend the time, but only if the application is filed within 45 days of entry of the appealable order (W.R.A.P. 2.01(a)(1)). W.R.A.P. 2.01(a), 1.03(a)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Application to extend time to appeal (excusable neglect)","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","45 days from entry of the appealable order.\nMust be filed before day 45, with a proposed notice and the docket fee or an IFP motion. Deemed denied if not granted within 14 days of filing. W.R.A.P. 2.01(a)(1)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Trial court order on extension application (else deemed denied)","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","14 days from filing of the extension application.\nIf denied or deemed denied, the docket fee is refunded. W.R.A.P. 2.01(a)(1)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Cross-appeal / other party’s notice of appeal","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the first notice of appeal.\nOr within the original 30 days, whichever is later. W.R.A.P. 2.01(a)(2)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Tolling motion: Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59 new trial or alter/amend","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","28 days from entry of judgment.\nThe court cannot extend this time (W.R.C.P. 6(b)(2)). W.R.C.P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e); W.R.A.P. 2.02(a)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Post-trial motion deemed denied","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","90 days from filing of the Rule 50(b), (c)(2), 52(b), 59 or 60(b) motion.\nUnless continued by order within the 90 days, for no more than 60 more days. The 30-day appeal time then runs from the deemed denial. W.R.C.P. 6(c)(4)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Notice of appeal after tolling motion","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order deciding the last tolling motion, or its deemed denial.\nA notice filed earlier takes effect on disposition. To challenge the ruling on the motion, file an amended notice within this time. W.R.A.P. 2.02(b), 2.01(a)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Pay docket fee after IFP denial","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from entry of the order denying in forma pauperis status.\nIf the fee is not paid, the appeal does not proceed. W.R.A.P. 2.09(c)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Appellee orders additional transcript (civil)","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from service of appellant’s partial-transcript designation.\nOr obtain a trial-court order directing the appellant to order those parts. W.R.A.P. 3.02(b)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Court reporter files transcript","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","60 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nThe trial court may extend it for good cause on a specific motion. W.R.A.P. 2.06(a)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Appellant’s brief","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","45 days from service of the notice that the appeal is docketed.\nFile the record designation in the trial court at the same time (W.R.A.P. 3.05(b)). W.R.A.P. 7.06(b)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Appellee’s brief","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","45 days from service of the appellant’s brief.\nFile any additional record designation at the same time. W.R.A.P. 7.06(c)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Reply brief","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\nLimited to new issues. Extensions are not considered absent extraordinary circumstances. W.R.A.P. 7.06(d), 7.03","#st-wy-appeals"],["Motion to extend briefing time","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","3 days from before the brief’s due date (working days).\nMust certify good cause. A later motion must explain the delay. W.R.A.P. 7.10(a)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Amicus motion with proposed brief","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","11 days from filing of the principal brief of the party supported.\nAn amicus supporting neither side: 11 days after the first party brief. W.R.A.P. 7.12(e)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Motion to move case from brief-only docket to argument","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from entry of the order assigning the brief-only docket.\nRequires good cause and is discretionary. W.R.A.P. 8.01(c)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Motion for extra oral argument time","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from before oral argument.\nDefault is 30 minutes per side. W.R.A.P. 8.02(a)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Motion for oral argument by videoconference","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","20 days from before the scheduled argument date.\nExceptional circumstances only. Later motions are allowed only for weather or unforeseen events. W.R.A.P. 8.01(e)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Response to an appellate motion","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from service of the motion.\nApplies to motions that may substantially affect disposition. W.R.A.P. 16(b)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Petition for rehearing","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from date the decision is rendered.\nFile and serve within the period. Suspends proceedings (W.R.A.P. 9.09). W.R.A.P. 9.08(a)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Answer brief after rehearing granted","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from entry of the order granting rehearing. W.R.A.P. 9.10","#st-wy-appeals"],["Mandate issues if no rehearing petition","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from decision.\nAfter the mandate issues, the court cannot hear a costs motion (9.11(c)). W.R.A.P. 9.11(a)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Motion for appellate costs or fees","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the final written opinion or order.\nMay be extended for good cause or absent objection. W.R.A.P. 10.06","#st-wy-appeals"],["Petition for writ of review (interlocutory/extraordinary)","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from entry of the order from which relief is sought.\nFiled on paper with the docket fee. Does not stay the lower court unless ordered (13.06). W.R.A.P. 13.03(a)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Response to petition for writ of review","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the petition. W.R.A.P. 13.03(b)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Writ of review petition deemed denied","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","40 days from filing of the petition.\nIf the reviewing court has not accepted review. W.R.A.P. 13.03(c)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Petition for review of agency action (district court)","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from service of the final agency decision (or of the denial of rehearing / decision after rehearing).\nApplies even where a statute sets a different time. Extension of up to 30 more days for excusable neglect (12.04(b)). W.R.A.P. 12.04(a)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Cross-petition for review of agency action","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","15 days from filing of the first petition for review. W.R.A.P. 12.04(c)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Motion to certify agency-review case to Supreme Court","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","30 days from filing of the petition for review.\n15 days to respond. The Supreme Court accepts or rejects within 30 days of receiving the certification order (12.09(d)). W.R.A.P. 12.09(b)","#st-wy-appeals"],["Extra time after service by mail","Wyoming · appeals · Deadlines","3 days from added to periods running from mail service.\nNot added for service through the e-filing system or for periods running from filing or a fixed date. W.R.A.P. 14.03","#st-wy-appeals"]]);
